var e=[];e.push({id:`respiratory-examination`,title:`Respiratory examination: inspection, palpation, percussion, auscultation and the signs that localise`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`The respiratory examination is a **structured bedside sequence - general survey, hands, neck, inspection, palpation, percussion and auscultation** - whose findings (tracheal position, expansion, percussion note, breath sounds, added sounds and vocal resonance) are combined to **localise the lesion and name the pathology**: consolidation, collapse, effusion, pneumothorax, fibrosis or hyperinflation.`,keywords:[`chest examination`,`clubbing`,`Schamroth sign`,`Lovibond angle`,`tracheal deviation`,`Trail sign`,`Hoover sign`,`tactile vocal fremitus`,`percussion note`,`stony dull`,`bronchial breathing`,`crackles`,`wheeze`,`pleural rub`,`aegophony`,`whispering pectoriloquy`,`vocal resonance`,`Skodaic resonance`,`Traube space`,`long case respiratory`],sections:[{heading:`Definition, purpose and why it matters in India`,points:[`The **respiratory examination** answers four questions in order: is the patient in distress, is the disease **unilateral or bilateral**, is the **mediastinum shifted** and which way, and is the underlying lesion **solid, fluid, air or scar**. [Macleod 15e]`,`**Chronic respiratory diseases** rose from about 4.5% of all DALYs in India in 1990 to about **6.4% in 2016**, with **COPD** contributing roughly three-quarters and **asthma** about one-fifth of that burden. [GBD India, Lancet Glob Health 2018]`,`India notified over **26 lakh TB cases in 2024**, and much of the long-case material in Indian exams is **post-TB fibrosis, bronchiectasis, effusion and collapse** - so the signs table is examined almost every session. [India TB Report 2025]`,`**Bedside signs are insensitive but specific**: in community-acquired pneumonia, bronchial breathing and dullness are present in only about a third to a half of radiologically proven cases, so a normal chest does not exclude pneumonia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Inter-observer agreement** is good for wheeze and tracheal deviation but only fair for crackles and percussion dullness, which is why examiners want the sign described precisely (site, timing, character) rather than just named. [JAMA Rational Clinical Examination]`,`The examination is done with the patient **sitting at 45 degrees** for the front and **sitting forward with arms folded** for the back, chest fully exposed, with a chaperone, good light and the examiner on the right. [Macleod 15e]`,`**Always compare side to side at the same level** (supraclavicular, infraclavicular, mammary, axillary, infra-axillary, suprascapular, interscapular, infrascapular) - asymmetry is the sign, not the absolute finding. [Hutchison 25e]`]},{heading:`General survey, vital signs and the bedside clues`,points:[`**Count the respiratory rate for a full 60 seconds** without telling the patient: normal adult rate is **12-20/min**; 21-24 scores 2 and **25 or more scores 3** on NEWS2, and a rate over 30 is a marker of severe illness. [RCP NEWS2 2017]`,`Look around the bed for **inhalers, spacer, nebuliser, oxygen concentrator, sputum mug (colour, volume, blood), ATT blister packs and a peak flow meter** - each is a free clue to the diagnosis. [Macleod 15e]`,`**Signs of respiratory distress**: tachypnoea, use of **accessory muscles** (sternocleidomastoid, scalenes, trapezius), **tripod position**, intercostal and suprasternal recession, nasal flaring, inability to complete sentences and **paradoxical abdominal movement** (diaphragm fatigue). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pursed-lip breathing** in emphysema creates positive end-expiratory pressure that splints small airways open and reduces air trapping. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Central cyanosis** (tongue and lips) appears when deoxygenated haemoglobin exceeds about **5 g/dL in capillaries** (roughly SpO2 under 85%); it is late, missed in anaemia and exaggerated in polycythaemia - use the oximeter. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Flapping tremor (asterixis)**, bounding pulse, warm peripheries, papilloedema and drowsiness indicate **CO2 retention** - check an ABG and avoid uncontrolled high-flow oxygen. [Macleod 15e]`,`**Pulsus paradoxus over 10 mmHg** (fall in systolic BP on inspiration) is seen in severe acute asthma and tamponade; its absence does not exclude severe asthma in an exhausted patient. [OHCM 11e]`,`Look for **Horner syndrome** (apical Pancoast tumour), **hoarseness** (left recurrent laryngeal nerve - left hilar or aortic lesion), **SVC obstruction** (distended non-pulsatile neck veins, facial plethora) and **cervical or supraclavicular lymph nodes** (TB, carcinoma, sarcoid). [Harrison 22e]`,`Check the **JVP and pedal oedema**: raised JVP with oedema in a chronic lung patient suggests **cor pulmonale**, with a parasternal heave and loud P2 of pulmonary hypertension. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology: how the signs are generated`,points:[`**Normal (vesicular) breath sounds** are generated by turbulent flow in the large and segmental airways; the aerated lung acts as a **low-pass filter**, so what reaches the chest wall is soft, low-pitched and heard mainly in inspiration. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bronchial breathing** occurs when airless lung (consolidation, collapse with a patent bronchus, fibrosis, compressed lung above an effusion) **conducts high-frequency sound from a patent bronchus** without filtering it - so both phases are heard, expiration is long and there is a gap. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diminished sounds** mean something stops sound reaching the wall: fluid or air in the pleura, a **blocked bronchus**, hyperinflated lung, obesity or a thick chest wall. [Macleod 15e]`,`**Tactile fremitus and vocal resonance** follow the same physics as breath sounds: solid lung with an open bronchus conducts better (increased); fluid, air or an occluded bronchus reflect sound away (decreased). [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Percussion** sets the chest wall vibrating to a depth of about **5-7 cm**; air-filled lung gives resonance, solid lung gives dullness, fluid gives **stony dullness** and excess air gives hyperresonance - deep lesions are therefore missed. [Macleod 15e]`,`**Crackles** are explosive equalisation of pressure as **closed small airways snap open** in inspiration (fine, late - fibrosis, oedema) or as air bubbles through secretions in larger airways (coarse, early - bronchiectasis, chronic bronchitis). [ERS Task Force 2016]`,`**Wheeze** is produced by **airway wall flutter** when a narrowed airway approaches flow limitation; pitch depends on the airway's mass and stiffness, not its calibre, so polyphonic wheeze means many airways and monophonic means one. [ERS Task Force 2016]`,`**Clubbing** is believed to follow **megakaryocytes and platelet clumps** that bypass the pulmonary capillary filter (shunt or tumour) and lodge in fingertip capillaries, releasing **PDGF and VEGF** that cause connective tissue proliferation and vascular hyperplasia. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Hands and clubbing: grading and causes`,points:[`**Clubbing** is selective bulbous enlargement of the distal digit from soft-tissue proliferation, defined by loss of the nail-fold angle; the **Lovibond (profile) angle** is normally about 160 degrees and **over 180 degrees** is clubbing. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Phalangeal depth ratio**: distal phalangeal depth exceeding interphalangeal joint depth (ratio **over 1**) is the most reproducible objective sign of clubbing. [JAMA Rational Clinical Examination]`,`**Schamroth window test**: the normal diamond-shaped window between apposed dorsal surfaces of opposite index fingers is **obliterated** in clubbing. [Macleod 15e]`,`**Grading of clubbing**: grade 1 nail-bed fluctuation and softening, grade 2 loss of the nail-fold angle, grade 3 increased convexity (parrot-beak or drumstick), **grade 4 hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy** (HPOA). [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Respiratory causes** to list: **lung cancer (especially NSCLC)**, bronchiectasis, lung abscess, empyema, **idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis** and asbestosis, cystic fibrosis and mesothelioma. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Clubbing is not a feature of COPD or asthma**: in a smoker with COPD, new clubbing must be taken as **lung cancer** until proved otherwise; in TB it suggests complicating bronchiectasis, empyema or abscess. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Non-respiratory causes**: cyanotic congenital heart disease, **infective endocarditis**, atrial myxoma, cirrhosis, inflammatory bowel disease, coeliac disease, thyroid acropachy and familial clubbing; **unilateral clubbing** suggests an arterial lesion (subclavian aneurysm, AV fistula). [Harrison 22e]`,`**HPOA** presents with painful swelling of wrists and ankles, periosteal new bone on the long bones on X-ray and clubbing; it is most often due to **lung adenocarcinoma** and may regress after resection. [Harrison 22e]`,`Other hand signs: **tar (nicotine) staining**, peripheral cyanosis, **fine tremor** of beta-agonist use, flapping tremor of CO2 retention, wasting of small hand muscles (**Pancoast tumour - T1 root**), and rheumatoid or sclerodermatous changes (CTD-ILD). [Macleod 15e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features: the respiratory history before the examination`,points:[`The **cardinal symptoms** are cough, sputum, haemoptysis, breathlessness, wheeze, chest pain and fever; record onset, duration, progression and triggers for each. [Harrison 22e]`,`Grade breathlessness with the **mMRC scale (0-4)**: grade 0 only on strenuous exercise, **grade 2 walks slower than peers or stops at own pace**, grade 4 too breathless to leave the house or breathless on dressing. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Sputum**: mucoid in asthma and chronic bronchitis, purulent in infection, **large-volume and layered** in bronchiectasis and lung abscess, rusty in pneumococcal pneumonia, pink frothy in pulmonary oedema. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pleuritic chest pain** (sharp, worse on breathing and coughing) localises the lesion to the parietal pleura - pneumonia, PE, pneumothorax or pleurisy; the visceral pleura and lung are insensitive. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cough over 2 weeks** in India mandates **sputum for NAAT (CBNAAT/Truenat)** under NTEP regardless of the examination findings. [NTEP 2024]`,`Ask the **exposure history**: smoking in **pack-years** (cigarettes per day divided by 20, times years), bidi and hookah use, **biomass fuel exposure** in women (hours per day, years), occupation (silica, coal, asbestos, cotton), birds and moulds, and travel. [GOLD 2025]`,`Ask the **past history**: TB and its treatment (completed or interrupted), childhood measles or whooping cough (bronchiectasis), atopy, previous intubation, HIV status and drug history (amiodarone, methotrexate, nitrofurantoin). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Orthopnoea and paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea** point to heart failure; **platypnoea** (worse upright) to hepatopulmonary syndrome or intracardiac shunt; **trepopnoea** (worse lying on one side) to unilateral lung or pleural disease. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Inspection of the chest`,points:[`Note the **shape**: **barrel chest** (AP to lateral ratio approaching 1:1, normal about 1:2 to 5:7) in hyperinflation; pectus excavatum and carinatum; kyphoscoliosis, which can cause restrictive respiratory failure. [Macleod 15e]`,`Look for **localised flattening or retraction** (fibrosis, collapse, old thoracoplasty) and **fullness of the hemithorax with widened intercostal spaces** (massive effusion or tension pneumothorax). [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Scars**: thoracotomy, intercostal drain sites in the mid-axillary line, **thoracoplasty** (old TB surgery), sternotomy, and radiotherapy tattoo marks point straight to the past history. [Macleod 15e]`,`**Chest movement**: inspect from the foot of the bed and from behind looking down over the shoulders - the **side that moves less is the side of disease**. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Hoover sign**: paradoxical **inward movement of the lower costal margins** on inspiration because a flattened diaphragm pulls the ribs in - a sign of severe hyperinflation in COPD. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Abdominal paradox** (abdomen moves in on inspiration while the chest moves out) indicates **diaphragm weakness or fatigue** and impending ventilatory failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Breathing patterns**: Kussmaul (deep, sighing - metabolic acidosis), **Cheyne-Stokes** (crescendo-decrescendo with apnoea - heart failure, stroke), Biot (irregular clusters - brainstem lesion) and prolonged expiration (airflow obstruction). [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for **dilated veins over the chest wall** with flow downwards (SVC obstruction), **gynaecomastia**, a visible apex beat and **pulsation of a parasternal heave** (right ventricular hypertrophy). [Macleod 15e]`]},{heading:`Palpation: trachea, apex, expansion and tactile fremitus`,points:[`**Trachea**: feel with one finger in the suprasternal notch comparing the gaps on each side; it is **pulled towards** collapse and upper-lobe fibrosis and **pushed away** by massive effusion and tension pneumothorax. [Macleod 15e]`,`**Trail sign**: the sternal head of the sternocleidomastoid becomes prominent on the side to which the trachea is deviated. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Cricosternal distance** is normally 3-4 finger breadths; it is **reduced in hyperinflation**, and a **tracheal tug** (descent of the trachea with inspiration) is seen in severe COPD. [Macleod 15e]`,`**Tracheal deviation reflects the upper mediastinum, the apex beat the lower mediastinum** - lower-lobe collapse can shift the apex without moving the trachea, so always check both (with the caveat that cardiomegaly and scoliosis shift the apex too). [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Chest expansion**: thumbs meet in the midline with the hands on the lower ribs; **over 5 cm at the level of the 4th intercostal space** with a tape is normal; unilateral reduction localises the side of disease. [Macleod 15e]`,`**Bilaterally reduced expansion** occurs in COPD, diffuse fibrosis, **ankylosing spondylitis** and neuromuscular weakness; **upper-zone expansion** is tested from behind with hands over the shoulders. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Tactile vocal fremitus** (patient says 'ninety-nine' or 'ek, do, teen' while the ulnar border of the hand is placed at each level): increased in consolidation, decreased or absent over effusion, pneumothorax and collapse with a blocked bronchus. [Hutchison 25e]`,`Palpate for **tenderness** (rib fracture, costochondritis, Tietze syndrome), **subcutaneous emphysema** (crepitus - pneumothorax, pneumomediastinum, after chest drain) and **palpable rhonchi or pleural rub**. [Macleod 15e]`]},{heading:`Percussion: notes, landmarks and eponymous areas`,points:[`**Technique**: the middle finger of the left hand is pressed firmly on the intercostal space, struck with the tip of the right middle finger by a loose **wrist movement**, comparing the same point on each side; **direct percussion** on the clavicle tests the apices. [Macleod 15e]`,`**Five notes**: resonant (normal), **hyperresonant** (pneumothorax, emphysema, large bulla), impaired (thickening, early consolidation), **dull** (consolidation, collapse, fibrosis, mass) and **stony dull** (effusion). [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Normal upper border of liver dullness** is the **5th intercostal space in the right mid-clavicular line**; it moves down in hyperinflation, and **obliteration of cardiac dullness** is a sign of emphysema or left pneumothorax. [Macleod 15e]`,`**Kronig isthmus**: the band of resonance over the lung apex above the clavicle; it is narrowed in **apical TB fibrosis** and apical tumours. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Traube space**: the crescent of resonance in the left lower anterior chest over the gastric air bubble, bounded by the 6th rib, left costal margin and anterior axillary line; it becomes dull in **left pleural effusion** and splenomegaly. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Skodaic resonance**: a band of hyperresonance just above a pleural effusion where the compressed, relaxed lung gives a tympanitic note. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Grocco (Grocco-Rauchfuss) triangle**: a paravertebral triangle of dullness on the **opposite** side of a large effusion from displacement of the mediastinum; **Garland triangle** is an area of relative resonance with bronchial breathing on the same side between the spine and the upper border of the effusion. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Ellis S-shaped curve (Damoiseau line)**: the upper border of a moderate effusion is highest in the axilla and lower anteriorly and posteriorly, because of lung recoil and capillary action. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Shifting dullness** in the chest occurs only in **hydropneumothorax**; a free effusion alone does not shift on change of position at the bedside. [Hutchison 25e]`]},{heading:`Auscultation: breath sounds`,points:[`Use the **diaphragm** of the stethoscope, ask the patient to breathe in and out through the open mouth, and listen through **one full respiratory cycle** at each point, comparing sides. [Macleod 15e]`,`**Vesicular breath sound**: low-pitched, rustling, **inspiration longer than expiration (about 3:1) with no gap**, louder in inspiration - the normal sound over most of the chest. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Bronchial breath sound**: high-pitched, hollow, **expiration as long as or longer than inspiration with a gap between them** - normal only over the trachea and right interscapular area. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Tubular bronchial breathing** (high-pitched) is heard over **consolidation**, collapse with a patent bronchus and the compressed lung above an effusion. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Cavernous bronchial breathing** (low-pitched, hollow) is heard over a **large cavity communicating with a bronchus**, typically in TB or lung abscess. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Amphoric breathing** (high-pitched, with metallic overtones, like blowing across a bottle mouth) is heard over a **large thin-walled cavity or an open (bronchopleural fistula) pneumothorax**. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Diminished vesicular breath sounds** occur in emphysema, obesity, effusion, pneumothorax and collapse with a blocked bronchus; a **silent chest** in acute asthma is a life-threatening sign of minimal airflow. [GINA 2025]`,`**Prolonged expiratory phase** (expiration longer than inspiration with vesicular quality) is a bedside marker of **airflow obstruction**; a **forced expiratory time over 6 seconds** suggests obstruction. [JAMA Rational Clinical Examination]`]},{heading:`Added sounds and vocal resonance`,points:[`**ERS nomenclature** recommends the terms **wheeze** (continuous, musical, usually expiratory), **rhonchus** (low-pitched continuous sound), **fine crackles** and **coarse crackles** (discontinuous), and **stridor**, squawk and pleural friction rub. [ERS Task Force 2016]`,`**Polyphonic expiratory wheeze** is diffuse airflow obstruction (asthma, COPD); a **fixed monophonic wheeze** in one place is a localised obstruction - **tumour, endobronchial TB or foreign body** - and needs bronchoscopy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fine late-inspiratory crackles** ('Velcro') at both bases are typical of **IPF and asbestosis**; they also occur in **early pulmonary oedema** and resolving pneumonia, and they do not clear with coughing. [ERS Task Force 2016]`,`**Coarse early- and mid-inspiratory crackles** (often also expiratory) that change with coughing are typical of **bronchiectasis** and chronic bronchitis; **early inspiratory crackles** alone suggest COPD. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pleural friction rub**: creaking, leathery sound in both phases, localised, often with pleuritic pain, **unchanged by coughing** and abolished by breath-holding (a pericardial rub persists with breath-holding). [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Stridor** is a harsh, mainly **inspiratory** sound from **extrathoracic upper airway** narrowing (larynx, upper trachea) and is an emergency; a **squawk** (short late-inspiratory wheeze) is heard in hypersensitivity pneumonitis and bronchiolitis obliterans. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Vocal resonance** follows the same rules as fremitus: increased (**bronchophony**) over consolidation; **whispering pectoriloquy** (clearly heard whispered words) over consolidation and small cavities; decreased over effusion, pneumothorax and blocked collapse. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Aegophony** ('E' heard as 'A', a nasal bleating quality) is heard over **consolidation** and at the **upper border of an effusion** where lung is compressed. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Other sounds**: **succussion splash** (hydropneumothorax), **coin test or bell-metal resonance** (pneumothorax), **Hamman sign** (crunching with the heartbeat - pneumomediastinum) and a **post-tussive suction** sound over a cavity. [Hutchison 25e]`]},{heading:`Putting it together: differential diagnosis by the signs that localise`,points:[`**Mediastinum shifted towards the lesion** with dullness: **collapse** (lobar or whole lung) or **fibrosis** (upper-lobe TB fibrosis, post-pneumonectomy). [Macleod 15e]`,`**Mediastinum shifted away** with stony dullness: **massive effusion**; with hyperresonance: **tension pneumothorax**; with dullness and absent sounds and no shift in a big hemithorax opacity, think **collapse plus effusion** or a mesothelioma fixing the mediastinum. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Mediastinum central**, dull note with bronchial breathing and increased resonance: **consolidation**; central with dull note, reduced sounds and reduced resonance: **pleural thickening** or a small to moderate effusion. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Bronchial breathing has four causes** to recite: consolidation, collapse with a patent bronchus, fibrosis, and the compressed lung over an effusion; **cavity** adds cavernous or amphoric variants. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Bilateral basal dullness**: bilateral effusions (heart failure, nephrotic syndrome, hypoalbuminaemia, malignancy) versus raised hemidiaphragms (obesity, ascites, bilateral diaphragm palsy). [Macleod 15e]`,`**Bilateral fine basal crackles with clubbing** suggest IPF or asbestosis; **without clubbing** consider pulmonary oedema, CTD-ILD or hypersensitivity pneumonitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Upper-zone signs** (flattening, deviation of trachea towards, bronchial breathing, coarse crackles) in India are **post-TB fibrosis or active TB** until proved otherwise; other causes are silicosis, sarcoidosis, ABPA and ankylosing spondylitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Normal chest examination with breathlessness**: think asthma between attacks, **pulmonary embolism**, early ILD, anaemia, pulmonary hypertension, metabolic acidosis, neuromuscular weakness and anxiety - see the breathlessness topic. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations that confirm the bedside findings`,points:[`**Pulse oximetry** at rest and after exertion (desaturation of **4% or more** on walking is significant); oximeters can **overestimate SpO2 in people with darker skin**, so treat borderline readings with caution. [BTS 2017; FDA 2025]`,`**Peak expiratory flow** at the bedside: best of three blows, compared with the patient's best or predicted - under 50% of best in acute asthma means a severe attack. [GINA 2025]`,`**Chest X-ray PA view** confirms the signs: a white hemithorax with trachea pulled towards is collapse, pushed away is effusion; the **silhouette sign** localises the lobe - see the chest imaging topic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Point-of-care lung ultrasound** extends the examination: absent lung sliding with a **lung point** (pneumothorax), anechoic fluid with a **spine sign** (effusion), multiple **B-lines** (interstitial oedema or fibrosis) and tissue-like lung with air bronchograms (consolidation). [Int Lung US Consensus 2023]`,`**Sputum examination**: Gram stain and culture, **NAAT for TB and rifampicin resistance** (CBNAAT/Truenat) as the first test under NTEP, and cytology where malignancy is suspected. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Spirometry** quantifies what the bedside suggests: obstruction (FEV1/FVC below LLN) in a barrel chest, restriction with low DLCO in bibasal fine crackles - see the PFT topic. [ATS/ERS 2022]`,`**ABG** if SpO2 is under 92%, the patient is drowsy, or CO2 retention is suspected from flap and bounding pulse - see the ABG topic. [BTS 2017]`,`**CT thorax** when examination and X-ray disagree or to characterise fibrosis (HRCT), cavity, bronchiectasis or mass. [Fleischner Society]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Children**: count the respiratory rate for 60 seconds - **fast breathing** is 60/min or more under 2 months, **50/min or more at 2-11 months** and **40/min or more at 1-5 years**; lower chest indrawing marks severe pneumonia. [WHO IMCI 2014]`,`**Infants and young children** have thin chest walls that transmit sounds widely, so breath sounds are louder and **bronchial breathing is less reliable**; use the bell or a paediatric diaphragm. [Nelson 22e]`,`**Elderly**: basal crackles that clear after a few deep breaths or coughs are often **atelectatic** and normal; kyphosis distorts the trachea and apex. [Macleod 15e]`,`**Obesity** dampens breath sounds and percussion, raises the diaphragm and can mimic basal dullness; examine in the sitting position. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pregnancy**: the respiratory rate is unchanged but tidal volume rises by about 30-40%, causing **physiological breathlessness**; the diaphragm rises by about 4 cm - basal crackles still need explanation. [Williams Obstetrics 26e]`,`**Ventilated and ICU patients**: unilateral absent breath sounds after intubation suggests **right main bronchus intubation**; sudden high airway pressure with hypotension and unilateral silence is tension pneumothorax until proved otherwise. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: a careful examination plus **SpO2, PEF and a chest X-ray** answers most questions at a PHC or district hospital without CT. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Red-flag signs and their prognostic meaning`,points:[`**Respiratory rate 30/min or more**, SpO2 under 92% on air, confusion or systolic BP under 90 mmHg mark severe illness and need for admission (CURB-65 and NEWS2 criteria). [BTS CAP 2009; RCP NEWS2 2017]`,`**Silent chest, cyanosis, exhaustion, drowsiness or bradycardia** in acute asthma are **life-threatening** features. [GINA 2025]`,`**Trachea deviated away from a hyperresonant, silent hemithorax with hypotension** is tension pneumothorax - decompress clinically, do not wait for an X-ray. [BTS Pleural 2023]`,`**Stridor** in an adult is upper airway obstruction (tumour, foreign body, laryngeal oedema, angio-oedema) and needs immediate ENT or airway assessment. [Harrison 22e]`,`**New clubbing, a fixed monophonic wheeze, hoarseness, Horner syndrome or supraclavicular nodes** in a smoker point to **lung cancer** and need urgent referral. [NICE NG12]`,`**Flapping tremor with drowsiness** in a COPD patient on oxygen suggests **hypercapnic respiratory failure** - target SpO2 88-92% and get an ABG. [BTS 2017]`,`**Paradoxical abdominal movement and use of accessory muscles** predict ventilatory failure and the need for non-invasive or invasive ventilation. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline and evidence updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**Pulse oximetry and skin pigmentation**: the US FDA issued **draft guidance in January 2025** requiring pulse oximeters to be tested across a wider range of skin tones (Monk Skin Tone scale), after evidence of **occult hypoxaemia** being missed more often in darker-skinned patients. [FDA 2025]`,`**BTS Pleural Disease Guideline 2023** emphasises **thoracic ultrasound** as an extension of the bedside examination before any pleural procedure and in suspected pneumothorax and effusion. [BTS Pleural 2023]`,`**International lung ultrasound consensus (2023)** standardises B-line, consolidation and pleural sliding definitions, supporting lung ultrasound as a bedside adjunct with higher sensitivity than auscultation for effusion and interstitial syndrome. [Int Lung US Consensus 2023]`,`**GOLD 2025** keeps the **mMRC breathlessness grade and CAT score** as the standard symptom assessment that must accompany the examination in COPD. [GOLD 2025]`,`**ERS lung sound nomenclature** (Task Force 2016) remains the current standard: examiners now expect **'wheeze' and 'crackles'**, not 'rhonchi' and 'crepitations', and 'rales' is discouraged. [ERS Task Force 2016]`,`**Digital and AI-assisted stethoscopes** with automated wheeze and crackle detection are under evaluation; none is yet recommended to replace clinical auscultation in guidelines. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Present in a fixed order**: general survey, hands, neck (trachea, nodes, JVP), then inspection, palpation, percussion, auscultation front and back, and end with a **summary that names the lesion, side and likely cause**. [Macleod 15e]`,`Classic viva: '**Causes of bronchial breathing**', '**causes of clubbing**', '**signs of collapse versus effusion**', '**where is Traube space**', and '**what is Skodaic resonance**'. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Common mistake**: calling crackles 'crepts' without timing - always say **fine or coarse, early or late inspiratory, and whether they change with coughing**. [ERS Task Force 2016]`,`**Common mistake**: declaring a 'massive effusion' when the trachea is central - a large opacity with a central or pulled trachea suggests **collapse with effusion, malignancy or a fixed mediastinum**. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Common mistake**: forgetting the **apex beat** for mediastinal shift and missing the **axillary area**, where middle-lobe and lingular signs are heard. [Macleod 15e]`,`Say what you would do next: **chest X-ray PA, sputum NAAT, SpO2 and spirometry**, then the specific test for the lesion found. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`In a long case with **post-TB sequelae**, describe both the **fibrosis (pulled trachea, upper-zone signs)** and any **bronchiectasis (coarse crackles, clubbing)** and **cor pulmonale (raised JVP, loud P2, oedema)**. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Signs that localise: the six classic chest lesions`,columns:[`Lesion`,`Movement and trachea`,`Percussion`,`Breath sounds`,`Fremitus and vocal resonance`,`Added sounds`],rows:[[`Consolidation`,`Reduced on affected side; trachea central`,`Dull`,`Bronchial (tubular)`,`Increased; whispering pectoriloquy, aegophony`,`Crackles; pleural rub`],[`Collapse with blocked bronchus`,`Reduced, flattened; trachea and apex pulled towards`,`Dull`,`Diminished or absent`,`Decreased`,`None`],[`Collapse with patent bronchus (upper lobe)`,`Reduced; trachea pulled towards`,`Dull`,`Bronchial`,`Increased`,`Occasional crackles`],[`Pleural effusion (large)`,`Reduced, fullness; trachea pushed away`,`Stony dull; Skodaic resonance above`,`Diminished or absent; bronchial at upper border`,`Decreased; aegophony at upper border`,`Rub occasionally`],[`Pneumothorax`,`Reduced; trachea pushed away if tension`,`Hyperresonant`,`Diminished or absent`,`Decreased`,`Coin test positive; click (left)`],[`Localised fibrosis (upper lobe, post-TB)`,`Reduced, flattened; trachea pulled towards`,`Impaired to dull`,`Bronchial`,`Increased`,`Coarse crackles`],[`Diffuse fibrosis (IPF)`,`Bilaterally reduced; trachea central`,`Normal or impaired at bases`,`Vesicular`,`Normal or increased`,`Fine late-inspiratory bibasal crackles`],[`Emphysema / COPD`,`Bilaterally reduced, barrel chest; tracheal tug, short cricosternal distance`,`Hyperresonant; loss of cardiac and liver dullness`,`Diminished vesicular, prolonged expiration`,`Decreased`,`Polyphonic wheeze; early crackles`]]},{heading:`Classification of breath sounds and added sounds`,columns:[`Sound`,`Character`,`Mechanism`,`Typical causes`],rows:[[`Vesicular`,`Low pitch, inspiration longer than expiration, no gap`,`Large-airway sound filtered by aerated lung`,`Normal lung`],[`Tubular bronchial`,`High pitch, expiration equal or longer, gap present`,`Airless lung transmits bronchial sound`,`Consolidation, collapse with patent bronchus, above effusion`],[`Cavernous bronchial`,`Low pitch, hollow`,`Resonance in a large cavity with an open bronchus`,`TB cavity, lung abscess`],[`Amphoric`,`High pitch, metallic, bottle-like`,`Large smooth-walled air space`,`Large thin-walled cavity, open pneumothorax`],[`Wheeze`,`Continuous, musical, mainly expiratory`,`Flutter of a narrowed airway wall`,`Polyphonic: asthma, COPD; monophonic: tumour, foreign body`],[`Fine crackles`,`Short, high pitch, late inspiratory, unchanged by cough`,`Sudden opening of closed small airways`,`IPF, asbestosis, early pulmonary oedema`],[`Coarse crackles`,`Longer, low pitch, early inspiratory and expiratory, change with cough`,`Air through secretions in larger airways`,`Bronchiectasis, chronic bronchitis, pneumonia`],[`Pleural rub`,`Creaking, both phases, localised`,`Inflamed pleural surfaces rubbing`,`Pneumonia, pulmonary infarction, pleurisy`],[`Stridor`,`Harsh, mainly inspiratory`,`Extrathoracic upper airway narrowing`,`Laryngeal tumour, foreign body, angio-oedema, tracheal stenosis`]]},{heading:`Clubbing: grades and causes`,columns:[`Grade or group`,`Finding or cause`,`Exam point`],rows:[[`Grade 1`,`Fluctuation and softening of the nail bed`,`Earliest, subjective`],[`Grade 2`,`Loss of nail-fold angle (Lovibond over 180 degrees)`,`Schamroth window obliterated`],[`Grade 3`,`Increased convexity: parrot-beak or drumstick`,`Phalangeal depth ratio over 1`],[`Grade 4`,`Hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy`,`Painful wrists and ankles; periostitis; adenocarcinoma lung`],[`Respiratory causes`,`Lung cancer, bronchiectasis, abscess, empyema, IPF, asbestosis, cystic fibrosis, mesothelioma`,`Not COPD or asthma`],[`Cardiac causes`,`Cyanotic congenital heart disease, infective endocarditis, atrial myxoma`,`Differential clubbing in reversed PDA`],[`GI and other`,`Cirrhosis, IBD, coeliac disease, thyroid acropachy, familial`,`Unilateral: subclavian aneurysm, AV fistula`]]},{heading:`Differentiating look-alike bedside pictures`,columns:[`Pair`,`Feature that separates them`,`Confirm with`],rows:[[`Collapse vs effusion (both dull)`,`Trachea towards in collapse, away in effusion; stony dull and Skodaic band in effusion`,`CXR PA; ultrasound`],[`Consolidation vs effusion`,`Bronchial breathing and increased resonance in consolidation; absent sounds and decreased resonance in effusion`,`CXR; ultrasound (air bronchogram vs anechoic fluid)`],[`Pneumothorax vs large bulla`,`Pneumothorax: no lung markings, lung edge; bulla: curved walls, markings at margins`,`CXR; CT before any drain`],[`Fine crackles: IPF vs pulmonary oedema`,`IPF: clubbing, velcro, chronic; oedema: raised JVP, S3, orthopnoea, change with diuretic`,`HRCT; NT-proBNP; echo`],[`Pleural rub vs pericardial rub`,`Pleural rub stops on breath-holding; pericardial persists`,`ECG; echo`],[`Wheeze vs stridor`,`Wheeze mainly expiratory, chest; stridor mainly inspiratory, loudest over neck`,`Flow-volume loop; laryngoscopy`],[`Upper-lobe fibrosis vs active TB`,`Fibrosis: pulled trachea, chronic; active TB: fever, weight loss, new symptoms`,`Sputum NAAT; compare old films`]]},{heading:`Drugs whose effects you find at the bedside`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Amiodarone`,`200 mg oral once daily maintenance after loading`,`Risk rises with cumulative dose and over months`,`Pulmonary fibrosis: bibasal fine crackles, low DLCO; also thyroid, liver toxicity`],[`Methotrexate`,`7.5-25 mg oral or SC once weekly`,`Pneumonitis usually in first year`,`Acute hypersensitivity pneumonitis: dry cough, crackles, hypoxaemia; stop and treat`],[`Nitrofurantoin`,`50-100 mg oral at night (prophylaxis)`,`Chronic use over 6 months`,`Chronic interstitial fibrosis; acute eosinophilic pneumonitis with fever`],[`Bleomycin`,`IV per oncology protocol`,`Toxicity rises above about 400 units cumulative`,`Fibrosis with fine crackles; worsened by high FiO2 - keep SpO2 88-92%`],[`Inhaled corticosteroid (budesonide, fluticasone)`,`Inhaled via MDI with spacer or DPI, twice daily`,`Long-term`,`Oral thrush, hoarse voice - rinse mouth; check at every review`],[`Non-selective beta-blocker incl. timolol eye drops`,`Oral or topical`,`Any duration`,`Bronchospasm and wheeze in asthma; avoid in asthma`],[`ACE inhibitor (ramipril, enalapril)`,`Oral daily`,`Cough from weeks to months after start`,`Dry cough with normal chest; resolves 1-4 weeks after stopping; switch to ARB`]]}],redFlags:[`Respiratory rate 30/min or more, SpO2 under 92% on air or use of accessory muscles at rest`,`Silent chest, cyanosis, drowsiness or exhaustion in acute asthma or COPD`,`Trachea deviated away from a hyperresonant silent hemithorax with hypotension - tension pneumothorax`,`Stridor or sudden voice change with breathlessness - upper airway obstruction`,`Paradoxical abdominal movement - diaphragm fatigue and impending ventilatory failure`,`Flapping tremor, bounding pulse and confusion in a COPD patient on oxygen - hypercapnia`,`New clubbing, fixed monophonic wheeze, Horner syndrome or supraclavicular node in a smoker`],pearls:[`Trachea = upper mediastinum, apex beat = lower mediastinum - check both before calling a shift.`,`Four causes of bronchial breathing: consolidation, collapse with patent bronchus, fibrosis, above an effusion.`,`Clubbing is never due to COPD or asthma alone - in a smoker, think cancer.`,`Fine late crackles that do not clear with cough = fibrosis or oedema; coarse crackles that change with cough = secretions.`,`Pleural rub disappears on breath-holding; pericardial rub does not.`,`Fixed monophonic wheeze = one obstructed airway: tumour, foreign body or endobronchial TB.`,`Large opaque hemithorax with central trachea = collapse plus effusion or malignancy fixing the mediastinum.`,`Stony dullness, Skodaic band above, aegophony at the upper border - the effusion triad.`,`Shifting dullness in the chest only in hydropneumothorax, with a succussion splash.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-examination-q1`,stem:`A 55-year-old man with treated TB 10 years ago has a flattened right upper chest, trachea deviated to the right, dull percussion and bronchial breathing with coarse crackles over the right upper zone. The most likely diagnosis is:`,options:[`Right upper lobe consolidation`,`Right upper lobe fibrosis`,`Right pleural effusion`,`Right pneumothorax`],answer:1,explanation:`Flattening with the trachea pulled towards the lesion, dullness, bronchial breathing and coarse crackles in the upper zone after TB is classic post-TB fibrosis. Consolidation leaves the trachea central; an effusion pushes the trachea away and gives absent breath sounds; a pneumothorax gives hyperresonance.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-examination-q2`,stem:`Over which lesion would you expect diminished breath sounds with decreased vocal resonance AND a trachea pulled towards the affected side?`,options:[`Massive pleural effusion`,`Lobar pneumonia`,`Tension pneumothorax`,`Lobar collapse due to an obstructing endobronchial tumour`],answer:3,explanation:`Collapse from a blocked bronchus reduces volume (pulling the trachea towards) and the occluded bronchus stops sound transmission, so breath sounds and resonance fall. Massive effusion and tension pneumothorax push the trachea away, and pneumonia gives bronchial breathing with increased resonance and a central trachea.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-examination-q3`,stem:`Clubbing in a 62-year-old smoker with long-standing COPD should first make you suspect:`,options:[`Bronchogenic carcinoma`,`Severe emphysema`,`Chronic hypercapnia`,`Cor pulmonale`],answer:0,explanation:`COPD itself does not cause clubbing, however severe the emphysema, hypercapnia or cor pulmonale; new clubbing in a smoker is lung cancer until proved otherwise, with bronchiectasis and fibrosis as the other causes to consider.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-examination-q4`,stem:`A band of hyperresonant (tympanitic) percussion note just above the upper border of a pleural effusion is called:`,options:[`Grocco triangle`,`Traube space`,`Skodaic resonance`,`Kronig isthmus`],answer:2,explanation:`Skodaic resonance is the tympanitic note over relaxed, compressed lung just above an effusion. Grocco triangle is paravertebral dullness on the opposite side, Traube space is the gastric bubble area that becomes dull in left effusion, and Kronig isthmus is the apical band of resonance.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-examination-q5`,stem:`Which feature best distinguishes a pleural friction rub from a pericardial friction rub at the bedside?`,options:[`The pleural rub is heard in both phases of respiration`,`The pleural rub disappears when the patient holds the breath`,`The pleural rub changes after coughing`,`The pleural rub is heard best with the bell`],answer:1,explanation:`A pleural rub needs pleural movement, so it vanishes on breath-holding while a pericardial rub continues with the heartbeat. Being heard in both phases does not separate them, a rub does not change with coughing (crackles do), and the diaphragm, not the bell, is used for rubs.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-examination-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Describe the clinical examination of the respiratory system. Tabulate the physical signs of consolidation, collapse, pleural effusion, pneumothorax and fibrosis. Add a note on clubbing.`,openingLines:[`The respiratory examination is a structured sequence of general survey, hands, neck, inspection, palpation, percussion and auscultation whose combined findings localise a lesion and identify it as solid, fluid, air or scar.`,`The key discriminators are the position of the mediastinum (trachea and apex), the percussion note, the type of breath sound and vocal resonance.`],answer:[{heading:`General survey and vital signs`,points:[`Respiratory rate for 60 seconds (normal 12-20/min), SpO2, pulse, BP, temperature, NEWS2.`,`Distress: accessory muscles, tripod position, pursed lips, inability to speak in sentences.`,`Cyanosis, flapping tremor, pallor, lymph nodes, JVP, pedal oedema, Horner syndrome.`]},{heading:`Hands and neck`,points:[`Clubbing (grade it), tar staining, tremor, wasting of small muscles.`,`Trachea position, Trail sign, cricosternal distance, tracheal tug.`]},{heading:`Inspection and palpation`,points:[`Shape (barrel, kyphoscoliosis), flattening, scars, movement, Hoover sign, breathing pattern.`,`Apex beat, expansion (over 5 cm normal), tactile fremitus.`]},{heading:`Percussion`,points:[`Resonant, hyperresonant, impaired, dull, stony dull; compare sides.`,`Liver dullness 5th ICS; cardiac dullness; Kronig isthmus; Traube space; Skodaic resonance.`]},{heading:`Auscultation`,points:[`Vesicular vs bronchial (tubular, cavernous, amphoric) breath sounds.`,`Added sounds: wheeze (poly- or monophonic), fine and coarse crackles, pleural rub, stridor.`,`Vocal resonance, bronchophony, aegophony, whispering pectoriloquy.`]},{heading:`Signs table`,points:[`Draw the table of movement, trachea, percussion, breath sounds, resonance and added sounds for each lesion.`]},{heading:`Clubbing`,points:[`Definition, Lovibond angle over 180 degrees, Schamroth sign, phalangeal depth ratio over 1.`,`Grades 1-4; respiratory, cardiac and GI causes; not in COPD; PDGF/VEGF theory; HPOA.`]}],mustDraw:[`Signs table for consolidation, collapse, effusion, pneumothorax and fibrosis`,`Lovibond angle diagram and Schamroth window`],markSplit:[{part:`Examination sequence`,marks:4},{part:`Signs table`,marks:4},{part:`Clubbing`,marks:2}],keywords:[`consolidation`,`collapse`,`effusion`,`pneumothorax`,`fibrosis`,`clubbing`,`bronchial breathing`]},{id:`respiratory-examination-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on bronchial breathing and its types.`,openingLines:[`Bronchial breathing is a high-pitched, hollow breath sound in which expiration is as long as or longer than inspiration with a gap between the phases.`,`It is heard when airless lung with a patent bronchus conducts large-airway sound to the chest wall without filtration.`],answer:[{heading:`Mechanism`,points:[`Normal aerated lung filters high frequencies, giving vesicular sound.`,`Solid lung with an open bronchus transmits the unfiltered bronchial sound.`]},{heading:`Types`,points:[`Tubular: high-pitched - consolidation, collapse with patent bronchus, above effusion.`,`Cavernous: low-pitched, hollow - large cavity communicating with a bronchus.`,`Amphoric: high-pitched, metallic - large thin-walled cavity, open pneumothorax.`]},{heading:`Causes`,points:[`Consolidation, collapse with patent bronchus, fibrosis, compressed lung over effusion, cavity.`,`Normal over the trachea and right interscapular area.`]},{heading:`Associated signs`,points:[`Increased vocal resonance, whispering pectoriloquy, aegophony.`]}],mustDraw:[`Phase diagram of vesicular versus bronchial breath sound`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and mechanism`,marks:1},{part:`Types`,marks:2},{part:`Causes and associated signs`,marks:2}],keywords:[`tubular`,`cavernous`,`amphoric`,`vesicular`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`Macleod's Clinical Examination, 15th edition, 2023`,`Hutchison's Clinical Methods, 25th edition, 2022`,`Pasterkamp H et al. Towards the standardisation of lung sound nomenclature. ERS Task Force, Eur Respir J 2016`,`Royal College of Physicians. National Early Warning Score (NEWS) 2, 2017`,`British Thoracic Society Guideline for Pleural Disease, Thorax 2023`,`Global Initiative for Chronic Obstructive Lung Disease (GOLD) Report 2025`,`India State-Level Disease Burden Initiative CRD Collaborators. Lancet Global Health 2018`]}),e.push({id:`respiratory-pft-spirometry`,title:`Pulmonary function tests: spirometry, flow-volume loops, lung volumes and DLCO`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Pulmonary function tests** measure airflow (**spirometry: FEV1, FVC, FEV1/FVC**), lung size (**TLC, RV by plethysmography or gas dilution**) and gas transfer (**DLCO**); under **ERS/ATS 2022** they are interpreted against the **lower limit of normal (z-score -1.645) from GLI equations**, classifying obstruction (FEV1/FVC below LLN), restriction (TLC below LLN) and mixed defects, with a **bronchodilator response of over 10% of the predicted FEV1 or FVC**.`,keywords:[`spirometry`,`FEV1`,`FVC`,`FEV1/FVC ratio`,`lower limit of normal`,`z-score`,`GLI`,`bronchodilator reversibility`,`flow-volume loop`,`body plethysmography`,`TLC`,`residual volume`,`DLCO`,`KCO`,`PRISm`,`peak expiratory flow`,`FeNO`,`six-minute walk test`,`methacholine challenge`,`Empey index`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology of under-use`,points:[`**Spirometry** measures the volume of air exhaled from full inspiration (TLC) with maximal effort: **FVC** is the total volume, **FEV1** the volume in the first second and **FEV1/FVC** the proportion exhaled in the first second (normally about 0.75-0.85 in young adults, falling with age). [ATS/ERS 2019]`,`**Static lung volumes** (TLC, RV, FRC) need body plethysmography, helium dilution or nitrogen washout, because spirometry cannot measure the air that stays in the lung after full expiration (**residual volume**). [ERS/ATS 2022]`,`**DLCO (TLCO)** measures carbon monoxide uptake across the alveolar-capillary membrane in a single 10-second breath-hold and reflects alveolar surface area, membrane thickness, capillary blood volume and haemoglobin. [ERS/ATS 2017]`,`**COPD is underdiagnosed worldwide** because spirometry is not done: in population studies about 70% or more of people with spirometric obstruction have no doctor diagnosis. [GOLD 2025]`,`In India, spirometry is largely confined to district and tertiary centres; the **INSEARCH study** estimated about 2% asthma and about 3.5% chronic bronchitis prevalence in adults by questionnaire, so the true spirometric burden is larger and mostly unconfirmed. [INSEARCH, IJTLD 2012]`,`Under **NP-NCD**, chronic respiratory disease is part of the NCD package, and **peak flow** is the practical screening tool in primary care where a spirometer is unavailable. [MoHFW NP-NCD 2023]`,`**Normal FEV1 decline** after age 25-30 is about **20-30 mL a year**; susceptible smokers lose 50-100 mL a year, and stopping smoking returns the slope towards normal (Fletcher-Peto curve). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Indications and contraindications`,points:[`**Diagnostic indications**: chronic breathlessness, cough or wheeze, abnormal chest X-ray, confirming **asthma (variable obstruction)** and **COPD (post-bronchodilator obstruction)**, and screening people with risk exposures. [ATS/ERS 2019]`,`**Monitoring indications**: COPD and ILD progression, response to therapy, drug toxicity (**amiodarone, methotrexate, bleomycin**), neuromuscular disease and post-transplant follow-up. [ATS/ERS 2019]`,`**Pre-operative and disability indications**: lung resection (predicted postoperative FEV1 and DLCO), upper abdominal and thoracic surgery in symptomatic patients, and occupational and compensation assessment. [ATS/ERS 2019]`,`**Relative contraindications** (forced expiration raises intrathoracic, intra-abdominal and intracranial pressure): **myocardial infarction within 1 week**, uncontrolled hypertension, recent pneumothorax, recent thoracic, abdominal, eye or brain surgery, haemoptysis and large aortic aneurysm. [ATS/ERS 2019]`,`**Infection control**: defer testing in suspected **active TB** or acute respiratory infection; use in-line bacterial/viral filters and good room ventilation. [ATS/ERS 2019]`,`**Withhold bronchodilators before a diagnostic BDR test**: SABA **4-6 hours**, SAMA **12 hours**, twice-daily LABA **24 hours**, and once-daily LABA or LAMA **36-48 hours**. [ATS/ERS 2019]`]},{heading:`Physiology: why the curves look the way they do`,points:[`**Effort-independence**: after the first 20-30% of the FVC, expiratory flow is limited by **dynamic airway compression** at the **equal pressure point**, so flow depends on lung elastic recoil and airway resistance, not on effort - which is why the FEV1 is reproducible. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Obstruction** (asthma, COPD, bronchiectasis) narrows the airways or loses elastic recoil, so FEV1 falls more than FVC, the ratio falls and the expiratory flow-volume limb becomes **concave ('scooped')**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Air trapping** in obstruction raises **RV and RV/TLC**; in emphysema loss of recoil also raises **TLC (hyperinflation)** and destruction of alveolar walls lowers **DLCO**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Parenchymal restriction** (ILD) stiffens the lung: TLC, FVC and FEV1 fall together, the ratio is normal or **high** (increased recoil holds airways open) and DLCO is low from thickened membranes and lost capillaries. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Extraparenchymal restriction** (obesity, kyphoscoliosis, neuromuscular weakness, pleural thickening) reduces TLC but the alveolar-capillary unit is normal, so DLCO is mildly low but **KCO (DLCO/VA) is normal or raised**. [ERS/ATS 2022]`,`**Neuromuscular weakness** reduces both TLC and expiratory reserve so **RV may rise**; a **fall in vital capacity of more than 25% on lying supine** suggests diaphragm weakness. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Upper airway obstruction** limits flow at a fixed maximum regardless of lung volume, producing a **plateau** on the flow-volume loop rather than a peak. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Doing the test: acceptability and repeatability (ATS/ERS 2019)`,points:[`Patient **seated, nose clip on, tight lip seal**; inhale fully and rapidly, then **blast out as hard and fast as possible** and keep going until no more air comes out, then inhale fully again for the inspiratory loop. [ATS/ERS 2019]`,`**Start of test**: back-extrapolated volume must be **under 5% of FVC or 0.100 L, whichever is greater**, with a sharp rise to peak flow. [ATS/ERS 2019]`,`**End of forced expiration** is met by a plateau (**under 0.025 L change in the last 1 second**), or **expiratory time of 15 seconds**, or FVC within repeatability of the largest previous FVC. [ATS/ERS 2019]`,`**Repeatability**: the two largest FVC and the two largest FEV1 values should be **within 0.150 L** in those over 6 years (within 0.100 L or 10% in children 6 or under). [ATS/ERS 2019]`,`Obtain at least **3 acceptable manoeuvres** (up to 8 in adults); report the **largest FEV1 and the largest FVC**, even from different blows. [ATS/ERS 2019]`,`Each session gets a **quality grade (A to F)**; grades A-C are usable for interpretation, and a lower grade should be reported with a caution. [ATS/ERS 2019]`,`**Common errors**: slow start, cough in the first second, early termination (falsely high FEV1/FVC), glottic closure, leak and submaximal inhalation. [ATS/ERS 2019]`]},{heading:`Interpretation: LLN, z-scores and the patterns (ERS/ATS 2022)`,points:[`Compare results with **reference equations** - **GLI 2012 spirometry**, GLI 2017 DLCO and GLI 2021 lung volumes - and call a value abnormal when it lies **below the LLN (5th percentile, z-score below -1.645)**. [ERS/ATS 2022]`,`**Obstruction** = **FEV1/FVC below the LLN**; the fixed ratio **0.70** overdiagnoses obstruction in the elderly and underdiagnoses it in the young, though **GOLD still uses post-BD FEV1/FVC under 0.70** for COPD. [ERS/ATS 2022; GOLD 2025]`,`**Restriction** can only be confirmed by a **TLC below the LLN**; a low FVC with normal ratio on spirometry alone is a '**possible restriction / non-specific pattern**' until lung volumes are measured. [ERS/ATS 2022]`,`**Mixed defect** = FEV1/FVC below LLN **and** TLC below LLN (for example, COPD with ILD or obesity). [ERS/ATS 2022]`,`**Non-specific pattern** = low FEV1 and/or FVC, normal FEV1/FVC and **normal TLC** - seen in early small-airway disease, obesity and poor effort. [ERS/ATS 2022]`,`**PRISm (preserved ratio impaired spirometry)** = FEV1/FVC 0.70 or more with **FEV1 under 80% predicted**; it carries higher mortality and some progress to COPD. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Severity in ERS/ATS 2022** is graded by FEV1 z-score: **mild -1.65 to -2.5, moderate -2.5 to -4, severe below -4**, replacing percent-predicted bands. [ERS/ATS 2022]`,`**GOLD severity** in COPD is still by post-BD **FEV1 % predicted**: GOLD 1 80% or more, GOLD 2 50-79%, GOLD 3 30-49%, GOLD 4 under 30%. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Hyperinflation** = TLC above the ULN; **air trapping** = RV/TLC above the ULN. [ERS/ATS 2022]`]},{heading:`Bronchodilator responsiveness`,points:[`Give **salbutamol 400 mcg (4 x 100 mcg) by MDI and spacer** and repeat spirometry after **10-15 minutes** (or ipratropium 160 mcg and wait 30 minutes). [ATS/ERS 2019]`,`**ERS/ATS 2022 definition**: a positive BDR is an increase in **FEV1 or FVC of more than 10% of the predicted value** for that person - this corrects the bias of the old rule against small, short and elderly patients. [ERS/ATS 2022]`,`**Old (2005) and GINA definition**: increase of **12% or more and 200 mL or more from baseline** in FEV1 or FVC; GINA 2025 still uses this for asthma diagnosis, so quote both. [GINA 2025; ATS/ERS 2005]`,`**BDR does not separate asthma from COPD**: many COPD patients have a positive BDR, and a negative BDR does not exclude asthma - especially on ICS. [GOLD 2025; GINA 2025]`,`**Increase in FVC with no change in ratio** after bronchodilator indicates **reduced air trapping** (volume response), common in severe COPD. [ERS/ATS 2022]`,`**Very large BDR** (FEV1 rise over 400 mL) in a smoker strongly suggests underlying asthma. [GOLD 2025]`]},{heading:`Flow-volume loops`,points:[`**Normal loop**: rapid rise to **peak expiratory flow**, then a straight linear descent to RV; the inspiratory limb is a symmetric semicircle below the axis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Obstruction**: low peak and a **concave, scooped expiratory limb**; in severe emphysema the curve shows an early sharp fall then a long tail ('**church spire**' or steeple pattern from airway collapse). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Restriction**: a small, tall, narrow loop ('**witch's hat**') with near-normal flow for the volume, shifted towards lower volumes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fixed upper airway obstruction** (tracheal stenosis after intubation, goitre, tumour encasing the trachea): **flattening of both inspiratory and expiratory limbs** - a box shape. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Variable extrathoracic obstruction** (vocal cord palsy, vocal cord dysfunction, laryngeal tumour): **inspiratory limb flattened**, because negative airway pressure collapses the extrathoracic airway in inspiration. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Variable intrathoracic obstruction** (tracheomalacia, lower tracheal tumour): **expiratory limb flattened**, because positive pleural pressure compresses the intrathoracic airway in expiration. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Empey index** (FEV1 in mL divided by PEF in L/min) **over 10** suggests upper airway obstruction; the **FEF50/FIF50 ratio** is about 1 in fixed, well above 1 in variable extrathoracic and well below 1 in variable intrathoracic obstruction. [Harrison 22e]`,`Upper airway obstruction may only appear after **50% or more narrowing** of the airway (around 8 mm diameter), so a normal loop does not exclude early stenosis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Lung volumes and DLCO patterns`,points:[`**Body plethysmography** measures all gas in the thorax including trapped air (gold standard); **helium dilution and nitrogen washout** measure only communicating gas and **underestimate TLC in severe obstruction**. [ERS/ATS 2022]`,`**DLCO is corrected for haemoglobin**: anaemia lowers and polycythaemia raises it; carboxyhaemoglobin in smokers also lowers it. [ERS/ATS 2017]`,`**Low DLCO with obstruction** points to **emphysema**; **normal or high DLCO with obstruction** points to asthma or chronic bronchitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Low DLCO with restriction and low KCO** = parenchymal disease (ILD, sarcoid, drug toxicity); **low DLCO, normal or high KCO** = extraparenchymal restriction or pneumonectomy. [ERS/ATS 2022]`,`**Isolated low DLCO with normal spirometry and volumes** suggests **pulmonary vascular disease** (chronic thromboembolic disease, pulmonary hypertension), early ILD, early emphysema or anaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Raised DLCO** occurs in **diffuse alveolar haemorrhage**, polycythaemia, left-to-right shunt, asthma and obesity. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Combined pulmonary fibrosis and emphysema (CPFE)** gives near-normal spirometry and volumes (the two cancel) but a **disproportionately low DLCO**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Other bedside and lab tests: PEF, FeNO, challenge tests and 6MWT`,points:[`**Peak expiratory flow**: best of three blows, record morning and evening for 2 weeks; **average diurnal variability over 10% in adults and over 13% in children** supports asthma. [GINA 2025]`,`**Occupational asthma**: serial PEF **at least 4 times a day for 3-4 weeks** covering work and rest days, analysed with OASYS, is the first objective test. [ERS 2012 occupational asthma]`,`**FeNO** reflects IL-13-driven airway eosinophilic inflammation: in adults **under 25 ppb is low and over 50 ppb is high** (children under 20 and over 35 ppb); ICS lower it and smoking lowers it. [ATS 2011]`,`**BTS/NICE/SIGN 2024** uses **FeNO 50 ppb or more in adults (35 ppb or more in children 5-16)** as a positive diagnostic test for asthma alongside blood eosinophils and spirometry. [BTS/NICE/SIGN NG245 2024]`,`**Methacholine challenge** measures airway hyperresponsiveness: **PD20 over 400 mcg** (or PC20 over 16 mg/mL) is normal and a normal test in a symptomatic patient off ICS makes current asthma unlikely. [ERS 2017 technical standard]`,`**Indirect challenges** (mannitol, exercise, eucapnic hyperventilation) are more specific for asthma and exercise-induced bronchoconstriction; a **fall in FEV1 of 10% or more** after exercise is positive. [GINA 2025]`,`**Six-minute walk test**: 30 m corridor, self-paced, standardised encouragement; healthy adults walk about **400-700 m**, the minimal important difference is about **25-33 m**, and desaturation of **4% or more or below 88%** is significant. [ERS/ATS 2014]`,`**Respiratory muscle tests**: **MIP (maximal inspiratory pressure)** and **SNIP** assess diaphragm strength; MIP more negative than -80 cmH2O excludes significant inspiratory weakness. [ERS 2019 respiratory muscle statement]`,`**Oscillometry (FOT)** needs only tidal breathing and detects small-airway disease; it is useful in children and in those unable to perform forced manoeuvres. [ERS 2020 oscillometry standard]`]},{heading:`Using PFTs in common clinical scenarios`,points:[`**Asthma**: obstruction that is **variable** - positive BDR, excessive PEF variability, positive challenge or improvement after 4 weeks of ICS (FEV1 rise over 12% and 200 mL); spirometry may be normal between attacks. [GINA 2025]`,`**COPD**: **post-BD FEV1/FVC under 0.70** in a symptomatic, exposed person; repeat spirometry if the ratio is between 0.60 and 0.80 because values near the threshold may change. [GOLD 2025]`,`**ILD**: reduced FVC and TLC with low DLCO; a **fall in FVC of 10% (or 5-10% with other progression)** over a year defines progressive pulmonary fibrosis. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`**Neuromuscular disease** (GBS, myasthenia, MND): track **FVC and MIP**; **FVC under 20 mL/kg, MIP weaker than -30 cmH2O or MEP under 40 cmH2O** predicts the need for ventilation in GBS (the 20-30-40 rule). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lung resection**: if FEV1 and DLCO are both over 80% predicted, proceed; otherwise calculate **predicted postoperative FEV1 and DLCO**, and if either is under 60% do cardiopulmonary exercise testing. [ACCP 2013; ERS/ESTS 2009]`,`**Drug monitoring**: baseline and serial **DLCO and FVC** for amiodarone and bleomycin; a fall in DLCO of more than about 10-15% from baseline prompts review. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Post-TB lung disease** in India commonly shows **mixed or restrictive patterns** with fixed obstruction, and is often misdiagnosed as COPD - see the post-TB lung disease topic. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Children**: spirometry is feasible from about **age 5-6**; ratio is normally higher (often over 0.90), so a fixed 0.70 cut-off misses most childhood obstruction - use LLN. [ERS/ATS 2022]`,`**Elderly**: falling elastic recoil lowers the normal ratio; over age 70 a ratio of 0.65-0.70 may be normal and the **LLN prevents overdiagnosis**. [ERS/ATS 2022]`,`**Pregnancy**: FEV1, FVC and PEF do not change significantly, so a reduced FEV1 in pregnancy is disease, not physiology; FRC falls by about 20% from the raised diaphragm. [Williams Obstetrics 26e]`,`**Obesity**: reduces **ERV and FRC** first; TLC falls only in severe obesity, and DLCO is often normal or high. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Race and ethnicity**: ATS 2023 recommends **race-neutral GLI Global equations**; Indian adults have historically been interpreted with Indian regional or GLI 'Other' equations, which lowers predicted values. [ATS 2023]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: a **portable spirometer or PEF meter** with a spacer-based BDR is adequate for diagnosing asthma and COPD at a district hospital. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Errors, pitfalls and safety`,points:[`**Early termination** shortens FVC and falsely raises FEV1/FVC, hiding obstruction; check that the volume-time curve reaches a plateau. [ATS/ERS 2019]`,`**Submaximal effort** gives a rounded, low peak and a falsely low FVC that may look restrictive - never call restriction without TLC. [ERS/ATS 2022]`,`**Spirometry-induced bronchospasm** (falling FEV1 with each blow) suggests very reactive airways; stop and give a bronchodilator. [ATS/ERS 2019]`,`**Syncope and chest pain** are rare adverse events; seat the patient and stop if symptoms occur. [ATS/ERS 2019]`,`**Percent predicted cut-offs of 80%** misclassify short, elderly and young people; LLN and z-scores are age-, sex- and height-appropriate. [ERS/ATS 2022]`,`**Borderline results near the LLN** (z-score -1.0 to -2.0) carry diagnostic uncertainty and should be interpreted with the clinical picture and repeat testing. [ERS/ATS 2022]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Refer for **full PFTs (volumes, DLCO)** when spirometry shows low FVC with normal ratio, unexplained breathlessness, suspected ILD or pulmonary vascular disease. [ERS/ATS 2022]`,`**COPD**: repeat spirometry at least **once a year** to track decline and recheck the diagnosis. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Asthma**: measure lung function at diagnosis, **3-6 months after starting ICS** (personal best) and then periodically, at least every 1-2 years. [GINA 2025]`,`**ILD**: FVC and DLCO every **3-6 months** to detect progression and guide antifibrotic therapy. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`**Neuromuscular disease**: FVC (sitting and supine) and MIP every **3-6 months**, more often in acute disease such as GBS. [Harrison 22e]`,`Refer for **bronchoscopy or CT** when the flow-volume loop shows an **upper airway obstruction pattern**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2019-2026)`,points:[`**ATS/ERS 2019 spirometry standard**: new acceptability criteria (BEV under 5% FVC or 0.100 L, 15-second end-of-test), repeatability 0.150 L, quality grades A-F and standard withholding times. [ATS/ERS 2019]`,`**ERS/ATS 2022 interpretive strategy**: use **LLN and z-scores**, severity by z-score (-1.65/-2.5/-4), **BDR over 10% of predicted**, 'non-specific pattern' and PRISm recognised. [ERS/ATS 2022]`,`**ATS 2023 statement**: stop using race-specific equations and adopt **GLI Global race-neutral equations** for spirometry. [ATS 2023]`,`**GOLD 2025** keeps the **fixed post-BD ratio under 0.70** for COPD diagnosis but describes **PRISm** and 'pre-COPD' and advises repeat spirometry near the threshold. [GOLD 2025]`,`**BTS/NICE/SIGN 2024 asthma guideline** makes **FeNO and blood eosinophils** first-line objective tests, with spirometry and BDR, before PEF variability or challenge tests. [BTS/NICE/SIGN NG245 2024]`,`**GINA 2025** still uses the **12% and 200 mL** BDR rule and PEF variability over 10% for adults - a deliberate difference from ERS/ATS 2022. [GINA 2025]`,`**GLI 2017 DLCO and GLI 2021 lung-volume** reference equations are now the recommended normal values for gas transfer and volumes. [ERS/ATS 2022]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: '**Draw flow-volume loops** of normal, obstructive, restrictive and the three upper airway obstruction patterns' - label PEF, TLC and RV on the volume axis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Classic question: '**Differentiate obstructive from restrictive** lung disease on PFTs' - ratio, TLC, RV/TLC, DLCO, loop shape. [ERS/ATS 2022]`,`**Common mistake**: diagnosing restriction from a low FVC alone; restriction needs **TLC below LLN**. [ERS/ATS 2022]`,`**Common mistake**: quoting only the 12% and 200 mL BDR rule; state the **2022 'over 10% of predicted'** definition too. [ERS/ATS 2022]`,`**Common mistake**: forgetting that the **DLCO must be corrected for haemoglobin** before calling it low in an anaemic Indian patient. [ERS/ATS 2017]`,`Viva: '**Why is the FEV1/FVC normal or high in ILD?**' - increased elastic recoil holds airways open and both volumes fall together. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: '**What raises DLCO?**' - alveolar haemorrhage, polycythaemia, left-to-right shunt, asthma, obesity, exercise. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`PFT patterns at a glance (ERS/ATS 2022)`,columns:[`Pattern`,`FEV1/FVC`,`FVC`,`TLC`,`RV/TLC`,`DLCO`,`Examples`],rows:[[`Obstructive`,`Below LLN`,`Normal or low`,`Normal or high`,`High`,`Normal (asthma) or low (emphysema)`,`Asthma, COPD, bronchiectasis`],[`Restrictive - parenchymal`,`Normal or high`,`Low`,`Below LLN`,`Normal`,`Low, KCO low`,`IPF, sarcoidosis, drug toxicity`],[`Restrictive - extraparenchymal`,`Normal`,`Low`,`Below LLN`,`Normal or high`,`Low or normal, KCO normal or high`,`Obesity, kyphoscoliosis, neuromuscular, pleural thickening`],[`Mixed`,`Below LLN`,`Low`,`Below LLN`,`Variable`,`Variable`,`COPD with ILD, post-TB lung disease`],[`Non-specific`,`Normal`,`Low`,`Normal`,`Normal or high`,`Normal`,`Small airway disease, obesity, poor effort`],[`PRISm (GOLD)`,`0.70 or more`,`Low`,`Not measured`,`Not measured`,`Not measured`,`Smokers at risk, obesity`],[`Isolated low DLCO`,`Normal`,`Normal`,`Normal`,`Normal`,`Low`,`Pulmonary vascular disease, early ILD, anaemia`]]},{heading:`Flow-volume loop patterns`,columns:[`Pattern`,`Expiratory limb`,`Inspiratory limb`,`Key index`,`Causes`],rows:[[`Normal`,`Sharp peak, linear fall`,`Semicircular`,`FEF50/FIF50 about 0.9-1`,`Healthy`],[`Obstructive`,`Low peak, concave (scooped)`,`Near normal`,`FEV1/FVC below LLN`,`Asthma, COPD`],[`Restrictive`,`Tall, narrow, small volume`,`Small`,`TLC below LLN`,`ILD, chest wall, obesity`],[`Fixed upper airway obstruction`,`Flat plateau`,`Flat plateau`,`Empey index over 10; FEF50/FIF50 about 1`,`Post-intubation tracheal stenosis, goitre`],[`Variable extrathoracic`,`Normal`,`Flattened`,`FEF50/FIF50 well above 1`,`Vocal cord palsy, vocal cord dysfunction`],[`Variable intrathoracic`,`Flattened`,`Normal`,`FEF50/FIF50 well below 1`,`Tracheomalacia, lower tracheal tumour`]]},{heading:`Severity grading: ERS/ATS 2022 versus GOLD`,columns:[`System`,`Measure`,`Mild`,`Moderate`,`Severe`,`Very severe`],rows:[[`ERS/ATS 2022 (any disease)`,`FEV1 z-score`,`-1.65 to -2.5`,`-2.5 to -4`,`Below -4`,`Not separated`],[`GOLD 2025 (COPD)`,`Post-BD FEV1 % predicted`,`GOLD 1: 80% or more`,`GOLD 2: 50-79%`,`GOLD 3: 30-49%`,`GOLD 4: under 30%`]]},{heading:`Key cut-offs for PFT-related tests`,columns:[`Test`,`Normal or positive cut-off`,`Source`],rows:[[`Obstruction`,`FEV1/FVC below LLN (z below -1.645); GOLD under 0.70 post-BD`,`ERS/ATS 2022; GOLD 2025`],[`BDR (2022)`,`FEV1 or FVC rise over 10% of predicted`,`ERS/ATS 2022`],[`BDR (GINA)`,`FEV1 rise 12% or more and 200 mL or more from baseline`,`GINA 2025`],[`PEF variability`,`Average diurnal over 10% adults, over 13% children`,`GINA 2025`],[`FeNO`,`Adults 50 ppb or more, children 35 ppb or more positive`,`BTS/NICE/SIGN 2024`],[`Methacholine`,`PD20 over 400 mcg or PC20 over 16 mg/mL normal`,`ERS 2017`],[`Repeatability`,`Two best FEV1 and FVC within 0.150 L`,`ATS/ERS 2019`],[`6MWT`,`MID about 25-33 m; desaturation 4% or more significant`,`ERS/ATS 2014`],[`Supine VC fall`,`Over 25% suggests diaphragm weakness`,`Harrison 22e`]]},{heading:`Drugs used in or withheld for lung function testing`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Salbutamol (BDR test)`,`400 mcg (4 x 100 mcg) MDI via spacer, one puff at a time`,`Repeat spirometry at 10-15 min; withhold SABA 4-6 h before`,`Tremor, tachycardia; caution in arrhythmia`],[`Ipratropium (BDR test)`,`160 mcg MDI via spacer`,`Repeat spirometry at 30 min; withhold SAMA 12 h before`,`Dry mouth; caution in glaucoma, urinary retention`],[`Formoterol or salmeterol (LABA)`,`Usual maintenance inhaled dose`,`Withhold 24 h before diagnostic BDR or challenge`,`Withholding may destabilise unstable asthma - do not withhold if severe`],[`Tiotropium, indacaterol, vilanterol (once-daily)`,`Usual maintenance inhaled dose`,`Withhold 36-48 h before diagnostic BDR`,`As above`],[`Methacholine (challenge)`,`Inhaled, doubling or quadrupling doses by dosimeter or nebuliser`,`Stop at 20% FEV1 fall (PD20) or maximum dose`,`Bronchospasm; avoid if FEV1 under 60% predicted or under 1.5 L, recent MI or stroke`],[`Mannitol (indirect challenge)`,`Inhaled dry powder, escalating to 635 mg cumulative`,`Stop at 15% FEV1 fall`,`Cough, bronchospasm; same contraindications as methacholine`],[`Montelukast`,`10 mg oral once daily`,`Withhold 24 h before a challenge test`,`Neuropsychiatric effects (boxed warning)`]]}],redFlags:[`Flattened inspiratory or expiratory limb (upper airway obstruction) - urgent CT neck/thorax and bronchoscopy`,`FVC under 20 mL/kg or MIP weaker than -30 cmH2O in GBS or myasthenia - ICU referral`,`Rapid fall of FVC 10% or more in a year in ILD - progressive fibrosis, refer`,`Isolated low DLCO with breathlessness - exclude pulmonary embolism and pulmonary hypertension`,`Progressive bronchospasm during spirometry - stop testing and treat`,`New restrictive pattern or DLCO fall on amiodarone, methotrexate or bleomycin - stop drug and refer`],pearls:[`Obstruction = ratio below LLN; restriction = TLC below LLN; never call restriction from spirometry alone.`,`2022 BDR = over 10% of predicted FEV1 or FVC; GINA keeps 12% and 200 mL - quote both.`,`Emphysema has a low DLCO; asthma has a normal or high DLCO.`,`Low DLCO with high KCO = extraparenchymal restriction or pneumonectomy.`,`Variable extrathoracic obstruction flattens inspiration; variable intrathoracic flattens expiration; fixed flattens both.`,`Empey index (FEV1 mL / PEF L/min) over 10 = think upper airway obstruction.`,`ILD ratio is normal or high because recoil holds the airways open.`,`Raised DLCO: blood in the alveoli (haemorrhage), more blood (polycythaemia, shunt), asthma, obesity.`,`CPFE: near-normal spirometry, very low DLCO.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-pft-spirometry-q1`,stem:`Per the ERS/ATS 2022 interpretive strategy, a positive bronchodilator response is defined as:`,options:[`FEV1 rise of 12% and 200 mL from baseline`,`FEV1 rise of 15% from baseline`,`FEV1 or FVC rise of more than 10% of the predicted value`,`PEF rise of 20% from baseline`],answer:2,explanation:`ERS/ATS 2022 replaced the 12% and 200 mL from-baseline rule with a change of more than 10% of the predicted FEV1 or FVC, reducing bias against small and elderly patients. The 12% and 200 mL rule is the older ATS/ERS 2005 definition still used by GINA; 15% and PEF 20% are not current spirometric definitions.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-pft-spirometry-q2`,stem:`A 45-year-old woman develops stridor 3 months after prolonged intubation. Her flow-volume loop shows flattening of both the inspiratory and expiratory limbs. The most likely lesion is:`,options:[`Fixed tracheal stenosis`,`Unilateral vocal cord palsy`,`Tracheomalacia`,`Severe asthma`],answer:0,explanation:`Flattening of both limbs indicates a fixed upper airway obstruction, classically post-intubation tracheal stenosis. Vocal cord palsy is a variable extrathoracic lesion flattening only the inspiratory limb, tracheomalacia is variable intrathoracic flattening only the expiratory limb, and asthma gives a scooped expiratory limb.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-pft-spirometry-q3`,stem:`A 60-year-old man has FEV1/FVC 0.62 (below LLN), TLC 125% predicted, RV/TLC raised and DLCO 45% predicted (Hb normal). The pattern best fits:`,options:[`Chronic asthma`,`Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis`,`Chronic bronchitis without emphysema`,`Emphysema`],answer:3,explanation:`Obstruction with hyperinflation, air trapping and a low DLCO indicates destruction of the alveolar-capillary surface, which is emphysema. Asthma and chronic bronchitis cause obstruction with a normal or raised DLCO, and IPF gives a restrictive pattern with a normal or high ratio and low TLC.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-pft-spirometry-q4`,stem:`A very obese man has reduced TLC and a reduced DLCO, but the KCO (DLCO/VA) is raised. This pattern indicates:`,options:[`Interstitial lung disease`,`Extraparenchymal restriction`,`Pulmonary hypertension`,`Emphysema`],answer:1,explanation:`When the lung itself is normal but cannot expand (obesity, chest wall or neuromuscular disease), DLCO falls with the smaller alveolar volume while KCO is normal or raised. ILD lowers both DLCO and KCO, pulmonary hypertension gives an isolated low DLCO with normal volumes, and emphysema raises TLC.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`respiratory-pft-spirometry-q5`,stem:`Which of the following raises the measured DLCO?`,options:[`Anaemia`,`Pulmonary embolism`,`Diffuse alveolar haemorrhage`,`Cigarette smoking just before the test`],answer:2,explanation:`Blood in the alveoli binds carbon monoxide and raises DLCO in diffuse alveolar haemorrhage. Anaemia lowers DLCO (less haemoglobin), pulmonary embolism lowers it by reducing capillary blood volume, and recent smoking lowers it through carboxyhaemoglobin back-pressure.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-pft-spirometry-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Describe spirometry: technique, quality criteria and interpretation as per current ERS/ATS standards. Draw flow-volume loops in obstructive, restrictive and upper airway obstruction. Add a note on DLCO.`,openingLines:[`Spirometry measures the volume and speed of air exhaled from full inspiration with maximal effort, giving FEV1, FVC and the FEV1/FVC ratio.`,`Under ERS/ATS 2022, results are compared with GLI reference equations and abnormal values are those below the lower limit of normal (z-score -1.645).`],answer:[{heading:`Indications and contraindications`,points:[`Diagnosis of breathlessness, asthma, COPD, ILD; monitoring; pre-operative; occupational.`,`Relative contraindications: MI within 1 week, recent surgery, pneumothorax, haemoptysis, aortic aneurysm.`]},{heading:`Technique and quality (ATS/ERS 2019)`,points:[`Seated, nose clip, full inspiration, maximal blast, continue to plateau or 15 s.`,`BEV under 5% FVC or 0.100 L; 3 acceptable blows; repeatability within 0.150 L; grade A-F.`]},{heading:`Interpretation (ERS/ATS 2022)`,points:[`Obstruction FEV1/FVC below LLN; restriction TLC below LLN; mixed; non-specific; PRISm.`,`Severity by FEV1 z-score: -1.65 to -2.5 mild, -2.5 to -4 moderate, below -4 severe.`,`BDR over 10% of predicted FEV1 or FVC (GINA: 12% and 200 mL).`]},{heading:`Flow-volume loops`,points:[`Normal, obstructive (scooped), restrictive (witch's hat).`,`Fixed UAO both limbs flat; variable extrathoracic inspiratory flat; variable intrathoracic expiratory flat.`]},{heading:`DLCO`,points:[`Single-breath CO uptake, 10 s breath-hold, Hb corrected.`,`Low: emphysema, ILD, pulmonary vascular disease, anaemia; high: haemorrhage, polycythaemia, shunt, asthma.`,`KCO separates parenchymal from extraparenchymal restriction.`]},{heading:`Pattern table`,points:[`Tabulate ratio, FVC, TLC, RV/TLC and DLCO for obstructive, restrictive and mixed.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow-volume loops: normal, obstructive, restrictive, fixed, variable extrathoracic, variable intrathoracic`,`Volume-time curve with FEV1 and FVC marked`],markSplit:[{part:`Technique and quality`,marks:2},{part:`Interpretation and BDR`,marks:3},{part:`Flow-volume loops`,marks:3},{part:`DLCO`,marks:2}],keywords:[`FEV1`,`FVC`,`LLN`,`z-score`,`GLI`,`BDR`,`flow-volume loop`,`DLCO`,`KCO`]},{id:`respiratory-pft-spirometry-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate obstructive and restrictive lung disease on pulmonary function tests.`,openingLines:[`Obstructive disease reduces airflow out of the lungs (FEV1/FVC below LLN), while restrictive disease reduces lung volume (TLC below LLN).`,`Lung volumes and DLCO are needed to confirm restriction and to separate parenchymal from extraparenchymal causes.`],answer:[{heading:`Spirometry`,points:[`Obstructive: FEV1 low, FVC normal or low, ratio below LLN, scooped loop.`,`Restrictive: FEV1 and FVC low proportionally, ratio normal or high, witch's hat loop.`]},{heading:`Lung volumes`,points:[`Obstructive: TLC normal or high, RV and RV/TLC high (air trapping).`,`Restrictive: TLC below LLN, RV low or normal.`]},{heading:`DLCO`,points:[`Obstructive: low in emphysema, normal or high in asthma.`,`Restrictive: low with low KCO in ILD; normal or high KCO in extraparenchymal causes.`]},{heading:`Examples`,points:[`Obstructive: asthma, COPD, bronchiectasis, bronchiolitis obliterans.`,`Restrictive: ILD, obesity, kyphoscoliosis, neuromuscular disease, pleural thickening.`]}],mustDraw:[`Side-by-side flow-volume loops and a comparison table`],markSplit:[{part:`Spirometry and loops`,marks:2},{part:`Volumes and DLCO`,marks:2},{part:`Examples`,marks:1}],keywords:[`obstructive`,`restrictive`,`TLC`,`RV/TLC`,`DLCO`,`KCO`]}],references:[`Graham BL et al. Standardization of Spirometry 2019 Update. ATS/ERS Technical Statement, Am J Respir Crit Care Med 2019`,`Stanojevic S et al. ERS/ATS technical standard on interpretive strategies for routine lung function tests, Eur Respir J 2022`,`Bhakta NR et al. Race and Ethnicity in Pulmonary Function Test Interpretation: An Official ATS Statement, 2023`,`Graham BL et al. ERS/ATS standards for single-breath carbon monoxide uptake in the lung, Eur Respir J 2017`,`Global Initiative for Chronic Obstructive Lung Disease (GOLD) Report 2025`,`Global Initiative for Asthma (GINA) Strategy Report 2025`,`BTS/NICE/SIGN Asthma: diagnosis, monitoring and chronic asthma management (NG245), 2024`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`]}),e.push({id:`respiratory-abg-interpretation`,title:`Arterial blood gas interpretation and acid-base disorders`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Arterial blood gas (ABG)** analysis measures **pH, PaCO2, PaO2 and bicarbonate** to assess oxygenation, ventilation and acid-base status; it is read **stepwise** - oxygenation and **A-a gradient**, pH, primary disorder, **expected compensation (Winter formula for metabolic acidosis)**, **anion gap with albumin correction**, **delta ratio** and **osmolar gap** - to uncover mixed disorders and their cause.`,keywords:[`ABG`,`VBG`,`acid-base`,`metabolic acidosis`,`metabolic alkalosis`,`respiratory acidosis`,`respiratory alkalosis`,`anion gap`,`delta ratio`,`osmolar gap`,`Winter formula`,`A-a gradient`,`P/F ratio`,`lactate`,`urine anion gap`,`urine chloride`,`Henderson-Hasselbalch`,`Stewart approach`,`base excess`,`type 2 respiratory failure`],sections:[{heading:`Definition, normal values and burden`,points:[`**Normal arterial values** at sea level breathing air: **pH 7.35-7.45, PaCO2 35-45 mmHg (4.7-6.0 kPa), HCO3 22-26 mmol/L, PaO2 80-100 mmHg (10.7-13.3 kPa), base excess -2 to +2 mmol/L**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acidaemia** (pH under 7.35) and **alkalaemia** (pH over 7.45) describe the blood; **acidosis** and **alkalosis** describe the processes, and more than one process can coexist (mixed disorder). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Henderson-Hasselbalch**: pH = 6.1 + log [HCO3 / (0.03 x PaCO2)] - pH depends on the **ratio** of bicarbonate (kidney) to PaCO2 (lung), which is why compensation moves the other value in the same direction. [Harrison 22e]`,`**India has about 101 million people with diabetes** (ICMR-INDIAB 2023), making **diabetic ketoacidosis** a leading cause of high anion gap acidosis in Indian emergency departments. [ICMR-INDIAB, Lancet Diabetes Endocrinol 2023]`,`**Methanol mass poisonings** from illicit liquor recur in India (for example **Kallakurichi, Tamil Nadu, June 2024**, with over 60 deaths) - a raised anion gap plus a raised osmolar gap must trigger this diagnosis. [TN Govt 2024]`,`**Sepsis**, CKD, acute diarrhoeal disease, organophosphate poisoning and COPD exacerbations are the other everyday Indian causes of acid-base derangement. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**Hypoxaemia** is PaO2 under 80 mmHg; **respiratory failure** is PaO2 under 60 mmHg (8 kPa) - **type 1** with normal or low PaCO2 and **type 2** with PaCO2 over 45-50 mmHg (6-6.5 kPa). [BTS 2017]`]},{heading:`Sampling technique and sources of error`,points:[`Use the **radial artery** first; perform a **modified Allen test** to confirm ulnar collateral flow, and use the brachial or femoral artery if the radial is unsuitable. [Harrison 22e]`,`Use a **pre-heparinised (dry lithium heparin) syringe**; liquid heparin dilutes the sample and falsely lowers PaCO2 and HCO3. [CLSI C46-A2]`,`**Expel air bubbles** immediately: air equilibration falsely raises PaO2 towards 150 mmHg and lowers PaCO2. [CLSI C46-A2]`,`Analyse within **30 minutes at room temperature** in plastic syringes; delay allows leucocyte and platelet metabolism to consume oxygen (**leucocyte larceny** in leukaemia) and generate CO2 and lactate. [CLSI C46-A2]`,`**Record FiO2 and oxygen device** on the request: a PaO2 cannot be interpreted without knowing what the patient was breathing. [BTS 2017]`,`Wait **at least 20-30 minutes** after a change in oxygen or ventilator settings before repeating the gas, so the new steady state is reached. [BTS 2017]`,`**Local anaesthetic** (lidocaine) before puncture is recommended for non-urgent samples; complications include haematoma, arterial spasm, thrombosis and rarely pseudoaneurysm. [BTS 2017]`]},{heading:`Physiology: buffers, compensation and gas exchange`,points:[`**Bicarbonate-carbonic acid** is the main extracellular buffer; the lungs excrete about 15,000 mmol of CO2 (volatile acid) a day and the kidneys excrete about 1 mmol/kg/day of fixed acid as **ammonium and titratable acid**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Respiratory compensation** for metabolic disorders starts within minutes and is complete in **12-24 hours**; **renal compensation** for respiratory disorders takes **3-5 days** - hence the separate acute and chronic formulas. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Compensation never overcorrects** the pH; a pH on the 'wrong' side of 7.40 or a PaCO2/HCO3 outside the expected range means a **second primary disorder**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Alveolar gas equation**: PAO2 = FiO2 x (Patm - 47) - PaCO2/0.8; on room air at sea level this is about **150 - 1.25 x PaCO2** mmHg. [Harrison 22e]`,`**A-a gradient** (PAO2 minus PaO2) is normally **under 10-15 mmHg in young adults** and rises with age (roughly **age/4 + 4**); it separates hypoventilation (normal gradient) from lung disease (raised gradient). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Five causes of hypoxaemia**: low inspired oxygen (altitude), **hypoventilation** (normal A-a), **V/Q mismatch** (commonest, corrects with oxygen), **shunt** (does not correct with oxygen) and **diffusion limitation** (exercise desaturation in ILD). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Potassium shifts with pH**: acidaemia moves K out of cells (hyperkalaemia) and alkalaemia moves it in - correcting acidosis in DKA unmasks total-body potassium deficit. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve** shifts right (easier unloading) with acidosis, raised CO2, fever and 2,3-DPG, and left with alkalosis and hypothermia - so SpO2 90% corresponds to PaO2 about 60 mmHg. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`The stepwise approach (the one examiners ask)`,points:[`**Step 1 - Oxygenation**: PaO2 against FiO2; calculate **P/F ratio** (normal over 400) and the **A-a gradient** on room air. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Step 2 - pH**: acidaemia under 7.35, alkalaemia over 7.45; a normal pH with abnormal PaCO2 and HCO3 indicates a **mixed disorder**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Step 3 - Primary disorder**: if pH and PaCO2 move in **opposite directions** it is respiratory; if pH and HCO3 move in the **same direction** it is metabolic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Step 4 - Compensation**: apply the formula for the primary disorder (table below); a measured value outside the expected range means an additional respiratory or metabolic disorder. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Step 5 - Anion gap**: AG = Na - (Cl + HCO3), normal about **8-12 mmol/L** (lab specific); **add 2.5 for every 1 g/dL fall in albumin below 4 g/dL**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Step 6 - Delta ratio** (if AG raised): (AG - 12) / (24 - HCO3); **under 0.4** pure normal-AG acidosis, **0.4-0.8** combined high-AG and normal-AG acidosis, **1-2** pure high-AG acidosis, **over 2** high-AG acidosis plus metabolic alkalosis (or chronic respiratory acidosis). [OHCM 11e]`,`**Step 7 - Osmolar gap** (if AG raised without obvious cause): measured minus calculated osmolality [2 Na + glucose/18 + urea/6 (mg/dL)]; **over 10 mOsm/kg** suggests methanol, ethylene glycol or other alcohols. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Step 8 - Clinical correlation**: the gas must fit the story; name the disorder(s), the cause and the next test. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification: compensation formulas`,points:[`**Metabolic acidosis (Winter formula)**: expected PaCO2 = **1.5 x HCO3 + 8 (plus or minus 2)**; a quick check is that PaCO2 approximates the last two digits of the pH. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Metabolic alkalosis**: expected PaCO2 = **0.7 x HCO3 + 21 (plus or minus 2)**, or PaCO2 rises about 0.7 mmHg per 1 mmol/L rise in HCO3; it rarely exceeds 55 mmHg. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute respiratory acidosis**: HCO3 rises **1 mmol/L per 10 mmHg** rise in PaCO2, and pH falls about 0.08 per 10 mmHg. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chronic respiratory acidosis**: HCO3 rises **3.5-4 mmol/L per 10 mmHg** rise in PaCO2, and pH falls about 0.03 per 10 mmHg. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute respiratory alkalosis**: HCO3 falls **2 mmol/L per 10 mmHg** fall in PaCO2. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chronic respiratory alkalosis**: HCO3 falls **4-5 mmol/L per 10 mmHg** fall in PaCO2; it is the only disorder in which compensation can bring the pH back to normal. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute-on-chronic respiratory acidosis** (typical COPD exacerbation): HCO3 lies between the acute and chronic expected values and pH is lower than the chronic formula predicts. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Causes: metabolic acidosis`,points:[`**High anion gap acidosis (GOLD MARK)**: Glycols (ethylene, propylene), **Oxoproline** (chronic paracetamol), **L-lactate**, D-lactate, **Methanol**, **Aspirin**, **Renal failure**, **Ketoacidosis** (diabetic, alcoholic, starvation). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lactic acidosis**: type A from tissue hypoperfusion (shock, sepsis, severe anaemia, CO poisoning); type B without hypoxia (metformin, liver failure, thiamine deficiency, malignancy, seizures). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Normal anion gap (hyperchloraemic) acidosis**: **diarrhoea**, renal tubular acidosis, ureteric diversion, acetazolamide, early CKD and **large-volume 0.9% saline**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Urine anion gap** (UNa + UK - UCl) in normal-AG acidosis: **negative** in diarrhoea (high ammonium excretion) and **positive** in distal RTA (kidney cannot excrete ammonium). [Harrison 22e]`,`**RTA types**: type 1 distal (urine pH over 5.5, low K, stones), type 2 proximal (bicarbonate wasting, Fanconi, low K), **type 4** (hypoaldosteronism, **high K**, diabetes, ACE inhibitors, spironolactone). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Euglycaemic DKA** on **SGLT2 inhibitors** (dapagliflozin, empagliflozin) presents with ketoacidosis at glucose under 250 mg/dL - check ketones in any unwell patient on these drugs. [ADA 2024 hyperglycaemic crises consensus]`]},{heading:`Causes: metabolic alkalosis, respiratory acidosis and respiratory alkalosis`,points:[`**Metabolic alkalosis - chloride-responsive (urine Cl under 20 mmol/L)**: vomiting, nasogastric suction, remote diuretic use, post-hypercapnia - corrects with **0.9% saline and potassium**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Metabolic alkalosis - chloride-resistant (urine Cl over 20 mmol/L)**: current diuretic use, **primary hyperaldosteronism, Cushing syndrome**, Bartter and Gitelman syndromes, severe hypokalaemia; check BP and renin-aldosterone. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Respiratory acidosis**: CNS depression (opioids, benzodiazepines, stroke), neuromuscular weakness (**GBS, myasthenia, organophosphate poisoning**, snake envenomation), chest wall disease, **COPD**, obesity hypoventilation and severe asthma with exhaustion. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Respiratory alkalosis**: anxiety, pain, fever, **early sepsis**, pulmonary embolism, pneumonia, pregnancy, altitude, liver failure and **early salicylate poisoning** (with later metabolic acidosis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Classic mixed disorders**: salicylate poisoning (respiratory alkalosis + high-AG acidosis), sepsis with vomiting (acidosis + alkalosis), COPD on diuretics (respiratory acidosis + metabolic alkalosis), cardiac arrest (respiratory + metabolic acidosis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Triple disorder**: for example, alcoholic ketoacidosis with vomiting and sepsis - high-AG acidosis, metabolic alkalosis and respiratory alkalosis together; the delta ratio over 2 reveals the alkalosis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features that should prompt an ABG`,points:[`**Kussmaul breathing** (deep, sighing) with dehydration and ketotic breath suggests DKA; with blurred vision ('snowfield') and abdominal pain after drinking suggests **methanol**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drowsiness, flapping tremor, bounding pulse and headache** in a COPD patient suggest CO2 narcosis - take an ABG before increasing oxygen. [BTS 2017]`,`**Tetany, perioral tingling and carpopedal spasm** with hyperventilation suggest **acute respiratory alkalosis** (fall in ionised calcium). [Harrison 22e]`,`**SpO2 under 92%** on air, a **NEWS2 score of 5 or more**, or any increase in oxygen requirement in a ward patient is an indication for a blood gas. [BTS 2017]`,`**Tinnitus, vomiting and hyperventilation** after a tablet overdose suggest **salicylate poisoning**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Severe metabolic alkalosis** (pH over 7.55) causes arrhythmias, seizures and hypoventilation, and is common with repeated vomiting in pyloric stenosis and in hyperemesis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations: ABG, VBG and supporting tests`,points:[`**VBG versus ABG**: venous pH is about **0.03-0.04 lower** and HCO3 about **1-2 mmol/L higher** - VBG is adequate for pH, bicarbonate, lactate, potassium and glucose in DKA and most metabolic disorders. [ADA 2024; Harrison 22e]`,`**Venous PCO2** is on average about 4-5 mmHg higher but with wide limits; a **venous PCO2 under 45 mmHg reliably excludes arterial hypercapnia**, while a raised value needs an ABG. [BTS 2017]`,`**Venous PO2 is useless** for oxygenation - use SpO2 or an arterial PaO2. [BTS 2017]`,`**Capillary (earlobe) gas** is an acceptable alternative for pH and PaCO2 in stable patients and in the long-term oxygen assessment clinic, but underestimates PaO2. [BTS 2015 home oxygen]`,`**Supporting tests**: serum electrolytes with chloride, albumin, glucose, urea and creatinine, **lactate**, **beta-hydroxybutyrate**, serum osmolality, urine pH, urine electrolytes and toxicology. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lactate**: over 2 mmol/L is raised and **over 4 mmol/L** with hypotension defines high-risk sepsis; repeat within 2-4 hours to guide resuscitation. [SSC 2021]`,`**Base excess** (the amount of acid needed to bring pH to 7.40 at PaCO2 40) is a quick measure of the metabolic component; a BE below -2 indicates metabolic acidosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Stewart (physicochemical) approach** explains acid-base through strong ion difference, PaCO2 and weak acids (albumin, phosphate); it explains **saline-induced hyperchloraemic acidosis** and hypoalbuminaemic alkalosis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Worked examples`,points:[`**Example 1 (DKA)**: pH 7.21, PaCO2 25, HCO3 10, Na 136, Cl 100. AG = 26 (raised); Winter expected PaCO2 = 23 plus or minus 2 (appropriate); delta ratio (26-12)/(24-10) = 1.0 - **pure high-AG metabolic acidosis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Example 2 (COPD exacerbation)**: pH 7.25, PaCO2 75, HCO3 32. Acute expected HCO3 about 27, chronic about 36; measured 32 lies between - **acute-on-chronic respiratory acidosis**; NIV indicated (pH under 7.35, PaCO2 over 49). [BTS/ICS 2016]`,`**Example 3 (vomiting)**: pH 7.52, PaCO2 48, HCO3 38; expected PaCO2 = 0.7 x 38 + 21 = 47.6 - **compensated metabolic alkalosis**; urine chloride under 20 confirms saline-responsive. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Example 4 (salicylate)**: pH 7.45, PaCO2 22, HCO3 15, AG 22; pH normal yet both values abnormal, Winter expects 30.5 but PaCO2 is 22 - **respiratory alkalosis plus high-AG metabolic acidosis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Example 5 (opioid overdose)**: on room air PaO2 55, PaCO2 70; PAO2 = 150 - 87.5 = 62.5; A-a gradient 7.5 (normal) - **pure hypoventilation**; give naloxone, not just oxygen. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Example 6 (pulmonary embolism)**: room air PaO2 62, PaCO2 30; PAO2 = 112.5; A-a gradient 50 - **widened gradient with respiratory alkalosis**, indicating V/Q mismatch. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Example 7 (diarrhoea)**: pH 7.28, PaCO2 30, HCO3 14, AG 10, urine anion gap negative - **normal-AG metabolic acidosis from GI bicarbonate loss**, appropriately compensated (expected PaCO2 29 plus or minus 2). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management principles in OPD and emergency`,points:[`**Treat the cause, not the number**: fluids and insulin for DKA, source control and fluids for sepsis lactic acidosis, dialysis for uraemic acidosis. [SSC 2021; ADA 2024]`,`**Controlled oxygen**: target **SpO2 94-98%** in most and **88-92%** in those at risk of hypercapnia (COPD, obesity hypoventilation, neuromuscular disease), using a **Venturi mask 24-28%**. [BTS 2017]`,`**NIV** for acute hypercapnic respiratory failure in COPD when **pH is under 7.35 and PaCO2 over 6.5 kPa (49 mmHg)** despite optimal medical therapy - see the AECOPD and NIV topic. [BTS/ICS 2016; ERS/ATS 2017]`,`**Sodium bicarbonate** is not routine: consider it in severe metabolic acidaemia (**pH 7.20 or less**) with **acute kidney injury** (BICAR-ICU trial), in DKA only if **pH under 7.0**, and in normal-AG acidosis from GI or renal loss. [BICAR-ICU 2018; ADA 2024]`,`**Methanol or ethylene glycol**: **fomepizole** (or ethanol if unavailable) to block alcohol dehydrogenase, bicarbonate for acidaemia, **folinic acid** for methanol and early **haemodialysis**. [EXTRIP 2015; Harrison 22e]`,`**Chloride-responsive metabolic alkalosis**: 0.9% saline and potassium chloride; **acetazolamide** for alkalosis in fluid-overloaded patients. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Respiratory alkalosis** from anxiety: reassurance and slow breathing - **do not use paper-bag rebreathing**, which can cause hypoxaemia if the cause is actually PE or MI. [OHCM 11e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: progesterone drives hyperventilation, so normal PaCO2 is **about 28-32 mmHg** with compensatory HCO3 of 18-21 mmol/L - a 'normal' PaCO2 of 40 in a pregnant asthmatic indicates impending respiratory failure. [Williams Obstetrics 26e]`,`**Elderly**: normal PaO2 falls with age; use **age/4 + 4** to estimate the upper limit of a normal A-a gradient. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CKD**: chronic high-AG plus normal-AG acidosis; treat persistent serum bicarbonate **under 18 mmol/L** with **oral sodium bicarbonate** (and avoid exceeding the upper normal limit) to protect bone and muscle. [KDIGO CKD 2024]`,`**Diabetes on SGLT2 inhibitors**: euglycaemic DKA - suspect with ketones and acidosis despite near-normal glucose; stop the drug before surgery (3 days, 4 for ertugliflozin). [ADA 2024; FDA]`,`**Children**: DKA bicarbonate is avoided because of the risk of **cerebral oedema**; pyloric stenosis causes hypochloraemic hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis. [ISPAD 2022]`,`**Altitude**: chronic respiratory alkalosis with low PaO2 and low A-a gradient is normal for high-altitude residents (for example Leh, Ladakh). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: without an ABG machine, **SpO2, serum bicarbonate, electrolytes, glucose and urine ketones** with a VBG on a point-of-care analyser cover most decisions. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications, prognosis and monitoring`,points:[`**Severe acidaemia (pH under 7.10-7.20)** reduces myocardial contractility, causes arteriolar vasodilatation, resistance to catecholamines and arrhythmias. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Severe alkalaemia (pH over 7.55)** causes arrhythmias, seizures, hypokalaemia, hypocalcaemia and reduced cerebral and coronary blood flow. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rapid correction of chronic hypercapnia** (overventilation) causes **post-hypercapnic metabolic alkalosis** and seizures - aim to lower PaCO2 gradually towards the patient's baseline. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lactate clearance** (fall of 10-20% in 2 hours) is associated with better survival in sepsis; persistent hyperlactataemia predicts death. [SSC 2021]`,`Repeat the gas **1-2 hours after starting NIV**; failure of pH and PaCO2 to improve at 4 hours is an indication to consider intubation. [BTS/ICS 2016]`,`In DKA, monitor **venous pH, bicarbonate, potassium and ketones every 2-4 hours**; resolution is ketones under 0.6 mmol/L and pH over 7.3 (or HCO3 18 or more). [ADA 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**ADA/EASD/JBDS/AACE/DTS 2024 hyperglycaemic crises consensus**: DKA defined by glucose **200 mg/dL or more** (or known diabetes), **ketones (beta-hydroxybutyrate 3.0 mmol/L or more)** and **pH under 7.3 or HCO3 under 18 mmol/L**; recognises euglycaemic DKA; bicarbonate only if pH under 7.0. [ADA 2024]`,`**Global ARDS definition 2023** allows **SpO2/FiO2 315 or less** (when SpO2 is 97% or less) in place of P/F, enabling diagnosis without an ABG in resource-limited settings. [Matthay, AJRCCM 2024]`,`**KDIGO CKD 2024** suggests alkali therapy in CKD when serum bicarbonate is persistently **under 18 mmol/L** (earlier 2012 advice used 22 mmol/L). [KDIGO CKD 2024]`,`**Surviving Sepsis 2021** recommends **lactate-guided resuscitation**, advises against bicarbonate for lactic acidaemia with **pH 7.2 or more**, and suggests it for pH under 7.2 with AKI. [SSC 2021]`,`**Oximetry bias** in darker skin (FDA draft guidance 2025) means an ABG should be done when SpO2 is borderline and decisions depend on it. [FDA 2025]`,`**BTS oxygen guideline (2017)** remains current: target ranges 94-98% and 88-92%, with ABG within 60 minutes for patients at risk of hypercapnia. [BTS 2017]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Viva: '**Write the Winter formula**' - expected PaCO2 = 1.5 x HCO3 + 8 plus or minus 2. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: '**Causes of high anion gap metabolic acidosis**' - GOLD MARK; '**normal-AG**' - diarrhoea, RTA, saline, acetazolamide, ureteric diversion. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: '**What is the delta ratio and why calculate it?**' - to detect a hidden normal-AG acidosis or metabolic alkalosis alongside a high-AG acidosis. [OHCM 11e]`,`**Common mistake**: forgetting the **albumin correction** - a low albumin in a septic Indian patient can hide a high anion gap. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake**: calling a COPD patient's high bicarbonate 'metabolic alkalosis' - it is usually **renal compensation** for chronic respiratory acidosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake**: interpreting PaO2 without the FiO2 - a PaO2 of 90 on 60% oxygen is severe hypoxaemia (P/F 150). [BTS 2017]`,`Expect to calculate **A-a gradient, AG, delta ratio and osmolar gap** from a given gas in the theory paper; show the formula, the numbers and the conclusion. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Primary acid-base disorders and expected compensation`,columns:[`Disorder`,`pH`,`Primary change`,`Compensation`,`Expected value`],rows:[[`Metabolic acidosis`,`Low`,`HCO3 falls`,`PaCO2 falls`,`PaCO2 = 1.5 x HCO3 + 8 (plus or minus 2)`],[`Metabolic alkalosis`,`High`,`HCO3 rises`,`PaCO2 rises`,`PaCO2 = 0.7 x HCO3 + 21 (plus or minus 2)`],[`Acute respiratory acidosis`,`Low`,`PaCO2 rises`,`HCO3 rises`,`HCO3 up 1 per 10 mmHg PaCO2`],[`Chronic respiratory acidosis`,`Low or near normal`,`PaCO2 rises`,`HCO3 rises`,`HCO3 up 3.5-4 per 10 mmHg PaCO2`],[`Acute respiratory alkalosis`,`High`,`PaCO2 falls`,`HCO3 falls`,`HCO3 down 2 per 10 mmHg PaCO2`],[`Chronic respiratory alkalosis`,`High or normal`,`PaCO2 falls`,`HCO3 falls`,`HCO3 down 4-5 per 10 mmHg PaCO2`]]},{heading:`Key formulas and cut-offs`,columns:[`Measure`,`Formula`,`Normal / interpretation`],rows:[[`Alveolar oxygen (room air, sea level)`,`PAO2 = 0.21 x (760 - 47) - PaCO2/0.8 = about 150 - 1.25 x PaCO2`,`Used for A-a gradient`],[`A-a gradient`,`PAO2 - PaO2`,`Under 10-15 mmHg young; about age/4 + 4; raised in V/Q mismatch, shunt, diffusion defect`],[`P/F ratio`,`PaO2 / FiO2`,`Over 400 normal; 300 or less ARDS range; under 100 severe`],[`Anion gap`,`Na - (Cl + HCO3)`,`About 8-12; add 2.5 per 1 g/dL albumin below 4`],[`Delta ratio`,`(AG - 12) / (24 - HCO3)`,`Under 0.4 NAGMA; 0.4-0.8 mixed; 1-2 pure HAGMA; over 2 plus metabolic alkalosis`],[`Osmolar gap`,`Measured - (2 Na + glucose/18 + urea/6)`,`Over 10 suggests toxic alcohol`],[`Urine anion gap`,`UNa + UK - UCl`,`Negative: diarrhoea; positive: distal RTA`],[`Urine chloride`,`Spot urine Cl`,`Under 20: saline-responsive alkalosis; over 20: saline-resistant`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of metabolic acidosis`,columns:[`Type`,`Causes`,`Discriminating test`],rows:[[`High AG - ketoacidosis`,`DKA, alcoholic, starvation`,`Beta-hydroxybutyrate 3 mmol/L or more; glucose`],[`High AG - lactic`,`Shock, sepsis, metformin, liver failure, thiamine deficiency`,`Lactate over 2 (over 4 high risk)`],[`High AG - toxic alcohol`,`Methanol, ethylene glycol`,`Osmolar gap over 10; visual loss (methanol); oxalate crystals (glycol)`],[`High AG - salicylate`,`Aspirin overdose`,`Coexisting respiratory alkalosis; salicylate level`],[`High AG - renal failure`,`AKI, advanced CKD`,`Urea, creatinine`],[`Normal AG - GI loss`,`Diarrhoea, fistula, ureteric diversion`,`Urine anion gap negative`],[`Normal AG - renal`,`RTA types 1, 2, 4; acetazolamide`,`Urine anion gap positive; urine pH; serum K`],[`Normal AG - iatrogenic`,`Large-volume 0.9% saline`,`High chloride; improves with balanced crystalloid`]]},{heading:`VBG versus ABG`,columns:[`Parameter`,`Venous vs arterial`,`Usable on VBG?`],rows:[[`pH`,`About 0.03-0.04 lower`,`Yes`],[`HCO3`,`About 1-2 mmol/L higher`,`Yes`],[`PCO2`,`About 4-5 mmHg higher, wide limits`,`Only to exclude hypercapnia (under 45)`],[`PO2`,`Much lower, unpredictable`,`No - use SpO2 or ABG`],[`Lactate, K, glucose`,`Similar`,`Yes`]]},{heading:`Drugs used in acid-base and blood gas disorders`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Sodium bicarbonate (8.4%)`,`50-100 mmol IV over 30-60 min in severe acidaemia; DKA only if pH under 7.0`,`Repeat by gas; aim pH over 7.2, not normal`,`Hypernatraemia, volume overload, hypokalaemia, rise in PaCO2 (needs ventilation)`],[`Oral sodium bicarbonate`,`500-1000 mg orally two to three times daily (CKD)`,`Long-term; start if HCO3 persistently under 18`,`Oedema, hypertension`],[`Potassium chloride`,`IV 10-20 mmol/h via peripheral line (max 40 mmol/L concentration peripherally)`,`Titrate to K 4-5 mmol/L`,`Hyperkalaemia, phlebitis; never IV bolus`],[`Acetazolamide`,`250-500 mg IV or oral once or twice daily`,`1-3 days for metabolic alkalosis`,`Hypokalaemia, normal-AG acidosis; avoid in sulfonamide allergy, liver failure`],[`Fomepizole`,`15 mg/kg IV load, then 10 mg/kg every 12 h`,`Until methanol or glycol cleared; increase frequency during dialysis`,`Headache, nausea; ethanol orally or IV is the alternative where unavailable`],[`Folinic acid (methanol)`,`1 mg/kg IV (max 50 mg) every 4-6 h`,`Until methanol cleared`,`Generally safe`],[`Naloxone (opioid hypoventilation)`,`0.4-2 mg IV, repeat every 2-3 min`,`Infusion if long-acting opioid`,`Acute withdrawal, pulmonary oedema`],[`Controlled oxygen (Venturi)`,`24-28% Venturi mask in hypercapnia risk`,`Target SpO2 88-92%; ABG within 60 min`,`Uncontrolled high-flow oxygen worsens hypercapnia`]]}],redFlags:[`pH under 7.20 or over 7.55`,`Rising PaCO2 with pH under 7.35 in COPD or asthma - NIV or ICU`,`Normal or rising PaCO2 in acute severe asthma - impending arrest`,`Lactate over 4 mmol/L or with hypotension - septic shock pathway`,`High anion gap with osmolar gap over 10 - toxic alcohol, call for dialysis`,`P/F ratio under 200 or SpO2 under 90% on high FiO2`,`Potassium under 3.3 mmol/L before insulin in DKA - correct first`],pearls:[`Winter: PaCO2 = 1.5 x HCO3 + 8 plus or minus 2 - or PaCO2 roughly equals the last two digits of pH.`,`Compensation never overshoots - pH on the 'wrong' side of 7.40 means a second disorder.`,`Always correct the anion gap for albumin: add 2.5 per 1 g/dL below 4.`,`Delta ratio over 2 = hidden metabolic alkalosis; under 0.4 = hidden normal-AG acidosis.`,`GOLD MARK for high anion gap acidosis.`,`Normal A-a gradient with hypoxaemia = hypoventilation; give naloxone or ventilation, not just oxygen.`,`Venous PCO2 under 45 excludes arterial hypercapnia.`,`Salicylate = respiratory alkalosis plus high-AG acidosis.`,`A normal PaCO2 in pregnancy or acute asthma is a danger sign.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-abg-interpretation-q1`,stem:`A patient with DKA has pH 7.20, HCO3 10 mmol/L and PaCO2 34 mmHg. What is the acid-base diagnosis?`,options:[`Metabolic acidosis with appropriate respiratory compensation`,`Metabolic acidosis with an additional respiratory acidosis`,`Metabolic acidosis with an additional respiratory alkalosis`,`Chronic respiratory acidosis`],answer:1,explanation:`Winter formula predicts PaCO2 = 1.5 x 10 + 8 = 23 plus or minus 2 (21-25). A measured PaCO2 of 34 is higher than expected, so there is a superimposed respiratory acidosis (tiring, pneumonia or sedation). Appropriate compensation would need PaCO2 21-25, a respiratory alkalosis would need it below 21, and the low bicarbonate excludes chronic respiratory acidosis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-abg-interpretation-q2`,stem:`A 40-year-old man is drowsy after an overdose. On room air: PaO2 50 mmHg, PaCO2 80 mmHg. The A-a gradient is about:`,options:[`0 mmHg - pure hypoventilation`,`40 mmHg - V/Q mismatch`,`60 mmHg - shunt`,`100 mmHg - diffusion defect`],answer:0,explanation:`PAO2 = 150 - 1.25 x 80 = 50 mmHg, and PaO2 is 50, so the gradient is about 0 - the hypoxaemia is entirely due to hypoventilation (drug-induced CNS depression). A gradient of 40-100 would indicate V/Q mismatch, shunt or diffusion problems in the lung, which is not the case here.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-abg-interpretation-q3`,stem:`A man with a high anion gap metabolic acidosis (AG 30) has an osmolar gap of 35 mOsm/kg and blurred vision after drinking illicit liquor. The most appropriate specific treatment is:`,options:[`IV thiamine`,`N-acetylcysteine`,`IV insulin infusion`,`Fomepizole (or ethanol) and haemodialysis`],answer:3,explanation:`High anion gap plus a high osmolar gap with visual symptoms after illicit liquor is methanol poisoning; alcohol dehydrogenase is blocked with fomepizole or ethanol, with folinic acid, bicarbonate and early haemodialysis. Thiamine is for Wernicke and some lactic acidosis, N-acetylcysteine for paracetamol and insulin for DKA.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-abg-interpretation-q4`,stem:`Which finding best indicates a coexisting metabolic alkalosis in a patient with a high anion gap acidosis?`,options:[`Delta ratio of 0.3`,`Delta ratio of 1.2`,`Delta ratio of 2.6`,`Urine anion gap that is negative`],answer:2,explanation:`A delta ratio over 2 means bicarbonate has fallen less than the anion gap has risen, so something is adding bicarbonate - a metabolic alkalosis (for example vomiting). A ratio of 0.3 indicates an added normal-AG acidosis, 1.2 is a pure high-AG acidosis, and a negative urine anion gap points to GI bicarbonate loss.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`respiratory-abg-interpretation-q5`,stem:`In a COPD patient, an ABG shows pH 7.36, PaCO2 60 mmHg and HCO3 32 mmol/L. This represents:`,options:[`Acute respiratory acidosis`,`Primary metabolic alkalosis`,`Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis`,`Compensated chronic respiratory acidosis`],answer:3,explanation:`For a 20 mmHg rise in PaCO2, chronic compensation raises HCO3 by 7-8 to about 31-32, which matches, and the pH is near normal - compensated chronic respiratory acidosis. Acute acidosis would give HCO3 about 26 and pH about 7.24; primary alkalosis would give a high pH; and a mixed acidosis would have a low HCO3.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-abg-interpretation-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Describe a stepwise approach to arterial blood gas interpretation. Discuss the causes and evaluation of metabolic acidosis with the role of anion gap, delta ratio and osmolar gap.`,openingLines:[`An arterial blood gas measures pH, PaCO2, PaO2 and bicarbonate to assess oxygenation, ventilation and acid-base balance.`,`A stepwise approach - oxygenation, pH, primary disorder, compensation, anion gap, delta ratio and osmolar gap - detects mixed disorders and points to the cause.`],answer:[{heading:`Normal values and sampling`,points:[`pH 7.35-7.45, PaCO2 35-45, HCO3 22-26, PaO2 80-100 mmHg, BE -2 to +2.`,`Radial artery, modified Allen test, heparinised syringe, no air, analyse within 30 min, note FiO2.`]},{heading:`Stepwise approach`,points:[`Oxygenation: PaO2, P/F ratio, A-a gradient (PAO2 = 150 - 1.25 x PaCO2).`,`pH, primary disorder, compensation formulas (Winter; 0.7 rule; 1/3.5 and 2/4-5 rules).`,`Anion gap with albumin correction, delta ratio, osmolar gap.`]},{heading:`High anion gap acidosis`,points:[`GOLD MARK: glycols, oxoproline, L- and D-lactate, methanol, aspirin, renal failure, ketoacidosis.`,`Tests: lactate, ketones, osmolar gap, salicylate level, renal function.`]},{heading:`Normal anion gap acidosis`,points:[`Diarrhoea, RTA, ureteric diversion, acetazolamide, saline.`,`Urine anion gap negative in diarrhoea, positive in distal RTA.`]},{heading:`Delta ratio and osmolar gap`,points:[`Delta ratio under 0.4, 0.4-0.8, 1-2, over 2 with meaning.`,`Osmolar gap over 10 - methanol, ethylene glycol.`]},{heading:`Management principles`,points:[`Treat the cause; bicarbonate only for pH 7.2 or less with AKI, DKA pH under 7.0, or bicarbonate-loss acidosis.`,`Fomepizole and dialysis for toxic alcohols.`]},{heading:`Worked example`,points:[`Show a DKA gas with AG, Winter check and delta ratio.`]}],mustDraw:[`Stepwise ABG flow chart`,`Table of compensation formulas`],markSplit:[{part:`Stepwise approach and formulas`,marks:4},{part:`Causes of metabolic acidosis`,marks:3},{part:`AG, delta ratio, osmolar gap`,marks:2},{part:`Example`,marks:1}],keywords:[`ABG`,`Winter formula`,`anion gap`,`delta ratio`,`osmolar gap`,`A-a gradient`]},{id:`respiratory-abg-interpretation-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on the alveolar-arterial (A-a) oxygen gradient.`,openingLines:[`The A-a gradient is the difference between alveolar (PAO2) and arterial (PaO2) oxygen tension.`,`It separates hypoxaemia from hypoventilation (normal gradient) from hypoxaemia due to lung disease (raised gradient).`],answer:[{heading:`Calculation`,points:[`PAO2 = FiO2 x (Patm - 47) - PaCO2/0.8; on room air about 150 - 1.25 x PaCO2.`,`Normal under 10-15 mmHg in the young; about age/4 + 4.`]},{heading:`Normal gradient hypoxaemia`,points:[`Hypoventilation (opioids, neuromuscular disease), low inspired oxygen (altitude).`]},{heading:`Raised gradient hypoxaemia`,points:[`V/Q mismatch (COPD, PE, pneumonia) - corrects with oxygen.`,`Shunt (ARDS, collapse, intracardiac) - does not correct.`,`Diffusion limitation (ILD) - worse on exercise.`]},{heading:`Limitations`,points:[`Less reliable on high FiO2; use P/F ratio in ventilated patients.`]}],mustDraw:[`Branch diagram of the five causes of hypoxaemia with A-a gradient`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and formula`,marks:2},{part:`Causes by gradient`,marks:2},{part:`Limitations`,marks:1}],keywords:[`alveolar gas equation`,`hypoventilation`,`shunt`,`V/Q mismatch`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`O'Driscoll BR et al. BTS guideline for oxygen use in healthcare and emergency settings, Thorax 2017`,`Umpierrez GE et al. Hyperglycemic Crises in Adults With Diabetes: A Consensus Report (ADA/EASD/JBDS/AACE/DTS), Diabetes Care 2024`,`Evans L et al. Surviving Sepsis Campaign: International Guidelines 2021`,`BTS/ICS Guideline for the Ventilatory Management of Acute Hypercapnic Respiratory Failure in Adults, Thorax 2016`,`Matthay MA et al. A New Global Definition of Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome, Am J Respir Crit Care Med 2024`,`KDIGO 2024 Clinical Practice Guideline for the Evaluation and Management of Chronic Kidney Disease`,`ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows, 2023`]}),e.push({id:`respiratory-chest-imaging`,title:`Chest X-ray and CT thorax: a systematic reading and the patterns that matter`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Chest imaging** begins with a **PA chest X-ray read systematically** (quality, then airway, bones, cardiac, diaphragm, lungs and hidden areas) using the **silhouette sign** to localise disease, and proceeds to **CT thorax** - **HRCT** for interstitial patterns (UIP, NSIP, ground glass, mosaic, tree-in-bud), **CTPA** for embolism and **PET-CT** for staging - with **lung ultrasound** as the bedside extension.`,keywords:[`chest X-ray`,`CXR`,`PA view`,`silhouette sign`,`lobar collapse`,`Golden S sign`,`sail sign`,`luftsichel`,`air bronchogram`,`cavity`,`miliary`,`HRCT`,`UIP`,`NSIP`,`tree-in-bud`,`ground glass opacity`,`mosaic attenuation`,`CTPA`,`PET-CT`,`lung ultrasound`,`Kerley B lines`,`computer-aided detection`],sections:[{heading:`Definition, role and Indian context`,points:[`**The chest X-ray** is the commonest imaging test in medicine; a **PA erect view** at full inspiration from 180 cm is standard, while an **AP view** (portable, supine) magnifies the heart and makes effusions and pneumothoraces harder to see. [Grainger and Allison 7e]`,`**Radiation doses**: PA chest X-ray about **0.02 mSv** (a few days of background radiation), standard CT chest about **5-7 mSv**, **low-dose CT about 1-1.5 mSv**, and FDG PET-CT about 10-25 mSv. [ICRP; ACR Appropriateness Criteria]`,`Under **NTEP**, a **chest X-ray is used alongside NAAT** to screen and diagnose presumptive TB, and any CXR abnormality suggestive of TB triggers sputum testing even without cough. [NTEP 2024]`,`The **100-day TB Mukt Bharat Abhiyaan (December 2024 - March 2025)** used **handheld digital X-ray with AI-based computer-aided detection** to screen vulnerable populations in high-burden districts. [MoHFW 2025]`,`**WHO (2021)** recommends **computer-aided detection (CAD)** software as an alternative to human readers for TB screening and triage on chest X-ray in people aged 15 years and above. [WHO TB screening 2021]`,`In India, **post-TB sequelae** (fibrosis, bronchiectasis, aspergilloma, destroyed lung) are among the commonest abnormal chest films, so prior films and treatment history change the interpretation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Imaging is not a diagnosis**: the same opacity can be TB, pneumonia or cancer - the report must be combined with history, sputum NAAT and follow-up imaging. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Film quality: projection, rotation, inspiration and penetration`,points:[`**Projection and position**: check the label (PA or AP, erect or supine); on a PA film the scapulae are rotated off the lung fields. [Grainger and Allison 7e]`,`**Rotation**: the medial ends of the clavicles should be **equidistant from the spinous process**; rotation makes one hemithorax look darker and shifts the mediastinum. [Grainger and Allison 7e]`,`**Inspiration**: adequate when about **6 anterior ribs or 10 posterior ribs** are seen above the diaphragm in the mid-clavicular line; poor inspiration crowds basal vessels and mimics cardiomegaly and basal shadowing. [Grainger and Allison 7e]`,`**Penetration**: the thoracic vertebrae should just be visible behind the heart; an under-penetrated film looks white (false consolidation), an over-penetrated one looks black (false emphysema). [Grainger and Allison 7e]`,`**Cardiothoracic ratio** over 0.5 on a good PA film suggests cardiomegaly; it cannot be judged on an AP film. [Grainger and Allison 7e]`,`**Normal landmarks**: right hemidiaphragm 1-3 cm higher than the left, left hilum up to 1-2 cm higher than the right, trachea central, and costophrenic angles sharp. [Grainger and Allison 7e]`]},{heading:`The systematic read: ABCDE and the hidden areas`,points:[`**A - Airway**: tracheal position and narrowing, carina, main bronchi; **B - Bones and soft tissue**: ribs (fractures, metastases, notching), clavicles, spine, breast shadows, subcutaneous emphysema. [Grainger and Allison 7e]`,`**C - Cardiac and mediastinum**: heart size and borders, aortic knob, mediastinal width, **hila** (size, density, position and concavity). [Grainger and Allison 7e]`,`**D - Diaphragm**: shape, height, costophrenic and cardiophrenic angles, **free air under the diaphragm**, gastric bubble. [Grainger and Allison 7e]`,`**E - Everything else (lung fields)**: compare zones side to side for opacity, lucency, nodules, cavities, lines and vascular markings, then look at tubes, lines and devices. [Grainger and Allison 7e]`,`**Review the hidden areas** last: **apices** (Pancoast, TB), **behind the heart** (LLL collapse, hiatus hernia), **below the diaphragm** (lower lobe disease, free gas), **hila** and the **lateral costophrenic angles**. [Grainger and Allison 7e]`,`**Compare with old films**: a nodule stable for 2 years (solid) is usually benign, and a new opacity in a known post-TB lung needs sputum testing. [Fleischner 2017]`,`**Lateral view** helps localise: the **retrosternal clear space** (anterior mediastinal mass), the **spine sign** (lower vertebrae should look darker; whiter means lower-lobe disease) and fissure position. [Felson]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology of the signs: why opacities look as they do`,points:[`**X-ray contrast depends on density**: air (black), fat, soft tissue and fluid (grey-white), and bone and calcium (white); two structures of the same density in contact lose the border between them. [Felson]`,`**Silhouette sign (Felson)**: a lung opacity that obliterates a heart or diaphragm border lies **in contact with that border**; an opacity that overlaps without obliterating it lies in front or behind. [Felson]`,`**Air bronchogram**: air-filled bronchi become visible only when the surrounding alveoli are filled (pus, fluid, blood, cells) - it proves the opacity is **intrapulmonary** and usually means consolidation. [Felson]`,`**Collapse (atelectasis)** causes volume loss: **fissure displacement** (most reliable), crowding of vessels, elevated hemidiaphragm, mediastinal and hilar shift towards the lesion, and compensatory hyperinflation of the rest. [Grainger and Allison 7e]`,`**Interstitial (reticular) pattern** reflects thickening of the septa and walls (fibrosis, oedema, lymphangitis); **alveolar (airspace) pattern** reflects filled airspaces with fluffy, confluent opacities and air bronchograms. [Grainger and Allison 7e]`,`**Kerley B lines** are 1-2 cm horizontal lines at the lung periphery from thickened interlobular septa - interstitial oedema, lymphangitis carcinomatosa or pneumoconiosis. [Grainger and Allison 7e]`,`**Upper-lobe venous diversion** in left heart failure reflects raised pulmonary venous pressure (about 13-18 mmHg); interstitial oedema appears at 18-25 and **alveolar (bat-wing) oedema** above about 25 mmHg. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Classification that matters: silhouette sign and lobar collapse`,points:[`**Silhouette sign map**: right heart border lost - **right middle lobe**; left heart border lost - **lingula**; right hemidiaphragm - **right lower lobe**; left hemidiaphragm or descending aorta - **left lower lobe**; aortic knob - **left upper lobe**; ascending aorta or SVC - **right upper lobe**. [Felson]`,`**Right upper lobe collapse**: triangular opacity at the right apex with the **horizontal fissure raised and concave**; a central mass gives the **reverse S sign of Golden** - think bronchogenic carcinoma. [Grainger and Allison 7e]`,`**Right middle lobe collapse**: loss of the right heart border with the horizontal fissure displaced down; on the lateral view a thin wedge between the horizontal and oblique fissures. [Grainger and Allison 7e]`,`**Right lower lobe collapse**: triangular opacity in the right lower zone medially, **loss of the medial right hemidiaphragm**, with the **right heart border preserved** (distinguishes it from RML collapse). [Grainger and Allison 7e]`,`**Left upper lobe collapse**: a **hazy veil-like opacity** over the left hemithorax with loss of the left heart border; the **luftsichel sign** is a crescent of lucency around the aortic knob from the hyperinflated superior segment of the left lower lobe. [Grainger and Allison 7e]`,`**Left lower lobe collapse**: a triangular opacity **behind the heart** (**sail sign**) with loss of the medial left hemidiaphragm and the descending aorta outline - easily missed if the retrocardiac area is not looked at. [Grainger and Allison 7e]`,`**Whole-lung collapse**: complete opacity of the hemithorax with **mediastinum shifted towards** it - distinguish from massive effusion, where the mediastinum shifts away. [Grainger and Allison 7e]`,`**Commonest causes of lobar collapse**: in adults **endobronchial tumour, mucus plugging** (post-operative, asthma, ABPA) and **endobronchial TB**; in children, **inhaled foreign body**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Common CXR patterns: consolidation, effusion, pneumothorax, cavity and miliary`,points:[`**Consolidation**: homogeneous opacity with **air bronchograms**, no volume loss, often lobar - pneumonia, but also TB, organising pneumonia, haemorrhage and adenocarcinoma. [Grainger and Allison 7e]`,`**Pleural effusion**: blunting of the costophrenic angle needs about **200 mL on PA** (about 50 mL on lateral); the **meniscus sign** curves up laterally; a supine film shows only diffuse haze. [BTS Pleural 2023]`,`**Pneumothorax**: a visible **visceral pleural line** with no lung markings beyond; a **deep sulcus sign** on supine films; BTS 2023 manages by symptoms and risk rather than size, though **over 2 cm at the hilum** is still called large. [BTS Pleural 2023]`,`**Cavity**: a gas-filled space with a wall over 4 mm; wall thickness **4 mm or less is usually benign, 15 mm or more is often malignant**; upper-lobe cavities in India are TB until proved otherwise. [Woodring; Harrison 22e]`,`**Causes of cavitation**: TB, lung abscess (with air-fluid level), **squamous cell carcinoma**, Staphylococcus, Klebsiella, septic emboli, GPA, fungal infection and pulmonary infarction. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Air-crescent (Monod) sign**: a fungus ball within an old cavity (aspergilloma) that moves with position; a **water-lily (camalote) sign** is a collapsed hydatid membrane floating in fluid. [Grainger and Allison 7e]`,`**Miliary pattern**: innumerable **1-3 mm nodules** uniformly throughout both lungs - **miliary TB** (commonest in India), haematogenous metastases (thyroid, renal, melanoma), silicosis, sarcoidosis and histoplasmosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hilar enlargement**: bilateral symmetrical nodes - **sarcoidosis**, lymphoma, TB; unilateral - **TB in young adults, carcinoma in smokers**; enlarged pulmonary arteries with pruning suggest pulmonary hypertension. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Heart failure on CXR (ABCDE)**: alveolar oedema (bat wing), Kerley **B lines**, cardiomegaly, **dilated upper-lobe vessels**, effusions. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Nodules and masses on CXR and CT`,points:[`A **nodule** is a rounded opacity **up to 3 cm**; over 3 cm it is a **mass** and is presumed malignant until proved otherwise. [Fleischner Glossary 2024]`,`**Benign features**: **central, laminated, diffuse or popcorn calcification**, fat (hamartoma), stability over 2 years for solid nodules, and small size; **malignant features**: spiculation, lobulation, upper-lobe location, growth, size over 8 mm and smoking history. [Fleischner 2017]`,`**Fleischner 2017** applies to incidental nodules in adults 35 and older, not to cancer screening or immunocompromised patients; solid nodules **under 6 mm** in low-risk patients need no routine follow-up - see the solitary pulmonary nodule topic. [Fleischner 2017]`,`**Subsolid nodules** (pure ground glass or part-solid) may be adenocarcinoma spectrum and need longer follow-up (up to 5 years); a **growing solid component** raises suspicion. [Fleischner 2017]`,`**Multiple nodules**: metastases (variable size, basal and peripheral), septic emboli, GPA, sarcoidosis, rheumatoid nodules, silicosis and fungal infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lung-RADS v2022** (ACR) categorises nodules found on **low-dose CT screening**, separately from Fleischner. [ACR Lung-RADS 2022]`]},{heading:`HRCT patterns that matter`,points:[`**HRCT** uses thin (1-1.25 mm) sections with a high-resolution algorithm, ideally with **prone and expiratory images**, to show the **secondary pulmonary lobule** - the basic unit of HRCT interpretation. [Fleischner Glossary 2024]`,`**UIP pattern**: **subpleural, basal-predominant reticulation with honeycombing** (clustered cysts 3-10 mm), with or without traction bronchiectasis - in the right clinical setting it is diagnostic of **IPF without biopsy**. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`**UIP categories (2022)**: UIP, probable UIP (no honeycombing), indeterminate, and **alternative diagnosis**; 'probable UIP' is now treated as UIP for IPF diagnosis in the right context. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`**NSIP pattern**: bilateral basal **ground glass with fine reticulation and traction bronchiectasis**, often with **subpleural sparing**, little honeycombing - typical of connective tissue disease ILD. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ground glass opacity (GGO)**: hazy increased attenuation that **does not obscure vessels** - oedema, infection (**PCP, COVID-19**, viral), haemorrhage, early ILD, hypersensitivity pneumonitis, adenocarcinoma spectrum. [Fleischner Glossary 2024]`,`**Crazy paving**: GGO with superimposed septal thickening - **pulmonary alveolar proteinosis**, PCP, COVID-19, ARDS and haemorrhage. [Fleischner Glossary 2024]`,`**Tree-in-bud**: centrilobular branching nodules from filled small airways - **endobronchial spread of TB**, NTM, bacterial bronchiolitis, aspiration and ABPA; in India it signals **active TB** until proved otherwise. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mosaic attenuation**: patchwork of differing lung density from **small airway disease** (air trapping that persists on expiratory CT), **vascular disease** (CTEPH, with variable vessel size) or infiltrative disease; the **three-density (headcheese) sign** suggests fibrotic hypersensitivity pneumonitis. [ATS/JRS/ALAT HP 2020]`,`**Nodule distribution**: **perilymphatic** (sarcoidosis, lymphangitis, silicosis), **centrilobular** (HP, infectious bronchiolitis, RB-ILD) and **random** (miliary TB, haematogenous metastases). [Fleischner Glossary 2024]`,`**Other signs**: **signet-ring sign** (bronchus wider than its artery - bronchiectasis), **halo sign** (nodule with GGO rim - angioinvasive aspergillosis), **reverse halo (atoll) sign** (organising pneumonia, mucormycosis) and **cysts** (LAM, Langerhans cell histiocytosis, LIP). [Fleischner Glossary 2024]`]},{heading:`When to order CT, CTPA, PET-CT and other tests`,points:[`**Contrast-enhanced CT thorax**: unresolved or atypical opacity, suspected lung cancer (with upper abdomen for staging), mediastinal mass, empyema, lung abscess and **haemoptysis** (CT before bronchoscopy). [ACR Appropriateness Criteria]`,`**HRCT (non-contrast)**: suspected ILD, bronchiectasis, unexplained breathlessness with crackles or low DLCO, and follow-up of fibrosis. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`**CTPA**: suspected PE when clinical probability is high or D-dimer is positive (Wells, YEARS) - see the pulmonary embolism topic; **V/Q scan** is preferred with severe contrast allergy, renal failure and in pregnancy with a normal CXR. [ESC 2019 PE]`,`**FDG PET-CT**: staging of NSCLC, characterising **solid nodules 8 mm or more** with intermediate probability; false positives from **TB, sarcoidosis and fungal infection** are common in India, and false negatives occur in carcinoid, lepidic adenocarcinoma and nodules under 8 mm. [NCCN NSCLC 2025]`,`**Low-dose CT screening**: USPSTF recommends annual LDCT for adults **50-80 years with 20 or more pack-years** who smoke now or quit within 15 years; ACS 2023 removed the years-since-quitting limit; India has **no national screening programme**, partly because TB-related nodules cause false positives. [USPSTF 2021; ACS 2023]`,`**MRI thorax** is used for Pancoast tumour (brachial plexus and chest wall invasion), cardiac and vascular assessment, and when radiation must be avoided. [ACR Appropriateness Criteria]`,`**Contrast safety**: iodinated contrast-associated AKI risk is significant mainly at **eGFR under 30 mL/min/1.73 m2**; withhold **metformin** after contrast in those with eGFR under 30 or AKI. [ACR-NKF 2020]`]},{heading:`Lung ultrasound basics`,points:[`**Normal lung**: pleural line with **lung sliding** and horizontal reverberation **A-lines**; on M-mode the **seashore sign**. [Int Lung US Consensus 2023]`,`**Pneumothorax**: absent lung sliding, A-lines only, **barcode (stratosphere) sign** on M-mode, and the **lung point** - where sliding lung meets the pneumothorax - which is about 100% specific. [Lichtenstein BLUE 2008]`,`**B-lines**: vertical, laser-like artefacts from the pleura to the screen edge that move with sliding; **3 or more per intercostal space** in several zones indicates **interstitial syndrome** (pulmonary oedema, ILD, ARDS, COVID). [Int Lung US Consensus 2023]`,`**Effusion**: anechoic space above the diaphragm with the **spine sign** (vertebrae visible above the diaphragm) and a floating lung; ultrasound detects **as little as 20 mL** and guides thoracocentesis. [BTS Pleural 2023]`,`**Consolidation**: tissue-like ('hepatised') lung with **dynamic air bronchograms** (moving with breath) - pneumonia; static bronchograms suggest obstructive atelectasis. [Int Lung US Consensus 2023]`,`**BLUE protocol**: a structured lung ultrasound sequence in acute breathlessness that separates pulmonary oedema, pneumonia, PE (with leg DVT), pneumothorax and COPD/asthma with about **90% accuracy**. [Lichtenstein BLUE 2008]`,`**Advantages**: no radiation, bedside, repeatable, better than CXR for effusion, pneumothorax and interstitial syndrome; **limitations**: cannot see deep lesions, obesity and subcutaneous emphysema degrade images. [Int Lung US Consensus 2023]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: a chest X-ray gives a fetal dose **under 0.01 mGy** and CT chest or CTPA under 1 mGy, far below the **50 mGy** threshold for fetal harm - never withhold a needed chest film. [ACOG 2017]`,`**Pregnancy and PE**: start with a CXR; if normal, **V/Q (perfusion) scan** gives lower breast dose, while CTPA is preferred if the CXR is abnormal. [ESC 2019 PE]`,`**Children**: follow **ALARA**, prefer ultrasound, and look for **inhaled foreign body** (air trapping on expiratory or decubitus films) and the thymic sail sign that is normal under 2 years. [Nelson 22e]`,`**Immunocompromised and HIV**: a normal CXR does not exclude PCP or TB - **HRCT** may show GGO or nodules; TB in advanced HIV often looks like primary TB (lower zone, nodes, no cavity). [Harrison 22e]`,`**ICU and supine films**: effusions layer posteriorly (diffuse haze), pneumothorax collects anteriorly (deep sulcus sign), and lines and tubes must be checked - the **ETT tip 5 cm (plus or minus 2) above the carina**. [Grainger and Allison 7e]`,`**CKD and diabetes**: avoid unnecessary contrast, hydrate if eGFR under 30, and hold metformin; use non-contrast HRCT or V/Q when appropriate. [ACR-NKF 2020]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: a good-quality **PA film with a lateral** and **lung ultrasound** answer most questions at a district hospital; CT is reserved for problems the film cannot solve. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications of imaging and common pitfalls`,points:[`**Contrast reactions**: mild (urticaria, nausea) are common; severe anaphylactoid reactions are rare (about 0.01-0.04% with low-osmolar agents) - have **adrenaline 0.5 mg IM** ready. [ACR Contrast Manual]`,`**Cumulative radiation** from repeated CT raises lifetime cancer risk, especially in children and young women; justify each scan and use low-dose protocols. [ICRP]`,`**Satisfaction of search**: stopping after the first abnormality misses a second (the nodule behind the heart, the rib metastasis, the pneumothorax). [Grainger and Allison 7e]`,`**Mimics**: nipple shadows (check bilateral and with markers), skin folds mimicking pneumothorax, breast shadows causing apparent basal opacity, and **pericardial fat pads**. [Grainger and Allison 7e]`,`**Lagging resolution**: pneumonia can take **6 weeks or longer** to clear radiologically, especially in the elderly - repeat CXR at 6-8 weeks if symptoms persist or the patient is at cancer risk, not routinely. [BTS CAP 2009]`,`**Over-reading CT**: incidental nodules and ground glass are very common; follow Fleischner rules to avoid harm from unnecessary biopsy. [Fleischner 2017]`]},{heading:`Referral and follow-up imaging`,points:[`**Urgent CXR within 2 weeks** for age 40 and over with unexplained haemoptysis (direct referral for suspected cancer), or two or more of cough, fatigue, breathlessness, chest pain, weight loss and appetite loss (one if ever smoked). [NICE NG12]`,`**Refer for CT and respiratory review**: CXR suggestive of lung cancer, persistent unexplained opacity, lobar collapse in an adult, hilar enlargement, or pleural effusion without obvious cause. [NICE NG12]`,`**TB response**: CXR at the end of intensive phase is not a substitute for sputum; residual lesions are expected and do not by themselves indicate treatment failure. [NTEP 2024]`,`**ILD**: repeat HRCT only when progression is suspected clinically or by FVC/DLCO decline, not routinely every year. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`**Interstitial lung abnormalities** found incidentally (in 4-9% of smokers) need PFTs and clinical review, and follow-up CT if risk features are present. [Fleischner ILA 2020]`,`**Pneumothorax**: repeat CXR after drain removal or aspiration and before air travel (wait at least **1-2 weeks after radiological resolution**). [BTS Pleural 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2020-2026)`,points:[`**ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022 IPF guideline** keeps the four HRCT categories, accepts **probable UIP as sufficient** for IPF diagnosis in the right setting, and defines **progressive pulmonary fibrosis**, including radiological progression. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`**Fleischner Society glossary 2024 update** revises definitions of thoracic imaging terms used in CT reporting. [Fleischner Glossary 2024]`,`**BTS Pleural Disease Guideline 2023** recommends **thoracic ultrasound guidance for all pleural procedures** and symptom-based management of pneumothorax rather than size alone. [BTS Pleural 2023]`,`**International lung ultrasound consensus 2023** standardises B-line and consolidation definitions and supports LUS as first-line in many acute settings. [Int Lung US Consensus 2023]`,`**ACR Lung-RADS v2022** updated categories for screening CT, including airway nodules and atypical pulmonary cysts. [ACR Lung-RADS 2022]`,`**ACS 2023 lung cancer screening** widened eligibility to adults 50-80 with 20 or more pack-years regardless of years since quitting. [ACS 2023]`,`**AI-assisted CXR reading (CAD for TB)** is now part of Indian TB case finding, supported by WHO 2021 guidance and used in the 2024-2025 TB Mukt Bharat campaign. [WHO 2021; MoHFW 2025]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Viva: '**What is the silhouette sign?**' - loss of a normal border when an opacity of soft-tissue density touches it; then name the six borders and lobes. [Felson]`,`Viva: '**Describe a CXR**' - always start with patient details, projection and quality (RIPE: rotation, inspiration, projection, exposure) before the abnormality. [Grainger and Allison 7e]`,`Viva: '**Collapse versus effusion on CXR**' - mediastinal shift towards versus away, fissure displacement, air bronchograms absent in both. [Grainger and Allison 7e]`,`Viva: '**Causes of miliary shadowing**' and '**causes of cavitation**' - lead with TB in India, then list the rest. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake**: calling every upper-zone opacity 'Koch's' without sputum NAAT; old fibrosis, aspergilloma and cancer all occur in post-TB lungs. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Common mistake**: missing the **retrocardiac** left lower lobe collapse and the apices - always say you checked the hidden areas. [Grainger and Allison 7e]`,`Viva: '**HRCT signs of IPF**' - subpleural basal reticulation, honeycombing, traction bronchiectasis, no features of an alternative diagnosis. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Silhouette sign and lobar localisation`,columns:[`Border lost`,`Lobe involved`,`Collapse appearance`],rows:[[`Ascending aorta / SVC (right upper mediastinum)`,`Right upper lobe`,`Raised horizontal fissure; Golden reverse S if central mass`],[`Right heart border`,`Right middle lobe`,`Horizontal fissure displaced down; wedge on lateral`],[`Right hemidiaphragm (medial)`,`Right lower lobe`,`Triangle at right base; right heart border preserved`],[`Aortic knob`,`Left upper lobe (apicoposterior)`,`Veil-like haze; luftsichel sign`],[`Left heart border`,`Lingula / left upper lobe`,`Haze over left mid zone`],[`Left hemidiaphragm / descending aorta`,`Left lower lobe`,`Sail sign behind the heart`]]},{heading:`HRCT patterns and their differential`,columns:[`Pattern`,`Description`,`Main causes`],rows:[[`UIP`,`Subpleural basal reticulation, honeycombing, traction bronchiectasis`,`IPF, RA-ILD, asbestosis, fibrotic HP`],[`NSIP`,`Basal GGO with fine reticulation, subpleural sparing`,`Connective tissue disease, drugs, idiopathic NSIP`],[`Ground glass`,`Hazy attenuation, vessels visible`,`Oedema, PCP, COVID-19, haemorrhage, HP`],[`Crazy paving`,`GGO plus septal thickening`,`Alveolar proteinosis, PCP, ARDS`],[`Tree-in-bud`,`Centrilobular branching nodules`,`Active TB, NTM, bronchiolitis, aspiration`],[`Mosaic attenuation`,`Patchy density; air trapping on expiration`,`Small airway disease, HP, CTEPH`],[`Random (miliary) nodules`,`Uniform 1-3 mm nodules, involve fissures`,`Miliary TB, metastases, fungal`],[`Perilymphatic nodules`,`Along fissures, septa, bronchovascular bundles`,`Sarcoidosis, lymphangitis, silicosis`],[`Cysts`,`Thin-walled air spaces`,`LAM, Langerhans cell histiocytosis, LIP`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of common CXR findings`,columns:[`Finding`,`Causes (Indian order of likelihood)`,`Next step`],rows:[[`Upper-zone opacity with cavity`,`TB, post-TB with aspergilloma, squamous carcinoma, abscess`,`Sputum NAAT; CT`],[`Miliary nodules`,`Miliary TB, metastases, silicosis, sarcoidosis`,`NAAT on sputum or other fluids; fundoscopy; CT`],[`Bilateral hilar enlargement`,`Sarcoidosis, TB, lymphoma, pulmonary hypertension`,`CT; ACE, EBUS-TBNA`],[`White hemithorax, mediastinum away`,`Massive effusion`,`Ultrasound and tap`],[`White hemithorax, mediastinum towards`,`Collapse, pneumonectomy, destroyed lung (post-TB)`,`CT; bronchoscopy`],[`Bilateral basal reticulation`,`IPF, CTD-ILD, asbestosis, oedema`,`HRCT; PFT with DLCO`],[`Bat-wing perihilar shadowing`,`Pulmonary oedema, PCP, alveolar haemorrhage`,`Echo, BNP; HIV test`]]},{heading:`Choosing the next imaging test`,columns:[`Question`,`Test`,`Key point`],rows:[[`Any respiratory symptom or presumptive TB`,`CXR PA (plus NAAT)`,`Lateral if localisation unclear`],[`Effusion, pneumothorax, consolidation at bedside`,`Lung ultrasound`,`Detects about 20 mL fluid; guides tap`],[`Interstitial disease, bronchiectasis`,`HRCT non-contrast`,`Prone and expiratory images`],[`Suspected PE`,`CTPA (V/Q if pregnant with normal CXR, contrast allergy, CKD)`,`After Wells/YEARS and D-dimer`],[`Mass, mediastinum, empyema, haemoptysis`,`Contrast-enhanced CT`,`Include upper abdomen for staging`],[`Lung cancer staging; nodule 8 mm or more`,`FDG PET-CT`,`False positives with TB and sarcoid`],[`Screening heavy smokers`,`Low-dose CT`,`USPSTF 50-80 years, 20 pack-years`]]},{heading:`Drugs and agents used in chest imaging`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Iodinated contrast (low or iso-osmolar, e.g. iohexol)`,`About 50-100 mL IV by pump for CT / CTPA (about 1-1.5 mL/kg)`,`Single dose`,`Contrast reaction; AKI risk mainly eGFR under 30; thyrotoxicosis risk`],[`Prednisolone premedication (prior contrast reaction)`,`50 mg oral at 13, 7 and 1 h before`,`Single course`,`Hyperglycaemia; does not prevent all reactions`],[`Antihistamine premedication (pheniramine or diphenhydramine)`,`Pheniramine 22.75 mg IV or diphenhydramine 50 mg IV/oral 1 h before`,`Single dose`,`Sedation - no driving`],[`Adrenaline for contrast anaphylaxis`,`0.5 mg (0.5 mL of 1 mg/mL) IM anterolateral thigh`,`Repeat every 5 min if needed`,`Tachycardia, arrhythmia; never withhold in anaphylaxis`],[`0.9% saline (contrast AKI prophylaxis)`,`1 mL/kg/h IV for 6-12 h before and after`,`Only if eGFR under 30 or AKI`,`Fluid overload in heart failure`],[`Metformin (hold)`,`Stop at time of contrast`,`Restart after 48 h if renal function stable`,`Lactic acidosis risk only with AKI or eGFR under 30`],[`18F-FDG for PET-CT`,`IV tracer per protocol after 4-6 h fast`,`Scan about 60 min after injection`,`Blood glucose should be under about 200 mg/dL; false positive in TB`]]}],redFlags:[`Tension pneumothorax signs or a large pneumothorax with breathlessness`,`Mediastinal widening with chest pain - exclude aortic dissection`,`Lobar collapse in an adult smoker - endobronchial tumour until proved otherwise`,`New mass, hilar enlargement or a cavity with thick irregular wall in a smoker`,`Free gas under the diaphragm on an erect film`,`Bilateral diffuse infiltrates with hypoxaemia - ARDS, PCP or alveolar haemorrhage`,`Miliary pattern - start TB work-up and look for meningitis`],pearls:[`RIPE for quality: Rotation, Inspiration, Projection, Exposure.`,`Silhouette sign: the lesion touches the border it hides.`,`Air bronchogram proves the opacity is in the lung.`,`Fissure displacement is the most reliable sign of collapse.`,`Sail sign = left lower lobe collapse; luftsichel = left upper lobe collapse; Golden S = RUL collapse with central mass.`,`Cavity wall 4 mm or less usually benign; 15 mm or more often malignant.`,`Tree-in-bud in India = active TB until proved otherwise.`,`UIP = subpleural, basal, honeycombing; NSIP = GGO with subpleural sparing.`,`Lung point on ultrasound is about 100% specific for pneumothorax.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-chest-imaging-q1`,stem:`On a PA chest X-ray, an opacity in the right lower zone obscures the right heart border but the right hemidiaphragm outline is preserved. The lesion lies in the:`,options:[`Right lower lobe`,`Right middle lobe`,`Posterior segment of right upper lobe`,`Right pleural space posteriorly`],answer:1,explanation:`By the silhouette sign, loss of the right heart border means the opacity touches it, which places it in the right middle lobe. A right lower lobe lesion would obscure the hemidiaphragm and leave the heart border visible; upper lobe and posterior pleural lesions do not touch the right heart border.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-chest-imaging-q2`,stem:`A triangular opacity is seen behind the heart with loss of the medial left hemidiaphragm and of the descending aorta outline. This is:`,options:[`Left upper lobe collapse`,`Lingular consolidation`,`Hiatus hernia`,`Left lower lobe collapse`],answer:3,explanation:`The retrocardiac triangle (sail sign) with loss of the left hemidiaphragm and descending aorta is left lower lobe collapse. Left upper lobe collapse gives an anterior veil with the luftsichel sign, lingular disease hides the left heart border, and a hiatus hernia usually shows an air-fluid level behind the heart.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-chest-imaging-q3`,stem:`HRCT in a 68-year-old man shows subpleural, basal-predominant reticulation with honeycombing and traction bronchiectasis. The pattern is:`,options:[`Usual interstitial pneumonia (UIP)`,`Non-specific interstitial pneumonia (NSIP)`,`Organising pneumonia`,`Hypersensitivity pneumonitis with mosaic attenuation`],answer:0,explanation:`Subpleural basal reticulation with honeycombing and traction bronchiectasis is the UIP pattern, diagnostic of IPF in the right context. NSIP shows ground glass with subpleural sparing and little honeycombing, organising pneumonia shows patchy consolidation or reverse halo, and HP shows upper/mid-zone centrilobular nodules and mosaic attenuation.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-chest-imaging-q4`,stem:`On lung ultrasound in a breathless trauma patient, which finding is most specific for pneumothorax?`,options:[`Absent lung sliding`,`Multiple B-lines`,`Lung point`,`Seashore sign on M-mode`],answer:2,explanation:`The lung point - the spot where sliding lung meets non-sliding pneumothorax - is about 100% specific. Absent sliding is sensitive but also occurs with pleurodesis, apnoea and main-stem intubation; B-lines exclude pneumothorax at that point; and the seashore sign is the normal M-mode appearance.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-chest-imaging-q5`,stem:`Which HRCT finding in a sputum-negative Indian patient with cough most strongly suggests active endobronchial spread of tuberculosis?`,options:[`Perilymphatic nodules along the fissures`,`Tree-in-bud centrilobular nodules`,`Subpleural honeycombing`,`Crazy-paving pattern`],answer:1,explanation:`Tree-in-bud nodules represent filled small airways and are the classic CT sign of active endobronchial TB spread, prompting bronchoalveolar lavage for NAAT. Perilymphatic nodules suggest sarcoid or silicosis, honeycombing indicates fibrosis such as UIP, and crazy paving suggests alveolar proteinosis or PCP.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-chest-imaging-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Describe a systematic approach to reading a chest X-ray. Explain the silhouette sign and describe the radiological features of lobar collapse. Add a note on HRCT patterns in interstitial lung disease.`,openingLines:[`A chest X-ray is read systematically - patient details and quality first, then airway, bones, cardiac, diaphragm, lungs and hidden areas - so that no abnormality is missed.`,`The silhouette sign localises an opacity by the normal border it obscures, and lobar collapse is recognised by direct signs (fissure shift) and indirect signs of volume loss.`],answer:[{heading:`Quality and systematic read`,points:[`Patient details, PA/AP, RIPE: rotation, inspiration (6 anterior ribs), projection, exposure.`,`ABCDE: airway, bones/soft tissue, cardiac/mediastinum/hila, diaphragm, everything (lungs, lines).`,`Hidden areas: apices, retrocardiac, below diaphragm, hila, costophrenic angles.`]},{heading:`Silhouette sign`,points:[`Definition (Felson); map of six borders to lobes.`,`Air bronchogram as proof of intrapulmonary disease.`]},{heading:`Lobar collapse`,points:[`Direct: fissure displacement, increased density; indirect: mediastinal shift, raised diaphragm, hilar shift, crowding of vessels.`,`RUL (Golden S), RML, RLL, LUL (luftsichel, veil), LLL (sail sign).`,`Causes: tumour, mucus plug, foreign body, endobronchial TB.`]},{heading:`HRCT patterns`,points:[`UIP, NSIP, ground glass, crazy paving, tree-in-bud, mosaic attenuation, nodule distributions, cysts.`,`2022 UIP categories.`]},{heading:`When to go beyond CXR`,points:[`CT, HRCT, CTPA, PET-CT, lung ultrasound with indications.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagrams of the five lobar collapse patterns on PA film`,`Table of silhouette sign borders and lobes`],markSplit:[{part:`Systematic read and quality`,marks:3},{part:`Silhouette sign`,marks:2},{part:`Lobar collapse`,marks:3},{part:`HRCT patterns`,marks:2}],keywords:[`silhouette sign`,`RIPE`,`Golden S`,`sail sign`,`luftsichel`,`UIP`,`NSIP`,`tree-in-bud`]},{id:`respiratory-chest-imaging-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on lung ultrasound in the evaluation of acute breathlessness.`,openingLines:[`Lung ultrasound is a radiation-free bedside test that uses pleural sliding and artefacts (A-lines, B-lines) to diagnose pneumothorax, effusion, interstitial syndrome and consolidation.`,`In acute breathlessness the BLUE protocol identifies the cause with about 90% accuracy.`],answer:[{heading:`Normal findings`,points:[`Lung sliding, A-lines, seashore sign on M-mode.`]},{heading:`Abnormal findings`,points:[`Pneumothorax: no sliding, barcode sign, lung point.`,`Interstitial syndrome: 3 or more B-lines per space.`,`Effusion: anechoic space, spine sign; consolidation: hepatised lung, dynamic air bronchograms.`]},{heading:`BLUE protocol`,points:[`Oedema (bilateral B-lines), pneumonia, PE (normal lung plus DVT), pneumothorax, COPD/asthma (A-lines).`]},{heading:`Advantages and limitations`,points:[`Bedside, no radiation, better than CXR for effusion; poor for deep lesions, obesity, emphysema.`]}],mustDraw:[`BLUE protocol flow chart`],markSplit:[{part:`Normal and abnormal signs`,marks:3},{part:`BLUE protocol`,marks:1},{part:`Advantages and limitations`,marks:1}],keywords:[`lung sliding`,`A-lines`,`B-lines`,`lung point`,`BLUE protocol`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`Grainger and Allison's Diagnostic Radiology, 7th edition, 2021`,`Felson's Principles of Chest Roentgenology, 5th edition, 2020`,`MacMahon H et al. Guidelines for Management of Incidental Pulmonary Nodules Detected on CT Images: Fleischner Society 2017`,`Raghu G et al. Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis (an Update) and Progressive Pulmonary Fibrosis in Adults: ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT Clinical Practice Guideline, 2022`,`Roberts ME et al. British Thoracic Society Guideline for Pleural Disease, Thorax 2023`,`Demi L et al. New International Guidelines and Consensus on the Use of Lung Ultrasound, J Ultrasound Med 2023`,`WHO Consolidated Guidelines on Tuberculosis, Module 2: Screening, 2021; NTEP Training Modules, MoHFW 2024`]});var t=[];t.push({id:`respiratory-haemoptysis`,title:`Haemoptysis: approach, massive haemoptysis and bronchial artery embolisation`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Haemoptysis** is the coughing up of blood from the **lower respiratory tract (below the vocal cords)**; in India it is most often due to **active or post-tuberculous lung disease**, and **massive (life-threatening) haemoptysis** - defined by its effect on the **airway, gas exchange or circulation** rather than by volume alone - kills by **asphyxia, not exsanguination**, so the bleeding lung is placed **down**, the good lung protected, and the **bronchial artery** (the source in about 90%) **embolised**.`,keywords:[`hemoptysis`,`massive haemoptysis`,`life-threatening haemoptysis`,`pseudohaemoptysis`,`bronchial artery embolisation`,`BAE`,`Rasmussen aneurysm`,`aspergilloma`,`post-tubercular sequelae`,`tranexamic acid`,`bronchial blocker`,`rigid bronchoscopy`,`CT angiography`,`pulmonary apoplexy`,`mitral stenosis`,`cryptogenic haemoptysis`,`paragonimiasis`,`Dieulafoy lesion of bronchus`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Haemoptysis** is expectoration of blood or blood-stained sputum originating **below the vocal cords** - from the trachea, bronchi or lung parenchyma. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pseudohaemoptysis** is blood coughed out that comes from **above the cords** (nose, nasopharynx, mouth, gums) or is **haematemesis** aspirated and then coughed - it must be excluded first. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Massive or life-threatening haemoptysis** has no single volume cut-off: thresholds of **100 to 1000 mL in 24 hours** are used, the classic figure being **600 mL or more in 24 hours**; current practice defines it by consequence - **airway obstruction, hypoxaemia or haemodynamic instability**. [Fishman 6e]`,`The **anatomical dead space is only about 150-200 mL**, so a bleed of this size can flood the airways and asphyxiate a patient long before blood loss causes shock. [Harrison 22e]`,`Most haemoptysis is minor: **massive haemoptysis is about 5% of all haemoptysis**, yet carries the highest risk of death. [Fishman 6e]`,`In western series the commonest causes are **acute bronchitis, bronchiectasis and lung cancer**; in India **active pulmonary TB and post-TB sequelae** (bronchiectasis, cavities, aspergilloma) lead the list. [Harrison 22e]`,`Indian bronchial artery embolisation series from tertiary centres (e.g. PGIMER Chandigarh, AIIMS New Delhi) consistently find **TB and post-TB lung disease** to be the dominant cause of haemoptysis needing intervention. [Indian J Radiol Imaging]`,`India has the world's largest TB burden (about a quarter of global cases), so **haemoptysis in India is presumed TB until proved otherwise** and enters the NTEP testing pathway. [WHO Global TB Report 2025]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Infection**: active TB (cavity erosion, endobronchial TB), pneumonia (rusty sputum in pneumococcal, **red-currant jelly in Klebsiella**), lung abscess, acute bronchitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Post-infective structural disease**: **post-TB bronchiectasis**, fibrocavitary disease, **aspergilloma / chronic pulmonary aspergillosis** in an old TB cavity, and broncholiths. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Neoplasm**: bronchogenic carcinoma (especially **squamous cell**, central), **carcinoid** (vascular endobronchial tumour), and endobronchial metastases (renal, breast, melanoma, colon). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cardiovascular**: **mitral stenosis** (pulmonary apoplexy from ruptured bronchial varices, pink frothy sputum in pulmonary oedema), left ventricular failure, **pulmonary embolism with infarction**, pulmonary AV malformation (HHT). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Vasculitis and alveolar haemorrhage**: granulomatosis with polyangiitis, microscopic polyangiitis, anti-GBM disease, SLE - often with anaemia, haematuria and diffuse infiltrates (see the Diffuse alveolar haemorrhage topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tropical and regional**: **paragonimiasis** (lung fluke from raw crab in North-East India - Manipur, Arunachal), leptospirosis pulmonary haemorrhage, dengue, hydatid cyst rupture, melioidosis. [API Textbook 12e]`,`**Haematological and drugs**: thrombocytopenia, **warfarin, DOACs, antiplatelets, thrombolytics**, bevacizumab (cavitating squamous tumours); anticoagulation usually unmasks a local lesion rather than causing bleeding alone. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Iatrogenic and trauma**: transbronchial or CT-guided biopsy, pulmonary artery catheter rupture, chest trauma, tracheo-innominate fistula after tracheostomy. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Rare causes**: **catamenial haemoptysis** (thoracic endometriosis, with menses), **Dieulafoy lesion of the bronchus**, cocaine inhalation, broncholithiasis. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Cryptogenic haemoptysis** - no cause after CT and bronchoscopy - accounts for roughly **10-20%**; it is commoner in smokers and needs follow-up because cancer can declare itself later. [Fishman 6e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology: why the bronchial circulation bleeds`,points:[`The lung has a **dual blood supply**: the **pulmonary arteries** (low pressure, about 25/10 mmHg, gas exchange) and the **bronchial arteries** (systemic pressure, nourish airways and visceral pleura). [Harrison 22e]`,`About **90% of massive haemoptysis comes from the bronchial arteries**, about 5% from the pulmonary arteries and the rest from **non-bronchial systemic collaterals** (intercostal, internal mammary, inferior phrenic, thyrocervical). [CIRSE 2022]`,`Chronic inflammation (TB, bronchiectasis, aspergilloma) releases **VEGF and angiogenic factors**, so bronchial arteries become **hypertrophied, tortuous and fragile** with **bronchial-to-pulmonary shunts** - systemic pressure in thin new vessels explains the brisk bleeding. [Fishman 6e]`,`Bronchial arteries usually arise from the **descending thoracic aorta at T5-T6**; the commonest pattern (Cauldwell type I) is **one right intercostobronchial trunk and two left bronchial arteries**. [CIRSE 2022]`,`The **anterior spinal artery** may arise from a right intercostobronchial trunk (and the artery of Adamkiewicz from T8-L1 intercostals) - non-target embolisation of it causes **spinal cord ischaemia**, the most feared BAE complication. [CIRSE 2022]`,`**Rasmussen aneurysm** is a **pulmonary artery pseudoaneurysm in the wall of a TB cavity**; it bleeds at pulmonary pressure and is missed by bronchial angiography - needs pulmonary angiography or CT angiography. [Fishman 6e]`,`In **mitral stenosis**, raised left atrial pressure distends **bronchial veins (bronchopulmonary varices)**, which rupture to cause sudden large bleeds (**pulmonary apoplexy**), usually early in the disease. [Braunwald 12e]`,`In **diffuse alveolar haemorrhage**, capillaritis disrupts the alveolar-capillary membrane; haemoptysis may be absent in a third despite a falling haemoglobin and diffuse infiltrates. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Death is from asphyxia**: clot fills the airways, blood spills into the good lung, and V/Q mismatch causes refractory hypoxaemia - hence the priority on **isolating the bleeding lung**. [Fishman 6e]`]},{heading:`Grading the bleed and the classification examiners ask`,points:[`Grade by volume in 24 hours, knowing schemes vary: **streaking** (blood flecks in sputum), **mild under 100 mL**, **moderate 100-600 mL** (some use 100-500) and **massive 600 mL or more** (or 100-150 mL per hour). [Fishman 6e]`,`Patients overestimate volume; anchor it with household measures: **teaspoon about 5 mL, tablespoon about 15 mL, cup about 240 mL**, or a graduated sputum cup at the bedside. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Life-threatening haemoptysis** (consequence-based definition): any volume causing **airway obstruction, SpO2 fall or need for oxygen/ventilation, haemodynamic instability or need for transfusion** - this drives management, not the number. [ERJ 2008 (Ibrahim)]`,`Classify by **source**: bronchial artery (most), non-bronchial systemic collateral, pulmonary artery (Rasmussen, PA catheter, AVM), and capillary (DAH). [CIRSE 2022]`,`Classify by **pattern**: localised (cavity, tumour, bronchiectasis - one side on CT) versus **diffuse** (DAH, coagulopathy, MS, LVF - bilateral infiltrates). [Harrison 22e]`,`Classify by **course**: first episode versus **recurrent** - recurrent small bleeds from an aspergilloma or post-TB cavity often precede a massive one (**sentinel bleed**). [Fishman 6e]`,`Sputum character points to cause: **frothy pink** (pulmonary oedema), **rusty** (pneumococcal pneumonia), **red-currant jelly** (Klebsiella), **foul with blood** (abscess), **purulent with blood** (bronchiectasis). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`First confirm it is **true haemoptysis**: coughed up, frothy, bright red, mixed with sputum - not vomited, not from the nose or gums. [Harrison 22e]`,`Estimate **volume, frequency and duration** and whether it is **increasing**; ask whether the patient can feel **which side** it comes from (a gurgling or warmth on one side is right about half the time). [Fishman 6e]`,`**TB history**: cough over 2 weeks, fever, night sweats, weight loss, contact - and **past ATT** (post-TB sequelae, recurrence, aspergilloma in an old cavity). [NTEP 2023]`,`**Cancer clues**: **age 40 or over, smoking (pack-years)**, weight loss, hoarseness, change in cough, bone pain, previous asbestos or biomass exposure. [NICE NG12]`,`**Bronchiectasis clues**: daily copious purulent sputum since childhood or after TB/measles/whooping cough, recurrent chest infections. [BTS 2019]`,`**Cardiac clues**: exertional breathlessness, orthopnoea, rheumatic fever, palpitations (AF), pregnancy (MS decompensates in pregnancy). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Systemic clues** for vasculitis/DAH: haematuria, oedema, sinusitis, epistaxis, rash, joint pains, ear discharge (GPA). [Harrison 22e]`,`**PE clues**: sudden pleuritic pain, breathlessness, leg swelling, recent surgery, immobilisation, OCP, cancer (see the Pulmonary embolism topic). [ESC 2019]`,`**Drugs and bleeding**: anticoagulants, antiplatelets, bleeding elsewhere; also **raw crab or crayfish ingestion** (paragonimiasis), cyclical bleeding with menses (catamenial). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Vitals first**: RR, SpO2, pulse, BP, postural drop and mental state decide whether this is life-threatening. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Examine the nose, nasopharynx, mouth and gums** with a torch to exclude an upper-airway source (pseudohaemoptysis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Clubbing**: bronchiectasis, lung abscess, lung cancer (not small-cell typically), and HPOA with bone pain - clubbing is **not a feature of uncomplicated TB or COPD**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lymph nodes**: supraclavicular/scalene nodes (lung cancer, TB); **Horner syndrome** and wasting of hand muscles (Pancoast tumour). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chest**: localised crackles (bronchiectasis, often coarse and post-tussive), **amphoric breath sounds** over a large cavity, fixed monophonic wheeze (endobronchial tumour or TB stenosis). [OHCM 11e]`,`**Heart**: **loud S1, opening snap and mid-diastolic rumble** with presystolic accentuation at the apex = mitral stenosis; signs of pulmonary hypertension (loud P2, parasternal heave). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Skin and mucosa**: telangiectasia on lips and tongue (**HHT** with pulmonary AVM), purpura (thrombocytopenia), vasculitic rash, saddle-nose (GPA). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Legs**: unilateral calf swelling (DVT and PE); **pallor** grades blood loss or anaemia of chronic disease. [ESC 2019]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis: is it really haemoptysis?`,points:[`**Haematemesis**: dark red or coffee-ground, **acidic pH**, food particles, nausea and vomiting, history of liver disease, alcohol or NSAIDs, with **melaena**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**True haemoptysis**: bright red, **frothy, alkaline pH**, mixed with sputum, preceded by cough or tickle, and blood-streaked sputum continues for days. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Upper-airway bleeding**: epistaxis (posterior bleed trickling back), gum bleeding, nasopharyngeal carcinoma - examination of nose and throat settles it. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Red pigment without blood**: rifampicin-coloured secretions are orange, and **Serratia marcescens** pigment produces red sputum (true pseudohaemoptysis). [Fishman 6e]`,`When doubt remains, sputum microscopy shows **haemosiderin-laden macrophages** in lower-respiratory bleeding, and upper GI endoscopy settles haematemesis. [Fishman 6e]`,`Once true haemoptysis is confirmed, the next differential is **localised versus diffuse** bleeding - which separates a structural lesion (TB, tumour, bronchiectasis) from DAH, coagulopathy and cardiac causes. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - OPD and district hospital`,points:[`**Sputum for TB first**: in India every patient with haemoptysis is a **presumptive TB** case - send sputum for **NAAT (CBNAAT/Truenat)** with rifampicin resistance, plus smear if NAAT is unavailable. [NTEP 2023]`,`**Chest X-ray PA view** for every patient: cavity, consolidation, mass, hilar enlargement, collapse, bronchiectasis, fungal ball with **air-crescent (Monod) sign**, or MS pattern; a **normal CXR occurs in 20-30%** and does not exclude cancer. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Blood tests**: CBC with platelets, **PT/INR, aPTT**, blood group and crossmatch, urea and creatinine, LFT; urine microscopy for **RBC casts** (pulmonary-renal syndrome). [Fishman 6e]`,`**SpO2 and ABG** in moderate or massive bleeding; **ECG and echocardiography** if MS, heart failure or PE is suspected. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Sputum culture** (bacterial, fungal), and **sputum cytology** as a low-cost adjunct when bronchoscopy is unavailable. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HIV and blood sugar** in every TB-suspect, as both change TB management and outcome. [NTEP 2023]`,`**D-dimer and CT pulmonary angiography** when PE is clinically likely; **Aspergillus IgG** and precipitins when a fungal ball or CPA is suspected. [ESC 2019]`]},{heading:`Investigations - advanced and tertiary`,points:[`**MDCT chest with contrast (CT angiography)** is the first-line imaging in a **stable** patient: it localises the bleeding side in about 70-100%, identifies the cause and **maps bronchial and non-bronchial systemic arteries before BAE**. [CIRSE 2022]`,`CT signs of the bleeding site: **ground-glass opacity and consolidation** from aspirated blood, a cavity with a fungal ball, a mass, or bronchiectasis; a **bronchial artery over 2 mm** at its origin is abnormal. [CIRSE 2022]`,`**Flexible bronchoscopy** localises the bleeding segment, takes washings (AFB, fungi, cytology) and biopsies a visible tumour; it is best done within 24-48 hours while bleeding is active. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Bronchoscopy is mandatory** in a patient **40 or over, especially a smoker**, with unexplained haemoptysis even if CT is normal, to exclude a central endobronchial cancer. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rigid bronchoscopy** under general anaesthesia is preferred in **massive bleeding**: wide channel for suction of clot, simultaneous ventilation and ability to place blockers or tamponade. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Diagnostic bronchial angiography** (done with BAE) shows hypertrophied tortuous arteries, hypervascular blush, bronchopulmonary shunting or aneurysm; frank **contrast extravasation is seen in only a minority**. [CIRSE 2022]`,`**Pulmonary angiography** is needed when a **Rasmussen aneurysm, AVM or PA pseudoaneurysm** is suspected or bleeding continues after adequate BAE. [CIRSE 2022]`,`**Vasculitis panel** (ANCA, anti-GBM, ANA, complement) and serial haemoglobin in suspected DAH, where bronchoalveolar lavage shows **progressively bloodier aliquots**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in the OPD (mild, non-massive haemoptysis)`,points:[`**Reassure but never ignore**: mild haemoptysis can be a **sentinel bleed** for a massive one, especially from a cavity, aspergilloma or Rasmussen aneurysm. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Treat the cause**: for drug-sensitive TB start the NTEP **2HRZE/4HRE** regimen once NAAT is positive (see the Pulmonary tuberculosis under NTEP topic). [NTEP 2023]`,`**Bronchiectasis exacerbation with streaky haemoptysis**: 14 days of oral antibiotics, e.g. **amoxicillin-clavulanate 625 mg three times daily** (or by sputum culture; ciprofloxacin 750 mg twice daily for Pseudomonas). [BTS 2019]`,`**Stop or hold antiplatelets and anticoagulants** where safe, and review the indication; check INR in patients on warfarin. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Oral tranexamic acid 500 mg-1 g three times daily for 5-7 days** is often used for recurrent minor bleeding; evidence is limited and it is avoided in active thrombosis. [Fishman 6e]`,`Avoid strong cough suppressants in significant bleeding (they hide retained clot); a short course of **codeine 15-30 mg** may ease a troublesome cough in streaking only. [Fishman 6e]`,`Advise **stopping smoking**, a sputum cup at home to measure volume, and to **come back at once if bleeding exceeds a teaspoon-to-tablespoon or is breathless**. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Refer** any patient aged 40 or over with unexplained haemoptysis, or with a CXR mass, for CT and bronchoscopy within 2 weeks. [NICE NG12]`]},{heading:`Massive haemoptysis: emergency and ICU protocol`,points:[`**Airway first**: high-flow oxygen, sit up if bleeding side unknown; once the side is known, **lie the patient bleeding-side down (lateral decubitus)** so blood does not spill into the good lung. [Fishman 6e]`,`Two **large-bore IV cannulae**, group and crossmatch, CBC, coagulation; **transfuse to haemoglobin 7-8 g/dL** unless active ischaemia, and **platelets if under 50,000/microlitre**. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Reverse anticoagulation**: warfarin - **vitamin K 10 mg IV plus 4-factor PCC 25-50 IU/kg** (or FFP 15 mL/kg); heparin - **protamine 1 mg per 100 units** given in the past 2-3 hours; dabigatran - **idarucizumab 5 g IV**. [ACC 2020]`,`**Intubate early** if gas exchange fails or airway is threatened, with a **large ETT (8.0 mm or more)** to allow therapeutic bronchoscopy. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Isolate the good lung**: advance the ETT over a bronchoscope into the **mainstem bronchus of the non-bleeding side** (left mainstem for a right-sided bleed), or use a **bronchial blocker** (Fogarty, Arndt, Uniblocker) in the bleeding bronchus; a **double-lumen tube** isolates well but small lumens block with clot and need an expert. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Tranexamic acid**: **1 g IV over 10 minutes then 1 g 8-hourly**, and/or **nebulised 500 mg three times daily**, as a bridge to definitive therapy; nebulised TXA reduced bleeding volume and need for intervention in RCTs of non-massive haemoptysis. [Chest 2018 (Wand)]`,`**Bronchoscopic haemostasis**: **iced saline 50 mL aliquots**, topical **adrenaline 1:20,000** (small aliquots), topical TXA, balloon tamponade, oxidised regenerated cellulose, and **APC or electrocautery** for a visible endobronchial tumour. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Bronchial artery embolisation is the first-line definitive treatment**: do CT angiography first if the patient is stable enough; go straight to angiography (or bronchoscopy for airway control) if not. [CIRSE 2022]`,`**Emergency surgery** (lobectomy or pneumonectomy) is reserved for failed BAE, **iatrogenic pulmonary artery rupture, chest trauma, and some aspergillomas**; emergency surgery carries much higher mortality than elective surgery after stabilisation. [Fishman 6e]`,`Avoid **systemic vasopressin or terlipressin** before angiography (it constricts bronchial arteries and hides the target), avoid heavy sedation, and monitor in an **ICU or HDU**. [CIRSE 2022]`]},{heading:`Bronchial artery embolisation (BAE) in detail`,points:[`**Indications**: massive or life-threatening haemoptysis, **recurrent moderate haemoptysis** impairing life, and a bridge to surgery or to anti-TB/antifungal treatment. [CIRSE 2022]`,`**Technique**: femoral access, aortography, selective catheterisation of each bronchial and non-bronchial systemic feeder with a **microcatheter**, and embolisation beyond any spinal branch. [CIRSE 2022]`,`**Embolic agents**: **non-absorbable particles (PVA or microspheres) usually 300-500 micrometres** - particles under 300 micrometres can cross bronchopulmonary shunts and cause infarction; NBCA glue is an alternative; **proximal coils are avoided** because they block future re-access. [CIRSE 2022]`,`**Results**: technical success over 90% and **immediate control in about 70-99%**; **rebleeding in about 10-30% within a year**, higher with **aspergilloma, post-TB fibrocavitary disease and missed non-bronchial collaterals**. [CIRSE 2022]`,`**Complications**: **chest pain** and **dysphagia** (commonest, transient), subintimal dissection, and rarely **spinal cord ischaemia (under 1%)**, bronchial or oesophageal necrosis, and stroke. [CIRSE 2022]`,`**Rasmussen aneurysm** needs **pulmonary artery embolisation** (coils or plugs) - bronchial embolisation alone will fail. [CIRSE 2022]`,`**Recurrent bleeding after BAE** is managed by repeat angiography looking for **new or missed non-bronchial systemic collaterals** (intercostal, internal mammary, phrenic) and re-embolisation. [CIRSE 2022]`,`BAE is a **bridge, not a cure**: the underlying disease (TB, CPA, bronchiectasis, cancer) must be treated or the bleed returns. [Fishman 6e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Aspergilloma**: bleeds recur after BAE in up to half; **surgical resection** is definitive in a fit patient with good lung function, otherwise **itraconazole 200 mg twice daily for at least 6 months** for chronic cavitary disease (see the ABPA and aspergillosis topic). [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**Mitral stenosis**: rest, oxygen, **diuretics and rate control (beta-blocker)** to lower left atrial pressure; definitive treatment is **balloon mitral valvotomy** or valve surgery. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Pregnancy**: think of MS, PE, TB and amniotic fluid/choriocarcinoma metastases; CT with shielding and BAE are allowed when life is at risk. [Harrison 22e]`,`**DAH and vasculitis**: haemoptysis plus diffuse infiltrates and falling haemoglobin - IV **methylprednisolone pulse** and specialist care; BAE has no role (see the Diffuse alveolar haemorrhage topic). [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Elderly smoker**: lung cancer is the leading concern; even after a normal CXR, refer for CT and bronchoscopy. [NICE NG12]`,`**Children**: commonest causes are lower respiratory infection, foreign body, bronchiectasis (cystic fibrosis), congenital heart disease; pseudohaemoptysis from epistaxis is frequent. [Nelson 22e]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: position bleeding side down, oxygen, IV TXA, correct coagulopathy, crossmatch and **transfer early** to a centre with BAE - do not wait for a CT that the district hospital cannot do. [ICMR STW]`,`**Paragonimiasis** in North-East India mimics smear-negative TB with chronic haemoptysis; diagnose by eggs in sputum and eosinophilia and treat with **praziquantel 25 mg/kg three times daily for 2 days**. [API Textbook 12e]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Asphyxia** from airway flooding is the commonest mode of death; exsanguination is uncommon. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Aspiration pneumonia and ARDS** after spilled blood; **anaemia and shock** in prolonged bleeds. [Fishman 6e]`,`Predictors of death: **aspergillosis, lung cancer, need for mechanical ventilation, chronic alcoholism, pulmonary artery involvement and bilateral infiltrates on admission**. [Crit Care Med 2012 (Fartoukh)]`,`Untreated massive haemoptysis historically carried a mortality around half or more; with **ICU care plus BAE** in-hospital mortality in modern series is under about 10-15%. [Fishman 6e]`,`Mortality is highest when haemoptysis is due to **lung cancer**, and with **post-TB aspergilloma** recurrence. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Cryptogenic haemoptysis** in non-smokers has a good prognosis and usually stops; in smokers, a later lung cancer diagnosis is possible, so follow-up is needed. [Fishman 6e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Admit**: any bleed over a few tablespoons in 24 hours, ongoing bleeding, hypoxaemia, haemodynamic change, coagulopathy, or known aspergilloma/cavity with fresh bleed. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Refer the same day** to a centre with **interventional radiology and bronchoscopy** for massive or recurrent moderate haemoptysis. [CIRSE 2022]`,`**Refer within 2 weeks** (suspected cancer pathway) when aged 40 or over with unexplained haemoptysis or a suspicious CXR. [NICE NG12]`,`After BAE: watch **for chest pain, dysphagia, and lower-limb weakness or sensory level** (spinal ischaemia) for 24-48 hours. [CIRSE 2022]`,`Follow-up at **1, 3 and 6 months** after BAE with symptom review; repeat CT if bleeding recurs; continue treating the cause (ATT, antifungals, bronchiectasis care). [CIRSE 2022]`,`**Cryptogenic haemoptysis in smokers**: repeat CT at around 6-12 months or earlier with recurrence. [Fishman 6e]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`**Complete ATT under NTEP** with Ni-kshay registration and Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana nutritional support - incomplete treatment leaves cavities that later bleed or harbour aspergilloma. [NTEP 2023]`,`**Post-TB follow-up**: NTEP advises follow-up after treatment completion for recurrence and post-TB lung disease; recurrent haemoptysis after cure needs CT, not automatic re-treatment. [NTEP 2023]`,`**Smoking cessation**: tobacco quit line **1800-112-356** and NTCP cessation centres; quitting lowers both lung cancer and bronchiectasis-related bleeding. [NTCP]`,`**Anticoagulant safety**: check INR targets, avoid NSAIDs with anticoagulants, and teach patients to report any blood in sputum. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rheumatic heart disease prevention**: benzathine penicillin secondary prophylaxis prevents progression to mitral stenosis. [WHO 2024]`,`Teach the family **how to position the patient bleeding-side down** and to come at once if bleeding increases. [Fishman 6e]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2026)`,points:[`**CIRSE Standards of Practice on BAE (2022)**: CT angiography before BAE in stable patients, particle size of at least 300 micrometres, avoid proximal coils, and look for non-bronchial systemic collaterals in recurrence. [CIRSE 2022]`,`**Nebulised tranexamic acid**: the Wand 2018 RCT (500 mg three times daily) reduced expectorated blood and need for BAE; a 2023 AIIMS New Delhi emergency-department trial found nebulised TXA at least as effective as IV for early cessation - adopted widely as a bridge. [Chest 2018 (Wand)]`,`**NTEP 2023 training and technical guidance**: haemoptysis of any duration is a presumptive TB symptom, and **NAAT is the first test** (upfront molecular testing) rather than smear. [NTEP 2023]`,`**ESC/EACTS 2025 valvular guidelines**: percutaneous mitral commissurotomy remains first-line for symptomatic rheumatic MS with suitable anatomy, relevant when MS presents with haemoptysis. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**NICE NG12** (suspected cancer) keeps the rule: **aged 40 or over with unexplained haemoptysis** - suspected cancer pathway referral. [NICE NG12]`,`**KDIGO 2024 ANCA vasculitis**: rituximab or cyclophosphamide with steroids for alveolar haemorrhage; reduced-dose steroid regimens and avacopan as a steroid-sparing option. [KDIGO 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **'Approach to a patient with massive haemoptysis'** - start with definition by consequence, ABC with bleeding-side-down, isolate good lung, TXA, CT angiography, BAE, surgery. [Fishman 6e]`,`Viva: **Which circulation bleeds?** - bronchial arteries in about 90%, systemic pressure; Rasmussen aneurysm is the pulmonary-artery exception. [CIRSE 2022]`,`Viva: **Why do patients die?** - asphyxia; dead space is only about 150 mL. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: **Feared complication of BAE?** - spinal cord ischaemia from embolising the anterior spinal artery; the commonest is chest pain. [CIRSE 2022]`,`Common mistake: putting the patient **bleeding side up** - always bleeding side down. [Fishman 6e]`,`Common mistake: treating haemoptysis as TB without testing - **always send NAAT**, and a known old TB patient may have aspergilloma, bronchiectasis or cancer, not relapse. [NTEP 2023]`,`Common mistake: accepting a normal CXR in a **smoker over 40** - CT and bronchoscopy are still required. [NICE NG12]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs and blood products in haemoptysis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Tranexamic acid (IV)`,`1 g IV over 10 min, then 1 g 8-hourly (reduce in renal failure)`,`Until bleeding settles, usually 3-5 days`,`Seizures at high dose, thrombosis; avoid in active VTE, DIC`],[`Tranexamic acid (nebulised)`,`500 mg (5 mL of 100 mg/mL injection) nebulised three times daily`,`Up to 5 days`,`Bronchospasm (rare); give with a bronchodilator if asthmatic`],[`Tranexamic acid (oral)`,`500 mg-1 g three times daily`,`5-7 days for recurrent minor bleeding`,`Nausea; avoid with past thrombosis, combined OCP`],[`Adrenaline (topical, bronchoscopic)`,`1:20,000 in small (1-2 mL) aliquots into the bleeding segment`,`Repeat as needed during bronchoscopy`,`Arrhythmia, hypertension if absorbed; avoid large total doses`],[`Iced (4 degrees C) saline lavage`,`50 mL aliquots via bronchoscope, up to about 500 mL`,`During bronchoscopy`,`Bradycardia; hypothermia with large volumes`],[`Vitamin K + 4-factor PCC`,`Vitamin K 10 mg IV slowly; PCC 25-50 IU/kg by INR (or FFP 15 mL/kg)`,`Single dose; recheck INR`,`Anaphylactoid reaction (vitamin K), thrombosis (PCC)`],[`Protamine sulphate`,`1 mg per 100 units heparin given in past 2-3 h (max 50 mg)`,`Single dose; partial for LMWH`,`Hypotension, anaphylaxis (fish allergy, prior protamine insulin)`],[`Platelets / packed red cells`,`Platelets if under 50,000/microlitre; RBC to Hb 7-8 g/dL`,`As needed`,`Transfusion reactions, TACO, TRALI`],[`Codeine phosphate`,`15-30 mg orally 6-hourly`,`Short course, streaking only`,`Sedation, constipation; avoid in massive bleed (clot retention)`],[`Itraconazole (CPA / aspergilloma)`,`200 mg orally twice daily with food`,`At least 6 months; check levels`,`Hepatotoxicity, heart failure, many interactions (rifampicin)`]]},{heading:`Grading haemoptysis and where to treat`,columns:[`Grade`,`Volume (24 h) / features`,`Setting`,`First steps`],rows:[[`Streaking`,`Blood flecks in sputum`,`OPD`,`CXR, sputum NAAT, treat cause, review`],[`Mild`,`Under 100 mL, stable`,`OPD or short stay`,`CXR, NAAT, CBC, coagulation, refer for CT if 40 or over`],[`Moderate`,`100-600 mL (some use 100-500), stable`,`Admit to ward/HDU`,`TXA, crossmatch, CT angiography, bronchoscopy`],[`Massive`,`600 mL or more, or 100-150 mL/hour`,`ICU`,`Bleeding side down, airway, TXA, BAE`],[`Life-threatening (any volume)`,`Airway obstruction, hypoxaemia, shock or transfusion need`,`ICU, resuscitation`,`Intubate (8.0 ETT), isolate good lung, emergency BAE or rigid bronchoscopy`]]},{heading:`Haemoptysis versus haematemesis versus pseudohaemoptysis`,columns:[`Feature`,`Haemoptysis`,`Haematemesis`,`Pseudohaemoptysis (upper airway)`],rows:[[`Preceded by`,`Cough, gurgle in chest`,`Nausea, vomiting`,`Epistaxis, hawking, gum bleeding`],[`Colour and texture`,`Bright red, frothy, mixed with sputum`,`Dark red or coffee-ground, may have food`,`Bright red, clots, no froth`],[`pH`,`Alkaline`,`Acidic`,`Neutral or alkaline`],[`Associated`,`Breathlessness, chest disease`,`Melaena, liver disease, NSAIDs`,`Nasal or oral source on examination`],[`Afterwards`,`Blood-streaked sputum for days`,`Melaena, no streaky sputum`,`Stops when the local source is treated`],[`Microscopy`,`Haemosiderin-laden macrophages`,`Food particles`,`Squamous cells`]]},{heading:`Causes of haemoptysis in India and the clue to each`,columns:[`Cause`,`Typical clue`,`Key test`],rows:[[`Active pulmonary TB`,`Cough over 2 weeks, fever, weight loss, upper-lobe cavity`,`Sputum NAAT (CBNAAT/Truenat)`],[`Post-TB bronchiectasis / fibrocavity`,`Completed ATT, recurrent bleeds, purulent sputum`,`HRCT chest`],[`Aspergilloma / CPA`,`Old TB cavity, air-crescent sign, recurrent bleeds`,`CT, Aspergillus IgG`],[`Lung cancer`,`Smoker 40 or over, weight loss, hoarseness, clubbing`,`CT chest, bronchoscopy, biopsy`],[`Mitral stenosis`,`Rheumatic history, opening snap, AF, pregnancy`,`Echocardiography`],[`Pulmonary embolism`,`Sudden pleuritic pain, DVT, risk factors`,`CT pulmonary angiography`],[`DAH / vasculitis`,`Diffuse infiltrates, falling Hb, haematuria`,`ANCA, anti-GBM, BAL, urine`],[`Paragonimiasis`,`North-East India, raw crab, eosinophilia, smear-negative`,`Sputum for eggs`],[`Coagulopathy / anticoagulants`,`Bleeding elsewhere, high INR`,`PT/INR, platelets`]]},{heading:`Investigations and what they answer`,columns:[`Investigation`,`Question it answers`,`Interpretation / cut-off`],rows:[[`Chest X-ray PA`,`Side and cause`,`Normal in 20-30%; does not exclude cancer`],[`Sputum NAAT`,`Is it TB, is it rifampicin-resistant?`,`Upfront under NTEP for every presumptive TB`],[`CBC, PT/INR, aPTT`,`Blood loss, coagulopathy`,`Platelets under 50,000 or INR high - correct`],[`CT angiography chest`,`Site, cause, arterial map for BAE`,`Bronchial artery over 2 mm at origin abnormal`],[`Flexible bronchoscopy`,`Exact segment, endobronchial lesion, samples`,`Mandatory if 40 or over smoker with unexplained bleed`],[`Rigid bronchoscopy`,`Airway control in massive bleed`,`Suction, ventilation, blockers`],[`Bronchial angiography`,`Target for embolisation`,`Hypertrophy, tortuosity, shunt, blush`],[`Echocardiography`,`MS, LV failure, pulmonary hypertension`,`MVA under 1.5 cm2 = significant MS`]]}],redFlags:[`Bleeding of 100 mL or more in 24 hours, or 100-150 mL in an hour`,`Any haemoptysis with breathlessness, SpO2 under 92% or rising oxygen need`,`Hypotension, tachycardia over 120/min or postural drop`,`Known cavity, aspergilloma or Rasmussen aneurysm with fresh bleeding`,`Haemoptysis on anticoagulants, or with platelets under 50,000/microlitre`,`Haemoptysis with haematuria, rising creatinine or diffuse infiltrates (pulmonary-renal syndrome)`,`Age 40 or over, smoker, with unexplained haemoptysis (cancer pathway)`],pearls:[`Patients with massive haemoptysis die of asphyxia, not blood loss - dead space is only about 150 mL.`,`Bleeding side down - always.`,`Bronchial arteries (systemic pressure) cause about 90% of massive haemoptysis; Rasmussen aneurysm is the pulmonary-artery exception.`,`In India, haemoptysis = presumptive TB until sputum NAAT says otherwise.`,`A normal CXR does not exclude lung cancer in a smoker over 40 - get CT and bronchoscopy.`,`Feared BAE complication: spinal cord ischaemia via the anterior spinal artery; commonest: chest pain.`,`Particles under 300 micrometres cross bronchopulmonary shunts - avoid them in BAE.`,`Red-currant jelly sputum = Klebsiella; rusty = pneumococcus; pink frothy = pulmonary oedema.`,`Mitral stenosis haemoptysis = pulmonary apoplexy from ruptured bronchial veins.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-haemoptysis-q1`,stem:`A 45-year-old man with treated pulmonary TB 5 years ago coughs up about 400 mL of blood over 6 hours. SpO2 is 89% on air; the CXR shows a left upper lobe cavity. What is the most appropriate immediate position for him while preparing for definitive care?`,options:[`Supine with the head down (Trendelenburg)`,`Right lateral decubitus (right side down)`,`Left lateral decubitus (left side down)`,`Prone position to drain the airways`],answer:2,explanation:`The bleeding lung is the left, so he is placed left side down: gravity keeps blood in the affected lung and protects the right (good) lung, which is what keeps him oxygenated. Right side down would let blood spill into the good lung. Trendelenburg and prone positions do not isolate one lung and worsen aspiration into both.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-haemoptysis-q2`,stem:`Which vessel is the source of bleeding in most cases of massive haemoptysis?`,options:[`Bronchial arteries`,`Pulmonary arteries`,`Pulmonary veins`,`Intercostal veins`],answer:0,explanation:`About 90% of massive haemoptysis arises from hypertrophied bronchial arteries carrying blood at systemic pressure, which is why bronchial artery embolisation works. Pulmonary arteries account for only about 5% (for example a Rasmussen aneurysm in a TB cavity), and venous sources are rare except for bronchial varices in mitral stenosis.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-haemoptysis-q3`,stem:`A day after bronchial artery embolisation for post-TB haemoptysis, a patient develops weakness of both legs with a sensory level at T6. The most likely cause is:`,options:[`Contrast-induced nephropathy with uraemic neuropathy`,`Non-target embolisation of the anterior spinal artery`,`Haematoma at the femoral puncture site`,`Guillain-Barre syndrome triggered by the procedure`],answer:1,explanation:`Paraparesis with a sensory level after BAE is spinal cord ischaemia from embolic material entering the anterior spinal artery, which can arise from a right intercostobronchial trunk. A femoral haematoma causes groin swelling and at most a femoral neuropathy, contrast nephropathy does not cause a sensory level, and Guillain-Barre syndrome is ascending without a sharp sensory level.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-haemoptysis-q4`,stem:`In bronchial artery embolisation, why are particles smaller than about 300 micrometres avoided?`,options:[`They are too expensive for routine use`,`They are absorbed within hours, so bleeding recurs`,`They cannot be seen on fluoroscopy`,`They can pass through bronchopulmonary shunts and cause pulmonary or systemic infarction`],answer:3,explanation:`Chronically inflamed lung has bronchial-to-pulmonary shunts; very small particles cross them and cause lung, bronchial or even systemic (stroke) infarction, so CIRSE advises particles of at least 300 micrometres. Visibility is from the contrast mixed with them, not their size, and PVA particles are non-absorbable, so early absorption is not the reason.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`respiratory-haemoptysis-q5`,stem:`A 58-year-old smoker has had two episodes of blood-streaked sputum. His CXR is normal and sputum NAAT is negative. What is the best next step?`,options:[`Reassure and review only if bleeding recurs`,`Start empirical anti-tuberculosis treatment`,`Contrast CT chest followed by bronchoscopy`,`Prescribe a 2-week course of oral tranexamic acid and discharge`],answer:2,explanation:`A smoker aged 40 or over with unexplained haemoptysis needs CT and bronchoscopy even when the CXR is normal, because a central endobronchial cancer can hide on a plain film. Reassurance and TXA alone miss cancer, and empirical ATT without bacteriological or radiological evidence is wrong under NTEP.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-haemoptysis-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Define massive haemoptysis. Enumerate its causes in India. Describe the emergency management, including the role of bronchial artery embolisation.`,openingLines:[`Massive haemoptysis is expectoration of blood from the lower respiratory tract of 600 mL or more in 24 hours (thresholds of 100-1000 mL are used), or of any volume that causes airway obstruction, hypoxaemia or haemodynamic instability.`,`It kills by asphyxia rather than exsanguination, and in India it is most often due to active or post-tuberculous lung disease.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and why it matters`,points:[`Volume-based: 600 mL or more in 24 h, or 100-150 mL/hour; consequence-based: airway, gas exchange or circulation compromised.`,`Dead space about 150-200 mL - asphyxia is the mode of death.`,`Source: bronchial arteries about 90%, pulmonary arteries about 5%, non-bronchial systemic collaterals the rest.`]},{heading:`Causes in India`,points:[`Infection: active TB, post-TB bronchiectasis and fibrocavity, aspergilloma, lung abscess, necrotising pneumonia.`,`Neoplasm: bronchogenic carcinoma, carcinoid.`,`Cardiovascular: mitral stenosis, PE, AV malformation.`,`Vasculitis/DAH, coagulopathy, anticoagulants; paragonimiasis in North-East India; iatrogenic.`]},{heading:`Immediate resuscitation`,points:[`Oxygen, bleeding side down (lateral decubitus), two large-bore cannulae, crossmatch, CBC, coagulation.`,`Reverse anticoagulation (vitamin K + PCC, protamine, idarucizumab), platelets if under 50,000.`,`Tranexamic acid 1 g IV then 1 g 8-hourly, or nebulised 500 mg TDS.`]},{heading:`Airway protection`,points:[`Intubate with 8.0 mm or larger ETT if airway or gas exchange threatened.`,`Selective mainstem intubation of the good lung, bronchial blocker, or double-lumen tube.`,`Rigid bronchoscopy for suction; iced saline, topical adrenaline 1:20,000, TXA, balloon tamponade.`]},{heading:`Localisation`,points:[`CXR at bedside; CT angiography if stable - localises side and maps bronchial and non-bronchial arteries.`,`Bronchoscopy localises the segment and treats endobronchial lesions.`]},{heading:`Bronchial artery embolisation`,points:[`First-line definitive therapy; microcatheter, particles 300-500 micrometres, avoid proximal coils.`,`Immediate control 70-99%; rebleeding 10-30% at a year (aspergilloma, missed collaterals).`,`Complications: chest pain, dysphagia, spinal cord ischaemia (under 1%).`,`Rasmussen aneurysm needs pulmonary artery embolisation.`]},{heading:`Surgery and definitive care`,points:[`Surgery for failed BAE, PA rupture, trauma, selected aspergilloma - elective after stabilisation is safer.`,`Treat the cause: ATT under NTEP, antifungals, bronchiectasis care, cancer treatment; follow-up at 1, 3, 6 months.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: massive haemoptysis - ABC, bleeding side down, TXA, airway isolation, CT angiography, BAE, surgery`,`Diagram of bronchial artery origin from the descending aorta with the anterior spinal artery`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition`,marks:1},{part:`Causes in India`,marks:2},{part:`Resuscitation and airway`,marks:3},{part:`Localisation`,marks:1},{part:`BAE and surgery`,marks:3}],keywords:[`massive haemoptysis`,`bronchial artery embolisation`,`bleeding side down`,`tranexamic acid`,`Rasmussen aneurysm`,`rigid bronchoscopy`]},{id:`respiratory-haemoptysis-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate haemoptysis from haematemesis and list the causes of pseudohaemoptysis.`,openingLines:[`Haemoptysis is blood coughed from below the vocal cords; haematemesis is blood vomited from the upper gastrointestinal tract.`,`Pseudohaemoptysis is expectorated blood that does not come from the lower respiratory tract.`],answer:[{heading:`Differentiating features`,points:[`Preceding symptom: cough versus nausea and vomiting.`,`Appearance: bright red, frothy, mixed with sputum versus dark, coffee-ground, food particles.`,`pH: alkaline versus acidic.`,`After the event: streaky sputum for days versus melaena.`,`Background: lung or heart disease versus liver disease, alcohol, NSAIDs.`,`Microscopy: haemosiderin-laden macrophages versus food debris.`]},{heading:`Causes of pseudohaemoptysis`,points:[`Posterior epistaxis and nasopharyngeal bleeding (including nasopharyngeal carcinoma).`,`Gum, tongue and oral bleeding.`,`Aspirated haematemesis.`,`Red pigment: Serratia marcescens; orange secretions from rifampicin.`]}],mustDraw:[`Three-column table: haemoptysis, haematemesis, pseudohaemoptysis`],markSplit:[{part:`Differentiating table`,marks:3},{part:`Pseudohaemoptysis causes`,marks:2}],keywords:[`haemoptysis`,`haematemesis`,`pseudohaemoptysis`,`alkaline pH`,`haemosiderin-laden macrophages`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Haemoptysis`,`Fishman's Pulmonary Diseases and Disorders, 6th edition, 2023 - Haemoptysis`,`CIRSE Standards of Practice on Bronchial Artery Embolisation, Cardiovascular and Interventional Radiology, 2022`,`Wand O et al. Inhaled tranexamic acid for hemoptysis treatment: a randomized controlled trial. Chest 2018`,`National TB Elimination Programme (NTEP) Training Modules and Technical Guidance, MoHFW, 2023`,`NICE NG12 Suspected cancer: recognition and referral (current update)`,`ERS/ESCMID Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis guideline, European Respiratory Journal, 2016`,`API Textbook of Medicine, 12th edition, 2022`]}),t.push({id:`respiratory-lung-cancer`,title:`Lung cancer: diagnosis, staging (TNM 9) and management`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Lung cancer** (bronchogenic carcinoma) is a malignant epithelial tumour of the airways or lung - about **85% non-small cell (NSCLC; adenocarcinoma commonest)** and **15% small cell (SCLC)** - the **world's leading cause of cancer death**; it is staged by the **IASLC TNM 9th edition (2024, in use from 2025)**, and treatment is chosen by **stage, performance status and biomarkers (EGFR, ALK and other drivers, PD-L1)**; in India it is often **mistreated as TB first**, delaying diagnosis.`,keywords:[`bronchogenic carcinoma`,`NSCLC`,`SCLC`,`adenocarcinoma`,`squamous cell carcinoma`,`TNM 9th edition`,`EGFR`,`ALK`,`PD-L1`,`osimertinib`,`pembrolizumab`,`durvalumab`,`EBUS-TBNA`,`PET-CT`,`Pancoast tumour`,`SVC obstruction`,`SIADH`,`Lambert-Eaton`,`LDCT screening`,`next-generation sequencing`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Lung cancer** is a malignancy arising from bronchial or alveolar epithelium; it is classified by the **WHO 2021 (5th edition) thoracic tumour classification**. [WHO 2021]`,`Globally it is the **commonest cancer and the leading cause of cancer death** - about **2.5 million new cases and 1.8 million deaths in 2022**. [GLOBOCAN 2022]`,`In India it is among the **leading cancers in men** in the population-based registries, with the **highest rates in the North-East (Aizawl, Mizoram)**. [ICMR-NCRP 2020]`,`Indian patients present **about a decade younger** than in the West and **mostly at stage III-IV**; **adenocarcinoma has overtaken squamous carcinoma** as the commonest type. [ICMR-NCRP 2020]`,`**TB misdiagnosis delay**: in several Indian series a third or more of patients receive **anti-TB treatment before the cancer is diagnosed**, often without bacteriological confirmation, losing months. [Indian J Cancer]`,`A **substantial minority of Indian patients, and most women, are never-smokers** - with more adenocarcinoma and EGFR mutations. [Lung India]`,`**EGFR mutations occur in about a quarter to a third of Indian adenocarcinomas** (higher than in Western series), and **ALK rearrangement in about 5%**. [Lung India]`,`Overall **5-year survival is about 25%** in high-income countries and much lower in India, because most patients present with advanced disease. [SEER 2024]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Tobacco smoking** causes about **80-90%** of lung cancer in the West; risk rises with **pack-years**, and **bidi** smoking is at least as harmful as cigarettes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Second-hand smoke** raises risk by about 20-30% in never-smokers. [IARC]`,`**Household air pollution** from biomass and coal cooking, and **outdoor air pollution (PM2.5)**, both IARC Group 1 carcinogens, explain much never-smoker lung cancer in Indian women. [IARC 2013]`,`**Occupational**: **asbestos** (multiplies risk with smoking), silica, radon, arsenic, chromium, nickel, beryllium, polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (coke ovens). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lung disease**: COPD (independent of smoking), **pulmonary fibrosis**, and **old TB scars** (scar carcinoma) increase risk. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Genetic and host**: family history, **HIV infection**, prior chest radiotherapy (Hodgkin lymphoma, breast cancer). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Driver mutations in never-smokers**: EGFR, ALK, ROS1, RET - more frequent in Asian, female, never-smoker adenocarcinoma. [NCCN NSCLC 2026]`]},{heading:`Pathology and pathophysiology`,points:[`**Adenocarcinoma (about 40-50%)**: peripheral, glandular, mucin and **TTF-1/Napsin A positive**; precursor is atypical adenomatous hyperplasia then **adenocarcinoma in situ** (pure ground-glass nodule). [WHO 2021]`,`**Squamous cell carcinoma (about 25%)**: central, from smoking-induced squamous metaplasia; **p40 and p63 positive**, keratin pearls, cavitation, and **PTHrP hypercalcaemia**. [WHO 2021]`,`**Small cell carcinoma (about 15%)**: central, neuroendocrine (**chromogranin, synaptophysin, INSM1**), nearly always in smokers, fast doubling time, early metastasis, and most paraneoplastic syndromes; **TP53 and RB1** loss are near universal. [WHO 2021]`,`**Large cell carcinoma** is a diagnosis of exclusion; **large cell neuroendocrine carcinoma** behaves like SCLC; **carcinoid** (typical, atypical) is low-grade neuroendocrine. [WHO 2021]`,`**Oncogenic drivers** create a dependence on one signalling pathway: **EGFR** (exon 19 deletion and L858R in about 90%), **ALK** and **ROS1** fusions, **KRAS G12C**, **BRAF V600E**, **MET exon 14 skipping**, **RET**, **NTRK**, **HER2** - each targetable. [NCCN NSCLC 2026]`,`**Immune evasion**: tumour **PD-L1** binds PD-1 on T cells to switch them off; **checkpoint inhibitors** release the brake - high tumour mutational burden in smokers predicts response. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Local growth** explains symptoms: bronchial obstruction (collapse, post-obstructive pneumonia), **recurrent laryngeal nerve** (hoarseness, more often left), phrenic nerve (raised hemidiaphragm), SVC, oesophagus and pericardium. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Spread**: lymphatic to hilar and mediastinal nodes, and blood-borne to **brain, bone, liver and adrenals**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Staging: IASLC TNM 9th edition (2024)`,points:[`**TNM 9th edition** (IASLC, published 2024, effective **1 January 2025**) keeps the **T categories unchanged from the 8th edition**. [IASLC TNM 9]`,`**T1** 3 cm or less (T1a 1 cm or less, T1b over 1-2, T1c over 2-3); **T2** over 3-5 cm or main bronchus, visceral pleura or atelectasis to hilum (T2a over 3-4, T2b over 4-5); **T3** over 5-7 cm or chest wall, phrenic nerve, parietal pericardium, or separate nodule in the same lobe; **T4** over 7 cm or invasion of mediastinum, heart, great vessels, trachea, carina, oesophagus, vertebra, diaphragm, or a nodule in another ipsilateral lobe. [IASLC TNM 9]`,`**N1** ipsilateral peribronchial or hilar; **N2** ipsilateral mediastinal or subcarinal - now split into **N2a (single N2 station) and N2b (multiple N2 stations)**; **N3** contralateral mediastinal or hilar, or any scalene or supraclavicular node. [IASLC TNM 9]`,`**M1a** contralateral lung nodule, pleural or pericardial nodules or malignant effusion; **M1b** single extrathoracic metastasis; **M1c** multiple extrathoracic metastases - now split into **M1c1 (one organ system) and M1c2 (multiple organ systems)**. [IASLC TNM 9]`,`Key stage shifts: **T1 N1 M0 moves to stage IIA**; **T1 N2a is IIB**; **T2 N2a is IIIA** while **T2 N2b is IIIB**; M1a and M1b are **IVA**, M1c1 and M1c2 are **IVB**. [IASLC TNM 9]`,`**SCLC** is staged by TNM too, but the clinical split into **limited stage** (encompassable in one radiation field, stage I-III) and **extensive stage** remains in use for treatment. [NCCN SCLC 2026]`,`**Performance status (ECOG 0-4)** is as important as stage: ECOG 0-1 tolerates combination therapy, ECOG 3-4 usually gets best supportive care unless a targetable driver is present. [ESMO 2023]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Cough** (commonest), **haemoptysis**, breathlessness, chest pain, weight loss and fatigue - a **change in a smoker's chronic cough** is a classic clue. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Non-resolving pneumonia** in the same segment, or a pneumonia that recurs in one lobe, suggests an obstructing tumour. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hoarseness** (left recurrent laryngeal nerve under the aortic arch), **dysphagia** (oesophageal compression), **stridor** (tracheal) point to local spread. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pancoast (superior sulcus) tumour**: shoulder and arm pain in the **C8-T1** distribution, wasting of hand muscles and **Horner syndrome** (ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis) - often without lung symptoms, mistaken for cervical spondylosis or frozen shoulder. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Metastatic symptoms**: bone pain, headache, seizures, focal deficits, jaundice, abdominal pain. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Smear-negative 'TB' that does not respond**: always revisit the diagnosis if a patient on ATT does not improve clinically or radiologically by 2 months. [NTEP 2023]`,`Ask **pack-years, bidi use, biomass exposure, occupation, family history** and prior TB. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Paraneoplastic syndromes`,points:[`**SIADH** (hyponatraemia, euvolaemic, concentrated urine) - mainly **SCLC**; treat with fluid restriction, tolvaptan or demeclocycline and chemotherapy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ectopic ACTH** (Cushing with hypokalaemia, hyperpigmentation, little cushingoid habitus) - **SCLC and carcinoid**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypercalcaemia of malignancy** from **PTHrP** - **squamous cell carcinoma**; give IV saline and **zoledronic acid 4 mg IV**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome** (anti-VGCC antibodies) - **SCLC**: proximal weakness that **improves with repeated effort**, reduced reflexes, autonomic symptoms. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anti-Hu encephalomyelitis, sensory neuronopathy, cerebellar degeneration**, limbic encephalitis - SCLC. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypertrophic pulmonary osteoarthropathy** (clubbing, painful periostitis of long bones) - **adenocarcinoma/NSCLC**, not SCLC. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Haematological**: migratory thrombophlebitis (**Trousseau**), DIC, anaemia; **gynaecomastia** from hCG (large cell). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`SVC obstruction and other oncological emergencies`,points:[`**SVC obstruction**: facial and arm swelling, **distended non-pulsatile neck veins**, dilated chest wall collaterals, headache worse on bending, **Pemberton sign** - lung cancer (especially SCLC) is the commonest cause (see the Mediastinal masses topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tissue diagnosis before treatment** in a stable patient, because steroids or radiation can spoil histology (especially lymphoma). [NCCN NSCLC 2026]`,`**Stridor or cerebral oedema** in SVCO is an emergency - **endovascular stenting** gives relief in hours; radiation and chemotherapy act over days (SCLC is very chemosensitive). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Malignant spinal cord compression**: back pain then weakness - **dexamethasone 16 mg/day**, MRI whole spine, surgery or radiotherapy within 24 hours. [NICE NG234]`,`**Brain metastases** with raised ICP: dexamethasone, then **stereotactic radiosurgery** for limited lesions; osimertinib and alectinib/lorlatinib have good CNS penetration. [ESMO 2023]`,`**Hypercalcaemia, SIADH, malignant pleural effusion, massive haemoptysis** and **febrile neutropenia** on chemotherapy are the other emergencies a physician meets. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Clubbing** (NSCLC more than SCLC), **cachexia** and pallor. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Supraclavicular or scalene lymph nodes** - their biopsy may stage (N3) and diagnose in one step. [NCCN NSCLC 2026]`,`**Collapse** (trachea pulled to the same side, dull, absent breath sounds) or **effusion** (trachea pushed away, stony dull). [OHCM 11e]`,`**Fixed monophonic wheeze** over a narrowed bronchus; stridor with tracheal involvement. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Horner syndrome** and small-muscle wasting of the hand - Pancoast tumour. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hepatomegaly, bony tenderness, focal neurology** - metastases; proximal weakness with reduced reflexes that improve after exercise - Lambert-Eaton. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Pulmonary tuberculosis**: upper-lobe cavity, fever, sputum NAAT positive; in India it coexists with cancer, so a **positive NAAT does not exclude cancer** if the lesion is mass-like or fails to respond. [NTEP 2023]`,`**Non-resolving pneumonia, lung abscess** and **organising pneumonia** - follow-up CXR at 6-8 weeks; persistent shadow needs CT. [BTS 2009]`,`**Aspergilloma** and **fungal nodules** in old cavities; **hydatid cyst** (water-lily sign). [Fishman 6e]`,`**Metastases** from breast, kidney, colon, thyroid, melanoma and germ-cell tumours - usually multiple, round, basal. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Benign tumours and granulomas**: hamartoma (popcorn calcification), tuberculoma (central calcification) - see the Solitary pulmonary nodule topic. [Fleischner 2017]`,`**Lymphoma and mediastinal masses** for hilar or mediastinal enlargement; **sarcoidosis** for symmetrical hilar nodes. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations and diagnostic work-up`,points:[`**Chest X-ray** is the first test: mass, collapse, hilar enlargement, effusion, raised hemidiaphragm; a normal film does not exclude central tumours. [NICE NG12]`,`**Contrast CT chest and upper abdomen (to include liver and adrenals)** for every suspected case - characterises the lesion, nodes and the best biopsy site. [NCCN NSCLC 2026]`,`**Choose the biopsy that gives diagnosis and highest stage together**: e.g. supraclavicular node, pleural fluid cytology, EBUS of an N2/N3 node, or liver lesion rather than the primary. [ACCP 2013]`,`**Bronchoscopy** for central lesions; **CT-guided transthoracic biopsy** for peripheral lesions; **EBUS-TBNA** for mediastinal nodes (the standard of mediastinal staging, replacing mediastinoscopy in most cases). [ESTS 2014]`,`**PET-CT** for staging any patient being considered for curative treatment; PET-positive mediastinal nodes still need **tissue confirmation**, and **TB and granulomas cause false-positive PET in India**. [NCCN NSCLC 2026]`,`**MRI brain** with contrast for stage II-IV disease or any neurological symptom (CT brain if MRI unavailable). [NCCN NSCLC 2026]`,`**Biomarkers in every non-squamous NSCLC** (and squamous in never-smokers): broad **NGS panel** for EGFR, ALK, ROS1, BRAF, KRAS G12C, MET exon 14, RET, NTRK, HER2, NRG1, plus **PD-L1 IHC (tumour proportion score)**; also test **EGFR and ALK in resected stage IB-IIIA** for adjuvant targeted therapy. [NCCN NSCLC 2026]`,`**Liquid biopsy (plasma ctDNA)** can find EGFR and other drivers when tissue is scanty; a negative plasma test needs tissue confirmation. [ESMO 2023]`,`**Fitness for surgery**: spirometry and **DLCO**; predicted postoperative FEV1 and DLCO **over 60% - fit**; 30-60% - stair climb or shuttle walk; **under 30% - CPET** (VO2 max **under 10 mL/kg/min is high risk**). [ACCP 2013]`,`**Bloods**: CBC, sodium (SIADH), calcium (PTHrP), LFT, ALP; tumour markers are not used for diagnosis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Treatment overview: NSCLC by stage`,points:[`**Stage I-II**: **lobectomy with systematic nodal dissection** (or **segmentectomy for peripheral tumours 2 cm or less**, per JCOG0802 and CALGB 140503); **SBRT (stereotactic radiotherapy)** for patients unfit for surgery. [NCCN NSCLC 2026]`,`**Resectable stage II-IIIA (and selected IIIB)**: **neoadjuvant or perioperative chemo-immunotherapy** - platinum doublet with **nivolumab** (CheckMate 816), **pembrolizumab** (KEYNOTE-671) or **durvalumab** (AEGEAN) - when no EGFR/ALK driver. [NCCN NSCLC 2026]`,`**Adjuvant targeted therapy**: **osimertinib 80 mg daily for 3 years** after resection of EGFR-mutant IB-IIIA (ADAURA); **alectinib 600 mg twice daily for 2 years** for ALK-positive (ALINA). [NCCN NSCLC 2026]`,`**Unresectable stage III**: **concurrent chemoradiation (60 Gy in 30 fractions)** with platinum doublet, then **durvalumab consolidation for 12 months** (PACIFIC); for EGFR-mutant disease, **osimertinib after chemoradiation** instead (LAURA 2024). [NCCN NSCLC 2026]`,`**Stage IV with a driver**: **EGFR** - **osimertinib 80 mg daily** (FLAURA), or osimertinib plus chemotherapy (FLAURA2) or amivantamab-lazertinib (MARIPOSA) for higher risk; **gefitinib 250 mg daily** or erlotinib/afatinib are cheaper alternatives in India - **gefitinib plus pemetrexed-carboplatin** improved survival in a Tata Memorial trial. [JCO 2020 (Noronha)]`,`**ALK-positive**: **alectinib 600 mg twice daily** or **lorlatinib 100 mg daily** (CROWN) first line; ROS1 - crizotinib, entrectinib or repotrectinib; other drivers have matched agents. [NCCN NSCLC 2026]`,`**Stage IV without a driver**: **PD-L1 50% or more** - **pembrolizumab 200 mg IV every 3 weeks** (or 400 mg every 6 weeks) alone, or atezolizumab/cemiplimab; **any PD-L1** - platinum doublet plus pembrolizumab (**pemetrexed for non-squamous**, carboplatin-paclitaxel for squamous), up to 2 years of immunotherapy. [ESMO 2023]`,`**No immunotherapy access (cost)**: platinum doublet - **carboplatin AUC 5 + pemetrexed 500 mg/m2** (non-squamous) or **carboplatin + paclitaxel/gemcitabine** (squamous) every 3 weeks for 4 cycles, then pemetrexed maintenance; PM-JAY covers standard chemotherapy. [ICMR Consensus]`,`**Pemetrexed** needs **folic acid 400-1000 microgram daily and vitamin B12 1000 microgram IM every 9 weeks** starting a week before, plus dexamethasone, and is avoided if creatinine clearance is under 45 mL/min. [NCCN NSCLC 2026]`,`**Immune-related adverse events** (pneumonitis, colitis, hepatitis, thyroiditis, hypophysitis, myocarditis, diabetes) are treated with **steroids** and by holding the drug - the family physician often sees them first. [ASCO 2021]`]},{heading:`Treatment overview: small cell lung cancer`,points:[`**Limited stage**: **concurrent chemoradiation** - cisplatin or carboplatin plus **etoposide for 4 cycles** with thoracic radiotherapy, then **durvalumab consolidation** (ADRIATIC 2024); rare T1-2 N0 cases get surgery. [NCCN SCLC 2026]`,`**Brain**: **prophylactic cranial irradiation** or **MRI surveillance every 3 months** after response, because brain relapse is common. [NCCN SCLC 2026]`,`**Extensive stage**: **carboplatin-etoposide plus atezolizumab** (IMpower133) or **durvalumab** (CASPIAN) for 4 cycles, then immunotherapy maintenance. [NCCN SCLC 2026]`,`**Relapse**: lurbinectedin, topotecan, or the DLL3-targeted bispecific **tarlatamab** (approved 2024); median survival in extensive stage is only about **12-13 months**. [NCCN SCLC 2026]`,`SCLC responds dramatically but briefly - **initial response rates are about 60-80%**, yet most relapse within a year. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Elderly (70 or over)**: decide on fitness, not age - a **geriatric assessment** helps; single-agent chemotherapy or immunotherapy if frail. [ESMO 2023]`,`**Pancoast tumour**: **induction chemoradiation followed by surgery** gives the best outcome. [NCCN NSCLC 2026]`,`**Coexisting TB**: complete or start ATT alongside cancer care; **rifampicin lowers levels of TKIs** (gefitinib, osimertinib, alectinib) - use rifabutin or adjust timing with the oncologist. [NTEP 2023]`,`**Immunotherapy and latent TB**: checkpoint inhibitors and steroid courses can reactivate TB - screen symptomatically and with CXR. [NTEP 2023]`,`**Pregnancy**: rare; imaging with shielding and multidisciplinary care; TKIs and immunotherapy are avoided. [ESMO 2023]`,`**CKD**: avoid cisplatin and pemetrexed at low clearance; carboplatin is dosed by **Calvert formula** on GFR. [NCCN NSCLC 2026]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: CT plus image-guided biopsy and **EGFR testing (often free via pharma programmes)** can be arranged from a district hospital; refer to a Regional Cancer Centre with PM-JAY cover. [ICMR Consensus]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Local**: post-obstructive pneumonia, lung abscess, massive haemoptysis, malignant pleural and pericardial effusion, SVCO. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Metastatic**: brain, bone (pathological fracture, cord compression), liver, adrenal (rarely adrenal insufficiency). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Treatment-related**: radiation pneumonitis and oesophagitis, chemotherapy myelosuppression and febrile neutropenia, TKI rash and diarrhoea, EGFR-TKI interstitial lung disease, immune-related events. [NCCN NSCLC 2026]`,`**5-year survival by TNM 9 stage**: about 85-90% for stage IA1, falling to about half in stage IIB, around 20-35% in stage III, and under 10% in stage IVB. [IASLC TNM 9]`,`With targeted therapy, **EGFR-mutant stage IV** now has median overall survival of **about 3 years or more**; ALK-positive on lorlatinib often longer. [ESMO 2023]`,`**Poor prognostic factors**: poor ECOG PS, weight loss over 10%, high LDH, hyponatraemia, brain or liver metastases, SCLC histology. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Screening, prevention and programme link`,points:[`**USPSTF 2021**: annual **low-dose CT (LDCT)** for adults **50-80 years with 20 or more pack-years** who smoke now or quit within the past 15 years; stop when quit over 15 years or if unfit for surgery. [USPSTF 2021]`,`**ACS 2023** removed the 'years since quitting' limit - screen anyone 50-80 with 20 pack-years regardless of when they quit. [ACS 2023]`,`Evidence: **NLST** cut lung cancer mortality by **20%** and **NELSON** by about **24% in men**; harms are false positives, overdiagnosis and radiation. [USPSTF 2021]`,`**India has no national lung cancer screening programme**; LDCT is not recommended outside research because **TB granulomas cause many false positives**; NP-NCD screens for oral, breast and cervical cancers only. [NP-NCD 2023]`,`**Tobacco control**: **COTPA 2003**, pictorial warnings, cessation services (**quit line 1800-112-356**, mCessation), and **varenicline or NRT** - quitting at any age reduces risk. [NTCP]`,`Cleaner fuel (**PM Ujjwala Yojana LPG**), occupational protection (asbestos, silica) and air quality action under **NCAP** reduce never-smoker cancers. [NCAP]`,`**Shared decision-making and smoking-cessation counselling** must accompany any screening. [USPSTF 2021]`]},{heading:`Palliative care, referral and follow-up`,points:[`**Early palliative care** from diagnosis in metastatic NSCLC improved quality of life, mood and even survival (Temel, NEJM 2010) - refer early, not at the end. [ASCO 2017]`,`**Breathlessness**: treat reversible causes (effusion - pleurodesis or indwelling pleural catheter, airway obstruction - stent or brachytherapy), fan, and **low-dose oral morphine 2.5-5 mg 4-hourly as needed** (see the End-stage lung disease topic). [ASCO 2021]`,`**Bone metastases**: single-fraction **8 Gy radiotherapy** for pain, **zoledronic acid 4 mg IV every 12 weeks** or denosumab to prevent skeletal events. [ESMO 2023]`,`**Haemoptysis and cough**: palliative thoracic radiotherapy; codeine linctus for cough. [ESMO 2023]`,`**Refer urgently** (within 2 weeks) for any CXR suggesting cancer or **age 40 or over with unexplained haemoptysis**; **urgent CXR** for 40 or over with 2 or more unexplained symptoms (or 1 if a smoker): cough, fatigue, breathlessness, chest pain, weight loss, appetite loss. [NICE NG12]`,`**Follow-up after curative treatment**: history, examination and **CT chest every 6 months for 2-3 years, then yearly**; continue smoking cessation support. [NCCN NSCLC 2026]`,`**On TKIs**: watch LFT, QTc (osimertinib), ILD symptoms, rash and diarrhoea; on immunotherapy check **TSH, glucose, LFT and cortisol** symptoms. [ASCO 2021]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**IASLC TNM 9th edition (2024, effective 2025)**: **N2 split into N2a and N2b**, **M1c split into M1c1 and M1c2**, and T1N1 down-staged to IIA. [IASLC TNM 9]`,`**Perioperative chemo-immunotherapy** (nivolumab, pembrolizumab, durvalumab) is now standard for resectable stage II-III NSCLC without EGFR/ALK drivers. [NCCN NSCLC 2026]`,`**Adjuvant alectinib (ALINA 2024)** for resected ALK-positive NSCLC and **osimertinib after chemoradiation (LAURA 2024)** for unresectable EGFR-mutant stage III. [NCCN NSCLC 2026]`,`**First-line EGFR intensification**: osimertinib plus chemotherapy (FLAURA2) and amivantamab plus lazertinib (MARIPOSA) added as options. [ESMO 2023]`,`**SCLC**: consolidation **durvalumab after chemoradiation in limited stage (ADRIATIC 2024)** and **tarlatamab** for relapsed disease. [NCCN SCLC 2026]`,`**Screening**: USPSTF 2021 lowered the age to 50 and pack-years to 20; **ACS 2023** dropped the years-since-quit criterion - note the difference between the two. [ACS 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic long question: **'Clinical features, paraneoplastic syndromes, staging and management of bronchogenic carcinoma'** - always draw the TNM table and a treatment-by-stage flow. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: **Which paraneoplastic syndrome with which type?** - SCLC: SIADH, ACTH, LEMS, anti-Hu; squamous: PTHrP hypercalcaemia; adenocarcinoma: HPOA, Trousseau. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: **What changed in TNM 9?** - N2a/N2b and M1c1/M1c2; T unchanged. [IASLC TNM 9]`,`Viva: **Why test EGFR before treating?** - EGFR-mutant patients do far better on osimertinib and get little benefit and more toxicity from immunotherapy. [NCCN NSCLC 2026]`,`Common mistake: **starting ATT for a smear-negative mass** without NAAT, biopsy or follow-up review. [NTEP 2023]`,`Common mistake: calling a **PET-positive mediastinal node N2 without tissue** - in India TB often lights up nodes. [NCCN NSCLC 2026]`,`Common mistake: **giving steroids before biopsy** in SVCO - they can melt a lymphoma and destroy the diagnosis. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Key drugs in lung cancer`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Osimertinib (3rd-gen EGFR TKI)`,`80 mg orally once daily`,`Until progression; adjuvant 3 years`,`Diarrhoea, rash, QT prolongation, ILD, cardiomyopathy`],[`Gefitinib (1st-gen EGFR TKI)`,`250 mg orally once daily`,`Until progression`,`Rash, diarrhoea, raised transaminases, ILD; rifampicin lowers levels`],[`Alectinib (ALK TKI)`,`600 mg orally twice daily with food`,`Until progression; adjuvant 2 years`,`Myalgia, raised CK, bradycardia, constipation, oedema`],[`Lorlatinib (ALK TKI)`,`100 mg orally once daily`,`Until progression`,`Hyperlipidaemia, weight gain, mood and cognitive effects`],[`Pembrolizumab (anti-PD-1)`,`200 mg IV every 3 weeks or 400 mg every 6 weeks`,`Up to 2 years (35 cycles)`,`Immune-related pneumonitis, colitis, thyroiditis, hepatitis; avoid in active autoimmune disease`],[`Durvalumab (anti-PD-L1)`,`10 mg/kg IV every 2 weeks or 1500 mg every 4 weeks`,`12 months after chemoradiation`,`Pneumonitis (with radiation), immune events`],[`Carboplatin + pemetrexed`,`Carboplatin AUC 5 + pemetrexed 500 mg/m2 IV every 3 weeks`,`4 cycles, then pemetrexed maintenance`,`Myelosuppression; folate and B12 needed; non-squamous only; avoid CrCl under 45`],[`Carboplatin + paclitaxel`,`AUC 5-6 + paclitaxel 175-200 mg/m2 IV every 3 weeks`,`4 cycles`,`Neuropathy, alopecia, hypersensitivity (premedicate)`],[`Cisplatin/carboplatin + etoposide (SCLC)`,`Etoposide 100 mg/m2 IV days 1-3 with platinum day 1, every 3 weeks`,`4 cycles (plus atezolizumab or durvalumab in ES)`,`Myelosuppression, nephro- and ototoxicity (cisplatin)`],[`Zoledronic acid`,`4 mg IV over 15 min (renally adjusted)`,`Every 3-4 weeks for hypercalcaemia; every 12 weeks for bone mets`,`Osteonecrosis of jaw, renal impairment, hypocalcaemia`]]},{heading:`TNM 9th edition (IASLC 2024) - stage grouping`,columns:[`Stage`,`TNM combinations`,`Treatment intent`],rows:[[`IA1-IA3`,`T1a-c N0 M0`,`Surgery (segmentectomy if 2 cm or less) or SBRT`],[`IB`,`T2a N0 M0`,`Surgery; adjuvant osimertinib if EGFR-mutant`],[`IIA`,`T2b N0, T1 N1 (new in TNM 9)`,`Surgery with perioperative or adjuvant therapy`],[`IIB`,`T2 N1, T3 N0, T1 N2a (new)`,`Perioperative chemo-immunotherapy plus surgery`],[`IIIA`,`T3 N1, T4 N0-1, T2 N2a, T3 N2a, T1 N2b`,`Multimodality: surgery or chemoradiation`],[`IIIB`,`T2 N2b, T4 N2a, T1-2 N3`,`Chemoradiation, durvalumab or osimertinib consolidation`],[`IIIC`,`T3-4 N3, T4 N2b`,`Chemoradiation and consolidation`],[`IVA`,`M1a (pleural/contralateral) or M1b (single extrathoracic)`,`Systemic therapy by biomarker`],[`IVB`,`M1c1 (multiple in one organ system) or M1c2 (multiple systems)`,`Systemic therapy, palliative care`]]},{heading:`Histological types and their associations`,columns:[`Type`,`Site`,`Associations`,`Marker / driver`],rows:[[`Adenocarcinoma (40-50%)`,`Peripheral`,`Never-smokers, women, Asians, scar; HPOA, Trousseau`,`TTF-1, Napsin A; EGFR, ALK, ROS1`],[`Squamous cell (about 25%)`,`Central`,`Smokers; cavitation, PTHrP hypercalcaemia, Pancoast`,`p40, p63; drivers rare`],[`Small cell (about 15%)`,`Central, hilar`,`Heavy smokers; SIADH, ACTH, LEMS, anti-Hu, SVCO`,`Chromogranin, synaptophysin, INSM1; TP53, RB1`],[`Large cell`,`Peripheral`,`Gynaecomastia (hCG); diagnosis of exclusion`,`None specific`],[`Carcinoid`,`Central, endobronchial`,`Young, non-smokers, haemoptysis, recurrent pneumonia`,`Chromogranin; low Ki-67`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of a lung mass in India`,columns:[`Condition`,`Favouring features`,`Discriminating test`],rows:[[`Lung cancer`,`Smoker 40+, weight loss, hoarseness, clubbing, hilar mass`,`CT-guided or bronchoscopic biopsy`],[`Tuberculosis / tuberculoma`,`Fever, upper lobe, satellite lesions, calcification`,`Sputum or BAL NAAT, biopsy with AFB`],[`Lung abscess`,`Fever, foul sputum, thick-walled cavity with air-fluid level`,`Response to antibiotics, CT`],[`Aspergilloma`,`Old cavity, air-crescent sign, mobile ball`,`Aspergillus IgG, CT prone/supine`],[`Metastasis`,`Known primary, multiple round nodules`,`Biopsy, search for primary`],[`Lymphoma`,`Mediastinal mass, B symptoms, nodes elsewhere`,`Core or excision biopsy`]]},{heading:`Work-up checklist and cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`Purpose`,`Key cut-off / rule`],rows:[[`CECT chest and upper abdomen`,`T, N, liver, adrenals`,`Nodes over 1 cm short axis suspicious`],[`PET-CT`,`Staging for curative intent`,`Positive nodes need tissue confirmation (TB false positives)`],[`EBUS-TBNA`,`Mediastinal staging and diagnosis`,`Sample highest-stage node first (N3 before N2)`],[`MRI brain`,`Brain metastases`,`Stage II-IV or neurological symptoms`],[`NGS panel + PD-L1`,`Treatment selection`,`PD-L1 TPS 50% or more: immunotherapy alone possible`],[`Spirometry + DLCO`,`Surgical fitness`,`ppoFEV1 and ppoDLCO over 60% fit; under 30% needs CPET`],[`CPET`,`Borderline fitness`,`VO2 max under 10 mL/kg/min high risk; over 20 safe`]]}],redFlags:[`Stridor, facial swelling or distended neck veins (SVC obstruction or tracheal compromise)`,`Back pain with leg weakness, sensory level or sphincter disturbance (cord compression)`,`Confusion, vomiting and polyuria with high calcium (hypercalcaemia)`,`Sodium under 125 mmol/L or seizures (SIADH)`,`Massive haemoptysis`,`Fever with neutrophil count under 500/microlitre on chemotherapy (febrile neutropenia)`,`Breathlessness, cough or hypoxia on immunotherapy or EGFR TKI (pneumonitis)`],pearls:[`Squamous = Sentral, Sigarette, Stone (calcium/PTHrP); small cell = SIADH, ACTH, LEMS.`,`Adenocarcinoma is now the commonest type, including in Indian women who have never smoked.`,`TNM 9: N2a/N2b and M1c1/M1c2 are new; T is unchanged.`,`Test every non-squamous NSCLC for EGFR, ALK and other drivers before starting immunotherapy.`,`PET-positive nodes in India may be TB - always get tissue.`,`No steroids before biopsy in SVC obstruction - lymphoma may vanish.`,`LEMS improves with repeated effort; myasthenia worsens.`,`USPSTF 2021: LDCT at 50-80 years, 20 pack-years, quit within 15 years.`,`Early palliative care improves quality of life and survival in metastatic NSCLC.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-lung-cancer-q1`,stem:`In the IASLC TNM 9th edition (2024), which change was introduced for lung cancer staging?`,options:[`T1 tumours were redefined as 4 cm or less`,`Malignant pleural effusion was reclassified as T4`,`N2 disease was subdivided into single-station (N2a) and multiple-station (N2b)`,`Supraclavicular nodes were moved from N3 to M1a`],answer:2,explanation:`TNM 9 kept the T descriptors of the 8th edition and split N2 into N2a (single station) and N2b (multiple stations), and M1c into M1c1 and M1c2. T1 remains 3 cm or less, malignant pleural effusion is still M1a, and supraclavicular nodes remain N3.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-lung-cancer-q2`,stem:`A 60-year-old heavy smoker has confusion, sodium 118 mmol/L with urine osmolality 520 mOsm/kg, euvolaemia and a central hilar mass. The most likely histology is:`,options:[`Squamous cell carcinoma`,`Adenocarcinoma`,`Carcinoid tumour`,`Small cell carcinoma`],answer:3,explanation:`Euvolaemic hyponatraemia with inappropriately concentrated urine is SIADH, the classic paraneoplastic syndrome of small cell carcinoma, which is central and almost always in smokers. Squamous carcinoma is associated with PTHrP hypercalcaemia, adenocarcinoma with HPOA and Trousseau syndrome, and carcinoid more often with ectopic ACTH or carcinoid syndrome.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-lung-cancer-q3`,stem:`A 48-year-old never-smoking woman has stage IV lung adenocarcinoma with an EGFR exon 19 deletion and PD-L1 TPS 60%. The preferred first-line treatment is:`,options:[`Osimertinib 80 mg daily`,`Pembrolizumab monotherapy`,`Carboplatin-pemetrexed plus pembrolizumab`,`Best supportive care`],answer:0,explanation:`A sensitising EGFR mutation takes priority over PD-L1: osimertinib gives far longer disease control, crosses into the brain, and immunotherapy gives little benefit in EGFR-mutant disease with more pneumonitis if a TKI follows. Pembrolizumab alone or with chemotherapy is used for driver-negative disease, and supportive care alone denies an effective oral therapy.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-lung-cancer-q4`,stem:`According to USPSTF 2021, who should be offered annual low-dose CT screening for lung cancer?`,options:[`All adults over 40 with chronic cough`,`Adults 50-80 years with 20 or more pack-years who smoke now or quit within 15 years`,`Adults 55-74 years with 30 or more pack-years only`,`Never-smokers over 60 exposed to biomass smoke`],answer:1,explanation:`USPSTF 2021 lowered the start age to 50 and the threshold to 20 pack-years, for current smokers or those who quit within 15 years, up to age 80. The 55-74 and 30 pack-year criteria were the older NLST-based 2013 rule. There is no LDCT recommendation for never-smokers or for cough as an indication - symptomatic patients need diagnostic, not screening, CT.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-lung-cancer-q5`,stem:`A 55-year-old smoker has right shoulder pain radiating down the inner arm, wasting of the small muscles of the right hand and right-sided ptosis with miosis. CXR shows an apical opacity. The best management sequence, if staging shows no distant spread, is:`,options:[`Immediate surgery without other treatment`,`Radiotherapy alone with palliative intent`,`Empirical anti-TB treatment for apical TB`,`Induction chemoradiation followed by surgical resection`],answer:3,explanation:`This is a Pancoast (superior sulcus) tumour with C8-T1 root and sympathetic chain involvement (Horner syndrome). Induction chemoradiation followed by surgery gives the best resection rate and survival. Upfront surgery alone has high incomplete resection rates, radiotherapy alone is for unfit patients, and treating it as apical TB is the classic Indian diagnostic delay.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-lung-cancer-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Describe the clinical features, paraneoplastic syndromes, staging (TNM 9th edition) and principles of management of non-small cell lung cancer.`,openingLines:[`Lung cancer is the commonest cancer and leading cause of cancer death worldwide; NSCLC forms about 85%, with adenocarcinoma now the commonest type.`,`It is staged by the IASLC TNM 9th edition (2024), and treatment depends on stage, performance status and biomarkers (EGFR, ALK, PD-L1).`],answer:[{heading:`Epidemiology and risk factors`,points:[`Smoking (cigarette, bidi), second-hand smoke, biomass and outdoor air pollution, asbestos, radon, old TB scars, COPD, ILD.`,`India: younger patients, late stage, never-smokers and EGFR mutations common, TB misdiagnosis delay.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Local: cough, haemoptysis, breathlessness, chest pain, non-resolving pneumonia.`,`Regional: hoarseness, SVCO, dysphagia, Pancoast with Horner syndrome, effusion.`,`Metastatic: bone, brain, liver, adrenal.`]},{heading:`Paraneoplastic syndromes`,points:[`Squamous: PTHrP hypercalcaemia.`,`Adenocarcinoma: HPOA, clubbing, Trousseau.`,`Small cell (for contrast): SIADH, ectopic ACTH, LEMS, anti-Hu.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`CXR, CECT chest with upper abdomen, PET-CT, MRI brain.`,`Tissue by bronchoscopy, CT-guided biopsy or EBUS-TBNA; biopsy the site giving highest stage.`,`NGS for EGFR, ALK, ROS1 and others; PD-L1 IHC; spirometry and DLCO for fitness.`]},{heading:`TNM 9th edition`,points:[`T1 3 cm or less, T2 3-5, T3 5-7, T4 over 7 or invading mediastinal structures.`,`N1 hilar, N2a single N2 station, N2b multiple, N3 contralateral or supraclavicular.`,`M1a intrathoracic, M1b single extrathoracic, M1c1 one system, M1c2 multiple systems.`]},{heading:`Management by stage`,points:[`I-II: lobectomy or segmentectomy; SBRT if unfit; adjuvant osimertinib/alectinib for drivers.`,`Resectable II-III: perioperative chemo-immunotherapy.`,`Unresectable III: concurrent chemoradiation then durvalumab 12 months (osimertinib if EGFR).`,`IV: driver - TKI (osimertinib, alectinib); no driver - pembrolizumab alone (PD-L1 50%+) or with platinum doublet.`]},{heading:`Palliation and follow-up`,points:[`Early palliative care, pleural drainage, palliative radiotherapy, bone protection, morphine for breathlessness.`,`CT every 6 months for 2-3 years after curative treatment; smoking cessation.`]}],mustDraw:[`TNM 9 stage grouping table`,`Flow chart: stage IV NSCLC - biomarker testing, driver-positive TKI versus PD-L1-guided immunotherapy`],markSplit:[{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Paraneoplastic syndromes`,marks:1.5},{part:`Investigations and staging`,marks:3},{part:`Management`,marks:3.5}],keywords:[`NSCLC`,`TNM 9`,`EGFR`,`ALK`,`PD-L1`,`osimertinib`,`pembrolizumab`,`PACIFIC`,`paraneoplastic`]},{id:`respiratory-lung-cancer-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on paraneoplastic syndromes in lung cancer.`,openingLines:[`Paraneoplastic syndromes are effects of a cancer remote from the tumour and its metastases, caused by hormones, peptides or cross-reacting antibodies.`,`They occur in about 10% of lung cancers, mostly small cell carcinoma, and may precede the diagnosis.`],answer:[{heading:`Endocrine`,points:[`SIADH - SCLC; euvolaemic hyponatraemia; fluid restriction, tolvaptan.`,`Ectopic ACTH - SCLC, carcinoid; hypokalaemic alkalosis.`,`PTHrP hypercalcaemia - squamous cell; saline, zoledronic acid.`,`Gynaecomastia - hCG, large cell.`]},{heading:`Neurological`,points:[`Lambert-Eaton (anti-VGCC) - improves with effort.`,`Anti-Hu encephalomyelitis, sensory neuronopathy, cerebellar degeneration, limbic encephalitis.`]},{heading:`Skeletal, haematological and skin`,points:[`Clubbing and HPOA - adenocarcinoma; periostitis on X-ray.`,`Trousseau migratory thrombophlebitis, DIC, anaemia.`,`Dermatomyositis, acanthosis nigricans.`]},{heading:`Clinical importance`,points:[`May be the first sign; may track tumour activity; treat the tumour to control them.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: syndrome - mediator - histological type`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition`,marks:.5},{part:`Endocrine`,marks:2},{part:`Neurological`,marks:1.5},{part:`Others and importance`,marks:1}],keywords:[`SIADH`,`PTHrP`,`Lambert-Eaton`,`HPOA`,`ectopic ACTH`,`anti-Hu`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Neoplasms of the lung`,`IASLC Staging Manual in Thoracic Oncology, TNM 9th edition, 2024 (Rami-Porta et al., J Thorac Oncol 2024)`,`NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines: Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer and Small Cell Lung Cancer, 2026`,`ESMO Clinical Practice Guideline: Oncogene-addicted and non-oncogene-addicted metastatic NSCLC, 2023 (living guideline)`,`US Preventive Services Task Force. Screening for Lung Cancer, 2021; American Cancer Society lung cancer screening guideline, 2023`,`WHO Classification of Thoracic Tumours, 5th edition, 2021`,`ICMR National Cancer Registry Programme Report 2020 and ICMR Consensus Document for Management of Lung Cancer`,`Noronha V et al. Gefitinib versus gefitinib plus pemetrexed and carboplatin in EGFR-mutant NSCLC. J Clin Oncol 2020`]}),t.push({id:`respiratory-solitary-pulmonary-nodule`,title:`Solitary pulmonary nodule and incidental lung nodules`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`A **solitary pulmonary nodule (SPN)** is a single, well-defined, round opacity **up to 3 cm** surrounded by aerated lung, without collapse, hilar enlargement or effusion; its management depends on **size, density (solid, part-solid, ground-glass), morphology and clinical risk** - using the **Fleischner 2017** rules for incidental nodules, the **Brock (or Mayo) model** to estimate cancer probability and **Lung-RADS v2022** in screening - while in India **tuberculous granulomas** are the commonest mimic.`,keywords:[`SPN`,`coin lesion`,`incidental nodule`,`Fleischner 2017`,`Brock model`,`PanCan`,`Mayo model`,`Herder model`,`Lung-RADS v2022`,`ground-glass nodule`,`part-solid nodule`,`subsolid nodule`,`volume doubling time`,`tuberculoma`,`hamartoma`,`PET-CT`,`CT-guided biopsy`,`navigational bronchoscopy`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`A **pulmonary nodule** is a rounded opacity, well or poorly defined, **3 cm or less** in diameter; a lesion **over 3 cm is a mass** and is presumed malignant until proved otherwise. [Fleischner Glossary 2024]`,`**Micronodule** is under 3 mm; nodules are described by **density**: **solid**, **part-solid** (ground-glass with a solid component) and **pure ground-glass** (lung markings seen through it); part-solid and ground-glass together are **subsolid**. [Fleischner 2017]`,`Incidental nodules are found on up to a **third of chest CTs** and in about **half of screening LDCTs** in smokers; the great majority are benign. [Harrison 22e]`,`The **malignancy risk is under 1% for solid nodules under 6 mm**, even in high-risk patients, and rises with size - roughly 1-2% at 6-8 mm and higher above 8 mm. [Fleischner 2017]`,`**Part-solid nodules carry the highest cancer risk** of any nodule of equal size (up to 60% or more if persistent), usually adenocarcinoma spectrum, though they grow slowly. [Fleischner 2017]`,`In India, **tuberculous granulomas (tuberculomas)** are the commonest benign cause and the main reason PET-CT and growth rules perform less well than in Western data. [Lung India]`,`Lung cancer presenting as an SPN is usually **stage IA** with **5-year survival of about 80-90%** after resection - the reason nodules are worth chasing. [IASLC TNM 9]`]},{heading:`Aetiology: what a nodule can be`,points:[`**Malignant**: primary lung cancer (adenocarcinoma commonest), **solitary metastasis** (colon, kidney, breast, sarcoma, melanoma, germ-cell), **carcinoid**, lymphoma. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Infective granulomas**: **tuberculoma**, non-tuberculous mycobacteria, histoplasmosis and cryptococcosis, **aspergilloma/aspergillus nodule**, and **hydatid cyst**. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Benign neoplasm**: **hamartoma** (commonest benign lung tumour, with fat and popcorn calcification), lipoma, chondroma. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Inflammatory**: **rheumatoid nodule**, granulomatosis with polyangiitis (often cavitating, multiple), sarcoid, organising pneumonia, round pneumonia (children). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Vascular**: **pulmonary AV malformation** (feeding artery and draining vein), infarct, haematoma. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Other**: intrapulmonary lymph node (perifissural, triangular), **rounded atelectasis** (asbestos, comet-tail sign), mucoid impaction, bronchogenic cyst, and skin or nipple shadows on CXR. [Fleischner 2017]`,`**Clinical risk factors for malignancy**: older age, **smoking**, prior cancer, family history of lung cancer, emphysema or fibrosis, and exposure to asbestos, radon or uranium. [ACCP 2013]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology: why size, density and growth predict cancer`,points:[`Malignant nodules grow at a measurable pace: **volume doubling time (VDT) of about 20-400 days for solid cancers**; VDT **under 400 days** is suspicious, **over 600 days** favours benign. [BTS 2015]`,`A **solid nodule stable for 2 years** is traditionally benign, but **subsolid nodules may take 3-5 years** to grow because adenocarcinoma in situ and minimally invasive adenocarcinoma double in 2-3 years. [Fleischner 2017]`,`A **doubling of volume is only a 26% increase in diameter**, so small changes in diameter matter - hence volumetry and same-technique thin-section CT. [BTS 2015]`,`Very rapid growth (VDT **under 20 days**) suggests infection, inflammation or haemorrhage rather than cancer. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ground-glass to part-solid evolution** follows the adenocarcinoma spectrum: atypical adenomatous hyperplasia, **adenocarcinoma in situ**, minimally invasive (solid part 5 mm or less), then invasive adenocarcinoma - the **solid part corresponds to invasion**. [WHO 2021]`,`**Spiculation** (corona radiata) reflects tumour infiltrating along interlobular septa and lymphatics, and **pleural retraction** reflects desmoplastic fibrosis. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Benign calcification** (central, diffuse, laminated, popcorn) reflects dystrophic calcification of healed granulomas or cartilage in hamartomas - cancers rarely calcify in these patterns. [Fishman 6e]`,`**FDG-PET uptake** reflects high glucose metabolism; active granulomas (TB, fungal) also take up FDG, and slow-growing adenocarcinoma in situ and carcinoid may not. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification: Fleischner 2017 for incidental nodules`,points:[`**Applies to**: incidental nodules in adults **35 years or older**; **not** to lung cancer screening, immunocompromised patients or patients with known cancer. [Fleischner 2017]`,`**Solid single, under 6 mm (under 100 mm3)**: low risk - no routine follow-up; high risk - optional CT at 12 months. [Fleischner 2017]`,`**Solid single, 6-8 mm (100-250 mm3)**: low risk - CT at 6-12 months, then consider CT at 18-24 months; high risk - CT at 6-12 months and again at 18-24 months. [Fleischner 2017]`,`**Solid single, over 8 mm (over 250 mm3)**: consider **CT at 3 months, PET-CT or tissue sampling**, whatever the risk. [Fleischner 2017]`,`**Multiple solid nodules**: under 6 mm - no routine follow-up (optional 12-month CT if high risk); 6 mm or more - CT at 3-6 months, then consider 18-24 months; manage by the **most suspicious nodule**. [Fleischner 2017]`,`**Pure ground-glass single**: under 6 mm - no routine follow-up; **6 mm or more - CT at 6-12 months to confirm persistence, then every 2 years until 5 years**. [Fleischner 2017]`,`**Part-solid single**: under 6 mm - no routine follow-up; **6 mm or more - CT at 3-6 months**; if persistent and solid part under 6 mm, **annual CT for 5 years**; a **solid component of 6 mm or more is highly suspicious**. [Fleischner 2017]`,`**Size is the average of long and short axes** on thin-section (1 mm) CT, rounded to the nearest millimetre; volumetry is preferred where software exists. [Fleischner 2017]`]},{heading:`Risk models and other classifications`,points:[`**Brock (PanCan) model**: age, sex, family history of lung cancer, emphysema, nodule size, **type (part-solid scores highest)**, upper-lobe location, number of nodules and spiculation - **recommended by BTS 2015** for initial risk. [BTS 2015]`,`**Mayo Clinic model**: age, smoking, prior extrathoracic cancer over 5 years ago, diameter, spiculation and upper-lobe location - endorsed by **ACCP 2013**. [ACCP 2013]`,`**ACCP 2013 thresholds**: probability **under 5% - CT surveillance**; **5-65% - PET-CT and/or non-surgical biopsy**; **over 65% - proceed to surgery** (after staging) in a fit patient. [ACCP 2013]`,`**BTS 2015 pathway**: under 5 mm (under 80 mm3) - discharge; 5-6 mm - CT at 1 year; 6 mm or more with **Brock under 10% - volumetric CT surveillance**; 8 mm or more with **Brock 10% or more - PET-CT** then **Herder model**. [BTS 2015]`,`**Herder model** adds PET uptake to Mayo: **under 10% - surveillance; 10-70% - image-guided biopsy; over 70% - excision or non-surgical treatment**. [BTS 2015]`,`**Lung-RADS v2022 (ACR)** is used only for screening LDCT: categories **1 (negative), 2 (benign), 3 (probably benign), 4A, 4B and 4X (suspicious)**, plus S for significant other findings. [ACR Lung-RADS 2022]`,`**Caution in India**: these models were derived in low-TB populations and **overestimate the benign-cancer separation of PET**; clinical judgment and TB testing remain essential. [Lung India]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Most SPNs are **asymptomatic and incidental** - found on CXR or CT done for trauma, pre-operative screening, COVID-19, or cardiac imaging. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask **age, smoking pack-years, bidi use, biomass exposure**, asbestos and silica work, and family history of lung cancer. [ACCP 2013]`,`Ask **past TB, contact with TB**, and fever, cough, weight loss - an active process needs NAAT, not surveillance. [NTEP 2023]`,`Ask **past cancer** - a new nodule in a patient with known malignancy is a metastasis until proved otherwise, and Fleischner rules do not apply. [Fleischner 2017]`,`Ask about **immunosuppression** (HIV, transplant, steroids, biologics, chemotherapy) - nodules then suggest fungal, nocardia or mycobacterial infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **rheumatoid arthritis, sinusitis and haematuria** (GPA), **epistaxis and telangiectasia** (HHT and AVM), and **rearing dogs or sheep** (hydatid). [Harrison 22e]`,`Most important single step: **obtain all previous chest images** - a nodule unchanged for 2 years (solid) needs no further work-up. [ACCP 2013]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Examination is usually normal** - its purpose is to look for clues to cause and to fitness for surgery. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Clubbing**, cachexia and supraclavicular nodes suggest malignancy (and supraclavicular node biopsy may give the stage). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rheumatoid hands and subcutaneous nodules** suggest rheumatoid lung nodules; **telangiectasia** suggests AVM. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Breast, thyroid, skin (melanoma), testes and abdomen** - search for a primary in suspected metastasis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Skin and nipple shadows**: on CXR, a nipple marker or repeat film settles a 'nodule' in the lower zones. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Functional status**: ECOG performance status, breathlessness and comorbidity, which decide whether surgery or SBRT is an option. [ACCP 2013]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis: features on CT`,points:[`**Favouring benign**: size under 6 mm, **benign calcification (central, diffuse, laminated, popcorn)**, **fat** (hamartoma), smooth margins, **perifissural triangular shape** (intrapulmonary lymph node), stability over 2 years (solid). [Fleischner 2017]`,`**Favouring malignant**: size over 8 mm, **spiculation**, lobulation, pleural retraction, **upper lobe**, part-solid density, **air bronchogram or bubbly lucencies**, growth, and **cavity wall over 15 mm**. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Tuberculoma**: upper lobe or superior segment of lower lobe, **satellite nodules**, central or laminated calcification, calcified hilar nodes; may be PET-avid. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Hamartoma**: smooth, lobulated, **fat density (-40 to -120 HU)** and popcorn calcification in about a third. [Fishman 6e]`,`**AV malformation**: feeding artery and draining vein on CT, enhances like vessels; never biopsy. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Rounded atelectasis**: pleural-based, next to pleural thickening, with the **comet-tail sign** of vessels swirling in - asbestos exposure. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Hydatid cyst**: smooth, water-density; ruptured cyst shows the **water-lily (camalote) sign**; do not needle it (anaphylaxis). [API Textbook 12e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line`,points:[`**Compare with previous imaging** first - free and decisive. [ACCP 2013]`,`A nodule seen on CXR needs a **thin-section (1 mm) non-contrast CT chest** to confirm it is real, measure it and characterise density and calcification. [Fleischner 2017]`,`**Sputum or BAL NAAT for TB** if there are symptoms, cavitation or a tree-in-bud pattern; a tuberculoma itself is often paucibacillary and NAAT-negative. [NTEP 2023]`,`**Blood tests**: CBC, ESR or CRP, HIV, glucose; **rheumatoid factor/anti-CCP and ANCA** when the history suggests them. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hydatid serology** (ELISA) and ultrasound abdomen when a cyst is suspected. [API Textbook 12e]`,`**Spirometry and DLCO** before any decision about surgery or biopsy. [ACCP 2013]`]},{heading:`Investigations - advanced: PET, biopsy and surgery`,points:[`**PET-CT** is useful for **solid nodules 8 mm or more** with intermediate risk; sensitivity is about **90-95%** but specificity is lower in TB-endemic regions because **granulomas take up FDG**. [ACCP 2013]`,`**False-negative PET**: nodules under 8 mm, **ground-glass and part-solid adenocarcinoma**, adenocarcinoma in situ, carcinoid and mucinous tumours. [ACCP 2013]`,`**CT-guided transthoracic needle biopsy**: diagnostic yield about **90%** for peripheral nodules; **pneumothorax in about 15-25%**, needing a drain in about 5%; small haemoptysis is common. [BTS 2015]`,`**Bronchoscopic biopsy**: **radial EBUS, electromagnetic navigation or robotic bronchoscopy** reach peripheral nodules with yields of about 70-80% and fewer pneumothoraces; a **bronchus sign** on CT predicts success. [CHEST 2023]`,`**EBUS-TBNA** samples enlarged or PET-avid mediastinal nodes, which stages and diagnoses in one step. [ACCP 2013]`,`**VATS wedge resection** is both diagnostic and therapeutic for high-probability nodules; frozen section then completion lobectomy or segmentectomy if malignant. [ACCP 2013]`,`A **non-diagnostic biopsy does not exclude cancer** - only a specific benign diagnosis (e.g. granuloma with AFB, hamartoma) is reassuring; otherwise repeat, resect or follow closely. [ACCP 2013]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`Step 1: **Is it a real nodule?** Repeat CXR with nipple markers or get CT for any CXR nodule. [Fleischner 2017]`,`Step 2: **Is it benign by definition?** Benign calcification, fat, typical perifissural node, or 2-year stability (solid) - **no follow-up**. [Fleischner 2017]`,`Step 3: **Estimate risk** (clinical risk factors plus Brock or Mayo) and **apply Fleischner 2017** for size and density. [BTS 2015]`,`Step 4: **Document a clear plan** - date and type of next CT - in the report and patient record, and give the patient a written copy; lost follow-up is the commonest failure. [ACCP 2013]`,`**Do not start empirical ATT** for an isolated asymptomatic nodule without bacteriological or histological evidence of TB. [NTEP 2023]`,`**Smoking cessation** advice at every visit - nodule detection is a 'teachable moment'. [USPSTF 2021]`,`**Explain uncertainty honestly**: most nodules are benign, but follow-up matters; patient anxiety is a recognised harm of surveillance. [ACCP 2013]`]},{heading:`Management in hospital / specialist`,points:[`**Low probability (under 5%)**: CT surveillance per Fleischner or BTS intervals. [ACCP 2013]`,`**Intermediate probability (5-65%)**: PET-CT for solid nodules 8 mm or more, then biopsy (CT-guided or navigational) or surgical biopsy depending on location and patient choice. [ACCP 2013]`,`**High probability (over 65%)**: **stage with PET-CT** and proceed to **surgical resection** (lobectomy or segmentectomy for peripheral tumours 2 cm or less) without preoperative biopsy in a fit patient. [ACCP 2013]`,`**Unfit for surgery**: **SBRT** after biopsy confirmation, or empirically when probability is high and biopsy is unsafe (multidisciplinary decision). [BTS 2015]`,`**Persistent part-solid nodule with solid part 6 mm or more** or growing: resection is usually advised (often sublobar). [Fleischner 2017]`,`**Persistent pure GGN**: surveillance for 5 years is safe; growth or new solid component prompts resection. [Fleischner 2017]`,`**Multidisciplinary nodule clinic** (pulmonologist, radiologist, thoracic surgeon, oncologist) improves adherence and reduces unnecessary biopsies. [BTS 2015]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Lung cancer screening LDCT**: use **Lung-RADS v2022**, not Fleischner (see the Lung cancer topic). [ACR Lung-RADS 2022]`,`**Known extrathoracic cancer**: treat new nodules as metastases until proven otherwise; follow the oncology staging protocol. [Fleischner 2017]`,`**Immunocompromised**: nodules often mean infection (fungal, nocardia, TB) - **early biopsy or BAL**, not surveillance (see the Immunocompromised lung infections topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Under 35 years**: cancer is rare; nodules are usually infective - manage case by case. [Fleischner 2017]`,`**Pregnancy**: defer non-urgent CT surveillance until after delivery unless highly suspicious. [Fleischner 2017]`,`**TB-endemic India**: PET-avid nodules frequently prove to be **granulomas**, so **tissue diagnosis is favoured before surgery** where feasible, and a **short-interval CT after treating any active infection** helps. [Lung India]`,`**Post-COVID CT nodules**: many are inflammatory and resolve; re-image at about 3 months before invasive work-up. [Fleischner 2020]`]},{heading:`Complications, prognosis and follow-up`,points:[`**Missed or delayed follow-up** is the main harm - a stage IA cancer can become unresectable. [ACCP 2013]`,`**Over-investigation harms**: radiation, biopsy pneumothorax and bleeding, surgery for benign disease, and anxiety. [USPSTF 2021]`,`**Growth definition in Lung-RADS v2022**: an increase in mean diameter of **more than 1.5 mm within 12 months**. [ACR Lung-RADS 2022]`,`**Stage IA resected adenocarcinoma** has **5-year survival around 80-90%**; adenocarcinoma in situ and minimally invasive adenocarcinoma are close to **100% disease-specific survival**. [IASLC TNM 9]`,`**Follow-up CT**: same scanner and protocol if possible, low dose, thin sections, and **volumetric comparison**. [BTS 2015]`,`**Stop surveillance** when a solid nodule is stable for 2 years (or volumetric stability for 1 year per BTS), or a subsolid nodule for 5 years. [BTS 2015]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates and exam angle`,points:[`**ACR Lung-RADS v2022** (released late 2022): adds **airway nodules and atypical pulmonary cysts**, **stepped management** (category 3 stable at 6 months becomes 2; 4A stable at 3 months becomes 3), and clarifies inflammatory/infectious findings. [ACR Lung-RADS 2022]`,`**Fleischner 2017** remains current: raised the size threshold to **6 mm**, gave volume equivalents, and lengthened subsolid follow-up to **5 years**. [Fleischner 2017]`,`**BTS 2015** remains the UK standard (Brock, volumetry, Herder); **IASLC TNM 9 (2024)** stages part-solid tumours by the **size of the solid (invasive) component**. [IASLC TNM 9]`,`**Screening eligibility** widened (USPSTF 2021; ACS 2023 drops years-since-quit), so more screen-detected nodules now reach primary care. [ACS 2023]`,`**AI-assisted nodule detection and volumetry** is increasingly used; it supports but does not replace radiologist review. [Fleischner 2024]`,`Viva: **'Define SPN; list benign calcification patterns; how do you follow a 7 mm solid nodule in a smoker?'** - CT at 6-12 months and 18-24 months. [Fleischner 2017]`,`Common mistakes: applying Fleischner to screening or cancer patients, calling eccentric calcification benign, and **starting ATT for a nodule without proof**. [Fleischner 2017]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Fleischner 2017 - incidental solitary nodule (adults 35+)`,columns:[`Nodule type`,`Under 6 mm`,`6-8 mm`,`Over 8 mm`],rows:[[`Solid, low risk`,`No routine follow-up`,`CT 6-12 months, consider CT 18-24 months`,`Consider CT at 3 months, PET-CT or tissue`],[`Solid, high risk`,`Optional CT at 12 months`,`CT 6-12 months and 18-24 months`,`Consider CT at 3 months, PET-CT or tissue`],[`Pure ground-glass`,`No routine follow-up`,`6 mm or more: CT 6-12 months, then every 2 years to 5 years`,`Same as 6 mm or more`],[`Part-solid`,`No routine follow-up`,`6 mm or more: CT 3-6 months; if solid part under 6 mm, annual CT for 5 years`,`Solid part 6 mm or more: highly suspicious - PET, biopsy or resection`]]},{heading:`Benign versus malignant features`,columns:[`Feature`,`Favours benign`,`Favours malignant`],rows:[[`Size`,`Under 6 mm`,`Over 8 mm; over 3 cm is a mass`],[`Calcification`,`Central, diffuse, laminated, popcorn`,`Eccentric or stippled (indeterminate)`],[`Margin`,`Smooth, well defined`,`Spiculated, lobulated, pleural tail`],[`Density`,`Fat (hamartoma), fully calcified`,`Part-solid; solid part 6 mm or more`],[`Growth`,`Stable 2 years (solid), 5 years (subsolid)`,`VDT 20-400 days`],[`Location`,`Lower lobe, perifissural`,`Upper lobe`],[`Cavity wall`,`4 mm or less`,`Over 15 mm`]]},{heading:`Lung-RADS v2022 (screening LDCT)`,columns:[`Category`,`Typical finding (solid nodule)`,`Management`,`Malignancy probability`],rows:[[`1 Negative`,`No nodule or benign calcification`,`Annual LDCT`,`Under 1%`],[`2 Benign`,`Solid under 6 mm baseline; GGN under 30 mm`,`Annual LDCT`,`Under 1%`],[`3 Probably benign`,`Solid 6 to under 8 mm baseline; new 4 to under 6 mm`,`LDCT at 6 months`,`1-2%`],[`4A Suspicious`,`Solid 8 to under 15 mm baseline; new 6 to under 8 mm`,`LDCT at 3 months; PET-CT if solid part 8 mm or more`,`5-15%`],[`4B Very suspicious`,`Solid 15 mm or more; new or growing 8 mm or more`,`Diagnostic CT, PET-CT, tissue sampling`,`Over 15%`],[`4X`,`Category 3-4 plus suspicious features (spiculation, nodes)`,`As 4B`,`Over 15%`]]},{heading:`Tools for the nodule work-up`,columns:[`Tool`,`Use`,`Limitations / complications`],rows:[[`Thin-section CT (1 mm)`,`Confirm, measure, characterise`,`Inter-reader variation; use same protocol`],[`Brock / Mayo / Herder models`,`Estimate cancer probability`,`Derived in low-TB populations`],[`PET-CT`,`Solid nodule 8 mm or more, intermediate risk`,`False positive in TB; false negative in GGN, carcinoid`],[`CT-guided needle biopsy`,`Peripheral nodules`,`Pneumothorax 15-25%, drain about 5%`],[`Navigational / robotic bronchoscopy`,`Peripheral nodule with bronchus sign`,`Yield 70-80%; availability`],[`VATS wedge resection`,`High probability; diagnosis and cure`,`Surgical risk; may remove benign nodules`]]},{heading:`Medicines relevant to the nodule pathway`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Lidocaine (biopsy local anaesthesia)`,`1-2% up to 3 mg/kg (4.5 mg/kg = about 300 mg max without adrenaline) subcutaneous`,`Single procedure`,`Toxicity: perioral numbness, seizures, arrhythmia`],[`Midazolam (bronchoscopy sedation)`,`1-2 mg IV, titrated in 0.5-1 mg steps`,`Procedure only`,`Respiratory depression; reverse with flumazenil`],[`Albendazole (hydatid, peri-operative)`,`400 mg orally twice daily with fatty meal`,`1-6 months around surgery`,`Hepatotoxicity, neutropenia; avoid in early pregnancy`],[`Varenicline (smoking cessation)`,`0.5 mg OD days 1-3, 0.5 mg BD days 4-7, then 1 mg BD`,`12 weeks`,`Nausea, vivid dreams; adjust in CKD`],[`Nicotine replacement (patch/gum)`,`Patch 21 mg/24 h if 10 or more cigarettes/day; gum 2-4 mg as needed`,`8-12 weeks, then taper`,`Skin irritation, sleep disturbance`]]}],redFlags:[`Any nodule over 3 cm (mass) - presume cancer`,`Growing solid nodule or new solid component in a subsolid nodule`,`Spiculated nodule over 8 mm in a smoker or ex-smoker`,`Nodule with haemoptysis, weight loss, hoarseness or supraclavicular node`,`New nodule in a patient with known cancer`,`Nodules in an immunocompromised patient with fever`,`Pneumothorax with breathlessness after CT-guided biopsy`],pearls:[`Old films first - stability is the cheapest test.`,`Under 6 mm solid in a low-risk adult: no follow-up (Fleischner 2017).`,`Over 8 mm solid: CT at 3 months, PET-CT or biopsy.`,`Solid stable 2 years = benign; subsolid needs 5 years.`,`Part-solid nodules are the most likely to be cancer; the solid part is the invasive part.`,`Benign calcification: central, diffuse, laminated, popcorn.`,`PET lies in India: TB lights up, adenocarcinoma in situ stays dark.`,`Fleischner is for incidental nodules; Lung-RADS is for screening.`,`A non-diagnostic biopsy is not a benign biopsy.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-solitary-pulmonary-nodule-q1`,stem:`A 52-year-old non-smoker has a 4 mm solid nodule found incidentally on a CT abdomen. There is no history of cancer. According to Fleischner 2017, the recommended follow-up is:`,options:[`CT at 3 months`,`PET-CT now`,`No routine follow-up`,`CT-guided biopsy`],answer:2,explanation:`A solid nodule under 6 mm in a low-risk patient carries a malignancy risk well under 1%, so Fleischner 2017 recommends no routine follow-up. CT at 3 months is for solid nodules over 8 mm, and PET-CT and biopsy are not reliable or justified for a nodule this small.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-solitary-pulmonary-nodule-q2`,stem:`Which calcification pattern in a pulmonary nodule is NOT considered a benign pattern?`,options:[`Popcorn calcification`,`Eccentric calcification`,`Laminated (concentric) calcification`,`Diffuse solid calcification`],answer:1,explanation:`Central, diffuse, laminated and popcorn calcification are benign patterns (granuloma or hamartoma). Eccentric or stippled calcification is indeterminate because a cancer can engulf a pre-existing granuloma or calcify dystrophically, so it does not exclude malignancy.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-solitary-pulmonary-nodule-q3`,stem:`A 60-year-old ex-smoker has a 14 mm persistent part-solid nodule in the right upper lobe; the solid component measures 8 mm. What is the most appropriate approach?`,options:[`Annual CT for 5 years`,`No follow-up, as subsolid nodules are indolent`,`Empirical anti-tuberculosis treatment for 6 months`,`Consider PET-CT and tissue diagnosis or resection`],answer:3,explanation:`A persistent part-solid nodule with a solid component of 6 mm or more is highly suspicious for invasive adenocarcinoma, so Fleischner advises PET-CT and biopsy or resection. Annual CT for 5 years is only for part-solid nodules whose solid part stays under 6 mm, ignoring it misses cancer, and empirical ATT has no place without evidence of TB.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-solitary-pulmonary-nodule-q4`,stem:`PET-CT of a 12 mm spiculated nodule in a patient from Bihar shows SUVmax 6.2. Which statement is most accurate?`,options:[`The nodule is definitely malignant and needs no tissue diagnosis`,`A tuberculous granuloma can produce this degree of uptake, so tissue confirmation is advisable`,`High SUV excludes adenocarcinoma in situ, so it must be a carcinoid`,`PET-CT is unreliable for nodules over 8 mm`],answer:1,explanation:`In TB-endemic regions active granulomas take up FDG avidly, so PET specificity falls and tissue confirmation before surgery is favoured. PET is most useful for solid nodules 8 mm or more, not unreliable for them. Carcinoid and adenocarcinoma in situ typically show low uptake, so a high SUV does not point to carcinoid.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-solitary-pulmonary-nodule-q5`,stem:`According to ACCP 2013, a fit patient with a solid nodule whose estimated probability of malignancy is 75% should usually be managed by:`,options:[`Surgical resection after staging`,`CT surveillance at 3, 9 and 24 months`,`PET-CT and then CT surveillance if PET is negative`,`Sputum cytology on three samples`],answer:0,explanation:`ACCP thresholds are under 5% for surveillance, 5-65% for PET and/or non-surgical biopsy, and over 65% for surgical diagnosis and treatment after staging. A negative PET does not lower a 75% pre-test probability enough to justify surveillance, and sputum cytology has very low yield for peripheral nodules.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-solitary-pulmonary-nodule-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Define solitary pulmonary nodule. Enumerate its causes and describe the approach to an incidentally detected lung nodule, including the Fleischner 2017 recommendations.`,openingLines:[`A solitary pulmonary nodule is a single, well-circumscribed, round opacity up to 3 cm in diameter, surrounded by aerated lung, without atelectasis, hilar enlargement or effusion.`,`Most are benign - in India commonly tuberculous granulomas - but an SPN may be an early, curable lung cancer.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes`,points:[`Malignant: primary lung cancer, solitary metastasis, carcinoid, lymphoma.`,`Infective: tuberculoma, fungal granuloma, aspergilloma, hydatid cyst.`,`Benign tumour: hamartoma; inflammatory: rheumatoid nodule, GPA; vascular: AVM, infarct; others: intrapulmonary node, rounded atelectasis.`]},{heading:`Clinical risk assessment`,points:[`Age, smoking, prior cancer, family history, emphysema, occupational exposure, TB history, immune status.`,`Compare with previous imaging.`]},{heading:`CT characterisation`,points:[`Size (average of axes), density (solid, part-solid, GGN), margins, calcification pattern, fat, location, growth (VDT).`,`Benign: calcification central, diffuse, laminated, popcorn; fat; stable 2 years.`]},{heading:`Fleischner 2017`,points:[`Solid under 6 mm: no follow-up (optional 12-month CT if high risk).`,`Solid 6-8 mm: CT 6-12 months, then 18-24 months.`,`Solid over 8 mm: CT 3 months, PET-CT or tissue.`,`GGN 6 mm or more: CT 6-12 months then 2-yearly to 5 years; part-solid 6 mm or more: CT 3-6 months, annual to 5 years; solid part 6 mm or more suspicious.`]},{heading:`Probability-based management`,points:[`Brock or Mayo model; under 5% surveillance, 5-65% PET/biopsy, over 65% surgery.`,`CT-guided or navigational biopsy; VATS wedge; SBRT if unfit.`]},{heading:`Indian context and follow-up`,points:[`TB granulomas give PET false positives; prefer tissue before surgery; no empirical ATT.`,`Document the plan; smoking cessation; Lung-RADS for screening CT.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: incidental nodule - old films, CT characterisation, risk estimate, Fleischner pathway, PET/biopsy/surgery`,`Diagram of benign versus suspicious calcification patterns`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and causes`,marks:2},{part:`Risk and CT features`,marks:2},{part:`Fleischner 2017`,marks:3},{part:`Further management and Indian context`,marks:3}],keywords:[`SPN`,`Fleischner 2017`,`Brock model`,`PET-CT`,`ground-glass nodule`,`tuberculoma`,`volume doubling time`]},{id:`respiratory-solitary-pulmonary-nodule-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on Lung-RADS.`,openingLines:[`Lung-RADS is the American College of Radiology system for reporting and managing findings on lung cancer screening low-dose CT; the current version is v2022.`,`It standardises nodule categories, follow-up intervals and expected malignancy risk, and is not used for incidental nodules (Fleischner applies there).`],answer:[{heading:`Categories`,points:[`0 incomplete; 1 negative; 2 benign (solid under 6 mm, GGN under 30 mm) - annual LDCT.`,`3 probably benign (solid 6 to under 8 mm) - LDCT 6 months.`,`4A suspicious (8 to under 15 mm) - LDCT 3 months or PET-CT.`,`4B (15 mm or more, new or growing 8 mm or more) and 4X - diagnostic CT, PET-CT, biopsy.`,`S modifier for significant other findings.`]},{heading:`v2022 changes`,points:[`Airway nodules and atypical cysts added.`,`Stepped management: stable category 3 becomes 2; stable 4A becomes 3.`,`Growth defined as over 1.5 mm increase in mean diameter in 12 months.`]},{heading:`Use and limits`,points:[`Screening population only (USPSTF 2021 eligibility); India has no national screening; TB causes false positives.`]}],mustDraw:[`Lung-RADS category table with management`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition`,marks:1},{part:`Categories`,marks:2.5},{part:`v2022 changes and limits`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`Lung-RADS v2022`,`LDCT`,`category 4A`,`stepped management`,`screening`]}],references:[`MacMahon H et al. Guidelines for Management of Incidental Pulmonary Nodules Detected on CT Images: From the Fleischner Society 2017. Radiology 2017`,`Callister MEJ et al. British Thoracic Society guidelines for the investigation and management of pulmonary nodules. Thorax 2015`,`Gould MK et al. Evaluation of individuals with pulmonary nodules: ACCP evidence-based clinical practice guidelines, 3rd edition. Chest 2013`,`American College of Radiology. Lung-RADS v2022 assessment categories, 2022`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Neoplasms of the lung and approach to the pulmonary nodule`,`Fishman's Pulmonary Diseases and Disorders, 6th edition, 2023`,`US Preventive Services Task Force. Screening for Lung Cancer, 2021`,`IASLC Staging Manual in Thoracic Oncology, TNM 9th edition, 2024`]}),t.push({id:`respiratory-mediastinal-masses`,title:`Mediastinal masses and superior vena cava obstruction`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`A **mediastinal mass** is localised to one of the three **ITMIG (2014) CT compartments** - **prevascular (anterior)**, where the **'4 Ts' (thymoma, teratoma/germ-cell tumour, 'terrible' lymphoma, thyroid)** live; **visceral (middle)**, dominated by **lymph nodes (TB in India), cysts and vascular lesions**; and **paravertebral (posterior)**, dominated by **neurogenic tumours** - and **superior vena cava obstruction**, most often from **lung cancer or lymphoma**, needs a **tissue diagnosis before steroids** unless stridor or cerebral oedema demands **urgent stenting**.`,keywords:[`anterior mediastinal mass`,`ITMIG classification`,`prevascular compartment`,`4 Ts`,`thymoma`,`Masaoka-Koga`,`myasthenia gravis`,`germ cell tumour`,`teratoma`,`seminoma`,`primary mediastinal B-cell lymphoma`,`retrosternal goitre`,`neurogenic tumour`,`bronchogenic cyst`,`SVC syndrome`,`SVCO stenting`,`Pemberton sign`,`hilum overlay sign`,`EBUS-TBNA`,`mediastinoscopy`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`The **mediastinum** is the space between the two pleural sacs, from the **thoracic inlet to the diaphragm** and from the **sternum to the vertebral column**. [Fishman 6e]`,`**ITMIG (International Thymic Malignancy Interest Group, 2014)** divides it on cross-sectional imaging into **prevascular, visceral and paravertebral compartments**, replacing the older lateral-radiograph anterior/middle/posterior scheme. [ITMIG 2014]`,`In adults about **half of mediastinal masses are prevascular (anterior)**; **thymic epithelial tumours, lymphoma, germ-cell tumours and goitre** make up most of them. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Thymoma is the commonest primary anterior mediastinal tumour in adults**, peaking at **40-60 years**; lymphoma and germ-cell tumours dominate in the young (germ-cell tumours mostly in **young men**). [Harrison 22e]`,`In **children**, about a third to a half of mediastinal masses are **neurogenic (posterior)** - neuroblastoma, ganglioneuroblastoma - and lymphoma is next. [Nelson 22e]`,`In India **tuberculous mediastinal lymphadenopathy** is the commonest cause of a middle (visceral) mediastinal mass, and **Pott's paravertebral abscess** and **extramedullary haematopoiesis in thalassaemia** are important posterior mimics. [API Textbook 12e]`,`**SVC obstruction** affects about 15,000 people a year in the US; **malignancy causes about 60-70%** (lung cancer most, then non-Hodgkin lymphoma), and **thrombosis around catheters and pacemaker leads** causes a rising share of the rest. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Compartments and their contents (ITMIG)`,points:[`**Prevascular (anterior)**: thymus, fat, lymph nodes, left brachiocephalic vein - lesions: **thymoma, thymic carcinoma, thymic cyst, germ-cell tumours, lymphoma, retrosternal goitre, parathyroid adenoma, lipoma**. [ITMIG 2014]`,`**Visceral (middle)**: heart, pericardium, aorta and great vessels, SVC, trachea, oesophagus, lymph nodes - lesions: **lymphadenopathy (TB, sarcoid, lymphoma, metastasis), bronchogenic and oesophageal duplication cysts, pericardial cyst, aortic aneurysm, hiatus hernia, oesophageal tumours**. [ITMIG 2014]`,`The **visceral-paravertebral boundary** is a line **1 cm behind the anterior margin of the vertebral bodies**. [ITMIG 2014]`,`**Paravertebral (posterior)**: paravertebral soft tissue and nerves - lesions: **neurogenic tumours** (schwannoma, neurofibroma, ganglioneuroma, neuroblastoma, paraganglioma), **paravertebral (Pott's) abscess**, extramedullary haematopoiesis, lateral meningocele, descending aortic aneurysm. [ITMIG 2014]`,`**Lymphoma** can appear in any compartment, but usually prevascular and visceral. [Harrison 22e]`,`The **'4 Ts' of the anterior mediastinum**: **Thymoma, Teratoma (germ-cell), Terrible lymphoma, Thyroid** - add parathyroid and lipoma for completeness. [OHCM 11e]`,`**Cardiophrenic angle masses**: pericardial cyst (classically right), fat pad, Morgagni hernia, lymph nodes. [Fishman 6e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Symptoms arise from **compression or invasion** of neighbours: trachea (cough, stridor), oesophagus (dysphagia), SVC (venous congestion), recurrent laryngeal nerve (hoarseness), phrenic nerve (raised hemidiaphragm), sympathetic chain (Horner). [Harrison 22e]`,`**SVC physiology**: the thin-walled, low-pressure SVC is easily compressed; venous return diverts through **azygos, internal mammary, lateral thoracic and vertebral collaterals**, which take weeks to develop - so **rapid obstruction (lymphoma, SCLC) causes worse symptoms** than slow benign obstruction. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cerebral and laryngeal oedema** from raised venous pressure, not haemodynamic collapse, are the life-threatening features of SVCO; reduced venous return can lower cardiac output in severe cases. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anterior mediastinal mass and anaesthesia**: general anaesthesia and muscle relaxation lose the tone that splints the airway open and reduce lung volume, so a compressed trachea or heart can **collapse completely on induction** - cardiorespiratory arrest. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Thymoma autoimmunity**: neoplastic thymic epithelium fails to select T cells properly, releasing autoreactive T cells - hence **myasthenia gravis, pure red cell aplasia and hypogammaglobulinaemia (Good syndrome)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Germ-cell tumours** arise from primordial germ cells that went astray during midline migration; **AFP** comes from yolk-sac elements and **beta-hCG** from syncytiotrophoblast. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neurogenic tumours** grow along nerve roots and can extend through the neural foramen (**dumbbell tumour**), compressing the cord. [Fishman 6e]`]},{heading:`Classification and staging examiners ask`,points:[`**WHO 2021 thymic epithelial tumours**: **thymoma types A, AB, B1, B2, B3** (increasing lymphocyte loss and atypia) and **thymic carcinoma** (most often squamous); type A and AB are most indolent. [WHO 2021]`,`**Masaoka-Koga staging**: **I** encapsulated; **IIa** microscopic transcapsular invasion; **IIb** macroscopic invasion into fat; **III** invasion of neighbouring organs (pericardium, lung, great vessels); **IVa** pleural or pericardial dissemination; **IVb** lymphatic or haematogenous metastasis. [Masaoka-Koga 1994]`,`**TNM staging for thymic tumours (IASLC/ITMIG)** is now used alongside Masaoka-Koga, staging by depth of invasion rather than size. [IASLC TNM 9]`,`**Germ-cell tumours**: **mature teratoma** (benign; fat, fluid, calcium, teeth), **seminoma** (AFP normal, beta-hCG normal or mildly raised) and **non-seminomatous GCT** (yolk sac, embryonal, choriocarcinoma, mixed; **AFP and/or beta-hCG high**). [Harrison 22e]`,`**A raised AFP excludes a pure seminoma** - the tumour is treated as non-seminomatous. [Harrison 22e]`,`**SVCO severity (Yu 2008)**: **0** radiological only; **1** mild oedema/plethora; **2** moderate with functional impairment; **3** severe - mild cerebral or laryngeal oedema, syncope on bending; **4** life-threatening - confusion, **stridor**, haemodynamic compromise; **5** fatal. [J Thorac Oncol 2008 (Yu)]`,`**Myasthenia gravis in thymoma**: **30-50% of thymoma patients have MG**, and **about 10-15% of MG patients have a thymoma**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`About **half are asymptomatic**, found on a CXR or CT done for another reason; symptoms suggest malignancy. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Local symptoms**: cough, chest pain or pressure, breathlessness, **orthopnoea or breathlessness lying flat (airway or cardiac compression)**, dysphagia, hoarseness. [Harrison 22e]`,`**SVCO symptoms**: facial and neck swelling, **puffiness worse in the morning and on bending**, headache, arm swelling, a tight collar, breathlessness. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Systemic symptoms**: fever, night sweats, weight loss over 10% (**B symptoms** - lymphoma, TB), itching (Hodgkin lymphoma), **alcohol-induced pain** in nodes (Hodgkin). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Myasthenic symptoms**: **fatigable ptosis, diplopia**, dysphagia, nasal speech, proximal weakness worse in the evening - point to thymoma. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Thyroid**: long-standing neck swelling extending retrosternally, symptoms of hyper- or hypothyroidism. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Young man with chest mass**: ask about testicular lumps and gynaecomastia (beta-hCG); **Klinefelter syndrome** carries higher risk of mediastinal GCT. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Back pain with fever and weight loss** - Pott's spine with paravertebral abscess; **thalassaemia history** - extramedullary haematopoiesis. [API Textbook 12e]`,`**Indwelling device**: central line, chemotherapy port, dialysis catheter or pacemaker - catheter-related SVC thrombosis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**SVCO signs**: facial plethora and oedema, **distended non-pulsatile neck veins**, dilated veins over the chest wall with **downward flow**, arm oedema, cyanosis, conjunctival suffusion. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pemberton sign**: raising both arms above the head for about a minute causes facial plethora, distended neck veins or stridor - **retrosternal goitre or SVCO**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Stridor, tracheal deviation**, positional breathlessness - airway compression; record **SpO2 lying and sitting**. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Lymph nodes**: cervical, supraclavicular, axillary - an easy biopsy site for lymphoma or TB. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Testes** in every young man with an anterior mediastinal mass; **thyroid** for a cervical component. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neurology**: fatigable ptosis (ice-pack test), Horner syndrome, **spinal cord signs** with dumbbell tumours or Pott's spine. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Others**: hepatosplenomegaly (lymphoma), gibbus (Pott's), pallor and frontal bossing (thalassaemia), pulsus paradoxus (pericardial effusion). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Anterior mass, 40-60 years, myasthenia** - thymoma; **young woman, B symptoms, SVCO** - Hodgkin or primary mediastinal B-cell lymphoma; **young man, high AFP/beta-hCG** - germ-cell tumour; **neck goitre extending down** - retrosternal thyroid. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Adolescent with rapidly enlarging mass, stridor, high LDH, pleural effusion** - **T-lymphoblastic lymphoma** (an oncological emergency). [Nelson 22e]`,`**Middle mass with necrotic nodes (central low attenuation, rim enhancement)** - **tuberculosis** in India; bilateral symmetrical hilar nodes with paratracheal nodes - **sarcoidosis**. [API Textbook 12e]`,`**Smooth water-density cyst** near the carina - bronchogenic cyst; at the right cardiophrenic angle - pericardial cyst. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Posterior mass with rib or foramen widening** - neurogenic tumour; **with vertebral destruction and disc loss** - Pott's spine; **bilateral paravertebral soft-tissue masses in thalassaemia** - extramedullary haematopoiesis. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Vascular lesions** (aortic aneurysm, dilated SVC or azygos) must be excluded with contrast CT **before any needle goes in**. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Pseudo-masses**: hiatus hernia (air-fluid level behind the heart), tortuous aorta, thymic rebound after chemotherapy, normal thymus in young people. [Fishman 6e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Chest X-ray PA and lateral**: widened mediastinum; **hilum overlay sign** (hilar vessels seen through the mass - not hilar, so anterior or posterior); **cervicothoracic sign** (a mass whose upper border is sharp above the clavicle is posterior). [Fishman 6e]`,`**Contrast CT chest** is the key test: compartment, fat, calcium, fluid, enhancement, invasion, vessels and nodes; **CT tells which compartment and therefore the differential**. [ITMIG 2014]`,`**MRI** separates cystic from solid lesions, shows **chemical shift (fat suppression) in normal thymus or thymic hyperplasia**, and shows **intraspinal extension** of neurogenic tumours. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Serum markers in every anterior mass in a young man**: **AFP, beta-hCG and LDH** - markedly raised values (AFP over 500 ng/mL or very high beta-hCG) can confirm non-seminomatous GCT without biopsy in an urgent setting. [NCCN 2026]`,`**Other bloods**: CBC (PRCA, lymphoma), **LDH** (lymphoma), **TSH and free T4**, calcium and PTH (parathyroid), **acetylcholine-receptor antibodies**, immunoglobulins (Good syndrome), HIV. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PET-CT** stages lymphoma and helps choose a biopsy target; thymomas show variable uptake, thymic carcinoma higher. [Lugano 2014]`,`**Testicular ultrasound** in germ-cell tumour to exclude a gonadal primary. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Echocardiography** for pericardial effusion and cardiac compression; **spirometry flow-volume loop** for fixed upper-airway obstruction (see the Pulmonary function tests topic). [Fishman 6e]`]},{heading:`Tissue diagnosis: choosing the route`,points:[`**Do not biopsy a resectable mass that looks like a thymoma** on CT - complete surgical resection is both diagnostic and curative, and transcapsular biopsy risks seeding. [NCCN Thymic 2026]`,`**Lymphoma needs a core or excision biopsy** - FNA cytology is not enough for subtype and architecture; take the most accessible peripheral node first. [Lugano 2014]`,`**CT-guided core needle biopsy** is the usual route for an anterior mass; **EBUS-TBNA (with NAAT for TB) and EUS-FNA** for visceral compartment nodes. [ACCP 2013]`,`**Cervical mediastinoscopy** reaches paratracheal and subcarinal nodes; **anterior mediastinotomy (Chamberlain procedure)** reaches the aortopulmonary window and anterior masses; **VATS** gives larger samples. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Pleural fluid** cytology, flow cytometry and ADA, or **bone marrow** biopsy can give the diagnosis without touching the mediastinum. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anaesthesia safety**: in anterior masses with **orthopnoea, stridor, tracheal narrowing over 50% on CT, or pericardial effusion**, biopsy under **local anaesthesia**, keep **spontaneous ventilation**, avoid muscle relaxants, and have **rigid bronchoscopy and cardiopulmonary bypass/ECMO** standby. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Steroids before biopsy** can shrink lymphoma within 24-48 hours and make histology non-diagnostic - give them first only for a true life threat. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management of the main mediastinal tumours`,points:[`**Thymoma**: **complete surgical resection (thymectomy with all thymic tissue and fat) via median sternotomy or minimally invasive approach** is the cornerstone; **postoperative radiotherapy** for incomplete resection or stage III; **cisplatin-based chemotherapy (CAP: cisplatin, doxorubicin, cyclophosphamide)** for unresectable or stage IV disease, often to make it operable. [NCCN Thymic 2026]`,`**Before thymectomy, stabilise myasthenia** (pyridostigmine, steroids, IVIG or plasma exchange) to avoid **postoperative myasthenic crisis**. [MGFA 2020]`,`**Thymectomy for non-thymomatous generalised AChR-positive MG** (age 18-65) improves outcomes and reduces steroid need - the **MGTX trial**. [MGFA 2020]`,`**Seminoma**: very radiosensitive and chemosensitive - **BEP (bleomycin, etoposide, cisplatin) for 3-4 cycles** or EP; cure rates around 90%. [NCCN 2026]`,`**Non-seminomatous GCT**: **BEP for 4 cycles** then **surgical resection of residual mass** once markers normalise; prognosis is poorer than gonadal primaries. [NCCN 2026]`,`**Mature teratoma**: surgical excision (chemotherapy does not work). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hodgkin lymphoma**: **ABVD-based chemotherapy, PET-adapted**, with or without involved-site radiotherapy; **primary mediastinal B-cell lymphoma**: **DA-EPOCH-R** or R-CHOP plus radiotherapy. [NCCN 2026]`,`**Retrosternal goitre**: usually removed through a **cervical incision**; obstructive symptoms are the indication; radioiodine is a choice in unfit patients. [ATA 2016]`,`**Neurogenic tumours**: surgical excision (VATS for most); dumbbell tumours need a combined neurosurgical and thoracic approach; **neuroblastoma** treatment is risk-stratified. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Tuberculous lymphadenitis**: the standard **NTEP 2HRZE/4HRE** regimen; nodes may enlarge paradoxically during treatment and are not a reason to change drugs (see the Extrapulmonary TB topic). [NTEP 2023]`]},{heading:`SVC obstruction: emergency and ward management`,points:[`**Assess severity (Yu grade)**: stridor, confusion or haemodynamic compromise (**grade 4**) means **emergency endovascular stenting** first, histology later. [J Thorac Oncol 2008 (Yu)]`,`**General measures**: sit up with the **head elevated**, oxygen, avoid IV lines in the arms, and avoid over-hydration. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Endovascular stenting** relieves symptoms within **24-72 hours in about 90-95%**; it does not disturb histology and is now often used first in severe or recurrent cases. [CIRSE 2022]`,`**Treat the cause**: chemotherapy for **SCLC, lymphoma and germ-cell tumours** (often resolves SVCO within 1-2 weeks); **radiotherapy** for NSCLC and other less chemosensitive tumours. [NCCN 2026]`,`**Steroids** (dexamethasone 4 mg every 6 hours) are useful for **steroid-responsive tumours (lymphoma, thymoma)** and to cover radiotherapy-related oedema - but **only after biopsy** unless the airway is threatened. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diuretics** give at most modest short-term relief and are not routinely needed. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Catheter-related thrombosis**: anticoagulation with **LMWH (enoxaparin 1 mg/kg twice daily)** then warfarin or a DOAC for **at least 3 months**; **remove the catheter** if not needed or not working; **catheter-directed thrombolysis** for severe acute cases. [ACCP 2016]`,`**Monitor** symptoms, SpO2, neurological status and after stenting give antiplatelet or anticoagulant therapy as per the interventional team. [CIRSE 2022]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Children**: anterior mediastinal masses (especially T-lymphoblastic lymphoma) carry the highest anaesthetic risk; diagnose from **peripheral blood, bone marrow or pleural fluid** where possible. [Nelson 22e]`,`**Pregnancy**: lymphoma and germ-cell tumours occur in young women; MRI without contrast is preferred; anaesthesia risks apply as above. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Thalassaemia**: paravertebral extramedullary haematopoiesis needs no biopsy if typical; hypertransfusion or radiotherapy if it compresses the cord. [API Textbook 12e]`,`**Elderly**: lung cancer metastatic nodes and goitre dominate; thymoma surgery is still worthwhile if fit. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HIV**: TB, lymphoma (including primary effusion and plasmablastic) and Kaposi sarcoma cause mediastinal nodes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fibrosing mediastinitis** (after TB or histoplasmosis) can cause slowly progressive SVCO and pulmonary artery or vein stenosis; stenting is the main option. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Acute mediastinitis**: oesophageal perforation (Boerhaave), post-sternotomy infection, or descending necrotising infection from the neck (odontogenic) - fever, chest pain, **Hamman crunch**; broad-spectrum antibiotics and surgical drainage. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: CXR, contrast CT if possible, FNA of a peripheral node with NAAT, AFP/beta-hCG; **refer early** if stridor or orthopnoea - do not sedate or attempt GA locally. [ICMR STW]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Airway or cardiovascular collapse under anaesthesia** with an anterior mass - the most avoidable death. [Fishman 6e]`,`**SVCO complications**: cerebral oedema, laryngeal oedema, thrombosis; SVCO itself rarely kills - **prognosis depends on the cause**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Thymoma**: 10-year survival is about **90% for Masaoka I-II** and falls in stages III-IV; **late recurrences** (over 10 years) occur, so follow-up is long. [NCCN Thymic 2026]`,`**Thymic carcinoma** is aggressive with 5-year survival about **50%** or lower. [NCCN Thymic 2026]`,`**Myasthenic crisis** (respiratory failure) after thymectomy or with infection; **PRCA and Good syndrome** may persist after thymoma removal. [MGFA 2020]`,`**Mediastinal NSGCT** has worse survival than testicular GCT (about 40-50% long-term); seminoma about 90%. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bleomycin lung toxicity** after BEP - avoid high inspired oxygen during later surgery. [NCCN 2026]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer the same day** for stridor, orthopnoea, syncope on bending, confusion or rapidly progressive facial swelling. [Fishman 6e]`,`**Refer urgently** (within 2 weeks) to a thoracic or oncology centre any new mediastinal mass; **young men with markers raised** go to oncology directly. [NICE NG12]`,`**After thymoma resection**: **CT chest every 6 months for 2 years, then yearly for at least 10 years** (5 years then alternate years in some protocols). [NCCN Thymic 2026]`,`**After germ-cell tumour treatment**: AFP, beta-hCG, LDH and CT at set intervals; long-term watch for cardiovascular disease and second cancers. [NCCN 2026]`,`**After SVC stenting**: clinical review for re-occlusion (about 10-20% need re-intervention). [CIRSE 2022]`,`**Myasthenia** follow-up with a neurologist; avoid drugs that worsen MG (aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones, magnesium, beta-blockers). [MGFA 2020]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2021-2026)`,points:[`**WHO 2021 (5th edition) thoracic tumours** keeps thymoma types A, AB, B1, B2, B3 and expands thymic carcinoma subtypes. [WHO 2021]`,`**NCCN Thymomas and Thymic Carcinomas 2026**: avoid biopsy of resectable thymoma; minimally invasive thymectomy acceptable in experienced centres; CAP for advanced disease. [NCCN Thymic 2026]`,`**MG therapy expansion (2021-2024)**: complement inhibitors (**eculizumab, ravulizumab, zilucoplan**) and FcRn blockers (**efgartigimod, rozanolixizumab**) approved for generalised AChR-positive MG; thymectomy remains recommended (MGFA 2020 update). [MGFA 2020]`,`**CIRSE 2022 and interventional practice**: endovascular SVC stenting is increasingly first-line for severe malignant SVCO, rather than reserved for radiotherapy failure. [CIRSE 2022]`,`**Lymphoma**: PET-adapted therapy (Lugano) and newer regimens (e.g. **brentuximab-AVD** or nivolumab-AVD for advanced Hodgkin) shape treatment of mediastinal lymphoma. [NCCN 2026]`,`**ITMIG 2014 CT compartments** are the reporting standard used in current radiology and surgical guidelines. [ITMIG 2014]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Viva: **'4 Ts of the anterior mediastinum?'** - thymoma, teratoma, terrible lymphoma, thyroid. [OHCM 11e]`,`Viva: **Commonest posterior mediastinal mass?** - neurogenic tumour; in India always consider **Pott's abscess**. [Fishman 6e]`,`Viva: **Which tumour markers in a young man with an anterior mass?** - AFP, beta-hCG, LDH; raised AFP rules out pure seminoma. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: **Hilum overlay sign** - hilar vessels visible through a mass mean the mass is not hilar. [Fishman 6e]`,`Viva: **Why biopsy before steroids in SVCO?** - lymphoma melts and the diagnosis is lost. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: **sending a patient with an anterior mass and orthopnoea for GA biopsy** without an anaesthetic plan. [Fishman 6e]`,`Common mistake: **needle biopsy of a resectable thymoma**, or FNA alone for lymphoma. [NCCN Thymic 2026]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs used in mediastinal masses and SVCO`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Dexamethasone`,`4 mg IV/oral every 6 hours (16 mg/day)`,`Short course, taper; only after biopsy unless life-threatening`,`Hyperglycaemia, insomnia, infection; spoils lymphoma histology`],[`Enoxaparin (catheter-related SVC thrombosis)`,`1 mg/kg SC twice daily (once daily if CrCl under 30)`,`Bridge to warfarin/DOAC; at least 3 months`,`Bleeding, HIT; avoid with active haemorrhage`],[`Pyridostigmine (myasthenia)`,`30-60 mg orally every 4-6 hours`,`Titrate to effect; usual max about 480 mg/day`,`Cholinergic effects: diarrhoea, cramps, salivation, bradycardia`],[`IVIG (myasthenic crisis, pre-thymectomy)`,`2 g/kg IV over 2-5 days`,`Single course`,`Headache, renal injury, thrombosis; check IgA`],[`BEP (germ-cell tumours)`,`Bleomycin 30 units weekly, etoposide 100 mg/m2 days 1-5, cisplatin 20 mg/m2 days 1-5, every 21 days`,`3-4 cycles`,`Bleomycin lung toxicity, nephro- and ototoxicity, neutropenia`],[`CAP (thymoma)`,`Cisplatin 50 mg/m2, doxorubicin 50 mg/m2, cyclophosphamide 500 mg/m2 IV every 3 weeks`,`4-6 cycles`,`Cardiotoxicity (doxorubicin), myelosuppression, nephrotoxicity`],[`ABVD (Hodgkin lymphoma)`,`Doxorubicin 25 mg/m2, bleomycin 10 units/m2, vinblastine 6 mg/m2, dacarbazine 375 mg/m2 IV days 1 and 15, every 28 days`,`2-6 cycles, PET-adapted`,`Bleomycin lung, cardiotoxicity, neuropathy, infertility (low)`],[`Antitubercular (HRZE)`,`Weight-band FDCs per NTEP`,`2 months HRZE, then 4 months HRE (longer for some sites)`,`Hepatitis, optic neuritis (E), gout (Z); rifampicin interactions`]]},{heading:`Mediastinal masses by ITMIG compartment`,columns:[`Compartment`,`Boundaries / contents`,`Common lesions`,`Clue`],rows:[[`Prevascular (anterior)`,`Sternum to anterior pericardium; thymus, fat, nodes`,`Thymoma, lymphoma, germ-cell tumour, retrosternal goitre, thymic cyst`,`4 Ts; hilum overlay sign`],[`Visceral (middle)`,`Heart, great vessels, trachea, oesophagus, nodes`,`Lymphadenopathy (TB, sarcoid, lymphoma, metastases), duplication cysts, aneurysm, hiatus hernia`,`Necrotic nodes = TB in India`],[`Paravertebral (posterior)`,`Behind a line 1 cm posterior to vertebral body front`,`Neurogenic tumours, Pott's abscess, extramedullary haematopoiesis, meningocele`,`Foraminal widening, vertebral destruction`]]},{heading:`Differential of an anterior mediastinal mass`,columns:[`Lesion`,`Typical patient`,`CT and lab clues`,`Management`],rows:[[`Thymoma`,`40-60 years, myasthenia`,`Smooth lobulated soft tissue; AChR antibodies`,`Resection without prior biopsy if resectable`],[`Thymic carcinoma`,`Middle age`,`Invasive, irregular, nodes, metastases`,`Biopsy, multimodality therapy`],[`Hodgkin / PMBCL`,`Young adult, often woman, B symptoms`,`Bulky mass, nodes elsewhere, high LDH`,`Core/excision biopsy; chemotherapy`],[`Lymphoblastic lymphoma`,`Adolescent`,`Rapid growth, effusion, high LDH`,`Diagnose from blood/marrow/fluid; urgent therapy`],[`Mature teratoma`,`Young adult`,`Fat, fluid, calcium, teeth`,`Excision`],[`Seminoma / NSGCT`,`Young man`,`Seminoma: AFP normal; NSGCT: AFP and/or beta-hCG high`,`BEP chemotherapy; resect residual NSGCT`],[`Retrosternal goitre`,`Older woman`,`Continuous with cervical thyroid, calcification, high attenuation`,`Thyroidectomy via neck`]]},{heading:`SVC obstruction severity (Yu 2008) and action`,columns:[`Grade`,`Features`,`Action`],rows:[[`0`,`Radiological obstruction only`,`Diagnose and treat the cause`],[`1 Mild`,`Head and neck oedema, plethora`,`Biopsy first, treat cause`],[`2 Moderate`,`Oedema with functional impairment (mild dysphagia, cough, visual or movement difficulty)`,`Biopsy first, treat cause`],[`3 Severe`,`Mild cerebral or laryngeal oedema, syncope on bending`,`Expedite biopsy; consider stent`],[`4 Life-threatening`,`Confusion, stridor, haemodynamic compromise`,`Emergency stent (plus steroids, radiotherapy as needed)`],[`5 Fatal`,`Death`,`-`]]},{heading:`Investigations and their purpose`,columns:[`Test`,`Purpose`,`Key point`],rows:[[`CXR PA and lateral`,`Detect and localise`,`Hilum overlay and cervicothoracic signs`],[`Contrast CT chest`,`Compartment, composition, invasion, vessels`,`Mandatory before any biopsy`],[`MRI`,`Cyst vs solid, thymic hyperplasia, spinal extension`,`Chemical-shift imaging`],[`AFP, beta-hCG, LDH`,`Germ-cell tumour, lymphoma`,`Raised AFP excludes pure seminoma`],[`AChR antibodies, CBC, immunoglobulins`,`Thymoma syndromes`,`MG, PRCA, Good syndrome`],[`PET-CT`,`Lymphoma staging, biopsy target`,`TB nodes are PET-avid too`],[`EBUS-TBNA / mediastinoscopy / CT-guided core`,`Tissue diagnosis`,`Core or excision for lymphoma; NAAT for TB`]]}],redFlags:[`Stridor or breathlessness lying flat with an anterior mediastinal mass`,`Confusion, headache and facial swelling (SVCO with cerebral oedema)`,`Syncope on bending or hypotension with a mediastinal mass`,`Rapidly enlarging mass in a child or adolescent (lymphoblastic lymphoma)`,`Back pain with leg weakness and a paravertebral mass (cord compression)`,`Myasthenic weakness with breathing or swallowing difficulty (crisis)`,`Fever, chest pain and crepitus after vomiting or neck infection (acute mediastinitis)`],pearls:[`Anterior mediastinum = 4 Ts: thymoma, teratoma, terrible lymphoma, thyroid.`,`Commonest posterior mass = neurogenic tumour; in India think Pott's abscess too.`,`Hilum overlay sign: hilar vessels seen through the mass - it is not hilar.`,`Raised AFP excludes pure seminoma.`,`30-50% of thymomas have MG; 10-15% of MG patients have a thymoma.`,`Resectable thymoma: resect, do not needle.`,`No steroids before biopsy in SVCO unless the airway is threatened.`,`Stridor in SVCO = stent first, histology later.`,`Anterior mass plus orthopnoea = no general anaesthesia without an airway plan.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-mediastinal-masses-q1`,stem:`A 23-year-old man has a large anterior mediastinal mass. Serum AFP is 2,400 ng/mL and beta-hCG is mildly raised. The most likely diagnosis is:`,options:[`Pure seminoma`,`Thymoma`,`Non-seminomatous germ-cell tumour`,`Mature teratoma`],answer:2,explanation:`A markedly raised AFP in a young man with an anterior mass indicates a non-seminomatous germ-cell tumour (yolk-sac elements make AFP). A raised AFP excludes pure seminoma, mature teratoma does not raise markers, and thymoma occurs mostly at 40-60 years without germ-cell markers.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-mediastinal-masses-q2`,stem:`A 50-year-old woman with fatigable ptosis and diplopia has a 5 cm well-circumscribed anterior mediastinal mass with no invasion on CT. What is the best next step after stabilising the myasthenia?`,options:[`Complete surgical resection (thymectomy)`,`CT-guided core biopsy before deciding`,`Radiotherapy alone`,`Pyridostigmine alone and yearly CT`],answer:0,explanation:`A resectable mass typical of thymoma in a patient with myasthenia should be resected completely without preoperative needle biopsy, which risks breaching the capsule and seeding. Radiotherapy is used after incomplete resection or for unresectable disease, and observation leaves a potentially invasive tumour untreated.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-mediastinal-masses-q3`,stem:`A 30-year-old man has facial swelling, distended neck veins and a large anterior mediastinal mass. He is breathing comfortably with no stridor or confusion. Which action should be avoided before tissue is obtained?`,options:[`Elevating the head of the bed`,`Measuring AFP, beta-hCG and LDH`,`Contrast CT of the chest`,`Starting high-dose dexamethasone`],answer:3,explanation:`In a stable patient, steroids can shrink a lymphoma within a day or two and make the biopsy non-diagnostic, so they are withheld until tissue is taken. Head elevation is a harmless supportive measure, contrast CT is essential to plan the biopsy, and tumour markers may give a diagnosis without biopsy.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-mediastinal-masses-q4`,stem:`On a frontal chest X-ray, the hilar vessels are clearly visible through a large mediastinal opacity. This is the:`,options:[`Cervicothoracic sign, showing a posterior mass`,`Hilum overlay sign, showing the mass is not arising from the hilum`,`Silhouette sign, showing the mass touches the heart border`,`Air-crescent sign, showing a fungal ball`],answer:1,explanation:`If hilar vessels are seen through the opacity, the mass lies in front of or behind the hilum rather than in it - the hilum overlay sign, typical of anterior mediastinal masses. The cervicothoracic sign concerns a mass above the clavicles, the silhouette sign concerns loss of a border, and the air-crescent sign is seen with aspergilloma.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`respiratory-mediastinal-masses-q5`,stem:`The commonest posterior (paravertebral) mediastinal mass in adults and children overall is a:`,options:[`Bronchogenic cyst`,`Thymoma`,`Neurogenic tumour`,`Pericardial cyst`],answer:2,explanation:`Neurogenic tumours (schwannoma and neurofibroma in adults, neuroblastoma and ganglioneuroma in children) dominate the paravertebral compartment. Thymoma is prevascular, bronchogenic cysts are usually visceral near the carina, and pericardial cysts lie at the cardiophrenic angle.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-mediastinal-masses-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Classify mediastinal masses by compartment. Discuss the clinical evaluation and investigation of an anterior mediastinal mass, and outline the management of thymoma.`,openingLines:[`Mediastinal masses are classified by the ITMIG (2014) CT-based compartments into prevascular, visceral and paravertebral, each with its own differential diagnosis.`,`In adults, half are prevascular, where the 4 Ts - thymoma, teratoma (germ-cell), terrible lymphoma and thyroid - predominate.`],answer:[{heading:`Classification (ITMIG)`,points:[`Prevascular: thymoma, thymic carcinoma, lymphoma, germ-cell tumours, goitre, thymic cyst.`,`Visceral: lymph nodes (TB, sarcoid, lymphoma, metastases), duplication cysts, aneurysm, hiatus hernia.`,`Paravertebral: neurogenic tumours, Pott's abscess, extramedullary haematopoiesis.`]},{heading:`Clinical evaluation`,points:[`Asymptomatic in half; compressive symptoms - cough, stridor, orthopnoea, dysphagia, hoarseness, SVCO.`,`Systemic: B symptoms, myasthenia, PRCA, testicular mass; examine nodes, testes, thyroid, Pemberton sign.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`CXR (hilum overlay sign), contrast CT, MRI, PET-CT.`,`AFP, beta-hCG, LDH; AChR antibodies; TFT; CBC.`,`Tissue: CT-guided core, mediastinoscopy, Chamberlain, VATS; core/excision for lymphoma; no biopsy of resectable thymoma.`,`Anaesthesia risk: orthopnoea or tracheal narrowing over 50% - local anaesthesia, spontaneous breathing.`]},{heading:`Thymoma - classification and staging`,points:[`WHO types A, AB, B1, B2, B3; thymic carcinoma separate.`,`Masaoka-Koga I-IVb; TNM by invasion.`,`Associations: MG (30-50%), PRCA, Good syndrome.`]},{heading:`Thymoma - management`,points:[`Stabilise MG; complete thymectomy (sternotomy or minimally invasive).`,`Postoperative radiotherapy for stage III or incomplete resection.`,`CAP chemotherapy for unresectable or metastatic disease, then reassess for surgery.`,`Follow-up CT 6-monthly for 2 years then yearly for 10 years.`]}],mustDraw:[`Lateral chest diagram showing ITMIG compartments with typical lesions`,`Flow chart: anterior mediastinal mass - markers, CT features, resect versus biopsy`],markSplit:[{part:`Classification`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical evaluation`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations`,marks:3},{part:`Thymoma management`,marks:3}],keywords:[`ITMIG`,`4 Ts`,`thymoma`,`Masaoka-Koga`,`myasthenia gravis`,`AFP`,`beta-hCG`,`hilum overlay sign`]},{id:`respiratory-mediastinal-masses-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on superior vena cava syndrome.`,openingLines:[`Superior vena cava syndrome is the set of symptoms and signs caused by obstruction of blood flow through the SVC, from external compression, invasion or thrombosis.`,`Malignancy - mainly lung cancer and lymphoma - causes about 60-70%, and catheter-related thrombosis most of the rest.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes`,points:[`Malignant: NSCLC, SCLC, non-Hodgkin lymphoma, germ-cell tumour, metastatic nodes, thymoma.`,`Benign: catheter or pacemaker-lead thrombosis, fibrosing mediastinitis (TB), retrosternal goitre, aortic aneurysm.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Facial and arm swelling, headache worse on bending, breathlessness.`,`Non-pulsatile distended neck veins, chest wall collaterals, plethora, Pemberton sign.`,`Yu grading 0-5; stridor or confusion = grade 4.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Contrast CT chest (level, cause, collaterals); tissue diagnosis by easiest route; markers in young men.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Head elevation, oxygen; biopsy before steroids.`,`Grade 4: emergency stenting.`,`Chemotherapy for SCLC, lymphoma, GCT; radiotherapy for NSCLC.`,`Catheter thrombosis: anticoagulation 3 months, remove catheter, thrombolysis if severe.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of SVC with azygos and chest wall collateral pathways`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and causes`,marks:1.5},{part:`Clinical features and grading`,marks:1.5},{part:`Investigations and management`,marks:2}],keywords:[`SVC syndrome`,`Yu grading`,`stent`,`lymphoma`,`biopsy before steroids`,`Pemberton sign`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Disorders of the mediastinum; Oncologic emergencies`,`Fishman's Pulmonary Diseases and Disorders, 6th edition, 2023 - Mediastinal masses`,`Carter BW et al. ITMIG Definition of Mediastinal Compartments on CT. J Thorac Oncol 2014`,`NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines: Thymomas and Thymic Carcinomas, 2026`,`Yu JB, Wilson LD, Detterbeck FC. Superior vena cava syndrome - a proposed classification system and algorithm for management. J Thorac Oncol 2008`,`Narayanaswami P et al. International Consensus Guidance for Management of Myasthenia Gravis: 2020 Update. Neurology 2021`,`WHO Classification of Thoracic Tumours, 5th edition, 2021`,`API Textbook of Medicine, 12th edition, 2022`]});var n=[];n.push({id:`respiratory-acute-severe-asthma`,title:`Acute severe asthma: emergency and inpatient management`,oneLiner:`**Acute severe asthma** is an exacerbation with any one of **PEF 33-50% of best or predicted, respiratory rate 25/min or more, heart rate 110/min or more, or inability to complete sentences in one breath**; it is treated with **controlled oxygen (SpO2 93-95%), repeated inhaled salbutamol with ipratropium, systemic corticosteroid within the first hour and IV magnesium** if the response is poor, while a **normal or rising PaCO2, silent chest or exhaustion** marks a life-threatening attack that needs ICU.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`status asthmaticus`,`acute asthma`,`asthma exacerbation`,`life-threatening asthma`,`near-fatal asthma`,`silent chest`,`pulsus paradoxus`,`salbutamol nebulisation`,`ipratropium bromide`,`magnesium sulphate`,`aminophylline`,`hydrocortisone`,`prednisolone`,`PEF`,`ABG`,`ketamine intubation`,`permissive hypercapnia`,`auto-PEEP`,`GINA 2025`,`BTS/SIGN`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`An **asthma exacerbation** is an acute or subacute worsening of symptoms and lung function from the patient's usual status, sometimes the first presentation of asthma; 'status asthmaticus' is the old term for a severe attack not responding to initial bronchodilators. [GINA 2025]`,`**Acute severe asthma** (BTS/SIGN) is any one of: **PEF 33-50%** of best or predicted, **respiratory rate 25/min or more**, **heart rate 110/min or more**, or **inability to complete sentences in one breath**. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`**Life-threatening asthma** is a severe attack plus any one of PEF under 33%, **SpO2 under 92%**, PaO2 under 8 kPa, a **'normal' PaCO2 (4.6-6.0 kPa)**, silent chest, cyanosis, poor respiratory effort, arrhythmia, exhaustion, altered consciousness or hypotension. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`**Near-fatal asthma** is a **raised PaCO2** and/or the need for mechanical ventilation with raised inflation pressures. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`Globally asthma affects about **260 million people and causes about 450,000 deaths a year**, most of them preventable and most in low- and middle-income countries. [GBD 2019]`,`India has about **13% of the world's asthma but over 40% of global asthma deaths** - mortality driven by under-use of ICS, reliever-only treatment and late presentation. [GBD India 2018]`,`The **INSEARCH study** found adult asthma prevalence of about **2%** in India (about 17-18 million adults), with higher rates in smokers, biomass users and the elderly. [INSEARCH 2012]`,`About **15-20% of asthma deaths** and 30-40% of emergency visits occur in patients labelled as having 'mild' asthma - severity of the usual disease does not predict severity of the attack. [GINA 2025]`]},{heading:`Triggers and risk factors for a fatal attack`,points:[`**Viral respiratory infection** (rhinovirus most often, also influenza, RSV, SARS-CoV-2) triggers about 80% of adult and child exacerbations. [GINA 2025]`,`Other triggers: **allergen exposure, air pollution (winter smog, Diwali fireworks, biomass smoke), cold air, NSAIDs or aspirin in AERD, beta-blockers including timolol eye drops, stress and pregnancy**. [GINA 2025]`,`The commonest modifiable cause is **poor adherence to ICS or no ICS at all**, often with reliance on oral salbutamol or theophylline syrups in Indian practice. [GINA 2025]`,`**Risk factors for asthma-related death**: past **near-fatal asthma needing intubation or ICU**, hospital or emergency visit in the past year, current or recent oral steroid use, **not using ICS**, and **using more than one SABA canister a month**. [GINA 2025]`,`Further death risks: **psychiatric illness or psychosocial problems**, poor adherence, no written action plan, **food allergy**, and comorbidity such as pneumonia, diabetes or arrhythmia. [GINA 2025]`,`**Brittle (type 2) asthma** - sudden falls in PEF from apparently good control within minutes to hours - and **aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease** carry a high risk of sudden fatal attacks. [Harrison 22e]`,`Patients with **poor perception of breathlessness** (elderly, long-standing disease, previous near-fatal attack) present late with severe obstruction and few complaints. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why the attack kills`,points:[`Airway narrowing comes from **smooth-muscle spasm, mucosal oedema and thick eosinophilic mucus plugs** - spasm reverses with beta2-agonists within minutes, but oedema and plugs need corticosteroids over hours to days. [Harrison 22e]`,`Narrowed airways close early in expiration, so the next breath starts before the lung empties - **dynamic hyperinflation with intrinsic PEEP (auto-PEEP)** - flattening the diaphragm and greatly raising the work of breathing. [Harrison 22e]`,`**V/Q mismatch** in plugged units causes hypoxaemia, while the drive to breathe causes hyperventilation, so the early ABG shows **hypoxaemia with a low PaCO2 (respiratory alkalosis)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`As the patient tires, alveolar ventilation falls and PaCO2 climbs back through the normal range - a **'normal' PaCO2 in a breathless asthmatic means fatigue** and impending respiratory arrest, and a raised PaCO2 means near-fatal asthma. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`**Pulsus paradoxus** (inspiratory fall in systolic BP over 10 mmHg, often over 25 mmHg in severe attacks) occurs because large negative intrathoracic pressures increase LV afterload and the hyperinflated lung and septal shift reduce LV filling in inspiration. [Harrison 22e]`,`Hyperinflation and auto-PEEP reduce venous return, so **positive-pressure ventilation can cause hypotension and cardiac arrest** soon after intubation unless the expiratory time is long. [Harrison 22e]`,`A **silent chest** means so little airflow that no wheeze is generated - it signals worsening, not improvement. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`**High-concentration oxygen** can raise PaCO2 in severe asthma by releasing hypoxic vasoconstriction and worsening V/Q mismatch, which is why a target range (not maximum oxygen) is used. [GINA 2025]`,`High-dose beta2-agonists cause **hypokalaemia (intracellular potassium shift), hyperglycaemia and type B lactic acidosis**, which can drive tachypnoea and be mistaken for worsening asthma. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification of severity - the one examiners ask`,points:[`**Moderate exacerbation**: increasing symptoms, **PEF over 50-75%** of best or predicted, no features of acute severe asthma. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`**Acute severe**: any one of **PEF 33-50%, RR 25/min or more, HR 110/min or more, cannot complete sentences in one breath**. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`**Life-threatening** (remember **33, 92, CHEST**): PEF under 33%, SpO2 under 92%, PaO2 under 8 kPa, normal PaCO2, Cyanosis, Hypotension, Exhaustion, Silent chest, Tachy- or bradyarrhythmia, plus poor effort and altered consciousness. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`**Near-fatal**: raised PaCO2 and/or needing mechanical ventilation with raised inflation pressures. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`**GINA grading** is simpler: **mild-moderate** (talks in phrases, prefers sitting, not agitated, RR raised, HR 100-120, SpO2 90-95%, PEF over 50%) versus **severe** (talks in words, sits hunched forward, agitated, RR over 30, accessory muscles, HR over 120, SpO2 under 90%, PEF 50% or less). [GINA 2025]`,`GINA classes **drowsiness, confusion or a silent chest** as life-threatening, needing immediate ICU transfer. [GINA 2025]`,`**Children 5 years and under** use different markers: severe if unable to speak or drink, central cyanosis, confusion, SpO2 under 92%, subcostal or subglottic retractions, HR over 180 (0-3 years) or over 150 (4-5 years), or silent chest. [GINA 2025]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history at the emergency door`,points:[`Ask the **time course** (hours versus days), what has been taken already, **how many reliever puffs in the last 24 hours** and whether they helped. [GINA 2025]`,`Ask for the **fatal-asthma risk markers**: previous intubation or ICU, admissions or emergency visits in the past year, oral steroid courses, and no ICS. [GINA 2025]`,`Ask the **trigger**: cold or fever, allergen, smoke, festival fireworks, new drug (NSAID, beta-blocker), stopping ICS. [GINA 2025]`,`Ask about **anaphylaxis features** (urticaria, lip swelling, hypotension, known food or sting exposure) - anaphylaxis presenting with wheeze needs IM adrenaline first. [Resuscitation Council UK 2021]`,`Ask about **chest pain** (pneumothorax, pneumomediastinum), **fever and purulent sputum** (pneumonia), and **orthopnoea or cardiac history** (cardiac asthma). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Atypical presentations**: the elderly may have little wheeze and present with confusion; poor perceivers present very late; children may present with **vomiting, refusing feeds or isolated cough**. [GINA 2025]`,`Do not delay treatment for a detailed history: **start bronchodilator and oxygen while the history is taken**. [GINA 2025]`]},{heading:`Examination findings and their meaning`,points:[`**Speech**: full sentences (mild), phrases (moderate), words only (severe), unable to speak (life-threatening). [GINA 2025]`,`**Posture**: sits upright or hunched forward, **tripod position**, refuses to lie flat; restlessness and agitation reflect hypoxaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Accessory muscle use** (sternomastoid, scalene), tracheal tug, intercostal and supraclavicular recession signal a high work of breathing. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Respiratory rate 25/min or more** and **pulse 110/min or more** in adults define a severe attack; **bradycardia, hypotension or arrhythmia** are pre-arrest signs. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`**Wheeze** is bilateral, polyphonic and expiratory; loud wheeze does not equal severity, and a **silent chest** is ominous. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`**Pulsus paradoxus over 25 mmHg** indicates severe obstruction, but its absence does not exclude it, so it is no longer used to grade attacks. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`**Cyanosis, drowsiness, confusion, paradoxical (thoraco-abdominal) breathing and feeble respiratory effort** mean imminent arrest. [GINA 2025]`,`Look for **tracheal deviation and unilateral absent breath sounds** (tension pneumothorax), **subcutaneous emphysema** (pneumomediastinum), urticaria or stridor (anaphylaxis, upper airway). [Harrison 22e]`,`Measure **PEF** (best of three, if the patient can manage) and SpO2 before and 15-30 minutes after the first bronchodilator. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Acute left ventricular failure ('cardiac asthma')**: cardiac history, orthopnoea, basal crackles, gallop, raised JVP, frothy sputum, raised NT-proBNP and pulmonary oedema on X-ray. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute exacerbation of COPD**: smoker or biomass exposure over 40, chronic sputum, fixed obstruction and chronic hypercapnia - oxygen target is **88-92%** (see the AECOPD topic). [GOLD 2025]`,`**Anaphylaxis**: sudden wheeze with urticaria, angioedema, hypotension or gut symptoms after a trigger - give **adrenaline 0.5 mg IM** first. [Resuscitation Council UK 2021]`,`**Pneumothorax**: sudden pleuritic pain, unilateral absent breath sounds, hyper-resonance; may complicate the asthma attack itself. [BTS 2023]`,`**Upper airway obstruction and inducible laryngeal obstruction (vocal cord dysfunction)**: **inspiratory stridor**, throat tightness, normal SpO2, flattened inspiratory loop, no response to bronchodilator. [GINA 2025]`,`**Pulmonary embolism**: sudden breathlessness with clear chest, hypoxaemia out of proportion, risk factors, tachycardia; wheeze is uncommon. [ESC 2019]`,`**Foreign body aspiration** (especially children): sudden choking, **monophonic or localised wheeze**, unilateral hyperinflation. [Nelson 22e]`,`**Dysfunctional breathing or panic**: sighing, tingling, dizziness, normal SpO2, normal PEF, low PaCO2 without hypoxaemia. [GINA 2025]`]},{heading:`Investigations - what and when`,points:[`**PEF (or FEV1)** at arrival, 15-30 minutes after starting treatment and then regularly - the objective guide to severity and response; express as % of best or predicted. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`**Pulse oximetry** for all; **arterial blood gas** if SpO2 is under 92% or there are life-threatening features, and repeat within 1 hour if PaCO2 is normal or raised, PaO2 is under 8 kPa or the patient deteriorates. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`ABG reading: **PaCO2 under 4.6 kPa (35 mmHg) is expected**; 4.6-6.0 kPa (35-45 mmHg) is life-threatening; **over 6.0 kPa (45 mmHg) is near-fatal**; a **pH under 7.35 with rising PaCO2** is an indication for ICU. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`**Chest X-ray is not routine**: order it for suspected **pneumothorax or pneumomediastinum, consolidation**, life-threatening asthma, failure to respond, or before ventilation. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`**Serum potassium and glucose** - repeated nebulised salbutamol and steroids cause hypokalaemia and hyperglycaemia; replace potassium if low. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`**Serum lactate** may rise from salbutamol (type B lactic acidosis); consider it when acidosis and tachypnoea persist despite improving PEF. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ECG** in older patients or with arrhythmia or very high doses of beta2-agonist; **theophylline level** if the patient takes oral theophylline before giving aminophylline. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`**CBC and CRP** if infection is suspected; **blood eosinophils** help later phenotyping but are suppressed after steroids. [GINA 2025]`,`Do **not** measure ABG by pulse oximetry alone in a tired patient - SpO2 can stay normal on oxygen while PaCO2 rises. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`]},{heading:`Management in primary care and the OPD`,points:[`Assess severity in minutes: speech, RR, pulse, SpO2, PEF; any **life-threatening feature means call an ambulance and treat while waiting**. [GINA 2025]`,`**Salbutamol 4-10 puffs by pMDI and spacer every 20 minutes for 1 hour** (one puff at a time, 4-6 breaths each) is as effective as a nebuliser in mild-moderate attacks. [GINA 2025]`,`In patients on MART, **budesonide-formoterol 200/6 one inhalation as needed** can be used as the reliever in a mild-moderate attack, to a maximum of 12 inhalations in 24 hours. [GINA 2025]`,`**Controlled oxygen** if available to SpO2 93-95% in adults (94-98% in children 6-11 years). [GINA 2025]`,`**Prednisolone 40-50 mg orally once daily for 5-7 days** in adults (1-2 mg/kg, maximum 40 mg, for 3-5 days in children 6-11) started within the first hour; no taper needed for courses under 2 weeks. [GINA 2025]`,`**Refer to hospital** if severe features, no improvement after 1 hour, high-risk history, or the patient cannot cope at home; send with a doctor's note and oxygen. [GINA 2025]`,`If sent home: **step up controller (ICS-containing)**, check inhaler technique, give a written action plan and **review within 2-7 days**. [GINA 2025]`,`**Do not give sedatives**, and avoid oral salbutamol, cough syrups and routine antibiotics. [GINA 2025]`]},{heading:`Emergency and inpatient management - the protocol`,points:[`**Sit the patient up, give oxygen to target SpO2 93-95%** (GINA) or 94-98% (BTS) - avoid uncontrolled high-flow oxygen. [GINA 2025]`,`**Salbutamol 5 mg nebulised (oxygen-driven at 6 L/min)** every 15-30 minutes, or **continuous nebulisation 5-10 mg/hour** in severe or life-threatening attacks not responding to bolus doses. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`Add **ipratropium bromide 0.5 mg (500 microgram) nebulised every 4-6 hours** in severe or life-threatening attacks or poor initial response - it reduces admissions when added early in the emergency department. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`**Prednisolone 40-50 mg orally daily for at least 5 days**, or **hydrocortisone 100 mg IV 6-hourly** (GINA: 200 mg/day in divided doses) if the patient cannot swallow or is vomiting; oral and IV are equally effective. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`**Magnesium sulphate 1.2-2 g IV over 20 minutes** as a single dose for acute severe asthma not responding to initial bronchodilator, or with FEV1 or PEF under 25-30% at presentation. [GINA 2025]`,`**Aminophylline** is not recommended routinely (GINA); BTS allows it after senior review in near-fatal or life-threatening attacks with poor response: **loading 5 mg/kg IV over 20 minutes** (omit if on oral theophylline) then **0.5-0.7 mg/kg/hour**, keeping levels 10-20 mg/L. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`**IV salbutamol** is reserved for patients in whom inhaled therapy cannot be used reliably; there is no advantage over inhaled routes. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`**Not recommended**: routine antibiotics (only with clear evidence of bacterial infection), sedatives, leukotriene antagonists, routine heliox and mucolytics. [GINA 2025]`,`**Monitor** PEF 15-30 minutes after starting, then 4-6 hourly; SpO2 continuously; pulse, RR, potassium and glucose; keep PEF chart before and after bronchodilator. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`**Admit** after initial treatment if any life-threatening or near-fatal feature, any severe feature persisting, or **PEF under 75% of best or predicted 1 hour after treatment** with risk factors; GINA uses pre-treatment FEV1 or PEF under 25% or post-treatment under 40% as admission markers. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`]},{heading:`ICU, NIV and intubation`,points:[`**Refer to ICU** for deteriorating PEF, persisting or worsening hypoxia, **hypercapnia, acidosis (pH falling)**, exhaustion or feeble respiration, drowsiness, confusion, coma or respiratory arrest. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`**NIV is not routinely recommended** in acute asthma - evidence is weak; if tried it must be in ICU or HDU with immediate access to intubation, and should never delay intubation. [GINA 2025]`,`**High-flow nasal oxygen** may help hypoxaemia but does not unload the respiratory muscles adequately in hypercapnic asthma; it is not a substitute for intubation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Intubation** by the most experienced operator, with a large tube (8 mm or more for bronchoscopic toilet); **ketamine** is preferred for induction because it bronchodilates, with a fluid bolus ready for post-intubation hypotension. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ventilator strategy**: **low tidal volume (6-8 mL/kg ideal body weight), low rate (8-12/min), high inspiratory flow, I:E 1:4 or longer, minimal or no external PEEP**, accepting permissive hypercapnia (pH 7.15-7.20 tolerated). [Harrison 22e]`,`Keep **plateau pressure under 30 cm H2O** and measure auto-PEEP; sudden hypotension after intubation should be treated by **disconnecting the ventilator for 30-60 seconds** to let trapped air escape, and by excluding tension pneumothorax. [Harrison 22e]`,`Avoid combined high-dose steroids and **neuromuscular blockers**, which cause critical illness myopathy; refractory cases may need volatile anaesthetics or **ECMO** in tertiary centres. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Discharge criteria and the discharge bundle`,points:[`Discharge when **PEF is over 75% of best or predicted** and diurnal PEF variability under 25%, symptoms are controlled, and the patient has been **stable on discharge medication for 12-24 hours**. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`**Complete the oral steroid course** (prednisolone 40-50 mg daily for at least 5 days); no taper needed under 2-3 weeks. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`**Start or step up an ICS-containing controller** - an exacerbation means the maintenance treatment was inadequate; GINA prefers ICS-formoterol MART after discharge. [GINA 2025]`,`**Check inhaler technique and adherence**, and teach spacer use - correct this before stepping up. [GINA 2025]`,`Give a **written personalised asthma action plan** with PEF zones and when to seek care. [BTS/NICE/SIGN 2024]`,`**Remove triggers**: smoking cessation, avoid biomass and incense smoke, stop NSAIDs or beta-blockers if implicated, influenza vaccination. [GINA 2025]`,`**Follow up in primary care within 2 working days** (BTS) or 2-7 days (GINA), and in a respiratory clinic within 4 weeks after a severe or life-threatening attack. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`Identify patients with **more than 2 exacerbations a year or any ICU admission** for specialist review for severe asthma and biologics (see the severe asthma topic). [GINA 2025]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: treat exactly as in non-pregnant women - salbutamol, ipratropium, steroids, magnesium are all safe; target **SpO2 94-98%**, involve obstetrics and monitor the fetus; the risk is under-treatment, not the drugs. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`**Children 6-11**: salbutamol 4-10 puffs by spacer every 20 minutes (or 2.5-5 mg nebulised), prednisolone 1-2 mg/kg (maximum 40 mg) for 3-5 days, oxygen to 94-98%. [GINA 2025]`,`**Children IV magnesium**: 40 mg/kg (maximum 2 g) IV over 20 minutes for severe attacks not responding to inhaled therapy. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`**Elderly**: blunted perception, comorbid cardiac disease (consider LVF), higher risk of hypokalaemia and arrhythmias with beta2-agonists and aminophylline. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diabetes**: steroids raise glucose - monitor capillary glucose 4-6 hourly and adjust insulin; do not withhold steroids. [ADA 2025]`,`**Aminophylline interactions**: levels rise with **ciprofloxacin, erythromycin, clarithromycin, fluconazole**, heart failure and liver disease, and fall with smoking, rifampicin and phenytoin. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Resource-limited setting (PHC, CHC)**: pMDI with a home-made **500 mL plastic-bottle spacer** works as well as a nebuliser; give oral prednisolone and refer early with oxygen. [GINA 2025]`,`**COVID-19 or influenza**: use pMDI with spacer rather than nebulisers to reduce aerosol, and give oseltamivir if influenza is likely. [GINA 2025]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Pneumothorax, pneumomediastinum and subcutaneous emphysema** from alveolar rupture under high pressures. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lobar or segmental collapse** from mucus plugging; may need physiotherapy or bronchoscopic toilet. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypokalaemia, lactic acidosis, tachyarrhythmias** from high-dose beta2-agonists; **seizures and arrhythmias** from aminophylline toxicity. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`**Respiratory arrest, hypoxic brain injury and death**; post-intubation hypotension and barotrauma in ventilated patients. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Critical illness myopathy** after steroids with neuromuscular blockade; steroid-induced hyperglycaemia and psychosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`A patient discharged after an exacerbation has a **high risk of another attack within weeks**, and a previous near-fatal attack is the strongest predictor of death. [GINA 2025]`,`Most asthma deaths have **preventable factors**: under-use of ICS, over-reliance on SABA, delayed presentation and inadequate follow-up. [NRAD 2014]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**GINA 2024-2025**: oxygen target **93-95% in adults and 94-98% in children 6-11**; controlled or titrated oxygen gives better outcomes than high-flow 100% oxygen. [GINA 2025]`,`**GINA 2024-2025**: in patients on maintenance-and-reliever therapy, **budesonide-formoterol** can serve as the reliever in mild-moderate exacerbations managed in primary care. [GINA 2025]`,`**GINA**: after an emergency visit, every patient should leave on **ICS-containing treatment** - never SABA alone - with a 5-7 day OCS course and review within 2-7 days. [GINA 2025]`,`**BTS/NICE/SIGN 2024** joint guideline covers diagnosis and chronic management (AIR and MART, FeNO and eosinophils in diagnosis); for **acute asthma, the BTS/SIGN 2019 (SIGN 158) recommendations remain in force**. [BTS/NICE/SIGN 2024]`,`**Conflict on oxygen**: GINA aims for **93-95%**, while BTS oxygen guidance aims for **94-98%** - both agree on avoiding hyperoxia and on titration. [BTS 2017]`,`**Aminophylline**: GINA advises against it; BTS/SIGN still allows it as a senior decision in life-threatening asthma - in India it (and etofylline-theophylline) is overused and should not be first line. [GINA 2025]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: 'Define acute severe asthma and outline its management' - start with the **BTS severity table** and the **O SHIT ME** mnemonic: Oxygen, Salbutamol, Hydrocortisone or prednisolone, Ipratropium, Theophylline (senior), Magnesium, Escalate to ICU. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`**A normal PaCO2 in acute asthma is a life-threatening sign** - the most-asked single fact. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`Common mistake: **giving 100% oxygen without a target**, or withholding oxygen from a hypoxaemic asthmatic for fear of CO2 retention (that fear applies to COPD). [GINA 2025]`,`Common mistake: **using aminophylline or Deriphyllin as first line** and **giving routine antibiotics**. [GINA 2025]`,`Common mistake: **delaying systemic steroids** - they take 4-6 hours to act, so give them in the first hour. [GINA 2025]`,`Viva: why **ketamine** for intubation (bronchodilator), why **low rate and long expiration** (to limit auto-PEEP), why **hypotension after intubation** (hyperinflation reducing venous return). [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: list the **discharge criteria** - PEF over 75%, stable 12-24 hours on discharge drugs, technique checked, action plan, steroid course, ICS, follow-up in 2 working days. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Severity of acute asthma in adults (BTS/SIGN)`,columns:[`Level`,`Defining features`,`Where to treat`],rows:[[`Moderate`,`Increasing symptoms; PEF over 50-75% best or predicted; no severe features`,`Primary care or ED; may go home if PEF over 75% after treatment`],[`Acute severe`,`Any one: PEF 33-50%; RR 25/min or more; HR 110/min or more; cannot complete sentences in one breath`,`Hospital; admit if not responding`],[`Life-threatening`,`Severe plus any one: PEF under 33%; SpO2 under 92%; PaO2 under 8 kPa; normal PaCO2 4.6-6.0 kPa; silent chest; cyanosis; poor effort; arrhythmia; exhaustion; altered consciousness; hypotension`,`Hospital, inform senior and ICU early`],[`Near-fatal`,`Raised PaCO2 and/or need for mechanical ventilation with raised inflation pressures`,`ICU`]]},{heading:`Drugs in acute severe asthma`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Oxygen`,`Nasal cannula or mask to SpO2 93-95% (GINA) / 94-98% (BTS)`,`Titrate continuously; drive nebulisers with oxygen 6 L/min`,`Hyperoxia can raise PaCO2; avoid uncontrolled 100% oxygen`],[`Salbutamol`,`pMDI + spacer 4-10 puffs every 20 min; or 5 mg nebulised every 15-30 min; or continuous 5-10 mg/hour`,`First hour intensively, then 1-4 hourly as response allows`,`Tremor, tachycardia, hypokalaemia, lactic acidosis, hyperglycaemia`],[`Ipratropium bromide`,`0.5 mg nebulised every 4-6 hours (children 0.25 mg)`,`Early hours of severe or life-threatening attacks`,`Dry mouth, urinary retention, angle-closure glaucoma if mask-delivered into eyes`],[`Prednisolone`,`40-50 mg orally once daily (children 1-2 mg/kg, maximum 40 mg)`,`At least 5 days (5-7 adults; 3-5 children); no taper`,`Hyperglycaemia, mood change, dyspepsia`],[`Hydrocortisone`,`100 mg IV 6-hourly if unable to take oral`,`Switch to oral prednisolone when able`,`As for prednisolone; fluid retention`],[`Magnesium sulphate`,`1.2-2 g IV over 20 min single dose (children 40 mg/kg, maximum 2 g)`,`Single dose; repeat dosing not evidence-based`,`Flushing, hypotension; caution in renal failure (hypermagnesaemia)`],[`Aminophylline (senior decision only)`,`5 mg/kg IV over 20 min loading (omit if on theophylline), then 0.5-0.7 mg/kg/hour`,`Level within 24 hours, keep 10-20 mg/L`,`Nausea, arrhythmia, seizures; interactions with ciprofloxacin, macrolides; not recommended by GINA`],[`Ketamine (ICU)`,`1-2 mg/kg IV for induction`,`For intubation only`,`Raised secretions, emergence reactions, hypertension`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of acute wheeze and breathlessness`,columns:[`Condition`,`Clue in history`,`Discriminating sign or test`],rows:[[`Acute severe asthma`,`Known asthma, variable symptoms, trigger, young`,`Polyphonic wheeze, low PEF, low PaCO2 early, rapid response to salbutamol`],[`AECOPD`,`Smoker or biomass over 40, chronic sputum`,`Fixed obstruction, chronic hypercapnia, barrel chest; target SpO2 88-92%`],[`Acute LVF (cardiac asthma)`,`Orthopnoea, PND, cardiac history`,`Basal crackles, gallop, raised JVP, high NT-proBNP, pulmonary oedema on X-ray`],[`Anaphylaxis`,`Exposure to food, drug, sting`,`Urticaria, angioedema, hypotension; adrenaline IM first`],[`Pneumothorax`,`Sudden pleuritic pain`,`Unilateral absent breath sounds, hyper-resonance, X-ray or ultrasound`],[`Inducible laryngeal obstruction`,`Throat tightness, sudden onset and offset`,`Inspiratory stridor, normal SpO2, laryngoscopy during attack`],[`Foreign body`,`Choking episode, child`,`Monophonic or localised wheeze, unilateral hyperinflation`],[`Pulmonary embolism`,`Risk factors, sudden onset`,`Clear chest, hypoxaemia, CTPA`]]},{heading:`Investigations and cut-offs in acute asthma`,columns:[`Test`,`When`,`Cut-off and meaning`],rows:[[`PEF`,`Arrival, 15-30 min after treatment, then 4-6 hourly`,`Over 75% mild-moderate, 33-50% severe, under 33% life-threatening; discharge when over 75%`],[`SpO2`,`All patients, continuous`,`Under 92% means life-threatening and needs ABG`],[`ABG`,`SpO2 under 92% or life-threatening features; repeat within 1 hour if abnormal`,`PaCO2 under 4.6 kPa expected; 4.6-6.0 kPa life-threatening; over 6.0 kPa near-fatal`],[`Serum potassium`,`On repeated salbutamol`,`Replace if under 3.5 mmol/L`],[`Chest X-ray`,`Suspected pneumothorax, consolidation, life-threatening, no response, before ventilation`,`Not routine`],[`Theophylline level`,`Before aminophylline in patients on oral theophylline; within 24 hours of infusion`,`Target 10-20 mg/L; toxicity over 20 mg/L`]]}],redFlags:[`PEF under 33% of best or predicted, or unable to perform PEF`,`SpO2 under 92% on air, or cyanosis`,`Normal or rising PaCO2 (4.6 kPa or more) or falling pH on ABG`,`Silent chest, feeble respiratory effort or paradoxical breathing`,`Drowsiness, confusion, exhaustion or altered consciousness`,`Bradycardia, arrhythmia or hypotension`,`Previous near-fatal asthma needing intubation or ICU`,`No improvement after 1 hour of intensive bronchodilator and steroid`],pearls:[`**33-92-CHEST**: PEF under 33%, SpO2 under 92%, Cyanosis, Hypotension, Exhaustion, Silent chest, Tachy- or bradyarrhythmia = life-threatening asthma.`,`A **'normal' PaCO2** in acute asthma is not reassuring - it means the patient is tiring.`,`**Oxygen target 93-95%** (GINA) in asthma versus **88-92%** in COPD with hypercapnic risk.`,`**Steroids in the first hour** - prednisolone 40-50 mg is as good as IV hydrocortisone if the patient can swallow.`,`**Magnesium 2 g IV over 20 minutes** once - for poor response or FEV1 under 25-30%.`,`**pMDI with spacer equals the nebuliser** in mild-moderate attacks - and a 500 mL bottle spacer works.`,`Ventilating asthma: **low rate, low volume, long expiration, accept high CO2**; disconnect the ventilator if BP drops after intubation.`,`Discharge rule: **PEF over 75%, 12-24 hours stable, ICS started, action plan, review in 2 working days**.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-acute-severe-asthma-q1`,stem:`A 26-year-old woman with asthma has been breathless for 6 hours. She speaks in short phrases, RR 28/min, pulse 118/min, SpO2 93% on air and PEF 40% of her best. ABG on air shows PaO2 9.1 kPa and PaCO2 5.2 kPa. How should this attack be classified?`,options:[`Moderate exacerbation`,`Acute severe asthma`,`Life-threatening asthma`,`Near-fatal asthma`],answer:2,explanation:`Her RR, pulse and PEF of 40% are features of acute severe asthma, but a PaCO2 of 5.2 kPa lies in the 'normal' range of 4.6-6.0 kPa, which in a breathless asthmatic signals fatigue and makes the attack life-threatening by BTS/SIGN criteria. Moderate exacerbation needs PEF over 50% with no severe features, and near-fatal asthma requires a raised PaCO2 above 6.0 kPa or the need for ventilation.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-acute-severe-asthma-q2`,stem:`In an adult with acute severe asthma in the emergency department, which oxygen saturation target does GINA 2025 recommend?`,options:[`88-92%`,`90-94%`,`93-95%`,`98-100%`],answer:2,explanation:`GINA recommends controlled oxygen to an SpO2 of 93-95% in adults (94-98% in children 6-11) because titrated oxygen gives better outcomes than high-flow 100% oxygen, which can raise PaCO2. The 88-92% target belongs to COPD with risk of hypercapnia, and aiming for 98-100% risks hyperoxia; BTS oxygen guidance uses 94-98%, a minor difference from GINA.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-acute-severe-asthma-q3`,stem:`A 35-year-old man with acute severe asthma has received three back-to-back salbutamol and ipratropium nebulisations and prednisolone 50 mg. One hour later his PEF is 30% of predicted. What is the most appropriate next drug?`,options:[`IV aminophylline 5 mg/kg loading dose`,`IV magnesium sulphate 2 g over 20 minutes`,`Oral montelukast 10 mg`,`IV amoxicillin-clavulanate`],answer:1,explanation:`A single dose of IV magnesium sulphate 1.2-2 g over 20 minutes reduces admissions in adults whose FEV1 or PEF stays under 25-30% or who fail to respond to initial bronchodilators. Aminophylline is not recommended by GINA and is only a senior decision after magnesium in BTS guidance; montelukast has no role in acute attacks; antibiotics are given only when there is clear evidence of bacterial infection.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-acute-severe-asthma-q4`,stem:`A patient with near-fatal asthma becomes profoundly hypotensive within minutes of intubation and manual bag ventilation at a rapid rate. Breath sounds are equal. What is the best immediate step?`,options:[`Start noradrenaline infusion and increase the ventilator rate`,`Insert bilateral chest drains immediately`,`Disconnect the patient from the bag for 30-60 seconds to allow exhalation`,`Give a second dose of IV magnesium sulphate`],answer:2,explanation:`Post-intubation hypotension in asthma is usually caused by dynamic hyperinflation and auto-PEEP from rapid ventilation, which reduces venous return; briefly disconnecting lets trapped gas escape and restores BP, followed by a low rate and long expiratory time. Faster ventilation worsens trapping, chest drains are for tension pneumothorax (breath sounds here are equal), and magnesium does not address the mechanism.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`respiratory-acute-severe-asthma-q5`,stem:`Which of the following is a BTS/SIGN criterion that must be met before discharging an adult admitted with acute severe asthma?`,options:[`PEF over 75% of best or predicted and stable on discharge medication for 12-24 hours`,`Normal chest X-ray`,`Blood eosinophil count under 150/microlitre`,`Completion of a 10-day IV hydrocortisone course`],answer:0,explanation:`BTS/SIGN discharge criteria include PEF over 75% of best or predicted with diurnal variability under 25%, and stability on the discharge drugs for 12-24 hours, with inhaler technique checked, an action plan and early follow-up. A chest X-ray is not routinely required, eosinophils are suppressed by steroids and do not guide discharge, and oral prednisolone for at least 5 days is the standard rather than prolonged IV hydrocortisone.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-acute-severe-asthma-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Define acute severe asthma. Describe the assessment of severity and the stepwise emergency and inpatient management of an adult with acute severe asthma, including criteria for ICU referral and discharge.`,openingLines:[`Acute severe asthma is an asthma exacerbation with any one of PEF 33-50% of best or predicted, respiratory rate 25/min or more, heart rate 110/min or more, or inability to complete sentences in one breath (BTS/SIGN).`,`It is a medical emergency; India carries over 40% of global asthma deaths, most of them preventable by early oxygen, bronchodilator and steroid.`],answer:[{heading:`Severity assessment`,points:[`Moderate: PEF over 50-75%, no severe features.`,`Acute severe: PEF 33-50%, RR 25 or more, HR 110 or more, cannot complete sentences.`,`Life-threatening: PEF under 33%, SpO2 under 92%, PaO2 under 8 kPa, normal PaCO2, silent chest, cyanosis, exhaustion, arrhythmia, hypotension, altered consciousness.`,`Near-fatal: raised PaCO2 or need for ventilation.`]},{heading:`Immediate investigations`,points:[`PEF, SpO2; ABG if SpO2 under 92% or life-threatening features.`,`Potassium, glucose; chest X-ray only if pneumothorax, consolidation, life-threatening or not responding.`]},{heading:`Immediate treatment (first hour)`,points:[`Sit up; oxygen to SpO2 93-95% (GINA) or 94-98% (BTS).`,`Salbutamol 5 mg nebulised oxygen-driven every 15-30 min, or continuous 5-10 mg/hour.`,`Ipratropium 0.5 mg nebulised 4-6 hourly.`,`Prednisolone 40-50 mg oral or hydrocortisone 100 mg IV 6-hourly.`]},{heading:`If poor response`,points:[`Magnesium sulphate 1.2-2 g IV over 20 min single dose.`,`Senior review: aminophylline 5 mg/kg load then 0.5-0.7 mg/kg/hour only in selected cases (not GINA-recommended).`,`No routine antibiotics, no sedatives.`]},{heading:`ICU referral and ventilation`,points:[`Deteriorating PEF, worsening hypoxia, hypercapnia, acidosis, exhaustion, drowsiness, arrest.`,`NIV not routine; intubate with ketamine; low tidal volume, low rate, long expiration, permissive hypercapnia, plateau under 30 cm H2O.`]},{heading:`Monitoring and discharge`,points:[`PEF 15-30 min after start then 4-6 hourly; SpO2, K+, glucose.`,`Discharge: PEF over 75%, stable 12-24 hours on discharge drugs, steroid 5 days, ICS-containing controller, technique, written action plan, review within 2 working days, clinic within 4 weeks.`]}],mustDraw:[`BTS severity table (moderate, severe, life-threatening, near-fatal)`,`Flow chart of first-hour management with escalation to magnesium and ICU`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and severity grading`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations`,marks:1},{part:`Initial management with doses`,marks:3},{part:`Escalation, ICU and ventilation`,marks:2},{part:`Discharge and follow-up`,marks:2}],keywords:[`PEF`,`life-threatening`,`normal PaCO2`,`salbutamol`,`ipratropium`,`magnesium`,`prednisolone`,`ICU`,`discharge`]},{id:`respiratory-acute-severe-asthma-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on mechanical ventilation in near-fatal asthma.`,openingLines:[`Near-fatal asthma is an attack with raised PaCO2 or the need for mechanical ventilation with raised inflation pressures.`,`The ventilator strategy aims to limit dynamic hyperinflation (auto-PEEP) by giving the lungs time to empty, accepting hypercapnia.`],answer:[{heading:`Indications`,points:[`Respiratory arrest, coma or drowsiness, exhaustion, rising PaCO2 with acidosis, refractory hypoxaemia.`,`NIV is not routine and must not delay intubation.`]},{heading:`Intubation`,points:[`Experienced operator, large tube (8 mm or more), ketamine induction, fluid bolus ready.`]},{heading:`Settings`,points:[`Tidal volume 6-8 mL/kg IBW, rate 8-12/min, high inspiratory flow, I:E 1:4 or longer, low external PEEP.`,`Permissive hypercapnia (pH 7.15-7.20 tolerated); plateau pressure under 30 cm H2O; measure auto-PEEP.`]},{heading:`Complications`,points:[`Hypotension from hyperinflation (disconnect 30-60 seconds), barotrauma and tension pneumothorax, critical illness myopathy with steroids and neuromuscular blockers.`,`Refractory: volatile anaesthetics, ECMO.`]}],mustDraw:[`Graph of expiratory flow not returning to zero before the next breath (auto-PEEP)`],markSplit:[{part:`Indications`,marks:1},{part:`Intubation and settings`,marks:2},{part:`Complications and rescue`,marks:2}],keywords:[`auto-PEEP`,`dynamic hyperinflation`,`permissive hypercapnia`,`ketamine`,`plateau pressure`]}],references:[`Global Initiative for Asthma (GINA). Global Strategy for Asthma Management and Prevention, 2025 update`,`British Thoracic Society/Scottish Intercollegiate Guidelines Network. SIGN 158 British guideline on the management of asthma, 2019 (acute asthma sections)`,`BTS/NICE/SIGN. Asthma: diagnosis, monitoring and chronic asthma management (NG245), November 2024`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Asthma; Approach to the patient with respiratory failure`,`BTS guideline for oxygen use in healthcare and emergency settings, 2017`,`Agarwal R et al. Guidelines for diagnosis and management of bronchial asthma: joint ICS/NCCP(I) recommendations. Lung India 2015`,`Jindal SK et al. Indian Study on Epidemiology of Asthma, Respiratory Symptoms and Chronic Bronchitis in adults (INSEARCH). Int J Tuberc Lung Dis 2012`]}),n.push({id:`respiratory-severe-asthma-biologics`,title:`Difficult-to-treat and severe asthma, phenotypes and biologics`,oneLiner:`**Difficult-to-treat asthma** is asthma uncontrolled despite **medium- or high-dose ICS-LABA** (or needing high dose to stay controlled); **severe asthma** is the subset that stays uncontrolled despite **good adherence and technique, optimised high-dose ICS-LABA and treatment of contributory factors** - it is phenotyped by **blood eosinophils, FeNO and IgE** and treated with **add-on LAMA, azithromycin and T2-targeted biologics (omalizumab, mepolizumab, benralizumab, dupilumab, tezepelumab)**.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`severe asthma`,`difficult asthma`,`refractory asthma`,`T2-high asthma`,`eosinophilic asthma`,`FeNO`,`omalizumab`,`mepolizumab`,`benralizumab`,`reslizumab`,`dupilumab`,`tezepelumab`,`depemokimab`,`tiotropium`,`azithromycin`,`bronchial thermoplasty`,`OCS-dependent asthma`,`clinical remission`,`ERS/ATS`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Difficult-to-treat asthma**: asthma that is uncontrolled despite **GINA Step 4-5 treatment (medium- or high-dose ICS with a second controller, or maintenance OCS)**, or that needs such treatment to maintain control and reduce exacerbations. [GINA 2025]`,`**Severe asthma**: the subset of difficult-to-treat asthma that is uncontrolled **despite adherence to optimised high-dose ICS-LABA and management of contributory factors**, or that worsens when high-dose treatment is reduced. [GINA 2025]`,`**ERS/ATS definition**: asthma requiring high-dose ICS plus a second controller (and/or systemic steroids for 50% or more of the previous year) to prevent it becoming uncontrolled, or which remains uncontrolled despite this. [ERS/ATS 2014]`,`'Uncontrolled' means **poor symptom control (ACQ over 1.5 or ACT under 20)**, **2 or more OCS bursts or 1 or more serious exacerbation (hospital, ICU, ventilation) in the past year**, or airflow limitation with FEV1 under 80% after bronchodilator. [ERS/ATS 2014]`,`About **17% of adults with asthma have difficult-to-treat asthma** but only **about 3.7% have true severe asthma** once technique, adherence and comorbidities are fixed. [Hekking, JACI 2015]`,`Severe asthma accounts for a disproportionate share of **asthma costs, admissions and deaths**, and of the long-term harm from **cumulative oral corticosteroid exposure** - as little as 0.5-1 g lifetime prednisolone raises the risk of diabetes, osteoporosis, cataract and fracture. [GINA 2025]`,`In India, true severe asthma is under-recognised because **adherence, inhaler technique, biomass and tobacco exposure, untreated rhinitis, ABPA and post-TB airway disease** confound most 'uncontrolled' asthma. [ICS/NCCP(I) 2015]`]},{heading:`Why asthma stays uncontrolled - contributory factors`,points:[`**Poor adherence** is found in up to 50% or more of difficult asthma - check prescription refills, dose counters, and ask non-judgementally. [GINA 2025]`,`**Incorrect inhaler technique** occurs in up to 70-80% of patients; watch the patient use the device every visit. [GINA 2025]`,`**Wrong diagnosis**: inducible laryngeal obstruction, dysfunctional breathing, COPD, bronchiectasis, heart failure, central airway tumour or post-TB obstruction. [GINA 2025]`,`**Comorbidities**: chronic rhinosinusitis with or without nasal polyps, GORD, **obesity, obstructive sleep apnoea**, anxiety and depression, ABPA, EGPA. [GINA 2025]`,`**Ongoing exposures**: active or passive smoking, vaping, **biomass and incense smoke**, occupational sensitisers, allergens at home (mould, cockroach, dust mite). [GINA 2025]`,`**Drugs**: non-selective beta-blockers (including timolol eye drops), aspirin and NSAIDs in AERD. [GINA 2025]`,`**Psychosocial factors**: cost of inhalers, steroid phobia, health literacy, depression - each needs addressing before escalation. [GINA 2025]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - the T2 and T2-low pathways that the biologics block`,points:[`Epithelial injury by viruses, allergens and pollutants releases the **alarmins TSLP, IL-33 and IL-25**, which drive both adaptive Th2 cells and **innate lymphoid cells (ILC2)** - the upstream target of **tezepelumab**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**IL-4** drives B-cell class switching to **IgE** and Th2 differentiation; **IL-13** causes goblet-cell metaplasia, mucus, airway hyperresponsiveness and epithelial iNOS (raised **FeNO**) - both act through **IL-4Ralpha**, the target of **dupilumab**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**IL-5** controls eosinophil maturation, release from marrow, survival and activation - blocked by **mepolizumab and reslizumab** (anti-IL-5) and **benralizumab** (anti-IL-5Ralpha, which also depletes eosinophils by antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity). [Harrison 22e]`,`**IgE** bound to FcepsilonRI on mast cells and basophils triggers degranulation on allergen cross-linking; **omalizumab** binds free IgE, lowering it and down-regulating FcepsilonRI. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Blood eosinophils** reflect IL-5 activity, while **FeNO** reflects IL-13 activity - the two are partly independent, and both predict exacerbation risk and response to ICS and biologics. [GINA 2025]`,`**T2-low asthma** (neutrophilic or paucigranulocytic) is linked to smoking, obesity, late onset, infection and IL-17 or IL-8 pathways; it responds poorly to ICS and biologics other than tezepelumab, and may benefit from azithromycin. [GINA 2025]`,`**Corticosteroid insensitivity** in severe asthma comes from reduced histone deacetylase-2 activity (smoking, oxidative stress), altered glucocorticoid receptor function and neutrophilic inflammation. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and phenotyping`,points:[`**Refractory type 2 inflammation** on high-dose ICS (GINA) is suggested by any of: **blood eosinophils 150/microlitre or more**, **FeNO 20 ppb or more**, sputum eosinophils 2% or more, clinically allergen-driven asthma, or need for maintenance OCS. [GINA 2025]`,`Repeat **blood eosinophils and FeNO up to 3 times**, at the lowest possible OCS dose, before calling asthma T2-low, because steroids suppress both. [GINA 2025]`,`**Allergic (atopic) phenotype**: childhood onset, sensitisation on skin prick test or specific IgE, symptoms with allergen exposure - omalizumab candidate. [GINA 2025]`,`**Late-onset eosinophilic phenotype**: adult onset, high eosinophils, **chronic rhinosinusitis with nasal polyps**, often AERD, frequent OCS need - anti-IL-5/5R or dupilumab candidate. [GINA 2025]`,`**Obesity-associated, T2-low** phenotype: mostly women, symptoms out of proportion to inflammation - weight loss, CPAP for OSA and pulmonary rehabilitation help more than drugs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Severe asthma with fixed airflow limitation** may overlap with COPD (asthma-COPD overlap) - treat with ICS-containing therapy, never LABA or LAMA alone. [GINA 2025]`,`**Eosinophils 1500/microlitre or more** should prompt a search for **EGPA, hypereosinophilic syndrome, ABPA and parasites (Strongyloides, filaria)** before attributing them to asthma. [GINA 2025]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Frequent **night waking, daily reliever use, limitation of activity** and **2 or more steroid courses a year** despite Step 4-5 treatment. [GINA 2025]`,`Ask about **every inhaler and how often it is actually used**, cost, beliefs, and who fills the prescriptions. [GINA 2025]`,`**Nasal blockage, loss of smell and polyps** suggest CRSwNP with eosinophilic or AERD phenotype; reaction to aspirin or NSAIDs confirms AERD. [GINA 2025]`,`**Brown mucus plugs, fleeting shadows, haemoptysis** suggest ABPA; **neuropathy, rash, sinusitis and cardiac symptoms** suggest EGPA. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Snoring and daytime sleepiness** (OSA), heartburn (GORD), panic and tingling (dysfunctional breathing), **inspiratory throat tightness** (laryngeal obstruction). [GINA 2025]`,`**Cumulative steroid harm**: weight gain, diabetes, fractures, cataract, mood change - quantify OCS courses in the last year. [GINA 2025]`,`Occupational history and home exposures (biomass, mosquito coil, pets, damp). [GINA 2025]`]},{heading:`Examination`,points:[`Watch the **inhaler technique** - this is part of the examination in every difficult-asthma patient. [GINA 2025]`,`**BMI and neck circumference** for obesity and OSA; **cushingoid features, bruising, thin skin** from steroids. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nasal polyps** on anterior rhinoscopy; oral candidiasis and **dysphonia** from ICS. [GINA 2025]`,`**Inspiratory stridor or monophonic wheeze** suggests upper or central airway obstruction rather than asthma. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Clubbing** is not a feature of asthma - think bronchiectasis, ILD or lung cancer. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mononeuritis multiplex, purpuric rash** - EGPA; **crackles** - bronchiectasis, ILD or heart failure. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Inducible laryngeal obstruction (vocal cord dysfunction)**: inspiratory stridor, throat tightness, normal SpO2, flattened inspiratory flow-volume loop; diagnosed by laryngoscopy during symptoms. [GINA 2025]`,`**Dysfunctional breathing**: sighing, chest tightness, tingling, Nijmegen questionnaire score 23 or more, normal or variable spirometry - breathing retraining helps. [GINA 2025]`,`**COPD or asthma-COPD overlap**: smoker or biomass exposure, persistent obstruction, emphysema on CT. [GOLD 2025]`,`**ABPA**: Aspergillus-specific IgE, total IgE 500 IU/mL or more, central bronchiectasis and mucus plugs (see the ABPA topic). [ISHAM 2024]`,`**EGPA**: asthma with eosinophils over 10% or 1000/microlitre, neuropathy, sinusitis, pulmonary infiltrates, ANCA positive in about 40%. [ACR/EULAR 2022]`,`**Bronchiectasis and post-TB airway disease**: daily purulent sputum, crackles, HRCT findings. [BTS 2019]`,`**Central airway obstruction** (tumour, tracheal stenosis, foreign body): monophonic wheeze, fixed flow-volume loop pattern, no bronchodilator response. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Heart failure**: orthopnoea, crackles, raised NT-proBNP, echo. [ESC 2023]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Confirm the diagnosis**: spirometry with bronchodilator reversibility (FEV1 over 12% and 200 mL), PEF variability, or bronchial challenge if spirometry is normal - consider stepping down ICS before testing if the diagnosis is doubtful. [GINA 2025]`,`**Blood eosinophil count** (repeat up to 3 times): 150/microlitre or more suggests T2 inflammation; 300/microlitre or more strongly predicts response to anti-IL-5/5R. [GINA 2025]`,`**FeNO**: 20 ppb or more on high-dose ICS suggests refractory T2 inflammation; 25 ppb or more supports dupilumab; 50 ppb or more is high. [GINA 2025]`,`**Total IgE and specific IgE or skin prick tests** to perennial aeroallergens - needed for omalizumab dosing (total IgE 30-1500 IU/mL in most dosing tables) and eligibility. [GINA 2025]`,`**Aspergillus-specific IgE (or skin test) and total IgE** in every Indian patient with severe asthma, to exclude ABPA. [ISHAM 2024]`,`**Strongyloides serology or stool test** (or empirical ivermectin) before OCS or biologics in patients from endemic areas, including most of India. [GINA 2025]`,`**HRCT chest** for atypical features: bronchiectasis, mucus plugs, emphysema, ILD; **ANCA** if EGPA suspected; **sinus CT** for CRSwNP. [GINA 2025]`,`**Sputum AFB/CBNAAT** in any patient with chronic productive cough in India; **DXA scan** for patients on long-term OCS. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**ACT (under 20 = uncontrolled) or ACQ-5 (over 1.5 = uncontrolled)** to measure control at baseline and after each change. [GINA 2025]`]},{heading:`Management in the OPD - optimise before escalating`,points:[`**Educate and fix technique and adherence first**, then review in 3-6 months before labelling severe asthma. [GINA 2025]`,`Switch to **ICS-formoterol maintenance-and-reliever therapy (MART)** if not already on it - it reduces severe exacerbations compared with fixed-dose ICS-LABA plus SABA. [GINA 2025]`,`**Optimise ICS dose**: a trial of high-dose ICS-LABA for 3-6 months (e.g. budesonide-formoterol 400/12 two puffs twice daily, or fluticasone furoate-vilanterol 200/25 once daily). [GINA 2025]`,`**Add-on LAMA**: **tiotropium soft-mist 2.5 microgram, 2 puffs once daily** (age 6 and over), or a **single-inhaler triple ICS-LABA-LAMA** (e.g. beclometasone-formoterol-glycopyrronium, fluticasone furoate-umeclidinium-vilanterol) - reduces exacerbations modestly and improves FEV1. [GINA 2025]`,`**Azithromycin 500 mg three times a week (or 250 mg daily) for at least 6 months** in adults with persistent symptoms on Step 5, after checking sputum for NTM, **ECG for QTc** and hearing - reduces exacerbations in both eosinophilic and non-eosinophilic asthma (AMAZES). [GINA 2025]`,`**Leukotriene receptor antagonist** (montelukast 10 mg at night) as add-on is less effective; warn about **neuropsychiatric adverse effects** (FDA boxed warning). [GINA 2025]`,`**Maintenance OCS** only as a last resort at the **lowest dose (prednisolone 7.5 mg/day or less)**, with bone protection, glucose, BP and eye checks. [GINA 2025]`,`**Non-drug**: smoking cessation, weight reduction, **pulmonary rehabilitation**, breathing retraining, CPAP for OSA, treatment of rhinitis with intranasal steroids, influenza and pneumococcal vaccines. [GINA 2025]`]},{heading:`Biologics - choice, criteria and assessment`,points:[`**Omalizumab (anti-IgE)**: age 6 and over, **sensitisation to a perennial aeroallergen**, total IgE and weight within the dosing table, exacerbations in the last year; SC every 2-4 weeks (75-600 mg by table); also for CRSwNP and chronic urticaria. [GINA 2025]`,`**Mepolizumab (anti-IL-5)**: **100 mg SC every 4 weeks** (40 mg in 6-11 years), eosinophils 150/microlitre or more (or 300 or more in the past year), exacerbations in the past year; also licensed for EGPA (300 mg), HES and CRSwNP. [GINA 2025]`,`**Benralizumab (anti-IL-5Ralpha)**: **30 mg SC every 4 weeks for 3 doses, then every 8 weeks**; eosinophils 300/microlitre or more (lower thresholds in OCS-dependent patients); almost completely depletes blood eosinophils; also approved for EGPA. [GINA 2025]`,`**Reslizumab (anti-IL-5)**: 3 mg/kg IV every 4 weeks, adults with eosinophils 400/microlitre or more; not marketed in India. [GINA 2025]`,`**Dupilumab (anti-IL-4Ralpha)**: **400 mg SC loading then 200 mg every 2 weeks**, or **600 mg then 300 mg every 2 weeks** for OCS-dependent asthma or with atopic dermatitis; eosinophils 150-1500/microlitre or **FeNO 25 ppb or more**; avoid if eosinophils over 1500. [GINA 2025]`,`**Tezepelumab (anti-TSLP)**: **210 mg SC every 4 weeks**, age 12 and over, **no biomarker threshold needed** - the option for T2-low severe asthma, though response is greater with higher eosinophils or FeNO; it did not reduce OCS dose in trials. [GINA 2025]`,`**Choosing**: allergic with high IgE - omalizumab; high eosinophils or OCS-dependent - anti-IL-5/5R or dupilumab; **nasal polyps** - dupilumab, omalizumab or mepolizumab; **atopic dermatitis** - dupilumab; low T2 markers - tezepelumab. [GINA 2025]`,`**Assess response at 4 months** (exacerbations, OCS dose, control, FEV1): good response - continue and review 3-6 monthly; unclear - extend to 6-12 months; no response - stop and switch to a different target. [GINA 2025]`,`**Clinical remission on treatment** (GINA 2024-2025 concept): no exacerbations, no OCS, good symptom control and stable lung function for at least 12 months - the new treatment goal in severe asthma. [GINA 2025]`,`Never stop ICS when a biologic is started; **taper OCS first**, then consider gradual ICS reduction to medium dose after 3-6 months of good response. [GINA 2025]`]},{heading:`Availability, cost and the Indian setting`,points:[`**Omalizumab, mepolizumab, benralizumab and dupilumab are marketed in India**; tezepelumab and reslizumab are not widely available - check current availability before planning. [CDSCO 2025]`,`Biologics cost **several lakh rupees a year**, are not on the NLEM and are usually not covered by PM-JAY; patient-assistance programmes and employer insurance are the usual routes. [NLEM 2022]`,`**Treat or exclude Strongyloides** (ivermectin 200 microgram/kg) and exclude **ABPA and TB** before biologics or long-term OCS in India. [GINA 2025]`,`**Low-cost steps** with real benefit - technique correction, MART, tiotropium, azithromycin, stopping biomass exposure - must be exhausted first in resource-limited settings. [GINA 2025]`,`**Bronchial thermoplasty** (radiofrequency ablation of airway smooth muscle over 3 bronchoscopies) reduces exacerbations modestly; GINA restricts it to **adults in a registry or trial**, and it is rarely done in India. [GINA 2025]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: continue ICS-LABA; **omalizumab registry data are reassuring**; data for other biologics are limited - continue only after specialist discussion; avoid starting a new biologic in pregnancy if possible. [GINA 2025]`,`**Children 6-11**: omalizumab, mepolizumab, benralizumab and dupilumab are approved; tezepelumab from 12 years; tiotropium from 6 years. [GINA 2025]`,`**Elderly**: look for **COPD overlap, heart failure and drug interactions**; long-term OCS is especially harmful (fractures, diabetes). [GINA 2025]`,`**Diabetes and osteoporosis**: reducing OCS is the main benefit of biologics; offer **calcium, vitamin D and bisphosphonate** when OCS 7.5 mg/day or more for 3 months. [ACR 2022]`,`**Live vaccines** are generally acceptable with biologics (not immunosuppressive), unlike high-dose OCS. [GINA 2025]`,`**Helminth infection** during biologic therapy: treat the infection; stop the biologic if the infection does not respond. [GINA 2025]`]},{heading:`Complications, adverse effects and prognosis`,points:[`**Omalizumab anaphylaxis** in about 0.1-0.2%, often delayed - observe after the first doses and give an adrenaline auto-injector where available. [GINA 2025]`,`**Dupilumab**: conjunctivitis, injection-site reactions, arthralgia and **transient eosinophilia** (rarely eosinophilic pneumonia or EGPA unmasked). [GINA 2025]`,`**Anti-IL-5/5R**: headache, injection-site reactions, rarely herpes zoster with mepolizumab (consider zoster vaccine). [GINA 2025]`,`**Unmasking of EGPA** when OCS is tapered on any biologic - watch for neuropathy, rash and cardiac symptoms. [GINA 2025]`,`**OCS complications**: diabetes, osteoporosis and fractures, cataract, glaucoma, weight gain, adrenal suppression, infections. [GINA 2025]`,`With biologics, **about half of patients have 50% or greater fewer exacerbations**, and a proportion achieve clinical remission; super-responders have high eosinophils, adult-onset disease and nasal polyps. [GINA 2025]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer to a specialist** when asthma remains uncontrolled after 3-6 months of optimised Step 4 treatment, or with 2 or more OCS courses a year, or with any life-threatening attack. [GINA 2025]`,`Each visit: **ACT or ACQ, exacerbations, OCS courses, SABA use, technique, adherence, FEV1**. [GINA 2025]`,`**Biologic review at 4 months**, then every 3-6 months; record OCS dose reduction and exacerbation rate. [GINA 2025]`,`**Azithromycin**: ECG at baseline and after 1 month; stop if QTc prolongs or hearing loss occurs. [GINA 2025]`,`**Long-term OCS**: glucose, BP, weight, DXA scan, eye examination every 1-2 years. [GINA 2025]`,`Share care with primary care: action plan, vaccinations, smoking cessation through **NP-NCD and Ayushman Arogya Mandir** services. [MoHFW 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**GINA 2024-2025** added a definition of **clinical remission on treatment** as the goal in severe asthma. [GINA 2025]`,`**GINA 2025**: difficult-to-treat and severe asthma decision tree retained; **tezepelumab** recommended for severe asthma regardless of biomarkers (age 12 and over). [GINA 2025]`,`**Benralizumab** gained approval for **EGPA** (2024) and for children 6-11 with severe eosinophilic asthma (2024). [FDA 2024]`,`**Dupilumab** gained approval for **COPD with type 2 inflammation** (2024), showing the same T2 logic across airway diseases. [FDA 2024]`,`**Depemokimab**, an ultra-long-acting anti-IL-5 given **100 mg SC every 6 months**, was licensed in 2025 in some countries for severe eosinophilic asthma and CRSwNP; not yet available in India. [GINA 2025]`,`**BTS/NICE/SIGN 2024** (NG245) recommends referral to specialist asthma services for patients uncontrolled on MART with moderate-dose ICS. [BTS/NICE/SIGN 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: 'Difference between difficult-to-treat and severe asthma' - **severe asthma is diagnosed only after adherence, technique and comorbidities are fixed**. [GINA 2025]`,`Viva: name the **target of each biologic** - IgE (omalizumab), IL-5 (mepolizumab, reslizumab), IL-5Ralpha (benralizumab), IL-4Ralpha (dupilumab), TSLP (tezepelumab). [GINA 2025]`,`Viva: why **dupilumab** is avoided with eosinophils over 1500 (transient eosinophilia, possible EGPA unmasking). [GINA 2025]`,`Common mistake: **escalating drugs without watching inhaler technique**. [GINA 2025]`,`Common mistake: **missing ABPA and Strongyloides** in an Indian patient being considered for biologics or long-term OCS. [ISHAM 2024]`,`Common mistake: stopping ICS when a biologic is started - **biologics are add-ons**. [GINA 2025]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Biologics for severe asthma`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Omalizumab (anti-IgE)`,`75-600 mg SC every 2-4 weeks by IgE and weight table; age 6 and over`,`Assess at 4 months; continue if response`,`Anaphylaxis 0.1-0.2% (observe after early doses); not if IgE outside dosing range`],[`Mepolizumab (anti-IL-5)`,`100 mg SC every 4 weeks (40 mg age 6-11); 300 mg in EGPA`,`Assess at 4 months`,`Headache, injection-site reaction, herpes zoster`],[`Benralizumab (anti-IL-5Ralpha)`,`30 mg SC every 4 weeks x3, then every 8 weeks`,`Assess at 4 months`,`Headache, pharyngitis; near-complete eosinophil depletion`],[`Reslizumab (anti-IL-5)`,`3 mg/kg IV every 4 weeks; adults`,`Assess at 4 months`,`Anaphylaxis; not marketed in India`],[`Dupilumab (anti-IL-4Ralpha)`,`400 mg then 200 mg SC every 2 weeks; or 600 mg then 300 mg every 2 weeks (OCS-dependent, atopic dermatitis)`,`Assess at 4 months`,`Conjunctivitis, transient eosinophilia, arthralgia; avoid if eosinophils over 1500`],[`Tezepelumab (anti-TSLP)`,`210 mg SC every 4 weeks; age 12 and over`,`Assess at 4 months`,`Pharyngitis, arthralgia; did not spare OCS in trials`],[`Tiotropium (add-on LAMA)`,`2.5 microgram x 2 puffs soft-mist once daily`,`Continuous`,`Dry mouth, urinary retention`],[`Azithromycin`,`500 mg three times a week or 250 mg daily`,`At least 6 months`,`QTc prolongation, hearing loss, macrolide resistance; exclude NTM`]]},{heading:`Difficult-to-treat versus severe asthma`,columns:[`Feature`,`Difficult-to-treat asthma`,`Severe asthma`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Uncontrolled on medium- or high-dose ICS-LABA, or needs it for control`,`Uncontrolled despite optimised high-dose ICS-LABA after fixing all modifiable factors`],[`Prevalence in adults with asthma`,`About 17%`,`About 3.7%`],[`Main cause`,`Poor adherence, poor technique, comorbidity, exposures, wrong diagnosis`,`Refractory T2 or T2-low airway inflammation`],[`Action`,`Educate, optimise, treat comorbidity, review 3-6 months`,`Phenotype; add-on LAMA, azithromycin, biologics`],[`Who manages`,`Primary care with specialist advice`,`Specialist severe asthma clinic`]]},{heading:`T2 biomarkers and what they mean`,columns:[`Biomarker`,`Cut-off`,`Meaning`,`Guides`],rows:[[`Blood eosinophils`,`150/microlitre or more on high-dose ICS`,`Refractory T2 inflammation`,`Anti-IL-5/5R, dupilumab`],[`Blood eosinophils`,`300/microlitre or more`,`Strong predictor of response`,`Mepolizumab, benralizumab`],[`Blood eosinophils`,`1500/microlitre or more`,`Not asthma alone - look for EGPA, HES, ABPA, parasites`,`Avoid dupilumab`],[`FeNO`,`20 ppb or more on high-dose ICS (25 ppb or more for dupilumab)`,`IL-13 activity`,`Dupilumab`],[`Total IgE with specific sensitisation`,`30-1500 IU/mL with perennial allergen sensitisation`,`Allergic phenotype`,`Omalizumab dosing and eligibility`],[`Sputum eosinophils`,`2% or more`,`Eosinophilic airway inflammation`,`Specialist centres`]]},{heading:`Mimics of severe asthma`,columns:[`Condition`,`Clue`,`Test`],rows:[[`Inducible laryngeal obstruction`,`Inspiratory stridor, throat tightness`,`Laryngoscopy during symptoms; flat inspiratory loop`],[`Dysfunctional breathing`,`Sighing, tingling, normal SpO2`,`Nijmegen questionnaire`],[`ABPA`,`Brown plugs, fleeting shadows`,`Aspergillus-specific IgE, total IgE 500 IU/mL or more, HRCT`],[`EGPA`,`Neuropathy, rash, sinusitis`,`Eosinophils over 1000, ANCA, biopsy`],[`Bronchiectasis or post-TB lung`,`Daily purulent sputum`,`HRCT, sputum culture and CBNAAT`],[`Central airway obstruction`,`Monophonic wheeze, no bronchodilator response`,`Flow-volume loop, CT, bronchoscopy`]]}],redFlags:[`Any life-threatening or near-fatal attack, or ICU admission, in the past year`,`Two or more oral steroid courses a year despite Step 4-5 treatment`,`Blood eosinophils 1500/microlitre or more, neuropathy or vasculitic rash (EGPA)`,`Haemoptysis, weight loss or fixed monophonic wheeze (TB, tumour, central airway lesion)`,`Brown mucus plugs or fleeting infiltrates (ABPA)`,`Maintenance OCS for over 3 months`,`Anaphylaxis after a biologic injection`],pearls:[`**Difficult is not severe**: most difficult asthma is technique, adherence, comorbidity or wrong diagnosis.`,`**Check before you step up**: technique, adherence, comorbidity, exposures, diagnosis.`,`**T2 markers on high-dose ICS**: eosinophils 150 or more, FeNO 20 or more, allergen-driven, or OCS-dependent.`,`Biologic targets: **E-5-5R-4R-TSLP** - omalizumab, mepolizumab, benralizumab, dupilumab, tezepelumab.`,`**Tezepelumab** is the one biologic that does not need a T2 biomarker.`,`**Assess at 4 months** - continue, extend or switch.`,`In India, **exclude ABPA, TB and Strongyloides** before biologics or long-term steroids.`,`**Azithromycin 500 mg thrice weekly for 6 months** - check QTc and exclude NTM first.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-severe-asthma-biologics-q1`,stem:`A 42-year-old man with asthma remains uncontrolled on budesonide-formoterol 400/12 two puffs twice daily. What is the first step before labelling him as having severe asthma?`,options:[`Start omalizumab after measuring total IgE`,`Check inhaler technique, adherence, comorbidities and exposures`,`Start maintenance prednisolone 10 mg daily`,`Refer for bronchial thermoplasty`],answer:1,explanation:`GINA defines severe asthma only after adherence, inhaler technique, comorbidities (rhinitis, GORD, obesity, OSA, anxiety) and exposures such as smoking and biomass smoke have been addressed and the diagnosis confirmed, because most difficult-to-treat asthma is due to these factors. Biologics and maintenance OCS come later, and bronchial thermoplasty is restricted to adults in a registry or trial.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-severe-asthma-biologics-q2`,stem:`Which biologic for severe asthma acts by blocking the IL-4 receptor alpha subunit, thereby inhibiting both IL-4 and IL-13 signalling?`,options:[`Benralizumab`,`Tezepelumab`,`Mepolizumab`,`Dupilumab`],answer:3,explanation:`Dupilumab binds IL-4Ralpha, which is shared by the IL-4 and IL-13 receptors, so it lowers IgE, FeNO and mucus production. Benralizumab targets the IL-5 receptor alpha and depletes eosinophils, mepolizumab neutralises IL-5 itself, and tezepelumab blocks the upstream epithelial alarmin TSLP.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-severe-asthma-biologics-q3`,stem:`A 30-year-old woman with severe asthma has blood eosinophils of 80/microlitre and FeNO 12 ppb on three occasions, no allergen sensitisation and frequent exacerbations despite high-dose ICS-LABA, tiotropium and good adherence. Which biologic is most appropriate?`,options:[`Omalizumab`,`Tezepelumab`,`Benralizumab`,`Mepolizumab`],answer:1,explanation:`Tezepelumab blocks TSLP upstream of both T2 and non-T2 pathways and reduced exacerbations in severe asthma regardless of eosinophil count, so it is the option for T2-low disease. Omalizumab needs allergic sensitisation and an IgE in the dosing range, while benralizumab and mepolizumab need raised blood eosinophils (150-300/microlitre or more) to work.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-severe-asthma-biologics-q4`,stem:`Before starting long-term oral corticosteroids or a biologic in a patient from rural Tamil Nadu with severe eosinophilic asthma, which infection should be specifically excluded or empirically treated?`,options:[`Strongyloides stercoralis`,`Giardia lamblia`,`Entamoeba histolytica`,`Trichomonas vaginalis`],answer:0,explanation:`Strongyloides can cause fatal hyperinfection when steroids or immune modulators are given, and it also raises eosinophils, so GINA advises serology or empirical ivermectin in endemic areas such as India before OCS or biologics. Giardia and Entamoeba are gut protozoa that do not cause steroid-related hyperinfection or eosinophilia, and Trichomonas is a genital infection irrelevant here.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-severe-asthma-biologics-q5`,stem:`A patient started on mepolizumab for severe eosinophilic asthma is reviewed. According to GINA, when should the initial response be formally assessed?`,options:[`After 2 weeks`,`After 4 months`,`After 2 years`,`After the first exacerbation`],answer:1,explanation:`GINA recommends an initial trial of at least 4 months, assessing exacerbations, OCS use, symptom control and lung function; a good responder continues, an unclear response is extended to 6-12 months, and a non-responder switches target. Two weeks is too early for exacerbation outcomes, two years delays switching, and waiting for an exacerbation is not a structured assessment.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-severe-asthma-biologics-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Define difficult-to-treat and severe asthma. Describe the systematic evaluation of a patient with uncontrolled asthma and the role of phenotyping and biologic therapy.`,openingLines:[`Difficult-to-treat asthma is asthma uncontrolled despite medium- or high-dose ICS-LABA, or needing such treatment for control; severe asthma is the subset uncontrolled despite optimised high-dose ICS-LABA after all modifiable factors are addressed (GINA 2025).`,`About 17% of adults with asthma have difficult-to-treat asthma, but only about 3.7% have true severe asthma.`],answer:[{heading:`Confirm diagnosis and find contributory factors`,points:[`Spirometry with reversibility, PEF variability, challenge test.`,`Technique, adherence, comorbidities (CRSwNP, GORD, obesity, OSA, anxiety), exposures (tobacco, biomass), drugs (beta-blockers, NSAIDs).`,`Exclude mimics: laryngeal obstruction, dysfunctional breathing, COPD, ABPA, EGPA, bronchiectasis, TB.`]},{heading:`Optimise treatment`,points:[`MART with ICS-formoterol; high-dose ICS-LABA trial for 3-6 months.`,`Add tiotropium 2.5 microgram x 2 puffs OD or triple inhaler; azithromycin 500 mg thrice weekly for 6 months.`,`Non-drug: rehab, weight loss, CPAP, smoking cessation.`]},{heading:`Phenotype`,points:[`Blood eosinophils 150 or more, FeNO 20 or more, allergen-driven, OCS-dependent = refractory T2.`,`Repeat up to 3 times at lowest OCS dose; check IgE, specific IgE, Aspergillus IgE, Strongyloides.`]},{heading:`Biologics`,points:[`Omalizumab (anti-IgE), mepolizumab (anti-IL-5), benralizumab (anti-IL-5R), dupilumab (anti-IL-4R), tezepelumab (anti-TSLP) with doses.`,`Choose by phenotype and comorbidity; assess at 4 months; continue, extend or switch.`]},{heading:`Other options and follow-up`,points:[`Lowest-dose OCS last resort with bone protection; bronchial thermoplasty in registries.`,`Goal: clinical remission on treatment; review 3-6 monthly.`]}],mustDraw:[`GINA difficult-to-treat and severe asthma decision tree`,`Table of biologics with targets and criteria`],markSplit:[{part:`Definitions`,marks:1},{part:`Evaluation and contributory factors`,marks:3},{part:`Optimisation and add-ons`,marks:2},{part:`Phenotyping and biologics`,marks:3},{part:`Follow-up and goal`,marks:1}],keywords:[`difficult-to-treat`,`severe asthma`,`adherence`,`T2`,`eosinophils`,`FeNO`,`biologics`,`remission`]},{id:`respiratory-severe-asthma-biologics-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on biologic therapy in severe asthma.`,openingLines:[`Biologics are monoclonal antibodies that block specific type 2 (or upstream epithelial) pathways and are add-on therapy for severe asthma uncontrolled on optimised high-dose ICS-LABA.`,`Choice is guided by phenotype: IgE and allergy, blood eosinophils, FeNO and comorbidities.`],answer:[{heading:`Agents and targets`,points:[`Omalizumab - IgE; mepolizumab, reslizumab - IL-5; benralizumab - IL-5Ralpha; dupilumab - IL-4Ralpha; tezepelumab - TSLP.`]},{heading:`Selection`,points:[`Allergic with IgE 30-1500 - omalizumab; eosinophils 300 or more - anti-IL-5/5R; FeNO 25 or more, atopic dermatitis - dupilumab; low T2 - tezepelumab.`]},{heading:`Practicalities`,points:[`Exclude ABPA, TB and Strongyloides; continue ICS; assess at 4 months.`,`Adverse effects: anaphylaxis (omalizumab), conjunctivitis and eosinophilia (dupilumab).`,`Cost and availability are the main barriers in India.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of the T2 cascade with each biologic's target marked`],markSplit:[{part:`Agents and targets`,marks:2},{part:`Selection criteria`,marks:2},{part:`Monitoring and adverse effects`,marks:1}],keywords:[`omalizumab`,`mepolizumab`,`benralizumab`,`dupilumab`,`tezepelumab`,`TSLP`]}],references:[`Global Initiative for Asthma (GINA). Difficult-to-treat and severe asthma guide and Global Strategy for Asthma Management and Prevention, 2025 update`,`Chung KF et al. International ERS/ATS guidelines on definition, evaluation and treatment of severe asthma. Eur Respir J 2014`,`Holguin F et al. Management of severe asthma: ERS/ATS guideline. Eur Respir J 2020`,`BTS/NICE/SIGN. Asthma: diagnosis, monitoring and chronic asthma management (NG245), November 2024`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Asthma`,`Agarwal R et al. Guidelines for diagnosis and management of bronchial asthma: joint ICS/NCCP(I) recommendations. Lung India 2015`,`Hekking PP et al. The prevalence of severe refractory asthma. J Allergy Clin Immunol 2015`]}),n.push({id:`respiratory-abpa-fungal`,title:`ABPA, aspergilloma, chronic pulmonary aspergillosis and mucormycosis`,oneLiner:`**Aspergillus** causes a spectrum set by host immunity - **ABPA** (hypersensitivity in asthma or bronchiectasis: **A. fumigatus-specific IgE 0.35 kUA/L or more plus total IgE 500 IU/mL or more**, treated with prednisolone or itraconazole), **aspergilloma and chronic pulmonary aspergillosis** (saprophytic or slowly invasive disease in old TB cavities, treated with **itraconazole or voriconazole for 6-12 months**) and **invasive aspergillosis** in the immunosuppressed (voriconazole or isavuconazole); **mucormycosis** is an angio-invasive mould infection of uncontrolled diabetes and steroid use, treated with **liposomal amphotericin B plus surgical debridement**.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis`,`ABPA`,`ISHAM-AWG 2024`,`high-attenuation mucus`,`central bronchiectasis`,`aspergilloma`,`fungal ball`,`chronic pulmonary aspergillosis`,`CCPA`,`Aspergillus IgG`,`galactomannan`,`invasive aspergillosis`,`halo sign`,`air-crescent sign`,`mucormycosis`,`black fungus`,`reverse halo sign`,`itraconazole`,`voriconazole`,`liposomal amphotericin B`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Aspergillus fumigatus** spores (2-3 micrometre conidia) are inhaled by everyone daily; disease depends on the host - **hyperreactive immunity gives ABPA, structural lung damage gives aspergilloma and CPA, and immunosuppression gives invasive aspergillosis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ABPA** is an immunological lung disease caused by hypersensitivity to Aspergillus colonising the airways, almost always in **asthma**, and also in **cystic fibrosis, bronchiectasis and COPD**. [ISHAM-AWG 2024]`,`ABPA is estimated in about **2.5% of adults with asthma worldwide (about 4.8 million people)**, with India estimated to carry about **1.4 million cases**; Indian tertiary asthma clinics find much higher rates, and **India leads ABPA research and the ISHAM guidelines**. [Denning, Med Mycol 2013]`,`**Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis (CPA)** affects about **3 million people worldwide**, mostly as a sequel of **pulmonary tuberculosis** - India, with the largest TB burden, has the most cases, many misdiagnosed as 'sputum-negative TB relapse'. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**Invasive aspergillosis** occurs in profound neutropenia, stem cell and solid organ transplant, high-dose steroids, and increasingly in ICU patients with **influenza or COVID-19**. [EORTC/MSGERC 2020]`,`**Mucormycosis** in India is estimated at about **140 per million population** - roughly 70 times the global rate; the 2021 COVID-19 second wave caused **over 45,000 cases ('black fungus')**, mostly rhino-orbito-cerebral in uncontrolled diabetes given steroids. [MoHFW 2021]`,`**Rhizopus arrhizus** causes most mucormycosis in India; uncommon agents such as **Apophysomyces** (trauma) and Rhizopus microsporus are more frequent in India than elsewhere. [ECMM/MSG-ERC 2019]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**ABPA**: poorly controlled asthma, atopy, cystic fibrosis, and genetic variants of HLA-DR2/DR5, IL-4Ralpha, IL-10, surfactant protein A2 and CFTR. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Aspergilloma and CPA**: pre-existing cavities or damaged lung - **healed TB (commonest), sarcoidosis, bronchiectasis, COPD and emphysematous bullae, NTM disease, previous pneumothorax, lung cancer surgery** - with mild immunodeficiency (diabetes, alcohol, low-dose steroids). [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**Invasive aspergillosis**: neutrophils under 500/microlitre for over 10 days, allogeneic HSCT, graft-versus-host disease, **prednisolone 0.3 mg/kg/day or more for 3 weeks or more**, T-cell immunosuppressants, ibrutinib, severe influenza or COVID-19 in ICU. [EORTC/MSGERC 2020]`,`**Mucormycosis**: **uncontrolled diabetes and DKA (commonest in India)**, systemic corticosteroids, haematological malignancy, transplant, **iron overload and deferoxamine therapy**, trauma and burns, and IV drug use. [ECMM/MSG-ERC 2019]`,`**COVID-associated mucormycosis (CAM)** drivers: hyperglycaemia (new or known diabetes), **inappropriate high-dose or prolonged steroids**, tocilizumab, prolonged oxygen and humidifier use, and raised ferritin. [ICMR 2021]`,`**Environmental load**: construction dust, compost, hospital renovation and agricultural work increase Aspergillus and Mucorales spore exposure. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**ABPA**: Aspergillus germinates in thick asthmatic mucus; fungal proteases damage epithelium and drive a **Th2 response (IL-4, IL-5, IL-13)** with high total and specific IgE, specific IgG and eosinophilia - it is hypersensitivity, not tissue invasion. [Harrison 22e]`,`Eosinophil- and fungus-laden **mucus plugs** obstruct proximal bronchi, causing **central bronchiectasis**, fleeting infiltrates, lobar collapse and finally **fibrosis** if untreated. [ISHAM-AWG 2024]`,`**High-attenuation mucus (HAM)** - plugs denser than paraspinal muscle on CT - comes from calcium salts, metals and desiccated mucus, and is pathognomonic of ABPA. [ISHAM-AWG 2024]`,`**Aspergilloma**: fungus grows saprophytically as a ball of hyphae, mucus and debris inside a cavity; bleeding comes from **hypertrophied bronchial arteries** in the cavity wall eroded by fungal toxins and friction. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CPA**: slowly progressive local invasion of the cavity wall, with new cavities, pericavitary infiltrates and **pleural thickening**, causing destruction and fibrosis over months to years. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**Invasive aspergillosis**: hyphae invade blood vessels, causing thrombosis and haemorrhagic infarction - seen as the **halo sign** (nodule with ground-glass rim of haemorrhage) early and the **air-crescent sign** as neutrophils recover and necrotic tissue retracts. [EORTC/MSGERC 2020]`,`**Mucormycosis**: broad hyphae invade arteries causing **thrombosis, infarction and black necrotic eschar**; acidosis and high glucose raise free iron (released from transferrin) which Mucorales use, and **GRP78 receptor** expression rises in DKA, explaining the diabetic link. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Deferoxamine** acts as a siderophore for Rhizopus, delivering iron to the fungus - hence its association; **voriconazole does not cover Mucorales** and its use has been linked to breakthrough mucormycosis. [ECMM/MSG-ERC 2019]`]},{heading:`ISHAM-AWG 2024 ABPA criteria and staging`,points:[`**Predisposing condition or compatible clinico-radiological presentation**: asthma, cystic fibrosis, bronchiectasis, COPD, or a typical presentation. [ISHAM-AWG 2024]`,`**Essential (both)**: **A. fumigatus-specific IgE 0.35 kUA/L or more** and **serum total IgE 500 IU/mL or more**; a total IgE under 500 is accepted only if all other criteria are met. [ISHAM-AWG 2024]`,`**Other criteria (any 2 of 3)**: **A. fumigatus-specific IgG** raised (or positive precipitins); **CT findings consistent with ABPA** (bronchiectasis, mucus impaction, HAM); **blood eosinophils 500/microlitre or more** in a steroid-naive patient (a historical count may be used). [ISHAM-AWG 2024]`,`The 2024 update makes **specific IgE (not the skin test) the screening test**, recommends screening **all adults with asthma in specialist care**, and recognises **ABPM** (allergic bronchopulmonary mycosis) from other fungi. [ISHAM-AWG 2024]`,`**Radiological classification**: ABPA-S (serological, no bronchiectasis), ABPA-B (bronchiectasis), **ABPA-HAM** (high-attenuation mucus - most severe, most relapses), ABPA-CPF (chronic pleuropulmonary fibrosis). [ISHAM-AWG 2024]`,`**Clinical stages**: 0 asymptomatic; 1 acute (1a without and 1b with mucoid impaction); 2 response; 3 exacerbation; 4 remission; 5 treatment-dependent (5a steroid-dependent, 5b on other therapy); 6 advanced (type 2 respiratory failure, cor pulmonale, extensive fibrosis). [ISHAM 2013/2024]`,`**Response** = 50% or greater improvement in symptoms and radiology with a **20% or greater fall in total IgE** at 8 weeks; **exacerbation** = clinical or radiological worsening with a **50% or greater rise in total IgE** over the new baseline. [ISHAM-AWG 2024]`]},{heading:`CPA, aspergilloma and invasive disease - definitions`,points:[`**CPA diagnosis (ERS/ESCMID)**: one or more cavities, with or without a fungal ball, or nodules on imaging, **for at least 3 months**, plus **Aspergillus IgG (or precipitins) raised or other microbiological evidence**, in a patient without major immunocompromise. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**Simple aspergilloma**: a single cavity with a fungal ball, few symptoms and **no radiological progression over at least 3 months**. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**Chronic cavitary PA (CCPA)**: one or more cavities, with or without fungal ball, with symptoms and progression over months; **chronic fibrosing PA (CFPA)** is CCPA with extensive fibrosis of at least two lobes. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**Aspergillus nodule**: one or more nodules, sometimes cavitating, mimicking tuberculoma or cancer - diagnosed on histology. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**Subacute invasive aspergillosis (SAIA)**: CPA-like disease evolving over **1-3 months** in mildly immunocompromised patients (diabetes, steroids, malnutrition), with hyphae invading tissue - treated like invasive aspergillosis. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**Invasive aspergillosis**: graded **proven, probable or possible** (EORTC/MSGERC) on host factor, clinical (CT) feature and mycological evidence (galactomannan, culture, PCR). [EORTC/MSGERC 2020]`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`**ABPA**: poorly controlled asthma, frequent exacerbations, **expectoration of brownish-black mucus plugs** (in about a third), low-grade fever, haemoptysis, and **fleeting infiltrates** often treated as TB. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Aspergilloma**: often asymptomatic; **haemoptysis** (up to 50-80%, occasionally massive) is the main symptom. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CPA**: **cough, weight loss, fatigue, breathlessness and haemoptysis** for months in a patient treated for TB - a 'sputum-negative TB relapse' with a new fungal ball or pleural thickening should raise suspicion. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**Invasive pulmonary aspergillosis**: persistent fever despite broad-spectrum antibiotics in neutropenia, pleuritic chest pain, cough and haemoptysis. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Rhino-orbito-cerebral mucormycosis**: facial pain and swelling, nasal blockage with blood-tinged or black discharge, **black eschar on palate or turbinates**, toothache or loose teeth, periorbital swelling, **ptosis, proptosis, ophthalmoplegia, vision loss** and cranial nerve palsies. [ICMR 2021]`,`**Pulmonary mucormycosis**: rapidly progressive pneumonia with fever, pleuritic pain and haemoptysis (sometimes fatal from vessel erosion) in neutropenic or diabetic patients. [ECMM/MSG-ERC 2019]`]},{heading:`Examination`,points:[`**ABPA**: polyphonic wheeze, coarse crackles over bronchiectasis, **clubbing** in advanced fibrotic disease, and signs of cor pulmonale in stage 6. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Aspergilloma and CPA**: features of old TB - **tracheal deviation, apical flattening, crackles** - with weight loss and sometimes clubbing. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mucormycosis**: inspect the **palate, nasal turbinates and face for black necrosis**; test visual acuity, pupils, eye movements and facial sensation. [ICMR 2021]`,`**Invasive aspergillosis**: few chest signs despite extensive CT lesions; look for skin lesions and sinus tenderness. [IDSA 2016]`,`Bedside **capillary glucose and urine ketones** in every suspected mucormycosis. [ICMR 2021]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Pulmonary TB**: ABPA infiltrates and CPA cavities are often treated empirically as TB - insist on **CBNAAT and culture**, and check Aspergillus IgE and IgG in patients 'not responding to ATT'. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**Eosinophilic pneumonia and EGPA**: peripheral infiltrates and eosinophilia, but no Aspergillus sensitisation; EGPA has neuropathy and vasculitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tropical pulmonary eosinophilia**: very high eosinophils (over 3000), high filarial antibodies, response to DEC. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Severe asthma with fungal sensitisation (SAFS)**: severe asthma sensitised to fungi but **total IgE under 1000 and no bronchiectasis or mucus plugs** (older definition) - a step before ABPA. [ISHAM-AWG 2024]`,`**Lung cancer, lung abscess, cavitating NTM or Klebsiella** mimic CPA and aspergilloma - CT, cultures and biopsy decide. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**Aspergillus versus Mucor in the immunocompromised**: **reverse halo sign, more than 10 nodules, pleural effusion, sinusitis and prior voriconazole** favour mucormycosis; a negative galactomannan does not exclude it. [ECMM/MSG-ERC 2019]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**ABPA first line**: **A. fumigatus-specific IgE** (screen all asthmatics in specialist care), **total IgE**, blood eosinophils, and **HRCT chest** for bronchiectasis, mucus impaction and HAM. [ISHAM-AWG 2024]`,`**Aspergillus skin prick test** (type I reaction) is cheap where IgE testing is unavailable, but is less sensitive than specific IgE. [ISHAM-AWG 2024]`,`**Aspergillus-specific IgG** is the key test for **CPA** (sensitivity about 90%) and supports ABPA; precipitins are the older, less sensitive alternative. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**Sputum KOH and fungal culture**, and **sputum CBNAAT and mycobacterial culture** to exclude TB in every cavity. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**Galactomannan**: serum index **0.5 or more** or BAL **1.0 or more** supports invasive aspergillosis; serum galactomannan is insensitive in non-neutropenic patients and in CPA. [EORTC/MSGERC 2020]`,`**CT signs in invasive aspergillosis**: dense nodule with **halo sign** (early), **air-crescent sign** (late), wedge-shaped infarct, cavity. [EORTC/MSGERC 2020]`,`**Mucormycosis**: **KOH mount and calcofluor white** of nasal tissue or BAL showing **broad (5-20 micrometre), ribbon-like, pauci-septate hyphae branching at right angles**; histopathology for tissue invasion; culture for species. [ECMM/MSG-ERC 2019]`,`**Contrast MRI of paranasal sinuses, orbit and brain** for rhino-orbito-cerebral disease; CT chest for pulmonary mucormycosis (reverse halo, vessel occlusion sign). [ICMR 2021]`,`Monitor **total IgE every 6-8 weeks** during ABPA treatment, and **itraconazole trough level (0.5-1 mg/L or more)** and **voriconazole trough (1-5.5 mg/L)** to ensure absorption and avoid toxicity. [ISHAM-AWG 2024]`]},{heading:`Management of ABPA`,points:[`**Asymptomatic ABPA (stage 0)** and ABPA-S with good asthma control: no specific treatment; optimise asthma and monitor IgE and symptoms. [ISHAM-AWG 2024]`,`**Acute ABPA**: either **oral prednisolone 0.5 mg/kg/day for 2 weeks, then tapered to stop over 3-4 months** (medium dose; higher doses add only side effects), **or itraconazole 200 mg twice daily for 4 months** as monotherapy. [ISHAM-AWG 2024]`,`**Prednisolone gives a faster and higher response rate**; itraconazole monotherapy is preferred when steroids are risky (diabetes, osteoporosis) - both reduce IgE and exacerbations. [ISHAM-AWG 2024]`,`**Recurrent exacerbations or steroid-dependent ABPA**: **prednisolone plus itraconazole** together; **voriconazole 200 mg twice daily** or posaconazole if itraconazole fails or is not tolerated. [ISHAM-AWG 2024]`,`**Biologics** (omalizumab, mepolizumab, benralizumab, dupilumab) are options for **steroid-dependent ABPA or frequent exacerbations** despite azoles; nebulised amphotericin is used in some centres to prevent exacerbations. [ISHAM-AWG 2024]`,`**Bronchoscopic clearance** of mucus plugs if **lobar collapse persists** despite treatment for 2-4 weeks. [ISHAM-AWG 2024]`,`Continue **ICS-formoterol or ICS-LABA** for asthma throughout - ABPA treatment does not replace asthma treatment. [GINA 2025]`,`Itraconazole: take **capsules with food and an acidic drink** (avoid PPIs and antacids); SUBA (super-bioavailable) itraconazole formulations widely sold in India give better absorption, but the trial-tested regimen is conventional 200 mg twice daily. [ISHAM-AWG 2024]`]},{heading:`Management of aspergilloma and CPA`,points:[`**Simple aspergilloma**, asymptomatic: observation with 3-6 monthly imaging; **surgical resection (usually lobectomy)** if haemoptysis or growing, in fit patients with adequate lung function. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**CCPA/CFPA**: oral **itraconazole 200 mg twice daily or voriconazole 150-200 mg twice daily** for a **minimum of 6 months**; an Indian RCT found **12 months reduced relapse** compared with 6 months. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**Posaconazole or isavuconazole** for azole intolerance; **IV micafungin or liposomal amphotericin** for azole resistance or failure. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`Assess **response at 3-6 months** by symptoms, weight, CT and falling Aspergillus IgG; relapse after stopping occurs in about a quarter to a third - long-term or repeated courses may be needed. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**Haemoptysis**: **tranexamic acid 500 mg-1 g orally 3 times daily** for mild bleeding; **bronchial artery embolisation** for severe or recurrent bleeding as a bridge, then surgery (see the haemoptysis topic). [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`Antifungals penetrate a fungus ball poorly, so a simple aspergilloma usually needs **surgery rather than drugs**; intracavitary amphotericin is reserved for unfit patients with bleeding. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Do not restart ATT empirically** in a treated TB patient with new symptoms and a fungal ball - confirm TB by CBNAAT and culture first. [MoHFW 2024]`]},{heading:`Invasive aspergillosis and mucormycosis - inpatient care`,points:[`**Invasive aspergillosis first line**: **voriconazole 6 mg/kg IV 12-hourly for 2 doses, then 4 mg/kg 12-hourly** (oral 200-300 mg 12-hourly when stable), or **isavuconazole 200 mg 8-hourly for 6 doses then 200 mg daily**; posaconazole is an alternative. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Liposomal amphotericin B 3-5 mg/kg/day** is the alternative for invasive aspergillosis; treat for **at least 6-12 weeks** and until immunosuppression resolves. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Mucormycosis triad**: (1) **early liposomal amphotericin B 5 mg/kg/day (10 mg/kg/day with CNS involvement)**; (2) **urgent, repeated surgical debridement** of necrotic tissue; (3) **reverse the predisposition** - control glucose and ketoacidosis, taper steroids, stop deferoxamine. [ECMM/MSG-ERC 2019]`,`If liposomal amphotericin is unavailable (as in the 2021 Indian shortage), use **amphotericin B deoxycholate 1-1.5 mg/kg/day** with saline pre-loading and electrolyte monitoring. [ICMR 2021]`,`**Step-down** after 3-6 weeks of response: **posaconazole delayed-release tablets 300 mg twice daily on day 1, then 300 mg daily**, or **isavuconazole 200 mg 8-hourly for 6 doses then 200 mg daily**, continued for months until clinical and radiological resolution. [ECMM/MSG-ERC 2019]`,`**Amphotericin monitoring**: daily creatinine, **potassium and magnesium**; give **500 mL normal saline before each dose**; nephrotoxicity, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia and infusion reactions are expected. [ECMM/MSG-ERC 2019]`,`**Voriconazole does not treat mucormycosis**; **posaconazole and isavuconazole** cover both Aspergillus and Mucorales. [ECMM/MSG-ERC 2019]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Diabetes**: control glucose before and during steroid therapy for ABPA; itraconazole monotherapy avoids steroid hyperglycaemia. [ISHAM-AWG 2024]`,`**Drug interactions**: itraconazole, voriconazole and posaconazole are **strong CYP3A4 inhibitors** - avoid with simvastatin, and adjust tacrolimus and cyclosporine; **rifampicin makes azole levels sub-therapeutic**, a common issue in TB-CPA overlap. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Itraconazole is negatively inotropic** - avoid in **heart failure**; it also causes hypokalaemia, hypertension and oedema. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Pregnancy**: azoles are teratogenic and avoided (especially first trimester); **amphotericin B is the antifungal of choice**; ABPA in pregnancy is treated with prednisolone. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Children with cystic fibrosis**: ABPA criteria and treatment are similar; screen annually with total IgE. [ISHAM-AWG 2024]`,`**CKD**: avoid IV voriconazole (cyclodextrin vehicle accumulates) with creatinine clearance under 50 mL/min - use oral voriconazole; amphotericin needs close renal monitoring. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Post-COVID and ICU**: think of **COVID- or influenza-associated pulmonary aspergillosis** in ventilated patients who deteriorate; BAL galactomannan helps. [ECMM/ISHAM 2020]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**ABPA**: recurrent exacerbations (about half within 2 years), progressive **bronchiectasis, fibrosis, type 2 respiratory failure and cor pulmonale**; HAM predicts relapse. [ISHAM-AWG 2024]`,`**Aspergilloma**: **massive haemoptysis** is the main killer; the fungus ball can spontaneously lyse in about 10%. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CPA**: progressive lung destruction; reported **5-year mortality around 40-50%**, worse with CFPA, NTM, COPD and low BMI. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**Invasive aspergillosis**: mortality 30-60%, higher in persistent neutropenia and CNS disease. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Mucormycosis**: overall mortality about **40-50%** (rhino-orbital lower; pulmonary, disseminated and cerebral up to 70-90%), with **orbital exenteration and maxillectomy** in survivors. [ECMM/MSG-ERC 2019]`,`**Drug toxicity**: steroid side effects in ABPA; azole hepatotoxicity, neuropathy, visual disturbance and photosensitivity (voriconazole); amphotericin nephrotoxicity. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and prevention`,points:[`**ABPA follow-up**: symptoms, spirometry and **total IgE every 6-8 weeks** during treatment and then 3-6 monthly; a new IgE baseline is set after treatment. [ISHAM-AWG 2024]`,`**Liver function tests** at baseline, 2-4 weeks and monthly on azoles; ECG for QTc with voriconazole and posaconazole. [IDSA 2016]`,`**CPA follow-up**: CT and Aspergillus IgG at 3-6 months, then 6-monthly; refer early to a chest physician and thoracic surgeon for resectable disease. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**Refer urgently (same day)**: any suspected mucormycosis (black nasal discharge, facial pain, eye signs in a diabetic), haemoptysis with aspergilloma, or invasive aspergillosis in the immunosuppressed. [ICMR 2021]`,`**Prevention of mucormycosis**: **judicious steroid use (right dose, right duration), tight glucose control**, clean humidifier water with sterile water, and early ENT and eye examination in high-risk patients. [ICMR 2021]`,`**Screen every patient with uncontrolled asthma for ABPA** with Aspergillus-specific IgE - a simple step that changes treatment. [ISHAM-AWG 2024]`,`Prevent CPA by **completing TB treatment** under NTEP and offering post-TB follow-up, where CPA can be detected early. [MoHFW 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates and exam angle`,points:[`**ISHAM-AWG 2024 (revised ABPA guidelines, led by Indian investigators)**: specific IgE as screening test, total IgE 500 as essential (with exception), CT features and eosinophils 500 as supporting, ABPM included, either prednisolone or itraconazole as first-line monotherapy. [ISHAM-AWG 2024]`,`**WHO Fungal Priority Pathogens List 2022** places **Aspergillus fumigatus** in the critical group and **Mucorales** in the high-priority group. [WHO 2022]`,`**EORTC/MSGERC 2020** definitions of invasive fungal disease remain the standard for proven, probable and possible categories. [EORTC/MSGERC 2020]`,`Viva favourite: 'ABPA criteria' - say **predisposition, two essentials (specific IgE, total IgE 500), and two of three others (IgG, CT, eosinophils 500)**. [ISHAM-AWG 2024]`,`Viva: **halo versus air-crescent versus reverse halo sign** - early IA, late IA with neutrophil recovery, and mucormycosis respectively. [EORTC/MSGERC 2020]`,`Common mistake: **treating ABPA or CPA as TB** without microbiological confirmation, and **using voriconazole for mucormycosis**. [ISHAM-AWG 2024]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Antifungal drugs for Aspergillus and Mucorales`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Prednisolone (ABPA)`,`0.5 mg/kg/day oral for 2 weeks, then taper`,`Stop over 3-4 months total`,`Hyperglycaemia, osteoporosis, weight gain; relapse on stopping`],[`Itraconazole`,`200 mg oral twice daily with food`,`4 months (ABPA); 6-12 months (CPA); trough 0.5-1 mg/L or more`,`Hepatotoxicity, heart failure, hypokalaemia, oedema; CYP3A4 interactions; poor absorption with PPIs`],[`Voriconazole`,`IA: 6 mg/kg IV 12-hourly x2 then 4 mg/kg 12-hourly; oral 200-300 mg 12-hourly; CPA/ABPA 150-200 mg 12-hourly`,`IA at least 6-12 weeks; trough 1-5.5 mg/L`,`Visual disturbance, hallucinations, photosensitivity, skin cancer, periostitis, hepatotoxicity, QTc; no Mucorales cover`],[`Isavuconazole`,`200 mg 8-hourly x6 doses, then 200 mg daily, IV or oral`,`Weeks to months`,`Fewer hepatic and visual effects; shortens QTc; covers Aspergillus and Mucorales`],[`Posaconazole`,`300 mg twice daily day 1, then 300 mg daily (delayed-release tablet or IV)`,`Step-down for months`,`Hepatotoxicity, QTc, hypokalaemia; covers Mucorales`],[`Liposomal amphotericin B`,`Mucor 5 mg/kg/day IV (10 mg/kg CNS); IA 3-5 mg/kg/day`,`3-6 weeks then oral step-down`,`Nephrotoxicity, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, infusion reactions; saline pre-load`],[`Amphotericin B deoxycholate`,`1-1.5 mg/kg/day IV over 4-6 hours`,`When liposomal unavailable`,`More nephrotoxicity, rigors, hypokalaemia, anaemia`],[`Tranexamic acid`,`500 mg-1 g oral 3 times daily`,`During haemoptysis`,`Thrombosis risk; avoid in active thromboembolism`]]},{heading:`ISHAM-AWG 2024 criteria for ABPA`,columns:[`Component`,`Requirement`],rows:[[`Predisposing condition`,`Asthma, cystic fibrosis, bronchiectasis, COPD, or compatible clinico-radiological picture`],[`Essential 1`,`A. fumigatus-specific IgE 0.35 kUA/L or more`],[`Essential 2`,`Serum total IgE 500 IU/mL or more (lower accepted only if all other criteria met)`],[`Other (2 of 3) - serology`,`Raised A. fumigatus-specific IgG or positive precipitins`],[`Other (2 of 3) - imaging`,`CT consistent with ABPA: bronchiectasis, mucus impaction, high-attenuation mucus`],[`Other (2 of 3) - blood`,`Eosinophils 500/microlitre or more in steroid-naive patient (historical allowed)`]]},{heading:`Spectrum of pulmonary aspergillosis`,columns:[`Entity`,`Host`,`Key test`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`ABPA`,`Asthma, CF - hypersensitivity`,`Specific IgE, total IgE 500 or more, HRCT`,`Prednisolone or itraconazole`],[`Simple aspergilloma`,`Old cavity, normal immunity`,`Fungal ball, Aspergillus IgG, stable 3 months`,`Observe or resect`],[`CCPA / CFPA`,`Post-TB, COPD, mild immunodeficiency`,`Cavities over 3 months, Aspergillus IgG`,`Itraconazole or voriconazole 6-12 months`],[`SAIA`,`Mild immunosuppression (diabetes, steroids)`,`Progression over 1-3 months, galactomannan, biopsy`,`As invasive aspergillosis`],[`Invasive aspergillosis`,`Neutropenia, transplant, high-dose steroids, ICU influenza or COVID`,`Halo sign, galactomannan`,`Voriconazole or isavuconazole`]]},{heading:`Invasive aspergillosis versus mucormycosis`,columns:[`Feature`,`Invasive aspergillosis`,`Mucormycosis`],rows:[[`Main host in India`,`Neutropenia, transplant, ICU`,`Uncontrolled diabetes, DKA, steroids`],[`Hyphae`,`Narrow, septate, acute-angle (45 degree) branching`,`Broad, ribbon-like, pauci-septate, right-angle branching`],[`CT sign`,`Halo sign, air-crescent sign`,`Reverse halo sign, more than 10 nodules, effusion`],[`Galactomannan`,`Positive`,`Negative`],[`Drug of choice`,`Voriconazole or isavuconazole`,`Liposomal amphotericin B plus surgery`],[`Voriconazole`,`First line`,`Ineffective; may select Mucorales`]]}],redFlags:[`Black nasal discharge, palatal eschar, facial swelling or eye signs in a diabetic or steroid-treated patient (mucormycosis)`,`Haemoptysis in a patient with an aspergilloma or post-TB cavity`,`Persistent fever with new nodules in neutropenia or after transplant`,`Lobar collapse from mucus plugging in ABPA`,`Type 2 respiratory failure or cor pulmonale in advanced ABPA`,`Diabetic ketoacidosis with sinusitis or new pulmonary infiltrate`,`Progressive weight loss and new cavities after completed TB treatment`],pearls:[`**ABPA = 2 essentials + 2 of 3**: specific IgE and total IgE 500, plus IgG, CT, eosinophils 500.`,`**High-attenuation mucus** on CT is pathognomonic of ABPA.`,`**Specific IgE** is now the ABPA screening test - screen every uncontrolled asthmatic.`,`**Prednisolone 0.5 mg/kg or itraconazole 200 mg BD for 4 months** - either for acute ABPA.`,`CPA = **cavity over 3 months + Aspergillus IgG**; treat **6-12 months** with an azole.`,`**Halo early, crescent late** in invasive aspergillosis; **reverse halo** in mucormycosis.`,`Mucor triad: **liposomal amphotericin + debridement + glucose control**.`,`**Voriconazole misses Mucor**; posaconazole and isavuconazole cover both.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-abpa-fungal-q1`,stem:`According to ISHAM-AWG 2024, which pair of tests forms the essential components for diagnosing ABPA in a patient with asthma?`,options:[`Aspergillus skin test and serum precipitins`,`Blood eosinophils 500/microlitre and central bronchiectasis`,`A. fumigatus-specific IgE 0.35 kUA/L or more and total IgE 500 IU/mL or more`,`Positive sputum culture and serum galactomannan`],answer:2,explanation:`The 2024 ISHAM-AWG criteria make A. fumigatus-specific IgE and total IgE of 500 IU/mL or more the essential components, with any two of raised specific IgG, compatible CT findings and eosinophils 500/microlitre or more as supporting criteria. The skin test is no longer essential, eosinophils and bronchiectasis are only supporting criteria, and galactomannan and sputum culture are used for invasive disease, not ABPA.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-abpa-fungal-q2`,stem:`A 50-year-old man treated for pulmonary TB 4 years ago has had cough, weight loss and intermittent haemoptysis for 6 months. Sputum CBNAAT is negative twice. CT shows a thick-walled upper lobe cavity with a fungal ball and pleural thickening that has enlarged over 4 months. Aspergillus IgG is raised. What is the most appropriate treatment?`,options:[`Restart the full first-line antitubercular regimen`,`Oral itraconazole 200 mg twice daily for 6-12 months`,`Prednisolone 0.5 mg/kg/day tapered over 4 months`,`Observation with yearly X-ray`],answer:1,explanation:`This is chronic cavitary pulmonary aspergillosis (symptoms and progression over 3 months with raised Aspergillus IgG), treated with itraconazole or voriconazole for at least 6 months; an Indian trial supports 12 months to reduce relapse. Restarting ATT without microbiological proof is a common Indian error, prednisolone is for ABPA and would worsen CPA, and observation suits only a stable asymptomatic simple aspergilloma.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-abpa-fungal-q3`,stem:`A 58-year-old woman with poorly controlled diabetes, recently given dexamethasone for COVID-19, presents with left facial pain, black nasal discharge and proptosis. Nasal KOH shows broad, aseptate hyphae branching at right angles. Which drug should be started?`,options:[`IV voriconazole`,`Oral fluconazole`,`IV caspofungin`,`IV liposomal amphotericin B`],answer:3,explanation:`Broad pauci-septate right-angle hyphae in a diabetic on steroids indicate rhino-orbital mucormycosis, treated with liposomal amphotericin B 5 mg/kg/day (10 mg/kg with CNS involvement) plus urgent surgical debridement and glucose control. Voriconazole and fluconazole have no activity against Mucorales, and echinocandins such as caspofungin are not effective as monotherapy.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-abpa-fungal-q4`,stem:`On CT chest of a neutropenic patient with leukaemia, a nodule surrounded by a ground-glass rim is seen. What does this sign represent?`,options:[`Haemorrhage around an angio-invasive fungal nodule, typical of early invasive aspergillosis`,`Necrotic lung retracting from viable tissue after neutrophil recovery`,`Central ground-glass with a peripheral consolidation ring, typical of mucormycosis`,`A fungal ball moving freely in a pre-existing cavity`],answer:0,explanation:`The halo sign is a nodule with a ground-glass rim caused by haemorrhagic infarction around angio-invasive hyphae and is an early sign of invasive aspergillosis in neutropenia. The air-crescent sign appears later as neutrophils recover, the reverse halo sign (central ground-glass with a consolidated rim) suggests mucormycosis, and a mobile fungal ball in a cavity is an aspergilloma.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-abpa-fungal-q5`,stem:`A patient with ABPA starts treatment. At 8 weeks, which change in total IgE defines a satisfactory response according to ISHAM-AWG?`,options:[`A fall of 10% or more`,`A fall of 20% or more`,`Normalisation below 100 IU/mL`,`A rise of up to 50%`],answer:1,explanation:`Response in ABPA is defined as clinical and radiological improvement with a fall of 20% or more in total IgE at 8 weeks; IgE rarely normalises, so normalisation is not the target, and a 10% fall is insufficient. A rise of 50% or more over the new baseline with worsening defines an exacerbation, not a response.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-abpa-fungal-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Describe the pathogenesis, diagnostic criteria (ISHAM-AWG 2024), staging and management of allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis. How will you differentiate it from pulmonary tuberculosis?`,openingLines:[`ABPA is an immunological lung disease caused by hypersensitivity to Aspergillus fumigatus colonising the airways of patients with asthma, cystic fibrosis or bronchiectasis.`,`India carries an estimated 1.4 million cases and Indian investigators led the 2024 ISHAM-AWG revised criteria.`],answer:[{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Germination in thick mucus, protease epithelial damage, Th2 response with IgE, IgG and eosinophils.`,`Mucus plugs cause central bronchiectasis, collapse, fibrosis; HAM pathognomonic.`]},{heading:`Diagnostic criteria (ISHAM-AWG 2024)`,points:[`Predisposing condition.`,`Essential: specific IgE 0.35 kUA/L or more and total IgE 500 IU/mL or more.`,`Two of: raised specific IgG, CT consistent, eosinophils 500 or more.`]},{heading:`Radiological types and stages`,points:[`ABPA-S, ABPA-B, ABPA-HAM, ABPA-CPF.`,`Stages 0-6: asymptomatic, acute, response, exacerbation, remission, treatment-dependent, advanced.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Acute: prednisolone 0.5 mg/kg for 2 weeks tapered over 3-4 months, or itraconazole 200 mg BD for 4 months.`,`Recurrent: combination; voriconazole; biologics for steroid dependence; bronchoscopy for persistent collapse.`,`Continue asthma treatment.`]},{heading:`Monitoring`,points:[`IgE every 6-8 weeks; response = 20% fall; exacerbation = 50% rise.`,`LFTs and itraconazole levels.`]},{heading:`ABPA versus TB`,points:[`ABPA: asthma, fleeting central infiltrates, high IgE and eosinophils, mucus plugs, CBNAAT negative.`,`TB: fever, weight loss, upper lobe cavity, CBNAAT or culture positive.`]}],mustDraw:[`ISHAM 2024 criteria box`,`Flow chart of ABPA treatment by stage`],markSplit:[{part:`Pathogenesis`,marks:2},{part:`Criteria and staging`,marks:3},{part:`Treatment and monitoring`,marks:3},{part:`Difference from TB`,marks:2}],keywords:[`ABPA`,`ISHAM-AWG 2024`,`total IgE`,`specific IgE`,`HAM`,`itraconazole`,`prednisolone`]},{id:`respiratory-abpa-fungal-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate invasive pulmonary aspergillosis from pulmonary mucormycosis.`,openingLines:[`Both are angio-invasive mould infections of immunocompromised hosts causing infarction and haemoptysis.`,`They differ in host, microscopy, imaging, biomarkers and drug of choice, and the distinction decides survival.`],answer:[{heading:`Host and organism`,points:[`IA: neutropenia, transplant, ICU influenza or COVID; narrow septate acute-angle hyphae.`,`Mucor: diabetes, DKA, steroids, iron overload; broad pauci-septate right-angle hyphae.`]},{heading:`Imaging and tests`,points:[`IA: halo and air-crescent signs; galactomannan positive.`,`Mucor: reverse halo, more than 10 nodules, effusion; galactomannan negative; KOH and histology.`]},{heading:`Treatment and outcome`,points:[`IA: voriconazole or isavuconazole; mortality 30-60%.`,`Mucor: liposomal amphotericin 5-10 mg/kg plus surgery; step-down posaconazole or isavuconazole; mortality 40-70%.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column comparison table`,`Sketch of hyphae: acute-angle septate versus right-angle ribbon-like`],markSplit:[{part:`Host and microbiology`,marks:2},{part:`Imaging and biomarkers`,marks:1.5},{part:`Treatment and prognosis`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`halo sign`,`reverse halo`,`galactomannan`,`voriconazole`,`amphotericin`,`debridement`]}],references:[`Agarwal R et al. Revised ISHAM-ABPA working group clinical practice guidelines for diagnosing, classifying and treating allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis/mycoses. Eur Respir J 2024`,`Denning DW et al. Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis: rationale and clinical guidelines for diagnosis and management (ESCMID/ERS). Eur Respir J 2016`,`Patterson TF et al. Practice guidelines for the diagnosis and management of aspergillosis: 2016 update by the IDSA. Clin Infect Dis 2016`,`Cornely OA et al. Global guideline for the diagnosis and management of mucormycosis (ECMM/MSG-ERC). Lancet Infect Dis 2019`,`Donnelly JP et al. Revision and update of the consensus definitions of invasive fungal disease (EORTC/MSGERC). Clin Infect Dis 2020`,`ICMR/MoHFW. Evidence-based advisory on screening, diagnosis and management of mucormycosis in the era of COVID-19, 2021`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Aspergillosis; Mucormycosis`,`WHO Fungal Priority Pathogens List, 2022`]}),n.push({id:`respiratory-aecopd-niv`,title:`Acute exacerbation of COPD and non-invasive ventilation`,oneLiner:`An **exacerbation of COPD** is an event of **worsening dyspnoea and/or cough and sputum over less than 14 days**, often with tachypnoea and tachycardia, driven by airway and systemic inflammation from infection or pollution (Rome 2021, GOLD); it is graded by the **Rome criteria (dyspnoea VAS, RR, HR, SpO2, CRP, ABG)** and treated with **controlled oxygen to SpO2 88-92%, short-acting bronchodilators, prednisolone 40 mg for 5 days, antibiotics when sputum is purulent**, and **NIV for respiratory acidosis (pH 7.35 or less with PaCO2 over 6.0-6.5 kPa)**.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`AECOPD`,`COPD exacerbation`,`Rome definition`,`Anthonisen criteria`,`acute hypercapnic respiratory failure`,`type 2 respiratory failure`,`NIV`,`BiPAP`,`IPAP`,`EPAP`,`Venturi mask`,`controlled oxygen`,`prednisolone 40 mg`,`ipratropium`,`CRP-guided antibiotics`,`pulmonary rehabilitation`,`home NIV`,`GOLD 2025`,`BTS/ICS`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**GOLD (from 2023, based on the Rome proposal 2021)**: an exacerbation is an event characterised by **dyspnoea and/or cough and sputum that worsen over less than 14 days**, which may be accompanied by **tachypnoea and/or tachycardia**, and is often associated with increased local and systemic inflammation caused by infection, pollution or other insult to the airways. [GOLD 2025]`,`The older definition - an acute worsening of respiratory symptoms that results in additional therapy - graded exacerbations **retrospectively by treatment** (mild: SABD only; moderate: plus antibiotics and/or OCS; severe: hospital or ED visit). [GOLD 2025]`,`Exacerbations are the **main drivers of COPD mortality, lung-function decline, poor quality of life and cost**; about half of hospital costs of COPD come from exacerbation admissions. [GOLD 2025]`,`India has an estimated **55 million people with COPD (GBD 2016)**, and COPD is the **second leading cause of death in India**; biomass smoke exposure explains a large share in women and non-smokers. [ICMR-PHFI-IHME India State-Level Disease Burden 2017]`,`**Mortality after hospitalisation** is high: in-hospital mortality is a few percent, but rises sharply in those needing ventilation, and **about 50% of patients die within 5 years** of an exacerbation admission. [GOLD 2025]`,`About **20% of patients are readmitted within 30 days**, and the strongest predictor of an exacerbation is **a previous exacerbation** (the 'frequent exacerbator' phenotype, 2 or more a year). [GOLD 2025]`]},{heading:`Triggers and risk factors`,points:[`**Respiratory viruses** (rhinovirus commonest, also influenza, RSV, SARS-CoV-2) trigger about half of exacerbations, often followed by bacterial infection. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Bacteria**: **Haemophilus influenzae, Streptococcus pneumoniae, Moraxella catarrhalis**; **Pseudomonas aeruginosa** and Enterobacterales in severe COPD (FEV1 under 30%), recent antibiotics or hospitalisation, and bronchiectasis. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Air pollution** (PM2.5, NO2, winter smog), cold weather, biomass and tobacco smoke - Indian winters and Diwali bring admission peaks. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Risk factors for frequent exacerbations**: previous exacerbations, severe airflow limitation, **blood eosinophils 300/microlitre or more**, chronic bronchitis, GORD, poor adherence and inhaler technique. [GOLD 2025]`,`About a third of exacerbations have **no identifiable cause**; non-adherence, sedatives and beta-blocker misuse can precipitate them. [GOLD 2025]`,`Mimics that also trigger worsening: **pneumonia, pulmonary embolism, heart failure, arrhythmia, pneumothorax and ACS** - GOLD asks clinicians to look actively for them. [GOLD 2025]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why patients go into type 2 failure`,points:[`Infection or pollution raises **airway inflammation (neutrophils, sometimes eosinophils)**, oedema and mucus, so expiratory flow limitation worsens. [Harrison 22e]`,`Faster breathing shortens expiratory time, causing **dynamic hyperinflation and intrinsic PEEP**, flattening the diaphragm and greatly increasing the work and oxygen cost of breathing. [Harrison 22e]`,`**V/Q mismatch** causes hypoxaemia; the fatigued, mechanically disadvantaged respiratory pump cannot maintain alveolar ventilation, so **PaCO2 rises and pH falls** (acute or acute-on-chronic respiratory acidosis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why uncontrolled oxygen harms**: high FiO2 releases hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction (more dead space ventilation), reduces haemoglobin's CO2 carriage (**Haldane effect**) and, to a lesser degree, reduces hypoxic drive - all raising PaCO2. [BTS 2017]`,`**How NIV works**: **EPAP offsets intrinsic PEEP** (reducing the inspiratory load needed to trigger flow) and keeps the upper airway open, while **IPAP (pressure support) increases tidal volume**, unloads the diaphragm and lowers PaCO2. [BTS/ICS 2016]`,`Systemic inflammation increases **cardiovascular risk** in the weeks after an exacerbation - MI and stroke risk rise in the following 30 days. [GOLD 2025]`,`Steroids shorten recovery by reducing airway inflammation; **eosinophilic exacerbations (eosinophils 2% or more, or 300/microlitre or more)** respond best. [GOLD 2025]`]},{heading:`Severity classification - Rome 2021 (GOLD) and the clinical grading`,points:[`**Rome mild**: dyspnoea VAS under 5, **RR under 24/min, HR under 95/min**, resting SpO2 92% or more on air (or usual oxygen) with a change of 3% or less, and **CRP under 10 mg/L** if measured. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Rome moderate**: at least **3 of 5**: dyspnoea VAS 5 or more, **RR 24/min or more, HR 95/min or more, SpO2 under 92% or a fall over 3%, CRP 10 mg/L or more**; ABG may show hypoxaemia (PaO2 60 mmHg or less) and/or hypercapnia without acidosis. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Rome severe**: as moderate, **plus hypercapnia (PaCO2 over 45 mmHg) and acidosis (pH under 7.35)** on ABG. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Hospitalised - no respiratory failure**: RR 20-30, no accessory muscles, no mental change, hypoxaemia corrected with 24-35% Venturi, no PaCO2 rise. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Acute respiratory failure, not life-threatening**: RR over 24, accessory muscles, no mental change, hypoxaemia improved with 24-35% Venturi, **PaCO2 raised (50-60 mmHg) above baseline**. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Acute respiratory failure, life-threatening**: **RR over 24, accessory muscles, acute change in mental status**, hypoxaemia not improved with Venturi or needing FiO2 over 40%, **PaCO2 over 60 mmHg or pH 7.25 or less**. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Anthonisen criteria** (increased dyspnoea, sputum volume, sputum purulence) remain used to decide antibiotics: type 1 all three, type 2 two, type 3 one. [Anthonisen 1987]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Increased breathlessness, cough, sputum volume and change to purulent (green or yellow) sputum** over hours to days; wheeze and chest tightness. [GOLD 2025]`,`Ask about **fever, myalgia, sore throat** (viral), **pleuritic pain** (pneumonia, pneumothorax, PE), **orthopnoea and ankle swelling** (heart failure or cor pulmonale), **calf pain or immobility** (PE). [GOLD 2025]`,`Ask the **baseline**: usual exercise tolerance (mMRC), usual SpO2 and **known chronic hypercapnia or past NIV**, home oxygen, previous ICU admissions - these set the oxygen target and ceiling of care. [BTS/ICS 2016]`,`Ask about **inhaler use, adherence and technique**, recent antibiotics and steroids, and **sedatives or opioids**. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Smoking and biomass history**, occupation, and **past TB** (common in India; post-TB obstruction mimics COPD). [GOLD 2025]`,`**Atypical presentations**: confusion, drowsiness and morning headache (hypercapnia), falls, or worsening ankle swelling without much cough in the elderly. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **advance care wishes** and treatment escalation plan early, especially in those with very severe COPD. [BTS/ICS 2016]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Tachypnoea, tachycardia, use of accessory muscles, pursed-lip breathing, tripod position** and paradoxical abdominal movement indicate high work of breathing. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Central cyanosis** (hypoxaemia) and signs of **hypercapnia** - **flapping tremor (asterixis), bounding pulse, warm peripheries, papilloedema, drowsiness, confusion**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hoover's sign** (inward movement of the lower rib margins on inspiration) from a flattened diaphragm, **reduced cricosternal distance**, barrel chest and hyper-resonance indicate hyperinflation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Wheeze and prolonged expiration**; a quiet chest with poor air entry means severe obstruction or fatigue. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Focal crackles and bronchial breathing** suggest pneumonia; **unilateral absent breath sounds** suggest pneumothorax. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Raised JVP, pedal oedema, loud P2, parasternal heave** - cor pulmonale; distinguish from LV failure by basal crackles and gallop. [Harrison 22e]`,`Record **GCS** - a drop in consciousness changes the plan from NIV to intubation. [BTS/ICS 2016]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Pneumonia**: fever, focal signs, new consolidation on chest X-ray - antibiotics are mandatory and the CURB-65 score applies (see the CAP topic). [GOLD 2025]`,`**Pulmonary embolism**: found in a notable minority of unexplained exacerbations needing admission; suspect with pleuritic pain, haemoptysis, hypotension, hypoxaemia out of proportion, or no infective features - Wells and CTPA. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Acute heart failure**: orthopnoea, basal crackles, raised NT-proBNP, cardiomegaly and effusions; often coexists. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Pneumothorax**: sudden onset, unilateral reduced breath sounds; bullae make clinical diagnosis hard - CT if unsure. [BTS 2023]`,`**Acute coronary syndrome and arrhythmias** (AF, multifocal atrial tachycardia): ECG and troponin. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Acute asthma**: younger, atopic, variable symptoms, good reversibility; oxygen target 93-95% rather than 88-92%. [GINA 2025]`,`**Pulmonary TB and post-TB lung disease**: weight loss, fever, haemoptysis, upper-zone shadows - send sputum CBNAAT in India. [MoHFW 2024]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Pulse oximetry** in all; **ABG** in all admitted patients and anyone with SpO2 under 92% or drowsiness - repeat **30-60 minutes after any oxygen change** and 1 hour after starting NIV. [BTS 2017]`,`**ABG reading**: pH under 7.35 with PaCO2 over 6.0 kPa (45 mmHg) is **acute respiratory acidosis**; a high bicarbonate with near-normal pH indicates **chronic compensated hypercapnia**. [BTS/ICS 2016]`,`**Chest X-ray** in all admitted patients to exclude pneumonia, pneumothorax, effusion and heart failure. [GOLD 2025]`,`**ECG** for arrhythmia, ischaemia and right ventricular strain; **troponin and NT-proBNP** where cardiac disease is suspected. [GOLD 2025]`,`**CBC with eosinophil count**, urea and electrolytes, glucose, **CRP** (helps decide antibiotics; CRP under 20 mg/L supports withholding them in primary care). [GOLD 2025]`,`**Sputum culture** if purulent sputum in hospitalised patients, in frequent exacerbators, or with risk factors for Pseudomonas; **sputum CBNAAT** if TB is suspected. [GOLD 2025]`,`**CTPA** if PE is suspected; **echocardiography** for heart failure or pulmonary hypertension. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Spirometry is not done during the exacerbation** - confirm the COPD diagnosis when stable if it has not been done. [GOLD 2025]`]},{heading:`Management at home and in primary care`,points:[`**Increase short-acting bronchodilator**: **salbutamol 2-4 puffs (100 microgram each) by pMDI and spacer every 1-4 hours as needed**, with or without ipratropium; pMDI with spacer works as well as a nebuliser. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Prednisolone 40 mg once daily for 5 days** (REDUCE trial: as effective as 14 days, with less steroid exposure); eosinophils under 2% predict less benefit. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Antibiotics for 5 (to 7) days** when sputum is **purulent plus increased dyspnoea or volume**, or all three Anthonisen symptoms; **CRP-guided prescribing (under 20 mg/L, withhold)** safely reduces use. [GOLD 2025]`,`**First-line antibiotics**: **amoxicillin 500 mg 3 times daily, doxycycline 200 mg on day 1 then 100 mg daily, or clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily** for 5 days; **amoxicillin-clavulanate 625 mg 3 times daily** if recent failure. [NICE NG114 2018]`,`In India, **avoid fluoroquinolones as first-line** in respiratory infection - they mask and breed resistance in undiagnosed TB; get sputum CBNAAT when TB is possible. [ICMR AMR guidelines 2022]`,`**Rescue packs** (pre-supplied prednisolone and antibiotic) with a **written action plan** for patients who can recognise exacerbations, with contact to their doctor within 2 days. [NICE NG115 2019]`,`**Refer for admission** if any severe feature: severe breathlessness, confusion, cyanosis, SpO2 under 90%, rapid worsening, failure of home treatment, serious comorbidity or poor support. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Do not give** methylxanthines (theophylline, doxofylline, aminophylline) - no benefit and more side effects. [GOLD 2025]`]},{heading:`Inpatient management and the oxygen protocol`,points:[`**Controlled oxygen to SpO2 88-92%** in all COPD patients at risk of hypercapnia: start with a **24% or 28% Venturi mask** (or nasal cannula 1-2 L/min), then titrate; avoid high-flow non-Venturi masks. [BTS 2017]`,`**Nebulise with compressed air (not oxygen)** in hypercapnic patients, giving oxygen separately by nasal cannula during the nebulisation; limit oxygen-driven nebulisers to 6 minutes if air is unavailable. [BTS 2017]`,`**Salbutamol 2.5-5 mg nebulised** 4-6 hourly (more often initially) **plus ipratropium 500 microgram** 6-8 hourly; switch to the patient's long-acting inhaler as soon as able. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Prednisolone 40 mg orally for 5 days** (IV hydrocortisone 100 mg 6-hourly only if unable to swallow); oral and IV are equally effective. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Antibiotics** for purulent sputum, pneumonia, or anyone needing **NIV or invasive ventilation** (antibiotics reduce mortality in ventilated patients); cover Pseudomonas only with risk factors (piperacillin-tazobactam) guided by culture. [GOLD 2025]`,`**VTE prophylaxis** (enoxaparin 40 mg SC daily) for all admitted patients without contraindication, as exacerbations raise thrombotic risk. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Fluid balance, nutrition, early mobilisation**, smoking cessation support and treatment of comorbidities (diabetes, heart failure, AF). [GOLD 2025]`,`**ICU or HDU referral**: severe dyspnoea not responding, **change in mental status**, persistent hypoxaemia (PaO2 under 40 mmHg/5.3 kPa) or worsening acidosis (pH under 7.25) despite oxygen and NIV, need for invasive ventilation, or haemodynamic instability. [GOLD 2025]`]},{heading:`Non-invasive ventilation - indications, settings and failure`,points:[`**Indications**: **acute hypercapnic respiratory failure with pH 7.35 or less and PaCO2 over 6.0-6.5 kPa (45-49 mmHg)** persisting after up to an hour of optimal medical therapy, severe dyspnoea with fatigue, or persistent hypoxaemia despite oxygen. [BTS/ICS 2016]`,`**Evidence**: NIV is **first-line ventilatory support** in AECOPD with acidosis - it reduces **intubation (about 65%) and mortality (about 46%)** and shortens stay (ERS/ATS strong recommendation). [ERS/ATS 2017]`,`**Start promptly**: within **60 minutes of the blood gas** that shows the need, and within 2 hours of arrival, in a ward with trained staff, monitoring and a documented **escalation plan (intubate or NIV is the ceiling)**. [BTS 2018]`,`**Settings (bilevel, spontaneous-timed)**: **EPAP 3-5 cm H2O** (higher in obesity or OSA), **IPAP starting about 15 cm H2O, raised by 2-5 cm H2O every 10 minutes to 20-30 cm H2O** as tolerated within the first hour; backup rate 16-20/min; I:E about 1:2-1:3. [BTS/ICS 2016]`,`**Oxygen** is entrained to keep SpO2 88-92%; a **full-face mask** is preferred initially; check leaks, synchrony, comfort, and keep the patient on NIV as much as possible in the first 24 hours. [BTS/ICS 2016]`,`**ABG at 1 hour and 4 hours** (and after each change): improving pH and PaCO2 and falling RR mean success; wean over 2-3 days when pH is normal and the cause is treated. [BTS/ICS 2016]`,`**NIV failure - intubate**: pH falling or under 7.25 despite optimal NIV at 1-4 hours, rising PaCO2, worsening consciousness, inability to protect the airway or clear secretions, haemodynamic instability, or respiratory arrest. [BTS/ICS 2016]`,`**Contraindications**: respiratory arrest, inability to protect the airway, haemodynamic instability, recent facial or upper GI surgery or facial trauma, copious secretions, vomiting, **undrained pneumothorax**, uncooperative patient; pH under 7.25 is not a contraindication but needs ICU-level care. [BTS/ICS 2016]`,`**High-flow nasal oxygen** is suggested only if NIV is not tolerated - ERS 2022 recommends a **trial of NIV before HFNO** in hypercapnic COPD. [ERS 2022]`]},{heading:`Invasive ventilation and ceiling of care`,points:[`**Indications for intubation**: NIV failure or intolerance, cardiac or respiratory arrest, reduced consciousness or agitation not controlled, massive aspiration or vomiting, **inability to clear secretions**, severe haemodynamic instability or ventricular arrhythmia, life-threatening hypoxaemia. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Ventilator strategy**: low tidal volume, **low rate and long expiratory time** to limit auto-PEEP, external PEEP up to about 80% of intrinsic PEEP, target pH not PaCO2 (avoid post-hypercapnic alkalosis by lowering PaCO2 too fast). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Extubate onto NIV** in hypercapnic patients - this reduces reintubation and mortality. [ERS/ATS 2017]`,`Discuss **ceiling of treatment** with the patient and family early; poor baseline function alone should not deny ICU, because survival after ventilation for AECOPD is better than often assumed. [BTS/ICS 2016]`,`**Palliative care**: low-dose opioids (oral morphine 2.5-5 mg) for refractory breathlessness, fan therapy, and symptom-focused care for end-stage disease (see the end-stage lung disease topic). [GOLD 2025]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Diabetes**: steroids cause hyperglycaemia - monitor capillary glucose 4-6 hourly and use insulin correction; do not withhold steroids. [ADA 2025]`,`**Heart failure and AF**: **cardioselective beta-blockers (bisoprolol, metoprolol) are safe** and should be continued; treat AF with rate control. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Elderly and frail**: delirium, falls and hospital-acquired disability are common - early mobilisation and review of sedatives; consider hospital-at-home schemes. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Obesity or OSA overlap**: needs **higher EPAP (often 8-10 cm H2O or more)** on NIV and is likely to need home NIV or CPAP after discharge. [BTS/ICS 2016]`,`**Post-TB obstructive disease** in India: behaves like COPD, but check sputum for TB and Aspergillus IgG (CPA) when there are cavities. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**Resource-limited settings (CHC, district hospital)**: Venturi masks or titrated nasal cannula, pMDI with spacer, oral prednisolone, oral antibiotics; NIV with a portable BiPAP and a trained nurse can be delivered in district HDUs. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Pregnancy** is rare in COPD; manage with the same principles and obstetric input. [GOLD 2025]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Acute hypercapnic respiratory failure**, **cor pulmonale** and right heart failure, **arrhythmias**, pneumothorax and VTE. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Post-exacerbation cardiovascular events**: risk of MI and stroke rises in the 30 days after an exacerbation. [GOLD 2025]`,`**NIV complications**: mask pressure ulcers on the nasal bridge, eye irritation, gastric distension, aspiration and claustrophobia. [BTS/ICS 2016]`,`Prognosis after hypercapnic failure needing NIV: **about a quarter die within a year** and many are readmitted; persistent hypercapnia after recovery predicts readmission and death. [BTS/ICS 2016]`,`Each exacerbation accelerates **FEV1 decline** and reduces quality of life; recovery to baseline can take **weeks**, and about 1 in 5 do not fully recover by 8 weeks. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Steroid and antibiotic harms**: hyperglycaemia, myopathy, C. difficile, resistance. [GOLD 2025]`]},{heading:`Discharge bundle, follow-up and prevention`,points:[`**Discharge when**: clinically stable, able to use the long-acting inhaler, managing at home (walking, eating, sleeping without frequent waking), and with follow-up arranged. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Discharge bundle**: check **inhaler technique**, **optimise maintenance therapy** (LABA+LAMA; add ICS if eosinophils 300/microlitre or more - Group E), smoking cessation, vaccines, written action plan, and comorbidity review. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Pulmonary rehabilitation within 4 weeks of discharge** reduces readmission and mortality; **starting it during admission is not recommended**. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Follow-up at 1-4 weeks** (symptoms, technique, need for oxygen) and **12-16 weeks** (spirometry, re-evaluate oxygen and inhaled therapy). [GOLD 2025]`,`**Do not prescribe LTOT at discharge on a single reading** - reassess oxygen at 8 weeks or later when stable (PaO2 7.3 kPa or less, or 8 kPa or less with polycythaemia, cor pulmonale or oedema). [BTS 2015]`,`**Home NIV**: for patients with **persistent hypercapnia (PaCO2 over 7 kPa / 53 mmHg) 2-4 weeks after an admission needing NIV** - reduces readmission or death (HOT-HMV trial). [ERS 2019]`,`**Vaccines**: annual influenza, pneumococcal (PCV20 or PCV15 then PPSV23), COVID-19, **RSV for those 60 years and over**, and Tdap if not previously vaccinated. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Prevention of further exacerbations**: escalate per GOLD (triple therapy, **azithromycin 250 mg daily** or roflumilast in selected patients, dupilumab or mepolizumab in eosinophilic disease); link to **NP-NCD and Ayushman Arogya Mandir** for follow-up, clean cooking fuel (**PM Ujjwala Yojana**) and tobacco cessation (NTCP, quitline 1800-11-2356). [GOLD 2025]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates and exam angle`,points:[`**GOLD 2023 onward** adopted the **Rome 2021 definition and severity grading** using objective markers (VAS, RR, HR, SpO2, CRP, ABG) rather than grading by treatment received. [GOLD 2025]`,`**GOLD 2025** added **dupilumab** (eosinophils 300 or more, chronic bronchitis) and **ensifentrine** to stable COPD escalation and emphasises **ruling out PE, heart failure and pneumonia** in every exacerbation. [GOLD 2025]`,`**GOLD 2026** counts **even one moderate exacerbation** in the past year as placing a patient in Group E, lowering the threshold for escalation. [GOLD 2026]`,`**BTS/ICS 2016 NIV guideline and BTS 2018 quality standards** remain the reference: NIV within 60 minutes of the qualifying ABG, documented escalation plan, and ABG at 1 and 4 hours. [BTS 2018]`,`**Guideline difference**: GOLD uses **PaCO2 over 45 mmHg (6.0 kPa) with pH 7.35 or less** for NIV, while BTS/ICS uses **PaCO2 over 6.5 kPa with pH under 7.35** after optimal medical treatment - both agree NIV is first-line. [BTS/ICS 2016]`,`Viva favourites: **why target 88-92%** (Haldane effect and V/Q), **why air-driven nebulisers**, **how EPAP counters intrinsic PEEP**, and the **NIV failure criteria**. [BTS/ICS 2016]`,`Common mistakes: **uncontrolled high-flow oxygen**, **aminophylline or doxofylline as routine**, **14-day steroid courses**, fluoroquinolones as first line, and **delaying NIV** in a patient with pH 7.30. [GOLD 2025]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in AECOPD`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Controlled oxygen`,`24-28% Venturi or nasal cannula 1-2 L/min to SpO2 88-92%`,`Titrate; ABG 30-60 min after each change`,`Hyperoxia raises PaCO2 and causes acidosis`],[`Salbutamol`,`2.5-5 mg nebulised (air-driven) 4-6 hourly, or 2-4 puffs pMDI + spacer every 1-4 hours`,`More often initially, then back to maintenance inhaler`,`Tremor, tachycardia, hypokalaemia, arrhythmia`],[`Ipratropium bromide`,`500 microgram nebulised 6-8 hourly`,`During the acute phase; stop if on tiotropium long term after recovery`,`Dry mouth, urinary retention, glaucoma`],[`Prednisolone`,`40 mg oral once daily`,`5 days, no taper`,`Hyperglycaemia, insomnia, myopathy; IV hydrocortisone 100 mg 6-hourly only if unable to swallow`],[`Amoxicillin`,`500 mg oral 3 times daily`,`5 days`,`Rash, diarrhoea; avoid in penicillin allergy`],[`Doxycycline`,`200 mg day 1, then 100 mg once daily`,`5 days total`,`Photosensitivity, oesophagitis; avoid in pregnancy and children under 8`],[`Clarithromycin`,`500 mg oral twice daily`,`5 days`,`QTc prolongation, CYP3A4 interactions`],[`Amoxicillin-clavulanate`,`625 mg oral 3 times daily or 1.2 g IV 8-hourly`,`5-7 days`,`Cholestatic jaundice, diarrhoea`],[`Piperacillin-tazobactam`,`4.5 g IV 6-8 hourly`,`Pseudomonas risk; de-escalate by culture`,`Hypersensitivity, C. difficile`],[`Enoxaparin (prophylaxis)`,`40 mg SC once daily`,`During admission`,`Bleeding; reduce dose if CrCl under 30 mL/min`]]},{heading:`Rome 2021 severity grading adopted by GOLD`,columns:[`Grade`,`Bedside variables`,`ABG`],rows:[[`Mild`,`VAS under 5; RR under 24; HR under 95; SpO2 92% or more with change 3% or less; CRP under 10 mg/L`,`Not needed`],[`Moderate`,`3 or more of: VAS 5 or more; RR 24 or more; HR 95 or more; SpO2 under 92% or fall over 3%; CRP 10 or more`,`May show hypoxaemia or hypercapnia without acidosis`],[`Severe`,`As moderate`,`PaCO2 over 45 mmHg and pH under 7.35`]]},{heading:`NIV in AECOPD - BTS/ICS`,columns:[`Item`,`Recommendation`],rows:[[`Indication`,`pH 7.35 or less and PaCO2 over 6.5 kPa after up to 1 hour of optimal medical therapy`],[`Timing`,`Within 60 minutes of the qualifying ABG; escalation plan documented`],[`Initial EPAP`,`3-5 cm H2O (higher in obesity or OSA)`],[`Initial IPAP`,`About 15 cm H2O, raise by 2-5 every 10 min to 20-30 cm H2O`],[`Backup rate`,`16-20/min, I:E 1:2-1:3`],[`Oxygen`,`Entrain to SpO2 88-92%`],[`Monitoring`,`ABG at 1 and 4 hours, SpO2, RR, GCS, leak and synchrony`],[`Failure`,`pH worsening or under 7.25, rising PaCO2, falling GCS, instability - intubate`],[`Contraindications`,`Arrest, unprotected airway, facial trauma, vomiting, copious secretions, undrained pneumothorax`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of worsening breathlessness in COPD`,columns:[`Condition`,`Clue`,`Test`],rows:[[`Infective AECOPD`,`Purulent sputum, viral prodrome`,`CRP, sputum culture`],[`Pneumonia`,`Fever, focal crackles`,`Chest X-ray consolidation`],[`Pulmonary embolism`,`Pleuritic pain, no infective features, hypoxaemia out of proportion`,`D-dimer of limited use; CTPA`],[`Acute heart failure`,`Orthopnoea, basal crackles, oedema`,`NT-proBNP, echo, X-ray`],[`Pneumothorax`,`Sudden pain, unilateral reduced breath sounds`,`X-ray, CT if bullae`],[`ACS or arrhythmia`,`Chest pain, palpitations`,`ECG, troponin`],[`TB or post-TB disease`,`Weight loss, haemoptysis, upper-zone shadows`,`Sputum CBNAAT`]]}],redFlags:[`pH under 7.35 with PaCO2 over 6.0-6.5 kPa (needs NIV)`,`pH under 7.25, drowsiness, confusion or falling GCS`,`SpO2 under 88% despite controlled oxygen, or PaO2 under 5.3 kPa`,`RR over 30/min with accessory muscle use and paradoxical breathing`,`Haemodynamic instability, new arrhythmia or chest pain`,`New consolidation, pneumothorax or suspected PE`,`Failure of home treatment or inability to cope at home`],pearls:[`**Rome 2021**: grade by **VAS, RR 24, HR 95, SpO2 92, CRP 10** - and ABG for severe.`,`**88-92%** oxygen target - Venturi 24-28%; **air-driven nebulisers** in hypercapnia.`,`**Prednisolone 40 mg for 5 days** - REDUCE trial.`,`**Antibiotics if sputum is purulent** plus one more cardinal symptom, or if ventilated - 5 days.`,`**NIV**: pH 7.35 or less with high PaCO2 after an hour of treatment - start within 60 minutes.`,`**EPAP fights auto-PEEP; IPAP blows off CO2**.`,`**ABG at 1 and 4 hours** on NIV; worsening pH means intubate.`,`**Rehab within 4 weeks of discharge, not during admission**; reassess oxygen at 8 weeks.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-aecopd-niv-q1`,stem:`A 68-year-old man with COPD is admitted with an exacerbation. On 28% Venturi oxygen, SpO2 is 90%. After 1 hour of nebulised bronchodilators and prednisolone, ABG shows pH 7.29, PaCO2 8.4 kPa, PaO2 8.1 kPa. He is alert. What is the next step?`,options:[`Increase oxygen to 60% via a non-rebreather mask`,`Start IV aminophylline infusion`,`Start bilevel non-invasive ventilation`,`Immediate endotracheal intubation`],answer:2,explanation:`Persistent respiratory acidosis (pH 7.29, PaCO2 over 6.5 kPa) after optimal medical therapy in an alert patient is the classic indication for NIV, which reduces intubation and mortality. Higher oxygen would worsen hypercapnia, aminophylline is not recommended in exacerbations, and intubation is reserved for NIV failure, arrest, reduced consciousness or an unprotected airway.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-aecopd-niv-q2`,stem:`Under the Rome 2021 criteria adopted by GOLD, which finding separates a severe exacerbation from a moderate one?`,options:[`Respiratory rate 24/min or more`,`CRP 10 mg/L or more`,`Need for antibiotics`,`PaCO2 over 45 mmHg with pH under 7.35`],answer:3,explanation:`In the Rome scheme both moderate and severe exacerbations share the bedside thresholds (dyspnoea VAS, RR 24 or more, HR 95 or more, SpO2 change and CRP 10 or more); severe is defined by hypercapnia with acidosis on ABG. Respiratory rate and CRP are components of the moderate grade, and grading by treatment such as antibiotics belongs to the older, retrospective definition.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-aecopd-niv-q3`,stem:`What is the recommended duration of oral prednisolone 40 mg daily for an acute exacerbation of COPD?`,options:[`1 day`,`5 days`,`14 days`,`6 weeks with taper`],answer:1,explanation:`The REDUCE trial showed that 5 days of prednisolone 40 mg is non-inferior to 14 days for re-exacerbation and reduces cumulative steroid exposure, so GOLD recommends 5 days without taper. A single dose is insufficient, 14 days adds side effects without benefit, and a 6-week tapered course is unnecessary and harmful.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-aecopd-niv-q4`,stem:`A patient on NIV for AECOPD has an ABG at 2 hours showing pH 7.22 (from 7.27) and PaCO2 rising; he is becoming drowsy despite good mask fit and adequate pressures. What should be done?`,options:[`Proceed to endotracheal intubation and invasive ventilation, if within the agreed ceiling of care`,`Switch to high-flow nasal oxygen`,`Increase EPAP to 15 cm H2O and continue NIV`,`Give IV sodium bicarbonate to correct the pH`],answer:0,explanation:`Worsening acidosis, rising PaCO2 and falling consciousness despite optimised NIV are criteria of NIV failure, and the patient should be intubated if that is within the documented escalation plan. HFNO is inferior to NIV in hypercapnic failure, raising EPAP alone does not increase ventilation and may worsen hyperinflation, and bicarbonate does not treat respiratory acidosis and can raise CO2.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-aecopd-niv-q5`,stem:`A 62-year-old woman is being discharged after a COPD exacerbation that needed NIV. Two weeks after discharge her stable PaCO2 is 7.6 kPa. Which intervention has trial evidence to reduce readmission or death?`,options:[`Long-term oral theophylline`,`Starting pulmonary rehabilitation on the ward before discharge`,`Home non-invasive ventilation with home oxygen`,`Long-term maintenance oral prednisolone 5 mg`],answer:2,explanation:`In patients with persistent hypercapnia (PaCO2 over 7 kPa) 2-4 weeks after an acidotic exacerbation, home NIV added to home oxygen prolonged the time to readmission or death in the HOT-HMV trial. Pulmonary rehabilitation helps when started within 4 weeks after discharge rather than during admission, theophylline does not reduce readmission, and maintenance oral steroids cause harm without benefit.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-aecopd-niv-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Define acute exacerbation of COPD. Discuss its causes, assessment of severity and management in hospital, including the role of non-invasive ventilation.`,openingLines:[`An exacerbation of COPD is an event of worsening dyspnoea and/or cough and sputum over less than 14 days, often with tachypnoea and tachycardia, associated with increased airway and systemic inflammation due to infection, pollution or other insult (GOLD 2025, Rome 2021).`,`It is the main driver of COPD mortality; about half of patients admitted with an exacerbation die within 5 years.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes`,points:[`Viruses (rhinovirus, influenza), bacteria (H. influenzae, S. pneumoniae, M. catarrhalis, Pseudomonas), pollution, non-adherence.`,`Exclude mimics: pneumonia, PE, heart failure, pneumothorax, ACS, arrhythmia, TB.`]},{heading:`Severity assessment`,points:[`Rome: VAS, RR 24, HR 95, SpO2 92, CRP 10; severe = PaCO2 over 45 mmHg with pH under 7.35.`,`GOLD hospital grading: no ARF, non-life-threatening ARF, life-threatening ARF.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`ABG, chest X-ray, ECG, CBC with eosinophils, CRP, electrolytes, sputum culture, CBNAAT if TB suspected, CTPA if PE suspected.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Oxygen 88-92% by Venturi; air-driven salbutamol 2.5-5 mg + ipratropium 500 microgram.`,`Prednisolone 40 mg for 5 days.`,`Antibiotics 5 days if purulent sputum or ventilated (amoxicillin, doxycycline, clarithromycin; Pseudomonas cover if risk).`,`VTE prophylaxis; no methylxanthines.`]},{heading:`NIV`,points:[`Indication: pH 7.35 or less, PaCO2 over 6.5 kPa after optimal therapy; start within 60 min.`,`EPAP 3-5, IPAP 15 rising to 20-30; ABG at 1 and 4 hours.`,`Failure criteria and contraindications; intubation if failing.`]},{heading:`Discharge and prevention`,points:[`Inhaler technique, LABA+LAMA (+ICS if eos 300 or more), rehab within 4 weeks, vaccines, smoking cessation.`,`Follow-up 1-4 and 12-16 weeks; home NIV if PaCO2 over 7 kPa at 2-4 weeks.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart of AECOPD hospital management with NIV decision point`,`Table of NIV settings and failure criteria`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and causes`,marks:2},{part:`Severity and investigations`,marks:2},{part:`Medical treatment with doses`,marks:2},{part:`NIV indications, settings, failure`,marks:3},{part:`Discharge bundle`,marks:1}],keywords:[`Rome 2021`,`88-92%`,`Venturi`,`prednisolone 40 mg 5 days`,`NIV`,`IPAP`,`EPAP`,`respiratory acidosis`]},{id:`respiratory-aecopd-niv-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`core`,question:`Write a short note on non-invasive ventilation in acute hypercapnic respiratory failure due to COPD.`,openingLines:[`NIV delivers bilevel positive pressure through a mask without an artificial airway and is first-line ventilatory support in acidotic exacerbations of COPD.`,`It reduces intubation by about 65% and mortality by about 46%.`],answer:[{heading:`Mechanism`,points:[`EPAP offsets intrinsic PEEP and splints the upper airway; IPAP adds pressure support, raises tidal volume and lowers PaCO2.`]},{heading:`Indications and contraindications`,points:[`pH 7.35 or less with PaCO2 over 6.5 kPa after optimal medical therapy.`,`Contraindications: arrest, unprotected airway, facial trauma, vomiting, copious secretions, undrained pneumothorax, instability.`]},{heading:`Settings and monitoring`,points:[`EPAP 3-5, IPAP 15 rising to 20-30 cm H2O in the first hour; backup rate 16-20; oxygen to 88-92%.`,`ABG at 1 and 4 hours; failure = worsening pH, rising PaCO2, falling GCS - intubate.`]}],mustDraw:[`Pressure-time graph showing IPAP and EPAP levels`],markSplit:[{part:`Mechanism`,marks:1},{part:`Indications and contraindications`,marks:2},{part:`Settings, monitoring and failure`,marks:2}],keywords:[`BiPAP`,`IPAP`,`EPAP`,`intrinsic PEEP`,`ABG at 1 hour`,`NIV failure`]}],references:[`Global Initiative for Chronic Obstructive Lung Disease (GOLD). Global Strategy for the Diagnosis, Management and Prevention of COPD, 2025 report (and 2026 update)`,`Celli BR et al. An updated definition and severity classification of COPD exacerbations: the Rome proposal. Am J Respir Crit Care Med 2021`,`Davidson AC et al. BTS/ICS guideline for the ventilatory management of acute hypercapnic respiratory failure in adults. Thorax 2016`,`Rochwerg B et al. Official ERS/ATS clinical practice guidelines: noninvasive ventilation for acute respiratory failure. Eur Respir J 2017`,`O'Driscoll BR et al. BTS guideline for oxygen use in adults in healthcare and emergency settings. Thorax 2017`,`NICE NG114. Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (acute exacerbation): antimicrobial prescribing, 2018`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease; Mechanical ventilatory support`,`India State-Level Disease Burden Initiative CRD Collaborators. The burden of chronic respiratory diseases across the states of India. Lancet Glob Health 2018`]});var r=[];r.push({id:`respiratory-bronchiectasis`,title:`Bronchiectasis`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Bronchiectasis** is a chronic lung disease defined by **permanent, abnormal dilatation of the bronchi on CT** together with a clinical syndrome of **daily cough, sputum and recurrent chest infections**, driven by a **vicious vortex of infection, neutrophilic inflammation, impaired mucociliary clearance and structural damage**; in India the commonest cause is **post-tuberculous lung damage**.`,keywords:[`non-cystic fibrosis bronchiectasis`,`NCFB`,`post-TB bronchiectasis`,`EMBARC`,`EMBARC-India`,`Bronchiectasis Severity Index`,`BSI`,`FACED`,`signet ring sign`,`Kartagener syndrome`,`primary ciliary dyskinesia`,`airway clearance`,`azithromycin maintenance`,`inhaled colistin`,`Pseudomonas eradication`,`brensocatib`,`DPP-1 inhibitor`,`Cole vicious cycle`,`traction bronchiectasis`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Bronchiectasis** is a clinical-radiological diagnosis: **persistent cough, sputum and/or recurrent infections** plus **abnormal, permanent bronchial dilatation on CT** - CT dilatation alone (for example traction bronchiectasis in fibrosis) is not the disease. [ERS 2017]`,`The term used in guidelines is **non-cystic fibrosis bronchiectasis**, because cystic fibrosis (CF) has its own CFTR-modulator-based pathway of care. [BTS 2019]`,`Prevalence is rising worldwide with more CT use and an ageing population; European and US estimates are of the order of **several hundred per 100 000 adults**, higher in women and in people over 70. [Harrison 22e]`,`The **EMBARC-India registry** (Dhar et al., Lancet Global Health 2019, over 2000 patients) found Indian patients **younger, more severe and more often infected with Pseudomonas aeruginosa** than European patients. [EMBARC-India 2019]`,`In EMBARC-India the commonest identified cause was **post-tuberculous bronchiectasis (about one-third)**, followed by idiopathic disease and **ABPA**, which is far more frequent in India than in Europe. [EMBARC-India 2019]`,`In Europe, about **40% of cases remain idiopathic** after full work-up and post-infective disease accounts for roughly a fifth. [EMBARC 2023]`,`Bronchiectasis is often **misdiagnosed in India as recurrent or relapsed smear-negative TB**, leading to repeated empirical ATT - a CBNAAT-negative chronic productive cough after treated TB should prompt HRCT. [EMBARC-India 2019]`,`Overlap with **COPD (bronchiectasis-COPD overlap)** and **asthma** is common and doubles exacerbation risk and mortality compared with either disease alone. [GOLD 2025]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Post-infective**: tuberculosis, childhood measles, pertussis, severe bacterial or adenoviral pneumonia, and **non-tuberculous mycobacteria (NTM)** - both a cause and a consequence. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ABPA** (allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis) causes **central (proximal) upper-lobe bronchiectasis** with high-attenuation mucus plugs; screen every patient with **total IgE and Aspergillus-specific IgE**. [BTS 2019]`,`**Immunodeficiency**: common variable immunodeficiency (CVID), X-linked agammaglobulinaemia, specific antibody deficiency, IgG subclass deficiency, **HIV** and post-chemotherapy or haematological malignancy - CVID matters because **immunoglobulin replacement** changes the course. [BTS 2019]`,`**Primary ciliary dyskinesia (PCD)**: autosomal recessive (DNAH5, DNAI1 and over 50 genes); **Kartagener syndrome** is the triad of **situs inversus, chronic sinusitis and bronchiectasis**, seen in about half of PCD; male infertility from immotile sperm. [ERS PCD 2017]`,`**Cystic fibrosis** (CFTR mutation): upper-lobe predominant disease, malabsorption, male infertility (absent vas), **sweat chloride 60 mmol/L or more** - consider in young patients and those with pancreatic or sinus disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Connective tissue and inflammatory disease**: rheumatoid arthritis, Sjogren syndrome, **inflammatory bowel disease (especially ulcerative colitis)**, and **alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency** (with basal emphysema). [BTS 2019]`,`**Obstruction**: inhaled foreign body, slow-growing tumour (carcinoid), extrinsic node compression (middle lobe syndrome in TB) - all give **localised** disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rare congenital**: Mounier-Kuhn syndrome (tracheobronchomegaly), Williams-Campbell syndrome (cartilage deficiency), **yellow nail syndrome** (yellow nails, lymphoedema, effusions) and **Young syndrome** (obstructive azoospermia). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Aspiration and reflux**, and **chronic smoke or biomass exposure**, contribute through repeated airway injury, especially in the elderly. [ERS 2017]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - from vicious cycle to vortex`,points:[`**Cole's vicious cycle** (1986): an initial insult impairs **mucociliary clearance**, retained mucus allows **bacterial colonisation**, bacteria trigger **neutrophilic inflammation**, and inflammation damages the wall, which further impairs clearance. [Harrison 22e]`,`The modern **vicious vortex** model (Flume 2018) shows the four components - **infection, inflammation, impaired clearance and structural damage** - each driving the others, so treatment must target more than one at a time. [Flume Lancet 2018]`,`**Neutrophil serine proteases** (neutrophil elastase, proteinase 3, cathepsin G) degrade elastin and cartilage, cleave cilia, stimulate mucus secretion and impair opsonisation; **sputum elastase activity predicts exacerbations and lung function decline**. [Chalmers AJRCCM 2017]`,`These proteases are activated inside the maturing neutrophil by **dipeptidyl peptidase-1 (DPP-1, cathepsin C)** - the target of **brensocatib**. [ASPEN NEJM 2025]`,`The wall loses elastic tissue and cartilage, so the bronchus dilates, its lining becomes ulcerated with **hypertrophied bronchial arteries** - the source of **haemoptysis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pseudomonas aeruginosa** forms biofilms and mucoid alginate in the damaged airway, resists eradication, and its chronic presence roughly **triples mortality** and increases hospitalisation. [Araujo ERJ 2018]`,`About **one in five patients have eosinophilic inflammation** (blood eosinophils 300 cells/microlitre or more), which predicts response to **inhaled corticosteroids**. [Shoemark AJRCCM 2022]`,`Coarse crackles arise from secretions in dilated airways, and **obstructive spirometry** reflects small-airway disease and mucus; advanced disease adds hypoxaemia, pulmonary hypertension and **cor pulmonale**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and severity scoring`,points:[`**Reid's morphological classification**: **cylindrical (tubular)** - uniformly dilated, mildest; **varicose** - beaded with constrictions; **cystic (saccular)** - balloon-like cysts, most severe. [Reid 1950]`,`Distribution points to cause: **upper lobe** - CF, TB, ABPA (central), sarcoidosis; **middle lobe and lingula** - NTM (Lady Windermere), PCD; **lower lobe** - post-infective, aspiration, immunodeficiency, alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency. [Harrison 22e]`,`The **Bronchiectasis Severity Index (BSI)** scores age, BMI, FEV1 % predicted, hospital admission in the past 2 years, exacerbations in the past year, MRC dyspnoea grade, Pseudomonas colonisation, other colonisation and radiological extent (3 or more lobes or cystic). [Chalmers AJRCCM 2014]`,`**BSI bands**: **0-4 mild, 5-8 moderate, 9 or more severe**; it predicts mortality, hospitalisation and exacerbations over 4 years and guides follow-up intensity. [Chalmers AJRCCM 2014]`,`**FACED** (FEV1, Age, Chronic Pseudomonas colonisation, Extension in lobes, Dyspnoea) scores 0-7: **0-2 mild, 3-4 moderate, 5-7 severe**; **E-FACED** adds exacerbations needing admission. [Martinez-Garcia ERJ 2014]`,`**Chronic infection** is defined as isolation of the same organism in **2 or more cultures at least 3 months apart in a year**. [ERS 2017]`,`**Exacerbation (consensus definition)**: deterioration in **3 or more** of cough, sputum volume or consistency, sputum purulence, breathlessness or exercise tolerance, fatigue or malaise, and haemoptysis, lasting **48 hours or more**, with a clinician decision to change treatment. [Hill ERJ 2017]`,`Endotyping by **neutrophilic vs eosinophilic** inflammation and **frequent exacerbator (2 or more per year)** status is the newer, treatment-oriented way to classify patients. [ERS 2025]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Chronic productive cough** most days, with **mucopurulent sputum** that is often large in volume (a cupful a day in severe disease) and worse in the morning on rising. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Recurrent chest infections** - frequent courses of antibiotics, often labelled as recurrent pneumonia, bronchitis or TB relapse. [BTS 2019]`,`**Haemoptysis** ranges from blood-streaked sputum to massive bleeding from bronchial arteries; **dry bronchiectasis (bronchiectasis sicca)** after TB may present with haemoptysis alone. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Breathlessness, wheeze, pleuritic pain, fatigue, weight loss** and **chronic rhinosinusitis** (about 60% in some series and almost universal in PCD). [EMBARC 2023]`,`Clues to cause: neonatal respiratory distress, dextrocardia, glue ear and infertility (PCD); asthma with brown plugs (ABPA); recurrent sinus, ear and gut infections (antibody deficiency); joint disease (RA); diarrhoea and blood (IBD). [BTS 2019]`,`Ask about **treated TB, number of ATT courses and CBNAAT results**, biomass exposure, smoking and childhood measles or whooping cough. [EMBARC-India 2019]`,`Consider bronchiectasis in **COPD with frequent exacerbations or Pseudomonas**, **asthma that is difficult to control**, **RA or IBD with chronic cough**, and anyone with a productive cough over 8 weeks. [BTS 2019]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Coarse crackles** - early to mid inspiratory and often expiratory, which may shift after coughing - over the affected lobes are the hallmark sign. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Wheeze and squeaks** from airway narrowing and secretions; a **localised fixed wheeze** suggests an obstructing lesion. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Clubbing** occurs in severe, suppurative or cystic disease and is now uncommon; its presence should also make you look for lung cancer, abscess and empyema. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Halitosis, purulent postnasal drip and nasal polyps** (CF, PCD); **dextrocardia** with an apex beat on the right in Kartagener syndrome. [ERS PCD 2017]`,`Signs of **post-TB fibrosis**: tracheal deviation towards the side, flattening of the upper chest and **bronchial breathing over a collapsed fibrotic lobe**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Advanced disease: **central cyanosis, raised JVP, loud P2, parasternal heave and pedal oedema** of cor pulmonale; low BMI predicts poor prognosis. [Chalmers AJRCCM 2014]`,`Record **SpO2, BMI, MRC dyspnoea grade and sputum colour** at every visit - they feed the severity scores. [BTS 2019]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**COPD**: smoker, less sputum, emphysema on CT; but up to a third of COPD patients have bronchiectasis on CT, and the two may coexist. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Active pulmonary TB**: fever, weight loss, cavity or new infiltrate; send sputum **CBNAAT (Truenat or Xpert)** before ever giving ATT again. [NTEP 2023]`,`**Non-tuberculous mycobacterial disease**: nodular-bronchiectatic pattern with **tree-in-bud in the middle lobe and lingula**; needs 3 sputum mycobacterial cultures. [ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020]`,`**Chronic aspergillosis and ABPA**: central bronchiectasis, raised IgE, eosinophilia (see the ABPA topic). [ISHAM 2024]`,`**Lung abscess**: acute or subacute, putrid sputum, single cavity with air-fluid level. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Interstitial lung disease with traction bronchiectasis**: dry cough, velcro crackles, reticulation - dilatation is secondary to fibrosis and not treated as bronchiectasis. [Aliberti Lancet Respir Med 2022]`,`**Asthma and chronic rhinosinusitis-related cough, and protracted bacterial bronchitis** (in children) can mimic it; HRCT separates them. [BTS 2019]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**HRCT chest (thin-section, non-contrast)** is the diagnostic test: **bronchoarterial ratio over 1 (signet ring sign)**, **lack of bronchial tapering** (tram tracks), and **airways visible within 1 cm of the costal pleura**. [Aliberti Lancet Respir Med 2022]`,`Supportive CT signs: **bronchial wall thickening, mucus plugging, tree-in-bud nodules, mosaic attenuation**, and cystic clusters with air-fluid levels. [Aliberti Lancet Respir Med 2022]`,`**Chest X-ray** is insensitive but may show tram lines, ring shadows and volume loss; a normal film does not exclude bronchiectasis. [BTS 2019]`,`**Minimum aetiology bundle for every adult**: CBC with differential, **serum IgG, IgA, IgM**, **total IgE and Aspergillus-specific IgE (or skin test)**, and sputum bacterial and mycobacterial culture. [ERS 2017]`,`**Selected tests**: sweat chloride and CFTR genetics, **nasal nitric oxide and high-speed video microscopy** for PCD, alpha-1 antitrypsin level, RF and anti-CCP, HIV, specific pneumococcal antibody response, and bronchoscopy for localised disease. [BTS 2019]`,`**Sputum culture when stable and at each exacerbation** - at least yearly - to detect Pseudomonas, H influenzae, S aureus (CF clue) and NTM. [BTS 2019]`,`**Spirometry** usually shows **obstruction**, sometimes mixed; record FEV1 yearly as it feeds the severity score and predicts survival. [BTS 2019]`,`Before long-term macrolide therapy: **3 sputum cultures negative for NTM**, ECG for **QTc**, and liver function tests. [BTS 2019]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`Treat the cause where possible: **immunoglobulin replacement** for antibody deficiency, **prednisolone or itraconazole for ABPA**, CFTR modulators for CF, and treating reflux or aspiration. [ERS 2017]`,`**Airway clearance** is the cornerstone for all patients with sputum: **active cycle of breathing technique (ACBT)** or **oscillating positive expiratory pressure** devices (Flutter, Acapella), **10-30 minutes once or twice daily**, taught by a physiotherapist. [BTS 2019]`,`**Mucoactives** in those who cannot clear sputum: **nebulised hypertonic saline 6-7%** (pre-treat with a bronchodilator) or **carbocisteine 750 mg three times daily** reducing to twice daily. [BTS 2019]`,`**Recombinant human DNase (dornase alfa) must not be used** in non-CF bronchiectasis - it increased exacerbations and FEV1 decline. [BTS 2019]`,`**Pulmonary rehabilitation** for patients limited by breathlessness (MRC 2 or more) improves exercise capacity and may reduce exacerbations. [ERS 2017]`,`**Inhaled corticosteroids are not routine**; use them for coexisting asthma, ABPA or COPD with eosinophilia, and consider them when blood eosinophils are **300 cells/microlitre or more**. [ERS 2025]`,`**Long-acting bronchodilators** (LABA or LAMA) for significant breathlessness or coexisting COPD or asthma; they do not reduce exacerbations by themselves. [ERS 2025]`,`**Self-management plan**: recognise an exacerbation, send a sputum sample before starting a **standby antibiotic**, and seek review if not better in 48-72 hours. [BTS 2019]`,`**Vaccines**: annual influenza and pneumococcal vaccination for all (see the Adult respiratory vaccines topic); stop smoking and avoid biomass smoke. [BTS 2019]`]},{heading:`Long-term antibiotics, Pseudomonas eradication and new drugs`,points:[`**Long-term macrolides** for adults with **3 or more exacerbations a year** despite airway clearance (BTS 2019, ERS 2017): **azithromycin 250 mg once daily or 500 mg three times a week**, or erythromycin 400 mg twice daily (BLESS). [BTS 2019]`,`Macrolide trials **EMBRACE, BAT and BLESS** each cut exacerbations by roughly **half**; benefit is seen with and without Pseudomonas, so macrolides work through anti-inflammatory as well as antibacterial effects. [Chalmers Lancet Respir Med 2019]`,`Before macrolides **exclude NTM** (macrolide monotherapy breeds resistant MAC), check **QTc** (avoid if over 450 ms in men or 470 ms in women), and warn about **hearing loss, tinnitus and GI upset**. [BTS 2019]`,`**Pseudomonas eradication** at first isolation: **ciprofloxacin 500-750 mg twice daily for 2 weeks**, or IV antipseudomonal therapy for 2 weeks, followed by **nebulised colistin for 3 months**; this clears the organism in about 40-50%. [BTS 2019]`,`**Inhaled antibiotics** for **chronic Pseudomonas with 3 or more exacerbations a year**: **nebulised colistimethate 1-2 million units twice daily** (PROMIS trials) or **gentamicin 80 mg twice daily**; give the first dose under observation for bronchospasm. [ERS 2017]`,`If Pseudomonas is absent and macrolides are not tolerated, alternatives are **inhaled gentamicin or long-term doxycycline 100 mg daily**. [BTS 2019]`,`**Brensocatib** (oral **DPP-1 inhibitor**, 10 mg or 25 mg once daily) reduced annual exacerbations by about **20%** versus placebo in **ASPEN** in patients with 2 or more exacerbations in the previous year; the 25 mg dose also slowed FEV1 decline. [ASPEN NEJM 2025]`,`Brensocatib was **approved by the US FDA in August 2025** for non-CF bronchiectasis in people aged 12 years and over - the first licensed drug for this disease; adverse effects include **hyperkeratosis, periodontal disease and headache**, and it is not yet marketed in India. [FDA 2025]`]},{heading:`Management of exacerbations and IPD care`,points:[`Send **sputum culture before antibiotics**, then treat empirically for **14 days** (BTS 2019; ERS 2025 suggests shorter courses may suffice in mild exacerbations with a sensitive organism). [BTS 2019]`,`Empirical oral choice without known Pseudomonas: **amoxicillin 500 mg three times daily** (or **1 g three times daily** in severe disease) or **clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily** if penicillin-allergic. [BTS 2019]`,`**Known Pseudomonas**: **ciprofloxacin 500-750 mg twice daily for 14 days**; adjust to the last culture sensitivity. [BTS 2019]`,`**Admit** for respiratory failure (SpO2 under 92% or rising PaCO2), sepsis, inability to cope at home, **massive haemoptysis**, or failure of oral antibiotics. [BTS 2019]`,`IV options for Pseudomonas: **ceftazidime 2 g every 8 hours** or **piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g every 8 hours**, with or without an aminoglycoside; single-drug therapy is enough when the organism is sensitive. [BTS 2019]`,`Inpatient care adds **controlled oxygen (target 88-92% if hypercapnic)**, intensive airway clearance with physiotherapy, nebulised bronchodilator and **VTE prophylaxis**. [BTS 2019]`,`**Massive haemoptysis** (see the Haemoptysis topic): lie bleeding side down, IV tranexamic acid, secure airway, urgent CT angiography and **bronchial artery embolisation**. [BTS 2019]`,`**Failure to improve** within 5-7 days: re-culture, consider resistant organism, NTM, ABPA flare, PE or a different diagnosis. [BTS 2019]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: continue airway clearance; amoxicillin and azithromycin are acceptable; avoid **tetracyclines, systemic aminoglycosides and, if possible, fluoroquinolones**; plan delivery with anaesthetic review in severe disease. [BTS 2019]`,`**Elderly**: frequent coexisting COPD, reflux and aspiration; check hearing and QTc before macrolides and watch drug interactions (statins, warfarin, antiarrhythmics). [ERS 2017]`,`**CKD**: reduce ciprofloxacin dose when eGFR is under 30 mL/min; avoid nebulised and systemic aminoglycosides or monitor levels closely. [BNF 2025]`,`**Diabetes**: higher risk of Pseudomonas and Klebsiella exacerbations; screen for TB and NTM when control worsens. [EMBARC-India 2019]`,`**Children**: protracted bacterial bronchitis can progress to bronchiectasis if untreated; PCD, CF and immunodeficiency are proportionally more common. [ERS Paediatric 2021]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: airway clearance by **postural drainage and huffing**, oral amoxicillin or ciprofloxacin per local culture, azithromycin from **Jan Aushadhi**, and HRCT only when it changes management. [EMBARC-India 2019]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Haemoptysis** - minor in up to half of patients during their illness, occasionally massive and life-threatening. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chronic respiratory failure and cor pulmonale** in extensive cystic disease, especially post-TB destroyed lung. [Harrison 22e]`,`**NTM infection**, **ABPA**, **empyema, lung abscess** and **pneumothorax** complicate the course. [ERS 2017]`,`**AA (secondary) amyloidosis** with nephrotic syndrome in long-standing suppurative disease, and rarely **metastatic brain abscess**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mortality** rises steeply with severity: BSI severe disease carries roughly **10% one-year and about 30% four-year mortality**, while mild disease is under 5% at 4 years. [Chalmers AJRCCM 2014]`,`Predictors of death: **frequent exacerbations, hospital admission, chronic Pseudomonas, low FEV1, low BMI and COPD overlap**. [EMBARC 2023]`,`**Depression and anxiety** are common and worsen adherence and quality of life; screen with PHQ-9. [ERS 2017]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up, prevention and counselling`,points:[`**Refer to a chest physician** for confirmation of diagnosis, aetiology work-up, frequent exacerbations (3 or more a year), chronic Pseudomonas, MRSA, NTM, haemoptysis, or decline in lung function. [BTS 2019]`,`**Mild disease**: review yearly in primary care; **moderate-severe**: every 3-6 months in specialist care. [BTS 2019]`,`At each review record **exacerbation count, sputum culture, BMI, MRC grade, SpO2 and spirometry** (FEV1 yearly), and recalculate the BSI. [BTS 2019]`,`On macrolides: **ECG and audiology if symptomatic**, liver function, and **sputum for NTM every 6-12 months**; review benefit after 6-12 months. [BTS 2019]`,`Refer for **surgical resection** if disease is **localised** with uncontrolled symptoms or recurrent haemoptysis despite best medical care. [BTS 2019]`,`Refer for **lung transplant assessment** at FEV1 under 30% predicted with rapid decline, hypoxaemia, hypercapnia or pulmonary hypertension. [ISHLT 2021]`,`**Prevent the commonest Indian cause**: early TB diagnosis, full ATT adherence and **post-TB follow-up** under NTEP to detect post-TB lung disease. [NTEP 2023]`,`**Childhood immunisation** (measles-rubella, pertussis under UIP, PCV) prevents post-infective bronchiectasis. [UIP 2024]`,`**Teach airway clearance** as a daily habit like brushing teeth; review technique at every visit. [BTS 2019]`,`**Smoking cessation** and reducing **biomass smoke** (LPG under Ujjwala) reduce exacerbations. [NP-NCD 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**ERS clinical practice guideline update 2025** (Chalmers et al.) re-examined airway clearance, mucoactives, ICS, bronchodilators and long-term antibiotics; it keeps **macrolides and inhaled antibiotics for frequent exacerbators** and supports **ICS only in eosinophilic or asthma-overlap phenotypes**. [ERS 2025]`,`**ASPEN (NEJM 2025)** made **brensocatib** the first anti-neutrophilic disease-modifying drug, and **FDA approval followed in August 2025**; it is the first treatment aimed at the inflammation arm of the vortex. [ASPEN NEJM 2025]`,`The **international consensus criteria (Aliberti, Lancet Respir Med 2022)** standardised the CT definition: bronchoarterial ratio, lack of tapering and peripheral airway visibility, and excluded traction bronchiectasis from the clinical disease. [Aliberti Lancet Respir Med 2022]`,`The 2017 **exacerbation consensus definition** (3 or more symptoms for 48 hours or more) is now the standard trial and clinical definition. [Hill ERJ 2017]`,`**EMBARC registry data (2023)** confirmed **wide geographic variation**: Indian patients have more post-TB disease, more Pseudomonas and worse outcomes. [EMBARC 2023]`,`**GOLD 2025** recommends CT to look for bronchiectasis in COPD patients with frequent exacerbations, and to treat the overlap by bronchiectasis principles. [GOLD 2025]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic viva: **define bronchiectasis, name the CT signs, list causes by lobe** and outline the work-up bundle.`,`Know **Kartagener's triad** and that **dornase alfa is harmful outside CF**.`,`Common mistake: treating every post-TB productive cough with **repeat ATT** without CBNAAT and HRCT.`,`Common mistake: starting **azithromycin without excluding NTM** - it creates macrolide-resistant MAC.`,`Remember the **14-day course** for exacerbations, unlike 5 days in CAP.`,`Name the **vicious vortex**: infection, inflammation, impaired clearance, structural damage - and a drug for each arm (antibiotics, brensocatib or macrolide, airway clearance, surgery).`,`Severity: **BSI (9 or more = severe) and FACED (5-7 = severe)** are the two scores examiners expect.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs used in bronchiectasis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Amoxicillin`,`500 mg-1 g oral three times daily`,`14 days for exacerbation`,`Rash, diarrhoea; avoid in penicillin allergy`],[`Clarithromycin`,`500 mg oral twice daily`,`14 days for exacerbation`,`QT prolongation, CYP3A4 interactions (statins, warfarin)`],[`Ciprofloxacin`,`500-750 mg oral twice daily`,`14 days (exacerbation or eradication)`,`Tendinopathy, QT, C difficile; masks TB; reduce dose if eGFR under 30`],[`Ceftazidime / piperacillin-tazobactam`,`2 g IV 8-hourly / 4.5 g IV 8-hourly`,`14 days for severe Pseudomonas exacerbation`,`Allergy, C difficile; adjust in renal failure`],[`Azithromycin (maintenance)`,`250 mg oral daily or 500 mg three times a week`,`Review at 6-12 months; continue if benefit`,`QTc prolongation, hearing loss, GI upset; exclude NTM first`],[`Colistimethate (nebulised)`,`1-2 million units twice daily`,`Long term for chronic Pseudomonas; 3 months after eradication`,`Bronchospasm (test dose, pre-bronchodilator), sore throat`],[`Gentamicin (nebulised)`,`80 mg twice daily`,`Long term`,`Bronchospasm, ototoxicity, nephrotoxicity in CKD`],[`Hypertonic saline 6-7%`,`4-5 mL nebulised once or twice daily`,`Long term, before airway clearance`,`Bronchospasm - pre-treat with salbutamol`],[`Carbocisteine`,`750 mg oral three times daily, then twice daily`,`Trial of 6 months`,`GI upset; avoid in active peptic ulcer`],[`Brensocatib`,`10 mg or 25 mg oral once daily`,`Long term (52 weeks in ASPEN)`,`Hyperkeratosis, periodontal disease, headache; not in India yet`]]},{heading:`Causes of bronchiectasis and the test that finds them`,columns:[`Cause`,`Clinical clue`,`CT distribution`,`Key test`],rows:[[`Post-tuberculous`,`Past ATT, fibrosis, haemoptysis`,`Upper lobes, with fibrosis and volume loss`,`History, CBNAAT to exclude active TB`],[`ABPA`,`Asthma, brown plugs, eosinophilia`,`Central, upper lobe, high-attenuation mucus`,`Total IgE over 500 IU/mL, Aspergillus-specific IgE`],[`Antibody deficiency (CVID)`,`Recurrent sinus, ear and gut infections`,`Lower lobes, diffuse`,`Serum IgG, IgA, IgM; vaccine response`],[`Primary ciliary dyskinesia`,`Neonatal distress, situs inversus, infertility`,`Middle and lower lobes`,`Nasal NO, video microscopy, genetics`],[`Cystic fibrosis`,`Young, malabsorption, sinus disease`,`Upper lobes`,`Sweat chloride 60 mmol/L or more, CFTR genetics`],[`NTM (MAC)`,`Thin elderly woman, chronic cough`,`Middle lobe and lingula, tree-in-bud`,`3 sputum mycobacterial cultures`],[`Rheumatoid arthritis, IBD`,`Joint or bowel disease`,`Diffuse or lower lobe`,`RF, anti-CCP; colonoscopy history`],[`Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency`,`Young emphysema, liver disease`,`Lower lobes with basal emphysema`,`Serum alpha-1 antitrypsin level`],[`Obstruction (foreign body, tumour)`,`Localised, fixed wheeze`,`Single lobe or segment`,`Bronchoscopy`]]},{heading:`Severity scores`,columns:[`Score`,`Components`,`Bands`,`Use`],rows:[[`Bronchiectasis Severity Index (BSI)`,`Age, BMI, FEV1, admissions, exacerbations, MRC, Pseudomonas, other colonisation, extent`,`0-4 mild, 5-8 moderate, 9 or more severe`,`Predicts mortality and admission; sets follow-up`],[`FACED`,`FEV1, Age, Chronic Pseudomonas, Extension, Dyspnoea`,`0-2 mild, 3-4 moderate, 5-7 severe`,`Predicts 5-year mortality; simple`],[`E-FACED`,`FACED plus exacerbations needing admission`,`0-3 mild, 4-6 moderate, 7-9 severe`,`Predicts exacerbations as well as death`],[`Reid classification`,`CT morphology`,`Cylindrical, varicose, cystic`,`Describes severity of airway damage`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of chronic productive cough`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating clinical feature`,`Discriminating investigation`],rows:[[`Bronchiectasis`,`Daily purulent sputum, coarse crackles, recurrent infections`,`HRCT: bronchoarterial ratio over 1, no tapering`],[`COPD`,`Smoker, breathlessness more than sputum`,`Spirometry post-BD FEV1/FVC under 0.7; emphysema on CT`],[`Active pulmonary TB`,`Fever, night sweats, weight loss`,`CBNAAT or culture positive`],[`NTM lung disease`,`Elderly, slow progression`,`Repeated NTM cultures, nodular-bronchiectatic CT`],[`Lung abscess`,`Acute onset, putrid sputum, aspiration risk`,`Thick-walled cavity with air-fluid level`],[`ILD with traction bronchiectasis`,`Dry cough, velcro crackles`,`Reticulation, honeycombing on HRCT`]]},{heading:`HRCT signs of bronchiectasis`,columns:[`Sign`,`What it means`,`Cut-off or appearance`],rows:[[`Signet ring sign`,`Dilated bronchus beside its smaller artery`,`Bronchoarterial ratio over 1 (inner diameter)`],[`Lack of tapering / tram tracks`,`Parallel walls along the airway`,`Bronchus wider than its parent over 2 cm`],[`Peripheral airway visibility`,`Airways seen near the pleura`,`Within 1 cm of costal pleura`],[`String of pearls / cysts`,`Varicose or cystic change`,`Clusters of cysts with air-fluid levels`],[`Tree-in-bud and mucus plugs`,`Small-airway infection, mucus`,`Centrilobular branching nodules`],[`Mosaic attenuation`,`Small-airway disease and air trapping`,`Patchy density, more on expiratory CT`]]}],redFlags:[`**Massive haemoptysis** (over 100-200 mL in 24 hours or any amount with hypoxia) - admit for airway protection and bronchial artery embolisation.`,`**SpO2 under 92%, rising PaCO2 or drowsiness** - acute respiratory failure.`,`**Sepsis**: hypotension, confusion, RR 30 or more during an exacerbation.`,`**Weight loss, night sweats and new cavity** - exclude active TB or NTM.`,`**New localised wheeze or lobar collapse in a smoker over 40** - bronchoscopy for tumour.`,`**Exacerbation not improving after 5-7 days** of appropriate antibiotics.`,`**Pleuritic pain with fever and effusion** - empyema.`],pearls:[`**Bronchiectasis = CT dilatation + clinical syndrome**; traction bronchiectasis in fibrosis is not the disease.`,`In India, think **post-TB first, ABPA second** - and always send total IgE.`,`**Signet ring sign**: bronchus bigger than its accompanying artery.`,`**Treat exacerbations for 14 days**, with a sputum sample sent first.`,`**Exclude NTM before azithromycin**; check QTc.`,`**Dornase alfa is for CF only** - harmful in non-CF bronchiectasis.`,`**First Pseudomonas isolate: try to eradicate** - ciprofloxacin 2 weeks then nebulised colistin 3 months.`,`Mnemonic for causes - **CAPTIVATE**: CF, ABPA, PCD, TB, Immunodeficiency, Vasculitis or connective tissue, Aspiration, Tumour or obstruction, Everything else (idiopathic).`,`**Brensocatib** blocks DPP-1, so neutrophil elastase is never activated - first drug approved for bronchiectasis (FDA 2025).`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-bronchiectasis-q1`,stem:`A 34-year-old man treated twice for smear-negative TB has daily purulent sputum and coarse basal crackles. Which single investigation best confirms bronchiectasis?`,options:[`Chest X-ray PA view`,`Spirometry with bronchodilator reversibility`,`High-resolution CT of the chest`,`Sputum CBNAAT`],answer:2,explanation:`HRCT is the diagnostic test, showing a bronchoarterial ratio over 1, lack of tapering and peripheral airways within 1 cm of the pleura. A chest X-ray is insensitive, spirometry only shows obstruction, and CBNAAT is needed to exclude active TB but cannot diagnose bronchiectasis.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-bronchiectasis-q2`,stem:`Before starting long-term azithromycin in a patient with bronchiectasis and 4 exacerbations a year, which step is essential?`,options:[`Exclude non-tuberculous mycobacterial infection with sputum cultures`,`Start inhaled corticosteroids for 3 months first`,`Confirm Pseudomonas colonisation`,`Perform a sweat chloride test`],answer:0,explanation:`Macrolide monotherapy in undetected NTM infection selects macrolide-resistant MAC, so 3 sputum mycobacterial cultures, an ECG for QTc and liver tests come first. Macrolides help with or without Pseudomonas, ICS are not a prerequisite, and sweat chloride is only for suspected CF.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-bronchiectasis-q3`,stem:`Which drug is contraindicated in non-cystic fibrosis bronchiectasis because it increased exacerbations in a randomised trial?`,options:[`Nebulised hypertonic saline`,`Carbocisteine`,`Nebulised colistimethate`,`Recombinant human DNase (dornase alfa)`],answer:3,explanation:`Dornase alfa, which helps in CF, increased exacerbations and FEV1 decline in non-CF bronchiectasis and is not recommended. Hypertonic saline and carbocisteine are acceptable mucoactives, and nebulised colistimethate is used for chronic Pseudomonas infection.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-bronchiectasis-q4`,stem:`Brensocatib, approved by the US FDA in 2025 for bronchiectasis, acts by which mechanism?`,options:[`Blocking the interleukin-5 receptor on eosinophils`,`Inhibiting dipeptidyl peptidase-1, preventing activation of neutrophil serine proteases`,`Inhibiting bacterial protein synthesis at the 50S ribosome`,`Cleaving extracellular DNA in purulent sputum`],answer:1,explanation:`Brensocatib reversibly inhibits DPP-1 (cathepsin C), so neutrophil elastase and related proteases are not activated in maturing neutrophils; ASPEN showed about 20% fewer exacerbations. Anti-IL-5 receptor is benralizumab, 50S inhibition describes macrolides, and DNA cleavage describes dornase.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`respiratory-bronchiectasis-q5`,stem:`A patient with bronchiectasis grows Pseudomonas aeruginosa for the first time. What does the BTS 2019 guideline advise?`,options:[`No treatment unless symptoms worsen`,`Lifelong oral ciprofloxacin`,`Eradication with ciprofloxacin for 2 weeks followed by nebulised colistin for 3 months`,`Immediate surgical resection of the affected lobe`],answer:2,explanation:`First isolation of Pseudomonas should prompt an eradication attempt - oral ciprofloxacin 500-750 mg twice daily for 2 weeks (or IV therapy) followed by 3 months of nebulised colistin - because chronic infection roughly triples mortality. Ignoring it, lifelong quinolones and surgery are not the recommended approach.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-bronchiectasis-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Define bronchiectasis. Discuss its aetiology, pathogenesis, diagnosis and management, with special reference to India.`,openingLines:[`Bronchiectasis is a chronic lung disease with permanent abnormal dilatation of the bronchi on CT and a clinical syndrome of chronic productive cough and recurrent infections.`,`In India post-tuberculous lung damage is the commonest cause, and patients are younger, sicker and more often infected with Pseudomonas than in Europe (EMBARC-India 2019).`],answer:[{heading:`Aetiology`,points:[`Post-infective: TB (commonest in India), measles, pertussis, NTM.`,`ABPA, immunodeficiency (CVID, HIV), PCD (Kartagener), CF.`,`RA, IBD, alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency; obstruction by foreign body or tumour.`,`Idiopathic in up to 40% in Europe.`]},{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Cole's vicious cycle, now the vicious vortex: infection, inflammation, impaired clearance, structural damage.`,`Neutrophil elastase (activated by DPP-1) destroys elastin and cartilage, causing dilatation.`,`Hypertrophied bronchial arteries cause haemoptysis; Pseudomonas biofilm causes chronic infection.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Chronic cough with copious purulent sputum, recurrent infections, haemoptysis, breathlessness.`,`Coarse crackles, clubbing in severe disease, signs of cor pulmonale late.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`HRCT: signet ring (BAR over 1), lack of tapering, airways within 1 cm of pleura.`,`Bundle: CBC, IgG/IgA/IgM, total and Aspergillus-specific IgE, sputum culture including AFB.`,`Spirometry; selected sweat test, nasal NO, alpha-1 antitrypsin, RF, HIV.`,`Severity by BSI or FACED.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Treat cause: immunoglobulin, ABPA steroids or itraconazole.`,`Airway clearance daily; hypertonic saline or carbocisteine; pulmonary rehabilitation; vaccines.`,`Exacerbations: sputum culture then 14 days antibiotics (amoxicillin, clarithromycin or ciprofloxacin).`,`3 or more exacerbations a year: azithromycin maintenance after excluding NTM; inhaled colistin for chronic Pseudomonas.`,`Pseudomonas eradication at first isolation; surgery for localised disease; BAE for haemoptysis.`,`New: brensocatib (ASPEN 2025, FDA approved).`]},{heading:`Indian perspective`,points:[`Avoid repeat empirical ATT; confirm with CBNAAT and HRCT.`,`Prevent through NTEP case-finding, UIP vaccines and smoke reduction.`]}],mustDraw:[`Vicious vortex diagram (infection, inflammation, impaired clearance, structural damage)`,`Table of causes by lobe distribution`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and aetiology`,marks:2},{part:`Pathogenesis with diagram`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis and severity`,marks:2},{part:`Management`,marks:3},{part:`Indian perspective`,marks:1}],keywords:[`bronchiectasis`,`post-TB`,`HRCT`,`airway clearance`,`macrolide`,`Pseudomonas`]},{id:`respiratory-bronchiectasis-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on long-term antibiotic therapy in bronchiectasis.`,openingLines:[`Long-term antibiotics in bronchiectasis are offered to frequent exacerbators (3 or more a year) despite optimal airway clearance.`,`The choice depends on chronic Pseudomonas status: inhaled antibiotics for chronic Pseudomonas, macrolides for others or as add-on.`],answer:[{heading:`Indications`,points:[`3 or more exacerbations a year despite airway clearance and treatment of cause.`,`Chronic infection: same organism in 2 or more cultures 3 months apart.`]},{heading:`Macrolides`,points:[`Azithromycin 250 mg daily or 500 mg three times weekly; erythromycin 400 mg twice daily.`,`EMBRACE, BAT, BLESS: about half the exacerbations.`,`Before: exclude NTM, check QTc and LFTs; monitor hearing.`]},{heading:`Inhaled antibiotics`,points:[`Colistimethate 1-2 MU nebulised twice daily or gentamicin 80 mg twice daily for chronic Pseudomonas.`,`First dose supervised with spirometry for bronchospasm.`]},{heading:`Eradication and alternatives`,points:[`New Pseudomonas: ciprofloxacin 2 weeks then nebulised colistin 3 months.`,`Doxycycline as alternative; brensocatib as a non-antibiotic option (ASPEN 2025).`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: frequent exacerbator, then Pseudomonas yes/no, then inhaled antibiotic or macrolide`],markSplit:[{part:`Indications`,marks:1},{part:`Macrolides with precautions`,marks:2},{part:`Inhaled antibiotics and eradication`,marks:2}],keywords:[`azithromycin`,`colistin`,`eradication`,`NTM`,`QTc`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Bronchiectasis chapter`,`Polverino E et al. European Respiratory Society guidelines for the management of adult bronchiectasis. Eur Respir J 2017`,`Chalmers JD et al. European Respiratory Society clinical practice guideline for the treatment of bronchiectasis in adults (update). Eur Respir J 2025`,`Hill AT et al. British Thoracic Society guideline for bronchiectasis in adults. Thorax 2019`,`Dhar R et al. Bronchiectasis in India: results from the EMBARC and Respiratory Research Network of India registry. Lancet Global Health 2019`,`Chalmers JD et al. Phase 3 trial of the DPP-1 inhibitor brensocatib in bronchiectasis (ASPEN). N Engl J Med 2025`,`Aliberti S et al. Criteria and definitions for the radiological and clinical diagnosis of bronchiectasis in adults for clinical trials: an international consensus recommendation. Lancet Respir Med 2022`,`Hill AT et al. Pulmonary exacerbation in adults with bronchiectasis: a consensus definition for clinical research. Eur Respir J 2017`]}),r.push({id:`respiratory-lung-abscess-aspiration`,title:`Lung abscess, aspiration pneumonia and empyema`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Aspiration** of gastric contents causes a sterile chemical **pneumonitis (Mendelson syndrome)**, whereas aspiration of colonised oropharyngeal secretions causes **aspiration pneumonia**, which can necrose into a **lung abscess** (a pus-filled cavity over 2 cm with an air-fluid level) or spread to the pleura as **empyema** (pus in the pleural space) - managed by **prolonged anaerobe-covering antibiotics plus drainage** of any infected pleural collection.`,keywords:[`aspiration pneumonitis`,`Mendelson syndrome`,`aspiration pneumonia`,`anaerobic pneumonia`,`putrid lung abscess`,`necrotising pneumonia`,`empyema thoracis`,`complicated parapneumonic effusion`,`Light classification`,`MIST2`,`intrapleural tPA DNase`,`VATS decortication`,`RAPID score`,`Streptococcus anginosus`,`Lemierre syndrome`,`split pleura sign`,`clindamycin`,`amoxicillin-clavulanate`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Aspiration pneumonitis (Mendelson syndrome, 1946)** is acute chemical lung injury after inhaling sterile acidic gastric contents, classically in obstetric anaesthesia or reduced consciousness. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Aspiration pneumonia** is infection after aspiration of **colonised oropharyngeal secretions** in someone with impaired swallowing or consciousness; it accounts for an estimated **5-15% of community-acquired pneumonia** and more in the elderly and nursing-home residents. [Mandell NEJM 2019]`,`**Lung abscess** is a localised area of **necrosis with a cavity over 2 cm** containing pus; **acute** if symptoms under 4-6 weeks, **chronic** if longer. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Primary** abscesses (about 80%) follow aspiration in a predisposed host; **secondary** ones follow bronchial obstruction (tumour, foreign body), septic emboli or spread from a neighbouring infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Empyema** is pus or organisms on Gram stain or culture in the pleural space; about **20-40% of pneumonia admissions** have a parapneumonic effusion and **5-10%** progress to complicated effusion or empyema. [BTS Pleural 2023]`,`Pleural infection carries roughly **10-20% one-year mortality** in adults and the incidence is rising, especially in the elderly. [BTS Pleural 2023]`,`In India, **tuberculous empyema**, **Staphylococcus aureus** (children and diabetics), **Klebsiella** and **melioidosis** are common, and empyema often presents late after unsupervised antibiotics. [API Textbook 2022]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Reduced consciousness**: alcohol intoxication, seizures, stroke, sedatives, general anaesthesia, head injury, drug overdose. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dysphagia**: stroke, Parkinson disease, motor neurone disease, oesophageal disease (achalasia, stricture, cancer), and nasogastric or PEG feeding. [Mandell NEJM 2019]`,`**Poor dental hygiene and periodontitis** raise the anaerobic load of saliva; an **edentulous** patient with a lung abscess should raise suspicion of an obstructing **carcinoma**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anaerobes** dominate classic abscess: **Peptostreptococcus, Prevotella, Fusobacterium nucleatum, Bacteroides** - with microaerophilic **Streptococcus anginosus (milleri) group**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Aerobic and necrotising causes**: **S aureus** (including PVL-positive MRSA after influenza), **Klebsiella pneumoniae** (alcoholics, diabetics - currant-jelly sputum), Pseudomonas, Nocardia, TB, **Burkholderia pseudomallei**, and **Entamoeba histolytica** from a ruptured liver abscess. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Septic emboli**: right-sided infective endocarditis in IV drug users, infected lines, and **Lemierre syndrome** (Fusobacterium necrophorum septic thrombophlebitis of the internal jugular vein after pharyngitis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Empyema risk factors**: diabetes, alcohol, poor dentition, IV drug use, aspiration, immunosuppression, and delayed drainage of a parapneumonic effusion; **post-surgical and traumatic** empyema follow chest procedures. [BTS Pleural 2023]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**Gastric acid at pH under 2.5 and volume over about 0.3 mL/kg** causes a chemical burn of the airways and alveoli within minutes, with neutrophilic inflammation, capillary leak and sometimes **ARDS**. [Marik NEJM 2001]`,`Aspiration pneumonitis is **initially sterile**; bacterial superinfection may follow, so antibiotics are not automatically needed. [Mandell NEJM 2019]`,`**Gravity decides the site**: supine aspiration goes to the **posterior segment of the right upper lobe and the superior (apical) segments of the lower lobes**; upright aspiration goes to the **basal segments**; the right side is favoured because the right main bronchus is wider and more vertical. [Harrison 22e]`,`Anaerobic pneumonitis evolves over **7-14 days** into tissue **necrosis**, and when the necrotic area communicates with a bronchus the pus drains, leaving a **cavity with an air-fluid level** and foul **putrid sputum**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Parapneumonic effusion** evolves in three stages: an **exudative** stage of sterile fluid from capillary leak, a **fibrinopurulent** stage of bacterial invasion, fibrin deposition and loculation, and an **organising** stage of fibroblast ingrowth forming a thick **pleural peel**. [ATS 1962; BTS Pleural 2023]`,`In the fibrinopurulent stage, **bacterial and neutrophil metabolism** consumes glucose and produces lactic acid and CO2, so the fluid shows **low pH, low glucose and high LDH** - the biochemistry that predicts the need for drainage. [Light Chest 2006]`,`**Plasminogen activator inhibitor** rises in infected pleural fluid, favouring fibrin loculation; extracellular **DNA** from dead neutrophils makes pus viscous - the rationale for **tPA plus DNase**. [MIST2 NEJM 2011]`]},{heading:`Classification and staging`,points:[`**Aspiration syndromes**: pneumonitis (chemical), pneumonia (bacterial), airway obstruction by particulate matter, and chronic aspiration with lipoid pneumonia or bronchiectasis. [Mandell NEJM 2019]`,`**Lung abscess** classification: by duration (**acute under 4-6 weeks, chronic over 6 weeks**), by mechanism (**primary vs secondary**), and by organism (**putrid anaerobic vs non-putrid aerobic**). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Parapneumonic effusion types (BTS 2023)**: **simple** (free-flowing, pH 7.2 or more), **complicated** (pH under 7.2, positive Gram stain or culture, or loculation) and **empyema** (frank pus). [BTS Pleural 2023]`,`**Light's 7 classes**: 1 non-significant (under 10 mm on decubitus), 2 typical parapneumonic, 3 borderline complicated (pH 7.0-7.2 or LDH over 1000 IU/L), 4 simple complicated, 5 complex complicated (loculated), 6 simple empyema, 7 complex empyema (multiloculated pus). [Light Chest 2006]`,`**ACCP categories (2000)** combine anatomy (size, loculation), bacteriology and chemistry (pH under 7.20) into categories 1-4; **categories 3 and 4 need drainage**. [ACCP 2000]`,`**RAPID score** predicts 3-month mortality in pleural infection from **R**enal (urea), **A**ge, **P**urulence (non-purulent fluid scores higher), **I**nfection source (hospital-acquired) and **D**ietary factors (low albumin): 0-2 low, 3-4 medium, 5-7 high risk. [Rahman Chest 2014]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Aspiration pneumonitis** is abrupt: witnessed vomiting followed within **2-5 hours** by breathlessness, wheeze, cough, hypoxia and sometimes fever. [Mandell NEJM 2019]`,`**Aspiration pneumonia** is a pneumonia in a person with dysphagia or reduced consciousness, often **in a dependent segment**, with an indolent course in the elderly. [Mandell NEJM 2019]`,`**Lung abscess** is often **subacute over weeks**: fever, night sweats, **weight loss**, cough with **copious foul-smelling (putrid) sputum** and sometimes haemoptysis - easily mistaken for TB. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Putrid sputum** or empyema fluid with a foul smell is virtually diagnostic of **anaerobic infection**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Empyema**: persisting or recurrent fever, pleuritic pain and breathlessness despite antibiotics for pneumonia beyond about **3 days** - the commonest clue. [BTS Pleural 2023]`,`Ask about **alcohol, seizures, stroke, dental disease, dysphagia, IV drug use, sore throat** (Lemierre), diabetes and recent chest procedures. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Empyema necessitans** - pus tracking through the chest wall to form a fluctuant swelling - is classic of **TB and actinomycosis**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Foul breath**, poor dentition, gingivitis, and signs of the predisposing condition (stroke, drowsiness, alcohol). [Harrison 22e]`,`Lung abscess may give **localised crackles and bronchial breathing**, and **amphoric breath sounds** over a large superficial cavity; **clubbing** appears within weeks in chronic abscess or empyema. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pleural effusion signs**: reduced expansion, **stony dull percussion**, absent breath sounds and vocal resonance, with tracheal shift away in a large collection. [Macleod 15e]`,`Pleural pus with a **bronchopleural fistula** may give a **succussion splash** and coughing up of large volumes of pus on lying on the healthy side. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sepsis signs**: fever, tachycardia, hypotension, confusion, and RR 30 or more - signal severe disease and urgent drainage. [SSC 2021]`,`**Neck tenderness and swelling along the sternomastoid** after pharyngitis with multiple lung cavities suggests **Lemierre syndrome**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Cavitating pulmonary TB**: apical, thin-walled cavity, little air-fluid level, chronic course; CBNAAT positive. [NTEP 2023]`,`**Cavitating carcinoma (squamous)**: older smoker, thick irregular wall over 15 mm, eccentric cavity, no fever response; needs bronchoscopy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Infected bulla or cyst**: thin wall, air-fluid level, patient often less ill; previous films show the bulla. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Granulomatosis with polyangiitis and septic emboli**: multiple cavitating nodules; check ANCA, blood cultures and echo. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Empyema with bronchopleural fistula vs lung abscess**: empyema is **lenticular, has obtuse angles with the chest wall, compresses lung and its air-fluid level differs in length on PA and lateral views**; abscess is spherical with equal air-fluid levels. [Fraser and Pare 4e]`,`**Pulmonary infarction, aspergilloma, hydatid cyst (water-lily sign) and melioidosis** are other causes of cavities in Indian practice. [API Textbook 2022]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Chest X-ray** shows a dependent-segment infiltrate in aspiration pneumonia, and a **thick-walled cavity with an air-fluid level** in lung abscess. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Contrast CT thorax** separates abscess from empyema: empyema shows the **split pleura sign** (enhancing visceral and parietal pleura separated by fluid) and compresses the lung. [BTS Pleural 2023]`,`**Thoracic ultrasound** should guide every pleural aspiration: **septations or echogenic fluid** indicate a complicated effusion and predict need for drainage. [BTS Pleural 2023]`,`**Diagnostic pleural tap**: measure **pH with a blood-gas analyser** (heparinised syringe, no air, no local anaesthetic contamination), glucose, LDH, protein, Gram stain, culture in blood-culture bottles, AFB and ADA. [BTS Pleural 2023]`,`**Drain the pleural space if** frank pus, organisms on Gram stain or culture, **pH under 7.2**, or a large loculated collection; in a **pH of 7.2-7.4 with LDH over 900 IU/L**, drainage should be considered. [BTS Pleural 2023]`,`**Blood cultures** (positive in only about 10-15% of anaerobic abscess), sputum Gram stain and culture, **sputum CBNAAT** for TB, and melioidosis culture in diabetics from endemic areas. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anaerobes are rarely grown from expectorated sputum** because of mouth contamination; pleural fluid in blood-culture bottles, CT-guided aspirate or protected bronchoscopic samples give better yield. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bronchoscopy** is indicated if the patient is **edentulous, a smoker over 40-45, fails to respond, or has an atypical site** - to exclude tumour or foreign body. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CBC, CRP, procalcitonin, glucose (HbA1c), renal function and albumin** (RAPID score); HIV in recurrent or unusual infection. [BTS Pleural 2023]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`**Aspiration pneumonitis**: suction, oxygen and observation; **do not give antibiotics or steroids routinely** - start antibiotics only if symptoms persist beyond about **48 hours** or bacterial infection is likely. [Mandell NEJM 2019]`,`**Aspiration pneumonia (community, no abscess or empyema)**: treat as CAP; **routine extra anaerobic cover is not recommended** (ATS/IDSA 2019) - **amoxicillin-clavulanate 625 mg three times daily for 5-7 days** is a sensible oral choice. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`,`Mild-moderate lung abscess in a stable patient can complete treatment with **oral amoxicillin-clavulanate 625 mg three times daily** or **clindamycin 300-450 mg four times daily** after initial IV therapy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Metronidazole alone fails** in about half of abscesses because it does not cover microaerophilic and aerobic streptococci; if used, it must be combined with a beta-lactam. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Swallow assessment** by speech therapist, upright feeding at 30-45 degrees, oral hygiene and dental treatment prevent recurrence. [NICE 2019]`,`Any patient with fever and an effusion should have a **diagnostic tap or be referred** - an effusion is never left untapped in a febrile pneumonia patient. [BTS Pleural 2023]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD - abscess and empyema`,points:[`**Lung abscess first line**: **amoxicillin-clavulanate 1.2 g IV every 8 hours**, or **ampicillin-sulbactam 3 g IV every 6 hours**, or **clindamycin 600 mg IV every 8 hours** (superior to penicillin in older trials). [Harrison 22e]`,`Severe, hospital-acquired or Klebsiella/Pseudomonas risk: **piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g IV every 6-8 hours** or a **carbapenem (meropenem 1 g every 8 hours)**; add **vancomycin or linezolid** for MRSA. [IDSA 2016]`,`**Duration of abscess treatment**: until the cavity has closed or left a small stable scar - usually **3-6 weeks or longer**; switch to oral once afebrile and improving. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Expected response**: fever settles in **3-4 days** and in most by **7-10 days**; radiological clearance lags for weeks and an initially enlarging cavity is not failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Abscess drainage**: postural drainage and physiotherapy; **CT-guided percutaneous drainage** for large (over 6-8 cm) abscesses failing antibiotics; **surgical resection (lobectomy)** for failure, haemorrhage or suspected cancer - needed in under 10%. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Empyema antibiotics (community-acquired)**: **amoxicillin-clavulanate**, or **ceftriaxone 2 g daily plus metronidazole 500 mg every 8 hours**; hospital-acquired: piperacillin-tazobactam or meropenem with MRSA cover as needed; avoid aminoglycosides (poor pleural penetration, inactivated by acid pus). [BTS Pleural 2023]`,`**Empyema antibiotic duration**: usually **3-6 weeks** guided by clinical, CRP and imaging response; shorter courses after effective drainage are being studied. [BTS Pleural 2023]`,`**Chest drain**: **small-bore (12-14 F) Seldinger drain** under ultrasound is adequate for most, flushed with 20-30 mL saline every 6 hours; large-bore drains are not superior. [BTS Pleural 2023]`,`**Intrapleural therapy (MIST2)**: **tPA (alteplase) 10 mg plus DNase (dornase alfa) 5 mg twice daily for 3 days** via the drain - improved drainage, reduced surgical referral and shortened stay; **either agent alone or streptokinase (MIST1) does not help**. [MIST2 NEJM 2011]`,`**Surgery (VATS debridement and decortication)**: if sepsis persists after **5-7 days** of drainage, antibiotics and intrapleural therapy, or for an organised thick peel; open decortication or **open window thoracostomy (Eloesser flap)** for frail patients. [BTS Pleural 2023]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: Mendelson syndrome is the classic obstetric risk - prevent with fasting, **H2 blockers or PPIs, sodium citrate** before general anaesthesia and regional anaesthesia where possible; amoxicillin-clavulanate and clindamycin are safe. [Williams Obstetrics 26e]`,`**Elderly and stroke**: silent aspiration is common; screen swallow, keep upright, treat dental disease, and review sedatives and antipsychotics. [NICE 2019]`,`**Diabetes**: Klebsiella, S aureus and **melioidosis** abscesses are more common; check HbA1c and treat melioidosis with **ceftazidime or meropenem** (see the Tropical pneumonias topic). [ICMR 2022]`,`**CKD**: adjust beta-lactam and carbapenem doses; avoid repeated contrast; **intrapleural tPA** has low systemic absorption. [BNF 2025]`,`**Anticoagulated patients**: intrapleural tPA carries a bleeding risk (about 2-4% pleural bleeding); check platelets and INR and weigh against surgery. [BTS Pleural 2023]`,`**Children**: empyema is usually pneumococcal or S aureus and responds well to drain plus **urokinase** or VATS; long-term outcome is excellent. [BTS Paediatric 2005]`,`**Tuberculous empyema** needs ATT plus drainage, sometimes decortication; it is common in India and is suggested by lymphocytic fluid with **ADA over 70 U/L** (see the Pleural effusion topic). [NTEP 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Aspiration pneumonitis** may progress to **ARDS** in a minority; mortality depends on volume and acidity of the aspirate. [Marik NEJM 2001]`,`**Lung abscess complications**: **empyema and bronchopleural fistula**, massive haemoptysis, spread to other lobes, brain abscess, and residual bronchiectasis or cavity. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mortality of primary lung abscess** is about **5-10%** with modern antibiotics, but much higher in secondary abscess with tumour, immunosuppression or S aureus/Klebsiella. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Empyema complications**: trapped lung with restrictive defect, bronchopleural fistula, **empyema necessitans**, sepsis and death; **pleural thickening** may persist. [BTS Pleural 2023]`,`**Adverse prognostic factors**: age, renal failure, low albumin, hospital-acquired infection, non-purulent fluid and delay in drainage (RAPID). [Rahman Chest 2014]`,`A **chronic cavity** after treatment may become colonised by Aspergillus (aspergilloma) or remain as a thin-walled cyst. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer urgently** any febrile pneumonia with an effusion, any cavity, or any patient not improving after 72 hours of antibiotics. [BTS Pleural 2023]`,`**Chest surgeon referral** if drainage fails at **5-7 days**, the fluid is organised, or the abscess is complicated by haemoptysis or fistula. [BTS Pleural 2023]`,`Monitor **temperature chart, CRP, drain output and ultrasound** every 2-3 days; repeat CT if not improving. [BTS Pleural 2023]`,`**Follow-up chest X-ray at 6-8 weeks** after treatment, earlier if symptoms recur; persistent opacity in a smoker needs CT and bronchoscopy for cancer. [BTS 2009 CAP]`,`After discharge, review **dental health, swallowing and alcohol use** - the drivers of recurrence. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Prevention and counselling`,points:[`**Oral care**: tooth brushing and dental treatment in the elderly and nursing-home residents reduce aspiration pneumonia. [NICE 2019]`,`**Dysphagia screening after stroke** before any oral intake, and modified diet with chin-tuck techniques. [AHA Stroke 2019]`,`**Head-of-bed elevation** for tube-fed patients; post-pyloric feeding has not clearly reduced pneumonia. [ESPEN 2023]`,`**Pre-anaesthetic fasting** (2 hours clear fluids, 6 hours light meal) and antacid prophylaxis in obstetrics. [ASA 2023]`,`**Alcohol and drug de-addiction**, seizure control and avoidance of unnecessary sedatives. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Early tap of parapneumonic effusions** prevents progression to organised empyema. [BTS Pleural 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**BTS Guideline for Pleural Disease 2023** replaced the 2010 guideline: ultrasound-guided drainage, **pH under 7.2 to drain**, consider drainage at pH 7.2-7.4 with **LDH over 900 IU/L**, and **intrapleural tPA-DNase** for failing drainage. [BTS Pleural 2023]`,`**ATS/IDSA CAP 2019** (current) - do not add anaerobic cover for suspected aspiration pneumonia unless **lung abscess or empyema** is suspected. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`,`**ERS/EACTS statement on pleural infection (2023)** endorses the MIST2 regimen, early surgical opinion and the RAPID score for risk stratification. [ERS/EACTS 2023]`,`**MIST3 (2024)** feasibility trial compared early VATS with early intrapleural therapy and standard care; it supports a definitive trial rather than routine early surgery. [MIST3 2024]`,`Lower or **once-daily or concurrent doses of tPA** (for example 5 mg) are increasingly used to reduce bleeding risk, with similar efficacy in observational series. [ERS/EACTS 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic viva: **pneumonitis vs pneumonia** after aspiration - timing, sterility, need for antibiotics.`,`Know the **sites of aspiration** in supine and upright positions and why the right side is affected more.`,`Classic X-ray question: **lung abscess vs empyema with air-fluid level** - spherical vs lenticular, equal vs unequal air-fluid levels, acute vs obtuse angles.`,`Name **Light's criteria** for exudate and the **pH under 7.2** rule for drainage.`,`Know **MIST2**: tPA 10 mg + DNase 5 mg twice daily for 3 days; neither alone works.`,`Common mistake: **metronidazole alone** for lung abscess - it misses microaerophilic streptococci.`,`Common mistake: **aminoglycosides for empyema** - they penetrate pus poorly and are inactivated by low pH.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Antibiotics and intrapleural agents`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Amoxicillin-clavulanate`,`1.2 g IV 8-hourly; then 625 mg oral 8-hourly`,`Abscess 3-6 weeks; aspiration pneumonia 5-7 days`,`Cholestatic jaundice, rash, diarrhoea; penicillin allergy`],[`Clindamycin`,`600 mg IV 8-hourly; then 300-450 mg oral 6-hourly`,`Abscess 3-6 weeks`,`C difficile colitis; good choice in penicillin allergy`],[`Ampicillin-sulbactam`,`3 g IV 6-hourly`,`Until oral switch`,`Rash, diarrhoea; adjust in renal failure`],[`Ceftriaxone + metronidazole`,`2 g IV daily + 500 mg IV/oral 8-hourly`,`Empyema 3-6 weeks total`,`Biliary sludge; metronidazole neuropathy, disulfiram reaction`],[`Piperacillin-tazobactam`,`4.5 g IV 6-8-hourly`,`Hospital-acquired or severe`,`Hypokalaemia, AKI with vancomycin`],[`Meropenem`,`1 g IV 8-hourly`,`Severe, ESBL, melioidosis`,`Seizures (lower than imipenem), C difficile; carbapenem stewardship`],[`Alteplase (tPA) intrapleural`,`10 mg in 30 mL saline via drain, clamp 1 hour, twice daily`,`6 doses over 3 days, with DNase`,`Pleural bleeding about 2-4%; caution with anticoagulants`],[`Dornase alfa (DNase) intrapleural`,`5 mg in 30 mL saline via drain, twice daily`,`6 doses over 3 days, with tPA`,`Ineffective alone; chest pain`]]},{heading:`Aspiration pneumonitis vs aspiration pneumonia`,columns:[`Feature`,`Aspiration pneumonitis`,`Aspiration pneumonia`],rows:[[`Aspirate`,`Sterile acidic gastric contents`,`Colonised oropharyngeal secretions`],[`Mechanism`,`Chemical burn and inflammation`,`Bacterial infection`],[`Typical patient`,`Young, drug overdose, anaesthesia, obstetrics`,`Elderly, dysphagia, stroke, poor dentition`],[`Onset`,`2-5 hours after witnessed aspiration`,`Days, often unwitnessed`],[`Course`,`Settles in 24-48 hours or progresses to ARDS`,`Pneumonia; may form abscess or empyema`],[`Antibiotics`,`Not routinely; only if persisting over 48 hours`,`Yes, as for CAP or HAP`],[`Steroids`,`Not recommended`,`Not recommended`]]},{heading:`Light's classification of parapneumonic effusion and empyema`,columns:[`Class`,`Features`,`Action`],rows:[[`1 Non-significant`,`Under 10 mm on decubitus film`,`No tap; antibiotics`],[`2 Typical parapneumonic`,`Over 10 mm, glucose over 40 mg/dL, pH over 7.2`,`Antibiotics alone`],[`3 Borderline complicated`,`pH 7.0-7.2 or LDH over 1000 IU/L, Gram stain negative`,`Antibiotics, repeat tap`],[`4 Simple complicated`,`pH under 7.0 or glucose under 40 or Gram/culture positive; not loculated`,`Chest drain plus antibiotics`],[`5 Complex complicated`,`As class 4 and multiloculated`,`Drain plus intrapleural tPA-DNase; consider VATS`],[`6 Simple empyema`,`Frank pus, single locule or free-flowing`,`Chest drain; consider decortication`],[`7 Complex empyema`,`Frank pus, multiple loculations`,`Drain plus intrapleural therapy; often VATS or decortication`]]},{heading:`Lung abscess vs empyema with air-fluid level`,columns:[`Feature`,`Lung abscess`,`Empyema with bronchopleural fistula`],rows:[[`Shape`,`Spherical`,`Lenticular (lens-shaped)`],[`Angle with chest wall`,`Acute`,`Obtuse`],[`Air-fluid level on PA and lateral`,`Equal length`,`Different lengths`],[`Wall`,`Thick, irregular`,`Thin, smooth, uniform`],[`Effect on lung`,`Destroys lung, vessels end at wall`,`Compresses and displaces lung and vessels`],[`CT sign`,`Cavity within parenchyma`,`Split pleura sign`],[`Treatment`,`Antibiotics; drainage rarely`,`Chest drain essential`]]},{heading:`Pleural fluid cut-offs in suspected pleural infection`,columns:[`Test`,`Cut-off`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`Appearance`,`Frank pus`,`Empyema - drain`],[`pH (blood-gas analyser)`,`Under 7.2`,`Complicated effusion - drain`],[`pH with LDH`,`pH 7.2-7.4 and LDH over 900 IU/L`,`Consider drainage (BTS 2023)`],[`Glucose`,`Under 40 mg/dL (2.2 mmol/L)`,`Complicated effusion (Light)`],[`LDH`,`Over 1000 IU/L`,`Intense pleural inflammation`],[`Gram stain or culture`,`Positive`,`Pleural infection - drain`],[`ADA`,`Over 40 U/L, lymphocytic`,`Suggests tuberculous effusion in India`]]}],redFlags:[`**Febrile pneumonia with a pleural effusion** - tap now; pus or pH under 7.2 needs a drain.`,`**Sepsis or septic shock** with a cavity or effusion.`,`**Massive haemoptysis** from a lung abscess.`,`**Fever persisting beyond 5-7 days** of appropriate antibiotics - look for empyema, obstruction or resistant organism.`,`**Edentulous patient or smoker over 45 with an abscess** - bronchoscopy for tumour.`,`**Hypoxia and diffuse infiltrates after witnessed aspiration** - evolving ARDS.`,`**Multiple cavities with neck swelling or IV drug use** - septic emboli, Lemierre syndrome or endocarditis.`],pearls:[`**Pneumonitis is sterile; pneumonia is infected** - no routine antibiotics for pneumonitis.`,`**Supine: posterior upper and superior lower segments; upright: basal segments**; right more than left.`,`**Putrid sputum = anaerobes.**`,`**Metronidazole alone fails** in lung abscess; combine with a beta-lactam or use clindamycin or co-amoxiclav.`,`**pH under 7.2 = drain** the pleural space.`,`**MIST2**: tPA 10 mg + DNase 5 mg twice daily for 3 days - **together, never alone**.`,`**Abscess is spherical; empyema is lenticular** with a split pleura sign.`,`**RAPID**: Renal, Age, Purulence, Infection source, Dietary (albumin).`,`Abscess in an **edentulous** patient: think **carcinoma** - no teeth, fewer anaerobes.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-lung-abscess-aspiration-q1`,stem:`A 25-year-old woman vomits and aspirates during an epileptic seizure. Three hours later she is breathless with SpO2 90% and bilateral infiltrates. What is the most appropriate initial management?`,options:[`Intravenous methylprednisolone`,`Oxygen, airway suction and close observation without routine antibiotics`,`Intravenous meropenem and vancomycin`,`Intravenous metronidazole alone`],answer:1,explanation:`This is aspiration pneumonitis - a sterile chemical injury - so treatment is supportive with oxygen and suction, adding antibiotics only if it persists beyond about 48 hours. Steroids are not beneficial, broad-spectrum antibiotics are not routinely indicated, and metronidazole alone is never adequate for aspiration infection.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-lung-abscess-aspiration-q2`,stem:`A bedridden alcoholic man aspirates while lying supine. Which lung segment is most likely to develop a lung abscess?`,options:[`Anterior segment of the left upper lobe`,`Lingula`,`Medial basal segment of the left lower lobe`,`Posterior segment of the right upper lobe`],answer:3,explanation:`In the supine position aspirated material drains by gravity into the posterior segment of the right upper lobe and the superior segments of the lower lobes, with the right side favoured by the wider, more vertical right main bronchus. Basal segments are affected in upright aspiration, and anterior segments or the lingula are uncommon sites.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-lung-abscess-aspiration-q3`,stem:`In a patient with pneumonia and a free-flowing pleural effusion, which pleural fluid finding by itself mandates chest tube drainage?`,options:[`pH 7.10 measured on a blood-gas analyser`,`Protein 4.0 g/dL`,`Lymphocyte predominance`,`Pleural fluid to serum protein ratio of 0.6`],answer:0,explanation:`A pleural fluid pH under 7.2 indicates a complicated parapneumonic effusion that will not resolve on antibiotics alone and needs drainage (BTS 2023). A high protein and a protein ratio over 0.5 merely confirm an exudate, and lymphocyte predominance points towards TB or malignancy rather than bacterial infection.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-lung-abscess-aspiration-q4`,stem:`The MIST2 trial showed benefit in pleural infection from which intrapleural regimen?`,options:[`Streptokinase 250 000 units twice daily for 3 days`,`DNase 5 mg alone twice daily for 3 days`,`Tissue plasminogen activator 10 mg plus DNase 5 mg twice daily for 3 days`,`Tissue plasminogen activator 10 mg alone once daily for 3 days`],answer:2,explanation:`MIST2 (NEJM 2011) found that combined tPA 10 mg and DNase 5 mg twice daily for 3 days improved pleural opacity and reduced surgical referral and hospital stay. Either agent alone was no better than placebo (DNase alone increased surgical referral), and streptokinase failed in MIST1.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-lung-abscess-aspiration-q5`,stem:`A chest X-ray shows a cavity with an air-fluid level. Which feature favours empyema with bronchopleural fistula over lung abscess?`,options:[`Spherical shape with acute angles to the chest wall`,`Air-fluid level of different lengths on PA and lateral views`,`Thick, irregular wall`,`Vessels ending abruptly at the cavity wall`],answer:1,explanation:`Because an empyema is lenticular and lies in the pleural space, its air-fluid level differs in length on PA and lateral views and it meets the chest wall at obtuse angles, compressing lung. A spherical shape with acute angles, a thick irregular wall and vessels ending at the wall all favour a parenchymal lung abscess.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-lung-abscess-aspiration-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the aetiology, clinical features, investigations and management of lung abscess. Add a note on how you would differentiate it from empyema.`,openingLines:[`Lung abscess is a localised area of pulmonary necrosis forming a cavity over 2 cm containing pus, usually with an air-fluid level.`,`Most are primary, following aspiration of anaerobe-laden oropharyngeal secretions in a patient with reduced consciousness, dysphagia or poor dentition.`],answer:[{heading:`Aetiology`,points:[`Primary: aspiration (alcohol, seizures, stroke, anaesthesia, dysphagia, poor dentition).`,`Organisms: anaerobes (Peptostreptococcus, Prevotella, Fusobacterium, Bacteroides), S anginosus group; S aureus, Klebsiella, melioidosis, TB.`,`Secondary: bronchial obstruction (tumour, foreign body), septic emboli (right IE, Lemierre), immunosuppression.`]},{heading:`Pathogenesis and sites`,points:[`Aspiration pneumonitis to necrosis over 1-2 weeks; cavity drains through a bronchus.`,`Supine: posterior RUL and superior lower-lobe segments; upright: basal segments.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Subacute fever, weight loss, cough with copious putrid sputum, haemoptysis.`,`Foul breath, poor dentition, crackles, amphoric breathing, clubbing if chronic.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`CXR: thick-walled cavity with air-fluid level; CT to define and exclude empyema or tumour.`,`Sputum Gram stain, culture, CBNAAT; blood cultures; CBC, glucose, HIV.`,`Bronchoscopy if edentulous, smoker over 45, non-response or atypical site.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Amoxicillin-clavulanate 1.2 g IV 8-hourly or clindamycin 600 mg IV 8-hourly; carbapenem or pip-taz if severe.`,`Oral step-down; total 3-6 weeks until cavity closes or small stable scar.`,`Postural drainage; CT-guided drainage for large non-resolving abscess; lobectomy in under 10%.`,`Treat cause: dental care, swallow assessment, alcohol de-addiction.`]},{heading:`Abscess vs empyema`,points:[`Spherical vs lenticular; acute vs obtuse angles; equal vs unequal air-fluid levels on two views.`,`CT split pleura sign in empyema; empyema needs a drain, abscess usually does not.`]}],mustDraw:[`CXR sketch of lung abscess vs empyema with air-fluid levels on PA and lateral`,`Aspiration sites in supine and upright positions`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and aetiology`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:1},{part:`Investigations`,marks:2},{part:`Management`,marks:3},{part:`Differentiation from empyema`,marks:2}],keywords:[`lung abscess`,`anaerobes`,`aspiration`,`clindamycin`,`air-fluid level`,`empyema`]},{id:`respiratory-lung-abscess-aspiration-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`chart-flow`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Draw a flow chart for the management of a parapneumonic effusion and empyema.`,openingLines:[`A parapneumonic effusion is any effusion associated with pneumonia; it evolves through exudative, fibrinopurulent and organising stages.`,`Management turns on ultrasound-guided tapping and the pleural fluid pH, with drainage when pH is under 7.2 or there is pus or organisms (BTS 2023).`],answer:[{heading:`Step 1 - assess`,points:[`Pneumonia with effusion: thoracic ultrasound and diagnostic tap.`,`Send pH (blood-gas analyser), glucose, LDH, protein, Gram stain, culture, ADA.`]},{heading:`Step 2 - decide`,points:[`pH 7.2 or more, clear fluid: antibiotics, repeat tap if not improving.`,`pH under 7.2, pus, organisms, or loculated: small-bore chest drain plus antibiotics.`,`pH 7.2-7.4 with LDH over 900 IU/L: consider drainage.`]},{heading:`Step 3 - escalate`,points:[`Poor drainage at 24-48 hours: intrapleural tPA 10 mg + DNase 5 mg twice daily for 3 days.`,`Persisting sepsis at 5-7 days: VATS decortication; frail patients: open window drainage.`]},{heading:`Antibiotics`,points:[`Co-amoxiclav or ceftriaxone + metronidazole; pip-taz or meropenem if hospital-acquired.`,`Avoid aminoglycosides; total 3-6 weeks; RAPID score for prognosis.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart from ultrasound and tap to antibiotics, drain, intrapleural therapy and surgery`],markSplit:[{part:`Assessment and tap`,marks:1},{part:`Drainage criteria`,marks:2},{part:`Escalation and antibiotics`,marks:2}],keywords:[`parapneumonic effusion`,`pH 7.2`,`chest drain`,`MIST2`,`VATS`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Lung abscess and pleural disease chapters`,`Roberts ME et al. British Thoracic Society Guideline for Pleural Disease. Thorax 2023`,`Rahman NM et al. Intrapleural use of tissue plasminogen activator and DNase in pleural infection (MIST2). N Engl J Med 2011`,`Bedawi EO et al. ERS/EACTS statement on the management of pleural infection in adults. Eur Respir J 2023`,`Metlay JP et al. Diagnosis and treatment of adults with community-acquired pneumonia: ATS/IDSA guideline. Am J Respir Crit Care Med 2019`,`Mandell LA, Niederman MS. Aspiration pneumonia. N Engl J Med 2019`,`Light RW. Parapneumonic effusions and empyema. Proc Am Thorac Soc 2006`,`ICMR Treatment Guidelines for Antimicrobial Use in Common Syndromes, 2nd edition, 2022`]}),r.push({id:`respiratory-hap-vap`,title:`Hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Hospital-acquired pneumonia (HAP)** is pneumonia not incubating at admission that develops **48 hours or more after admission**, and **ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP)** develops **48 hours or more after endotracheal intubation**; in India they are dominated by **carbapenem-resistant Acinetobacter, Klebsiella and Pseudomonas**, so empirical therapy follows the **local antibiogram**, is de-escalated on culture and usually stopped at **7 days**.`,keywords:[`HAP`,`VAP`,`nosocomial pneumonia`,`ventilator-associated events`,`VAE`,`CPIS`,`MDR risk factors`,`carbapenem-resistant Acinetobacter`,`CRE`,`NDM`,`colistin`,`polymyxin B`,`ceftazidime-avibactam`,`aztreonam`,`cefiderocol`,`sulbactam-durlobactam`,`de-escalation`,`VAP bundle`,`ICMR AMRSN`,`IDSA/ATS 2016`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**HAP**: pneumonia not incubating at the time of admission and occurring **48 hours or more after admission**; **VAP**: pneumonia **48 hours or more after endotracheal intubation**. [IDSA/ATS 2016]`,`The old category **healthcare-associated pneumonia (HCAP)** was **removed** in 2016 because it overestimated MDR risk and drove unnecessary broad-spectrum therapy; such patients are now treated as CAP with individual MDR assessment. [IDSA/ATS 2016]`,`**Ventilated HAP** is HAP that needs intubation after onset - it has the worst prognosis, worse than VAP. [ERS/ESICM/ESCMID/ALAT 2017]`,`HAP occurs in about **5-10 per 1000 admissions** and is the **commonest hospital-acquired infection causing death**; VAP affects roughly **10-25% of ventilated patients** depending on definition. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Attributable mortality of VAP is about 13%**, higher in surgical and mid-severity patients, though crude mortality is 20-50%. [Melsen Lancet Infect Dis 2013]`,`**Indian ICUs report device-associated VAP rates several-fold higher than US NHSN benchmarks**, with Gram-negative non-fermenters predominant (INICC and ICMR-AIIMS surveillance). [ICMR-AIIMS HAI Network 2022]`,`The **ICMR Antimicrobial Resistance Surveillance Network (AMRSN)** reports **carbapenem resistance in over 80% of Acinetobacter baumannii**, around **half of Klebsiella pneumoniae**, and about a third of Pseudomonas aeruginosa from ICU isolates. [ICMR AMRSN 2023]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Organisms**: Gram-negative bacilli - **Acinetobacter baumannii, Klebsiella pneumoniae, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, E coli, Enterobacter** - and **S aureus (MSSA and MRSA)**; in India Gram-negatives account for the large majority. [ICMR AMRSN 2023]`,`**Early-onset HAP/VAP (under 5 days)** without risk factors may involve community organisms (S pneumoniae, H influenzae, MSSA), but in Indian ICUs MDR organisms are common even early. [ICMR 2022]`,`**MDR risk factors for VAP**: **IV antibiotics in the previous 90 days**, **septic shock at the time of VAP**, **ARDS preceding VAP**, **5 or more days of hospitalisation before VAP**, and **acute renal replacement therapy before VAP**. [IDSA/ATS 2016]`,`**MDR risk for HAP**: **IV antibiotics in the previous 90 days**; MRSA risk adds a unit MRSA prevalence over 20% (or unknown) and high mortality risk (ventilatory support for HAP or septic shock). [IDSA/ATS 2016]`,`**Host factors**: age over 70, COPD, reduced consciousness, supine position, dysphagia, malnutrition, recent surgery (thoracic, upper abdominal), immunosuppression. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Device and care factors**: intubation and **re-intubation**, prolonged ventilation, deep sedation, nasogastric tubes, contaminated respiratory equipment and hands of staff. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acid suppression** may modestly increase pneumonia, though REVISE (2024) found pantoprazole did not significantly raise VAP in ventilated patients. [REVISE NEJM 2024]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Within days of admission, the **oropharynx is colonised by hospital Gram-negative bacilli**, driven by illness, antibiotics and loss of fibronectin on epithelial cells. [Harrison 22e]`,`The **endotracheal tube bypasses glottic defences**: secretions pool above the cuff and leak through **micro-channels in the cuff folds** into the lower airway (micro-aspiration). [Harrison 22e]`,`A **biofilm** forms on the inner tube surface within hours, which ventilation and suctioning dislodge into distal airways - a reservoir that antibiotics cannot sterilise. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Impaired cough, mucociliary clearance and sedation**, and critical-illness immunoparalysis, reduce clearance of inoculated bacteria. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Gastric colonisation** under acid suppression and enteral feeding can seed the oropharynx in a retrograde manner. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Resistance mechanisms in India**: **ESBLs** (CTX-M), **carbapenemases - NDM (metallo-beta-lactamase) and OXA-48-like** in Enterobacterales, **OXA-23** in Acinetobacter, and efflux and porin loss in Pseudomonas - these decide which new drug works. [ICMR AMRSN 2023]`,`**Metallo-beta-lactamases (NDM)** are not inhibited by avibactam, so **ceftazidime-avibactam must be combined with aztreonam** (which MBLs cannot hydrolyse) for NDM producers. [IDSA AMR Guidance 2024]`]},{heading:`Classification and diagnostic criteria`,points:[`**Clinical diagnosis (IDSA/ATS 2016)**: **new or progressive infiltrate** plus clinical signs of infection - **new fever, purulent sputum, leucocytosis or leucopenia, and worsening oxygenation**. [IDSA/ATS 2016]`,`**Clinical Pulmonary Infection Score (CPIS)** scores temperature, WBC, secretions, PaO2/FiO2, CXR and culture; **over 6 suggests VAP**, but its accuracy is poor and it is not recommended for decisions. [IDSA/ATS 2016]`,`**CDC/NHSN Ventilator-Associated Events (VAE)** - a surveillance, not clinical, definition: **VAC** (sustained rise in daily minimum PEEP of 3 cmH2O or more, or FiO2 of 0.20 or more, for 2 or more days after 2 days of stability), then **IVAC** (plus infection signs and new antibiotics), then **possible VAP** (plus positive respiratory specimen). [CDC NHSN 2024]`,`**Early vs late onset**: under 5 days vs 5 days or more of hospitalisation; IDSA 2016 no longer uses it alone to choose therapy, but ICMR uses it with MDR risk. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Ventilator-associated tracheobronchitis (VAT)**: fever and purulent secretions with positive culture but **no new infiltrate**; routine antibiotics are not recommended. [ERS/ESICM 2017]`,`**High mortality risk** in HAP: need for ventilatory support due to HAP, or septic shock - drives double Gram-negative and MRSA cover. [IDSA/ATS 2016]`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`**New fever (38 degrees C or more) or hypothermia**, new or increased purulent secretions, cough and breathlessness in a ward patient. [IDSA/ATS 2016]`,`In the ventilated patient: **rising FiO2 or PEEP needs**, increasing suction requirement, tachypnoea on spontaneous modes, new crackles or bronchial breathing. [IDSA/ATS 2016]`,`**Atypical presentations** in the elderly: confusion, falls, reduced oral intake, new AF or hypotension without fever. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sepsis features**: hypotension, rising lactate, oliguria, thrombocytopenia - assess with SOFA. [SSC 2021]`,`Look for alternative fever sources: **line infection, catheter-associated UTI, C difficile, sinusitis, drug fever, DVT**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Tachypnoea, tachycardia and falling SpO2** are the earliest signs; record trends rather than single values. [IDSA/ATS 2016]`,`**Focal crackles, bronchial breathing or dullness** over a dependent zone; in ventilated patients auscultation is often unhelpful. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Purulent endotracheal secretions** - volume, colour and frequency of suction matter. [IDSA/ATS 2016]`,`**Stony dullness** suggests parapneumonic effusion or empyema, which needs a tap. [BTS Pleural 2023]`,`Check **lines, wounds, catheter, calves and sacrum** for other infection sources and complications. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Atelectasis and lobar collapse** from mucus plugging: volume loss, no fever pattern, improves with physiotherapy or bronchoscopy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cardiogenic pulmonary oedema / fluid overload**: bilateral infiltrates, raised BNP, positive balance, responds to diuresis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ARDS** of another cause: bilateral opacities with P/F under 300 within a week of a known insult (Berlin / global definition 2023). [ATS 2023]`,`**Pulmonary embolism**: sudden hypoxia with clear lungs; CT pulmonary angiography. [ESC 2019]`,`**Aspiration pneumonitis**: witnessed aspiration, rapid onset, often settles in 48 hours. [Mandell NEJM 2019]`,`**Drug fever, transfusion reaction (TRALI), pulmonary haemorrhage** and tumour progression. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Respiratory sample before antibiotics**: sputum, or in VAP a **semi-quantitative endotracheal aspirate** - IDSA prefers non-invasive sampling to bronchoscopy. [IDSA/ATS 2016]`,`**Quantitative thresholds** if used: **endotracheal aspirate 10^5-10^6 CFU/mL, BAL 10^4 CFU/mL, protected specimen brush 10^3 CFU/mL**. [ERS/ESICM 2017]`,`**Blood cultures** (2 sets) in all; positive in about 15% and identify bacteraemia from other sources. [IDSA/ATS 2016]`,`**Rapid molecular panels** (multiplex PCR for organisms and resistance genes such as NDM, KPC, OXA-48, mecA) allow earlier targeted therapy where available. [IDSA AMR Guidance 2024]`,`**Procalcitonin is not recommended to decide whether to start** antibiotics, but a falling PCT with clinical improvement supports **stopping**. [IDSA/ATS 2016]`,`**Chest X-ray** daily is not needed; **lung ultrasound** (dynamic air bronchograms) and **CT** help in uncertain cases. [ERS/ESICM 2017]`,`**ABG, lactate, CBC, renal and liver function** for severity and dosing; **antibiogram** of the unit for empirical choice. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Request carbapenemase typing** (NDM vs OXA-48 vs KPC) from the lab on carbapenem-resistant isolates - it decides between ceftazidime-avibactam alone and the aztreonam combination. [IDSA AMR Guidance 2024]`]},{heading:`Empirical management - IDSA/ATS 2016 and ICMR 2022`,points:[`**Start empirical antibiotics promptly after cultures**, choosing by **MDR risk, mortality risk and the local unit antibiogram** - every hospital should publish its own. [IDSA/ATS 2016]`,`**HAP without MDR risk or high mortality risk**: one agent active against MSSA and Pseudomonas - **piperacillin-tazobactam 4.5 g IV every 6 hours**, **cefepime 2 g IV every 8 hours**, or **levofloxacin 750 mg daily**. [IDSA/ATS 2016]`,`**MDR risk or high mortality risk**: **two antipseudomonal agents of different classes** (for example a beta-lactam plus amikacin 15-20 mg/kg daily or a fluoroquinolone) plus **MRSA cover**. [IDSA/ATS 2016]`,`**MRSA cover**: **vancomycin 15 mg/kg IV every 8-12 hours** (loading 25-30 mg/kg in severe illness, target AUC 400-600) or **linezolid 600 mg IV every 12 hours**. [IDSA/ATS 2016]`,`**ICMR 2022 approach for late-onset or MDR-risk HAP/VAP in Indian ICUs**: **meropenem 1 g IV every 8 hours (extended infusion)** where carbapenem resistance is low, or **colistin or polymyxin B-based therapy** where carbapenem-resistant Acinetobacter or Enterobacterales predominate. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Colistin (colistimethate)**: **loading 9 million units** then **4.5 million units every 12 hours**, adjusted for renal function; **polymyxin B** 2.5-3 mg/kg/day in 2 doses (loading 2-2.5 mg/kg) needs no renal adjustment and is preferred for non-urinary infections. [International Consensus Polymyxins 2019]`,`**Extended or continuous infusion of beta-lactams** (meropenem over 3 hours, piperacillin-tazobactam over 4 hours) improves target attainment; BLING III (2024) showed a non-significant mortality reduction and meta-analysis a significant one. [BLING III JAMA 2024]`,`**Inhaled antibiotics** (amikacin or colistin) as **adjunct** are suggested only for VAP due to organisms susceptible only to aminoglycosides or polymyxins. [IDSA/ATS 2016]`]},{heading:`Targeted therapy for resistant organisms (IDSA AMR Guidance 2024)`,points:[`**ESBL Enterobacterales**: **meropenem** (or ertapenem when not critically ill); piperacillin-tazobactam is not preferred even if reported susceptible (MERINO). [IDSA AMR Guidance 2024]`,`**KPC or OXA-48 carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales**: **ceftazidime-avibactam 2.5 g IV every 8 hours** over 2 hours; alternatives meropenem-vaborbactam (KPC) and imipenem-relebactam (KPC). [IDSA AMR Guidance 2024]`,`**NDM (metallo-beta-lactamase) CRE - common in India**: **ceftazidime-avibactam plus aztreonam (2 g IV every 8 hours) given together**, or **cefiderocol 2 g IV every 8 hours** over 3 hours. [IDSA AMR Guidance 2024]`,`**Difficult-to-treat Pseudomonas**: **ceftolozane-tazobactam 3 g IV every 8 hours** for pneumonia, ceftazidime-avibactam, imipenem-relebactam or cefiderocol, guided by testing. [IDSA AMR Guidance 2024]`,`**Carbapenem-resistant Acinetobacter baumannii (CRAB)**: **sulbactam-durlobactam (1 g/1 g IV every 6 hours) plus a carbapenem** is preferred; alternative **high-dose ampicillin-sulbactam (6-9 g sulbactam/day)** combined with polymyxin B, minocycline, tigecycline or cefiderocol. [IDSA AMR Guidance 2024]`,`**Avoid tigecycline monotherapy** for pneumonia - low serum and lung levels and excess mortality in trials. [IDSA AMR Guidance 2024]`,`Cefepime-enmetazobactam (approved 2024) and Indian-developed **cefepime-zidebactam** are emerging options; use only with microbiology and stewardship input. [Expert opinion 2025]`]},{heading:`De-escalation, duration and monitoring`,points:[`**Review at 48-72 hours**: de-escalate to the narrowest active agent once culture and sensitivity are known; stop MRSA cover if cultures and nasal MRSA PCR are negative. [IDSA/ATS 2016]`,`**Duration: 7 days for both HAP and VAP**, including non-fermenters, if clinical response is good. [IDSA/ATS 2016]`,`The PneumA trial (Chastre 2003) showed **8 days equivalent to 15 days** overall, with more recurrence for non-fermenters but no excess mortality; iDIAPASON (2022) could not prove 8 days non-inferior for **Pseudomonas VAP**, so some clinicians extend selected cases. [iDIAPASON Lancet Respir Med 2022]`,`**Clinical response** is judged at 48-72 hours by fever, oxygenation, secretions, haemodynamics and WBC - not by the chest X-ray, which lags. [IDSA/ATS 2016]`,`**Non-response at 72 hours**: re-culture, consider resistant organism, empyema, abscess, other infection site, non-infective mimic, and under-dosing. [ERS/ESICM 2017]`,`**Therapeutic drug monitoring** for vancomycin (AUC 400-600) and aminoglycosides (trough under 1-2 mg/L), and daily creatinine for colistin. [ASHP/IDSA 2020]`,`**Antimicrobial stewardship** - prospective audit and feedback, ICMR AMSP guidance - reduces carbapenem and colistin use without worse outcomes. [ICMR AMSP 2018]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**CKD and AKI**: adjust beta-lactams, carbapenems and colistin to creatinine clearance; **polymyxin B and linezolid need no renal adjustment**; avoid combining vancomycin with piperacillin-tazobactam where possible (AKI). [Sanford 2025]`,`**Augmented renal clearance** (young trauma, sepsis with CrCl over 130 mL/min) leads to under-dosing of beta-lactams - use extended infusions or higher doses. [ESICM 2024]`,`**Pregnancy**: beta-lactams, carbapenems and vancomycin are acceptable; avoid aminoglycosides, tetracyclines and fluoroquinolones unless no alternative. [Sanford 2025]`,`**Elderly**: atypical presentation, aspiration, and higher risk of C difficile and linezolid thrombocytopenia with longer courses. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Immunocompromised**: broaden the differential to PCP, CMV, fungi and nocardia (see the immunocompromised lung infection topic). [ATS 2020]`,`**Resource-limited ICU**: use the local antibiogram, polymyxin B and old drugs wisely, and invest in hand hygiene and bundles - prevention is cheaper than reserve antibiotics. [ICMR 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Septic shock, ARDS, empyema, lung abscess** and prolonged ventilation and ICU stay. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drug toxicity**: colistin nephrotoxicity (up to 40-60%) and neurotoxicity, vancomycin AKI, linezolid thrombocytopenia and serotonin syndrome, C difficile infection. [Sanford 2025]`,`**Mortality** is highest with CRAB, CRE and ventilated HAP; crude ICU mortality with CRAB pneumonia in Indian ICUs often exceeds 50%. [ICMR AMRSN 2023]`,`**Recurrence and superinfection** with more resistant organisms follow long or broad courses. [IDSA/ATS 2016]`,`**Post-ICU syndrome**: weakness, cognitive and psychological sequelae in survivors. [SCCM 2023]`]},{heading:`Prevention - VAP and HAP bundles`,points:[`**Avoid intubation** where possible with **NIV or high-flow nasal oxygen**, and avoid re-intubation. [SHEA/IDSA/APIC 2022]`,`**Minimise sedation**, with **daily spontaneous awakening and breathing trials** to extubate early. [SHEA/IDSA/APIC 2022]`,`**Head of bed elevated 30-45 degrees**, early mobilisation, and changing ventilator circuits only when soiled. [SHEA/IDSA/APIC 2022]`,`**Subglottic secretion drainage tubes** in patients expected to be ventilated over 48-72 hours, and **cuff pressure 20-30 cmH2O**. [SHEA/IDSA/APIC 2022]`,`**Oral care with tooth brushing**; **routine chlorhexidine mouthwash is no longer recommended** because of a possible mortality signal. [SHEA/IDSA/APIC 2022]`,`**Hand hygiene, contact precautions for MDR carriers, and environmental cleaning** cut cross-transmission of Acinetobacter and Klebsiella. [WHO 2022]`,`**Non-ventilated HAP prevention**: oral care, dysphagia screening, upright position, early mobility and incentive spirometry after surgery. [SHEA/IDSA/APIC 2022]`,`**National programme link**: the **National Action Plan on AMR (NAP-AMR, with its updated 2.0 version)**, Kayakalp hospital standards and ICMR AMSP support stewardship and infection control. [MoHFW NAP-AMR]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**IDSA Guidance on treatment of antimicrobial-resistant Gram-negative infections (2024 version)** recommends **sulbactam-durlobactam** for CRAB and **ceftazidime-avibactam plus aztreonam or cefiderocol** for MBL producers. [IDSA AMR Guidance 2024]`,`**SHEA/IDSA/APIC Compendium 2022** dropped routine chlorhexidine oral care and made **avoiding intubation, sedation minimisation and SAT/SBT** essential practices. [SHEA/IDSA/APIC 2022]`,`**BLING III (2024)** and its meta-analysis support **continuous or extended beta-lactam infusion** in critically ill sepsis, including pneumonia. [BLING III JAMA 2024]`,`**ICMR AMRSN annual reports (2023-2024)** document rising carbapenem resistance and falling colistin susceptibility in Klebsiella - empirical colistin is not a guarantee. [ICMR AMRSN 2023]`,`**ICMR Treatment Guidelines 2022** remain the Indian reference for empirical HAP/VAP, emphasising the unit antibiogram and stewardship. [ICMR 2022]`,`**REVISE (2024)**: pantoprazole in ventilated patients reduced clinically important GI bleeding without a significant increase in VAP. [REVISE NEJM 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Define **HAP and VAP with the 48-hour rule**, and explain why **HCAP was dropped**.`,`List the **five MDR risk factors for VAP** (IV antibiotics in 90 days, septic shock, ARDS, 5 or more days in hospital, acute RRT).`,`Know the **7-day course** and the evidence (PneumA).`,`Know why **ceftazidime-avibactam fails against NDM** and is paired with **aztreonam**.`,`Recite the **VAP bundle** - head up, sedation hold, SBT, subglottic drainage, cuff pressure, oral care, hand hygiene.`,`Common mistake: **treating a positive tracheal culture without clinical pneumonia** (colonisation) or continuing empirical MRSA cover after negative cultures.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for HAP/VAP`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Piperacillin-tazobactam`,`4.5 g IV 6-hourly (extended 4-hour infusion)`,`7 days`,`AKI with vancomycin, hypokalaemia; not for ESBL bacteraemia`],[`Cefepime`,`2 g IV 8-hourly`,`7 days`,`Neurotoxicity (encephalopathy, myoclonus) in renal failure`],[`Meropenem`,`1-2 g IV 8-hourly (3-hour infusion)`,`7 days`,`Seizures (less than imipenem), C difficile; valproate interaction`],[`Amikacin`,`15-20 mg/kg IV once daily`,`Short (3-5 days) in combination`,`Nephro- and ototoxicity; trough monitoring`],[`Levofloxacin`,`750 mg IV/oral daily`,`7 days`,`QT, tendinopathy, dysglycaemia; masks TB`],[`Vancomycin`,`15-20 mg/kg IV 8-12-hourly; load 25-30 mg/kg`,`Stop if MRSA excluded`,`AKI, red man syndrome; AUC 400-600`],[`Linezolid`,`600 mg IV/oral 12-hourly`,`7 days`,`Thrombocytopenia, serotonin syndrome, lactic acidosis`],[`Colistimethate`,`9 MU load, then 4.5 MU IV 12-hourly`,`7 days; adjust to CrCl`,`Nephrotoxicity, neurotoxicity; resistance rising`],[`Polymyxin B`,`2-2.5 mg/kg load, then 1.25-1.5 mg/kg IV 12-hourly`,`7 days`,`Nephrotoxicity, skin pigmentation; no renal adjustment`],[`Ceftazidime-avibactam (+ aztreonam for NDM)`,`2.5 g IV 8-hourly over 2 hours (+ aztreonam 2 g 8-hourly)`,`7 days`,`Avibactam does not inhibit MBL; adjust in renal failure`],[`Cefiderocol`,`2 g IV 8-hourly over 3 hours`,`7 days`,`Higher mortality signal in CRAB (CREDIBLE-CR); costly`],[`Sulbactam-durlobactam`,`1 g/1 g IV 6-hourly over 3 hours, with imipenem or meropenem`,`7-14 days`,`Only for CRAB; limited availability in India`]]},{heading:`MDR and MRSA risk factors (IDSA/ATS 2016)`,columns:[`Setting`,`Risk factor for MDR Gram-negatives`,`Risk factor for MRSA`],rows:[[`VAP`,`IV antibiotics within 90 days; septic shock; ARDS before VAP; 5 or more days in hospital; acute RRT`,`IV antibiotics within 90 days; unit MRSA over 10-20% or unknown`],[`HAP`,`IV antibiotics within 90 days; high mortality risk (ventilation for HAP, septic shock)`,`IV antibiotics within 90 days; unit MRSA over 20% or unknown; high mortality risk`],[`Unit level`,`Over 10% of Gram-negatives resistant to the planned monotherapy`,`MRSA prevalence among S aureus isolates`]]},{heading:`Empirical regimen by risk`,columns:[`Situation`,`Regimen`,`Add`],rows:[[`HAP, no MDR risk, not high mortality`,`Pip-taz or cefepime or levofloxacin (one agent)`,`Nothing`],[`HAP or VAP with MDR risk`,`Antipseudomonal beta-lactam + amikacin or fluoroquinolone`,`Vancomycin or linezolid if MRSA risk`],[`Indian ICU with high carbapenem resistance`,`Meropenem (extended) +/- polymyxin B or colistin per antibiogram`,`MRSA cover if risk`],[`Known NDM-CRE`,`Ceftazidime-avibactam + aztreonam, or cefiderocol`,`De-escalate as soon as possible`],[`Known CRAB`,`Sulbactam-durlobactam + carbapenem, or high-dose sulbactam + polymyxin B`,`Infectious disease input`]]},{heading:`Diagnostic thresholds and tools`,columns:[`Test`,`Threshold`,`Comment`],rows:[[`Endotracheal aspirate (quantitative)`,`10^5-10^6 CFU/mL`,`Non-invasive sampling preferred`],[`Bronchoalveolar lavage`,`10^4 CFU/mL`,`When diagnosis uncertain`],[`Protected specimen brush`,`10^3 CFU/mL`,`Rarely used`],[`CPIS`,`Over 6`,`Poor accuracy; not for decisions`],[`Procalcitonin`,`Falling or under 0.5 ng/mL`,`Support stopping, not starting`],[`VAE (VAC)`,`PEEP rise 3 cmH2O or FiO2 rise 0.20 for 2 days`,`Surveillance only`]]},{heading:`VAP prevention bundle (SHEA/IDSA/APIC 2022)`,columns:[`Element`,`Target`,`Why it works`],rows:[[`Avoid intubation`,`NIV or HFNO where appropriate`,`No tube, no VAP`],[`Sedation minimisation with SAT and SBT`,`Daily`,`Shorter ventilation`],[`Head of bed elevation`,`30-45 degrees`,`Less aspiration`],[`Subglottic secretion drainage`,`If ventilation over 48-72 hours`,`Removes pooled secretions above cuff`],[`Cuff pressure`,`20-30 cmH2O`,`Prevents leakage and tracheal injury`],[`Oral care`,`Tooth brushing; no routine chlorhexidine`,`Reduces oral bacterial load`],[`Early mobilisation and hand hygiene`,`Every patient`,`Less cross-infection and deconditioning`]]}],redFlags:[`**Septic shock** - vasopressors needed, lactate over 2 mmol/L.`,`**Rising FiO2 or PEEP needs** in a ventilated patient.`,`**Need for intubation** after HAP onset (ventilated HAP - highest mortality).`,`**No clinical improvement at 72 hours** despite appropriate empirical therapy.`,`**Carbapenem-resistant organism isolated** - immediate infectious disease and stewardship input.`,`**Parapneumonic effusion or cavity** on imaging.`,`**AKI on colistin or vancomycin** - adjust drug and dose.`],pearls:[`**48 hours after admission = HAP; 48 hours after intubation = VAP.**`,`**HCAP is dead** (IDSA/ATS 2016).`,`**7 days is enough** for most HAP/VAP.`,`**NDM? Ceftazidime-avibactam plus aztreonam** - avibactam cannot inhibit metallo-enzymes.`,`**CRAB? Sulbactam-durlobactam plus a carbapenem** (IDSA 2024).`,`**Procalcitonin helps you stop, not start.**`,`**Polymyxin B for lung, colistin for urine** - colistimethate is excreted as active drug in urine.`,`**Chlorhexidine mouthwash is out; tooth brushing is in.**`,`Know your **unit antibiogram** - the best empirical guideline is local.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-hap-vap-q1`,stem:`Which of the following is listed by IDSA/ATS 2016 as a risk factor for multidrug-resistant VAP?`,options:[`Age over 65 years`,`Diabetes mellitus`,`Acute renal replacement therapy before VAP onset`,`Admission from home`],answer:2,explanation:`The five MDR risk factors for VAP are IV antibiotics in the previous 90 days, septic shock at VAP, ARDS preceding VAP, 5 or more days of hospitalisation and acute renal replacement therapy before VAP. Age, diabetes and admission from home are not among the validated MDR risk factors.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-hap-vap-q2`,stem:`A ventilated patient has VAP due to a Klebsiella pneumoniae producing NDM-1 carbapenemase. Which regimen is most appropriate?`,options:[`Ceftazidime-avibactam alone`,`Meropenem-vaborbactam`,`Tigecycline monotherapy`,`Ceftazidime-avibactam combined with aztreonam`],answer:3,explanation:`NDM is a metallo-beta-lactamase that avibactam and vaborbactam cannot inhibit, but it cannot hydrolyse aztreonam; avibactam protects aztreonam from co-produced ESBLs, so the combination works. Ceftazidime-avibactam or meropenem-vaborbactam alone fail against MBLs, and tigecycline monotherapy is avoided in pneumonia because of low lung levels and excess mortality.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`respiratory-hap-vap-q3`,stem:`What is the recommended duration of antibiotic therapy for most patients with VAP who respond clinically?`,options:[`7 days`,`3 days`,`14 days`,`21 days`],answer:0,explanation:`IDSA/ATS 2016 recommends 7 days for HAP and VAP, based on trials such as PneumA where 8 days matched 15 days with less resistance emergence. Three days is too short for confirmed VAP, and 14-21 days increases toxicity, cost and resistance without better outcomes.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-hap-vap-q4`,stem:`Which practice was removed from routine VAP prevention recommendations in the SHEA/IDSA/APIC 2022 compendium?`,options:[`Daily spontaneous awakening and breathing trials`,`Routine chlorhexidine oral care`,`Head-of-bed elevation to 30-45 degrees`,`Avoiding intubation with non-invasive ventilation where safe`],answer:1,explanation:`Chlorhexidine oral care is no longer recommended routinely because meta-analyses suggested a possible increase in mortality without clear VAP benefit; tooth brushing is preferred. Sedation interruption with breathing trials, head elevation and avoiding intubation remain essential practices.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-hap-vap-q5`,stem:`Regarding procalcitonin in HAP and VAP, which statement reflects IDSA/ATS 2016?`,options:[`A procalcitonin under 0.5 ng/mL rules out VAP and antibiotics should be withheld`,`Procalcitonin should replace cultures in the ICU`,`Procalcitonin plus clinical criteria can guide discontinuation of antibiotics`,`Procalcitonin above 2 ng/mL mandates adding MRSA cover`],answer:2,explanation:`IDSA/ATS recommends against using procalcitonin to decide whether to start antibiotics but suggests using it with clinical criteria to stop them. A low value does not rule out VAP, it never replaces cultures, and it does not indicate the organism or need for MRSA cover.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-hap-vap-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Define hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia. Discuss the risk factors for multidrug-resistant pathogens, empirical antibiotic therapy in the Indian context, and prevention.`,openingLines:[`HAP is pneumonia occurring 48 hours or more after admission and not incubating at admission; VAP occurs 48 hours or more after endotracheal intubation (IDSA/ATS 2016).`,`In Indian ICUs they are dominated by carbapenem-resistant Gram-negative bacilli, so empirical therapy must follow the local antibiogram and be de-escalated within 72 hours.`],answer:[{heading:`Definitions and burden`,points:[`HAP, VAP, ventilated HAP; HCAP removed in 2016.`,`VAP attributable mortality about 13%; Indian VAP rates several-fold higher than US benchmarks.`,`ICMR AMRSN: carbapenem resistance over 80% in Acinetobacter, about half in Klebsiella.`]},{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Oropharyngeal colonisation, micro-aspiration past the cuff, ETT biofilm, impaired clearance.`]},{heading:`MDR risk factors`,points:[`VAP: IV antibiotics in 90 days, septic shock, ARDS, 5 or more days in hospital, acute RRT.`,`MRSA: IV antibiotics in 90 days, unit MRSA over 20%, high mortality risk.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`New infiltrate plus fever, purulent secretions, leucocytosis, worsening oxygenation.`,`Endotracheal aspirate and blood cultures before antibiotics; PCT to stop, not start.`]},{heading:`Empirical therapy`,points:[`Low risk: one antipseudomonal agent (pip-taz, cefepime, levofloxacin).`,`MDR risk: two antipseudomonal agents plus vancomycin or linezolid if MRSA risk.`,`Indian ICU: meropenem extended infusion +/- polymyxin B per antibiogram (ICMR 2022).`,`Targeted: NDM - ceftazidime-avibactam + aztreonam; CRAB - sulbactam-durlobactam.`,`Review at 48-72 hours, de-escalate, total 7 days.`]},{heading:`Prevention`,points:[`Avoid intubation, sedation hold and SBT, head up 30-45 degrees, subglottic drainage, cuff 20-30 cmH2O, tooth brushing, hand hygiene.`,`Stewardship under NAP-AMR and ICMR AMSP.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: suspected HAP/VAP, cultures, risk assessment, empirical regimen, 48-72 hour review, de-escalation, 7 days`],markSplit:[{part:`Definitions and burden`,marks:1},{part:`Pathogenesis and MDR risk factors`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:1},{part:`Empirical and targeted therapy`,marks:4},{part:`Prevention bundle`,marks:2}],keywords:[`HAP`,`VAP`,`MDR`,`antibiogram`,`de-escalation`,`VAP bundle`]},{id:`respiratory-hap-vap-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on the VAP prevention bundle.`,openingLines:[`VAP is the commonest ICU-acquired infection in ventilated patients, and most of it is preventable with a bundle of simple, evidence-based measures.`,`The SHEA/IDSA/APIC 2022 compendium lists essential and additional practices, and it no longer recommends routine chlorhexidine oral care.`],answer:[{heading:`Avoid and shorten ventilation`,points:[`NIV or HFNO to avoid intubation; avoid re-intubation.`,`Minimise sedation; daily spontaneous awakening and breathing trials.`,`Early mobilisation.`]},{heading:`Reduce aspiration`,points:[`Head of bed 30-45 degrees.`,`Subglottic secretion drainage tubes if ventilation over 48-72 hours.`,`Cuff pressure 20-30 cmH2O.`]},{heading:`Reduce contamination`,points:[`Oral care with tooth brushing; no routine chlorhexidine.`,`Change circuits only when soiled; hand hygiene; contact precautions for MDR carriers.`]},{heading:`Audit`,points:[`Bundle compliance checklist and VAE surveillance (CDC NHSN).`]}],mustDraw:[`Table of bundle elements with target and rationale`],markSplit:[{part:`Avoid and shorten ventilation`,marks:2},{part:`Reduce aspiration`,marks:2},{part:`Contamination and audit`,marks:1}],keywords:[`VAP bundle`,`subglottic drainage`,`sedation hold`,`head elevation`,`oral care`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Health care-associated pneumonia chapter`,`Kalil AC et al. Management of adults with hospital-acquired and ventilator-associated pneumonia: IDSA/ATS clinical practice guidelines. Clin Infect Dis 2016`,`Torres A et al. International ERS/ESICM/ESCMID/ALAT guidelines for HAP and VAP. Eur Respir J 2017`,`ICMR Treatment Guidelines for Antimicrobial Use in Common Syndromes, 2nd edition, 2022`,`ICMR Antimicrobial Resistance Research and Surveillance Network Annual Report, 2023`,`Tamma PD et al. IDSA 2024 Guidance on the Treatment of Antimicrobial-Resistant Gram-Negative Infections. Clin Infect Dis 2024`,`Klompas M et al. Strategies to prevent ventilator-associated pneumonia, ventilator-associated events and nonventilator hospital-acquired pneumonia: 2022 update (SHEA/IDSA/APIC). Infect Control Hosp Epidemiol 2022`,`Dulhunty JM et al. Continuous vs intermittent beta-lactam infusion in sepsis (BLING III). JAMA 2024`]}),r.push({id:`respiratory-severe-cap-icu`,title:`Severe community-acquired pneumonia and sepsis: inpatient and ICU care`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Severe community-acquired pneumonia (sCAP)** is CAP meeting **1 major (septic shock needing vasopressors or respiratory failure needing mechanical ventilation) or 3 or more minor IDSA/ATS criteria**; it is managed in a monitored bed with the **Surviving Sepsis bundle**, **a beta-lactam plus a macrolide** within the first hour, **hydrocortisone for severe CAP (CAPE COD 2023, ATS 2024)**, influenza co-treatment and targeted cover for Indian pathogens such as **scrub typhus and melioidosis**.`,keywords:[`severe CAP`,`sCAP`,`IDSA/ATS minor criteria`,`SMART-COP`,`sepsis-3`,`septic shock`,`qSOFA`,`SOFA`,`hour-1 bundle`,`Surviving Sepsis Campaign 2021`,`CAPE COD`,`hydrocortisone`,`ceftriaxone azithromycin`,`Legionella`,`oseltamivir`,`norepinephrine`,`melioidosis`,`scrub typhus`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Severe CAP (IDSA/ATS 2007, retained 2019)**: **1 major criterion** (septic shock needing vasopressors, or respiratory failure needing mechanical ventilation) **or 3 or more minor criteria** - these patients need ICU or high-dependency care. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`,`**Sepsis (Sepsis-3, 2016)** is life-threatening organ dysfunction due to a dysregulated host response to infection, operationalised as an **acute rise in SOFA score of 2 or more**. [Sepsis-3 JAMA 2016]`,`**Septic shock** is sepsis needing **vasopressors to keep MAP at 65 mmHg or more** with **lactate over 2 mmol/L** despite adequate fluid resuscitation - hospital mortality over 40%. [Sepsis-3 JAMA 2016]`,`**Pneumonia is the commonest source of sepsis** (about half of cases), and globally sepsis caused an estimated **49 million cases and 11 million deaths in 2017 - about one in five deaths**. [Rudd Lancet 2020]`,`About **10-20% of hospitalised CAP needs ICU**, and ICU mortality of severe CAP is around **20-50%**, highest with shock and delayed ICU admission. [ERS/ESICM/ESCMID/ALAT 2023]`,`**Lower respiratory infections are among the leading infectious causes of death in India** (GBD), and South Asia carries a disproportionate share of the global sepsis burden. [GBD 2021]`,`In Indian ICUs, sCAP is often caused or mimicked by **tropical infections** - scrub typhus, leptospirosis, melioidosis, dengue, malaria - and by **TB**, so empirical therapy must think beyond pneumococcus. [ICMR 2022]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Streptococcus pneumoniae** remains the commonest identified bacterial cause of severe CAP worldwide and in India. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Respiratory viruses**: **influenza** (H1N1pdm09, H3N2), **SARS-CoV-2**, RSV - found in up to a third of severe CAP and often co-infected with bacteria. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`,`**Staphylococcus aureus** (including PVL-producing MRSA) after influenza causes **necrotising, cavitating pneumonia** with haemoptysis and leucopenia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Legionella pneumophila**: water cooling systems, hotels, hospitals; severe pneumonia with diarrhoea, confusion, **hyponatraemia**, raised CK and LFTs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Gram-negative bacilli**: **Klebsiella pneumoniae** in alcoholics and diabetics, **Pseudomonas** in structural lung disease (bronchiectasis, severe COPD) or recent hospital antibiotics. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`,`**India-specific**: **Burkholderia pseudomallei (melioidosis)** in diabetic farmers in the monsoon, **Orientia tsutsugamushi (scrub typhus)**, **Leptospira** with pulmonary haemorrhage, and **pulmonary TB** presenting as acute pneumonia. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Host risks**: age over 65, COPD, diabetes, CKD, heart failure, alcohol, smoking, asplenia, HIV, malnutrition and immunosuppression. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Alveolar invasion activates **alveolar macrophages and neutrophils** through pattern-recognition receptors (TLR2, TLR4), releasing **TNF-alpha, IL-1 and IL-6**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pneumolysin** (pneumococcus) and other toxins injure the alveolar-capillary membrane, causing **protein-rich oedema, shunt and hypoxaemia** that responds poorly to oxygen. [Harrison 22e]`,`A **dysregulated host response** - simultaneous hyperinflammation and immunosuppression - drives distant organ dysfunction: **vasodilatation, capillary leak, microthrombosis and mitochondrial dysfunction**. [SSC 2021]`,`**Septic shock** is distributive: low systemic vascular resistance, relative hypovolaemia from leak, and septic cardiomyopathy; **lactate rises** from adrenergic glycolysis as well as hypoperfusion. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Relative adrenal insufficiency and glucocorticoid-receptor resistance** in critical illness justify **hydrocortisone**, which dampens NF-kB-driven inflammation and restores vascular responsiveness to catecholamines. [CAPE COD NEJM 2023]`,`**Cardiac complications** - MI, arrhythmia (new AF) and heart failure - occur in about a fifth of hospitalised CAP, through demand ischaemia, plaque inflammation and direct myocardial invasion by pneumococcus. [Corrales-Medina Lancet 2013]`,`Progression to **ARDS** (bilateral opacities, P/F 300 or less) follows in a minority and drives ventilator-dependence and death. [ATS 2023]`]},{heading:`Severity assessment and classification`,points:[`**IDSA/ATS minor criteria (9)**: **RR 30/min or more, PaO2/FiO2 250 or less, multilobar infiltrates, confusion, BUN 20 mg/dL or more (urea about 43 mg/dL or more), WBC under 4000/microlitre, platelets under 100 000/microlitre, temperature under 36 degrees C, and hypotension needing aggressive fluids**. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`,`**3 or more minor criteria** predicts need for ICU; patients with 3 or more without shock or ventilation still benefit from **early ICU or HDU admission** rather than ward care. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`,`**CURB-65** (see the CAP topic) predicts mortality but **under-identifies ICU need** in young patients with hypoxaemia; **SMART-COP** predicts need for intensive respiratory or vasopressor support. [Charles CID 2008]`,`**SMART-COP**: Systolic BP under 90 (2), Multilobar (1), Albumin under 3.5 g/dL (1), RR high for age (1), Tachycardia 125 or more (1), Confusion (1), Oxygen low for age (2), pH under 7.35 (2); **3 or more** means high risk. [Charles CID 2008]`,`**qSOFA** (RR 22 or more, altered mentation, SBP 100 or less) predicts poor outcome but the **SSC 2021 recommends against using qSOFA alone as a screening tool** compared with SIRS, NEWS or MEWS. [SSC 2021]`,`**SOFA** scores six organs (respiratory P/F, platelets, bilirubin, MAP/vasopressors, GCS, creatinine/urine output) 0-4 each; an acute rise of 2 or more defines sepsis. [Sepsis-3 JAMA 2016]`,`**NEWS2** of 7 or more triggers emergency review and is the practical ward screen in many Indian hospitals. [RCP 2017]`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Fever or hypothermia, **cough, purulent or rusty sputum, pleuritic pain and breathlessness**, with rapid progression over 1-3 days. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Confusion, falls and functional decline** may be the only features in the elderly; hypothermia predicts worse outcome. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`,`**Legionella clues**: high fever, relative bradycardia, diarrhoea, confusion, hyponatraemia, no response to beta-lactams. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Scrub typhus clue**: fever with an **eschar** (axilla, groin, under the breast), lymphadenopathy, pneumonitis and ARDS-like picture. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Melioidosis clue**: diabetic farmer in the monsoon, septic pneumonia with multiple abscesses (liver, spleen, prostate). [ICMR 2022]`,`**Influenza clue**: abrupt fever, myalgia, cough during the season or outbreak, with secondary bacterial pneumonia days later. [IDSA Influenza 2018]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Vital signs first**: RR 30 or more, SpO2 under 92%, SBP under 90 mmHg, HR over 125, temperature under 36 degrees C, and confusion are the signs of severity. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`,`**Consolidation signs**: dullness, bronchial breathing, increased vocal resonance, whispering pectoriloquy and coarse crackles. [Macleod 15e]`,`**Perfusion**: **capillary refill time over 3 seconds**, mottling, cold peripheries, oliguria under 0.5 mL/kg/hour. [SSC 2021]`,`**Work of breathing**: accessory muscles, paradoxical abdominal movement, inability to speak - signs of impending respiratory failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for **eschar, rash, jaundice, conjunctival suffusion** (leptospirosis), **hepatosplenomegaly** and **herpes labialis** (pneumococcus). [ICMR 2022]`,`**Pleural effusion signs** - tap any effusion over 1 cm in a febrile patient (see the Lung abscess and empyema topic). [BTS Pleural 2023]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Acute heart failure / pulmonary oedema**: orthopnoea, raised JVP, BNP, bilateral infiltrates, responds to diuretics - and can coexist. [ESC 2023]`,`**Pulmonary embolism with infarction**: pleuritic pain, hypoxia out of proportion, wedge opacity; CTPA. [ESC 2019]`,`**Pulmonary TB** presenting acutely: subacute fever, upper-lobe or cavity; send CBNAAT before starting fluoroquinolones. [NTEP 2023]`,`**Tropical febrile illnesses with lung involvement**: scrub typhus, leptospirosis (pulmonary haemorrhage), dengue, falciparum malaria (ARDS) - test early (see the Tropical pneumonias topic). [ICMR 2022]`,`**Non-infective mimics**: organising pneumonia, eosinophilic pneumonia, vasculitis with alveolar haemorrhage, drug reactions and lung cancer with post-obstructive pneumonia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**COVID-19 and influenza** viral pneumonia without bacterial co-infection. [IDSA COVID 2024]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Chest X-ray** to confirm infiltrate, extent, cavity and effusion; **lung ultrasound** at the bedside; CT if diagnosis unclear. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`,`**Blood cultures (2 sets) and sputum Gram stain and culture** before antibiotics in all severe CAP, without delaying antibiotics beyond 1 hour. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`,`**Urinary pneumococcal and Legionella antigen** in severe CAP; **nasal MRSA PCR** (negative predictive value over 95%, allowing early stopping of MRSA cover). [ATS/IDSA 2019]`,`**Influenza and SARS-CoV-2 PCR** (multiplex respiratory panel) when they circulate; **sputum CBNAAT** for TB. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`,`**Lactate**: measure at presentation and repeat in 2-4 hours if over 2 mmol/L; lactate over 4 mmol/L defines high-risk sepsis needing aggressive resuscitation. [SSC 2021]`,`**ABG** (P/F ratio, pH, PaCO2), CBC, renal and liver function, electrolytes (sodium for Legionella), glucose, CRP, coagulation and **troponin and ECG**. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`,`**Procalcitonin is not used to decide whether to start antibiotics** in CAP, but it can support early stopping. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`,`**India-specific tests**: scrub typhus IgM ELISA, leptospira IgM, dengue NS1/IgM, malaria antigen, and **blood culture for B pseudomallei** with the lab alerted. [ICMR 2022]`]},{heading:`Empirical antibiotics for severe CAP`,points:[`**Give antibiotics within 1 hour** in septic shock or probable sepsis; delays raise mortality by hours. [SSC 2021]`,`**Standard severe CAP regimen**: **beta-lactam plus macrolide** - **ceftriaxone 1-2 g IV daily** (or cefotaxime 1-2 g 8-hourly, or amoxicillin-clavulanate 1.2 g 8-hourly) **plus azithromycin 500 mg IV/oral daily**. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`,`**Alternative**: beta-lactam plus a **respiratory fluoroquinolone** (levofloxacin 750 mg daily or moxifloxacin 400 mg daily); in India, **avoid fluoroquinolones until TB is excluded** because they mask and select resistance. [ICMR 2022]`,`The **beta-lactam plus macrolide** combination is preferred to beta-lactam plus quinolone in severe CAP because of observational mortality benefit from macrolide immunomodulation. [ERS/ESICM/ESCMID/ALAT 2023]`,`**ICMR 2022 (India)**: severe CAP - **ceftriaxone 2 g IV daily** (or cefotaxime) **plus azithromycin 500 mg daily**; add **doxycycline 100 mg twice daily** where scrub typhus is endemic, and use **pip-taz** only with Pseudomonas risk. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Add MRSA cover** (vancomycin or linezolid) and **Pseudomonas cover** (pip-taz 4.5 g 6-hourly, cefepime 2 g 8-hourly, or meropenem 1 g 8-hourly) **only** if previously isolated from the respiratory tract or if recent hospitalisation with IV antibiotics in 90 days plus locally validated risk. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`,`**Melioidosis suspected**: **ceftazidime 2 g IV 6-hourly (or 40 mg/kg 8-hourly) or meropenem 1 g 8-hourly for at least 10-14 days**, then eradication with **co-trimoxazole for 3-6 months**. [Darwin Guideline 2020]`,`**Duration**: at least **5 days** and until clinically stable for 48 hours; **7 days** for MRSA or Pseudomonas; longer for Legionella with complications, S aureus bacteraemia, abscess or empyema. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`]},{heading:`Sepsis bundle, respiratory support and corticosteroids in ICU`,points:[`**Hour-1 bundle**: measure lactate, take blood cultures, give broad-spectrum antibiotics, start **30 mL/kg balanced crystalloid** for hypotension or lactate 4 mmol/L or more, and start vasopressors if MAP stays under 65 mmHg. [SSC 2021]`,`**Fluids**: prefer **balanced crystalloids (Ringer lactate)** over saline; reassess with dynamic measures (passive leg raise, capillary refill) - restrictive and liberal strategies gave similar outcomes (CLOVERS 2023). [SSC 2021]`,`**Norepinephrine** is the first-line vasopressor, may start peripherally; **add vasopressin 0.03 U/min** when norepinephrine reaches 0.25-0.5 microgram/kg/min; adrenaline or dobutamine for myocardial dysfunction. [SSC 2021]`,`**Hydrocortisone for severe CAP**: **CAPE COD (NEJM 2023)** - hydrocortisone **200 mg/day infusion** for 4 or 8 days with taper (total 8 or 14 days) cut 28-day mortality from **11.9% to 6.2%** in ICU patients with severe CAP. [CAPE COD NEJM 2023]`,`**ATS 2024 guideline** suggests **corticosteroids for adults hospitalised with severe CAP** (conditional recommendation) and **not** for less severe CAP; the **SCCM 2024 focused update** agrees. [ATS 2024]`,`**Do not give steroids** in **influenza pneumonia** (higher mortality), suspected **Aspergillus** or untreated **TB**; CAPE COD excluded influenza and patients already in septic shock. [ATS 2024]`,`**Septic shock**: **hydrocortisone 200 mg/day (50 mg IV 6-hourly)** if norepinephrine of 0.25 microgram/kg/min or more is needed for 4 hours or more. [SSC 2021]`,`**Respiratory support**: **high-flow nasal oxygen** for acute hypoxaemic respiratory failure (target SpO2 92-96%); NIV only for hypercapnic COPD overlap; intubate early if failing (HACOR or ROX index); ventilate with **6 mL/kg predicted body weight and plateau under 30 cmH2O** (see the ARDS and ventilation topics). [SSC 2021]`,`**Other ICU care**: VTE prophylaxis, stress-ulcer prophylaxis if bleeding risk, glucose 144-180 mg/dL, early enteral nutrition, and daily review for de-escalation. [SSC 2021]`]},{heading:`Atypicals, influenza and co-infections`,points:[`**Atypical cover** with the macrolide (or doxycycline) treats **Mycoplasma, Chlamydophila and Legionella**; Legionella-confirmed severe disease can use **levofloxacin 750 mg daily or azithromycin for 7-14 days**. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`,`**Influenza with CAP**: give **oseltamivir 75 mg twice daily for 5 days** to all hospitalised patients **regardless of symptom duration**, plus standard antibacterial therapy. [IDSA Influenza 2018]`,`**Oseltamivir in renal impairment**: **30 mg twice daily if CrCl 30-60 mL/min**, 30 mg once daily if 10-30 mL/min; longer courses may be used in critically ill or immunocompromised patients. [IDSA Influenza 2018]`,`Under India's **seasonal influenza (H1N1) categorisation**, **Category C** (breathlessness, hypotension, altered sensorium, haemoptysis) means hospitalisation and immediate oseltamivir. [MoHFW 2023]`,`**COVID-19 severe pneumonia**: dexamethasone 6 mg daily for 10 days if on oxygen, plus **tocilizumab or baricitinib** in progressive disease (see the COVID topic). [IDSA COVID 2024]`,`**Scrub typhus pneumonitis**: **doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for 7 days**; in severe disease **IV doxycycline plus azithromycin** was superior to either alone (INTREST, NEJM 2025). [INTREST NEJM 2025]`,`**Post-influenza S aureus**: add **linezolid or vancomycin** if necrotising pneumonia, cavities or haemoptysis; linezolid also suppresses toxin (PVL) production. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: ceftriaxone plus azithromycin is safe; avoid doxycycline and fluoroquinolones where possible; give oseltamivir for influenza in any trimester; target SpO2 95% or more; hydrocortisone is acceptable. [ACOG 2023]`,`**Elderly**: atypical presentation, aspiration risk, frailty; ceilings of care should be discussed early and documented. [NICE 2019]`,`**CKD**: no adjustment for ceftriaxone, azithromycin or doxycycline; adjust levofloxacin, oseltamivir, cefepime and pip-taz; avoid nephrotoxins. [Sanford 2025]`,`**Diabetes**: higher risk of Klebsiella, S aureus, melioidosis and TB; use **IV insulin infusion** in ICU with target 144-180 mg/dL. [SSC 2021]`,`**HIV or immunosuppression**: add cover for PCP when CD4 under 200 or on chronic steroids, and think of TB and fungi. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`,`**Resource-limited district hospital**: give the **first dose of ceftriaxone and azithromycin before referral**, start oxygen and fluids, and refer to an ICU-capable centre. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**ARDS, septic shock, AKI, DIC, delirium** and multi-organ failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Parapneumonic effusion and empyema** (persistent fever after 72 hours), **lung abscess** and **necrotising pneumonia**. [BTS Pleural 2023]`,`**Cardiac events**: MI, new AF and heart failure in about one in five hospitalised CAP; check troponin and ECG. [Corrales-Medina Lancet 2013]`,`**Clinical stability (Halm criteria)**: HR 100 or less, RR 24 or less, SBP 90 or more, temperature 37.8 degrees C or less, SpO2 90% or more on air, normal mentation and able to eat - permits IV-to-oral switch and discharge planning. [Halm JAMA 1998]`,`**Long-term mortality** stays raised for years after severe CAP and sepsis, largely from cardiovascular events and frailty; survivors often have **post-ICU syndrome**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Failure to improve at 72 hours**: wrong organism (TB, Legionella, scrub, melioidosis, resistant bacteria), empyema, abscess, drug fever, non-infective diagnosis or obstructing lesion. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and prevention`,points:[`**Refer to ICU or HDU** if 1 major or 3 or more minor criteria, SMART-COP 3 or more, lactate over 2 mmol/L, or NEWS2 of 7 or more. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`,`**Switch to oral** (amoxicillin-clavulanate or cefuroxime plus azithromycin) once clinically stable and able to swallow. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`,`**Follow-up chest X-ray is not routine** if symptoms resolve; repeat at **6-8 weeks** in smokers over 50 or if symptoms persist, to exclude cancer. [ATS/IDSA 2019]`,`**Review at 6 weeks** for function, cognition, mood and cardiovascular risk; screen for diabetes, HIV and TB where appropriate. [NICE 2019]`,`**Vaccinate**: influenza annually, pneumococcal (PCV20 or PCV15 then PPSV23), RSV in older adults, and COVID boosters per national advice (see the Adult respiratory vaccines topic). [ACIP 2024]`,`**Stop smoking**, reduce alcohol and biomass exposure; optimise diabetes and heart failure under **NP-NCD** at Ayushman Arogya Mandirs. [NP-NCD 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**CAPE COD (NEJM 2023)**: hydrocortisone 200 mg/day reduced 28-day mortality in severe CAP in ICU (6.2% vs 11.9%). [CAPE COD NEJM 2023]`,`**ATS 2024 clinical practice guideline on corticosteroids in CAP**: suggests steroids for **severe CAP**, not for non-severe CAP. [ATS 2024]`,`**SCCM 2024 focused update on corticosteroids**: suggests steroids for sepsis/septic shock, ARDS and severe CAP. [SCCM 2024]`,`**ERS/ESICM/ESCMID/ALAT severe CAP guideline 2023**: recommends macrolide combination therapy, HFNO before intubation where appropriate, and corticosteroids for severe CAP with shock. [ERS/ESICM/ESCMID/ALAT 2023]`,`**Surviving Sepsis Campaign 2021** (current): no qSOFA alone for screening, 1-hour antibiotics in shock, balanced crystalloids, norepinephrine first, capillary refill to guide resuscitation. [SSC 2021]`,`**INTREST (NEJM 2025)**: combination IV doxycycline plus azithromycin for severe scrub typhus improves outcomes - relevant to Indian severe CAP mimics. [INTREST NEJM 2025]`,`**ICMR Treatment Guidelines 2022** remain the Indian reference: ceftriaxone plus azithromycin, add doxycycline for scrub-endemic areas, and avoid empirical fluoroquinolones without TB exclusion. [ICMR 2022]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Recite the **2 major and 9 minor IDSA/ATS criteria** - examiners expect all of them.`,`Know **Sepsis-3** definitions of sepsis and septic shock, and why **qSOFA is not a screening tool** (SSC 2021).`,`State the **hour-1 bundle** in order: lactate, cultures, antibiotics, 30 mL/kg fluid, vasopressor.`,`Know **CAPE COD** (hydrocortisone 200 mg/day, mortality 6.2 vs 11.9%) and that **influenza was excluded**.`,`Indian angle: **avoid fluoroquinolones before TB is excluded**, and think of **scrub typhus and melioidosis**.`,`Common mistake: adding **MRSA and Pseudomonas cover to every ICU CAP** - only with prior isolation or validated risk.`,`Common mistake: **waiting for 48 hours of symptoms** before oseltamivir - give it to all hospitalised influenza regardless.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in severe CAP and sepsis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Ceftriaxone`,`1-2 g IV once daily (2 g in severe)`,`Minimum 5 days, until stable 48 hours`,`Biliary pseudolithiasis; no renal adjustment`],[`Azithromycin`,`500 mg IV/oral once daily`,`3-5 days (7-14 for Legionella)`,`QT prolongation, GI upset`],[`Amoxicillin-clavulanate`,`1.2 g IV 8-hourly`,`5-7 days`,`Cholestatic jaundice, rash`],[`Levofloxacin`,`750 mg IV/oral once daily`,`5-7 days`,`QT, tendinopathy, dysglycaemia; masks TB`],[`Piperacillin-tazobactam`,`4.5 g IV 6-hourly`,`7 days if Pseudomonas`,`Hypokalaemia, AKI with vancomycin`],[`Vancomycin / linezolid`,`15-20 mg/kg IV 8-12-hourly / 600 mg IV 12-hourly`,`7 days if MRSA; stop if PCR negative`,`AKI; thrombocytopenia, serotonin syndrome`],[`Doxycycline`,`100 mg oral/IV twice daily`,`7 days (scrub typhus)`,`Avoid in pregnancy and under 8 years (short courses acceptable in children)`],[`Ceftazidime / meropenem (melioidosis)`,`2 g IV 6-hourly / 1 g IV 8-hourly`,`10-14 days or more, then co-trimoxazole 3-6 months`,`Seizures, C difficile; adjust in renal failure`],[`Oseltamivir`,`75 mg oral twice daily`,`5 days (longer if critically ill)`,`Nausea, neuropsychiatric effects; 30 mg bd if CrCl 30-60`],[`Hydrocortisone`,`200 mg/day IV infusion (or 50 mg 6-hourly)`,`CAPE COD: 4 or 8 days then taper, total 8 or 14 days`,`Hyperglycaemia; avoid in influenza, aspergillosis, untreated TB`],[`Norepinephrine`,`0.05-0.5 microgram/kg/min IV infusion`,`Titrate to MAP 65 mmHg`,`Ischaemia, arrhythmia, extravasation`],[`Vasopressin`,`0.03 U/min IV`,`Add when NE 0.25-0.5 microgram/kg/min`,`Digital and mesenteric ischaemia`]]},{heading:`IDSA/ATS criteria for severe CAP`,columns:[`Type`,`Criterion`,`Threshold`],rows:[[`Major`,`Septic shock`,`Vasopressors needed`],[`Major`,`Respiratory failure`,`Invasive mechanical ventilation needed`],[`Minor`,`Respiratory rate`,`30/min or more`],[`Minor`,`PaO2/FiO2`,`250 or less`],[`Minor`,`Multilobar infiltrates`,`2 or more lobes`],[`Minor`,`Confusion`,`Disorientation to person, place or time`],[`Minor`,`Uraemia`,`BUN 20 mg/dL or more`],[`Minor`,`Leucopenia`,`WBC under 4000/microlitre (infection-related)`],[`Minor`,`Thrombocytopenia`,`Platelets under 100 000/microlitre`],[`Minor`,`Hypothermia`,`Core temperature under 36 degrees C`],[`Minor`,`Hypotension`,`Needing aggressive fluid resuscitation`]]},{heading:`Organism clues in severe CAP (Indian context)`,columns:[`Organism`,`Clinical clue`,`Test`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`S pneumoniae`,`Abrupt rigor, rusty sputum, herpes labialis`,`Urinary antigen, blood culture`,`Ceftriaxone`],[`Legionella`,`Diarrhoea, confusion, hyponatraemia, high CK`,`Urinary Legionella antigen`,`Azithromycin or levofloxacin`],[`S aureus (post-influenza)`,`Cavities, haemoptysis, leucopenia`,`Sputum and blood culture, nasal PCR`,`Vancomycin or linezolid if MRSA`],[`Klebsiella`,`Alcoholic or diabetic, currant-jelly sputum, bulging fissure`,`Sputum and blood culture`,`Ceftriaxone or carbapenem if ESBL`],[`Influenza`,`Season, myalgia, outbreak`,`Respiratory PCR`,`Oseltamivir plus antibacterial`],[`Scrub typhus`,`Eschar, lymphadenopathy, thrombocytopenia`,`IgM ELISA`,`Doxycycline; add azithromycin if severe`],[`Melioidosis`,`Diabetic farmer, monsoon, multiple abscesses`,`Blood culture (alert lab)`,`Ceftazidime or meropenem, then co-trimoxazole`],[`Pulmonary TB`,`Subacute, upper lobe, cavity, weight loss`,`Sputum CBNAAT`,`ATT under NTEP; avoid empirical quinolone`]]},{heading:`Sepsis definitions and scores`,columns:[`Term`,`Definition`,`Use`],rows:[[`Sepsis (Sepsis-3)`,`Infection with acute SOFA rise of 2 or more`,`Defines organ dysfunction`],[`Septic shock`,`Vasopressor for MAP 65 or more plus lactate over 2 mmol/L after fluids`,`Mortality over 40%`],[`qSOFA`,`RR 22 or more, altered mentation, SBP 100 or less`,`Prognostic; not for screening alone (SSC 2021)`],[`NEWS2`,`Aggregate of 7 physiological parameters`,`7 or more: emergency response`],[`SMART-COP`,`8 weighted variables`,`3 or more: likely intensive support`],[`Halm stability`,`HR 100 or less, RR 24 or less, SBP 90 or more, T 37.8 or less, SpO2 90% or more, normal mentation, eating`,`Switch to oral, plan discharge`]]}],redFlags:[`**Septic shock**: SBP under 90 or MAP under 65 despite fluids, or lactate over 4 mmol/L.`,`**Respiratory failure**: RR 30 or more, SpO2 under 90% on oxygen, rising PaCO2 or exhaustion.`,`**Confusion** or GCS fall.`,`**3 or more IDSA/ATS minor criteria** - ICU or HDU even without shock.`,`**Cavitation, haemoptysis and leucopenia after influenza** - necrotising S aureus pneumonia.`,`**Eschar or diabetic farmer with sepsis** - scrub typhus or melioidosis needing specific therapy.`,`**No improvement at 72 hours** of appropriate treatment.`],pearls:[`**1 major or 3 minor = severe CAP = ICU.**`,`**Beta-lactam plus macrolide** is the backbone of severe CAP therapy.`,`**CAPE COD**: hydrocortisone 200 mg/day halves mortality in severe CAP - but **not in influenza**.`,`**Antibiotics within 1 hour** in septic shock; cultures first, but never delay.`,`**Norepinephrine first, vasopressin second.**`,`**qSOFA predicts, it does not screen** (SSC 2021).`,`**Oseltamivir for every hospitalised flu patient**, whatever the day of illness.`,`In India: **no empirical quinolone before CBNAAT**, and **doxycycline when scrub is possible**.`,`Mnemonic for minor criteria - **CURB + 5**: Confusion, Urea, RR, BP (aggressive fluids) plus multilobar, P/F 250, WBC under 4000, platelets under 100 000, temperature under 36.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-severe-cap-icu-q1`,stem:`Which of the following is a MAJOR criterion for severe community-acquired pneumonia in the IDSA/ATS guideline?`,options:[`Respiratory rate of 30/min or more`,`PaO2/FiO2 ratio of 250 or less`,`Septic shock requiring vasopressors`,`Multilobar infiltrates`],answer:2,explanation:`The two major criteria are septic shock needing vasopressors and respiratory failure needing invasive mechanical ventilation. Respiratory rate of 30 or more, P/F of 250 or less and multilobar infiltrates are minor criteria, of which 3 or more also define severe CAP.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-severe-cap-icu-q2`,stem:`In the CAPE COD trial (NEJM 2023) of severe CAP in the ICU, which intervention reduced 28-day mortality?`,options:[`Methylprednisolone 1 mg/kg/day for 5 days`,`Dexamethasone 6 mg daily for 10 days`,`Hydrocortisone 200 mg/day infusion with taper over 8-14 days`,`Fludrocortisone 50 microgram daily alone`],answer:2,explanation:`CAPE COD gave hydrocortisone 200 mg/day by infusion for 4 or 8 days then tapered (total 8 or 14 days) and reduced 28-day mortality from 11.9% to 6.2%. Methylprednisolone was tested in ESCAPe without significant benefit, dexamethasone 6 mg is the COVID-19 regimen, and fludrocortisone alone was not studied.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-severe-cap-icu-q3`,stem:`A 60-year-old diabetic rice farmer from coastal Odisha presents in the monsoon with septic shock, pneumonia and splenic abscesses. Which empirical antibiotic is most appropriate?`,options:[`Meropenem 1 g IV 8-hourly`,`Ceftriaxone 2 g IV daily plus azithromycin`,`Amoxicillin-clavulanate 1.2 g IV 8-hourly`,`Levofloxacin 750 mg IV daily`],answer:0,explanation:`This is classic melioidosis (Burkholderia pseudomallei), which is intrinsically resistant to ceftriaxone, aminoglycosides and most standard CAP regimens; intensive therapy is ceftazidime or meropenem for at least 10-14 days followed by co-trimoxazole eradication for 3-6 months. Ceftriaxone with azithromycin, co-amoxiclav as initial therapy and levofloxacin are not reliable.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`respiratory-severe-cap-icu-q4`,stem:`According to the Surviving Sepsis Campaign 2021, which statement about qSOFA is correct?`,options:[`It is the recommended single screening tool for sepsis`,`It should not be used alone as a screening tool compared with SIRS, NEWS or MEWS`,`A qSOFA of 2 or more defines septic shock`,`It includes serum lactate`],answer:1,explanation:`SSC 2021 recommends against qSOFA as a single screening tool because it is specific but insensitive, missing many patients who need early treatment. It does not define septic shock (vasopressors plus lactate over 2 mmol/L do), and its three items are respiratory rate, mentation and systolic BP - not lactate.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-severe-cap-icu-q5`,stem:`A patient admitted with severe CAP has confirmed influenza A on day 4 of symptoms. What is the correct approach?`,options:[`Start hydrocortisone 200 mg/day and withhold antivirals as beyond 48 hours`,`Give oseltamivir only if symptoms began within 48 hours`,`Stop antibacterial therapy because the cause is viral`,`Start oseltamivir 75 mg twice daily and continue antibacterial therapy`],answer:3,explanation:`All hospitalised patients with influenza should receive oseltamivir regardless of symptom duration, and CAP with influenza still needs antibacterial therapy because bacterial co-infection is common. The 48-hour window applies only to outpatients, and steroids should be avoided in influenza pneumonia because they increase mortality.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-severe-cap-icu-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`How will you identify severe community-acquired pneumonia? Outline the management of a patient with severe CAP and septic shock, including the role of corticosteroids.`,openingLines:[`Severe CAP is CAP with 1 major (septic shock needing vasopressors or respiratory failure needing ventilation) or 3 or more minor IDSA/ATS criteria, and needs ICU or HDU care.`,`Management rests on the Surviving Sepsis hour-1 bundle, beta-lactam plus macrolide therapy, organ support and, since CAPE COD 2023 and ATS 2024, hydrocortisone in severe CAP.`],answer:[{heading:`Identification`,points:[`Major and 9 minor IDSA/ATS criteria; SMART-COP 3 or more; NEWS2 7 or more.`,`Sepsis-3: SOFA rise of 2 or more; septic shock: vasopressors plus lactate over 2 mmol/L.`]},{heading:`Immediate resuscitation (hour-1 bundle)`,points:[`Lactate, blood cultures, antibiotics within 1 hour.`,`30 mL/kg balanced crystalloid; norepinephrine for MAP 65; vasopressin as second agent.`,`Oxygen to SpO2 92-96%; HFNO; intubate with lung-protective ventilation if failing.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Blood and sputum cultures, urinary pneumococcal and Legionella antigen, nasal MRSA PCR.`,`Influenza/SARS-CoV-2 PCR, CBNAAT, ABG, lactate, troponin; tropical tests (scrub IgM, melioidosis culture).`]},{heading:`Antibiotics`,points:[`Ceftriaxone 2 g daily plus azithromycin 500 mg daily (ICMR 2022, ATS/IDSA 2019).`,`MRSA or Pseudomonas cover only with prior isolation or validated risk.`,`Doxycycline for scrub; ceftazidime/meropenem for melioidosis; oseltamivir for influenza.`,`Minimum 5 days and stable 48 hours; de-escalate on culture.`]},{heading:`Corticosteroids`,points:[`CAPE COD: hydrocortisone 200 mg/day, 8-14 days, mortality 6.2% vs 11.9%.`,`ATS 2024 and SCCM 2024: suggest steroids in severe CAP; septic shock: hydrocortisone if NE 0.25 for 4 hours.`,`Avoid in influenza, aspergillosis, untreated TB.`]},{heading:`Supportive care and follow-up`,points:[`VTE prophylaxis, glucose 144-180, enteral nutrition, delirium prevention.`,`Halm stability for oral switch; vaccination and risk-factor control after discharge.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: severe CAP recognition, hour-1 bundle, antibiotics, steroids, organ support, de-escalation`,`Table of major and minor criteria`],markSplit:[{part:`Severity criteria and definitions`,marks:2},{part:`Sepsis bundle and organ support`,marks:3},{part:`Antibiotics including Indian pathogens`,marks:3},{part:`Corticosteroids`,marks:2}],keywords:[`severe CAP`,`minor criteria`,`sepsis bundle`,`ceftriaxone azithromycin`,`hydrocortisone`,`CAPE COD`]},{id:`respiratory-severe-cap-icu-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on corticosteroids in severe community-acquired pneumonia.`,openingLines:[`Corticosteroids dampen the dysregulated inflammatory response in severe CAP and restore vascular responsiveness in septic shock.`,`CAPE COD (NEJM 2023) showed hydrocortisone reduces mortality in ICU severe CAP, and ATS 2024 now suggests steroids for severe CAP only.`],answer:[{heading:`Rationale`,points:[`Hyperinflammation, critical-illness corticosteroid insufficiency, glucocorticoid-receptor resistance.`]},{heading:`Evidence`,points:[`CAPE COD 2023: hydrocortisone 200 mg/day, 28-day mortality 6.2% vs 11.9%.`,`ESCAPe 2022: methylprednisolone 40 mg/day, no significant mortality benefit.`,`Earlier meta-analyses: less ARDS, shorter stay.`]},{heading:`Recommendations`,points:[`ATS 2024: suggest steroids in severe CAP; not in non-severe CAP.`,`SCCM 2024 and ERS/ESICM 2023 agree for severe CAP, especially with shock.`]},{heading:`Cautions`,points:[`Avoid in influenza, aspergillosis, untreated TB; monitor glucose; watch for GI bleeding and secondary infection.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table of trials: CAPE COD, ESCAPe - drug, dose, result`],markSplit:[{part:`Rationale`,marks:1},{part:`Evidence`,marks:2},{part:`Recommendations and cautions`,marks:2}],keywords:[`hydrocortisone`,`CAPE COD`,`ATS 2024`,`severe CAP`,`influenza`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Pneumonia and Sepsis chapters`,`Metlay JP et al. Diagnosis and treatment of adults with community-acquired pneumonia: ATS/IDSA official clinical practice guideline. Am J Respir Crit Care Med 2019`,`Evans L et al. Surviving Sepsis Campaign: international guidelines for management of sepsis and septic shock 2021. Crit Care Med 2021`,`Dequin PF et al. Hydrocortisone in severe community-acquired pneumonia (CAPE COD). N Engl J Med 2023`,`Corticosteroid use in community-acquired pneumonia: an official American Thoracic Society clinical practice guideline (focused update). Am J Respir Crit Care Med 2024`,`Martin-Loeches I et al. ERS/ESICM/ESCMID/ALAT guidelines for the management of severe community-acquired pneumonia. Intensive Care Med 2023`,`ICMR Treatment Guidelines for Antimicrobial Use in Common Syndromes, 2nd edition, 2022`,`Singer M et al. The Third International Consensus Definitions for Sepsis and Septic Shock (Sepsis-3). JAMA 2016`]});var i=[];i.push({id:`respiratory-drtb`,title:`Drug-resistant TB: diagnosis and the BPaLM and shorter regimens under NTEP`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Drug-resistant TB** is disease caused by *M. tuberculosis* resistant to one or more anti-TB drugs; **RR/MDR-TB** (resistance to rifampicin, with or without isoniazid) is now diagnosed by **universal NAAT-based drug-susceptibility testing** and treated under NTEP with the **6-month all-oral BPaLM regimen** (bedaquiline, pretomanid, linezolid, moxifloxacin), a **9-11-month shorter oral bedaquiline regimen** or an **18-20-month longer oral regimen**.`,keywords:[`DR-TB`,`MDR-TB`,`RR-TB`,`pre-XDR`,`XDR-TB`,`Hr-TB`,`BPaLM`,`BPaL`,`bedaquiline`,`pretomanid`,`linezolid`,`delamanid`,`clofazimine`,`CBNAAT`,`Truenat`,`line probe assay`,`PMDT`,`Ni-kshay`,`QTc`,`UDST`],sections:[{heading:`Definitions and epidemiology`,points:[`**Rifampicin-resistant TB (RR-TB)**: resistance to rifampicin detected by phenotypic or genotypic testing, with or without resistance to other drugs; it is managed exactly like MDR-TB. [WHO 2021]`,`**MDR-TB**: resistance to at least **rifampicin and isoniazid**, the two most powerful first-line drugs. [WHO 2021]`,`**Pre-XDR-TB** (WHO 2021 definition): MDR/RR-TB that is also resistant to **any fluoroquinolone** (levofloxacin or moxifloxacin). [WHO 2021]`,`**XDR-TB** (WHO 2021 definition): MDR/RR-TB resistant to a fluoroquinolone **plus at least one other Group A drug (bedaquiline or linezolid)**; the old definition based on injectables was dropped when injectables left the regimens. [WHO 2021]`,`**Isoniazid-resistant, rifampicin-susceptible TB (Hr-TB)** is a separate entity treated with **6 months of rifampicin, ethambutol, pyrazinamide and levofloxacin** (6(H)REZ-Lfx). [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`Globally about **400,000 people** develop MDR/RR-TB each year and fewer than half are diagnosed and treated; **India carries about a quarter of the world's MDR/RR-TB**, the largest share of any country. [WHO Global TB Report 2024]`,`India's **First National Anti-TB Drug Resistance Survey (2014-16)** found MDR-TB in **2.84% of new** and **11.6% of previously treated** patients, and **fluoroquinolone resistance in about 22% of MDR-TB** isolates - the reason FQ resistance must be tested before any regimen is chosen. [NDRS 2014-16]`,`**Primary (transmitted) resistance** now causes most MDR-TB in India, so a new patient with no past treatment can still have MDR-TB - hence **universal drug-susceptibility testing (UDST)** for every diagnosed patient. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`Resistance arises from **spontaneous chromosomal mutations** (rifampicin about 1 in 10^8 bacilli, isoniazid about 1 in 10^6) selected by **monotherapy or inadequate regimens**; there are no plasmids in *M. tuberculosis*. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acquired resistance** follows irregular treatment, wrong doses, poor-quality drugs, adding a single drug to a failing regimen, and private-sector prescriptions outside guidelines. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Previous TB treatment** is the strongest individual risk factor, especially **treatment failure, loss to follow-up and recurrence**. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`**Contacts of a known MDR-TB patient**, healthcare workers, prisoners and people from high-burden settings are at higher risk of transmitted resistance. [WHO 2025]`,`**HIV infection, diabetes and malabsorption** lower drug levels or increase progression; poor rifampicin absorption in advanced HIV has been linked to acquired rifamycin resistance. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Persistent sputum positivity at the end of the intensive phase** or clinical deterioration on first-line treatment should trigger repeat NAAT and DST. [NTEP 2023]`]},{heading:`Mechanism of resistance and drug action`,points:[`**Rifampicin resistance**: over 95% is due to mutations in an **81-bp rifampicin resistance-determining region (RRDR) of rpoB**, which is why a single NAAT cartridge can detect it in 2 hours. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Isoniazid resistance**: **katG mutations** (catalase-peroxidase that activates the prodrug) cause high-level resistance; **inhA promoter mutations** cause low-level resistance and **cross-resistance to ethionamide**. [WHO 2021]`,`**Fluoroquinolone resistance** comes from **gyrA/gyrB** mutations in DNA gyrase; **second-line injectable resistance** from **rrs** (amikacin) and **eis** (kanamycin) mutations. [WHO 2021]`,`**Bedaquiline** (a diarylquinoline) inhibits **mycobacterial ATP synthase**; resistance arises through **Rv0678 (mmpR5) efflux-pump mutations**, which also confer **cross-resistance to clofazimine**. [WHO 2025]`,`**Pretomanid and delamanid** are nitroimidazoles: prodrugs activated by the **deazaflavin-dependent nitroreductase (ddn)** that block mycolic acid synthesis and release nitric oxide, killing non-replicating bacilli. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Linezolid** binds the **23S rRNA of the 50S ribosome**; it also inhibits human mitochondrial protein synthesis, which explains its time- and dose-dependent **myelosuppression, neuropathy and lactic acidosis**. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Bedaquiline, clofazimine, moxifloxacin and delamanid prolong QT** by blocking hERG potassium channels; their effect is additive, so combined regimens need ECG monitoring. [WHO 2025]`]},{heading:`Classification of resistance and WHO drug groups`,points:[`Resistance types by pattern: **mono-resistance** (one first-line drug), **poly-resistance** (more than one first-line drug other than both H and R), **MDR, pre-XDR and XDR**, and **RR-TB**. [WHO 2021]`,`**Group A** (include all three if possible): **levofloxacin or moxifloxacin, bedaquiline, linezolid**. [WHO 2025]`,`**Group B** (add one or both): **clofazimine, cycloserine or terizidone**. [WHO 2025]`,`**Group C** (to complete a regimen when A and B cannot be used): **ethambutol, delamanid, pyrazinamide, imipenem-cilastatin or meropenem (with clavulanate), amikacin (streptomycin), ethionamide or prothionamide, and PAS**. [WHO 2025]`,`**Kanamycin and capreomycin are no longer recommended** because they were linked to worse outcomes; amikacin is only a last resort with audiometry. [WHO 2025]`,`By history NTEP also records **new versus previously treated** and by **site** (pulmonary versus extrapulmonary), which decides regimen eligibility. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`DR-TB is **clinically indistinguishable from drug-susceptible TB**: cough over 2 weeks, fever, weight loss, night sweats, haemoptysis - resistance is a laboratory diagnosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Suspect DR-TB when there is **no clinical or microbiological response after 2 months** of first-line treatment, or when a patient worsens despite good adherence. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`**Recurrent TB**, a patient returning after **loss to follow-up**, and a contact of a known DR-TB patient are presumptive DR-TB until proved otherwise. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`Patients have often had **months of private-sector treatment**, so the history must record every drug, dose and duration, including fluoroquinolones given for 'chest infection'. [MoHFW 2024]`,`Look for **comorbidities that change the regimen**: HIV, diabetes, alcohol use, pregnancy, psychiatric illness (cycloserine), heart disease (QT drugs), neuropathy and visual problems (linezolid). [WHO 2025]`,`**Extrapulmonary DR-TB** (lymph node, CNS, spine) presents as non-healing disease on first-line ATT; obtain tissue or fluid for NAAT and culture. [INDEX-TB 2016]`]},{heading:`Examination`,points:[`Record **weight and BMI at every visit**: weight and BMI decide doses, and BMI under 16 or weight loss despite treatment signals poor response or malnutrition. [NTEP 2023]`,`Look for **pallor** (baseline before linezolid), **jaundice** (hepatotoxic drugs) and **signs of respiratory failure** (cyanosis, RR over 30, SpO2 under 94%) that need admission. [NTEP 2023]`,`Chest signs - **bronchial breathing, crackles, amphoric breath sounds over cavities**, and tracheal shift from fibrosis - do not differ from drug-sensitive disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`Baseline **visual acuity (Snellen) and colour vision (Ishihara)** before linezolid and ethambutol; document any baseline deficit. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`Baseline **peripheral neuropathy screen** (vibration, ankle reflexes, the Brief Peripheral Neuropathy Screen) before linezolid. [WHO 2025]`,`Screen for **depression and psychosis** before cycloserine, and for **hearing loss** (audiometry) if amikacin is unavoidable. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Non-response from poor adherence** to first-line ATT: check pill counts, Ni-kshay adherence records and family supervision before labelling resistance. [NTEP 2023]`,`**Non-tuberculous mycobacteria**: smear-positive but NAAT-negative for MTB, or culture grows a rapid or slow NTM - see the NTM topic. [ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020]`,`**Post-TB sequelae** (bronchiectasis, fibrosis, aspergilloma) cause persistent symptoms with **negative NAAT and culture** - not treatment failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Paradoxical reaction or IRIS** (especially after starting ART) causes worsening nodes or effusions while cultures are converting. [WHO 2025]`,`**Alternative diagnosis**: lung cancer, chronic pulmonary aspergillosis, melioidosis, nocardia or ILD mistaken for TB. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations and the NTEP diagnostic algorithm`,points:[`**Universal DST**: every diagnosed TB patient gets a **rapid molecular test for rifampicin resistance** - **CBNAAT (Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra) or Truenat MTB-RIF Dx** - at diagnosis. [NTEP 2023]`,`If **rifampicin resistance is detected**, send a further sample for **first-line LPA** (katG, inhA - isoniazid), **second-line LPA** (gyrA, gyrB - fluoroquinolones; rrs, eis - injectables) and **liquid culture (MGIT) DST**. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`If rifampicin is **sensitive**, NTEP also does **FL-LPA for isoniazid resistance** to find Hr-TB. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`**Xpert MTB/XDR** (10-colour cartridge) detects resistance to **isoniazid, fluoroquinolones, ethionamide and amikacin** in under 90 minutes, as a reflex test after RR is found. [WHO 2024]`,`**Liquid culture DST for bedaquiline, linezolid, clofazimine, delamanid and pretomanid** is done at reference laboratories; **targeted next-generation sequencing** was endorsed by WHO in 2024 for comprehensive resistance profiles. [WHO 2024]`,`**Low-level RR with very low bacillary load** on Xpert Ultra ('trace' results) should be confirmed before starting a DR-TB regimen. [WHO 2024]`,`**Pre-treatment evaluation**: CBC, LFT, renal function, electrolytes (K, Mg, Ca), **HbA1c**, **HIV**, **TSH**, urine pregnancy test, **ECG with QTcF**, chest X-ray, visual and hearing tests. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`**Follow-up**: monthly sputum smear and culture until conversion and through treatment; **culture conversion** is two consecutive negative cultures at least 30 days apart. [WHO 2021]`]},{heading:`Regimens: BPaLM, BPaL and the shorter oral regimen`,points:[`**BPaLM (6 months, 26 weeks)**: **bedaquiline 400 mg OD for 2 weeks then 200 mg three times a week for 24 weeks, pretomanid 200 mg OD, linezolid 600 mg OD and moxifloxacin 400 mg OD** - preferred for MDR/RR-TB with no FQ resistance in patients **aged 14 years or over**. [WHO 2025]`,`**BPaL** (moxifloxacin dropped) is used when **fluoroquinolone resistance (pre-XDR)** is confirmed; in TB-PRACTECAL, BPaLM gave **89% favourable outcome versus 52% with the old standard care**. [Lancet/NEJM TB-PRACTECAL 2022]`,`India approved and began rolling out **BPaLM in 2024** under NTEP as the preferred regimen for eligible MDR/RR-TB and pre-XDR-TB. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**BPaLM is not used** in pregnancy or breastfeeding, children under 14, **CNS, osteoarticular or disseminated TB**, or after more than 1 month's prior exposure to bedaquiline, linezolid, pretomanid or delamanid. [WHO 2025]`,`Extend BPaL(M) to **9 months (39 weeks)** if sputum culture is still positive between months 4 and 6; linezolid can be reduced to **300 mg** for toxicity after the first months. [WHO 2025]`,`**Shorter oral bedaquiline-containing regimen (9-11 months)**: **4-6 months Bdq(6), Lfx, Cfz, Z, E, high-dose H, Eto, then 5 months Lfx, Cfz, Z, E** - for FQ-sensitive MDR/RR-TB not eligible for BPaLM, including children. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`The shorter regimen is excluded if there is **resistance to FQ**, **both katG and inhA mutations**, more than 1 month of second-line drugs, extensive disease or severe EPTB. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`WHO 2025 also lists **9-month endTB regimens** (for example bedaquiline, delamanid, linezolid, levofloxacin, clofazimine - BDLLfxC) as options, including in **pregnancy and children** when BPaLM cannot be used. [WHO 2025]`]},{heading:`Longer oral regimen and Hr-TB`,points:[`**Longer oral M/XDR-TB regimen (18-20 months)**: all three Group A drugs plus at least one Group B drug, so that **at least 4 effective drugs** are given at the start and 3 after bedaquiline stops at 6 months. [WHO 2025]`,`Typical NTEP longer regimen: **18-20 months Lfx/Mfx, Bdq (6 months or longer), Lzd, Cfz, Cs**, modified by DST and toxicity. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`Use the longer regimen for **XDR-TB**, failure of a shorter regimen, extensive EPTB, or resistance or intolerance that rules out shorter regimens. [WHO 2025]`,`**Cycloserine 10-15 mg/kg/day (usually 500-750 mg)** with **pyridoxine 50 mg for every 250 mg** to prevent seizures and neuropathy. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`**Clofazimine 100 mg OD** causes reversible **pink-brown skin and conjunctival discolouration**, dry skin and QT prolongation. [WHO 2025]`,`**Hr-TB**: **6 months of rifampicin, ethambutol, pyrazinamide and levofloxacin**, dosed by weight band; add no isoniazid. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`]},{heading:`Programmatic care: DR-TB centres, admission and adverse-effect monitoring`,points:[`Treatment begins at a **Nodal DR-TB Centre (N-DR-TBC) or District DR-TB Centre (D-DR-TBC)** after pre-treatment evaluation; **admission is not mandatory** and is reserved for the sick or those with comorbidity. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`**Admit** for respiratory failure, massive haemoptysis, severe malnutrition, uncontrolled diabetes, serious adverse events, psychiatric illness on cycloserine, or QTcF over 500 ms. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`**ECG with QTcF** at baseline, at **2 weeks**, then monthly while on bedaquiline; **stop QT-prolonging drugs if QTcF exceeds 500 ms** and correct potassium, magnesium and calcium. [WHO 2025]`,`**CBC** at baseline and regularly (weekly to monthly in the first months) on linezolid; hold linezolid for **haemoglobin under 8 g/dL, platelets under 75,000 or neutrophils under 750**. [WHO 2025]`,`**Linezolid neuropathy** is graded at every visit; **optic neuritis** (falling acuity, colour vision loss) means stop linezolid permanently. [WHO 2025]`,`**LFT** monthly with pretomanid and bedaquiline; **TSH** every 3 months with ethionamide or PAS (hypothyroidism); creatinine and electrolytes monthly. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`Every serious adverse event is reported through **aDSM (active drug safety monitoring) in Ni-kshay**. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: do not use BPaLM; avoid **aminoglycosides (fetal ototoxicity) and ethionamide (teratogenic, worsens vomiting)**; a 9-month endTB regimen or individualised bedaquiline-based regimen is used with specialist input. [WHO 2025]`,`**Children**: the shorter oral bedaquiline regimen and **bedaquiline and delamanid can be used at all ages**; child-friendly dispersible formulations are now available. [WHO 2025]`,`**HIV**: start ART within 2 weeks; avoid **efavirenz with bedaquiline** (lowers levels) - **dolutegravir-based ART** is preferred. [WHO 2025]`,`**Diabetes**: control glucose tightly, because diabetes raises the risk of linezolid neuropathy and of treatment failure. [NTEP 2023]`,`**Renal failure**: reduce frequency of **ethambutol, pyrazinamide, levofloxacin, cycloserine** to thrice weekly; bedaquiline, linezolid and moxifloxacin need no change. [WHO 2025]`,`**Liver disease**: prefer regimens with fewer hepatotoxic drugs; bedaquiline and pretomanid need close LFT monitoring. [WHO 2025]`]},{heading:`Complications, outcomes and prognosis`,points:[`Global **treatment success for MDR/RR-TB is about 68%** (2020 cohort), dragged down by deaths and loss to follow-up on long regimens. [WHO Global TB Report 2024]`,`With BPaL in **Nix-TB, 90% of XDR or treatment-intolerant MDR-TB patients** had a favourable outcome; ZeNix showed **linezolid 600 mg for 26 weeks** kept efficacy with less toxicity. [NEJM 2020, 2022]`,`**Outcome definitions** (WHO 2021): cured, treatment completed, treatment failed, died, lost to follow-up, not evaluated; **sustained treatment success** is no recurrence at 6 months after treatment. [WHO 2021]`,`Sequelae include **post-TB lung disease**, chronic pulmonary aspergillosis, haemoptysis and cor pulmonale - see the NTM and post-TB lung disease topic. [Harrison 22e]`,`Drug harms: **permanent linezolid neuropathy**, hearing loss with amikacin, hypothyroidism and psychiatric illness - the reason injectables were removed. [WHO 2025]`]},{heading:`Follow-up, contacts and prevention`,points:[`Monthly clinical review with weight, symptoms, adherence and adverse effects; monthly culture; **post-treatment follow-up every 6 months for 2 years** under NTEP. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`**Household contacts** of DR-TB are screened by symptoms and chest X-ray; **TB preventive treatment with levofloxacin for 6 months** is recommended for contacts of MDR/RR-TB - see the TB infection and TPT topic. [WHO 2024]`,`**Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana** pays **Rs 1000 per month** by DBT for the whole treatment period (raised from Rs 500 in 2024), and Ni-kshay Mitras provide food baskets. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**Airborne infection control**: separate cough triage, ventilation, N95 for staff, surgical mask for the patient, and cough hygiene at home. [NTEP 2023]`,`Prevention of acquired resistance: **fixed-dose combinations, directly observed or digitally monitored treatment (99DOTS, MERM boxes)**, and never adding a single drug to a failing regimen. [NTEP 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**WHO 2022** recommended the **6-month BPaLM/BPaL regimen** for MDR/RR-TB and pre-XDR-TB in people aged 14 or over. [WHO 2022]`,`**WHO 2025 consolidated Module 4** added **9-month endTB regimens** (BDLLfxC, BLMZ and others) and extended options to children, adolescents and pregnant women. [WHO 2025]`,`**India 2024**: BPaLM approved and rolled out under NTEP; **Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana raised to Rs 1000 per month**. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**WHO 2024** recommended **6 months of levofloxacin** as TPT for contacts of MDR/RR-TB (VQUIN and TB-CHAMP trials). [WHO 2024]`,`**WHO 2024** endorsed **targeted next-generation sequencing** and low-complexity automated NAATs (Xpert MTB/XDR) for second-line resistance. [WHO 2024]`,`**WHO 2021** redefined **pre-XDR and XDR** around fluoroquinolones, bedaquiline and linezolid instead of injectables. [WHO 2021]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Define **pre-XDR and XDR by the 2021 WHO definitions** - quoting injectables is the commonest mistake. [WHO 2021]`,`Write the **BPaLM doses exactly**: bedaquiline 400 mg OD x 2 weeks then 200 mg thrice weekly x 22 weeks; pretomanid 200 mg, linezolid 600 mg, moxifloxacin 400 mg daily for 26 weeks. [WHO 2025]`,`Name the **three linezolid toxicities** (myelosuppression, peripheral neuropathy, optic neuropathy) and the **QT threshold of 500 ms**. [WHO 2025]`,`Explain **inhA versus katG**: inhA gives low-level INH resistance and ethionamide cross-resistance; both mutations together exclude the shorter regimen. [NTEP PMDT 2021]`,`Remember the **Group A drugs**: 'Lfx/Mfx, Bdq, Lzd - **LBL**'. [WHO 2025]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drug-resistant TB drugs: doses and adverse effects (adult)`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Bedaquiline (Group A)`,`400 mg oral OD x 2 weeks, then 200 mg three times weekly; take with food`,`24 weeks in BPaLM; 6 months (can be longer) in other regimens`,`QT prolongation, hepatotoxicity, arthralgia, nausea; avoid with efavirenz`],[`Pretomanid`,`200 mg oral OD with food`,`26 weeks (39 if extended)`,`Hepatotoxicity, nausea, peripheral neuropathy and myelosuppression mainly through linezolid; not in pregnancy or under 14 years`],[`Linezolid (Group A)`,`600 mg oral OD (300 mg if toxicity); longer regimen 600 mg OD`,`26 weeks in BPaLM; often 6 months or more in longer regimen`,`Anaemia, thrombocytopenia, peripheral and optic neuropathy, lactic acidosis, serotonin syndrome with SSRIs`],[`Moxifloxacin / levofloxacin (Group A)`,`Moxifloxacin 400 mg OD; levofloxacin 750-1000 mg OD by weight`,`Whole regimen`,`QT prolongation (moxifloxacin more), tendinopathy, dysglycaemia, insomnia, seizures`],[`Clofazimine (Group B)`,`100 mg oral OD`,`Whole regimen in shorter and longer regimens`,`Skin and conjunctival pink-brown discolouration, ichthyosis, QT prolongation, abdominal pain`],[`Cycloserine (Group B)`,`10-15 mg/kg/day, usually 500-750 mg in divided doses, with pyridoxine 50 mg per 250 mg`,`Longer regimen`,`Depression, psychosis, suicidal ideation, seizures; avoid in epilepsy or psychiatric illness`],[`Delamanid (Group C)`,`100 mg oral BD`,`6 months (can be extended)`,`QT prolongation, nausea; used when bedaquiline or linezolid cannot be`],[`Ethionamide (Group C)`,`15-20 mg/kg/day, max 1 g`,`Shorter regimen intensive phase`,`GI intolerance, hypothyroidism, hepatotoxicity, gynaecomastia; teratogenic`]]},{heading:`Definitions of drug-resistant TB (WHO 2021)`,columns:[`Type`,`Definition`,`Usual NTEP regimen`],rows:[[`Hr-TB`,`Isoniazid-resistant, rifampicin-susceptible`,`6 months R, E, Z plus levofloxacin`],[`RR-TB`,`Rifampicin resistance with or without other resistance`,`Managed as MDR-TB`],[`MDR-TB`,`Resistance to at least rifampicin and isoniazid`,`BPaLM 6 months, or shorter oral 9-11 months, or longer oral 18-20 months`],[`Pre-XDR-TB`,`MDR/RR-TB plus resistance to any fluoroquinolone`,`BPaL 6 months or longer oral regimen`],[`XDR-TB`,`MDR/RR-TB plus fluoroquinolone plus bedaquiline and/or linezolid resistance`,`Individualised longer regimen at a nodal DR-TB centre`]]},{heading:`Choosing the regimen for MDR/RR-TB`,columns:[`Regimen`,`Composition and duration`,`Eligible`,`Not eligible`],rows:[[`BPaLM`,`Bdq, Pa, Lzd, Mfx for 26 weeks`,`MDR/RR-TB, FQ-sensitive, age 14 or over`,`Pregnancy, breastfeeding, CNS/bone/miliary TB, prior exposure over 1 month to Bdq/Pa/Lzd/Dlm`],[`BPaL`,`Bdq, Pa, Lzd for 26 weeks`,`Pre-XDR (FQ-resistant) TB, age 14 or over`,`Same exclusions as BPaLM`],[`Shorter oral Bdq regimen`,`4-6 Bdq(6) Lfx Cfz Z E Hh Eto / 5 Lfx Cfz Z E (9-11 months)`,`FQ-sensitive MDR/RR-TB incl. children, not eligible for BPaLM`,`FQ resistance, katG plus inhA mutations, prior second-line over 1 month, severe EPTB`],[`Longer oral regimen`,`Group A all three plus Group B; 18-20 months`,`XDR-TB, failure of shorter regimen, extensive disease`,`Individualised to DST and tolerance`]]},{heading:`Diagnostic tests for drug resistance`,columns:[`Test`,`Detects`,`Time`,`Use under NTEP`],rows:[[`CBNAAT Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra`,`MTB and rifampicin resistance (rpoB)`,`About 2 hours`,`Upfront test for all presumptive TB at district level`],[`Truenat MTB / MTB-RIF Dx`,`MTB and rifampicin resistance`,`About 1 hour, chip-based, battery-operated`,`Peripheral labs and PHCs`],[`Xpert MTB/XDR`,`Isoniazid, fluoroquinolone, ethionamide, amikacin resistance`,`Under 90 minutes`,`Reflex test after RR detected`],[`First-line LPA`,`rpoB, katG, inhA`,`1-3 days on smear-positive sputum`,`Isoniazid resistance in all diagnosed TB`],[`Second-line LPA`,`gyrA, gyrB (FQ), rrs, eis (injectables)`,`1-3 days`,`All RR-TB before regimen choice`],[`Liquid culture DST (MGIT)`,`Phenotypic resistance incl. bedaquiline, linezolid, clofazimine, delamanid, pretomanid`,`2-6 weeks`,`Reference labs; monitoring cultures`],[`Targeted NGS`,`Mutations across many drugs at once`,`Days`,`Reference labs; endorsed by WHO 2024`]]},{heading:`Adverse-effect monitoring schedule`,columns:[`Test`,`When`,`Action threshold`],rows:[[`ECG with QTcF`,`Baseline, 2 weeks, then monthly on Bdq/Dlm/Cfz/Mfx`,`QTcF over 500 ms: stop QT drugs, correct K/Mg/Ca`],[`CBC`,`Baseline, then frequently in the first 2 months, then monthly on linezolid`,`Hb under 8 g/dL, platelets under 75,000, ANC under 750: hold linezolid`],[`Visual acuity and Ishihara`,`Baseline and monthly on linezolid or ethambutol`,`Any loss: stop linezolid and refer to ophthalmology`],[`Neuropathy screen`,`Every visit`,`Grade 2 or more: reduce or stop linezolid`],[`LFT`,`Baseline and monthly`,`ALT over 3 times ULN with symptoms or over 5 times without: hold hepatotoxic drugs`],[`TSH`,`Baseline and every 3 months on Eto or PAS`,`Raised: start levothyroxine, continue drugs`]]}],redFlags:[`QTcF over 500 ms, syncope or palpitations on bedaquiline, delamanid, clofazimine or moxifloxacin`,`Sudden fall in vision or colour vision on linezolid (optic neuropathy)`,`Haemoglobin under 8 g/dL or platelets under 75,000 on linezolid`,`Jaundice or ALT over 5 times the upper limit, or ALT over 3 times with symptoms`,`Suicidal ideation, psychosis or seizures on cycloserine`,`Massive haemoptysis or respiratory failure (SpO2 under 90%)`,`Persistent culture positivity after 4 months or clinical deterioration on a DR-TB regimen`],pearls:[`**Every** diagnosed TB patient in India needs a rapid rifampicin-resistance test - universal DST.`,`**Pre-XDR = MDR plus FQ resistance; XDR = pre-XDR plus bedaquiline or linezolid resistance** (WHO 2021).`,`BPaLM is **6 months, all oral, 4 drugs**; drop moxifloxacin (BPaL) if FQ-resistant.`,`Group A drugs are '**LBL**': levofloxacin/moxifloxacin, bedaquiline, linezolid.`,`**inhA mutation** means ethionamide is useless; katG plus inhA together exclude the shorter regimen.`,`**Bedaquiline plus efavirenz** is a classic interaction - switch to dolutegravir.`,`Cycloserine needs **pyridoxine 50 mg per 250 mg**.`,`**QTcF over 500 ms** - stop all QT-prolonging drugs.`,`Kanamycin and capreomycin are **no longer recommended**.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-drtb-q1`,stem:`A 34-year-old man has MDR-TB. Second-line line probe assay shows a gyrA mutation, and phenotypic DST shows linezolid resistance. Under the WHO 2021 definitions, this is:`,options:[`Pre-XDR-TB`,`MDR-TB with poly-resistance`,`XDR-TB`,`Rifampicin-resistant TB only`],answer:2,explanation:`XDR-TB (WHO 2021) is MDR/RR-TB resistant to a fluoroquinolone plus at least one more Group A drug, bedaquiline or linezolid - here gyrA (FQ) plus linezolid resistance. Pre-XDR stops at fluoroquinolone resistance alone, and poly-resistance refers to first-line drugs other than both H and R, so neither fits.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-drtb-q2`,stem:`Which is the correct BPaLM dose schedule for an adult with fluoroquinolone-susceptible MDR-TB?`,options:[`Bedaquiline 400 mg OD for 2 weeks then 200 mg three times weekly, pretomanid 200 mg OD, linezolid 600 mg OD, moxifloxacin 400 mg OD for 26 weeks`,`Bedaquiline 200 mg OD, pretomanid 100 mg OD, linezolid 1200 mg OD, moxifloxacin 800 mg OD for 9 months`,`Bedaquiline 400 mg OD throughout, pretomanid 200 mg BD, linezolid 600 mg BD, levofloxacin 750 mg OD for 18 months`,`Bedaquiline 400 mg three times weekly, pretomanid 200 mg OD, linezolid 300 mg OD, moxifloxacin 400 mg OD for 12 weeks`],answer:0,explanation:`BPaLM is 26 weeks: bedaquiline loads at 400 mg daily for 2 weeks and then 200 mg three times a week, with daily pretomanid 200 mg, linezolid 600 mg and moxifloxacin 400 mg. Linezolid 1200 mg was the original Nix-TB dose and caused much more neuropathy, and 18-month durations describe the longer regimen, not BPaLM.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-drtb-q3`,stem:`A patient on a bedaquiline-containing regimen has a QTcF of 520 ms on the monthly ECG. The best immediate step is:`,options:[`Continue all drugs and repeat the ECG in a month`,`Add a beta-blocker and continue`,`Reduce bedaquiline to 100 mg and continue moxifloxacin`,`Stop QT-prolonging drugs and correct potassium, magnesium and calcium`],answer:3,explanation:`A QTcF over 500 ms is the threshold at which bedaquiline and other QT-prolonging drugs (moxifloxacin, clofazimine, delamanid) are stopped and electrolytes corrected, with repeat ECGs. Continuing unchanged risks torsades, and dose-reducing bedaquiline is not a recognised strategy; beta-blockers do not make the drugs safe.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-drtb-q4`,stem:`Which finding makes a patient with MDR-TB ineligible for the shorter oral bedaquiline-containing regimen?`,options:[`Age 10 years`,`Both katG and inhA mutations on line probe assay`,`HIV infection on dolutegravir-based ART`,`Isolated cervical lymph node TB`],answer:1,explanation:`The shorter regimen relies on high-dose isoniazid and ethionamide; katG plus inhA mutations mean high-level isoniazid and ethionamide resistance, so the regimen loses two drugs. Children can receive it, HIV on dolutegravir is compatible, and limited lymph node disease is not a severe extrapulmonary exclusion.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`respiratory-drtb-q5`,stem:`A woman on a longer DR-TB regimen develops blurred vision and red-green colour loss in month 5. Which drug is most likely responsible and should be stopped?`,options:[`Clofazimine`,`Bedaquiline`,`Cycloserine`,`Linezolid`],answer:3,explanation:`Linezolid causes time-dependent optic neuropathy through mitochondrial toxicity, and visual loss means it should be stopped permanently with ophthalmology referral. Clofazimine causes skin discolouration, bedaquiline mainly QT prolongation, and cycloserine neuropsychiatric effects rather than optic neuropathy.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-drtb-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Define and classify drug-resistant tuberculosis. Describe the diagnostic algorithm and management of MDR/RR-TB under the National TB Elimination Programme, including the BPaLM regimen.`,openingLines:[`Drug-resistant TB is TB caused by M. tuberculosis resistant to one or more anti-TB drugs; MDR-TB is resistance to at least rifampicin and isoniazid.`,`India carries about a quarter of the world's MDR/RR-TB, and NTEP now offers universal NAAT-based DST and the 6-month all-oral BPaLM regimen.`],answer:[{heading:`Definitions (WHO 2021)`,points:[`Mono-, poly-resistance, Hr-TB, RR-TB, MDR-TB.`,`Pre-XDR: MDR/RR plus any fluoroquinolone resistance.`,`XDR: pre-XDR plus bedaquiline and/or linezolid resistance.`]},{heading:`Burden and risk factors`,points:[`NDRS 2014-16: MDR 2.84% new, 11.6% previously treated; FQ resistance about 22% of MDR.`,`Previous treatment, failure, contacts of MDR, HIV, diabetes, poor adherence.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`UDST: CBNAAT or Truenat for rifampicin resistance in all diagnosed TB.`,`If RR: FL-LPA, SL-LPA or Xpert MTB/XDR, and liquid culture DST.`,`Pre-treatment evaluation: CBC, LFT, RFT, electrolytes, HbA1c, HIV, TSH, ECG-QTcF, pregnancy test, vision and hearing.`]},{heading:`Regimens`,points:[`WHO drug groups A (Lfx/Mfx, Bdq, Lzd), B (Cfz, Cs), C.`,`BPaLM 26 weeks with doses; BPaL for FQ resistance; eligibility and exclusions.`,`Shorter oral Bdq regimen 9-11 months; longer oral 18-20 months; Hr-TB 6 months REZ-Lfx.`]},{heading:`Monitoring and adverse effects`,points:[`Monthly smear and culture; culture conversion definition.`,`QTcF over 500 ms stop; CBC and vision for linezolid; LFT, TSH; aDSM in Ni-kshay.`]},{heading:`Programme support and prevention`,points:[`N-DR-TBC and D-DR-TBC; Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana Rs 1000/month; Ni-kshay Mitra.`,`Contact screening and 6 months levofloxacin TPT; infection control; post-treatment follow-up 2 years.`]}],mustDraw:[`NTEP DR-TB diagnostic algorithm flow chart from NAAT to LPA to regimen`,`Regimen selection table (BPaLM, BPaL, shorter, longer)`],markSplit:[{part:`Definitions and classification`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnostic algorithm`,marks:3},{part:`Regimens including BPaLM`,marks:3},{part:`Monitoring and programme support`,marks:2}],keywords:[`MDR-TB`,`pre-XDR`,`XDR`,`UDST`,`BPaLM`,`LPA`,`QTcF`,`Ni-kshay`]},{id:`respiratory-drtb-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`core`,question:`Write a short note on the BPaLM regimen.`,openingLines:[`BPaLM is a 6-month all-oral regimen of bedaquiline, pretomanid, linezolid and moxifloxacin for MDR/RR-TB, recommended by WHO in 2022 and rolled out by NTEP in 2024.`,`It replaces 9-20-month regimens with higher success and fewer drugs.`],answer:[{heading:`Composition and doses`,points:[`Bedaquiline 400 mg OD x 2 weeks then 200 mg thrice weekly x 22 weeks.`,`Pretomanid 200 mg, linezolid 600 mg, moxifloxacin 400 mg once daily for 26 weeks.`]},{heading:`Eligibility and exclusions`,points:[`MDR/RR-TB age 14 or over; BPaL if FQ-resistant.`,`Exclude pregnancy, breastfeeding, CNS/bone/miliary TB, prior exposure over 1 month.`]},{heading:`Evidence`,points:[`TB-PRACTECAL: 89% favourable versus 52% with standard care; Nix-TB and ZeNix for BPaL.`]},{heading:`Monitoring`,points:[`ECG-QTcF, CBC, vision, neuropathy, LFT; extend to 39 weeks if culture positive at months 4-6.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table of the four drugs with dose, mechanism and main toxicity`],markSplit:[{part:`Composition and doses`,marks:2},{part:`Eligibility`,marks:1},{part:`Evidence and monitoring`,marks:2}],keywords:[`BPaLM`,`bedaquiline`,`pretomanid`,`linezolid`,`moxifloxacin`,`TB-PRACTECAL`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Tuberculosis`,`WHO consolidated guidelines on tuberculosis, Module 4: Treatment and care, 2025`,`WHO consolidated guidelines on tuberculosis, Module 3: Diagnosis, 2024 update`,`Guidelines for Programmatic Management of Drug-Resistant TB in India, NTEP, MoHFW, 2021`,`MoHFW / Central TB Division - introduction of BPaLM regimen under NTEP, 2024`,`Report of the First National Anti-Tuberculosis Drug Resistance Survey, India 2014-16`,`WHO Global Tuberculosis Report 2024`,`Nyang'wa BT et al. TB-PRACTECAL, N Engl J Med 2022`]}),i.push({id:`respiratory-extrapulmonary-tb`,title:`Extrapulmonary tuberculosis: lymph node, pleural, abdominal, CNS, spinal and genitourinary`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Extrapulmonary TB (EPTB)** is TB of organs other than the lung parenchyma - commonest in **lymph nodes and pleura** - that is **paucibacillary**, so diagnosis rests on **tissue or fluid for NAAT, culture and histology** plus surrogate markers such as **ADA**; it is treated with the standard **2HRZE/4HRE** regimen, extended to **12 months for CNS and osteoarticular TB**, with **corticosteroids for TB meningitis** (and selectively for pericarditis), following the **INDEX-TB guidelines (2016)** and NTEP.`,keywords:[`EPTB`,`INDEX-TB`,`scrofula`,`TB lymphadenitis`,`TB meningitis`,`TBM`,`Pott's spine`,`spinal TB`,`abdominal TB`,`ileocaecal TB`,`genitourinary TB`,`female genital TB`,`pericardial TB`,`ADA`,`Xpert Ultra`,`paradoxical reaction`,`tuberculoma`,`cold abscess`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**EPTB** is TB involving organs other than the lungs, such as pleura, lymph nodes, abdomen, genitourinary tract, skin, joints and bones, and meninges; a patient with both lung and extrapulmonary disease is classified as **pulmonary TB**. [WHO 2021]`,`Pleural TB is counted as **extrapulmonary** in WHO and NTEP reporting, though it is managed with the respiratory team - see the Pleural effusion topic for the effusion work-up. [WHO 2021]`,`Globally about **16% of notified TB** is extrapulmonary; in India EPTB makes up roughly **one-fifth to one-quarter of notified cases** in the India TB Report. [WHO Global TB Report 2024]`,`**Lymph node TB** is the commonest form, followed by **pleural TB**; abdominal, skeletal, genitourinary and CNS TB follow. [Harrison 22e]`,`EPTB is commoner in **HIV infection (especially CD4 under 200), children, women and people of South Asian origin**. [Harrison 22e]`,`The **INDEX-TB guidelines (2016)**, developed by ICMR, the Central TB Division and WHO India, are India's site-specific evidence-based guidance for EPTB. [INDEX-TB 2016]`]},{heading:`Pathogenesis and why EPTB is paucibacillary`,points:[`During primary infection bacilli spread by **lymphatics to hilar nodes and by the blood to any organ**, seeding foci that may reactivate years later - the basis of EPTB in any site. [Harrison 22e]`,`Sites with **high oxygen tension and blood flow** - lung apices, renal cortex, vertebral bodies and the growing ends of long bones - are favoured. [Harrison 22e]`,`Most damage is from the **host's delayed-type hypersensitivity**: caseating granulomas, exudates and fibrosis, not bacillary load - hence few organisms in fluids and low culture yield. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TB meningitis** follows rupture of a subependymal or subpial focus (the **Rich focus**) into the subarachnoid space; thick **basal exudate** entraps cranial nerves, causes **vasculitis and infarcts** and blocks CSF flow, causing **hydrocephalus**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Spinal TB** starts in the **anterior vertebral body near the disc (paradiscal)**; the avascular disc is destroyed late, vertebrae collapse anteriorly causing **gibbus and kyphosis**, and pus tracks as a **cold abscess** (psoas, paravertebral). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Abdominal TB** arises from swallowed sputum, haematogenous spread or contiguous nodes; **Peyer's patches in the ileocaecal region** are the commonest gut site, and the peritoneum forms ascites or adhesions. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Genitourinary TB** begins in the renal cortex and spreads down the collecting system, producing **papillary necrosis, strictures and a small fibrosed bladder**; in women haematogenous spread to the **fallopian tubes** causes infertility. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pericardial TB** progresses from effusive to effusive-constrictive and **constrictive** stages as fibrin and fibrosis thicken the pericardium. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and grading the examiner asks`,points:[`**Jones and Campbell staging of TB lymphadenitis**: 1 enlarged firm mobile nodes; 2 rubbery matted nodes; 3 central softening (abscess); 4 **collar-stud abscess**; 5 **sinus formation**. [Jones and Campbell 1962]`,`**Modified BMRC grading of TB meningitis**: **Grade I** - GCS 15 with no focal deficit; **Grade II** - GCS 11-14, or 15 with focal deficit; **Grade III** - GCS 10 or less. Mortality rises steeply with grade. [BMRC; Thwaites 2004]`,`**Lancet consensus case definition for TBM (Marais 2010)**: definite (AFB, culture or NAAT positive in CSF), **probable (score 10 or more without imaging, 12 or more with imaging)** and possible. [Marais 2010]`,`**Spinal TB** is classified by site (paradiscal, central, anterior, posterior, skip lesions) and by neurological deficit; kyphosis is measured as **Konstam's angle**. [Tuli 2016]`,`**Abdominal TB** is classified as **gastrointestinal, peritoneal (wet ascitic, dry plastic, fibrotic-fixed), nodal and solid-organ** TB. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Pericardial TB** stages: dry, effusive, effusive-constrictive and constrictive. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features by site`,points:[`**Lymph node TB**: painless, slowly enlarging **cervical** nodes (posterior triangle and jugular chain), matting, cold abscess and discharging sinus; constitutional symptoms in only about half. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Pleural TB**: acute or subacute fever, pleuritic pain and breathlessness in a young adult, with a unilateral moderate effusion. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Abdominal TB**: chronic abdominal pain, distension, fever, weight loss, **subacute intestinal obstruction** (doughy abdomen, recurrent colic) or a **right iliac fossa mass**. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**TB meningitis**: **2-8 weeks of headache, fever, vomiting**, then confusion, cranial nerve palsies (**VI commonest**), hemiparesis, seizures and coma - the subacute course distinguishes it from bacterial meningitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Spinal TB (Pott's disease)**: back pain, stiffness, constitutional symptoms, **gibbus**, cold abscess, and **paraplegia** (Pott's paraplegia); thoracolumbar spine is commonest. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Genitourinary TB**: frequency, dysuria, haematuria, flank pain, **sterile pyuria**, epididymo-orchitis, and in women **infertility, menstrual disturbance and pelvic pain**. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Pericardial TB**: fever, chest pain, breathlessness, raised JVP, pulsus paradoxus, or features of constriction (Kussmaul sign, pericardial knock, ascites). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Cold abscess**: fluctuant, non-tender, without warmth or erythema - the opposite of a pyogenic abscess. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Collar-stud abscess**: a superficial abscess communicating with a deep caseous node through a hole in the deep fascia. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`**Gibbus** (sharp angular kyphosis), paraspinal muscle spasm, tenderness, and **psoas abscess** presenting as a groin swelling or flexion deformity of the hip. [Tuli 2016]`,`**Neck stiffness, Kernig's sign, papilloedema, VI nerve palsy** and choroid tubercles on fundoscopy suggest TBM or miliary TB. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Doughy abdomen**, ascites, and a firm, mildly tender **right iliac fossa mass** suggest abdominal TB. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Beaded vas deferens** and a craggy epididymis suggest male genital TB. [Bailey and Love 28e]`,`Always examine the chest and take a **chest X-ray** - up to a third of EPTB has coexisting pulmonary TB, which changes infectivity and classification. [INDEX-TB 2016]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Cervical lymphadenopathy**: reactive nodes, lymphoma (rubbery, B symptoms), metastatic carcinoma (hard, older patient), NTM in children, sarcoidosis, Kikuchi disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lymphocytic exudative effusion**: TB versus malignancy (older, ADA low, cytology positive) and lymphoma. [Light 2013]`,`**Ileocaecal TB versus Crohn's disease**: TB has **transverse ulcers, patulous ileocaecal valve, caseating confluent granulomas, necrotic nodes**; Crohn's has longitudinal ulcers, skip lesions, perianal disease and **comb sign**. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Chronic meningitis**: TBM versus cryptococcal meningitis (India ink, CrAg), partially treated bacterial meningitis, neurocysticercosis and carcinomatous meningitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Spondylodiscitis**: TB (slow, multiple levels, large cold abscess, posterior element sparing early) versus **brucellosis** (lower lumbar, less destruction), pyogenic (acute) and metastasis (**disc spared**). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sterile pyuria**: GU TB versus partially treated UTI, chlamydia, interstitial nephritis, renal stones and analgesic nephropathy. [OHCM 11e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - principles and per site`,points:[`**Principle**: obtain tissue or fluid for **Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra or Truenat, liquid culture with DST, and histopathology**; a negative NAAT does not exclude EPTB because of low bacillary load. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**TST and IGRA cannot diagnose active EPTB** - they only show infection; INDEX-TB advises against **serological tests** (banned in India since 2012). [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Lymph node**: **FNAC** showing epithelioid granulomas with caseation, AFB stain and Xpert on aspirate; excision biopsy if FNAC is inconclusive. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Pleural fluid**: lymphocytic exudate with **ADA over 40 U/L** strongly supports TB in India; Xpert on fluid has low sensitivity (about 50%), so **pleural biopsy** (histology plus culture) has the highest yield. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Ascitic fluid**: high-protein, **SAAG under 1.1 g/dL**, lymphocytic, **ADA of 39 U/L or more**; CT shows thickened peritoneum, omental caking and necrotic nodes; **colonoscopy with ileal and caecal biopsy** for culture and NAAT. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**CSF**: raised opening pressure, **lymphocytic pleocytosis 100-500 cells**, **protein 1-5 g/L**, **glucose under 50% of blood glucose**; **Xpert Ultra on CSF** is the initial test (send at least 6 mL); **MRI with contrast** shows basal enhancement, hydrocephalus, infarcts and tuberculomas. [WHO 2024]`,`**Spine**: **MRI** is the investigation of choice (paradiscal oedema, abscess, skip lesions, cord compression); **CT-guided biopsy** for Xpert, culture and histology. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Genitourinary**: **three to five early-morning urine samples** for culture and Xpert; CT urography (**putty kidney, thimble bladder, golf-hole ureteric orifice**); **endometrial biopsy** in the premenstrual phase for women; **a positive PCR alone must not be used to treat infertile women**. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Pericardial fluid**: lymphocytic exudate, **ADA over 40 U/L** and unstimulated interferon-gamma; echocardiography for effusion and constriction. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**HIV with CD4 200 or less or seriously ill**: add **urine LF-LAM**, which finds disseminated TB rapidly. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Treatment - standard regimen and duration`,points:[`Most EPTB (lymph node, pleural, abdominal, genitourinary, pericardial) receives the NTEP regimen **2HRZE + 4HRE (6 months)**, daily, as weight-band fixed-dose combinations. [NTEP 2023]`,`**CNS TB (meningitis, tuberculoma) and osteoarticular/spinal TB**: **2HRZE + 10HRE (12 months total)** under NTEP; INDEX-TB allows 9-12 months individualised by response. [NTEP 2023]`,`**Abdominal TB**: **6 months is as good as 9 months** in Indian trials, so routine extension is not needed. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`Adult FDC dosing (H75 R150 Z400 E275 per tablet): **25-34 kg 2 tablets, 35-49 kg 3, 50-64 kg 4, 65-75 kg 5, over 75 kg 6** daily. [NTEP 2023]`,`Every EPTB patient needs **universal DST** where a specimen exists; RR found on tissue or fluid means a DR-TB regimen - see the Drug-resistant TB topic. [NTEP 2023]`,`**Residual nodes or masses at the end of 6 months are common** and are not by themselves a reason to extend treatment. [INDEX-TB 2016]`]},{heading:`Steroids, surgery and inpatient care`,points:[`**TBM - dexamethasone for all HIV-negative patients**: Grade II/III **0.4 mg/kg/day IV week 1, 0.3 week 2, 0.2 week 3, 0.1 week 4**, then oral tapering over 4 weeks; Grade I starts at 0.3 mg/kg/day. [Thwaites NEJM 2004]`,`Steroids reduce **TBM mortality by about 25%** in HIV-negative adults; the **ACT HIV trial (2023)** found **no survival benefit in HIV-positive TBM**. [NEJM 2023]`,`**TB pericarditis**: prednisolone (IMPI regimen **120 mg/day tapering over 6 weeks**) did not reduce mortality but **halved constrictive pericarditis**; WHO suggests steroids may be used, avoiding them in HIV because of Kaposi sarcoma risk. [IMPI NEJM 2014]`,`**TBM with hydrocephalus**: communicating hydrocephalus may respond to **acetazolamide and furosemide**; obstructive hydrocephalus or worsening sensorium needs **ventriculoperitoneal shunt or endoscopic third ventriculostomy**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Spinal TB surgery** for **neurological deficit not improving on ATT, instability, progressive kyphosis, large abscess or diagnostic doubt**; most patients recover with ATT alone (Tuli's 'middle path'). [Tuli 2016]`,`**Abdominal TB**: surgery for **perforation, complete obstruction, massive bleeding or fistula**; strictures often respond to ATT or endoscopic dilatation. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Pericardiocentesis** for tamponade; **pericardiectomy** for established constriction. [ESC 2015]`,`**Admit** TBM, spinal TB with deficit, tamponade, intestinal obstruction or perforation, and any EPTB with sepsis or inability to take oral drugs. [INDEX-TB 2016]`]},{heading:`Paradoxical reactions and special situations`,points:[`A **paradoxical reaction** is worsening of existing lesions or appearance of new ones after initial improvement on ATT, in a patient with drug-susceptible TB and good adherence. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`Paradoxical reactions occur in about **10-25% of lymph node TB** and are commoner with HIV on ART (**TB-IRIS**); new nodes, abscesses, effusions or **enlarging tuberculomas** may appear. [Harrison 22e]`,`Manage by **continuing ATT**, aspirating fluctuant nodes, excluding drug resistance (repeat NAAT and culture), and adding **prednisolone** for severe or CNS reactions. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**HIV**: start ART within 2 weeks for most EPTB, but **delay ART 4-8 weeks in TB meningitis** because early ART increases severe adverse events. [WHO 2021]`,`**Pregnancy**: first-line drugs are safe; give **pyridoxine** with isoniazid; genital TB is a cause of infertility and ectopic pregnancy. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Children**: TBM and miliary TB are commoner under 5; treat CNS TB for 12 months under NTEP. [NTEP 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**TBM**: mortality about **20-30% overall and up to 50% in Grade III**; survivors may have hydrocephalus, epilepsy, cognitive impairment, blindness and hemiparesis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Spinal TB**: Pott's paraplegia, severe kyphosis and late-onset paraplegia from cord stretching over the gibbus. [Tuli 2016]`,`**Abdominal TB**: strictures, obstruction, perforation, malabsorption and adhesions. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Genitourinary TB**: obstructive uropathy, **contracted bladder**, CKD and **tubal infertility** (often irreversible). [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Pleural TB**: pleural thickening and trapped lung; **pericardial TB**: constrictive pericarditis in up to a quarter without steroids. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and programme link`,points:[`Notify every EPTB case on **Ni-kshay**; the patient gets **Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana Rs 1000 per month** and treatment support like pulmonary TB. [MoHFW 2024]`,`Follow-up is **clinical** (weight, symptoms, node size, neurological status) because sputum is not available; imaging is repeated only to guide decisions. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`Refer to **neurology/neurosurgery** for TBM, **spine surgery** for deficits or instability, **gastro-surgery** for obstruction, and **cardiology** for tamponade or constriction. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`Screen **household contacts** if there is coexisting pulmonary TB, and offer **TPT** to eligible contacts - see the TB infection and TPT topic. [NTEP 2023]`,`Offer **HIV testing and diabetes screening** to all; manage both in parallel. [NTEP 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**WHO 2024** recommends **Xpert Ultra on CSF** as the initial test for TBM and NAAT on other extrapulmonary specimens. [WHO 2024]`,`**ACT HIV trial (2023)**: dexamethasone did not improve survival in **HIV-associated TBM**; steroids remain standard in HIV-negative TBM. [NEJM 2023]`,`**High-dose rifampicin for TBM** is under trial and **not yet recommended** for routine use. [WHO 2025]`,`**NTEP 2023-24** continues 12-month treatment for CNS and osteoarticular TB and introduced the higher **Ni-kshay Poshan payment (2024)**. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**WHO 2022** paediatric guidance: a **6-month intensive regimen (HRZEto)** is an option for children with TBM. [WHO 2022]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Name the **INDEX-TB guidelines (2016)** - examiners expect this source for Indian EPTB. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`Know **ADA cut-offs**: pleural **40 U/L**, ascitic **39 U/L**, pericardial **40 U/L**. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`Steroids: **always in TBM (HIV-negative)**, consider in pericarditis, **not routine in pleural TB**. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Paradoxical reaction is not treatment failure** - continue ATT. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`Pleural TB with lung involvement is still notified as **pulmonary TB**. [WHO 2021]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for extrapulmonary TB (adult)`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Isoniazid (H)`,`5 mg/kg oral daily (in FDC); pyridoxine 10 mg daily`,`6 months; 12 months CNS/bone`,`Hepatitis, peripheral neuropathy`],[`Rifampicin (R)`,`10 mg/kg oral daily (in FDC)`,`6 months; 12 months CNS/bone`,`Hepatitis, orange secretions, many drug interactions`],[`Pyrazinamide (Z)`,`25 mg/kg oral daily (in FDC)`,`First 2 months`,`Hepatitis, hyperuricaemia, arthralgia`],[`Ethambutol (E)`,`15 mg/kg oral daily (in FDC)`,`Whole course`,`Optic neuritis (colour vision); reduce in renal failure`],[`Dexamethasone (TBM)`,`0.4 mg/kg/day IV (Grade II/III), weekly reduction`,`4 weeks IV, then oral taper over 4 weeks`,`Hyperglycaemia, GI bleed; no survival benefit in HIV`],[`Prednisolone (pericarditis)`,`120 mg/day oral tapering (IMPI) or 1 mg/kg`,`6 weeks`,`Avoid in HIV (Kaposi sarcoma); hyperglycaemia`],[`Acetazolamide (communicating hydrocephalus)`,`10-20 mg/kg/day oral in divided doses with furosemide`,`Weeks, guided by response`,`Metabolic acidosis, hypokalaemia`]]},{heading:`Diagnosis and duration by site (INDEX-TB / NTEP)`,columns:[`Site`,`Best specimen and test`,`Supportive cut-off or finding`,`Duration`,`Steroids`],rows:[[`Lymph node`,`FNAC: cytology, AFB, Xpert; excision biopsy`,`Caseating granulomas`,`6 months`,`No (unless severe paradoxical)`],[`Pleural`,`Pleural fluid and biopsy`,`Lymphocytic exudate, ADA over 40 U/L`,`6 months`,`Not routine`],[`Abdominal`,`Colonoscopic biopsy, ascitic fluid, CT`,`SAAG under 1.1, ADA 39 U/L or more`,`6 months`,`No`],[`CNS (TBM, tuberculoma)`,`CSF Xpert Ultra, culture; MRI`,`Lymphocytes, protein high, glucose under 50% of blood`,`12 months`,`Yes - dexamethasone`],[`Spinal / bone`,`MRI; CT-guided biopsy Xpert and histology`,`Paradiscal lesion, cold abscess`,`12 months`,`No`],[`Genitourinary`,`3-5 early-morning urines; endometrial biopsy`,`Sterile pyuria; CT urography changes`,`6 months`,`No`],[`Pericardial`,`Pericardial fluid, echo`,`ADA over 40 U/L`,`6 months`,`Consider (not in HIV)`]]},{heading:`BMRC grading of TB meningitis`,columns:[`Grade`,`Features`,`Approximate mortality`],rows:[[`I`,`GCS 15, no focal neurological deficit`,`Lowest (about 10-15%)`],[`II`,`GCS 11-14, or GCS 15 with focal deficit`,`Intermediate`],[`III`,`GCS 10 or less`,`Up to 50%`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of key EPTB presentations`,columns:[`Presentation`,`TB clue`,`Main mimic`,`Discriminating feature`],rows:[[`Cervical nodes`,`Matted, cold abscess, sinus`,`Lymphoma`,`Rubbery discrete nodes, B symptoms, biopsy`],[`Ileocaecal disease`,`Transverse ulcers, necrotic nodes, caseation`,`Crohn's disease`,`Longitudinal ulcers, skip lesions, perianal disease, comb sign`],[`Chronic meningitis`,`Basal exudate, low glucose, VI palsy`,`Cryptococcal meningitis`,`CSF CrAg / India ink positive, HIV`],[`Spondylitis`,`Paradiscal, large abscess, gibbus`,`Metastasis`,`Disc space preserved, pedicle involved`],[`Sterile pyuria`,`Early-morning urine culture positive`,`Chlamydia / partially treated UTI`,`NAAT for chlamydia, response to antibiotics`]]}],redFlags:[`Headache with fever for over a week, altered sensorium or cranial nerve palsy (suspect TBM)`,`Back pain with leg weakness, sensory level or bladder involvement (Pott's paraplegia)`,`Raised JVP, pulsus paradoxus or hypotension with pericardial effusion (tamponade)`,`Abdominal distension, vomiting and absolute constipation (intestinal obstruction) or peritonitis`,`Worsening sensorium on ATT in TBM (hydrocephalus, infarct, enlarging tuberculoma)`,`Visual blurring on ethambutol or new jaundice on ATT`],pearls:[`EPTB is **paucibacillary** - get tissue, send NAAT, culture and histology together.`,`ADA **40 pleural, 39 ascitic, 40 pericardial** - INDEX-TB cut-offs.`,`**CNS and bone TB: 12 months** (2HRZE + 10HRE); everything else 6 months.`,`**Steroids in every HIV-negative TBM**; not routinely in pleural TB.`,`**Delay ART 4-8 weeks in TBM**, unlike other TB.`,`**Cold abscess**: no heat, no redness, no tenderness.`,`**Disc destroyed in TB, preserved in metastasis.**`,`**Paradoxical reaction is not failure** - continue ATT, exclude resistance.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-extrapulmonary-tb-q1`,stem:`A 28-year-old woman has 3 weeks of headache, fever and vomiting, GCS 13 and a right VI nerve palsy. CSF shows 180 cells (90% lymphocytes), protein 2.1 g/L and glucose 35% of blood glucose. Xpert Ultra on CSF detects MTB, rifampicin not detected. Which is the correct plan?`,options:[`2HRZE + 4HRE with no steroids`,`2HRZE + 10HRE with dexamethasone tapering over 6-8 weeks`,`2HRZE + 4HRE with prednisolone only if she deteriorates`,`BPaLM for 26 weeks`],answer:1,explanation:`This is BMRC Grade II TB meningitis (GCS 11-14 with focal deficit), drug-sensitive. CNS TB needs 12 months (2HRZE + 10HRE) under NTEP and dexamethasone for HIV-negative TBM, which reduces mortality. A 6-month regimen is too short for CNS disease, withholding steroids loses the mortality benefit, and BPaLM is for rifampicin-resistant TB.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-extrapulmonary-tb-q2`,stem:`Which ascitic fluid profile most strongly supports tuberculous peritonitis?`,options:[`SAAG 1.8 g/dL, protein 1.2 g/dL, neutrophils 500/mm3`,`SAAG 2.0 g/dL, ADA 10 U/L, lymphocytes predominant`,`SAAG 0.8 g/dL, protein 4.5 g/dL, lymphocytes predominant, ADA 52 U/L`,`SAAG 0.6 g/dL, cytology showing adenocarcinoma cells, ADA 15 U/L`],answer:2,explanation:`TB peritonitis gives a low-SAAG (under 1.1) high-protein lymphocytic exudate with ADA of 39 U/L or more. A high SAAG points to portal hypertension, a neutrophil count over 250 with low protein suggests spontaneous bacterial peritonitis, and malignant cells with low ADA indicate peritoneal carcinomatosis.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-extrapulmonary-tb-q3`,stem:`Six weeks into ATT for cervical node TB, a patient who was improving develops a new fluctuant node. He is adherent and the original isolate was rifampicin-sensitive. What is the best next step?`,options:[`Continue ATT, aspirate the node and send it for Xpert and culture`,`Stop ATT and start a DR-TB regimen`,`Add a fluoroquinolone to the current regimen`,`Excise all nodes and stop treatment`],answer:0,explanation:`New or enlarging nodes after initial improvement are typical of a paradoxical reaction. ATT is continued, fluctuant nodes are aspirated, and the aspirate is retested to exclude resistance. Switching to a DR-TB regimen without evidence or adding a single drug risks acquired resistance, and surgery is not a replacement for ATT.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-extrapulmonary-tb-q4`,stem:`On MRI of the dorsal spine, which feature best favours tuberculous over metastatic disease?`,options:[`Involvement of a single pedicle with preserved disc`,`Destruction of the intervertebral disc with a large paravertebral abscess`,`Multiple sclerotic vertebral bodies`,`A collapsed vertebra with a convex posterior border and normal adjacent discs`],answer:1,explanation:`Spinal TB starts paradiscally, involves the disc and two adjacent vertebrae and forms large cold abscesses. Metastases typically spare the disc and involve the pedicle and posterior elements, and sclerotic vertebrae suggest prostate or breast metastasis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-extrapulmonary-tb-q5`,stem:`In a patient with HIV and newly diagnosed TB meningitis who is not on ART, when should ART be started according to WHO guidance?`,options:[`Within 48 hours of ATT`,`Within 2 weeks of ATT, as for other TB`,`After completing 12 months of ATT`,`Deferred 4-8 weeks after starting ATT`],answer:3,explanation:`For TB meningitis, early ART increases severe adverse events including fatal IRIS, so ART is delayed 4-8 weeks after ATT. The 2-week rule applies to other forms of TB, starting within 48 hours is unsafe, and waiting until ATT ends leaves HIV untreated for too long.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-extrapulmonary-tb-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Describe the pathogenesis, clinical features, diagnosis and management of tuberculous meningitis. Add a note on the role of corticosteroids.`,openingLines:[`Tuberculous meningitis is the most severe form of extrapulmonary TB, a subacute basal meningitis caused by rupture of a Rich focus into the subarachnoid space.`,`It is graded by the modified BMRC scale, treated for 12 months with ATT and dexamethasone, and carries up to 50% mortality in Grade III.`],answer:[{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Haematogenous seeding, Rich focus, rupture into subarachnoid space.`,`Basal exudate: cranial nerve palsies, vasculitis and infarcts, hydrocephalus.`]},{heading:`Clinical features and grading`,points:[`Prodrome of 2-8 weeks; headache, fever, vomiting, confusion, VI palsy, seizures.`,`BMRC grade I, II, III with GCS and focal deficit.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`CSF: lymphocytes, protein 1-5 g/L, glucose under 50% of blood; Xpert Ultra, culture.`,`MRI: basal enhancement, hydrocephalus, infarcts, tuberculomas; CXR for miliary; HIV test.`,`Lancet consensus score for probable TBM.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`2HRZE + 10HRE (12 months) under NTEP; DST on CSF.`,`Dexamethasone 0.4 mg/kg/day tapered over 6-8 weeks in HIV-negative patients.`,`Hydrocephalus: acetazolamide, furosemide, VP shunt or ETV.`,`In HIV, ART after 4-8 weeks.`]},{heading:`Role of corticosteroids`,points:[`Thwaites 2004: about 25% lower mortality; no reduction in disability.`,`ACT HIV 2023: no survival benefit in HIV-positive TBM.`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Hydrocephalus, infarcts, SIADH, seizures, paradoxical tuberculoma; mortality up to 50% in Grade III.`]}],mustDraw:[`CSF comparison table: TB versus bacterial versus viral versus cryptococcal meningitis`,`Flow chart of TBM management with steroid taper`],markSplit:[{part:`Pathogenesis`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features and grading`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment and steroids`,marks:3},{part:`Complications`,marks:1}],keywords:[`TBM`,`BMRC`,`Rich focus`,`dexamethasone`,`Xpert Ultra`,`hydrocephalus`,`INDEX-TB`]},{id:`respiratory-extrapulmonary-tb-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate ileocaecal tuberculosis from Crohn's disease.`,openingLines:[`Ileocaecal TB and Crohn's disease look alike clinically, endoscopically and on imaging, and misdiagnosis in either direction is harmful in India.`,`INDEX-TB advises tissue diagnosis and, where doubt persists, an ATT trial with objective endoscopic reassessment.`],answer:[{heading:`Clinical`,points:[`TB: fever, night sweats, ascites, pulmonary TB; Crohn's: perianal disease, diarrhoea, extraintestinal features.`]},{heading:`Endoscopy`,points:[`TB: transverse ulcers, patulous ileocaecal valve, fewer than 4 segments; Crohn's: longitudinal ulcers, aphthous ulcers, cobblestoning, skip lesions.`]},{heading:`Histology and microbiology`,points:[`TB: large confluent caseating granulomas, AFB, Xpert or culture positive; Crohn's: small non-caseating granulomas.`]},{heading:`Imaging`,points:[`TB: necrotic nodes, ascites, short segment; Crohn's: comb sign, fibrofatty proliferation, long segments.`]},{heading:`If still uncertain`,points:[`ATT trial with clinical and endoscopic response at 2-3 months; never give steroids before excluding TB.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column comparison table of ileocaecal TB versus Crohn's disease`],markSplit:[{part:`Clinical and endoscopic differences`,marks:2},{part:`Histology and imaging`,marks:2},{part:`Approach when uncertain`,marks:1}],keywords:[`ileocaecal TB`,`Crohn's disease`,`transverse ulcers`,`caseating granuloma`,`comb sign`,`ATT trial`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Tuberculosis`,`Index-TB Guidelines: Guidelines on extra-pulmonary tuberculosis for India, ICMR and Central TB Division, 2016`,`Technical and Operational Guidelines for TB Control in India, NTEP, MoHFW, 2023`,`WHO consolidated guidelines on tuberculosis, Module 3: Diagnosis, 2024`,`Thwaites GE et al. Dexamethasone for tuberculous meningitis, N Engl J Med 2004`,`Donovan J et al. ACT HIV trial, N Engl J Med 2023`,`Mayosi BM et al. IMPI trial, N Engl J Med 2014`,`Marais S et al. Tuberculous meningitis: a uniform case definition, Lancet Infect Dis 2010`]}),i.push({id:`respiratory-tb-special-situations`,title:`TB in special situations: HIV, diabetes, pregnancy, liver and kidney disease, and ATT adverse effects`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`TB in **special situations** needs the standard **2HRZE/4HRE** backbone adjusted for the host: **ART within 2 weeks** with rifampicin-compatible drugs (**dolutegravir 50 mg twice daily**) and **cotrimoxazole** in HIV, **bidirectional TB-diabetes screening**, unchanged first-line drugs in **pregnancy**, **thrice-weekly pyrazinamide and ethambutol** in renal failure, stepwise **reintroduction after drug-induced liver injury**, and **nutritional support** (Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana, RATIONS trial) for the undernourished.`,keywords:[`HIV-TB`,`TB-DM`,`bidirectional screening`,`ART timing`,`dolutegravir`,`rifabutin`,`cotrimoxazole prophylaxis`,`IRIS`,`DILI`,`drug-induced hepatitis`,`rechallenge`,`TB in pregnancy`,`renal dosing`,`Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana`,`RATIONS trial`,`Ni-kshay Mitra`,`pyridoxine`,`ATT adverse effects`],sections:[{heading:`Why special situations matter: burden and risk factors`,points:[`India has the **world's largest TB burden**, about a quarter of global cases; **undernutrition is the largest population-attributable risk factor** for TB in India, ahead of alcohol, smoking, diabetes and HIV. [WHO Global TB Report 2024]`,`**HIV** raises the risk of TB about **18-fold**, and TB is the **leading cause of death in people with HIV**. [WHO 2024]`,`**Diabetes triples the risk of TB**, worsens outcomes (delayed culture conversion, failure, relapse, death), and TB worsens glycaemic control. [Harrison 22e]`,`Indian studies show **diabetes in roughly 15-25% of people with TB**, many undiagnosed until TB screening. [NTEP-NPNCD Framework 2017]`,`**Pregnancy and the postpartum period** raise TB risk and TB in pregnancy increases maternal death, prematurity and low birth weight. [WHO 2022]`,`**Chronic liver and kidney disease, alcohol use and old age** increase drug toxicity, so the regimen must be adapted rather than abandoned. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pharmacology behind the adjustments`,points:[`**Rifampicin is a potent inducer of CYP3A4, CYP2C9, UGT1A1 and P-glycoprotein** - it lowers levels of protease inhibitors, dolutegravir, oral contraceptives, warfarin, sulfonylureas, azoles, methadone and corticosteroids. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Rifabutin** is a weaker inducer and is used in place of rifampicin when **boosted protease inhibitors** are essential. [WHO 2021]`,`**Isoniazid** is acetylated by **NAT2**; slow acetylators (common in Indians) have higher levels and more neuropathy and hepatotoxicity; it also causes **pyridoxine deficiency**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Isoniazid, rifampicin and pyrazinamide are hepatotoxic**; pyrazinamide's toxicity is dose-related and the least likely to be tolerated on rechallenge. [ATS 2006]`,`**Ethambutol and pyrazinamide are renally excreted**, so they accumulate in CKD; isoniazid and rifampicin are hepatically cleared and need no renal adjustment. [WHO 2022]`,`**Streptomycin crosses the placenta** and causes fetal eighth-nerve damage - the only first-line drug avoided in pregnancy. [WHO 2022]`]},{heading:`HIV-associated TB: presentation and diagnosis`,points:[`With **CD4 over 350**, TB looks like typical cavitary disease; with **low CD4** it is often **smear-negative, extrapulmonary, disseminated, with lower-zone infiltrates or a normal chest X-ray**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Screen every PLHIV at every visit with the **WHO four-symptom screen** (current cough, fever, weight loss, night sweats), plus chest X-ray or CRP where available. [WHO 2021]`,`Test with **NAAT (Xpert Ultra or Truenat)** as the initial test; add **urine LF-LAM** if CD4 is 200 or less, the patient is seriously ill, or an inpatient with symptoms. [WHO 2024]`,`Every TB patient is offered **HIV testing** (single-window ICTC services); every PLHIV without TB is offered **TB preventive treatment** - see the TB infection and TPT topic. [NTEP 2023]`,`**TB-IRIS**: worsening fever, nodes or infiltrates after ART starts, usually in the first 2-8 weeks, commoner with **low CD4 and early ART**. [WHO 2021]`]},{heading:`HIV-associated TB: treatment`,points:[`Treat TB with the **same 6-month daily 2HRZE/4HRE regimen**; give pyridoxine. [NTEP 2023]`,`**Start ART within 2 weeks of ATT** regardless of CD4 count; in **TB meningitis delay ART 4-8 weeks**. [WHO 2021]`,`Preferred ART: **TLD (tenofovir, lamivudine, dolutegravir)** with an **extra dolutegravir 50 mg dose 12 hours later (50 mg BD)** while on rifampicin and for **2 weeks after** rifampicin stops. [NACO 2021]`,`**Efavirenz 600 mg** can be used with rifampicin without dose change; **boosted lopinavir or atazanavir** need **rifabutin 150 mg daily** instead of rifampicin. [WHO 2021]`,`**Cotrimoxazole preventive therapy (960 mg once daily)** for all HIV-TB patients, which reduces mortality. [NACO 2021]`,`**Prednisone 1.5 mg/kg/day for 2 weeks then 0.75 mg/kg/day for 2 weeks** reduces paradoxical TB-IRIS when ART starts within 30 days in patients with CD4 of 100 or less (PredART), except in rifampicin resistance or Kaposi sarcoma. [PredART NEJM 2018]`,`Moderate IRIS is managed with **NSAIDs or prednisolone** while **continuing both ATT and ART**. [WHO 2021]`]},{heading:`TB and diabetes`,points:[`**Bidirectional screening** under the **NTEP-NP-NCD collaborative framework**: every TB patient is tested for diabetes (RBS, FBS or HbA1c) and every person with diabetes is screened for TB symptoms at each visit. [NTEP-NPNCD Framework 2017]`,`Interpret glucose with care: **TB causes stress hyperglycaemia**, so re-test after 2-3 months of ATT before labelling new diabetes. [WHO-IUATLD 2011]`,`**Insulin** is preferred when glucose is markedly high or the patient is sick; **metformin** is suitable for most (watch lactic acidosis and GI upset), and DPP-4 inhibitors have little interaction. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**Rifampicin lowers sulfonylurea levels** - doses may need increasing and then decreasing when rifampicin stops. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`The ATT regimen and duration are **unchanged** in diabetes under NTEP; extension is individualised for slow responders. [NTEP 2023]`,`Diabetes adds to **isoniazid neuropathy** - give pyridoxine and examine feet. [NTEP 2023]`]},{heading:`Pregnancy, breastfeeding and the newborn`,points:[`**H, R, Z and E are safe in pregnancy** and the standard 6-month regimen is used; give **pyridoxine 10-25 mg daily**. [WHO 2022]`,`**Avoid streptomycin and other aminoglycosides** (fetal ototoxicity); ethionamide is avoided in DR-TB. [WHO 2022]`,`**Rifampicin makes combined oral contraceptives unreliable** - advise a copper IUD, DMPA or barrier method. [WHO 2021]`,`**Breastfeeding should continue**; drug levels in milk are low and do not treat or harm the baby. [WHO 2022]`,`Newborn of a mother with infectious TB: exclude active TB, then give **TB preventive treatment (6 months isoniazid)** followed by **BCG**; mother wears a mask while infectious. [NTEP 2021]`,`Give **vitamin K to the newborn** when the mother received rifampicin late in pregnancy. [WHO 2022]`]},{heading:`Drug-induced liver injury (DILI): definition and stopping rules`,points:[`Check baseline **LFT** in those at risk: alcohol use, chronic hepatitis B or C, HIV, pregnancy and postpartum, elderly, malnutrition, pre-existing liver disease. [NTEP 2023]`,`**Stop hepatotoxic drugs (H, R, Z)** if **ALT is over 3 times ULN with symptoms** (nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain) or jaundice, or **over 5 times ULN without symptoms**. [ATS 2006]`,`**Bilirubin over 2-3 mg/dL** or clinical jaundice also mandates stopping. [ATS 2006]`,`While liver tests recover, if the patient is very ill or sputum positive, give a **non-hepatotoxic bridging regimen: ethambutol plus levofloxacin** (with streptomycin or amikacin if needed). [NTEP 2023]`,`Exclude **viral hepatitis A, B, C and E**, alcohol, and other hepatotoxins before blaming ATT. [ATS 2006]`,`Isolated mild ALT rise (under 3 times ULN) is common, usually transient, and needs only **monitoring**, not stopping. [ATS 2006]`]},{heading:`DILI: reintroduction`,points:[`Reintroduce when **ALT falls below 2 times ULN and bilirubin is normal**, one drug at a time with LFT checks. [NTEP 2023]`,`**Rifampicin first** (with ethambutol), at full dose; after **3-7 days add isoniazid**; recheck ALT. [ATS 2006]`,`If the injury was **severe (jaundice)**, do **not reintroduce pyrazinamide**; treat with **2HRE plus 7HRE (9 months total)**. [ATS 2006]`,`If rifampicin is the culprit, use a rifampicin-free regimen such as **H, E, levofloxacin for 12-18 months** under specialist care. [WHO 2022]`,`**Pre-existing chronic liver disease**: use fewer hepatotoxic drugs - for example **9 months of rifampicin, isoniazid and ethambutol (no pyrazinamide)**, or one hepatotoxic drug plus ethambutol and a fluoroquinolone in decompensated cirrhosis. [WHO 2022]`]},{heading:`Kidney disease and dialysis`,points:[`**Creatinine clearance under 30 mL/min or on haemodialysis**: **pyrazinamide 25 mg/kg and ethambutol 15 mg/kg three times a week**; isoniazid and rifampicin at usual daily doses. [WHO 2022]`,`Give the doses **after dialysis** on dialysis days. [ATS/CDC/IDSA 2016]`,`Give **pyridoxine** routinely - CKD and dialysis increase isoniazid neuropathy. [ATS/CDC/IDSA 2016]`,`**Avoid aminoglycosides**; if unavoidable, dose by levels and audiometry. [WHO 2022]`,`Renal transplant: rifampicin drastically lowers **tacrolimus, cyclosporine and steroid levels** - increase immunosuppressant doses with level monitoring, or use rifabutin. [ATS/CDC/IDSA 2016]`]},{heading:`Other ATT adverse effects and their management`,points:[`**Peripheral neuropathy (isoniazid)**: prevent with pyridoxine 10 mg daily; treat with **pyridoxine 50-100 mg daily**. [NTEP 2023]`,`**Optic neuritis (ethambutol)**: reduced acuity and **red-green colour loss**; stop ethambutol and refer; test colour vision monthly in children and CKD. [WHO 2022]`,`**Arthralgia and hyperuricaemia (pyrazinamide)**: NSAIDs; acute gout needs specialist review. [WHO 2022]`,`**Rifampicin**: orange urine and tears (stains soft lenses), flu-like syndrome, **thrombocytopenia and haemolysis** - immune reactions mean rifampicin must never be restarted. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cutaneous reactions**: itch without rash - antihistamine and continue; **rash - stop all drugs**, then rechallenge one at a time; **SJS/TEN** - never rechallenge the culprit. [WHO 2022]`,`**GI upset** is commonest: give drugs with a light meal or at bedtime and exclude hepatitis. [NTEP 2023]`]},{heading:`Nutrition and social support`,points:[`**Undernutrition (BMI under 18.5)** doubles TB mortality and raises relapse; all patients get **nutritional assessment at diagnosis**. [NTEP 2023]`,`**Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana** pays **Rs 1000 per month** by direct benefit transfer for the duration of treatment (raised from Rs 500 in November 2024). [MoHFW 2024]`,`**Energy-dense nutritional supplementation** is given for the first 2 months to underweight patients (BMI under 18.5) under the 2024 NTEP scheme. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**Pradhan Mantri TB Mukt Bharat Abhiyaan (2022)**: **Ni-kshay Mitra** donors provide monthly food baskets and support. [MoHFW 2022]`,`**RATIONS trial (Jharkhand, Lancet 2023)**: food baskets to **household contacts reduced TB incidence by about 40-50%**, and early weight gain in patients was linked to **lower mortality**. [Lancet 2023]`,`Screen and treat **alcohol use and tobacco**; refer to tobacco cessation (quitline 1800-11-2356) and de-addiction services. [NTEP 2023]`]},{heading:`Inpatient care and red flags`,points:[`Admit for **jaundice or hepatic encephalopathy**, severe IRIS, **respiratory failure**, BMI under 14 or inability to walk, SJS/TEN, and uncontrolled diabetes with ketosis. [NTEP 2023]`,`Severely undernourished patients need **refeeding precautions** (phosphate, potassium, thiamine). [WHO 2013]`,`**Differentiated TB care** (2024): triage every patient at diagnosis for severity - BMI, oedema, respiratory rate, SpO2 and ability to stand - and admit high-risk patients. [NTEP 2024]`,`In fulminant hepatitis, coordinate with hepatology and give the **non-hepatotoxic bridging regimen**. [ATS 2006]`]},{heading:`Follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Review **every 2-4 weeks in the intensive phase** in high-risk groups, checking symptoms of hepatitis, weight and adherence. [NTEP 2023]`,`**LFT monthly** in chronic liver disease, HIV, alcohol use and after DILI rechallenge. [ATS/CDC/IDSA 2016]`,`**HbA1c every 3 months** in TB-DM; **CD4 and viral load** per NACO schedule in HIV. [NACO 2021]`,`Monthly weight and BMI; a **weight gain of 5% or more in 2 months** predicts better outcome. [Lancet 2023]`,`After cure, follow up for **post-TB lung disease** and recurrence - see the NTM and post-TB lung disease topic. [NTEP 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**NTEP 2024 differentiated TB care** with severity triage at diagnosis and admission of high-risk patients. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana raised to Rs 1000/month and energy-dense nutrition for underweight patients (2024)**. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**WHO 2024** recommends **urine LF-LAM** for PLHIV with CD4 200 or less or serious illness, and Xpert Ultra as the initial test. [WHO 2024]`,`**WHO 2022 Module 4 update**: first-line regimen unchanged in pregnancy, with explicit guidance on renal and hepatic modifications. [WHO 2022]`,`**RATIONS trial (2023)** provided the trial evidence behind food support for patients and contacts. [Lancet 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**ART within 2 weeks** - except **TBM, 4-8 weeks** - is the most asked fact. [WHO 2021]`,`**Dolutegravir 50 mg BD with rifampicin**, continued for 2 weeks after rifampicin stops. [NACO 2021]`,`DILI stopping rule **'3 with symptoms, 5 without'** and reintroduction order **R, then H, Z last or never**. [ATS 2006]`,`Renal dosing: **'Z and E thrice weekly; H and R unchanged'**. [WHO 2022]`,`**Streptomycin is the only first-line drug contraindicated in pregnancy**. [WHO 2022]`]}],tables:[{heading:`First-line ATT and key adjunct drugs: dosing and adverse effects`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Isoniazid`,`5 mg/kg oral daily (max 300 mg)`,`6 months`,`Hepatitis, peripheral neuropathy, drug-induced lupus; give pyridoxine`],[`Rifampicin`,`10 mg/kg oral daily (max 600 mg)`,`6 months`,`Hepatitis, orange secretions, flu-like illness, thrombocytopenia; CYP induction`],[`Pyrazinamide`,`25 mg/kg oral daily; CKD 25 mg/kg thrice weekly`,`2 months`,`Hepatitis (dose-related), hyperuricaemia, arthralgia`],[`Ethambutol`,`15 mg/kg oral daily; CKD 15 mg/kg thrice weekly`,`6 months`,`Optic neuritis, red-green colour loss`],[`Rifabutin`,`150 mg oral daily with boosted PI`,`Replaces rifampicin`,`Uveitis, neutropenia; fewer interactions`],[`Dolutegravir (with rifampicin)`,`50 mg oral BD`,`Until 2 weeks after rifampicin stops`,`Insomnia, weight gain`],[`Cotrimoxazole`,`960 mg oral OD`,`Throughout ATT and beyond per CD4`,`Rash, SJS, cytopenias, hyperkalaemia`],[`Pyridoxine`,`10 mg daily (prevention); 50-100 mg (treatment of neuropathy)`,`Throughout`,`Very high doses cause sensory neuropathy`]]},{heading:`Regimen modifications by situation`,columns:[`Situation`,`Key change`,`Watch for`],rows:[[`HIV`,`Same ATT; ART within 2 weeks (TBM 4-8 weeks); DTG 50 mg BD; CPT`,`IRIS, drug interactions`],[`Diabetes`,`Same ATT; bidirectional screening; insulin if uncontrolled`,`Neuropathy, slow conversion, sulfonylurea failure`],[`Pregnancy`,`Same ATT; no streptomycin; pyridoxine`,`OCP failure; newborn TPT then BCG`],[`CKD / dialysis`,`Z and E thrice weekly after dialysis; H, R unchanged`,`Neuropathy, ototoxicity`],[`Chronic liver disease`,`Fewer hepatotoxic drugs (e.g. 9 HRE, no Z)`,`Monthly LFT, decompensation`],[`DILI on ATT`,`Stop H, R, Z; bridge with E + Lfx; reintroduce R then H`,`Omit Z if severe; 9 months HRE`]]},{heading:`DILI: criteria and actions`,columns:[`Finding`,`Action`],rows:[[`ALT under 3 times ULN, no symptoms`,`Continue ATT, repeat LFT in 1-2 weeks`],[`ALT over 3 times ULN with symptoms or jaundice`,`Stop H, R, Z`],[`ALT over 5 times ULN without symptoms`,`Stop H, R, Z`],[`Patient ill or infectious after stopping`,`Bridge with ethambutol plus levofloxacin (plus aminoglycoside if needed)`],[`ALT under 2 times ULN and bilirubin normal`,`Reintroduce rifampicin, then isoniazid after 3-7 days`],[`Severe DILI with jaundice`,`Do not reintroduce pyrazinamide; give 9 months HRE`]]},{heading:`Rifampicin drug interactions in practice`,columns:[`Drug`,`Effect of rifampicin`,`What to do`],rows:[[`Dolutegravir`,`Levels fall`,`Increase to 50 mg BD`],[`Boosted protease inhibitors`,`Levels fall sharply`,`Use rifabutin 150 mg daily`],[`Combined oral contraceptive`,`Contraceptive failure`,`Copper IUD, DMPA or barrier`],[`Warfarin`,`INR falls`,`Frequent INR; dose increase`],[`Sulfonylureas`,`Glucose rises`,`Increase dose or use insulin/metformin`],[`Prednisolone`,`Levels halve`,`Double the steroid dose`],[`Tacrolimus / cyclosporine`,`Levels fall, rejection risk`,`Level monitoring or rifabutin`]]}],redFlags:[`Jaundice, persistent vomiting or right upper quadrant pain on ATT (stop H, R, Z and check LFT)`,`Confusion, asterixis or bleeding on ATT (acute liver failure)`,`Mucosal involvement, blistering or skin peeling (SJS/TEN)`,`Fall in visual acuity or colour vision on ethambutol`,`New fever, breathlessness or enlarging nodes soon after starting ART with low CD4 (IRIS versus new infection)`,`BMI under 14, inability to stand, or SpO2 under 94% at diagnosis (high-risk TB, admit)`,`Diabetic ketoacidosis or uncontrolled hyperglycaemia during ATT`],pearls:[`**ART within 2 weeks of ATT**, but **4-8 weeks in TBM**.`,`**Dolutegravir 50 mg BD** while on rifampicin and for 2 weeks after.`,`**Bidirectional screening**: every TB patient for diabetes, every diabetic for TB.`,`DILI: **3 with symptoms, 5 without** - stop H, R, Z.`,`Reintroduce **rifampicin first, isoniazid next, pyrazinamide last or never**.`,`CKD: **Z and E thrice weekly after dialysis**; H and R unchanged.`,`**Streptomycin** is the only first-line drug banned in pregnancy.`,`Rifampicin **doubles the steroid requirement** and defeats the pill.`,`**RATIONS**: food for families cut TB incidence by about 40-50%.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-tb-special-situations-q1`,stem:`A 32-year-old man with newly diagnosed pulmonary TB is HIV-positive with CD4 180 cells/microlitre. He starts 2HRZE. Which ART plan is correct?`,options:[`Start TLD after completing ATT`,`Start TLD within 2 weeks with an extra dolutegravir 50 mg dose 12 hours later`,`Start TLD at 8 weeks because CD4 is under 200`,`Start lopinavir-ritonavir with rifampicin at standard doses`],answer:1,explanation:`WHO and NACO advise ART within 2 weeks of ATT regardless of CD4 for non-meningeal TB, and rifampicin halves dolutegravir levels, so dolutegravir is given 50 mg twice daily. Delaying until after ATT or to 8 weeks increases mortality, and boosted lopinavir cannot be given with rifampicin at standard doses - rifabutin is needed instead.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-tb-special-situations-q2`,stem:`Three weeks into 2HRZE, a patient has ALT 280 U/L (ULN 40), bilirubin 3.5 mg/dL and vomiting. Sputum was smear-positive. What is the best immediate management?`,options:[`Stop isoniazid, rifampicin and pyrazinamide and give ethambutol with levofloxacin until liver tests recover`,`Continue all drugs and repeat LFT in 2 weeks`,`Stop only pyrazinamide and continue the rest`,`Stop all drugs and restart all four together when ALT is normal`],answer:0,explanation:`Symptomatic ALT over 3 times ULN with jaundice is DILI, so all three hepatotoxic drugs are stopped. A smear-positive patient should not be left untreated, so a non-hepatotoxic bridge (ethambutol plus levofloxacin) is given. Continuing risks liver failure, stopping only pyrazinamide is unsafe because the culprit is unknown, and restarting all four together cannot identify the culprit.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-tb-special-situations-q3`,stem:`A man on maintenance haemodialysis is diagnosed with pulmonary TB. Which dosing schedule is correct?`,options:[`All four drugs at half dose daily`,`Isoniazid and rifampicin thrice weekly; pyrazinamide and ethambutol daily`,`Avoid pyrazinamide and ethambutol and give 9 months of isoniazid and rifampicin`,`Isoniazid and rifampicin daily; pyrazinamide 25 mg/kg and ethambutol 15 mg/kg thrice weekly after dialysis`],answer:3,explanation:`Pyrazinamide and ethambutol are renally cleared, so in CrCl under 30 or dialysis they are given at full dose three times weekly after dialysis, while hepatically cleared isoniazid and rifampicin stay daily. Halving doses lowers peak levels and efficacy, the reversed schedule makes no pharmacological sense, and dropping both drugs is unnecessary.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-tb-special-situations-q4`,stem:`A 26-year-old woman, 20 weeks pregnant, has smear-positive pulmonary TB. Which statement is correct?`,options:[`Delay ATT until after delivery`,`Use streptomycin in place of ethambutol to protect vision`,`Give standard 2HRZE/4HRE with pyridoxine; breastfeeding can continue after delivery`,`Omit pyrazinamide and treat for 9 months`],answer:2,explanation:`First-line H, R, Z and E are safe in pregnancy, and untreated TB is far more harmful than the drugs; pyridoxine is added, and breastfeeding continues. Streptomycin is the one drug contraindicated because it causes fetal ototoxicity, delaying treatment raises maternal and fetal risk, and current WHO guidance does not require omitting pyrazinamide.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-tb-special-situations-q5`,stem:`The RATIONS trial in Jharkhand showed that providing food baskets to household contacts of TB patients:`,options:[`Had no effect on TB incidence but improved weight`,`Reduced TB incidence among contacts by about 40-50%`,`Increased drug-resistant TB among contacts`,`Replaced the need for TB preventive treatment`],answer:1,explanation:`RATIONS (Lancet 2023) was a cluster-randomised trial in which nutritional support to household contacts cut incident TB by roughly 40-50%, and patient weight gain was linked to lower mortality. It did not replace TPT, and there was no signal of increased drug resistance; the weight-only result misstates the main finding.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-tb-special-situations-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Discuss the management of HIV-TB co-infection. Add a note on the management of drug-induced hepatitis during antitubercular treatment.`,openingLines:[`TB is the leading cause of death in people with HIV, and HIV raises the risk of TB about 18-fold.`,`Management rests on early diagnosis, the standard ATT regimen, ART within 2 weeks with rifampicin-compatible drugs, cotrimoxazole prophylaxis and watchfulness for IRIS and hepatotoxicity.`],answer:[{heading:`Diagnosis in HIV`,points:[`Four-symptom screen at every visit; Xpert Ultra as initial test; urine LF-LAM if CD4 200 or less or seriously ill.`,`Atypical presentation at low CD4: smear-negative, extrapulmonary, normal CXR.`]},{heading:`ATT and ART`,points:[`2HRZE/4HRE daily with pyridoxine.`,`ART within 2 weeks; TBM 4-8 weeks.`,`TLD with dolutegravir 50 mg BD; efavirenz acceptable; rifabutin with boosted PIs.`]},{heading:`Prophylaxis and IRIS`,points:[`Cotrimoxazole 960 mg OD for all.`,`IRIS: continue ATT and ART; NSAIDs or prednisolone; prophylactic prednisone if CD4 100 or less (PredART).`]},{heading:`DILI - recognition`,points:[`ALT over 3 times ULN with symptoms or jaundice, or over 5 times without.`,`Exclude viral hepatitis and alcohol.`]},{heading:`DILI - management`,points:[`Stop H, R, Z; bridge with ethambutol plus levofloxacin if ill or infectious.`,`Reintroduce when ALT under 2 times ULN: rifampicin, then isoniazid after 3-7 days.`,`Omit pyrazinamide after severe DILI; 9 months HRE.`]},{heading:`Programme support`,points:[`Single-window ICTC-NTEP services, Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana Rs 1000/month, TPT for PLHIV without TB.`]}],mustDraw:[`DILI management flow chart from stopping to reintroduction`,`Table of rifampicin interactions with ART`],markSplit:[{part:`Diagnosis in HIV`,marks:2},{part:`ATT, ART timing and interactions`,marks:3},{part:`CPT and IRIS`,marks:1},{part:`Drug-induced hepatitis`,marks:4}],keywords:[`HIV-TB`,`ART timing`,`dolutegravir`,`rifabutin`,`CPT`,`IRIS`,`DILI`,`reintroduction`]},{id:`respiratory-tb-special-situations-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on TB and diabetes: bidirectional screening and management.`,openingLines:[`Diabetes triples the risk of TB and worsens TB outcomes, while TB worsens glycaemic control.`,`India's NTEP-NP-NCD collaborative framework mandates bidirectional screening of both conditions.`],answer:[{heading:`Burden`,points:[`Diabetes in roughly 15-25% of Indian TB patients; many newly detected.`]},{heading:`Bidirectional screening`,points:[`Every TB patient: RBS, FBS or HbA1c; every diabetic: TB symptom screen each visit, NAAT if positive.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Standard ATT; insulin if uncontrolled; metformin suitable; rifampicin lowers sulfonylureas.`,`Pyridoxine for neuropathy; HbA1c every 3 months.`]},{heading:`Outcomes`,points:[`Delayed conversion, failure, relapse, death; recheck glucose after 2-3 months for stress hyperglycaemia.`]}],mustDraw:[`Bidirectional screening flow chart between NTEP and NP-NCD clinics`],markSplit:[{part:`Burden and interaction`,marks:1},{part:`Bidirectional screening`,marks:2},{part:`Management`,marks:2}],keywords:[`TB-DM`,`bidirectional screening`,`NP-NCD`,`insulin`,`rifampicin`,`HbA1c`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Tuberculosis and HIV`,`WHO consolidated guidelines on tuberculosis, Module 4: Treatment, 2022 update and 2025 consolidation`,`Technical and Operational Guidelines for TB Control in India, NTEP, MoHFW, 2023`,`National Guidelines for HIV Care and Treatment, NACO, 2021`,`National Framework for Joint TB-Diabetes Collaborative Activities, NTEP and NPCDCS (NP-NCD), 2017`,`ATS Official Statement: Hepatotoxicity of Antituberculosis Therapy, 2006; ATS/CDC/IDSA Treatment of Drug-Susceptible TB, 2016`,`Bhargava A et al. RATIONS trial, Lancet 2023`,`Meintjes G et al. PredART trial, N Engl J Med 2018`]}),i.push({id:`respiratory-ntm-post-tb`,title:`Non-tuberculous mycobacteria and post-tuberculosis lung disease`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`**Non-tuberculous mycobacteria (NTM)** are environmental mycobacteria (chiefly **M. avium complex and M. abscessus**) that cause chronic lung disease in damaged lungs, diagnosed only when **clinical, radiological and microbiological criteria (ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020)** are all met and treated with **macrolide-based multidrug regimens for 12 months after culture conversion**; **post-tuberculosis lung disease (PTLD)** is chronic respiratory abnormality attributable at least in part to previous TB - airway, parenchymal, pleural and vascular - that needs **assessment at the end of treatment, rehabilitation and prevention of misdiagnosis as recurrent TB**.`,keywords:[`NTM`,`atypical mycobacteria`,`MOTT`,`MAC`,`Mycobacterium avium complex`,`M. abscessus`,`M. kansasii`,`Lady Windermere syndrome`,`Runyon classification`,`amikacin liposome inhalation`,`PTLD`,`post-TB lung disease`,`post-TB sequelae`,`destroyed lung`,`tuberculous bronchiectasis`,`MPT64`,`pulmonary rehabilitation`],sections:[{heading:`Definitions and epidemiology`,points:[`**NTM** are mycobacteria other than the *M. tuberculosis* complex and *M. leprae*; more than **190 species** exist in soil and water, and they are **not transmitted person to person** in general (possible exception: M. abscessus in cystic fibrosis). [Harrison 22e]`,`NTM lung disease is rising worldwide, especially in **older adults, women with bronchiectasis, COPD and post-TB lungs**; MAC is the commonest pathogen globally. [ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020]`,`In India NTM are isolated in a small but important proportion of **smear-positive, NAAT-negative** samples and among patients labelled 'treatment failure' or 'presumptive MDR-TB'; many are **misdiagnosed and treated as TB**. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Post-TB lung disease (PTLD)** is defined as **evidence of chronic respiratory abnormality, with or without symptoms, attributable at least in part to previous pulmonary TB**. [International Post-TB Symposium 2019]`,`About **half of people cured of pulmonary TB have abnormal lung function**, and TB survivors have about **three times the mortality** of the general population, much of it respiratory. [Lancet Respir Med 2021]`,`With about 2.5 million TB patients treated each year, India has the **largest population of TB survivors** and so the largest PTLD burden in the world. [WHO Global TB Report 2024]`]},{heading:`Organisms and risk factors`,points:[`**Slow growers**: **M. avium complex (M. avium, M. intracellulare, M. chimaera)**, **M. kansasii**, M. xenopi, M. malmoense. [ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020]`,`**Rapid growers (colonies in under 7 days)**: **M. abscessus**, M. fortuitum, M. chelonae - also cause skin and post-surgical wound infections (laparoscopy ports, cosmetic procedures). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Runyon classification**: I photochromogens (**M. kansasii**, M. marinum), II scotochromogens (M. scrofulaceum, M. gordonae), III non-chromogens (**MAC**), IV rapid growers. [Harrison 22e]`,`Host risks: **bronchiectasis, previous TB, COPD, cystic fibrosis, silicosis, GERD**, thoracic skeletal abnormalities, and immune defects (**HIV with CD4 under 50, anti-TNF therapy, anti-interferon-gamma autoantibodies**). [ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020]`,`Risk factors for PTLD: **extensive or cavitary disease, delayed diagnosis, multiple TB episodes, MDR-TB, smoking, biomass smoke, HIV and diabetes**. [PTLD Clinical Standards 2021]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`NTM are inhaled from **aerosols of water** (showers, hot tubs, municipal supply biofilms) and soil; they colonise damaged airways where mucociliary clearance is poor. [Harrison 22e]`,`Host defence depends on the **IL-12/interferon-gamma axis** - defects in this axis (genetic or autoantibodies) explain disseminated NTM in apparently healthy adults. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nodular-bronchiectatic MAC** in slender postmenopausal women (**Lady Windermere syndrome**) is linked to pectus excavatum, scoliosis, mitral valve prolapse and habitual cough suppression. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PTLD** arises because TB heals by **fibrosis and cavitation**: matrix metalloproteinases destroy lung matrix, granulomas fibrose, and airways distort - causing bronchiectasis, obstruction and restriction. [PTLD Clinical Standards 2021]`,`Residual cavities are colonised by **Aspergillus (aspergilloma, chronic pulmonary aspergillosis)** and NTM, and **bronchial artery hypertrophy** causes haemoptysis; **Rasmussen aneurysm** is a pulmonary artery aneurysm in a cavity wall. [Harrison 22e]`,`Long-standing destruction leads to **hypoxaemia, pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale**. [PTLD Clinical Standards 2021]`]},{heading:`Classification: NTM phenotypes and the PTLD spectrum`,points:[`NTM lung disease patterns: **fibrocavitary** (older male smokers, upper-lobe cavities like TB), **nodular-bronchiectatic** (middle lobe and lingula, tree-in-bud), **hypersensitivity-like ('hot tub lung')** and **disseminated** (advanced HIV). [ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020]`,`PTLD **airway** disease: obstructive airflow limitation, **traction bronchiectasis**, tracheobronchial stenosis and broncholithiasis. [PTLD Clinical Standards 2021]`,`PTLD **parenchymal** disease: residual cavities, fibrosis, volume loss and **destroyed lung** (a whole lobe or lung non-functional). [PTLD Clinical Standards 2021]`,`PTLD **pleural** disease: pleural thickening, **fibrothorax**, chronic empyema and bronchopleural fistula. [PTLD Clinical Standards 2021]`,`PTLD **vascular** disease: **pulmonary hypertension**, bronchial artery hypertrophy and Rasmussen aneurysm. [PTLD Clinical Standards 2021]`,`**Complications** that cut across: chronic pulmonary aspergillosis, NTM infection, **TB recurrence** and lung cancer. [PTLD Clinical Standards 2021]`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`NTM lung disease: **chronic productive cough, fatigue, weight loss, low-grade fever and haemoptysis** over months - indistinguishable from TB on symptoms. [ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020]`,`Suspect NTM when **sputum is AFB smear-positive but NAAT negative for MTB**, when 'TB' fails to respond despite adherence and susceptible DST, or in recurrent 'TB' with bronchiectasis. [ICMR 2022]`,`PTLD: **persistent breathlessness, cough and sputum after cure**, recurrent chest infections, haemoptysis, and reduced exercise tolerance. [PTLD Clinical Standards 2021]`,`Many PTLD patients are **labelled COPD or 'TB relapse'** and given repeated empirical ATT without microbiological proof. [PTLD Clinical Standards 2021]`,`Disseminated MAC in advanced HIV: **fever, night sweats, weight loss, diarrhoea, hepatosplenomegaly and anaemia**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Clubbing** suggests bronchiectasis, chronic suppuration or associated lung cancer - not TB itself. [OHCM 11e]`,`**Tracheal shift towards a fibrosed upper lobe**, flattening of the chest, crowded ribs and coarse crackles indicate post-TB fibrosis. [OHCM 11e]`,`**Amphoric breath sounds** over a large cavity; dull percussion and absent breath sounds with fibrothorax. [OHCM 11e]`,`**Loud P2, raised JVP, parasternal heave and pedal oedema** indicate cor pulmonale. [Harrison 22e]`,`A **slender woman with pectus excavatum or scoliosis** and chronic cough suggests nodular-bronchiectatic MAC. [Harrison 22e]`,`Record **SpO2 at rest and on exertion** and BMI - both guide oxygen and rehabilitation. [PTLD Clinical Standards 2021]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Recurrent TB versus PTLD**: recurrence needs **NAAT and culture positive for MTB**; residual imaging changes and old scars alone are not recurrence. [NTEP 2023]`,`**Xpert can remain positive from dead bacilli for years** after successful treatment; a positive 'trace' or low result in a previously treated person needs **culture confirmation**. [WHO 2024]`,`**NTM versus TB**: NAAT negative for MTB, culture grows mycobacteria that are **MPT64 antigen negative**, and line probe or species identification confirms NTM. [ICMR 2022]`,`**Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis**: cavity with a fungal ball or pericavitary thickening, **Aspergillus IgG positive** - see the Aspergillosis topic. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**COPD versus post-TB obstruction**: post-TB obstruction occurs in non-smokers with radiological sequelae and may respond poorly to bronchodilators. [PTLD Clinical Standards 2021]`,`**Lung cancer in a scar**: a new mass, rising cavity wall thickness or persistent haemoptysis in a smoker needs CT and biopsy. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA clinical criteria**: pulmonary symptoms, **nodular or cavitary opacities on chest X-ray, or bronchiectasis with multiple small nodules on HRCT**, and appropriate exclusion of other diagnoses. [ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020]`,`**Microbiological criteria**: positive culture from **at least two separate expectorated sputum samples**, or **one bronchial wash or lavage**, or a lung biopsy with granulomas or AFB plus a positive culture from biopsy, sputum or wash. [ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020]`,`**Species identification** (molecular line probe, sequencing) is essential - M. gordonae is usually a contaminant, while MAC, M. kansasii and M. abscessus are true pathogens. [ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020]`,`**Susceptibility testing**: **macrolide and amikacin** for MAC; macrolide (including **inducible erm(41) resistance**) and amikacin for M. abscessus; **rifampicin** for M. kansasii. [ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020]`,`**HRCT** shows tree-in-bud nodules and bronchiectasis in the middle lobe and lingula (nodular-bronchiectatic) or thick-walled upper-lobe cavities (fibrocavitary). [Harrison 22e]`,`PTLD assessment at the **end of TB treatment**: symptoms, **spirometry**, **SpO2**, **6-minute walk test**, chest X-ray, and quality-of-life score (e.g. St George's Respiratory Questionnaire). [PTLD Clinical Standards 2021]`,`PTLD extended work-up: HRCT for bronchiectasis and cavities, **Aspergillus IgG**, sputum for NTM, **echocardiography** for pulmonary hypertension, and ABG if SpO2 is low. [PTLD Clinical Standards 2021]`]},{heading:`Treatment of NTM lung disease`,points:[`**Not every positive culture needs treatment**: weigh symptoms, progression, species and comorbidity; a period of **watchful waiting** is reasonable in mild nodular-bronchiectatic MAC. [ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020]`,`**Macrolide-susceptible nodular-bronchiectatic MAC**: **azithromycin 500 mg (or clarithromycin 1000 mg), rifampicin 600 mg and ethambutol 25 mg/kg, three times a week**. [ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020]`,`**Cavitary or severe MAC**: **daily azithromycin 250-500 mg, rifampicin 450-600 mg and ethambutol 15 mg/kg**, plus **IV amikacin or streptomycin for the first 2-3 months**. [ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020]`,`Continue MAC treatment for **at least 12 months after sputum culture conversion**; **never use a macrolide alone** - it selects resistance quickly. [ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020]`,`**Refractory MAC** (culture positive after 6 months): add **amikacin liposome inhalation suspension (ALIS) 590 mg once daily**. [ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020]`,`**M. kansasii**: **rifampicin, ethambutol and either isoniazid or azithromycin** for a fixed **12 months** - it behaves much like TB and responds well. [ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020]`,`**M. abscessus**: intensive phase of **IV amikacin plus imipenem or cefoxitin or tigecycline**, with a macrolide only if there is no inducible resistance, then oral/inhaled agents (clofazimine, linezolid, inhaled amikacin); cure rates are low and **surgery** may be needed. [ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020]`,`**Disseminated MAC in HIV**: clarithromycin or azithromycin plus ethambutol (with or without rifabutin) and prompt ART. [NACO 2021]`]},{heading:`Management of post-TB lung disease`,points:[`**Smoking and biomass smoke cessation** are the first intervention; offer brief advice and quitline referral (1800-11-2356). [PTLD Clinical Standards 2021]`,`**Pulmonary rehabilitation** (exercise training, education, breathing techniques) improves 6MWT and quality of life in PTLD - see the Pulmonary rehabilitation topic. [PTLD Clinical Standards 2021]`,`**Obstructive PTLD**: a trial of **long-acting bronchodilators (LAMA or LABA)**; use ICS cautiously because they raise the risk of **TB recurrence, NTM and pneumonia**. [PTLD Clinical Standards 2021]`,`**Bronchiectatic PTLD**: airway clearance, treatment of exacerbations based on sputum culture, and long-term macrolides only after **excluding NTM** (macrolide monotherapy breeds resistant MAC). [BTS 2019]`,`**Haemoptysis**: tranexamic acid for minor bleeds; **bronchial artery embolisation** for massive haemoptysis - see the Haemoptysis topic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chronic pulmonary aspergillosis**: **itraconazole 200 mg BD for at least 6 months**; surgery for simple aspergilloma with recurrent bleeding. [ERS/ESCMID 2016]`,`**Long-term oxygen** if resting PaO2 is 55 mmHg or less (or 56-59 with cor pulmonale), and **vaccination against influenza, pneumococcus and COVID-19**. [PTLD Clinical Standards 2021]`,`Severe localised destroyed lung with recurrent infection or bleeding may need **pneumonectomy or lobectomy** after optimisation. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**HIV**: disseminated MAC when CD4 is under 50; **primary prophylaxis is not needed if ART is started at once**. [NACO 2021]`,`**Anti-TNF and biologic therapy**: screen for latent TB and assess NTM in bronchiectasis before starting. [ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020]`,`**Pregnancy**: avoid aminoglycosides; azithromycin and ethambutol are acceptable; specialist care. [ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020]`,`**Children**: NTM cause **cervical lymphadenitis in children aged 1-5**, treated by **surgical excision** rather than ATT. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Elderly**: drug toxicity (ethambutol optic neuropathy, amikacin ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity, macrolide QT and hearing loss) needs closer monitoring. [ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Fibrocavitary MAC has **5-year mortality of about 25-40%** if untreated; nodular-bronchiectatic disease progresses slowly. [Harrison 22e]`,`**M. abscessus** has the poorest cure rates (often under 50%) and frequent relapse. [ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020]`,`PTLD complications: **recurrent infections, massive haemoptysis, chronic respiratory failure, cor pulmonale, CPA, lung cancer** and early death. [PTLD Clinical Standards 2021]`,`PTLD causes **lost productivity and catastrophic costs**, and a large share of the lifetime TB disease burden occurs after cure. [Lancet Respir Med 2021]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Refer suspected NTM to a **chest physician** with access to species identification and DST; do not start empirical anti-NTM therapy on a single sputum. [ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020]`,`On NTM therapy: **monthly sputum culture**, visual acuity and colour vision (ethambutol), audiometry and creatinine (amikacin), and ECG (macrolides with other QT drugs). [ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020]`,`PTLD: assess at **end of TB treatment** and again at **12 months**; NTEP also schedules post-treatment review every 6 months for 2 years. [PTLD Clinical Standards 2021]`,`Refer for **haemoptysis over 100-200 mL/day**, worsening hypoxaemia, suspected cancer, CPA or surgery assessment. [Harrison 22e]`,`Before any repeat ATT, insist on **microbiological confirmation (NAAT plus culture)** to avoid treating PTLD as recurrence. [NTEP 2023]`]},{heading:`Prevention and programme link`,points:[`The best prevention of PTLD is **early TB diagnosis** (active case finding, chest X-ray screening) and full adherence to treatment. [PTLD Clinical Standards 2021]`,`NTEP **post-treatment follow-up** and Ni-kshay records give an opportunity to screen survivors for symptoms and lung function. [NTEP 2023]`,`Patients with PTLD and obstruction can be linked to **NP-NCD chronic respiratory disease services** at Ayushman Arogya Mandirs. [MoHFW 2023]`,`Reduce exposure to **biomass smoke** (Ujjwala LPG) and tobacco in TB survivors. [MoHFW 2023]`,`NTM prevention is limited: **hot-water heaters set above 55 degrees C**, avoid hot tubs in bronchiectasis, and sterile technique in procedures. [ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020** NTM guideline: three-times-weekly regimen for non-cavitary MAC, **azithromycin preferred over clarithromycin**, ALIS for refractory MAC, and fixed 12-month regimens for M. kansasii. [ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020]`,`**Clinical standards for PTLD (Migliori et al., 2021)** recommend assessment of every patient at the end of TB treatment with spirometry, SpO2 and 6MWT. [PTLD Clinical Standards 2021]`,`The **2nd International Post-TB Symposium (2023)** widened the focus to **post-TB health and wellbeing**, including mental health, economic and cardiovascular consequences. [IJTLD 2023]`,`**WHO 2024** notes that molecular tests may stay positive after cure, and recommends culture to confirm recurrence in previously treated patients. [WHO 2024]`,`The **British Thoracic Society 2017** NTM guideline remains a widely cited companion reference. [BTS 2017]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Know the **NTM diagnostic criteria**: clinical plus radiological plus **two positive sputum cultures** (or one BAL). [ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020]`,`**Lady Windermere syndrome** = nodular-bronchiectatic MAC in the middle lobe and lingula of slender older women. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Never give macrolide monotherapy** for MAC; treat for **12 months after culture conversion**. [ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020]`,`**Smear positive, NAAT negative = think NTM**. [ICMR 2022]`,`PTLD definition: chronic respiratory abnormality attributable at least in part to previous TB - **not recurrence**. [International Post-TB Symposium 2019]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for NTM lung disease (adult)`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Azithromycin`,`500 mg oral thrice weekly (nodular) or 250-500 mg daily (cavitary)`,`Until 12 months after culture conversion`,`QT prolongation, hearing loss, GI upset; never as monotherapy`],[`Clarithromycin (alternative)`,`1000 mg thrice weekly or 500 mg BD`,`As above`,`More interactions (CYP3A4 inhibitor), bitter taste, QT`],[`Rifampicin`,`600 mg thrice weekly or 450-600 mg daily`,`As above`,`Hepatitis, interactions, lowers clarithromycin levels`],[`Ethambutol`,`25 mg/kg thrice weekly or 15 mg/kg daily`,`As above`,`Optic neuritis - monthly vision check`],[`Amikacin IV`,`15 mg/kg daily or 25 mg/kg thrice weekly`,`First 2-3 months in cavitary MAC or M. abscessus`,`Ototoxicity, nephrotoxicity; audiometry and creatinine`],[`Amikacin liposome inhalation (ALIS)`,`590 mg inhaled once daily`,`Added for refractory MAC`,`Dysphonia, cough, bronchospasm, allergic alveolitis`],[`Itraconazole (CPA in PTLD)`,`200 mg oral BD`,`At least 6 months`,`Hepatotoxicity, heart failure, interactions; check levels`]]},{heading:`NTM species and their behaviour`,columns:[`Species`,`Growth / Runyon group`,`Typical disease`,`Treatment backbone`],rows:[[`M. avium complex`,`Slow, non-chromogen (III)`,`Fibrocavitary, nodular-bronchiectatic, disseminated in HIV`,`Macrolide + rifampicin + ethambutol (± amikacin)`],[`M. kansasii`,`Slow, photochromogen (I)`,`Upper-lobe cavitary disease like TB`,`Rifampicin + ethambutol + isoniazid or azithromycin, 12 months`],[`M. abscessus`,`Rapid (IV)`,`Bronchiectasis, cystic fibrosis, skin and wound infection`,`IV amikacin + imipenem/cefoxitin/tigecycline ± macrolide`],[`M. fortuitum / M. chelonae`,`Rapid (IV)`,`Post-surgical and injection-site infection`,`Two active drugs by susceptibility; debridement`],[`M. gordonae`,`Slow, scotochromogen (II)`,`Usually a contaminant`,`Rarely treated`]]},{heading:`TB, NTM and PTLD - telling them apart`,columns:[`Feature`,`Active / recurrent TB`,`NTM lung disease`,`Post-TB lung disease`],rows:[[`Smear`,`Positive or negative`,`Often positive`,`Negative`],[`NAAT for MTB`,`Positive`,`Negative`,`Negative, or positive from dead bacilli (needs culture)`],[`Culture`,`MTB, MPT64 positive`,`Mycobacteria, MPT64 negative, species identified`,`Negative`],[`Imaging`,`New infiltrate or cavity`,`Nodular-bronchiectatic or fibrocavitary`,`Stable fibrosis, bronchiectasis, old cavities`],[`Treatment`,`ATT under NTEP`,`Species-specific multidrug regimen`,`Rehabilitation, bronchodilators, airway clearance`]]},{heading:`ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020 criteria for NTM lung disease`,columns:[`Domain`,`Requirement`],rows:[[`Clinical`,`Pulmonary or systemic symptoms`],[`Radiological`,`Nodular or cavitary opacities on CXR, or bronchiectasis with multiple small nodules on HRCT`],[`Exclusion`,`Appropriate exclusion of other diagnoses (TB, cancer, CPA)`],[`Microbiological`,`Positive culture from 2 or more separate sputa, or 1 bronchial wash/BAL, or biopsy with granulomas/AFB plus positive culture`]]},{heading:`PTLD assessment at the end of TB treatment`,columns:[`Test`,`Finding to look for`,`Action`],rows:[[`Symptom review and quality of life (SGRQ)`,`Breathlessness, cough, sputum, haemoptysis`,`Plan rehabilitation`],[`Spirometry`,`Obstructive, restrictive or mixed pattern`,`Bronchodilator trial if obstructive`],[`SpO2 at rest and exercise; 6MWT`,`Desaturation, reduced distance`,`Rehabilitation; oxygen assessment`],[`Chest X-ray / HRCT`,`Cavities, bronchiectasis, fibrosis, destroyed lung`,`Airway clearance; CPA and NTM work-up`],[`Echocardiography (if indicated)`,`Pulmonary hypertension`,`Oxygen; specialist referral`]]}],redFlags:[`Haemoptysis over 100-200 mL in 24 hours or with hypoxaemia`,`Resting SpO2 under 90% or signs of cor pulmonale`,`New mass or thickening cavity wall in a TB scar in a smoker (cancer)`,`Sputum smear-positive but MTB-NAAT negative in a patient about to start or continue ATT`,`Fever, weight loss and new cavity after TB cure (recurrence, NTM or CPA - needs culture)`,`Hearing loss or visual change on amikacin or ethambutol`],pearls:[`**Smear positive, NAAT negative - think NTM.**`,`NTM diagnosis needs **two positive sputum cultures** plus clinical and radiological criteria.`,`**Lady Windermere**: slender older woman, middle lobe and lingula MAC.`,`**Azithromycin + rifampicin + ethambutol** for MAC; 12 months after conversion.`,`**Never macrolide monotherapy** - including 'bronchiectasis azithromycin' before excluding NTM.`,`**M. abscessus** is the hardest NTM to cure; check erm(41).`,`PTLD is **chronic abnormality after TB - not recurrence**; culture before repeating ATT.`,`Xpert can **stay positive for years** after cure.`,`Assess every TB patient at **end of treatment**: spirometry, SpO2, 6MWT.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-ntm-post-tb-q1`,stem:`A 62-year-old thin woman has 2 years of chronic cough. HRCT shows bronchiectasis and tree-in-bud nodules in the right middle lobe and lingula. Two sputum samples are AFB smear-positive, Xpert MTB/RIF is negative, and both cultures grow M. avium complex. Which is the most appropriate treatment?`,options:[`Standard 2HRZE/4HRE under NTEP`,`Azithromycin alone 250 mg daily long term`,`Azithromycin, rifampicin and ethambutol three times weekly until 12 months after culture conversion`,`BPaLM for 26 weeks`],answer:2,explanation:`This is nodular-bronchiectatic MAC meeting ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020 criteria; macrolide-susceptible non-cavitary disease is treated with thrice-weekly azithromycin, rifampicin and ethambutol for 12 months after conversion. Standard ATT omits the macrolide that MAC depends on, azithromycin alone breeds macrolide resistance, and BPaLM is a regimen for drug-resistant M. tuberculosis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-ntm-post-tb-q2`,stem:`Under ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020 criteria, which microbiological finding is sufficient (with clinical and radiological criteria) to diagnose NTM lung disease?`,options:[`One positive culture from a single expectorated sputum`,`Positive cultures from two separate expectorated sputum samples`,`A positive tuberculin skin test`,`A single positive smear without culture`],answer:1,explanation:`Because NTM are environmental and can contaminate a single sample, the criteria require positive cultures from at least two separate expectorated sputa, or one bronchial wash or lavage, or a biopsy with compatible histology and culture. One sputum or a smear alone is insufficient, and the tuberculin test cannot tell NTM disease from TB infection.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-ntm-post-tb-q3`,stem:`A 45-year-old man completed treatment for pulmonary TB 3 years ago. He has stable breathlessness, scarring on chest X-ray and a positive Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra with a 'trace' result. Sputum culture is pending. What is the best course?`,options:[`Await liquid culture and assess for post-TB lung disease before restarting ATT`,`Restart 2HRZE/4HRE immediately as recurrent TB`,`Start a DR-TB regimen because of previous treatment`,`Ignore the result and discharge him`],answer:0,explanation:`Xpert can detect DNA from non-viable bacilli for years after cure, so a trace result in a previously treated patient with stable symptoms needs culture confirmation, while his breathlessness is assessed as possible post-TB lung disease. Empirical ATT risks unnecessary toxicity, a DR-TB regimen has no basis without resistance, and ignoring a positive result could miss true recurrence.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`respiratory-ntm-post-tb-q4`,stem:`Which rapidly growing NTM is notorious for inducible macrolide resistance through the erm(41) gene?`,options:[`M. kansasii`,`M. gordonae`,`M. avium`,`M. abscessus`],answer:3,explanation:`M. abscessus subspecies abscessus and bolletii carry a functional erm(41) gene, so they may appear macrolide-susceptible initially but become resistant on extended incubation. M. kansasii and M. avium are slow growers without this mechanism, and M. gordonae is usually a contaminant.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-ntm-post-tb-q5`,stem:`A man cured of TB has a large left upper-lobe cavity and recurrent small haemoptysis. Aspergillus-specific IgG is strongly positive and HRCT shows a fungal ball with pericavitary thickening. What is the most appropriate first treatment?`,options:[`Repeat ATT for 6 months`,`Oral itraconazole 200 mg twice daily for at least 6 months`,`Inhaled corticosteroid-LABA`,`Long-term azithromycin`],answer:1,explanation:`This is chronic pulmonary aspergillosis in a post-TB cavity; oral itraconazole 200 mg twice daily for at least 6 months is first-line, with surgery or bronchial artery embolisation for serious bleeding. Repeat ATT needs microbiological proof, ICS may worsen fungal and mycobacterial infection, and azithromycin does not treat Aspergillus.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-ntm-post-tb-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Define post-tuberculosis lung disease. Describe its spectrum, assessment and management, and how you would differentiate it from recurrent TB and NTM disease.`,openingLines:[`Post-TB lung disease is evidence of chronic respiratory abnormality, with or without symptoms, attributable at least in part to previous pulmonary TB.`,`About half of TB survivors have abnormal lung function, and India, with the largest number of TB survivors, carries the largest PTLD burden.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and burden`,points:[`International Post-TB Symposium 2019 definition; about 50% abnormal lung function; three times higher mortality.`]},{heading:`Spectrum`,points:[`Airway: obstruction, bronchiectasis, stenosis, broncholithiasis.`,`Parenchymal: cavities, fibrosis, destroyed lung; pleural: fibrothorax, empyema.`,`Vascular: pulmonary hypertension, Rasmussen aneurysm; complications: CPA, NTM, cancer, recurrence.`]},{heading:`Assessment at end of treatment`,points:[`Symptoms, SGRQ, spirometry, SpO2, 6MWT, chest X-ray; HRCT, Aspergillus IgG, echo when indicated.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Smoking and biomass cessation; pulmonary rehabilitation; vaccines.`,`Bronchodilators; cautious ICS; airway clearance; itraconazole for CPA; BAE for haemoptysis; LTOT; surgery for destroyed lung.`]},{heading:`Differentiating recurrence and NTM`,points:[`Recurrence needs NAAT plus culture; Xpert can stay positive after cure.`,`NTM: smear positive, NAAT negative, MPT64 negative, species identification; ATS/ERS 2020 criteria.`]},{heading:`Prevention and programme`,points:[`Early diagnosis; adherence; NTEP post-treatment follow-up 2 years; link to NP-NCD respiratory services.`]}],mustDraw:[`Spectrum diagram of PTLD (airway, parenchyma, pleura, vessels, complications)`,`Table comparing recurrent TB, NTM and PTLD`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and burden`,marks:1},{part:`Spectrum`,marks:2},{part:`Assessment`,marks:2},{part:`Management`,marks:3},{part:`Differentiation`,marks:2}],keywords:[`PTLD`,`post-TB`,`spirometry`,`pulmonary rehabilitation`,`CPA`,`recurrence`,`NTM`]},{id:`respiratory-ntm-post-tb-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on pulmonary disease due to Mycobacterium avium complex.`,openingLines:[`Mycobacterium avium complex is a slow-growing environmental non-tuberculous mycobacterium and the commonest cause of NTM lung disease.`,`It is diagnosed by the ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA 2020 criteria and treated with a macrolide-based three-drug regimen.`],answer:[{heading:`Phenotypes`,points:[`Fibrocavitary (older male smokers, COPD), nodular-bronchiectatic (Lady Windermere), hot tub lung, disseminated (HIV CD4 under 50).`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Symptoms, CXR or HRCT findings, two positive sputum cultures or one BAL; macrolide and amikacin susceptibility.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Nodular: azithromycin, rifampicin, ethambutol thrice weekly; cavitary: daily plus amikacin 2-3 months.`,`12 months after conversion; ALIS if refractory; never macrolide monotherapy.`]},{heading:`Monitoring`,points:[`Monthly culture, vision, audiometry, ECG.`]}],mustDraw:[`Treatment table for nodular versus cavitary MAC`],markSplit:[{part:`Phenotypes`,marks:1},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment and monitoring`,marks:2}],keywords:[`MAC`,`Lady Windermere`,`azithromycin`,`ethambutol`,`ALIS`,`ATS/ERS 2020`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Nontuberculous Mycobacterial Infections`,`Daley CL et al. Treatment of Nontuberculous Mycobacterial Pulmonary Disease: an official ATS/ERS/ESCMID/IDSA clinical practice guideline, 2020`,`Haworth CS et al. British Thoracic Society guidelines for the management of NTM pulmonary disease, Thorax 2017`,`Migliori GB et al. Clinical standards for the assessment, management and rehabilitation of post-TB lung disease, Int J Tuberc Lung Dis 2021`,`Allwood BW et al. Post-tuberculosis lung health: perspectives from the First International Symposium, Int J Tuberc Lung Dis 2020`,`Nightingale R et al. Post-TB health and wellbeing (Second International Post-Tuberculosis Symposium), Int J Tuberc Lung Dis 2023`,`Technical and Operational Guidelines for TB Control in India, NTEP, MoHFW, 2023`]});var a=[];a.push({id:`respiratory-ild-ipf`,title:`Interstitial lung disease approach and idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Interstitial lung disease (ILD)** is a group of over 200 disorders that inflame or scar the alveolar walls and interstitium, causing **restrictive physiology with a low DLCO**; **idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF)** is the commonest fibrosing idiopathic interstitial pneumonia of older adults, defined by a **usual interstitial pneumonia (UIP) pattern** on HRCT or histology after exclusion of known causes, and is slowed (not reversed) by the **antifibrotics nintedanib and pirfenidone**.`,keywords:[`ILD`,`diffuse parenchymal lung disease`,`DPLD`,`IPF`,`UIP`,`usual interstitial pneumonia`,`NSIP`,`idiopathic interstitial pneumonia`,`progressive pulmonary fibrosis`,`PPF`,`nintedanib`,`pirfenidone`,`nerandomilast`,`velcro crackles`,`honeycombing`,`multidisciplinary discussion`,`acute exacerbation of IPF`,`GAP index`,`transbronchial lung cryobiopsy`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**ILD (diffuse parenchymal lung disease)** is an umbrella for more than 200 disorders that injure the alveolar epithelium, capillary endothelium and interstitium, sharing breathlessness, diffuse shadows on imaging, restriction and a reduced DLCO. [Harrison 22e]`,`**IPF** is a chronic, progressive fibrosing interstitial pneumonia of unknown cause, limited to the lungs, in older adults, with a **UIP pattern** on HRCT and/or histology. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`IPF incidence in Europe and North America is roughly **3-9 per 100,000 per year**, rising steeply after 60; it is **commoner in men and in ever-smokers**, and rare under 50. [Harrison 22e]`,`Untreated IPF has a **median survival of about 3-5 years** from diagnosis, worse than many cancers. [Harrison 22e]`,`The **ILD-India registry** (27 centres, 1,084 patients, 2012-2015) found **hypersensitivity pneumonitis the commonest ILD (about 47%)**, followed by **CTD-ILD (about 14%) and IPF (about 14%)** - the reverse of Western series where IPF leads. [Singh S, AJRCCM 2017]`,`In India ILD is under-diagnosed and frequently **misread as tuberculosis**, so many patients receive empirical ATT before HRCT is ever done. [ILD-India registry 2017]`,`**Interstitial lung abnormalities (ILA)** - incidental non-dependent changes on CT in people not suspected of ILD - are found in about 4-9% of smokers and 2-7% of non-smokers, and a minority progress. [Fleischner Society 2020]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`Ask the **five-question cause history** for every ILD: **occupation and environment, drugs, connective tissue disease, smoking, and family history** - most non-IPF ILDs are found this way. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Exposures**: birds, feather pillows, moulds, damp homes and **desert (evaporative) air coolers** (hypersensitivity pneumonitis); silica, coal, asbestos (pneumoconioses - see the Occupational lung disease topic). [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`**Drugs**: amiodarone, methotrexate, nitrofurantoin, bleomycin, checkpoint inhibitors and many more - see the CTD-ILD and drug-induced lung disease topic and pneumotox.com. [Pneumotox 2024]`,`**Connective tissue disease**: systemic sclerosis, rheumatoid arthritis, inflammatory myositis, Sjogren syndrome and MCTD - ILD may precede the joint or skin disease by years. [ACR/CHEST 2023]`,`**IPF risk factors**: age over 60, male sex, cigarette smoking (odds about 1.6-2.9), metal and wood dust, gastro-oesophageal reflux and chronic viral infection are associated, though none is proven causal. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Genetics of IPF**: the **MUC5B promoter variant rs35705950** is the strongest common risk allele, and rare **telomere-related gene mutations (TERT, TERC, RTEL1, PARN)** and surfactant genes (SFTPC, SFTPA2) cause familial pulmonary fibrosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Familial pulmonary fibrosis** (two or more first-degree relatives) accounts for about 5-20% of cases; short telomeres also cause early greying, cryptogenic cirrhosis and marrow failure, and increase toxicity after transplant. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Smoking-related ILDs** - respiratory bronchiolitis-ILD, desquamative interstitial pneumonia and pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis - improve mainly with **smoking cessation**. [ATS/ERS 2013]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Current model of IPF: **repeated micro-injury to an ageing, genetically susceptible alveolar epithelium** with failed repair - not primary inflammation - which is why steroids do not help and antifibrotics do. [Harrison 22e]`,`Injured, senescent **type II alveolar epithelial cells** (short telomeres, endoplasmic reticulum stress) release **TGF-beta, PDGF, FGF and CTGF**, which recruit fibroblasts and turn them into collagen-secreting **myofibroblasts**. [Harrison 22e]`,`The histological hallmark is the **fibroblast focus** - a subepithelial cluster of active myofibroblasts at the edge of dense scar - and their number predicts mortality. [Harrison 22e]`,`Scar stiffens the lung and shrinks it: **compliance falls, TLC, FVC and FRC fall**, and radial traction on airways keeps **FEV1/FVC normal or high** - the restrictive pattern. [Harrison 22e]`,`Thickened alveolar-capillary membrane and loss of capillary bed lower the **DLCO** - usually the earliest and most sensitive abnormality - and cause **exertional desaturation** because transit time shortens with exercise. [Harrison 22e]`,`Breathing is **rapid and shallow** because stiff lungs make deep breaths costly and J-receptor stimulation drives the rate; PaCO2 is low until end-stage disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`Repeated alveolar collapse and fibrosis remodel distal airspaces into thick-walled cysts - **honeycombing** - and pull airways open as **traction bronchiectasis**. [Fleischner Society 2018]`,`**Nintedanib** is a tyrosine kinase inhibitor blocking PDGF, FGF and VEGF receptors; **pirfenidone** reduces TGF-beta signalling and fibroblast proliferation; **nerandomilast** is an oral preferential **PDE4B inhibitor** with anti-inflammatory and antifibrotic effects. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`Hypoxic vasoconstriction and capillary loss cause **pulmonary hypertension (group 3)** in about 30-50% of advanced IPF, the main driver of right heart failure. [ESC/ERS 2022]`]},{heading:`Classification that examiners ask`,points:[`**ILD of known cause**: CTD-ILD, drug and radiation, hypersensitivity pneumonitis, pneumoconiosis. [ATS/ERS 2013]`,`**Idiopathic interstitial pneumonias (IIPs)** - major: chronic fibrosing (**IPF, idiopathic NSIP**), smoking-related (**RB-ILD, DIP**), acute or subacute (**COP, AIP**); rare: **LIP and pleuroparenchymal fibroelastosis (PPFE)**; plus unclassifiable IIP. [ATS/ERS 2013]`,`**Granulomatous ILD**: sarcoidosis and hypersensitivity pneumonitis (both have their own topics). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Other forms**: lymphangioleiomyomatosis (LAM), pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis, pulmonary alveolar proteinosis, eosinophilic pneumonias and diffuse alveolar haemorrhage. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HRCT patterns for UIP (2022)**: **UIP**, **probable UIP**, **indeterminate for UIP** and **alternative diagnosis** - the 2018 'typical/probable/indeterminate/alternative' scheme carried forward. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`**UIP pattern**: subpleural, basal-predominant (often heterogeneous) reticulation with **honeycombing**, with or without peripheral traction bronchiectasis; **probable UIP** has the same distribution and traction bronchiectasis but **no honeycombing**. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`**Features suggesting an alternative diagnosis**: cysts, marked mosaic attenuation or **three-density pattern**, predominant ground-glass, profuse micronodules or centrilobular nodules, consolidation, and upper or peribronchovascular predominance with subpleural sparing. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`**Progressive pulmonary fibrosis (PPF)** is a behaviour, not a disease: any fibrotic ILD other than IPF that worsens by the 2022 criteria (see the table). [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`The typical IPF patient is a **man over 60, ex-smoker, with insidious exertional breathlessness and a dry cough for over 6 months**, often treated first for asthma, COPD, heart failure or TB. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dry, distressing cough** occurs in about 70-85% of IPF and is independently linked to progression and poor quality of life. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tempo matters**: hours to days points to infection, heart failure, AIP, eosinophilic pneumonia or haemorrhage; weeks to months to COP, HP or drug ILD; months to years to IPF, fibrotic HP and pneumoconiosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **Raynaud phenomenon, skin thickening, joint swelling, morning stiffness, dry eyes and mouth, rash, muscle weakness and mechanic's hands** - features of an occult CTD. [ACR/CHEST 2023]`,`Ask about **pets and birds (pigeon feeding), feather bedding, air coolers, damp walls**, farming, sugarcane bagasse, and any job with dust or fumes - with dates. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`List **every drug for the past years**, including cancer therapy, nitrofurantoin prophylaxis and amiodarone. [Pneumotox 2024]`,`**Haemoptysis, fever, weight loss** are unusual in IPF and point to TB, malignancy, vasculitis or infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **reflux symptoms, snoring and daytime sleepiness** (GORD and OSA are common comorbidities) and about **ankle swelling** (pulmonary hypertension). [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Fine, bilateral, basal, end-inspiratory 'velcro' crackles** are heard in over 90% of IPF and appear early - often before the chest X-ray changes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Clubbing** is seen in about 25-50% of IPF; it is rare in sarcoidosis and HP, and clubbing with crackles in India also suggests asbestosis or bronchiectasis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mid-inspiratory squeaks** suggest bronchiolitis such as hypersensitivity pneumonitis rather than IPF. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`Tachypnoea with shallow breaths, reduced chest expansion and **resting or exertional desaturation** on a pulse oximeter (walk the patient). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Signs of pulmonary hypertension**: loud P2, left parasternal heave, raised JVP, pedal oedema - late findings that change prognosis. [ESC/ERS 2022]`,`**CTD clues**: sclerodactyly, telangiectasia, digital pits, puffy fingers, synovitis, Gottron papules, heliotrope rash, mechanic's hands, proximal weakness. [ACR/CHEST 2023]`,`**Cyanosis** appears late in ILD and signals severe hypoxaemia. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Fibrotic hypersensitivity pneumonitis** is the commonest IPF mimic in India - look for exposure, mid-upper zone predominance, mosaic attenuation and BAL lymphocytosis. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`**CTD-ILD**: NSIP pattern, younger, female, autoantibodies; RA-ILD often shows UIP and needs serology to separate it from IPF. [ACR/CHEST 2023]`,`**Asbestosis**: basal UIP-like fibrosis with **pleural plaques** and a long exposure history (see the Occupational lung disease topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Heart failure**: basal crackles and interstitial shadows that clear with diuretics, with raised NT-proBNP and cardiomegaly. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Miliary or post-TB fibrosis**: upper-zone fibrosis, volume loss, cavities or calcified nodes - check sputum CBNAAT before immunosuppression. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Lymphangitis carcinomatosa**: thickened, beaded interlobular septa and hilar nodes in a patient with known or occult cancer. [Fleischner Society 2018]`,`**COPD with emphysema** (combined pulmonary fibrosis and emphysema, CPFE): upper-zone emphysema plus basal fibrosis gives near-normal spirometry with a **disproportionately low DLCO**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line and advanced`,points:[`**Chest X-ray**: basal reticular shadows and small lungs; normal in up to 10% of ILD, so a normal film never excludes it. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Spirometry and DLCO**: restriction (**FVC under 80% predicted, FEV1/FVC 0.7 or more**) and **DLCO under 80%** (often under 60%); lung volumes confirm a low TLC. [ATS/ERS 2022]`,`**Six-minute walk test**: distance and nadir SpO2; **desaturation below 88%** predicts mortality and is used for ambulatory oxygen and transplant listing. [ISHLT 2021]`,`**HRCT thorax**: thin sections (1.25 mm or less), non-contrast, with **prone images** (to separate dependent atelectasis) and **expiratory images** (air trapping in HP) - the single most important test. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`**Serology for every new ILD**: ANA (indirect immunofluorescence), RF, anti-CCP, ESR, CRP; add anti-Scl-70, anti-centromere, anti-Ro/La, ANCA, CK, aldolase and an **extended myositis panel** if there are any clues. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`**Bronchoalveolar lavage**: lymphocytes **over 30%** point away from IPF (HP, sarcoid, NSIP, drug); also excludes infection. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`**Transbronchial lung cryobiopsy (TBLC)**: diagnostic yield about 80%, accepted in 2022 as an alternative to surgical biopsy in experienced centres. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`**Surgical lung biopsy (VATS)**: for unclassified ILD where the result will change management; avoid when FVC or DLCO is very low or during an exacerbation (high mortality). [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`**Echocardiography** for suspected pulmonary hypertension and **NT-proBNP**; **sputum CBNAAT** if TB is possible; **arterial blood gas** when SpO2 is low. [ESC/ERS 2022]`,`**Multidisciplinary discussion (MDD)** of pulmonologist, radiologist and pathologist (plus rheumatologist) is the reference standard for ILD diagnosis. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - IPF`,points:[`**Antifibrotic therapy is offered to every confirmed IPF**: either **nintedanib or pirfenidone**, which halve the rate of FVC decline (about 100 mL/year saved) - they do not improve FVC or symptoms. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`**Nintedanib 150 mg orally twice daily with food** (12 hours apart), reduced to **100 mg twice daily** for diarrhoea or raised transaminases; check LFT monthly for 3 months, then 3-monthly. [Ofev SmPC 2024]`,`**Pirfenidone** with food, titrated: **267 mg three times daily week 1, 534 mg three times daily week 2, then 801 mg three times daily (2,403 mg/day)**; Japan licenses 1,800 mg/day and many Indian patients tolerate only lower doses. [Esbriet SmPC 2023]`,`Pirfenidone needs **sun protection** (photosensitive rash), monthly LFT for 6 months, and avoidance of **fluvoxamine** (CYP1A2 inhibitor); smoking lowers its levels. [Esbriet SmPC 2023]`,`Do **not** give **prednisolone plus azathioprine plus N-acetylcysteine** (PANTHER-IPF: more deaths), warfarin, ambrisentan, imatinib or sildenafil for IPF. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2015, 2022]`,`**Anti-reflux treatment is no longer recommended** to improve IPF outcomes; treat reflux only for its symptoms. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`**Pulmonary rehabilitation** improves 6MWD, breathlessness and quality of life and should be offered to all. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2015]`,`**Smoking cessation**, influenza, pneumococcal, COVID-19 and RSV (60 years and over) vaccination - see the Adult respiratory vaccines topic. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Cough**: low-dose controlled-release **morphine 5 mg twice daily** reduced IPF cough by about 39% in PACIFY (2023); add a laxative. [Wu Z, Lancet Respir Med 2023]`,`**Oxygen**: **ambulatory oxygen** for exertional desaturation improves quality of life (AmbOx); **long-term oxygen** if resting PaO2 is 55 mmHg or less (SpO2 88% or less). [BTS 2015]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD - acute exacerbation and admission`,points:[`**Acute exacerbation of IPF (AE-IPF)**: acute worsening of breathlessness, usually under 1 month, with **new bilateral ground-glass or consolidation on a UIP background**, not fully explained by heart failure or fluid overload. [Collard, AJRCCM 2016]`,`AE-IPF occurs in about **5-15% of patients per year**, may be triggered (infection, surgery, aspiration) or idiopathic, and carries **in-hospital mortality around 50%**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Admit** for resting SpO2 under 88%, breathlessness at rest, new bilateral infiltrates, suspected pneumonia or PE, or right heart failure. [BTS 2023]`,`Work-up: CT pulmonary angiography or HRCT, **echocardiography, NT-proBNP, blood and sputum cultures, respiratory viral PCR, procalcitonin**, and **sputum CBNAAT** in India. [Collard, AJRCCM 2016]`,`Treat with **broad-spectrum antibiotics** until infection is excluded, controlled oxygen (target SpO2 88-92% or higher if no hypercapnia), and **high-flow nasal oxygen** for rising demand. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`**Corticosteroids** are conditionally suggested (commonly IV methylprednisolone 500-1,000 mg daily for 3 days, then oral prednisolone tapering) despite weak evidence; **cyclophosphamide is not recommended** (EXAFIP 2020: higher mortality). [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`**Invasive ventilation** is generally not advised because survival is very poor; consider it only as a bridge to transplant, and start **advance care planning** early. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2015]`,`Continue antifibrotics through the exacerbation unless contraindicated; give **VTE prophylaxis**. [BTS 2023]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: nintedanib is teratogenic and contraindicated; women of childbearing age need effective contraception during and for 3 months after. [Ofev SmPC 2024]`,`**Elderly and low body weight**: start pirfenidone slowly and accept the highest tolerated dose; reduce nintedanib to 100 mg twice daily if diarrhoea or weight loss. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`**Liver disease**: nintedanib is not recommended in Child-Pugh B or C; pirfenidone is contraindicated in severe hepatic impairment. [Ofev SmPC 2024]`,`**CKD**: pirfenidone is contraindicated with CrCl under 30 mL/min or dialysis; nintedanib needs no adjustment in mild-moderate renal impairment. [Esbriet SmPC 2023]`,`**Anticoagulation**: nintedanib has a VEGF-related bleeding risk - use with care on full-dose anticoagulants or recent bleeding, and stop before major surgery. [Ofev SmPC 2024]`,`**Diabetes**: avoid long courses of steroids, which have no benefit in IPF and worsen glycaemia; PDE4 inhibitors can cause weight loss. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`**Resource-limited settings**: generic nintedanib and pirfenidone are available in India at much lower cost; where HRCT or MDD is unavailable, refer to a tertiary chest centre rather than start empirical steroids or ATT. [ILD-India registry 2017]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Pulmonary hypertension** (group 3) and **cor pulmonale**: inhaled **treprostinil** improved 6MWD in PH-ILD (INCREASE 2021). [ESC/ERS 2022]`,`**Lung cancer** risk is increased about 4-5 times, mostly in fibrotic lower lobes; nodules need a low threshold for investigation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute exacerbation**, pneumothorax, pulmonary embolism, infections (including TB and NTM) and **respiratory failure** are the usual terminal events. [Harrison 22e]`,`Comorbid **OSA, GORD, coronary artery disease, depression and anxiety** worsen symptoms and survival. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`**GAP index** (Gender, Age, Physiology: FVC and DLCO) stages I-III with **1-year mortality about 6%, 16% and 39%**. [Ley B, Ann Intern Med 2012]`,`**Poor prognostic markers**: FVC decline of 10% or more (or 5-10% with symptoms) in 6-12 months, DLCO under 40%, 6MWT desaturation below 88%, extensive honeycombing and pulmonary hypertension. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer every suspected ILD** to a respiratory centre with HRCT and MDD; do not start steroids or ATT empirically. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`Review every **3-6 months**: symptoms, weight, **FVC, DLCO, 6MWT and SpO2**, adverse effects and LFT. [NICE CG163]`,`Repeat **HRCT** for unexplained worsening, suspected cancer or exacerbation - not routinely. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`**Transplant referral**: at diagnosis of IPF or any UIP, or when **FVC under 80% or DLCO under 40%**, any significant decline, oxygen need, or 6MWT desaturation; age generally under 65-70. [ISHLT 2021]`,`**Lung transplant** median survival is about 5-6 years for IPF; in India it is available at a few centres (Chennai, Hyderabad, Delhi NCR) and costs are high. [ISHLT 2021]`,`**Palliative care** from the time of diagnosis: opioids for breathlessness, fan therapy, oxygen and advance directives - see the End-stage lung disease topic. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`]},{heading:`Prevention and counselling`,points:[`**Stop smoking** and avoid second-hand and biomass smoke; smoking accelerates decline and lowers pirfenidone levels. [Harrison 22e]`,`Remove **identified antigens** (birds, feather bedding, mould, coolers) - even in apparent IPF, because fibrotic HP is often missed. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`Counsel on **side effects of antifibrotics**: take with food, diarrhoea management with loperamide, sun protection, and not to stop without discussion. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`**Family screening** is reasonable in familial pulmonary fibrosis; relatives who smoke should stop. [Harrison 22e]`,`**NP-NCD** chronic respiratory disease services and Ayushman Arogya Mandirs can follow up oxygen and rehabilitation; **PM-JAY** covers inpatient care for exacerbations. [MoHFW 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2026)`,points:[`**ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022**: introduced **progressive pulmonary fibrosis**; suggested **nintedanib for PPF** after failure of standard management; accepted **TBLC** as an alternative to surgical biopsy. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`The 2022 update allows a **confident IPF diagnosis without biopsy** when HRCT shows **probable UIP** in the right clinical context after MDD. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`2022 recommends **against anti-acid treatment** for improving IPF outcomes and makes no recommendation for genomic classifiers. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`**Nerandomilast 2025**: FIBRONEER-IPF and FIBRONEER-ILD (NEJM 2025) showed **18 mg twice daily slowed FVC decline** vs placebo in IPF and PPF; the US FDA approved it for IPF in October 2025 - Indian availability is not yet established. [Richeldi L, NEJM 2025]`,`**PACIFY 2023**: low-dose morphine is the first drug with trial evidence for IPF cough. [Wu Z, Lancet Respir Med 2023]`,`**ACR/CHEST 2023**: guidance on screening for ILD with HRCT and PFT in systemic autoimmune rheumatic diseases (see the CTD-ILD topic). [ACR/CHEST 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: 'Approach to a patient with diffuse parenchymal lung disease' - answer with tempo, cause history, HRCT pattern, serology, BAL/biopsy and MDD.`,`Define **UIP on HRCT** precisely: subpleural, basal, honeycombing, traction bronchiectasis - and list the features that argue against it. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`**Common mistake**: giving steroids for IPF; they increase harm (PANTHER) - steroids are for NSIP, COP, HP, sarcoid and CTD-ILD. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2015]`,`**Common mistake**: calling restriction from spirometry alone - a low FVC with normal ratio needs TLC to confirm. [ATS/ERS 2022]`,`Know the **two antifibrotics, their doses and the side effect of each**: nintedanib diarrhoea, pirfenidone photosensitivity and nausea; both raise transaminases.`,`Know the **PPF criteria** (2 of 3 in a year): symptoms, FVC down 5% or DLCO down 10%, radiological progression. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Antifibrotic and supportive drugs in IPF / PPF`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Nintedanib`,`150 mg orally twice daily with food`,`Lifelong; drop to 100 mg twice daily for intolerance; LFT monthly x 3 months then 3-monthly`,`Diarrhoea (about 60%), nausea, raised ALT, weight loss, bleeding; contraindicated in pregnancy; avoid Child-Pugh B/C`],[`Pirfenidone`,`801 mg orally three times daily with food (2,403 mg/day)`,`267 mg TDS week 1, 534 mg TDS week 2, full dose week 3; lifelong`,`Nausea, anorexia, photosensitive rash, raised ALT; avoid fluvoxamine; contraindicated CrCl under 30 or severe liver disease`],[`Nerandomilast`,`18 mg orally twice daily`,`Long term (trial data 52 weeks plus)`,`Diarrhoea, weight loss; PDE4 class - watch mood; approved in USA 2025, not yet routine in India`],[`Morphine (controlled release)`,`5 mg orally twice daily`,`Review at 2-4 weeks`,`Constipation, drowsiness; for refractory cough or breathlessness`],[`Oxygen`,`Ambulatory or long-term, titrated to SpO2 88-92% or above`,`LTOT 15 hours a day or more if resting PaO2 55 mmHg or less`,`Fire risk with smoking; check hypercapnia`],[`Methylprednisolone (AE-IPF only)`,`500-1,000 mg IV daily for 3 days, then prednisolone taper`,`Taper over weeks`,`Hyperglycaemia, infection; weak evidence; not for stable IPF`]]},{heading:`HRCT categories for UIP (ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022)`,columns:[`Category`,`Distribution`,`Features`,`What it means`],rows:[[`UIP`,`Subpleural, basal predominant, often heterogeneous`,`Honeycombing with or without peripheral traction bronchiectasis`,`IPF if no known cause - no biopsy needed`],[`Probable UIP`,`Subpleural, basal predominant`,`Reticulation with peripheral traction bronchiectasis or bronchiolectasis; no honeycombing`,`IPF in the right clinical context after MDD; biopsy optional`],[`Indeterminate for UIP`,`Subpleural, basal predominant`,`Subtle reticulation, mild ground-glass, or fibrosis not suggesting any specific cause`,`Consider BAL and TBLC or surgical biopsy`],[`Alternative diagnosis`,`Upper or mid zone, peribronchovascular, subpleural sparing`,`Cysts, mosaic or three-density pattern, predominant GGO, micronodules, consolidation`,`Think HP, NSIP, sarcoid, LAM, organising pneumonia`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of fibrotic ILD`,columns:[`Disease`,`Clinical clue`,`HRCT clue`,`Discriminating test`],rows:[[`IPF`,`Man over 60, ex-smoker, clubbing, velcro crackles`,`Basal subpleural UIP with honeycombing`,`MDD; serology negative; BAL lymphocytes under 30%`],[`Fibrotic HP`,`Bird, mould or cooler exposure`,`Mid-upper zone, mosaic attenuation, three-density sign, centrilobular nodules`,`BAL lymphocytosis; specific IgG; exposure history`],[`CTD-ILD (SSc, myositis)`,`Younger women, Raynaud, skin or muscle signs`,`NSIP with lower-zone ground-glass and subpleural sparing`,`ANA, Scl-70, myositis panel`],[`RA-ILD`,`Joint disease, high-titre RF and anti-CCP`,`Often UIP`,`RF, anti-CCP`],[`Asbestosis`,`Shipyard, construction, brake lining exposure`,`Basal fibrosis with pleural plaques`,`Occupational history; plaques`],[`Fibrotic sarcoidosis`,`Younger, eye or skin lesions`,`Upper-zone perihilar fibrosis, perilymphatic nodules, nodes`,`EBUS-TBNA granulomas; ACE limited`],[`Chronic heart failure`,`Orthopnoea, PND, oedema`,`Smooth septal thickening, effusions`,`NT-proBNP, echo; clears with diuretics`]]},{heading:`Progressive pulmonary fibrosis - 2 of 3 within the past year (2022)`,columns:[`Domain`,`Criterion`,`Note`],rows:[[`Symptoms`,`Worsening respiratory symptoms`,`Not explained by another cause`],[`Physiology`,`Absolute FVC decline of 5% predicted or more, or absolute DLCO (Hb-corrected) decline of 10% predicted or more`,`Within 1 year`],[`Radiology`,`Increased traction bronchiectasis, new GGO with traction bronchiectasis, new fine reticulation, increased reticulation, new or more honeycombing, more lobar volume loss`,`Compared with earlier HRCT`],[`Applies to`,`Fibrotic ILD other than IPF (HP, CTD-ILD, NSIP, unclassifiable, sarcoid)`,`Then consider nintedanib`]]},{heading:`Investigations and their cut-offs in ILD`,columns:[`Test`,`Typical ILD result`,`Cut-off / meaning`],rows:[[`Spirometry`,`Low FVC, FEV1/FVC normal or high`,`FVC under 80% predicted; ratio 0.7 or more`],[`DLCO`,`Reduced, often first abnormality`,`Under 80% abnormal; under 40% = advanced, transplant referral`],[`6-minute walk`,`Desaturation on exercise`,`Nadir SpO2 under 88% = poor prognosis, needs ambulatory oxygen`],[`BAL differential`,`Lymphocytes in HP, sarcoid, NSIP`,`Over 30% argues against IPF; CD4/CD8 over 3.5 favours sarcoid`],[`Serial FVC`,`Annual decline`,`5-10% = possible progression; 10% or more = significant`],[`Echocardiography`,`Raised estimated PASP, RV dilatation`,`Suspect PH; confirm with right heart catheter (mPAP over 20 mmHg)`]]}],redFlags:[`Resting SpO2 under 88% or breathless at rest`,`Rapid worsening over days to weeks with new bilateral ground-glass shadows (acute exacerbation, AIP, pneumocystis)`,`Haemoptysis with ILD shadows - diffuse alveolar haemorrhage or vasculitis`,`Rapidly progressive ILD with dermatomyositis skin signs (anti-MDA5)`,`Signs of right heart failure: raised JVP, oedema, loud P2`,`Fever and new infiltrates in a patient on immunosuppression or antifibrotics`,`Weight loss and a new nodule in fibrotic lung - exclude lung cancer`],pearls:[`**Velcro crackles + clubbing + man over 60 = IPF until proved otherwise.**`,`**DLCO is the most sensitive PFT in ILD**; FEV1/FVC is normal or high because of traction.`,`**IPF is a failed-repair disease, not an inflammatory one** - so steroids harm and antifibrotics help.`,`**In India, HP outnumbers IPF** (ILD-India registry) - always take the bird, mould and cooler history.`,`**Probable UIP + compatible clinical picture + MDD = IPF without biopsy** (2022).`,`**Antifibrotics slow decline by about half; they do not reverse fibrosis** - start early, do not wait for worsening.`,`**Nintedanib = diarrhoea; pirfenidone = photosensitivity and nausea.**`,`**PPF: 2 of 3 in one year - Symptoms, Spirometry/DLCO, Scan.**`,`**Cyclophosphamide in AE-IPF increased mortality** (EXAFIP) - a favourite MCQ trap.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-ild-ipf-q1`,stem:`A 66-year-old retired clerk, ex-smoker, has 2 years of exertional breathlessness and dry cough. He has clubbing and bibasal fine end-inspiratory crackles. HRCT shows subpleural basal reticulation with honeycombing and traction bronchiectasis. ANA, RF and anti-CCP are negative and he has no relevant exposures. What is the next best step?`,options:[`Surgical lung biopsy to confirm the diagnosis`,`Oral prednisolone 1 mg/kg with azathioprine`,`Diagnose IPF after multidisciplinary discussion and start an antifibrotic`,`Empirical antitubercular treatment for 2 months`],answer:2,explanation:`A definite UIP pattern on HRCT with no identifiable cause allows a confident IPF diagnosis at MDD without biopsy, and antifibrotic therapy (nintedanib or pirfenidone) should follow. Surgical biopsy adds risk without changing management here. Prednisolone-azathioprine (with NAC) increased deaths in PANTHER-IPF. Empirical ATT without microbiological evidence delays correct care.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-ild-ipf-q2`,stem:`Which pulmonary function finding is most typical of established idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis?`,options:[`Reduced FVC, preserved or raised FEV1/FVC and reduced DLCO`,`Reduced FEV1/FVC with raised TLC and normal DLCO`,`Normal FVC with raised DLCO`,`Reduced FEV1/FVC with reduced DLCO and raised RV`],answer:0,explanation:`Fibrosis stiffens the lung (low FVC and TLC) while radial traction keeps airways open, so FEV1/FVC is preserved or high; the thickened membrane lowers DLCO. A low ratio with raised TLC and normal DLCO describes asthma; a low ratio with low DLCO and air trapping describes emphysema; a raised DLCO suggests alveolar haemorrhage or polycythaemia.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-ild-ipf-q3`,stem:`A patient with IPF on pirfenidone develops a red, itchy rash on the face and forearms after working outdoors. What is the most appropriate advice?`,options:[`Switch immediately to prednisolone`,`Add fluvoxamine to reduce pruritus`,`Stop all antifibrotic therapy permanently`,`Use sun protection and temporarily reduce the pirfenidone dose, then re-escalate`],answer:3,explanation:`Photosensitive rash is a known pirfenidone effect; it is managed with sunscreen, covering clothes and temporary dose reduction before re-titration, keeping the patient on an antifibrotic. Steroids do not treat IPF. Fluvoxamine is a CYP1A2 inhibitor that sharply raises pirfenidone levels and is contraindicated. Permanent withdrawal is unnecessary for a manageable effect.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-ild-ipf-q4`,stem:`According to the ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022 guideline, which treatment is NOT recommended for acute exacerbation of IPF?`,options:[`Supplemental oxygen`,`Intravenous cyclophosphamide added to steroids`,`Corticosteroids`,`Antibiotics until infection is excluded`],answer:1,explanation:`The 2022 guideline recommends against cyclophosphamide in AE-IPF because the EXAFIP trial showed higher 3-month mortality when it was added to steroids. Corticosteroids receive a conditional recommendation for use despite weak evidence, while oxygen and empirical antibiotics are standard supportive care pending exclusion of infection.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`respiratory-ild-ipf-q5`,stem:`A woman with fibrotic hypersensitivity pneumonitis, despite antigen avoidance and mycophenolate, has worse breathlessness and a 7% predicted fall in FVC over 10 months with more traction bronchiectasis on HRCT. What is the best next drug?`,options:[`Pirfenidone at 801 mg three times daily`,`N-acetylcysteine`,`Nintedanib 150 mg twice daily`,`Ambrisentan`],answer:2,explanation:`She meets the 2022 definition of progressive pulmonary fibrosis (worse symptoms, FVC fall of 5% or more and radiological progression), for which nintedanib is suggested based on INBUILD. Pirfenidone has insufficient evidence in PPF and the guideline asks for further research. NAC monotherapy has no benefit, and ambrisentan is harmful in IPF.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-ild-ipf-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Describe the approach to a patient with suspected interstitial lung disease. Discuss the diagnosis and management of idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis.`,openingLines:[`Interstitial lung disease is a group of over 200 disorders of the alveolar wall and interstitium producing restrictive physiology with low DLCO and diffuse shadows on imaging.`,`IPF is a chronic progressive fibrosing interstitial pneumonia of unknown cause in older adults with a UIP pattern on HRCT or histology (ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022).`],answer:[{heading:`Classification`,points:[`ILD of known cause (CTD, drug, HP, pneumoconiosis); idiopathic interstitial pneumonias (IPF, NSIP, RB-ILD, DIP, COP, AIP, LIP, PPFE); granulomatous (sarcoid); others (LAM, PLCH, PAP).`,`Indian context: ILD-India registry - HP 47%, CTD-ILD 14%, IPF 14%.`]},{heading:`Clinical approach`,points:[`Tempo of illness; occupational, environmental (birds, coolers), drug, CTD, smoking and family history.`,`Examination: velcro crackles, clubbing, CTD signs, PH signs, exertional SpO2.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`CXR, spirometry with lung volumes and DLCO, 6MWT, HRCT with prone and expiratory images.`,`Serology: ANA, RF, anti-CCP, myositis panel; BAL, TBLC or surgical biopsy when HRCT not diagnostic; MDD.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis of IPF`,points:[`Exclusion of known causes plus UIP or probable UIP on HRCT, or a UIP histology, decided at MDD.`,`Draw the HRCT category table (UIP, probable, indeterminate, alternative).`]},{heading:`Management of IPF`,points:[`Nintedanib 150 mg BD or pirfenidone 801 mg TDS (titrated); nerandomilast 18 mg BD approved in the USA 2025.`,`Pulmonary rehabilitation, oxygen, vaccination, smoking cessation, cough control (low-dose morphine), comorbidity care.`,`Avoid steroids-azathioprine-NAC, warfarin, ambrisentan; anti-reflux therapy no longer advised.`]},{heading:`Acute exacerbation, prognosis and transplant`,points:[`AE-IPF: exclude infection, PE, heart failure; oxygen/HFNO, antibiotics, steroids; no cyclophosphamide; mortality about 50%.`,`GAP index; median survival 3-5 years; early transplant referral (ISHLT 2021) and palliative care.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: suspected ILD to history, HRCT, serology, BAL/biopsy, MDD`,`Table of HRCT UIP categories`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and classification`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical approach and investigations`,marks:3},{part:`IPF diagnosis`,marks:1},{part:`IPF treatment, AE and prognosis`,marks:4}],keywords:[`ILD`,`IPF`,`UIP`,`MDD`,`nintedanib`,`pirfenidone`,`acute exacerbation`]},{id:`respiratory-ild-ipf-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on progressive pulmonary fibrosis and antifibrotic therapy.`,openingLines:[`Progressive pulmonary fibrosis (PPF) is a fibrotic ILD other than IPF that worsens in 2 of 3 domains within a year despite standard treatment (ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022).`,`Antifibrotics slow, but do not reverse, the loss of lung function.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition`,points:[`2 of 3 in the past year: worsening symptoms; FVC fall of 5% or more or DLCO fall of 10% or more; radiological progression.`,`Seen in fibrotic HP, CTD-ILD, idiopathic NSIP, unclassifiable ILD, sarcoidosis.`]},{heading:`Antifibrotic drugs`,points:[`Nintedanib 150 mg BD - INBUILD 2019 halved FVC decline in PPF; suggested in 2022.`,`Pirfenidone - evidence insufficient for PPF; nerandomilast 18 mg BD effective in FIBRONEER-ILD 2025.`]},{heading:`Monitoring`,points:[`LFT monthly initially; weight, diarrhoea; FVC/DLCO every 3-6 months; contraception.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: PPF criteria (symptoms, physiology, radiology)`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and criteria`,marks:2},{part:`Drugs and evidence`,marks:2},{part:`Monitoring`,marks:1}],keywords:[`PPF`,`INBUILD`,`nintedanib`,`nerandomilast`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Interstitial lung diseases`,`Raghu G et al. Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis (an Update) and Progressive Pulmonary Fibrosis in Adults: ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT Clinical Practice Guideline. Am J Respir Crit Care Med 2022`,`Travis WD et al. ATS/ERS Update of the International Multidisciplinary Classification of the Idiopathic Interstitial Pneumonias, 2013`,`Singh S et al. Interstitial Lung Disease in India: Results of a Prospective Registry (ILD-India). Am J Respir Crit Care Med 2017`,`Richeldi L et al. Nerandomilast in Patients with Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis (FIBRONEER-IPF). N Engl J Med 2025`,`Collard HR et al. Acute Exacerbation of Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis: An International Working Group Report. Am J Respir Crit Care Med 2016`,`Leard LE et al. ISHLT Consensus Document for the Selection of Lung Transplant Candidates, 2021`]}),a.push({id:`respiratory-hypersensitivity-pneumonitis`,title:`Hypersensitivity pneumonitis`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`**Hypersensitivity pneumonitis (HP)** is an **immune-mediated ILD** caused by repeated inhalation of an organic antigen (birds, moulds, thermophilic actinomycetes) or chemical in a sensitised person; the ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020 guideline divides it into **non-fibrotic and fibrotic HP**, and the cornerstone of treatment is **antigen avoidance**, with steroids or mycophenolate for persistent disease and **nintedanib for progressive fibrosis**.`,keywords:[`HP`,`extrinsic allergic alveolitis`,`EAA`,`bird fancier's lung`,`pigeon breeder's lung`,`farmer's lung`,`bagassosis`,`air cooler lung`,`humidifier lung`,`hot tub lung`,`mushroom worker's lung`,`three-density sign`,`headcheese sign`,`mosaic attenuation`,`BAL lymphocytosis`,`serum precipitins`,`specific IgG`,`fibrotic HP`,`antigen avoidance`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Hypersensitivity pneumonitis** is an immune-mediated ILD of the small airways and lung parenchyma that follows **exposure to an inciting antigen in a susceptible person**. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`The older name **extrinsic allergic alveolitis** and the acute/subacute/chronic division have been replaced by **non-fibrotic and fibrotic HP**, because fibrosis predicts prognosis. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`HP accounts for roughly 2-15% of ILD in Western series, with annual incidence about 1-2 per 100,000, higher in farming and bird-keeping communities. [Harrison 22e]`,`In the **ILD-India registry HP was the commonest ILD at about 47%** of 1,084 patients - air coolers and birds were the main exposures. [Singh S, AJRCCM 2017]`,`Only a small fraction of exposed people develop HP (about 1-15% of pigeon breeders), so **host susceptibility** (HLA class II, genetics, a second hit such as a viral infection) matters. [Harrison 22e]`,`In up to **30-60% of HP no antigen is identified** even after detailed history - this does not exclude the diagnosis. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`**Non-smokers are affected more** than smokers, because nicotine suppresses macrophage and lymphocyte activation. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology - antigens, with Indian sources`,points:[`**Avian proteins** (droppings, feathers, bloom): pigeon breeders, parrot and budgerigar keepers, **pigeon feeding at balconies and temples**, and **feather pillows and duvets**. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`**Desert (evaporative) air coolers**: stagnant water in the tank and khus or wood-wool pads grow moulds and actinomycetes - '**air cooler lung**' is a distinctive Indian cause. [Indian J Chest Dis Allied Sci]`,`**Thermophilic actinomycetes** (Saccharopolyspora rectivirgula, Thermoactinomyces): **farmer's lung** from mouldy hay and grain, and **bagassosis** from mouldy sugarcane bagasse in sugar mills of Maharashtra, UP and Tamil Nadu. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Moulds in damp homes**: Aspergillus, Penicillium, Alternaria on leaking walls and air conditioners; **humidifier and air-conditioner lung**. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`**Mushroom worker's lung**, malt worker's lung (Aspergillus clavatus), cheese washer's lung (Penicillium) and wood dust (sequoiosis, maple bark stripper's lung). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hot tub lung** from Mycobacterium avium complex aerosols, and machine operator's lung from contaminated metal-working fluids. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chemicals**: isocyanates (paints, polyurethane foam), acid anhydrides and some drugs can act as haptens. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Particles under 5 micrometres reach the **terminal bronchioles and alveoli**, so HP centres on the small airways - hence centrilobular nodules and air trapping on CT. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Type III (immune complex) reaction**: specific IgG binds inhaled antigen and fixes complement, giving the early neutrophilic response 4-8 hours after exposure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Type IV (Th1 cell-mediated) reaction** dominates later: IFN-gamma-driven CD8 and CD4 lymphocytes and macrophages form **poorly formed non-necrotising granulomas** - explaining BAL lymphocytosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Classic histological triad**: cellular interstitial pneumonia (peribronchiolar), **cellular bronchiolitis**, and poorly formed granulomas or giant cells. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`Continued exposure shifts the response to **Th2/Th17 with fibrosis** - fibrotic HP can look like UIP or fibrotic NSIP, usually with **bronchiolocentric fibrosis**. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`Small-airway inflammation causes **air trapping**, so PFT can be restrictive, obstructive or mixed, with a low DLCO. [Harrison 22e]`,`Specific IgG shows **exposure, not disease** - many exposed, healthy people have precipitins. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`]},{heading:`Classification (ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020) and diagnostic confidence`,points:[`**Non-fibrotic HP**: purely inflammatory (cellular) disease on HRCT and histology - often reversible with avoidance. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`**Fibrotic HP**: radiological or histological fibrosis (with or without inflammation) - partly irreversible, may become **progressive pulmonary fibrosis**. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`HRCT is graded as **typical HP, compatible with HP, or indeterminate for HP**. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`Diagnosis combines **exposure identification + HRCT category + BAL lymphocytosis + histopathology**, discussed at MDD. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`Confidence levels: **definite (90% or more), high (80-89%), moderate (70-79%), low (51-69%)** and not excluded. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`Identified exposure + typical HRCT + BAL lymphocytosis gives a **high-confidence diagnosis without biopsy**. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`The **CHEST 2021 guideline** uses a similar approach and supports the same exposure, imaging and BAL triad. [CHEST 2021]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Inflammatory (former acute) presentation**: fever, chills, malaise, dry cough and breathlessness **4-8 hours after heavy exposure**, settling in 24-48 hours - mistaken for flu or pneumonia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Recurrent 'pneumonias'** that improve in hospital (away from the antigen) and recur at home are a classic clue. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`**Insidious presentation**: progressive exertional breathlessness, dry cough, fatigue and weight loss over months to years with low-level exposure (feather pillow, cooler). [Harrison 22e]`,`Take a structured **exposure questionnaire**: birds at home, neighbours or work, feather bedding, **coolers and their cleaning**, damp, ACs, humidifiers, farming, sugarcane, hobbies. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`Ask about **improvement away from home** (holidays, hospital) and worsening on return, and **seasonality** (coolers in summer). [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`In India HP is often labelled **TB or asthma** and treated empirically; ask about prior ATT courses. [ILD-India registry 2017]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Bilateral fine inspiratory crackles**, often more mid-zone than in IPF. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mid-inspiratory squeaks** suggest bronchiolitis and are a recognised HP sign. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`Fever, tachypnoea and hypoxaemia in the inflammatory form; weight loss in long-standing disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Clubbing is uncommon** but can occur in fibrotic HP and predicts worse outcome. [Harrison 22e]`,`Signs of **pulmonary hypertension** in advanced fibrotic disease. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**IPF**: older, male, smoker, basal UIP without mosaic attenuation and BAL lymphocytes under 30%. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`**Sarcoidosis**: perilymphatic nodules, hilar nodes, **well-formed** non-necrotising granulomas and BAL CD4/CD8 over 3.5. [ATS 2020]`,`**Pulmonary TB and miliary TB**: randomly distributed nodules, cavities, AFB/CBNAAT positive - mandatory to exclude in India. [NTEP 2024]`,`**NSIP (idiopathic or CTD)**: lower-zone GGO with subpleural sparing, autoantibodies. [ACR/CHEST 2023]`,`**Asthma and ABPA**: wheeze, eosinophilia and raised IgE, unlike HP's lymphocytic picture. [GINA 2025]`,`**Organising pneumonia, respiratory bronchiolitis-ILD** (smokers) and **atypical pneumonia** (fever, consolidation, response to antibiotics). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Organic dust toxic syndrome**: flu-like illness after heavy mould dust in many exposed workers at once, without sensitisation or lasting disease. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Chest X-ray**: may be normal or show fine nodular or ground-glass shadows, upper-mid zone fibrosis in chronic disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HRCT typical non-fibrotic HP**: **ill-defined centrilobular nodules**, diffuse ground-glass, and **mosaic attenuation with air trapping** on expiratory images. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`**HRCT typical fibrotic HP**: reticulation with traction bronchiectasis in a **mid-lung or upper-zone** or random distribution, plus small-airway signs; the **three-density pattern** (formerly headcheese sign) is highly specific. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`**PFT**: restrictive or mixed pattern, **reduced DLCO**, and exertional desaturation on 6MWT. [Harrison 22e]`,`**BAL lymphocytosis**: lymphocytes **30% or more** (often over 40-50% in non-fibrotic HP) supports HP; the CD4/CD8 ratio is usually low but too variable to rely on. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`**Serum antigen-specific IgG (precipitins)** to avian proteins and moulds is suggested to identify the exposure; a positive test shows sensitisation, not disease. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`**Transbronchial or cryobiopsy, or surgical biopsy** when HRCT is indeterminate; inhalational challenge and lymphocyte proliferation tests are not routine. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`Exclude TB with **sputum or BAL CBNAAT and culture**, and do **ANA, RF, anti-CCP and myositis panel** to look for CTD. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Home visit or environmental survey** (cooler water, damp walls, bird nests in AC ducts) can identify the source when history fails. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD`,points:[`**Antigen avoidance** is the single most important step and improves survival in fibrotic HP too: remove birds, feather bedding, damp and mould; stop or replace coolers. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`Practical cooler advice: **drain and dry the tank weekly, change pads each season, or switch to a fan or sealed AC** with serviced filters. [Indian practice]`,`Removing birds from the home is not enough - **feathers persist in dust for months**, so the house needs thorough cleaning and soft furnishings may need replacing. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Non-fibrotic HP** with persistent symptoms or significant impairment after avoidance: **prednisolone 0.5 mg/kg/day (about 30-40 mg) for 4-8 weeks**, then taper over 3-6 months to the lowest effective dose. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Steroid-sparing agents** for relapse or long courses: **mycophenolate mofetil 1-1.5 g twice daily** or **azathioprine 2 mg/kg/day** (check TPMT where available); MMF also helps fibrotic HP with inflammation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fibrotic HP that progresses** despite avoidance and immunosuppression meets PPF criteria: add **nintedanib 150 mg twice daily** (INBUILD). [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`Give **PJP prophylaxis** (cotrimoxazole 960 mg three times weekly) when prednisolone is 20 mg or more for over 4 weeks with a second immunosuppressant, and **bone protection** (calcium, vitamin D). [ACR 2022]`,`Before steroids in India, **exclude active TB** (CBNAAT) and consider TB preventive treatment in high-risk patients. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Pulmonary rehabilitation, oxygen and vaccination** as in other fibrotic ILDs. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD`,points:[`**Admit** for hypoxaemia (SpO2 under 90%), breathlessness at rest, or diagnostic uncertainty with fever and infiltrates. [BTS 2023]`,`Remove from exposure, give **controlled oxygen**, and start empirical antibiotics if pneumonia cannot be excluded. [Harrison 22e]`,`Severe inflammatory HP: **prednisolone 0.5-1 mg/kg/day** or IV methylprednisolone in respiratory failure, after excluding infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute exacerbation of fibrotic HP** behaves like AE-IPF with high mortality - manage similarly. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`Before discharge, ensure the **source is removed** - otherwise readmission is likely. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Children**: HP (often bird-related) is the commonest childhood ILD in some series - ask about pets and pillows. [Nelson 22e]`,`**Pregnancy**: prednisolone and azathioprine are acceptable; **mycophenolate is teratogenic** (stop 6 weeks before conception); nintedanib is contraindicated. [BSR 2022]`,`**Diabetes and elderly**: prefer early steroid-sparing therapy to limit hyperglycaemia, osteoporosis and infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Occupational cases** (farmers, sugar-mill workers): job modification, respirators (powered air-purifying), and compensation under the **Employees' Compensation Act**. [MoLE India]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: if BAL and biopsy are unavailable, an **exposure history + typical HRCT** with improvement after avoidance is strong evidence. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Non-fibrotic HP** usually recovers fully if the antigen is removed early. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`**Fibrotic HP** has a prognosis approaching IPF when a UIP-like pattern or extensive honeycombing is present. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Poor prognostic factors**: fibrosis, honeycombing, **unidentified antigen**, continued exposure, low BAL lymphocytes, older age and clubbing. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`Complications: pulmonary hypertension, respiratory failure, emphysema (farmer's lung in non-smokers) and steroid toxicity. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute exacerbation of fibrotic HP** carries a high short-term mortality, similar to acute exacerbation of IPF. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer** suspected HP for HRCT, BAL and MDD; do not start steroids before the exposure is sought and TB excluded. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`Review at **4-12 weeks** after avoidance or starting steroids with symptoms, SpO2, **FVC and DLCO**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Then **every 3-6 months**: PFT, weight, glucose, BP, blood count and LFT for MMF or azathioprine. [ACR/CHEST 2023]`,`Repeat **HRCT** if FVC falls or symptoms worsen, to detect progressive fibrosis. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`Refer **progressive fibrotic HP** for antifibrotic therapy and transplant assessment. [ISHLT 2021]`]},{heading:`Prevention and counselling`,points:[`Explain that **even small, continued exposure** keeps the disease active - avoidance must be complete. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`**Public-health message in cities**: discourage feeding pigeons at balconies; use netting to stop roosting; clean droppings wet, wearing a mask. [Indian practice]`,`**Cooler hygiene** each summer and repair of damp walls and leaking AC units. [Indian practice]`,`Workplace controls in farms and sugar mills: **dry storage of hay and bagasse**, ventilation and respiratory protection. [Harrison 22e]`,`Counsel that **re-exposure can trigger relapse years later**, so avoidance is lifelong, and recheck the home if symptoms return. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020**: non-fibrotic vs fibrotic HP; HRCT typical/compatible/indeterminate; diagnostic confidence levels; BAL lymphocyte analysis and specific IgG suggested. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`**CHEST 2021**: parallel diagnostic guideline emphasising exposure assessment and BAL. [CHEST 2021]`,`**ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022**: progressive fibrotic HP is included under **PPF** with nintedanib suggested. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`**FIBRONEER-ILD (NEJM 2025)**: nerandomilast slowed FVC decline in PPF, which included fibrotic HP patients. [Maher TM, NEJM 2025]`,`Steroid dosing in HP remains **expert opinion**; no RCT has defined the dose or duration. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic short note: **'Hypersensitivity pneumonitis'** - give definition, antigen table with Indian examples, HRCT signs, BAL, avoidance and steroids.`,`Viva: '**What is the three-density sign?**' - normal lung, ground-glass and low-attenuation lobules side by side on one CT slice. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`**Common mistake**: treating with steroids while the bird or cooler stays in the house.`,`**Common mistake**: taking positive precipitins as diagnostic - they only show exposure.`,`Know that **smokers get HP less often**, and that HP is **the commonest ILD in India**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in hypersensitivity pneumonitis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Prednisolone`,`0.5 mg/kg/day orally (about 30-40 mg)`,`4-8 weeks, taper over 3-6 months`,`Hyperglycaemia, osteoporosis, infection, TB reactivation`],[`Mycophenolate mofetil`,`500 mg BD rising to 1-1.5 g BD orally`,`Long term (1-2 years or more)`,`Diarrhoea, cytopenia, infection; teratogenic`],[`Azathioprine`,`1-2 mg/kg/day orally`,`Long term`,`Marrow suppression (TPMT deficiency), hepatitis, pancreatitis`],[`Nintedanib`,`150 mg BD orally with food`,`Lifelong for progressive fibrotic HP`,`Diarrhoea, raised ALT, bleeding; contraindicated in pregnancy`],[`Cotrimoxazole prophylaxis`,`960 mg orally three times weekly`,`While on high-dose combined immunosuppression`,`Rash, hyperkalaemia, cytopenia`]]},{heading:`Antigens causing HP (with Indian examples)`,columns:[`Disease`,`Source`,`Antigen`],rows:[[`Bird fancier's / pigeon breeder's lung`,`Pigeons, parrots, budgerigars, feather pillows`,`Avian serum proteins, bloom, droppings`],[`Air cooler lung`,`Desert coolers with stagnant water and pads`,`Moulds, thermophilic actinomycetes`],[`Farmer's lung`,`Mouldy hay, grain`,`Saccharopolyspora rectivirgula`],[`Bagassosis`,`Mouldy sugarcane bagasse`,`Thermoactinomyces sacchari`],[`Mushroom worker's lung`,`Mushroom compost`,`Thermophilic actinomycetes`],[`Humidifier / AC lung`,`Contaminated humidifiers, AC units`,`Moulds, bacteria`],[`Hot tub lung`,`Indoor hot tubs`,`Mycobacterium avium complex`],[`Chemical worker's lung`,`Paints, foams, plastics`,`Isocyanates`]]},{heading:`Non-fibrotic vs fibrotic HP`,columns:[`Feature`,`Non-fibrotic HP`,`Fibrotic HP`],rows:[[`HRCT`,`Centrilobular nodules, GGO, mosaic attenuation`,`Reticulation, traction bronchiectasis, three-density sign, honeycombing`],[`Histology`,`Cellular bronchiolitis, interstitial pneumonia, poorly formed granulomas`,`Bronchiolocentric fibrosis, UIP-like or fibrotic NSIP`],[`BAL lymphocytes`,`Often over 40-50%`,`Lower, may be under 30%`],[`Reversibility`,`Usually reversible with avoidance`,`Partly irreversible`],[`Treatment`,`Avoidance with or without steroids`,`Avoidance, MMF, nintedanib if progressive`],[`Prognosis`,`Good`,`Can approach IPF`]]},{heading:`HP vs IPF vs sarcoidosis`,columns:[`Feature`,`HP`,`IPF`,`Sarcoidosis`],rows:[[`Age/sex`,`Any, often women in India`,`Over 60, men`,`20-50`],[`Exposure`,`Birds, moulds, coolers`,`None`,`None specific`],[`HRCT zone`,`Mid-upper, diffuse`,`Basal subpleural`,`Upper, perilymphatic`],[`Hallmark CT sign`,`Mosaic, centrilobular nodules`,`Honeycombing`,`Hilar nodes, perilymphatic nodules`],[`BAL`,`Lymphocytes over 30%, CD4/CD8 low`,`Neutrophils, lymphocytes under 30%`,`Lymphocytes, CD4/CD8 over 3.5`],[`Granuloma`,`Poorly formed`,`None`,`Well formed`]]}],redFlags:[`Resting hypoxaemia (SpO2 under 90%) or breathlessness at rest`,`Recurrent febrile episodes with bilateral infiltrates not responding to antibiotics`,`Rapid decline in FVC or DLCO despite avoidance`,`Fever, cough and weight loss with cavity or nodes - TB must be excluded`,`Signs of right heart failure`,`New infiltrates while on steroids or MMF - opportunistic infection`],pearls:[`**HP is the commonest ILD in India** (ILD-India registry, about 47%).`,`**Antigen avoidance is the treatment**; steroids only speed recovery.`,`**Air cooler lung** is the Indian HP - ask about coolers every summer.`,`**Three-density sign** (normal, ground-glass, air trapping) is highly specific for fibrotic HP.`,`**BAL lymphocytes 30% or more** supports HP; precipitins show exposure, not disease.`,`**Smokers are protected** from HP.`,`**Mid-inspiratory squeaks** = bronchiolitis = think HP.`,`Fibrotic HP that progresses = **PPF = nintedanib**.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-hypersensitivity-pneumonitis-q1`,stem:`A 42-year-old non-smoking woman from Jaipur has 6 months of dry cough and exertional breathlessness that are worse in summer. HRCT shows centrilobular ground-glass nodules and mosaic attenuation with air trapping on expiratory images. The most relevant history to seek is:`,options:[`Use of a desert air cooler at home`,`Long-term amiodarone`,`Work as a stone cutter`,`Childhood measles`],answer:0,explanation:`Centrilobular nodules with mosaic attenuation and air trapping are typical non-fibrotic HP, and summer worsening points to a contaminated evaporative air cooler, a recognised Indian source of moulds and actinomycetes. Amiodarone causes high-attenuation shadows and NSIP or OP; stone cutting causes silicosis with upper-zone nodules; childhood measles predisposes to bronchiectasis.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-hypersensitivity-pneumonitis-q2`,stem:`Which BAL finding best supports a diagnosis of hypersensitivity pneumonitis?`,options:[`Neutrophils 60% with lymphocytes 5%`,`Eosinophils 40%`,`Lymphocytes 55%`,`Haemosiderin-laden macrophages over 20%`],answer:2,explanation:`A marked BAL lymphocytosis (30% or more, often over 50%) supports HP in the right clinical and radiological setting. A neutrophil-predominant lavage fits IPF or infection, BAL eosinophilia over 25% suggests eosinophilic pneumonia, and haemosiderin-laden macrophages indicate diffuse alveolar haemorrhage.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-hypersensitivity-pneumonitis-q3`,stem:`A pigeon breeder with non-fibrotic HP asks what will most improve his long-term outcome. The best answer is:`,options:[`Lifelong prednisolone 10 mg daily`,`Wearing a surgical mask while handling birds`,`Starting pirfenidone`,`Complete removal from the antigen, including the birds and contaminated dust`],answer:3,explanation:`Antigen avoidance is the cornerstone of HP treatment and is linked to better survival; non-fibrotic disease is often fully reversible once exposure stops. Low-dose lifelong steroids do not replace avoidance and add toxicity. A surgical mask does not filter fine avian antigens adequately. Pirfenidone has no role in non-fibrotic HP.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-hypersensitivity-pneumonitis-q4`,stem:`On HRCT, the 'three-density pattern' in fibrotic hypersensitivity pneumonitis refers to:`,options:[`Honeycombing, emphysema and consolidation`,`Normal lung, ground-glass opacity and low-attenuation lobules with air trapping`,`Nodules, cavities and calcified nodes`,`Septal thickening, effusion and cardiomegaly`],answer:1,explanation:`The three-density (formerly headcheese) pattern shows lobules of normal lung, ground-glass inflammation and hypo-attenuating air-trapped lobules side by side, reflecting combined alveolitis and small-airway disease; it is highly specific for fibrotic HP. Honeycombing with emphysema suggests CPFE, cavities with nodes suggest TB, and septal thickening with effusion suggests heart failure.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-hypersensitivity-pneumonitis-q5`,stem:`Under the ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020 guideline, HP is classified primarily by:`,options:[`Duration: acute, subacute and chronic`,`Type of antigen: avian, microbial and chemical`,`Presence or absence of fibrosis on imaging or histology`,`Serum precipitin titre`],answer:2,explanation:`The 2020 guideline replaced the acute/subacute/chronic scheme with non-fibrotic and fibrotic HP because fibrosis, not duration, predicts outcome and treatment response. Antigen type guides avoidance but is not the classification, and precipitin titres show exposure only and do not stage disease.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-hypersensitivity-pneumonitis-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Discuss the aetiology, classification, diagnosis and management of hypersensitivity pneumonitis with reference to India.`,openingLines:[`Hypersensitivity pneumonitis is an immune-mediated ILD caused by inhaled antigens in a sensitised host, classified as non-fibrotic or fibrotic (ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020).`,`It is the commonest ILD in India (about 47% in the ILD-India registry), commonly from birds and air coolers.`],answer:[{heading:`Aetiology`,points:[`Avian proteins, thermophilic actinomycetes, moulds (air coolers, damp), bagasse, isocyanates; antigen unidentified in up to 30-60%.`,`Host susceptibility; non-smokers more often.`]},{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Type III and type IV reactions; triad of cellular bronchiolitis, interstitial pneumonia and poorly formed granulomas; fibrosis with persistence.`]},{heading:`Classification and clinical features`,points:[`Non-fibrotic vs fibrotic; flu-like episodes 4-8 hours after exposure or insidious dyspnoea; crackles and squeaks.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Exposure history, HRCT (centrilobular nodules, mosaic, three-density sign), BAL lymphocytes 30% or more, specific IgG, biopsy if needed, MDD with confidence levels.`,`Exclude TB and CTD.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Antigen avoidance (cooler hygiene, remove birds and feathers); prednisolone 0.5 mg/kg tapering; MMF or azathioprine; nintedanib for progressive fibrotic HP.`,`Rehab, oxygen, vaccines, PJP prophylaxis and bone protection; follow-up PFT every 3-6 months.`]},{heading:`Prognosis`,points:[`Non-fibrotic good with avoidance; fibrotic with honeycombing approaches IPF.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table of antigens with Indian examples`,`Diagnostic flow: exposure, HRCT, BAL, biopsy, MDD`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition, aetiology and pathogenesis`,marks:3},{part:`Classification and diagnosis`,marks:4},{part:`Management and prognosis`,marks:3}],keywords:[`HP`,`air cooler lung`,`BAL lymphocytosis`,`antigen avoidance`,`fibrotic HP`]},{id:`respiratory-hypersensitivity-pneumonitis-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Differentiate fibrotic hypersensitivity pneumonitis from idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis.`,openingLines:[`Fibrotic HP is the commonest mimic of IPF and the distinction matters because HP improves with antigen avoidance and immunosuppression.`,`The key discriminators are exposure, HRCT distribution, small-airway signs and BAL lymphocytosis.`],answer:[{heading:`Clinical`,points:[`HP: exposure, any age, non-smoker, squeaks; IPF: man over 60, ex-smoker, clubbing.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`HRCT: HP mid-upper with mosaic and three-density sign vs IPF basal subpleural UIP.`,`BAL: HP lymphocytes 30% or more vs IPF under 30%; histology bronchiolocentric with granulomas vs UIP.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`HP: avoidance, steroids/MMF, nintedanib if progressive; IPF: antifibrotics, no steroids.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column comparison table`],markSplit:[{part:`Clinical`,marks:1},{part:`HRCT, BAL, histology`,marks:3},{part:`Treatment and prognosis`,marks:1}],keywords:[`fibrotic HP`,`IPF`,`three-density sign`,`UIP`]}],references:[`Raghu G et al. Diagnosis of Hypersensitivity Pneumonitis in Adults: An Official ATS/JRS/ALAT Clinical Practice Guideline. Am J Respir Crit Care Med 2020`,`Fernandez Perez ER et al. Diagnosis and Evaluation of Hypersensitivity Pneumonitis: CHEST Guideline and Expert Panel Report, 2021`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Hypersensitivity pneumonitis and pulmonary infiltrates with eosinophilia`,`Singh S et al. Interstitial Lung Disease in India: Results of a Prospective Registry (ILD-India). Am J Respir Crit Care Med 2017`,`Raghu G et al. ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT Clinical Practice Guideline on IPF and Progressive Pulmonary Fibrosis, 2022`,`Flaherty KR et al. Nintedanib in Progressive Fibrosing Interstitial Lung Diseases (INBUILD). N Engl J Med 2019`]}),a.push({id:`respiratory-sarcoidosis`,title:`Sarcoidosis`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`**Sarcoidosis** is a multisystem disease of unknown cause characterised by **non-necrotising (non-caseating) epithelioid granulomas**, most often in the **lungs and hilar lymph nodes**; diagnosis needs a **compatible clinical picture, granulomas on biopsy and exclusion of other causes - above all tuberculosis in India** (ATS 2020), and many patients need no treatment, while those with organ-threatening disease receive **prednisolone 20-40 mg/day, with methotrexate as the steroid-sparing agent** (ERS 2021).`,keywords:[`sarcoid`,`non-caseating granuloma`,`bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy`,`BHL`,`Scadding stage`,`Lofgren syndrome`,`Heerfordt syndrome`,`lupus pernio`,`erythema nodosum`,`serum ACE`,`EBUS-TBNA`,`cardiac sarcoidosis`,`neurosarcoidosis`,`uveitis`,`hypercalcaemia`,`methotrexate`,`infliximab`,`CD4/CD8 ratio`,`FDG-PET`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Sarcoidosis** is a systemic granulomatous disease of unknown cause; the lungs or intrathoracic nodes are involved in **over 90%** of patients. [Harrison 22e]`,`Incidence varies about tenfold: roughly **10-20 per 100,000 per year in northern Europe and up to 35-70 in African-American women**, lower in East Asia. [Harrison 22e]`,`Peak onset is **20-50 years**, with a second peak in women over 50; women are slightly more affected. [Harrison 22e]`,`In India sarcoidosis was once thought rare, but tertiary centres now report it regularly and it formed a distinct share of the **ILD-India registry**; true prevalence is unknown. [ILD-India registry 2017]`,`In India the main problem is **misdiagnosis as tuberculosis**: many patients receive one or more ATT courses before the diagnosis is made. [Indian J Chest Dis Allied Sci]`,`About **half to two-thirds** of patients remit within 2-5 years; **10-30%** develop chronic progressive disease. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`Cause unknown; the leading theory is an **exaggerated Th1/Th17 immune response to an unidentified antigen** in a genetically susceptible host. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Candidate antigens**: mycobacterial proteins (mKatG, ESAT-6), Propionibacterium acnes, and inorganic dusts - mycobacterial DNA is found in some granulomas but the disease is not infectious. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Genetic factors**: HLA-DRB1 alleles; **HLA-DRB1*03 is linked to Lofgren syndrome** and a good prognosis; family clustering raises risk several-fold. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Exposures** linked in studies: agricultural dust, insecticides, moulds, metal dust, firefighting and World Trade Center dust. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Beryllium** causes chronic beryllium disease, a sarcoid mimic - always ask about aerospace, electronics and dental alloy work. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Non-smokers** are affected more than smokers. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drug-induced sarcoid-like reactions** occur with checkpoint inhibitors, interferon, anti-TNF agents and antiretroviral immune reconstitution. [ERS 2021]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Antigen-presenting cells present antigen via HLA class II to **CD4+ Th1 cells**, which release **IFN-gamma, IL-2 and TNF-alpha**, recruiting macrophages that become epithelioid cells and giant cells. [Harrison 22e]`,`The **granuloma** is tightly formed and **non-necrotising**, with a rim of lymphocytes and may contain **Schaumann and asteroid bodies** (not specific). [Harrison 22e]`,`**TNF-alpha** sustains granulomas - which is why **infliximab** works, and why anti-TNF therapy can reactivate TB. [ERS 2021]`,`Activated granuloma macrophages express **1-alpha hydroxylase**, converting 25(OH)D to **1,25(OH)2D** without feedback - causing **hypercalciuria (common) and hypercalcaemia (about 10%)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Granuloma epithelioid cells produce **ACE**, so serum ACE reflects total granuloma load, not diagnosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Compartmentalisation of CD4 cells in the lung lowers peripheral CD4 counts and causes **cutaneous anergy** (negative tuberculin test). [Harrison 22e]`,`Lung granulomas follow **lymphatics** (bronchovascular bundles, interlobular septa, subpleura, fissures), giving the perilymphatic CT pattern; persistent granulomas heal by **fibrosis** in upper zones. [Fleischner Society 2018]`]},{heading:`Classification - Scadding stages and syndromes`,points:[`**Scadding staging** is based on the chest X-ray, not on severity: **stage 0** normal, **I** bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy (BHL) only, **II** BHL plus parenchymal infiltrates, **III** infiltrates without BHL, **IV** fibrosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Spontaneous remission falls with stage: about **55-90% in stage I, 40-70% in stage II, 10-20% in stage III and 0% in stage IV**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lofgren syndrome**: acute **erythema nodosum, BHL, fever and polyarthralgia or ankle periarthritis** - over 90% remit within 2 years and biopsy is not needed. [ATS 2020]`,`**Heerfordt syndrome (uveoparotid fever)**: **uveitis, parotid enlargement, facial nerve palsy and fever**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Organ involvement** is graded with the **WASOG 2014 organ assessment instrument** (highly probable, at least probable, possible). [WASOG 2014]`,`**Cardiac sarcoidosis** is classified by HRS 2014 or JCS 2016 criteria (histology, or extracardiac sarcoid plus imaging or ECG features). [HRS 2014]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Up to **30-50% are asymptomatic**, found by BHL on a routine or pre-employment chest X-ray. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Respiratory**: dry cough, exertional breathlessness, chest discomfort; wheeze from endobronchial disease; haemoptysis is rare (think TB or aspergilloma in fibrotic cavities). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Constitutional**: fatigue (very common and disabling), low-grade fever, weight loss and night sweats - easily mistaken for TB. [ERS 2021]`,`**Eyes**: redness, pain, photophobia or blurred vision (anterior uveitis), floaters (posterior uveitis); dry eyes - eye involvement in about 20-30%. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Heart**: palpitations, syncope, heart block or sudden death - clinically in about 5% but found at autopsy in about 25%. [HRS 2014]`,`**Nervous system** (about 5-10%): facial palsy, other cranial neuropathies, aseptic meningitis, hypothalamic-pituitary disease (diabetes insipidus), and small-fibre neuropathy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Calcium**: polyuria, thirst, renal colic and kidney stones from hypercalciuria. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Joints and skin**: ankle swelling, painful red shins (erythema nodosum), skin plaques, papules or changes in old scars and tattoos. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Chest is often **clear despite marked X-ray changes** - a classic dissociation; crackles are uncommon except in fibrosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Clubbing is rare** in sarcoidosis; its presence suggests another diagnosis or advanced fibrosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Erythema nodosum**: tender red nodules on the shins (non-specific, also TB, streptococcal, drugs, IBD, leprosy). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lupus pernio**: violaceous indurated plaques on nose, cheeks and ears - associated with chronic disease, upper-airway and bone involvement. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Peripheral lymphadenopathy** (non-tender, rubbery), **hepatosplenomegaly**, parotid swelling, and **lacrimal gland** enlargement. [Harrison 22e]`,`Eye signs: ciliary flush, keratic precipitates, iris nodules on slit lamp; facial nerve palsy (LMN). [Harrison 22e]`,`Cardiac: bradycardia or irregular pulse; signs of heart failure or right heart strain in sarcoid-associated PH. [HRS 2014]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Tuberculosis** is the first exclusion in India: **necrotising granulomas**, AFB/CBNAAT or culture positive, asymmetrical or necrotic nodes, and usually a strongly positive tuberculin or IGRA. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Lymphoma**: B symptoms, bulky asymmetrical anterior mediastinal nodes, raised LDH; diagnose by core or excision biopsy, not FNA alone. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Metastatic carcinoma** and **sarcoid-like reaction** in nodes draining a cancer. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fungal infection** (histoplasmosis, cryptococcosis), **brucellosis** and **leprosy** cause granulomas - do special stains and cultures. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypersensitivity pneumonitis**: poorly formed granulomas, exposure history, no nodes. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`**Chronic beryllium disease, silicosis** (eggshell nodes) and **GPA** (necrotising granulomas with vasculitis, ANCA). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drug-induced sarcoid-like reaction** (checkpoint inhibitors, interferon) and **CVID** (granulomatous-lymphocytic ILD with low immunoglobulins). [ERS 2021]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Chest X-ray**: symmetrical BHL with right paratracheal node - the **1-2-3 (Garland) sign**; reticulonodular upper-zone shadows. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HRCT/CECT thorax**: **perilymphatic nodules** along bronchovascular bundles, fissures and subpleura, upper-mid zone predominance, symmetrical hilar and mediastinal nodes (may calcify), and upper-lobe fibrosis with hilar retraction in stage IV. [Fleischner Society 2018]`,`**Tissue diagnosis**: **EBUS-TBNA** of mediastinal nodes is suggested over mediastinoscopy (yield about 80-90% in stage I-II); add **transbronchial or endobronchial biopsy**; easiest sites are **skin, peripheral nodes, conjunctiva or salivary glands**. [ATS 2020]`,`**Every biopsy in India needs AFB smear, CBNAAT (Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra) and mycobacterial culture**, plus fungal stains, before calling granulomas sarcoid. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Serum ACE** is raised in about 60% but lacks sensitivity and specificity (also raised in TB, leprosy, silicosis, HP, hyperthyroidism, diabetes, Gaucher) and falls with ACE inhibitors - **ATS 2020 does not support it for diagnosis**. [ATS 2020]`,`**Baseline tests recommended by ATS 2020**: serum **calcium**, creatinine and **alkaline phosphatase**, CBC, **12-lead ECG** for all, and an **eye examination** for all. [ATS 2020]`,`Check **24-hour urine calcium**, and measure both **25(OH)D and 1,25(OH)2D** before giving vitamin D. [ATS 2020]`,`**BAL**: lymphocytosis with **CD4/CD8 ratio over 3.5** is fairly specific (about 90%) but only about 50% sensitive. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PFT**: restriction or obstruction (endobronchial disease or fibrosis) and reduced DLCO; **6MWT**; echo for PH. [ERS 2021]`,`**Cardiac work-up** if ECG, Holter or symptoms are abnormal: **cardiac MRI (late gadolinium enhancement)** first, or **FDG-PET** with a ketogenic diet preparation; **FDG-PET-CT** also finds a biopsy site and active disease. [ATS 2020]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD`,points:[`**Treat to prevent death or permanent organ damage, or to improve quality of life** - not to normalise the X-ray; many stage I and asymptomatic stage II patients need only observation. [ERS 2021]`,`**Lofgren syndrome**: NSAIDs (e.g. **naproxen 250-500 mg twice daily**) for arthralgia and erythema nodosum; short low-dose prednisolone if severe. [ERS 2021]`,`**Pulmonary sarcoidosis needing treatment**: **prednisolone 20-40 mg/day** (about 0.3-0.5 mg/kg) for **2-6 weeks**, then taper to **5-10 mg/day**, total about **6-12 months**; higher doses add toxicity, not benefit. [ERS 2021]`,`**Methotrexate 10-20 mg once weekly** with **folic acid 5 mg weekly** (on another day) is the first steroid-sparing agent if disease persists or steroid toxicity occurs; it takes about 6 months to act. [ERS 2021]`,`**Alternatives**: azathioprine 50-150 mg/day, leflunomide 10-20 mg/day, mycophenolate 1-1.5 g twice daily; **hydroxychloroquine 200-400 mg/day** for skin disease and hypercalcaemia. [ERS 2021]`,`**Infliximab 3-5 mg/kg IV** at weeks 0, 2 and then every 4-8 weeks for refractory pulmonary, cardiac, neurological or lupus pernio disease - **screen for latent TB (IGRA, CXR) and hepatitis B first**. [ERS 2021]`,`**Inhaled steroids** are not effective for parenchymal disease but may help cough or airway hyperreactivity. [ERS 2021]`,`**Hypercalcaemia**: hydration, avoid vitamin D supplements and sunlight excess, and prednisolone (usually responds within days); hydroxychloroquine or ketoconazole if steroids fail. [ERS 2021]`,`**Fatigue**: exclude hypothyroidism, depression, OSA and small-fibre neuropathy; **pulmonary rehabilitation** and a trial of methylphenidate are suggested. [ERS 2021]`,`**Anterior uveitis**: topical steroid and cycloplegic under an ophthalmologist; posterior uveitis needs systemic therapy. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD`,points:[`**Admit** for complete heart block, ventricular tachycardia, syncope, **severe hypercalcaemia (over 3 mmol/L or 12 mg/dL)** with AKI, neurosarcoidosis or respiratory failure. [ERS 2021]`,`**Cardiac sarcoidosis**: prednisolone (often 30-40 mg/day) with or without methotrexate; infliximab for persistent disease; **pacemaker or ICD** for high-grade block or VT/ reduced EF - heart block in a young person (under 60) should prompt a search for sarcoid. [HRS 2014, ERS 2021]`,`**Neurosarcoidosis**: high-dose steroids (IV methylprednisolone pulses for severe disease), methotrexate, and **infliximab is recommended** for persistent disease. [ERS 2021]`,`**Severe hypercalcaemia**: IV normal saline, bisphosphonate if needed, and prednisolone 20-40 mg/day. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Haemoptysis in fibrocystic stage IV**: look for **aspergilloma** and TB; bronchial artery embolisation if massive (see the Haemoptysis topic). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**India and TB**: tuberculin is often negative (anergy) but IGRA may be positive from latent infection; consider **TB preventive treatment** before high-dose steroids or anti-TNF in IGRA-positive patients. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Never give an empirical 'ATT trial'** to diagnose sarcoidosis - it delays diagnosis and causes drug toxicity; get tissue and microbiology instead. [ATS 2020]`,`**Pregnancy**: sarcoidosis often improves in pregnancy and may relapse postpartum; prednisolone, azathioprine and hydroxychloroquine are acceptable; **methotrexate and leflunomide are contraindicated** (stop 3 months before conception; leflunomide needs washout). [ERS 2021]`,`**Diabetes and osteoporosis**: use the lowest steroid dose, add bone protection, and consider early methotrexate. [ERS 2021]`,`**Vitamin D**: avoid blind supplementation - check 1,25(OH)2D and urinary calcium first. [ATS 2020]`,`**Elderly and immunosuppressed**: lymphoma and TB are more likely mimics; biopsy is mandatory. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Overall mortality is about **1-5%**, mainly from **pulmonary fibrosis with pulmonary hypertension** and **cardiac sarcoidosis** (heart block, VT, sudden death). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sarcoid-associated pulmonary hypertension** (group 5) in about 5-20% of chronic disease, with poor survival. [ESC/ERS 2022]`,`**Stage IV fibrocystic disease**: bronchiectasis, **aspergilloma**, chronic pulmonary aspergillosis and recurrent infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nephrocalcinosis, kidney stones and CKD** from hypercalciuria; **blindness** from untreated uveitis or glaucoma. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Poor prognostic factors**: age over 40 at onset, **lupus pernio**, chronic uveitis, cardiac or neuro involvement, nephrocalcinosis, stage III-IV, and African ancestry. [Harrison 22e]`,`Relapse after stopping steroids occurs in about **20-70%**, usually within the first year. [ERS 2021]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer** suspected sarcoidosis to a chest physician for EBUS-TBNA and TB exclusion; refer all patients for a **baseline eye examination**. [ATS 2020]`,`Untreated patients: review **every 3-6 months for 2 years** with symptoms, CXR, **spirometry and DLCO**, calcium and creatinine. [ERS 2021]`,`On treatment: PFT at 3 months; **methotrexate needs CBC, LFT and creatinine every 4-12 weeks**; avoid alcohol. [ERS 2021]`,`**Annual ECG and eye examination**, plus urinary calcium and renal function. [ATS 2020]`,`Refer **refractory, cardiac, neuro, ocular posterior or PH cases** to tertiary centres; transplant referral for advanced fibrosis or PH. [ISHLT 2021]`]},{heading:`Prevention and counselling`,points:[`No primary prevention exists; explain that **most patients recover without treatment** and that it is **not infectious and not cancer**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Counsel to **report palpitations, fainting, eye redness or visual change immediately**. [ERS 2021]`,`Avoid **unprescribed vitamin D and calcium supplements** and excessive sun exposure if calcium is high. [ATS 2020]`,`On steroids: **sick-day rules**, bone protection, glucose checks, and vaccines (influenza, pneumococcal; avoid live vaccines on high-dose immunosuppression). [ACIP 2024]`,`Avoid occupational dusts and smoking; consider beryllium exposure in at-risk workers. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ATS 2020 diagnosis and detection guideline**: diagnosis rests on three criteria; **no node sampling needed in Lofgren, lupus pernio or Heerfordt**; EBUS-TBNA preferred over mediastinoscopy; baseline calcium, creatinine, ALP, ECG and eye exam for all. [ATS 2020]`,`**ERS 2021 treatment guideline**: glucocorticoids for symptomatic pulmonary disease at risk; **methotrexate** as second line; **infliximab** for persistent disease; treatment for cardiac, neuro and fatigue defined. [ERS 2021]`,`**BTS Clinical Statement on pulmonary sarcoidosis (2021)** supports the same thresholds and a low initial steroid dose. [BTS 2021]`,`**2025**: the phase 3 EFZO-FIT trial of efzofitimod for pulmonary sarcoidosis did not meet its primary steroid-sparing endpoint - steroids and methotrexate remain the mainstay. [aTyr Pharma 2025]`,`Growing evidence supports **methotrexate as an alternative first-line drug** in selected patients to reduce steroid exposure, though steroids remain the standard first line. [ERS 2021]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Viva: '**How will you differentiate sarcoidosis from TB in India?**' - necrosis, microbiology (CBNAAT, culture), node pattern, ACE limits, response to steroids is NOT a diagnostic test.`,`List **causes of BHL**: sarcoidosis, TB, lymphoma, metastasis, fungal, silicosis, berylliosis, HIV.`,`Know the **Scadding stages with their remission rates**.`,`Know **Lofgren and Heerfordt syndromes** and why Lofgren needs no biopsy.`,`**Common mistake**: treating an asymptomatic stage I patient with steroids.`,`**Common mistake**: giving vitamin D to a sarcoid patient with low 25(OH)D without checking calcium and 1,25(OH)2D.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in sarcoidosis (ERS 2021)`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Prednisolone`,`20-40 mg/day orally`,`2-6 weeks, taper to 5-10 mg; total 6-12 months`,`Weight gain, diabetes, osteoporosis, TB reactivation, cataract`],[`Methotrexate`,`10-20 mg orally or SC once weekly + folic acid 5 mg weekly`,`At least 6-12 months; effect by 6 months`,`Hepatotoxicity, cytopenia, pneumonitis; teratogenic; avoid alcohol`],[`Azathioprine`,`50-150 mg/day orally (2 mg/kg)`,`Long term`,`Marrow suppression (TPMT), hepatitis, pancreatitis`],[`Leflunomide`,`10-20 mg/day orally`,`Long term`,`Diarrhoea, hepatotoxicity, hypertension; teratogenic`],[`Hydroxychloroquine`,`200-400 mg/day orally (max 5 mg/kg)`,`Months to years`,`Retinopathy - annual eye check after 5 years`],[`Infliximab`,`3-5 mg/kg IV weeks 0, 2, then every 4-8 weeks`,`Refractory disease`,`TB reactivation, infections, heart failure worsening; screen IGRA and HBV`],[`Naproxen (Lofgren)`,`250-500 mg BD orally`,`Weeks`,`Dyspepsia, renal impairment`]]},{heading:`Scadding chest X-ray stages`,columns:[`Stage`,`Chest X-ray`,`Approximate spontaneous remission`],rows:[[`0`,`Normal (extrapulmonary disease)`,`Not applicable`],[`I`,`Bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy only`,`55-90%`],[`II`,`BHL with parenchymal infiltrates`,`40-70%`],[`III`,`Parenchymal infiltrates without BHL`,`10-20%`],[`IV`,`Fibrosis, volume loss, cysts, hilar retraction`,`0%`]]},{heading:`Sarcoidosis versus tuberculosis`,columns:[`Feature`,`Sarcoidosis`,`Tuberculosis`],rows:[[`Granuloma`,`Tight, non-necrotising`,`Caseating (necrotising)`],[`Microbiology`,`AFB, CBNAAT, culture negative`,`AFB, CBNAAT or culture positive`],[`Nodes`,`Symmetrical BHL with right paratracheal`,`Often unilateral, necrotic, rim-enhancing`],[`Lung parenchyma`,`Perilymphatic nodules, upper zone`,`Tree-in-bud, cavities, consolidation`],[`Tuberculin test`,`Often negative (anergy)`,`Usually positive`],[`Clinical well-being`,`Often well despite big X-ray`,`Unwell, weight loss, fever`],[`Response`,`Steroids (not diagnostic)`,`ATT`]]},{heading:`Baseline and organ screening tests (ATS 2020)`,columns:[`Test`,`Why`,`Abnormal result / action`],rows:[[`Serum calcium, 24-h urine calcium`,`Granuloma 1,25(OH)2D production`,`Hypercalcaemia or hypercalciuria - treat; avoid vitamin D`],[`Creatinine, ALP`,`Renal and hepatic involvement`,`Raised - look for nephrocalcinosis or hepatic granulomas`],[`12-lead ECG (all)`,`Screen cardiac sarcoid`,`Any block or arrhythmia - Holter, echo, cardiac MRI or FDG-PET`],[`Ophthalmology exam (all)`,`Uveitis may be silent`,`Uveitis - topical or systemic therapy`],[`Spirometry and DLCO`,`Pulmonary severity`,`Restriction, obstruction, low DLCO - consider treatment`],[`Serum ACE`,`Granuloma load`,`Not for diagnosis; may help follow-up`],[`Echocardiography`,`Pulmonary hypertension`,`Raised PASP - right heart catheter`]]},{heading:`Indications for treatment (ERS 2021)`,columns:[`Organ`,`Treat when`,`First line`],rows:[[`Lung`,`Symptomatic with impaired or falling PFT, or at risk of mortality or disability`,`Prednisolone 20-40 mg/day`],[`Heart`,`Any confirmed cardiac sarcoid`,`Steroids with or without methotrexate; device therapy`],[`Nervous system`,`Any significant neurosarcoidosis`,`Steroids, then methotrexate or infliximab`],[`Eye`,`Anterior uveitis / posterior uveitis`,`Topical steroid / systemic therapy`],[`Calcium`,`Hypercalcaemia or nephrocalcinosis`,`Prednisolone; hydroxychloroquine`],[`Skin`,`Lupus pernio or disfiguring lesions`,`Hydroxychloroquine, methotrexate, infliximab`]]}],redFlags:[`Syncope, palpitations or heart block on ECG - cardiac sarcoidosis`,`Serum calcium over 3 mmol/L (12 mg/dL) or acute kidney injury`,`Red painful eye or visual loss`,`Facial palsy, seizures, headache or polyuria - neurosarcoidosis`,`Falling FVC/DLCO or rising PASP on echo`,`Fever, weight loss and necrotic nodes - TB or lymphoma not yet excluded`,`Haemoptysis in stage IV disease - aspergilloma`],pearls:[`**Sarcoidosis is a diagnosis of exclusion - in India exclude TB on every biopsy (AFB, CBNAAT, culture).**`,`**Well patient, big X-ray** - classic sarcoid dissociation.`,`**1-2-3 (Garland) sign**: right paratracheal plus both hila.`,`**Lofgren = EN + BHL + fever + ankle arthritis**; good prognosis, no biopsy.`,`**Heerfordt = uveitis + parotid + facial palsy + fever.**`,`**ACE is neither sensitive nor specific**; do not diagnose or exclude with it.`,`**ECG and eye exam for every patient** (ATS 2020).`,`**Prednisolone 20-40 mg is enough**; methotrexate is the steroid-sparer; infliximab for refractory (screen TB).`,`**Never an empirical ATT trial** - get tissue.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-sarcoidosis-q1`,stem:`A 34-year-old woman has painful red nodules on both shins, fever, swollen ankles and bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy on chest X-ray. What is the most appropriate management?`,options:[`EBUS-TBNA of mediastinal nodes before any treatment`,`Start four-drug antitubercular therapy`,`NSAIDs and observation, without biopsy`,`Prednisolone 1 mg/kg for 1 year`],answer:2,explanation:`This is Lofgren syndrome (erythema nodosum, BHL, fever and ankle periarthritis), which has a high spontaneous remission rate; ATS 2020 suggests no lymph node sampling, and NSAIDs with follow-up are adequate. Node biopsy adds risk without changing care. ATT without microbiological evidence is inappropriate, and high-dose year-long steroids are unnecessary and toxic.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-sarcoidosis-q2`,stem:`Hypercalcaemia in sarcoidosis is mainly caused by:`,options:[`Parathyroid hormone-related peptide from granulomas`,`Extrarenal 1-alpha hydroxylase activity in granuloma macrophages`,`Bone destruction by granulomas`,`Secondary hyperparathyroidism`],answer:1,explanation:`Activated macrophages in granulomas express 1-alpha hydroxylase that converts 25(OH)D to 1,25(OH)2D without normal feedback, increasing gut calcium absorption and causing hypercalciuria and hypercalcaemia. PTHrP is the mechanism in squamous cancers; bony sarcoid is uncommon and not the main cause; PTH is suppressed, not raised.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-sarcoidosis-q3`,stem:`A 45-year-old man with biopsy-proven pulmonary sarcoidosis is found to have a new second-degree Mobitz II block on ECG. Which is the most appropriate next investigation?`,options:[`Repeat serum ACE level`,`Cardiac MRI with late gadolinium enhancement`,`Spirometry`,`Tuberculin skin test`],answer:1,explanation:`Heart block in a patient with sarcoidosis suggests cardiac involvement; cardiac MRI (or FDG-PET) is the recommended imaging and guides steroids and device therapy (pacemaker or ICD). Serum ACE reflects granuloma load but not cardiac involvement. Spirometry assesses the lung only, and a tuberculin test does not evaluate the conduction defect.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-sarcoidosis-q4`,stem:`Regarding serum ACE in sarcoidosis, which statement is correct?`,options:[`A normal level excludes sarcoidosis`,`A raised level is diagnostic in a patient with BHL`,`Levels may be raised in TB, silicosis and hyperthyroidism and fall with ACE inhibitors`,`It is recommended by ATS 2020 as the first diagnostic test`],answer:2,explanation:`Serum ACE is raised in only about 60% of sarcoidosis and also in TB, leprosy, silicosis, HP, hyperthyroidism and diabetes, and ACE inhibitors lower it, so it neither confirms nor excludes the diagnosis. A normal value does not exclude disease, a raised value with BHL is still non-diagnostic, and ATS 2020 does not support using it to diagnose sarcoidosis.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-sarcoidosis-q5`,stem:`A patient with symptomatic stage II pulmonary sarcoidosis has relapsed twice when prednisolone was tapered below 10 mg/day and now has steroid-induced diabetes. According to ERS 2021, the preferred next agent is:`,options:[`Infliximab`,`Inhaled budesonide`,`Cyclophosphamide`,`Methotrexate`],answer:3,explanation:`ERS 2021 suggests methotrexate as the steroid-sparing second-line drug for persistent disease or steroid toxicity, usually 10-20 mg weekly with folic acid. Infliximab is reserved for disease persisting despite steroids and methotrexate (third line, after TB screening). Inhaled steroids do not control parenchymal sarcoid, and cyclophosphamide is reserved for refractory neuro or cardiac disease.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-sarcoidosis-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Describe the clinical features, diagnosis and management of sarcoidosis. How will you differentiate it from tuberculosis in India?`,openingLines:[`Sarcoidosis is a multisystem disease of unknown cause with non-necrotising granulomas, involving the lungs and intrathoracic nodes in over 90%.`,`Diagnosis needs a compatible picture, non-necrotising granulomas and exclusion of other causes - especially TB in India (ATS 2020).`],answer:[{heading:`Pathogenesis and staging`,points:[`Th1 response, TNF-alpha, epithelioid granulomas; 1-alpha hydroxylase and ACE production.`,`Scadding stages 0-IV with remission rates.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Asymptomatic BHL; cough, dyspnoea, fatigue; eye, skin (EN, lupus pernio), heart, nervous system, calcium, joints.`,`Lofgren and Heerfordt syndromes.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`CXR (1-2-3 sign), HRCT perilymphatic nodules; EBUS-TBNA; skin or node biopsy.`,`AFB, CBNAAT and culture on every specimen; calcium, creatinine, ALP, ECG, eye exam; ACE limited; BAL CD4/CD8 over 3.5.`]},{heading:`Sarcoidosis vs TB`,points:[`Necrosis, microbiology, node pattern, tuberculin anergy, general well-being; no empirical ATT trial.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Observe stage I and asymptomatic; NSAIDs for Lofgren.`,`Prednisolone 20-40 mg/day tapered over 6-12 months; methotrexate second line; infliximab third line after TB screening.`,`Cardiac: steroids plus devices; hypercalcaemia: hydration, steroids; uveitis: topical/systemic.`]},{heading:`Follow-up and prognosis`,points:[`PFT, calcium, ECG and eye exam; mortality 1-5% from fibrosis-PH and heart.`]}],mustDraw:[`Scadding stages table`,`Sarcoidosis vs TB comparison table`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition, pathogenesis and staging`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis and TB differentiation`,marks:3},{part:`Management and follow-up`,marks:3}],keywords:[`sarcoidosis`,`non-caseating granuloma`,`EBUS-TBNA`,`Scadding`,`methotrexate`,`TB`]},{id:`respiratory-sarcoidosis-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy.`,openingLines:[`Bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy is symmetrical enlargement of hilar nodes on chest imaging; in India the main causes are sarcoidosis and tuberculosis.`,`Tissue diagnosis by EBUS-TBNA with microbiology is the key step.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes`,points:[`Sarcoidosis, TB, lymphoma, metastatic carcinoma, fungal infection, silicosis (eggshell), berylliosis, HIV, drug-induced sarcoid-like reaction.`]},{heading:`Approach`,points:[`History of fever, weight loss, EN, uveitis, occupation; CECT thorax; EBUS-TBNA with AFB, CBNAAT, culture, cytology, flow cytometry.`]},{heading:`Pointers`,points:[`Symmetrical with right paratracheal and well patient: sarcoid; necrotic rim-enhancing: TB; bulky anterior mediastinal with B symptoms: lymphoma.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow: BHL to CECT to EBUS-TBNA with microbiology`],markSplit:[{part:`Causes`,marks:2},{part:`Approach and investigations`,marks:2},{part:`Discriminating features`,marks:1}],keywords:[`BHL`,`sarcoidosis`,`tuberculosis`,`lymphoma`,`EBUS`]}],references:[`Crouser ED et al. Diagnosis and Detection of Sarcoidosis: An Official ATS Clinical Practice Guideline. Am J Respir Crit Care Med 2020`,`Baughman RP et al. ERS Clinical Practice Guidelines on Treatment of Sarcoidosis. Eur Respir J 2021`,`Thillai M et al. BTS Clinical Statement on Pulmonary Sarcoidosis. Thorax 2021`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Sarcoidosis`,`Birnie DH et al. HRS Expert Consensus Statement on the Diagnosis and Management of Arrhythmias Associated With Cardiac Sarcoidosis, 2014`,`Judson MA et al. The WASOG Sarcoidosis Organ Assessment Instrument, 2014`,`National TB Elimination Programme (NTEP), Training Modules and Guidelines for Programmatic Management, MoHFW 2024`]}),a.push({id:`respiratory-ctd-drug-ild`,title:`Connective-tissue disease ILD and drug-induced lung disease`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`**CTD-ILD** is interstitial lung disease caused by a systemic autoimmune rheumatic disease - most often **systemic sclerosis (NSIP pattern, anti-Scl-70), rheumatoid arthritis (UIP pattern) and inflammatory myositis (anti-synthetase, anti-MDA5)** - screened for with **HRCT and PFT** (ACR/CHEST 2023) and treated with **mycophenolate first line, plus tocilizumab, rituximab or nintedanib**; **drug-induced ILD** (amiodarone, methotrexate, nitrofurantoin, bleomycin, checkpoint inhibitors) is diagnosed by timing and exclusion and managed by **stopping the drug and giving steroids** by severity.`,keywords:[`CTD-ILD`,`SSc-ILD`,`RA-ILD`,`anti-synthetase syndrome`,`anti-MDA5`,`rapidly progressive ILD`,`NSIP`,`mycophenolate`,`tocilizumab`,`rituximab`,`cyclophosphamide`,`nintedanib`,`amiodarone lung`,`methotrexate pneumonitis`,`nitrofurantoin lung`,`bleomycin toxicity`,`checkpoint inhibitor pneumonitis`,`pneumotox`,`IPAF`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**CTD-ILD** is ILD occurring in systemic autoimmune rheumatic diseases (SARDs): systemic sclerosis (SSc), rheumatoid arthritis (RA), idiopathic inflammatory myopathy (IIM), Sjogren disease, SLE and mixed CTD (MCTD). [ACR/CHEST 2023]`,`**SSc**: ILD on HRCT in about **50-65%**, clinically significant in about 25-40%; ILD is now the **leading cause of SSc death**. [EULAR 2023]`,`**RA**: clinically evident ILD in about **5-10%**, subclinical HRCT changes in up to 30%; RA-ILD shortens survival, especially with UIP. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Myositis**: ILD in about **20-80%** depending on antibody - near-universal in anti-synthetase syndrome and frequent rapidly progressive ILD with **anti-MDA5**, especially in Asians. [Harrison 22e]`,`In the **ILD-India registry CTD-ILD was about 14%** of ILD, the second commonest after HP; RA and SSc were the main causes. [Singh S, AJRCCM 2017]`,`**Interstitial pneumonia with autoimmune features (IPAF)** describes ILD with some autoimmune clinical, serological or morphological features that do not meet any CTD criteria. [ERS/ATS 2015]`,`**Drug-induced ILD (DI-ILD)** accounts for about **3-5% of ILD**; the **Pneumotox** database (pneumotox.com) lists well over 1,000 culprit drugs. [Pneumotox 2024]`,`Drug toxicity is rising with **anticancer therapies**: checkpoint inhibitor pneumonitis occurs in about **3-5%** (higher with combinations and in lung cancer) and trastuzumab deruxtecan ILD in about 10-15%. [ASCO 2021]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**SSc-ILD risk**: **diffuse cutaneous SSc, anti-Scl-70 (topoisomerase I)**, anti-Th/To, early disease (first 3-5 years), male sex and African ancestry; **anti-centromere is protective**. [EULAR 2023]`,`**RA-ILD risk**: **older age, male sex, smoking, high-titre RF and anti-CCP**, and the **MUC5B promoter variant** (shared with IPF). [ACR/CHEST 2023]`,`**Myositis-ILD antibodies**: **anti-Jo-1** (commonest anti-synthetase), PL-7, PL-12 (ILD-dominant), EJ, OJ; **anti-MDA5** (amyopathic dermatomyositis with rapidly progressive ILD); anti-Ro52 adds risk. [ACR/CHEST 2023]`,`**Sjogren**: NSIP, **lymphocytic interstitial pneumonia (LIP)**, cysts and follicular bronchiolitis; risk of MALT lymphoma. [Harrison 22e]`,`**SLE**: ILD less common; acute lupus pneumonitis, **diffuse alveolar haemorrhage**, pleuritis and **shrinking lung syndrome** (diaphragm dysfunction). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drug toxicity risk factors**: high cumulative dose (bleomycin, amiodarone), older age, renal impairment, pre-existing ILD, oxygen and radiotherapy, combination therapy and **Japanese ancestry for EGFR-TKI ILD**. [Pneumotox 2024]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**SSc-ILD**: endothelial injury and autoimmunity drive TGF-beta and **IL-6** signalling with fibroblast activation - the rationale for **tocilizumab (anti-IL-6R)** and **nintedanib**. [EULAR 2023]`,`Most CTD-ILD shows **NSIP**, a homogeneous (temporally uniform) cellular or fibrotic pattern that is more steroid- and immunosuppressant-responsive than UIP. [Harrison 22e]`,`**RA-ILD** is the exception: **UIP is the commonest pattern** and behaves like IPF, so antifibrotics are used in progressive cases. [ACR/CHEST 2023]`,`**Anti-MDA5** disease is an interferon-driven vasculopathy with macrophage activation (very high **ferritin**), causing diffuse alveolar damage and rapidly progressive ILD. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drugs injure the lung** by direct cytotoxicity (bleomycin free radicals, busulfan), **phospholipidosis** (amiodarone foamy macrophages), immune hypersensitivity (methotrexate, nitrofurantoin) and **T-cell disinhibition** (checkpoint inhibitors). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bleomycin** is inactivated by bleomycin hydrolase, which is scarce in lung and skin - so those organs are hit; it is renally cleared, so **renal impairment raises toxicity**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Drugs produce **any histological pattern**: NSIP, organising pneumonia, HP-like, DAD, eosinophilic pneumonia, alveolar haemorrhage and pulmonary oedema. [Pneumotox 2024]`]},{heading:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**HRCT/histology patterns by CTD**: SSc - fibrotic NSIP; RA - UIP more than NSIP; myositis - NSIP with organising pneumonia (NSIP-OP); Sjogren - NSIP, LIP; SLE - acute pneumonitis, DAH. [ACR/CHEST 2023]`,`**SSc-ILD extent** (Goh staging): **extensive disease if HRCT extent over 20%**, or 10-30% with FVC under 70% - predicts mortality. [Goh, AJRCCM 2008]`,`**Rapidly progressive ILD (RP-ILD)**: worsening over days to weeks to respiratory failure - the anti-MDA5 emergency. [ACR/CHEST 2023]`,`**Drug-induced ILD is graded by CTCAE v5**: grade 1 asymptomatic (radiological only); grade 2 symptomatic, limiting instrumental ADL; grade 3 severe, needs oxygen; grade 4 life-threatening; grade 5 death. [CTCAE v5]`,`**Drug-induced lung patterns**: **acute** (hypersensitivity, eosinophilic, DAD, oedema) versus **chronic** (fibrosing NSIP/UIP-like) - nitrofurantoin shows both. [Pneumotox 2024]`,`**Progressive pulmonary fibrosis** criteria (ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022) apply to CTD-ILD and fibrotic DI-ILD (see the ILD and IPF topic). [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Exertional breathlessness and **dry cough**, often masked by reduced activity from joint or muscle disease - **ask about stairs and walking pace**. [ACR/CHEST 2023]`,`**ILD may precede the CTD** by years, especially in anti-synthetase syndrome and RA - ask about Raynaud, dry eyes and mouth, joint swelling, rash, muscle weakness. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anti-synthetase syndrome**: ILD, myositis, **mechanic's hands**, inflammatory arthritis, Raynaud and fever. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anti-MDA5**: little or no muscle weakness, **painful palmar papules, skin ulcers** over knuckles and elbows, alopecia, and breathlessness worsening over weeks. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drug history in time order**: start dates, cumulative dose, recent cancer cycles (bleomycin, immunotherapy), long-term nitrofurantoin prophylaxis, amiodarone, methotrexate, leflunomide, biologics. [Pneumotox 2024]`,`**Methotrexate pneumonitis**: subacute fever, dry cough and breathlessness, usually in the **first year** of treatment, not dose related. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Amiodarone toxicity**: insidious breathlessness, cough, weight loss and fever after months, typically at **over 400 mg/day** or high cumulative dose. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Checkpoint inhibitor pneumonitis**: new cough or breathlessness, median onset about **2-3 months** after starting, but can occur any time, including after stopping. [ASCO 2021]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Bibasal fine crackles** in CTD-ILD; **clubbing is uncommon** except in RA-ILD with UIP. [Harrison 22e]`,`**SSc**: sclerodactyly, puffy fingers, digital pits and ulcers, telangiectasia, microstomia, calcinosis; **abnormal nailfold capillaries**. [EULAR 2023]`,`**RA**: symmetrical small-joint synovitis or deformity, rheumatoid nodules. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dermatomyositis**: **heliotrope rash, Gottron papules**, V-sign, shawl sign; **mechanic's hands** (cracked lateral fingers) in anti-synthetase. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sjogren**: dry eyes and mouth, parotid swelling; **SLE**: malar rash, oral ulcers, serositis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pulmonary hypertension** signs (especially SSc): loud P2, RV heave - SSc patients need annual PH screening. [ESC/ERS 2022]`,`**Drug clues**: blue-grey skin and thyroid dysfunction (amiodarone), skin hyperpigmentation and flagellate rash (bleomycin), rash and eosinophilia (hypersensitivity). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Infection** in an immunosuppressed patient - **Pneumocystis**, CMV, bacterial pneumonia and **TB** - must be excluded before calling it ILD flare or drug toxicity. [ATS 2020]`,`**Methotrexate pneumonitis vs PJP**: both cause fever, dry cough and ground-glass; do **induced sputum/BAL for Pneumocystis PCR** and check beta-D-glucan and LDH. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Heart failure** (amiodarone is given for heart disease): raised NT-proBNP, effusions, response to diuretics. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Aspiration pneumonia** in SSc with oesophageal dysmotility and myositis with pharyngeal weakness. [EULAR 2023]`,`**Lymphangitis carcinomatosa or cancer progression** in a patient on cancer therapy. [ASCO 2021]`,`**IPF and fibrotic HP** in RA-ILD-like UIP - serology and exposure history separate them. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`**Diffuse alveolar haemorrhage** (SLE, vasculitis): falling Hb, haemoptysis, rising DLCO. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Screening for CTD-ILD**: **HRCT plus PFT (spirometry, lung volumes, DLCO)** in SSc, RA, IIM, MCTD and Sjogren patients at increased risk; **chest X-ray, 6MWD or ambulatory desaturation alone are not adequate screens**. [ACR/CHEST 2023]`,`**Serology**: ANA (IIF pattern), anti-Scl-70, anti-centromere, anti-RNA polymerase III, RF, anti-CCP, anti-Ro/SSA (Ro52), anti-La, dsDNA, **extended myositis panel** (Jo-1, PL-7, PL-12, MDA5, EJ, OJ), CK and aldolase. [ACR/CHEST 2023]`,`**PFT**: restrictive pattern with **reduced DLCO**; in SSc an isolated low DLCO (with FVC/DLCO ratio over 1.6) suggests **pulmonary hypertension**. [ESC/ERS 2022]`,`**Anti-MDA5 RP-ILD work-up**: **ferritin** (high levels predict mortality), LDH, CRP, ABG, and exclusion of infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drug-induced ILD**: HRCT pattern, CBC with **eosinophils**, LFT (nitrofurantoin hepatitis), and **BAL** to exclude infection; BAL lymphocytosis in methotrexate/nitrofurantoin, **foamy macrophages** in amiodarone (exposure, not proof). [Pneumotox 2024]`,`**Amiodarone**: CT may show **high-attenuation consolidation and liver** (iodine); a fall in **DLCO of 15-20% or more** from baseline supports toxicity. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bleomycin**: baseline and serial **DLCO** before each cycle; a significant fall (about 25% or more from baseline) prompts stopping. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Echocardiography** yearly in SSc (PH screening, DETECT algorithm) and **cardiac MRI** if myocarditis is suspected in myositis. [ESC/ERS 2022]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - CTD-ILD`,points:[`**SSc-ILD first line**: **mycophenolate mofetil** - start 500 mg twice daily, increase to **1-1.5 g twice daily** (target 2-3 g/day); SLS-II showed it equal to cyclophosphamide with less toxicity. [ACR/CHEST 2023]`,`**SSc-ILD other first-line options**: **tocilizumab 162 mg SC weekly** (FOCUSSED; FDA approved 2021 for SSc-ILD) and **rituximab 1 g IV x 2, two weeks apart**, repeated every 6 months; **cyclophosphamide** when others fail. [ACR/CHEST 2023]`,`**Nintedanib 150 mg twice daily** slowed FVC decline by about 44% in SSc-ILD (SENSCIS) and is used for progressive disease, often added to MMF. [Distler O, NEJM 2019]`,`**Avoid prednisolone over 15 mg/day in SSc** - it precipitates **scleroderma renal crisis**; check BP and creatinine regularly. [EULAR 2023]`,`**RA-ILD**: mycophenolate, azathioprine, rituximab or cyclophosphamide for inflammatory/NSIP disease; **nintedanib for progressive fibrotic (UIP) disease**. [ACR/CHEST 2023]`,`**Methotrexate in RA-ILD**: recent data do not show that MTX causes RA-ILD; stable ILD is not an absolute reason to stop it, but decide with the rheumatologist and watch for pneumonitis. [ACR/CHEST 2023]`,`**Myositis-ILD**: glucocorticoids plus a steroid-sparing agent (**MMF, azathioprine, rituximab, tacrolimus**); IVIG and JAK inhibitors in refractory cases. [ACR/CHEST 2023]`,`**Supportive care**: PJP prophylaxis with **cotrimoxazole 960 mg three times weekly** on combined immunosuppression, bone protection, vaccination, rehabilitation, oxygen and reflux treatment in SSc. [ACR 2022]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD/IPD - drug-induced lung disease`,points:[`**Step 1 for all**: stop the suspected drug, exclude infection and other causes, then grade severity; **rechallenge is usually not advised**. [Pneumotox 2024]`,`**Checkpoint inhibitor pneumonitis grade 1**: consider holding the drug, repeat imaging in 3-4 weeks. [ASCO 2021]`,`**Grade 2**: **hold the drug, prednisolone 1-2 mg/kg/day**, reassess in 48-72 hours, taper over **4-6 weeks**; resume only if back to grade 0-1. [ASCO 2021]`,`**Grade 3-4**: **permanently discontinue**, admit, IV **methylprednisolone 1-2 mg/kg/day**; if no improvement in 48 hours add **infliximab, mycophenolate, IVIG or cyclophosphamide**; empirical antibiotics. [ASCO 2021]`,`**Amiodarone**: stop the drug (it persists for months because of its **half-life of about 50-100 days**), **prednisolone 40-60 mg/day tapered over 4-12 months**; disease may worsen initially after stopping. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Methotrexate pneumonitis**: stop methotrexate, prednisolone **about 1 mg/kg/day** with taper, cover PJP if not excluded; **do not rechallenge**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nitrofurantoin**: acute form resolves within days of stopping; the chronic fibrotic form may need steroids and may not fully reverse. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bleomycin**: stop permanently; steroids are commonly used though evidence is weak; during later anaesthesia **avoid high FiO2** (target SpO2 88-92%) and limit fluids. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD - rapidly progressive CTD-ILD`,points:[`**Admit** for SpO2 under 90%, worsening over days, anti-MDA5 with breathlessness, or suspected infection on immunosuppression. [ACR/CHEST 2023]`,`**Exclude infection first** with blood cultures, sputum and BAL (PJP PCR, CMV, bacterial cultures, **CBNAAT**). [ACR/CHEST 2023]`,`**IV methylprednisolone 500-1,000 mg daily for 3 days**, then prednisolone 1 mg/kg/day. [ACR/CHEST 2023]`,`**Anti-MDA5 RP-ILD**: **combination therapy** - high-dose steroids plus a **calcineurin inhibitor (tacrolimus)** plus **IV cyclophosphamide**, with rituximab, IVIG, JAK inhibitors (tofacitinib) or plasma exchange in refractory cases. [ACR/CHEST 2023]`,`**IV cyclophosphamide** 500-750 mg/m2 monthly (or 500 mg every 2 weeks) with hydration and mesna as indicated; check counts at day 10-14. [ACR/CHEST 2023]`,`Discuss **ICU care and ECMO** only as a bridge to recovery or transplant; mortality of anti-MDA5 RP-ILD remains around 30-50%. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: MMF, methotrexate, leflunomide, cyclophosphamide and nintedanib are **teratogenic** - switch to azathioprine, hydroxychloroquine or low-dose steroids before conception. [BSR 2022]`,`**SSc with PH or severe ILD** has high maternal risk - pre-conception counselling at a tertiary centre. [EULAR 2023]`,`**Elderly**: nitrofurantoin long-term prophylaxis is best avoided (**Beers criteria**), particularly with CrCl under 30 mL/min. [AGS Beers 2023]`,`**Renal impairment**: reduce bleomycin and methotrexate; methotrexate is **contraindicated at eGFR under 30**. [BNF 2024]`,`**India**: screen for **latent TB (IGRA or TST) and hepatitis B** before rituximab, tocilizumab, anti-TNF or JAK inhibitors; TB preventive treatment when positive. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Cost**: generic MMF, cyclophosphamide and nintedanib reduce cost; tocilizumab and rituximab biosimilars are available in India. [Indian practice]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**SSc-ILD**: about 40% have progressive disease; **10-year mortality is higher with extensive (over 20%) HRCT disease** or FVC under 70%. [EULAR 2023]`,`**RA-ILD with UIP** has a median survival of about **3-5 years**, similar to IPF. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anti-MDA5 RP-ILD**: very high early mortality, but survivors often remain in remission. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drug-induced ILD** usually improves after withdrawal; **DAD-pattern** toxicity (bleomycin, high-grade checkpoint pneumonitis) carries high mortality. [Pneumotox 2024]`,`Complications: **pulmonary hypertension**, infection from immunosuppression, aspiration, lung cancer and steroid toxicity. [ACR/CHEST 2023]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up, monitoring and counselling`,points:[`**SSc and myositis**: **PFT every 3-6 months in the first year**, then less often if stable; HRCT when clinically indicated. [ACR/CHEST 2023]`,`**RA, Sjogren, MCTD**: PFT every 3-12 months in the first year, then less often if stable. [ACR/CHEST 2023]`,`**Joint chest-rheumatology clinic** decisions; refer progressive disease for antifibrotics and transplant assessment. [ACR/CHEST 2023]`,`**MMF monitoring**: CBC and LFT every 2 weeks while titrating, then every 1-3 months. [BSR 2022]`,`**Amiodarone**: baseline CXR and PFT with DLCO, TFT and LFT every 6 months. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Checkpoint inhibitors**: ask about cough and breathlessness at every cycle; low threshold for CT. [ASCO 2021]`,`**Stop smoking** - it worsens RA, RA-ILD and drug toxicity. [ACR/CHEST 2023]`,`Report **new cough, breathlessness or fever immediately** when on methotrexate, amiodarone or cancer immunotherapy. [ASCO 2021]`,`Use the **lowest effective amiodarone dose** (200 mg/day maintenance) and review the indication. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Vaccinate** before immunosuppression (influenza, pneumococcal, COVID-19, hepatitis B; avoid live vaccines). [ACIP 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ACR/CHEST 2023 screening and monitoring guideline**: HRCT and PFT to screen at-risk SARD patients; against CXR or 6MWD as screens; defined PFT intervals. [ACR/CHEST 2023]`,`**ACR/CHEST 2023 treatment guideline**: **MMF first line** for SSc, RA, myositis, MCTD and Sjogren ILD; **against glucocorticoids first line in SSc-ILD**; combination therapy for RP-ILD. [ACR/CHEST 2023]`,`**EULAR 2023 SSc update** (published 2024-25): MMF, cyclophosphamide, rituximab, tocilizumab and nintedanib all recommended for SSc-ILD. [EULAR 2023]`,`**RECITAL (2023)**: rituximab was **not superior to cyclophosphamide** in severe CTD-ILD but caused fewer adverse events. [Maher TM, Lancet Respir Med 2023]`,`**FIBRONEER-ILD (2025)**: nerandomilast slowed FVC decline in PPF including CTD-ILD. [Maher TM, NEJM 2025]`,`**ASCO 2021 irAE guideline** (with later living updates) remains the standard for checkpoint pneumonitis grading and steroid dosing. [ASCO 2021]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Viva: '**Which CTD causes UIP?**' - RA; most others cause NSIP.`,`Viva: '**Why avoid high-dose steroids in SSc?**' - scleroderma renal crisis.`,`Short note: **amiodarone lung** - dose, high-attenuation CT, foamy macrophages, DLCO fall, stop drug and steroids.`,`**Methotrexate pneumonitis vs PJP** is a favourite case discussion - always do BAL or induced sputum.`,`**Common mistake**: escalating immunosuppression for 'ILD flare' without excluding infection or TB.`,`Know **anti-MDA5 = amyopathic DM + RP-ILD + high ferritin + skin ulcers**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for CTD-ILD`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Mycophenolate mofetil`,`500 mg BD rising to 1-1.5 g BD orally`,`At least 2 years`,`Diarrhoea, cytopenia, infection; teratogenic`],[`Tocilizumab`,`162 mg SC weekly`,`Long term`,`Infection, neutropenia, raised lipids and ALT, bowel perforation; masks CRP`],[`Rituximab`,`1 g IV on day 1 and 15, repeat every 6 months`,`Long term`,`Infusion reactions, hypogammaglobulinaemia, HBV reactivation`],[`Cyclophosphamide`,`500-750 mg/m2 IV monthly x 6 (or oral 1-2 mg/kg/day)`,`6-12 months then switch to MMF`,`Marrow suppression, haemorrhagic cystitis, infertility, malignancy`],[`Nintedanib`,`150 mg BD orally with food`,`Long term`,`Diarrhoea, raised ALT, bleeding; not in pregnancy`],[`Tacrolimus (myositis RP-ILD)`,`0.075 mg/kg/day orally in 2 doses, trough 5-10 ng/mL`,`Long term`,`Nephrotoxicity, hypertension, tremor, diabetes`],[`Prednisolone`,`0.5-1 mg/kg/day (avoid over 15 mg in SSc)`,`Taper over months`,`Renal crisis in SSc, diabetes, osteoporosis`]]},{heading:`CTD and their lung patterns`,columns:[`Disease`,`Key antibody`,`Usual ILD pattern`,`Other lung features`],rows:[[`Systemic sclerosis`,`Anti-Scl-70 (high risk), anti-centromere (low risk)`,`Fibrotic NSIP`,`Pulmonary hypertension, aspiration`],[`Rheumatoid arthritis`,`RF, anti-CCP (high titre)`,`UIP more than NSIP`,`Nodules, effusion, bronchiectasis, Caplan syndrome`],[`Anti-synthetase syndrome`,`Anti-Jo-1, PL-7, PL-12`,`NSIP with organising pneumonia`,`Mechanic's hands, arthritis`],[`Anti-MDA5 dermatomyositis`,`Anti-MDA5`,`Rapidly progressive ILD, DAD`,`High ferritin, skin ulcers, pneumomediastinum`],[`Sjogren disease`,`Anti-Ro/SSA, anti-La`,`NSIP, LIP, cysts`,`Follicular bronchiolitis, lymphoma`],[`SLE`,`dsDNA, anti-Sm`,`Uncommon NSIP`,`Pleuritis, lupus pneumonitis, DAH, shrinking lung`]]},{heading:`Important drug-induced lung diseases`,columns:[`Drug`,`Pattern and timing`,`Risk factors / clue`,`Management`],rows:[[`Amiodarone`,`NSIP, OP; months to years`,`Over 400 mg/day, older age; high-attenuation CT, DLCO fall`,`Stop; prednisolone 40-60 mg tapered over months`],[`Methotrexate`,`Hypersensitivity pneumonitis; first year`,`Not dose related; fever, eosinophils; exclude PJP`,`Stop; steroids; no rechallenge`],[`Nitrofurantoin`,`Acute (days, eosinophilia, effusion) or chronic (fibrosis)`,`Long-term prophylaxis, older women, hepatitis`,`Stop; steroids for chronic form`],[`Bleomycin`,`DAD, fibrosis; during or after therapy`,`Cumulative dose over 400 units, age over 70, renal failure, oxygen, radiation`,`Stop permanently; steroids; low FiO2 later`],[`Checkpoint inhibitors`,`OP, NSIP, HP-like; median 2-3 months`,`Combination therapy, NSCLC, prior ILD or radiation`,`Grade-based: hold and steroids; stop for grade 3-4`],[`EGFR TKIs, trastuzumab deruxtecan`,`ILD/DAD; weeks to months`,`Japanese ancestry, pre-existing ILD`,`Stop; steroids`]]},{heading:`Checkpoint inhibitor pneumonitis grading (CTCAE v5 / ASCO 2021)`,columns:[`Grade`,`Features`,`Action`],rows:[[`1`,`Radiological only, asymptomatic`,`Consider holding; repeat CT in 3-4 weeks`],[`2`,`Symptomatic, limits instrumental ADL`,`Hold; prednisolone 1-2 mg/kg/day; taper over 4-6 weeks`],[`3`,`Severe symptoms, limits self-care, needs oxygen`,`Stop permanently; admit; IV methylprednisolone 1-2 mg/kg`],[`4`,`Life-threatening respiratory compromise`,`As grade 3, ICU; add infliximab, MMF or IVIG if no response in 48 h`]]},{heading:`Screening and monitoring for CTD-ILD (ACR/CHEST 2023)`,columns:[`Disease`,`Screen with`,`PFT monitoring in first year`],rows:[[`Systemic sclerosis`,`HRCT + PFT at diagnosis`,`Every 3-6 months`],[`Inflammatory myositis`,`HRCT + PFT at diagnosis`,`Every 3-6 months`],[`RA (with risk factors)`,`HRCT + PFT`,`Every 3-12 months`],[`Sjogren, MCTD`,`HRCT + PFT if risk factors`,`Every 3-12 months`],[`Not recommended alone`,`Chest X-ray, 6MWD, ambulatory desaturation`,`Not applicable`]]}],redFlags:[`Breathlessness worsening over days with dermatomyositis skin signs or anti-MDA5 - rapidly progressive ILD`,`SpO2 under 90% or need for oxygen in a patient on immunotherapy (grade 3-4 pneumonitis)`,`Fever, dry cough and ground-glass on methotrexate or immunosuppression - PJP vs drug pneumonitis`,`New hypertension or rising creatinine in SSc on steroids - scleroderma renal crisis`,`Haemoptysis with falling haemoglobin in SLE or vasculitis - alveolar haemorrhage`,`FVC fall of 10% or more in 6-12 months in CTD-ILD`],pearls:[`**RA = UIP; most other CTDs = NSIP.**`,`**Anti-Scl-70 predicts SSc-ILD; anti-centromere protects.**`,`**MMF is first line for CTD-ILD; steroids over 15 mg/day in SSc risk renal crisis.**`,`**Anti-MDA5: amyopathic DM, skin ulcers, high ferritin, RP-ILD - treat with triple therapy early.**`,`**Screen with HRCT and PFT, not chest X-ray** (ACR/CHEST 2023).`,`**Amiodarone: high-attenuation CT, foamy macrophages, DLCO falls; worsens after stopping because of long half-life.**`,`**Methotrexate pneumonitis: first year, not dose-related - always exclude PJP.**`,`**Bleomycin: keep FiO2 low for life.**`,`**Checkpoint pneumonitis grade 2 = hold + prednisolone 1-2 mg/kg; grade 3-4 = stop for good.**`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-ctd-drug-ild-q1`,stem:`A 38-year-old woman with diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis and anti-Scl-70 antibody has an FVC of 68% predicted and fibrotic NSIP involving 25% of the lungs on HRCT. What is the preferred first-line treatment?`,options:[`Prednisolone 1 mg/kg/day`,`Mycophenolate mofetil titrated to 1-1.5 g twice daily`,`Pirfenidone`,`Observation with yearly PFT`],answer:1,explanation:`Extensive SSc-ILD (over 20% on HRCT, FVC under 70%) needs treatment, and ACR/CHEST 2023 recommends mycophenolate first line, based on SLS-II. High-dose prednisolone is advised against as first line in SSc because it can precipitate scleroderma renal crisis. Pirfenidone lacks supportive trial evidence in SSc-ILD, and observation is unsafe in extensive disease.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-ctd-drug-ild-q2`,stem:`Which connective tissue disease most often shows a usual interstitial pneumonia (UIP) pattern on HRCT?`,options:[`Systemic sclerosis`,`Sjogren disease`,`Rheumatoid arthritis`,`Anti-synthetase syndrome`],answer:2,explanation:`RA-ILD is the CTD most commonly associated with a UIP pattern, especially in older male smokers with high-titre anti-CCP, and it behaves much like IPF. Systemic sclerosis typically gives fibrotic NSIP, Sjogren disease gives NSIP or LIP with cysts, and anti-synthetase syndrome gives NSIP with organising pneumonia.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-ctd-drug-ild-q3`,stem:`A 68-year-old man on amiodarone 400 mg/day for 18 months develops progressive breathlessness and weight loss. CT shows peripheral consolidation of high attenuation and a dense liver. DLCO has fallen by 30% from baseline. The best management is:`,options:[`Stop amiodarone and start prednisolone 40-60 mg/day tapered over several months`,`Continue amiodarone and add an inhaled steroid`,`Reduce amiodarone to 200 mg/day and recheck in 6 months`,`Start antitubercular treatment empirically`],answer:0,explanation:`High-attenuation consolidation (iodine), a dense liver and a large DLCO fall in a patient on high-dose amiodarone indicate amiodarone pulmonary toxicity; the drug must be stopped and systemic steroids given over months because of its very long half-life. Continuing the drug at any dose risks progression, inhaled steroids are ineffective, and empirical ATT is unjustified.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-ctd-drug-ild-q4`,stem:`A patient on pembrolizumab for lung cancer has new cough and breathlessness on walking around the house, with SpO2 95% on air and bilateral ground-glass on CT. Infection has been excluded. The correct management is:`,options:[`Continue pembrolizumab and give antibiotics`,`Stop pembrolizumab permanently and give IV infliximab immediately`,`Hold pembrolizumab and start prednisolone 1-2 mg/kg/day with taper over 4-6 weeks`,`Continue pembrolizumab with inhaled budesonide`],answer:2,explanation:`Symptomatic pneumonitis limiting instrumental ADL without oxygen need is grade 2: ASCO advises holding the checkpoint inhibitor and giving prednisolone 1-2 mg/kg/day with a 4-6 week taper, resuming only if it settles to grade 1 or less. Continuing the drug ignores the toxicity; permanent discontinuation and infliximab are for grade 3-4 or steroid-refractory disease; inhaled steroids are not adequate.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`respiratory-ctd-drug-ild-q5`,stem:`A woman with amyopathic dermatomyositis, painful palmar papules and skin ulcers develops breathlessness over 3 weeks with serum ferritin of 2,500 ng/mL. Which antibody is most likely?`,options:[`Anti-Mi-2`,`Anti-TIF1-gamma`,`Anti-Jo-1`,`Anti-MDA5`],answer:3,explanation:`Anti-MDA5 dermatomyositis is typically clinically amyopathic, with palmar papules and skin ulcers, very high ferritin and rapidly progressive ILD needing early combination immunosuppression. Anti-Mi-2 gives classic DM with good prognosis, anti-TIF1-gamma is linked to cancer, and anti-Jo-1 causes anti-synthetase syndrome with myositis and more chronic ILD.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-ctd-drug-ild-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Discuss interstitial lung disease in connective tissue diseases with emphasis on screening and management of systemic sclerosis-associated ILD.`,openingLines:[`CTD-ILD is ILD occurring in systemic autoimmune rheumatic diseases, and in systemic sclerosis it is now the leading cause of death.`,`ACR/CHEST 2023 recommends HRCT and PFT screening of at-risk patients and mycophenolate as first-line treatment.`],answer:[{heading:`Spectrum`,points:[`SSc (NSIP), RA (UIP), myositis (NSIP-OP, anti-MDA5 RP-ILD), Sjogren (LIP), SLE (pneumonitis, DAH), IPAF.`]},{heading:`Risk factors and pathogenesis`,points:[`Diffuse SSc, anti-Scl-70, early disease; IL-6 and TGF-beta driven fibrosis.`]},{heading:`Screening and evaluation`,points:[`HRCT plus PFT with DLCO at diagnosis; not CXR or 6MWD; PFT every 3-6 months in first year.`,`Goh staging: extensive if over 20% or 10-30% with FVC under 70%; echo for PH.`]},{heading:`Treatment of SSc-ILD`,points:[`MMF 1-1.5 g BD first line; tocilizumab 162 mg SC weekly; rituximab; cyclophosphamide; nintedanib 150 mg BD for progression.`,`Avoid prednisolone over 15 mg/day (renal crisis); PJP prophylaxis; reflux control; rehab, oxygen.`]},{heading:`Other CTDs and RP-ILD`,points:[`RA-ILD: MMF/rituximab, nintedanib for UIP progression; anti-MDA5: steroid pulse + tacrolimus + cyclophosphamide.`]},{heading:`Monitoring and prognosis`,points:[`PFT, HRCT when needed; transplant referral; extensive disease and FVC under 70% predict death.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: CTD vs ILD pattern vs antibody`,`Treatment ladder for SSc-ILD`],markSplit:[{part:`Spectrum and risk factors`,marks:2},{part:`Screening and evaluation`,marks:3},{part:`Treatment`,marks:4},{part:`Prognosis`,marks:1}],keywords:[`SSc-ILD`,`MMF`,`tocilizumab`,`nintedanib`,`ACR/CHEST 2023`,`scleroderma renal crisis`]},{id:`respiratory-ctd-drug-ild-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on drug-induced lung disease.`,openingLines:[`Drug-induced lung disease is injury to the airways, interstitium, pleura or vessels caused by a drug; over 1,000 drugs are listed on pneumotox.com.`,`Diagnosis rests on timing, compatible pattern, exclusion of infection and improvement on withdrawal.`],answer:[{heading:`Common culprits`,points:[`Amiodarone, methotrexate, nitrofurantoin, bleomycin, busulfan, checkpoint inhibitors, EGFR TKIs, trastuzumab deruxtecan.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Temporal link, HRCT pattern (NSIP, OP, HP-like, DAD), BAL to exclude infection, DLCO fall.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Stop drug; steroids by severity (CTCAE grade); no rechallenge in severe cases; low FiO2 after bleomycin.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: drug, pattern, risk factor, management`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and culprits`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:1},{part:`Management`,marks:2}],keywords:[`pneumotox`,`amiodarone`,`methotrexate`,`bleomycin`,`checkpoint inhibitor`]}],references:[`Johnson SR et al. 2023 ACR/CHEST Guideline for the Screening and Monitoring of Interstitial Lung Disease in People with Systemic Autoimmune Rheumatic Diseases. Arthritis Rheumatol 2024`,`Johnson SR et al. 2023 ACR/CHEST Guideline for the Treatment of Interstitial Lung Disease in People with Systemic Autoimmune Rheumatic Diseases. Arthritis Rheumatol 2024`,`Del Galdo F et al. EULAR Recommendations for the Treatment of Systemic Sclerosis: 2023 Update. Ann Rheum Dis 2025`,`Schneider BJ et al. Management of Immune-Related Adverse Events in Patients Treated With Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor Therapy: ASCO Guideline Update 2021`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Interstitial lung diseases and drug-induced lung disease`,`Pneumotox Online: The Drug-Induced Respiratory Disease Website (pneumotox.com), Camus P, accessed 2024`,`Distler O et al. Nintedanib for Systemic Sclerosis-Associated Interstitial Lung Disease (SENSCIS). N Engl J Med 2019`]});var o=[];o.push({id:`respiratory-occupational-lung`,title:`Occupational lung disease: silicosis, coal workers, asbestosis and byssinosis`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Pneumoconiosis** is the non-neoplastic reaction of the lung to inhaled **mineral dust** retained in the lung - fibrogenic with **silica, coal and asbestos** - while **byssinosis** is airway narrowing from **cotton, flax or hemp dust**; all are preventable, **notifiable** and compensable, and in India **silicosis with silicotuberculosis** is the commonest and deadliest.`,keywords:[`pneumoconiosis`,`silicosis`,`silicotuberculosis`,`coal workers pneumoconiosis`,`black lung`,`progressive massive fibrosis`,`PMF`,`Caplan syndrome`,`asbestosis`,`pleural plaques`,`mesothelioma`,`byssinosis`,`Monday fever`,`ILO classification of radiographs`,`eggshell calcification`,`stone crushers`,`agate workers Khambhat`,`slate pencil Mandsaur`,`Employees' Compensation Act`,`ESI occupational disease`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Pneumoconiosis** is the accumulation of dust in the lungs and the tissue reaction to it; the classic fibrogenic dusts are **crystalline silica, coal mine dust and asbestos**, while iron, tin and barium cause benign non-fibrotic deposits. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Occupational lung disease** is wider: it also covers occupational asthma, hypersensitivity pneumonitis, byssinosis, occupational COPD, inhalation fever and occupational lung cancer and mesothelioma. [Harrison 22e]`,`Globally about **15% of COPD and asthma** and roughly **one-quarter of IPF** burden is attributable to occupational exposure (population attributable fraction). [ATS/ERS Occupational Burden Statement 2019]`,`**Silicosis** remains the commonest pneumoconiosis worldwide; GBD 2019 estimated over **2.6 million prevalent pneumoconiosis cases** with silicosis the largest share and India and China carrying most of it. [GBD 2019]`,`**India**: ICMR-NIOH estimated about **3 million workers exposed to silica in mining and quarrying, 5.3 million in construction** and many more in stone crushing, foundries, glass and ceramics - about 10 million at risk in all. [ICMR-NIOH]`,`Indian hotspots: **sandstone mining and stone carving (Jodhpur, Karauli, Rajasthan)**, **stone crushers (Haryana, Delhi NCR, Jharkhand)**, **agate grinding (Khambhat, Gujarat)**, **slate-pencil cutting (Mandsaur, MP)**, quartz crushing (Godhra, Gujarat) and glass/ceramic units (Firozabad, Khurja). [ICMR-NIOH]`,`Field surveys by NIOH found silicosis in roughly **one-third to over one-half** of slate-pencil and some stone-mine workers, many dying in their 30s-40s - and most cases are never certified or compensated. [ICMR-NIOH]`,`**Coal workers' pneumoconiosis (CWP)** clusters in the Jharia-Dhanbad, Raniganj, Singrauli and Talcher coalfields; **asbestos** exposure is in asbestos-cement sheet factories, ship-breaking (Alang, Gujarat) and brake-lining work; **byssinosis** in textile mills (Mumbai, Ahmedabad, Coimbatore, Kanpur). [ICMR-NIOH]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Respirable particles (about 0.5-5 micrometres)** reach the alveoli; larger ones are caught on the mucociliary escalator - so fine dust from dry cutting and grinding is far more dangerous than visible coarse dust. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Crystalline silica (quartz, cristobalite, tridymite)**: sandstone (up to 95% quartz), granite, slate, agate, quartzite, foundry sand, sandblasting, glass and ceramics, and **engineered (artificial) stone** benchtops with over 90% silica. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Freshly fractured silica** is most toxic because its surface carries reactive silanol groups and free radicals - explaining the explosive disease in sandblasters and dry stone cutters. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Coal**: risk rises with cumulative respirable dust and coal **rank** (anthracite more than bituminous), and with the quartz content of the rock cut alongside the seam. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Asbestos** fibres: **serpentine chrysotile (white)**, which forms over 90% of asbestos used and is still imported into India for cement sheets, and **amphiboles - crocidolite (blue), amosite (brown), tremolite** - which are long, thin, persist in lung and are most mesotheliomagenic. [IARC]`,`**Byssinosis**: raw **cotton, flax, hemp or sisal** dust in the blowing, carding and spinning rooms; the active agent is mainly **Gram-negative bacterial endotoxin** on the plant bracts. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cofactors**: smoking (multiplies lung-cancer risk with asbestos and adds COPD), poor ventilation, absence of wet methods, long shifts, migrant contract labour without surveillance, and HIV or diabetes (raising TB risk in silicosis). [WHO]`,`**Latency**: chronic silicosis and CWP after **10-20 years**, asbestosis after **15-20 years**, pleural plaques **20-30 years**, and mesothelioma **30-40 years** after first exposure. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why dust scars the lung`,points:[`**Silica**: alveolar macrophages ingest particles, the phagolysosome ruptures and activates the **NLRP3 inflammasome**, releasing **IL-1beta, TNF-alpha and TGF-beta** that recruit fibroblasts; the dying macrophage frees silica to be re-ingested - a self-perpetuating cycle that continues after exposure stops. [Harrison 22e]`,`The lesion is the **silicotic nodule**: a whorled, concentric, hyalinised collagen nodule with birefringent particles under polarised light, centred on lymphatics, so it concentrates in the **upper zones and hilar nodes**. [Robbins 10e]`,`**Why silicosis invites TB**: silica poisons macrophages and impairs their killing of *Mycobacterium tuberculosis*; risk of active TB is about **3-4 times** higher with exposure alone and many times higher (often quoted up to 30-fold) with established silicosis. [ATS 2019]`,`**Silica and autoimmunity**: silica is an adjuvant that increases **systemic sclerosis (Erasmus syndrome), rheumatoid arthritis, ANCA vasculitis, SLE and chronic kidney disease**, and is an **IARC Group 1 lung carcinogen**. [IARC]`,`**Coal**: the **coal macule** (dust-laden macrophages around respiratory bronchioles) with **focal centrilobular emphysema**; nodules coalesce into **progressive massive fibrosis (PMF)**, which may cavitate and expel black fluid (**melanoptysis**). [Robbins 10e]`,`**Asbestos**: long fibres (over 5-10 micrometres) cannot be fully engulfed - **frustrated phagocytosis** generates oxidants and fibrogenic cytokines; fibrosis begins around **respiratory bronchioles in the lower lobes and subpleura** and progresses to a UIP-like honeycomb lung. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Asbestos (ferruginous) bodies** are fibres coated with iron-protein, golden-brown and beaded (dumb-bell); they mark exposure, not disease, and with fibrosis confirm asbestosis histologically. [Robbins 10e]`,`**Byssinosis**: endotoxin and plant-derived agents cause **non-IgE mast-cell histamine release and neutrophilic airway inflammation**, so bronchoconstriction is worst on return after a break (**Monday chest tightness**) and tolerance builds through the week; years of exposure give fixed obstruction. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification - the ones examiners ask`,points:[`**Silicosis by tempo**: **chronic** (simple nodular, 10 or more years of low exposure), **accelerated** (5-10 years of heavier exposure, faster progression) and **acute silicoproteinosis** (weeks to 5 years of massive exposure, alveolar proteinosis-like, often fatal). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Simple vs complicated**: simple pneumoconiosis has small opacities under 10 mm; **complicated pneumoconiosis = PMF**, a large opacity of **10 mm or more**, causing restriction, cor pulmonale and early death. [ILO 2022]`,`**ILO International Classification of Radiographs of Pneumoconioses (revised 2022)** codes a PA chest film against standard images for quality (1-4), small opacities, large opacities and pleural change. [ILO 2022]`,`ILO small opacities: rounded **p (under 1.5 mm), q (1.5-3 mm), r (3-10 mm)** and irregular **s, t, u** of the same sizes; silicosis and CWP give rounded p/q/r, asbestosis gives irregular s/t/u in lower zones. [ILO 2022]`,`ILO **profusion** is a 12-point scale from **0/- to 3/+** in 4 major categories (0-3); **category 1/0 or more** is taken as radiological evidence of pneumoconiosis for surveillance and compensation. [ILO 2022]`,`ILO **large opacities**: **A** = one or more opacities over 10 mm summing up to 50 mm; **B** = larger than A but not exceeding the equivalent of the right upper zone; **C** = exceeding the right upper zone. [ILO 2022]`,`**Byssinosis grading (Schilling, as used by WHO)**: grade 0 none; **1/2** occasional first-day chest tightness; **1** chest tightness or breathlessness on every first working day; **2** on first and other days; **3** grade 2 with permanent incapacity from reduced ventilatory function. [WHO]`,`**Asbestos-related disease spectrum**: pleural plaques, benign asbestos pleural effusion, diffuse pleural thickening, rounded atelectasis, asbestosis, lung cancer and mesothelioma (see the Malignant effusion and mesothelioma topic). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`The **occupational history is the diagnosis**: ask every breathless or 'TB' patient about **job title, actual tasks, materials, dry or wet methods, masks, duration, years since first exposure** and co-workers with similar illness. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Simple silicosis and CWP** are often **asymptomatic** and found on a screening X-ray; symptoms appear with PMF - **progressive exertional breathlessness, cough and sputum**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Silicotuberculosis** presents as fever, weight loss, haemoptysis and a changing X-ray in a stone worker - and is often treated as smear-negative TB for years while the silicosis is never named. [NTEP]`,`**Acute silicoproteinosis**: rapidly progressive breathlessness, weight loss and hypoxaemia within months of sandblasting or quartz grinding - a young worker with a 'white-out' chest. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Asbestosis**: insidious exertional breathlessness and dry cough 15-20 years after exposure; **pleural plaques** are symptomless; new chest pain or effusion suggests **mesothelioma**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Byssinosis**: **chest tightness on the first day back at work after the weekend (Monday fever)**, easing by mid-week; later persistent cough and breathlessness like COPD; **mill fever** (fever, malaise in new workers) is a separate endotoxin reaction. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Melanoptysis** (black sputum) in a coal miner means a cavitating PMF lesion - exclude TB co-infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **joint pain, Raynaud's and skin tightening** (silica-associated autoimmunity), smoking, and household exposure (wives washing asbestos-laden clothes develop plaques and mesothelioma). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Simple silicosis and CWP usually have **a normal chest examination** despite a heavily nodular X-ray - clinical-radiological dissociation is a clue. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PMF**: reduced upper-zone expansion, tracheal deviation towards the larger mass, coarse crackles and, late, **cor pulmonale** (raised JVP, loud P2, parasternal heave, pedal oedema). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Asbestosis**: **fine bibasal end-inspiratory 'Velcro' crackles** and **clubbing in about one-third** - clubbing is common in asbestosis and uncommon in silicosis or CWP. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rheumatoid nodules and arthritis** with multiple peripheral 0.5-5 cm lung nodules in a miner = **Caplan syndrome**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sclerodactyly, Raynaud's and digital pits** in a silica worker = **Erasmus syndrome** (silica-associated systemic sclerosis). [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for **signs of TB** (lymphadenopathy, pleural effusion, cachexia) and of **lung cancer or mesothelioma** (effusion, chest wall mass, hoarseness, supraclavicular node). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Byssinosis**: wheeze and prolonged expiration, best demonstrated by measuring **FEV1 before and after a Monday shift**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Miliary or post-primary TB** is the commonest misdiagnosis of silicosis in India; silicosis nodules are **stable, upper-zone, with eggshell hilar nodes and no fever**, while TB changes in weeks and tests positive on CBNAAT. [NTEP]`,`**Sarcoidosis** gives upper-zone nodules and hilar nodes too, but bilateral symmetrical hilar nodes, raised ACE, uveitis, erythema nodosum and no dust history; **berylliosis** mimics sarcoidosis exactly and needs a beryllium lymphocyte proliferation test. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis** vs asbestosis: both lower-zone UIP-like with clubbing; asbestosis has **pleural plaques, asbestos bodies and exposure history** and progresses more slowly. [ATS/ERS 2022]`,`**Hypersensitivity pneumonitis** (bagassosis, farmer's lung, bird fancier's) is an organic-dust immune reaction with mid-upper zone ground glass and mosaic attenuation - see the Hypersensitivity pneumonitis topic. [ATS/JRS/ALAT 2020]`,`**Occupational asthma** (isocyanates, flour, wood dust) improves on days off but is IgE or sensitiser-mediated and persists; **byssinosis** is worst on the first day back and has tolerance across the week. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lung cancer or metastases** vs PMF: PMF is bilateral, upper-zone, migrates towards the hilum with peripheral emphysema; a unilateral, growing or PET-avid mass needs biopsy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Benign pneumoconioses** (siderosis in welders, stannosis, baritosis) give dense small opacities with **normal lung function** and need no treatment. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**PA chest X-ray read by ILO classification** is the cornerstone of diagnosis, surveillance and compensation; a reader certified (e.g. NIOSH B-reader or AIR-Pneumo in India) should grade it. [ILO 2022]`,`**Silicosis X-ray**: bilateral small rounded opacities, **upper-zone predominance**, hilar enlargement with **eggshell calcification (in about 5-10%)**, and PMF masses that migrate towards the hila. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HRCT** is more sensitive: centrilobular and perilymphatic nodules, subpleural nodules, calcified nodes, conglomerate masses; **acute silicoproteinosis** shows ground glass with **crazy-paving**. [Fleischner]`,`**Asbestos CT**: **calcified pleural plaques on the diaphragm and posterolateral chest wall** (pathognomonic of exposure), subpleural curvilinear lines and lower-lobe reticulation; **rounded atelectasis** with a 'comet-tail' of vessels. [Fleischner]`,`**Spirometry**: silicosis and asbestosis are **restrictive with reduced DLCO** (often mixed with obstruction from dust COPD); byssinosis shows **obstruction and a cross-shift FEV1 fall** (a fall of 5% or more is significant, 10% or more marked). [ATS/ERS 2022]`,`**Screen every silicosis patient for TB**: symptom screen, **sputum CBNAAT (Truenat or GeneXpert) and culture**; in suspected silicotuberculosis do not rely on X-ray change alone. [NTEP 2023]`,`**Bronchoscopy/BAL** shows milky lavage fluid in silicoproteinosis and asbestos bodies (over 1 per mL of BAL suggests significant exposure); **lung biopsy is rarely needed** when exposure and imaging fit. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Other tests**: ABG and 6-minute walk in advanced disease, **echocardiography** for pulmonary hypertension, rheumatoid factor/ANA when autoimmunity is suspected, and urine protein and creatinine (silica nephropathy). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`**There is no drug that reverses silicosis, CWP or asbestosis** - the most effective treatment is **removal from further exposure**, with re-deployment and compensation so the worker is not forced back to dust. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Stop smoking** (brief advice plus nicotine replacement or varenicline 0.5 mg daily titrated to 1 mg twice daily for 12 weeks) - it multiplies asbestos lung-cancer risk and adds COPD. [WHO]`,`**Vaccinate**: annual influenza and pneumococcal vaccine (PCV20, or PCV15 followed by PPSV23) - see the Adult respiratory vaccines topic. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Treat airflow obstruction as COPD**: LAMA or LABA-LAMA inhalers, and inhaled steroid only for asthma features or eosinophils of 300/microlitre or more - see the COPD topic. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Byssinosis**: early disease is reversible - move the worker out of the carding room, use dust extraction and respirators, and treat bronchospasm with **salbutamol 100-200 microgram as needed** or a LAMA. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TB preventive treatment (TPT)** for silicosis patients with TB infection after ruling out active TB - **3HP (weekly isoniazid plus rifapentine for 12 weeks)** or 6H - as silicosis is a listed high-risk group. [NTEP TPT 2021]`,`**Silicotuberculosis** is treated with the **standard NTEP regimen** (HRZE 2 months then HRE 4 months, weight-banded FDC) - response is slower and relapse higher, so follow closely; check rifampicin resistance with CBNAAT first. [NTEP 2023]`,`**Pulmonary rehabilitation and long-term oxygen** when resting PaO2 is 55 mmHg or less (or under 60 with cor pulmonale) - see the Pulmonary rehabilitation and long-term home oxygen topic. [BTS 2015]`,`**Certify and notify**: give the worker a written diagnosis of occupational disease so they can claim compensation - this paper is often the most valuable thing a family physician gives. [EC Act 1923]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency`,points:[`**Admit** for suspected silicotuberculosis with haemoptysis, acute silicoproteinosis, hypoxaemia (SpO2 under 90%), decompensated cor pulmonale or pneumothorax. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Controlled oxygen** to SpO2 **88-92%** if hypercapnic or with chronic lung disease, otherwise 94-98% - see the Respiratory failure and oxygen therapy topic. [BTS 2017]`,`**Acute silicoproteinosis**: **whole-lung lavage** in a specialist centre gives temporary relief; steroids are of unproven benefit; refer early for **lung transplant** assessment. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pneumothorax** in PMF or emphysema is a secondary pneumothorax - needs admission and usually a chest drain (see the Pneumothorax topic). [BTS 2023]`,`**Progressive fibrosis** despite exposure removal (asbestosis, some silicosis): **nintedanib 150 mg twice daily** may be considered as progressive pulmonary fibrosis under specialist care. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`**Cor pulmonale**: diuretics (furosemide 40 mg daily titrated), oxygen, treat hypoxaemia; avoid pulmonary vasodilators outside a specialist centre. [ESC/ERS 2022]`,`**Lung transplant** is the only curative option for end-stage silicosis or asbestosis in suitable patients under 65. [ISHLT 2021]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**HIV with silicosis** sharply multiplies TB risk - screen at every visit and give TPT; test every silicotuberculosis patient for HIV. [NTEP 2023]`,`**Diabetes** adds to TB risk; screen silicosis patients for diabetes and diabetic TB patients for dust exposure (bi-directional screening). [NTEP 2023]`,`**Women and children** in home-based agate and stone work (Khambhat) are exposed outside the Factories Act net - ask household members, not just the worker. [ICMR-NIOH]`,`**Engineered stone workers** develop **accelerated silicosis in their 20s-30s** with autoimmune disease; Australia banned engineered stone from **1 July 2024** after a national epidemic. [Safe Work Australia 2024]`,`**Pregnancy**: no specific silicosis issue, but continue TB screening; ATT is safe in pregnancy (avoid streptomycin). [NTEP 2023]`,`**Resource-limited settings**: where ILO readers or HRCT are unavailable, a typical X-ray with documented exposure suffices for a **medical board** to certify in states such as Rajasthan. [Rajasthan Pneumoconiosis Policy 2019]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Silicosis**: TB (commonest cause of death in India), PMF, chronic respiratory failure, **cor pulmonale**, pneumothorax, lung cancer, autoimmune disease and CKD. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Silicosis keeps progressing after exposure stops** because of retained silica; acute silicoproteinosis is usually fatal within months to a few years. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Simple CWP** has near-normal life expectancy; **PMF** shortens life through respiratory failure and cor pulmonale. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Asbestos and smoking are multiplicative**: lung-cancer risk is about 5-fold with asbestos, 10-fold with smoking and about **50-fold with both**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mesothelioma** has no safe threshold of asbestos exposure and median survival is about **12 months** from diagnosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Byssinosis** is reversible early; continued exposure for many years gives **irreversible COPD-like obstruction**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer to a chest physician or NIOH/state pneumoconiosis board** to confirm the diagnosis, grade the X-ray by ILO and certify for compensation. [ICMR-NIOH]`,`**Follow-up every 3-6 months**: TB symptom screen, weight, SpO2, and **sputum CBNAAT** if any new cough, fever or X-ray change. [NTEP 2023]`,`**Spirometry and DLCO yearly** to track decline; **chest X-ray yearly** or on new symptoms; echo if breathlessness is out of proportion. [ATS/ERS 2022]`,`Asbestos-exposed people need **annual review for new chest pain, effusion or weight loss** (mesothelioma, lung cancer); low-dose CT lung cancer screening is reasonable in high-risk smokers with exposure. [NCCN 2025]`,`**Workplace surveillance**: pre-placement and **periodic chest X-ray with spirometry** - under the Mines Rules miners have a periodic medical examination at least every 5 years. [Mines Rules 1955]`,`**Refer urgently** for haemoptysis, rapidly enlarging mass, unilateral effusion, or SpO2 under 90%. [BTS 2023]`]},{heading:`Prevention, notification, compensation and programme link`,points:[`**Hierarchy of control**: elimination/substitution (non-silica abrasives, ban on sandblasting with sand), **engineering - wet drilling and cutting, local exhaust ventilation, enclosure**, administrative (rotation, shorter exposure) and lastly **respirators (N95/FFP2 or better, fit-tested)**. [WHO/ILO]`,`**Exposure limits** for respirable crystalline silica: **OSHA PEL 50 microgram/m3 (8-hour)** and **ACGIH TLV 25 microgram/m3**; India's Factories Act limits are older and less strict. [OSHA 2016]`,`**Notifiable**: silicosis, asbestosis, byssinosis and coal miners' pneumoconiosis are notifiable occupational diseases under **Section 89 of the Factories Act 1948 (Third Schedule)** and the **Mines Act 1952**; the doctor who diagnoses must notify the Chief Inspector of Factories or DGMS. [Factories Act 1948]`,`**Compensation**: pneumoconioses are listed occupational diseases under **Schedule III of the Employees' Compensation Act 1923** and the **ESI Act 1948** (occupational disease benefit, permanent disablement and dependants' benefit). [EC Act 1923]`,`The **four Labour Codes** - including the **Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code 2020** and the **Code on Social Security 2020** - came into force in November 2025, consolidating the Factories, Mines, EC and ESI Acts; listed occupational diseases continue. [MoLE 2025]`,`**State schemes**: the **Rajasthan Pneumoconiosis Policy 2019** pays about Rs 3 lakh on certification and Rs 2 lakh to the family on death, plus pension and Palanhar support; Haryana and others have similar schemes. [Rajasthan Pneumoconiosis Policy 2019]`,`**ICMR-NIOH (Ahmedabad)** runs training in ILO reading and occupational disease surveillance; the **NHRC** has repeatedly directed states to identify, rehabilitate and compensate silicosis victims. [NHRC]`,`**Byssinosis prevention**: humidification, dust extraction in blowing and carding rooms, washing or steaming cotton, pre-placement spirometry and moving symptomatic workers early. [WHO]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline and policy updates (2022-2026)`,points:[`**ILO 2022 revision** of the International Classification of Radiographs of Pneumoconioses adds **digital standard images** so digital radiographs can be classified validly. [ILO 2022]`,`**Labour Codes in force from 21 November 2025**: OSH Code 2020 and Code on Social Security 2020 replace the Factories, Mines, EC and ESI Acts as the legal basis for notification and compensation. [MoLE 2025]`,`**Engineered stone silicosis** is the major new epidemic; Australia's ban (2024) and UK and US alerts stress **wet cutting, extraction and screening** of benchtop fabricators. [Safe Work Australia 2024]`,`**Progressive pulmonary fibrosis** framework lets fibrotic pneumoconiosis that progresses be treated with **nintedanib** (conditional). [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT 2022]`,`**NTEP TB-free workplace and TPT expansion** include silicosis among high-risk groups for systematic TB screening and **3HP** TPT. [NTEP 2023]`,`**GOLD 2025** recognises occupational dusts and biomass among non-smoking COPD causes ('COPD-P' pollution type) - ask exposure history in every COPD patient. [GOLD 2025]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic viva**: 'stone worker with X-ray nodules - what is your diagnosis and what will you do?' Answer: silicosis, rule out silicotuberculosis with CBNAAT, ILO grading, remove from exposure, notify and compensate.`,`**Eggshell calcification** of hilar nodes: silicosis (classic), also sarcoidosis, CWP, treated lymphoma. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Upper-zone pneumoconioses** = **silicosis, CWP, berylliosis** (plus TB, sarcoidosis, ankylosing spondylitis); **lower-zone** = **asbestosis** (plus IPF, CTD-ILD). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Caplan syndrome** = rheumatoid arthritis plus pneumoconiosis with multiple peripheral nodules (first described in coal miners). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Commonest manifestation of asbestos exposure** = pleural plaques; **earliest** = benign asbestos pleural effusion (within 10-20 years). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake**: giving repeated empirical ATT to a stone worker without microbiological confirmation, or never writing the word 'silicosis' - the patient then loses compensation.`,`**Most mesotheliomagenic fibre**: crocidolite (blue asbestos); **most used fibre**: chrysotile. [IARC]`]}],tables:[{heading:`The four pneumoconioses side by side`,columns:[`Feature`,`Silicosis`,`Coal workers' pneumoconiosis`,`Asbestosis`,`Byssinosis`],rows:[[`Agent`,`Crystalline silica (quartz)`,`Coal mine dust`,`Asbestos fibres (chrysotile, amphiboles)`,`Cotton, flax, hemp dust (endotoxin)`],[`Indian occupations`,`Stone crushing, sandstone mining, agate, slate pencil, foundry, glass`,`Coal mining (Jharia, Raniganj)`,`AC sheet factories, ship-breaking, brake linings`,`Textile mills - blowing, carding rooms`],[`Zone on X-ray`,`Upper, rounded p/q/r, eggshell nodes`,`Upper, rounded p/q`,`Lower, irregular s/t/u, plaques`,`Normal X-ray`],[`Lung function`,`Restrictive or mixed, low DLCO`,`Normal (simple) to mixed (PMF)`,`Restrictive, low DLCO`,`Obstructive, cross-shift FEV1 fall`],[`Hallmark`,`Silicotic nodule; TB risk`,`Coal macule, focal emphysema, melanoptysis`,`Asbestos bodies, pleural plaques, clubbing`,`Monday chest tightness`],[`Cancer link`,`Lung cancer (IARC Group 1)`,`Little extra`,`Lung cancer (x50 with smoking), mesothelioma`,`None`],[`Associations`,`TB, systemic sclerosis, RA, CKD`,`Caplan syndrome, COPD`,`Rounded atelectasis, benign effusion`,`Mill fever, COPD`]]},{heading:`ILO classification of radiographs (2022) - what to write`,columns:[`Element`,`Codes`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`Film quality`,`1 to 4`,`1 good, 2 acceptable, 3 poor but readable, 4 unacceptable`],[`Small rounded opacities`,`p, q, r`,`Under 1.5 mm, 1.5-3 mm, 3-10 mm (silicosis, CWP)`],[`Small irregular opacities`,`s, t, u`,`Same widths, linear/reticular (asbestosis)`],[`Profusion`,`0/- to 3/+ (12 points)`,`1/0 or more = consistent with pneumoconiosis`],[`Zones`,`Upper, middle, lower each side`,`Record which of 6 zones are involved`],[`Large opacities`,`A, B, C`,`A: over 10 mm, sum up to 50 mm; B: up to right upper zone area; C: more than right upper zone`],[`Pleural`,`Plaques, diffuse thickening, calcification`,`Site, width, extent; costophrenic angle obliteration`],[`Symbols`,`e.g. es, tb, ax, co, pi`,`Eggshell nodes, TB, coalescence, cor pulmonale abnormal heart, pleural thickening interlobar`]]},{heading:`Drugs and interventions used`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Isoniazid + rifapentine (3HP) - TPT`,`Weekly weight-band dosing (adult over 50 kg: H 900 mg + P 900 mg) orally`,`12 weekly doses`,`Flu-like reaction, hepatitis; rule out active TB first; not with rifampicin-resistant contact`],[`HRZE fixed-dose combination - silicotuberculosis`,`Weight-banded FDC orally daily (e.g. 4 tablets for 55-69 kg)`,`2 months HRZE then 4 months HRE`,`Hepatitis, optic neuritis (E), gout (Z), rifampicin interactions`],[`Tiotropium (LAMA)`,`18 microgram DPI or 2.5 microgram x2 soft mist once daily`,`Long term`,`Dry mouth, urinary retention, glaucoma caution`],[`Salbutamol (SABA)`,`100-200 microgram MDI with spacer as needed`,`As needed; review if over twice weekly`,`Tremor, tachycardia, hypokalaemia`],[`Nintedanib`,`150 mg orally twice daily (100 mg twice daily if intolerant)`,`Long term, LFTs monthly for 3 months`,`Diarrhoea, raised transaminases, bleeding risk; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Varenicline`,`0.5 mg daily days 1-3, 0.5 mg twice daily days 4-7, then 1 mg twice daily`,`12 weeks (extend 12 more if quit)`,`Nausea, vivid dreams; reduce in severe CKD`],[`Oxygen (LTOT)`,`1-2 L/min nasal cannula to SpO2 over 90%`,`At least 15 hours/day`,`Fire risk with smoking; CO2 retention if over-titrated`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of nodules or fibrosis in a dust worker`,columns:[`Condition`,`Distribution`,`Discriminating feature`,`Confirming test`],rows:[[`Silicosis`,`Upper zone, perilymphatic nodules`,`Stable, afebrile, eggshell nodes, exposure history`,`ILO X-ray/HRCT plus history`],[`Pulmonary TB / silicotuberculosis`,`Upper zone, cavities, tree-in-bud`,`Fever, weight loss, changing X-ray`,`Sputum CBNAAT and culture`],[`Sarcoidosis`,`Upper/mid, perilymphatic`,`Symmetric hilar nodes, uveitis, raised ACE, no dust`,`Non-caseating granuloma on EBUS biopsy`],[`Miliary TB`,`Diffuse 1-3 mm random nodules`,`Acute febrile illness, choroid tubercles`,`CBNAAT on sputum/BAL, liver or marrow`],[`Asbestosis`,`Lower zone, subpleural`,`Pleural plaques, clubbing, exposure`,`HRCT plus history; asbestos bodies`],[`Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis`,`Lower, subpleural honeycombing`,`No plaques, no exposure, older smoker`,`HRCT UIP pattern, MDT`],[`Metastases`,`Lower zone, random, varying size`,`Known primary, rapid growth`,`CT, biopsy`]]},{heading:`Indian legal and compensation framework`,columns:[`Instrument`,`What it does`,`Doctor's duty`],rows:[[`Factories Act 1948, Section 89, Third Schedule (now OSH Code 2020)`,`Lists notifiable diseases including silicosis, asbestosis, byssinosis`,`Notify the Chief Inspector of Factories`],[`Mines Act 1952 / Mines Rules 1955 (now OSH Code 2020)`,`Notification of pneumoconiosis in miners; periodic medical exams`,`Notify DGMS; periodic exam with chest X-ray`],[`Employees' Compensation Act 1923, Schedule III (now Code on Social Security 2020)`,`Compensation for listed occupational diseases`,`Certify diagnosis and disability`],[`ESI Act 1948 (now Code on Social Security 2020)`,`Occupational disease, disablement and dependants' benefit for insured workers`,`ESI medical board referral`],[`Rajasthan Pneumoconiosis Policy 2019`,`Lump sum on certification and on death, pension, rehabilitation`,`Online registration; pneumoconiosis board certification`]]}],redFlags:[`Fever, weight loss or haemoptysis in a silica worker - presume silicotuberculosis until sputum CBNAAT and culture are done`,`Rapidly progressive breathlessness and diffuse ground glass after sandblasting or quartz grinding - acute silicoproteinosis`,`SpO2 under 90% at rest, or signs of cor pulmonale (raised JVP, oedema)`,`New unilateral pleural effusion or chest wall pain in an asbestos-exposed person - exclude mesothelioma`,`Enlarging or unilateral mass in PMF, or new haemoptysis in an asbestos worker who smokes - exclude lung cancer`,`Sudden pleuritic pain and breathlessness in PMF or emphysema - secondary pneumothorax`],pearls:[`The occupational history is the investigation: 'What do you do, what do you handle, how long, any mask, any co-worker ill?'`,`Silicosis = upper zone + eggshell nodes + TB risk; asbestosis = lower zone + plaques + clubbing + cancer.`,`ILO profusion 1/0 or more = radiological pneumoconiosis; large opacity over 10 mm = PMF (A, B, C).`,`Silicosis progresses even after exposure stops - remove early and screen for TB lifelong.`,`Asbestos x smoking is multiplicative (about 50-fold lung-cancer risk) - stopping smoking is the best single intervention.`,`Byssinosis = Monday chest tightness (cotton endotoxin); occupational asthma = better on holidays but not tolerant through the week.`,`Every silicosis certificate you write is worth lakhs to the family - name the disease, notify, and refer to the board.`,`Caplan = RA + pneumoconiosis nodules; Erasmus = silica + systemic sclerosis.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-occupational-lung-q1`,stem:`A 34-year-old slate-pencil cutter from Mandsaur has 8 years of dry cutting. Chest X-ray shows bilateral upper-zone small rounded opacities with eggshell calcification of hilar nodes. Over 2 months he has developed fever and 6 kg weight loss. What is the most appropriate next step?`,options:[`Start oral prednisolone for accelerated silicosis`,`Sputum CBNAAT and culture to look for silicotuberculosis`,`Start empirical HRZE without testing as TB is almost certain`,`Arrange whole-lung lavage`],answer:1,explanation:`Silicosis raises TB risk many-fold, and fever with weight loss in a silica worker means silicotuberculosis until proved otherwise; NTEP requires microbiological confirmation with CBNAAT (which also detects rifampicin resistance) and culture. Empirical ATT without testing misses drug resistance and wrongly labels patients; steroids are unproven and dangerous with possible TB; whole-lung lavage is for acute silicoproteinosis, not chronic nodular silicosis.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-occupational-lung-q2`,stem:`On the ILO classification of radiographs of pneumoconioses, which reading is the conventional threshold taken as radiological evidence of pneumoconiosis?`,options:[`Profusion 0/1`,`Any large opacity of 5 mm`,`Profusion category 1/0 or more`,`Small irregular opacities of type s in one zone at 0/0`],answer:2,explanation:`The ILO uses a 12-point profusion scale from 0/- to 3/+, and a profusion of 1/0 or more is taken as consistent with pneumoconiosis for surveillance and compensation. A reading of 0/1 means the reader considered category 1 but settled on normal; large opacities are defined as over 10 mm, not 5 mm; and 0/0 is a normal film whatever the shape code.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-occupational-lung-q3`,stem:`Which of the following findings is the commonest manifestation of past asbestos exposure?`,options:[`Malignant pleural mesothelioma`,`Asbestosis with honeycombing`,`Rounded atelectasis`,`Calcified parietal pleural plaques`],answer:3,explanation:`Pleural plaques on the parietal pleura and diaphragm are the commonest asbestos-related finding and are a marker of exposure, usually symptomless, appearing 20-30 years after exposure. Asbestosis needs heavier exposure, mesothelioma is rare though strongly linked to amphiboles, and rounded atelectasis is an uncommon pleuro-parenchymal lesion with a comet-tail sign.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-occupational-lung-q4`,stem:`A 45-year-old textile mill worker in the carding room complains of chest tightness on the first day back at work after the weekly holiday, which settles by the middle of the week. What is the most likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Byssinosis`,`Bagassosis`,`Occupational asthma due to isocyanates`,`Metal fume fever`],answer:0,explanation:`First-working-day ('Monday') chest tightness that improves through the week reflects tolerance to cotton-dust endotoxin and is the hallmark of byssinosis (Schilling grade 1). Bagassosis is hypersensitivity pneumonitis from mouldy sugar-cane bagasse; isocyanate asthma is sensitiser-driven without weekly tolerance; metal fume fever follows welding galvanised metal, though it too can be worse on Mondays, but the carding room points to cotton.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-occupational-lung-q5`,stem:`A 52-year-old coal miner with seropositive rheumatoid arthritis has multiple well-defined 1-3 cm peripheral nodules on chest X-ray appearing over a short time. What is the eponym?`,options:[`Erasmus syndrome`,`Loeffler syndrome`,`Caplan syndrome`,`Blesovsky syndrome`],answer:2,explanation:`Caplan syndrome is rheumatoid arthritis with pneumoconiosis (first described in Welsh coal miners) producing multiple peripheral necrobiotic nodules of 0.5-5 cm. Erasmus syndrome is silica exposure with systemic sclerosis; Loeffler syndrome is transient eosinophilic infiltrates from parasites or drugs; Blesovsky syndrome is rounded atelectasis from asbestos pleural disease.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-occupational-lung-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Define pneumoconiosis. Discuss the aetiopathogenesis, clinical features, diagnosis and management of silicosis, and the role of the family physician in its prevention and compensation in India.`,openingLines:[`Pneumoconiosis is the accumulation of mineral dust in the lungs and the tissue reaction to it; silicosis is fibrotic nodular lung disease caused by inhaled respirable crystalline silica.`,`India has an estimated 10 million workers at risk of silica exposure, and silicotuberculosis is the major cause of death - making silicosis a preventable, notifiable and compensable disease.`],answer:[{heading:`Aetiology and at-risk occupations`,points:[`Respirable crystalline silica (quartz) 0.5-5 micrometres; freshly fractured is most toxic.`,`Indian occupations: stone crushing, sandstone mining, agate grinding (Khambhat), slate-pencil cutting (Mandsaur), foundries, glass, ceramics, sandblasting, engineered stone.`]},{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Macrophage ingestion, phagolysosome rupture, NLRP3 inflammasome, IL-1beta and TGF-beta, fibroblast activation, silicotic nodule; repeated cycles continue after exposure stops.`,`Macrophage damage impairs TB killing - silicotuberculosis; silica as adjuvant - systemic sclerosis, RA, CKD; IARC Group 1 carcinogen.`]},{heading:`Types and clinical features`,points:[`Chronic (over 10 years), accelerated (5-10 years), acute silicoproteinosis (under 5 years, massive exposure).`,`Simple: often asymptomatic, normal examination; PMF: breathlessness, cough, cor pulmonale; silicotuberculosis: fever, weight loss, haemoptysis.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Occupational history plus ILO-classified PA chest X-ray: upper-zone rounded p/q/r opacities, eggshell hilar nodes, PMF (large opacity over 10 mm).`,`HRCT, spirometry (restrictive/mixed, low DLCO), sputum CBNAAT and culture for TB, echocardiography for pulmonary hypertension.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`No reversing drug: remove from exposure, stop smoking, vaccinate, bronchodilators, pulmonary rehabilitation, LTOT when indicated.`,`TB: screen at every visit, TPT (3HP) when latent, standard NTEP regimen for active disease; whole-lung lavage for acute form; transplant referral.`]},{heading:`Prevention and the family physician`,points:[`Hierarchy of control: substitution, wet methods, exhaust ventilation, respirators; pre-placement and periodic X-ray surveillance.`,`Notify under the Factories/Mines Act (now OSH Code 2020); certify for compensation under the EC Act/ESI (now Code on Social Security 2020) and state schemes such as Rajasthan's 2019 policy.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: silica particle to macrophage to cytokines to fibroblast to silicotic nodule, with the TB arm`,`Table of ILO small and large opacity codes`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and aetiology`,marks:1},{part:`Pathogenesis`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical types and features`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis including ILO`,marks:2},{part:`Management and TB`,marks:2},{part:`Prevention, notification, compensation`,marks:1}],keywords:[`silicosis`,`silicotuberculosis`,`ILO classification`,`PMF`,`notifiable`,`compensation`,`NIOH`]},{id:`respiratory-occupational-lung-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate silicosis from asbestosis.`,openingLines:[`Both are fibrogenic pneumoconioses with long latency, notifiable and compensable, but they differ in agent, site, radiology and the diseases that follow.`,`Silicosis is an upper-zone nodular disease that predisposes to TB; asbestosis is a lower-zone interstitial fibrosis that predisposes to lung cancer and mesothelioma.`],answer:[{heading:`Agent and exposure`,points:[`Silicosis: crystalline silica - stone, mining, foundry, glass.`,`Asbestosis: asbestos fibres - AC sheets, ship-breaking, insulation, brake linings.`]},{heading:`Pathology and radiology`,points:[`Silicosis: silicotic nodule; upper-zone rounded p/q/r opacities, eggshell hilar nodes, PMF.`,`Asbestosis: peribronchiolar then subpleural fibrosis with asbestos bodies; lower-zone irregular s/t/u opacities, pleural plaques.`]},{heading:`Clinical and complications`,points:[`Silicosis: clubbing uncommon; TB, autoimmune disease, CKD, cor pulmonale.`,`Asbestosis: Velcro crackles and clubbing common; lung cancer (multiplicative with smoking) and mesothelioma.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column comparison table: agent, zone, opacity type, hallmark, complications`],markSplit:[{part:`Agent and exposure`,marks:1},{part:`Pathology and radiology`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical and complications`,marks:2}],keywords:[`silicosis`,`asbestosis`,`pleural plaques`,`eggshell calcification`,`mesothelioma`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Occupational and Environmental Lung Disease`,`International Labour Office. Guidelines for the use of the ILO International Classification of Radiographs of Pneumoconioses, revised edition 2022`,`ICMR-National Institute of Occupational Health, Ahmedabad - silicosis and occupational disease publications`,`National TB Elimination Programme: Guidelines for Programmatic Management of TB Preventive Treatment in India, 2021, and NTEP Training Modules 2023`,`Raghu G et al. Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis and progressive pulmonary fibrosis in adults: ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT clinical practice guideline, 2022`,`Blanc PD et al. The occupational burden of nonmalignant respiratory diseases: ATS/ERS statement, 2019`,`Government of India: Occupational Safety, Health and Working Conditions Code 2020 and Code on Social Security 2020 (in force November 2025); Factories Act 1948; Employees' Compensation Act 1923`,`Government of Rajasthan: Pneumoconiosis Policy 2019`]}),o.push({id:`respiratory-pneumothorax`,title:`Pneumothorax: primary, secondary, tension and management`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Pneumothorax** is air in the pleural space; it is **primary spontaneous** without known lung disease, **secondary** with underlying lung disease (COPD, TB), **traumatic or iatrogenic**, and **tension** when a one-way valve causes haemodynamic collapse - and **BTS 2023** now manages it by **symptoms and high-risk features, not size**, offering **conservative, ambulatory, aspiration or drain** options.`,keywords:[`pneumothorax`,`PSP`,`SSP`,`primary spontaneous pneumothorax`,`secondary spontaneous pneumothorax`,`tension pneumothorax`,`needle decompression`,`needle aspiration`,`chest drain`,`intercostal drain`,`ambulatory pleural vent`,`pleurodesis`,`VATS bullectomy`,`lung point`,`catamenial pneumothorax`,`Birt-Hogg-Dube`,`re-expansion pulmonary oedema`,`Hamman sign`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Pneumothorax** is air in the pleural space with secondary lung collapse; the visible rim is the visceral pleural line with no lung markings beyond it. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Primary spontaneous pneumothorax (PSP)** occurs without clinically apparent lung disease; incidence about **18-28 per 100,000 per year in men and 1.2-6 in women**. [BTS 2023]`,`**Secondary spontaneous pneumothorax (SSP)** occurs with underlying lung disease, peaks over 55 years and carries higher mortality because the reserve is small. [BTS 2023]`,`PSP typically affects **tall thin young men aged 15-35**, usually at rest - not during exertion. [Harrison 22e]`,`**In India**, **tuberculosis and COPD** are the leading causes of SSP, with TB (active or post-TB lung disease) prominent in younger SSP patients in Indian series. [Indian J Chest Dis 2018]`,`**Iatrogenic pneumothorax** (transthoracic biopsy, central lines, thoracocentesis, ventilation) is now as common as PSP in hospital practice. [BTS 2023]`,`**Recurrence** after a first PSP is about **30% (most within the first year)**, and higher after SSP. [BTS 2023]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**PSP**: rupture of apical subpleural **blebs and bullae** (emphysema-like changes on CT in up to 80-90%) or diffuse pleural porosity. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Smoking** is the dominant modifiable risk: lifetime risk of PSP about **12% in male smokers vs 0.1% in non-smokers**, with a dose-response relation; **cannabis** also raises risk. [BTS 2023]`,`**SSP causes**: COPD (commonest worldwide), **TB and post-TB lung**, cystic fibrosis, ILD, necrotising pneumonia, **Pneumocystis jirovecii in HIV**, lung cancer, sarcoidosis, PMF. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cystic lung diseases**: **LAM** (women, tuberous sclerosis), **Birt-Hogg-Dube syndrome** (FLCN mutation, renal tumours, fibrofolliculomas), Langerhans cell histiocytosis (smokers). [BTS 2023]`,`**Catamenial pneumothorax**: thoracic endometriosis, right-sided, within 72 hours of menses onset. [BTS 2023]`,`**Connective tissue**: Marfan syndrome, Ehlers-Danlos (vascular), homocystinuria. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Iatrogenic**: CT-guided lung biopsy (commonest), subclavian or jugular lines, thoracocentesis, pleural biopsy, bronchoscopic biopsy, mechanical ventilation (barotrauma), acupuncture. [BTS 2023]`,`**Traumatic**: penetrating or blunt chest injury with rib fractures; covered under ATLS. [ATLS 10e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Pleural pressure is normally **negative (about -5 cmH2O)** because the lung recoils inward and the chest wall outward; a breach lets air in until pressures equalise and the lung collapses by its own elastic recoil. [Harrison 22e]`,`The chest wall springs out, so the **hemithorax enlarges**, the diaphragm flattens, and vital capacity and PaO2 fall from **V/Q mismatch and shunt** through the collapsed lung. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tension pneumothorax**: a **one-way valve** lets air in on inspiration but not out, so pleural pressure becomes positive, the mediastinum shifts, **venous return falls** and cardiac output collapses - obstructive shock. [ATLS 10e]`,`Tension develops fastest with **positive-pressure ventilation** (intubated patients, NIV, CPR); in spontaneously breathing patients hypoxaemia and respiratory distress dominate before hypotension. [ATLS 10e]`,`**Spontaneous reabsorption** of air is about **1.5-2% of the hemithorax volume per day**; breathing high-concentration oxygen raises the nitrogen gradient and speeds it up to about 4-fold. [BTS 2023]`,`**Re-expansion pulmonary oedema**: rapid re-expansion of a lung collapsed for over 72 hours causes capillary leak from reperfusion injury - hence no suction early and controlled drainage. [BTS 2023]`,`**Why SSP is dangerous**: a small air volume in a lung with little reserve (COPD) causes severe hypoxaemia and hypercapnia, and air leaks persist because diseased lung heals poorly. [BTS 2023]`]},{heading:`Classification and size`,points:[`**Spontaneous (primary, secondary)**, **traumatic** and **iatrogenic**; tension is a physiological state, not a cause, and can complicate any type. [BTS 2023]`,`**Size by BTS 2010**: interpleural distance at the level of the hilum - **under 2 cm small, 2 cm or more large** (about 50% of hemithorax volume). [BTS 2010]`,`**ACCP** used apex-to-cupola distance of **3 cm** to define large; the two methods disagree, which is one reason BTS 2023 moved away from size. [ACCP 2001]`,`**BTS 2023**: management depends on **symptoms and high-risk characteristics**; size matters only for deciding whether there is a **safe pocket for intervention**. [BTS 2023]`,`**High-risk characteristics (BTS 2023)**: haemodynamic instability, significant hypoxia, bilateral pneumothorax, underlying lung disease, **age 50 or more with significant smoking history**, haemopneumothorax, and tension physiology. [BTS 2023]`,`**Light index** (100 minus (lung diameter cubed / hemithorax diameter cubed) x 100) estimates percentage collapse - mainly used in research. [Harrison 22e]`,`Any pneumothorax in a patient **over 50 who smokes** should be treated as **secondary** until proved otherwise. [BTS 2023]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Sudden ipsilateral pleuritic chest pain** and **breathlessness**, usually at rest; pain may settle within 24 hours even though air persists. [Harrison 22e]`,`PSP patients often **delay presentation for days**; breathlessness is often mild because healthy lungs compensate. [BTS 2023]`,`**SSP**: breathlessness out of proportion to size, often presenting as a 'COPD exacerbation that fails to respond'. [BTS 2023]`,`**Tension**: rapidly worsening distress, anxiety, chest pain, and in ventilated patients **rising airway pressures, falling SpO2 and hypotension**. [ATLS 10e]`,`Ask about **smoking, cannabis, previous episodes, family history** (Birt-Hogg-Dube, Marfan), **timing with menses**, HIV risk, recent procedures, and **occupation (pilot, diver)**. [BTS 2023]`,`**Atypical**: shoulder-tip pain, isolated cough, or found incidentally on a chest X-ray done for something else. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Small pneumothorax may have **no signs**; large ones show **reduced expansion, hyper-resonant percussion, absent or reduced breath sounds and reduced vocal resonance** on the affected side. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tension**: tachycardia over 120, hypotension, **distended neck veins** (may be absent with hypovolaemia), hypoxaemia, contralateral **tracheal deviation** (a late sign) and hyperexpanded silent hemithorax. [ATLS 10e]`,`**Hamman's sign**: a crunching sound synchronous with the heartbeat in left-sided pneumothorax or pneumomediastinum. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Subcutaneous emphysema** (crepitus) suggests a drain-related leak, trauma or ruptured airway. [BTS 2023]`,`In COPD, signs are masked by hyperinflation - a **unilateral silent chest** or asymmetric breath sounds should prompt imaging. [BTS 2023]`,`Look for **marfanoid habitus, skin fibrofolliculomas** (Birt-Hogg-Dube) and signs of TB or HIV. [BTS 2023]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Giant bulla** mimics pneumothorax on X-ray: bulla has a concave inner margin and septations; **CT distinguishes** - inserting a drain into a bulla causes a bronchopleural fistula. [BTS 2023]`,`**Pulmonary embolism**: sudden pleuritic pain and breathlessness, but no hyper-resonance and a normal lung edge - see the Pulmonary embolism topic. [ESC 2019]`,`**Acute coronary syndrome and aortic dissection** for sudden chest pain - ECG and troponin. [ESC 2023]`,`**Skin fold** on a supine film gives a false line that extends beyond the chest wall and has lung markings lateral to it. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute asthma or COPD exacerbation**: bilateral wheeze; a pneumothorax may complicate either - look for asymmetry. [GINA 2025]`,`**Pneumomediastinum** (asthma, vomiting - Boerhaave, cocaine): air outlining mediastinum and neck crepitus. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Massive pleural effusion** shifts the trachea away too, but is stony dull, not hyper-resonant. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Erect PA chest X-ray in inspiration** is first-line; routine **expiratory films are not recommended**. [BTS 2023]`,`**Supine films** (trauma, ICU) miss anterior pneumothorax; look for the **deep sulcus sign**, a hyperlucent hemithorax and a sharp heart border. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lung ultrasound**: **absent lung sliding, absent B-lines, barcode (stratosphere) sign on M-mode**; the **lung point** is 100% specific - more sensitive than supine CXR. [BTS 2023]`,`**CT thorax** for complex cases: to distinguish bulla from pneumothorax, assess underlying lung disease, plan surgery, or find cysts (LAM, BHD). [BTS 2023]`,`**ABG** in SSP or hypoxaemia: hypoxaemia, and hypercapnia in COPD guides oxygen target. [BTS 2017]`,`**Sputum CBNAAT** in SSP with cavitation or TB features; HIV test if PCP suspected. [NTEP 2023]`,`**Tension pneumothorax is a clinical diagnosis** - never delay decompression for an X-ray. [ATLS 10e]`]},{heading:`Management in the emergency department and OPD (BTS 2023)`,points:[`**Step 1 - is it tension?** If so, immediate decompression; otherwise assess symptoms and high-risk characteristics. [BTS 2023]`,`**PSP with minimal symptoms and no high-risk features**: **conservative management regardless of size** - discharge with safety-net advice and review in 2-4 days (PSP trial: non-inferior, fewer complications and recurrences). [BTS 2023]`,`**PSP symptomatic** wanting intervention: options are **needle aspiration** (16-18G cannula, 2nd ICS midclavicular or 4th-5th ICS mid-axillary, stop after 2.5 L), **ambulatory device** (e.g. Rocket Pleural Vent) or a **small-bore Seldinger chest drain**. [BTS 2023]`,`**Ambulatory management** reduces hospital stay but causes more adverse events than standard care (RAMPP trial); use where there are pathways for follow-up. [BTS 2023]`,`**SSP**: admit all; **small-bore chest drain (14F or less, Seldinger)** for most; needle aspiration only for small SSP with few symptoms; ambulatory devices only in specialist hands. [BTS 2023]`,`**Oxygen**: give to hypoxaemic patients to SpO2 94-98% (88-92% if at risk of hypercapnia); BTS 2023 does not advise high-flow oxygen solely to speed reabsorption in non-hypoxic patients. [BTS 2023]`,`**Analgesia**: paracetamol 1 g 6-hourly (max 4 g/day) plus NSAID or opioid as needed. [BTS 2023]`,`Advise **no smoking, no flying, no diving** while the pneumothorax persists, and to return at once with worsening breathlessness. [BTS 2023]`]},{heading:`Inpatient care: tension, drains and persistent air leak`,points:[`**Tension in adults**: needle decompression at the **4th or 5th intercostal space just anterior to the mid-axillary line** (ATLS 10th edition), or **finger thoracostomy** in trauma/ventilated patients; in **children the 2nd ICS midclavicular line** remains standard. [ATLS 10e]`,`Use a cannula of **at least 5 cm (8 cm preferred)** as chest wall thickness often exceeds 4.5 cm at the 2nd ICS; follow immediately with a chest drain. [ATLS 10e]`,`**Chest drain site**: the **triangle of safety** - anterior border of latissimus dorsi, lateral border of pectoralis major, line above the nipple (5th ICS) and apex below the axilla - inserting just above the rib. [BTS 2023]`,`Connect to an **underwater seal**; **no routine suction**; if needed later use low-pressure high-volume suction (-10 to -20 cmH2O). **Never clamp a bubbling drain**. [BTS 2023]`,`**Persistent air leak or failure to re-expand after 3-5 days**: refer to **thoracic surgery**; if unfit, consider **autologous blood patch pleurodesis**, endobronchial valves, or talc slurry. [BTS 2023]`,`**Remove the drain** once the lung is re-expanded and bubbling has stopped; a check X-ray after removal is advised. [BTS 2023]`,`**Re-expansion pulmonary oedema**: cough and breathlessness after drainage - supportive care with oxygen; prevent by avoiding early suction. [BTS 2023]`,`**Mechanically ventilated patients** with pneumothorax always need a chest drain. [BTS 2023]`]},{heading:`Recurrence prevention and special situations`,points:[`**Definitive intervention** offered after: **second ipsilateral** or first contralateral episode, bilateral simultaneous, persistent air leak, **haemopneumothorax**, tension episode, **pregnancy-related**, or **high-risk occupation** (pilots, divers). [BTS 2023]`,`BTS 2023 also allows offering surgery after a **first PSP** after shared decision-making, especially in those wanting to minimise recurrence. [BTS 2023]`,`**VATS bullectomy with surgical pleurectomy** (or pleural abrasion) has the lowest recurrence (about 1-5%); **talc pleurodesis** via chest drain is for those unfit for surgery. [BTS 2023]`,`**Flying**: do not fly with an existing pneumothorax; most airlines advise waiting at least **1-2 weeks after radiological resolution**; after definitive surgery flying is safe once recovered. [BTS Air Travel 2022]`,`**Diving**: avoid scuba diving permanently after pneumothorax unless the patient has had **bilateral surgical pleurectomy**. [BTS 2023]`,`**Pregnancy**: conservative or aspiration first; plan delivery with early epidural and assisted second stage to avoid prolonged Valsalva; definitive surgery after delivery. [BTS 2023]`,`**Catamenial**: VATS with diaphragm repair plus hormonal suppression (GnRH analogues) with gynaecology. [BTS 2023]`,`**HIV with PCP**: often bilateral and with persistent leak; treat PCP (co-trimoxazole) and drain early. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Children**: same principles; 2nd ICS midclavicular for needle decompression. [ATLS 10e]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Tension pneumothorax and cardiac arrest** - a reversible cause in the **4 Hs and 4 Ts** of ALS. [ERC 2025]`,`**Persistent air leak (bronchopleural fistula)**, especially in SSP, TB and necrotising pneumonia. [BTS 2023]`,`**Haemopneumothorax** (torn adhesion vessel) - may need surgery if drain output exceeds 1.5 L at once or 200 mL/hour. [ATLS 10e]`,`**Drain complications**: malposition, organ injury, infection/empyema, surgical emphysema, re-expansion oedema. [BTS 2023]`,`**Recurrence**: about 30% after PSP and 40% or more after SSP without definitive treatment. [BTS 2023]`,`**SSP mortality** is substantially higher than PSP (reported around 1-10% in hospital series) because of underlying disease. [BTS 2023]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Conservatively managed PSP**: review in **2-4 days** with repeat imaging, then until resolution. [BTS 2023]`,`**Respiratory clinic follow-up at 2-4 weeks** for all, to confirm resolution, advise on smoking, flying and diving, and discuss recurrence risk. [BTS 2023]`,`**Smoking cessation** at every contact - the strongest modifiable factor for recurrence. [BTS 2023]`,`**CT thorax** after resolution if recurrence, family history, or suspicion of cystic lung disease (LAM, BHD) - refer to genetics for BHD. [BTS 2023]`,`**Refer to thoracic surgery** for recurrence, persistent leak, occupation-related or patient-preference definitive management. [BTS 2023]`,`In India, **refer SSP to a district or tertiary hospital** with drain facilities and TB evaluation. [ICMR STW]`]},{heading:`Prevention and counselling`,points:[`**Stop smoking tobacco and cannabis** - lowers recurrence substantially. [BTS 2023]`,`Explain **warning symptoms** (worse breathlessness, chest pain) and to come back immediately. [BTS 2023]`,`**Avoid air travel** until resolution and **diving** for life unless definitive surgery. [BTS 2023]`,`Treat the underlying lung disease (**COPD, TB** under NTEP) - see the COPD and Tuberculosis topics. [NTEP 2023]`,`**Procedural safety**: ultrasound-guided thoracocentesis and line insertion lower iatrogenic pneumothorax. [BTS 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (BTS 2023 and after)`,points:[`**BTS Pleural Disease Guideline 2023** replaces 2010: decisions by **symptoms and high-risk characteristics, not size**. [BTS 2023]`,`**Conservative management** is acceptable for minimally symptomatic PSP of any size (based on the 2020 PSP trial). [BTS 2023]`,`**Ambulatory management** (pleural vent) is an option for PSP (RAMPP trial 2020). [BTS 2023]`,`**Surgery can be offered after a first PSP** after discussing recurrence risk and patient values. [BTS 2023]`,`**Autologous blood patch** and endobronchial valves are options for persistent air leak in non-surgical candidates. [BTS 2023]`,`**Needle decompression site** in adults is 4th/5th ICS anterior to mid-axillary line (ATLS 10e), now reflected in trauma and ALS teaching. [ATLS 10e]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic question**: 'tall thin young smoker with sudden pleuritic pain' - PSP; what is BTS 2023 management? Conservative if minimally symptomatic, regardless of size.`,`**Tension is a clinical diagnosis**; decompress before X-ray - and know the site (adult 4th/5th ICS anterior to mid-axillary line).`,`**Triangle of safety** boundaries are a favourite viva question.`,`**Common mistake**: draining a giant bulla mistaken for pneumothorax - get a CT if unsure.`,`**Common mistake**: clamping a bubbling drain, or suction early after a long-standing collapse.`,`**Signs**: hyper-resonance with reduced breath sounds and vocal resonance - contrast with effusion (stony dull).`,`**Lung ultrasound**: absent sliding plus lung point - examiners love 'barcode sign'.`]}],tables:[{heading:`PSP vs SSP vs tension`,columns:[`Feature`,`Primary spontaneous`,`Secondary spontaneous`,`Tension`],rows:[[`Patient`,`Tall young smoker, no lung disease`,`Over 50, COPD, TB, ILD, PCP`,`Any, especially ventilated or trauma`],[`Symptoms`,`Mild breathlessness, pleuritic pain`,`Severe breathlessness, hypoxaemia`,`Distress, hypotension, shock`],[`Default management`,`Conservative if minimal symptoms`,`Admit; small-bore drain`,`Immediate decompression then drain`],[`Recurrence`,`About 30%`,`About 40% or more`,`Depends on cause`],[`Surgery`,`After second episode or by choice`,`Earlier; persistent leak`,`Consider after episode`]]},{heading:`BTS 2023 decision table`,columns:[`Situation`,`Option`,`Practical point`],rows:[[`Tension physiology`,`Needle decompression or finger thoracostomy`,`Adult 4th/5th ICS anterior to MAL; then drain`],[`PSP, minimal symptoms, no high-risk feature`,`Conservative`,`Review in 2-4 days; safety-net`],[`PSP, symptomatic`,`Needle aspiration or ambulatory device`,`Aspirate max 2.5 L; drain if fails`],[`SSP`,`Admit, chest drain 14F or less`,`Aspiration only if small and few symptoms`],[`Persistent leak 3-5 days`,`Thoracic surgical referral`,`Blood patch or valves if unfit`],[`Recurrence or high-risk job`,`VATS bullectomy + pleurectomy`,`Talc pleurodesis if unfit`]]},{heading:`Drugs and interventions`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Oxygen`,`Titrate to SpO2 94-98% (88-92% if hypercapnic risk)`,`Until hypoxaemia resolves`,`CO2 retention in COPD if uncontrolled`],[`Paracetamol`,`1 g orally or IV 6-hourly (max 4 g/day)`,`As needed`,`Hepatotoxicity in overdose; reduce in low weight or liver disease`],[`Ibuprofen`,`400 mg orally 8-hourly with food`,`Short course`,`GI bleed, AKI; avoid in CKD and asthma with NSAID sensitivity`],[`Lidocaine 1% (local anaesthetic for drain)`,`Up to 3 mg/kg infiltrated`,`Single procedure`,`Toxicity: perioral tingling, seizures, arrhythmia`],[`Sterile talc (pleurodesis)`,`4-5 g slurry via drain or poudrage at VATS`,`Once`,`Pain, fever; graded large-particle talc avoids ARDS`],[`Autologous blood patch`,`50-100 mL of patient's blood via drain`,`Repeat once if needed`,`Infection, drain blockage`]]},{heading:`Imaging signs`,columns:[`Modality`,`Sign`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`Erect CXR`,`Visceral pleural line, no markings beyond`,`Pneumothorax`],[`Erect CXR`,`Interpleural distance at hilum 2 cm or more`,`Large (BTS 2010)`],[`Supine CXR`,`Deep sulcus sign`,`Anterior-basal pneumothorax`],[`Ultrasound`,`Absent lung sliding, no B-lines, barcode sign`,`Suggests pneumothorax`],[`Ultrasound`,`Lung point`,`Confirms pneumothorax (100% specific)`],[`CT`,`Septations, concave margin`,`Bulla, not pneumothorax`]]}],redFlags:[`Hypotension, tachycardia over 120, distended neck veins or tracheal deviation - tension pneumothorax, decompress now`,`Any pneumothorax in a ventilated or NIV patient with rising airway pressure and falling SpO2`,`SpO2 under 92% or significant breathlessness with underlying lung disease (SSP)`,`Bilateral pneumothorax`,`Haemopneumothorax or large drain blood loss`,`Worsening breathlessness after aspiration or in conservative management`,`Pneumothorax with HIV or suspected TB (risk of persistent leak and empyema)`],pearls:[`BTS 2023: treat the patient, not the X-ray - symptoms and high-risk features decide, not size.`,`Tension is clinical: decompress before imaging; adult site 4th/5th ICS just anterior to mid-axillary line.`,`Triangle of safety: latissimus dorsi, pectoralis major, 5th ICS, apex below axilla.`,`Never clamp a bubbling drain; no early suction.`,`Over 50 and a smoker = treat as secondary pneumothorax.`,`Lung point on ultrasound = 100% specific.`,`Smoking is the strongest modifiable risk for PSP and recurrence.`,`No diving for life unless bilateral surgical pleurectomy.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-pneumothorax-q1`,stem:`A 22-year-old tall, thin smoker presents with left pleuritic chest pain for 1 day. He is comfortable, SpO2 98% on air, BP 120/76 mmHg. Chest X-ray shows a left pneumothorax with a 2.5 cm interpleural distance at the hilum. According to BTS 2023, what is the preferred management?`,options:[`Large-bore chest drain with suction`,`Needle aspiration because the pneumothorax is over 2 cm`,`Conservative management with outpatient review in 2-4 days`,`Immediate VATS pleurectomy`],answer:2,explanation:`BTS 2023 manages primary spontaneous pneumothorax by symptoms and high-risk features rather than size; a minimally symptomatic PSP with no high-risk characteristic can be managed conservatively regardless of size, supported by the PSP trial. Aspiration on the basis of the 2 cm cut-off was the 2010 approach; large-bore drains with suction are not routine; surgery is for recurrence or by informed choice, not urgent.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-pneumothorax-q2`,stem:`A motorcyclist with chest trauma becomes hypotensive with absent breath sounds on the right and distended neck veins. Per ATLS 10th edition, where should needle decompression be performed in this adult?`,options:[`2nd intercostal space midclavicular line`,`4th or 5th intercostal space just anterior to the mid-axillary line`,`7th intercostal space posterior axillary line`,`Subxiphoid approach`],answer:1,explanation:`ATLS 10th edition moved adult needle decompression to the 4th or 5th intercostal space just anterior to the mid-axillary line, because the chest wall is thinner there and a standard cannula more often reaches the pleura. The 2nd ICS midclavicular site remains for children; the 7th posterior axillary site risks the diaphragm and abdominal organs; the subxiphoid approach is for pericardiocentesis.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-pneumothorax-q3`,stem:`A 65-year-old with COPD presents breathless with SpO2 86%. Chest X-ray shows a 1.5 cm right apical pneumothorax. What is the most appropriate management?`,options:[`Discharge with outpatient review as the pneumothorax is small`,`Admit, controlled oxygen and insert a small-bore chest drain`,`High-flow oxygen through a non-rebreather mask alone`,`Immediate referral for talc pleurodesis without drainage`],answer:1,explanation:`This is a secondary pneumothorax with hypoxaemia; BTS 2023 advises admission for all SSP and a small-bore (14F or less) Seldinger drain for symptomatic patients, with oxygen titrated to 88-92% given hypercapnia risk. Size is small but symptoms and underlying disease make it high risk, so discharge is unsafe; uncontrolled high-flow oxygen risks CO2 retention; pleurodesis without re-expansion is not appropriate.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-pneumothorax-q4`,stem:`On bedside lung ultrasound, which finding is most specific for pneumothorax?`,options:[`Multiple B-lines`,`Absent lung sliding`,`Lung point`,`Seashore sign on M-mode`],answer:2,explanation:`The lung point - where sliding lung meets the non-sliding pneumothorax edge - is virtually 100% specific. Absent lung sliding is sensitive but also occurs with pleurodesis, apnoea or mainstem intubation; B-lines arise from the visceral pleura and therefore exclude pneumothorax at that spot; the seashore sign is the normal M-mode pattern, whereas pneumothorax gives the barcode sign.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-pneumothorax-q5`,stem:`A 28-year-old professional scuba diving instructor has a first episode of primary spontaneous pneumothorax, now resolved. What advice is most appropriate?`,options:[`Return to diving after 6 weeks if X-ray is normal`,`Return to diving after 2 weeks with a chest drain on standby`,`Diving is safe once he stops smoking`,`Avoid diving permanently unless he has bilateral surgical pleurectomy`],answer:3,explanation:`Pressure changes during ascent expand any trapped air, so a recurrence underwater can be fatal; BTS advises avoiding diving permanently after a pneumothorax unless definitive bilateral surgical pleurectomy has been done, and high-risk occupations are an indication to offer surgery after a first episode. A time-based return or stopping smoking does not remove the recurrence risk.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-pneumothorax-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Classify pneumothorax. Describe the clinical features, diagnosis and management of spontaneous pneumothorax according to the BTS 2023 guideline, including recurrence prevention.`,openingLines:[`Pneumothorax is air in the pleural space; it is classified as spontaneous (primary or secondary), traumatic and iatrogenic, and any type can become a tension pneumothorax.`,`The BTS 2023 guideline manages it by symptoms and high-risk characteristics rather than size, offering conservative, ambulatory, aspiration or drain options.`],answer:[{heading:`Classification and causes`,points:[`PSP: no known lung disease, apical blebs, tall young smokers.`,`SSP: COPD, TB, ILD, PCP, CF, LAM, BHD, catamenial; traumatic and iatrogenic; tension physiology.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Sudden pleuritic pain and breathlessness; SSP more breathless.`,`Signs: reduced expansion, hyper-resonance, absent breath sounds; tension: hypotension, raised JVP, tracheal shift.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Erect PA CXR, interpleural distance at hilum (2 cm = large, BTS 2010); ultrasound - absent sliding, lung point.`,`CT for bulla vs pneumothorax and underlying disease; ABG in SSP.`]},{heading:`Management (BTS 2023)`,points:[`Tension: decompress at 4th/5th ICS anterior to MAL, then drain.`,`PSP minimal symptoms: conservative; symptomatic: aspiration, ambulatory device or small drain.`,`SSP: admit, small-bore drain, controlled oxygen; persistent leak at 3-5 days: surgery, blood patch.`]},{heading:`Recurrence prevention and advice`,points:[`VATS bullectomy with pleurectomy after second episode, bilateral, persistent leak, high-risk job, or by choice after first.`,`Stop smoking; no flying until resolved; no diving unless bilateral pleurectomy.`]}],mustDraw:[`BTS 2023 algorithm flow chart`,`Triangle of safety diagram`],markSplit:[{part:`Classification and causes`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:1},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Management algorithm`,marks:3},{part:`Recurrence prevention and advice`,marks:2}],keywords:[`PSP`,`SSP`,`BTS 2023`,`aspiration`,`chest drain`,`pleurodesis`,`tension`]},{id:`respiratory-pneumothorax-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`core`,question:`Tension pneumothorax: recognition and emergency management.`,openingLines:[`Tension pneumothorax is a pneumothorax with a one-way valve that raises pleural pressure, shifts the mediastinum and reduces venous return, causing obstructive shock.`,`It is a clinical diagnosis and must be decompressed before imaging.`],answer:[{heading:`Recognition`,points:[`Distress, tachycardia, hypotension, hypoxaemia, raised JVP, absent breath sounds, hyper-resonance, late tracheal deviation.`,`Ventilated patient: rising airway pressures, falling SpO2, hypotension.`]},{heading:`Emergency management`,points:[`High-flow oxygen; needle decompression adult 4th/5th ICS just anterior to MAL (child 2nd ICS MCL) with at least 5-8 cm cannula, or finger thoracostomy.`,`Follow with chest drain in triangle of safety; treat cause; reassess ABC.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of mediastinal shift and decompression sites`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and pathophysiology`,marks:1},{part:`Recognition`,marks:2},{part:`Management`,marks:2}],keywords:[`tension pneumothorax`,`needle decompression`,`finger thoracostomy`,`obstructive shock`]}],references:[`Roberts ME et al. British Thoracic Society Guideline for Pleural Disease, Thorax 2023`,`MacDuff A et al. BTS Pleural Disease Guideline 2010: management of spontaneous pneumothorax`,`American College of Surgeons. Advanced Trauma Life Support (ATLS), 10th edition, 2018`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Disorders of the Pleura`,`Brown SGA et al. Conservative versus interventional treatment for spontaneous pneumothorax (PSP trial), NEJM 2020`,`Hallifax RJ et al. Ambulatory management of primary spontaneous pneumothorax (RAMPP), Lancet 2020`,`Coker RK et al. BTS Clinical Statement on Air Travel, Thorax 2022`]}),o.push({id:`respiratory-ards`,title:`Acute respiratory distress syndrome`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**ARDS** is acute, diffuse, **inflammatory lung injury** with increased alveolar-capillary permeability, giving **bilateral opacities** and **hypoxaemia not explained by cardiac failure** within **1 week** of an insult; the **2023 global definition** extends Berlin to **HFNO, SpO2/FiO2 and lung ultrasound**, and only **lung-protective ventilation (6 mL/kg PBW, plateau 30 or less) and prone positioning** clearly save lives.`,keywords:[`ARDS`,`acute lung injury`,`Berlin definition`,`global definition 2023`,`PaO2/FiO2`,`SpO2/FiO2`,`lung-protective ventilation`,`low tidal volume`,`plateau pressure`,`driving pressure`,`PEEP`,`prone positioning`,`ECMO`,`diffuse alveolar damage`,`VILI`,`ARDSNet`,`dexamethasone`,`conservative fluid strategy`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**ARDS** is acute diffuse inflammatory lung injury with increased pulmonary vascular permeability and loss of aerated tissue, causing hypoxaemia, bilateral opacities, increased shunt and dead space and reduced compliance. [Global Definition 2023]`,`**Berlin definition (2012)**: onset within **1 week** of a known insult or new/worsening symptoms; **bilateral opacities** not fully explained by effusion, collapse or nodules; respiratory failure **not fully explained by cardiac failure or fluid overload**; and PaO2/FiO2 300 or less on **PEEP or CPAP 5 cmH2O or more**. [Berlin 2012]`,`**LUNG SAFE (50 countries, 2016)**: ARDS was **10% of ICU admissions and 23% of ventilated patients**; it was **under-recognised (about 60% identified by clinicians)**; hospital mortality **35% mild, 40% moderate, 46% severe**. [LUNG SAFE 2016]`,`Global incidence is uncertain because resource-limited ICUs often lack ABG and CT - one driver of the **2023 global definition**. [Global Definition 2023]`,`**COVID-19** caused the largest ARDS burden in history, including India's 2021 delta wave, and showed that many patients meet ARDS criteria while on **HFNO** before intubation. [Global Definition 2023]`,`In India, **tropical infections** - severe falciparum malaria, **scrub typhus, leptospirosis**, dengue, influenza H1N1 - are prominent causes alongside sepsis and pneumonia; Indian ICU series report high mortality (often 40-60%). [ISCCM]`,`Overall ARDS mortality is about **35-45%**; most deaths are from sepsis and multi-organ failure rather than refractory hypoxaemia. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Direct (pulmonary) injury**: **pneumonia (commonest cause overall)**, aspiration of gastric contents, inhalation injury, pulmonary contusion, near drowning, COVID-19 and influenza. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Indirect (extrapulmonary) injury**: **sepsis (commonest indirect cause)**, severe trauma with shock, **acute pancreatitis**, multiple transfusions (**TRALI**), burns, drug overdose, cardiopulmonary bypass. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Indian tropical causes**: falciparum and vivax malaria, scrub typhus, leptospirosis (pulmonary haemorrhage), dengue, hantavirus, organophosphate and paraquat poisoning, snake bite with sepsis. [ISCCM]`,`**Risk modifiers**: chronic alcohol use (raises risk), smoking, hypoalbuminaemia, obesity, older age, high-volume ventilation, positive fluid balance; **diabetes is associated with lower risk** in some cohorts. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drugs**: aspirin overdose, opioids, chemotherapy (bleomycin), immune checkpoint inhibitors, amiodarone. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ventilator-induced lung injury** can cause or worsen ARDS - so even patients without ARDS benefit from lower tidal volumes. [ATS 2024]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - the 'why'`,points:[`**Exudative phase (days 1-7)**: injury to **alveolar epithelium (type I cells) and capillary endothelium**, neutrophil influx, cytokines (IL-1, IL-6, IL-8, TNF), protein-rich oedema and **hyaline membranes - diffuse alveolar damage (DAD)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Proliferative phase (days 7-21)**: type II pneumocytes proliferate and clear oedema; most survivors recover here. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fibrotic phase (after 3 weeks)** in some: fibrosis, bullae and long-term restriction and low DLCO. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypoxaemia is mainly shunt** from flooded and collapsed alveoli - so it **responds poorly to oxygen** and needs **PEEP** to recruit alveoli. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Baby lung concept**: the aerated lung may be only the size of a child's lung, so a 'normal' tidal volume over-distends it - the basis for **6 mL/kg predicted body weight**. [Gattinoni]`,`**VILI mechanisms**: **volutrauma** (over-distension), **barotrauma** (high pressure, pneumothorax), **atelectrauma** (cyclic opening and closing) and **biotrauma** (cytokine release spreading to other organs). [ATS 2024]`,`**Driving pressure** (plateau minus PEEP = tidal volume / compliance) reflects strain on the baby lung; values **over 15 cmH2O are linked to higher mortality**. [Amato NEJM 2015]`,`**Gravity-dependent collapse** of dorsal lung in the supine patient explains why **prone positioning** improves V/Q matching and spreads stress more evenly. [PROSEVA 2013]`,`**Increased dead space** (microthrombi, capillary loss) causes hypercapnia and is an independent predictor of death. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification - Berlin and the 2023 global definition`,points:[`**Berlin severity (PEEP 5 or more)**: **mild PaO2/FiO2 201-300, moderate 101-200, severe 100 or less**; mortality rises with each band. [Berlin 2012]`,`**2023 global definition** keeps Berlin's core and adds three categories: **non-intubated ARDS, intubated ARDS and modified definition for resource-limited settings**. [Global Definition 2023]`,`**Non-intubated ARDS**: PaO2/FiO2 300 or less, or **SpO2/FiO2 315 or less (with SpO2 97% or less)**, on **HFNO 30 L/min or more** or NIV/CPAP with end-expiratory pressure 5 cmH2O or more. [Global Definition 2023]`,`**Intubated ARDS severity**: **mild** P/F 201-300 or S/F 236-315; **moderate** P/F 101-200 or S/F 149-235; **severe** P/F 100 or less or S/F 148 or less. [Global Definition 2023]`,`**Imaging** may now be **chest X-ray, CT or lung ultrasound** (bilateral B-lines and/or consolidations by a trained operator). [Global Definition 2023]`,`**Resource-limited modification**: neither PEEP nor a minimum oxygen flow is required; **SpO2/FiO2 315 or less** with bilateral opacities on X-ray or ultrasound suffices. [Global Definition 2023]`,`**Kigali modification (2016)** was the forerunner, using S/F 315 or less and ultrasound in Rwanda. [Riviello 2016]`,`**Murray Lung Injury Score** (CXR quadrants, P/F, PEEP, compliance) over 3 is still used for **ECMO referral**. [Murray 1988]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Rapidly progressive breathlessness** within hours to 1 week of the insult - typically **12-72 hours** after sepsis, aspiration or trauma. [Harrison 22e]`,`The history is that of the **cause**: fever and cough (pneumonia), abdominal pain (pancreatitis), transfusion within 6 hours (TRALI), febrile illness with eschar (scrub typhus). [Harrison 22e]`,`Early on there is **tachypnoea and anxiety** with surprisingly clear lungs on auscultation despite marked hypoxaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Silent hypoxaemia** (low SpO2 with little distress) was characteristic of early COVID pneumonia - monitor oximetry not symptoms. [WHO 2023]`,`Ask about **orthopnoea, PND, chest pain and cardiac history** to weigh cardiogenic pulmonary oedema. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Tachypnoea (often over 30/min), tachycardia, use of accessory muscles, cyanosis** and SpO2 that barely rises with oxygen. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bilateral crackles**, sometimes bronchial breathing over consolidation. [Harrison 22e]`,`Signs of the cause: **eschar** (scrub typhus), **jaundice with conjunctival suffusion** (leptospirosis), Grey Turner or Cullen sign (pancreatitis), shock (sepsis). [ISCCM]`,`**Absence of raised JVP, S3 and peripheral oedema** favours ARDS over cardiac failure, but they do not exclude coexisting overload. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ventilated patient**: high plateau pressures and low compliance (often under 40 mL/cmH2O). [ATS 2024]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Cardiogenic pulmonary oedema**: history of heart disease, raised JVP, S3, cardiomegaly, **raised BNP**, LV dysfunction on echo, rapid response to diuretics. [ESC 2021]`,`**Diffuse alveolar haemorrhage**: haemoptysis (may be absent), falling haemoglobin, haemorrhagic BAL; vasculitis, leptospirosis - see the Diffuse alveolar haemorrhage topic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute eosinophilic pneumonia**: BAL eosinophils over 25%, often new smokers; steroid-responsive. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute interstitial pneumonia (Hamman-Rich)** and **acute exacerbation of IPF**: DAD without a known trigger. [ATS/ERS 2022]`,`**Bilateral pneumonia without ARDS physiology**, PCP in HIV, miliary TB, and **cryptogenic organising pneumonia**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Volume overload in AKI** - fluid balance, urine output and response to ultrafiltration help. [KDIGO 2024]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**ABG**: PaO2/FiO2 ratio (on PEEP 5 or more), PaCO2, lactate; **SpO2/FiO2** when ABG is unavailable (valid only when SpO2 is 97% or less). [Global Definition 2023]`,`**Chest X-ray**: bilateral patchy or confluent opacities with air bronchograms, often sparing costophrenic angles; may lag behind the clinical picture by 12-24 hours. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lung ultrasound**: bilateral B-lines, subpleural consolidations, irregular pleural line and spared areas - helps separate from cardiogenic oedema (smooth pleural line, homogeneous B-lines). [Global Definition 2023]`,`**CT thorax**: dependent consolidation with ventral ground glass - the gravity gradient. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Echocardiography** to assess LV function and **acute cor pulmonale** (RV dilatation, present in about 20-25% of moderate-severe ARDS). [ATS 2024]`,`**Find the cause**: blood cultures, procalcitonin, sputum or tracheal aspirate culture, CBNAAT, influenza/COVID PCR, malaria antigen, scrub typhus IgM ELISA, leptospira IgM, lipase. [ISCCM]`,`**NT-proBNP under 300 pg/mL** makes acute heart failure unlikely; a high value does not exclude ARDS. [ESC 2021]`,`**BAL** when infection or alternative diagnosis (DAH, eosinophilic pneumonia) is uncertain. [ATS 2024]`]},{heading:`Initial management at district hospital and before ICU`,points:[`**Recognise early**: tachypnoea with SpO2 under 92% on oxygen and bilateral infiltrates after an insult means likely ARDS - **call for ICU transfer early**. [WHO 2023]`,`**Oxygen** to SpO2 **92-96%** (avoid both hypoxaemia and hyperoxia); escalate quickly to **HFNO** if available. [ESICM 2023]`,`**Treat the cause within the hour**: sepsis bundle with blood cultures and **empirical antibiotics** (e.g. ceftriaxone 2 g IV daily plus azithromycin 500 mg IV daily for CAP; add **doxycycline 100 mg twice daily** when scrub typhus is suspected). [SSC 2021]`,`**Awake prone positioning** for COVID-related AHRF on HFNO reduces intubation; evidence is weaker for non-COVID causes. [ESICM 2023]`,`**Avoid fluid overload**: give fluid only for hypoperfusion, then aim for neutral or negative balance once shock resolves. [SSC 2021]`,`**Do not delay intubation** in deteriorating patients: rising RR, falling ROX index, exhaustion or altered sensorium. [ESICM 2023]`]},{heading:`ICU management - ventilation and adjuncts`,points:[`**Low tidal volume 4-8 mL/kg (start 6) of predicted body weight** - PBW men = 50 + 0.91 x (height cm - 152.4); women = 45.5 + 0.91 x (height cm - 152.4) - reduced mortality by about 9% absolute in ARMA. [ARDSNet 2000]`,`**Plateau pressure 30 cmH2O or less**; aim for **driving pressure under 15 cmH2O**. [ATS 2024]`,`**Higher PEEP** (without prolonged recruitment manoeuvres) is suggested for **moderate-severe ARDS**; avoid prolonged high-pressure recruitment manoeuvres (ART trial harm). [ATS 2024]`,`**Permissive hypercapnia** accepting pH 7.20 or more; oxygen target **SpO2 88-95% or PaO2 55-80 mmHg**. [ARDSNet 2000]`,`**Prone positioning for 12 hours or more a day (PROSEVA used about 16 hours)** in **severe ARDS (P/F under 150)** - mortality fell from 33% to 16%. [ATS 2024]`,`**Neuromuscular blockade**: not routine; ATS 2024 suggests against routine continuous NMBA (ROSE trial), reserving it for ventilator dyssynchrony or refractory hypoxaemia. [ATS 2024]`,`**Corticosteroids**: ATS 2024 suggests corticosteroids for ARDS (conditional); **dexamethasone 6 mg daily for 10 days** in COVID-19 needing oxygen; DEXA-ARDS regimen **20 mg IV daily days 1-5, then 10 mg days 6-10**; ESICM 2023 makes no recommendation for non-COVID ARDS. [ATS 2024]`,`**Conservative fluid strategy** once shock has resolved (FACTT) - more ventilator-free days without more kidney failure. [ARDSNet FACTT 2006]`,`**VV-ECMO** in selected severe ARDS at an experienced centre - EOLIA criteria: P/F under 50 for 3 hours, under 80 for 6 hours, or pH under 7.25 with PaCO2 60 mmHg or more for 6 hours despite optimal care. [ATS 2024]`,`**Do not use** high-frequency oscillatory ventilation (OSCILLATE harm), routine inhaled nitric oxide, beta-agonists or statins for ARDS. [ESICM 2023]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: target PaO2 70 mmHg or more (SpO2 95% or more) for fetal oxygenation, left lateral tilt, avoid excessive permissive hypercapnia; prone is possible with padding; deliver if it improves maternal status. [SMFM 2022]`,`**Elderly**: higher mortality; discuss goals of care early and document ceiling of treatment. [ESICM 2023]`,`**CKD/AKI**: fluid removal by ultrafiltration when diuretic-resistant; dose antimicrobials by eGFR. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Children**: use **PALICC-2 (2023)** paediatric ARDS criteria using oxygenation index (OI) or OSI. [PALICC-2 2023]`,`**Resource-limited**: use **SpO2/FiO2 and ultrasound** to diagnose, simple ventilators with PBW tidal volumes, prone positioning (cheap, needs only staff), and transfer early. [Global Definition 2023]`,`**Obesity**: tidal volume still by **PBW, not actual weight**; often higher PEEP needed for chest wall load. [ATS 2024]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Barotrauma** (pneumothorax, pneumomediastinum), **VAP**, catheter infections, DVT/PE, stress ulcer bleeding, delirium. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Multi-organ failure and sepsis** cause most deaths; refractory hypoxaemia causes only about 10-15%. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Survivors**: **ICU-acquired weakness**, reduced 6-minute walk at 5 years, **post-intensive care syndrome** (cognitive impairment, depression, PTSD), though lung function often returns near normal with mild DLCO reduction. [Herridge NEJM 2011]`,`**Poor prognostic factors**: age, severe P/F, high dead-space fraction, high driving pressure, chronic liver disease, immunosuppression, multi-organ failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fibroproliferative ARDS** after 2-3 weeks has worse survival and long-term restriction. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer/transfer to ICU** when SpO2 cannot be held at 92% or more on 6 L/min or more of oxygen, RR over 30, or ROX index falling. [ESICM 2023]`,`**Refer to an ECMO centre early** - before multiorgan failure and before 7 days of injurious ventilation. [ELSO 2021]`,`**Monitoring in ICU**: plateau and driving pressure every shift, ABG, fluid balance, lactate, daily sedation interruption and **spontaneous breathing trial**. [ATS 2024]`,`**After discharge**: follow-up clinic at 6-12 weeks - spirometry, DLCO, 6-minute walk, mood and cognition screening, rehabilitation. [ESICM 2023]`,`Family physician role: **PICS screening**, physiotherapy, return-to-work planning and caregiver support. [SCCM]`]},{heading:`Prevention`,points:[`**Lung-protective ventilation for all ventilated patients**, even without ARDS (theatre and ICU). [ATS 2024]`,`**Early sepsis recognition and antibiotics**, restrictive transfusion (haemoglobin threshold 7 g/dL), and judicious fluids. [SSC 2021]`,`**Aspiration prevention**: head-of-bed elevation, careful sedation, swallowing assessment after stroke. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Vaccination**: influenza, pneumococcal and COVID-19 in at-risk adults. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Vector and infection control** in India: malaria (NCVBDC), scrub typhus awareness and early doxycycline, leptospirosis prophylaxis in floods (doxycycline 200 mg weekly). [NCVBDC]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2025)`,points:[`**Global definition of ARDS (2023, published 2024)**: adds non-intubated ARDS on HFNO 30 L/min or more, SpO2/FiO2 cut-offs, ultrasound imaging and a resource-limited version. [Global Definition 2023]`,`**ESICM 2023 ARDS guideline**: HFNO recommended over conventional oxygen in AHRF; low tidal volume and prone positioning for moderate-severe ARDS; **against routine NMBA infusion and against prolonged recruitment manoeuvres**; dexamethasone for COVID-ARDS; no recommendation on steroids in non-COVID ARDS. [ESICM 2023]`,`**ATS 2024 update**: conditional recommendations **for corticosteroids**, **for VV-ECMO in selected severe ARDS**, **against routine NMBA**, and for **higher PEEP without prolonged recruitment** in moderate-severe ARDS. [ATS 2024]`,`**PALICC-2 (2023)** updates paediatric ARDS definitions and ventilation. [PALICC-2 2023]`,`**Severe CAP**: hydrocortisone 200 mg/day for 4-7 days (CAPE COD) reduced mortality - relevant to pneumonia-related ARDS. [ATS 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic question**: define ARDS (Berlin), list causes, and describe lung-protective ventilation - **6 mL/kg PBW, plateau 30 or less, PEEP, prone**.`,`**What reduces mortality?** Low tidal volume, prone positioning in severe ARDS, conservative fluids (ventilator days); ECMO in selected cases; corticosteroids (conditional).`,`**Common mistake**: calculating tidal volume from actual body weight in an obese patient.`,`**Common mistake**: using PaO2/FiO2 off PEEP, or SpO2/FiO2 when SpO2 is 98-100% (the curve is flat).`,`**Shunt** is the main mechanism - hence poor response to oxygen and need for PEEP.`,`**Know PBW formula**: men 50 + 0.91 x (height - 152.4), women 45.5 + 0.91 x (height - 152.4).`]}],tables:[{heading:`Berlin 2012 vs global definition 2023`,columns:[`Criterion`,`Berlin 2012`,`Global 2023`],rows:[[`Timing`,`Within 1 week of insult or new symptoms`,`Same`],[`Imaging`,`Bilateral opacities on CXR or CT`,`CXR, CT or lung ultrasound (bilateral B-lines/consolidation)`],[`Origin of oedema`,`Not fully explained by cardiac failure or fluid overload`,`Same; can coexist with overload`],[`Oxygenation`,`P/F 300 or less on PEEP/CPAP 5 or more`,`P/F 300 or less or S/F 315 or less (SpO2 97% or less)`],[`Support required`,`Invasive or non-invasive PEEP/CPAP`,`Also HFNO 30 L/min or more; none in resource-limited version`],[`Severity`,`Mild 201-300, moderate 101-200, severe 100 or less`,`Same bands for intubated; S/F 315, 235, 148 equivalents`]]},{heading:`Lung-protective ventilation targets`,columns:[`Parameter`,`Target`,`Note`],rows:[[`Tidal volume`,`6 mL/kg PBW (4-8)`,`PBW from height and sex, not actual weight`],[`Plateau pressure`,`30 cmH2O or less`,`Reduce Vt to 4 mL/kg if higher`],[`Driving pressure`,`Under 15 cmH2O`,`Plateau minus PEEP`],[`PEEP`,`Higher PEEP in moderate-severe`,`ARDSNet FiO2-PEEP table`],[`Oxygen`,`SpO2 88-95%, PaO2 55-80 mmHg`,`Avoid hyperoxia`],[`pH`,`7.20 or more`,`Permissive hypercapnia; raise RR up to 35`],[`Prone`,`12-16 hours/day if P/F under 150`,`Start early (within 36 hours)`]]},{heading:`Drugs and adjuncts in ARDS`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Dexamethasone (COVID-19 on oxygen)`,`6 mg IV or orally once daily`,`Up to 10 days`,`Hyperglycaemia, secondary infection, mucormycosis`],[`Dexamethasone (DEXA-ARDS regimen)`,`20 mg IV daily days 1-5, then 10 mg days 6-10`,`10 days`,`Hyperglycaemia, neuromyopathy`],[`Hydrocortisone (severe CAP)`,`200 mg/day IV infusion`,`4-7 days then taper over 8-14 days`,`Hyperglycaemia; avoid in influenza pneumonia`],[`Cisatracurium`,`15 mg IV bolus then 37.5 mg/hour (ACURASYS)`,`Up to 48 hours, only if needed`,`ICU-acquired weakness; needs deep sedation`],[`Furosemide`,`20-40 mg IV, titrated or infusion`,`Until neutral/negative balance`,`Hypokalaemia, AKI, ototoxicity`],[`Enoxaparin (prophylaxis)`,`40 mg SC once daily`,`Throughout immobility`,`Bleeding; reduce in CrCl under 30`],[`Doxycycline (scrub typhus cause)`,`100 mg orally or via NG twice daily`,`7 days`,`GI upset; use azithromycin in pregnancy`]]},{heading:`ARDS vs cardiogenic pulmonary oedema`,columns:[`Feature`,`ARDS`,`Cardiogenic oedema`],rows:[[`History`,`Sepsis, pneumonia, trauma, pancreatitis`,`IHD, valve disease, hypertension`],[`JVP, S3`,`Normal, absent`,`Raised, present`],[`X-ray`,`Patchy peripheral, normal heart, few effusions`,`Cardiomegaly, upper lobe diversion, Kerley B, effusions`],[`Ultrasound`,`Patchy B-lines, irregular pleural line, consolidations`,`Homogeneous B-lines, smooth pleural line`],[`BNP`,`Usually under 100 pg/mL`,`Raised`],[`Oedema protein`,`High (fluid/plasma protein over 0.7)`,`Low (under 0.6)`],[`Response to diuretic`,`Slow`,`Rapid`]]},{heading:`Causes - direct vs indirect`,columns:[`Direct (pulmonary)`,`Indirect (extrapulmonary)`,`Indian tropical`],rows:[[`Pneumonia (bacterial, viral, COVID)`,`Sepsis`,`Falciparum malaria`],[`Aspiration of gastric contents`,`Severe trauma, shock`,`Scrub typhus`],[`Inhalation injury`,`Acute pancreatitis`,`Leptospirosis`],[`Pulmonary contusion`,`Massive transfusion (TRALI)`,`Dengue, H1N1 influenza`],[`Near drowning`,`Burns, drug overdose`,`Paraquat, organophosphate`]]}],redFlags:[`SpO2 under 92% despite 6 L/min or more of oxygen, or rising oxygen needs - transfer to ICU`,`Respiratory rate over 30/min, accessory muscle use or exhaustion`,`ROX index under 3.85 at 12 hours on HFNO (or falling) - prepare for intubation`,`Altered sensorium, shock or rising lactate`,`Refractory hypoxaemia P/F under 80 despite optimal ventilation and prone - refer for ECMO`,`New hypotension in a ventilated patient - exclude pneumothorax`],pearls:[`Berlin: 1 week, bilateral opacities, not cardiac, P/F 300 or less on PEEP 5.`,`Global 2023: adds HFNO 30 L/min, S/F 315 or less, and lung ultrasound.`,`Mild 300-200-100: moderate and severe bands at 200 and 100.`,`Tidal volume 6 mL/kg PBW, plateau 30 or less, driving pressure under 15.`,`Prone 16 hours in severe ARDS (PROSEVA) - cheapest life-saving ICU therapy.`,`Hypoxaemia in ARDS is shunt - it needs PEEP, not just more oxygen.`,`Avoid HFOV, routine NMBA and prolonged recruitment manoeuvres.`,`PBW not actual weight - the obese patient gets the same Vt as a lean one of equal height.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-ards-q1`,stem:`A 40-year-old man with severe falciparum malaria develops bilateral infiltrates 48 hours after admission. On mechanical ventilation with PEEP 10 cmH2O and FiO2 0.8, PaO2 is 72 mmHg; echocardiography is normal. How is this ARDS graded by the Berlin definition?`,options:[`Mild ARDS`,`Moderate ARDS`,`Not ARDS because malaria is an indirect cause`,`Severe ARDS`],answer:3,explanation:`PaO2/FiO2 = 72/0.8 = 90, which is 100 or less on PEEP of at least 5, making it severe ARDS. Mild is 201-300 and moderate 101-200; the cause, direct or indirect, does not affect whether the definition is met - sepsis and malaria are recognised indirect causes, and a normal echo removes cardiac failure as the explanation.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-ards-q2`,stem:`A woman 160 cm tall weighing 95 kg is ventilated for ARDS. What initial tidal volume is appropriate?`,options:[`About 315 mL`,`About 570 mL`,`About 760 mL`,`About 950 mL`],answer:0,explanation:`Tidal volume is set by predicted body weight: women 45.5 + 0.91 x (160 - 152.4) = about 52.4 kg, and 6 mL/kg gives about 315 mL. Using actual weight gives 570 mL at 6 mL/kg or 760 mL at 8 mL/kg and over-distends the baby lung; 950 mL is 10 mL/kg of actual weight, the old injurious practice that ARMA showed increased mortality.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-ards-q3`,stem:`Under the 2023 global definition of ARDS, which patient can be diagnosed with ARDS without intubation?`,options:[`A patient on 2 L/min nasal oxygen with unilateral consolidation`,`A patient on HFNO 40 L/min with bilateral opacities and SpO2/FiO2 of 200 within 3 days of pneumonia, normal echo`,`A patient with bilateral opacities explained entirely by acute LV failure on NIV`,`A patient with bilateral opacities 3 weeks after an insult on HFNO`],answer:1,explanation:`The 2023 global definition adds non-intubated ARDS: HFNO 30 L/min or more (or NIV/CPAP 5 or more), SpO2/FiO2 315 or less, bilateral opacities, onset within 1 week and not fully explained by cardiac failure. Unilateral disease on low-flow oxygen fails the imaging and support criteria; opacities fully explained by LV failure are excluded; onset 3 weeks after the insult fails the 1-week timing.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-ards-q4`,stem:`A patient with ARDS has P/F ratio 110 on FiO2 0.7 and PEEP 12 after 12 hours of optimised low tidal volume ventilation. Which intervention has the strongest evidence for reducing mortality at this point?`,options:[`High-frequency oscillatory ventilation`,`Inhaled nitric oxide`,`Prone positioning for at least 12-16 hours a day`,`Routine continuous cisatracurium infusion for 48 hours`],answer:2,explanation:`In severe ARDS with P/F under 150, prone positioning for prolonged sessions halved 28-day mortality in PROSEVA and is strongly recommended. HFOV increased mortality in OSCILLATE; inhaled nitric oxide improves oxygenation transiently without survival benefit; routine NMBA showed no benefit in ROSE and is not recommended routinely by ESICM 2023 or ATS 2024.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-ards-q5`,stem:`What is the main mechanism of hypoxaemia in early ARDS?`,options:[`Intrapulmonary shunt through flooded and collapsed alveoli`,`Alveolar hypoventilation`,`Diffusion impairment from thickened membranes`,`Low inspired oxygen`],answer:0,explanation:`In early ARDS blood perfuses alveoli filled with protein-rich fluid or collapsed, so it passes without gas exchange - a shunt, which is why hypoxaemia responds poorly to raising FiO2 and improves with PEEP and prone positioning that recruit alveoli. Hypoventilation causes hypercapnia with a normal A-a gradient; diffusion limitation contributes little at rest; low inspired oxygen is an altitude cause.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-ards-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Define acute respiratory distress syndrome. Discuss its causes, pathophysiology, diagnostic criteria (Berlin and 2023 global definition) and management.`,openingLines:[`ARDS is acute diffuse inflammatory lung injury with increased alveolar-capillary permeability, producing bilateral opacities and hypoxaemia not fully explained by cardiac failure, within one week of an insult.`,`Its mortality is 35-45%, and lung-protective ventilation and prone positioning are the interventions proven to reduce it.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes`,points:[`Direct: pneumonia, aspiration, inhalation, contusion, drowning, COVID.`,`Indirect: sepsis, trauma, pancreatitis, TRALI, burns; Indian: malaria, scrub typhus, leptospirosis, dengue.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Exudative (DAD, hyaline membranes), proliferative, fibrotic phases.`,`Shunt-driven hypoxaemia, low compliance, baby lung, VILI (volutrauma, barotrauma, atelectrauma, biotrauma).`]},{heading:`Diagnostic criteria`,points:[`Berlin: 1 week, bilateral opacities, not cardiac, P/F 300 or less on PEEP 5; mild/moderate/severe 300/200/100.`,`Global 2023: non-intubated on HFNO 30 L/min or more, S/F 315 or less, ultrasound, resource-limited version.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`ABG, CXR, ultrasound, CT, echo, BNP; cause work-up: cultures, CBNAAT, malaria, scrub typhus, lipase.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Treat cause; oxygen/HFNO; early ICU; Vt 6 mL/kg PBW, plateau 30 or less, driving pressure under 15, higher PEEP.`,`Prone 12-16 h if P/F under 150; conservative fluids; steroids (conditional); ECMO for refractory; avoid HFOV, routine NMBA.`]},{heading:`Prognosis and follow-up`,points:[`Mortality 35-45%; survivors have ICU-acquired weakness and PICS; follow-up with PFT, 6MWT and rehabilitation.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart of ARDS phases`,`Table of Berlin vs global 2023 criteria`,`Ventilation targets box`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and causes`,marks:2},{part:`Pathophysiology`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnostic criteria`,marks:2},{part:`Management`,marks:3},{part:`Prognosis`,marks:1}],keywords:[`ARDS`,`Berlin`,`global definition`,`lung-protective ventilation`,`prone`,`ECMO`]},{id:`respiratory-ards-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Lung-protective ventilation in ARDS.`,openingLines:[`Lung-protective ventilation limits tidal volume and pressure to avoid ventilator-induced lung injury in the small aerated 'baby lung' of ARDS.`,`It is the only ventilator strategy proven to reduce mortality (ARMA trial: 39.8% to 31%).`],answer:[{heading:`Targets`,points:[`Vt 6 mL/kg PBW (4-8), plateau 30 or less, driving pressure under 15, PEEP by FiO2-PEEP table (higher in moderate-severe).`,`SpO2 88-95%, PaO2 55-80, pH 7.20 or more (permissive hypercapnia).`]},{heading:`Adjuncts`,points:[`Prone 12-16 h for P/F under 150; NMBA only if dyssynchrony; avoid prolonged recruitment manoeuvres and HFOV.`,`Conservative fluids, daily sedation hold and SBT; ECMO if refractory.`]}],mustDraw:[`PBW formula and ventilation target box`],markSplit:[{part:`Rationale (VILI, baby lung)`,marks:1},{part:`Targets`,marks:2},{part:`Adjuncts`,marks:2}],keywords:[`low tidal volume`,`plateau pressure`,`driving pressure`,`PEEP`,`prone`]}],references:[`Matthay MA et al. A new global definition of acute respiratory distress syndrome, Am J Respir Crit Care Med 2024 (consensus 2023)`,`ARDS Definition Task Force. Acute respiratory distress syndrome: the Berlin definition, JAMA 2012`,`Grasselli G et al. ESICM guidelines on acute respiratory distress syndrome: definition, phenotyping and respiratory support strategies, Intensive Care Med 2023`,`Qadir N et al. An update on management of adult patients with ARDS: an official ATS clinical practice guideline, Am J Respir Crit Care Med 2024`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome`,`ARDS Network. Ventilation with lower tidal volumes (ARMA), NEJM 2000; Guerin C et al. PROSEVA, NEJM 2013`,`Bellani G et al. LUNG SAFE, JAMA 2016`,`Evans L et al. Surviving Sepsis Campaign international guidelines 2021`]}),o.push({id:`respiratory-respiratory-failure-oxygen`,title:`Respiratory failure, oxygen therapy and high-flow nasal oxygen`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Respiratory failure** is failure of gas exchange - **type 1 (hypoxaemic): PaO2 under 60 mmHg (8 kPa) with normal or low PaCO2**, **type 2 (hypercapnic): PaCO2 over 45 mmHg (6 kPa)** - and **oxygen is a prescribed drug** titrated to a **target saturation of 94-98%**, or **88-92%** in those at risk of hypercapnia, with **HFNO** and **NIV** as the next steps before intubation.`,keywords:[`respiratory failure`,`type 1 respiratory failure`,`type 2 respiratory failure`,`hypoxaemia`,`hypercapnia`,`A-a gradient`,`oxygen therapy`,`target saturation`,`Venturi mask`,`non-rebreather mask`,`HFNC`,`HFNO`,`ROX index`,`NIV`,`BiPAP`,`CPAP`,`oxygen toxicity`,`absorption atelectasis`,`oxygen-induced hypercapnia`,`oxygen concentrator`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Respiratory failure** is the inability of the respiratory system to maintain adequate gas exchange: **PaO2 under 60 mmHg (8 kPa)** and/or **PaCO2 over 45 mmHg (6 kPa)** breathing air at sea level. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Type 1 (hypoxaemic)**: low PaO2, normal or low PaCO2 - a gas-exchange (lung) failure. **Type 2 (hypercapnic, ventilatory)**: high PaCO2, usually with low PaO2 - a pump failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute, chronic and acute-on-chronic**: acute hypercapnia lowers pH (under 7.35); chronic hypercapnia has a near-normal pH with raised bicarbonate; acute-on-chronic shows high PaCO2, high bicarbonate and low pH. [BTS 2017]`,`Two further types are sometimes taught: **type 3 (perioperative, atelectasis)** and **type 4 (shock, hypoperfusion)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute hypoxaemic respiratory failure** is the commonest reason for ICU admission; COVID-19 exposed the need for oxygen at scale, with India's 2021 second wave creating a national oxygen crisis. [MoHFW 2021]`,`**Oxygen is an essential medicine** in the **WHO Model List** and **India's NLEM 2022**; hundreds of **PSA oxygen plants** were installed in Indian public hospitals after 2021 (PM CARES). [NLEM 2022]`,`Audits show oxygen is often given **without prescription or target**, and over-oxygenation is common - both linked to harm. [BTS 2017]`]},{heading:`Causes`,points:[`**Type 1 causes**: pneumonia, ARDS, pulmonary oedema, pulmonary embolism, asthma, ILD, pneumothorax, COVID-19, atelectasis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Type 2 - lung/airway**: **COPD exacerbation** (commonest), severe life-threatening asthma, bronchiectasis, cystic fibrosis, upper airway obstruction. [BTS 2017]`,`**Type 2 - central drive**: opioid or benzodiazepine overdose, brainstem stroke, encephalitis, **obesity hypoventilation**, hypothyroidism. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Type 2 - neuromuscular**: Guillain-Barre syndrome, myasthenic crisis, **organophosphate poisoning and snake-bite (neurotoxic krait/cobra)** - important in rural India, motor neurone disease, high cervical cord injury - see the Neuromuscular causes topic. [ICMR STW]`,`**Type 2 - chest wall**: kyphoscoliosis, post-TB thoracoplasty and fibrothorax (common in India), flail chest, morbid obesity. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Iatrogenic hypercapnia**: excessive oxygen in COPD, sedatives, opioids and residual neuromuscular blockade. [BTS 2017]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - the five mechanisms of hypoxaemia`,points:[`**Alveolar gas equation**: PAO2 = FiO2 x (Patm - 47) - PaCO2/0.8; on room air at sea level about 150 - PaCO2/0.8; **normal A-a gradient** is about 5-10 mmHg in the young and roughly **(age/4) + 4**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**V/Q mismatch** - the commonest cause (COPD, asthma, pneumonia, PE); raised A-a gradient; **corrects well with oxygen**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Shunt** (ARDS, lobar consolidation, intracardiac shunt, hepatopulmonary syndrome); raised A-a gradient; **poor response to oxygen** - needs PEEP or recruitment. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypoventilation** (drugs, neuromuscular disease) - **normal A-a gradient**, PaCO2 high; corrects with ventilation, and oxygen alone masks it. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diffusion impairment** (ILD, emphysema) - mainly on exercise; and **low inspired PO2** (high altitude, e.g. Leh, Sikkim) with normal A-a gradient. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Oxyhaemoglobin dissociation curve**: SpO2 90% corresponds to PaO2 about 60 mmHg - the 'shoulder' below which saturation falls steeply; above 97%, large PaO2 changes barely alter SpO2. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypercapnia** arises when alveolar ventilation falls: PaCO2 = k x VCO2 / VA; the load (resistance, elastance, dead space) exceeds the pump (drive, muscles), and the pump fatigues. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why oxygen raises PaCO2 in COPD**: mainly **worsened V/Q matching from release of hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction** and the **Haldane effect** (oxygenated haemoglobin carries less CO2), with only a small part from reduced hypoxic drive. [BTS 2017]`]},{heading:`Classification and oxygen targets`,points:[`**BTS 2017 targets**: **94-98%** for most acutely ill adults; **88-92%** for those at risk of hypercapnic failure; review if target cannot be held. [BTS 2017]`,`**At risk of hypercapnia**: COPD, **morbid obesity (OHS)**, cystic fibrosis, **chest wall deformity** (kyphoscoliosis, thoracoplasty), **neuromuscular disease**, bronchiectasis with fixed obstruction, and previous hypercapnic failure. [BTS 2017]`,`**Other guidelines**: TSANZ 2022 suggests **92-96%** for most and 88-92% for hypercapnic risk; BMJ Rapid Recommendation 2018 advises **stopping oxygen at SpO2 over 96%** (and not starting at 93% or more in most). [TSANZ 2022]`,`**Over-oxygenation kills**: the IOTA meta-analysis showed liberal oxygen (SpO2 above about 96%) increased mortality in acutely ill adults. [Lancet IOTA 2018]`,`**Acute MI or stroke**: give oxygen only if **SpO2 under 90%** (MI) or under 94% (stroke) - routine oxygen gives no benefit (AVOID, DETO2X, SO2S). [ESC 2023]`,`**Exceptions needing high oxygen regardless of SpO2**: **carbon monoxide poisoning**, cyanide, cluster headache, pneumothorax (in hypoxaemic patients), sickle crisis with hypoxaemia, cardiac arrest during CPR. [BTS 2017]`,`**Pulse oximetry limitations**: overestimates SpO2 in **darker skin** (occult hypoxaemia), unreliable with poor perfusion, nail polish, carboxyhaemoglobin (falsely normal) and methaemoglobin (reads about 85%). [FDA 2025]`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`**Hypoxaemia**: breathlessness, tachypnoea, tachycardia, restlessness, confusion, central cyanosis (needs about 5 g/dL deoxygenated haemoglobin - absent in anaemia). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypercapnia**: headache (morning), drowsiness, **flapping tremor (asterixis)**, warm peripheries, bounding pulse, papilloedema, and **CO2 narcosis** (coma). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Respiratory muscle fatigue**: **paradoxical abdominal movement**, accessory muscle use, inability to complete sentences, rising RR then sudden slowing - a pre-arrest sign. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neuromuscular weakness**: low single-breath count, weak cough, staccato speech; forced vital capacity under 20 mL/kg warns of failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**History**: chronic lung disease, home oxygen, sleep disordered breathing, sedative drugs, poisoning, snake bite, recent surgery. [ICMR STW]`]},{heading:`Examination`,points:[`**Count respiratory rate for a full minute** - the most sensitive early warning sign; RR over 24 is abnormal, over 30 severe (NEWS2). [NEWS2 2017]`,`**SpO2 with FiO2**: the same SpO2 of 94% means something different on air and on 15 L/min - always record the oxygen being given. [BTS 2017]`,`**Signs of the cause**: wheeze (asthma, COPD), crackles (pneumonia, oedema, ILD), silent chest, tracheal shift (pneumothorax), pin-point pupils (opioids), fasciculations and miosis (organophosphate), ptosis (krait bite, myasthenia). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cor pulmonale**: raised JVP, loud P2, ankle oedema - suggests chronic hypoxaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**GCS** and ability to protect the airway decide between NIV and intubation. [BTS/ICS 2016]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of the breathless hypoxaemic patient`,points:[`**Normal A-a gradient with high PaCO2** = hypoventilation - look at drive, nerves, muscles, chest wall. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Raised A-a gradient that corrects with oxygen** = V/Q mismatch (airways, PE, pneumonia). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Raised A-a gradient not correcting with 100% oxygen** = shunt (ARDS, consolidation, AV malformation, intracardiac). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Breathless with normal SpO2**: metabolic acidosis (DKA Kussmaul breathing), anaemia, anxiety/panic, early PE, CO poisoning (falsely normal SpO2). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cyanosis with normal PaO2**: methaemoglobinaemia (dapsone, nitrites, aniline dyes) - chocolate-brown blood, SpO2 around 85%; treat with methylene blue 1-2 mg/kg IV. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Platypnoea-orthodeoxia** (worse sitting up): hepatopulmonary syndrome, PFO - see The lung in systemic disease topic. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Pulse oximetry** to screen and titrate, with **ABG** for all patients with SpO2 below target, at risk of hypercapnia, unwell, or with suspected acidosis - see the Arterial blood gas topic. [BTS 2017]`,`**Repeat ABG 30-60 minutes** after any change in oxygen in patients at risk of hypercapnia. [BTS 2017]`,`**Venous blood gas**: venous pH is close to arterial and a **venous PCO2 under 45 mmHg** helps rule out hypercapnia, but VBG cannot replace ABG for PaO2. [BTS 2017]`,`**Chest X-ray, ECG, lung ultrasound**; CBC, electrolytes, glucose; troponin and BNP; D-dimer/CTPA where PE is suspected. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neuromuscular disease**: serial **FVC (under 20 mL/kg or a 30% fall)**, negative inspiratory force (weaker than -30 cmH2O) and single breath count - the '20/30/40 rule'. [Harrison 22e]`,`**P/F ratio** on ABG and **S/F ratio** from oximetry quantify type 1 failure; **ROX index** on HFNO. [Global Definition 2023]`]},{heading:`Oxygen therapy - devices and practice in OPD, ward and district hospital`,points:[`**Nasal cannula 1-4 L/min**: FiO2 about **24-40%** (roughly +3-4% per litre); comfortable, allows eating; actual FiO2 varies with breathing pattern. [BTS 2017]`,`**Simple face mask 5-10 L/min**: FiO2 about **35-60%**; never under 5 L/min (CO2 rebreathing). [BTS 2017]`,`**Venturi (fixed-performance) mask**: **24% (2-4 L/min), 28% (4-6 L/min), 35% (8-10 L/min), 40% (10-12 L/min), 60% (15 L/min)** - accurate FiO2, the device of choice in COPD; increase flow by 50% if RR over 30. [BTS 2017]`,`**Non-rebreather (reservoir) mask 15 L/min**: FiO2 about **60-90%** - for critical illness, shock, CO poisoning, major trauma; switch down as soon as stable. [BTS 2017]`,`**Start**: critically ill - 15 L/min reservoir mask; most hypoxaemic patients - nasal cannula 2-6 L/min or face mask 5-10 L/min; **COPD/hypercapnic risk - 24% or 28% Venturi or 1-2 L/min nasal cannula** targeting 88-92%. [BTS 2017]`,`**Prescribe** oxygen on the drug chart with the **target range, device and flow**; nurses titrate within the range and record on the observation chart. [BTS 2017]`,`**Weaning**: step down when stable at the upper end of target; stop when SpO2 stays in range on the lowest flow for two observations. [BTS 2017]`,`**Never stop oxygen abruptly in hypercapnic patients** - rebound hypoxaemia; step down to 24-28%. [BTS 2017]`,`**Humidification** is not needed for short-term low-flow oxygen; consider it for prolonged high flow or tracheostomy. [BTS 2017]`,`**Oxygen concentrators** give 5-10 L/min at about **90-95% purity**; in India cylinders are **black with a white shoulder**, and remaining duration (minutes) = cylinder contents in litres x (current pressure / full pressure) / flow in L/min. [MoHFW 2021]`]},{heading:`High-flow nasal oxygen, ROX index and NIV vs HFNO`,points:[`**HFNO** delivers heated (37 degrees C), fully humidified gas at **30-60 L/min** with **FiO2 0.21-1.0**, matching or exceeding the patient's inspiratory flow so FiO2 is precise. [ERS 2022]`,`**Mechanisms**: **washout of nasopharyngeal dead space**, a small **PEEP effect (about 1 cmH2O per 10 L/min with mouth closed)**, reduced work of breathing, better secretion clearance and comfort. [ERS 2022]`,`**Evidence**: FLORALI (2015) - in non-hypercapnic AHRF HFNO reduced 90-day mortality versus standard oxygen or NIV and reduced intubation in P/F 200 or less. [FLORALI 2015]`,`**ROX index = (SpO2/FiO2) / respiratory rate**; **4.88 or more at 2, 6 or 12 hours predicts HFNO success**; **under 2.85 at 2 h, 3.47 at 6 h or 3.85 at 12 h predicts failure** - escalate and consider intubation. [Roca 2019]`,`**NIV (BiPAP)** is first-line for **acute hypercapnic failure in COPD with pH under 7.35 and PaCO2 over 45 mmHg** after initial medical therapy, and **CPAP/NIV for cardiogenic pulmonary oedema** - see the AECOPD and NIV topic. [ERS/ATS 2017]`,`**HFNO vs NIV**: HFNO is suggested over NIV for **de novo hypoxaemic failure**; NIV over HFNO for **hypercapnic COPD**, with HFNO as an alternative if NIV is not tolerated; HFNO post-extubation in low-risk patients and NIV/HFNO in high-risk. [ERS 2022]`,`**Delayed intubation harms**: failing HFNO or NIV beyond 48 hours is linked to higher mortality - set a review time and failure criteria (ROX, HACOR score over 5 at 1 hour for NIV). [ESICM 2023]`,`**Intubation criteria**: inability to protect airway, GCS falling, exhaustion, haemodynamic instability, refractory hypoxaemia (SpO2 under 88% on FiO2 1.0), worsening acidosis despite NIV. [BTS/ICS 2016]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**COPD exacerbation in an ambulance or OPD**: 28% Venturi or 1-2 L/min cannula to 88-92%; pre-hospital titrated oxygen halved mortality versus high-flow oxygen (Austin 2010). [BMJ 2010]`,`**Pregnancy**: target **94-98%**; left lateral tilt; fetal oxygenation depends on maternal PaO2 - escalate early. [BTS 2017]`,`**Children**: target 94-98% in most acute illness; WHO recommends oxygen at **SpO2 under 90%** in resource-limited settings, nasal prongs 0.5-1 L/min in infants. [WHO 2016]`,`**Neuromuscular disease**: oxygen alone may mask hypoventilation - measure PaCO2 and FVC, consider NIV early. [BTS 2017]`,`**Paraquat poisoning, bleomycin and amiodarone lung**: high FiO2 worsens injury - give oxygen only for SpO2 under about 88-90%. [BTS 2017]`,`**High altitude (Ladakh, Sikkim, Himalayan treks)**: SpO2 is physiologically lower; oxygen 2-4 L/min and descent for HAPE. [WMS 2024]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: concentrators with flow splitters, oximeters and the WHO Oxygen Access Scale-up; use bubble CPAP in children where HFNO is unavailable. [WHO 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications - oxygen toxicity and hypercapnia risk`,points:[`**Oxygen-induced hypercapnia and acidosis** in COPD and other at-risk groups - avoid by targeting 88-92% and using Venturi masks. [BTS 2017]`,`**Absorption atelectasis**: high FiO2 (over 0.6) washes out nitrogen, so poorly ventilated alveoli collapse - increases shunt. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pulmonary oxygen toxicity (Lorrain Smith effect)**: reactive oxygen species cause tracheobronchitis and DAD after prolonged **FiO2 over 0.6 for more than 24-48 hours**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CNS oxygen toxicity (Paul Bert effect)**: seizures in hyperbaric oxygen above about 2 atmospheres. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Retinopathy of prematurity** and bronchopulmonary dysplasia in preterm infants. [WHO 2016]`,`**Hyperoxia in MI and post-cardiac arrest** increases infarct size and harms - titrate. [ESC 2023]`,`**Fire hazard**: no smoking or open flame near oxygen; burns from smoking on home oxygen. [BTS 2015]`,`**Nasal dryness, epistaxis, pressure sores** from masks and cannulae. [BTS 2017]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer to HDU/ICU** when SpO2 cannot be held in target on FiO2 0.4-0.5 or 6 L/min or more, RR over 30, NEWS2 7 or more, or pH under 7.25 in hypercapnic failure. [NEWS2 2017]`,`**Monitor**: SpO2 continuously in acute failure, RR and NEWS2 at least 4-hourly, ABG after changes, and ROX at 2, 6 and 12 hours on HFNO. [BTS 2017]`,`**Before discharge**: off oxygen or assessed for **long-term oxygen therapy** - LTOT needs stable PaO2 55 mmHg or less (or under 60 with cor pulmonale or polycythaemia) on two ABGs 3 weeks apart - see the Pulmonary rehabilitation and long-term home oxygen topic. [BTS 2015]`,`Do not prescribe **home oxygen at discharge** without reassessing 6-8 weeks later - many no longer qualify. [BTS 2015]`,`Give **oxygen alert cards** to patients with previous hypercapnic failure stating their target range. [BTS 2017]`]},{heading:`Prevention, programme and system issues in India`,points:[`**Prevent the causes**: smoking cessation, COPD and asthma control, vaccination, early pneumonia treatment, snake-bite and organophosphate poisoning first aid. [ICMR STW]`,`**Medical oxygen** is in **NLEM 2022** and was declared an essential drug with price capping of oxygen and concentrators (NPPA 2021). [NLEM 2022]`,`**PSA plants, liquid medical oxygen tanks and concentrators** were scaled up nationally after 2021; facility oxygen audits and trained 'oxygen stewards' are recommended. [MoHFW 2021]`,`**Pulse oximeters at Ayushman Arogya Mandirs** allow community detection of hypoxaemia in pneumonia and COVID. [MoHFW]`,`**Rational oxygen use**: prescribe with a target, avoid wasteful flows, and wean promptly to conserve supply. [MoHFW 2021]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2025)`,points:[`**BTS 2017 emergency oxygen guideline** (O'Driscoll, with 2019-2023 targeted updates) remains the UK standard: **94-98% / 88-92%**, prescription with target. [BTS 2017]`,`**ERS 2022 HFNC guideline**: HFNO over conventional oxygen in AHRF; over NIV in AHRF (conditional); NIV first in hypercapnic COPD; HFNO post-extubation. [ERS 2022]`,`**ESICM 2023**: recommends HFNO over conventional oxygen for non-COVID AHRF to reduce intubation; no recommendation for or against NIV over HFNO in non-COVID AHRF. [ESICM 2023]`,`**TSANZ 2022** oxygen guideline: SpO2 **92-96%** general target. [TSANZ 2022]`,`**Pulse oximetry and skin pigmentation**: FDA draft guidance 2025 requires testing across skin tones - interpret 'normal' SpO2 cautiously in dark-skinned patients. [FDA 2025]`,`**ICU oxygen trials** (e.g. Mega-ROX) have not shown a clear advantage of very conservative over usual targets in ventilated adults - avoid both extremes. [Mega-ROX 2025]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic question**: 'oxygen therapy - indications, devices, targets, complications' - draw the device-FiO2 table and say 94-98 / 88-92.`,`**Venturi mask colours vary by manufacturer** - always quote the percentage and the flow printed on the valve.`,`**Common mistake**: giving 15 L/min non-rebreather to a COPD patient and not repeating the ABG.`,`**Common mistake**: assuming SpO2 99% is safe - in CO poisoning it is falsely normal; in hypercapnic patients it can hide a PaCO2 of 90.`,`**ROX index** - know the formula and the 4.88 cut-off.`,`**Why oxygen causes hypercapnia** - V/Q mismatch and Haldane effect, not just 'loss of hypoxic drive'.`,`**Normal A-a gradient + high CO2** = hypoventilation - examiners love this.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Type 1 vs type 2 respiratory failure`,columns:[`Feature`,`Type 1 (hypoxaemic)`,`Type 2 (hypercapnic)`],rows:[[`Definition`,`PaO2 under 60 mmHg, PaCO2 normal or low`,`PaCO2 over 45 mmHg, PaO2 usually low`],[`Mechanism`,`V/Q mismatch, shunt, diffusion`,`Alveolar hypoventilation (pump failure)`],[`A-a gradient`,`Raised`,`Normal if pure hypoventilation; raised in COPD`],[`Examples`,`Pneumonia, ARDS, oedema, PE, ILD`,`COPD, OHS, GBS, myasthenia, opioids, kyphoscoliosis`],[`Oxygen target`,`94-98%`,`88-92%`],[`Support`,`Oxygen, HFNO, CPAP, intubation`,`Controlled oxygen, NIV, intubation`]]},{heading:`Oxygen devices and FiO2`,columns:[`Device`,`Flow`,`Approximate FiO2`,`Best use`],rows:[[`Nasal cannula`,`1-4 L/min`,`24-40%`,`Mild hypoxaemia, long-term, COPD at 1-2 L/min`],[`Simple face mask`,`5-10 L/min`,`35-60%`,`Moderate hypoxaemia, not hypercapnic`],[`Venturi mask`,`2-15 L/min by valve`,`24, 28, 35, 40, 60% fixed`,`Hypercapnic risk - precise FiO2`],[`Non-rebreather reservoir mask`,`15 L/min`,`60-90%`,`Critical illness, CO poisoning, trauma`],[`HFNO`,`30-60 L/min`,`21-100%`,`AHRF, post-extubation, NIV intolerance`],[`CPAP/NIV`,`Pressure-set`,`Up to 100%`,`Cardiogenic oedema (CPAP); hypercapnic COPD (BiPAP)`]]},{heading:`Drugs and doses (oxygen as a drug and reversal agents)`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Oxygen (most acute illness)`,`Device and flow to SpO2 94-98%`,`Wean as improves; stop when stable on air`,`Hyperoxia harm; fire risk`],[`Oxygen (hypercapnic risk)`,`24-28% Venturi or 1-2 L/min cannula to 88-92%`,`ABG at 30-60 min; titrate`,`CO2 retention if over-titrated; never stop abruptly`],[`Naloxone (opioid hypoventilation)`,`0.4 mg IV/IM, repeat every 2-3 min up to 2 mg (titrate 0.1 mg in dependence)`,`Infusion if long-acting opioid`,`Withdrawal, pulmonary oedema; short half-life`],[`Flumazenil (benzodiazepine)`,`0.2 mg IV, repeat 0.1 mg to max 1 mg`,`Single use`,`Seizures in mixed overdose or chronic use - avoid routinely`],[`Atropine (organophosphate)`,`1.8-3 mg IV, double every 5 min until chest clear, HR over 80, SBP over 80`,`Infusion 10-20% of loading dose per hour`,`Atropine toxicity: delirium, hyperthermia, ileus`],[`Neostigmine + atropine (cobra bite)`,`Neostigmine 1.5-2 mg IM (adult) with atropine 0.6 mg IV`,`If response, 0.5 mg every 30 min with ASV`,`Bradycardia, secretions; krait neurotoxicity responds poorly`],[`Methylene blue (methaemoglobinaemia)`,`1-2 mg/kg IV over 5 min`,`Repeat once after 1 hour if needed`,`Avoid in G6PD deficiency, serotonergic drugs`]]},{heading:`ROX index and HFNO decisions`,columns:[`Time on HFNO`,`ROX predicting success`,`ROX predicting failure`,`Action`],rows:[[`2 hours`,`4.88 or more`,`Under 2.85`,`Failure: prepare to intubate`],[`6 hours`,`4.88 or more`,`Under 3.47`,`Failure: escalate`],[`12 hours`,`4.88 or more`,`Under 3.85`,`Failure: intubate`],[`Between thresholds`,`Uncertain`,`Uncertain`,`Reassess in 1-2 hours; senior review`]]},{heading:`Mechanisms of hypoxaemia`,columns:[`Mechanism`,`A-a gradient`,`Response to oxygen`,`Example`],rows:[[`V/Q mismatch`,`Raised`,`Good`,`COPD, asthma, PE, pneumonia`],[`Shunt`,`Raised`,`Poor`,`ARDS, lobar collapse, AVM`],[`Hypoventilation`,`Normal`,`Good (but CO2 rises)`,`Opioids, GBS, OHS`],[`Diffusion impairment`,`Raised (exercise)`,`Good`,`ILD, emphysema`],[`Low inspired oxygen`,`Normal`,`Good`,`High altitude`]]}],redFlags:[`SpO2 below target despite FiO2 0.4-0.5 (or 6 L/min or more), or RR over 30 - escalate to HDU/ICU`,`pH under 7.35 with PaCO2 over 45 mmHg after initial therapy - start NIV; pH under 7.25 needs ICU`,`Drowsiness, confusion, flapping tremor or falling GCS - CO2 narcosis or exhaustion`,`Paradoxical abdominal breathing or slowing RR in a tiring patient - impending arrest`,`ROX index under 3.85 at 12 hours on HFNO`,`FVC under 20 mL/kg or a fall of 30% in neuromuscular weakness (GBS, myasthenia, krait bite)`,`Normal SpO2 with headache and multiple family members affected - suspect carbon monoxide`],pearls:[`Oxygen is a drug: prescribe device, flow and target range.`,`94-98% for most; 88-92% if at risk of hypercapnia (BTS 2017).`,`Venturi = fixed FiO2; 24 and 28% for COPD.`,`ROX = (SpO2/FiO2)/RR; 4.88 or more means HFNO is working.`,`Normal A-a gradient with high PaCO2 = hypoventilation.`,`Shunt does not correct with oxygen - needs PEEP.`,`Oxygen-induced hypercapnia is mostly V/Q mismatch and Haldane effect.`,`Never stop oxygen abruptly in a CO2 retainer - step down.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-respiratory-failure-oxygen-q1`,stem:`A 68-year-old with known COPD arrives with an exacerbation; SpO2 is 82% on air. What is the most appropriate initial oxygen prescription before the ABG result?`,options:[`15 L/min via non-rebreather mask targeting 98-100%`,`28% Venturi mask targeting SpO2 88-92%`,`Simple face mask at 2 L/min`,`No oxygen until the ABG is available`],answer:1,explanation:`BTS 2017 advises starting a 24% or 28% Venturi mask (or 1-2 L/min nasal cannula) with a target of 88-92% in patients at risk of hypercapnia, then checking an ABG. High-flow reservoir oxygen risks oxygen-induced hypercapnia and acidosis; a face mask under 5 L/min causes CO2 rebreathing; withholding oxygen in severe hypoxaemia is dangerous.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-respiratory-failure-oxygen-q2`,stem:`A patient on HFNO at 50 L/min with FiO2 0.6 has SpO2 93% and respiratory rate 36/min at 12 hours. What is the ROX index and its implication?`,options:[`About 7.5 - HFNO success likely`,`About 2.6 - meets the 2-hour success threshold`,`About 4.3 - continue unchanged as the patient is improving`,`About 4.3 - falls below 4.88 and needs close review for escalation`],answer:3,explanation:`ROX = (SpO2/FiO2)/RR = (93/0.6)/36 = 155/36, about 4.3. A value of 4.88 or more predicts HFNO success; at 12 hours under 3.85 predicts failure, so 4.3 lies in the uncertain zone and requires close reassessment and readiness to intubate rather than complacency. The other calculated values are arithmetic errors.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`respiratory-respiratory-failure-oxygen-q3`,stem:`A 25-year-old found unresponsive after a heroin overdose has PaO2 55 mmHg and PaCO2 70 mmHg on room air at sea level. What is the A-a gradient and the mechanism?`,options:[`About 8 mmHg - pure hypoventilation`,`About 40 mmHg - shunt`,`About 40 mmHg - V/Q mismatch from aspiration`,`About 95 mmHg - diffusion impairment`],answer:0,explanation:`PAO2 = 150 - 70/0.8 = 150 - 87.5 = 62.5 mmHg; A-a gradient = 62.5 - 55, about 8 mmHg, which is normal, so hypoxaemia is fully explained by alveolar hypoventilation from opioid-induced drive failure. A raised gradient would point to shunt, V/Q mismatch (for example from aspiration) or diffusion impairment; treatment is ventilation and naloxone, not just oxygen.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-respiratory-failure-oxygen-q4`,stem:`What is the main reason PaCO2 rises when a patient with COPD receives uncontrolled high-concentration oxygen?`,options:[`Loss of hypoxic ventilatory drive is the sole mechanism`,`Increased CO2 production from oxygen metabolism`,`Worsened V/Q matching from release of hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction, plus the Haldane effect`,`Absorption atelectasis of the whole lung`],answer:2,explanation:`Oxygen relieves hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction, diverting blood to poorly ventilated units and increasing dead space, while the Haldane effect reduces haemoglobin's CO2 carriage; these dominate over the modest fall in ventilatory drive. Oxygen does not increase CO2 production; absorption atelectasis causes shunt rather than hypercapnia; reduced drive contributes but is not the sole mechanism.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-respiratory-failure-oxygen-q5`,stem:`A family of four presents with headache and vomiting after sleeping in a closed room with a coal brazier (angithi). The SpO2 reads 98% on air. What is the correct oxygen management?`,options:[`No oxygen because SpO2 is normal`,`Titrate oxygen to SpO2 94-98%`,`Nasal cannula 2 L/min for comfort`,`100% oxygen via non-rebreather mask at 15 L/min regardless of SpO2`],answer:3,explanation:`Carbon monoxide poisoning gives a falsely normal SpO2 because standard oximeters read carboxyhaemoglobin as oxyhaemoglobin; high-concentration oxygen shortens COHb half-life from about 4-5 hours to about 60-90 minutes and is indicated regardless of SpO2, with hyperbaric oxygen considered in severe cases. Titrating to SpO2 or giving low-flow oxygen undertreats the poisoning.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-respiratory-failure-oxygen-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Define and classify respiratory failure. Discuss the principles of oxygen therapy including devices, target saturations, high-flow nasal oxygen and complications.`,openingLines:[`Respiratory failure is failure of pulmonary gas exchange with PaO2 under 60 mmHg (type 1) or PaCO2 over 45 mmHg (type 2) breathing air at sea level.`,`Oxygen is a drug and must be prescribed with a target range - 94-98% for most and 88-92% for those at risk of hypercapnia (BTS 2017).`],answer:[{heading:`Classification and causes`,points:[`Type 1: pneumonia, ARDS, oedema, PE, ILD; type 2: COPD, OHS, neuromuscular, drugs, chest wall; acute, chronic, acute-on-chronic.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Five mechanisms of hypoxaemia with A-a gradient; hypercapnia from load-pump imbalance; oxygen-induced hypercapnia mechanism.`]},{heading:`Assessment`,points:[`Clinical signs of hypoxaemia and hypercapnia; SpO2 with FiO2; ABG and repeat; FVC in neuromuscular disease.`]},{heading:`Oxygen therapy`,points:[`Devices and FiO2: nasal cannula, face mask, Venturi, reservoir mask; prescribe target; titrate and wean; special cases (CO, MI, paraquat).`]},{heading:`HFNO and NIV`,points:[`HFNO mechanisms (dead space washout, PEEP, humidity); FLORALI; ROX index; NIV for hypercapnic COPD; intubation criteria.`]},{heading:`Complications`,points:[`Hypercapnia, absorption atelectasis, pulmonary and CNS toxicity, ROP, fire; prevention by targeting.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table of oxygen devices with flow and FiO2`,`Oxyhaemoglobin dissociation curve`,`Escalation ladder: cannula to HFNO/NIV to intubation`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and classification`,marks:2},{part:`Pathophysiology`,marks:2},{part:`Oxygen devices and targets`,marks:3},{part:`HFNO, ROX and NIV`,marks:2},{part:`Complications`,marks:1}],keywords:[`respiratory failure`,`oxygen therapy`,`target saturation`,`Venturi`,`HFNO`,`ROX index`,`oxygen toxicity`]},{id:`respiratory-respiratory-failure-oxygen-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`High-flow nasal oxygen: mechanism, indications and the ROX index.`,openingLines:[`High-flow nasal oxygen delivers heated humidified gas at 30-60 L/min with FiO2 up to 1.0 through wide-bore nasal cannulae.`,`It reduces intubation in acute hypoxaemic respiratory failure and is monitored with the ROX index.`],answer:[{heading:`Mechanisms`,points:[`Precise FiO2, dead-space washout, PEEP effect about 1 cmH2O per 10 L/min, humidity aids secretions, less work of breathing.`]},{heading:`Indications`,points:[`De novo AHRF (FLORALI), post-extubation, NIV intolerance in COPD, pre-oxygenation, palliative breathlessness.`]},{heading:`ROX index`,points:[`(SpO2/FiO2)/RR; 4.88 or more predicts success; under 2.85 (2 h), 3.47 (6 h), 3.85 (12 h) predicts failure - escalate.`]}],mustDraw:[`Box of ROX thresholds over time`],markSplit:[{part:`Mechanisms`,marks:2},{part:`Indications`,marks:1},{part:`ROX index and failure criteria`,marks:2}],keywords:[`HFNO`,`HFNC`,`ROX index`,`FLORALI`,`dead space`]}],references:[`O'Driscoll BR et al. BTS guideline for oxygen use in adults in healthcare and emergency settings, Thorax 2017`,`Oczkowski S et al. ERS clinical practice guidelines: high-flow nasal cannula in acute respiratory failure, Eur Respir J 2022`,`Grasselli G et al. ESICM guidelines on ARDS: definition, phenotyping and respiratory support strategies, Intensive Care Med 2023`,`Rochwerg B et al. Official ERS/ATS clinical practice guidelines: noninvasive ventilation for acute respiratory failure, Eur Respir J 2017`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Approach to the Patient with Respiratory Failure`,`Roca O et al. An index combining respiratory rate and oxygenation to predict outcome of nasal high-flow therapy (ROX), Am J Respir Crit Care Med 2019`,`Barnett A et al. Thoracic Society of Australia and New Zealand position statement on acute oxygen use in adults, Respirology 2022`,`Ministry of Health and Family Welfare, Government of India: National Guidelines on rational use of oxygen, 2021; National List of Essential Medicines 2022`]});var s=[];s.push({id:`respiratory-mechanical-ventilation`,title:`Mechanical ventilation basics for the physician`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Invasive mechanical ventilation** is positive-pressure support through an endotracheal or tracheostomy tube for patients who cannot **protect the airway, oxygenate or ventilate**; it is started with **lung-protective settings (tidal volume 6-8 mL/kg predicted body weight, plateau pressure under 30 cmH2O)**, adjusted to the ABG, troubleshot with **DOPE**, and ended as early as possible by a daily **spontaneous breathing trial** while the **VAP bundle** prevents its commonest complication.`,keywords:[`mechanical ventilation`,`ventilator`,`rapid sequence intubation`,`RSI`,`volume control`,`pressure control`,`pressure support ventilation`,`SIMV`,`PEEP`,`plateau pressure`,`driving pressure`,`auto-PEEP`,`DOPE mnemonic`,`spontaneous breathing trial`,`RSBI`,`weaning`,`ventilator-associated pneumonia bundle`,`predicted body weight`,`ABCDEF bundle`,`tracheostomy`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Invasive mechanical ventilation (IMV)** delivers positive-pressure breaths through an **endotracheal tube or tracheostomy** to replace or assist the work of the respiratory muscles and to improve gas exchange. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Non-invasive ventilation (NIV)** gives the same support through a mask; it is first-line in hypercapnic COPD exacerbation and cardiogenic pulmonary oedema and is covered in the COPD exacerbation topic, while this topic deals with **intubated patients**. [ERS/ATS 2017]`,`In the international Esteban cohort about **one-third of ICU patients needed IMV**, the main reasons being acute respiratory failure (about two-thirds), coma and COPD exacerbation, and hospital mortality of ventilated patients was about **31%**. [Esteban JAMA 2002]`,`In India the common reasons a district or medical college ICU ventilates are **organophosphate poisoning, neurotoxic snake bite (krait), sepsis with pneumonia, Guillain-Barre syndrome, tetanus, head injury and severe COPD or asthma** - several are reversible with good ventilator care alone. [ISCCM 2020]`,`**Ventilator-associated pneumonia** in Indian ICUs is dominated by **carbapenem-resistant Acinetobacter baumannii and Klebsiella pneumoniae**, with carbapenem resistance in A. baumannii over 80% in the ICMR antimicrobial resistance network - prevention matters more than any antibiotic. [ICMR AMRSN 2023]`,`**Prolonged mechanical ventilation** is defined as ventilation for **21 days or more for at least 6 hours a day**; these patients usually need a tracheostomy and long-term weaning facility. [NAMDRC 2005]`]},{heading:`Indications for intubation and ventilation`,points:[`**Failure to protect the airway**: GCS **8 or less**, absent gag or cough, pooling secretions or vomitus, as in head injury, stroke, overdose and OP poisoning. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Failure of oxygenation**: SpO2 under 90% (PaO2 under 60 mmHg) despite high-flow oxygen, HFNO or NIV, or a **PaO2/FiO2 ratio falling below 150** with rising work of breathing. [ESICM 2023]`,`**Failure of ventilation**: acute respiratory acidosis with **pH under 7.25 and rising PaCO2** despite NIV, or a drowsy patient who cannot tolerate the mask. [BTS/ICS 2016]`,`**Excessive work of breathing**: respiratory rate over 35, use of accessory muscles, **paradoxical abdominal movement**, inability to speak and exhaustion - intubate before the arrest, not after it. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neuromuscular failure**: in Guillain-Barre syndrome and myasthenia the **20/30/40 rule** - vital capacity under 20 mL/kg, maximal inspiratory pressure weaker than -30 cmH2O, or maximal expiratory pressure under 40 cmH2O - predicts the need for ventilation. [AAN 2023]`,`**Anticipated course**: shock with lactic acidosis, airway burns or angioedema, severe agitation needing CT or procedures, and transfer of an unstable patient over long distances. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Failure of NIV** - no improvement in pH, PaCO2 or respiratory rate after **1-2 hours**, or worsening at any time - is itself an indication; delayed intubation after failed NIV or HFNO increases mortality. [ERS/ATS 2017]`]},{heading:`Physiology: why positive pressure helps and how it harms`,points:[`**Equation of motion**: airway pressure = (flow x resistance) + (volume / compliance) + PEEP, so the ventilator pressure reflects both the airway (resistance) and the lung and chest wall (compliance) - the basis of all troubleshooting. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Peak pressure** is measured during inspiratory flow and rises with resistance or poor compliance; **plateau pressure** is measured during a **0.5-second end-inspiratory hold** with no flow and reflects only alveolar pressure (compliance). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Driving pressure** = plateau pressure minus PEEP (= tidal volume / respiratory system compliance); a value **under 15 cmH2O** is associated with lower mortality in ARDS. [Amato NEJM 2015]`,`**PEEP** keeps alveoli open at end-expiration, recruits collapsed lung, reduces shunt and improves oxygenation, but high PEEP reduces venous return and can overdistend normal lung. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Haemodynamics**: positive intrathoracic pressure reduces **venous return (RV preload)** and LV afterload - post-intubation hypotension is common in the hypovolaemic septic patient, while the failing LV benefits. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ventilator-induced lung injury (VILI)** has four parts: **volutrauma** (overstretch), **barotrauma** (air leak), **atelectrauma** (repetitive opening and closing) and **biotrauma** (cytokine release spreading to other organs). [ESICM 2023]`,`**Predicted body weight** sets tidal volume because lung size depends on height and sex, not on fat: men **50 + 0.91 x (height cm - 152.4)** kg, women **45.5 + 0.91 x (height cm - 152.4)** kg. [ARDSNet NEJM 2000]`,`**Auto-PEEP (intrinsic PEEP)** occurs when expiration is incomplete before the next breath, as in asthma and COPD; trapped gas raises intrathoracic pressure, causes hypotension and makes triggering hard. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Rapid sequence intubation - the essentials`,points:[`**Prepare (SOAP-ME)**: Suction, Oxygen, Airway devices (laryngoscope, bougie, supraglottic airway, tube 7.0-7.5 in women and 7.5-8.0 in men), Positioning (ear-to-sternal-notch), Monitoring and **waveform capnography**, Medicines drawn and labelled. [DAS 2018]`,`**Pre-oxygenate for 3 minutes** (or 8 vital-capacity breaths) with a tight-fitting mask or NIV; **NIV pre-oxygenation reduced peri-intubation hypoxaemia** compared with face-mask oxygen in the PREOXI trial, and nasal cannula oxygen during apnoea extends safe apnoea time. [PREOXI NEJM 2024]`,`**Resuscitate before induction**: fluid bolus, push-dose or infused **noradrenaline** ready, because induction plus positive pressure can cause cardiac arrest in shock. [SCCM RSI 2023]`,`**Induction agents**: **ketamine 1-2 mg/kg IV** (keeps BP, bronchodilates - asthma, shock), **etomidate 0.2-0.3 mg/kg IV** (haemodynamically neutral, single-dose adrenal suppression), **propofol 1-2 mg/kg IV** (hypotension - reduce dose in shock). [SCCM RSI 2023]`,`**Paralytics**: **succinylcholine 1-1.5 mg/kg IV** (onset 45-60 s, lasts 8-10 min) or **rocuronium 1.2 mg/kg IV** (onset 60 s, lasts 45-70 min; reversed by **sugammadex 16 mg/kg**); SCCM found no clear difference in first-pass success between them. [SCCM RSI 2023]`,`**Avoid succinylcholine** in hyperkalaemia, burns or crush injury after 24-48 hours, denervation (GBS, spinal cord injury, stroke after days), myopathies and a history of **malignant hyperthermia**. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Video laryngoscopy** increased first-attempt success over direct laryngoscopy in critically ill adults (DEVICE trial) - use it where available, but every physician must also be competent with a Macintosh blade. [DEVICE NEJM 2023]`,`**Confirm tube position** with sustained **waveform capnography (end-tidal CO2 trace for at least 6 breaths)**, bilateral chest rise and auscultation, then chest X-ray (tip 3-5 cm above the carina, tube at about **21 cm at the teeth in women, 23 cm in men**); no trace means wrong place. [DAS 2018]`]},{heading:`Modes of ventilation - the classification examiners ask`,points:[`**Assist-control volume control (VC-AC)**: the clinician sets tidal volume, rate, flow and PEEP; every breath, triggered or not, receives the full set volume - **pressure varies with lung mechanics**, so a rising peak pressure is a warning. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pressure control (PC-AC)**: the clinician sets inspiratory pressure, inspiratory time, rate and PEEP; **tidal volume varies** with compliance and resistance, so a falling minute volume is the warning; decelerating flow may improve comfort and distribution. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pressure support ventilation (PSV)**: a purely spontaneous mode - the patient triggers each breath, the ventilator adds a set pressure (usually 5-15 cmH2O), and the breath **cycles off when flow falls to about 25% of peak**; no backup rate, so apnoea alarms are essential. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Synchronised intermittent mandatory ventilation (SIMV)**: a set number of mandatory breaths synchronised to the patient, with spontaneous (often pressure-supported) breaths in between; SIMV as a weaning mode **prolongs weaning** compared with PSV or T-piece trials. [ATS/CHEST 2017]`,`**CPAP**: a constant pressure throughout the cycle with spontaneous breaths only - used with or without pressure support during a spontaneous breathing trial. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dual and advanced modes**: **PRVC** (pressure-regulated volume control, a pressure breath adjusted to reach a target volume), **APRV** (airway pressure release ventilation) and **NAVA** are specialist modes; none has shown a mortality benefit over well-applied VC or PC. [ESICM 2023]`,`**Triggering**: flow trigger (1-3 L/min) or pressure trigger (-1 to -2 cmH2O); too sensitive causes auto-triggering from circuit leak or cardiac oscillation, too insensitive causes **ineffective efforts and asynchrony**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Initial ventilator settings after intubation`,points:[`**Mode**: VC-AC (or PC-AC) for the first 24 hours in most patients, with full rest of the respiratory muscles while the cause is treated. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tidal volume 6-8 mL/kg predicted body weight** in everyone, and **6 mL/kg (range 4-8)** in ARDS - large tidal volumes injure even normal lungs. [ESICM 2023]`,`**Respiratory rate 14-20/min**, higher (up to 30-35) in metabolic acidosis to match the patient's pre-intubation minute ventilation, and lower (**10-12**) in asthma and COPD to allow full expiration. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PEEP 5 cmH2O** to start, raised with FiO2 using the ARDSNet PEEP-FiO2 table in hypoxaemic failure. [ARDSNet NEJM 2000]`,`**FiO2 1.0 immediately after intubation**, then titrated down within 30-60 minutes to **SpO2 92-96%** (88-92% in hypercapnic COPD) - hyperoxia is not harmless. [BTS 2017]`,`**Inspiratory flow 60 L/min with an I:E of 1:2** in most; in obstructive disease use **high flow (80-100 L/min) and I:E of 1:4 or 1:5** to lengthen expiration. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Check at 15-30 minutes**: ABG, plateau pressure (target **under 30 cmH2O**), driving pressure (**under 15**), BP, and the chest X-ray for tube position and pneumothorax. [ESICM 2023]`,`**Write the sedation and analgesia plan** at the same time: analgesia-first with **fentanyl 25-100 microgram/h**, then light sedation with **propofol 0.3-3 mg/kg/h or dexmedetomidine 0.2-1.4 microgram/kg/h**, target **RASS -2 to 0**; avoid benzodiazepine infusions. [SCCM PADIS 2018]`]},{heading:`ABG-guided adjustment`,points:[`**Ventilation (PaCO2) is changed by minute ventilation** (rate x tidal volume): new rate = current rate x current PaCO2 / desired PaCO2 - change rate first because tidal volume is fixed by lung protection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Oxygenation (PaO2) is changed by FiO2 and mean airway pressure** (PEEP, inspiratory time); aim for **PaO2 60-80 mmHg or SpO2 92-96%** and bring FiO2 under 0.6 as soon as possible. [ESICM 2023]`,`**Permissive hypercapnia**: accept PaCO2 above normal if **pH stays 7.20 or above** to keep plateau pressure and tidal volume safe - contraindicated in raised intracranial pressure and severe pulmonary hypertension. [ESICM 2023]`,`**In COPD, ventilate to the patient's usual PaCO2 and a normal pH**, not to 40 mmHg - normalising PaCO2 in a chronic retainer causes **post-hypercapnic metabolic alkalosis**, seizures and failed weaning. [BTS/ICS 2016]`,`**In severe metabolic acidosis** (DKA, salicylate, methanol, sepsis) the patient's own minute ventilation may be 20-30 L/min; set a high rate and check the ABG within 30 minutes, because a 'normal' rate causes catastrophic acidaemia and arrest. [Harrison 22e]`,`**In raised intracranial pressure** keep **PaCO2 35-40 mmHg**; brief hyperventilation to 30-35 mmHg is a bridge only for impending herniation, as cerebral vasoconstriction causes ischaemia. [BTF 2016]`,`**Respiratory alkalosis on the ventilator** (pain, anxiety, fever, sepsis, over-setting) is corrected by treating the cause and reducing the set rate; if the patient is overbreathing the set rate, the problem is the patient, not the ventilator. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pressures and troubleshooting - DOPE`,points:[`**High peak pressure with a normal plateau (peak-plateau gap over 10 cmH2O)** means **high airway resistance**: kinked or bitten tube, secretions, bronchospasm or small tube - suction, bite block, bronchodilator. [Harrison 22e]`,`**High peak and high plateau pressure** means **low compliance**: pneumothorax, right main-bronchus intubation, ARDS or pulmonary oedema, atelectasis, abdominal distension, or **auto-PEEP**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Low peak pressure with a low exhaled volume** means a **leak or disconnection**: cuff leak, extubation, circuit disconnection or bronchopleural fistula. [Harrison 22e]`,`**DOPE** for sudden desaturation or rising pressure: **Displacement** of the tube, **Obstruction** (secretions, kink, biting), **Pneumothorax**, **Equipment failure** (oxygen supply, circuit, ventilator); add **S for stacked breaths** (auto-PEEP). [AHA PALS 2020]`,`**First action**: disconnect from the ventilator and **hand-ventilate with 100% oxygen** with a bag - if the patient improves, the problem is the ventilator or circuit; if bagging is hard, the problem is the tube or the patient. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Then**: check capnography trace and tube length at the teeth, pass a suction catheter (it will not pass a blocked tube), auscultate both sides, look at the trachea and neck veins; **tension pneumothorax** is decompressed by needle in the 4th-5th intercostal space mid-axillary line followed by a chest drain. [ATLS 2018]`,`**Auto-PEEP** is measured with a **0.5-1 second end-expiratory hold**; treat by **disconnecting briefly to let the chest empty**, lowering rate and tidal volume, shortening inspiratory time, and bronchodilating. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Patient-ventilator asynchrony** (double triggering, ineffective efforts, flow starvation) causes distress and wasted work; adjust trigger, flow and inspiratory time before deepening sedation. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Daily care of the ventilated patient - ABCDEF and the VAP bundle`,points:[`**ABCDEF (ICU Liberation) bundle**: **A**ssess and treat pain, **B**oth spontaneous awakening and breathing trials, **C**hoice of sedation (light, non-benzodiazepine), **D**elirium assess and prevent (CAM-ICU), **E**arly mobility, **F**amily engagement - full compliance lowers mortality and delirium. [SCCM PADIS 2018]`,`**Head of bed elevated 30-45 degrees** unless contraindicated - reduces aspiration of gastric contents. [SHEA/IDSA 2022]`,`**Daily sedation interruption paired with an SBT** (the ABC trial approach) shortens ventilation and ICU stay. [SHEA/IDSA 2022]`,`**Oral care with toothbrushing** is recommended; **routine chlorhexidine mouthwash is no longer recommended** because of a possible mortality signal. [SHEA/IDSA 2022]`,`**Subglottic secretion drainage tubes** for patients expected to need ventilation over **48-72 hours**, cuff pressure kept at **20-30 cmH2O**, and circuits changed only when **visibly soiled or malfunctioning**. [SHEA/IDSA 2022]`,`**Early enteral nutrition** within 24-48 hours, **thromboprophylaxis** (enoxaparin 40 mg SC daily or unfractionated heparin 5000 units SC 8-12 hourly), and glucose kept at 140-180 mg/dL. [SCCM/ASPEN 2022]`,`**Stress ulcer prophylaxis**: **pantoprazole 40 mg IV daily** reduced clinically important upper GI bleeding without changing mortality in ventilated patients (REVISE); stop when the patient is extubated and eating. [REVISE NEJM 2024]`,`**Avoid intubation and re-intubation** where possible (NIV, HFNO), and use the oral not the nasal route - the strongest VAP prevention is a shorter time on the tube. [SHEA/IDSA 2022]`]},{heading:`Weaning: readiness, spontaneous breathing trial and extubation`,points:[`**Screen every morning for readiness**: cause improving, **PaO2/FiO2 150-200 or more on PEEP 5-8 and FiO2 0.4-0.5 or less**, pH 7.25 or above, haemodynamically stable on no or low-dose vasopressor, and able to initiate breaths. [ATS/CHEST 2017]`,`**Spontaneous breathing trial (SBT)** for **30-120 minutes** on **pressure support 5-8 cmH2O with PEEP 5** (preferred - more patients pass and are extubated) or on a T-piece. [ATS/CHEST 2017]`,`**SBT failure criteria**: respiratory rate over 35 for 5 minutes, **SpO2 under 90%**, heart rate over 140 or a 20% change, SBP over 180 or under 90, agitation, sweating, accessory muscle use or a fall in consciousness - stop the trial and rest the patient on full support. [ATS/CHEST 2017]`,`**Rapid shallow breathing index (RSBI)** = respiratory rate / tidal volume in litres; **under 105** predicts successful weaning (Yang-Tobin), but it is a screen, not a substitute for the SBT. [Yang-Tobin NEJM 1991]`,`**Before extubation** check cough strength, secretion volume (suctioning more often than every 2 hours predicts failure) and mental status; do a **cuff-leak test** in high-risk patients (traumatic intubation, over 6 days on the tube, large tube, women). [ATS/CHEST 2017]`,`**Failed cuff leak**: give **systemic corticosteroids at least 4 hours before extubation** (for example methylprednisolone 20 mg IV 4-hourly, started 12 hours before) to reduce post-extubation stridor. [ATS/CHEST 2017]`,`**High-risk patients** (age over 65, cardiac disease, COPD, hypercapnia during SBT) should be **extubated directly to NIV or high-flow nasal oxygen**; NIV is not a rescue for established post-extubation failure, which needs re-intubation. [ERS/ATS 2017]`,`**Extubation failure** (re-intubation within 48-72 hours) occurs in about **10-20%** and carries high mortality; consider **tracheostomy** if weaning is prolonged - early tracheostomy (day 1-4) did not reduce mortality in TracMan. [TracMan JAMA 2013]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Acute severe asthma**: ketamine induction, large tube, **low rate (10-12), tidal volume 6-8 mL/kg, high flow and long expiration**, accept PaCO2 rise with pH over 7.20, and measure auto-PEEP and plateau pressure often. [GINA 2025]`,`**COPD**: low minute ventilation, long expiratory time, apply extrinsic PEEP at about 80% of measured auto-PEEP to ease triggering, and plan early extubation to NIV. [GOLD 2025]`,`**ARDS**: 6 mL/kg PBW, plateau under 30, higher PEEP in moderate-severe disease, **prone positioning for 16 hours a day if PaO2/FiO2 under 150**; the full protocol is in the ARDS topic. [ATS 2024]`,`**Pregnancy**: difficult airway (oedema, aspiration risk), use a smaller tube (6.0-7.0), left uterine displacement, and target the **pregnancy-normal PaCO2 of 30-32 mmHg**; foetal oxygenation needs maternal PaO2 over 70 mmHg. [Williams Obstetrics 26e]`,`**Obesity**: tidal volume by height (PBW), not actual weight; ramped position for intubation, higher PEEP to offset chest wall load, and reverse Trendelenburg. [ESICM 2023]`,`**Organophosphate poisoning (common in India)**: ventilate for respiratory failure at presentation or the **intermediate syndrome** (neck flexor and proximal weakness at 24-96 hours); continue atropine titration to dry chest, and wean by neck-lift and cough strength. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**Neurotoxic snake bite (krait, cobra)**: ventilation for ptosis progressing to neck and respiratory weakness; patients recover fully with ventilation and antivenom, so **never withhold ventilation** in a district hospital - even manual bagging saves life. [MoHFW SBE 2017]`,`**Elderly and frail**: discuss goals of care and the likely outcome with the family before or soon after intubation; frailty predicts prolonged ventilation and poor long-term function. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Airway**: tube displacement, oesophageal or main-bronchus intubation, cuff injury, **post-extubation stridor**, and late **tracheal stenosis or tracheomalacia** after prolonged intubation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lung**: barotrauma (pneumothorax, pneumomediastinum), VILI, atelectasis, **oxygen toxicity** and **ventilator-associated pneumonia** (pneumonia after 48 hours on the ventilator). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cardiovascular**: post-intubation hypotension and arrest, reduced cardiac output from high PEEP or auto-PEEP, and venous thromboembolism. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Muscle and nerve**: **ventilator-induced diaphragm dysfunction** begins within 24-48 hours of full rest, and **ICU-acquired weakness** (critical illness polyneuropathy and myopathy) prolongs weaning. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Brain**: **delirium** in up to 60-80% of ventilated patients, linked to benzodiazepines and deep sedation, and long-term cognitive impairment (post-intensive care syndrome). [SCCM PADIS 2018]`,`**Gut and other**: stress ulcer bleeding, ileus, sinusitis (nasal tubes), pressure injuries and **acalculous cholecystitis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Prognosis** depends on the cause: drug overdose and OP poisoning have good outcomes, while ARDS (about 35-45% mortality), sepsis with multiorgan failure and end-stage lung disease have poor outcomes. [LUNG SAFE JAMA 2016]`]},{heading:`Referral, transfer and follow-up`,points:[`A district hospital without an ICU should **intubate, stabilise and transfer** with a transport ventilator or bag, oxygen for twice the journey time, capnography and a trained escort; never transfer an unsecured failing airway. [ISCCM 2020]`,`**Refer early to a tertiary ICU** for ARDS needing prone ventilation or ECMO assessment, refractory hypoxaemia (PaO2/FiO2 under 80 despite optimisation), or prolonged weaning. [ATS 2024]`,`**Monitor** every ventilated patient with continuous SpO2 and ECG, **end-tidal CO2**, plateau and peak pressure each shift, ABG at least daily and after changes, and a daily chest X-ray only when clinically indicated. [Harrison 22e]`,`**After discharge** screen ICU survivors for **post-intensive care syndrome** - weakness, cognitive decline, anxiety, depression and PTSD - and for tracheal stenosis (stridor or effort dyspnoea weeks after extubation). [SCCM 2012]`,`**Tracheostomy patients at home**: teach the family suction, tube care and humidification, and what to do if the tube blocks or falls out; review at an ENT or pulmonology clinic for decannulation. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Prevention and counselling`,points:[`**Prevent intubation** where you can: early recognition of respiratory failure, timely **NIV for hypercapnic COPD**, HFNO for hypoxaemic failure, and treating the cause early. [ERS/ATS 2017]`,`**Prevent the commonest Indian causes**: pesticide safe-storage and restriction (OP poisoning), sleeping under a net or on a raised cot (krait bite), tetanus immunisation, and seat-belt and helmet use (head injury). [MoHFW 2024]`,`**Advance care planning** for end-stage COPD, ILD, cancer and frailty: document wishes about intubation before the crisis; Indian law permits withholding or withdrawing life support under the **Supreme Court 2018 and 2023 guidance on advance directives**. [Supreme Court of India 2023]`,`**Counsel families** in plain words: the ventilator supports breathing while the illness is treated, it does not cure; explain daily trials of weaning, the tube's discomfort and the risk of infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hand hygiene, closed suction systems and antimicrobial stewardship** reduce VAP and the spread of carbapenem-resistant organisms in the ICU. [ICMR AMSP 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**ESICM 2023 ARDS guideline**: low tidal volume (4-8 mL/kg PBW) and **prone position for at least 16 hours** in moderate-severe ARDS; recommends against routine prolonged high-pressure recruitment manoeuvres. [ESICM 2023]`,`**ATS 2024 ARDS update**: suggests **corticosteroids** for ARDS, **VV-ECMO** in selected severe ARDS, **neuromuscular blockade** in early severe ARDS, and higher PEEP without prolonged recruitment manoeuvres. [ATS 2024]`,`**SCCM 2023 RSI guideline**: no recommendation favouring etomidate over other induction agents, and no difference between **rocuronium and succinylcholine** for first-pass success. [SCCM RSI 2023]`,`**Trials changing practice**: **DEVICE (2023)** - video laryngoscopy improves first-pass success; **PREOXI (2024)** - NIV pre-oxygenation reduces hypoxaemia; **REVISE (2024)** - pantoprazole reduces GI bleeding in ventilated patients. [NEJM 2023-2024]`,`**SHEA/IDSA/APIC 2022 VAE compendium**: essential practices are avoiding intubation, minimising sedation, **paired SAT and SBT**, early mobility, toothbrushing, head-up and subglottic drainage; chlorhexidine oral care was dropped. [SHEA/IDSA 2022]`,`**ERS 2023 and ESICM 2023** support **HFNO or NIV after extubation** in patients at high risk of failure; the choice between them depends on hypercapnia and tolerance. [ESICM 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic viva**: 'Peak pressure is high - what do you do?' Answer with the **peak-plateau logic** and DOPE, starting by disconnecting and hand-bagging with 100% oxygen. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake**: setting tidal volume by actual body weight - always calculate **predicted body weight from height**. [ARDSNet NEJM 2000]`,`**Common mistake**: 'normalising' PaCO2 in a COPD retainer, or using a normal respiratory rate after intubating a patient with DKA. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Remember the targets**: VT 6-8 mL/kg PBW, plateau under 30, driving pressure under 15, SpO2 92-96%, pH over 7.20, cuff pressure 20-30, head-up 30-45 degrees. [ESICM 2023]`,`**Weaning one-liner**: daily screen, **SAT then SBT on PS 5-8 for 30-120 minutes**, extubate if passed, high-risk to NIV or HFNO; RSBI under 105. [ATS/CHEST 2017]`,`**Mode one-liner**: in volume control watch the pressure, in pressure control watch the volume. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs used for intubation and sedation of the ventilated adult`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Ketamine`,`1-2 mg/kg IV for induction`,`Onset 30-60 s, lasts 10-15 min; infusion 0.5-2 mg/kg/h for sedation`,`Raises BP and HR (preferred in shock, asthma); emergence reactions; caution in uncontrolled hypertension, ACS`],[`Etomidate`,`0.2-0.3 mg/kg IV`,`Single dose; onset 30-60 s`,`Haemodynamically neutral; transient adrenal suppression; myoclonus`],[`Propofol`,`1-2 mg/kg IV induction; 0.3-3 mg/kg/h infusion`,`Titrate to RASS -2 to 0`,`Hypotension, propofol infusion syndrome over 4 mg/kg/h for over 48 h; check triglycerides`],[`Succinylcholine`,`1-1.5 mg/kg IV`,`Onset 45-60 s, lasts 8-10 min`,`Hyperkalaemia; avoid in burns/crush after 24-48 h, denervation, myopathy, malignant hyperthermia`],[`Rocuronium`,`1.2 mg/kg IV for RSI`,`Onset 60 s, lasts 45-70 min; reversal sugammadex 16 mg/kg`,`Prolonged paralysis means sedation must be ensured; anaphylaxis rare`],[`Fentanyl`,`25-100 microgram/h IV infusion (bolus 25-50 microgram)`,`Analgesia-first; daily interruption`,`Respiratory depression, chest wall rigidity with fast bolus, ileus`],[`Dexmedetomidine`,`0.2-1.4 microgram/kg/h IV (no loading dose in ICU)`,`Light sedation, rousable; helps delirium and weaning`,`Bradycardia, hypotension; avoid in heart block`],[`Noradrenaline (peri-intubation)`,`0.05-0.5 microgram/kg/min IV infusion`,`Start before induction in shock`,`Peripheral line only short-term; extravasation necrosis`]]},{heading:`Modes of ventilation compared`,columns:[`Mode`,`Clinician sets`,`Varies with lung mechanics`,`Best use / caution`],rows:[[`Volume control (VC-AC)`,`Tidal volume, rate, flow, PEEP, FiO2`,`Peak and plateau pressure`,`Initial mode, ARDS lung protection; watch pressures`],[`Pressure control (PC-AC)`,`Inspiratory pressure, Ti, rate, PEEP, FiO2`,`Tidal volume and minute volume`,`Pressure-limited ventilation, leaks; watch volumes`],[`Pressure support (PSV)`,`Support pressure, PEEP, trigger, cycle %`,`Tidal volume, rate (patient-set)`,`Weaning and SBT; no backup rate - apnoea alarm`],[`SIMV`,`Mandatory rate and volume or pressure, plus PS`,`Spontaneous breath size`,`Older mode; slows weaning compared with PSV or T-piece`],[`CPAP`,`Constant pressure only`,`Everything else is the patient's`,`SBT, NIV for pulmonary oedema and OSA`],[`PRVC / APRV`,`Target volume (PRVC) or high and low pressure (APRV)`,`Pressure (PRVC); volume (APRV)`,`Specialist use; no proven mortality benefit`]]},{heading:`Reading airway pressures - differential diagnosis at the bedside`,columns:[`Pattern`,`Meaning`,`Causes`,`Immediate action`],rows:[[`High peak, normal plateau`,`Raised airway resistance`,`Kinked or bitten tube, secretions, bronchospasm, small tube`,`Suction, bite block, bronchodilator`],[`High peak, high plateau`,`Low respiratory system compliance`,`Pneumothorax, main-bronchus intubation, ARDS, oedema, abdominal distension, auto-PEEP`,`Examine chest, check tube depth, X-ray or ultrasound, decompress if tension`],[`Low peak, low exhaled volume`,`Leak`,`Cuff leak, disconnection, extubation, bronchopleural fistula`,`Check connections, cuff pressure and capnography trace`],[`Rising auto-PEEP`,`Air trapping`,`Asthma, COPD, high rate, long inspiratory time`,`Disconnect briefly, lower rate, lengthen expiration, bronchodilate`],[`Sudden desaturation, any pressure`,`DOPE`,`Displacement, Obstruction, Pneumothorax, Equipment failure`,`Hand-bag with 100% oxygen and work through DOPE`]]},{heading:`Initial settings and targets - cut-offs to remember`,columns:[`Parameter`,`Starting setting`,`Target / limit`,`Adjust when`],rows:[[`Tidal volume`,`6-8 mL/kg PBW (6 in ARDS)`,`Plateau under 30 cmH2O`,`Plateau over 30 - reduce by 1 mL/kg to minimum 4`],[`Respiratory rate`,`14-20/min (10-12 obstructive; high in metabolic acidosis)`,`pH 7.30-7.45 (over 7.20 permissive)`,`Use new RR = RR x PaCO2 / desired PaCO2`],[`PEEP`,`5 cmH2O`,`Driving pressure under 15`,`Raise with FiO2 by ARDSNet table in hypoxaemia`],[`FiO2`,`1.0 at intubation`,`SpO2 92-96% (88-92% in COPD)`,`Wean below 0.6 quickly`],[`I:E ratio`,`1:2`,`No auto-PEEP`,`1:4-1:5 in asthma or COPD`],[`Cuff pressure`,`20-30 cmH2O`,`No leak, no mucosal ischaemia`,`Check each shift`],[`SBT readiness`,`PEEP 8 or less, FiO2 0.5 or less`,`PaO2/FiO2 150-200 or more, RSBI under 105`,`Screen every morning`]]}],redFlags:[`Sudden desaturation or loss of the capnography trace in a ventilated patient - tube displacement until proved otherwise`,`Rising peak and plateau pressure with hypotension and unilateral absent breath sounds - tension pneumothorax`,`Hypotension or arrest within minutes of intubation in a septic or hypovolaemic patient`,`GCS 8 or less, pooling secretions or failing NIV after 1-2 hours - intubate now`,`Neck flexor weakness, falling single-breath count or vital capacity under 20 mL/kg in GBS, OP poisoning or krait bite`,`Refractory hypoxaemia (PaO2/FiO2 under 80) or plateau over 30 despite protective settings - refer for prone or ECMO`,`Stridor after extubation or effort dyspnoea weeks later - post-extubation oedema or tracheal stenosis`],pearls:[`In volume control watch the pressure; in pressure control watch the volume.`,`Tidal volume follows height (predicted body weight), never actual weight.`,`High peak with normal plateau is the airway; high peak with high plateau is the lung or pleura.`,`DOPE: Displacement, Obstruction, Pneumothorax, Equipment - and the first move is to hand-bag with 100% oxygen.`,`No trace, wrong place: waveform capnography is the only reliable confirmation of tracheal intubation.`,`Ventilate the COPD patient to his own PaCO2 and a normal pH, not to 40 mmHg.`,`Match the pre-intubation minute ventilation in metabolic acidosis, or the patient arrests.`,`SIMV is a poor weaning mode - use daily SBTs on PS 5-8 or T-piece.`,`Chlorhexidine mouthwash is out; toothbrushing, head-up, paired SAT-SBT and subglottic drainage are in.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-mechanical-ventilation-q1`,stem:`A 30-year-old man ventilated for asthma on volume control suddenly sets off the high-pressure alarm. Peak pressure is 52 cmH2O and plateau pressure is 22 cmH2O. Breath sounds are equal with widespread wheeze. What is the most likely problem?`,options:[`Right main-bronchus intubation`,`Tension pneumothorax`,`Increased airway resistance from bronchospasm or secretions`,`Worsening lung compliance from pulmonary oedema`],answer:2,explanation:`A wide peak-plateau gap (30 cmH2O) with a normal plateau means the problem is resistance to flow in the tube or airways - bronchospasm, secretions or a kinked or bitten tube. Pneumothorax, main-bronchus intubation and pulmonary oedema all reduce compliance and raise the plateau pressure along with the peak, and the first two give unequal breath sounds.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-mechanical-ventilation-q2`,stem:`A 170 cm tall man weighing 95 kg is intubated for ARDS. What initial tidal volume is appropriate?`,options:[`About 570 mL (6 mL/kg actual weight)`,`About 400 mL (6 mL/kg predicted body weight)`,`About 760 mL (8 mL/kg actual weight)`,`About 950 mL (10 mL/kg actual weight)`],answer:1,explanation:`Predicted body weight for a man is 50 + 0.91 x (170 - 152.4) = about 66 kg, so 6 mL/kg PBW is about 400 mL. Lung size follows height and sex, not fat; using actual weight in an obese patient gives a tidal volume of 570-950 mL that overdistends the lung and raises mortality, as shown by the ARDSNet low tidal volume trial.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-mechanical-ventilation-q3`,stem:`A ventilated patient with a head injury on volume control at a rate of 14/min has PaCO2 60 mmHg. You want PaCO2 of 40 mmHg without changing tidal volume. What new rate should you set?`,options:[`10/min`,`16/min`,`18/min`,`21/min`],answer:3,explanation:`PaCO2 is inversely proportional to alveolar ventilation, so new rate = 14 x 60 / 40 = 21/min. A rate of 16 or 18 would leave the patient hypercapnic, which raises intracranial pressure through cerebral vasodilatation, and 10/min would worsen the hypercapnia. Tidal volume is left fixed because it is set for lung protection.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-mechanical-ventilation-q4`,stem:`During a spontaneous breathing trial on pressure support 5 cmH2O and PEEP 5 cmH2O, a patient has a respiratory rate of 38/min and tidal volume of 280 mL after 20 minutes, with sweating. What is the best next step?`,options:[`Stop the trial, return to full support and look for the cause of failure`,`Extubate to high-flow nasal oxygen because the trial has lasted 20 minutes`,`Switch to SIMV with a low mandatory rate to train the muscles`,`Extubate and give methylprednisolone to prevent stridor`],answer:0,explanation:`A respiratory rate over 35, a rapid shallow breathing index of about 136 (over 105) and sweating are SBT failure criteria, so the patient goes back to full rest and the reason for failure (fluid overload, secretions, weakness, sedation) is treated before the next daily trial. Extubation now would likely fail, and SIMV is known to prolong weaning compared with daily SBTs; steroids are for a failed cuff-leak test, not for a failed SBT.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-mechanical-ventilation-q5`,stem:`Which practice is no longer recommended as part of the ventilator-associated pneumonia prevention bundle in the SHEA/IDSA/APIC 2022 compendium?`,options:[`Elevating the head of the bed to 30-45 degrees`,`Daily paired sedation interruption and spontaneous breathing trials`,`Routine oral care with chlorhexidine mouthwash`,`Subglottic secretion drainage in patients ventilated over 48-72 hours`],answer:2,explanation:`The 2022 compendium removed routine chlorhexidine oral care because meta-analyses showed no VAP benefit outside cardiac surgery and a possible increase in mortality; toothbrushing is advised instead. Head-of-bed elevation, paired SAT and SBT, and subglottic drainage remain recommended practices that shorten ventilation or reduce aspiration.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-mechanical-ventilation-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the indications for invasive mechanical ventilation. Describe the common modes, the initial ventilator settings and how you would troubleshoot a ventilated patient who suddenly desaturates.`,openingLines:[`Invasive mechanical ventilation is positive-pressure support through an endotracheal or tracheostomy tube for patients who cannot protect the airway, oxygenate or ventilate.`,`It is started with lung-protective settings - tidal volume 6-8 mL/kg predicted body weight and plateau pressure under 30 cmH2O - and is troubleshot by peak-plateau logic and DOPE.`],answer:[{heading:`Indications`,points:[`Airway protection: GCS 8 or less, absent gag, secretions.`,`Hypoxaemic failure: SpO2 under 90% despite HFNO or NIV, PaO2/FiO2 falling below 150 with distress.`,`Hypercapnic failure: pH under 7.25 with rising PaCO2 despite NIV, or NIV failure at 1-2 hours.`,`Work of breathing, neuromuscular failure (VC under 20 mL/kg), shock, anticipated course.`]},{heading:`Modes`,points:[`Volume control assist-control: set volume, pressure varies.`,`Pressure control: set pressure and Ti, volume varies.`,`Pressure support: spontaneous, flow-cycled at 25% of peak, used for weaning.`,`SIMV and CPAP; PRVC and APRV as specialist modes.`]},{heading:`Initial settings`,points:[`VC-AC, VT 6-8 mL/kg PBW (6 in ARDS); PBW men 50 + 0.91 x (height - 152.4).`,`Rate 14-20 (10-12 in asthma/COPD, high in metabolic acidosis), PEEP 5, FiO2 1.0 then to SpO2 92-96%.`,`Flow 60 L/min, I:E 1:2 (1:4 in obstructive disease).`,`Check ABG, plateau under 30, driving pressure under 15, chest X-ray at 30 minutes.`]},{heading:`ABG-guided adjustment`,points:[`PaCO2 by minute ventilation: new RR = RR x PaCO2 / target PaCO2.`,`PaO2 by FiO2 and PEEP; permissive hypercapnia with pH over 7.20.`]},{heading:`Troubleshooting sudden desaturation`,points:[`Disconnect and hand-ventilate with 100% oxygen.`,`DOPE: Displacement, Obstruction, Pneumothorax, Equipment failure; plus stacked breaths.`,`High peak with normal plateau = resistance; both high = compliance; low pressures = leak.`,`Needle decompression and chest drain for tension pneumothorax.`]},{heading:`Care bundle and weaning`,points:[`Head-up 30-45, paired SAT-SBT, toothbrushing, subglottic drainage, DVT and stress ulcer prophylaxis.`,`Daily SBT on PS 5-8 for 30-120 minutes; RSBI under 105; high-risk extubated to NIV or HFNO.`]}],mustDraw:[`Pressure-time curve showing peak and plateau pressure with an end-inspiratory hold`,`DOPE troubleshooting flow chart`],markSplit:[{part:`Indications`,marks:2},{part:`Modes`,marks:2},{part:`Initial settings and ABG adjustment`,marks:3},{part:`Troubleshooting with diagram`,marks:3}],keywords:[`indications`,`volume control`,`pressure control`,`PBW`,`plateau pressure`,`DOPE`]},{id:`respiratory-mechanical-ventilation-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on weaning from mechanical ventilation and the spontaneous breathing trial.`,openingLines:[`Weaning is the process of liberating a patient from mechanical ventilation; most patients can be extubated after passing a single spontaneous breathing trial.`,`Daily screening, paired sedation interruption and an SBT on low pressure support shorten ventilation (ATS/CHEST 2017).`],answer:[{heading:`Readiness criteria`,points:[`Cause improving, PaO2/FiO2 150-200 or more on PEEP 8 or less and FiO2 0.5 or less.`,`Haemodynamically stable, awake enough, able to initiate breaths, pH 7.25 or more.`]},{heading:`The SBT`,points:[`Pressure support 5-8 cmH2O with PEEP 5, or T-piece, for 30-120 minutes.`,`RSBI (f/VT in litres) under 105 predicts success.`]},{heading:`Failure criteria`,points:[`RR over 35, SpO2 under 90%, HR over 140 or 20% change, SBP over 180 or under 90.`,`Agitation, sweating, accessory muscles - return to full support and find the cause.`]},{heading:`Extubation`,points:[`Assess cough, secretions, consciousness; cuff-leak test in high-risk patients; steroids at least 4 hours before if failed.`,`High-risk patients extubated directly to NIV or HFNO; tracheostomy if weaning is prolonged.`]}],mustDraw:[`Weaning flow: daily screen, SAT, SBT, extubation or rest`],markSplit:[{part:`Readiness`,marks:1},{part:`SBT method and RSBI`,marks:2},{part:`Failure criteria and extubation`,marks:2}],keywords:[`weaning`,`SBT`,`RSBI`,`cuff leak`,`post-extubation NIV`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Mechanical ventilatory support`,`ESICM guidelines on acute respiratory distress syndrome: definition, phenotyping and respiratory support strategies, 2023`,`American Thoracic Society clinical practice guideline update: management of ARDS, 2024`,`Society of Critical Care Medicine Clinical Practice Guidelines for Rapid Sequence Intubation in the Critically Ill Adult Patient, 2023`,`ATS/CHEST clinical practice guideline: Liberation from mechanical ventilation in critically ill adults, 2017`,`SHEA/IDSA/APIC Strategies to prevent ventilator-associated pneumonia, ventilator-associated events and non-ventilator hospital-acquired pneumonia in acute-care hospitals, 2022 update`,`SCCM PADIS guideline (pain, agitation/sedation, delirium, immobility, sleep) in adult ICU patients, 2018`,`ICMR Antimicrobial Resistance Research and Surveillance Network annual report, 2023`]}),s.push({id:`respiratory-alveolar-haemorrhage-vasculitis`,title:`Diffuse alveolar haemorrhage and pulmonary vasculitis`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`**Diffuse alveolar haemorrhage (DAH)** is bleeding into the alveolar spaces from the pulmonary microcirculation, recognised by the triad of **haemoptysis, a falling haemoglobin and diffuse alveolar infiltrates** with hypoxaemia, confirmed by **progressively bloodier BAL aliquots** and a **raised DLCO**; the commonest treatable cause is **pulmonary capillaritis of ANCA-associated vasculitis or anti-GBM disease**, treated with **pulse methylprednisolone plus rituximab or cyclophosphamide**, while in India **leptospirosis** is a major infective cause.`,keywords:[`DAH`,`diffuse alveolar haemorrhage`,`pulmonary capillaritis`,`pulmonary-renal syndrome`,`granulomatosis with polyangiitis`,`Wegener`,`microscopic polyangiitis`,`anti-GBM disease`,`Goodpasture`,`ANCA`,`PR3`,`MPO`,`haemosiderin-laden macrophages`,`PEXIVAS`,`rituximab`,`cyclophosphamide`,`avacopan`,`leptospirosis pulmonary haemorrhage`,`plasma exchange`,`Chapel Hill`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**DAH** is a clinicopathological syndrome of widespread intra-alveolar bleeding from **alveolar capillaries, arterioles and venules**, distinct from haemoptysis arising in the bronchial circulation (bronchiectasis, TB, tumour). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pulmonary vasculitis** means inflammation of the pulmonary vessels; in practice it is mostly **small-vessel ANCA-associated vasculitis (AAV)** - GPA, MPA and EGPA - plus anti-GBM disease, and rarely Behcet disease and Takayasu arteritis involving the pulmonary arteries. [Chapel Hill 2012]`,`**ANCA-associated vasculitis** has an annual incidence of about **10-20 per million** in Europe, peaks at **60-70 years**, and is classified by antibody (PR3 or MPO) as well as by clinical syndrome. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anti-GBM disease** is rare (about **1 per million per year**) with a bimodal age distribution - young men with lung haemorrhage (often smokers) and older women with isolated nephritis. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**DAH occurs in a minority of AAV patients** - more often in MPA than GPA - but it is the manifestation with the highest early mortality, roughly doubling the risk of death. [EULAR 2022]`,`**In India**, Indian tertiary-centre series (PGIMER, CMC Vellore, AIIMS) report **GPA as the commonest AAV**, often presenting late with renal failure, and AAV is frequently first treated as **tuberculosis** because of cavitating nodules. [Indian J Rheumatol 2020]`,`**Leptospirosis pulmonary haemorrhage syndrome** is the most important infective cause of DAH in India, seen in monsoon outbreaks in **Kerala, Gujarat, Maharashtra, Tamil Nadu, Karnataka and Andaman**, with mortality often over 50% once it develops. [NCDC Leptospirosis 2015]`]},{heading:`Aetiology - three histological patterns`,points:[`**Pulmonary capillaritis** (neutrophil infiltration and fibrinoid necrosis of alveolar septa): **GPA, MPA, anti-GBM disease, SLE, antiphospholipid syndrome, IgA vasculitis, cryoglobulinaemia, Behcet** and drug-induced ANCA vasculitis (**propylthiouracil, hydralazine, levamisole-cut cocaine**). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bland pulmonary haemorrhage** (bleeding without inflammation): **coagulopathy, thrombocytopenia, anticoagulants and antiplatelets, mitral stenosis, severe left heart failure**, idiopathic pulmonary haemosiderosis in children, and anti-GBM disease in some cases. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diffuse alveolar damage with haemorrhage**: ARDS, **haematopoietic stem cell transplant** (peri-engraftment DAH), cytotoxic drugs (amiodarone, nitrofurantoin, bleomycin), crack cocaine inhalation and radiation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Infections causing DAH**: **leptospirosis** (most important in India), **dengue and falciparum malaria**, **hantavirus**, scrub typhus, influenza and COVID-19, CMV and Staphylococcus aureus sepsis in the immunocompromised. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mitral stenosis** (rheumatic, common in India) causes haemoptysis from bronchial varices and pulmonary apoplexy and, with chronic leak, **haemosiderosis** - exclude it with echocardiography in every case. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Smoking, hydrocarbon exposure and infection** unmask lung haemorrhage in anti-GBM disease by exposing the normally hidden alveolar basement membrane epitope. [KDIGO 2024]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why the signs and the tests look as they do`,points:[`**ANCA-mediated injury**: cytokine-primed neutrophils express **PR3 or MPO** on their surface; ANCA binds, activates the neutrophils, which release reactive oxygen species, proteases and **neutrophil extracellular traps** that destroy capillary walls (necrotising, pauci-immune vasculitis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Alternative complement pathway (C5a)** amplifies neutrophil activation in AAV - the rationale for the oral **C5a receptor blocker avacopan**. [ADVOCATE NEJM 2021]`,`**Anti-GBM disease**: IgG autoantibodies against the **non-collagenous (NC1) domain of the alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen** bind the glomerular and alveolar basement membranes, giving **linear IgG staining** on immunofluorescence. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Pulmonary-renal syndrome**: the alveolar and glomerular capillaries share structure and target antigens, so DAH and **rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis** occur together - AAV causes most cases, anti-GBM disease most of the rest. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why DLCO rises**: free haemoglobin in the alveoli binds inhaled carbon monoxide, so **DLCO rises (often over 130% of predicted or by over 30%)** in acute bleeding - the only common cause of a raised DLCO with infiltrates. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why haemoptysis may be absent**: alveolar volume is large and blood may not reach the large airways, so **up to one-third have no haemoptysis** and present only with anaemia, hypoxaemia and infiltrates. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Haemosiderin-laden macrophages** appear in BAL about **48-72 hours** after bleeding as macrophages digest red cells; they persist for weeks, so they show recent or recurrent bleeding, not its timing. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Granuloma formation in GPA** causes necrotising nodules that **cavitate**, sinus and nasal destruction (saddle nose) and **subglottic stenosis** - these are absent in MPA, which is a pure small-vessel vasculitis without granulomas. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification - Chapel Hill 2012 and ACR/EULAR 2022`,points:[`**Chapel Hill 2012 nomenclature**: large-vessel (Takayasu, giant cell arteritis), medium-vessel (PAN, Kawasaki), small-vessel - **ANCA-associated (MPA, GPA, EGPA)** and **immune-complex (anti-GBM, cryoglobulinaemic, IgA vasculitis, hypocomplementaemic urticarial)** - and variable-vessel (Behcet, Cogan). [Chapel Hill 2012]`,`**ACR/EULAR 2022 GPA classification** (after diagnosing small- or medium-vessel vasculitis): nasal bloody discharge, ulcers or crusting **+3**, cartilaginous involvement **+2**, conductive hearing loss +1, **c-ANCA or PR3-ANCA +5**, lung nodules, mass or cavitation +2, granuloma on biopsy +2, sinus inflammation on imaging +1, pauci-immune GN +1, p-ANCA or MPO-ANCA -1, eosinophils 1000/microlitre or more -4; **score 5 or more = GPA**. [ACR/EULAR 2022]`,`**ACR/EULAR 2022 MPA classification**: **p-ANCA or MPO-ANCA +6**, fibrosis or ILD on chest imaging +3, pauci-immune GN +3, nasal involvement -3, c-ANCA or PR3-ANCA -1, eosinophils 1000/microlitre or more -4; **score 5 or more = MPA**. [ACR/EULAR 2022]`,`**These are classification criteria for research**, not diagnostic criteria; diagnosis needs clinical judgement, ANCA and, where possible, tissue. [ACR/EULAR 2022]`,`**Disease severity (EULAR 2022)**: **organ- or life-threatening** disease (DAH, RPGN, mononeuritis, cardiac, CNS) versus **non-organ-threatening** (limited sinus, skin, arthritis) - the split that decides induction intensity. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Severity of DAH** is graded clinically by hypoxaemia: mild (on room air or low-flow oxygen), moderate (high FiO2 or NIV) and severe (**mechanical ventilation**), the last carrying the worst prognosis. [KDIGO 2024]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Acute or subacute breathlessness, cough and haemoptysis** over days, often with fever - easily mistaken for pneumonia; haemoptysis ranges from streaks to frank blood and is absent in up to one-third. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Prodrome of systemic vasculitis** for weeks to months: fever, weight loss, myalgia, **arthralgia**, night sweats - also mistaken for TB in India. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ENT clues to GPA**: chronic sinusitis, **bloody nasal crusts**, epistaxis, nasal septal perforation, **saddle nose**, otitis media with conductive deafness, and hoarseness or stridor from **subglottic stenosis**. [ACR/EULAR 2022]`,`**Renal clues**: reduced urine output, **cola-coloured urine**, oedema and new hypertension from rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Other organs**: purpura and skin ulcers, **mononeuritis multiplex (foot drop)**, red eye (scleritis), orbital mass and proptosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ask about drugs and exposures**: propylthiouracil, hydralazine, anticoagulants, antiplatelets, cocaine, smoking, hydrocarbons, and **recent wading through floodwater, paddy fields or sewage** (leptospirosis). [NCDC Leptospirosis 2015]`,`**Ask about SLE features and pregnancy losses** (SLE, antiphospholipid syndrome), palpitations and orthopnoea (mitral stenosis), and transplant or chemotherapy history. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Tachypnoea, hypoxaemia and pallor** with often **surprisingly few chest signs** - scattered crackles in the presence of widespread infiltrates. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nose and ears**: crusting, septal perforation, **saddle-nose deformity**, otitis media - GPA until proved otherwise. [ACR/EULAR 2022]`,`**Skin**: **palpable purpura**, nail-fold infarcts, livedo reticularis (APS) and malar rash (SLE). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Eyes**: scleritis, episcleritis, orbital pseudotumour in GPA; **conjunctival suffusion** (redness without discharge) is the classic sign of leptospirosis. [NCDC Leptospirosis 2015]`,`**Hypertension, oedema and dipstick haematuria with proteinuria** at the bedside signal glomerulonephritis - always dip the urine in unexplained haemoptysis. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Neurological**: wrist or foot drop (mononeuritis multiplex); **calf tenderness, jaundice and oliguria** suggest Weil disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mid-diastolic murmur with loud S1 and opening snap** points to mitral stenosis as a bland cause. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Pneumonia, including TB**: fever and infiltrates with haemoptysis but Hb stable, BAL not progressively bloody, sputum NAAT or cultures positive - and **AAV cavities are often misread as TB** in India. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pulmonary oedema** (LV failure, mitral stenosis, fluid overload in AKI): orthopnoea, raised JVP, cardiomegaly, BNP raised, rapid clearing with diuretics. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**ARDS from sepsis**: bilateral infiltrates and hypoxaemia but no fall in Hb; BAL may be pink but not progressively bloodier. [ESICM 2023]`,`**Haemoptysis from an airway source** (bronchiectasis, tumour, aspergilloma) aspirated into both lungs: localised source at bronchoscopy, airway disease on CT - see the haemoptysis topic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Leptospirosis and other tropical fevers** (dengue, malaria, scrub typhus): febrile illness with thrombocytopenia, jaundice and AKI - the pulmonary-renal picture without ANCA. [NCDC Leptospirosis 2015]`,`**Eosinophilic lung disease** and **EGPA**: asthma and eosinophilia, rather than haemorrhage, dominate - see the eosinophilic lung topic. [ACR/EULAR 2022]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line, then tertiary`,points:[`**First line in any hospital**: CBC (falling Hb, platelets), PT/INR and aPTT, **creatinine and urea**, **urine dipstick and microscopy for red cell casts and dysmorphic RBCs**, urine protein-creatinine ratio, ABG, chest X-ray and ECG. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Chest X-ray and HRCT**: bilateral patchy or diffuse **ground-glass and consolidation**, often sparing the periphery and apices; GPA adds **nodules, masses and cavities**; interlobular septal thickening appears after a few days (crazy paving). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Serology - send before treatment, but never wait for it**: **ANCA by antigen-specific immunoassay (PR3 and MPO ELISA)** as the first test - immunofluorescence (c-ANCA, p-ANCA) is no longer required first; plus **anti-GBM antibody**, ANA, anti-dsDNA, C3 and C4, antiphospholipid antibodies and cryoglobulins. [International consensus on ANCA testing 2017]`,`**Infection screen in India**: **leptospira IgM ELISA or PCR** (blood in week 1, urine after), dengue NS1 and IgM, malaria smear or rapid test, **scrub typhus IgM**, blood cultures and sputum NAAT for TB. [NCDC Leptospirosis 2015]`,`**Bronchoscopy with BAL**: **sequential aliquots becoming progressively bloodier** confirm DAH; **haemosiderin-laden macrophages over 20%** of alveolar macrophages support it; BAL also excludes infection (cultures, PCR for CMV and PJP). [ATS 2012]`,`**DLCO raised (over 130% predicted or by over 30% from baseline)** supports active bleeding, but most patients are too ill for lung function tests. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tissue diagnosis**: **renal biopsy** is the highest-yield biopsy (pauci-immune crescentic GN in AAV, **linear IgG** in anti-GBM disease); nasal or lung biopsy (surgical, not transbronchial) is less often needed. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Echocardiography** for mitral stenosis and LV function, and **BNP** where heart failure is possible. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`**DAH is never an OPD disease**: any patient with haemoptysis plus falling Hb, hypoxaemia or diffuse infiltrates is **referred the same day** with oxygen. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Before referral**: give oxygen to SpO2 94-98%, secure IV access, send CBC, creatinine, urine dipstick and ANCA if possible, **stop anticoagulants and antiplatelets**, and record the urine findings in the letter. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Suspected leptospirosis with any lung involvement**: give the first dose of **ceftriaxone 1 g IV (or 2 g in severe disease)** or **benzylpenicillin 1.5 million units IV** and refer to a hospital with dialysis and ventilator. [NCDC Leptospirosis 2015]`,`**Mild leptospirosis in OPD** (no jaundice, AKI, bleeding or breathlessness): **doxycycline 100 mg orally twice daily for 7 days** (or amoxicillin 500 mg thrice daily, or azithromycin in pregnancy and children). [NCDC Leptospirosis 2015]`,`**Long-term follow-up of AAV in the community**: monitor BP, urine dipstick, CBC and creatinine on the schedule set by the rheumatologist or nephrologist, check for infection on immunosuppression, and ensure **cotrimoxazole prophylaxis** is being taken. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Maintenance therapy** given after remission: **rituximab 500 mg IV every 6 months for 18-24 months** (preferred) or **azathioprine 1.5-2 mg/kg/day** (check TPMT where available and CBC weekly at first), or methotrexate 20-25 mg weekly if eGFR is preserved. [EULAR 2022]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency`,points:[`**Supportive care**: oxygen, HFNO or NIV, and **intubation with PEEP 8-10 cmH2O** if needed (PEEP tamponades bleeding); transfuse red cells to Hb over 7 g/dL, correct INR with vitamin K and FFP, and platelets over 50,000. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Start immunosuppression before biopsy or ANCA results** when capillaritis is likely: **pulse methylprednisolone 500-1000 mg IV daily for 3 days**, then oral prednisolone. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Reduced-dose steroid taper (PEXIVAS)**: prednisolone 1 mg/kg/day (maximum 75 mg) tapered to about **5 mg by 6 months** was non-inferior to standard dosing and caused fewer serious infections - now the standard. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Induction with rituximab**: **375 mg/m2 IV weekly for 4 weeks or 1 g IV on days 1 and 15**; preferred in relapsing disease, PR3-ANCA, young patients wishing to preserve fertility and the elderly. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Induction with cyclophosphamide**: **IV 15 mg/kg at weeks 0, 2 and 4, then every 3 weeks** for 3-6 months (reduce by 2.5 mg/kg for age over 60 and over 70, and for eGFR under 30; maximum 1.2 g), with **mesna**, hydration and antiemetics - preferred in severe RPGN (creatinine over 4 mg/dL) and where rituximab is unaffordable. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Combination of rituximab with low-dose cyclophosphamide** can be considered for the sickest patients with severe glomerulonephritis or DAH. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Plasma exchange in AAV**: **PEXIVAS showed no reduction in death or ESKD**, including in the DAH subgroup, so it is **not routine for DAH**; KDIGO 2024 and EULAR 2022 reserve it for **creatinine over 3.4 mg/dL (300 micromol/L)** with RPGN, and for DAH with hypoxaemia only as rescue. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Anti-GBM disease**: **daily plasma exchange (about 4 L, albumin; FFP if bleeding) for 14 days or until antibodies are undetectable**, plus pulse steroids and **oral cyclophosphamide 2-3 mg/kg/day for 3 months** - plasma exchange is standard here, unlike in AAV. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Avacopan 30 mg orally twice daily** with rituximab or cyclophosphamide allows faster steroid withdrawal (ADVOCATE) and is an option, though cost limits use in India. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Leptospirosis pulmonary haemorrhage**: **benzylpenicillin 1.5 million units IV 6-hourly or ceftriaxone 1-2 g IV daily for 7 days**, lung-protective ventilation, early dialysis; high-dose methylprednisolone has been used but its benefit is unproven. [NCDC Leptospirosis 2015]`,`**SLE-related DAH**: pulse steroids plus cyclophosphamide or rituximab; exclude infection and give broad antibiotics while waiting for BAL cultures. [EULAR SLE 2023]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: avoid cyclophosphamide (teratogenic in the first trimester) and use **steroids, azathioprine, IVIG or rituximab** case by case; propylthiouracil-associated vasculitis requires stopping the drug. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Elderly**: MPO-AAV with renal failure is common; reduce cyclophosphamide dose, prefer the reduced-dose steroid regimen, and watch closely for infection - the main cause of early death. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**CKD and dialysis**: rituximab needs no renal dose adjustment; cyclophosphamide does; dialysis-dependent patients with no chance of recovery after 3-4 months may stop induction to avoid infection. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Children**: **idiopathic pulmonary haemosiderosis** (recurrent DAH, iron-deficiency anaemia, sometimes with coeliac disease - Lane-Hamilton syndrome) and SLE are the main causes. [Nelson 22e]`,`**Haematopoietic stem cell transplant**: peri-engraftment DAH within 30 days is treated with high-dose steroids; infection must be excluded by BAL. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: IV cyclophosphamide is cheap and available, and biosimilar rituximab has lowered cost in India; give **cotrimoxazole prophylaxis** throughout induction. [EULAR 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Respiratory failure** needing ventilation, and recurrent DAH leading to **pulmonary fibrosis or emphysema**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**End-stage kidney disease** in about 20-25% of AAV patients with renal involvement within 5 years; creatinine at presentation is the best predictor. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Infections** (bacterial pneumonia, PJP, CMV, TB reactivation in India, HBV reactivation) are the commonest cause of death in the first year - more than active vasculitis. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Treatment toxicity**: cyclophosphamide causes cytopenia, **haemorrhagic cystitis and bladder cancer**, infertility; rituximab causes **hypogammaglobulinaemia** and infusion reactions; steroids cause diabetes, osteoporosis and infection. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Relapse** occurs in up to 50% of GPA (PR3-ANCA) over 5 years, less in MPA. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Untreated GPA** had a 1-year mortality of about 80-90%; with modern therapy **5-year survival is about 75-85%**, but DAH needing ventilation carries in-hospital mortality of 25-50%. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer immediately** to a centre with ICU, dialysis, bronchoscopy, rheumatology or nephrology and plasma exchange. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**During induction**: CBC 1-2 weekly (cyclophosphamide nadir at 10-14 days; hold if WBC under 4000), creatinine, urine dipstick, glucose and BP. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**In remission**: review every 1-3 months for the first year, then 3-6 monthly; check **urine dipstick at every visit** - new haematuria often heralds relapse; ANCA rise alone is not a reason to treat. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Immunoglobulin G level** before each rituximab dose; hold or give IVIG if IgG is very low with recurrent infection. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Bone protection** (calcium, vitamin D, bisphosphonate if indicated), cardiovascular risk control and **vaccination** (influenza, pneumococcal, hepatitis B, recombinant zoster; avoid live vaccines). [EULAR 2022]`,`**Screen for latent TB and hepatitis B** before induction in India; HBsAg or anti-HBc positive patients need antiviral prophylaxis with rituximab. [EULAR 2022]`]},{heading:`Prevention and counselling`,points:[`**PJP prophylaxis**: **cotrimoxazole 400/80 mg daily or 800/160 mg thrice weekly** during induction with rituximab or cyclophosphamide and for at least 6 months after the last rituximab dose. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Leptospirosis prevention**: avoid wading in floodwater, wear boots and gloves, rodent control; **doxycycline 200 mg orally once a week** during exposure for high-risk workers in outbreak areas. [NCDC Leptospirosis 2015]`,`**Stop smoking** - smoking precipitates lung haemorrhage in anti-GBM disease. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Avoid offending drugs**: switch from propylthiouracil to carbimazole where possible, avoid hydralazine in a patient with prior drug-induced vasculitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Counsel on fertility**: cyclophosphamide can cause premature ovarian failure and azoospermia; offer rituximab or fertility preservation where possible. [EULAR 2022]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**KDIGO 2024 ANCA vasculitis guideline**: rituximab or cyclophosphamide induction with a **reduced-dose glucocorticoid taper**, avacopan as an option, and plasma exchange considered for creatinine over 3.4 mg/dL rather than routinely. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**EULAR 2022 AAV recommendations**: rituximab preferred for relapsing disease and maintenance for 24-48 months; plasma exchange considered in RPGN, **not routinely in DAH**. [EULAR 2022]`,`**ACR/EULAR 2022 classification criteria** for GPA, MPA and EGPA replaced the 1990 ACR criteria and include ANCA type. [ACR/EULAR 2022]`,`**PEXIVAS (2020)** remains the trial behind both changes: no benefit of plasma exchange on death or ESKD, and the reduced-dose steroid regimen was non-inferior with fewer infections. [PEXIVAS NEJM 2020]`,`**Guideline conflict**: older practice and the ACR 2021 guideline allowed plasma exchange for DAH with hypoxaemia; current EULAR and KDIGO advice is to reserve it as rescue in DAH and use it routinely only in anti-GBM disease. [ACR 2021]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic triad**: haemoptysis, falling haemoglobin and diffuse infiltrates; **haemoptysis may be absent**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rising DLCO** with infiltrates is DAH until proved otherwise - every other ILD lowers DLCO. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Progressively bloodier BAL aliquots** are diagnostic; haemosiderin-laden macrophages take 48-72 hours to appear. [ATS 2012]`,`**Pulmonary-renal syndrome**: GPA, MPA, anti-GBM, SLE - and in India, **leptospirosis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**c-ANCA/PR3 = GPA** (granulomas, ENT, cavities); **p-ANCA/MPO = MPA** (no granulomas, ILD); **linear IgG = anti-GBM**. [ACR/EULAR 2022]`,`**Common mistake**: giving plasma exchange to every DAH, or starting ATT for cavitating nodules without checking ANCA and urine. [KDIGO 2024]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs used for DAH and pulmonary vasculitis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Methylprednisolone (pulse)`,`500-1000 mg IV daily`,`3 days, then oral prednisolone`,`Hyperglycaemia, infection, psychosis, hypertension`],[`Prednisolone (reduced-dose)`,`1 mg/kg/day oral, maximum 75 mg`,`Taper to about 5 mg by 6 months (PEXIVAS)`,`Diabetes, osteoporosis, infection - give bone and PJP prophylaxis`],[`Rituximab`,`375 mg/m2 IV weekly x 4, or 1 g IV days 1 and 15`,`Maintenance 500 mg every 6 months for 18-24 months`,`Infusion reactions, hypogammaglobulinaemia, HBV reactivation, PML (rare)`],[`Cyclophosphamide IV`,`15 mg/kg at weeks 0, 2, 4 then 3-weekly (max 1.2 g)`,`3-6 months; reduce for age and eGFR`,`Neutropenia (nadir day 10-14), haemorrhagic cystitis, infertility, bladder cancer; give mesna`],[`Avacopan`,`30 mg orally twice daily`,`Up to 12 months with induction`,`Hepatotoxicity - monitor LFT; costly`],[`Azathioprine`,`1.5-2 mg/kg/day orally`,`Maintenance 24-48 months`,`Cytopenia (TPMT deficiency), hepatitis, pancreatitis`],[`Cotrimoxazole (prophylaxis)`,`400/80 mg daily or 800/160 mg thrice weekly orally`,`During induction and 6 months after last rituximab`,`Rash, hyperkalaemia, marrow suppression`],[`Benzylpenicillin or ceftriaxone (leptospirosis)`,`1.5 MU IV 6-hourly, or ceftriaxone 1-2 g IV daily`,`7 days`,`Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction; allergy`]]},{heading:`Causes of DAH by histological pattern`,columns:[`Pattern`,`Causes`,`Clue`],rows:[[`Pulmonary capillaritis`,`GPA, MPA, anti-GBM, SLE, APS, drug-induced ANCA vasculitis, Behcet`,`Glomerulonephritis, ENT disease, ANCA or anti-GBM positive`],[`Bland haemorrhage`,`Coagulopathy, anticoagulants, thrombocytopenia, mitral stenosis, LV failure, IPH`,`Deranged coagulation, echo abnormal, no nephritis`],[`Diffuse alveolar damage`,`ARDS, HSCT, amiodarone, bleomycin, cocaine, radiation`,`Sepsis, transplant or drug history`],[`Infection-related`,`Leptospirosis, dengue, falciparum malaria, hantavirus, scrub typhus`,`Monsoon fever, thrombocytopenia, jaundice, AKI`]]},{heading:`GPA, MPA and anti-GBM disease compared`,columns:[`Feature`,`GPA`,`MPA`,`Anti-GBM disease`],rows:[[`Antibody`,`PR3-ANCA (c-ANCA) in most`,`MPO-ANCA (p-ANCA) in most`,`Anti-GBM IgG (alpha-3 NC1 of type IV collagen)`],[`Granulomas`,`Yes`,`No`,`No`],[`ENT disease`,`Sinusitis, saddle nose, subglottic stenosis`,`Rare`,`Absent`],[`Lung`,`Nodules, cavities, DAH`,`DAH, ILD (UIP pattern)`,`DAH, especially in smokers`],[`Kidney biopsy`,`Pauci-immune crescentic GN`,`Pauci-immune crescentic GN`,`Linear IgG along the GBM`],[`Plasma exchange`,`Selected only`,`Selected only`,`Standard`],[`Relapse`,`Common`,`Less common`,`Rare once controlled`]]},{heading:`Investigations and their interpretation`,columns:[`Test`,`Finding in DAH or vasculitis`,`Interpretation`],rows:[[`Haemoglobin serially`,`Fall of 1.5-2 g/dL or more without other loss`,`Supports alveolar bleeding`],[`Urine microscopy`,`Dysmorphic RBCs, red cell casts, proteinuria`,`Glomerulonephritis - pulmonary-renal syndrome`],[`PR3/MPO ELISA`,`Positive`,`AAV; negative ANCA does not exclude (about 10% negative)`],[`Anti-GBM antibody`,`Positive`,`Anti-GBM disease; about 30% also ANCA positive (double positive)`],[`BAL`,`Sequential aliquots bloodier; haemosiderin macrophages over 20%`,`Confirms DAH; excludes infection`],[`DLCO`,`Over 130% predicted or rise over 30%`,`Active alveolar bleeding`],[`HRCT`,`Diffuse ground glass, consolidation; nodules and cavities in GPA`,`Supports DAH; cavities favour GPA over MPA`]]}],redFlags:[`Haemoptysis with a falling haemoglobin and bilateral infiltrates`,`Haemoptysis with haematuria, red cell casts or rising creatinine - pulmonary-renal syndrome`,`Rising oxygen requirement or SpO2 under 92% in a patient with known vasculitis or SLE`,`Monsoon fever with jaundice, oliguria and haemoptysis - severe leptospirosis`,`Stridor in a patient with GPA - subglottic stenosis`,`Fever or new infiltrates in a patient on rituximab or cyclophosphamide - opportunistic infection`],pearls:[`DAH triad: haemoptysis, falling Hb, diffuse infiltrates - and haemoptysis is missing in one-third.`,`The only ILD-like picture with a high DLCO is alveolar haemorrhage.`,`Progressively bloodier BAL aliquots confirm DAH; haemosiderin-laden macrophages over 20% support it.`,`Always dip the urine in a patient with haemoptysis.`,`c-ANCA/PR3 = GPA (granulomas, nose, cavities); p-ANCA/MPO = MPA; linear IgG = anti-GBM.`,`Plasma exchange is standard in anti-GBM disease but not routine in ANCA DAH (PEXIVAS).`,`In India think leptospirosis first in a monsoon pulmonary-renal syndrome.`,`Infection, not vasculitis, kills most AAV patients in year one - give cotrimoxazole prophylaxis.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-alveolar-haemorrhage-vasculitis-q1`,stem:`A 45-year-old woman has 5 days of breathlessness, haemoglobin falling from 11 to 8 g/dL and bilateral ground-glass shadows. Which lung function finding most supports active alveolar haemorrhage?`,options:[`Reduced FEV1/FVC ratio`,`Reduced total lung capacity`,`Reduced DLCO`,`Raised DLCO`],answer:3,explanation:`Free haemoglobin in the alveoli binds inhaled carbon monoxide, so DLCO rises (often over 130% predicted) during active bleeding. A reduced DLCO is the expected finding in ILD, pneumonia and emphysema, not in DAH; a low FEV1/FVC indicates airway obstruction and a reduced TLC indicates restriction, neither of which is specific for haemorrhage.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-alveolar-haemorrhage-vasculitis-q2`,stem:`Which statement correctly summarises the PEXIVAS trial in severe ANCA-associated vasculitis?`,options:[`Plasma exchange did not reduce death or ESKD, and a reduced-dose steroid regimen was non-inferior to standard dosing`,`Plasma exchange reduced mortality in patients with diffuse alveolar haemorrhage`,`Reduced-dose steroids increased relapse and death compared with standard dosing`,`Rituximab was superior to cyclophosphamide for induction`],answer:0,explanation:`PEXIVAS randomised 704 patients in a factorial design: adding plasma exchange did not reduce death or ESKD (including in the DAH subgroup), and the reduced-dose glucocorticoid taper was non-inferior with fewer serious infections. It did not compare rituximab with cyclophosphamide (that was RAVE and RITUXVAS), and it showed no mortality benefit of plasma exchange in DAH.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-alveolar-haemorrhage-vasculitis-q3`,stem:`A 32-year-old farmer from coastal Karnataka presents in August with 5 days of fever, myalgia, conjunctival suffusion, jaundice, oliguria and haemoptysis with bilateral infiltrates. Platelets are 60,000/microlitre. What is the most appropriate immediate antimicrobial?`,options:[`Oral doxycycline 100 mg twice daily as an outpatient`,`Oral amoxicillin-clavulanate and review in 2 days`,`IV benzylpenicillin 1.5 million units 6-hourly or IV ceftriaxone`,`IV meropenem plus vancomycin`],answer:2,explanation:`This is severe leptospirosis (Weil disease) with pulmonary haemorrhage syndrome, which needs IV benzylpenicillin or ceftriaxone for 7 days with ICU support and early dialysis. Oral doxycycline is only for mild disease without organ involvement, oral amoxicillin-clavulanate delays effective treatment, and meropenem with vancomycin is broader than needed and not first choice for leptospira.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-alveolar-haemorrhage-vasculitis-q4`,stem:`A 24-year-old male smoker has haemoptysis, rising creatinine and red cell casts. Renal biopsy shows linear IgG along the glomerular basement membrane. Which treatment plan is correct?`,options:[`Rituximab alone without steroids`,`Daily plasma exchange plus pulse steroids and cyclophosphamide`,`Oral prednisolone alone and repeat biopsy in 4 weeks`,`Plasma exchange alone until antibodies disappear`],answer:1,explanation:`Linear IgG on biopsy defines anti-GBM disease, where daily plasma exchange removes the pathogenic antibody while pulse methylprednisolone and cyclophosphamide stop its production - KDIGO recommends all three. Plasma exchange alone allows antibody to return, steroids alone are inadequate, and rituximab alone without steroids is not standard therapy for this rapidly destructive disease.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-alveolar-haemorrhage-vasculitis-q5`,stem:`In the ACR/EULAR 2022 classification criteria for granulomatosis with polyangiitis, which single item carries the highest positive score?`,options:[`Pulmonary nodules, mass or cavitation on imaging`,`Granuloma on biopsy`,`Positive c-ANCA or PR3-ANCA`,`Nasal bloody discharge, ulcers or crusting`],answer:2,explanation:`Positive c-ANCA or PR3-ANCA scores +5, enough alone to reach the classification threshold of 5. Nasal bloody discharge or crusting scores +3, while lung nodules or cavitation and granuloma on biopsy each score +2. Eosinophilia of 1000/microlitre or more subtracts 4 because it points towards EGPA.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-alveolar-haemorrhage-vasculitis-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Define diffuse alveolar haemorrhage. Enumerate its causes and describe the approach to diagnosis and management of a patient with pulmonary-renal syndrome.`,openingLines:[`Diffuse alveolar haemorrhage is bleeding into the alveolar spaces from the pulmonary microcirculation, presenting with haemoptysis, a falling haemoglobin and diffuse alveolar infiltrates with hypoxaemia.`,`When it accompanies rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis it forms the pulmonary-renal syndrome, most often due to ANCA-associated vasculitis or anti-GBM disease, and in India also leptospirosis.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes by histology`,points:[`Capillaritis: GPA, MPA, anti-GBM, SLE, APS, drugs (PTU, hydralazine).`,`Bland: coagulopathy, anticoagulants, mitral stenosis, LV failure, idiopathic pulmonary haemosiderosis.`,`Diffuse alveolar damage: ARDS, HSCT, amiodarone, cocaine.`,`Infections: leptospirosis, dengue, malaria, hantavirus.`]},{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`ANCA-activated neutrophils destroy capillaries; anti-GBM antibody binds type IV collagen alpha-3 chain.`,`Shared target in alveolar and glomerular capillaries explains the pulmonary-renal syndrome.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Breathlessness, haemoptysis (absent in one-third), anaemia; ENT disease, purpura, mononeuritis, haematuria.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`CBC, coagulation, creatinine, urine microscopy for red cell casts.`,`PR3 and MPO ELISA, anti-GBM, ANA, dsDNA, complement; leptospira IgM, dengue, malaria.`,`HRCT, BAL with progressively bloodier aliquots, raised DLCO, renal biopsy.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Oxygen, ventilation with PEEP, correct coagulopathy, transfuse.`,`Pulse methylprednisolone 500-1000 mg x 3 days, then reduced-dose taper (PEXIVAS).`,`Rituximab 375 mg/m2 weekly x 4 or cyclophosphamide 15 mg/kg pulses.`,`Plasma exchange: standard in anti-GBM; in AAV only if creatinine over 3.4 mg/dL or as rescue.`,`Maintenance rituximab 500 mg 6-monthly; cotrimoxazole prophylaxis.`]},{heading:`Prognosis`,points:[`Infection is the main early killer; ESKD in 20-25%; relapse common in GPA.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: haemoptysis with anaemia and infiltrates to diagnosis and treatment`,`Table comparing GPA, MPA and anti-GBM disease`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and causes`,marks:2},{part:`Pathogenesis and clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations`,marks:3},{part:`Management`,marks:3}],keywords:[`DAH`,`pulmonary-renal syndrome`,`ANCA`,`anti-GBM`,`PEXIVAS`,`rituximab`]},{id:`respiratory-alveolar-haemorrhage-vasculitis-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Differentiate between granulomatosis with polyangiitis, microscopic polyangiitis and anti-GBM disease.`,openingLines:[`GPA and MPA are ANCA-associated necrotising small-vessel vasculitides; anti-GBM disease is an immune-complex small-vessel vasculitis caused by antibodies to type IV collagen.`,`All three cause pulmonary-renal syndrome, but antibody, granulomas, ENT disease and biopsy findings separate them.`],answer:[{heading:`Antibody and pathology`,points:[`GPA: PR3/c-ANCA, necrotising granulomas.`,`MPA: MPO/p-ANCA, no granulomas.`,`Anti-GBM: anti-alpha-3 type IV collagen IgG, linear IgG on biopsy.`]},{heading:`Clinical`,points:[`GPA: sinusitis, saddle nose, subglottic stenosis, cavitating nodules.`,`MPA: RPGN, DAH, ILD, mononeuritis.`,`Anti-GBM: young male smokers with DAH, or older women with nephritis alone.`]},{heading:`Treatment and outcome`,points:[`GPA and MPA: steroids plus rituximab or cyclophosphamide; relapse common in GPA.`,`Anti-GBM: plasma exchange plus steroids and cyclophosphamide; relapse rare.`]}],mustDraw:[`Three-column comparison table`],markSplit:[{part:`Antibody and pathology`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment`,marks:1}],keywords:[`GPA`,`MPA`,`anti-GBM`,`PR3`,`MPO`,`linear IgG`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Diffuse alveolar haemorrhage; The vasculitis syndromes`,`KDIGO 2024 Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of ANCA-Associated Vasculitis`,`EULAR recommendations for the management of ANCA-associated vasculitis: 2022 update`,`2022 ACR/EULAR classification criteria for granulomatosis with polyangiitis and microscopic polyangiitis`,`2012 Revised International Chapel Hill Consensus Conference Nomenclature of Vasculitides`,`Walsh M et al. Plasma exchange and glucocorticoids in severe ANCA-associated vasculitis (PEXIVAS), NEJM 2020`,`NCDC/MoHFW National Guidelines: Diagnosis, Case Management, Prevention and Control of Leptospirosis, 2015`]}),s.push({id:`respiratory-eosinophilic-lung`,title:`Eosinophilic lung disease: tropical pulmonary eosinophilia, Loeffler, eosinophilic pneumonias and EGPA`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`**Eosinophilic lung diseases** are disorders with **pulmonary infiltrates plus blood eosinophilia (over 500/microlitre) or BAL eosinophils over 25%**; in India the key one is **tropical pulmonary eosinophilia** (filarial hypersensitivity, AEC over 3000, very high IgE, cured by **DEC 6 mg/kg/day for 21 days**), alongside **Loeffler syndrome**, **acute and chronic eosinophilic pneumonia** (steroid-responsive), drug reactions and **EGPA** (asthma, eosinophilia and vasculitis, treated with steroids plus **mepolizumab** or cyclophosphamide/rituximab).`,keywords:[`pulmonary eosinophilia`,`PIE syndrome`,`tropical pulmonary eosinophilia`,`TPE`,`Weingarten syndrome`,`diethylcarbamazine`,`DEC`,`Loeffler syndrome`,`acute eosinophilic pneumonia`,`chronic eosinophilic pneumonia`,`Carrington disease`,`EGPA`,`Churg-Strauss syndrome`,`mepolizumab`,`benralizumab`,`hypereosinophilic syndrome`,`Strongyloides`,`absolute eosinophil count`,`DRESS`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Eosinophilia** is an absolute eosinophil count (AEC) **over 500/microlitre**; **hypereosinophilia** is **1500/microlitre or more** on two occasions at least a month apart or with tissue eosinophilia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Eosinophilic lung disease** requires lung infiltrates plus either **peripheral eosinophilia**, **BAL eosinophils over 25%** (normal under 2%), or eosinophilic infiltration on lung biopsy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Grouping**: known cause (parasites - TPE, Loeffler, Strongyloides; drugs; fungi - ABPA), unknown cause confined to the lung (**acute and chronic eosinophilic pneumonia**), and systemic disease (**EGPA, hypereosinophilic syndrome**). [Harrison 22e]`,`**India carries about 40% of the global lymphatic filariasis burden**, with endemic districts in states such as Bihar, Uttar Pradesh, Odisha, Jharkhand, Andhra Pradesh, Telangana, Kerala and Tamil Nadu - the setting for **tropical pulmonary eosinophilia**. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**TPE** develops in **under 1%** of people infected with filaria, mostly young adult men (male to female about 4:1) living in or visiting endemic areas. [Harrison 22e]`,`**EGPA** is the rarest ANCA-associated vasculitis, with an incidence of about **1-3 per million per year**, almost always in people with adult-onset asthma. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Chronic eosinophilic pneumonia** is commonest in **middle-aged women who do not smoke**, about half with asthma; **acute eosinophilic pneumonia** typically affects **young men who have just started smoking or vaping**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Filarial**: *Wuchereria bancrofti* (most of India) and *Brugia malayi* (Kerala and some eastern states) cause TPE when microfilariae are trapped and destroyed in the lungs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Larval lung migration (Loeffler syndrome)**: *Ascaris lumbricoides* (commonest), hookworm (*Ancylostoma, Necator*) and *Strongyloides stercoralis*; also *Toxocara* (visceral larva migrans) and *Paragonimus* in the North-East. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Strongyloides hyperinfection** is a risk in anyone from an endemic area given **corticosteroids**, HTLV-1 infection or transplant immunosuppression - it may present with eosinophilic infiltrates or, paradoxically, without eosinophilia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drugs**: **nitrofurantoin, NSAIDs, sulfonamides and cotrimoxazole, daptomycin, minocycline, phenytoin, carbamazepine, ACE inhibitors, mesalazine**, and drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms (**DRESS**). [Pneumotox 2024]`,`**Smoke and inhalants**: new-onset cigarette smoking, **vaping**, crack cocaine, heavy dust (World Trade Center workers) trigger **acute eosinophilic pneumonia**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fungal**: **ABPA** from *Aspergillus fumigatus* is common in Indian asthma clinics and is covered in its own topic. [ISHAM-AWG 2024]`,`**Systemic**: EGPA, **hypereosinophilic syndrome** (including the FIP1L1-PDGFRA myeloid neoplasm), lymphoma and other malignancy, and rarely sarcoidosis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why eosinophils damage the lung`,points:[`**Eosinophils** are produced under **IL-5**, recruited by **eotaxins (CCL11)**, and release **major basic protein, eosinophil cationic protein, eosinophil peroxidase and eosinophil-derived neurotoxin**, which injure epithelium, endothelium, myocardium and nerves. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TPE mechanism**: microfilariae are cleared from blood and trapped in the pulmonary capillaries, where an **exaggerated Th2 response** (very high IgE, antifilarial IgG4 and IgE) destroys them - so **blood is negative for microfilariae** while the lung is inflamed. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why TPE is nocturnal**: *W. bancrofti* microfilariae are **nocturnally periodic**, peaking in blood around midnight, and cough and wheeze in TPE are classically worse at night. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Untreated TPE progresses** from acute eosinophilic alveolitis to histiocytic infiltration and finally **interstitial fibrosis**, so late treatment leaves a restrictive defect - the reason for early DEC. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Loeffler syndrome** is a transient hypersensitivity to larvae passing through the alveoli **10-14 days after ingesting Ascaris eggs**; ova appear in stool only **6-8 weeks** later, which is why early stool tests are negative. [Harrison 22e]`,`**EGPA** has two linked arms: **eosinophilic tissue infiltration** (lung, heart, gut) driven by IL-5, and **ANCA-mediated small-vessel vasculitis** (neuropathy, glomerulonephritis, purpura) - **MPO-ANCA is positive in only about 30-40%**. [EULAR 2022]`,`**ANCA-positive EGPA** tends to have vasculitic features (neuropathy, renal disease, purpura), while **ANCA-negative EGPA** has more **cardiac and lung** involvement - the heart is the main cause of death. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Anti-IL-5 therapy** (mepolizumab, benralizumab) works because IL-5 is the key survival signal for eosinophils - it controls the eosinophilic arm and asthma in EGPA. [MIRRA NEJM 2017]`]},{heading:`Classification and diagnostic criteria`,points:[`**TPE diagnostic criteria**: residence in a filarial area, **paroxysmal nocturnal cough and wheeze**, diffuse infiltrates, **AEC over 3000/microlitre**, **total IgE over 1000 IU/mL**, high antifilarial antibodies, no microfilariae in blood, and **rapid response to DEC**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute eosinophilic pneumonia (Philit criteria)**: febrile illness **under 1 month**, bilateral infiltrates, **hypoxaemia (PaO2 under 60 mmHg or SpO2 under 90%)**, **BAL eosinophils over 25%**, and no infection or other cause. [Philit 2002]`,`**Chronic eosinophilic pneumonia**: symptoms over **2-4 weeks**, peripheral infiltrates, **blood eosinophils over 1000/microlitre and/or BAL eosinophils over 40%**, and no other cause. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ACR/EULAR 2022 EGPA classification** (in diagnosed small- or medium-vessel vasculitis): obstructive airway disease **+3**, nasal polyps **+3**, mononeuritis multiplex +1, **eosinophils 1000/microlitre or more +5**, extravascular eosinophilic inflammation on biopsy +2, c-ANCA or PR3-ANCA -3, haematuria -1; **score 6 or more = EGPA**. [ACR/EULAR 2022]`,`**EGPA phases (Lanham)**: prodromal (asthma, rhinitis, nasal polyps), eosinophilic (blood and tissue eosinophilia, infiltrates), and vasculitic (neuropathy, purpura, organ damage) - years may separate them. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Revised Five-Factor Score (FFS 2011)** for EGPA prognosis: **age over 65, cardiac insufficiency, GI involvement, renal insufficiency (creatinine 150 micromol/L or more)** each +1, and **absence of ENT disease** +1; FFS 1 or more means severe disease. [FVSG 2011]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**TPE**: weeks to months of **dry, paroxysmal cough worse at night**, wheeze and breathlessness, low-grade fever, weight loss and malaise in a young adult from an endemic district - often treated for months as **asthma or TB**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Loeffler syndrome**: mild dry cough, low fever and wheeze lasting **days to 2 weeks**, often unnoticed, sometimes with urticaria; recent travel, farm or barefoot exposure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute eosinophilic pneumonia**: fever, cough, pleuritic pain and rapidly **worsening breathlessness over 1-7 days**, often within weeks of **starting smoking or vaping**; may need ventilation within days and mimics ARDS or severe CAP. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chronic eosinophilic pneumonia**: weeks to months of cough, breathlessness, fever, night sweats and **weight loss** in a middle-aged woman, half with asthma - mistaken for TB or pneumonia that does not respond to antibiotics. [Harrison 22e]`,`**EGPA**: **adult-onset, steroid-dependent asthma** with nasal polyps and sinusitis, then fever, weight loss, **numbness or foot drop** (mononeuritis), rash, abdominal pain, chest pain or palpitations (myocarditis). [EULAR 2022]`,`**Drug-induced**: new drug started days to weeks (nitrofurantoin, NSAIDs, antiepileptics), with rash, fever, lymphadenopathy and hepatitis in DRESS. [Pneumotox 2024]`,`**Always ask about leukotriene antagonists or steroid tapering** in EGPA: montelukast and steroid withdrawal can unmask, not cause, EGPA. [EULAR 2022]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Wheeze and scattered crackles** with few other signs in TPE, Loeffler and chronic eosinophilic pneumonia; examination may be normal. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hepatosplenomegaly and lymphadenopathy** can accompany TPE; look for **lymphoedema or hydrocele** as a clue to filarial exposure (although TPE patients usually have neither). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tachypnoea, hypoxaemia and bilateral crackles** in acute eosinophilic pneumonia, resembling ARDS. [Harrison 22e]`,`**EGPA signs**: nasal polyps, **palpable purpura or subcutaneous nodules**, mononeuritis multiplex (foot or wrist drop, glove-and-stocking loss), tachycardia, new murmur, pericardial rub or heart failure. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Strongyloides clues**: larva currens (a fast-moving serpiginous urticarial rash on buttocks and thighs), abdominal pain and diarrhoea; in hyperinfection, gram-negative sepsis and meningitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**DRESS**: facial oedema, widespread morbilliform rash, fever, lymphadenopathy and hepatomegaly. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Bronchial asthma**: variable obstruction but AEC rarely over 1500 and no infiltrates; TPE has a **much higher AEC (over 3000)** and IgE, and responds to DEC. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ABPA**: asthma with **central bronchiectasis, mucus plugs** (high-attenuation mucus), Aspergillus-specific IgE and total IgE over 500 IU/mL. [ISHAM-AWG 2024]`,`**Miliary or pulmonary TB**: fever and miliary mottling, but eosinophilia is not a feature; TPE mottling clears with DEC. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bacterial pneumonia and ARDS**: mimic acute eosinophilic pneumonia, but BAL has neutrophils not eosinophils, and procalcitonin is usually high. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cryptogenic organising pneumonia**: also migratory peripheral consolidation responding to steroids, but BAL is lymphocytic and blood eosinophils are normal. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypereosinophilic syndrome**: AEC over 1500 with organ damage, but no asthma and no vasculitis; test for **FIP1L1-PDGFRA** (imatinib-responsive). [WHO 2022]`,`**GPA and MPA**: no asthma or eosinophilia; eosinophils of 1000 or more score against them in ACR/EULAR 2022. [ACR/EULAR 2022]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line, then advanced`,points:[`**CBC with absolute eosinophil count** (not just percentage) - TPE over 3000/microlitre, CEP usually over 1000, AEP often **normal at presentation and rising later**, EGPA usually over 1500. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Stool examination for ova and larvae** on 3 samples, plus **Strongyloides serology** where available; remember Loeffler ova appear only 6-8 weeks after infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Total IgE** (TPE often over 1000 IU/mL; ABPA over 500), **filarial antigen test (ICT, Og4C3)** and antifilarial IgG/IgG4 where available - microfilariae on night smear are usually **absent** in TPE. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chest X-ray**: TPE shows bilateral **reticulonodular or miliary mottling** in mid and lower zones; Loeffler shows **transient migratory** infiltrates; CEP shows **peripheral, upper-zone consolidation - the photographic negative of pulmonary oedema**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HRCT**: AEP shows ground-glass, septal thickening and **bilateral small pleural effusions**; CEP shows peripheral consolidation; EGPA shows patchy ground-glass, consolidation and bronchial wall thickening. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PFT**: TPE gives a **mixed restrictive and obstructive** pattern with reduced DLCO; EGPA and CEP with asthma show obstruction. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bronchoscopy with BAL**: **BAL eosinophils over 25%** confirm eosinophilic pneumonia (often over 40% in CEP and about 50% in TPE) and BAL also excludes infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**EGPA work-up**: **MPO-ANCA**, urine microscopy, creatinine, **troponin, NT-proBNP, ECG and echocardiography (cardiac MRI if available)**, nerve conduction study and biopsy of the most accessible affected tissue. [EULAR 2022]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`**TPE**: **diethylcarbamazine (DEC) 6 mg/kg/day orally in 3 divided doses for 21 days** - symptoms improve within 7-10 days and AEC falls; repeat the course for relapse (about 12-20%). [WHO 2023]`,`**DEC side effects**: fever, headache, nausea, myalgia and itching from **dying microfilariae (Mazzotti-like reaction)**; avoid in pregnancy and in **onchocerciasis or loiasis** (not seen in India) where it causes severe reactions. [WHO 2023]`,`**Persistent TPE after two DEC courses**: add **prednisolone 0.5-1 mg/kg/day for 2-4 weeks then taper** to prevent fibrosis; refer if PFT or HRCT shows fibrosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Loeffler syndrome**: usually self-limiting; treat the worm once identified with **albendazole 400 mg orally single dose** (Ascaris, hookworm), and avoid steroids unless severe. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Strongyloides**: **ivermectin 200 microgram/kg orally once daily for 1-2 days**, and give it empirically before high-dose steroids in a patient from an endemic area with eosinophilia. [WHO 2023]`,`**Drug-induced eosinophilic pneumonia**: **stop the drug** - most resolve in days to weeks; give prednisolone for hypoxaemia or slow recovery, and record the allergy. [Pneumotox 2024]`,`**Chronic eosinophilic pneumonia**: **prednisolone 0.5 mg/kg/day for 2 weeks**, then taper slowly over **6 months**; dramatic response within 48 hours is almost diagnostic; inhaled steroids help with asthma and lower relapse. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency`,points:[`**Acute eosinophilic pneumonia with respiratory failure**: oxygen, NIV or ventilation, and **methylprednisolone 60-125 mg IV 6-hourly** until improvement, then **prednisolone 40-60 mg/day tapered over 2 weeks**; relapse is rare and stopping smoking is essential. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Empirical antibiotics** (as for severe CAP) until BAL excludes infection in acute eosinophilic pneumonia; do not delay steroids once BAL eosinophilia is shown. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Severe EGPA** (FFS 1 or more, cardiac, renal, CNS, gut or mononeuritis): **pulse methylprednisolone 500-1000 mg IV for 3 days**, then prednisolone, plus **cyclophosphamide** (15 mg/kg IV pulses) **or rituximab**. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Non-severe EGPA** (asthma, sinus, skin, mild infiltrates): **prednisolone 0.5-1 mg/kg/day** tapered, with **mepolizumab 300 mg SC every 4 weeks** for relapsing or refractory disease or to spare steroids. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Benralizumab 30 mg SC every 4 weeks** was non-inferior to mepolizumab in relapsing-refractory EGPA (MANDARA) and is an alternative anti-IL-5 option. [MANDARA NEJM 2024]`,`**Hypereosinophilic syndrome with organ damage** (AEC over 1500, cardiac or neurological involvement): high-dose steroids urgently; **imatinib** for FIP1L1-PDGFRA positive disease; refer to haematology. [WHO 2022]`,`**Strongyloides hyperinfection**: **ivermectin 200 microgram/kg daily until stool and sputum are negative for 2 weeks**, reduce immunosuppression, and cover gram-negative sepsis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: **DEC is avoided** in pregnancy - defer to after delivery if mild, or use steroids for severe TPE; albendazole and ivermectin are avoided in the first trimester. [WHO 2023]`,`**Children**: Loeffler syndrome from Ascaris and **Toxocara** (pica, puppies) is commonest; DEC dose for TPE is the same 6 mg/kg/day; deworm under the national programme. [Nelson 22e]`,`**Before steroids or biologics in India**: screen for and treat **Strongyloides** (or give empirical ivermectin) and exclude TB in anyone starting long-term immunosuppression. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Elderly**: drug-induced eosinophilic pneumonia (nitrofurantoin for recurrent UTI, NSAIDs) is the commonest cause - review every new medication. [Pneumotox 2024]`,`**Asthmatic with rising eosinophils on steroid taper or biologic switch**: think of unmasked **EGPA** and look for neuropathy, purpura and cardiac involvement. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: TPE is a clinical-therapeutic diagnosis - **AEC, chest X-ray and a DEC trial** are enough in an endemic district when stool is clear. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**TPE**: untreated or late-treated disease leads to **interstitial fibrosis and chronic restrictive lung disease** in up to 20%; with early DEC, most recover fully. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute eosinophilic pneumonia**: can cause ARDS needing ventilation, but with steroids **mortality is low and relapse is rare** if the trigger stops. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chronic eosinophilic pneumonia**: relapses in **about half** during or after steroid taper, usually responding to retreatment; long-term low-dose steroid or inhaled steroid may be needed. [Harrison 22e]`,`**EGPA**: **cardiac involvement (myocarditis, cardiomyopathy, pericarditis)** causes about half of deaths; other complications are permanent neuropathy, renal failure and gut perforation; 5-year survival is about 90% with treatment. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Steroid toxicity** from long courses in CEP and EGPA: diabetes, osteoporosis, cataract and infection - a key reason for anti-IL-5 steroid-sparing therapy. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Hypereosinophilic syndrome**: endomyocardial fibrosis (Loeffler endocarditis), thromboembolism and neuropathy. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer** any patient with eosinophilia plus **hypoxaemia, neuropathy, cardiac symptoms, haematuria or AEC over 5000/microlitre** to a pulmonologist, rheumatologist or haematologist. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TPE follow-up**: repeat AEC and chest X-ray at the end of DEC and at **3 months**; persistent AEC or symptoms means relapse or another diagnosis - repeat DEC and do PFT. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CEP follow-up**: monthly AEC and chest X-ray during the taper, as eosinophilia often rises before symptoms return. [Harrison 22e]`,`**EGPA follow-up**: every 1-3 months in the first year with **AEC, creatinine, urine dipstick, ANCA where positive, and cardiac assessment (ECG, NT-proBNP, echo)**; spirometry and asthma control at each visit. [EULAR 2022]`,`**On mepolizumab**: AEC falls within weeks; give for at least 1 year; monitor for herpes zoster and keep inhaled asthma therapy going. [EULAR 2022]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and national programme link`,points:[`**Elimination of lymphatic filariasis** under the **National Center for Vector Borne Diseases Control (NCVBDC)** uses **mass drug administration** with DEC plus albendazole (DA) or **ivermectin plus DEC plus albendazole (IDA)** in endemic districts, with India targeting elimination by **2027**. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Mosquito control** (Culex for bancroftian filaria, Mansonia for Brugia) - nets, repellents, removal of stagnant water and drain cleaning. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Deworming**: the **National Deworming Day** gives albendazole 400 mg (200 mg at 1-2 years) twice a year to children 1-19 years; hand washing, footwear and safe sanitation prevent Ascaris and hookworm. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**Smoking and vaping**: counsel young adults that new smoking or vaping can cause life-threatening acute eosinophilic pneumonia, and that recurrence follows restarting. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drug allergy record**: write the culprit drug prominently in the patient's record and card after drug-induced eosinophilic pneumonia or DRESS. [Pneumotox 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**ACR/EULAR 2022 EGPA classification criteria** replaced the ACR 1990 six-item criteria, giving most weight to **eosinophils 1000/microlitre or more (+5)** and asthma or nasal polyps (+3 each). [ACR/EULAR 2022]`,`**EULAR 2022 AAV recommendations**: mepolizumab for relapsing or refractory non-severe EGPA; cyclophosphamide or rituximab with steroids for organ-threatening EGPA. [EULAR 2022]`,`**MANDARA (NEJM 2024)**: benralizumab 30 mg every 4 weeks was non-inferior to mepolizumab for remission in EGPA and allowed more patients to stop oral steroids; it has since been approved for EGPA. [MANDARA NEJM 2024]`,`**India lymphatic filariasis elimination**: expansion of **triple-drug IDA MDA** and a target of elimination by 2027 under NCVBDC. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Guideline difference**: ACR 2021 suggests mepolizumab for non-severe EGPA as well as refractory disease, while EULAR 2022 positions it mainly for relapsing or refractory disease. [ACR 2021]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**TPE one-liner**: young man from endemic area, nocturnal cough and wheeze, **AEC over 3000, IgE over 1000**, mottling on X-ray, **no microfilariae in blood**, DEC 6 mg/kg/day for 3 weeks. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CEP one-liner**: middle-aged non-smoking woman with asthma, peripheral infiltrates - **photographic negative of pulmonary oedema** - and dramatic steroid response. [Harrison 22e]`,`**AEP one-liner**: young new smoker with ARDS-like illness, **BAL eosinophils over 25% with normal blood eosinophils**, rapid steroid response, rarely relapses. [Harrison 22e]`,`**EGPA one-liner**: adult-onset asthma, nasal polyps, eosinophilia, mononeuritis; **MPO-ANCA in only 30-40%**; the heart kills. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Common mistake**: starting high-dose steroids in eosinophilia without excluding **Strongyloides**, or treating TPE as asthma for years. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mnemonic for causes (PLATE-D)**: Parasites (TPE, Loeffler, Strongyloides), Lymphoma and HES, ABPA, Trouble-makers like smoke (AEP), EGPA, Drugs.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for eosinophilic lung disease`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Diethylcarbamazine (DEC)`,`6 mg/kg/day orally in 3 divided doses`,`21 days; repeat for relapse`,`Fever, headache, myalgia from dying microfilariae; avoid in pregnancy, onchocerciasis, loiasis`],[`Albendazole`,`400 mg orally single dose`,`Repeat in 2 weeks if needed`,`Abdominal pain; avoid in first trimester`],[`Ivermectin`,`200 microgram/kg orally daily`,`1-2 days; until negative in hyperinfection`,`Avoid in pregnancy and in Loa loa co-infection`],[`Prednisolone (CEP)`,`0.5 mg/kg/day orally`,`2 weeks, then taper over 6 months`,`Diabetes, osteoporosis, infection; relapse on taper`],[`Methylprednisolone (AEP)`,`60-125 mg IV 6-hourly`,`Until improved, then prednisolone 40-60 mg tapered over 2 weeks`,`Hyperglycaemia, infection`],[`Mepolizumab`,`300 mg SC every 4 weeks (EGPA)`,`At least 12 months`,`Injection reaction, headache, herpes zoster`],[`Benralizumab`,`30 mg SC every 4 weeks (EGPA)`,`At least 12 months`,`Headache, pharyngitis; costly`],[`Cyclophosphamide`,`15 mg/kg IV at weeks 0, 2, 4 then 3-weekly`,`3-6 months (severe EGPA)`,`Cytopenia, haemorrhagic cystitis, infertility`]]},{heading:`Eosinophilic lung syndromes compared`,columns:[`Syndrome`,`Typical patient`,`Blood AEC`,`Imaging`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`Tropical pulmonary eosinophilia`,`Young man, filarial area, nocturnal cough`,`Over 3000; IgE over 1000`,`Reticulonodular or miliary mottling`,`DEC 6 mg/kg/day x 21 days`],[`Loeffler syndrome`,`Any age, recent worm exposure`,`Mild to moderate`,`Transient migratory infiltrates`,`Self-limiting; albendazole`],[`Acute eosinophilic pneumonia`,`Young new smoker or vaper`,`Often normal at first`,`Bilateral GGO, septal lines, small effusions`,`IV steroids, stop smoking`],[`Chronic eosinophilic pneumonia`,`Middle-aged non-smoking woman with asthma`,`Over 1000`,`Peripheral upper-zone consolidation`,`Prednisolone taper over 6 months`],[`EGPA`,`Adult-onset asthma, nasal polyps`,`Over 1500 usually`,`Patchy migratory infiltrates`,`Steroids + mepolizumab or CYC/RTX`],[`Drug-induced`,`New drug (nitrofurantoin, NSAIDs)`,`Variable`,`Variable`,`Stop drug, steroids if severe`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of pulmonary infiltrates with eosinophilia`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating feature`,`Key test`],rows:[[`TPE`,`AEC over 3000, IgE over 1000, nocturnal symptoms`,`Filarial antibody/antigen; response to DEC`],[`ABPA`,`Central bronchiectasis, mucus plugs`,`Aspergillus-specific IgE, total IgE over 500`],[`Asthma alone`,`No infiltrates, AEC rarely over 1500`,`Spirometry with reversibility`],[`Strongyloides`,`Larva currens, steroid exposure`,`Stool larvae, serology`],[`Hypereosinophilic syndrome`,`AEC over 1500 with organ damage, no asthma`,`FIP1L1-PDGFRA, bone marrow`],[`Organising pneumonia`,`Migratory consolidation, no eosinophilia`,`BAL lymphocytosis, biopsy`]]},{heading:`ACR/EULAR 2022 EGPA classification criteria`,columns:[`Item`,`Score`],rows:[[`Obstructive airway disease`,`+3`],[`Nasal polyps`,`+3`],[`Mononeuritis multiplex`,`+1`],[`Blood eosinophils 1000/microlitre or more`,`+5`],[`Extravascular eosinophilic-predominant inflammation on biopsy`,`+2`],[`c-ANCA or PR3-ANCA positive`,`-3`],[`Haematuria`,`-1`],[`Classify as EGPA`,`Total 6 or more`]]}],redFlags:[`Acute febrile illness with hypoxaemia and bilateral infiltrates in a young new smoker - acute eosinophilic pneumonia`,`Asthma with eosinophilia plus foot drop, purpura or haematuria - EGPA vasculitic phase`,`Eosinophilia with chest pain, palpitations, heart failure or raised troponin - eosinophilic myocarditis`,`AEC over 5000/microlitre or eosinophilia with neurological signs - hypereosinophilic syndrome`,`Patient on steroids from an endemic area with sepsis, rash or GI symptoms - Strongyloides hyperinfection`,`Fever, rash, facial oedema and hepatitis after a new drug - DRESS`],pearls:[`TPE: AEC over 3000, IgE over 1000, blood negative for microfilariae, DEC 6 mg/kg/day for 21 days.`,`Chronic eosinophilic pneumonia is the photographic negative of pulmonary oedema.`,`Acute eosinophilic pneumonia: BAL eosinophils over 25% while blood eosinophils are normal.`,`Loeffler: stool is negative early because ova appear only 6-8 weeks after infection.`,`EGPA: asthma, eosinophilia, mononeuritis - MPO-ANCA in only one-third; the heart kills.`,`Give ivermectin before steroids to an Indian patient with unexplained eosinophilia.`,`Mepolizumab 300 mg every 4 weeks in EGPA - three times the asthma dose.`,`Five-Factor Score: age over 65, heart, gut, kidney, and no ENT disease.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-eosinophilic-lung-q1`,stem:`A 24-year-old man from coastal Odisha has 3 months of nocturnal paroxysmal cough and wheeze, AEC 6800/microlitre, IgE 3200 IU/mL, diffuse reticulonodular shadows and a negative night blood smear. What is the treatment of choice?`,options:[`Inhaled budesonide-formoterol and review in 3 months`,`Diethylcarbamazine 6 mg/kg/day in divided doses for 21 days`,`Four-drug antitubercular treatment`,`Single-dose albendazole 400 mg`],answer:1,explanation:`This is tropical pulmonary eosinophilia: endemic area, nocturnal symptoms, AEC over 3000, IgE over 1000 and no circulating microfilariae. DEC 6 mg/kg/day for 3 weeks is curative and prevents fibrosis. Treating as asthma or TB delays cure, and single-dose albendazole treats intestinal worms, not the filarial hypersensitivity.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-eosinophilic-lung-q2`,stem:`A 20-year-old student who started smoking 3 weeks ago develops fever and severe breathlessness over 4 days, SpO2 84% on air and bilateral ground-glass shadows with small effusions. Blood eosinophils are normal. BAL shows 38% eosinophils and cultures are negative. What is the diagnosis?`,options:[`Chronic eosinophilic pneumonia`,`Loeffler syndrome`,`Severe bacterial community-acquired pneumonia`,`Acute eosinophilic pneumonia`],answer:3,explanation:`Acute eosinophilic pneumonia fits: illness under 1 month, hypoxaemic respiratory failure, bilateral infiltrates, BAL eosinophils over 25% and a new smoking habit; blood eosinophils are often normal at presentation. Chronic eosinophilic pneumonia is subacute with high blood eosinophils, Loeffler syndrome is mild and transient, and bacterial pneumonia gives neutrophilic BAL with positive cultures.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-eosinophilic-lung-q3`,stem:`A 50-year-old non-smoking woman with asthma has 2 months of cough, weight loss and night sweats. Chest X-ray shows bilateral peripheral upper-zone consolidation sparing the hila. AEC is 2400/microlitre. What is the most likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Chronic eosinophilic pneumonia`,`Pulmonary tuberculosis`,`Acute eosinophilic pneumonia`,`Cardiogenic pulmonary oedema`],answer:0,explanation:`Peripheral consolidation sparing the central lung - the photographic negative of pulmonary oedema - in a middle-aged non-smoking woman with asthma and blood eosinophilia is classic chronic eosinophilic pneumonia, which responds within 48 hours to prednisolone. TB does not cause marked eosinophilia, acute eosinophilic pneumonia runs a course under 1 month with normal blood eosinophils at first, and pulmonary oedema is central and perihilar.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-eosinophilic-lung-q4`,stem:`In the ACR/EULAR 2022 classification criteria for EGPA, which item carries the highest score?`,options:[`Nasal polyps`,`Obstructive airway disease`,`Blood eosinophil count of 1000/microlitre or more`,`Mononeuritis multiplex`],answer:2,explanation:`A blood eosinophil count of 1000/microlitre or more scores +5. Obstructive airway disease and nasal polyps score +3 each, extravascular eosinophilic inflammation on biopsy scores +2, and mononeuritis multiplex scores only +1. A positive c-ANCA/PR3 subtracts 3 and haematuria subtracts 1; a total of 6 or more classifies EGPA.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`respiratory-eosinophilic-lung-q5`,stem:`A 60-year-old farmer from Tamil Nadu with eosinophilia and new lung infiltrates is about to start high-dose prednisolone for suspected chronic eosinophilic pneumonia. Stool examination has not been done. What should be given first?`,options:[`Diethylcarbamazine for 21 days`,`Prophylactic fluconazole`,`Isoniazid preventive therapy`,`Empirical ivermectin 200 microgram/kg`],answer:3,explanation:`Strongyloides is endemic in rural India and can cause fatal hyperinfection once high-dose steroids are started, so empirical ivermectin (or stool and serology testing) comes first. DEC is for filarial TPE, not a routine pre-steroid measure; fluconazole prophylaxis has no role here; and TB preventive therapy is decided by TB infection testing, not routinely before a short steroid course.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-eosinophilic-lung-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Classify pulmonary eosinophilia. Discuss the aetiopathogenesis, clinical features, diagnosis and management of tropical pulmonary eosinophilia.`,openingLines:[`Pulmonary eosinophilia means lung infiltrates with blood eosinophilia over 500/microlitre or BAL eosinophils over 25%.`,`Tropical pulmonary eosinophilia is a hypersensitivity reaction to filarial microfilariae trapped in the lung, seen in endemic India, and is cured by diethylcarbamazine.`],answer:[{heading:`Classification`,points:[`Known cause: parasites (TPE, Loeffler, Strongyloides), drugs, fungi (ABPA).`,`Unknown cause, lung only: acute and chronic eosinophilic pneumonia.`,`Systemic: EGPA, hypereosinophilic syndrome.`]},{heading:`Aetiopathogenesis of TPE`,points:[`W. bancrofti or B. malayi microfilariae trapped in pulmonary capillaries.`,`Exaggerated Th2 response: very high IgE, antifilarial IgG4; blood clear of microfilariae.`,`Untreated: alveolitis progresses to interstitial fibrosis.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Young adult male from endemic area; paroxysmal nocturnal cough, wheeze, fever, weight loss.`,`Wheeze, crackles, sometimes hepatosplenomegaly and lymphadenopathy.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`AEC over 3000, IgE over 1000 IU/mL, filarial antibodies; night smear negative.`,`X-ray reticulonodular or miliary mottling; PFT mixed with low DLCO; BAL eosinophils about 50%.`,`Exclude stool parasites, TB, ABPA; response to DEC confirms.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`DEC 6 mg/kg/day in 3 divided doses for 21 days.`,`Relapse 12-20%: repeat DEC; persistent disease: add prednisolone.`,`Follow-up AEC and X-ray at 3 months; PFT for fibrosis.`]},{heading:`Prevention`,points:[`Elimination of LF under NCVBDC: MDA with DA or IDA; vector control; target 2027.`]}],mustDraw:[`Classification chart of pulmonary eosinophilia`,`Flow chart for evaluating infiltrates with eosinophilia`],markSplit:[{part:`Classification`,marks:2},{part:`Pathogenesis and clinical features`,marks:3},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Management and prevention`,marks:3}],keywords:[`TPE`,`DEC`,`filaria`,`IgE`,`AEC`,`pulmonary eosinophilia`]},{id:`respiratory-eosinophilic-lung-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (EGPA).`,openingLines:[`EGPA (Churg-Strauss syndrome) is an eosinophil-rich necrotising granulomatous small-vessel vasculitis occurring in patients with asthma and eosinophilia.`,`It is an ANCA-associated vasculitis, but MPO-ANCA is positive in only 30-40%.`],answer:[{heading:`Phases and features`,points:[`Prodromal: adult-onset asthma, rhinitis, nasal polyps.`,`Eosinophilic: eosinophilia, migratory infiltrates, gut and heart infiltration.`,`Vasculitic: mononeuritis multiplex, purpura, glomerulonephritis.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`ACR/EULAR 2022: eosinophils 1000 or more +5, asthma +3, polyps +3; 6 or more classifies.`,`MPO-ANCA, urine, creatinine, troponin, NT-proBNP, echo, nerve conduction, biopsy.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Severe (FFS 1 or more): pulse steroids plus cyclophosphamide or rituximab.`,`Non-severe or relapsing: steroids plus mepolizumab 300 mg SC 4-weekly; benralizumab alternative.`]},{heading:`Prognosis`,points:[`Cardiac involvement causes about half of deaths; 5-year survival about 90%.`]}],mustDraw:[`Three phases of EGPA as a timeline`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and phases`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:1},{part:`Treatment and prognosis`,marks:2}],keywords:[`EGPA`,`Churg-Strauss`,`mepolizumab`,`Five-Factor Score`,`MPO-ANCA`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Eosinophilic pneumonias; Filarial infections`,`2022 ACR/EULAR classification criteria for eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis`,`EULAR recommendations for the management of ANCA-associated vasculitis: 2022 update`,`Wechsler ME et al. Benralizumab versus mepolizumab for EGPA (MANDARA), NEJM 2024`,`WHO Model Formulary and guidance on lymphatic filariasis treatment, 2023`,`NCVBDC (MoHFW) National Programme for Elimination of Lymphatic Filariasis guidelines, 2024`,`Pneumotox online: the drug-induced respiratory disease website, 2024`]}),s.push({id:`respiratory-opportunistic-lung`,title:`Lung infections in the immunocompromised: PCP, CMV, nocardia and fungal`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`**Pulmonary infections in the immunocompromised host** are predicted by the **type of immune defect** - neutropenia (gram-negatives, Aspergillus, Mucorales), **T-cell defects** (HIV with low CD4, transplant, steroids: **Pneumocystis, CMV, TB, Nocardia, Cryptococcus, Histoplasma**) and humoral or splenic defects (encapsulated bacteria); **Pneumocystis pneumonia** is treated with **cotrimoxazole 15-20 mg/kg/day of trimethoprim for 21 days**, with **prednisolone if PaO2 is under 70 mmHg**, and prevented by cotrimoxazole prophylaxis.`,keywords:[`immunocompromised host pneumonia`,`Pneumocystis jirovecii`,`PCP`,`PJP`,`cotrimoxazole`,`trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole`,`CMV pneumonitis`,`ganciclovir`,`letermovir`,`nocardiosis`,`cryptococcosis`,`histoplasmosis`,`talaromycosis`,`beta-D-glucan`,`galactomannan`,`CD4 count`,`solid organ transplant`,`febrile neutropenia`,`opportunistic infection`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Immunocompromised host**: anyone whose innate or adaptive immunity is impaired by disease (HIV, haematological cancer, primary immunodeficiency) or treatment (chemotherapy, transplant drugs, steroids, biologics) - the lung is the commonest site of serious infection in this group. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Opportunistic infection** means infection with an organism of low virulence that rarely harms a healthy host, such as *Pneumocystis jirovecii*, CMV, *Nocardia* and *Cryptococcus*. [Harrison 22e]`,`**India has about 25 lakh people living with HIV** (India HIV Estimates 2023), and **tuberculosis remains the commonest opportunistic lung infection and cause of death** among them, followed by bacterial pneumonia and PJP. [NACO 2023]`,`**PJP in HIV** occurs mainly at **CD4 under 200 cells/microlitre**; in India it is diagnosed less often than in the West because of limited BAL access, but series show it in about 5-15% of HIV pneumonias in hospital. [NACO 2021]`,`**Non-HIV PJP is rising**: transplant recipients, haematological malignancy, rheumatic disease on steroids and rituximab now account for most PJP in high-income countries, with **mortality of 30-60%**, much higher than in HIV (10-20%). [ECIL 2016]`,`**India performs over 15,000 solid organ transplants a year** (mostly living-donor kidney and liver), and with rising chemotherapy, biologic and steroid use the primary care physician increasingly sees these patients first. [NOTTO 2023]`]},{heading:`Aetiology - organisms predicted by the immune defect`,points:[`**Neutropenia (ANC under 500/microlitre)** and neutrophil dysfunction: *Pseudomonas*, Enterobacterales, *S. aureus*, and after 7-10 days **Aspergillus, Mucorales** and *Candida*. [IDSA 2023]`,`**T-cell (cell-mediated) defect** - HIV, transplant, high-dose steroids, purine analogues, alemtuzumab: **Pneumocystis, CMV, TB and NTM, Nocardia, Cryptococcus, Histoplasma, Talaromyces, Toxoplasma, VZV, Strongyloides** and *Legionella*. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Humoral defect or asplenia** (myeloma, CLL, rituximab, splenectomy, sickle cell): **encapsulated bacteria** - *S. pneumoniae*, *H. influenzae*, *N. meningitidis*. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HIV by CD4 count**: TB and bacterial pneumonia at any count; **PJP under 200**; **Cryptococcus and Toxoplasma under 100**; **CMV, disseminated MAC, Histoplasma and Talaromyces under 50-100**. [NIH OI 2024]`,`**Post-solid-organ transplant timeline (Fishman)**: **first month** - hospital bacteria, Candida, donor-derived infection; **1-6 months** - opportunists (**PJP, CMV, Nocardia, Aspergillus, TB reactivation**); **after 6 months** - community pathogens unless rejection needs heavier immunosuppression. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**Steroids**: **prednisolone 20 mg/day or more for 4 weeks or longer** carries a meaningful PJP risk, especially combined with another immunosuppressant (cyclophosphamide, rituximab, methotrexate). [ECIL 2016]`,`**Indian endemic fungi**: **Histoplasma capsulatum** in the Gangetic plains (West Bengal, Uttar Pradesh, Bihar, Assam) and **Talaromyces marneffei** in Manipur and the North-East, both disseminating in advanced HIV. [WHO 2020]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why each organism behaves as it does`,points:[`**Pneumocystis** is a fungus that attaches to type I pneumocytes; without CD4 T cells and alveolar macrophage help it multiplies, filling alveoli with **foamy eosinophilic exudate**, thickening septa and causing **diffusion impairment and hypoxaemia out of proportion to signs**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why steroids help in PJP**: killing organisms with cotrimoxazole releases antigens that cause a surge of inflammation and worsening hypoxaemia at days 3-5; **adjunctive steroids blunt this** and reduce death and intubation in HIV with PaO2 under 70 mmHg. [NIH OI 2024]`,`**Why non-HIV PJP is sicker**: these patients have a fuller inflammatory response with fewer organisms, so illness is **abrupt and severe**, often unmasked as steroids are tapered. [ECIL 2016]`,`**Why beta-D-glucan and LDH rise**: **1,3-beta-D-glucan** is a cell-wall component of Pneumocystis (and Candida and Aspergillus, but **not Cryptococcus or Mucorales**), and LDH reflects lung injury. [ECIL 2016]`,`**CMV** is latent in leucocytes and endothelium; immunosuppression, especially with **antilymphocyte therapy**, allows reactivation; CMV is also immunomodulatory, raising the risk of rejection and of other opportunists. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**Nocardia** are aerobic, gram-positive, **branching, beaded filamentous bacteria that are weakly acid-fast**; inhaled from soil, they form suppurative abscesses that spread by blood, with a striking tropism for the **brain**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cryptococcus** is inhaled from soil and pigeon droppings; its polysaccharide capsule resists phagocytosis and **antigen (CrAg) is shed into blood weeks before meningitis** - the basis of CrAg screening. [WHO 2022]`,`**Histoplasma** yeasts survive inside macrophages and disseminate to the reticuloendothelial system, causing **hepatosplenomegaly, lymphadenopathy, pancytopenia, oral ulcers and adrenal destruction**. [WHO 2020]`]},{heading:`Classification - clinicoradiological patterns`,points:[`**Diffuse bilateral interstitial or ground-glass** pattern: **PJP, CMV**, viral (influenza, RSV, COVID-19), drug toxicity, pulmonary oedema, diffuse alveolar haemorrhage. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Focal consolidation**: bacterial pneumonia, Legionella, TB, Nocardia, early fungal infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nodules with or without cavitation**: **Aspergillus (halo sign, air crescent), Mucorales (reverse halo), Nocardia, Cryptococcus**, TB, septic emboli, lymphoma or PTLD. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Miliary pattern**: TB, Histoplasma, Talaromyces, disseminated Cryptococcus. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tempo of illness**: acute (hours to a day) - bacteria, oedema, haemorrhage; subacute (days to weeks) - **PJP, CMV, Nocardia, fungi, TB** - a simple and examinable discriminator. [Harrison 22e]`,`**EORTC/MSGERC 2020 categories** for invasive fungal disease: **proven** (tissue or sterile-site culture), **probable** (host factor + clinical feature + mycological evidence) and **possible** (host + clinical, no mycology). [EORTC/MSGERC 2020]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Define the immune defect first**: HIV status and last CD4 and viral load, ART and prophylaxis adherence, transplant type and date, chemotherapy cycle and nadir, steroid dose and duration, biologics (rituximab, TNF inhibitors, JAK inhibitors). [Harrison 22e]`,`**PJP in HIV**: **subacute (2-4 weeks) dry cough, low-grade fever and progressive exertional breathlessness**, often with oral thrush and weight loss in a patient not on ART or prophylaxis. [NIH OI 2024]`,`**PJP in non-HIV patients**: abrupt fever and breathlessness over days, often **during steroid taper**, rapidly progressing to respiratory failure. [ECIL 2016]`,`**CMV pneumonitis**: fever, dry cough and breathlessness **1-6 months after transplant** (especially lung and stem-cell transplant), often with leucopenia, hepatitis, colitis or graft dysfunction. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**Nocardiosis**: subacute fever, productive cough, **haemoptysis**, weight loss - easily mistaken for TB - with **headache, seizures or focal deficit** from brain abscess or nodular skin lesions. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cryptococcosis**: cough and chest pain with nodules, or few lung symptoms but **headache, fever and altered sensorium** of meningitis. [WHO 2022]`,`**Histoplasmosis and talaromycosis**: prolonged fever, weight loss, cough, diarrhoea, oral ulcers and, in talaromycosis, **umbilicated papules on the face** resembling molluscum. [WHO 2020]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**PJP**: tachypnoea and **desaturation on exertion** with a near-normal chest - fine crackles at most; **sudden pleuritic pain** suggests pneumothorax from pneumatoceles. [NIH OI 2024]`,`**Bedside exercise test**: a fall of SpO2 by **3-4% or more after walking or 1 minute of sit-to-stand** strongly suggests PJP in an HIV patient with a normal resting SpO2. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mouth**: **oral candidiasis** (CD4 likely under 200), hairy leucoplakia, palatal ulcers (histoplasmosis), black eschar on palate or nose (mucormycosis). [NIH OI 2024]`,`**Skin**: umbilicated papules (cryptococcus, talaromycosis, histoplasmosis), subcutaneous abscesses (Nocardia), dermatomal scars (VZV). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Eyes**: **fundoscopy** for CMV retinitis (pizza-pie appearance) in HIV with CD4 under 50, and papilloedema in cryptococcal meningitis. [NIH OI 2024]`,`**Abdomen and nodes**: hepatosplenomegaly and lymphadenopathy suggest disseminated TB, histoplasmosis, talaromycosis or lymphoma. [WHO 2020]`,`**Neurology**: neck stiffness, focal signs or confusion demand brain imaging and LP - **Nocardia and Cryptococcus spread to the brain**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Tuberculosis** - the first consideration in every Indian immunocompromised patient; in advanced HIV it shows **lower-zone or miliary disease, lymphadenopathy and often a normal X-ray**; send **CBNAAT/Truenat and urine LF-LAM**. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Bacterial pneumonia**: acute onset, lobar consolidation, high procalcitonin, purulent sputum; commonest pneumonia in HIV at all CD4 counts. [NIH OI 2024]`,`**Invasive aspergillosis and mucormycosis**: neutropenia or steroids plus diabetes, nodules with halo or reverse halo, haemoptysis - detailed in the aspergillosis and mucormycosis topic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drug-induced pneumonitis**: methotrexate, bleomycin, mTOR inhibitors (sirolimus, everolimus), checkpoint inhibitors, amiodarone. [Pneumotox 2024]`,`**Non-infective mimics**: pulmonary oedema (fluid overload, cardiotoxic chemotherapy), diffuse alveolar haemorrhage, **engraftment syndrome**, lymphangitic carcinoma, PTLD and radiation pneumonitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Respiratory viruses** (influenza, RSV, COVID-19, parainfluenza) cause severe disease with prolonged shedding; multiplex PCR on nasopharyngeal swab or BAL distinguishes them. [IDSA 2023]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line, then tertiary`,points:[`**First line**: CBC (ANC, lymphocyte count), **CD4 count and HIV viral load**, creatinine, LFT, **LDH**, blood cultures x2, SpO2 at rest and on exertion, ABG, chest X-ray, sputum Gram stain, culture, **CBNAAT** and **urine LF-LAM** in HIV. [NACO 2021]`,`**HRCT chest early** - it is abnormal when the X-ray is normal: **PJP shows diffuse ground-glass with upper-zone predominance and cysts**; a **normal HRCT makes PJP very unlikely**. [ECIL 2016]`,`**PJP diagnosis**: **induced sputum** (3% saline) or **BAL** for immunofluorescence or **PCR** (BAL PCR is most sensitive; quantitative PCR helps separate colonisation from disease); **serum beta-D-glucan over 80 pg/mL** supports PJP, and a negative value makes it unlikely. [ECIL 2016]`,`**CMV**: **quantitative CMV PCR in plasma** (viral load) and BAL PCR; definitive diagnosis of pneumonitis needs histology with **owl's-eye inclusions** or compatible imaging plus BAL CMV with no other cause. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**Nocardia**: Gram stain shows **branching beaded rods**, **modified Kinyoun stain is weakly acid-fast**; tell the lab - cultures need **up to 2-3 weeks**; MRI brain in every case of pulmonary nocardiosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cryptococcus**: **serum CrAg lateral flow assay** (sensitivity over 95%) and **lumbar puncture with opening pressure, India ink and CSF CrAg** in every immunocompromised patient with pulmonary cryptococcosis or positive serum CrAg. [WHO 2022]`,`**Histoplasma**: **urine or serum Histoplasma antigen**, bone marrow aspirate and culture, skin or node biopsy (small intracellular yeasts), and adrenal imaging. [WHO 2020]`,`**Aspergillus**: **serum and BAL galactomannan** (BAL index 1.0 or more is positive) and Aspergillus PCR; bronchoscopy with BAL is the single most useful test in a non-diagnostic work-up. [EORTC/MSGERC 2020]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`**Mild PJP** (PaO2 70 mmHg or more, SpO2 over 94% at rest, able to take oral drugs): **cotrimoxazole double strength (160/800 mg) 2 tablets three times daily** (15-20 mg/kg/day trimethoprim) for **21 days in HIV**, with close review at 48-72 hours. [NIH OI 2024]`,`**Cotrimoxazole adverse effects**: rash (up to 20-50% in HIV), fever, **hyperkalaemia, rising creatinine, bone marrow suppression, hepatitis** and rarely Stevens-Johnson syndrome - check CBC, potassium and creatinine at day 7 and 14. [NIH OI 2024]`,`**Alternatives for mild-moderate PJP**: **clindamycin 450 mg orally 6-hourly plus primaquine 30 mg base daily** (check **G6PD** first), or dapsone 100 mg daily plus trimethoprim 5 mg/kg 8-hourly, or atovaquone 750 mg twice daily with food. [NIH OI 2024]`,`**Start or optimise ART within 2 weeks of PJP treatment** (and within 2 weeks for most OIs), but **defer ART by 4-6 weeks in cryptococcal meningitis** to reduce IRIS mortality. [NACO 2021]`,`**Mild isolated pulmonary cryptococcosis** with negative CSF and serum CrAg: **fluconazole 400 mg orally daily for 6-12 months**. [IDSA 2010]`,`**Mild to moderate histoplasmosis**: **itraconazole 200 mg three times daily for 3 days, then twice daily for at least 12 months**, with drug levels where available. [WHO 2020]`,`**Prophylaxis prescribing in the clinic** - cotrimoxazole, valganciclovir, fluconazole, isoniazid - is the most important thing primary care does; check adherence and interactions (rifampicin lowers azoles, azoles raise tacrolimus). [NACO 2021]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency`,points:[`**Admit** any immunocompromised patient with fever and new infiltrate plus SpO2 under 94%, PaO2 under 70 mmHg, neutropenia, sepsis, neurological signs or inability to take oral drugs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Moderate-severe PJP**: **cotrimoxazole IV 15-20 mg/kg/day of trimethoprim in 3-4 divided doses**, switched to oral when better, total **21 days** (HIV) or **14-21 days** (non-HIV). [NIH OI 2024]`,`**Adjunctive prednisolone in HIV PJP with PaO2 under 70 mmHg or A-a gradient 35 mmHg or more** on room air, started within 72 hours: **40 mg twice daily days 1-5, 40 mg once daily days 6-10, 20 mg once daily days 11-21** (or IV methylprednisolone at 75% of the dose). [NIH OI 2024]`,`**Severe PJP alternatives**: **IV clindamycin 600-900 mg 6-8 hourly plus oral primaquine 30 mg** (G6PD normal), or **IV pentamidine 4 mg/kg daily** (hypoglycaemia, hypotension, pancreatitis, nephrotoxicity). [NIH OI 2024]`,`**CMV pneumonitis**: **IV ganciclovir 5 mg/kg 12-hourly** (renally adjusted) for at least 2-3 weeks until clinical recovery and viral clearance, then **valganciclovir 900 mg orally twice daily** or maintenance; **foscarnet** for resistance; reduce immunosuppression. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**Pulmonary nocardiosis**: mild - **cotrimoxazole 5-10 mg/kg/day trimethoprim** (higher, 15 mg/kg, for severe or CNS) in 2-4 doses; severe or CNS - add **imipenem 500 mg IV 6-hourly** with or without **amikacin**, or use linezolid; total **6-12 months** (12 months or more in CNS disease or ongoing immunosuppression). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cryptococcal meningitis or severe pulmonary disease in HIV**: **single high-dose liposomal amphotericin B 10 mg/kg** plus **14 days of flucytosine 100 mg/kg/day and fluconazole 1200 mg/day**, then fluconazole 800 mg for 8 weeks and 200 mg maintenance; therapeutic LPs for raised pressure. [WHO 2022]`,`**Severe histoplasmosis or talaromycosis**: **liposomal amphotericin B 3 mg/kg/day for 1-2 weeks** (deoxycholate 0.7-1 mg/kg if unavailable), then **itraconazole 200 mg twice daily** for at least 12 months. [WHO 2020]`,`**Febrile neutropenia with infiltrates**: empirical **anti-pseudomonal beta-lactam** (piperacillin-tazobactam or cefepime, meropenem where ESBL or carbapenem resistance is common in India), plus **voriconazole** if CT suggests invasive mould. [IDSA 2023]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: cotrimoxazole remains the PJP treatment of choice (give folic acid; watch neonatal jaundice near term); avoid primaquine and flucytosine; **amphotericin is preferred over azoles** in the first trimester. [NIH OI 2024]`,`**CKD and kidney transplant**: reduce cotrimoxazole dose by half if CrCl 15-30 mL/min; it also **raises creatinine by blocking tubular secretion** without changing GFR; adjust ganciclovir and flucytosine to creatinine clearance. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**Drug interactions in transplant**: azoles (voriconazole, posaconazole, itraconazole) raise **tacrolimus, cyclosporine and sirolimus** levels - reduce dose and monitor; **rifampicin** lowers them dangerously. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**Rheumatology patients**: rituximab, cyclophosphamide or **prednisolone 20 mg or more for 4 weeks** with another immunosuppressant warrant PJP prophylaxis; methotrexate and cotrimoxazole together raise marrow toxicity - monitor CBC. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Diabetes**: uncontrolled diabetes plus steroids is the setting for **mucormycosis** in India - control glucose and use steroids only when indicated. [ICMR 2021]`,`**Children with HIV**: cotrimoxazole prophylaxis for all HIV-exposed infants from 6 weeks until HIV is excluded, and for HIV-infected children per NACO criteria. [NACO 2021]`,`**Elderly and frail on chronic steroids** (COPD, polymyalgia, ILD): low threshold for HRCT and BAL when a pneumonia fails to respond. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**PJP**: respiratory failure, **pneumothorax** (cysts and pneumatoceles), and paradoxical worsening at days 3-5; mortality about **10-20% in HIV** and **30-60% in non-HIV** patients, higher if ventilated. [ECIL 2016]`,`**Immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome (IRIS)** after ART: worsening of TB, Cryptococcus, PJP or CMV with fever and new infiltrates - continue ART and treat the infection, steroids for moderate TB IRIS. [NACO 2021]`,`**CMV**: graft rejection, bacterial and fungal superinfection, and in HSCT recipients pneumonitis mortality remains high (about 30-50%). [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**Nocardia**: brain abscess, empyema and relapse if treatment is too short; mortality is high in disseminated disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cryptococcal meningitis** mortality is about 20-30% even with good therapy, mostly from **raised intracranial pressure** - manage with repeated LPs. [WHO 2022]`,`**Drug toxicity**: cotrimoxazole marrow suppression and hyperkalaemia, ganciclovir **neutropenia**, amphotericin **hypokalaemia and nephrotoxicity** (pre-hydrate with 1 L saline and replace potassium). [WHO 2022]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer the same day** any immunocompromised patient with fever and hypoxaemia, neutropenic fever, neurological signs or failure to improve in 48-72 hours - bronchoscopy and HRCT need a tertiary centre. [Harrison 22e]`,`**After PJP**: continue **secondary prophylaxis with cotrimoxazole** until CD4 is over 200 for 3 months on ART (HIV), or for as long as immunosuppression lasts (non-HIV). [NIH OI 2024]`,`**On cotrimoxazole long-term**: CBC, potassium and creatinine at 2 weeks, then every 3 months. [NACO 2021]`,`**After CMV disease**: weekly CMV PCR until undetectable, then for relapse; CBC twice weekly on ganciclovir for neutropenia. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**On azoles**: LFT monthly, ECG for QT, itraconazole or voriconazole trough levels where available; continue antifungal for at least 12 months for histoplasmosis and talaromycosis. [WHO 2020]`,`**HIV follow-up at the ART centre**: CD4 and viral load per NACO schedule, adherence counselling, and stopping primary prophylaxis when immune recovery is sustained. [NACO 2021]`]},{heading:`Prevention, prophylaxis and national programme link`,points:[`**PJP prophylaxis in HIV (NACO)**: **cotrimoxazole 160/800 mg once daily** for adults with **WHO stage 3 or 4 disease or CD4 under 350**; stop after sustained immune recovery on ART as per NACO criteria (NIH stops when CD4 is over 200 for at least 3 months). [NACO 2021]`,`**PJP prophylaxis in non-HIV**: cotrimoxazole **single strength daily or double strength three times weekly** for allogeneic HSCT, ALL, alemtuzumab, purine analogues, **SOT for 6-12 months**, and prednisolone 20 mg or more for 4 weeks plus another immunosuppressant; alternatives are dapsone 100 mg daily or atovaquone. [ECIL 2016]`,`**CMV prevention**: **valganciclovir 900 mg daily for 3-6 months** in high-risk (donor positive, recipient negative) SOT recipients, or pre-emptive therapy guided by weekly PCR; **letermovir 480 mg daily to day 100** (extendable to day 200) in CMV-seropositive allogeneic HSCT, and letermovir for 200 days is now an option in D+/R- kidney transplant. [AST IDCOP 2019]`,`**Cryptococcal antigen screening**: **serum CrAg in all HIV patients with CD4 under 200**; if positive with negative CSF, give **pre-emptive fluconazole 800 mg daily for 2 weeks, 400 mg for 8 weeks, then 200 mg**. [WHO 2022]`,`**TB preventive therapy** for all PLHIV after excluding active TB (isoniazid 6 months or 3HP), and **latent TB testing before biologics and transplant** - covered in the TB infection topic. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Vaccination before immunosuppression**: pneumococcal (PCV then PPSV23, or PCV20/21), annual inactivated influenza, COVID-19, hepatitis B and recombinant zoster; **avoid live vaccines** once immunosuppressed. [IDSA 2013]`,`**Environmental advice**: avoid construction dust, bird and bat droppings (Cryptococcus, Histoplasma), gardening without gloves (Nocardia); safe food and water. [NIH OI 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**NIH/CDC/IDSA adult OI guidelines (continuously updated, PJP section 2023-2024)**: cotrimoxazole 15-20 mg/kg/day trimethoprim for 21 days, steroids for PaO2 under 70 mmHg, ART within 2 weeks of PJP treatment. [NIH OI 2024]`,`**WHO 2022 cryptococcal guideline**: **single-dose liposomal amphotericin 10 mg/kg plus 14 days of flucytosine and fluconazole** became the preferred induction, and CrAg screening threshold raised to **CD4 under 200**. [WHO 2022]`,`**IDSA 2023 febrile neutropenia and ECIL updates** emphasise risk-stratified empirical therapy and early CT for suspected mould infection. [IDSA 2023]`,`**Letermovir** extended to 200 days in high-risk HSCT (2023) and approved for CMV prophylaxis in high-risk kidney transplant (2023) - an alternative to valganciclovir with less marrow toxicity. [FDA 2023]`,`**Guideline difference on PJP dose**: several meta-analyses and some non-HIV guidance support **lower-dose cotrimoxazole (10-15 mg/kg/day trimethoprim)** with similar efficacy and fewer side effects, while NIH still recommends 15-20 mg/kg/day - use the standard dose in severe disease. [ECIL 2016]`,`**NACO National Guidelines for HIV Care and Treatment 2021** remain the Indian reference for cotrimoxazole prophylaxis, OI management and ART timing. [NACO 2021]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**First question in any immunocompromised pneumonia**: what is the immune defect and how severe - this predicts the organism better than the X-ray. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PJP triad for the viva**: subacute dry cough, **exertional desaturation with a near-normal chest**, raised LDH and beta-D-glucan, bilateral ground-glass on HRCT. [NIH OI 2024]`,`**Steroid rule**: PaO2 under 70 mmHg or A-a gradient 35 or more - **prednisolone 40 BD x 5, 40 OD x 5, 20 OD x 11**. [NIH OI 2024]`,`**Nocardia one-liner**: branching, beaded, **weakly acid-fast**, cavitating lung plus brain abscess - treat with cotrimoxazole for 6-12 months; always image the brain. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistakes**: forgetting G6PD before primaquine or dapsone, not doing an LP in pulmonary cryptococcosis, and starting ART immediately in cryptococcal meningitis. [WHO 2022]`,`**Mnemonic for CD4 thresholds (2-1-5)**: under **200** PJP, under **100** Crypto and Toxo, under **50** CMV and MAC. [NIH OI 2024]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for opportunistic lung infections`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Cotrimoxazole (PJP treatment)`,`15-20 mg/kg/day trimethoprim IV or oral in 3-4 doses (2 DS tablets 8-hourly in 60-80 kg)`,`21 days (HIV); 14-21 days (non-HIV)`,`Rash, hyperkalaemia, marrow suppression, hepatitis, SJS; reduce in CrCl 15-30`],[`Prednisolone (PJP adjunct)`,`40 mg BD days 1-5, 40 mg OD days 6-10, 20 mg OD days 11-21`,`Start within 72 h if PaO2 under 70 mmHg`,`Hyperglycaemia, other OIs (CMV, TB)`],[`Clindamycin + primaquine`,`Clindamycin 600-900 mg IV 6-8 hourly or 450 mg oral 6-hourly + primaquine 30 mg base daily`,`21 days`,`Haemolysis in G6PD deficiency, methaemoglobinaemia, C. difficile`],[`Cotrimoxazole (prophylaxis)`,`160/800 mg daily, or 80/400 mg daily, or 160/800 mg thrice weekly`,`While CD4 low or immunosuppressed`,`As above; desensitise if mild allergy`],[`Ganciclovir / valganciclovir`,`Ganciclovir 5 mg/kg IV 12-hourly; valganciclovir 900 mg oral BD (treatment) or daily (prophylaxis)`,`2-3 weeks induction then maintenance; prophylaxis 3-6 months`,`Neutropenia, thrombocytopenia; adjust for renal function`],[`Letermovir`,`480 mg oral or IV daily (240 mg with cyclosporine)`,`To day 100-200 after HSCT`,`Nausea; interacts with tacrolimus, voriconazole`],[`Liposomal amphotericin B`,`10 mg/kg single dose (crypto); 3 mg/kg/day (histo, talaromyces)`,`Single dose or 1-2 weeks`,`Hypokalaemia, nephrotoxicity, infusion reactions`],[`Fluconazole`,`1200 mg/day induction; 400-800 mg/day consolidation; 200 mg maintenance`,`Months; pulmonary crypto 6-12 months`,`Hepatitis, QT prolongation, raises tacrolimus`],[`Itraconazole`,`200 mg TDS x 3 days, then 200 mg BD`,`At least 12 months (histoplasmosis)`,`Hepatitis, heart failure, interactions; take capsule with food`]]},{heading:`Immune defect and the likely pathogens`,columns:[`Immune defect`,`Typical setting`,`Likely lung pathogens`],rows:[[`Neutropenia`,`Chemotherapy, aplastic anaemia, AML`,`Pseudomonas, Enterobacterales, S. aureus, Aspergillus, Mucorales`],[`T-cell defect`,`HIV, transplant, high-dose steroids, alemtuzumab`,`PJP, CMV, TB, Nocardia, Cryptococcus, Histoplasma, Talaromyces`],[`Humoral defect / asplenia`,`Myeloma, CLL, rituximab, splenectomy`,`S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae, N. meningitidis`],[`SOT, first month`,`Post-surgery`,`Hospital bacteria, Candida, donor-derived`],[`SOT, 1-6 months`,`Peak immunosuppression`,`PJP, CMV, Nocardia, Aspergillus, TB`],[`SOT, after 6 months`,`Stable graft`,`Community pathogens; opportunists if rejection treated`]]},{heading:`HIV CD4 count and pulmonary opportunists`,columns:[`CD4 (cells/microlitre)`,`Pathogens`,`Prophylaxis`],rows:[[`Any count`,`TB, bacterial pneumonia`,`TB preventive therapy; pneumococcal vaccine`],[`Under 350 (NACO) / under 200 (NIH)`,`Pneumocystis`,`Cotrimoxazole daily`],[`Under 200`,`Cryptococcus (CrAg positive)`,`CrAg screen and pre-emptive fluconazole`],[`Under 100`,`Toxoplasma, Cryptococcus, Histoplasma, Talaromyces`,`Cotrimoxazole covers Toxoplasma`],[`Under 50`,`CMV, disseminated MAC`,`Fundoscopy for CMV; ART is the main prevention`]]},{heading:`Diagnostic tests and their interpretation`,columns:[`Test`,`Used for`,`Interpretation`],rows:[[`Serum 1,3-beta-D-glucan`,`PJP, Candida, Aspergillus`,`Over 80 pg/mL supports; negative argues strongly against PJP; not raised in Cryptococcus or Mucorales`],[`BAL Pneumocystis PCR / IF`,`PJP`,`Most sensitive; low PCR load may mean colonisation`],[`LDH`,`PJP`,`Raised in over 90%; very high levels carry worse prognosis`],[`Galactomannan`,`Invasive aspergillosis`,`Serum index 0.5 or more; BAL 1.0 or more positive`],[`Serum CrAg LFA`,`Cryptococcus`,`Positive: do LP; positive weeks before meningitis`],[`Plasma CMV PCR`,`CMV`,`Rising load guides pre-emptive therapy; disease needs tissue or BAL`],[`Modified Kinyoun stain`,`Nocardia`,`Weakly acid-fast branching beaded filaments; culture up to 2-3 weeks`],[`Urine Histoplasma antigen`,`Disseminated histoplasmosis`,`High sensitivity in advanced HIV`]]}],redFlags:[`Immunocompromised patient with fever and SpO2 under 94% at rest or a fall of 4% or more on exertion`,`Fever with absolute neutrophil count under 500/microlitre - febrile neutropenia`,`Headache, confusion, seizures or focal signs with a lung lesion - Nocardia or Cryptococcus in the brain`,`Sudden pleuritic pain and breathlessness in suspected PJP - pneumothorax`,`Haemoptysis with nodules in a neutropenic or diabetic patient on steroids - invasive mould`,`Worsening infiltrates after starting ART - IRIS or a missed infection`,`Fever with new infiltrate in a transplant recipient 1-6 months after surgery`],pearls:[`Name the immune defect before you name the bug.`,`PJP: hypoxaemia out of proportion to the chest signs; desaturation on exertion is the bedside clue.`,`A normal HRCT or a negative beta-D-glucan makes PJP very unlikely.`,`Steroids in PJP if PaO2 under 70 mmHg - 40 BD x 5, 40 OD x 5, 20 OD x 11.`,`Check G6PD before primaquine or dapsone.`,`Nocardia: weakly acid-fast, branching, loves the brain - image the head, treat 6-12 months.`,`Every pulmonary cryptococcosis in the immunocompromised needs a lumbar puncture.`,`Delay ART 4-6 weeks in cryptococcal meningitis, but start within 2 weeks in PJP.`,`CD4 2-1-5: 200 PJP, 100 Crypto and Toxo, 50 CMV and MAC.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-opportunistic-lung-q1`,stem:`A 34-year-old man with HIV, CD4 count 62/microlitre, has 3 weeks of dry cough and breathlessness. PaO2 on room air is 58 mmHg and BAL is positive for Pneumocystis. Along with cotrimoxazole, what else should be started?`,options:[`Caspofungin 70 mg IV loading dose`,`Immediate ART on the same day as cotrimoxazole, without steroids`,`Prednisolone 40 mg twice daily, tapered over 21 days`,`Inhaled budesonide 400 microgram twice daily`],answer:2,explanation:`With PaO2 under 70 mmHg, adjunctive prednisolone (40 mg BD days 1-5, 40 mg OD days 6-10, 20 mg OD days 11-21) started within 72 hours reduces the inflammatory surge from dying organisms and lowers death and intubation. ART should start within 2 weeks, but it does not replace steroids; caspofungin has no proven role as first-line PJP therapy; and inhaled steroids do not have this effect.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-opportunistic-lung-q2`,stem:`A kidney transplant recipient with PJP develops severe cotrimoxazole-induced rash. You plan clindamycin plus primaquine. Which test must be done before primaquine?`,options:[`Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) level`,`Serum tacrolimus trough level`,`Thiopurine methyltransferase (TPMT) activity`,`Serum galactomannan`],answer:0,explanation:`Primaquine (like dapsone) causes oxidant haemolysis and methaemoglobinaemia in G6PD deficiency, which is common in India, so G6PD must be checked first. Tacrolimus levels matter with azoles, not primaquine; TPMT testing is for azathioprine; and galactomannan is a test for Aspergillus, not a safety test before an antimalarial.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-opportunistic-lung-q3`,stem:`A 45-year-old renal transplant recipient 4 months after surgery has fever, productive cough, a cavitating right upper lobe nodule and a ring-enhancing brain lesion. Sputum shows gram-positive branching beaded filaments that are weakly acid-fast on modified Kinyoun stain. What is the best treatment?`,options:[`Four-drug antitubercular therapy for 6 months`,`Voriconazole 6 mg/kg IV 12-hourly`,`Penicillin G for 6 weeks`,`High-dose cotrimoxazole plus imipenem, continued for at least 12 months`],answer:3,explanation:`Weakly acid-fast, branching, beaded gram-positive filaments with lung cavitation and brain abscess are typical of Nocardia; CNS disease in a transplant recipient needs high-dose cotrimoxazole plus imipenem (with or without amikacin) and treatment for at least 12 months. Penicillin for 6 weeks is the treatment of actinomycosis, which is not acid-fast; ATT treats TB, which is not branched; and voriconazole treats Aspergillus, a mould rather than a bacterium.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-opportunistic-lung-q4`,stem:`A man with HIV (CD4 80/microlitre) has a 2 cm lung nodule; biopsy shows encapsulated budding yeasts and serum cryptococcal antigen is positive. He has no headache. What is the most appropriate next step?`,options:[`Fluconazole 400 mg daily for 6 months without further tests`,`Lumbar puncture with opening pressure and CSF cryptococcal antigen`,`Start ART today and review in 1 month`,`Surgical resection of the nodule`],answer:1,explanation:`Every immunocompromised patient with pulmonary cryptococcosis or a positive serum CrAg needs a lumbar puncture, because meningitis is often silent and changes induction to amphotericin plus flucytosine and the timing of ART (delayed 4-6 weeks if CSF is positive). Fluconazole alone is only for isolated pulmonary disease after CNS involvement is excluded, starting ART first risks fatal IRIS, and resection is rarely needed.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-opportunistic-lung-q5`,stem:`A 58-year-old woman with granulomatosis with polyangiitis starts rituximab and prednisolone 60 mg/day. Which statement about Pneumocystis prophylaxis is correct?`,options:[`Not needed because she does not have HIV`,`Needed only if CD4 count falls under 200/microlitre`,`Indicated - cotrimoxazole for the duration of high-dose steroids and at least 6 months after rituximab`,`Indicated - inhaled pentamidine is the first-line agent`],answer:2,explanation:`Prednisolone 20 mg or more for 4 weeks combined with rituximab carries substantial PJP risk, so cotrimoxazole (single strength daily or double strength thrice weekly) is given through induction and for at least 6 months after the last rituximab dose. Non-HIV PJP is common and lethal, CD4 thresholds apply to HIV rather than to steroid-treated patients, and inhaled pentamidine is a weak second-line option.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-opportunistic-lung-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Describe the approach to a patient with pulmonary infiltrates who is immunocompromised. Discuss the diagnosis, treatment and prevention of Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia.`,openingLines:[`Pulmonary infiltrates in the immunocompromised host are caused by infections predicted by the type and severity of the immune defect, and by non-infective mimics such as drug toxicity, oedema and haemorrhage.`,`Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia is a fungal opportunistic pneumonia of T-cell-deficient hosts, treated with cotrimoxazole and adjunctive steroids when PaO2 is under 70 mmHg.`],answer:[{heading:`Define the immune defect`,points:[`Neutropenia: gram-negatives, Aspergillus, Mucorales.`,`T-cell defect: PJP, CMV, TB, Nocardia, Cryptococcus, Histoplasma.`,`Humoral or asplenia: encapsulated bacteria.`,`HIV by CD4; transplant by time since surgery (Fishman timeline).`]},{heading:`Clinico-radiological clues`,points:[`Tempo: acute (bacteria, oedema) vs subacute (PJP, CMV, Nocardia, fungi, TB).`,`Pattern: diffuse GGO (PJP, CMV, virus), consolidation (bacteria), nodules or cavities (moulds, Nocardia, Crypto), miliary (TB, histo).`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`CBC, CD4, LDH, blood cultures, sputum CBNAAT, urine LF-LAM, HRCT early.`,`Beta-D-glucan, galactomannan, CrAg, CMV PCR, Histoplasma antigen.`,`Bronchoscopy with BAL: PCR, cultures, cytology; biopsy if needed.`]},{heading:`PJP - diagnosis`,points:[`Subacute dry cough, exertional desaturation, normal chest; LDH and beta-D-glucan raised.`,`HRCT diffuse ground-glass; induced sputum or BAL IF/PCR.`]},{heading:`PJP - treatment`,points:[`Cotrimoxazole 15-20 mg/kg/day trimethoprim in 3-4 doses for 21 days.`,`Prednisolone if PaO2 under 70 or A-a gradient 35 or more: 40 BD x 5, 40 OD x 5, 20 OD x 11.`,`Alternatives: clindamycin + primaquine (G6PD), IV pentamidine, atovaquone.`,`ART within 2 weeks.`]},{heading:`PJP - prevention`,points:[`Cotrimoxazole 160/800 daily: HIV with CD4 under 350 (NACO) or WHO stage 3-4.`,`Non-HIV: transplant, HSCT, ALL, steroids 20 mg or more for 4 weeks with another agent.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table of immune defect and likely organisms`,`Flow chart: immunocompromised patient with infiltrate`],markSplit:[{part:`Approach by immune defect and pattern`,marks:3},{part:`Investigations`,marks:2},{part:`PJP diagnosis and treatment`,marks:3},{part:`Prophylaxis`,marks:2}],keywords:[`immune defect`,`CD4`,`PJP`,`cotrimoxazole`,`prednisolone`,`BAL`]},{id:`respiratory-opportunistic-lung-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`uncommon`,question:`Write a short note on pulmonary nocardiosis.`,openingLines:[`Nocardiosis is an infection with aerobic, gram-positive, branching, beaded, weakly acid-fast bacteria acquired by inhalation from soil, mainly in patients with T-cell immunosuppression.`,`It causes a subacute suppurative pneumonia with cavitating nodules and spreads by blood, especially to the brain.`],answer:[{heading:`Risk factors`,points:[`Transplant, long-term steroids, HIV, malignancy, COPD on steroids, diabetes.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Fever, cough, haemoptysis, weight loss - mimics TB.`,`Nodules, cavities, consolidation, empyema; brain abscess, skin abscesses.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Gram stain: branching beaded rods; modified Kinyoun weakly acid-fast.`,`Culture up to 2-3 weeks - inform the lab; MRI brain in every case.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Cotrimoxazole; add imipenem with or without amikacin, or linezolid, in severe or CNS disease.`,`Duration 6-12 months; 12 months or more in CNS disease or ongoing immunosuppression.`]}],mustDraw:[`Labelled sketch of branching beaded filaments versus Actinomyces`],markSplit:[{part:`Organism and risk factors`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:1},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:1},{part:`Treatment`,marks:2}],keywords:[`Nocardia`,`modified Kinyoun`,`cotrimoxazole`,`brain abscess`,`weakly acid-fast`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Infections in the immunocompromised host; Pneumocystis infections; Nocardiosis`,`NIH/CDC/HIVMA-IDSA Guidelines for the Prevention and Treatment of Opportunistic Infections in Adults and Adolescents with HIV, updated 2024`,`NACO National Guidelines for HIV Care and Treatment, 2021`,`ECIL-5 guidelines for the diagnosis, treatment and prophylaxis of Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia in haematology patients, 2016`,`AST Infectious Diseases Community of Practice guidelines (Pneumocystis, CMV, Nocardia in solid organ transplantation), 2019`,`WHO Guidelines for diagnosing, preventing and managing cryptococcal disease among adults, adolescents and children living with HIV, 2022`,`WHO Guidelines for diagnosing and managing disseminated histoplasmosis among people living with HIV, 2020`,`EORTC/MSGERC revised definitions of invasive fungal disease, 2020`]});var c=[];c.push({id:`respiratory-hypoventilation-sleep`,title:`Obesity hypoventilation, central sleep apnoea and other sleep-related breathing disorders`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`**Obesity hypoventilation syndrome (OHS)** is **awake daytime hypercapnia (PaCO2 45 mmHg or more) in a person with BMI 30 or more** after other causes of hypoventilation are excluded, while **central sleep apnoea (CSA)** is recurrent loss of breathing effort in sleep - classically **Cheyne-Stokes breathing in heart failure** - and both are treated with positive airway pressure chosen to fit the mechanism.`,keywords:[`OHS`,`Pickwickian syndrome`,`obesity hypoventilation`,`central sleep apnoea`,`CSA`,`Cheyne-Stokes breathing`,`Cheyne-Stokes respiration`,`SERVE-HF`,`adaptive servo-ventilation`,`ASV`,`BPAP-ST`,`AVAPS`,`serum bicarbonate screen`,`sleep hypoventilation`,`opioid-induced central apnoea`,`high-altitude periodic breathing`,`phrenic nerve stimulation`,`ICSD-3-TR`,`narcolepsy`,`insomnia CBT-I`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Obesity hypoventilation syndrome** = **BMI 30 kg/m2 or more** plus **awake PaCO2 45 mmHg or more at sea level**, with sleep-disordered breathing, after excluding lung, chest-wall, neuromuscular, metabolic and drug causes of hypoventilation. [ATS 2019]`,`OHS is a **diagnosis of exclusion**: the obese patient with COPD, kyphoscoliosis, myopathy or severe hypothyroidism who retains CO2 does not have OHS until those are ruled out. [Harrison 22e]`,`About **90% of OHS patients also have OSA**, and roughly **70% have severe OSA (AHI 30/h or more)**; the remaining 10% have pure sleep hypoventilation without obstructive events. [ATS 2019]`,`OHS is found in about **8-20% of obese patients referred to sleep clinics**, and the prevalence rises steeply with BMI - it is commonest above **BMI 40**. [ATS 2019]`,`Community prevalence in adults is estimated around **0.4%**, and it is badly under-diagnosed - many patients are first recognised during an ICU admission for **acute-on-chronic hypercapnic respiratory failure**, often labelled as COPD. [ATS 2019]`,`**India**: NFHS-5 (2019-21) found **24% of women and 22.9% of men aged 15-49 overweight or obese (BMI 25 or more)**, with central obesity at lower BMI in South Asians, so OHS and OSA referrals are rising in Indian sleep labs. [NFHS-5 2021]`,`**Central sleep apnoea** = repeated cessation of airflow in sleep **without respiratory effort**; diagnosed when there are **5 or more central events per hour** and they make up **more than 50%** of all apnoeas and hypopnoeas. [ICSD-3-TR 2023]`,`**Cheyne-Stokes breathing (CSB)** with central apnoea occurs in about **25-40% of people with HFrEF** and in many with atrial fibrillation or after stroke; it is an independent marker of higher mortality in heart failure. [AASM 2025]`]},{heading:`Classification: ICSD-3-TR sleep-related breathing disorders`,points:[`The **International Classification of Sleep Disorders, 3rd edition text revision (ICSD-3-TR, 2023)** groups sleep-related breathing disorders into four: **obstructive sleep apnoea, central sleep apnoea syndromes, sleep-related hypoventilation disorders and sleep-related hypoxaemia disorder**. [ICSD-3-TR 2023]`,`**Central sleep apnoea syndromes**: CSA with Cheyne-Stokes breathing, CSA due to a medical disorder without CSB, **high-altitude periodic breathing**, CSA due to a medication or substance (opioids), primary (idiopathic) CSA, CSA of prematurity/infancy and **treatment-emergent CSA**. [ICSD-3-TR 2023]`,`**Sleep-related hypoventilation disorders**: OHS, **congenital central alveolar hypoventilation syndrome (PHOX2B mutation, Ondine's curse)**, late-onset central hypoventilation with hypothalamic dysfunction, idiopathic central alveolar hypoventilation, and hypoventilation due to a medication or a medical disorder. [ICSD-3-TR 2023]`,`**Sleep hypoventilation (AASM scoring)**: PaCO2 (or surrogate) **over 55 mmHg for 10 minutes or more**, or a rise of **10 mmHg or more above the awake supine value to over 50 mmHg** for 10 minutes or more. [AASM Scoring Manual v3 2023]`,`**Hypercapnic CSA** (high loop gain is not the driver): seen with opioids, brainstem lesions, neuromuscular disease and OHS - the drive or the pump fails, so PaCO2 is high. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Non-hypercapnic CSA** (unstable control, normal or low PaCO2): Cheyne-Stokes in heart failure, high-altitude periodic breathing, idiopathic CSA and treatment-emergent CSA. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Treatment-emergent CSA** (formerly complex sleep apnoea): central apnoeas that appear during CPAP titration for OSA once obstructive events clear; most resolve within 2-3 months of continued CPAP. [ICSD-3-TR 2023]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology of OHS - why the CO2 stays high`,points:[`**Mechanical load**: abdominal and chest-wall fat lowers **chest-wall compliance** and **expiratory reserve volume (ERV)**, so the patient breathes at low lung volumes with basal airway closure and V/Q mismatch, and the work of breathing rises 2-3-fold. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Blunted central drive**: OHS patients have a reduced ventilatory response to CO2 and hypoxia compared with equally obese eucapnic people - it is partly reversible with PAP therapy, which shows it is acquired rather than fixed. [ATS 2019]`,`**Leptin resistance**: leptin is a respiratory stimulant; OHS patients have high leptin but central leptin resistance, so the expected rise in ventilation with obesity fails. [Harrison 22e]`,`**The night-time CO2 ratchet**: during apnoeas CO2 rises; if the interapnoeic breaths are too short to blow it off, CO2 accumulates over the night, and the kidney retains **bicarbonate** that does not fully clear by morning. [ATS 2019]`,`**Bicarbonate blunts drive**: the retained HCO3- lowers the CSF pH response to each rise in CO2, resetting the chemoreceptor so daytime hypercapnia becomes established - the basis of the **bicarbonate screening test**. [ATS 2019]`,`**REM sleep** is the most vulnerable stage: skeletal muscle atonia leaves the diaphragm alone against the fat load, so the deepest desaturations and largest CO2 rises occur in REM. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypoxaemia drives the complications**: chronic alveolar hypoxia causes **pulmonary vasoconstriction, pulmonary hypertension, right heart failure (cor pulmonale)** and **secondary polycythaemia**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why oxygen alone is dangerous**: high-flow oxygen removes hypoxic drive, worsens V/Q mismatch (releases hypoxic vasoconstriction) and via the Haldane effect raises PaCO2 - so OHS is treated with ventilatory support, not oxygen alone. [BTS 2017]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology of central sleep apnoea and Cheyne-Stokes breathing`,points:[`**Apnoeic threshold**: in NREM sleep breathing depends entirely on the chemical drive from PaCO2; when PaCO2 falls below the apnoeic threshold (only about **2-6 mmHg below eupnoea**), breathing stops. [Harrison 22e]`,`**High loop gain** is the core of non-hypercapnic CSA: an over-sensitive controller (high chemosensitivity) plus a sluggish plant overshoots, so hyperventilation drives PaCO2 below threshold, apnoea follows, CO2 rises and the cycle repeats. [Harrison 22e]`,`**In heart failure**: pulmonary congestion stimulates **J receptors**, causing chronic hyperventilation with a **low PaCO2 (often under 38 mmHg)** close to the apnoeic threshold; raised left atrial pressure correlates with CSB severity. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Prolonged circulation time** from low cardiac output delays the signal from lung to carotid body, so CSB in heart failure has a **long cycle length - typically 45-90 seconds**, versus under 40 seconds in idiopathic CSA. [AASM Scoring Manual v3 2023]`,`**Crescendo-decrescendo pattern**: breathing waxes and wanes, with the arousal often at the peak of hyperpnoea rather than at the end of the apnoea (opposite of OSA) - a clue on the polysomnogram. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Consequences in HF**: repeated hypoxaemia and arousals surge **sympathetic activity**, raise BNP and favour arrhythmia (AF, ventricular ectopy), which is why CSB marks a poorer prognosis. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Opioid-induced CSA**: mu-opioids depress the pre-Botzinger complex, producing slow, irregular **ataxic (Biot) breathing** and central apnoeas; risk rises with morphine-equivalent dose over about 200 mg/day and with methadone. [AASM 2025]`,`**High-altitude periodic breathing**: hypoxic hyperventilation lowers PaCO2 to near threshold; almost universal above **4000 m** - relevant to Indian troops, pilgrims and tourists in Ladakh, Sikkim and Himachal. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**OHS** presents like severe OSA - loud snoring, witnessed apnoeas, choking arousals, **excessive daytime sleepiness** (Epworth score often over 10) - plus **morning headache**, fatigue and poor concentration from nocturnal CO2 retention. [ATS 2019]`,`**Breathlessness on exertion and leg swelling** suggest pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale; OHS patients are breathless out of proportion to their spirometry. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Atypical first presentation**: acute-on-chronic hypercapnic respiratory failure with drowsiness or coma, often after sedatives, opioids, surgery or a chest infection; a large share are mislabelled as COPD exacerbation. [ATS 2019]`,`**CSA with CSB** is often silent: the patient with heart failure may report only **paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea, poor sleep, insomnia or fatigue**; daytime sleepiness is less marked than in OSA. [AASM 2025]`,`The **bed partner** reports a **waxing-waning breathing pattern with pauses** and little snoring - ask specifically, because the patient is unaware. [Harrison 22e]`,`Take a **drug history**: opioids (tramadol, methadone, buprenorphine), benzodiazepines, gabapentinoids, alcohol - all worsen hypoventilation and central apnoea. [AASM 2025]`,`Screen for **narcolepsy and insomnia** when sleepiness persists: cataplexy (emotion-triggered weakness), sleep paralysis and hypnagogic hallucinations point to narcolepsy type 1; difficulty initiating or maintaining sleep 3 nights a week for 3 months is chronic insomnia. [ICSD-3-TR 2023]`,`**Driving and occupation**: ask about near-miss accidents, dozing at the wheel and driving profession (truck, bus, taxi drivers) - this changes urgency. [ERS 2021]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**BMI usually over 35-40** with large neck circumference (over 43 cm in men, 40 cm in women) and crowded oropharynx (Mallampati 3-4) when OSA coexists. [ATS 2019]`,`**Awake SpO2 under 93-94% on room air** in an obese patient without lung disease is a red flag for hypoventilation and should prompt an ABG. [ATS 2019]`,`**Signs of cor pulmonale**: raised JVP, loud P2, parasternal heave, tricuspid regurgitation murmur, hepatomegaly and pitting pedal oedema. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Plethoric face and conjunctival suffusion** reflect secondary polycythaemia; **central cyanosis** appears when SpO2 falls under about 85%. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Signs of CO2 retention**: warm hands, bounding pulse, flapping tremor (asterixis), papilloedema in severe cases, and drowsiness. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Look for mimics**: kyphoscoliosis, myopathy or diaphragm weakness (paradoxical abdominal movement when supine), goitre or myxoedema facies, and features of COPD. [Harrison 22e]`,`In suspected CSA, examine for **heart failure** (third heart sound, crackles, raised JVP), **atrial fibrillation** and **neurological deficit** from stroke. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Cheyne-Stokes breathing awake** in a patient with heart failure is an ominous sign of very low cardiac output and is sometimes visible at the bedside in the ward. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Eucapnic OSA**: same symptoms but daytime PaCO2 under 45 mmHg and bicarbonate usually normal - treated with CPAP alone. [ATS 2019]`,`**COPD with hypercapnia (overlap syndrome)**: FEV1/FVC under 0.70 on spirometry; obese COPD patients retain CO2 at milder airflow obstruction. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Neuromuscular disease** (motor neurone disease, myasthenia, Duchenne, post-polio, diaphragm palsy): **VC falls by more than 20-25% supine**, low maximal inspiratory pressure, orthopnoea. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chest-wall disease**: severe kyphoscoliosis (Cobb angle over 100 degrees), thoracoplasty for old TB and fibrothorax - common causes of hypercapnia in Indian post-TB patients. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Severe hypothyroidism** causes both hypoventilation and OSA - always check TSH before labelling OHS. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drug-induced hypoventilation**: opioids, benzodiazepines, alcohol, barbiturates; and **metabolic alkalosis** (diuretics, vomiting) giving compensatory hypercapnia, so raised bicarbonate is not always OHS. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Narcolepsy, idiopathic hypersomnia and insufficient sleep** cause sleepiness without breathing events - polysomnography is normal and MSLT is abnormal. [ICSD-3-TR 2023]`,`**Obstructive vs central hypopnoea**: snoring, flattening of the nasal pressure signal and thoraco-abdominal paradox mean obstruction; their absence with reduced effort means central. [AASM Scoring Manual v3 2023]`]},{heading:`Investigations - stepwise`,points:[`**Serum bicarbonate first** (venous, from routine biochemistry): in an obese patient with OSA and low-to-moderate suspicion, **HCO3- under 27 mmol/L makes OHS very unlikely** and ABG can be skipped. [ATS 2019]`,`**Arterial blood gas** if bicarbonate is 27 mmol/L or more, or straight away if suspicion is high (BMI over 40, severe OSA, awake SpO2 low, cor pulmonale): **PaCO2 45 mmHg or more with pH 7.35 or more** confirms chronic hypercapnia. [ATS 2019]`,`**Polysomnography (level 1)** with **transcutaneous or end-tidal CO2** is ideal: shows AHI, the obstructive vs central split, REM desaturation and sleep hypoventilation; home sleep apnoea testing is not adequate for OHS or CSA. [AASM 2025]`,`**Overnight oximetry** in a district hospital: sustained desaturation (SpO2 under 88-90% for long periods, especially in clusters) without sawtooth recovery suggests hypoventilation rather than pure OSA. [ATS 2019]`,`**Spirometry**: OHS shows a mild **restrictive pattern** (FVC and FEV1 reduced proportionately, low ERV) - an obstructive ratio suggests COPD overlap instead. [ATS 2019]`,`**Rule out mimics**: TSH, CBC (haematocrit over 52% in men suggests polycythaemia), chest X-ray, electrolytes and creatinine, and maximal inspiratory pressure or supine VC if neuromuscular disease is possible. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cardiac work-up**: ECG (right axis, P pulmonale, RV strain) and **echocardiography** for pulmonary hypertension in OHS; echo, NT-proBNP and LVEF in CSA, because LVEF decides whether ASV is allowed. [AASM 2025]`,`**Narcolepsy work-up** if sleepiness persists on adequate PAP: **MSLT mean sleep latency 8 minutes or less with 2 or more SOREMPs**, or **CSF hypocretin-1 110 pg/mL or less** for type 1. [ICSD-3-TR 2023]`]},{heading:`Management of stable OHS in OPD`,points:[`**PAP therapy is first line** for every stable ambulatory patient with OHS - it lowers PaCO2, improves sleepiness and quality of life and reduces admissions. [ATS 2019]`,`**CPAP rather than NIV** is the initial treatment for stable OHS with **coexisting severe OSA (AHI 30/h or more)** - the Pickwick trial showed CPAP and NIV gave similar long-term hospital days, PaCO2 and mortality. [ATS 2019]`,`**Bilevel NIV (BPAP-ST)** is used when OSA is mild or absent (pure hypoventilation), when hypoxaemia or hypercapnia persists on adequate CPAP, or after an acute hypercapnic episode. [ATS 2019]`,`**NIV settings to start**: EPAP set to abolish obstructive events (often **8-10 cmH2O** in morbid obesity), **pressure support 8-10 cmH2O** above EPAP, and a **backup rate of about 12-16/min**; volume-assured modes (AVAPS/iVAPS) target 8 mL/kg ideal body weight. [BTS/ICS 2016]`,`**Weight loss** is the only treatment aimed at the cause: ATS suggests aiming for a **sustained loss of 25-30% of body weight**, which usually needs bariatric surgery or effective drugs. [ATS 2019]`,`**GLP-1 and dual agonists**: **tirzepatide** (titrated weekly to 10-15 mg subcutaneously) reduced AHI by about 25-29 events/h in SURMOUNT-OSA and is now an option alongside PAP in obese OSA; it helps OHS by weight loss. [FDA 2024]`,`**Supplemental oxygen** is added to PAP only if SpO2 stays under 88-90% despite optimal pressures; oxygen alone is not a treatment for OHS. [ATS 2019]`,`**Avoid respiratory depressants**: opioids, benzodiazepines, alcohol and sedating antihistamines; **acetazolamide and medroxyprogesterone are not recommended** as routine therapy. [ATS 2019]`,`**Adherence**: aim for PAP use **4 hours or more a night on 70% of nights** - PaCO2 falls in proportion to hours of use; a fall in bicarbonate and morning headache relief show it is working. [ATS 2019]`]},{heading:`Management of central sleep apnoea`,points:[`**Treat the cause first**: optimise heart-failure therapy (ARNI, beta-blocker, MRA, SGLT2 inhibitor, diuretic, CRT where indicated) - CSB often improves as filling pressure falls. [AASM 2025]`,`**CPAP** is the first PAP option for CSA with HF; the **CANPAP trial (2005)** found no survival benefit overall, but patients whose AHI fell under 15 on CPAP did better. [AASM 2025]`,`**SERVE-HF (2015)**: **adaptive servo-ventilation (ASV)** in HFrEF with **LVEF 45% or less and predominantly central apnoea** increased **cardiovascular mortality (HR 1.34)** and all-cause mortality - ASV is contraindicated in this group. [AASM 2025]`,`**ADVENT-HF (2024)**: a different (peak-flow-triggered) ASV device in HFrEF with mixed or obstructive apnoea showed no benefit on the primary outcome but also no excess deaths - it did not lift the SERVE-HF caution. [Lancet Respir Med 2024]`,`**ASV is acceptable** for CSA with **LVEF over 45%**, opioid-induced CSA, idiopathic CSA and treatment-emergent CSA that persists on CPAP. [AASM 2025]`,`**Other options**: **transvenous phrenic nerve stimulation** (remede system) for moderate-severe CSA, **low-flow nocturnal oxygen**, and **acetazolamide 250 mg at bedtime** - conditional, specialist-led choices. [AASM 2025]`,`**Opioid-induced CSA**: taper the opioid where possible; if not, ASV or BPAP-ST with backup rate. [AASM 2025]`,`**High-altitude periodic breathing**: gradual ascent (not more than 500 m/day of sleeping altitude above 3000 m) and **acetazolamide 125 mg twice daily** started a day before ascent. [WMS 2024]`]},{heading:`Inpatient and emergency management`,points:[`**Admit**: drowsiness or confusion, **pH under 7.35 with PaCO2 over 45 mmHg** (acute-on-chronic acidosis), SpO2 under 88% despite oxygen, decompensated cor pulmonale, or arrhythmia. [BTS/ICS 2016]`,`**Controlled oxygen** to a target of **SpO2 88-92%** via Venturi mask (24-28%) - uncontrolled high-flow oxygen worsens hypercapnia in OHS just as in COPD. [BTS 2017]`,`**NIV (BPAP)** for acute hypercapnic acidosis: start IPAP around 15 cmH2O rising to 20-25, **EPAP 8 cmH2O or more** (higher than in COPD to splint the obese upper airway), recheck ABG at 1-2 hours. [BTS/ICS 2016]`,`**Intubate** if NIV fails (worsening pH or consciousness at 1-4 hours), if the airway cannot be protected, or in cardiac arrest - anticipate a **difficult airway and desaturation**; preoxygenate head-up. [BTS/ICS 2016]`,`**Position semi-upright (reverse Trendelenburg or 30-45 degrees)**: supine posture shifts abdominal fat cephalad and collapses the bases. [BTS/ICS 2016]`,`**Diuresis** for cor pulmonale, **thromboprophylaxis** (weight-adjusted enoxaparin), and avoid sedatives and opioids. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Discharge on NIV** if OHS is suspected after an acute hypercapnic admission, then confirm the diagnosis and titrate PAP in a sleep lab **within 2-3 months**. [ATS 2019]`,`Patients with severe CSB in acute decompensated heart failure usually improve as congestion clears; treat the heart failure and reassess sleep breathing once stable. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Peri-operative**: OHS carries higher risk of postoperative respiratory failure, heart failure, ICU transfer and prolonged stay; screen obese surgical patients with STOP-BANG plus bicarbonate, and bring the patient's own PAP to hospital (see the Pre-operative pulmonary evaluation topic). [SASM 2016]`,`**Pregnancy**: obese pregnant women with OSA or OHS have more pre-eclampsia, gestational diabetes and caesarean delivery; CPAP is safe throughout pregnancy, and pressures may need increasing as weight rises. [ATS 2019]`,`**Elderly**: CSA rises with age, AF and stroke; avoid long-acting benzodiazepines for insomnia, which worsen central and obstructive apnoea. [AASM 2025]`,`**CKD and dialysis**: CSA and OSA are common from fluid overload and metabolic acidosis; nocturnal dialysis and fluid control reduce sleep apnoea. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Stroke**: up to half of stroke patients have sleep apnoea; CSB appears with bilateral hemispheric or brainstem lesions and often improves over weeks. [AASM 2025]`,`**Children**: congenital central hypoventilation (PHOX2B) presents with neonatal apnoea and cyanosis in sleep and needs lifelong ventilation; obese adolescents can also develop OHS. [ICSD-3-TR 2023]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: if polysomnography is unavailable, a high bicarbonate plus ABG hypercapnia and overnight oximetry is enough to start auto-CPAP or BPAP-ST, with follow-up ABG to confirm response. [ATS 2019]`,`**Opioid substitution therapy and chronic pain clinics**: screen patients on methadone or buprenorphine for central apnoea before adding benzodiazepines or gabapentinoids. [AASM 2025]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Untreated OHS** carries high mortality: about **23% at 18 months** in hospitalised patients, versus about 9% in equally obese people without hypoventilation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pulmonary hypertension** is present in about **50% of OHS** patients (much more than in OSA alone), leading to **cor pulmonale**. [ATS 2019]`,`**Cardiometabolic**: heart failure, systemic hypertension, insulin resistance and type 2 diabetes, and atrial fibrillation are more frequent than in eucapnic obesity. [ATS 2019]`,`**Acute-on-chronic hypercapnic respiratory failure**, often triggered by infection, surgery, sedation or uncontrolled oxygen. [BTS/ICS 2016]`,`**Secondary polycythaemia** raises the risk of venous thromboembolism and stroke; it reverses with effective PAP. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neurocognitive and road safety**: sleepiness doubles to triples the risk of road traffic accidents, and morning headaches, poor memory and depression impair work. [ERS 2021]`,`**CSA in heart failure** independently predicts death and readmission; treating the heart failure is what changes prognosis. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**With PAP adherence**, PaCO2, bicarbonate, sleepiness and pulmonary pressures improve over weeks to months, and survival improves in observational cohorts. [ATS 2019]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring; driving and counselling`,points:[`**Refer to a sleep or pulmonary centre** (most district hospitals lack polysomnography): suspected OHS, any CSA, failure of CPAP, or sleepiness with a professional driver. [AASM 2025]`,`**Review at 4-8 weeks** after starting PAP: download the device data (hours of use, residual AHI, leak) and recheck **ABG or bicarbonate**; target awake PaCO2 under 45 mmHg. [ATS 2019]`,`**Then every 6-12 months**: weight, BP, sugar, adherence, mask fit, haematocrit, and echocardiography if pulmonary hypertension was present. [ATS 2019]`,`**Persistent hypercapnia on CPAP** after 2-3 months of good use means switch to BPAP-ST or volume-assured NIV. [ATS 2019]`,`**Driving**: advise patients with excessive sleepiness from untreated OSA, OHS or narcolepsy **not to drive until treated and alert**; European rules bar driving with moderate-severe OSA and sleepiness until adherence and alertness are shown. [EU Directive 2014/85]`,`**India**: there is no OSA-specific driving rule; licence applicants self-declare fitness on **Form 1**, and transport-vehicle drivers need a medical certificate on **Form 1-A** - the doctor should record sleepiness and the advice given. [Central Motor Vehicles Rules 1989]`,`**Counselling**: weight loss with diet and activity (NP-NCD lifestyle clinics), avoid alcohol and sedatives, sleep hygiene, and never stop PAP before surgery or travel. [NP-NCD 2023]`,`**Insomnia pointer**: first line for chronic insomnia is **CBT-I** (stimulus control, sleep restriction, cognitive therapy); hypnotics are short-term adjuncts. [ESRS 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**ICSD-3-TR (2023)** revised the text of sleep-related breathing disorders and kept treatment-emergent CSA as a distinct entity. [ICSD-3-TR 2023]`,`**AASM Scoring Manual version 3 (2023)** consolidated the rules for central apnoea, Cheyne-Stokes breathing (cycle length 40 seconds or more) and sleep hypoventilation. [AASM Scoring Manual v3 2023]`,`**AASM clinical practice guideline on treatment of central sleep apnoea (2025)**: conditional recommendations for CPAP, ASV (not in HFrEF with LVEF 45% or less), BPAP with backup rate, phrenic nerve stimulation, low-flow oxygen and acetazolamide. [AASM 2025]`,`**ADVENT-HF (Lancet Respir Med 2024)**: ASV neutral in HFrEF with sleep apnoea, without the excess mortality of SERVE-HF; guidelines still advise against ASV for predominant CSA with LVEF 45% or less. [Lancet Respir Med 2024]`,`**Tirzepatide approved for moderate-severe OSA in obesity (US FDA, December 2024)** after SURMOUNT-OSA - the first drug approved for OSA; it is an adjunct, not a replacement, for PAP in OHS. [FDA 2024]`,`**European Insomnia Guideline update (ESRS 2023)** reaffirmed CBT-I as first-line therapy; orexin receptor antagonists (daridorexant) were added as an option for short-term use. [ESRS 2023]`,`**ATS 2019 OHS guideline** remains current: bicarbonate screen, CPAP first with severe OSA, NIV at discharge after acute hypercapnia, and 25-30% weight loss target. [ATS 2019]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic question**: Define OHS and outline its diagnosis and management - start with the triad **obesity + awake hypercapnia + exclusion of other causes**. [ATS 2019]`,`**Commonest mistake**: diagnosing OHS from overnight oximetry alone - hypercapnia must be shown on an awake ABG. [ATS 2019]`,`**Second mistake**: giving high-flow oxygen to an obese drowsy patient - it worsens CO2 narcosis; target 88-92%. [BTS 2017]`,`**Bicarbonate cut-off 27 mmol/L** is the screening number examiners want; PaCO2 cut-off is 45 mmHg. [ATS 2019]`,`**SERVE-HF in one line**: ASV kills in HFrEF (LVEF 45% or less) with predominant central apnoea. [AASM 2025]`,`**Cheyne-Stokes vs Biot**: CSB is a smooth crescendo-decrescendo with apnoea (HF, stroke); Biot (ataxic) breathing is irregular clusters with pauses (opioids, medulla lesions). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Viva**: why is OHS a CO2-retaining disease but OSA usually not? Because interapnoeic compensation and renal bicarbonate excretion fail in OHS. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Sleep-related breathing disorders compared`,columns:[`Feature`,`Obstructive sleep apnoea`,`Obesity hypoventilation syndrome`,`CSA with Cheyne-Stokes breathing`],rows:[[`Core defect`,`Upper airway collapse in sleep`,`Load plus blunted drive; failed CO2 clearance`,`High loop gain; PaCO2 near apnoeic threshold`],[`Typical patient`,`Obese, large neck, snorer`,`BMI over 35-40, oedema, sleepy`,`HFrEF, AF, stroke; often not obese`],[`Awake PaCO2`,`Normal (under 45 mmHg)`,`45 mmHg or more (defining)`,`Low-normal (often under 38 mmHg)`],[`Serum bicarbonate`,`Usually under 27 mmol/L`,`27 mmol/L or more`,`Normal or low`],[`Effort during apnoea`,`Present (paradox)`,`Mixed; hypoventilation in REM`,`Absent`],[`Oximetry pattern`,`Sawtooth dips with quick recovery`,`Sustained REM desaturation`,`Cyclic dips, cycle 45-90 s`],[`First treatment`,`CPAP, weight loss`,`CPAP if severe OSA; else BPAP-ST`,`Optimise HF; CPAP; ASV only if LVEF over 45%`]]},{heading:`Diagnostic criteria and cut-offs`,columns:[`Entity`,`Criterion`,`Number to remember`,`Source`],rows:[[`OHS`,`BMI plus awake hypercapnia, other causes excluded`,`BMI 30 or more; PaCO2 45 mmHg or more`,`ATS 2019`],[`OHS screen`,`Serum bicarbonate rules out when low`,`HCO3- under 27 mmol/L`,`ATS 2019`],[`Sleep hypoventilation`,`CO2 rise during sleep`,`Over 55 mmHg for 10 min, or 10 mmHg rise to over 50`,`AASM Scoring Manual v3`],[`Central sleep apnoea`,`Central events per hour of sleep`,`5/h or more and over 50% of events central`,`ICSD-3-TR 2023`],[`Cheyne-Stokes breathing`,`Crescendo-decrescendo cycles`,`3 or more cycles, cycle length 40 s or more`,`AASM Scoring Manual v3`],[`Narcolepsy`,`MSLT or CSF hypocretin`,`Mean latency 8 min or less with 2+ SOREMPs; hypocretin 110 pg/mL or less`,`ICSD-3-TR 2023`],[`Chronic insomnia`,`Frequency and duration`,`3 nights/week for 3 months with daytime impairment`,`ICSD-3-TR 2023`]]},{heading:`Positive airway pressure and drug treatments`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`CPAP (device)`,`Fixed or auto 5-20 cmH2O via nasal or oronasal mask`,`Nightly, lifelong; review download at 4-8 weeks`,`Mask leak, dry nose, aerophagia; fails if pure hypoventilation`],[`BPAP-ST / AVAPS (device)`,`EPAP 8-10, pressure support 8-10 cmH2O, backup rate 12-16/min`,`Nightly; titrate to PaCO2 under 45 mmHg`,`Leak, asynchrony; needs trained follow-up`],[`Adaptive servo-ventilation (device)`,`Auto EPAP with variable pressure support`,`Nightly; specialist titration`,`Contraindicated in HFrEF LVEF 45% or less with predominant CSA (SERVE-HF)`],[`Acetazolamide`,`250 mg orally at bedtime (CSA); 125 mg twice daily (altitude)`,`Altitude: from 1 day before ascent until 2 days at top altitude`,`Paraesthesia, polyuria, metabolic acidosis, sulfonamide allergy; avoid in OHS with hypercapnia`],[`Tirzepatide`,`2.5 mg SC weekly, increase by 2.5 mg every 4 weeks to 10-15 mg`,`Long term with lifestyle change`,`Nausea, vomiting, pancreatitis; avoid with medullary thyroid cancer or MEN2 history`],[`Controlled oxygen`,`Venturi 24-28% or 1-2 L/min bleed into PAP circuit`,`Only if SpO2 under 88-90% on optimal PAP`,`Worsens hypercapnia if used alone; fire risk with smoking`],[`Modafinil (narcolepsy, residual sleepiness)`,`100-200 mg orally each morning`,`Long term with review`,`Headache, insomnia, raised BP; reduces OCP efficacy`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of chronic daytime hypercapnia`,columns:[`Cause`,`Discriminating clue`,`Key test`],rows:[[`Obesity hypoventilation`,`BMI 30 or more, OSA, normal ratio on spirometry`,`ABG plus polysomnography`],[`COPD`,`Smoker or biomass exposure, wheeze`,`FEV1/FVC under 0.70 post-bronchodilator`],[`Neuromuscular disease`,`Orthopnoea, weakness, paradoxical abdomen`,`Supine VC fall over 20-25%, low MIP`],[`Kyphoscoliosis / post-TB fibrothorax`,`Visible deformity or old TB`,`Chest X-ray, severe restriction`],[`Hypothyroidism`,`Myxoedema, bradycardia, cold intolerance`,`TSH`],[`Drugs and alcohol`,`Opioid, benzodiazepine, alcohol use`,`Drug history, urine toxicology`],[`Metabolic alkalosis`,`Diuretics, vomiting; PaCO2 rise is compensatory`,`Primary high HCO3- with pH over 7.45`]]}],redFlags:[`Obese patient who is **drowsy or confused** with SpO2 under 88% - suspect acute hypercapnic respiratory failure; ABG and NIV now.`,`**pH under 7.35 with PaCO2 over 45 mmHg** in a known or suspected OHS patient.`,`**Cor pulmonale** (raised JVP, oedema, hepatomegaly) in an obese snorer.`,`Excessive sleepiness in a **professional driver** or someone with a near-miss or crash.`,`Cheyne-Stokes breathing while **awake** in heart failure - very low output; admit.`,`Haematocrit over 55% or **awake SpO2 under 90%** with no lung disease.`,`Central apnoeas on a polysomnogram in someone taking **high-dose opioids with benzodiazepines**.`],pearls:[`OHS triad: **Obesity, Hypercapnia awake, Sleep-disordered breathing** - and a diagnosis of exclusion.`,`**Bicarbonate under 27 mmol/L rules OHS out**; 27 or more means do an ABG.`,`With severe OSA, **CPAP first** even in OHS (Pickwick); BPAP if pure hypoventilation or CPAP fails.`,`**Never oxygen alone** in OHS - it raises PaCO2; target SpO2 88-92%.`,`**SERVE-HF**: no ASV in HFrEF with LVEF 45% or less and predominant CSA.`,`Cheyne-Stokes in HF has a **long cycle (45-90 s)** because circulation time is prolonged.`,`OHS needs a **25-30% sustained weight loss** to reverse - bariatric surgery or GLP-1-based drugs.`,`**Discharge on NIV** after an acute hypercapnic admission in suspected OHS; titrate within 2-3 months.`,`Treatment-emergent CSA on CPAP usually **settles within 2-3 months** - keep CPAP going.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-hypoventilation-sleep-q1`,stem:`A 46-year-old man with BMI 38 kg/m2 and moderate OSA on a home sleep study is being considered for OHS. Clinical suspicion is moderate. Which result makes OHS very unlikely and allows you to skip an arterial blood gas?`,options:[`Awake SpO2 of 95% on room air`,`Serum bicarbonate of 24 mmol/L`,`Epworth sleepiness score of 6`,`Normal chest X-ray`],answer:1,explanation:`ATS 2019 recommends serum bicarbonate as the screen in low-to-moderate probability: a value under 27 mmol/L has a high negative predictive value and an ABG can be skipped. A normal awake SpO2 does not exclude hypercapnia, sleepiness scores do not measure CO2, and a normal X-ray says nothing about ventilation.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-hypoventilation-sleep-q2`,stem:`A 62-year-old man with ischaemic cardiomyopathy (LVEF 30%) on full guideline-directed therapy has an AHI of 38/h, 80% of events central with a crescendo-decrescendo pattern. Which treatment should be avoided?`,options:[`Continuous positive airway pressure`,`Optimising diuretic dose`,`Adaptive servo-ventilation`,`Cardiac resynchronisation if eligible`],answer:2,explanation:`SERVE-HF showed that adaptive servo-ventilation increased cardiovascular and all-cause mortality in HFrEF with LVEF 45% or less and predominantly central apnoea, so it is contraindicated here. CPAP is an accepted first PAP option, and optimising diuretics and CRT reduce filling pressures and often improve Cheyne-Stokes breathing.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-hypoventilation-sleep-q3`,stem:`A 52-year-old woman with BMI 44 kg/m2 has awake PaCO2 52 mmHg, pH 7.37, normal TSH, normal spirometry ratio and severe OSA (AHI 58/h). What is the preferred initial long-term treatment per ATS 2019?`,options:[`Nocturnal oxygen at 2 L/min`,`Bilevel NIV with a backup rate`,`Medroxyprogesterone with acetazolamide`,`CPAP with weight-loss programme`],answer:3,explanation:`For stable OHS with severe OSA, ATS 2019 suggests CPAP rather than NIV as initial therapy, supported by the Pickwick trial showing similar outcomes; weight loss is added. Oxygen alone worsens hypercapnia, BPAP is reserved for pure hypoventilation or CPAP failure, and respiratory stimulant drugs are not recommended.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`respiratory-hypoventilation-sleep-q4`,stem:`An obese man is brought drowsy to casualty with SpO2 78% on air. ABG on air: pH 7.26, PaCO2 72 mmHg, HCO3- 32 mmol/L. What is the most appropriate immediate step?`,options:[`Non-rebreather mask at 15 L/min to reach SpO2 98%`,`Controlled oxygen to SpO2 88-92% and start bilevel NIV`,`Intravenous sodium bicarbonate`,`Intravenous doxapram infusion as sole therapy`],answer:1,explanation:`This is acute-on-chronic hypercapnic respiratory failure (raised bicarbonate shows chronicity). Controlled oxygen to 88-92% with bilevel NIV is the standard of care. High-flow oxygen to 98% worsens CO2 retention, bicarbonate infusion adds CO2 load, and doxapram is no substitute for NIV.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-hypoventilation-sleep-q5`,stem:`Why does Cheyne-Stokes breathing in heart failure have a longer cycle length (often over 60 seconds) than idiopathic central sleep apnoea?`,options:[`Prolonged lung-to-chemoreceptor circulation time from low cardiac output`,`Upper airway collapse during each apnoea`,`Opioid depression of the pre-Botzinger complex`,`Retention of bicarbonate by the kidney overnight`],answer:0,explanation:`Low cardiac output delays the arrival of blood gas changes at the carotid body, so the feedback loop is slower and each crescendo-decrescendo cycle lasts 45-90 seconds. Upper airway collapse defines obstructive events, opioid depression causes irregular ataxic breathing, and bicarbonate retention is the mechanism of OHS.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-hypoventilation-sleep-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Define obesity hypoventilation syndrome. Discuss its pathophysiology, diagnosis and management.`,openingLines:[`Obesity hypoventilation syndrome is awake daytime hypercapnia (PaCO2 45 mmHg or more) in a person with BMI 30 kg/m2 or more, with sleep-disordered breathing, after other causes of hypoventilation are excluded (ATS 2019).`,`About 90% have coexisting OSA; untreated it carries about 23% mortality at 18 months, yet it is under-diagnosed and often mislabelled as COPD.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`Triad: BMI 30 or more, awake PaCO2 45 mmHg or more, exclusion of COPD, neuromuscular, chest-wall, thyroid and drug causes.`,`8-20% of obese patients in sleep clinics; commoner above BMI 40; rising in India with NFHS-5 obesity trends.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Reduced chest-wall compliance and ERV, increased work of breathing.`,`Blunted CO2 and hypoxic drive; leptin resistance.`,`Overnight CO2 rise during apnoeas, renal bicarbonate retention, reset chemoreceptor - daytime hypercapnia.`,`Hypoxaemia leads to pulmonary hypertension, cor pulmonale and polycythaemia.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Snoring, apnoeas, sleepiness, morning headache, exertional breathlessness, oedema.`,`Awake SpO2 low, signs of right heart failure, plethora; acute hypercapnic failure as first presentation.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Serum bicarbonate screen: under 27 mmol/L rules out; 27 or more, do ABG.`,`ABG: PaCO2 45 mmHg or more with compensated pH.`,`Polysomnography with CO2 monitoring; spirometry, TSH, CBC, chest X-ray, ECG, echocardiography.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Stable: CPAP first if severe OSA; BPAP-ST if pure hypoventilation or CPAP failure; adherence 4 h or more nightly.`,`Weight loss target 25-30%: lifestyle, GLP-1-based drugs, bariatric surgery.`,`Avoid sedatives; oxygen only added to PAP; no respiratory stimulants.`,`Acute: controlled oxygen 88-92%, NIV with higher EPAP, discharge on NIV and titrate within 2-3 months.`]},{heading:`Prognosis and follow-up`,points:[`Review at 4-8 weeks with device download and ABG or bicarbonate; then 6-12 monthly.`,`Driving advice, peri-operative precautions and NP-NCD lifestyle linkage.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: obese patient with OSA - bicarbonate - ABG - polysomnography - CPAP or BPAP`,`Diagram of the vicious cycle: apnoea, CO2 rise, bicarbonate retention, blunted drive, daytime hypercapnia`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and epidemiology`,marks:1},{part:`Pathophysiology`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:1},{part:`Diagnosis with cut-offs`,marks:2},{part:`Management stable and acute`,marks:3},{part:`Follow-up and prognosis`,marks:1}],keywords:[`OHS`,`bicarbonate 27`,`PaCO2 45`,`CPAP`,`BPAP-ST`,`Pickwick`,`weight loss`]},{id:`respiratory-hypoventilation-sleep-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on central sleep apnoea and Cheyne-Stokes breathing in heart failure.`,openingLines:[`Central sleep apnoea is recurrent cessation of airflow without respiratory effort during sleep (5 or more central events per hour, over half of all events), and Cheyne-Stokes breathing is its crescendo-decrescendo form seen in heart failure.`,`It affects 25-40% of patients with HFrEF and predicts higher mortality.`],answer:[{heading:`Mechanism`,points:[`Pulmonary congestion stimulates J receptors, chronic hyperventilation, PaCO2 near the apnoeic threshold.`,`High loop gain and long circulation time give cycles of 45-90 seconds.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Bed partner reports waxing-waning breathing; insomnia, PND, fatigue.`,`Polysomnography: central apnoeas, 3 or more CSB cycles of 40 s or more; echo for LVEF.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Optimise HF therapy and CRT first.`,`CPAP; ASV contraindicated if LVEF 45% or less (SERVE-HF); ASV acceptable with LVEF over 45%.`,`Phrenic nerve stimulation, low-flow oxygen, acetazolamide as specialist options (AASM 2025).`]}],mustDraw:[`Sketch of crescendo-decrescendo airflow with central apnoea and the loop-gain cycle`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and mechanism`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:1},{part:`Treatment including SERVE-HF`,marks:2}],keywords:[`central sleep apnoea`,`Cheyne-Stokes`,`loop gain`,`SERVE-HF`,`ASV`,`LVEF 45%`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - chapters on sleep apnoea and disorders of ventilation`,`Mokhlesi B et al. Evaluation and Management of Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome: An Official American Thoracic Society Clinical Practice Guideline, Am J Respir Crit Care Med 2019`,`American Academy of Sleep Medicine. Treatment of central sleep apnea in adults: an AASM clinical practice guideline, J Clin Sleep Med 2025`,`American Academy of Sleep Medicine. International Classification of Sleep Disorders, 3rd edition, text revision (ICSD-3-TR), 2023; AASM Manual for the Scoring of Sleep and Associated Events, version 3, 2023`,`Cowie MR et al. Adaptive servo-ventilation for central sleep apnea in systolic heart failure (SERVE-HF), N Engl J Med 2015; Bradley TD et al. ADVENT-HF, Lancet Respir Med 2024`,`Davidson AC et al. BTS/ICS Guideline for the Ventilatory Management of Acute Hypercapnic Respiratory Failure in Adults, Thorax 2016; O'Driscoll BR et al. BTS Guideline for Oxygen Use in Adults in Healthcare and Emergency Settings, Thorax 2017`,`Malhotra A et al. Tirzepatide for the treatment of obstructive sleep apnea and obesity (SURMOUNT-OSA), N Engl J Med 2024`,`Riemann D et al. The European Insomnia Guideline: an update (ESRS), J Sleep Res 2023; International Institute for Population Sciences. NFHS-5 India Report, 2021`]}),c.push({id:`respiratory-pulmonary-rehab-home-oxygen`,title:`Pulmonary rehabilitation and long-term home oxygen`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Pulmonary rehabilitation (PR)** is a comprehensive, assessment-based programme of **supervised exercise training, education and behaviour change** that improves breathlessness, exercise capacity and quality of life in chronic lung disease, while **long-term oxygen therapy (LTOT)** for **15 hours or more a day** prolongs survival only in **severe chronic resting hypoxaemia (PaO2 55 mmHg or less, or 56-59 mmHg with cor pulmonale or polycythaemia)**.`,keywords:[`pulmonary rehabilitation`,`PR`,`telerehabilitation`,`exercise training`,`6-minute walk test`,`6MWT`,`LTOT`,`long-term oxygen therapy`,`home oxygen`,`oxygen concentrator`,`ambulatory oxygen`,`portable oxygen concentrator`,`NOTT`,`MRC trial`,`LOTT`,`INOX`,`cor pulmonale`,`oxygen fire safety`,`post-TB lung disease`,`Borg scale`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Pulmonary rehabilitation** is a comprehensive intervention based on thorough patient assessment, followed by patient-tailored therapies including **exercise training, education and behaviour change**, designed to improve the physical and psychological condition of people with chronic respiratory disease and promote long-term health-enhancing behaviour. [ATS/ERS 2013]`,`**Long-term oxygen therapy (LTOT)** is supplemental oxygen used at home for **at least 15 hours a day** (ideally continuously) to correct chronic resting hypoxaemia. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Global burden**: chronic respiratory diseases affect over **450 million people** and COPD is the third leading cause of death worldwide. [GOLD 2025]`,`**India**: an estimated **55 million people have COPD** and chronic respiratory diseases cause about **11% of all deaths**, the second-largest NCD killer after cardiovascular disease. [GBD India 2018]`,`**Post-TB lung disease**: India notifies over **2.5 million TB cases a year**, and up to half of survivors have persistent lung impairment - a large, growing group needing PR and sometimes oxygen. [WHO Global TB Report 2024]`,`**Access gap**: fewer than **5%** of eligible COPD patients worldwide receive PR, and in India structured programmes exist mainly in tertiary centres - home-based and tele-rehabilitation are the realistic route. [ATS 2023]`,`**Home oxygen in India** expanded sharply after the COVID-19 second wave (2021), when domestic **oxygen concentrators** became widely available, with both good access and many unsupervised users. [MoHFW 2021]`]},{heading:`Indications and who benefits`,points:[`**COPD**: anyone with **mMRC breathlessness grade 2 or more** or functional limitation despite optimal inhalers (GOLD groups B and E). [GOLD 2025]`,`**After a COPD exacerbation**: start PR **within 3-4 weeks of hospital discharge** - this reduces readmission; starting during the admission is not recommended. [ATS 2023]`,`**Interstitial lung disease**: PR improves 6MWD, breathlessness and quality of life in IPF and other ILD; benefits are greatest in milder disease and wane faster. [ATS 2023]`,`**Post-tuberculosis lung disease**: PR is recommended for survivors with breathlessness or impairment - included in the International Clinical Standards for post-TB lung disease. [IJTLD Clinical Standards 2021]`,`**Others**: bronchiectasis, **pulmonary hypertension** (supervised, low-intensity), asthma with persistent limitation, before and after **lung resection or transplant**, and post-COVID breathlessness. [ATS 2023]`,`**Relative contraindications**: unstable angina or recent MI, severe symptomatic aortic stenosis, uncontrolled arrhythmia or hypertension, severe cognitive or psychiatric illness that prevents participation, and locomotor problems that prevent exercise. [BTS 2013]`,`**Age, smoking status and oxygen use are not barriers**: elderly patients, current smokers (with cessation support) and those on LTOT all benefit. [BTS 2013]`]},{heading:`Physiology: why exercise helps when lungs cannot change`,points:[`**Peripheral muscle dysfunction**: COPD limbs show fewer type I (oxidative) fibres, fewer mitochondria and capillaries, so lactate appears early and drives ventilation - training reverses this. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lower lactate means lower ventilation**: after training, the same workload produces less lactic acid and CO2, lowering minute ventilation, respiratory rate and **dynamic hyperinflation**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Slower breathing** lengthens expiratory time, reducing air-trapping in COPD - which is why breathlessness improves without any change in FEV1. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Desensitisation to dyspnoea**: graded exposure lowers fear and the perceived intensity of breathlessness, breaking the **breathlessness-inactivity-deconditioning spiral**. [ATS/ERS 2013]`,`**Hypoxaemia physiology**: chronic alveolar hypoxia causes **hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction**, raising pulmonary artery pressure and leading to RV hypertrophy and **cor pulmonale**; renal erythropoietin causes polycythaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why LTOT saves lives**: correcting hypoxaemia for most of the day slows the rise in pulmonary pressure, lowers haematocrit and reduces nocturnal hypoxic stress on the heart. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why only severe hypoxaemia**: on the steep part of the oxyhaemoglobin curve (PaO2 under 60 mmHg), small PaO2 gains give large saturation gains; above it, extra oxygen adds little content. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Assessment before PR and before oxygen`,points:[`**Baseline assessment**: mMRC, **CAT score**, spirometry, comorbidities (cardiac, musculoskeletal, anxiety, depression), BMI and nutrition, and resting and exertional SpO2. [ATS/ERS 2013]`,`**Field walking tests**: **6-minute walk test (6MWT)** - minimal important difference about **30 m**; **incremental shuttle walk test (ISWT)** - MID about **35-50 m**, used to set walking speed. [ERS/ATS 2014]`,`**Quality-of-life tools**: CAT (MID 2 points), SGRQ (MID 4 units), CRQ (MID 0.5 per item); used before and after PR to show benefit. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Cardiac safety**: ECG for all, and exercise test or cardiology review for chest pain, arrhythmia or uncontrolled BP before exercise training. [BTS 2013]`,`**Screen for oxygen need with pulse oximetry**: refer for ABG if resting **SpO2 92% or less** in stable disease; SpO2 is not accurate enough on its own to prescribe LTOT. [BTS 2015]`,`**Clinical stability**: assess for LTOT only when stable for at least **8 weeks after an exacerbation**, on optimal therapy. [BTS 2015]`,`**Two ABGs at least 3 weeks apart** on room air at rest, both meeting criteria, are needed before LTOT is started. [BTS 2015]`,`**Exertional assessment** for ambulatory oxygen: 6MWT or shuttle walk on air and then on oxygen - look for **SpO2 88% or less on exertion** and whether oxygen improves distance or breathlessness. [ATS 2020]`]},{heading:`Components of pulmonary rehabilitation`,points:[`**Duration and frequency**: **6-12 weeks** (usually 8), with at least **two supervised sessions a week** plus home exercise; 12 or more sessions are needed for benefit. [BTS 2013]`,`**Endurance training**: walking or cycling for **20-30 minutes** at **60-80% of peak work rate**, or a **Borg dyspnoea score of 4-6** (moderate to somewhat severe); **interval training** suits those too breathless for continuous effort. [ATS/ERS 2013]`,`**Resistance (strength) training**: upper and lower limb, **2-4 sets of 6-12 repetitions at 50-85% of one-repetition maximum**, 2-3 times a week - addresses quadriceps weakness that predicts mortality. [ATS/ERS 2013]`,`**Breathing techniques**: **pursed-lip breathing** (slows expiration, splints airways, reduces air trapping), diaphragmatic breathing, recovery positions (forward lean with arms supported). [ATS/ERS 2013]`,`**Education and self-management**: disease understanding, **inhaler technique**, written action plan for exacerbations, energy conservation, airway clearance (active cycle of breathing, oscillating PEP device) in bronchiectasis. [ATS/ERS 2013]`,`**Nutrition**: identify **BMI under 21 kg/m2** or weight loss (common in Indian COPD from biomass and post-TB) and give calorie-protein supplements combined with exercise. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Psychosocial support**: screen for anxiety and depression (each present in up to 40% of COPD), and use CBT-based breathlessness management. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Inspiratory muscle training** is not routinely added; neuromuscular electrical stimulation is for those unable to exercise. [ATS/ERS 2013]`,`**Oxygen during training**: used if exertional SpO2 falls under about 88%, so training intensity can be achieved. [ATS 2023]`]},{heading:`Evidence for PR`,points:[`**Cochrane review (2015, 65 RCTs)**: PR in stable COPD gives a clinically important improvement in **quality of life** and **6MWD of about 44 m** above usual care - effects larger than any inhaler. [Cochrane 2015]`,`**Post-exacerbation PR** (Cochrane 2016): reduces hospital readmissions and improves quality of life when started soon after discharge. [Cochrane 2016]`,`**ATS 2023 clinical practice guideline**: strong recommendation for PR in stable COPD and after COPD hospitalisation; conditional for ILD and pulmonary hypertension; **telerehabilitation is an acceptable alternative** to centre-based PR. [ATS 2023]`,`**Benefits fade over 6-12 months** unless exercise continues; maintenance programmes and repeat courses are reasonable. [ATS/ERS 2013]`,`**Home-based PR** with structured programmes and phone follow-up gives results similar to centre-based PR in RCTs - useful in rural India. [ATS 2023]`,`**PR does not change FEV1 or mortality in stable COPD** - its value is in symptoms, function and admissions. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Cost-effectiveness**: PR is among the most cost-effective COPD interventions, ahead of most inhaled drugs per quality-adjusted life year. [GOLD 2025]`]},{heading:`LTOT criteria and the trial evidence`,points:[`**COPD LTOT criteria**: resting **PaO2 55 mmHg (7.3 kPa) or less**, or **SaO2 88% or less**, confirmed twice over 3 weeks in stable disease. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Or PaO2 56-59 mmHg (7.3-8.0 kPa)** with **cor pulmonale / peripheral oedema, pulmonary hypertension, or polycythaemia (haematocrit over 55%)**. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Target**: titrate flow to raise resting **PaO2 to 60 mmHg (8 kPa) or more / SpO2 90% or more**, without unacceptable rise in PaCO2 or fall in pH. [BTS 2015]`,`**NOTT (1980)**: continuous oxygen (mean about 18 h/day) lowered mortality compared with 12 h nocturnal oxygen. [NOTT 1980]`,`**MRC trial (1981)**: **15 h/day** oxygen improved survival versus no oxygen - the origin of the 15-hour rule. [MRC 1981]`,`**LOTT (2016)**: in **moderate** resting (SpO2 89-93%) or exercise-induced desaturation, long-term oxygen gave no benefit in death, admissions or quality of life. [NEJM 2016]`,`**INOX (2020)**: nocturnal oxygen for isolated nocturnal desaturation in COPD did not reduce death or progression to LTOT. [NEJM 2020]`,`**ILD and other causes**: same thresholds are applied by extrapolation in ILD, bronchiectasis, post-TB disease, CF and pulmonary hypertension (strong recommendation in ILD). [ATS 2020]`,`**Not for breathlessness alone**: in non-hypoxaemic breathless patients oxygen is no better than air - use a **hand-held fan**, breathing techniques and palliative measures. [BTS 2015]`]},{heading:`Prescribing and delivering home oxygen`,points:[`**Prescription must state**: source, **flow rate (L/min) at rest, on exertion and in sleep**, delivery device, and **hours per day (15 or more)**. [BTS 2015]`,`**Nasal cannula** at **1-4 L/min** is standard - each 1 L/min adds roughly 3-4% to FiO2 (about 24% at 1 L/min, 28% at 2 L/min); humidification is not needed at these flows. [BTS 2015]`,`**Night and exertion**: many patients need flow raised by **1 L/min during sleep and exercise**, unless hypercapnia worsens. [BTS 2015]`,`**Hypercapnic patients**: start at **1 L/min** and recheck ABG; a rise in PaCO2 of over 1 kPa (7.5 mmHg) or acidosis needs specialist review or NIV. [BTS 2015]`,`**Oxygen concentrator** (most Indian homes): pulls nitrogen out of room air by pressure-swing adsorption over zeolite; a **5 L/min model** delivers about **90-95% oxygen** at low flows, purity falling at the top flow; needs **steady electricity** (about 300-400 W) and a **backup cylinder** for power cuts. [WHO 2015]`,`**Cylinders**: useful for backup, transport and areas with unreliable power; refilling cost and weight limit their use for 15 h/day. [WHO 2015]`,`**Portable oxygen concentrators** (battery, often **pulse-dose**) suit mobile patients and air travel, but pulse settings are not equivalent to continuous L/min - check SpO2 on exertion with the device used. [ATS 2020]`,`**Hour meter**: concentrators record running hours, which is the best objective check of the 15 h/day adherence. [BTS 2015]`,`**Cost and support in India**: concentrators are bought or rented; some states, NGOs and palliative care networks lend them - discuss cost before prescribing so the patient is not left without supply. [MoHFW 2021]`]},{heading:`Ambulatory and short-burst oxygen`,points:[`**Ambulatory oxygen** is oxygen during exercise and daily activities from a portable source. [BTS 2015]`,`**Offer to patients already on LTOT who go out of the house**, so that the 15 h/day target is met and they are not housebound. [BTS 2015]`,`**Severe exertional desaturation (SpO2 88% or less) in COPD without resting hypoxaemia**: ATS 2020 makes a conditional suggestion for ambulatory oxygen if the walk test shows benefit; BTS advises against routine use - an area where guidelines differ. [ATS 2020]`,`**ILD with exertional desaturation**: ambulatory oxygen is suggested; the **AmbOx trial (2018)** showed improved quality of life and breathlessness in fibrotic ILD. [ATS 2020]`,`**Short-burst oxygen** (before or after exertion for symptom relief) is **not recommended** - it does not help breathlessness or recovery. [BTS 2015]`,`**Assessment protocol**: walk test on air, then repeat after rest with oxygen at 2-4 L/min (or portable device settings); prescribe the lowest flow that keeps SpO2 90% or more. [BTS 2015]`]},{heading:`Safety - smoking, fire and falls`,points:[`**Smoking with oxygen** causes **facial and airway burns and house fires**; patients and family must not smoke (including bidi, hookah or e-cigarettes) in the house. [BTS 2015]`,`**Current smokers**: the risk-benefit of LTOT is unclear and the fire risk is real - BTS advises that home oxygen is generally not prescribed to people who continue to smoke, after a documented risk assessment; offer intensive cessation support first. [BTS 2015]`,`**Keep oxygen at least 2 m from naked flames** - in Indian homes this includes the **gas stove, chulha, diya, agarbatti and mosquito coils**; do not cook while wearing a cannula. [BTS 2015]`,`**Fire breaks** (thermal fuses) fitted in the tubing stop flame travelling back to the concentrator. [BTS 2015]`,`**Avoid oil-based creams** (petroleum jelly) on the face or nose; use **water-based lubricants** for dry nasal mucosa. [BTS 2015]`,`**Trip hazard**: long tubing causes falls in elderly patients; tape tubing, keep it short and use a single route through the house. [BTS 2015]`,`**Electrical safety**: use a dedicated earthed socket, not an extension board; keep the concentrator 30 cm from walls and curtains for ventilation. [WHO 2015]`,`**Over-oxygenation in hypercapnic patients** causes morning headache and drowsiness - teach the family that more flow is not better. [BTS 2015]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Post-TB lung disease**: often mixed obstructive-restrictive with bronchiectasis and fibrosis; apply the same LTOT criteria, check for hypercapnia (chest-wall restriction) and offer PR; NTEP follows up treated patients for **2 years**. [IJTLD Clinical Standards 2021]`,`**ILD**: desaturation on exertion is marked and precedes resting hypoxaemia; higher flows (sometimes 4-6 L/min or more) may be needed - a 10 L/min concentrator or cylinder may be required. [ATS 2020]`,`**Pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale**: maintain **SpO2 90% or more** at rest and during sleep; oxygen reduces hypoxic vasoconstriction. [ESC/ERS 2022]`,`**Elderly and frail**: chair-based and interval exercise, falls prevention and simplified home programmes; cognitive impairment needs a carer trained in oxygen safety. [ATS/ERS 2013]`,`**Pregnancy with chronic lung disease**: keep SpO2 at least 94-95% for fetal oxygenation; PR-type exercise at moderate intensity is safe. [BTS 2017]`,`**Air travel**: patients on LTOT need in-flight oxygen; those with resting SpO2 in the borderline range need a **hypoxic challenge test** before flying. [BTS 2022]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: a home-based programme using walking at a set pace, stairs, chair-rise and water-bottle weights, with phone follow-up, is effective and costs almost nothing. [ATS 2023]`,`**Palliative care**: in end-stage disease, oxygen is continued for comfort if hypoxaemic; for breathlessness without hypoxaemia, fan, positioning and low-dose opioids are preferred (see End-stage lung disease topic). [BTS 2015]`]},{heading:`Complications, adherence and prognosis`,points:[`**Poor adherence**: only about half of LTOT patients reach 15 h/day; common reasons are misunderstanding (using oxygen only when breathless), cost of power and embarrassment. [BTS 2015]`,`**PR drop-out** is 20-30%, linked to transport difficulty, exacerbations, depression and current smoking. [ATS 2023]`,`**Nasal dryness, epistaxis and ear pressure sores** from cannulae; use soft tubing and water-based gel. [BTS 2015]`,`**CO2 retention** with excess flow in hypercapnic patients. [BTS 2015]`,`**Burns and fires**: most home oxygen burns are related to smoking; mortality from such fires is significant. [BTS 2015]`,`**Prognosis**: patients qualifying for LTOT have advanced disease - roughly 50% 5-year survival in the treated arms of the original trials; LTOT plus PR plus smoking cessation give the best outcomes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Psychological dependence on oxygen** and reduced activity when oxygen is used without assessment - avoid prescribing for breathlessness alone. [BTS 2015]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer for PR** every COPD patient with mMRC 2 or more and every patient after a COPD admission; refer ILD and post-TB lung disease with limitation. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Refer for oxygen assessment**: stable patient with **SpO2 92% or less**, or SpO2 94% or less with cor pulmonale, polycythaemia or pulmonary hypertension. [BTS 2015]`,`**LTOT review**: within **3 months** of starting (repeat ABG on prescribed flow), then **every 6-12 months** - SpO2, hours used, smoking status, safety, and whether the prescription is still needed. [BTS 2015]`,`**Withdraw LTOT** if the patient no longer meets criteria after recovery (for example, oxygen started at discharge after an exacerbation) - up to a third of such patients no longer qualify at 3 months. [BTS 2015]`,`**After PR**: repeat 6MWT and CAT at the end of the course, give a written home exercise plan, and review every 6-12 months; repeat PR after a decline or new exacerbation. [ATS/ERS 2013]`,`**Annual review**: vaccines (influenza, pneumococcal), inhaler technique, nutrition, mood and exacerbation plan. [GOLD 2025]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`**Smoking cessation** is the single most important step - brief advice plus **nicotine replacement or varenicline**, and the national **quitline 1800-11-2356**. [NTCP 2023]`,`**Biomass smoke** reduction with LPG (Pradhan Mantri Ujjwala Yojana) and better ventilation lowers COPD progression in women. [MoHFW 2023]`,`**NP-NCD (2023)** includes chronic respiratory diseases: screening, spirometry and follow-up through **Ayushman Arogya Mandir** and district NCD clinics, where a home exercise programme can be started. [NP-NCD 2023]`,`**Teach the family** the oxygen safety rules and how to recognise CO2 narcosis (drowsiness, headache, confusion). [BTS 2015]`,`**Stay active**: daily walking, aiming for gradually rising step counts, maintains PR gains. [ATS 2023]`,`**NTEP post-treatment follow-up** at 6, 12, 18 and 24 months is an opportunity to screen TB survivors for breathlessness and refer for PR. [NTEP 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**ATS Clinical Practice Guideline on Pulmonary Rehabilitation (2023)**: PR for stable COPD, after COPD hospitalisation, ILD and pulmonary hypertension; telerehabilitation acceptable. [ATS 2023]`,`**GOLD 2025/2026 reports**: PR recommended for groups B and E and after exacerbation; telerehabilitation and home-based programmes recognised as alternatives. [GOLD 2025]`,`**ATS 2020 Home Oxygen guideline** remains the current US statement: LTOT 15 h/day or more for severe resting hypoxaemia in COPD and ILD; ambulatory oxygen conditionally suggested for severe exertional hypoxaemia. [ATS 2020]`,`**BTS 2022 clinical statement on air travel** updated pre-flight assessment and in-flight oxygen advice for chronic lung disease. [BTS 2022]`,`**Guideline difference**: ATS conditionally supports ambulatory oxygen for severe exertional desaturation without resting hypoxaemia; BTS 2015 does not routinely - assess benefit on a walk test before prescribing. [ATS 2020]`,`**NP-NCD operational guidelines (2023)** brought chronic respiratory diseases into the national NCD programme. [NP-NCD 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**LTOT numbers**: **55 / 59 / 15** - PaO2 55 or less, or 56-59 with cor pulmonale or haematocrit over 55%, for 15 h/day or more. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Classic mistake**: prescribing LTOT from a single SpO2 reading during or soon after an exacerbation - criteria need two ABGs in stable disease 3 weeks apart. [BTS 2015]`,`**Second mistake**: using oxygen for breathlessness in a non-hypoxaemic patient - it does not help (use a fan). [BTS 2015]`,`**Two treatments that reduce mortality in COPD** relevant here: **LTOT** in severe hypoxaemia and **smoking cessation** (PR reduces readmissions). [GOLD 2025]`,`**Viva**: why does PR improve breathlessness without changing FEV1? Because it trains peripheral muscles, lowers lactate and ventilatory demand, and reduces dynamic hyperinflation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**6MWT MID 30 m**; PR typically adds 40-45 m. [ERS/ATS 2014]`,`**LOTT and INOX** are the trials that say no to oxygen for moderate or nocturnal-only desaturation. [GOLD 2025]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Oxygen sources, smoking cessation and related prescriptions`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Oxygen via concentrator (LTOT)`,`Nasal cannula 1-4 L/min to keep SpO2 90% or more at rest`,`15 h/day or more; +1 L/min in sleep and exercise; ABG at 3 months`,`CO2 retention, nasal dryness; fire risk with smoking`],[`Oxygen via cylinder`,`Same flows; backup and transport`,`As backup during power cuts`,`Heavy, refill cost; store upright away from heat`],[`Portable oxygen concentrator`,`Pulse-dose settings 1-5 (not equal to L/min)`,`During activity; verify SpO2 on exertion`,`Battery life; inadequate in high-flow need`],[`Nicotine replacement (patch + gum/lozenge)`,`Patch 21 mg/24 h if over 10 cigarettes/day plus 2-4 mg gum as needed`,`8-12 weeks, taper`,`Skin irritation, sleep disturbance; caution recent MI`],[`Varenicline`,`0.5 mg daily days 1-3, 0.5 mg twice daily days 4-7, then 1 mg twice daily`,`12 weeks (repeat 12 if quit)`,`Nausea, vivid dreams; reduce dose if eGFR under 30`],[`Oral nutritional supplement`,`Extra 500-1000 kcal/day with protein, plus exercise`,`Through PR and until BMI over 21`,`Bloating; check diabetes control`]]},{heading:`LTOT eligibility and the trials`,columns:[`Situation`,`Criterion`,`Decision`,`Evidence`],rows:[[`Severe resting hypoxaemia`,`PaO2 55 mmHg or less or SaO2 88% or less, twice, stable`,`LTOT 15 h/day or more`,`NOTT 1980, MRC 1981`],[`Borderline with end-organ effects`,`PaO2 56-59 mmHg with oedema, PH or haematocrit over 55%`,`LTOT`,`GOLD 2025`],[`Moderate resting desaturation`,`SpO2 89-93%`,`No LTOT`,`LOTT 2016`],[`Isolated nocturnal desaturation`,`Night-time dips, awake normal`,`No nocturnal oxygen (exclude OSA/OHS)`,`INOX 2020`],[`Exertional desaturation only`,`SpO2 88% or less on walking`,`Ambulatory O2 if walk test shows benefit (ILD stronger)`,`ATS 2020, AmbOx 2018`],[`Breathless, not hypoxaemic`,`SpO2 normal`,`No oxygen; fan and breathing techniques`,`BTS 2015`]]},{heading:`PR prescription (FITT)`,columns:[`Element`,`Endurance`,`Resistance`,`Notes`],rows:[[`Frequency`,`3-5 days/week (2 supervised)`,`2-3 days/week`,`Total 6-12 weeks`],[`Intensity`,`60-80% peak work rate or Borg 4-6`,`50-85% of 1-repetition maximum`,`Interval training if too breathless`],[`Time`,`20-30 min (can be split)`,`2-4 sets of 6-12 reps`,`Warm-up and cool-down`],[`Type`,`Walking, cycling, stairs`,`Quadriceps, arms, weights or bands`,`Add oxygen if SpO2 under 88%`]]},{heading:`Outcome measures used in PR`,columns:[`Measure`,`What it tests`,`Minimal important difference`],rows:[[`6-minute walk test`,`Functional exercise capacity`,`About 30 m`],[`Incremental shuttle walk test`,`Maximal exercise capacity`,`About 35-50 m`],[`CAT score`,`COPD health status`,`2 points`],[`SGRQ`,`Health-related quality of life`,`4 units`],[`mMRC`,`Breathlessness grade 0-4`,`Change of 1 grade`],[`Borg CR-10`,`Breathlessness or exertion during exercise`,`About 1 unit`]]},{heading:`Differential: why is this patient breathless or hypoxaemic despite treatment`,columns:[`Possibility`,`Clue`,`Action`],rows:[[`Poor oxygen adherence`,`Hour meter low, uses O2 only when breathless`,`Re-educate on 15 h/day`],[`Hypercapnia from excess oxygen`,`Morning headache, drowsiness`,`ABG; reduce flow; consider NIV`],[`Concentrator failure or low purity`,`SpO2 low despite correct flow`,`Check device alarm, servicing`],[`Coexisting OSA or OHS`,`Snoring, obesity, nocturnal desaturation`,`Sleep study; PAP`],[`Heart failure or pulmonary embolism`,`Oedema, sudden worsening`,`Echo, BNP, CTPA`],[`Deconditioning and anxiety`,`Fear of exertion, normal SpO2`,`PR and CBT-based breathlessness programme`]]}],redFlags:[`Drowsiness, confusion or morning headache on home oxygen - suspect **CO2 narcosis**; ABG now.`,`**Facial burn** or fire with home oxygen - emergency care for airway burn.`,`**Chest pain, syncope or new arrhythmia** during a PR session - stop exercise and assess.`,`Exertional **SpO2 under 80%** or resting SpO2 under 88% on prescribed oxygen - urgent review.`,`Rapidly worsening oedema or **cor pulmonale** despite LTOT.`,`Acute exacerbation features (fever, purulent sputum, rising breathlessness) - treat before continuing PR.`],pearls:[`LTOT = **55 / 59 / 15**: PaO2 55 or less, or 59 or less with cor pulmonale or haematocrit over 55, for 15 h/day.`,`LTOT is one of the few things that **prolongs life in COPD**; PR is the one that most improves how it feels to live.`,`**PR after admission within 4 weeks**, not during admission.`,`**PR improves symptoms without changing FEV1** - it treats muscles, not airways.`,`**LOTT and INOX**: no oxygen for moderate or nocturnal-only desaturation.`,`**Never prescribe oxygen for breathlessness alone** - a hand-held fan helps more.`,`**No smoking, no flame within 2 m** - including the kitchen gas stove and agarbatti.`,`Concentrator **hour meter** is the honest adherence check.`,`Review LTOT at **3 months** - many started at discharge no longer qualify.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-pulmonary-rehab-home-oxygen-q1`,stem:`A 64-year-old man with COPD, stable for 3 months, has two room-air ABGs 4 weeks apart showing PaO2 57 and 58 mmHg. Haematocrit is 58% and there is pedal oedema. What is the best recommendation?`,options:[`No oxygen, as PaO2 is above 55 mmHg`,`Nocturnal oxygen only`,`Long-term oxygen for at least 15 hours a day`,`Short-burst oxygen before exertion`],answer:2,explanation:`A PaO2 of 56-59 mmHg qualifies for LTOT when there is cor pulmonale, oedema or polycythaemia (haematocrit over 55%), and LTOT must be used for at least 15 hours a day to give the survival benefit. Withholding oxygen ignores the end-organ criterion, nocturnal-only oxygen does not meet the hours needed, and short-burst oxygen has no proven benefit.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-pulmonary-rehab-home-oxygen-q2`,stem:`A patient with stable COPD has a resting SpO2 of 91% and no cor pulmonale. He asks for a home oxygen concentrator. Which trial is most relevant to your advice?`,options:[`The Long-Term Oxygen Treatment Trial (LOTT, 2016)`,`The Nocturnal Oxygen Therapy Trial (NOTT, 1980)`,`The MRC home oxygen trial (1981)`,`The SERVE-HF trial (2015)`],answer:0,explanation:`LOTT studied moderate resting (SpO2 89-93%) or exertional desaturation and found no benefit of long-term oxygen on death, admissions or quality of life, so oxygen is not indicated here. NOTT and the MRC trial enrolled severely hypoxaemic patients and established LTOT benefit in that group, while SERVE-HF concerns ventilation for central apnoea in heart failure.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-pulmonary-rehab-home-oxygen-q3`,stem:`A 70-year-old woman is admitted with a COPD exacerbation. When is pulmonary rehabilitation best started according to current guidance?`,options:[`On day 1 of admission, during the acute illness`,`Only after 12 months of stability`,`Not at all, because she has had an exacerbation`,`Within about 3-4 weeks of hospital discharge`],answer:3,explanation:`Early post-discharge PR, started within about 3-4 weeks, reduces readmissions and improves quality of life. Starting full PR during the admission is not recommended because it did not improve outcomes, waiting a year wastes the window of greatest benefit, and an exacerbation is a reason to refer, not a contraindication.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-pulmonary-rehab-home-oxygen-q4`,stem:`In pulmonary rehabilitation for COPD, which mechanism best explains improved exercise tolerance despite an unchanged FEV1?`,options:[`Bronchodilation from repeated exercise`,`Improved peripheral muscle oxidative capacity lowering lactate and ventilatory demand`,`Regression of emphysematous bullae`,`Increase in diffusing capacity for carbon monoxide`],answer:1,explanation:`Training increases type I fibres, mitochondria and capillaries in limb muscles, so less lactate and CO2 are produced at a given workload; ventilation, breathing rate and dynamic hyperinflation fall. Exercise does not bronchodilate in a lasting way, emphysema does not regress, and DLCO does not change with training.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-pulmonary-rehab-home-oxygen-q5`,stem:`A 68-year-old man qualifies for LTOT but continues to smoke 15 bidis a day at home, where his wife cooks on a gas stove. What is the most appropriate action?`,options:[`Prescribe oxygen at a lower flow so that the fire risk is reduced`,`Prescribe oxygen and advise smoking only near an open window`,`Do a documented risk assessment, offer intensive cessation support and generally withhold home oxygen while he smokes`,`Prescribe cylinder oxygen instead of a concentrator, which cannot ignite`],answer:2,explanation:`Smoking with oxygen causes facial burns and house fires; BTS advises a documented risk assessment and that home oxygen is generally not prescribed to continuing smokers, with intensive cessation support first. A lower flow still enriches the local air, a window does not remove the risk at the cannula, and cylinder oxygen supports combustion just as a concentrator does.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-pulmonary-rehab-home-oxygen-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`What is pulmonary rehabilitation? Describe its components, indications and the evidence for its use in chronic respiratory disease, with reference to India.`,openingLines:[`Pulmonary rehabilitation is a comprehensive, assessment-based intervention of exercise training, education and behaviour change that improves the physical and psychological condition of people with chronic respiratory disease (ATS/ERS 2013).`,`It is the most effective non-drug therapy in COPD, improving breathlessness, exercise capacity and quality of life and reducing readmissions after exacerbation.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and burden`,points:[`ATS/ERS 2013 definition; India has about 55 million COPD patients and a large post-TB population, but PR reaches under 5%.`]},{heading:`Indications and contraindications`,points:[`COPD mMRC 2 or more, after exacerbation (within 3-4 weeks), ILD, post-TB lung disease, bronchiectasis, PH, pre/post lung surgery.`,`Contraindications: unstable angina, recent MI, severe AS, uncontrolled arrhythmia, inability to participate.`]},{heading:`Assessment`,points:[`mMRC, CAT, 6MWT or ISWT, ECG, SpO2 at rest and exercise, nutrition, anxiety and depression.`]},{heading:`Components`,points:[`Endurance training 60-80% peak work rate or Borg 4-6, 20-30 min; resistance 2-4 sets of 6-12 reps.`,`6-12 weeks, at least 2 supervised sessions/week.`,`Breathing techniques, education, inhaler technique, action plan, nutrition, psychosocial support, oxygen if desaturating.`]},{heading:`Mechanism and evidence`,points:[`Peripheral muscle conditioning lowers lactate and ventilation; less dynamic hyperinflation.`,`Cochrane 2015: 6MWD about 44 m gain and better quality of life; post-exacerbation PR cuts readmissions; ATS 2023 endorses telerehab.`]},{heading:`Indian adaptation and follow-up`,points:[`Home-based programmes using walking, stairs and water-bottle weights; NP-NCD and Ayushman Arogya Mandir; NTEP post-TB follow-up.`,`Maintenance exercise; repeat PR after decline.`]}],mustDraw:[`Breathlessness-inactivity-deconditioning spiral and where PR breaks it`,`Table of the FITT exercise prescription`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition`,marks:1},{part:`Indications and contraindications`,marks:2},{part:`Components with prescription`,marks:3},{part:`Mechanism and evidence`,marks:2},{part:`Indian context and follow-up`,marks:2}],keywords:[`pulmonary rehabilitation`,`6MWT`,`Borg`,`FITT`,`post-exacerbation`,`telerehabilitation`,`post-TB`]},{id:`respiratory-pulmonary-rehab-home-oxygen-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on long-term oxygen therapy: indications, prescription and safety.`,openingLines:[`Long-term oxygen therapy is home oxygen for at least 15 hours a day to correct chronic resting hypoxaemia, and it prolongs survival in severely hypoxaemic COPD (NOTT 1980, MRC 1981).`,`It is prescribed only on ABG criteria in stable disease, never for breathlessness alone.`],answer:[{heading:`Indications`,points:[`PaO2 55 mmHg or less (SaO2 88% or less), or 56-59 with cor pulmonale, PH or haematocrit over 55%.`,`Two ABGs 3 weeks apart, stable 8 weeks after exacerbation; LOTT and INOX - no benefit in moderate or nocturnal-only desaturation.`]},{heading:`Prescription`,points:[`Concentrator with nasal cannula 1-4 L/min to SpO2 90% or more (PaO2 60 or more), 15 h/day or more, +1 L/min in sleep and exertion; backup cylinder.`,`Ambulatory oxygen for mobile LTOT users; review at 3 months and 6-12 monthly.`]},{heading:`Safety`,points:[`No smoking; no flame within 2 m (gas stove, diya, agarbatti); fire breaks; water-based gel; tubing trip hazard; watch for CO2 retention.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: SpO2 92% or less in stable disease - ABG twice - criteria met - prescribe and review`],markSplit:[{part:`Indications with numbers`,marks:2},{part:`Prescription and follow-up`,marks:2},{part:`Safety`,marks:1}],keywords:[`LTOT`,`PaO2 55`,`15 hours`,`NOTT`,`MRC`,`LOTT`,`concentrator`,`fire safety`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - COPD and disorders of ventilation chapters`,`Spruit MA et al. An Official ATS/ERS Statement: Key Concepts and Advances in Pulmonary Rehabilitation, Am J Respir Crit Care Med 2013`,`Rochester CL et al. Pulmonary Rehabilitation for Adults with Chronic Respiratory Disease: An Official ATS Clinical Practice Guideline, Am J Respir Crit Care Med 2023`,`Jacobs SS et al. Home Oxygen Therapy for Adults with Chronic Lung Disease: An Official ATS Clinical Practice Guideline, Am J Respir Crit Care Med 2020`,`Hardinge M et al. British Thoracic Society Guidelines for Home Oxygen Use in Adults, Thorax 2015; Bolton CE et al. BTS Guideline on Pulmonary Rehabilitation in Adults, Thorax 2013`,`Global Initiative for Chronic Obstructive Lung Disease (GOLD) Report, 2025`,`Long-Term Oxygen Treatment Trial Research Group, N Engl J Med 2016; Lacasse Y et al. INOX trial, N Engl J Med 2020; McCarthy B et al. Cochrane Review of pulmonary rehabilitation for COPD, 2015`,`Ministry of Health and Family Welfare, Government of India. Operational Guidelines of the National Programme for Prevention and Control of Non-Communicable Diseases (NP-NCD), 2023; Allwood BW et al. Clinical standards for post-tuberculosis lung disease, IJTLD 2021`]}),c.push({id:`respiratory-preop-pulmonary`,title:`Pre-operative pulmonary evaluation and postoperative pulmonary complications`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Postoperative pulmonary complications (PPCs)** - **atelectasis, pneumonia, respiratory failure, bronchospasm and exacerbation of chronic lung disease** - are as common and as costly as cardiac complications; risk is predicted mainly by **the operation (upper abdominal, thoracic, long, emergency)** and the patient (**age, ASA class, COPD, heart failure, low SpO2, recent infection**) using **ARISCAT** and **STOP-BANG**, and is cut by **smoking cessation, prehabilitation, lung-expansion manoeuvres, good analgesia and early mobilisation**.`,keywords:[`PPC`,`postoperative pulmonary complications`,`ARISCAT`,`STOP-BANG`,`ACP 2006`,`ppoFEV1`,`ppoDLCO`,`VO2max`,`cardiopulmonary exercise testing`,`CPET`,`stair climb`,`shuttle walk test`,`lung resection`,`prehabilitation`,`incentive spirometry`,`I COUGH`,`atelectasis`,`ERAS`,`residual neuromuscular blockade`,`sugammadex`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Postoperative pulmonary complications** are new respiratory events after surgery that alter the clinical course: **atelectasis, pneumonia, respiratory failure (unplanned ventilation or reintubation), bronchospasm, aspiration pneumonitis, pleural effusion, pneumothorax and exacerbation of chronic lung disease**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Incidence** varies with definition and operation - about **5% after all non-cardiac surgery**, rising to **20-40% after upper abdominal and thoracic surgery**. [ACP 2006]`,`**As common as cardiac complications** and equally important: PPCs prolong hospital stay by 1-2 weeks and are associated with higher 30-day mortality. [ACP 2006]`,`In the **ARISCAT** cohort (2010) of 2464 surgical patients, PPCs occurred in **5%** and 30-day mortality was **19.5% with a PPC versus 0.5% without**. [ARISCAT 2010]`,`**Postoperative respiratory failure** (ventilation over 48 hours or reintubation) is the most lethal PPC, with mortality over 25%. [Harrison 22e]`,`**India**: rising surgical volumes under **PM-JAY**, high rates of tobacco use (GATS-2: **28.6% of adults**), biomass-related COPD and **post-TB lung disease** mean many surgical patients carry hidden pulmonary risk. [GATS-2 2017]`,`**The family physician's role**: identify risk, optimise (smoking, inhalers, infection, nutrition, OSA), decide whether tests are needed, and communicate risk - not to clear or forbid surgery. [ACP 2006]`]},{heading:`Risk factors: patient-related and procedure-related`,points:[`**Patient factors with good evidence**: **age over 60** (risk rises even in healthy elderly), **ASA class 2 or more**, **COPD**, **congestive heart failure**, **functional dependence**, and **low albumin (under 3.5 g/dL)**. [ACP 2006]`,`**Other patient factors**: **preoperative SpO2 under 96%**, **respiratory infection in the last month**, **anaemia (Hb 10 g/dL or less)**, current smoking, OSA, pulmonary hypertension, impaired sensorium and weight loss. [ARISCAT 2010]`,`**Not strong independent risks**: **obesity** (even morbid) and **well-controlled asthma** were not significant predictors in the ACP review - though obesity matters through OSA and OHS. [ACP 2006]`,`**Procedure factors are often stronger than patient factors**: **surgical site** (upper abdominal, thoracic, aortic aneurysm repair, head and neck, neurosurgery), **duration over 2-3 hours**, **emergency surgery** and **general anaesthesia**. [ACP 2006]`,`**Distance from the diaphragm matters**: upper abdominal incisions disturb diaphragm function far more than lower abdominal or peripheral operations - laparoscopy reduces but does not abolish this. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anaesthesia factors**: high tidal volumes, high FiO2, **residual neuromuscular blockade**, long-acting opioids and sedatives, and large fluid volumes. [BJA consensus 2019]`,`**Intraoperative transfusion** and nasogastric tubes left in routinely add risk. [ACP 2006]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology: why lungs fail after surgery`,points:[`**General anaesthesia reduces FRC by about 20%** within minutes of induction: loss of diaphragm tone lets abdominal contents push cephalad, causing **compression atelectasis** in dependent zones in up to 90% of patients. [Harrison 22e]`,`**High FiO2 causes absorption atelectasis**: oxygen behind closed airways is absorbed with no nitrogen to splint the alveoli open. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Upper abdominal and thoracic surgery** cause **diaphragm dysfunction** through reflex phrenic inhibition, lowering VC by **50-60%** and FRC by about 30% for up to 1-2 weeks. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pain splinting**: incisional pain gives shallow rapid breathing without sighs, so atelectasis persists and secretions are retained - adequate analgesia is lung protection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Impaired cough and mucociliary clearance** from anaesthetics, opioids, dry gases and intubation lead to retained secretions, atelectasis and secondary **pneumonia**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Residual neuromuscular blockade** (TOF ratio under 0.9) weakens pharyngeal muscles, raises aspiration risk and causes hypoxaemia in recovery. [ESAIC 2023]`,`**Opioids and sedatives** depress central drive and pharyngeal tone, and in OSA lead to post-operative obstructive events, which peak on **night 2-3** with REM rebound. [SASM 2016]`,`**Lung resection** removes functioning gas-exchange tissue; the remaining lung must handle the whole cardiac output - so post-resection lung function and exercise capacity predict who tolerates surgery. [ACCP 2013]`]},{heading:`Risk scores: ARISCAT and STOP-BANG`,points:[`**ARISCAT (Canet 2010)** uses 7 factors: **age, preoperative SpO2, respiratory infection in the last month, anaemia, incision site, duration and emergency surgery**. [ARISCAT 2010]`,`**ARISCAT scoring**: age 51-80 = 3, over 80 = 16; SpO2 91-95% = 8, 90% or less = 24; infection in last month = 17; Hb 10 g/dL or less = 11; upper abdominal = 15, intrathoracic = 24; duration 2-3 h = 16, over 3 h = 23; emergency = 8. [ARISCAT 2010]`,`**ARISCAT risk bands**: **under 26 low (about 1.6% PPC)**, **26-44 intermediate (about 13%)**, **45 or more high (about 42%)**. [ARISCAT 2010]`,`**STOP-BANG** screens for OSA: **Snoring, Tired, Observed apnoea, Pressure (hypertension), BMI over 35, Age over 50, Neck over 40 cm, Gender male** - one point each. [SASM 2016]`,`**STOP-BANG bands**: **0-2 low, 3-4 intermediate, 5-8 high** risk of moderate-severe OSA; a score of 2 or more STOP items plus male sex, BMI over 35 or large neck also counts as high. [SASM 2016]`,`**ASA physical status** class is simple and correlates with PPC risk; class 2 or more roughly doubles to quadruples it. [ACP 2006]`,`**Other tools**: Gupta calculator for postoperative respiratory failure and pneumonia (NSQIP-derived), and the Arozullah respiratory failure index - useful in audits but ARISCAT is the one examiners ask. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical assessment - history`,points:[`**Exercise tolerance** is the single best bedside question: inability to climb **two flights of stairs** or walk 400 m without stopping signals higher cardiopulmonary risk. [ACC/AHA 2024]`,`**Chronic lung disease control**: cough, sputum, wheeze, breathlessness grade (mMRC), recent exacerbations or steroid courses, and current inhalers and technique. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Recent respiratory infection** within 4 weeks increases PPCs - postpone elective surgery where possible. [ARISCAT 2010]`,`**Smoking history**: pack-years, current use, bidi and smokeless tobacco; ask about readiness to quit now. [WHO 2020]`,`**OSA symptoms**: STOP-BANG questions; known OSA with CPAP use and settings. [SASM 2016]`,`**Past TB**, previous chest surgery, kyphoscoliosis, neuromuscular disease, and previous anaesthetic problems (difficult intubation, prolonged ventilation). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drugs**: long-term or recent oral steroids (adrenal suppression), anticoagulants, **GLP-1 receptor agonists** (aspiration risk from delayed gastric emptying). [Multisociety GLP-1 2024]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Resting SpO2 on room air** - a number that enters ARISCAT directly; under 96% raises risk and 90% or less raises it sharply. [ARISCAT 2010]`,`**Wheeze, prolonged expiration and decreased breath sounds** indicate active obstruction that should be treated before elective surgery. [ACP 2006]`,`**Crackles and signs of heart failure** (raised JVP, S3, oedema) - optimise before surgery. [ACC/AHA 2024]`,`**Airway and neck**: neck circumference over 40 cm, Mallampati 3-4, retrognathia - OSA and difficult airway risk. [SASM 2016]`,`**Chest-wall deformity**, scars of thoracoplasty, tracheal deviation from old TB fibrosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nutritional status**: BMI under 18.5, muscle wasting, low albumin - all predict poor healing and PPCs. [ACP 2006]`,`**Simple bedside tests**: a forced expiratory time over 6-9 seconds and a match test failure suggest significant airflow obstruction where spirometry is unavailable. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations: who needs what`,points:[`**Routine spirometry is not recommended** before non-thoracic surgery; it does not add to clinical assessment and **must not be used alone to deny surgery**. [ACP 2006]`,`**Spirometry is indicated** for **lung resection**, for **undiagnosed breathlessness or wheeze**, and when it is unclear whether known COPD or asthma is optimally controlled. [ACP 2006]`,`**Chest X-ray**: not routine; reasonable in patients over 50 having upper abdominal, thoracic or aortic surgery, or with known cardiopulmonary disease or new symptoms. [ACP 2006]`,`**ABG is not routine**; it is useful when SpO2 is low or hypercapnia is suspected (COPD, OHS, neuromuscular disease) to set a baseline. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Serum albumin** (under 3.5 g/dL is a risk factor), **haemoglobin** (for ARISCAT), and **bicarbonate** in obese patients with high STOP-BANG (27 mmol/L or more suggests hypoventilation). [ACP 2006]`,`**Sleep study** only if STOP-BANG is high and there are features of hypoventilation, severe pulmonary hypertension or resting hypoxaemia - otherwise proceed with OSA precautions. [SASM 2016]`,`**Echocardiography** if pulmonary hypertension or heart failure is suspected - pulmonary hypertension markedly raises perioperative mortality. [ESC/ERS 2022]`,`**Sputum for TB (NAAT)** in any patient with cough over 2 weeks before elective surgery - active TB defers elective surgery. [NTEP 2023]`]},{heading:`Evaluation before lung resection`,points:[`**Cardiac risk first**: use the **Thoracic Revised Cardiac Risk Index (ThRCRI)**; a score over 1.5, poor exercise tolerance or active cardiac disease needs cardiology assessment before the lung pathway. [ACCP 2013]`,`**Measure both FEV1 and DLCO** in every resection candidate - DLCO predicts complications independently, even when FEV1 is normal. [ACCP 2013]`,`**Predicted postoperative FEV1 (anatomical method)**: **ppoFEV1 = preoperative FEV1 x (1 - segments removed / 19)**; the same formula gives **ppoDLCO** (19 segments: right 10, left 9). [ACCP 2013]`,`**For pneumonectomy, use perfusion scintigraphy**: ppoFEV1 = preoperative FEV1 x (1 - fraction of total perfusion in the lung to be removed). [ACCP 2013]`,`**Both ppoFEV1 and ppoDLCO over 60% predicted** = low risk; proceed with resection up to pneumonectomy. [ACCP 2013]`,`**Either between 30% and 60%** = do a **low-technology exercise test**: **stair climb over 22 m** or **shuttle walk over 400 m** means low risk; below these, do **formal CPET**. [ACCP 2013]`,`**Either under 30%** = go straight to **CPET**. [ACCP 2013]`,`**CPET VO2max**: **over 20 mL/kg/min (or over 75% predicted)** low risk; **10-20 mL/kg/min** intermediate; **under 10 mL/kg/min (or under 35% predicted)** high risk - consider sublobar resection, SBRT or non-surgical options. [ACCP 2013]`,`**ERS/ESTS 2009 differs slightly**: it sends anyone with FEV1 or DLCO under 80% predicted to CPET first, then calculates ppo values - both pathways use the same VO2max cut-offs. [ERS/ESTS 2009]`]},{heading:`Pre-operative optimisation in OPD (including prehabilitation)`,points:[`**Smoking cessation**: stopping **at least 4-8 weeks before surgery** lowers PPCs and wound complications; any stop is beneficial, and the old fear that short-term cessation raises risk is not supported. [WHO 2020]`,`**Cessation drugs**: **nicotine replacement** (patch 21 mg/24 h plus short-acting form) or **varenicline** (0.5 mg daily for 3 days, 0.5 mg twice daily for 4 days, then 1 mg twice daily for 12 weeks), started as early as possible. [WHO 2020]`,`**COPD**: continue maintenance LAMA/LABA/ICS up to and including the day of surgery; treat any exacerbation and **postpone elective surgery** until recovered. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Asthma**: aim for good control; if **FEV1 is under 80% of personal best**, a short course of **oral prednisolone (for example 40-50 mg daily for 5 days)** before surgery; give salbutamol before induction. [GINA 2025]`,`**Steroid cover**: patients on high-dose ICS or oral steroids for over 2-3 weeks in the past months need **perioperative hydrocortisone** (for major surgery, 100 mg IV at induction then 200 mg/24 h) to prevent adrenal crisis. [GINA 2025]`,`**Respiratory infection**: treat and defer elective surgery about 4 weeks after a lower respiratory infection. [ARISCAT 2010]`,`**Prehabilitation**: 2-6 weeks of **aerobic and resistance exercise, inspiratory muscle training, nutrition and psychological support** before major abdominal or lung surgery reduces PPCs and length of stay. [ERAS/ESTS 2019]`,`**Nutrition**: albumin under 3.5 g/dL or weight loss over 10% in 6 months - oral supplements for 7-14 days before major surgery. [ESPEN 2021]`,`**Teach incentive spirometry and deep-breathing** before surgery, when the patient can learn it pain-free. [ACP 2006]`]},{heading:`Intra-operative and postoperative prevention (IPD)`,points:[`**Lung-expansion manoeuvres** after upper abdominal or thoracic surgery: **deep-breathing exercises and incentive spirometry** (10 breaths every hour while awake), and **CPAP** for those who cannot do them - ACP recommends lung expansion; trials of incentive spirometry alone show modest benefit. [ACP 2006]`,`**Early mobilisation**: sit out of bed and walk on **postoperative day 0-1**; early mobility is the most effective single bundle element. [ERAS Society 2019]`,`**I COUGH bundle**: **Incentive spirometry, Coughing and deep breathing, Oral care (chlorhexidine), Understanding (patient education), Getting out of bed (3 times a day), Head-of-bed elevation (30 degrees)** - reduced pneumonia and unplanned intubation. [I COUGH 2013]`,`**Analgesia**: multimodal (paracetamol, NSAID if safe), **regional or epidural blocks** for thoracic and upper abdominal surgery, and minimal opioids - better analgesia lets the patient breathe deeply and cough. [ERAS Society 2019]`,`**Protective ventilation in theatre**: **tidal volume 6-8 mL/kg predicted body weight**, individualised PEEP, the lowest FiO2 that keeps SpO2 adequate, and recruitment manoeuvres when needed. [BJA consensus 2019]`,`**Reverse neuromuscular blockade fully**: quantitative monitoring to a **train-of-four ratio of 0.9 or more**, with **sugammadex (2-4 mg/kg IV)** or neostigmine as appropriate. [ESAIC 2023]`,`**Selective, not routine, nasogastric decompression** - only for nausea, vomiting or distension. [ACP 2006]`,`**OSA precautions**: continue the patient's CPAP in recovery and on the ward, minimise opioids, nurse semi-upright, and monitor SpO2 continuously for the first nights. [SASM 2016]`,`**VTE prophylaxis**: weight-based LMWH (for example enoxaparin 40 mg SC daily) plus mechanical methods - pulmonary embolism is a preventable postoperative pulmonary event. [ACCP 2012]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Elderly**: age is an independent risk even when fit; prehabilitation, delirium prevention and avoidance of long-acting sedatives are key. [ACP 2006]`,`**Obesity and OHS**: obesity alone is a weak risk, but OHS (bicarbonate 27 mmol/L or more, hypercapnia) is a strong one - consider delaying elective surgery to start PAP (see Obesity hypoventilation topic). [SASM 2016]`,`**Pulmonary hypertension**: high perioperative mortality; elective surgery should be done in a centre with PH expertise. [ESC/ERS 2022]`,`**Neuromuscular disease and kyphoscoliosis**: VC under 1 L or under 50% predicted signals risk of ventilator dependence; plan NIV after extubation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Post-TB lung disease**: spirometry, CXR and ABG if breathless; exclude active TB with sputum NAAT; treat bronchiectatic sputum before surgery. [NTEP 2023]`,`**Pregnancy**: physiological fall in FRC and raised oxygen consumption make desaturation fast - left uterine displacement, pre-oxygenation and aspiration prophylaxis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diabetes on GLP-1 receptor agonists**: most can continue; consider a **clear liquid diet for 24 hours before surgery**, and hold or use a full-stomach approach if there are GI symptoms or during dose escalation. [Multisociety GLP-1 2024]`,`**Resource-limited hospitals**: history, exercise tolerance, SpO2, ARISCAT and STOP-BANG need no equipment, and incentive spirometry can be replaced by deep-breathing and bottle-blowing exercises. [ACP 2006]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Atelectasis**: commonest PPC, often days 1-3 with low-grade fever and hypoxaemia; treat with mobilisation, chest physiotherapy and CPAP. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Postoperative pneumonia**: days 3-5 onward; treat as hospital-acquired pneumonia per local antibiogram (see HAP/VAP topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Respiratory failure** after extubation: consider **NIV or high-flow nasal oxygen** early in high-risk patients after abdominal or thoracic surgery. [ERS/ATS 2017]`,`**Bronchospasm**: in asthma or COPD, from intubation, histamine-releasing drugs or beta-blockers; treat with nebulised salbutamol and ipratropium. [GINA 2025]`,`**Aspiration pneumonitis**: emergency surgery, full stomach, GLP-1 RA use, residual blockade. [Multisociety GLP-1 2024]`,`**After lung resection**: air leak, bronchopleural fistula, atrial fibrillation (up to 20%), post-pneumonectomy pulmonary oedema; 30-day mortality about 1-2% after lobectomy and 5-7% after pneumonectomy. [ACCP 2013]`,`**A PPC predicts long-term mortality** and loss of independence in the elderly, beyond the hospital stay. [ARISCAT 2010]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and communication`,points:[`**Refer to a pulmonologist** before elective surgery for: uncontrolled asthma or COPD, resting SpO2 under 92%, suspected OHS or pulmonary hypertension, neuromuscular weakness, and all lung resection candidates. [ACP 2006]`,`**Write the risk, not a clearance**: state the ARISCAT band, STOP-BANG score, what has been optimised and specific recommendations (CPAP, regional analgesia, HDU bed). [ACC/AHA 2024]`,`**Postpone elective surgery** for active infection, exacerbation, uncontrolled heart failure, or active TB until treated. [ACP 2006]`,`**After discharge**: review breathing, wound, mobilisation and smoking status at 1-2 weeks; continue cessation support beyond surgery. [WHO 2020]`,`**After lung resection**: pulmonary rehabilitation improves exercise capacity (see Pulmonary rehabilitation topic). [ATS 2023]`,`**Known OSA diagnosed perioperatively** needs a definitive sleep study and CPAP follow-up. [SASM 2016]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`**Surgery is a teachable moment**: smokers are more likely to quit before an operation; brief advice and the **quitline 1800-11-2356** (National Tobacco Control Programme). [NTCP 2023]`,`**Counsel on breathing exercises, coughing with a pillow over the wound and walking** from day 1. [ERAS Society 2019]`,`**Vaccines**: influenza and pneumococcal vaccination for COPD and elderly patients ahead of planned surgery where time allows (see Adult respiratory vaccines topic). [GOLD 2025]`,`**Oral hygiene** (brushing, chlorhexidine mouthwash) reduces postoperative pneumonia. [I COUGH 2013]`,`**NP-NCD screening** of adults 30 and over at Ayushman Arogya Mandir picks up uncontrolled diabetes, hypertension and tobacco use well before surgery. [NP-NCD 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**ACC/AHA 2024 guideline on perioperative cardiovascular management for non-cardiac surgery** - the cardiac side of preoperative assessment; functional capacity and risk calculators used together with pulmonary risk. [ACC/AHA 2024]`,`**ESAIC 2023 guideline on peri-operative management of neuromuscular block**: quantitative monitoring for every patient given a neuromuscular blocker, extubate only at a train-of-four ratio of 0.9 or more, and sugammadex for deep block - directly reducing residual-blockade PPCs. [ESAIC 2023]`,`**Multisociety guidance on GLP-1 receptor agonists (2024)**: most patients continue the drug; 24-hour clear liquid diet before surgery, replacing the 2023 ASA advice to hold weekly agents for a week. [Multisociety GLP-1 2024]`,`**GINA 2025** perioperative advice: assess control, short oral steroid if FEV1 under 80% of best, steroid cover for those on long-term high-dose therapy. [GINA 2025]`,`**GOLD 2025** surgery section: postpone elective surgery during exacerbation; lung-volume reduction and resection evaluated by a multidisciplinary team. [GOLD 2025]`,`**ACCP 2013 (Brunelli) algorithm** for lung resection remains the current reference used in exams and practice. [ACCP 2013]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic question**: risk factors for PPCs and how to reduce them - split into **patient vs procedure** factors and **pre-, intra- and post-operative** measures. [ACP 2006]`,`**Commonest mistake**: ordering routine spirometry and CXR for every surgical patient, or using a low FEV1 alone to deny surgery. [ACP 2006]`,`**Remember the surprises**: obesity and controlled asthma are **not** major independent risk factors; low albumin and functional dependence **are**. [ACP 2006]`,`**ppoFEV1 calculation** (19 segments) and the **60% / 30%** and **20 / 10 mL/kg/min** cut-offs are favourite viva numbers. [ACCP 2013]`,`**Smoking cessation 4-8 weeks** before surgery - and quitting later is still better than not quitting. [WHO 2020]`,`**I COUGH** mnemonic for postoperative prevention. [I COUGH 2013]`]}],tables:[{heading:`ARISCAT score`,columns:[`Factor`,`Category`,`Points`],rows:[[`Age`,`51-80 years / over 80 years`,`3 / 16`],[`Preoperative SpO2`,`91-95% / 90% or less`,`8 / 24`],[`Respiratory infection in last month`,`Yes`,`17`],[`Preoperative anaemia`,`Hb 10 g/dL or less`,`11`],[`Surgical incision`,`Upper abdominal / intrathoracic`,`15 / 24`],[`Duration of surgery`,`2-3 hours / over 3 hours`,`16 / 23`],[`Emergency surgery`,`Yes`,`8`],[`Risk bands`,`Under 26 low (1.6%); 26-44 intermediate (13%); 45+ high (42%)`,`Total`]]},{heading:`Perioperative drugs relevant to pulmonary risk`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Salbutamol`,`2.5-5 mg nebulised or 4-8 puffs by spacer before induction`,`Pre-induction and as needed`,`Tachycardia, tremor, hypokalaemia`],[`Maintenance LAMA/LABA/ICS`,`Usual inhaled dose`,`Continue including morning of surgery`,`Missing doses risks bronchospasm`],[`Prednisolone (asthma with FEV1 under 80% best)`,`40-50 mg orally once daily`,`5 days before surgery`,`Hyperglycaemia, wound healing (minor at short course)`],[`Hydrocortisone (steroid cover)`,`100 mg IV at induction, then 200 mg/24 h (major surgery)`,`Taper to usual dose over 1-3 days`,`Hyperglycaemia, fluid retention`],[`Varenicline`,`0.5 mg daily x3 d, 0.5 mg twice daily x4 d, then 1 mg twice daily`,`12 weeks, start 1-2 weeks before quit date`,`Nausea, abnormal dreams; halve if eGFR under 30`],[`Nicotine patch`,`21 mg/24 h (over 10 cigarettes/day)`,`8-12 weeks with taper`,`Skin reaction; safe perioperatively`],[`Sugammadex`,`2-4 mg/kg IV (16 mg/kg immediate reversal)`,`At end of surgery to TOF 0.9 or more`,`Anaphylaxis (rare), bradycardia; reduces hormonal contraceptive effect for 7 days`],[`Enoxaparin (VTE prophylaxis)`,`40 mg SC once daily`,`Until mobile; extended after major cancer surgery`,`Bleeding; adjust if CrCl under 30`]]},{heading:`Lung resection physiological evaluation (ACCP 2013)`,columns:[`Step`,`Result`,`Interpretation`,`Next step`],rows:[[`ppoFEV1 and ppoDLCO`,`Both over 60% predicted`,`Low risk`,`Proceed with resection`],[`ppoFEV1 or ppoDLCO`,`Either 30-60%`,`Needs exercise test`,`Stair climb or shuttle walk`],[`Stair climb / shuttle walk`,`Over 22 m / over 400 m`,`Low risk`,`Proceed`],[`Stair climb / shuttle walk`,`Below these`,`Uncertain`,`Formal CPET`],[`ppoFEV1 or ppoDLCO`,`Either under 30%`,`High risk`,`Formal CPET`],[`CPET VO2max`,`Over 20 mL/kg/min or over 75%`,`Low risk`,`Proceed`],[`CPET VO2max`,`10-20 mL/kg/min`,`Moderate risk`,`Individual MDT decision`],[`CPET VO2max`,`Under 10 mL/kg/min or under 35%`,`High risk`,`Sublobar resection, SBRT or non-surgical`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of postoperative hypoxaemia`,columns:[`Cause`,`Typical timing`,`Discriminating feature`,`First action`],rows:[[`Atelectasis`,`Day 0-3`,`Basal dullness, low-grade fever, improves with deep breathing`,`Mobilise, physiotherapy, CPAP`],[`Residual blockade / opioid narcosis`,`Recovery room`,`Weak, drowsy, slow or shallow breathing`,`Reverse; naloxone 40-100 mcg IV aliquots`],[`Aspiration pneumonitis`,`Peri-induction`,`Witnessed vomiting, right lower infiltrate`,`Suction, oxygen, supportive`],[`Pneumonia`,`Day 3-7`,`Fever, purulent sputum, new infiltrate`,`Cultures and HAP antibiotics`],[`Pulmonary embolism`,`Day 3-14`,`Sudden breathlessness, tachycardia, clear CXR`,`CTPA; anticoagulate`],[`Pulmonary oedema`,`Day 0-3`,`Fluid overload, crackles, raised JVP, BNP high`,`Diuretic, review fluids`],[`Obstructive sleep apnoea events`,`Nights 1-3`,`Snoring and dips during sleep, high STOP-BANG`,`CPAP, cut opioids`]]},{heading:`Who needs which pre-operative pulmonary test`,columns:[`Test`,`Indicated`,`Not indicated`],rows:[[`Spirometry`,`Lung resection; unexplained dyspnoea; uncertain control of COPD/asthma`,`Routine before non-thoracic surgery`],[`DLCO`,`All lung resection candidates`,`Routine screening`],[`Chest X-ray`,`Over 50 with upper abdominal/thoracic/AAA surgery; cardiopulmonary disease`,`Healthy young patients`],[`ABG`,`Low SpO2, suspected hypercapnia (COPD, OHS, neuromuscular)`,`Routine`],[`Polysomnography`,`High STOP-BANG with hypoventilation, PH or hypoxaemia`,`All high STOP-BANG (use precautions)`],[`CPET`,`Lung resection with ppo values under 30% or failed stair climb`,`Low-risk resection`]]}],redFlags:[`Active **lower respiratory infection or COPD/asthma exacerbation** before elective surgery - postpone.`,`Resting **SpO2 under 92%** on room air without known cause - investigate before elective surgery.`,`**Bicarbonate 27 mmol/L or more** or hypercapnia in an obese patient - suspected OHS; delay elective surgery for PAP.`,`Signs of **pulmonary hypertension or right heart failure** - surgery only in an experienced centre.`,`**Cough over 2 weeks** with weight loss - test for TB before elective surgery.`,`**ppoFEV1 or ppoDLCO under 30%**, or VO2max under 10 mL/kg/min, before lung resection.`,`Postoperative **drowsiness with slow breathing** or sudden breathlessness with tachycardia - opioid narcosis or PE.`],pearls:[`**The operation predicts PPCs more than the patient**: upper abdominal and thoracic, long, emergency.`,`**Obesity and controlled asthma are not major independent risks**; low albumin and functional dependence are.`,`**ARISCAT** = Age, SpO2, Infection, Anaemia, Incision, Duration, Emergency.`,`**STOP-BANG 5-8 = high risk OSA**; proceed with precautions unless hypoventilation or PH.`,`**ppoFEV1 = FEV1 x (1 - segments removed/19)**; 60% and 30% are the cut-offs.`,`**VO2max over 20 safe, under 10 prohibitive** (mL/kg/min).`,`**Stair climb over 22 m or shuttle walk over 400 m** = low risk.`,`**I COUGH**: Incentive spirometry, Cough, Oral care, Understanding, Get up, Head up.`,`**Never let spirometry alone deny non-thoracic surgery.**`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-preop-pulmonary-q1`,stem:`Which of the following is NOT one of the seven variables in the ARISCAT score for postoperative pulmonary complications?`,options:[`Preoperative SpO2`,`Upper abdominal incision`,`Duration of surgery`,`Body mass index`],answer:3,explanation:`ARISCAT uses age, preoperative SpO2, respiratory infection in the last month, anaemia, incision site, surgical duration and emergency surgery. Body mass index is not included - obesity was not an independent predictor in the ARISCAT cohort or the ACP review, although it matters through OSA and hypoventilation. SpO2, upper abdominal incision and duration all carry points.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-preop-pulmonary-q2`,stem:`A 62-year-old man needs a right lower lobectomy (5 of 19 segments) for lung cancer. His FEV1 is 70% predicted and DLCO 72% predicted. What are his predicted postoperative FEV1 and the next step per ACCP 2013?`,options:[`About 52%; proceed only after a stair climb or shuttle walk test`,`About 65%; proceed without further tests`,`About 18%; refer for non-surgical treatment`,`About 52%; go straight to pneumonectomy evaluation with perfusion scan`],answer:0,explanation:`ppoFEV1 = 70 x (1 - 5/19) = 70 x 14/19, about 52%; ppoDLCO is about 53%. With either value between 30% and 60%, ACCP recommends a low-technology exercise test (stair climb over 22 m or shuttle walk over 400 m means low risk). Proceeding without testing requires both values over 60%, 18% is a miscalculation, and a perfusion scan is for pneumonectomy.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`respiratory-preop-pulmonary-q3`,stem:`A lung resection candidate has a VO2max of 9 mL/kg/min on cardiopulmonary exercise testing. How is this interpreted?`,options:[`Low risk, proceed with lobectomy`,`High risk - consider sublobar resection, SBRT or non-surgical treatment`,`Intermediate risk, proceed with pneumonectomy`,`Not interpretable; repeat spirometry instead`],answer:1,explanation:`VO2max under 10 mL/kg/min (or under 35% predicted) predicts high mortality after major anatomical resection, so minimally invasive or non-surgical options are considered. Over 20 mL/kg/min is low risk, 10-20 is intermediate, and spirometry cannot replace CPET once it has been done.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-preop-pulmonary-q4`,stem:`According to the ACP review of preoperative pulmonary risk, which patient factor is a well-established independent predictor of postoperative pulmonary complications?`,options:[`Well-controlled asthma`,`Morbid obesity alone`,`Serum albumin under 3.5 g/dL`,`Diabetes mellitus`],answer:2,explanation:`Low serum albumin (under 3.5 g/dL) was among the strongest patient-related predictors, alongside age, ASA class, COPD, heart failure and functional dependence. Well-controlled asthma and obesity alone were not significant independent risks, and diabetes was not identified as a major pulmonary risk factor in that review.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-preop-pulmonary-q5`,stem:`A 55-year-old smoker (30 pack-years) is booked for elective cholecystectomy in 6 weeks. What is the best advice about smoking?`,options:[`Stop now with pharmacotherapy support - stopping at least 4-8 weeks before surgery reduces complications`,`Continue until surgery, because stopping within 8 weeks increases pulmonary complications`,`Stop only on the morning of surgery`,`Smoking status does not affect laparoscopic surgery outcomes`],answer:0,explanation:`Cessation at least 4-8 weeks before surgery reduces pulmonary and wound complications, and any cessation helps; NRT or varenicline improves success. The belief that short-term cessation raises risk has not been confirmed, stopping only on the day gives little benefit, and laparoscopic surgery still carries anaesthetic and pulmonary risk from smoking.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-preop-pulmonary-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the risk factors for postoperative pulmonary complications. How will you evaluate and optimise a patient before elective upper abdominal surgery, and what measures prevent PPCs after surgery?`,openingLines:[`Postoperative pulmonary complications - atelectasis, pneumonia, respiratory failure, bronchospasm and exacerbation of lung disease - occur in about 5% of non-cardiac and up to 20-40% of upper abdominal and thoracic operations, and are as important as cardiac complications.`,`Risk depends more on the operation than the patient and is quantified with ARISCAT; prevention is a bundle across the pre-, intra- and post-operative periods.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and burden`,points:[`List of PPCs; incidence; 30-day mortality 19.5% with PPC vs 0.5% without (ARISCAT).`]},{heading:`Risk factors`,points:[`Patient: age over 60, ASA 2+, COPD, CHF, functional dependence, albumin under 3.5, SpO2 low, recent infection, anaemia, smoking, OSA, PH.`,`Procedure: upper abdominal/thoracic/AAA, over 2-3 h, emergency, GA, residual blockade.`,`Not major: obesity alone, controlled asthma.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`FRC falls 20% under GA; diaphragm dysfunction; pain splinting; impaired cough; opioids.`]},{heading:`Evaluation`,points:[`History (exercise tolerance, symptoms, smoking), exam, SpO2; ARISCAT and STOP-BANG.`,`Selective tests: spirometry only if undiagnosed dyspnoea or uncertain control; CXR over 50 with high-risk site; albumin; bicarbonate if obese.`]},{heading:`Pre-operative optimisation`,points:[`Smoking cessation 4-8 weeks; inhalers continued; prednisolone if asthma FEV1 under 80% best; treat infection and postpone.`,`Prehabilitation, nutrition, teach incentive spirometry; OSA - bring CPAP.`]},{heading:`Intra- and post-operative prevention`,points:[`Protective ventilation 6-8 mL/kg PBW, PEEP, full reversal TOF 0.9+, regional analgesia, laparoscopy.`,`I COUGH bundle, early mobilisation, multimodal analgesia, selective NG tube, CPAP/NIV in high risk, VTE prophylaxis.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: patient vs procedure risk factors`,`ARISCAT table with risk bands`,`Timeline: pre-, intra-, post-operative preventive measures`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and burden`,marks:1},{part:`Risk factors`,marks:2},{part:`Evaluation and scores`,marks:2},{part:`Pre-operative optimisation`,marks:2},{part:`Intra- and post-operative prevention`,marks:3}],keywords:[`PPC`,`ARISCAT`,`STOP-BANG`,`smoking cessation`,`incentive spirometry`,`I COUGH`,`early mobilisation`]},{id:`respiratory-preop-pulmonary-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`chart-flow`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Draw a flow chart of the physiological evaluation of a patient for lung resection.`,openingLines:[`Physiological evaluation before lung resection estimates whether the patient can tolerate loss of lung tissue, using cardiac risk, ppoFEV1, ppoDLCO and exercise testing (ACCP 2013).`,`Both FEV1 and DLCO are measured in every candidate.`],answer:[{heading:`Step 1: cardiac risk`,points:[`Thoracic RCRI; cardiology review if over 1.5, poor exercise capacity or active cardiac disease.`]},{heading:`Step 2: ppo values`,points:[`ppoFEV1 and ppoDLCO = preop value x (1 - segments removed/19); perfusion scan for pneumonectomy.`,`Both over 60%: proceed. Either 30-60%: stair climb or shuttle walk. Either under 30%: CPET.`]},{heading:`Step 3: exercise testing`,points:[`Stair climb over 22 m or SWT over 400 m: proceed; else CPET.`,`VO2max over 20 mL/kg/min: proceed; 10-20: MDT; under 10: sublobar, SBRT or non-surgical.`]}],mustDraw:[`The ACCP 2013 algorithm as a flow chart with 60% / 30% and 20 / 10 mL/kg/min cut-offs`],markSplit:[{part:`Cardiac step`,marks:1},{part:`ppo calculation and cut-offs`,marks:2},{part:`Exercise testing and CPET`,marks:2}],keywords:[`ppoFEV1`,`ppoDLCO`,`stair climb`,`shuttle walk`,`CPET`,`VO2max`,`ThRCRI`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - preoperative evaluation chapter`,`Qaseem A, Snow V, Fitterman N et al. Risk assessment for and strategies to reduce perioperative pulmonary complications for patients undergoing noncardiothoracic surgery: a guideline from the American College of Physicians, Ann Intern Med 2006 (with Smetana GW systematic review)`,`Canet J et al. Prediction of postoperative pulmonary complications in a population-based surgical cohort (ARISCAT), Anesthesiology 2010`,`Brunelli A et al. Physiologic evaluation of the patient with lung cancer being considered for resectional surgery: ACCP Evidence-Based Clinical Practice Guidelines, 3rd edition, Chest 2013; Brunelli A et al. ERS/ESTS clinical guidelines on fitness for radical therapy in lung cancer patients, Eur Respir J 2009`,`Chung F et al. Society of Anesthesia and Sleep Medicine guidelines on preoperative screening and assessment of adult patients with obstructive sleep apnea, Anesth Analg 2016`,`Thompson A et al. 2024 AHA/ACC Guideline for Perioperative Cardiovascular Management for Noncardiac Surgery, Circulation 2024`,`Batchelor TJP et al. Guidelines for enhanced recovery after lung surgery: ERAS Society and ESTS recommendations, Eur J Cardiothorac Surg 2019; Cassidy MR et al. I COUGH: reducing postoperative pulmonary complications with a multidisciplinary patient care program, JAMA Surg 2013`,`Fuchs-Buder T et al. Peri-operative management of neuromuscular blockade: a guideline from the European Society of Anaesthesiology and Intensive Care, Eur J Anaesthesiol 2023; Kindel TL et al. Multisociety clinical practice guidance for the safe use of GLP-1 receptor agonists in the perioperative period, 2024; World Health Organization. Tobacco and postsurgical outcomes, 2020; Global Initiative for Asthma (GINA) 2025 and GOLD 2025 reports`]}),c.push({id:`respiratory-tropical-pneumonias`,title:`Tropical and zoonotic pneumonias in India: melioidosis, leptospirosis, scrub typhus, hantavirus and avian influenza`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Tropical and zoonotic pneumonias** are lung infections acquired from **soil, water, animals or vectors** - **melioidosis** (*Burkholderia pseudomallei*, in diabetics, treated with **ceftazidime or meropenem then cotrimoxazole**), **leptospiral pulmonary haemorrhage**, **scrub typhus ARDS** (treated with **doxycycline and/or azithromycin**), **influenza H1N1/H5N1** (oseltamivir), **Nipah** and **Q fever** - which standard CAP regimens miss and which must be suspected from **season, place, exposure and an eschar**.`,keywords:[`melioidosis`,`Burkholderia pseudomallei`,`Whitmore's disease`,`leptospirosis`,`Weil's disease`,`leptospiral pulmonary haemorrhage syndrome`,`scrub typhus`,`Orientia tsutsugamushi`,`eschar`,`INTREST trial`,`hantavirus pulmonary syndrome`,`avian influenza H5N1`,`H1N1 swine flu`,`oseltamivir`,`Nipah virus`,`Q fever`,`Coxiella burnetii`,`acute undifferentiated febrile illness`,`ceftazidime`,`doxycycline`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Tropical and zoonotic pneumonias** are lower respiratory infections whose pathogens live in **soil and water (melioidosis), animals (leptospirosis, Q fever, hantavirus, avian influenza, Nipah) or vectors (scrub typhus)** - they are not covered reliably by standard CAP regimens. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Melioidosis**: an estimated **165,000 cases and 89,000 deaths a year worldwide**, and modelling places **India as the country with the largest predicted burden**, mostly undiagnosed. [Nature Microbiology 2016]`,`**Melioidosis in India** clusters along the **south-west coast (Udupi, Mangaluru), Odisha, Tamil Nadu, Kerala and Puducherry**, peaking in the **monsoon** when soil organisms are aerosolised by rain and wind. [ICMR 2023]`,`**Leptospirosis**: about **1 million cases and 59,000 deaths a year** globally; in India it is endemic in **Kerala, Tamil Nadu, Gujarat, Maharashtra, Karnataka and the Andaman Islands**, with post-flood epidemics (Mumbai, Kerala 2018). [NCDC 2023]`,`**Scrub typhus** is one of the commonest causes of **acute undifferentiated febrile illness (AUFI)** in India, from the Himalayan states and the North-East to Tamil Nadu, and was a major cause of the **acute encephalitis syndrome** outbreaks in Gorakhpur. [ICMR 2015]`,`**Influenza A H1N1 (pdm09)** has been seasonal in India since the 2009 pandemic, with peaks in the monsoon and winter; **avian influenza H5N1** circulates in Indian poultry and India reported its first human H5N1 death in **2021 (Haryana)**, with sporadic cases since. [NCDC 2024]`,`**Nipah virus** has caused outbreaks in **West Bengal (2001, 2007)** and repeatedly in **Kerala (2018, 2019, 2021, 2023, 2024 and 2025)**, with case fatality of **40-75%**. [NCDC 2024]`,`**Q fever** (*Coxiella burnetii*) is under-recognised in India; livestock handlers, veterinarians and abattoir workers show high seroprevalence. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology, exposure and risk factors`,points:[`**Melioidosis**: *Burkholderia pseudomallei*, a Gram-negative environmental bacillus, acquired by **inoculation** (barefoot paddy work), **inhalation** (monsoon storms) or ingestion. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Melioidosis host factors**: **diabetes mellitus in over half (up to 75% in Indian series)**, alcohol use, chronic kidney disease, chronic lung disease, thalassaemia and steroid therapy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Leptospirosis**: *Leptospira interrogans* shed in the **urine of rats**, cattle and dogs; enters through cut skin or mucosa during **paddy farming, flood wading, sewer and abattoir work**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Scrub typhus**: *Orientia tsutsugamushi* transmitted by the bite of **larval trombiculid mites (chiggers)** in scrub vegetation, farmland and grass - **farmers, women collecting firewood or grass**, and soldiers. [ICMR 2015]`,`**Hantavirus**: inhalation of aerosolised **rodent excreta**; Old World strains (Hantaan, Seoul) cause **haemorrhagic fever with renal syndrome**, New World strains (Sin Nombre, Andes) cause **hantavirus cardiopulmonary syndrome**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Avian influenza H5N1**: close contact with **sick or dead poultry**, live bird markets, slaughtering or undercooked poultry; limited human-to-human spread. [WHO 2024]`,`**Nipah**: reservoir is the **Pteropus fruit bat**; spread through **fruit or raw date-palm sap contaminated by bat saliva or urine**, pigs (Malaysia) and **person-to-person by droplets** in families and hospitals. [WHO 2024]`,`**Q fever**: inhalation of dust contaminated by **birth products of cattle, sheep and goats**; extremely infectious (a single organism can infect). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why each damages the lung`,points:[`**Melioidosis**: a **facultative intracellular** organism that survives in macrophages, forms abscesses in lung, liver, spleen and prostate, and can stay **latent for years** and reactivate with diabetes or steroids. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Melioidosis drug resistance** is intrinsic: it resists **aminoglycosides, polymyxins, penicillin and first- and second-generation cephalosporins** - which is why ordinary CAP regimens fail. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Leptospiral pulmonary haemorrhage**: a **vasculitis-like endothelial injury** with immune deposits on the alveolar basement membrane, causing **diffuse alveolar haemorrhage** without major coagulopathy - the leading cause of death in severe leptospirosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Scrub typhus**: *Orientia* infects **endothelial cells**, causing **disseminated vasculitis and perivasculitis**, increased capillary permeability, interstitial pneumonitis and **non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema (ARDS)**; the same process causes myocarditis, meningoencephalitis, AKI and hepatitis. [ICMR 2015]`,`**Eschar**: the necrotic site of the chigger bite where the organism multiplies before spreading through lymphatics - a painless black scab with a red rim. [ICMR 2015]`,`**Hantavirus cardiopulmonary syndrome**: virus infects pulmonary capillary endothelium and immune activation causes **massive capillary leak** into the lungs and **myocardial depression**, so shock and pulmonary oedema develop within hours. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Influenza**: viral replication destroys ciliated epithelium, causing **primary viral pneumonia** and predisposing to **secondary bacterial pneumonia** (*S. aureus*, pneumococcus); H5N1 binds alpha-2,3 receptors deep in the lung, causing severe lower-tract disease and cytokine storm. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nipah**: a henipavirus with tropism for **endothelium and neurons** - vasculitis, thrombosis and parenchymal necrosis in brain and lung, giving encephalitis with or without ARDS. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history and syndromes`,points:[`**Melioidosis pneumonia** (about half of presentations): acute fever, cough and breathlessness rapidly progressing to **septic shock**, or a subacute illness with **upper lobe cavitation, weight loss and night sweats mimicking TB**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Melioidosis elsewhere**: **liver and splenic abscesses** (multiple small, honeycomb), **prostatic abscess**, septic arthritis and osteomyelitis, skin abscess, and **parotid abscess in children**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Leptospirosis**: biphasic illness - abrupt fever, headache, **severe calf myalgia**, **conjunctival suffusion** - then in severe cases **Weil's disease (jaundice, AKI, bleeding)** or **pulmonary haemorrhage** with haemoptysis and rapid hypoxaemia, sometimes without jaundice. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Scrub typhus**: fever over 5 days, headache, myalgia, cough, **eschar** (in roughly half of Indian patients), lymphadenopathy and later breathlessness, jaundice, oliguria or altered sensorium. [ICMR 2015]`,`**Hantavirus cardiopulmonary syndrome**: 3-5 day prodrome of fever and myalgia, then sudden cough, breathlessness and shock; HFRS gives fever, haemorrhage, hypotension and AKI. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Influenza H1N1**: fever, cough, sore throat, myalgia; danger signs are **breathlessness, chest pain, haemoptysis, drowsiness, hypotension or worsening of chronic disease**. [MoHFW 2023]`,`**Avian influenza H5N1**: high fever and cough after poultry contact, with rapid progression to **pneumonia and ARDS** in days; diarrhoea and conjunctivitis may occur. [WHO 2024]`,`**Nipah**: fever, headache, vomiting, then **encephalitis (drowsiness, seizures, segmental myoclonus)**; up to half of Kerala patients had **respiratory illness or ARDS**, which drives person-to-person spread. [NCDC 2024]`,`**Q fever**: self-limiting flu-like illness, **atypical pneumonia** or granulomatous **hepatitis** acutely; chronic Q fever is mainly **culture-negative endocarditis** in people with valve disease or grafts. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings that point to the cause`,points:[`**Eschar**: search the **axilla, groin, inframammary fold, waist band, behind the ears and genitalia** - a painless black scab, the single most useful sign of scrub typhus. [ICMR 2015]`,`**Conjunctival suffusion** (redness without discharge) and **calf tenderness** point to leptospirosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hepatosplenomegaly with tender spleen** in a febrile diabetic suggests melioidosis splenic abscesses; prostatic tenderness on rectal exam suggests prostatic abscess. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Regional lymphadenopathy** near an eschar or generalised lymphadenopathy, with relative bradycardia, suggests scrub typhus. [ICMR 2015]`,`**Jaundice with AKI and bleeding** = Weil's syndrome; **haemoptysis with falling haemoglobin** = pulmonary haemorrhage. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bilateral crackles with hypoxaemia** out of proportion to signs suggest ARDS from scrub typhus, leptospirosis, hantavirus or influenza. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neurological signs** (drowsiness, myoclonus, neck stiffness) with pneumonia: Nipah, scrub typhus meningoencephalitis or severe influenza. [NCDC 2024]`,`**New regurgitant murmur** with prolonged fever and animal exposure: think chronic Q fever endocarditis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Typical bacterial CAP** (pneumococcus): lobar consolidation, responds to beta-lactam within 48-72 hours - failure in a monsoon-season diabetic should prompt cultures for melioidosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pulmonary TB**: upper lobe cavities with weight loss resemble chronic melioidosis - send sputum culture for both, and melioidosis grows in 24-48 hours. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dengue**: fever, myalgia, thrombocytopenia and capillary leak with pleural effusion, but **leucopenia, haemoconcentration** and no eschar; NS1 or IgM confirms. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Severe malaria**: ARDS, jaundice and AKI overlap with leptospirosis and scrub typhus - **always do a malaria RDT or smear** in every febrile patient. [NCVBDC 2023]`,`**Enteric fever**: fever, relative bradycardia and hepatitis but rarely ARDS; blood culture confirms. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diffuse alveolar haemorrhage from vasculitis** (GPA, SLE): resembles leptospiral haemorrhage but has no animal exposure, and ANCA or ANA are positive (see Diffuse alveolar haemorrhage topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`**COVID-19 and other viral pneumonias**: bilateral ground-glass change; test with multiplex PCR. [MoHFW 2023]`,`**Japanese encephalitis and HSV encephalitis**: differential for Nipah when encephalitis predominates; travel and outbreak context decide. [NCDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Baseline in every case**: CBC (platelets, leucocytes), creatinine, electrolytes (leptospirosis gives hypokalaemia), LFT, CK, urine, blood sugar, **malaria test, dengue NS1/IgM**, chest X-ray, SpO2 and ABG if hypoxaemic. [ICMR STW 2019]`,`**Melioidosis**: **blood, sputum, pus and urine culture** - tell the laboratory, because the organism can be dismissed as a Pseudomonas contaminant; selective **Ashdown medium**, bipolar **safety-pin** Gram-negative rods, gentamicin and colistin resistance; ultrasound of liver, spleen and prostate in every case. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Melioidosis serology** (indirect haemagglutination) is unreliable in endemic areas because of background positivity - culture is the gold standard. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Leptospirosis**: **blood PCR in the first week**, **IgM ELISA after day 5**, and **microscopic agglutination test (MAT)** as the reference (a fourfold rise in paired sera, or a high single titre such as 1:400 in endemic areas); **modified Faine's criteria** support diagnosis. [NCDC 2023]`,`**Scrub typhus**: **IgM ELISA** (positive after about day 5, with region-specific OD cut-offs), **PCR of eschar crust or blood** (best early), indirect immunofluorescence as reference; **Weil-Felix (OX-K)** is cheap but insensitive. [ICMR 2015]`,`**Influenza**: nasopharyngeal and throat swab **RT-PCR** in viral transport medium; for H5N1, notify and send to NIV Pune or an ICMR VRDL laboratory. [MoHFW 2023]`,`**Nipah**: **RT-PCR of throat swab, blood, urine and CSF** at a BSL-3/4 laboratory (NIV Pune, NIV Kerala unit); samples need triple packaging and full PPE. [NCDC 2024]`,`**Q fever**: **phase II antibodies** rise in acute infection; **phase I IgG 1:800 or more** suggests chronic infection; echocardiography for endocarditis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chest imaging patterns**: melioidosis - consolidation, nodules and cavities; leptospiral haemorrhage - diffuse bilateral alveolar opacities; scrub typhus - bilateral interstitial infiltrates, effusions; hantavirus - rapid bilateral oedema. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - empirical approach and mild disease`,points:[`**Empirical rule for AUFI in India**: after malaria and dengue tests, give **doxycycline 100 mg orally twice daily** - it covers scrub typhus, other rickettsioses and leptospirosis; use **azithromycin** in pregnancy and children. [ICMR STW 2019]`,`**Scrub typhus (mild)**: **doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for 7 days** or **azithromycin 500 mg once daily for 5 days**; fever usually settles within **48 hours** - persistent fever means reconsider the diagnosis. [ICMR 2015]`,`**Leptospirosis (mild)**: **doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for 7 days**, or amoxicillin 500 mg 8-hourly, or azithromycin. [NCDC 2023]`,`**Influenza H1N1 - MoHFW categories**: **A** (mild, no risk factor) - symptomatic care, no oseltamivir, no test; **B** (high fever or high-risk group: pregnancy, age 65 or over, under 5, chronic disease) - **oseltamivir** without testing; **C** (red flags) - test, admit, oseltamivir. [MoHFW 2023]`,`**Oseltamivir dose**: **75 mg orally twice daily for 5 days** in adults; reduce to 30 mg twice daily if CrCl 30-60 mL/min and 30 mg once daily if 10-30; start as early as possible, ideally within 48 hours, but still give in severe or hospitalised patients after 48 hours. [WHO 2024]`,`**Q fever (acute)**: **doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for 14 days**; screen for valve disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Melioidosis is never managed in OPD**: a febrile diabetic from an endemic area with pneumonia or abscess needs admission and parenteral therapy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Paracetamol for fever; avoid NSAIDs** until dengue is excluded, and avoid aspirin in children with influenza (Reye's syndrome). [NCVBDC 2023]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and ICU`,points:[`**Melioidosis intensive phase**: **ceftazidime 40 mg/kg (up to 2 g) IV 6-hourly** or **meropenem 25 mg/kg (up to 1 g) IV 8-hourly** (meropenem 2 g 8-hourly for neurological disease and preferred in septic shock) for **at least 10-14 days**, longer (4-8 weeks) for deep abscesses, osteomyelitis or neuromelioidosis. [Darwin Guideline 2020]`,`**Melioidosis eradication phase**: **cotrimoxazole twice daily by weight** - 160/800 mg (under 40 kg), 240/1200 mg (40-60 kg), 320/1600 mg (over 60 kg) - plus **folic acid 5 mg daily**, for **at least 3 months** (6 months for neurological or bone disease); **amoxicillin-clavulanate** if cotrimoxazole is not tolerated or in pregnancy. [Darwin Guideline 2020]`,`**Drain abscesses** (prostatic, joint, large liver abscess) and control blood sugar; add cotrimoxazole during the intensive phase for neurological, bone, prostatic and skin disease. [Darwin Guideline 2020]`,`**Severe scrub typhus**: **IV doxycycline 200 mg loading then 100 mg 12-hourly plus IV azithromycin 500 mg daily for 7 days** - the **INTREST trial (2023)** showed the combination beat either drug alone. [NEJM 2023]`,`**Severe leptospirosis**: **IV benzylpenicillin 1.5 million units 6-hourly** or **ceftriaxone 1-2 g IV daily for 7 days**; watch for Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction in the first hours. [NCDC 2023]`,`**Leptospiral pulmonary haemorrhage**: early **lung-protective ventilation** (6 mL/kg PBW, high PEEP), early **dialysis** for AKI (often hypokalaemic, non-oliguric), platelet transfusion if bleeding; plasma exchange and high-dose methylprednisolone are used in some centres but evidence is weak. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Severe or hospitalised influenza (H1N1, H5N1)**: **oseltamivir 75 mg twice daily** immediately regardless of symptom duration, usually for 5 days and extended (often 10 days) for severe, immunocompromised or H5N1 disease; add antibiotics such as ceftriaxone plus cover for *S. aureus* if secondary bacterial pneumonia is suspected. [WHO 2024]`,`**Nipah**: supportive ICU care in an **airborne-infection isolation room** with N95, gown, gloves and eye protection; ribavirin and remdesivir are unproven; the **m102.4 monoclonal antibody** has been supplied for compassionate use in Kerala outbreaks. [NCDC 2024]`,`**Hantavirus cardiopulmonary syndrome**: careful fluids, early inotropes and **ECMO** for refractory shock; ribavirin helps HFRS if given early but not HCPS. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ARDS from any cause**: low tidal volume ventilation, prone positioning when PaO2/FiO2 under 150, conservative fluids (see ARDS topic). [ATS 2024]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: **azithromycin** for scrub typhus (doxycycline is avoided, although short courses are increasingly considered acceptable in severe disease); amoxicillin or ceftriaxone for leptospirosis; **amoxicillin-clavulanate** for melioidosis eradication; **oseltamivir** is safe and indicated at any stage. [ICMR 2015]`,`**Scrub typhus in pregnancy** carries high fetal loss and preterm birth - treat early. [ICMR 2015]`,`**Diabetes**: the single strongest risk for melioidosis; any diabetic with fever, pneumonia and abscesses in the monsoon from an endemic district is melioidosis until cultures say otherwise. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CKD**: adjust ceftazidime, meropenem, cotrimoxazole and oseltamivir to creatinine clearance; cotrimoxazole raises potassium and creatinine. [Darwin Guideline 2020]`,`**Children**: doxycycline for up to 7 days is acceptable at any age for rickettsial disease (no significant tooth staining with short courses); parotid abscess is a classic paediatric melioidosis sign. [CDC 2024]`,`**Elderly and immunocompromised**: atypical presentations without fever; influenza needs early oseltamivir. [WHO 2024]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: if cultures are not available, start doxycycline for AUFI and refer; for a sick diabetic with pneumonia in the monsoon, give **ceftazidime or meropenem** rather than a standard CAP regimen before referral. [ICMR STW 2019]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Melioidosis mortality** is **10-50%** (higher with septic shock and delayed appropriate therapy); **relapse** occurs mainly with short or poorly adhered eradication therapy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Leptospiral pulmonary haemorrhage syndrome** carries mortality of **over 50%**; Weil's disease 5-15%. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Scrub typhus mortality**: about **6% untreated and 1.4% treated** (systematic review median), rising with ARDS, shock, meningoencephalitis and multi-organ failure. [PLoS NTD 2017]`,`**H5N1**: about **50% case fatality** among the roughly 970 human cases reported to WHO since 2003. [WHO 2024]`,`**Nipah**: case fatality **40-75%**; survivors may have relapsing or late-onset encephalitis months later. [WHO 2024]`,`**Hantavirus cardiopulmonary syndrome**: mortality about **35%**, mostly in the first 48 hours of the cardiopulmonary phase. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chronic Q fever endocarditis** is fatal without long-term treatment. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Multi-organ involvement**: AKI, myocarditis, hepatitis, encephalitis and DIC are common to severe forms - organ support decides survival. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer or admit**: SpO2 under 94%, respiratory rate over 24/min, hypotension, jaundice with oliguria, altered sensorium, haemoptysis, or a diabetic with pneumonia and suspected melioidosis. [ICMR STW 2019]`,`**Fever not settling in 48 hours on doxycycline** argues against scrub typhus - look for melioidosis, enteric fever, TB or abscess. [ICMR 2015]`,`**Melioidosis follow-up**: monthly visits during eradication for adherence, **CBC, creatinine and potassium** (cotrimoxazole toxicity), and glycaemic control; review for relapse for at least a year. [Darwin Guideline 2020]`,`**After leptospiral AKI**: check creatinine and potassium until normal; most recover fully. [NCDC 2023]`,`**Q fever with valve disease**: serology every 3 months for chronic infection; chronic endocarditis needs **doxycycline plus hydroxychloroquine for at least 18 months**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Notify** suspected H5N1, Nipah, leptospirosis clusters and unusual pneumonia clusters to the **district surveillance unit (IDSP/IHIP)** the same day. [NCDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`**Melioidosis**: diabetics in endemic areas should wear **boots and gloves** in paddy fields and soil, cover wounds, and avoid outdoor exposure during monsoon storms. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Leptospirosis**: **National Programme for Prevention and Control of Leptospirosis (NPPCL)** under NCDC; rodent control, protective boots, and **doxycycline 200 mg once weekly** chemoprophylaxis for high-risk exposure (flood relief, paddy workers in outbreaks). [NCDC 2023]`,`**Scrub typhus**: wear full clothing, use repellents and avoid sitting on grass; there is no vaccine. [ICMR 2015]`,`**Influenza**: annual vaccination for high-risk groups and health workers (see Adult respiratory vaccines topic); **oseltamivir 75 mg once daily for 10 days** as post-exposure prophylaxis for high-risk contacts and H5N1 contacts. [WHO 2024]`,`**Avian influenza**: cull and disinfect infected flocks, protect cullers with PPE and oseltamivir prophylaxis; cook poultry and eggs thoroughly. [NCDC 2024]`,`**Nipah**: avoid **fallen or bat-bitten fruit and raw date-palm sap**; trace contacts and monitor them for **21 days**; strict hospital infection control. [NCDC 2024]`,`**One Health**: the **National One Health Programme for Prevention and Control of Zoonoses** links human, animal and environmental surveillance; doctors report through IDSP/IHIP. [NCDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**WHO Clinical Practice Guidelines for Influenza (2024)**: oseltamivir for severe or high-risk influenza, **baloxavir** for non-severe influenza in high-risk patients, and no routine antivirals for non-severe low-risk illness. [WHO 2024]`,`**H5N1 clade 2.3.4.4b** spread to mammals and **US dairy cattle (2024)** with mostly mild human conjunctivitis cases - WHO and India have intensified One Health surveillance of poultry and cattle. [WHO 2024]`,`**INTREST trial (NEJM 2023)**: combined IV doxycycline and azithromycin for severe scrub typhus reduced the composite of death, persistent complications and fever at day 7 - now adopted in Indian practice. [NEJM 2023]`,`**Nipah 2023-2025 Kerala outbreaks** were contained with rapid RT-PCR at the NIV Kerala unit, contact tracing and isolation; ICMR is developing indigenous monoclonal antibodies. [NCDC 2024]`,`**Updated Darwin melioidosis guideline (2020)** remains the reference: weight-based cotrimoxazole and longer intensive phases for deep-seated infection. [Darwin Guideline 2020]`,`**MoHFW seasonal influenza guidance** retains the A/B/C categorisation for H1N1 triage and testing. [MoHFW 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic case**: a diabetic farmer from coastal Karnataka in the monsoon with pneumonia, splenic abscesses and gentamicin-resistant Gram-negative bacilli = **melioidosis**; treat with ceftazidime or meropenem then cotrimoxazole. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake**: treating melioidosis with ceftriaxone or an aminoglycoside - both fail. [Darwin Guideline 2020]`,`**Always look for the eschar** before ordering expensive tests in fever with pneumonitis. [ICMR 2015]`,`**Leptospirosis triad for viva**: conjunctival suffusion, calf tenderness, and jaundice with AKI; pulmonary haemorrhage is the killer. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diagnose by timing**: PCR in week 1, IgM after day 5, MAT for confirmation (leptospirosis). [NCDC 2023]`,`**Nipah reservoir** = Pteropus fruit bat; **H5N1** CFR about 50%; **Q fever** chronic marker = phase I IgG. [WHO 2024]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for tropical and zoonotic pneumonias`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Ceftazidime (melioidosis)`,`40 mg/kg up to 2 g IV 6-hourly`,`10-14 days minimum; 4-8 weeks for deep foci`,`Diarrhoea, C. difficile; adjust in CKD`],[`Meropenem (melioidosis, severe)`,`25 mg/kg up to 1 g IV 8-hourly (2 g for CNS)`,`As ceftazidime; preferred in septic shock`,`Seizures at high dose, C. difficile; adjust in CKD`],[`Cotrimoxazole + folic acid (melioidosis eradication)`,`160/800 mg (under 40 kg), 240/1200 mg (40-60 kg), 320/1600 mg (over 60 kg) twice daily; folic acid 5 mg daily`,`3 months (6 for CNS or bone)`,`Hyperkalaemia, rising creatinine, rash/SJS, marrow suppression; avoid first trimester and near term`],[`Doxycycline`,`100 mg orally or IV twice daily (200 mg IV load in severe scrub typhus)`,`7 days (scrub, lepto); 14 days (acute Q fever)`,`Oesophageal ulcer (take upright with water), photosensitivity; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Azithromycin`,`500 mg orally or IV once daily`,`5 days (mild scrub); 7 days IV with doxycycline (severe)`,`GI upset, QT prolongation`],[`Benzylpenicillin / ceftriaxone (severe leptospirosis)`,`1.5 MU IV 6-hourly / 1-2 g IV once daily`,`7 days`,`Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction, allergy`],[`Oseltamivir`,`75 mg orally twice daily; prophylaxis 75 mg once daily`,`5 days (10 in severe/H5N1); prophylaxis 10 days`,`Nausea, neuropsychiatric events (rare); 30 mg doses in CKD`],[`Doxycycline + hydroxychloroquine (chronic Q fever)`,`100 mg twice daily + 200 mg three times daily`,`At least 18 months`,`Retinal toxicity (eye checks), photosensitivity`]]},{heading:`Comparing the tropical pneumonias`,columns:[`Infection`,`Exposure clue`,`Key sign`,`Lung picture`,`Diagnosis`],rows:[[`Melioidosis`,`Diabetic, monsoon, coastal paddy farmer`,`Splenic/liver/prostate abscesses`,`Consolidation, nodules, upper lobe cavities`,`Culture (Ashdown), bipolar rods`],[`Leptospirosis`,`Flood, paddy, rats, sewer work`,`Conjunctival suffusion, calf tenderness`,`Diffuse alveolar haemorrhage`,`PCR wk 1, IgM ELISA, MAT`],[`Scrub typhus`,`Farm, grass, firewood collection`,`Eschar, lymphadenopathy`,`Interstitial infiltrates, ARDS, effusion`,`IgM ELISA, eschar PCR`],[`Hantavirus`,`Rodent excreta in closed spaces`,`Shock, thrombocytopenia, haemoconcentration`,`Rapid bilateral oedema`,`Serology, RT-PCR`],[`Influenza H1N1/H5N1`,`Season or sick poultry contact`,`High fever, myalgia; rapid ARDS (H5N1)`,`Bilateral infiltrates, secondary bacterial`,`RT-PCR swab`],[`Nipah`,`Bats, raw date-palm sap, Kerala outbreak`,`Encephalitis, myoclonus`,`ARDS`,`RT-PCR (NIV Pune)`],[`Q fever`,`Cattle, goats, birthing products`,`Hepatitis, culture-negative endocarditis`,`Atypical pneumonia`,`Phase II (acute), phase I IgG (chronic)`]]},{heading:`MoHFW categorisation of influenza-like illness (H1N1)`,columns:[`Category`,`Who`,`Test`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`A`,`Mild fever, cough, sore throat; no risk factor`,`Not needed`,`Symptomatic; home isolation; no oseltamivir`],[`B (i)`,`Category A plus high fever and severe sore throat or myalgia`,`Not needed`,`Oseltamivir; home isolation`],[`B (ii)`,`Category A plus high-risk group (pregnancy, 65+, under 5, chronic disease, immunosuppression)`,`Not needed`,`Oseltamivir; home isolation`],[`C`,`Red flags: breathlessness, chest pain, drowsiness, hypotension, haemoptysis, cyanosis`,`RT-PCR`,`Admit, oseltamivir, supportive care`]]},{heading:`Investigations and cut-offs`,columns:[`Infection`,`Best early test`,`Confirmatory / reference`,`Pitfall`],rows:[[`Melioidosis`,`Blood and sputum culture (alert lab)`,`Culture on Ashdown medium`,`Serology unreliable in endemic areas; misidentified as Pseudomonas`],[`Leptospirosis`,`Blood PCR (days 1-7)`,`MAT fourfold rise or high single titre`,`IgM negative in first 5 days`],[`Scrub typhus`,`Eschar or blood PCR`,`IgM ELISA (from day 5), IFA`,`Weil-Felix insensitive`],[`Influenza`,`Nasopharyngeal RT-PCR`,`Subtyping at NIV/VRDL`,`Rapid antigen tests miss many`],[`Nipah`,`Throat swab and urine RT-PCR`,`NIV Pune BSL-4`,`Needs full PPE and triple packaging`],[`Q fever`,`Phase II IgM/IgG`,`Phase I IgG 1:800 or more for chronic`,`Early serology negative; repeat at 2-4 weeks`]]}],redFlags:[`**Diabetic with pneumonia and septic shock** in the monsoon from an endemic area - suspect melioidosis; start meropenem or ceftazidime.`,`**Haemoptysis with falling haemoglobin and hypoxaemia** in suspected leptospirosis - pulmonary haemorrhage; ICU.`,`**SpO2 under 94%, rising respiratory rate or bilateral infiltrates** in scrub typhus - ARDS developing.`,`**Fever with encephalitis or ARDS** in a Nipah outbreak area or after contact - isolate and notify.`,`**Pneumonia after contact with sick or dead poultry** - suspect H5N1; isolate, oseltamivir, notify.`,`**Jaundice with oliguria or bleeding** - Weil's disease.`,`**Hypotension with haemoconcentration and thrombocytopenia** after rodent exposure - hantavirus.`],pearls:[`**Melioidosis = diabetes + monsoon + abscesses**; ceftazidime or meropenem, then **3 months of cotrimoxazole**.`,`Melioidosis is **resistant to gentamicin and colistin** - the lab clue on Ashdown medium.`,`**Look for the eschar** in the axilla, groin and under the breasts.`,`**Doxycycline is the empirical drug for AUFI** in India after malaria and dengue tests.`,`**Severe scrub typhus**: IV doxycycline plus IV azithromycin (INTREST 2023).`,`**Leptospirosis kills by pulmonary haemorrhage** more than by jaundice.`,`**Oseltamivir in hospitalised influenza at any time**, not only within 48 hours.`,`**Nipah**: fruit bat reservoir, raw date-palm sap, 21-day contact follow-up.`,`**Q fever chronic = phase I IgG high**; doxycycline plus hydroxychloroquine for 18 months.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-tropical-pneumonias-q1`,stem:`A 52-year-old diabetic paddy farmer from Udupi presents in August with fever, cough, right upper lobe consolidation and multiple small splenic abscesses. Blood culture grows Gram-negative bacilli with bipolar staining that are resistant to gentamicin and colistin. What is the most appropriate treatment?`,options:[`Ceftriaxone plus azithromycin for 7 days`,`IV ceftazidime or meropenem for at least 2 weeks, then oral cotrimoxazole for 3 months`,`Gentamicin plus piperacillin-tazobactam for 14 days`,`Four-drug antitubercular therapy`],answer:1,explanation:`This is melioidosis: diabetes, monsoon, coastal Karnataka, splenic abscesses and gentamicin-colistin-resistant bipolar bacilli. Treatment is an intensive phase of ceftazidime or meropenem followed by at least 3 months of weight-based cotrimoxazole. A standard CAP regimen with ceftriaxone fails, aminoglycosides are intrinsically inactive, and the culture result excludes TB as the explanation.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-tropical-pneumonias-q2`,stem:`A 40-year-old woman from Vellore has fever for 8 days, an eschar in the groin, bilateral infiltrates, SpO2 88% and creatinine 2.4 mg/dL. Scrub typhus IgM is positive. Based on the INTREST trial, which regimen is preferred?`,options:[`Oral doxycycline 100 mg twice daily alone`,`IV chloramphenicol alone`,`IV ceftriaxone plus oral azithromycin`,`IV doxycycline plus IV azithromycin for 7 days`],answer:3,explanation:`INTREST (NEJM 2023) showed that in severe scrub typhus, combined IV doxycycline and IV azithromycin reduced the composite of death, persistent complications and persistent fever compared with either drug alone. Oral doxycycline alone suits mild disease, chloramphenicol is an older second-line drug, and ceftriaxone has no activity against Orientia.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-tropical-pneumonias-q3`,stem:`A sewage worker in Mumbai has had fever and severe calf pain for 3 days after the floods, with conjunctival suffusion. Which test is most likely to confirm leptospirosis at this stage?`,options:[`Leptospira PCR on blood`,`IgM ELISA for leptospirosis`,`Weil-Felix OX-K agglutination`,`Microscopic agglutination test on a single sample`],answer:0,explanation:`In the first week leptospires circulate in blood, so PCR is the most sensitive test. IgM ELISA usually turns positive only after about day 5, a single early MAT is often negative and needs paired sera, and Weil-Felix OX-K detects scrub typhus antibodies, not leptospirosis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-tropical-pneumonias-q4`,stem:`A 24-week pregnant woman has fever 38.9 C, cough and myalgia during an H1N1 season. She has no breathlessness, chest pain or drowsiness and SpO2 is 97%. Under MoHFW categorisation, what is the correct approach?`,options:[`Category A: symptomatic care only, no oseltamivir`,`Category C: admit and wait for RT-PCR before oseltamivir`,`Category B: start oseltamivir 75 mg twice daily for 5 days without waiting for a test`,`Avoid oseltamivir because of pregnancy`],answer:2,explanation:`Pregnancy places her in a high-risk group, so she is category B: start oseltamivir empirically and isolate at home, with no test needed. Category A applies only to mild illness without risk factors, category C requires red flags and even then treatment should not wait for the result, and oseltamivir is safe and recommended in pregnancy.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-tropical-pneumonias-q5`,stem:`During a Kerala outbreak, a 30-year-old nurse who cared for a patient with encephalitis and ARDS develops fever, headache and cough. Regarding Nipah virus, which statement is correct?`,options:[`Its natural reservoir is the domestic rat, and it spreads only through urine`,`Person-to-person spread by respiratory droplets occurs, and contacts are followed for 21 days`,`Ribavirin is a proven treatment that halves mortality`,`Weil-Felix testing confirms the diagnosis`],answer:1,explanation:`Nipah spreads between people through respiratory droplets and secretions, especially in families and hospitals, and contacts are monitored for 21 days. The reservoir is the Pteropus fruit bat, not the rat; ribavirin has no proven benefit; diagnosis is RT-PCR at NIV Pune, while Weil-Felix is an old test for rickettsial infection.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-tropical-pneumonias-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the aetiology, clinical features, diagnosis and management of melioidosis. Add a note on its differentiation from pulmonary tuberculosis.`,openingLines:[`Melioidosis is an infection by the Gram-negative environmental bacillus Burkholderia pseudomallei, acquired from soil and water by inoculation, inhalation or ingestion, and India is predicted to carry the largest burden in the world.`,`It mainly affects diabetics in the monsoon, presents as pneumonia, sepsis or abscesses, and needs ceftazidime or meropenem followed by months of cotrimoxazole.`],answer:[{heading:`Epidemiology and risk`,points:[`Global 165,000 cases and 89,000 deaths a year; India - coastal Karnataka, Odisha, Tamil Nadu, Kerala, Puducherry; monsoon.`,`Diabetes (over half), alcohol, CKD, thalassaemia, steroids; farmers.`]},{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Intracellular survival in macrophages, abscess formation, latency with reactivation.`,`Intrinsic resistance to aminoglycosides, polymyxins, penicillin and early cephalosporins.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Pneumonia (about half), septic shock, liver/splenic/prostatic abscesses, bone and joint, parotid abscess in children.`,`Chronic form with upper lobe cavities, weight loss - mimics TB.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Culture of blood, sputum, pus, urine on Ashdown medium; bipolar safety-pin rods; gentamicin-colistin resistance.`,`Ultrasound abdomen and prostate; serology unreliable in endemic areas.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Intensive: ceftazidime 40 mg/kg 6-hourly or meropenem 25 mg/kg 8-hourly, at least 10-14 days; longer for deep foci.`,`Eradication: weight-based cotrimoxazole plus folic acid for at least 3 months; amoxicillin-clavulanate in pregnancy.`,`Drain abscesses; control diabetes; ICU support.`]},{heading:`Differentiation from TB and prevention`,points:[`Melioidosis: acute onset possible, abscesses in spleen/prostate, culture positive in 24-48 h, no response to ATT; TB: chronic, AFB/NAAT positive.`,`Prevention: boots and gloves for diabetics in paddy fields; avoid monsoon storm exposure.`]}],mustDraw:[`Treatment timeline: intensive phase then eradication phase with drugs and durations`,`Comparison table melioidosis vs pulmonary TB`],markSplit:[{part:`Aetiology and epidemiology`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment`,marks:3},{part:`Differentiation from TB`,marks:1}],keywords:[`Burkholderia pseudomallei`,`diabetes`,`monsoon`,`ceftazidime`,`meropenem`,`cotrimoxazole`,`Ashdown`]},{id:`respiratory-tropical-pneumonias-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate between scrub typhus and leptospirosis presenting with fever and respiratory distress.`,openingLines:[`Scrub typhus (Orientia tsutsugamushi, chigger bite) and leptospirosis (Leptospira, rodent urine) are the two commonest bacterial causes of acute febrile illness with lung involvement in India.`,`Both respond to doxycycline, but their lung lesions differ: ARDS from vasculitis in scrub typhus, and alveolar haemorrhage in leptospirosis.`],answer:[{heading:`Exposure and signs`,points:[`Scrub typhus: farm and grass exposure, eschar, lymphadenopathy.`,`Leptospirosis: flood, paddy, sewer; conjunctival suffusion, calf tenderness, jaundice with AKI.`]},{heading:`Lung involvement`,points:[`Scrub typhus: interstitial pneumonitis, ARDS, effusion.`,`Leptospirosis: diffuse alveolar haemorrhage with haemoptysis and falling haemoglobin; mortality over 50%.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis and treatment`,points:[`Scrub: IgM ELISA, eschar PCR; doxycycline or azithromycin; severe - IV doxycycline plus azithromycin.`,`Lepto: PCR week 1, IgM after day 5, MAT; doxycycline mild; penicillin or ceftriaxone severe; dialysis and protective ventilation.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column comparison table: exposure, sign, lung lesion, test, treatment`],markSplit:[{part:`Exposure and clinical signs`,marks:2},{part:`Lung involvement`,marks:1},{part:`Diagnosis and treatment`,marks:2}],keywords:[`eschar`,`conjunctival suffusion`,`pulmonary haemorrhage`,`ARDS`,`doxycycline`,`INTREST`,`MAT`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - chapters on melioidosis, leptospirosis, rickettsial diseases, hantavirus, influenza, Nipah and Q fever`,`Sullivan RP et al. 2020 review and revision of the 2015 Darwin melioidosis treatment guideline; paradigm drift not shift, PLoS Negl Trop Dis 2020; Limmathurotsakul D et al. Predicted global distribution of Burkholderia pseudomallei and burden of melioidosis, Nature Microbiology 2016`,`Rahi M et al. DHR-ICMR Guidelines for diagnosis and management of rickettsial diseases in India, Indian J Med Res 2015; ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows of India (acute febrile illness), 2019`,`INTREST Trial Investigators (Varghese GM et al.). Trial of intravenous doxycycline and azithromycin for severe scrub typhus, N Engl J Med 2023`,`National Centre for Disease Control. National Programme for Prevention and Control of Leptospirosis - guidelines for diagnosis and management, 2023; NCDC Nipah virus disease guidelines and outbreak advisories, 2023-2024`,`Ministry of Health and Family Welfare. Seasonal Influenza (H1N1) guidelines on categorisation, treatment and clinical management, 2023 update`,`World Health Organization. Clinical practice guidelines for influenza, 2024; WHO cumulative number of confirmed human cases of avian influenza A(H5N1), 2024`,`Bonell A et al. Estimating the burden of scrub typhus: a systematic review, PLoS Negl Trop Dis 2017`]});var l=[];l.push({id:`respiratory-adult-respiratory-vaccines`,title:`Adult respiratory vaccines: influenza, pneumococcal, RSV, pertussis and COVID`,oneLiner:`Adult respiratory vaccination means a **yearly inactivated influenza vaccine**, a **pneumococcal conjugate vaccine (PCV20 or PCV21 alone, or PCV15/PCV13 followed by PPSV23)**, a **single dose of RSV prefusion-F vaccine** in older or high-risk adults, **Tdap** once in adulthood and in every pregnancy, and **COVID-19 vaccination** for high-risk groups - offered to everyone over 50-65 and to younger adults with chronic heart, lung, kidney, liver disease, diabetes or immunosuppression.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`adult immunisation`,`influenza vaccine`,`flu shot`,`trivalent influenza vaccine`,`PCV13`,`PCV15`,`PCV20`,`PCV21`,`PPSV23`,`Prevenar`,`Pneumovax`,`RSV vaccine`,`Arexvy`,`Abrysvo`,`mRESVIA`,`Tdap`,`Boostrix`,`COVID-19 vaccine`,`API adult immunisation`,`ACIP`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Adult respiratory vaccines** are those that prevent lower respiratory tract infection and its cardiac and metabolic sequelae in adults: **influenza, pneumococcal, respiratory syncytial virus (RSV), pertussis (Tdap) and COVID-19**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lower respiratory infection** remains one of the leading infectious causes of death worldwide, and deaths concentrate at the extremes of age and in people with chronic disease. [GBD 2021]`,`Seasonal **influenza** causes an estimated **290,000-650,000 respiratory deaths a year worldwide**, most in adults over 65. [WHO 2023]`,`**Streptococcus pneumoniae** is the commonest bacterial cause of community-acquired pneumonia in adults in India and worldwide, and invasive pneumococcal disease (bacteraemia, meningitis) carries a case fatality of about 10-20% in older adults. [Harrison 22e]`,`**RSV** in adults over 60 causes a hospitalisation burden comparable to influenza in high-income settings, with exacerbations of COPD and heart failure as common presentations. [ACIP 2024]`,`In India, influenza circulates **year-round with seasonal peaks** - a monsoon peak (July-September) across most of the country and a winter peak in the southern states - as shown by **ICMR-IDSP influenza surveillance**. [ICMR 2024]`,`**Adult vaccine coverage in India is very low**: there is no adult schedule under the Universal Immunization Programme except **Td in pregnancy**, so adult vaccination depends on the clinician who offers it. [API 2024]`,`The **Universal Immunization Programme (UIP)** gives **PCV to infants** (nationwide since 2021), which reduces carriage and gives some indirect (herd) protection to adults. [MoHFW 2021]`]},{heading:`Who needs them: risk groups`,points:[`**Age** is the dominant risk factor: influenza every year for **all adults**, especially **over 50-65**; pneumococcal conjugate vaccine for **all adults 50 and over** (ACIP 2024) or **65 and over** (API 2024); RSV for **all 75 and over** and **60-74 at increased risk**. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Chronic lung disease**: COPD, asthma, bronchiectasis, interstitial lung disease, post-TB lung disease - all should receive influenza, pneumococcal and (if 60 or over) RSV vaccines. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Chronic heart disease** (heart failure, coronary disease, cardiomyopathy), **diabetes**, **chronic kidney disease** and nephrotic syndrome, and **chronic liver disease or cirrhosis**. [API 2024]`,`**Immunocompromise**: HIV, malignancy, transplant, **asplenia or sickle cell disease**, long-term corticosteroids (prednisolone 20 mg/day or more for 2 weeks or more), biologics and DMARDs. [ACIP 2024]`,`**CSF leak and cochlear implant** - specific indications for pneumococcal vaccine with a shorter PCV-to-PPSV23 interval of 8 weeks. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Smoking and alcohol use disorder** both independently raise invasive pneumococcal disease risk and are indications for pneumococcal vaccine below 50. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Pregnancy**: influenza (any trimester), **Tdap at 27-36 weeks** of each pregnancy and **RSVpreF (Abrysvo) at 32-36 weeks** to protect the newborn. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Healthcare workers, residents of old-age homes, Hajj and Umrah pilgrims** and caregivers of infants (cocooning for pertussis and influenza). [API 2024]`]},{heading:`Immunology: why the vaccines are built as they are`,points:[`**Polysaccharide vaccines (PPSV23)** stimulate B cells directly (**T-cell-independent**), so they give no immunological memory, no booster response, no effect on nasopharyngeal carriage and can cause **hyporesponsiveness** with repeat doses. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Conjugate vaccines (PCV13, PCV15, PCV20, PCV21)** link the capsular polysaccharide to a carrier protein (**CRM197**), recruiting **T-helper cells** - producing class-switched, high-affinity IgG, memory B cells and reduced carriage. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Serotype coverage** decides choice: PCV20 adds 7 serotypes to PCV13; **PCV21 (Capvaxive)** is designed around serotypes that cause most adult invasive disease and covers several not in PCV20. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Influenza** vaccine induces antibodies against **haemagglutinin (HA)**; **antigenic drift** (point mutations) means yearly strain updates and yearly vaccination, while **antigenic shift** (reassortment) causes pandemics. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Immunosenescence** in older adults blunts the antibody response, which is why **high-dose, adjuvanted (MF59) or recombinant influenza vaccines** are preferred in 65 and over where available. [ACIP 2024]`,`**RSV vaccines target the prefusion F (pre-F) protein**: pre-F displays the most potent neutralising epitopes, which are lost when F flips to its postfusion form - the structural insight behind all three licensed vaccines. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Arexvy** is pre-F protein with **AS01E adjuvant**; **Abrysvo** is bivalent (RSV A and B) pre-F without adjuvant; **mRESVIA** is an mRNA vaccine encoding pre-F. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Acellular pertussis** immunity wanes within 5-10 years, which explains adolescent and adult pertussis presenting as prolonged cough and transmission to unvaccinated infants. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Maternal IgG crosses the placenta** actively in the third trimester, so Tdap and RSVpreF given at the right gestation protect the newborn before the infant can be vaccinated. [ACIP 2024]`]},{heading:`Classification of adult respiratory vaccines`,points:[`**Inactivated/split or subunit**: inactivated influenza vaccine (IIV), recombinant influenza vaccine (RIV) - safe in pregnancy and immunosuppression. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Live attenuated**: **LAIV (intranasal)** - contraindicated in pregnancy, immunosuppression and in contacts of severely immunocompromised people; not routinely used in Indian adults. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Polysaccharide**: **PPSV23** - 23 serotypes, 0.5 mL IM or SC. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Conjugate**: PCV13 (Prevenar 13), PCV15 (Vaxneuvance), PCV20 (Prevenar 20), PCV21 (Capvaxive) - 0.5 mL IM. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Protein subunit**: RSV pre-F (Arexvy adjuvanted, Abrysvo non-adjuvanted); **Corbevax** (RBD protein) COVID vaccine made in India. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Toxoid plus acellular antigens**: Td and **Tdap** (reduced-dose diphtheria and pertussis antigens for adults). [ACIP 2024]`,`**mRNA**: COVID-19 mRNA vaccines and **mRESVIA** (RSV); **viral vector**: Covishield (ChAdOx1); **whole-virion inactivated**: Covaxin. [WHO SAGE 2024]`]},{heading:`Clinical features: the diseases the vaccines prevent`,points:[`**Influenza**: abrupt fever, myalgia, headache, dry cough and prostration; complications are primary viral pneumonia, **secondary Staphylococcus aureus or pneumococcal pneumonia**, myocarditis and **acute MI in the week after infection**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pneumococcal pneumonia**: sudden rigor, pleuritic pain, rusty sputum and lobar consolidation; invasive disease adds bacteraemia and meningitis, highest in asplenia and alcohol use disorder. [Harrison 22e]`,`**RSV in older adults**: cold-like illness that often presents as **wheeze, COPD or asthma exacerbation or decompensated heart failure** rather than as pneumonia, and is under-diagnosed without PCR. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Adult pertussis**: a **cough lasting over 2-3 weeks** with paroxysms, post-tussive vomiting or inspiratory whoop; often missed and labelled as post-viral cough. [Harrison 22e]`,`**COVID-19** in vaccinated adults is usually mild, but older adults and immunocompromised people still face pneumonia, thrombosis and hospitalisation. [WHO SAGE 2024]`,`**Opportunistic history in the OPD**: every visit by an older adult or a person with COPD, diabetes, heart failure or CKD is a chance to ask **which vaccines and when**, and write the date in the record. [API 2024]`]},{heading:`Before vaccinating: screening and examination`,points:[`Check for **anaphylaxis to a previous dose or component** - the only absolute contraindication to the inactivated vaccines. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Moderate or severe acute illness with fever** is a precaution - defer until better; a mild cold is not a reason to delay. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Egg allergy is no longer a contraindication or precaution** for any influenza vaccine, regardless of severity - no extra observation beyond routine. [ACIP 2023]`,`**Previous Guillain-Barre syndrome within 6 weeks of influenza vaccine** is a precaution; weigh risk and benefit. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Bleeding disorder or anticoagulation**: IM injection with a fine needle (23G or finer) and firm pressure for 2 minutes without rubbing is acceptable. [ACIP 2024]`,`Ask about **immunosuppressive plans** - vaccinate at least **2 weeks before** chemotherapy, rituximab, splenectomy or transplant where possible. [ACIP 2024]`,`Observe for **15 minutes** after vaccination (30 minutes after a past immediate reaction), with adrenaline 1:1000 ready - **0.5 mg IM** in the anterolateral thigh for anaphylaxis. [API 2024]`]},{heading:`Differential considerations: choosing between products`,points:[`**PCV20 or PCV21 alone** completes the pneumococcal series in one dose - simplest where available. [ACIP 2024]`,`**PCV15 (or PCV13 in India) followed by PPSV23** after at least **1 year** (8 weeks if immunocompromised, CSF leak or cochlear implant) - used where PCV20/21 is not available or affordable. [ACIP 2024]`,`**PPSV23 alone is no longer adequate** - an adult who had only PPSV23 should receive PCV20 or PCV21 at least 1 year later. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Arexvy vs Abrysvo vs mRESVIA**: all give one dose; efficacy against RSV lower respiratory disease was about **83% (Arexvy), 67-86% (Abrysvo) and 84% (mRESVIA)** in the first season; only **Abrysvo is approved in pregnancy**. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Standard-dose vs high-dose influenza vaccine** in 65 and over: high-dose, adjuvanted or recombinant vaccines are preferred (ACIP), but **any available standard-dose vaccine is better than none**. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Td vs Tdap**: Tdap once in adulthood (then Td or Tdap every 10 years), and **Tdap in every pregnancy**; India's UIP gives **Td** in pregnancy, and **Tdap** is available privately. [FOGSI 2023]`]},{heading:`Investigations: serology and records`,points:[`**No routine serology** is needed before or after influenza, pneumococcal, RSV or Tdap vaccination - the decision is clinical and schedule-based. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Haemagglutination-inhibition titre of 1:40 or more** is the conventional correlate of about 50% protection against influenza - a research measure, not a clinic test. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pneumococcal serotype-specific IgG** is measured only in immunodeficiency work-up (to test polysaccharide responsiveness), not to check vaccine success. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diagnostic confirmation of the disease** matters for public health: influenza and RSV by **RT-PCR on nasopharyngeal swab**, pertussis by **PCR in the first 3 weeks** of cough and culture/serology later. [Harrison 22e]`,`Record **product, batch number, date, site and next dose due** in the patient's card and the clinic register; a **vaccination card** for adults avoids repeat or missed doses. [API 2024]`,`Report **adverse events following immunisation (AEFI)** through the national AEFI surveillance system; serious AEFI needs investigation within the timelines of the AEFI guidelines. [MoHFW 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD: schedules and doses`,points:[`**Influenza**: inactivated vaccine **0.5 mL IM in the deltoid, once every year**, using the current-season formulation; two doses 4 weeks apart only for children under 9 getting their first season. [WHO 2024]`,`**Timing in India**: for most of India give the **Southern Hemisphere formulation in April-June** before the monsoon peak; for the southern states with a winter peak, the **Northern Hemisphere formulation in September-October**. [ICMR 2024]`,`**Pneumococcal**: **PCV20 or PCV21 0.5 mL IM single dose**; or **PCV13/PCV15 0.5 mL IM then PPSV23 0.5 mL IM or SC at least 1 year later** (8 weeks in high-risk). [ACIP 2024]`,`**RSV**: **single 0.5 mL IM dose** for all adults 75 and over and those 60-74 at increased risk (and 50-59 at increased risk per ACIP 2025); **no repeat dose** is currently recommended. [ACIP 2025]`,`**Tdap**: **0.5 mL IM once** in adulthood for those who never had it, then **Td or Tdap every 10 years**; in pregnancy at **27-36 weeks**, ideally early in that window. [ACIP 2024]`,`**COVID-19**: offer an updated dose to **older adults, immunocompromised and those with comorbidities**, about **6-12 months after the last dose** per WHO SAGE; national guidance in India currently has no routine adult booster drive. [WHO SAGE 2024]`,`**Co-administration**: influenza, PCV, RSV, Tdap and COVID vaccines can be given at the **same visit at different sites** (at least 2.5 cm apart); more local reactions but no loss of immunity. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Paracetamol** for post-vaccination fever or arm pain; do not give it routinely beforehand as prophylaxis. [API 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency settings`,points:[`**Inpatient vaccination at discharge** (after an admission for pneumonia, COPD exacerbation or heart failure) is one of the most effective missed-opportunity fixes - give influenza and PCV before the patient leaves. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Acute illness**: give vaccines once the patient is afebrile and stable; an admission for an exacerbation is not a contraindication once the acute phase settles. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Before elective splenectomy**: pneumococcal, Hib and meningococcal vaccines **at least 2 weeks before**; after emergency splenectomy, give them **14 days after** surgery or before discharge if follow-up is uncertain. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Anaphylaxis after a vaccine**: adrenaline **0.5 mg IM (0.5 mL of 1:1000)**, repeat every 5 minutes as needed, oxygen, IV fluids, and observe; document as a contraindication to that product. [Resuscitation Council UK 2021]`,`**Outbreak control in hospitals and old-age homes**: vaccinate unvaccinated staff and residents and give oseltamivir prophylaxis during an influenza outbreak. [CDC 2024]`,`**Wound management**: Td or Tdap if the last tetanus-containing dose was over 5 years ago for a dirty wound or over 10 years for a clean wound; Tdap preferred if never given. [ACIP 2024]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: inactivated influenza vaccine in **any trimester**; **Tdap 27-36 weeks**; **Abrysvo 32-36 weeks** (seasonally) or infant nirsevimab - not both; COVID vaccine where offered; **LAIV contraindicated**. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Elderly**: prefer high-dose or adjuvanted influenza vaccine if available; RSV and PCV at the same opportunity; check frailty-related falls risk is not a reason to defer. [ACIP 2024]`,`**CKD and dialysis**: influenza yearly, PCV series, plus **hepatitis B** (higher dose); antibody responses are lower but benefit is clear. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Diabetes**: all vaccines as for age, and PCV even below 50; influenza vaccination is associated with fewer cardiovascular events and admissions. [ADA 2025]`,`**HIV**: give **PCV regardless of CD4 count** (PCV20/21, or PCV13/15 then PPSV23 8 weeks later); avoid LAIV; response is better once CD4 is over 200. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Biologics and immunosuppressants**: vaccinate 2 weeks before starting; after **rituximab** wait about 6 months for a useful response; inactivated vaccines are safe at any time. [ACR 2022]`,`**Post-TB lung disease and COPD in India** (often biomass-related): these patients have the highest pneumonia risk and should be prioritised for influenza and pneumococcal vaccines. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Hajj and Umrah pilgrims**: influenza and pneumococcal vaccines recommended alongside the mandatory meningococcal ACWY vaccine. [API 2024]`]},{heading:`Adverse effects, efficacy and prognosis`,points:[`**Local reactions** (pain, redness, swelling) are the commonest; **adjuvanted vaccines (Arexvy, Shingrix-type AS01)** cause more fatigue, myalgia and fever for 1-2 days. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Guillain-Barre syndrome**: influenza vaccine carries at most about **1-2 extra cases per million doses**; an FDA warning (January 2025) notes a small GBS risk after **Arexvy and Abrysvo** in 60 and over. [FDA 2025]`,`**Influenza vaccine effectiveness** is about **40-60%** in matched seasons, lower in the elderly; it also reduces **cardiovascular events**, with an RCT (IAMI) showing lower mortality after MI. [ESC 2023]`,`**PCV13 efficacy (CAPiTA trial)**: about **45% against vaccine-type pneumococcal CAP** and **75% against vaccine-type invasive disease** in adults 65 and over. [Harrison 22e]`,`**RSV vaccine protection** persists over at least **two to three seasons** with some waning, which is why a single dose (not annual) is advised for now. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Myocarditis after mRNA COVID vaccines** is rare, mostly in young males after the second dose, and usually mild. [WHO SAGE 2024]`,`**PPSV23 local reactions** are more marked when revaccinated within 5 years; avoid unnecessary repeat doses. [ACIP 2024]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Review the vaccine record at every chronic-disease visit** (NCD clinic, COPD and asthma follow-up, dialysis) and flag what is due. [API 2024]`,`**Influenza every year** before the local season; **PCV once** (series complete with PCV20/21, or PCV13/15 plus PPSV23); **RSV once**; **Td/Tdap every 10 years**. [ACIP 2024]`,`Refer to a specialist only for **anaphylaxis to a vaccine**, suspected **primary immunodeficiency** or complex transplant/biologic schedules. [API 2024]`,`For a patient who had **PCV13 and PPSV23 before 65**, give **PCV20 or PCV21 at least 5 years after** the last pneumococcal dose to complete the series. [ACIP 2024]`,`Report and review **serious AEFI** (anaphylaxis, GBS, death) through the district AEFI committee. [MoHFW 2024]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme links`,points:[`**Counsel on myths**: the injectable influenza vaccine **cannot cause influenza** (it is inactivated); a cold after vaccination is coincidental rhinovirus infection. [WHO 2024]`,`**Strong physician recommendation** is the single biggest predictor that an adult will accept a vaccine - name the vaccine and the date. [API 2024]`,`**Cocooning**: vaccinate household contacts of newborns (Tdap and influenza) and of immunocompromised patients. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Non-vaccine prevention** belongs in the same conversation: smoking cessation, clean cooking fuel (**Pradhan Mantri Ujjwala Yojana**), hand hygiene and masks in crowded places during peaks. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**Programme links**: Td in pregnancy and infant PCV under **UIP/U-WIN**; NCD clinics under **NP-NCD** and **Ayushman Arogya Mandir** are the natural place to add adult vaccine counselling. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**Cost in India**: influenza vaccine costs a few hundred rupees; PCV and RSV vaccines are expensive, so prioritise by risk when money is limited - influenza for everyone at risk, PCV for those over 65 or with comorbidities. [API 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**ACIP October 2024**: pneumococcal conjugate vaccine age threshold **lowered from 65 to 50**; **PCV21 (Capvaxive)** added as an option after FDA approval in June 2024. [ACIP 2024]`,`**ACIP June 2024**: RSV vaccine for **all adults 75 and over** and **60-74 at increased risk**, a **single lifetime dose for now**; **ACIP April 2025** extended it to **50-59 at increased risk**. [ACIP 2025]`,`**mRESVIA (mRNA-1345)** approved in May 2024 as the third RSV vaccine; FDA (January 2025) added a GBS warning for Arexvy and Abrysvo. [FDA 2025]`,`**Influenza vaccines became trivalent from 2024-25**: WHO advised removing the **B/Yamagata** lineage, which has not been detected since March 2020. [WHO 2023]`,`**Egg allergy**: ACIP (2023) removed all extra precautions for egg-allergic people receiving influenza vaccine. [ACIP 2023]`,`**COVID-19**: WHO SAGE (2023-2024) moved to risk-based revaccination of high-priority groups every 6-12 months; US advice in 2025 moved to individual (shared) decision-making - Indian practice offers it to high-risk adults where vaccine is available. [WHO SAGE 2024]`,`**GOLD 2025** lists **RSV vaccine for people with COPD aged 60 and over**, alongside influenza, PCV20/21 (or PCV15 then PPSV23), Tdap, zoster and COVID-19. [GOLD 2025]`,`**API adult immunisation recommendations (2024)** provide the Indian adult schedule, with pneumococcal and influenza vaccines for those over 50-65 and high-risk adults. [API 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic question**: 'Write the adult immunisation schedule for a 68-year-old with COPD and diabetes' - answer influenza yearly, PCV, RSV, Tdap, zoster and COVID with doses and intervals. [API 2024]`,`**Why conjugate is better than polysaccharide** - T-cell-dependent response, memory, carriage reduction - is the commonest viva on pneumococcal vaccines. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Commonest mistake**: giving PPSV23 first and then PCV soon after - the interval after PPSV23 must be **at least 1 year**, because PPSV23 blunts the later conjugate response. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Egg allergy is not a contraindication** - examiners test this because old textbooks still say it is. [ACIP 2023]`,`**RSV vaccine is a single dose, not annual** - a common error in answers. [ACIP 2024]`,`**Tdap in every pregnancy**, even if given in a previous pregnancy, because maternal antibodies wane quickly. [ACIP 2024]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Adult respiratory vaccines: dose, schedule and cautions`,columns:[`Vaccine`,`Dose and route`,`Schedule`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Inactivated influenza (trivalent, standard dose)`,`0.5 mL IM deltoid`,`Every year before the local season (April-June for most of India)`,`Sore arm, low fever; anaphylaxis to prior dose is the only contraindication; egg allergy is NOT a contraindication`],[`High-dose or adjuvanted influenza`,`0.5 mL IM (high-dose 0.7 mL in some products)`,`Yearly, preferred in 65 and over where available`,`More local reactions; same contraindications`],[`PCV20 (Prevenar 20) or PCV21 (Capvaxive)`,`0.5 mL IM`,`Single dose completes the series`,`Local pain, fatigue; anaphylaxis to component or diphtheria toxoid (CRM197)`],[`PCV13 or PCV15 then PPSV23`,`0.5 mL IM, then PPSV23 0.5 mL IM or SC`,`PPSV23 at least 1 year after PCV (8 weeks if immunocompromised, CSF leak, cochlear implant)`,`PPSV23: marked local reaction if repeated early; no memory`],[`RSV pre-F (Arexvy, Abrysvo) or mRNA (mRESVIA)`,`0.5 mL IM`,`Single dose: all 75+, 60-74 (and 50-59) at increased risk; Abrysvo at 32-36 weeks in pregnancy`,`Fatigue, myalgia; small GBS signal (Arexvy, Abrysvo); not with nirsevimab for the same infant`],[`Tdap (Boostrix, Adacel)`,`0.5 mL IM`,`Once in adulthood, then Td/Tdap every 10 years; every pregnancy at 27-36 weeks`,`Local reaction; encephalopathy within 7 days of a pertussis vaccine is a contraindication to pertussis component`],[`COVID-19 (updated mRNA, protein subunit, Covaxin, Corbevax)`,`As per product, IM`,`High-priority adults every 6-12 months per WHO SAGE`,`Myocarditis (rare, young males, mRNA); anaphylaxis to component`]]},{heading:`Pneumococcal pathways for adults (ACIP 2024)`,columns:[`Previous vaccination`,`Option A`,`Option B`,`Interval`],rows:[[`None or unknown`,`PCV20 or PCV21 once`,`PCV15 (or PCV13) then PPSV23`,`PPSV23 at least 1 year after PCV (8 weeks if high-risk)`],[`PPSV23 only`,`PCV20 or PCV21 once`,`PCV15 once`,`At least 1 year after PPSV23`],[`PCV13 only`,`PCV20 or PCV21 once`,`PPSV23 once`,`At least 1 year after PCV13 (8 weeks if high-risk)`],[`PCV13 and PPSV23 (series incomplete for age)`,`PCV20 or PCV21 once`,`Shared decision`,`At least 5 years after last pneumococcal dose`],[`PCV20 or PCV21 already`,`Series complete`,`No further dose`,`Not applicable`]]},{heading:`Polysaccharide versus conjugate pneumococcal vaccine`,columns:[`Feature`,`PPSV23`,`PCV (13, 15, 20, 21)`],rows:[[`Immune response`,`T-cell-independent`,`T-cell-dependent via carrier protein CRM197`],[`Memory and booster effect`,`None; hyporesponsiveness on repeat`,`Memory B cells; boostable`],[`Effect on nasopharyngeal carriage`,`None`,`Reduces carriage; herd effect`],[`Serotypes`,`23`,`13, 15, 20 or 21`],[`Use in children under 2`,`Poorly immunogenic`,`Immunogenic (UIP uses PCV)`],[`Current adult role`,`Only after PCV13 or PCV15`,`First choice; PCV20/21 alone completes series`]]},{heading:`Indications by risk group`,columns:[`Group`,`Influenza`,`Pneumococcal`,`RSV`,`Tdap / COVID`],rows:[[`Healthy adult 19-49`,`Yearly (recommended for all)`,`Not needed`,`Not indicated`,`Tdap once; COVID per national advice`],[`Adult 50-64`,`Yearly`,`PCV (ACIP 2024: all 50+)`,`If at increased risk (ACIP 2025)`,`Td/Tdap every 10 years`],[`Adult 65-74`,`Yearly, high-dose preferred`,`PCV for all`,`If at increased risk`,`COVID every 6-12 months (high priority)`],[`75 and over`,`Yearly, high-dose preferred`,`PCV for all`,`All, single dose`,`COVID high priority`],[`COPD, asthma, heart failure, diabetes, CKD, liver disease (any adult age)`,`Yearly`,`PCV at any age`,`If 50 and over`,`COVID high priority`],[`Pregnancy`,`Any trimester`,`Only if indicated`,`Abrysvo 32-36 weeks (seasonal)`,`Tdap 27-36 weeks every pregnancy`],[`Immunocompromised, asplenia, HIV`,`Yearly (inactivated only)`,`PCV; PPSV23 interval 8 weeks`,`If 50 and over at risk`,`COVID high priority; no live vaccines`]]},{heading:`RSV vaccines compared`,columns:[`Feature`,`Arexvy (GSK)`,`Abrysvo (Pfizer)`,`mRESVIA (Moderna)`],rows:[[`Platform`,`Pre-F protein + AS01E adjuvant`,`Bivalent pre-F protein, no adjuvant`,`mRNA encoding pre-F`],[`First-season efficacy against RSV LRTD`,`About 83%`,`About 67-86% (by definition)`,`About 84%`],[`Use in pregnancy`,`No`,`Yes, 32-36 weeks`,`No`],[`Main safety note`,`Reactogenicity; GBS signal`,`GBS signal`,`Local and systemic reactions`],[`Dosing`,`Single 0.5 mL IM`,`Single 0.5 mL IM`,`Single 0.5 mL IM`]]}],redFlags:[`Anaphylaxis after a vaccine (stridor, hypotension, urticaria within minutes) - give adrenaline 0.5 mg IM and admit.`,`Ascending weakness or areflexia within 6 weeks of a vaccine - suspect Guillain-Barre syndrome and admit.`,`Chest pain, breathlessness or palpitations within a week of an mRNA COVID vaccine in a young male - check troponin and ECG for myocarditis.`,`Pneumonia in an asplenic or sickle cell patient - risk of overwhelming pneumococcal sepsis; admit and treat at once.`,`Paroxysmal cough with apnoea or cyanosis in a young infant whose mother was not vaccinated - suspect pertussis and admit.`,`Influenza-like illness with breathlessness, SpO2 under 94% or altered sensorium in an older adult - admit and start oseltamivir.`],pearls:[`**Egg allergy is not a contraindication** to any influenza vaccine (ACIP 2023).`,`**PCV before PPSV23, never the other way round** - PPSV23 first blunts the later PCV response; the gap after PPSV23 is at least 1 year.`,`**PCV20 or PCV21 alone = series complete.**`,`**RSV vaccine is one dose for life (so far)** - not annual like influenza.`,`**Tdap in every pregnancy at 27-36 weeks**; RSVpreF (Abrysvo) at 32-36 weeks.`,`**India's influenza season is the monsoon** for most states - vaccinate in April-June with the Southern Hemisphere vaccine.`,`**Influenza vaccine is a cardiovascular drug**: it cuts post-MI deaths (IAMI trial).`,`**Discharge = vaccination opportunity** after any admission for pneumonia, COPD or heart failure.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-adult-respiratory-vaccines-q1`,stem:`A 66-year-old man with COPD received PPSV23 two years ago and no other pneumococcal vaccine. What is the best next step according to current recommendations?`,options:[`Repeat PPSV23 now`,`Give PCV20 or PCV21 once now`,`No further pneumococcal vaccine is needed`,`Give PCV13 now and PPSV23 again 8 weeks later`],answer:1,explanation:`An adult who has had only PPSV23 should receive a conjugate vaccine at least 1 year later; PCV20 or PCV21 alone then completes the series. Repeating PPSV23 adds no memory and increases local reactions; stopping leaves him without conjugate protection; PCV13 followed by another early PPSV23 is outdated and over-vaccinates.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-adult-respiratory-vaccines-q2`,stem:`A 34-year-old woman with a history of urticaria and lip swelling after eating eggs asks about influenza vaccination. What is the correct advice?`,options:[`Influenza vaccine is contraindicated`,`Give only after skin testing with the vaccine`,`Give in hospital with 2 hours of observation`,`Give any age-appropriate influenza vaccine with routine observation`],answer:3,explanation:`Since 2023 ACIP advises that people with egg allergy of any severity can receive any influenza vaccine with no extra precautions beyond the routine observation and readiness for anaphylaxis that apply to every vaccinee. Skin testing, prolonged observation and avoidance are outdated advice that leaves allergic patients unprotected.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-adult-respiratory-vaccines-q3`,stem:`Which property explains why conjugate pneumococcal vaccines reduce nasopharyngeal carriage while PPSV23 does not?`,options:[`Recruitment of T-helper cells by the carrier protein, producing memory and mucosal IgG`,`Higher number of serotypes in the conjugate vaccine`,`Live attenuated organisms colonising the nasopharynx`,`Adjuvant-induced innate immune activation alone`],answer:0,explanation:`Linking polysaccharide to the carrier protein CRM197 makes the response T-cell-dependent, giving class-switched high-affinity IgG, memory and mucosal effects that cut carriage and create herd protection. PPSV23 actually has more serotypes (23), so serotype number is not the reason; neither vaccine is live, and adjuvant alone does not create memory.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-adult-respiratory-vaccines-q4`,stem:`A 72-year-old woman with heart failure received an RSV vaccine last year. She asks whether she needs another dose this season. What is the correct answer?`,options:[`Yes, RSV vaccine is given every year like influenza`,`Yes, a booster is due at 12 months`,`No, currently a single dose is recommended and protection lasts at least two to three seasons`,`No, RSV vaccine is not recommended for heart failure`],answer:2,explanation:`RSV vaccines (Arexvy, Abrysvo, mRESVIA) are currently recommended as a single dose because protection persists over at least two to three seasons; revaccination is not yet advised. Heart failure is precisely a condition that makes a 60-74-year-old eligible, so the claim that it is not recommended is wrong, and annual or 12-month boosting is not current advice.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-adult-respiratory-vaccines-q5`,stem:`In which month is it best to give influenza vaccine to an adult with diabetes living in Delhi?`,options:[`January, before the winter peak`,`April-May, before the monsoon peak, using the Southern Hemisphere formulation`,`October, using the Northern Hemisphere formulation`,`Any month; timing does not matter in India`],answer:1,explanation:`Surveillance shows that most of India, including the north, has its main influenza peak in the monsoon (July-September), so the Southern Hemisphere formulation in April-June is best. October with the Northern Hemisphere formulation suits the southern states with a winter peak; January is too late; timing matters because protection wanes within months.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-adult-respiratory-vaccines-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss adult immunisation against respiratory infections. Write the schedule you would follow for a 68-year-old man with COPD and type 2 diabetes attending your clinic.`,openingLines:[`Adult respiratory vaccination comprises yearly inactivated influenza vaccine, pneumococcal conjugate vaccine (PCV20/21, or PCV13/15 followed by PPSV23), a single dose of RSV vaccine, Tdap and risk-based COVID-19 vaccination.`,`Older adults and people with COPD, diabetes, heart, kidney or liver disease are the priority, and each clinic visit is an opportunity to vaccinate.`],answer:[{heading:`Burden and rationale`,points:[`Influenza, pneumococcus and RSV cause most vaccine-preventable pneumonia and exacerbations in older adults.`,`COPD and diabetes multiply the risk of pneumonia, hospitalisation and death; vaccines also reduce cardiovascular events.`,`India has no adult UIP schedule except Td in pregnancy - adult vaccination depends on the physician.`]},{heading:`Influenza`,points:[`Inactivated trivalent vaccine 0.5 mL IM yearly; high-dose or adjuvanted preferred at 65 and over where available.`,`Timing: April-June (Southern Hemisphere formulation) for most of India; September-October in southern states.`,`Egg allergy is not a contraindication; only anaphylaxis to a prior dose is.`]},{heading:`Pneumococcal`,points:[`PCV20 or PCV21 single dose, or PCV13/15 then PPSV23 at least 1 year later.`,`Explain why conjugate first: T-cell-dependent memory; PPSV23 first blunts the later response.`]},{heading:`RSV`,points:[`Single 0.5 mL IM dose of Arexvy, Abrysvo or mRESVIA - he qualifies at 68 with COPD and diabetes.`,`Efficacy about 67-84% against RSV lower respiratory disease in the first season; small GBS signal.`]},{heading:`Tdap, COVID-19 and others`,points:[`Tdap once if never given, then Td/Tdap every 10 years.`,`COVID-19 updated vaccine every 6-12 months (WHO SAGE high-priority group).`,`Recombinant zoster vaccine (2 doses) is also listed by GOLD for COPD over 50.`]},{heading:`Practical points`,points:[`All can be co-administered at different sites; observe 15 minutes.`,`Record batch, date and next due date; report AEFI.`,`Counsel: inactivated vaccine cannot cause flu; smoking cessation and clean fuel alongside.`]}],mustDraw:[`Schedule table for a 68-year-old with COPD and diabetes`,`PCV-PPSV23 pathway flow chart`],markSplit:[{part:`Burden and rationale`,marks:1},{part:`Influenza`,marks:2},{part:`Pneumococcal with immunology`,marks:3},{part:`RSV, Tdap, COVID`,marks:2},{part:`Schedule for the patient and practical points`,marks:2}],keywords:[`influenza`,`PCV20`,`PPSV23`,`RSV vaccine`,`Tdap`,`COPD`,`API 2024`]},{id:`respiratory-adult-respiratory-vaccines-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate between polysaccharide and conjugate pneumococcal vaccines.`,openingLines:[`PPSV23 is a 23-valent plain polysaccharide vaccine that gives a T-cell-independent response; PCVs link polysaccharide to a carrier protein (CRM197) to give a T-cell-dependent response.`,`This difference explains memory, carriage reduction and why conjugate vaccine is now the first choice in adults.`],answer:[{heading:`Immunology`,points:[`PPSV23: B-cell only, IgM-dominant, no memory, hyporesponsiveness on repeat dosing.`,`PCV: T-helper help, class switch to high-affinity IgG, memory B cells, boostable.`]},{heading:`Clinical consequences`,points:[`PPSV23 does not reduce carriage; PCV reduces carriage and gives herd protection.`,`PPSV23 is poorly immunogenic under 2 years; PCV works in infants (UIP).`]},{heading:`Use in adults`,points:[`PCV20 or PCV21 alone completes the series.`,`PCV13/15 then PPSV23 at least 1 year later (8 weeks if high-risk) broadens serotype cover.`,`PPSV23 alone is no longer adequate.`]}],mustDraw:[`Comparison table: PPSV23 vs PCV`],markSplit:[{part:`Definitions`,marks:1},{part:`Immunology`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical use`,marks:2}],keywords:[`PPSV23`,`PCV`,`CRM197`,`T-cell-dependent`,`carriage`,`hyporesponsiveness`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - chapter on immunization principles and vaccine use`,`CDC/ACIP Adult Immunization Schedule, United States, 2025 (including October 2024 pneumococcal and June 2024/April 2025 RSV recommendations)`,`Association of Physicians of India (API) adult immunisation recommendations, 2024`,`Global Initiative for Chronic Obstructive Lung Disease (GOLD) Report, 2025`,`WHO SAGE roadmap for prioritising uses of COVID-19 vaccines, updated 2023-2024; WHO influenza vaccine composition recommendations 2023-2025`,`Ministry of Health and Family Welfare, Government of India - Universal Immunization Programme and AEFI surveillance guidelines, 2024`,`FOGSI guidance on maternal immunisation (Tdap and influenza in pregnancy), 2023`]}),l.push({id:`respiratory-pleural-other`,title:`Malignant effusion, mesothelioma, chylothorax and pleural thickening`,oneLiner:`A **malignant pleural effusion** (malignant cells in pleural fluid or pleura) signals advanced cancer and is palliated by **therapeutic aspiration, talc pleurodesis or an indwelling pleural catheter (IPC)**; **mesothelioma** is an asbestos-related pleural cancer with a 30-40-year latency; **chylothorax** is lymph in the pleural space with **triglycerides over 110 mg/dL**; and **fibrothorax or trapped (non-expandable) lung** follows a pleural peel that stops the lung re-expanding.`,frequency:`less-common`,keywords:[`malignant pleural effusion`,`MPE`,`indwelling pleural catheter`,`IPC`,`talc pleurodesis`,`talc poudrage`,`mesothelioma`,`asbestos`,`pleural plaques`,`chylothorax`,`pseudochylothorax`,`cholesterol effusion`,`fibrothorax`,`trapped lung`,`non-expandable lung`,`decortication`,`LENT score`,`thoracic duct`,`BTS 2023`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Malignant pleural effusion (MPE)** is an effusion with malignant cells in the fluid or on pleural biopsy; a **paramalignant effusion** occurs in cancer without pleural invasion (lymphatic obstruction, SVC obstruction, post-obstructive pneumonia, drugs, radiotherapy). [Harrison 22e]`,`**MPE is the second commonest cause of exudate** in India after tuberculosis and the commonest cause of a large exudate in people over 50 in Western series. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Causes of MPE**: lung cancer (about one-third), **breast cancer**, lymphoma, ovarian and gastrointestinal cancers, and mesothelioma; about 5-10% have an unknown primary. [BTS 2023]`,`**Median survival after MPE diagnosis is about 3-12 months**, varying with tumour type (worst in lung cancer and mesothelioma, best in breast and haematological cancers). [BTS 2023]`,`**Malignant pleural mesothelioma** incidence is still rising in countries that used asbestos late; **India continues to import and use chrysotile asbestos** (cement sheets, brake linings), so a later epidemic is expected. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chylothorax** accounts for under 3% of effusions; **trauma and surgery** (oesophagectomy, cardiac and thoracic surgery) cause about half and **lymphoma** is the commonest non-traumatic cause. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pseudochylothorax (cholesterol effusion)** is rare and arises in long-standing (usually over 5 years) effusions with thick pleura - **old tuberculous pleurisy and rheumatoid arthritis** in India. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fibrothorax and trapped lung** are late sequelae of **tuberculous pleurisy, empyema and haemothorax** - common in India because of the TB burden. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Asbestos** causes the whole spectrum: **pleural plaques, benign asbestos pleural effusion, diffuse pleural thickening, rounded atelectasis, asbestosis and mesothelioma**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Amphiboles (crocidolite, amosite)** are far more carcinogenic for mesothelioma than **chrysotile** (serpentine), because long thin fibres persist in the pleura; chrysotile is still carcinogenic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mesothelioma risk** is dose-related but has **no safe threshold**; latency is **30-40 years**, and household (washing work clothes) and environmental exposures are recognised. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Germline BAP1 mutation** (BAP1 tumour predisposition syndrome) causes familial mesothelioma, uveal melanoma and renal cancer; **prior chest radiotherapy** is another cause. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chylothorax causes**: traumatic (surgery, penetrating injury, central line), **malignant (lymphoma, metastatic cancer)**, lymphangioleiomyomatosis (LAM), TB, **filariasis**, sarcoidosis, SVC or subclavian thrombosis, cirrhosis with chylous ascites, Gorham and yellow-nail syndromes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Thoracic duct anatomy explains the side**: the duct runs on the right of the aorta in the lower chest and crosses to the left at about T4-T6, so injury **below T5 gives right chylothorax and above it gives left**. [Gray's Anatomy 42e]`,`**Fibrothorax** follows inadequately drained **empyema, haemothorax or tuberculous effusion**, and also asbestos, uraemia, drug-induced (ergot, bromocriptine, methysergide) and post-CABG pleuritis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**MPE forms** because tumour on the parietal pleura **blocks lymphatic stomata** (reduced exit) and tumour-derived **VEGF and inflammatory cytokines raise capillary permeability** (increased entry). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pleurodesis** works by inducing **pleural inflammation and fibrosis** that fuse visceral and parietal pleura; it needs **apposition of the pleural surfaces**, so it fails when the lung cannot re-expand. [BTS 2023]`,`**Talc** is the most effective sclerosant; **graded (large-particle) talc** is used because small particles caused ARDS with ungraded talc. [BTS 2023]`,`**Non-expandable lung (NEL)** exists when visceral pleural peel, endobronchial obstruction or stiff lung prevents re-expansion; draining fluid then creates **steeply negative pleural pressure**, causing chest pain, cough and **pneumothorax ex vacuo**. [BTS 2023]`,`**Trapped lung** is a mature fibrous peel from a remote inflammation (stable, often transudate-like); **lung entrapment** is the same mechanics from an active process such as tumour or infection. [Light's Pleural Diseases 6e]`,`**Pleural elastance** above about **14.5 cmH2O/L** on manometry indicates non-expandable lung and predicts failure of pleurodesis. [Light's Pleural Diseases 6e]`,`**Chyle** contains chylomicrons (dietary long-chain triglyceride), lymphocytes, fat-soluble vitamins and immunoglobulins; 1.5-2.5 L/day flows through the thoracic duct, so a persistent leak causes **malnutrition, lymphopenia and immunosuppression**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pseudochylous fluid** arises from **cholesterol released by degenerating cells** in a long-standing effusion that cannot escape through a thickened pleura - no chylomicrons, no thoracic duct leak. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mesothelioma** grows as a **rind encasing the lung**, invading chest wall, mediastinum and diaphragm, producing restrictive physiology and severe neuropathic chest pain. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`**Asbestos-related pleural disease**: pleural plaques (benign marker of exposure), benign asbestos pleural effusion, diffuse pleural thickening (DPT), rounded atelectasis, mesothelioma. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mesothelioma histology** (WHO 2021): **epithelioid** (about 60%, best prognosis), **sarcomatoid** (worst, poor response to chemotherapy) and **biphasic**; 'mesothelioma in situ' was added. [WHO 2021]`,`**Mesothelioma staging** uses the **IASLC/UICC TNM** (8th edition, with 9th edition 2024 revision for pleural mesothelioma). [IASLC 2024]`,`**Chylous vs pseudochylous vs empyema**: all milky; chylothorax TG over 110 mg/dL with chylomicrons; pseudochylothorax cholesterol over 200 mg/dL with crystals; empyema clears on centrifugation. [Light's Pleural Diseases 6e]`,`**Chylothorax**: traumatic (including iatrogenic) and non-traumatic (malignant, idiopathic, other medical). [Harrison 22e]`,`**MPE by lung expandability**: expandable lung (pleurodesis or IPC both options) versus non-expandable lung (IPC preferred). [BTS 2023]`,`**LENT prognostic score** (pleural fluid LDH, ECOG performance status, neutrophil-to-lymphocyte ratio, tumour type): low, moderate and high risk with median survival about 319, 130 and 44 days. [Clive 2014, BTS 2023]`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`**Breathlessness** is the dominant MPE symptom and correlates with effusion size; large effusions may cause orthopnoea and cough. [BTS 2023]`,`**Mesothelioma**: insidious **dull, poorly localised chest pain** (often with neuropathic quality from intercostal nerve invasion), breathlessness, weight loss, sweats; ask about **asbestos exposure 30-40 years ago**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Occupational history** for asbestos in India: **asbestos-cement sheet factories, ship-breaking (Alang), brake-lining, insulation, construction, textile and mining**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chylothorax**: breathlessness without fever or pleuritic pain (chyle is not irritant), with weight loss and recurrent infections if chronic; ask about **recent thoracic surgery, lymph nodes, or filarial disease**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pseudochylothorax**: often asymptomatic or mild breathlessness in someone with **TB pleurisy many years ago** or long-standing rheumatoid arthritis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fibrothorax**: progressive exertional breathlessness, chest tightness and a **shrunken hemithorax** after past empyema, haemothorax or TB. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Trapped lung clue**: an effusion that **reaccumulates to the same size** repeatedly and causes **chest pain or cough when drained**. [BTS 2023]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Effusion signs**: reduced expansion, **stony dull percussion**, absent breath sounds and vocal resonance, with bronchial breathing at the upper border. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mediastinal shift**: a large MPE usually pushes the trachea **away**; a large effusion with **no shift or shift towards it** suggests **mesothelioma encasement, endobronchial obstruction with collapse, or a fixed mediastinum**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mesothelioma**: **contracted hemithorax** with reduced movement, chest wall mass at prior biopsy or drain sites (tract seeding), and **clubbing** (uncommon). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fibrothorax**: flattened, **retracted hemithorax**, crowded ribs, reduced expansion, dullness and diminished breath sounds, with the trachea pulled **towards** the affected side. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for the **primary**: breast lump, lymph nodes (Virchow), hepatomegaly, ovarian mass, and signs of lymphoma (splenomegaly, B symptoms). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chylothorax**: look for lymphadenopathy, **limb lymphoedema or chyluria** (filariasis), yellow nails, and signs of SVC obstruction. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Tuberculous pleural effusion** is the main differential of a lymphocytic exudate in India - **ADA over 40 U/L** favours TB, but lymphoma and some mesotheliomas can also raise ADA (see the Pleural effusion topic). [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**Mesothelioma vs metastatic adenocarcinoma**: mesothelioma is **calretinin, WT1, D2-40 (podoplanin) and CK5/6 positive**; adenocarcinoma is **TTF-1, CEA, Ber-EP4, MOC-31 and claudin-4** positive. [WHO 2021]`,`**Mesothelioma vs reactive mesothelial hyperplasia**: **loss of BAP1** on IHC or **CDKN2A (p16) homozygous deletion / MTAP loss** confirms malignancy. [WHO 2021]`,`**Chylothorax vs pseudochylothorax vs empyema**: TG over 110 (chyle), cholesterol over 200 with crystals (pseudochyle), and a supernatant that **clears on centrifugation** (empyema). [Light's Pleural Diseases 6e]`,`**Pleural plaques vs mesothelioma**: plaques are discrete, often **calcified, bilateral, on the parietal pleura and diaphragm**, sparing apices and costophrenic angles; mesothelioma is nodular, circumferential and involves the mediastinal pleura. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diffuse pleural thickening vs plaques**: DPT involves the **visceral pleura** with **costophrenic angle obliteration** and causes restriction; plaques usually do not. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rounded atelectasis**: a peripheral comet-tail (vessels curving into a subpleural mass) next to thickened pleura - mimics lung cancer on CT. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Chest X-ray** and **thoracic ultrasound** (septations, pleural nodules, diaphragm thickening over 7 mm suggest malignancy) before any pleural procedure. [BTS 2023]`,`**Pleural fluid cytology** detects malignancy in about **60%** overall (higher in adenocarcinoma, lower in mesothelioma); a second sample adds little, so go to biopsy if two are negative. [BTS 2023]`,`**Contrast CT thorax** (venous phase, ideally before drainage) showing **nodular pleural thickening, mediastinal pleural involvement, parietal thickening over 1 cm or circumferential thickening** suggests malignancy (Leung criteria). [BTS 2023]`,`**Pleural biopsy**: **image-guided (CT or ultrasound) cutting-needle biopsy** or **local anaesthetic thoracoscopy** (diagnostic yield over 90% and allows talc poudrage at the same time). [BTS 2023]`,`**Chylothorax**: pleural fluid **triglycerides over 110 mg/dL (1.24 mmol/L) confirms**, under 50 mg/dL excludes, 50-110 needs **lipoprotein electrophoresis for chylomicrons**; cholesterol is usually under 200 mg/dL. [Light's Pleural Diseases 6e]`,`**Pseudochylothorax**: **cholesterol over 200 mg/dL** (often much higher), **cholesterol crystals** (rhomboid, birefringent under polarised light), TG usually under 110. [Light's Pleural Diseases 6e]`,`**Locate the chyle leak**: CT for lymphoma or mass, **lymphangiography or dynamic contrast MR lymphangiography**, and lymphoscintigraphy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pleural manometry** during aspiration: elastance over 14.5 cmH2O/L or a pressure falling below **-20 cmH2O** indicates non-expandable lung - stop drainage. [Light's Pleural Diseases 6e]`,`**Mesothelioma markers**: serum mesothelin (SMRP) and pleural fluid **hyaluronic acid** support but never replace histology. [ESMO 2022]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and day care`,points:[`**Asymptomatic MPE**: no drainage; observe and treat the cancer (systemic therapy may control it, e.g. breast, lymphoma, EGFR-mutant lung cancer). [BTS 2023]`,`**Symptomatic MPE, first presentation**: **therapeutic aspiration of up to 1.5 L** for symptom relief and to see whether breathlessness improves and the lung re-expands; stop for chest tightness or cough. [BTS 2023]`,`**Recurrent symptomatic MPE with expandable lung**: offer **either an IPC or talc pleurodesis** as first-line definitive treatment, guided by **patient preference** - both relieve breathlessness equally. [BTS 2023]`,`**IPC** (tunnelled catheter) allows **home drainage by family or community nurse** (usually 3 times a week, or daily for faster spontaneous pleurodesis); fewer hospital days than talc. [BTS 2023]`,`**Talc slurry**: **4 g graded talc** in 50 mL saline via a small-bore chest drain once the lung has re-expanded, or **talc poudrage at thoracoscopy** - equally effective. [BTS 2023]`,`**NSAIDs are acceptable** for pain after pleurodesis; they did not reduce pleurodesis success in the TIME1 trial. [BTS 2023]`,`**Pleural plaques**: reassure (they are benign), record exposure and counsel; no follow-up imaging is needed for plaques alone. [BTS 2023]`,`**Pseudochylothorax**: usually no treatment; **therapeutic aspiration** for breathlessness; treat active TB if present; **decortication** if the lung is trapped and the patient is symptomatic. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD: definitive procedures`,points:[`**Non-expandable lung with MPE**: **IPC is preferred**; talc pleurodesis will fail because the surfaces cannot meet. [BTS 2023]`,`**IPC plus talc via the catheter** (IPC-PLUS trial) raises pleurodesis rates at 5 weeks compared with IPC alone and allows catheter removal. [BTS 2023]`,`**Chylothorax - conservative first**: drainage, **low-fat diet with medium-chain triglycerides (MCT)** (MCTs go directly to the portal vein, bypassing the lymphatics), or **nil by mouth with TPN** for high output. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Octreotide** reduces lymph flow in chylothorax - commonly **100-200 micrograms SC every 8 hours** (off-label); evidence is mainly observational. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chylothorax - intervene** if output is over **1-1.5 L/day for 5 days** or persists over **2 weeks**, or there is nutritional decline: **thoracic duct embolisation** (interventional radiology) or **thoracic duct ligation**; pleurodesis or pleuroperitoneal shunt are alternatives. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Malignant chylothorax**: treat the cause - **chemotherapy or radiotherapy for lymphoma**; LAM responds to **sirolimus**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fibrothorax**: **surgical decortication** (VATS or open) if symptomatic restriction persists after about **6 months** of resolution of the acute process; early decortication within weeks for organised empyema. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Trapped lung**: do not insert a chest drain for **pneumothorax ex vacuo** after a tap; observe, and consider IPC for symptomatic recurrence or decortication in fit patients. [BTS 2023]`]},{heading:`Mesothelioma: treatment`,points:[`**Multidisciplinary team** decision with histological diagnosis and staging (CT, PET-CT where available); most patients present with unresectable disease. [ESMO 2022]`,`**First-line nivolumab 3 mg/kg every 2 weeks plus ipilimumab 1 mg/kg every 6 weeks** for up to 2 years improved survival over chemotherapy (CheckMate 743, median OS 18.1 vs 14.1 months) - greatest benefit in **non-epithelioid** tumours. [ESMO 2022]`,`**Pemetrexed 500 mg/m2 plus cisplatin 75 mg/m2 every 3 weeks** for 4-6 cycles (with folic acid and vitamin B12 premedication), plus **bevacizumab** in fit patients (MAPS), remains standard, especially for epithelioid disease. [ESMO 2022]`,`**Pembrolizumab plus pemetrexed-platinum** improved overall survival (KEYNOTE-483/IND.227) and was FDA-approved in 2024 for unresectable mesothelioma. [FDA 2024]`,`**Extended pleurectomy-decortication is not recommended** - the **MARS2 trial (2024)** showed worse survival and more harm with surgery added to chemotherapy. [MARS2 2024]`,`**Palliation**: IPC for effusion, **radiotherapy for painful chest wall masses**, opioids plus neuropathic agents for pain, and early palliative care referral. [BTS 2023]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Elderly or frail with short prognosis** (high LENT score): repeated therapeutic aspiration or IPC is better than an admission for talc. [BTS 2023]`,`**Pregnancy**: MPE is rare; chylothorax may follow delivery (thoracic duct strain); manage conservatively with drainage and diet. [Light's Pleural Diseases 6e]`,`**Neonates and children**: congenital chylothorax is the commonest cause of pleural effusion in the newborn; manage with MCT feeds and octreotide in a NICU. [Nelson 22e]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: where IPC is unavailable or unaffordable, **bedside talc slurry or repeated aspiration**; povidone-iodine and tetracycline/doxycycline are cheaper but less effective and more painful. [BTS 2023]`,`**Anticoagulated patients**: hold warfarin or DOAC before IPC insertion or thoracoscopy per procedural bleeding-risk guidance. [BTS 2023]`,`**Workers' compensation**: asbestosis and mesothelioma are **notifiable occupational diseases** under the Factories Act and compensable under the **Employees' Compensation Act** in India. [DGFASLI 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**IPC complications**: pleural infection (about 5%, usually treatable without removing the catheter), catheter blockage, **tract metastasis** (especially mesothelioma) and loculation. [BTS 2023]`,`**Talc pleurodesis**: pleuritic pain and fever in the first 48 hours, pleurodesis failure (about 20-30%), and rarely **ARDS** (avoided by graded talc). [BTS 2023]`,`**Re-expansion pulmonary oedema** after rapid removal of large volumes - limit aspiration to **1.5 L** at a sitting unless manometry is used. [BTS 2023]`,`**Chylothorax complications**: **malnutrition, lymphopenia and hypogammaglobulinaemia with infections**, electrolyte losses and dehydration. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mesothelioma prognosis**: median survival about **12-18 months** on modern therapy; **sarcomatoid histology, poor performance status and high white count** predict worse outcome. [ESMO 2022]`,`**Fibrothorax** causes chronic **restrictive ventilatory defect**, chest wall deformity and, if bilateral, type 2 respiratory failure. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Refer every **unexplained exudate after TB is reasonably excluded** to a pleural service for thoracoscopy or image-guided biopsy. [BTS 2023]`,`Refer a **new diagnosis of MPE** to oncology, and early to **palliative care** for symptom control. [BTS 2023]`,`**IPC follow-up**: track drainage volumes; when three consecutive drainages are under 50 mL with clear ultrasound, consider catheter removal (spontaneous pleurodesis). [BTS 2023]`,`**Chylothorax**: weigh and check albumin, lymphocyte count and electrolytes regularly; record daily chest drain output. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Asbestos-exposed workers**: occupational history, spirometry and chest X-ray as part of periodic medical examination; counsel to stop smoking (asbestos plus smoking multiplies lung cancer risk). [DGFASLI 2023]`]},{heading:`Prevention and counselling`,points:[`**No safe level of asbestos exposure**: advocate substitution, wet methods, respirators and medical surveillance; India banned asbestos mining but still uses chrysotile in products. [WHO 2024]`,`**Smoking cessation**: asbestos plus smoking raises lung cancer risk multiplicatively (about 50-fold together). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Prompt drainage of empyema and haemothorax** and complete TB treatment prevent fibrothorax and trapped lung. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Filariasis elimination** (mass drug administration under the National Centre for Vector Borne Diseases Control) removes a cause of chylous effusion in endemic areas. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Counsel patients with MPE** honestly on prognosis and choice between IPC and talc, so the decision fits their goals and home support. [BTS 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**BTS Guideline for Pleural Disease (2023)**: in symptomatic MPE with expandable lung, **IPC or talc pleurodesis are both first-line** by patient choice; IPC for non-expandable lung; talc slurry and poudrage are equivalent. [BTS 2023]`,`**BTS 2023** supports **daily IPC drainage** to speed spontaneous pleurodesis and **talc via IPC** to improve pleurodesis rates. [BTS 2023]`,`**BTS 2023** recommends **thoracic ultrasound by trained operators** before every pleural procedure and uses the term **non-expandable lung**. [BTS 2023]`,`**MARS2 (2024)**: extended pleurectomy-decortication worsens outcomes in mesothelioma - surgery is no longer standard. [MARS2 2024]`,`**Pembrolizumab with chemotherapy** approved for mesothelioma (FDA 2024), joining nivolumab-ipilimumab as first-line immunotherapy. [FDA 2024]`,`**IASLC 9th edition TNM (2024)** revised pleural mesothelioma T staging using pleural thickness measurements. [IASLC 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**'Milky pleural fluid - differential?'** - chylothorax, pseudochylothorax and empyema; centrifuge, then measure TG and cholesterol and look for crystals. [Light's Pleural Diseases 6e]`,`**TG over 110 mg/dL = chylothorax**; cholesterol over 200 with crystals = pseudochylothorax. [Light's Pleural Diseases 6e]`,`**Effusion with no mediastinal shift**: think mesothelioma, endobronchial obstruction or fixed mediastinum. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Commonest mistake**: inserting a chest drain for pneumothorax ex vacuo after a tap in a trapped lung - it will not re-expand. [BTS 2023]`,`**Asbestos body** (ferruginous body) in sputum or tissue indicates exposure, not disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mesothelioma IHC**: calretinin, WT1, D2-40, CK5/6 positive; TTF-1 and CEA negative. [WHO 2021]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs and agents`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Graded talc (slurry)`,`4 g in 50 mL saline via chest drain; clamp 1 hour`,`Single instillation once lung re-expanded`,`Pleuritic pain, fever; rare ARDS (ungraded talc); fails if lung non-expandable`],[`Talc via IPC`,`4 g via IPC`,`Single dose, then continue drainage`,`Pain, catheter blockage`],[`Octreotide (chylothorax)`,`100-200 micrograms SC every 8 hours`,`1-2 weeks, then review output`,`Gallstones, hyperglycaemia, GI upset; off-label`],[`Medium-chain triglyceride diet`,`Low-fat diet with MCT oil`,`Until leak closes`,`Diarrhoea; essential fatty acid deficiency if prolonged`],[`Nivolumab plus ipilimumab`,`Nivolumab 3 mg/kg IV 2-weekly + ipilimumab 1 mg/kg IV 6-weekly`,`Up to 2 years`,`Immune colitis, hepatitis, pneumonitis, endocrinopathies`],[`Pemetrexed plus cisplatin (+/- bevacizumab)`,`Pemetrexed 500 mg/m2 + cisplatin 75 mg/m2 IV every 3 weeks`,`4-6 cycles`,`Myelosuppression (give folic acid and B12), nephrotoxicity, ototoxicity`],[`Sirolimus (LAM chylothorax)`,`2 mg orally daily, titrate to trough 5-15 ng/mL`,`Long-term`,`Mouth ulcers, hyperlipidaemia, infections`]]},{heading:`Milky pleural fluid: chylous, pseudochylous or empyema`,columns:[`Feature`,`Chylothorax`,`Pseudochylothorax`,`Empyema`],rows:[[`After centrifugation`,`Stays milky`,`Stays milky`,`Supernatant clears`],[`Triglycerides`,`Over 110 mg/dL`,`Usually under 110 mg/dL`,`Variable`],[`Cholesterol`,`Under 200 mg/dL`,`Over 200 mg/dL`,`Variable`],[`Chylomicrons`,`Present`,`Absent`,`Absent`],[`Cholesterol crystals`,`Absent`,`Present`,`Absent`],[`Onset`,`Acute or subacute`,`Years (often over 5)`,`Days to weeks`],[`Pleura`,`Normal`,`Thickened, calcified`,`Thickened, loculated`],[`Typical cause`,`Surgery, lymphoma, filariasis`,`Old TB, rheumatoid arthritis`,`Pneumonia`]]},{heading:`Asbestos-related pleural disease`,columns:[`Entity`,`Latency`,`Imaging`,`Significance`],rows:[[`Pleural plaques`,`20-30 years`,`Discrete, often calcified, bilateral; diaphragm and parietal pleura`,`Marker of exposure; benign; not premalignant`],[`Benign asbestos pleural effusion`,`Under 20 years (earliest)`,`Small exudate, often bloody, eosinophilic`,`Diagnosis of exclusion; may precede DPT`],[`Diffuse pleural thickening`,`Variable`,`Visceral pleura, costophrenic angle blunted`,`Restriction, breathlessness`],[`Rounded atelectasis`,`Variable`,`Subpleural mass with comet-tail sign`,`Mimics lung cancer`],[`Mesothelioma`,`30-40 years`,`Nodular, circumferential, mediastinal pleura involved`,`Malignant; poor prognosis`]]},{heading:`Malignant effusion: IPC versus talc pleurodesis`,columns:[`Feature`,`Indwelling pleural catheter`,`Talc pleurodesis`],rows:[[`Lung must re-expand`,`No - works in non-expandable lung`,`Yes`],[`Hospital stay`,`Day case`,`Usually 2-5 days`],[`Breathlessness relief`,`Equivalent`,`Equivalent`],[`Re-intervention`,`Less`,`More (failure about 20-30%)`],[`Downsides`,`Infection, home drainage burden, consumable cost`,`Pain, fever, admission`],[`BTS 2023 position`,`First-line option; preferred if NEL`,`First-line option if lung expandable`]]},{heading:`Investigation cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`Cut-off`,`Interpretation`],rows:[[`Pleural fluid triglycerides`,`Over 110 mg/dL`,`Chylothorax`],[`Pleural fluid triglycerides`,`Under 50 mg/dL`,`Chylothorax unlikely`],[`Pleural fluid cholesterol`,`Over 200 mg/dL with crystals`,`Pseudochylothorax`],[`Pleural elastance on manometry`,`Over 14.5 cmH2O/L`,`Non-expandable lung`],[`Pleural pressure during tap`,`Below -20 cmH2O`,`Stop drainage`],[`Cytology yield in MPE`,`About 60%`,`Biopsy if negative and suspicion remains`],[`Pleural thickening on CT`,`Over 1 cm, nodular, mediastinal, circumferential`,`Suggests malignancy`]]}],redFlags:[`Rapidly reaccumulating effusion causing breathlessness at rest or SpO2 under 92% - urgent drainage.`,`Chylothorax output over 1 L/day - admit for nutrition support and consideration of duct embolisation.`,`Fever, rigors and cloudy fluid from an indwelling pleural catheter - IPC-related pleural infection.`,`Large effusion with contralateral mediastinal shift and hypotension - urgent decompression.`,`New chest wall mass at a previous drain or biopsy site in mesothelioma - tract metastasis.`,`Unilateral exudate with asbestos exposure, weight loss and chest pain - urgent referral for biopsy.`,`Severe chest pain or cough during aspiration - stop at once (non-expandable lung).`],pearls:[`**TG over 110 mg/dL = chylothorax**; under 50 rules it out.`,`**Cholesterol over 200 with crystals = pseudochylothorax** - old TB or rheumatoid.`,`**Empyema clears on centrifugation**; chyle and pseudochyle stay milky.`,`**Thoracic duct crosses at T5**: injury below gives right chylothorax, above gives left.`,`**IPC or talc - patient's choice** if the lung expands; **IPC if it does not** (BTS 2023).`,`**Pleural plaques are benign** markers of exposure, not precursors of mesothelioma.`,`**MCT bypasses the lymphatics** - the basis of the chylothorax diet.`,`**No surgery for mesothelioma after MARS2** - immunotherapy is first line.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-pleural-other-q1`,stem:`A 45-year-old man develops a right-sided milky pleural effusion 5 days after oesophagectomy. Pleural fluid triglycerides are 340 mg/dL and cholesterol 90 mg/dL. Drain output is 600 mL/day. What is the most appropriate initial management?`,options:[`Immediate thoracic duct ligation`,`Talc pleurodesis`,`Drainage with nil by mouth or a low-fat MCT diet, and nutritional support`,`Decortication`],answer:2,explanation:`Triglycerides over 110 mg/dL confirm a chylothorax, here post-surgical. Initial care is conservative: drainage, reduced chyle flow with nil by mouth/TPN or a low-fat MCT diet, with octreotide considered. Duct ligation or embolisation is reserved for output over 1-1.5 L/day for 5 days or persistence beyond 2 weeks; pleurodesis and decortication are not first steps.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-pleural-other-q2`,stem:`A 60-year-old woman with metastatic lung adenocarcinoma has a recurrent symptomatic effusion. After aspiration, chest X-ray shows a persistent pneumothorax with the lung not re-expanding. What is the preferred definitive management?`,options:[`Talc slurry pleurodesis via chest drain`,`Indwelling pleural catheter`,`Repeated large-volume aspiration of 3 L at each visit`,`Intercostal drain with suction for the pneumothorax`],answer:1,explanation:`This is non-expandable lung (pneumothorax ex vacuo after drainage). BTS 2023 prefers an indwelling pleural catheter because pleurodesis needs pleural apposition and will fail. Suction drainage will not re-expand a trapped lung, and removing 3 L at once risks re-expansion oedema and pain.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-pleural-other-q3`,stem:`Which immunohistochemistry pattern best supports epithelioid mesothelioma over metastatic adenocarcinoma?`,options:[`TTF-1 positive, CEA positive`,`Ber-EP4 positive, calretinin negative`,`Napsin A positive, WT1 negative`,`Calretinin, WT1 and D2-40 positive; TTF-1 and CEA negative`],answer:3,explanation:`Mesothelial markers are calretinin, WT1, D2-40 (podoplanin) and CK5/6, while adenocarcinoma markers are TTF-1, napsin A, CEA, Ber-EP4, MOC-31 and claudin-4. The other patterns are those of lung adenocarcinoma. BAP1 loss or CDKN2A deletion further separates mesothelioma from benign reactive mesothelium.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`respiratory-pleural-other-q4`,stem:`A 58-year-old man treated for tuberculous pleurisy 15 years ago has a stable left effusion with thick calcified pleura. Fluid is milky with cholesterol 480 mg/dL, triglycerides 60 mg/dL and rhomboid crystals. The diagnosis is`,options:[`Pseudochylothorax`,`Chylothorax`,`Empyema`,`Malignant pleural effusion`],answer:0,explanation:`A long-standing effusion with thick pleura, cholesterol over 200 mg/dL, cholesterol crystals and low triglycerides is a pseudochylothorax (cholesterol effusion), typical after old TB pleurisy or rheumatoid arthritis. Chylothorax needs TG over 110 mg/dL and chylomicrons; empyema clears on centrifugation and is acute.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-pleural-other-q5`,stem:`In malignant pleural mesothelioma, which statement reflects current evidence?`,options:[`Extended pleurectomy-decortication added to chemotherapy improves survival`,`Nivolumab plus ipilimumab improves survival over chemotherapy, especially in non-epithelioid histology`,`Sarcomatoid histology has the best prognosis`,`Pleural plaques are premalignant and need yearly CT`],answer:1,explanation:`CheckMate 743 showed dual immunotherapy prolonged survival over pemetrexed-platinum, with most benefit in non-epithelioid tumours. MARS2 (2024) found extended pleurectomy-decortication worsened outcomes. Epithelioid, not sarcomatoid, histology carries the best prognosis, and plaques are benign markers of exposure needing no surveillance CT.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-pleural-other-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Discuss the diagnosis and management of malignant pleural effusion. Add a note on non-expandable lung.`,openingLines:[`Malignant pleural effusion is an effusion with malignant cells in fluid or pleura, most often from lung or breast cancer, lymphoma or mesothelioma, and it signals advanced disease with a median survival of 3-12 months.`,`Management is palliative: relieve breathlessness with the least time in hospital, choosing between aspiration, talc pleurodesis and an indwelling pleural catheter.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes and mechanism`,points:[`Lung, breast, lymphoma, ovary, GI, mesothelioma; paramalignant effusions without pleural invasion.`,`Lymphatic stomatal block plus VEGF-driven permeability.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Ultrasound, then aspiration: cytology positive in about 60%.`,`Contrast CT before drainage: nodular, mediastinal, circumferential or over 1 cm thickening.`,`Image-guided biopsy or local anaesthetic thoracoscopy (yield over 90%).`,`Exclude TB in India: ADA, Xpert, biopsy histology.`]},{heading:`Prognosis`,points:[`LENT score (LDH, ECOG, NLR, tumour type) guides how invasive to be.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Asymptomatic: observe and treat the cancer.`,`Symptomatic: therapeutic aspiration up to 1.5 L.`,`Recurrent, expandable lung: IPC or talc (4 g graded) by patient choice (BTS 2023).`,`Non-expandable lung: IPC; talc via IPC improves pleurodesis.`,`Palliative care, opioids for breathlessness, oncology referral.`]},{heading:`Non-expandable lung`,points:[`Visceral peel, endobronchial block or stiff lung prevents re-expansion.`,`Clues: chest pain on drainage, pneumothorax ex vacuo, elastance over 14.5 cmH2O/L.`,`Do not insert a drain for pneumothorax ex vacuo; choose IPC.`]}],mustDraw:[`MPE management flow chart (BTS 2023)`,`IPC vs talc comparison table`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition, causes, mechanism`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:3},{part:`Management`,marks:3},{part:`Non-expandable lung`,marks:2}],keywords:[`malignant pleural effusion`,`IPC`,`talc`,`LENT`,`non-expandable lung`,`BTS 2023`]},{id:`respiratory-pleural-other-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on chylothorax.`,openingLines:[`Chylothorax is the accumulation of chyle in the pleural space from disruption or obstruction of the thoracic duct, confirmed by pleural fluid triglycerides over 110 mg/dL.`,`Surgery/trauma and lymphoma are the commonest causes.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes`,points:[`Traumatic: oesophagectomy, cardiac surgery, central lines.`,`Non-traumatic: lymphoma, metastases, LAM, TB, filariasis, SVC thrombosis.`,`Side: injury below T5 right, above T5 left.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Milky fluid that stays milky after centrifugation.`,`TG over 110 mg/dL; 50-110 needs chylomicron electrophoresis; cholesterol under 200.`,`Lymphangiography or CT to find the leak or mass.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Drainage; low-fat MCT diet or NPO with TPN; octreotide.`,`Duct embolisation or ligation if over 1-1.5 L/day for 5 days or persisting over 2 weeks.`,`Treat cause: radiotherapy/chemotherapy for lymphoma, sirolimus for LAM.`,`Watch for malnutrition, lymphopenia and infection.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: chylothorax vs pseudochylothorax vs empyema`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and causes`,marks:1.5},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:1.5},{part:`Management`,marks:2}],keywords:[`chylothorax`,`triglycerides 110`,`thoracic duct`,`MCT`,`octreotide`,`embolisation`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Disorders of the pleura`,`British Thoracic Society Guideline for Pleural Disease, Thorax 2023`,`Light RW. Pleural Diseases, 6th edition, 2013`,`ESMO Clinical Practice Guideline: Pleural mesothelioma, 2022`,`WHO Classification of Thoracic Tumours, 5th edition, 2021`,`Lim E, et al. MARS2 trial: extended pleurectomy decortication versus no surgery for pleural mesothelioma, Lancet Respiratory Medicine 2024`,`ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows - Pleural effusion, 2023`]}),l.push({id:`respiratory-neuromuscular-chest-wall`,title:`Neuromuscular and chest wall causes of respiratory failure`,oneLiner:`**Neuromuscular and chest wall respiratory failure** is **hypercapnic (type 2) ventilatory pump failure** with normal lungs, caused by weakness of the respiratory muscles (GBS, myasthenia, ALS, muscular dystrophy, diaphragm paralysis) or a stiff deformed chest wall (kyphoscoliosis, thoracoplasty); it is tracked by **vital capacity, MIP/NIF and SNIP** rather than SpO2, and treated with **non-invasive ventilation and cough assistance** while the cause is treated.`,frequency:`less-common`,keywords:[`neuromuscular respiratory failure`,`ventilatory pump failure`,`Guillain-Barre syndrome`,`myasthenic crisis`,`20-30-40 rule`,`negative inspiratory force`,`maximal inspiratory pressure`,`SNIP`,`diaphragm paralysis`,`sniff test`,`kyphoscoliosis`,`Cobb angle`,`motor neurone disease`,`ALS`,`Duchenne muscular dystrophy`,`post-polio syndrome`,`home NIV`,`cough assist`,`peak cough flow`,`thoracoplasty`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Ventilatory pump failure** is failure to move air because of weakness of the respiratory muscles, their nerves or the neuromuscular junction, or a stiff chest wall - the lungs themselves are normal, so the cardinal abnormality is **hypercapnia with a normal A-a gradient**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Guillain-Barre syndrome (GBS)** is the commonest cause of acute neuromuscular respiratory failure worldwide; **20-30% of patients need mechanical ventilation**. [EAN/PNS 2023]`,`**Myasthenic crisis** (weakness needing ventilation or bulbar airway protection) occurs in about **15-20% of people with myasthenia gravis** at some time, usually in the first 2 years. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ALS/motor neurone disease**: respiratory failure is the **usual cause of death**, typically 2-5 years after onset. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Duchenne muscular dystrophy** (about 1 in 3,500-5,000 male births) causes respiratory failure in the late teens; NIV and steroids have pushed median survival towards the 30s. [DMD Care Considerations 2018]`,`**Indian causes that dominate in emergency departments**: GBS, **snake-bite neuroparalysis (krait, cobra)**, **organophosphate poisoning with intermediate syndrome**, **hypokalaemic periodic paralysis**, tetanus and botulism, alongside myasthenia. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**Chest wall causes in India** include **post-TB thoracoplasty and fibrothorax**, severe kyphoscoliosis (idiopathic or tuberculous - Pott's spine) and **post-polio** deformity; India was certified polio-free in 2014, but survivors now develop post-polio syndrome. [WHO 2014]`]},{heading:`Aetiology by level`,points:[`**Brainstem and spinal cord**: stroke, high cervical cord injury (lesions **above C3-C5** abolish diaphragm function), transverse myelitis, NMOSD, poliomyelitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anterior horn cell**: ALS/MND, **spinal muscular atrophy**, polio and post-polio syndrome. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Peripheral nerve**: **GBS**, critical illness polyneuropathy, **phrenic nerve injury** (cardiac surgery, tumour, neuralgic amyotrophy), diphtheria, porphyria, arsenic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neuromuscular junction**: **myasthenia gravis**, Lambert-Eaton syndrome, **botulism**, **organophosphate poisoning**, **snake envenomation (neurotoxic)**, aminoglycosides and hypermagnesaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Muscle**: **Duchenne and Becker dystrophy**, myotonic dystrophy, **Pompe disease (acid maltase deficiency - early diaphragm involvement)**, polymyositis, critical illness myopathy, hypokalaemia and hypophosphataemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chest wall**: **kyphoscoliosis**, thoracoplasty, fibrothorax, ankylosing spondylitis (mild), **flail chest**, severe obesity (see the Obesity hypoventilation topic). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Precipitants of crisis**: infection (especially aspiration pneumonia), surgery, **drugs** (aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones, macrolides, magnesium, beta-blockers, neuromuscular blockers in myasthenia), and sedatives or high-flow oxygen in chronic hypoventilation. [MGFA 2020]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**Weak inspiratory muscles** reduce tidal volume, so the patient breathes **rapid and shallow** - raising dead-space ventilation and PaCO2 even before vital capacity falls far. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Reduced vital capacity (VC)** with preserved FEV1/FVC, reduced TLC, raised RV (weak expiratory muscles) and **normal DLCO corrected for volume (high KCO)** is the PFT signature of neuromuscular restriction. [Harrison 22e]`,`**REM sleep is the first casualty**: in REM, accessory and intercostal muscles are atonic and only the diaphragm works, so **nocturnal REM hypoventilation precedes daytime hypercapnia** by months to years. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diaphragm weakness** causes **orthopnoea** because supine the abdominal contents push a weak diaphragm up; a **fall in VC of over 25% from sitting to supine** indicates significant bilateral weakness (normal under 10%). [ATS/ERS 2002]`,`**Weak expiratory and bulbar muscles** impair cough (peak cough flow falls) and swallowing, causing **aspiration, mucus plugging and atelectasis** - the commonest trigger of acute decompensation. [CHEST 2023]`,`**Microatelectasis and low lung compliance** follow chronic shallow breathing without sighs, adding to the work of breathing. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Kyphoscoliosis** reduces chest wall compliance and puts respiratory muscles at a mechanical disadvantage; the **Cobb angle** correlates with risk, and chronic hypoxia and hypercapnia lead to **pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chronic hypercapnia** blunts the central CO2 drive and causes renal bicarbonate retention, which itself further depresses ventilation (raised bicarbonate is an early clue). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why SpO2 misleads**: hypoxaemia appears late and is corrected by oxygen while CO2 keeps rising - **oxygen without ventilatory support can precipitate CO2 narcosis**. [BTS 2017]`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`**By tempo**: acute (GBS, myasthenic crisis, botulism, snake bite, organophosphate, hypokalaemia, cord injury) versus chronic progressive (ALS, DMD, SMA, kyphoscoliosis, post-polio). [Harrison 22e]`,`**By level**: central, cord, anterior horn, nerve, junction, muscle, chest wall (see aetiology). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diaphragm paralysis**: unilateral (often asymptomatic) versus bilateral (orthopnoea, hypoventilation). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Kyphoscoliosis severity by Cobb angle**: under 70 degrees rarely causes respiratory failure; **over 100 degrees** carries a high risk of hypercapnic failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Myasthenia (MGFA classification)** class I (ocular) to class V (intubation needed = crisis). [MGFA 2020]`,`**GBS disability scale** (Hughes, 0-6) with grade 5 = needs ventilation. [EAN/PNS 2023]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Exertional breathlessness and orthopnoea** that are out of proportion to a clear chest X-ray suggest diaphragm weakness. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Symptoms of nocturnal hypoventilation**: **morning headache**, unrefreshing sleep, daytime sleepiness, poor concentration, nightmares, frequent waking. [CHEST 2023]`,`**Bulbar symptoms**: nasal speech, choking on liquids, weak cough, recurrent chest infections and weight loss. [Harrison 22e]`,`**GBS**: ascending symmetrical weakness after diarrhoea (Campylobacter) or a respiratory infection, with areflexia, back pain and autonomic instability. [EAN/PNS 2023]`,`**Myasthenia**: **fatigable** ptosis, diplopia, dysarthria, dysphagia and proximal weakness worse in the evening; crisis may follow infection or a new drug. [MGFA 2020]`,`**Indian toxic history**: snake bite at night while sleeping on the floor (**krait** - painless, ptosis on waking), pesticide ingestion (**intermediate syndrome** at 24-96 hours), and binge carbohydrates or thyrotoxicosis (hypokalaemic paralysis). [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**Chronic**: progressive weakness (ALS with fasciculations and mixed UMN/LMN signs; DMD in boys with Gowers sign), past polio, TB surgery or childhood scoliosis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Paradoxical abdominal movement** (abdomen moves **in** with inspiration, best seen supine) = bilateral diaphragm weakness. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rapid shallow breathing, accessory muscle use** (sternomastoid) and **inability to complete sentences** indicate impending failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Single breath count** (counting aloud after a full breath) under about **20** suggests VC under 2 L; under 10 suggests VC near 1 L. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neck flexor weakness** parallels diaphragm weakness (both C3-C5 related) - a useful bedside sign in GBS and myasthenia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Weak cough**, pooled secretions, staccato speech and a **wet voice** indicate bulbar failure and aspiration risk. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Signs of CO2 retention**: flapping tremor, warm peripheries, bounding pulse, papilloedema, drowsiness. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chest wall**: measure spinal curvature, look for thoracoplasty scars and rib crowding, and for **cor pulmonale** (raised JVP, loud P2, pedal oedema). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Hypercapnia with a normal A-a gradient and clear chest** = hypoventilation: neuromuscular, chest wall, central (drugs, brainstem) or obesity hypoventilation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**GBS vs myasthenic crisis**: GBS has areflexia, sensory symptoms and ascending pattern; myasthenia has fatigable ocular-bulbar weakness, **normal reflexes** and no sensory loss. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Myasthenic vs cholinergic crisis**: cholinergic crisis has **miosis, salivation, bradycardia, fasciculations and diarrhoea** from excess pyridostigmine - stop the anticholinesterase and ventilate in both. [MGFA 2020]`,`**GBS vs hypokalaemic paralysis**: hypokalaemic paralysis is rapid (hours), with potassium under 3 mmol/L and preserved sensation; respiratory involvement is uncommon. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Unilateral raised hemidiaphragm**: phrenic palsy versus subpulmonic effusion, lobar collapse, hepatomegaly or subphrenic abscess - ultrasound and sniff test separate them. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neuromuscular restriction vs ILD**: ILD has reduced DLCO and crackles; neuromuscular disease has **normal or high KCO** and clear lungs. [ATS/ERS 2022]`,`**Krait bite vs GBS** in rural India: krait gives descending paralysis with ptosis first and no sensory loss; history of sleeping on the floor and abdominal pain. [ICMR STW 2023]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Bedside VC (forced or slow)** every 4-6 hours in acute GBS or myasthenia; **VC under 20 mL/kg** is the trigger for ICU care. [EAN/PNS 2023]`,`**The 20-30-40 rule**: **VC under 20 mL/kg, MIP (NIF) weaker than -30 cmH2O, MEP under 40 cmH2O** - or a fall of over 30% in any - predicts need for intubation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**EGRIS (Erasmus GBS Respiratory Insufficiency Score)** uses days from onset to admission, facial or bulbar weakness and MRC sum score to predict ventilation within the first week. [EAN/PNS 2023]`,`**ABG**: raised PaCO2 and bicarbonate with normal A-a gradient; **hypercapnia is a late sign** in acute weakness - do not wait for it. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sitting and supine VC**: a fall over 25% indicates diaphragm weakness; **SNIP under 40 cmH2O** in ALS predicts nocturnal hypoventilation and death. [NICE NG42]`,`**Diaphragm**: **fluoroscopic sniff test** shows paradoxical upward movement of a paralysed hemidiaphragm; **ultrasound thickening fraction under 20%** or absent excursion confirms paralysis; phrenic nerve conduction for the cause. [ATS/ERS 2002]`,`**Overnight oximetry with transcutaneous CO2** (or polysomnography) detects nocturnal hypoventilation before daytime hypercapnia. [CHEST 2023]`,`**Peak cough flow**: **under 270 L/min** puts the patient at risk during infections; **under 160 L/min** means cough is ineffective. [CHEST 2023]`,`**Cause-specific tests**: nerve conduction and CSF (albuminocytological dissociation) in GBS; **AChR and MuSK antibodies**, repetitive nerve stimulation and single-fibre EMG in myasthenia; CK and genetics in DMD; dried blood spot acid alpha-glucosidase for Pompe; potassium, magnesium, phosphate. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Kyphoscoliosis**: standing spine X-ray for Cobb angle, echocardiography for pulmonary hypertension. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD: chronic neuromuscular and chest wall disease`,points:[`**Monitor every 3-6 months**: symptoms of hypoventilation, sitting and supine FVC, SNIP or MIP, peak cough flow, bicarbonate or ABG, and overnight oximetry or capnography. [CHEST 2023]`,`**Start home NIV** when there is **symptomatic nocturnal hypoventilation**, **daytime PaCO2 45 mmHg or more**, **FVC under 50% predicted**, or **FVC under 80% with symptoms**, or **SNIP/MIP under 40 cmH2O** in ALS. [CHEST 2023]`,`**Bilevel NIV in spontaneous-timed mode** with a backup rate; typical start **IPAP 10-12 cmH2O and EPAP 4-5 cmH2O**, titrated to normalise CO2 and abolish desaturation. [CHEST 2023]`,`**Cough augmentation**: lung volume recruitment (breath-stacking with an Ambu bag), **manually assisted cough**, and **mechanical insufflation-exsufflation** (cough assist, +40/-40 cmH2O) when PCF is under 270 L/min. [CHEST 2023]`,`**ALS**: **riluzole 50 mg orally twice daily** (monitor liver enzymes), early gastrostomy (before FVC falls under 50%), and NIV which prolongs survival by about **7 months** in those without severe bulbar disease. [NICE NG42]`,`**DMD**: daily **deflazacort 0.9 mg/kg** or prednisolone 0.75 mg/kg (or vamorolone) from 4-6 years; cardiac review with ACE inhibitor; FVC from age 5-6. [DMD Care Considerations 2018]`,`**Myasthenia maintenance**: **pyridostigmine 30-60 mg orally 4-6 hourly**, prednisolone (start low, e.g. 10 mg and increase slowly), azathioprine, and **thymectomy** for AChR-positive generalised MG under 65. [MGFA 2020]`,`**Vaccinate** (influenza, pneumococcal), treat chest infections early with antibiotics and physiotherapy, and give an **action plan with an emergency card** (no high-flow oxygen without CO2 check, avoid sedatives). [CHEST 2023]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency`,points:[`**Admit to HDU/ICU** any acute weakness with **VC under 20 mL/kg, MIP weaker than -30 cmH2O, bulbar weakness, rapid progression or autonomic instability**. [EAN/PNS 2023]`,`**Elective intubation is safer than crash intubation** - intubate on the 20-30-40 trend, bulbar failure with aspiration, or rising PaCO2; avoid **suxamethonium** (hyperkalaemia in denervation, unpredictable in myasthenia). [Harrison 22e]`,`**NIV is generally avoided in GBS** (progressive, autonomic, bulbar), but can be tried in **myasthenic crisis without bulbar failure or severe hypercapnia** and may avert intubation. [MGFA 2020]`,`**GBS**: **IVIg 0.4 g/kg/day for 5 days** or **plasma exchange (5 exchanges over 1-2 weeks)** within 2 weeks (up to 4 weeks) of onset; **corticosteroids are not recommended**. [EAN/PNS 2023]`,`**Myasthenic crisis**: **IVIg 2 g/kg over 2-5 days or plasma exchange**, then high-dose steroids; **hold pyridostigmine** while ventilated; stop offending drugs; treat infection. [MGFA 2020]`,`**Neurotoxic snake bite**: **polyvalent anti-snake venom 10 vials IV** (repeat per protocol) plus ventilatory support; neostigmine with atropine trial helps in **cobra** (post-synaptic) but not reliably in **krait**. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**Organophosphate intermediate syndrome**: proximal, neck flexor and respiratory weakness at 24-96 hours despite atropine; needs ventilatory support, not more pralidoxime. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**Acute-on-chronic hypercapnic failure** (DMD, kyphoscoliosis, ALS): **controlled oxygen to SpO2 88-92%** and NIV; respect advance directives about intubation and tracheostomy. [BTS 2017]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Unilateral diaphragm paralysis**: usually needs only reassurance; **diaphragm plication** for symptomatic patients; neuralgic amyotrophy often recovers over 1-3 years. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bilateral diaphragm paralysis**: nocturnal NIV; **diaphragm pacing** for high cord injury with intact phrenic nerves. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pregnancy**: kyphoscoliosis and neuromuscular disease reduce reserve further as the uterus enlarges; check FVC before and during pregnancy (**FVC under 1 L** carries high risk) and plan anaesthesia early. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Surgery and anaesthesia**: assess FVC, PCF and need for NIV and cough assist after extubation; avoid suxamethonium and volatile agents in DMD (rhabdomyolysis, hyperkalaemia). [DMD Care Considerations 2018]`,`**Children**: SMA and DMD need multidisciplinary clinics; **nusinersen, risdiplam and onasemnogene** have changed SMA; DMD gene and exon-skipping therapies are emerging. [Nelson 22e]`,`**Post-polio syndrome**: new weakness, fatigue and hypoventilation 15-40 years after polio; conserve energy, treat scoliosis-related hypoventilation with NIV. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Resource-limited settings**: where bilevel NIV is unaffordable, **schedule regular ABG/bicarbonate and oximetry**, teach breath-stacking with an Ambu bag, and link to the **National Policy for Rare Diseases 2021** for DMD, SMA and Pompe funding. [MoHFW 2021]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Aspiration pneumonia, atelectasis and mucus plugging** are the commonest acute complications and causes of death. [CHEST 2023]`,`**Cor pulmonale** from chronic hypoxia and hypercapnia in kyphoscoliosis and DMD; NIV reverses pulmonary hypertension partly. [Harrison 22e]`,`**GBS prognosis**: about 80% walk independently at 6 months; mortality is about **3-7%**, higher in ventilated older patients; **autonomic arrhythmias** cause sudden death. [EAN/PNS 2023]`,`**ALS prognosis**: median survival 2-5 years from onset; NIV improves survival and quality of life; tracheostomy ventilation prolongs life but needs informed choice. [NICE NG42]`,`**DMD**: **cardiomyopathy** is now the leading cause of death, as respiratory care has improved. [DMD Care Considerations 2018]`,`**Kyphoscoliosis with home NIV** has good long-term survival, better than COPD on home NIV. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Refer any patient with **orthopnoea, morning headache or VC under 80%** with neuromuscular disease to a respiratory/sleep service for NIV assessment. [CHEST 2023]`,`**Same-day referral** for acute weakness with breathlessness, bulbar signs or VC under 20 mL/kg. [EAN/PNS 2023]`,`**Follow-up of home NIV**: hours of use, mask fit, leak, symptoms, bicarbonate or PaCO2, and overnight oximetry/capnography after any change. [CHEST 2023]`,`**Tracheostomy ventilation** is discussed when NIV is needed **over 16-20 hours a day**, with bulbar failure and repeated aspiration, or when NIV fails. [CHEST 2023]`,`**Advance care planning** in ALS and DMD early - before a crisis - about NIV, tracheostomy and feeding. [NICE NG42]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme links`,points:[`**Influenza and pneumococcal vaccination** for all with chronic neuromuscular or chest wall disease (see the Adult respiratory vaccines topic). [CHEST 2023]`,`**Caution with oxygen and sedatives**: carry an alert card stating the target SpO2 of 88-92%. [BTS 2017]`,`**Snake-bite prevention**: sleep on a cot under a net, use a torch at night - krait bites happen on the floor at night. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**Pesticide safety**: safe storage and restriction of highly hazardous pesticides reduce organophosphate poisoning. [WHO 2023]`,`**Genetic counselling** for DMD (X-linked; carrier testing of mothers and sisters) and SMA. [Nelson 22e]`,`**Programme links**: **National Policy for Rare Diseases 2021** (Centres of Excellence; financial support for DMD, SMA, Pompe), **Rashtriya Bal Swasthya Karyakram** screening of children for developmental and neuromuscular delay, and **PM-JAY** for ICU ventilation. [MoHFW 2021]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**CHEST Guideline on Respiratory Management of Neuromuscular Weakness (2023)**: NIV may start with **FVC under 80% plus symptoms**, not only FVC under 50%; individualise using MIP/SNIP, PaCO2 and nocturnal oximetry; mechanical insufflation-exsufflation recommended. [CHEST 2023]`,`**EAN/PNS Guideline on GBS (2023)**: IVIg or plasma exchange; no corticosteroids; **no second IVIg dose** for poor prognosis (SID-GBS trial); EGRIS to predict ventilation. [EAN/PNS 2023]`,`**Myasthenia gravis** therapies approved 2023-2024: **FcRn blockers (efgartigimod, rozanolixizumab)** and **complement C5 inhibitors (eculizumab, ravulizumab, zilucoplan)** for refractory generalised AChR-positive disease. [FDA 2024]`,`**ALS**: **tofersen** (antisense to SOD1) approved in 2023 for SOD1-ALS; sodium phenylbutyrate-taurursodiol was withdrawn in 2024 after a negative phase 3 trial. [FDA 2024]`,`**DMD**: **vamorolone** (2023) as a steroid alternative with less growth and bone harm; **givinostat** (2024); **delandistrogene moxeparvovec** gene therapy (2023, label extended 2024). [FDA 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**The 20-30-40 rule** - VC under 20 mL/kg, MIP weaker than -30 cmH2O, MEP under 40 cmH2O - is the most asked number. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why not trust SpO2 in GBS?** Hypoxaemia and hypercapnia are late; track VC and MIP. [EAN/PNS 2023]`,`**Supine VC fall over 25%** and paradoxical abdominal movement = bilateral diaphragm weakness. [ATS/ERS 2002]`,`**Sniff test**: paralysed hemidiaphragm moves paradoxically up during a sharp sniff. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake**: giving high-flow oxygen to a drowsy DMD or kyphoscoliosis patient without checking CO2. [BTS 2017]`,`**Steroids do not help GBS** but are central in myasthenia and DMD. [EAN/PNS 2023]`,`**Neostigmine helps cobra, not reliably krait** neuroparalysis - ASV and ventilation are the mainstays. [ICMR STW 2023]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs used in neuromuscular respiratory failure`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`IVIg (GBS)`,`0.4 g/kg/day IV`,`5 days`,`Headache, aseptic meningitis, thrombosis, renal injury; check IgA`],[`IVIg (myasthenic crisis)`,`2 g/kg IV total`,`Over 2-5 days`,`As above; fluid load`],[`Plasma exchange`,`5 exchanges (about 200-250 mL/kg total)`,`Over 1-2 weeks`,`Hypotension, line sepsis, hypocalcaemia`],[`Pyridostigmine`,`30-60 mg orally 4-6 hourly (max about 360 mg/day)`,`Titrate to response; hold when ventilated in crisis`,`Cholinergic effects: diarrhoea, salivation, bradycardia`],[`Prednisolone (myasthenia)`,`Start 10 mg/day orally, increase to 0.75-1 mg/kg`,`Taper to lowest dose; steroid-sparing agent`,`Early worsening of MG, diabetes, osteoporosis`],[`Riluzole (ALS)`,`50 mg orally twice daily`,`Long-term`,`Raised transaminases, neutropenia, fatigue`],[`Deflazacort (DMD)`,`0.9 mg/kg orally daily`,`Long-term`,`Weight gain, cataract, growth delay, osteoporosis`],[`Neostigmine with atropine (cobra bite)`,`Neostigmine 0.5 mg IV (or 0.04 mg/kg) after atropine 0.6 mg IV`,`Repeat as per response`,`Bradycardia, secretions; less useful in krait`],[`Polyvalent anti-snake venom`,`10 vials IV over 1 hour`,`Repeat per protocol for neurotoxicity`,`Anaphylactoid reactions - adrenaline ready`]]},{heading:`Causes by level`,columns:[`Level`,`Acute causes`,`Chronic causes`,`Clue`],rows:[[`Spinal cord / brainstem`,`Cord injury, transverse myelitis, stroke`,`Syringomyelia, MS`,`Level, sensory signs`],[`Anterior horn cell`,`Polio (historical)`,`ALS, SMA, post-polio`,`Fasciculations, no sensory loss`],[`Peripheral nerve`,`GBS, critical illness polyneuropathy, phrenic injury`,`CIDP`,`Areflexia, sensory signs`],[`Neuromuscular junction`,`Myasthenic crisis, botulism, organophosphate, snake bite`,`Myasthenia, LEMS`,`Fatigability, ptosis, normal reflexes`],[`Muscle`,`Hypokalaemia, hypophosphataemia, critical illness myopathy`,`DMD, myotonic dystrophy, Pompe`,`Raised CK, proximal weakness`],[`Chest wall`,`Flail chest`,`Kyphoscoliosis, thoracoplasty, fibrothorax`,`Deformity, Cobb angle`]]},{heading:`Bedside and laboratory thresholds`,columns:[`Measure`,`Threshold`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`Vital capacity`,`Under 20 mL/kg (or fall over 30%)`,`ICU and consider intubation`],[`MIP / NIF`,`Weaker than -30 cmH2O`,`Impending failure`],[`MEP`,`Under 40 cmH2O`,`Weak cough, aspiration risk`],[`Sitting-to-supine VC fall`,`Over 25%`,`Bilateral diaphragm weakness`],[`SNIP`,`Under 40 cmH2O`,`Nocturnal hypoventilation in ALS; NIV`],[`FVC (chronic)`,`Under 50%, or under 80% with symptoms`,`Start home NIV`],[`Daytime PaCO2`,`45 mmHg or more`,`Start home NIV`],[`Peak cough flow`,`Under 270 L/min (risk), under 160 (ineffective)`,`Cough augmentation`],[`Cobb angle`,`Over 100 degrees`,`High risk of hypercapnic failure`],[`Diaphragm ultrasound thickening fraction`,`Under 20%`,`Diaphragm paralysis`]]},{heading:`GBS versus myasthenic crisis versus cholinergic crisis`,columns:[`Feature`,`GBS`,`Myasthenic crisis`,`Cholinergic crisis`],rows:[[`Pattern`,`Ascending, symmetrical`,`Fatigable ocular-bulbar and proximal`,`Generalised weakness with fasciculations`],[`Reflexes`,`Absent`,`Normal`,`Normal or reduced`],[`Sensory`,`Paraesthesia, pain`,`Normal`,`Normal`],[`Autonomic`,`Dysautonomia`,`None`,`Miosis, salivation, bradycardia, diarrhoea`],[`Treatment`,`IVIg or plasma exchange; no steroids`,`IVIg or plasma exchange, then steroids`,`Stop anticholinesterase, atropine, ventilate`],[`NIV`,`Avoid`,`May be tried if no bulbar failure`,`Ventilate as needed`]]}],redFlags:[`VC under 20 mL/kg, MIP weaker than -30 cmH2O or a fall over 30% - ICU now.`,`Bulbar weakness with choking, pooled secretions or a weak cough - airway at risk.`,`Inability to count to 10 in one breath or to lift the head off the pillow.`,`Paradoxical abdominal breathing or orthopnoea with rapid shallow breathing.`,`Drowsiness, flap or rising bicarbonate/PaCO2 in chronic weakness - acute-on-chronic hypercapnic failure.`,`Autonomic instability in GBS: wide BP swings, arrhythmia, ileus.`,`Ptosis or neck weakness after a night-time snake bite or pesticide exposure.`],pearls:[`**20-30-40**: VC under 20 mL/kg, MIP weaker than -30, MEP under 40 = intubate.`,`**REM sleep fails first** - nocturnal hypoventilation precedes daytime hypercapnia.`,`**Supine VC fall over 25%** = diaphragm weakness.`,`**Neck flexor weakness mirrors diaphragm weakness** (C3-C5).`,`**High KCO with low VC** = extrapulmonary restriction (muscle or chest wall).`,`**No NIV in GBS; maybe NIV in myasthenic crisis.**`,`**PCF under 270 L/min** - start cough assist.`,`**C3, 4, 5 keeps the diaphragm alive.**`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-neuromuscular-chest-wall-q1`,stem:`A 32-year-old man with Guillain-Barre syndrome on day 3 has SpO2 97% on room air. His vital capacity has fallen from 45 mL/kg to 18 mL/kg and MIP is -25 cmH2O. What is the most appropriate next step?`,options:[`Reassure as SpO2 is normal and repeat VC tomorrow`,`Start non-invasive ventilation in the ward`,`Give intravenous methylprednisolone`,`Transfer to ICU and plan elective intubation`],answer:3,explanation:`VC under 20 mL/kg and MIP weaker than -30 cmH2O meet the 20-30-40 rule, predicting imminent respiratory failure, so ICU transfer with elective intubation is safest. Normal SpO2 is falsely reassuring because hypoxaemia is late; NIV is generally avoided in progressive GBS; corticosteroids do not benefit GBS.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-neuromuscular-chest-wall-q2`,stem:`A 55-year-old woman has severe orthopnoea and a clear chest X-ray. Sitting FVC is 2.4 L and supine FVC is 1.5 L. What is the likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Bilateral diaphragm weakness`,`Left ventricular failure`,`Asthma`,`Interstitial lung disease`],answer:0,explanation:`A fall in FVC of about 37% on lying down (over 25%) indicates bilateral diaphragm weakness: supine, the abdominal contents push the weak diaphragm up. Heart failure would show congestion on X-ray; asthma is obstructive and variable; ILD gives crackles, reduced DLCO and no positional VC drop of this size.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-neuromuscular-chest-wall-q3`,stem:`In a patient with ALS, which finding is an accepted indication to start home non-invasive ventilation?`,options:[`SpO2 of 95% on room air while awake`,`SNIP under 40 cmH2O or FVC under 50% predicted`,`Fasciculations in the tongue`,`Serum CK twice normal`],answer:1,explanation:`SNIP under 40 cmH2O, FVC under 50% (or under 80% with symptoms), daytime hypercapnia or nocturnal hypoventilation are NIV triggers, and NIV prolongs survival in ALS. Daytime SpO2 is a late and insensitive marker; tongue fasciculations show bulbar involvement but are not themselves an NIV criterion; CK is irrelevant to ventilation.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-neuromuscular-chest-wall-q4`,stem:`A 17-year-old boy with Duchenne muscular dystrophy is drowsy with a chest infection. SpO2 is 84% on air. The casualty officer starts 15 L/min oxygen by non-rebreather mask. What is the main risk?`,options:[`Oxygen toxicity of the retina`,`Absorption atelectasis causing pneumothorax`,`Worsening hypercapnia and CO2 narcosis`,`Hypoglycaemia`],answer:2,explanation:`Neuromuscular hypoventilation causes chronic hypercapnia; uncontrolled oxygen raises PaCO2 further through worsened V/Q matching and loss of hypoxic drive, causing CO2 narcosis. Target SpO2 is 88-92% with NIV. Retinal toxicity is a neonatal issue; absorption atelectasis does not cause pneumothorax; oxygen does not cause hypoglycaemia.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-neuromuscular-chest-wall-q5`,stem:`A farmer woke at 4 am with abdominal pain and, by morning, has bilateral ptosis, nasal speech and breathlessness. There is no visible bite mark. He slept on the floor. What is the most likely cause?`,options:[`Guillain-Barre syndrome`,`Hypokalaemic periodic paralysis`,`Organophosphate poisoning`,`Common krait bite`],answer:3,explanation:`Krait bites are often painless and unnoticed, happen at night to people sleeping on the floor, and cause abdominal pain then descending neuroparalysis starting with ptosis. GBS is ascending with areflexia over days; hypokalaemic paralysis spares the eyes and bulbar muscles; organophosphate poisoning gives a cholinergic syndrome with miosis and secretions.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-neuromuscular-chest-wall-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Describe the causes, assessment and management of respiratory failure due to neuromuscular disease. How will you monitor a patient with Guillain-Barre syndrome?`,openingLines:[`Neuromuscular respiratory failure is hypercapnic ventilatory pump failure from weakness of the respiratory muscles, their nerves or the neuromuscular junction, with normal lungs and a normal A-a gradient.`,`It is detected by falling vital capacity and inspiratory pressures long before hypoxaemia, and is treated with ventilatory support and cough assistance while the cause is treated.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes`,points:[`Cord (C3-C5 injury), anterior horn (ALS, SMA, polio), nerve (GBS, phrenic), junction (myasthenia, botulism, organophosphate, snake bite), muscle (DMD, Pompe, hypokalaemia), chest wall (kyphoscoliosis, thoracoplasty).`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Shallow rapid breathing, raised dead space, REM hypoventilation first, weak cough and aspiration, chronic hypercapnia with high bicarbonate.`]},{heading:`Assessment`,points:[`Symptoms: orthopnoea, morning headache, somnolence, bulbar symptoms.`,`Signs: paradoxical abdomen, single breath count, neck flexor weakness, CO2 retention signs.`,`Tests: VC sitting and supine, MIP, MEP, SNIP, PCF, ABG, overnight oximetry-capnography, sniff test/ultrasound.`]},{heading:`Monitoring GBS`,points:[`VC, MIP and MEP every 4-6 hours; the 20-30-40 rule; EGRIS score at admission.`,`Watch bulbar function, neck flexion, autonomic instability (ECG, BP).`,`Do not rely on SpO2 or ABG.`]},{heading:`Acute management`,points:[`ICU, elective intubation, avoid suxamethonium.`,`GBS: IVIg 0.4 g/kg/day x 5 days or plasma exchange; no steroids.`,`Myasthenic crisis: IVIg or PLEX, hold pyridostigmine, trial NIV if no bulbar failure.`]},{heading:`Chronic management`,points:[`Home bilevel NIV when FVC under 50% (or under 80% with symptoms), PaCO2 45 mmHg or more, SNIP under 40.`,`Cough assist when PCF under 270 L/min; vaccination; controlled oxygen.`,`Advance care planning; tracheostomy if NIV over 16-20 h/day.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: monitoring and escalation in GBS (20-30-40 rule)`,`Table of causes by level`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and causes`,marks:2},{part:`Pathophysiology`,marks:1},{part:`Assessment and thresholds`,marks:3},{part:`Management acute and chronic`,marks:4}],keywords:[`20-30-40 rule`,`vital capacity`,`MIP`,`SNIP`,`NIV`,`GBS`,`myasthenic crisis`,`cough assist`]},{id:`respiratory-neuromuscular-chest-wall-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on diaphragmatic paralysis.`,openingLines:[`Diaphragmatic paralysis is loss of diaphragm contraction from phrenic nerve, C3-C5 cord or muscle disease, unilateral (often asymptomatic) or bilateral (orthopnoea and hypoventilation).`,`Diagnosis rests on the sniff test or diaphragm ultrasound and a large supine fall in vital capacity.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes`,points:[`Phrenic injury (cardiac surgery, tumour, trauma), neuralgic amyotrophy, cord injury, ALS, GBS, myopathies, idiopathic.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Unilateral: raised hemidiaphragm on X-ray, exertional breathlessness.`,`Bilateral: severe orthopnoea, paradoxical abdominal movement, nocturnal hypoventilation.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Fluoroscopic sniff test: paradoxical upward movement.`,`Ultrasound: thickening fraction under 20%, absent excursion.`,`VC fall over 25% supine; phrenic nerve conduction; transdiaphragmatic pressure.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Unilateral: reassure, treat cause, plication if symptomatic.`,`Bilateral: nocturnal NIV; diaphragm pacing in high cord injury with intact phrenic nerves.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of normal vs paradoxical movement on sniff`],markSplit:[{part:`Causes`,marks:1},{part:`Features`,marks:1},{part:`Investigations`,marks:1.5},{part:`Management`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`sniff test`,`phrenic nerve`,`plication`,`orthopnoea`,`paradoxical movement`,`ultrasound`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Disorders of ventilation; Guillain-Barre syndrome; Myasthenia gravis`,`Khan A, et al. Respiratory Management of Patients With Neuromuscular Weakness: CHEST Guideline and Expert Panel Report, Chest 2023`,`European Academy of Neurology/Peripheral Nerve Society Guideline on diagnosis and treatment of Guillain-Barre syndrome, 2023`,`International Consensus Guidance for Management of Myasthenia Gravis, 2020 update (MGFA)`,`NICE NG42: Motor neurone disease - assessment and management, 2016 (updated 2019)`,`Birnkrant DJ, et al. Diagnosis and management of Duchenne muscular dystrophy (DMD Care Considerations), Lancet Neurology 2018`,`ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows - Snake bite and organophosphate poisoning, 2023; National Policy for Rare Diseases, MoHFW 2021`,`BTS Guideline for oxygen use in adults in healthcare and emergency settings, 2017`]}),l.push({id:`respiratory-inhaler-devices`,title:`Inhaler devices, spacers and nebulisation: choosing and teaching technique`,oneLiner:`Inhaler therapy delivers drug as **1-5 micrometre particles** to the airways via a **pressurised metered-dose inhaler (pMDI) - best with a spacer**, a **dry powder inhaler (DPI)** that needs a **fast, forceful inhalation (at least 30, ideally 60 L/min)**, a **soft mist inhaler (SMI)** or a **nebuliser**; the right device is the one the patient can **use correctly, afford and will keep using**, and technique must be **demonstrated and checked at every visit**.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`inhaler technique`,`pMDI`,`metered-dose inhaler`,`dry powder inhaler`,`DPI`,`Rotahaler`,`Revolizer`,`Turbuhaler`,`Synchrobreathe`,`soft mist inhaler`,`Respimat`,`spacer`,`valved holding chamber`,`Zerostat`,`face mask`,`nebuliser`,`respules`,`peak inspiratory flow`,`In-Check DIAL`,`teach-back`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Inhaled therapy** puts a small dose directly onto the airway, so it acts faster and with far fewer systemic effects than oral drugs - **100 micrograms of inhaled salbutamol** works like several milligrams orally. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Four device families**: pressurised metered-dose inhaler (**pMDI**, with or without spacer), breath-actuated MDI, dry powder inhaler (**DPI**, single-dose capsule or multi-dose), **soft mist inhaler (SMI)**, and **nebuliser** (jet, ultrasonic or mesh). [ERS/ISAM 2011]`,`**Incorrect technique is the rule, not the exception**: across studies **50-90% of patients make at least one error** and about a third make a critical error that stops the drug reaching the lungs. [GINA 2025]`,`**Poor technique predicts poor control**: the CRITIKAL study linked **insufficient inspiratory effort** with DPIs and **poor coordination** with pMDIs to uncontrolled asthma and exacerbations. [GINA 2025]`,`**Healthcare workers also get it wrong** - many doctors and nurses cannot demonstrate correct technique, which is why training of the trainer matters. [GINA 2025]`,`In India, **inhaler phobia** (fear of addiction, steroid fear, stigma) and preference for oral bronchodilators and syrups are major reasons for poor asthma and COPD control; **India has over 40% of global asthma deaths**. [GBD India 2018]`,`**India's device market** is dominated by **low-cost capsule DPIs (Rotahaler, Revolizer)** and pMDIs, with locally made spacers and breath-actuated MDIs; NLEM 2022 lists salbutamol, ipratropium and budesonide inhalers and (added in 2022) budesonide-formoterol and tiotropium. [NLEM 2022]`]},{heading:`Aerosol science: how particles reach the lung`,points:[`**Particle size (mass median aerodynamic diameter)**: particles over 5 micrometres impact in the mouth and throat, **1-5 micrometres deposit in the airways**, and under 1 micrometre are largely exhaled. [ERS/ISAM 2011]`,`**Three deposition mechanisms**: **inertial impaction** (large, fast particles at bends - the throat), **sedimentation** (gravity, in small airways, helped by a **10-second breath-hold**) and diffusion (very small particles). [ERS/ISAM 2011]`,`**Fast inhalation through a pMDI increases throat impaction**, so the pMDI needs a **slow, deep breath (about 3-5 seconds)**, while a DPI needs a **fast, forceful breath** to deaggregate the powder. [GINA 2025]`,`**Typical lung deposition**: pMDI alone about **10-20%**, pMDI with spacer higher and with less throat deposition, DPIs **10-40% depending on device and flow**, SMI about **40-50%**, jet nebuliser about **10%** of the nominal dose. [ERS/ISAM 2011]`,`**Device resistance and flow**: high-resistance DPIs (e.g. HandiHaler, Turbuhaler) need more effort to reach the same flow; low-resistance capsule devices (Rotahaler, Breezhaler) need high flow but less effort. [ERS/ISAM 2011]`,`**Peak inspiratory flow (PIF)** at least **30 L/min is the minimum** for most DPIs and **60 L/min or more is optimal**; many older COPD patients, especially women and after exacerbations, cannot reach it. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Spacers** slow the aerosol, let large particles settle on the chamber wall (less throat deposition, less oral thrush and hoarseness from ICS) and **remove the need for hand-breath coordination**. [GINA 2025]`,`**Static charge** on plastic spacers attracts particles; washing in detergent and **air-drying without rinsing** reduces static, while antistatic or metal spacers avoid it. [GINA 2025]`]},{heading:`Classification of devices`,points:[`**pMDI**: propellant (HFA) drives a metered dose at high speed - cheap, portable, all drug classes; needs coordination and slow inhalation; use with a spacer for ICS and in children. [GINA 2025]`,`**Breath-actuated MDI** (e.g. Synchrobreathe in India, Autohaler): fires automatically when the patient inhales, removing the coordination problem. [ERS/ISAM 2011]`,`**Single-dose capsule DPI**: **Rotahaler, Revolizer** (common and cheap in India), Breezhaler, HandiHaler - capsule is pierced or opened and powder inhaled; patient can see and hear the dose. [ERS/ISAM 2011]`,`**Multi-dose DPI**: reservoir (**Turbuhaler**) or blister (Accuhaler/Diskus, Ellipta) - convenient, dose counter, no capsule handling; moisture-sensitive. [ERS/ISAM 2011]`,`**Soft mist inhaler (Respimat)**: a spring generates a slow-moving mist lasting about **1.5 seconds**, needing little inspiratory effort and less coordination than a pMDI. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Nebulisers**: **jet** (compressed air or oxygen, 6-8 L/min), **ultrasonic** (unsuitable for suspensions such as budesonide), **vibrating mesh** (efficient, quiet, portable, costlier). [ERS 2017]`,`**Interfaces**: mouthpiece (preferred when possible), **face mask** (infants, young children, very ill or confused patients), and **valved holding chambers** with mask or mouthpiece. [GINA 2025]`]},{heading:`Correct technique: step by step`,points:[`**pMDI**: remove cap, **shake** (suspension), prime if new or unused, **breathe out fully away from the device**, seal lips, **press once at the start of a slow deep breath (3-5 seconds)**, **hold breath 10 seconds** (or as long as comfortable), wait 30-60 seconds before a second puff. [GINA 2025]`,`**pMDI with spacer (mouthpiece)**: shake, attach, breathe out, seal lips on spacer, **press once**, then either **one slow deep breath with 10-second hold** or **4-6 tidal breaths**; **one puff at a time**. [GINA 2025]`,`**pMDI with spacer and face mask (young child)**: hold the mask with a **tight seal over nose and mouth**, press once, let the child take **5-10 tidal breaths** (or about 30 seconds), watching the valve move; a crying child receives very little drug. [GINA 2025]`,`**Capsule DPI (Rotahaler/Revolizer)**: load a capsule, pierce or twist to open, **breathe out away from the device**, seal lips, **breathe in fast and deep**, hold 10 seconds; **check the capsule is empty** and repeat the inhalation if powder remains. [ERS/ISAM 2011]`,`**Turbuhaler**: hold **upright** while twisting the grip back and forth until it clicks, breathe out away, inhale **forcefully and deeply**; never shake it or breathe into it. [ERS/ISAM 2011]`,`**SMI (Respimat)**: turn the base half a turn until it clicks, open cap, breathe out, seal lips, **press while breathing in slowly and deeply**, hold 10 seconds; insert the cartridge only once at first use. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Jet nebuliser**: fill volume about **3-4 mL** (drug plus saline), flow **6-8 L/min**, tidal breathing through a mouthpiece until sputtering (about **10 minutes**), mouthpiece preferred to mask for adults. [ERS 2017]`,`**After any ICS**: **rinse mouth, gargle and spit** (or brush teeth) to cut oral thrush and dysphonia; wash the face after a mask. [GINA 2025]`]},{heading:`Common errors by device`,points:[`**pMDI errors**: poor **coordination** (pressing before or after the breath), **inhaling too fast**, not shaking, not exhaling first, no breath-hold, firing two puffs at once, and stopping the breath when the cold propellant hits the throat ('cold-Freon effect'). [GINA 2025]`,`**Spacer errors**: multiple puffs into the spacer at once, delay between firing and inhaling, poor mask seal, washing and wiping dry (static), using an incompatible spacer. [GINA 2025]`,`**DPI errors**: **insufficient inspiratory effort** (the commonest critical error), **exhaling into the device** (moisture clumps powder), not loading or not piercing the capsule, tilting the device after loading, and storing capsules in humid places outside the blister. [GINA 2025]`,`**Capsule-specific errors in India**: **swallowing rotacaps** instead of inhaling them, reusing a capsule, not checking it is empty. [GINA 2025]`,`**SMI errors**: not turning the base fully, pointing the mist at the cheek or tongue, failing to inhale slowly. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Nebuliser errors**: under-filling, using a broken or dirty kit, mask not sealing, poor cleaning, and **using an oxygen-driven nebuliser in hypercapnic COPD for long periods**. [BTS 2017]`,`**Adherence errors masquerading as technique**: not refilling, running out without a dose counter, using the controller only when symptomatic, or using the wrong inhaler (reliever vs controller). [GINA 2025]`]},{heading:`Assessing the patient's ability: examination at the desk`,points:[`**Ask the patient to show you** with their own device - never accept 'I know how' - and score against a device checklist. [GINA 2025]`,`**Measure PIF** against the device's resistance with an **In-Check DIAL** where available; PIF under 30 L/min rules out most DPIs. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Hand strength and dexterity**: arthritis, stroke or tremor make pMDI pressing, capsule loading and twisting hard - consider spacer with a haler aid, breath-actuated MDI or nebuliser. [GINA 2025]`,`**Cognition**: dementia or delirium impairs multistep techniques - pMDI with spacer and tidal breathing, or nebuliser, with a carer trained. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Vision and literacy**: use **pictorial cards and demonstration in the local language**, not written leaflets alone. [GINA 2025]`,`**Check for local side effects**: oral candidiasis, hoarse voice, and look at the patient's dose counter or capsule count to confirm use. [GINA 2025]`]},{heading:`Choosing a device: the differential`,points:[`**Under 4 years**: pMDI with **valved spacer and face mask**; nebuliser only if the child cannot use this. [GINA 2025]`,`**4-5 years**: pMDI with spacer and **mouthpiece**, or face mask if the child cannot use a mouthpiece. [GINA 2025]`,`**6 years and over and adults**: pMDI with spacer, DPI (if PIF adequate), breath-actuated MDI or SMI - choose by **ability, preference, cost and drug availability**. [GINA 2025]`,`**Low PIF (under 30 L/min) or frail elderly**: avoid DPIs; use **pMDI with spacer, SMI or nebuliser**. [GOLD 2025]`,`**MART (ICS-formoterol reliever and maintenance)** needs a device that delivers budesonide-formoterol - in India **Rotacap/Revolizer, pMDI or breath-actuated MDI** are available. [GINA 2025]`,`**Use one type of device** for all inhaled drugs where possible - mixed DPI and pMDI techniques cause errors. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Do not switch devices without the patient and without training** - non-consensual switching worsens control. [GINA 2025]`,`**Acute attack in clinic**: **pMDI with spacer is as effective as a nebuliser** for mild-moderate exacerbations in adults and children, with less tachycardia and shorter stays. [GINA 2025]`]},{heading:`Investigations and objective checks`,points:[`**Device technique checklist** scored at every visit, with errors classed as critical (no or little drug reaches the lung) or non-critical. [GINA 2025]`,`**PIF with In-Check DIAL** set to the resistance of the patient's DPI; repeat after an exacerbation when PIF is lower. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Peak expiratory flow or spirometry** before and after a supervised dose can show whether a poorly responding patient is actually getting drug. [GINA 2025]`,`**Dose counters, pharmacy refill records and capsule counts** check adherence; electronic monitors are used in research and severe asthma clinics. [GINA 2025]`,`**FeNO** falling after directly observed ICS indicates poor adherence rather than steroid resistance (severe asthma clinics). [GINA 2025]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD: teaching and doses`,points:[`**Teach with a placebo device**: **explain, demonstrate, let the patient do it, correct, repeat (teach-back)** - up to 2-3 rounds are often needed; one session is not enough. [GINA 2025]`,`**Re-check technique at every visit**: errors return within weeks; re-teaching restores control. [GINA 2025]`,`**Reliever pMDI dose**: **salbutamol 100 micrograms per puff, 1-2 puffs as needed**; for an exacerbation in the clinic, **4-10 puffs via spacer every 20 minutes in the first hour**. [GINA 2025]`,`**Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 micrograms, 1 inhalation as needed** (Track 1 AIR/MART), maximum **12 inhalations in a day** for adults (see the Asthma topic for the ladder). [GINA 2025]`,`**Nebulised doses (adult)**: salbutamol **2.5-5 mg**, ipratropium **500 micrograms**, budesonide respules **0.5-1 mg twice daily**; children salbutamol 2.5 mg and ipratropium 250 micrograms. [BNF 2025]`,`**Label each device** by purpose (reliever vs controller) and colour, and write an **asthma or COPD action plan** that names the device and puff count. [GINA 2025]`,`**Counsel on myths**: inhalers are **not addictive**, the inhaled steroid dose is tiny compared with tablets, and inhalers are not a 'last resort'. [GINA 2025]`,`**Cost-conscious choice in India**: capsule DPIs and generic pMDIs with a locally made spacer are cheapest; **Jan Aushadhi Kendras** stock generic inhalers; a **500 mL plastic bottle spacer** is an emergency substitute. [GINA 2025]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency: nebuliser or spacer`,points:[`**Mild-moderate acute asthma**: pMDI with spacer is first choice; **nebulise if life-threatening, if the patient cannot use a spacer, or if oxygen-driven delivery is needed**. [GINA 2025]`,`**Acute asthma**: drive nebulisers with **oxygen at 6-8 L/min** (target SpO2 93-95% in adults). [GINA 2025]`,`**Acute COPD with hypercapnia**: drive nebulisers with **compressed air** and give oxygen separately by nasal cannula (target 88-92%); if oxygen-driven, **limit to about 6 minutes**. [BTS 2017]`,`**Infection control**: nebulisation is an **aerosol-generating procedure** - use a single-patient kit, a side room or filters when respiratory viruses (COVID, influenza) are suspected; spacers are preferred. [WHO 2024]`,`**Ventilated and NIV patients**: pMDI with an in-line spacer or a vibrating mesh nebuliser placed in the circuit. [ERS 2017]`,`**Discharge**: switch back to the patient's own device, **check technique before discharge** and provide a written plan - a key step in every asthma or COPD discharge bundle. [GINA 2025]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Elderly**: arthritis, low PIF and cognitive decline - pMDI with spacer, SMI or nebuliser; involve the carer. [GOLD 2025]`,`**After an exacerbation**: PIF is lower for weeks, so a DPI that worked before may fail - reassess. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Young children**: spacer with mask; **tidal breathing, not breath-hold**; distract rather than force the mask on a crying child; replace mask by mouthpiece by 4-5 years. [GINA 2025]`,`**Pregnancy**: no change in device choice; continue inhaled therapy - the inhaled route minimises fetal exposure. [GINA 2025]`,`**Ipratropium via face-mask nebuliser** can precipitate **acute angle-closure glaucoma** - use a mouthpiece or protect the eyes in those at risk. [BNF 2025]`,`**Hot and humid climates**: keep DPIs and capsules **dry and out of the bathroom**; do not open capsule blisters in advance. [ERS/ISAM 2011]`,`**Resource-limited settings**: a cut **500 mL plastic bottle spacer** works nearly as well as a commercial spacer for children and adults in acute asthma. [WHO 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and adverse effects`,points:[`**Oral candidiasis and dysphonia** from ICS deposition in the mouth and larynx - reduced by spacer and mouth rinse. [GINA 2025]`,`**Paradoxical bronchospasm** after an inhaler (rare, often excipient or cold propellant related) - stop and change formulation. [BNF 2025]`,`**Tremor, tachycardia and hypokalaemia** with high-dose nebulised beta2-agonists; **dry mouth and urinary retention** with antimuscarinics. [BNF 2025]`,`**Contaminated nebulisers** can spread Pseudomonas and other organisms - clean and dry after each use. [ERS 2017]`,`**Facial skin and eye deposition** with masks (steroid rash, cataract risk with long-term ICS by mask) - wash face after nebulised ICS. [GINA 2025]`,`**Over-reliance on home nebulisers** can delay presentation in severe attacks - action plans must say when to seek help. [GINA 2025]`]},{heading:`Follow-up, cleaning and device care`,points:[`**Check technique at every visit**, after any exacerbation, when changing device and whenever control is poor - before stepping up treatment. [GINA 2025]`,`**Spacer cleaning**: wash **about once a month** (and when visibly dirty) in warm water with mild detergent, **do not rinse, let it air-dry**; replace if cracked or valve sticks. [GINA 2025]`,`**pMDI actuator**: rinse the plastic holder in warm water weekly and dry; never put the canister in water. [ERS/ISAM 2011]`,`**DPI**: wipe the mouthpiece with a dry cloth; **never wash** a DPI. [ERS/ISAM 2011]`,`**Nebuliser**: rinse cup and mask after every use, air-dry, disinfect as per manufacturer, replace kits regularly; service the compressor. [ERS 2017]`,`**Know when it is empty**: use dose counters; the 'float test' is unreliable and may damage the valve. [GINA 2025]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme links`,points:[`**Address inhaler stigma** with families, schools and employers; inhalers are safe and the standard of care in children and adults. [GINA 2025]`,`**NP-NCD and Ayushman Arogya Mandir**: chronic respiratory disease care includes inhaler training by community health officers and pharmacists. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**Pharmacists and nurses as trainers** improve technique and control - use every contact. [GINA 2025]`,`**Environmental impact**: pMDI HFA propellants are potent greenhouse gases; where clinically suitable and affordable, a DPI has a lower carbon footprint - but never at the cost of control. [GINA 2025]`,`**Smoking cessation and clean cooking fuel** remain the foundation, whatever the device. [GOLD 2025]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**GINA 2024-2025**: AIR/MART with ICS-formoterol is preferred from 12 years; device choice must allow budesonide-formoterol delivery; environmental impact named as a consideration in device choice. [GINA 2025]`,`**GOLD 2025**: device choice should be individualised using **PIF, dexterity, cognition and preference**, with technique and adherence checked before any escalation. [GOLD 2025]`,`**NICE/BTS/SIGN asthma guideline NG245 (2024)**: AIR/MART approach; check technique and adherence before escalation; consider the carbon footprint of inhalers in shared decisions. [NICE NG245]`,`**Low global-warming-potential propellants** (HFA-152a, HFO-1234ze) are entering pMDIs from 2025 to cut the carbon footprint without forcing a switch to DPIs. [GINA 2025]`,`**NLEM 2022** added budesonide-formoterol and tiotropium inhalers, improving access to guideline-concordant inhaled therapy in India. [NLEM 2022]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**'Slow and deep for pMDI, fast and forceful for DPI'** - the single most tested technique point. [GINA 2025]`,`**Spacer is as good as a nebuliser** for mild-moderate acute asthma - examiners expect the Cochrane-based statement. [GINA 2025]`,`**Check technique before stepping up** - commonest cause of 'uncontrolled' asthma or COPD. [GINA 2025]`,`**Particle size 1-5 micrometres** and the three deposition mechanisms (impaction, sedimentation, diffusion). [ERS/ISAM 2011]`,`**Device by age**: under 4 spacer with mask, 4-5 spacer with mouthpiece, 6 and over DPI possible. [GINA 2025]`,`**Common mistake**: oxygen-driven nebuliser for a hypercapnic COPD patient for long periods. [BTS 2017]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Common inhaled drugs and doses by device`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Salbutamol pMDI`,`100 micrograms/puff; 1-2 puffs as needed; 4-10 puffs via spacer every 20 min in acute attack`,`As needed; review if over 2 days a week`,`Tremor, tachycardia, hypokalaemia`],[`Salbutamol nebulised`,`2.5-5 mg in 3-4 mL, oxygen 6-8 L/min (asthma)`,`Every 20 min for 1 hour, then as needed`,`As above; air-driven in hypercapnic COPD`],[`Ipratropium`,`pMDI 20 micrograms 2 puffs 6-hourly; nebulised 500 micrograms (child 250)`,`Acute: added to salbutamol for 1 hour in severe attacks`,`Dry mouth, urinary retention, angle-closure glaucoma via mask`],[`Budesonide-formoterol (DPI, pMDI, BA-MDI)`,`200/6 micrograms 1 inhalation as needed (max 12/day) or twice daily plus as needed`,`Long-term; review every 3 months`,`Thrush, dysphonia; rinse mouth`],[`Budesonide respules`,`0.5-1 mg nebulised twice daily`,`Long-term or short course`,`Thrush; facial deposition by mask`],[`Tiotropium`,`DPI 18 micrograms once daily or SMI 2.5 micrograms x 2 puffs once daily`,`Long-term`,`Dry mouth, urinary retention, glaucoma`],[`Fluticasone-salmeterol`,`pMDI or DPI, 1-2 inhalations twice daily per strength`,`Long-term`,`Thrush; pneumonia risk in COPD with ICS`]]},{heading:`Device comparison`,columns:[`Device`,`Inhalation needed`,`Advantages`,`Disadvantages`],rows:[[`pMDI alone`,`Slow, deep (3-5 s) with coordination`,`Cheap, portable, all drugs`,`Coordination errors, throat deposition, propellant`],[`pMDI + spacer`,`Slow deep breath or tidal breathing`,`No coordination needed, more lung and less throat deposition, any age`,`Bulky, cleaning, static`],[`Breath-actuated MDI`,`Moderate slow inhalation`,`Removes coordination problem`,`Needs enough flow to trigger`],[`Capsule DPI (Rotahaler, Revolizer)`,`Fast, forceful (PIF 30-60+ L/min)`,`Cheap in India, see and hear dose, no propellant`,`Capsule handling, moisture, low PIF fails`],[`Multi-dose DPI (Turbuhaler, Accuhaler)`,`Fast, forceful`,`Dose counter, convenient`,`Cost, humidity, low PIF fails`],[`Soft mist inhaler`,`Slow, deep`,`Slow mist, high deposition, low effort`,`Cartridge loading, cost`],[`Nebuliser`,`Tidal breathing`,`Any age or severity, no technique`,`Time, cost, cleaning, aerosol generation`]]},{heading:`Device choice by age and situation`,columns:[`Patient`,`First choice`,`Alternative`,`Avoid`],rows:[[`Under 4 years`,`pMDI + valved spacer + face mask`,`Nebuliser`,`DPI`],[`4-5 years`,`pMDI + spacer + mouthpiece`,`Spacer with mask`,`DPI (most)`],[`6 years to adult`,`pMDI + spacer or DPI by preference`,`BA-MDI, SMI`,`pMDI without spacer for ICS in children`],[`Elderly, arthritis or cognitive impairment`,`pMDI + spacer (carer-assisted) or SMI`,`Nebuliser`,`Capsule DPI if dexterity poor`],[`PIF under 30 L/min`,`pMDI + spacer, SMI`,`Nebuliser`,`DPI`],[`Mild-moderate acute attack`,`pMDI + spacer`,`Nebuliser`,`Oral bronchodilator alone`],[`Severe or life-threatening attack`,`Oxygen-driven nebuliser`,`pMDI + spacer`,`Delay to treat`]]},{heading:`Critical errors to check`,columns:[`Device`,`Critical error`,`Fix`],rows:[[`pMDI`,`Pressing before or long after starting to breathe in`,`Add a spacer or switch to BA-MDI`],[`pMDI`,`Inhaling too fast`,`Teach slow 3-5 s breath; use spacer`],[`Spacer`,`Several puffs fired at once`,`One puff at a time, then breathe`],[`DPI`,`Weak inspiratory effort`,`Check PIF; train or switch device`],[`DPI`,`Exhaling into device`,`Breathe out away from the mouthpiece`],[`Capsule DPI`,`Swallowing capsule or not piercing it`,`Demonstrate loading and check capsule empty`],[`All`,`No breath-hold`,`Hold 10 seconds or as long as comfortable`]]},{heading:`Nebuliser versus pMDI with spacer`,columns:[`Feature`,`Nebuliser`,`pMDI + spacer`],rows:[[`Efficacy in mild-moderate acute asthma`,`Equivalent`,`Equivalent (Cochrane)`],[`Time per dose`,`About 10 minutes plus set-up`,`1-2 minutes`],[`Tachycardia in children`,`More`,`Less`],[`Oxygen delivery`,`Can be oxygen-driven`,`Oxygen given separately`],[`Infection risk`,`Aerosol-generating`,`Lower`],[`Cost and portability`,`Needs power and machine`,`Cheap and portable`]]}],redFlags:[`Needing the reliever more often than every 3-4 hours or no response to 10 puffs via spacer - send to emergency.`,`Unable to speak in sentences, SpO2 under 92% or silent chest - oxygen-driven nebulisation and admit.`,`Drowsiness or confusion while using an oxygen-driven nebuliser in COPD - suspect CO2 narcosis; check ABG.`,`Paradoxical bronchospasm after a new inhaler.`,`Eye pain, halos and red eye after ipratropium by face mask - acute angle-closure glaucoma.`,`Child on home nebuliser with repeated night-time use and poor feeding - assess for severe exacerbation.`],pearls:[`**pMDI: slow and deep; DPI: fast and forceful.**`,`**Spacer = nebuliser** for mild-moderate acute asthma, with fewer side effects.`,`**Check technique and adherence before every step-up.**`,`**1-5 micrometres** reaches the airways; over 5 hits the throat.`,`**One puff, one breath** - never several puffs into a spacer.`,`**Wash spacer, do not rinse or wipe** - air-dry to cut static.`,`**PIF under 30 L/min - no DPI.**`,`**Under 4 years: spacer with mask**; 4-5: mouthpiece; 6+: DPI possible.`,`**Air-drive the nebuliser in hypercapnic COPD.**`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-inhaler-devices-q1`,stem:`A 72-year-old woman with COPD uses a Rotahaler for tiotropium and remains symptomatic. Her peak inspiratory flow measured against the device resistance is 25 L/min. What is the best change?`,options:[`Switch to a multi-dose high-resistance DPI`,`Add oral theophylline`,`Switch to the same drug by soft mist inhaler or pMDI with spacer`,`Ask her to inhale harder through the Rotahaler`],answer:2,explanation:`A PIF of 25 L/min is below the minimum of about 30 L/min that DPIs need to deaggregate powder, so she gets little drug. A soft mist inhaler or pMDI with spacer needs only slow inhalation. A high-resistance DPI needs even more effort; theophylline adds toxicity without fixing delivery; simply urging harder rarely raises PIF enough.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-inhaler-devices-q2`,stem:`Which instruction is correct for a pressurised metered-dose inhaler used without a spacer?`,options:[`Breathe in fast and forcefully as you press`,`Breathe out fully into the mouthpiece before pressing`,`Press twice quickly, then inhale once`,`Press once at the start of a slow deep breath, then hold the breath for about 10 seconds`],answer:3,explanation:`Slow inhalation over 3-5 seconds reduces throat impaction and the breath-hold allows sedimentation in small airways. Fast forceful inhalation is the DPI technique and increases throat deposition with a pMDI; exhaling into the mouthpiece and firing two puffs at once are classic errors that reduce the delivered dose.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-inhaler-devices-q3`,stem:`A 3-year-old with an acute wheezing episode in a primary health centre has SpO2 95% and moderate distress. Which delivery method is preferred for salbutamol?`,options:[`pMDI with valved spacer and face mask`,`Rotahaler`,`Oral salbutamol syrup`,`pMDI without spacer, pressed during inspiration`],answer:0,explanation:`For children under 4, a pMDI with valved spacer and tight-fitting face mask is the preferred method and is as effective as a nebuliser in mild-moderate attacks, with less tachycardia. A 3-year-old cannot generate the flow for a DPI or coordinate a bare pMDI, and oral salbutamol is slower, weaker and causes more systemic effects.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-inhaler-devices-q4`,stem:`How should a plastic spacer be cleaned to keep drug delivery high?`,options:[`Rinse under running water after every puff and wipe dry with a towel`,`Wash about monthly in warm water with mild detergent, do not rinse, and let it air-dry`,`Boil for 10 minutes every day`,`Never wash it, to preserve the static charge`],answer:1,explanation:`Detergent leaves a thin film that reduces static charge on the plastic wall, so washing without rinsing and air-drying improves the amount of drug available. Wiping dry with a towel builds static that attracts drug particles; boiling can warp the chamber and valve; static charge reduces, not preserves, delivery.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-inhaler-devices-q5`,stem:`A 64-year-old man with COPD and known chronic hypercapnia (PaCO2 58 mmHg) presents with an exacerbation. How should nebulised bronchodilators be given?`,options:[`Oxygen-driven at 8 L/min for every dose throughout admission`,`Avoid nebulisers entirely and give oral bronchodilators`,`Air-driven nebuliser with supplemental oxygen by nasal cannula to SpO2 88-92%`,`Ultrasonic nebuliser with budesonide only`],answer:2,explanation:`In hypercapnic COPD, oxygen-driven nebulisers at 6-8 L/min can raise PaCO2, so nebulisers are driven by compressed air with controlled oxygen given separately to a target of 88-92% (if oxygen is the only driver, limit to about 6 minutes). Oral bronchodilators are inferior; ultrasonic nebulisers handle suspensions poorly and an ICS is not a bronchodilator.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-inhaler-devices-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Describe the types of inhaler devices. How will you choose a device and teach inhaler technique to a patient with asthma or COPD in primary care?`,openingLines:[`Inhaler devices deliver 1-5 micrometre particles to the airways: pMDIs (with or without spacer), breath-actuated MDIs, dry powder inhalers, soft mist inhalers and nebulisers.`,`The best device is the one the patient can use correctly, can afford and will use regularly - and technique must be checked at every visit before any step-up.`],answer:[{heading:`Principles of aerosol deposition`,points:[`Particle size 1-5 micrometres; impaction, sedimentation, diffusion.`,`pMDI: slow deep inhalation; DPI: fast forceful inhalation (PIF 30, ideally 60 L/min).`]},{heading:`Types of devices`,points:[`pMDI, pMDI with spacer, BA-MDI, capsule DPI (Rotahaler, Revolizer), multi-dose DPI (Turbuhaler), SMI, nebuliser (jet, ultrasonic, mesh).`,`Advantages and disadvantages of each (draw table).`]},{heading:`Choosing a device`,points:[`Age: under 4 spacer with mask; 4-5 spacer with mouthpiece; 6+ DPI possible.`,`PIF, dexterity, cognition, preference, cost and drug availability (MART needs ICS-formoterol device).`,`One device type for all drugs; no switch without training.`]},{heading:`Teaching technique`,points:[`Explain, demonstrate with placebo device, patient demonstrates, correct, repeat (teach-back).`,`Step-by-step technique for pMDI, spacer and DPI.`,`Mouth rinse after ICS; device labels; written action plan.`]},{heading:`Common errors and follow-up`,points:[`Coordination, fast inhalation, exhaling into DPI, weak effort, multiple puffs into spacer.`,`Check at every visit, after exacerbations and before step-up; spacer cleaning monthly.`]},{heading:`Nebuliser versus spacer`,points:[`Equivalent efficacy in mild-moderate acute asthma; spacer faster, cheaper, less tachycardia.`,`Nebuliser for life-threatening attacks or inability to use a spacer; air-driven in hypercapnic COPD.`]}],mustDraw:[`Device comparison table`,`Flow chart: choosing a device by age, PIF and dexterity`],markSplit:[{part:`Deposition principles`,marks:1},{part:`Types with pros and cons`,marks:3},{part:`Choosing a device`,marks:2},{part:`Teaching and errors`,marks:3},{part:`Nebuliser vs spacer`,marks:1}],keywords:[`pMDI`,`spacer`,`DPI`,`Rotahaler`,`SMI`,`nebuliser`,`PIF`,`teach-back`]},{id:`respiratory-inhaler-devices-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`core`,question:`Differentiate between a pressurised metered-dose inhaler and a dry powder inhaler.`,openingLines:[`A pMDI uses a propellant to fire a metered dose and needs slow inhalation with coordination; a DPI is breath-actuated and needs a fast, forceful inhalation to disperse the powder.`,`The difference in inspiratory technique is the most important practical point.`],answer:[{heading:`Mechanism`,points:[`pMDI: HFA propellant, device-driven aerosol.`,`DPI: patient's inspiratory flow deaggregates the powder.`]},{heading:`Technique`,points:[`pMDI: shake, exhale, press at start of slow 3-5 s breath, hold 10 s.`,`DPI: load, exhale away from device, fast deep breath, hold 10 s; do not shake or breathe into it.`]},{heading:`Suitability`,points:[`pMDI with spacer: any age, low PIF, acute attacks.`,`DPI: 6 years and over with PIF at least 30 (ideally 60) L/min; no coordination needed.`]},{heading:`Other differences`,points:[`pMDI: propellant carbon footprint, cold-Freon effect.`,`DPI: humidity-sensitive, no propellant; capsule DPIs cheapest in India.`]}],mustDraw:[`Comparison table pMDI vs DPI`],markSplit:[{part:`Mechanism`,marks:1},{part:`Technique`,marks:2},{part:`Suitability and other points`,marks:2}],keywords:[`pMDI`,`DPI`,`inspiratory flow`,`coordination`,`spacer`,`Rotahaler`]}],references:[`Global Initiative for Asthma (GINA) Global Strategy for Asthma Management and Prevention, 2025 update`,`Global Initiative for Chronic Obstructive Lung Disease (GOLD) Report, 2025`,`Laube BL, et al. What the pulmonary specialist should know about the new inhalation therapies (ERS/ISAM Task Force), European Respiratory Journal 2011`,`NICE/BTS/SIGN Asthma: diagnosis, monitoring and chronic asthma management (NG245), 2024`,`BTS Guideline for oxygen use in adults in healthcare and emergency settings, 2017`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Asthma and COPD chapters`,`National List of Essential Medicines (NLEM), Ministry of Health and Family Welfare, India, 2022`,`Cates CJ, et al. Holding chambers (spacers) versus nebulisers for beta-agonist treatment of acute asthma, Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews 2013`]});var u=[];u.push({id:`respiratory-respiratory-procedures`,title:`Respiratory procedures: thoracocentesis, chest drain, pleural biopsy and bronchoscopy`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`Pleural and airway procedures are invasive diagnostic and therapeutic steps - **ultrasound-guided thoracocentesis**, **intercostal chest drain** inserted in the **triangle of safety** (preferably small-bore by **Seldinger**), **closed (Abrams/Cope) or image-guided pleural biopsy**, **medical thoracoscopy**, and **flexible bronchoscopy with BAL, biopsy and EBUS-TBNA** - each with defined indications, consent, technique and complications that a physician must know (BTS Clinical Statement on Pleural Procedures 2023).`,keywords:[`thoracentesis`,`pleural tap`,`pleural aspiration`,`intercostal drain`,`ICD`,`tube thoracostomy`,`Seldinger`,`underwater seal`,`triangle of safety`,`re-expansion pulmonary oedema`,`Abrams needle`,`Cope needle`,`medical thoracoscopy`,`pleuroscopy`,`fibreoptic bronchoscopy`,`BAL`,`EBUS-TBNA`,`transbronchial biopsy`,`cryobiopsy`,`rigid bronchoscopy`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Thoracocentesis (pleural aspiration)** is needle aspiration of pleural fluid or air - **diagnostic** (20-60 mL) or **therapeutic** (to relieve breathlessness). [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`An **intercostal chest drain (ICD, tube thoracostomy)** is a tube left in the pleural space and connected to an **underwater seal** or valve, to drain air, fluid, blood or pus continuously. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`**Pleural biopsy** samples the parietal pleura - blindly with an **Abrams or Cope needle**, with an **ultrasound/CT-guided cutting needle**, or under direct vision at **medical (local anaesthetic) thoracoscopy**. [BTS Pleural Guideline 2023]`,`**Flexible bronchoscopy** inspects the airway to subsegmental level and samples by **bronchial wash, BAL, brush, endobronchial and transbronchial biopsy**; **EBUS-TBNA** samples mediastinal and hilar nodes through the airway wall. [Harrison 22e]`,`Pleural effusion affects about **1.5 million people a year in the USA**, and in India **tuberculosis is the commonest cause of an exudative effusion** in young adults, so pleural tap and biopsy are among the commonest procedures in a district hospital. [Harrison 22e]`,`Chest drain insertion carries real risk: the **UK National Patient Safety Agency (2008)** reported 12 deaths and 15 serious harms from drains, mostly from **misplacement without imaging** - the reason ultrasound guidance became the standard. [NPSA 2008]`,`With ultrasound guidance the **iatrogenic pneumothorax rate after thoracocentesis falls to about 1-4%**, compared with about 6-10% with landmark technique. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`]},{heading:`Indications`,points:[`**Diagnostic thoracocentesis**: any new unexplained effusion, except a typical bilateral transudate in obvious heart failure, which is treated first and tapped only if it fails to settle or has atypical features. [BTS Pleural Guideline 2023]`,`**Therapeutic aspiration**: breathlessness from a large effusion, and **primary spontaneous pneumothorax** needing intervention (needle aspiration is an option before a drain). [BTS Pleural Guideline 2023]`,`**Chest drain**: tension pneumothorax after needle decompression, secondary or large symptomatic pneumothorax, pneumothorax on a ventilator, **pleural infection (pH under 7.2, pus or organisms)**, haemothorax, and malignant effusion before talc pleurodesis. [BTS Pleural Guideline 2023]`,`**Pleural biopsy**: an undiagnosed **lymphocytic exudate** after fluid analysis, suspected TB with negative fluid tests, and suspected malignancy or **mesothelioma**. [BTS Pleural Guideline 2023]`,`**Medical thoracoscopy**: undiagnosed exudate, especially when malignancy is likely, with the bonus of **talc poudrage** and fluid drainage in the same sitting. [BTS Pleural Guideline 2023]`,`**Flexible bronchoscopy - diagnostic**: haemoptysis, suspected central lung cancer, **non-resolving pneumonia**, sputum-negative or sputum-scarce TB (BAL for **CBNAAT/Truenat**), ILD (BAL, TBB, cryobiopsy), unexplained localised wheeze or collapse, and opportunistic infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bronchoscopy - therapeutic**: removal of **mucus plugs** causing lobar collapse, foreign body, airway stent, debulking of tumour, balloon dilatation of stenosis, and **endobronchial valves** for emphysema. [Harrison 22e]`,`**EBUS-TBNA**: mediastinal staging of lung cancer, diagnosis of **mediastinal lymphadenopathy** (TB versus sarcoidosis versus lymphoma versus metastasis) and of central lesions next to the airway. [ACCP Lung Cancer 2013]`]},{heading:`Applied anatomy and physiology`,points:[`The **intercostal neurovascular bundle** (vein, artery, nerve - VAN from above down) runs in the **costal groove on the lower border of each rib**, so every needle and drain goes **just above the upper border of the rib below**. [Gray's Anatomy 42e]`,`Near the spine the intercostal artery is **tortuous and exposed in the intercostal space**, especially in the elderly - so posterior taps stay **lateral to about 6 cm from the midline (outside the paravertebral zone)**. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`The **triangle of safety** is bounded by the **anterior border of latissimus dorsi, the lateral border of pectoralis major, a horizontal line at the level of the nipple (5th intercostal space)**, with its apex below the axilla - it avoids the internal thoracic artery, the long thoracic nerve, breast tissue and the diaphragm. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`The **hemidiaphragm rises to the 4th-5th intercostal space in expiration**, so a low drain below the nipple line risks **liver or spleen puncture** - ultrasound shows the diaphragm moving and the safe depth. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`**Normal pleural pressure is about -3 to -5 cmH2O**; draining a large effusion against a lung that cannot expand (**trapped or non-expandable lung**) makes pleural pressure fall steeply, causing chest discomfort and cough. [Light Pleural Diseases 6e]`,`**Re-expansion pulmonary oedema** is a permeability oedema: a lung collapsed for **more than 72 hours** develops surfactant loss and endothelial injury, and sudden re-expansion with reperfusion leaks protein-rich fluid into the alveoli. [Light Pleural Diseases 6e]`,`An **underwater seal** works as a one-way valve: the tube tip sits **2-3 cm under water**, so air leaves on expiration and cough but cannot return, and the bottle must stay **below chest level** so water is not sucked back. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`]},{heading:`Classification of procedures and drains`,points:[`**Drain size**: **small-bore (8-14 F)** inserted by Seldinger technique - first choice for pneumothorax, free-flowing effusions and **pleural infection**; **large-bore (20 F or more, often 24-32 F)** by blunt dissection - for haemothorax, trauma and failed small-bore drainage. [BTS Pleural Guideline 2023]`,`**Trocar insertion is abandoned**: forcing a sharp trocar through the chest wall causes lung, heart and liver injury, and is not used in modern practice. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`**Pleural biopsy routes**: blind closed needle (**Abrams** - reverse-bevel hook, **Cope** - hook needle), **image-guided cutting needle** (ultrasound or CT), **local anaesthetic thoracoscopy**, and **video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery (VATS)** under general anaesthesia. [BTS Pleural Guideline 2023]`,`**Thoracoscopy types**: medical (**pleuroscopy**, by a chest physician, under local anaesthetic and sedation, single port, rigid or semi-rigid scope) versus surgical **VATS** (general anaesthesia, single-lung ventilation, multiple ports). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bronchoscopy types**: **flexible** (local anaesthetic, sedation, day-care) and **rigid** (general anaesthesia, wide working channel - for **massive haemoptysis, large foreign bodies, central airway obstruction and stents**). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Advanced bronchoscopy**: **linear EBUS** (mediastinal nodes), **radial EBUS**, electromagnetic or **robotic navigation** (peripheral nodules), and **transbronchial lung cryobiopsy (TBLC)** for ILD. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT IPF 2022]`]},{heading:`Pre-procedure assessment and consent`,points:[`Confirm the **side and site with the patient, the imaging and bedside ultrasound** immediately before the procedure - wrong-side procedures are a recognised never event. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`Use a **safety checklist (LocSSIP)**: identity, consent, side, allergy, anticoagulants, observations and equipment checked aloud before the skin is touched. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`Written **consent** names the common risks: **pain, failure, pneumothorax, bleeding, infection, organ injury and drain displacement**, plus re-expansion oedema for large-volume drainage. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`**Anticoagulants before elective procedures**: stop **warfarin to an INR under about 1.5**, withhold **DOACs for 24-48 hours** (longer in renal impairment), withhold **clopidogrel/prasugrel** before drains and biopsy; aspirin can usually continue; never delay an emergency procedure for these. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`**Contraindications are relative**: uncorrected coagulopathy, skin infection or herpes zoster at the site, very small fluid depth on ultrasound (under 1 cm), and an uncooperative patient. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`Before bronchoscopy: **nil by mouth 4 hours for food and 2 hours for clear fluid**, check SpO2, ECG in cardiac disease, and platelet count and clotting if **transbronchial biopsy** is planned. [BTS Bronchoscopy 2013]`,`Bronchoscopy is avoided or deferred in **unstable angina or MI in the last 4-6 weeks**, refractory hypoxaemia, uncontrolled arrhythmia and severe uncontrolled asthma. [BTS Bronchoscopy 2013]`]},{heading:`Thoracocentesis technique`,points:[`Sit the patient **upright leaning forward on a table**; mark the site with **ultrasound at the point of maximum fluid depth** (usually the mid- or posterior axillary line) and puncture **immediately**, without moving the patient after marking. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`Infiltrate **lidocaine 1% (maximum 3 mg/kg)** down to the pleura, **aspirating as you go** - drawing fluid confirms the depth; if fluid cannot be aspirated with the local anaesthetic needle, do not proceed. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`**Diagnostic tap**: 21 G green needle on a 50 mL syringe, **20-60 mL** sent for protein, LDH, glucose, pH, cell count, Gram stain, culture (in **blood-culture bottles**), AFB/CBNAAT, **ADA** and cytology. [BTS Pleural Guideline 2023]`,`**pH** is measured in a **heparinised blood-gas syringe**, air expelled, run promptly; **lidocaine or air in the sample falsely changes the pH**, and pH is not done in frank pus (which needs a drain anyway). [BTS Pleural Guideline 2023]`,`**Cytology** yields only about **60% for malignancy** and adds little beyond the second sample; send **at least 25-50 mL** fresh. [BTS Pleural Guideline 2023]`,`**Therapeutic tap**: a cannula or catheter system with a three-way tap; **stop at 1.5 L at a sitting**, or earlier if chest tightness, cough or vasovagal symptoms occur. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`**Pleural manometry is not routinely recommended** - trials did not show that it reduces chest discomfort or re-expansion oedema. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`A **chest X-ray afterwards is not needed** after an uncomplicated ultrasound-guided tap; take one if air was aspirated, there are symptoms, or several passes were needed. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`]},{heading:`Chest drain insertion - Seldinger and blunt dissection`,points:[`Position: **supine with the arm behind the head** (or semi-recumbent at 45 degrees), drain sited in the **triangle of safety**, confirmed with **ultrasound for fluid**; give analgesia and consider IV sedation. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`**Seldinger steps**: local anaesthetic; introducer needle aspirating fluid or air; **guidewire** through the needle; small skin nick; **dilator only to about 1 cm beyond the pleural depth** (never to the hub); drain over the wire; remove wire; three-way tap; **suture and transparent dressing**. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`**Blunt dissection (large-bore)**: 1.5-2 cm skin incision parallel to the rib, blunt dissection with forceps **over the upper border of the rib**, **finger sweep** to feel lung and adhesions, then the tube guided in with forceps - no trocar. [ATLS 10e]`,`Direct the tube **apically for air and basally for fluid**, although a well-functioning drain in any position need not be moved. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`Signs of a working intrapleural drain: **fluid column swings with respiration**, air **bubbles on expiration or cough** when there is an air leak, and the drain is seen inside the pleura on X-ray. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`**Do not clamp a bubbling drain** - this can convert a pneumothorax into a tension pneumothorax; a drain for a large effusion is clamped for about an hour after the first **1-1.5 L** to limit re-expansion oedema. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`**Suction is not routine**; if used for a persistent air leak, use **high-volume low-pressure wall suction at -10 to -20 cmH2O** on a dedicated pleural unit. [BTS Pleural Guideline 2023]`,`**Needle decompression of tension pneumothorax** in adults is now at the **4th-5th intercostal space just anterior to the mid-axillary line** (chest wall is thinner there), while the 2nd space mid-clavicular line remains in children. [ATLS 10e]`]},{heading:`Pleural biopsy and medical thoracoscopy`,points:[`**Closed Abrams biopsy**: under local anaesthetic at the site of maximal fluid, the hooked notch is pulled against the parietal pleura, with **at least 4-6 specimens** taken at the **3, 6 and 9 o'clock positions - never 12 o'clock** (neurovascular bundle). [Light Pleural Diseases 6e]`,`**Yield of closed biopsy in TB** is about **70-80% on histology (caseating granuloma)** and rises to **about 90% when tissue culture/CBNAAT is added** - so it remains useful in high-TB-burden India. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Yield of closed biopsy in malignancy** is only about **40-60%**, because tumour is patchy and often over the diaphragm and lower posterior pleura. [BTS Pleural Guideline 2023]`,`**Image-guided cutting-needle biopsy** (ultrasound or CT) of pleural thickening or nodules gives **about 80-90% sensitivity for malignancy** and is preferred over blind biopsy when cancer is suspected. [BTS Pleural Guideline 2023]`,`**Medical thoracoscopy** gives a diagnostic yield of **over 90% for malignancy and close to 100% for TB**, with direct biopsy of visible lesions and **talc poudrage** in the same sitting. [BTS Pleural Guideline 2023]`,`Thoracoscopy needs a **free pleural space** - a dense adhesions (obliterated space on ultrasound) contraindicate it; other risks are surgical emphysema, prolonged air leak, empyema and port-site tumour seeding (mesothelioma). [Harrison 22e]`,`Thoracoscopic appearance: **sago-grain tubercles** with adhesions suggest TB; **nodules, masses and candle-wax lesions** suggest malignancy; pleural plaques suggest asbestos exposure. [Light Pleural Diseases 6e]`]},{heading:`Bronchoscopy and EBUS - technique and samples`,points:[`**Topical lidocaine** (spray to the nose and pharynx, then aliquots of 1-2% via the scope) with total dose kept **under about 8 mg/kg**; light **midazolam with or without fentanyl/alfentanil** sedation; oxygen to keep **SpO2 over 90%**. [BTS Bronchoscopy 2013]`,`**BAL**: wedge the scope in a segmental bronchus and instil **100-300 mL saline in aliquots** - send for cell count and differential, cultures, AFB/**CBNAAT**, fungal stain, PCP stain and cytology. [Harrison 22e]`,`**BAL cell patterns**: **lymphocytes over 15%** suggest sarcoidosis, hypersensitivity pneumonitis or drug reaction (very high in HP); **eosinophils over 25%** suggest eosinophilic pneumonia; neutrophils suggest infection, IPF or ARDS; **milky PAS-positive** fluid suggests alveolar proteinosis. [ATS BAL 2012]`,`**Haemosiderin-laden macrophages over 20%** and **progressively bloodier sequential aliquots** confirm diffuse alveolar haemorrhage. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Transbronchial biopsy (TBB)** samples small lung parenchyma for sarcoidosis, lymphangitis and infection; **transbronchial cryobiopsy** gives larger samples with **about 80% diagnostic yield in ILD**. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT IPF 2022]`,`**Linear EBUS-TBNA** reaches mediastinal stations **2, 4, 7 and hilar 10-11**, with **sensitivity about 89-90% for mediastinal staging**; **EUS-B** through the oesophagus adds **stations 8 and 9** and the left adrenal. [ACCP Lung Cancer 2013]`,`**Stations 5 and 6 (aortopulmonary window and para-aortic)** are not reached by EBUS - they need EUS (station 5 sometimes) or VATS/anterior mediastinotomy. [ESTS 2014]`,`**Negative needle staging with a high clinical suspicion of N2 disease** should be confirmed by **surgical mediastinoscopy** before surgery. [ESTS 2014]`,`For **TB in India**, BAL CBNAAT is an accepted NTEP specimen and has much higher yield than induced sputum in smear-negative disease; EBUS-TBNA of nodes diagnoses **TB lymphadenitis** with granuloma plus Xpert. [NTEP 2024]`]},{heading:`Drain care and inpatient monitoring`,points:[`Record **swing, bubbling and volume** every shift; keep the bottle **below the chest, upright and never lifted** above the patient; secure tubing to avoid kinks. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`**Flush small-bore drains** in pleural infection with **20-30 mL sterile saline every 6-8 hours** through the three-way tap to keep them patent. [BTS Pleural Guideline 2023]`,`**Intrapleural tPA 10 mg plus DNase 5 mg twice daily for 3 days** is used for pleural infection failing to drain (MIST-2 regimen) - details in the Lung abscess and empyema topic. [BTS Pleural Guideline 2023]`,`**A drain that stops swinging** is kinked, blocked, displaced or the lung has fully expanded - check the tubing, flush, and X-ray before blaming the lung. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`**Subcutaneous emphysema** around a drain means a side-hole lies outside the pleura, a blocked drain, or a large air leak - X-ray, check the drain, and refer if the airway or face is involved. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`**Remove the drain** for pneumothorax when bubbling has stopped and the lung is up on X-ray; for effusion when output is **under about 150-200 mL in 24 hours**; pull it out in **expiration or during a Valsalva** and close the wound. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`Give **regular analgesia** (paracetamol, NSAID if no contraindication, opioid if needed) so the patient can breathe deeply, cough and mobilise. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and their management`,points:[`**Pneumothorax** after thoracocentesis is about **1-4% with ultrasound** - most need no drain; a needle in a lung that cannot expand produces a non-expandable lung (pneumothorax ex vacuo), which also needs no drain. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`**Re-expansion pulmonary oedema**: cough, frothy sputum, hypoxaemia within **1-2 hours (up to 24 hours)** of rapid drainage; risk with **collapse over 72 hours, large volume, young age and early suction**; treat with oxygen, CPAP/NIV and support - diuretics have no proven role. [Light Pleural Diseases 6e]`,`**Haemorrhage**: intercostal artery laceration can cause a massive haemothorax - fresh blood in a previously serous drain needs urgent **CT angiography and thoracic surgical or interventional radiology** input. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`**Organ puncture**: liver, spleen, stomach, heart - prevented by **ultrasound, the triangle of safety and controlled dilation**. [NPSA 2008]`,`**Vasovagal syncope** and pleural shock: bradycardia and hypotension during the procedure - stop, lie flat, raise legs; **atropine 0.6 mg IV** if bradycardia persists. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`**Drain site infection and empyema** in about 1-2% - aseptic technique, removal as soon as the drain has done its job. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`**Bronchoscopy complications**: overall mortality about **0.01-0.04%**; **pneumothorax 1-4% after TBB (about 10% after cryobiopsy)**, bleeding, hypoxaemia, bronchospasm, laryngospasm, lidocaine toxicity and **transient fever after BAL**. [BTS Bronchoscopy 2013]`,`**Lidocaine toxicity**: perioral tingling, tinnitus, confusion, seizures, arrhythmia - stop, give oxygen, benzodiazepine for seizures, and **20% lipid emulsion 1.5 mL/kg** for cardiovascular collapse. [AAGBI LAST 2010]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Ventilated patients**: positive pressure turns any pleural breach into a tension pneumothorax - every pneumothorax on a ventilator needs a **drain, not observation**. [BTS Pleural Guideline 2023]`,`**Anticoagulated or thrombocytopenic patients**: use ultrasound, a small needle, the most lateral site and an experienced operator; correct when the procedure is elective. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`**Pregnancy**: ultrasound guidance without radiation; position in left lateral tilt; drain site may need to be higher because the diaphragm is raised. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`**Children**: foreign-body removal is by **rigid bronchoscopy under general anaesthesia**; chest drains are smaller (8-12 F) and placed under sedation. [BTS Paediatric Pleural 2005]`,`**Elderly**: the intercostal artery is tortuous and exposed - keep lateral; delirium after sedation is commoner, so use the least sedation. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`**Resource-limited district hospital**: a closed pleural biopsy with ADA and histology still gives a TB diagnosis in most cases when thoracoscopy is unavailable; a glass underwater-seal bottle remains acceptable if kept below chest level. [ICMR STW 2023]`,`**Infection control**: bronchoscopy is an **aerosol-generating procedure** - use an N95 respirator in suspected TB or COVID, and **high-level disinfection of scopes** (2% glutaraldehyde for at least 20 minutes or automated reprocessors) to prevent mycobacterial pseudo-outbreaks. [BTS Bronchoscopy 2013]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and documentation`,points:[`Refer to a **chest physician or pleural service** for an undiagnosed exudate after two taps, suspected mesothelioma, recurrent malignant effusion (talc or **indwelling pleural catheter**) and septated pleural infection. [BTS Pleural Guideline 2023]`,`Refer to **thoracic surgery** for persistent air leak beyond **3-5 days** in pneumothorax, haemothorax with ongoing bleeding, organised empyema failing drainage and fibrinolytics, and trapped lung needing decortication. [BTS Pleural Guideline 2023]`,`**Document**: indication, consent, ultrasound findings, site, technique, drain size, fluid volume and appearance, samples sent, complications and the plan for review. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`After discharge, review results of **cytology, culture, ADA and histology**; repeat X-ray at **4-6 weeks** after drainage of infection or pneumothorax. [BTS Pleural Guideline 2023]`,`Bronchoscopy day-case patients go home with an escort, **no driving for 24 hours after sedation**, and a written warning to return for breathlessness, chest pain or haemoptysis of more than streaks. [BTS Bronchoscopy 2013]`,`Training: competence in pleural procedures should be assessed and logged; **a physician inserting drains should have ultrasound competence** (Level 1 thoracic ultrasound). [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**BTS Clinical Statement on Pleural Procedures 2023**: ultrasound guidance for **all pleural procedures for fluid**, a procedural safety checklist, and procedure performed **immediately after ultrasound marking**. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`**BTS Pleural Disease Guideline 2023**: **routine pleural manometry is not recommended**; image-guided biopsy or thoracoscopy preferred over blind biopsy when cancer is suspected. [BTS Pleural Guideline 2023]`,`**BTS 2023 pneumothorax**: **conservative management** is acceptable for minimally symptomatic primary spontaneous pneumothorax regardless of size; needle aspiration or **ambulatory devices** before a drain when intervention is needed. [BTS Pleural Guideline 2023]`,`**BTS 2023 malignant effusion**: talc pleurodesis and **indwelling pleural catheter** are both first-line, chosen by patient preference; IPC for non-expandable lung. [BTS Pleural Guideline 2023]`,`**ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT IPF guideline 2022**: **transbronchial lung cryobiopsy** is an acceptable alternative to surgical lung biopsy in experienced centres. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT IPF 2022]`,`**ATLS 10th edition**: adult needle decompression moved to the **4th-5th intercostal space anterior to the mid-axillary line**; smaller (28-32 F) drains for haemothorax. [ATLS 10e]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Viva: boundaries of the triangle of safety** - latissimus dorsi, pectoralis major, nipple line (5th space), apex below the axilla. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`**Viva: why insert above the rib?** - the neurovascular bundle runs in the costal groove under the lower border of each rib. [Gray's Anatomy 42e]`,`**Common mistake**: clamping a bubbling drain, or lifting the bottle above the chest during transfer. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`,`**Common mistake**: sending pleural fluid pH in a plain tube or after lidocaine contamination - it must be a heparinised blood-gas syringe. [BTS Pleural Guideline 2023]`,`**Classic MCQ**: EBUS cannot reach stations 5 and 6; EUS-B reaches 8 and 9. [ESTS 2014]`,`**Classic MCQ**: re-expansion oedema risk is highest when the lung has been collapsed for more than 3 days and is re-expanded rapidly. [Light Pleural Diseases 6e]`,`**Long question**: chest drain - indications, triangle of safety, Seldinger technique, underwater seal diagram, complications and removal. [BTS Pleural Procedures 2023]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Pleural and airway procedures at a glance`,columns:[`Procedure`,`Main indications`,`Key technical point`,`Main complications`],rows:[[`Diagnostic thoracocentesis`,`New unexplained effusion`,`Ultrasound mark, 21 G needle, 20-60 mL, pH in blood-gas syringe`,`Pneumothorax 1-4%, vasovagal, bleeding`],[`Therapeutic thoracocentesis`,`Breathless with large effusion`,`Stop at 1.5 L or earlier if chest tightness or cough`,`Re-expansion oedema, pneumothorax`],[`Small-bore Seldinger drain (8-14 F)`,`Pneumothorax, effusion, pleural infection`,`Triangle of safety, dilator only 1 cm beyond pleura`,`Blockage (flush), displacement, organ injury`],[`Large-bore drain (24-32 F)`,`Haemothorax, trauma, failed small-bore`,`Blunt dissection over upper border of rib, finger sweep, no trocar`,`Pain, bleeding, infection`],[`Closed pleural biopsy (Abrams/Cope)`,`Lymphocytic exudate, suspected TB`,`4-6 bites at 3, 6, 9 o'clock - never 12`,`Pneumothorax, bleeding; low yield in cancer`],[`Image-guided pleural biopsy`,`Pleural thickening or nodules, suspected cancer`,`Ultrasound or CT-guided cutting needle`,`Pneumothorax, bleeding`],[`Medical thoracoscopy`,`Undiagnosed exudate, suspected malignancy`,`Free pleural space needed; talc poudrage possible`,`Surgical emphysema, air leak, empyema`],[`Flexible bronchoscopy`,`Haemoptysis, central tumour, non-resolving pneumonia, smear-negative TB, ILD`,`Topical lidocaine under 8 mg/kg, light sedation, SpO2 over 90%`,`Hypoxaemia, bleeding, pneumothorax after TBB`],[`EBUS-TBNA`,`Mediastinal staging, mediastinal lymphadenopathy`,`Stations 2, 4, 7, 10, 11 (not 5, 6)`,`Minor bleeding, rare mediastinitis`],[`Rigid bronchoscopy`,`Foreign body, massive haemoptysis, central airway obstruction, stents`,`General anaesthesia, wide channel`,`Dental, laryngeal or airway injury`]]},{heading:`Drugs used around pleural and bronchoscopic procedures`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Lidocaine 1% (local infiltration)`,`Up to 3 mg/kg (about 20 mL of 1% in 70 kg) without adrenaline`,`Single procedure; aspirate before injecting`,`Toxicity: perioral tingling, seizures, arrhythmia`],[`Lidocaine topical (bronchoscopy)`,`Spray plus 1-2% aliquots via scope; total under about 8 mg/kg`,`Single procedure`,`Toxicity in elderly, liver or heart failure - use less`],[`Midazolam`,`1-2 mg IV, titrated in 0.5-1 mg steps (elderly 0.5-1 mg)`,`Procedure only; flumazenil 200 microgram IV to reverse`,`Respiratory depression, hypotension, paradoxical agitation`],[`Fentanyl`,`25-50 microgram IV`,`Procedure only; naloxone 0.4 mg IV to reverse`,`Respiratory depression, chest-wall rigidity at high dose`],[`Atropine`,`0.6 mg IV`,`Repeat to 3 mg for vasovagal bradycardia`,`Tachycardia, urinary retention, angle-closure glaucoma`],[`Sterile talc (pleurodesis)`,`4 g as slurry via drain or poudrage at thoracoscopy`,`Single dose; clamp 1 h, then drain`,`Fever, pain; ARDS rare with large-particle graded talc`],[`Alteplase (tPA) plus dornase alfa (DNase)`,`tPA 10 mg plus DNase 5 mg intrapleural`,`Twice daily for 3 days (6 doses)`,`Pleural bleeding; avoid with bronchopleural fistula`],[`20% lipid emulsion`,`1.5 mL/kg IV bolus, then 15 mL/kg/h`,`For local anaesthetic systemic toxicity`,`Pancreatitis, lipaemia - used only in arrest or collapse`]]},{heading:`Pleural fluid tests to send and their cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`Container / handling`,`Key cut-off or meaning`],rows:[[`Protein and LDH (with serum)`,`Plain tube`,`Light's criteria: fluid/serum protein over 0.5, LDH over 0.6, or LDH over two-thirds upper normal serum`],[`pH`,`Heparinised blood-gas syringe, no air, no lidocaine`,`Under 7.2 in suspected infection means drain`],[`Glucose`,`Fluoride tube`,`Under 3.3 mmol/L (60 mg/dL): infection, TB, rheumatoid, malignancy`],[`ADA`,`Plain tube`,`Over 40 IU/L with lymphocytic exudate strongly suggests TB in India`],[`Cytology`,`25-50 mL fresh fluid`,`About 60% sensitivity for malignancy`],[`Gram stain and culture`,`Plain tube plus blood-culture bottles`,`Bottles raise culture yield in pleural infection`],[`AFB smear, culture, CBNAAT`,`Sterile container`,`Low yield in fluid (paucibacillary); biopsy better`],[`Haematocrit`,`EDTA tube`,`Fluid/blood ratio over 0.5 means haemothorax`],[`Triglycerides`,`Plain tube`,`Over 1.24 mmol/L (110 mg/dL) means chylothorax`]]},{heading:`Diagnostic yield of pleural biopsy methods`,columns:[`Method`,`Yield in TB`,`Yield in malignancy`,`Where it fits`],rows:[[`Pleural fluid ADA plus lymphocytes`,`High sensitivity and specificity in India (ADA over 40 IU/L)`,`Not applicable`,`First line in high-TB-burden settings`],[`Fluid cytology`,`Not applicable`,`About 60%`,`First line for suspected malignancy`],[`Closed (Abrams/Cope) biopsy`,`70-80% histology, about 90% with culture`,`40-60%`,`District hospital, suspected TB`],[`Image-guided cutting needle`,`About 80-90%`,`About 80-90%`,`Pleural thickening or nodules on CT/ultrasound`],[`Medical thoracoscopy`,`Close to 100%`,`Over 90%`,`Undiagnosed exudate; allows talc poudrage`],[`VATS`,`Close to 100%`,`Over 95%`,`Loculated or failed thoracoscopy; fit for general anaesthesia`]]},{heading:`Chest drain troubleshooting`,columns:[`Problem`,`Likely cause`,`Action`],rows:[[`Not swinging`,`Kinked, blocked, displaced, or lung fully expanded`,`Check tubing, flush small-bore drain, chest X-ray`],[`Continuous bubbling`,`Persistent air leak or leak in the circuit`,`Check connections; X-ray; refer to surgery if over 3-5 days`],[`Surgical emphysema`,`Side-hole outside pleura, blocked drain, large leak`,`X-ray, reposition or replace; urgent help if face or airway`],[`Sudden fresh blood`,`Intercostal or organ injury`,`Resuscitate, CT angiography, thoracic surgery`],[`Breathless after rapid drainage`,`Re-expansion oedema`,`Clamp, oxygen, CPAP or NIV, supportive care`],[`Drain fallen out`,`Poor suture or dressing`,`Occlusive dressing; X-ray; reinsert only if still indicated`]]}],redFlags:[`Hypotension, rising heart rate and tracheal shift after a pleural procedure - think tension pneumothorax or haemothorax.`,`Fresh blood pouring into a drain that was draining serous fluid - intercostal artery or organ injury.`,`Frothy pink sputum and falling SpO2 within hours of draining a large effusion - re-expansion pulmonary oedema.`,`Expanding surgical emphysema involving the face, neck or voice.`,`Drain bubbling for more than 3-5 days or a pneumothorax that will not re-expand.`,`Seizures, confusion or arrhythmia after local anaesthetic - local anaesthetic systemic toxicity.`,`Haemoptysis of more than streaks, chest pain or breathlessness after transbronchial biopsy - pneumothorax or bleeding.`],pearls:[`Triangle of safety: Latissimus dorsi, Pectoralis major, Nipple line - apex in the axilla.`,`Go over the top of the rib - the vein, artery and nerve (VAN) hide under the lower border.`,`Ultrasound for every pleural procedure for fluid, and puncture immediately after marking (BTS 2023).`,`Never clamp a bubbling drain; never lift the bottle above the chest.`,`Stop a therapeutic tap at 1.5 L or at chest tightness - routine manometry is no longer recommended.`,`Pleural fluid pH goes in a heparinised blood-gas syringe; pH under 7.2 in infection means drain.`,`Abrams biopsy: 3, 6, 9 o'clock - never 12 o'clock.`,`Closed biopsy is good for TB (granulomas are diffuse) and poor for cancer (tumour is patchy).`,`EBUS reaches 2, 4, 7, 10, 11; EUS-B adds 8 and 9; nobody's needle reaches 5 and 6 easily.`,`Needle decompression in adults: 4th-5th space anterior to mid-axillary line (ATLS 10e).`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-respiratory-procedures-q1`,stem:`A 34-year-old man needs an intercostal drain for a large secondary pneumothorax. Which set of landmarks defines the triangle of safety?`,options:[`Sternal border, mid-clavicular line and the 2nd intercostal space`,`Anterior border of latissimus dorsi, lateral border of pectoralis major and a horizontal line at the nipple level`,`Posterior axillary line, inferior angle of scapula and the 8th rib`,`Mid-clavicular line, anterior axillary line and the costal margin`],answer:1,explanation:`The triangle of safety is bounded by the anterior border of latissimus dorsi, the lateral border of pectoralis major and a line at the nipple level (5th intercostal space), with its apex below the axilla. It avoids the internal thoracic vessels, breast and diaphragm. The 2nd space mid-clavicular area is a needle decompression site in children, not a drain site, and a site near the 8th rib or costal margin risks liver or spleen injury.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-respiratory-procedures-q2`,stem:`During thoracocentesis the needle is passed just above the upper border of a rib. What is the main reason?`,options:[`The parietal pleura is thinnest there`,`The long thoracic nerve runs along the upper border of the rib`,`The intercostal neurovascular bundle lies in the costal groove at the lower border of the rib above`,`The intercostal muscles are absent at the upper border`],answer:2,explanation:`The intercostal vein, artery and nerve run in the costal groove on the lower border of each rib, so passing over the upper border of the rib below avoids them. The long thoracic nerve runs on the surface of serratus anterior in the mid-axillary line and is protected by staying behind pectoralis major, not by the rib level. Pleural thickness and intercostal muscles are not the reason.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-respiratory-procedures-q3`,stem:`A 60-year-old woman with pneumonia has a moderate effusion. Pleural fluid is turbid but not frank pus. Which handling of the sample gives a reliable pH to decide on drainage?`,options:[`A heparinised blood-gas syringe with air expelled, run promptly in a blood-gas analyser`,`A plain tube sent to the biochemistry laboratory the next morning`,`Litmus paper dipped in the fluid at the bedside`,`The sample drawn through the same syringe used for lidocaine infiltration`],answer:0,explanation:`Pleural fluid pH must be measured in a heparinised blood-gas syringe, without air, in a blood-gas analyser; a pH under 7.2 in suspected infection is an indication for a chest drain. Delay and air raise the pH, residual lidocaine (acidic) lowers it, and indicator paper is too inaccurate - each of these can wrongly change the decision to drain.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-respiratory-procedures-q4`,stem:`Which factor most increases the risk of re-expansion pulmonary oedema after drainage of a pleural effusion or pneumothorax?`,options:[`Drainage of 500 mL over 2 hours`,`Use of a small-bore Seldinger drain`,`Lung collapse present for more than 72 hours followed by rapid re-expansion`,`Older age over 70 years`],answer:2,explanation:`Re-expansion oedema is a permeability oedema seen when a lung collapsed for more than about 72 hours is re-expanded rapidly, especially with large volumes or early suction. It is commoner in younger patients, not older. Slow drainage of 500 mL is safe, and drain size is not the main determinant - the speed and volume of re-expansion are.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-respiratory-procedures-q5`,stem:`A patient with lung cancer has enlarged lymph nodes on PET-CT. Which mediastinal station cannot be sampled by linear EBUS-TBNA?`,options:[`Station 7 (subcarinal)`,`Station 4R (right lower paratracheal)`,`Station 10L (left hilar)`,`Station 5 (aortopulmonary window)`],answer:3,explanation:`Station 5 in the aortopulmonary window and station 6 (para-aortic) lie outside the reach of the airway wall and need EUS, VATS or anterior mediastinotomy. Subcarinal station 7, paratracheal 4R and hilar 10L are routinely sampled by EBUS-TBNA, which has about 90% sensitivity for mediastinal staging.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-respiratory-procedures-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Describe the indications, technique and complications of intercostal chest drain insertion. Add a note on the care and removal of the drain.`,openingLines:[`An intercostal chest drain is a tube placed in the pleural space and connected to an underwater seal or valve to drain air, fluid, blood or pus.`,`Modern practice (BTS 2023) uses ultrasound guidance, the triangle of safety and small-bore Seldinger drains for most indications.`],answer:[{heading:`Indications`,points:[`**Pneumothorax**: tension (after needle decompression), secondary, large symptomatic, on a ventilator.`,`**Pleural infection**: pH under 7.2, frank pus, organisms on Gram stain or culture.`,`**Haemothorax**, malignant effusion before talc pleurodesis, post-thoracic surgery.`]},{heading:`Pre-procedure`,points:[`Consent, checklist, confirm side clinically and with ultrasound.`,`Elective: stop warfarin (INR under about 1.5), withhold DOAC 24-48 hours; analgesia and sedation.`]},{heading:`Site and anatomy`,points:[`**Triangle of safety**: latissimus dorsi, pectoralis major, nipple line (5th space), apex below axilla.`,`Insert **above the upper border of the rib** to avoid the neurovascular bundle.`]},{heading:`Technique`,points:[`**Seldinger**: lidocaine 1% up to 3 mg/kg, aspirate air or fluid, guidewire, dilator to 1 cm beyond pleura, drain, suture, dressing.`,`**Blunt dissection** for large-bore: incision, blunt dissection, finger sweep, no trocar.`,`Connect to **underwater seal** (tube 2-3 cm under water, bottle below chest); confirm swing and X-ray.`]},{heading:`Complications`,points:[`Early: malposition, organ injury, haemorrhage, **re-expansion oedema**, vasovagal.`,`Late: blockage, infection or empyema, surgical emphysema, displacement, persistent air leak.`]},{heading:`Care and removal`,points:[`Record swing, bubbling, volume; flush small-bore drains 20-30 mL saline 6-8 hourly; **never clamp a bubbling drain**.`,`Remove when bubbling stops and lung expands, or effusion output under 150-200 mL/24 h; remove in expiration or Valsalva.`]}],mustDraw:[`Triangle of safety on a lateral chest wall sketch`,`Cross-section of an intercostal space showing the VAN bundle under the rib`,`Underwater seal bottle with tube 2-3 cm under water`],markSplit:[{part:`Indications`,marks:2},{part:`Anatomy and site with diagram`,marks:2},{part:`Technique`,marks:3},{part:`Complications`,marks:2},{part:`Care and removal`,marks:1}],keywords:[`chest drain`,`triangle of safety`,`Seldinger`,`underwater seal`,`re-expansion oedema`,`BTS 2023`]},{id:`respiratory-respiratory-procedures-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on flexible bronchoscopy and EBUS: indications, samples and complications.`,openingLines:[`Flexible bronchoscopy is endoscopic inspection of the airways to subsegmental level under topical anaesthesia and light sedation, with sampling by wash, BAL, brush and biopsy.`,`EBUS-TBNA adds real-time ultrasound-guided needle aspiration of mediastinal and hilar nodes.`],answer:[{heading:`Indications`,points:[`Diagnostic: haemoptysis, suspected central cancer, non-resolving pneumonia, sputum-negative TB (BAL CBNAAT), ILD.`,`Therapeutic: mucus plug, foreign body, stent, debulking; EBUS for mediastinal staging and lymphadenopathy.`]},{heading:`Samples`,points:[`BAL 100-300 mL saline: cells, cultures, CBNAAT, cytology; lymphocytes over 15% suggest sarcoid/HP.`,`TBB or cryobiopsy for parenchyma; EBUS stations 2, 4, 7, 10, 11 (not 5, 6).`]},{heading:`Complications`,points:[`Hypoxaemia, bleeding, pneumothorax 1-4% after TBB, bronchospasm, fever after BAL, lidocaine toxicity (keep under 8 mg/kg).`,`Mortality about 0.01-0.04%; avoid within 4-6 weeks of MI.`]}],mustDraw:[`IASLC lymph node map marking EBUS-accessible stations`],markSplit:[{part:`Indications`,marks:2},{part:`Samples and EBUS`,marks:2},{part:`Complications`,marks:1}],keywords:[`bronchoscopy`,`BAL`,`EBUS-TBNA`,`transbronchial biopsy`,`cryobiopsy`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Bronchoscopy, thoracoscopy and pleural procedures`,`Asciak R, et al. British Thoracic Society Clinical Statement on Pleural Procedures. Thorax 2023;78(Suppl 3)`,`Roberts ME, et al. British Thoracic Society Guideline for Pleural Disease. Thorax 2023;78(Suppl 3)`,`Du Rand IA, et al. British Thoracic Society guideline for diagnostic flexible bronchoscopy in adults. Thorax 2013;68(Suppl 1)`,`Light RW. Pleural Diseases, 6th edition, 2013`,`Raghu G, et al. ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT Clinical Practice Guideline: Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis (an update) and progressive pulmonary fibrosis, 2022`,`American College of Surgeons. Advanced Trauma Life Support, 10th edition, 2018`,`ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows of India, 2023 - Pleural effusion`]}),u.push({id:`respiratory-post-covid-lung`,title:`Post-COVID condition and post-COVID lung disease`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`**Post-COVID-19 condition (long COVID)** is, by the **WHO 2021 Delphi definition**, symptoms that usually begin **within 3 months of SARS-CoV-2 infection, last at least 2 months** and cannot be explained by another diagnosis; its lung component ranges from **reduced DLCO and dysfunctional breathing** to **post-COVID organising pneumonia, fibrotic-like change and pulmonary embolism**, managed by exclusion of serious causes, **pacing-based rehabilitation** and targeted treatment.`,keywords:[`long COVID`,`post-acute sequelae of SARS-CoV-2`,`PASC`,`post-COVID syndrome`,`U09.9`,`post-COVID fibrosis`,`post-COVID organising pneumonia`,`post-exertional malaise`,`POTS`,`brain fog`,`1-minute sit-to-stand test`,`DLCO`,`post-intensive care syndrome`,`COVID-associated mucormycosis`,`MICHELLE trial`,`rivaroxaban`,`pacing`,`dysfunctional breathing`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**WHO clinical case definition (Delphi, 2021)**: a condition in people with probable or confirmed SARS-CoV-2 infection, **usually 3 months from onset**, with symptoms that **last at least 2 months** and are not explained by an alternative diagnosis; symptoms may be new, persist from the acute illness, or fluctuate and relapse. [WHO 2021]`,`**NICE time bands**: acute COVID-19 up to **4 weeks**, **ongoing symptomatic COVID-19 4-12 weeks**, and **post-COVID-19 syndrome beyond 12 weeks** - long COVID covers the last two. [NICE NG188 2024]`,`**NASEM 2024** defines long COVID as an **infection-associated chronic condition present for at least 3 months**, continuous, relapsing-remitting or progressive, affecting one or more organ systems - and it does not require a positive test. [NASEM 2024]`,`It is coded as **ICD-10 U09.9 (post COVID-19 condition)**. [WHO ICD-10]`,`About **6% of people with symptomatic infection** have persisting fatigue, breathlessness or cognitive problems at 3 months (GBD pooled analysis), and the risk is higher after **hospital and ICU admission** (up to half of ICU survivors). [JAMA GBD Long COVID 2022]`,`Risk per infection has **fallen with vaccination and the Omicron era**, but because so many people are infected, long COVID remains a large absolute burden. [NASEM 2024]`,`India's second wave (2021) left many survivors of severe pneumonia with **persistent breathlessness**, and a parallel epidemic of **COVID-associated mucormycosis** (over 45,000 cases reported by mid-2021). [MoHFW 2021]`,`India's response was the **MoHFW National Comprehensive Guidelines for Management of Post-COVID Sequelae (2021)**, with post-COVID follow-up clinics at district and medical college level. [MoHFW 2021]`]},{heading:`Risk factors`,points:[`**Severity of acute illness**: hospital admission, oxygen need, **mechanical ventilation and ICU stay** are the strongest risks for lung sequelae. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Female sex, middle age (35-69 years)**, obesity, smoking, pre-existing asthma or COPD, diabetes and anxiety or depression raise the risk of long COVID. [NICE NG188 2024]`,`**Being unvaccinated** raises risk; vaccination before infection roughly lowers the odds of long COVID in observational studies. [NASEM 2024]`,`**Reinfection** adds cumulative risk with each episode. [NASEM 2024]`,`**Steroid and tocilizumab use with uncontrolled diabetes** was the key driver of COVID-associated mucormycosis in India. [ICMR 2021]`,`**Prolonged immobility, high D-dimer and cancer** add to post-discharge venous thromboembolism risk. [ASH COVID VTE 2023]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**Viral persistence**: SARS-CoV-2 RNA and proteins persist in gut and other tissue reservoirs in some patients for months, driving ongoing immune activation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Immune dysregulation and autoimmunity**: persistent T-cell activation, autoantibodies (including against G-protein-coupled receptors) and reactivation of latent **Epstein-Barr virus**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Endothelial injury and microclots**: SARS-CoV-2 enters endothelium via **ACE2**, causing endotheliitis, platelet activation and microthrombi - explaining thrombosis and part of the exercise intolerance. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Autonomic dysfunction** (small-fibre neuropathy, dysautonomia) produces **postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome (POTS)**, palpitations and fatigue. [NICE NG188 2024]`,`**Lung injury after severe COVID** is diffuse alveolar damage; most heals, but some patients develop **organising pneumonia** (steroid-responsive) or **fibrotic-like change** with traction bronchiectasis, usually non-progressive. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Reduced DLCO** is the commonest lung-function abnormality, from residual interstitial change and **pulmonary microvascular damage**, even with normal spirometry. [Lancet Huang 2021]`,`**Dysfunctional breathing** (upper-chest breathing, hyperventilation) follows illness and anxiety and causes breathlessness out of proportion to lung damage. [BTS COVID Follow-up 2020]`,`**Deconditioning and post-intensive care syndrome**: muscle loss, ICU-acquired weakness, cognitive impairment and PTSD add to symptoms after ICU. [NICE NG188 2024]`]},{heading:`Classification - clinical phenotypes`,points:[`**Respiratory**: breathlessness, cough, exertional desaturation, reduced DLCO, residual CT change. [NICE NG188 2024]`,`**Fatigue with post-exertional malaise (PEM)**: crash 12-48 hours after effort, overlapping with **ME/CFS** - graded exercise worsens it. [NICE ME/CFS 2021]`,`**Cardiovascular and autonomic**: chest pain, palpitations, **POTS**, myocarditis or pericarditis. [ESC 2022]`,`**Neurocognitive**: brain fog, memory and concentration problems, headache, **anosmia or parosmia**, sleep disturbance. [NICE NG188 2024]`,`**Psychological**: anxiety, depression and **PTSD**, especially after ICU. [NICE NG188 2024]`,`**Post-COVID lung disease (radiological)**: ground glass resolving, **organising pneumonia**, **fibrotic-like change** (reticulation, traction bronchiectasis), and vascular disease (PE, chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Complications of acute treatment**: steroid-induced diabetes, mucormycosis, aspergillosis, tracheal stenosis after intubation or tracheostomy, and critical illness polyneuropathy. [MoHFW 2021]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Commonest symptoms**: fatigue, breathlessness, cognitive dysfunction (brain fog), chest tightness, palpitations, cough, sleep problems, joint and muscle pain, altered smell or taste. [WHO 2021]`,`Ask about the **acute illness**: date, severity, oxygen, ICU or ventilation, steroids, anticoagulation and vaccination status. [MoHFW 2021]`,`Ask about **PEM**: does activity cause a crash later that day or the next day - this changes the rehabilitation plan. [NICE ME/CFS 2021]`,`Ask about **orthostatic symptoms**: dizziness, palpitations and fatigue on standing that ease on lying down - suggests POTS. [NICE NG188 2024]`,`**Red-flag history**: breathlessness worsening rather than improving, **pleuritic chest pain, haemoptysis, calf swelling**, syncope, or chest pain on exertion. [NICE NG188 2024]`,`In a diabetic who received steroids ask about **facial pain, nasal blockage, black nasal discharge, toothache, eye swelling or visual loss** - mucormycosis. [ICMR 2021]`,`Ask about **work, family and finances** - long COVID often means lost income; screen mood with **PHQ-9 and GAD-7**. [NICE NG188 2024]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Record **resting SpO2, pulse, BP and respiratory rate**; resting SpO2 is often normal even when exercise SpO2 falls. [NICE NG188 2024]`,`**1-minute sit-to-stand test**: a fall of **SpO2 by 3% or more** on exertion is abnormal and needs investigation (lung disease or PE). [NICE NG188 2024]`,`**Active stand test**: lying 5 minutes then standing up to 10 minutes; a sustained heart-rate rise of **30 beats/min or more without orthostatic hypotension** (40 or more at 12-19 years) suggests POTS. [NICE NG188 2024]`,`**Fine end-inspiratory crackles** suggest residual interstitial change or fibrosis; clubbing is unusual and points to another cause. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for **calf tenderness or swelling, raised JVP and a loud P2** (PE or pulmonary hypertension). [Harrison 22e]`,`Check the **neck for a tracheostomy scar and stridor** - post-intubation tracheal stenosis is missed as asthma. [BTS COVID Follow-up 2020]`,`Examine the **nose, palate and eye** in diabetics after steroids: palatal ulcer, black eschar, proptosis, ophthalmoplegia. [ICMR 2021]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Pulmonary embolism or CTEPH**: breathlessness with exertional desaturation, clear lungs - see the Pulmonary embolism topic in cardiovascular. [ESC PE 2019]`,`**Heart failure, myocarditis or ischaemia**: orthopnoea, oedema, raised BNP, ECG change. [ESC 2022]`,`**Anaemia, thyroid disease, uncontrolled diabetes and new hypertension** cause fatigue and breathlessness - simple blood tests. [NICE NG188 2024]`,`**Pulmonary tuberculosis** reactivating after steroids and lymphopenia - do sputum CBNAAT in any cough over 2 weeks. [NTEP 2024]`,`**Unmasked asthma or COPD**, and **tracheal stenosis** after intubation. [BTS COVID Follow-up 2020]`,`**Pre-existing ILD unmasked** by COVID, distinguished by pre-COVID imaging and progression. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT IPF 2022]`,`**Depression, anxiety and dysfunctional breathing** - Nijmegen questionnaire score over 23 suggests hyperventilation syndrome. [BTS COVID Follow-up 2020]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**First line**: CBC, renal and liver function, **HbA1c**, TSH, CRP, ferritin, vitamin D and B12 if indicated, **BNP or NT-proBNP**, troponin if chest pain, and **12-lead ECG**. [NICE NG188 2024]`,`**Chest X-ray at 12 weeks** for anyone with persisting respiratory symptoms after COVID pneumonia; earlier if red flags. [BTS COVID Follow-up 2020]`,`**Full lung function with DLCO** for persistent breathlessness or abnormal X-ray - **DLCO is reduced in up to half of patients with severe disease at 6 months**. [Lancet Huang 2021]`,`**HRCT thorax** if X-ray or DLCO is abnormal - look for ground glass, consolidation (organising pneumonia), reticulation and traction bronchiectasis. [BTS COVID Follow-up 2020]`,`**D-dimer may stay raised for weeks after COVID**, so a positive result is less useful - with clinical suspicion of PE go straight to **CTPA or V/Q scan**. [ESC PE 2019]`,`**Echocardiography** for suspected pulmonary hypertension, myocarditis or heart failure; **cardiac MRI** for suspected myocarditis. [ESC 2022]`,`**6-minute walk test** for distance and desaturation, and to plan rehabilitation. [ATS 6MWT 2002]`,`For suspected mucormycosis: **KOH mount and biopsy of nasal tissue**, and contrast **MRI of paranasal sinuses, orbit and brain**. [ICMR 2021]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Validate and explain**: long COVID is real, usually improves, and has no single cure - shared decision-making and a named clinician. [NICE NG188 2024]`,`**Pacing (energy management)** - stay within an energy envelope, plan and rest before crashes; **avoid graded exercise therapy if post-exertional malaise is present**. [NICE ME/CFS 2021]`,`**Breathing retraining** for dysfunctional breathing: nasal, low, slow diaphragmatic breathing, taught by a physiotherapist. [BTS COVID Follow-up 2020]`,`**Pulmonary rehabilitation** for persistent breathlessness without PEM, individualised and symptom-titrated. [WHO Rehabilitation 2023]`,`**POTS**: fluids 2-3 L/day, salt 6-10 g/day if no hypertension, compression garments, recumbent exercise; drugs (low-dose propranolol, ivabradine, fludrocortisone, midodrine) are specialist and off-label. [NICE NG188 2024]`,`**Smell training** twice daily for 12 weeks with 4 odours for anosmia or parosmia. [NICE NG188 2024]`,`Treat **mood, sleep and pain**: CBT and SSRIs for depression or anxiety, sleep hygiene; avoid unproven supplements and repeated antibiotics. [NICE NG188 2024]`,`**No antiviral or antifibrotic is proven** for long COVID; nirmatrelvir-ritonavir trials for established long COVID were negative. [NASEM 2024]`]},{heading:`Management of post-COVID lung disease and inpatient care`,points:[`**Post-COVID organising pneumonia** (persistent consolidation and ground glass at 6 weeks with functional impairment): **prednisolone about 0.5 mg/kg/day** tapered over about 3 weeks improved symptoms, DLCO and CT in a UK cohort - specialist decision after excluding infection. [Myall Ann Am Thorac Soc 2021]`,`**Fibrotic-like change** is mostly non-progressive and improves over 1-2 years; **antifibrotics (pirfenidone, nintedanib) are not recommended outside trials**. [BTS COVID Follow-up 2020]`,`Repeat **lung function and HRCT at about 6-12 months**; refer to an ILD clinic if there is **progression** (falling FVC or DLCO, worsening fibrosis). [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT IPF 2022]`,`**Admit**: SpO2 under 94% at rest, exertional desaturation with suspected PE, chest pain with ECG change, syncope, haemoptysis, or suspected mucormycosis. [MoHFW 2021]`,`**PE after COVID**: treat as for any PE with a DOAC, e.g. **apixaban 10 mg twice daily for 7 days then 5 mg twice daily**, or **rivaroxaban 15 mg twice daily for 21 days then 20 mg once daily**, for at least 3 months (provoked). [ESC PE 2019]`,`**Extended post-discharge prophylaxis is not routine**; the **MICHELLE trial** showed benefit of **rivaroxaban 10 mg daily for 35 days** in selected high-risk patients (IMPROVE VTE score 4 or more, or 2-3 with D-dimer over twice normal). [ASH COVID VTE 2023]`,`**Long-term oxygen** is rarely needed; assess with ABG if resting SpO2 is 92% or less, and recheck after recovery because many come off oxygen. [BTS Home Oxygen 2015]`,`**COVID-associated mucormycosis**: urgent surgical debridement, **liposomal amphotericin B 5 mg/kg/day IV** (10 mg/kg for CNS), glycaemic control and stopping steroids - see the ABPA, aspergilloma and mucormycosis topic. [ICMR 2021]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Diabetes**: steroid-induced hyperglycaemia may persist; review HbA1c, and remember the mucormycosis link. [ICMR 2021]`,`**Elderly**: multimorbidity and frailty dominate; screen for falls, delirium after ICU and polypharmacy. [NICE NG188 2024]`,`**Pregnancy**: long COVID symptoms overlap with normal pregnancy breathlessness; CTPA or V/Q is still done if PE is suspected. [RCOG 2022]`,`**Children**: long COVID is less common; exclude MIS-C history and organic disease; school adjustments. [NICE NG188 2024]`,`**Athletes and heavy manual workers**: screen for myocarditis before return to strenuous activity. [ESC Sports Cardiology 2020]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: 1-minute sit-to-stand with a pulse oximeter, X-ray and ECG identify most patients needing referral. [MoHFW 2021]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Most patients improve within 6-12 months**, but a minority remain symptomatic beyond 2 years. [NASEM 2024]`,`After severe pneumonia, **fibrotic-like CT change persists at 1 year in a minority (roughly a fifth to a third)** of hospitalised patients, mostly non-progressive. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Raised risk of new diabetes, cardiovascular events, stroke and VTE** in the year after infection. [NASEM 2024]`,`**CTEPH** after COVID-related PE - persistent breathlessness 3 months after PE needs echo and V/Q scan. [ESC PE 2019]`,`**Tracheal stenosis** after prolonged intubation or tracheostomy - see the Upper airway obstruction topic. [BTS COVID Follow-up 2020]`,`**Loss of work and income**, isolation and depression are major, often overlooked consequences. [WHO 2021]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and return to work`,points:[`Refer to a **post-COVID or respiratory clinic** for abnormal X-ray at 12 weeks, reduced DLCO, exertional desaturation, or suspected organising pneumonia or fibrosis. [BTS COVID Follow-up 2020]`,`Refer **cardiology** for chest pain with abnormal ECG, troponin or echo; **neurology** for persistent cognitive deficit; **ENT/ophthalmology urgently** for suspected mucormycosis. [NICE NG188 2024]`,`Follow up at **6 weeks, 12 weeks and then 3-6 monthly** with symptoms, SpO2, sit-to-stand, and lung function where abnormal. [MoHFW 2021]`,`**Return to work**: phased return, reduced hours, adjusted duties and flexible breaks, agreed with the employer and occupational health. [NICE NG188 2024]`,`**Return to exercise**: start only when **at least 7 days symptom-free** of acute illness, with graded stages over at least a week; stop if PEM or chest pain recur. [BJSM Salman 2021]`,`**After myocarditis**, avoid competitive sport for **3-6 months** and resume only after normal biomarkers, echo and Holter. [ESC Sports Cardiology 2020]`]},{heading:`Prevention and counselling`,points:[`**Up-to-date COVID-19 vaccination** reduces severe disease and long COVID risk - advise the current booster recommendation for high-risk adults. [WHO SAGE 2024]`,`Early antiviral treatment of high-risk acute COVID, smoking cessation and weight control reduce severe illness. [WHO COVID Therapeutics 2025]`,`**Rational steroid use** during acute COVID (only if hypoxaemic) and glucose control to prevent mucormycosis. [ICMR 2021]`,`Counsel that **symptoms fluctuate**, setbacks are expected, and improvement is the usual course. [NICE NG188 2024]`,`Link to **Ayushman Arogya Mandir** for NCD screening (diabetes, hypertension) after COVID and to tele-consultation (eSanjeevani) for follow-up. [MoHFW 2021]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**NASEM 2024 long COVID definition**: chronic condition for at least 3 months after infection, no test required. [NASEM 2024]`,`**NICE NG188 (updated 2024)** retains the 4-week and 12-week time bands, 1-minute sit-to-stand, active stand test and multidisciplinary rehabilitation. [NICE NG188 2024]`,`**WHO Clinical Management of COVID-19 living guideline (2023-2025)** includes rehabilitation for post-COVID condition and recommends against routine antivirals for established symptoms. [WHO COVID Therapeutics 2025]`,`**WHO rehabilitation guidance 2023**: assess for PEM and exertional desaturation before exercise-based rehabilitation. [WHO Rehabilitation 2023]`,`**ASH 2023** thromboprophylaxis guidance: post-discharge anticoagulation not routine; individual decision in high-risk patients. [ASH COVID VTE 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Viva: WHO definition** - 3 months from onset, at least 2 months duration, no alternative explanation. [WHO 2021]`,`**Viva: bedside test for exertional desaturation** - 1-minute sit-to-stand; fall of 3% or more is abnormal. [NICE NG188 2024]`,`**Common mistake**: prescribing graded exercise to someone with post-exertional malaise. [NICE ME/CFS 2021]`,`**Common mistake**: excluding PE with a raised-but-unhelpful D-dimer, or attributing desaturation to anxiety. [ESC PE 2019]`,`**Classic MCQ**: commonest PFT abnormality after COVID pneumonia is a **reduced DLCO**. [Lancet Huang 2021]`,`**Short note**: post-COVID organising pneumonia - persistent consolidation, steroid-responsive. [Myall Ann Am Thorac Soc 2021]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs used in post-COVID condition and its lung complications`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Prednisolone (post-COVID organising pneumonia)`,`About 0.5 mg/kg/day oral`,`Taper over about 3 weeks; specialist`,`Hyperglycaemia, infection, mucormycosis risk in diabetes`],[`Apixaban (PE)`,`10 mg twice daily oral`,`7 days, then 5 mg twice daily for at least 3 months`,`Bleeding; avoid in severe renal failure, pregnancy`],[`Rivaroxaban (PE)`,`15 mg twice daily oral with food`,`21 days, then 20 mg once daily for at least 3 months`,`Bleeding; drug interactions`],[`Rivaroxaban (post-discharge prophylaxis, MICHELLE)`,`10 mg once daily oral`,`35 days, high-risk only`,`Bleeding; not routine`],[`Propranolol (POTS, off-label)`,`10 mg oral once or twice daily`,`Titrate to symptoms`,`Avoid in asthma; fatigue, hypotension`],[`Ivabradine (POTS, off-label)`,`2.5-5 mg oral twice daily`,`Specialist titration`,`Phosphenes, bradycardia; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Sertraline (depression, anxiety)`,`50 mg oral once daily`,`Titrate by 25-50 mg; continue 6 months after remission`,`Nausea, hyponatraemia in elderly, bleeding with antiplatelets`],[`Liposomal amphotericin B (mucormycosis)`,`5 mg/kg/day IV (10 mg/kg if CNS)`,`Until stable, then step down to posaconazole or isavuconazole`,`Nephrotoxicity, hypokalaemia, infusion reactions`]]},{heading:`Time bands and definitions`,columns:[`Source`,`Definition`,`Time frame`],rows:[[`WHO Delphi 2021`,`Post-COVID-19 condition, not explained by another diagnosis`,`Usually 3 months from onset, lasting at least 2 months`],[`NICE NG188`,`Acute COVID-19`,`Up to 4 weeks`],[`NICE NG188`,`Ongoing symptomatic COVID-19`,`4-12 weeks`],[`NICE NG188`,`Post-COVID-19 syndrome`,`Beyond 12 weeks`],[`NASEM 2024`,`Long COVID - infection-associated chronic condition`,`At least 3 months, continuous, relapsing or progressive`]]},{heading:`Breathlessness after COVID - differential diagnosis`,columns:[`Cause`,`Clue`,`Test`],rows:[[`Pulmonary embolism / CTEPH`,`Exertional desaturation, clear chest, pleuritic pain`,`CTPA or V/Q; echo`],[`Organising pneumonia`,`Persistent consolidation, crackles, reduced DLCO`,`HRCT`],[`Fibrotic-like change`,`Fine crackles, restrictive PFT`,`HRCT, serial PFT`],[`Myocarditis / heart failure`,`Chest pain, palpitations, oedema`,`ECG, troponin, BNP, echo, cardiac MRI`],[`Dysfunctional breathing`,`Sighing, tingling, normal SpO2 and tests`,`Nijmegen questionnaire over 23`],[`Tracheal stenosis`,`Stridor after intubation or tracheostomy`,`Flow-volume loop, CT, bronchoscopy`],[`Deconditioning`,`Gradual improvement with activity`,`6-minute walk test`],[`Anaemia, thyroid, diabetes`,`Fatigue, pallor`,`CBC, TSH, HbA1c`]]},{heading:`Post-COVID assessment - tests and cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`Abnormal result`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`1-minute sit-to-stand`,`SpO2 fall of 3% or more`,`Exertional desaturation - investigate lung and PE`],[`Active stand test`,`Heart-rate rise 30/min or more without hypotension`,`POTS`],[`Chest X-ray at 12 weeks`,`Persistent shadowing`,`HRCT and respiratory referral`],[`DLCO`,`Under 80% predicted`,`Residual interstitial or vascular disease`],[`D-dimer`,`Often raised for weeks after infection`,`Low specificity - use imaging if PE suspected`],[`PHQ-9 / GAD-7`,`10 or more`,`Moderate depression or anxiety`]]}],redFlags:[`Resting SpO2 under 94% or a fall of 3% or more on the 1-minute sit-to-stand test.`,`Pleuritic chest pain, haemoptysis, calf swelling or syncope - suspect pulmonary embolism.`,`Exertional chest pain, palpitations with syncope, or abnormal ECG/troponin - myocarditis or ischaemia.`,`Facial pain, black nasal discharge, palatal eschar, proptosis or visual loss in a diabetic after steroids - mucormycosis.`,`Stridor after intubation or tracheostomy - tracheal stenosis.`,`Progressive breathlessness with falling FVC or DLCO - progressive fibrosis.`,`Suicidal thoughts or severe depression.`],pearls:[`WHO definition: 3 months from onset, 2 months duration, no other explanation.`,`NICE bands: 4 weeks acute, 4-12 weeks ongoing, over 12 weeks post-COVID syndrome.`,`Reduced DLCO is the commonest lung-function abnormality after COVID pneumonia.`,`1-minute sit-to-stand: fall of 3% or more SpO2 is abnormal.`,`Post-exertional malaise means pacing, not graded exercise.`,`D-dimer stays high after COVID - if you suspect PE, image.`,`Post-COVID organising pneumonia responds to steroids; fibrotic-like change mostly does not progress.`,`Steroids plus uncontrolled diabetes plus COVID = mucormycosis - look in the nose and palate.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-post-covid-lung-q1`,stem:`According to the WHO 2021 clinical case definition, post-COVID-19 condition is diagnosed when symptoms usually start within 3 months of infection and last for at least how long?`,options:[`2 weeks`,`4 weeks`,`2 months`,`6 months`],answer:2,explanation:`The WHO Delphi definition requires symptoms usually beginning within 3 months of onset of COVID-19, lasting at least 2 months and not explained by an alternative diagnosis. Four weeks marks the end of acute COVID in NICE time bands, and 2 weeks or 6 months are not part of either definition.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-post-covid-lung-q2`,stem:`A 52-year-old man discharged after severe COVID pneumonia 4 months ago has exertional breathlessness. Spirometry is normal. Which lung function abnormality is most commonly found in such patients?`,options:[`Reduced diffusing capacity for carbon monoxide (DLCO)`,`Reduced FEV1/FVC ratio`,`Raised residual volume`,`Raised DLCO`],answer:0,explanation:`A reduced DLCO is the commonest abnormality after COVID pneumonia, reflecting residual interstitial and microvascular damage, and it is often present with normal spirometry. A reduced FEV1/FVC ratio and raised residual volume indicate obstruction and air trapping, which are not typical; a raised DLCO suggests alveolar haemorrhage or polycythaemia.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-post-covid-lung-q3`,stem:`A 38-year-old teacher with long COVID describes being bed-bound for two days after a short walk. Which management approach is most appropriate?`,options:[`Graded exercise therapy increasing walking time by a fixed amount each week`,`A trial of antifibrotic therapy`,`Pacing within an energy envelope with symptom-led activity`,`Long-term oral corticosteroids`],answer:2,explanation:`Crashing 12-48 hours after exertion is post-exertional malaise; NICE advises pacing within an energy envelope and avoiding graded exercise therapy with fixed increments, which can worsen symptoms. Antifibrotics and long-term steroids have no role in fatigue-predominant long COVID without lung disease.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-post-covid-lung-q4`,stem:`Three months after COVID, a 60-year-old woman has resting SpO2 of 96% that falls to 91% after a 1-minute sit-to-stand test. Chest X-ray is clear. What is the most important next step?`,options:[`Reassure and refer for breathing retraining`,`Imaging for pulmonary embolism, with CTPA or a V/Q scan`,`Start home oxygen`,`Rely on a D-dimer level to decide`],answer:1,explanation:`A fall of 3% or more on the sit-to-stand test is abnormal, and exertional desaturation with a clear X-ray is pulmonary embolism until proved otherwise. D-dimer often stays raised after COVID, so it neither confirms nor safely excludes PE when suspicion is real. Breathing retraining treats dysfunctional breathing only after organic causes are excluded, and oxygen does not address the cause.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`respiratory-post-covid-lung-q5`,stem:`A 55-year-old diabetic treated with high-dose dexamethasone for COVID develops facial pain, nasal blockage and a black eschar on the palate. What is the first-line antifungal?`,options:[`Fluconazole`,`Voriconazole`,`Itraconazole`,`Liposomal amphotericin B`],answer:3,explanation:`COVID-associated rhino-orbital mucormycosis is treated with urgent debridement and liposomal amphotericin B 5 mg/kg/day, plus glucose control and stopping steroids. Voriconazole has no activity against Mucorales and can even select for it; fluconazole and itraconazole are ineffective as primary therapy.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-post-covid-lung-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Define post-COVID-19 condition. Describe the evaluation of persistent breathlessness after COVID-19 and outline the management of post-COVID lung disease in primary care.`,openingLines:[`Post-COVID-19 condition (WHO 2021) is a condition in people with probable or confirmed SARS-CoV-2 infection, usually 3 months from onset, with symptoms lasting at least 2 months that are not explained by an alternative diagnosis.`,`Persistent breathlessness has many causes - residual lung injury, pulmonary embolism, cardiac disease, deconditioning and dysfunctional breathing - and the first job is to exclude the dangerous ones.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and time bands`,points:[`WHO 2021 definition; NICE: acute up to 4 weeks, ongoing 4-12 weeks, post-COVID syndrome over 12 weeks.`,`About 6% of symptomatic infections; higher after ICU.`]},{heading:`Mechanisms`,points:[`Viral persistence, immune dysregulation, endothelial injury and microclots, dysautonomia, residual lung damage, deconditioning.`]},{heading:`History and examination`,points:[`Acute severity, ICU, steroids, vaccination; red flags - pleuritic pain, haemoptysis, syncope, calf swelling.`,`SpO2 at rest and after **1-minute sit-to-stand (fall 3% or more abnormal)**; active stand test for POTS; crackles, JVP, stridor.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`CBC, HbA1c, TSH, BNP, ECG; chest X-ray at 12 weeks; PFT with DLCO; HRCT if abnormal.`,`CTPA or V/Q if PE suspected (D-dimer unreliable); echo for pulmonary hypertension or myocarditis.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Pacing if PEM; breathing retraining; pulmonary rehabilitation; treat mood and sleep.`,`Organising pneumonia: prednisolone about 0.5 mg/kg tapered over 3 weeks (specialist); fibrotic-like change - monitor, no antifibrotics outside trials.`,`PE: DOAC for at least 3 months.`]},{heading:`Follow-up and return to work`,points:[`Review at 6 and 12 weeks then 3-6 monthly; phased return to work; refer for progression or red flags.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: breathlessness after COVID - red flags, sit-to-stand, X-ray, DLCO, CTPA, HRCT`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and time bands`,marks:2},{part:`Evaluation (history, examination, tests)`,marks:4},{part:`Management`,marks:3},{part:`Follow-up`,marks:1}],keywords:[`long COVID`,`WHO definition`,`sit-to-stand`,`DLCO`,`organising pneumonia`,`pacing`]},{id:`respiratory-post-covid-lung-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on post-COVID organising pneumonia and fibrosis.`,openingLines:[`After severe COVID pneumonia, residual lung change ranges from resolving ground glass to steroid-responsive organising pneumonia and fibrotic-like change.`,`Most fibrotic-like change is non-progressive and improves over 1-2 years.`],answer:[{heading:`Features`,points:[`Persistent breathlessness, crackles, reduced DLCO; HRCT shows consolidation and ground glass (OP) or reticulation with traction bronchiectasis.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`OP: prednisolone about 0.5 mg/kg/day tapered over 3 weeks after excluding infection.`,`Fibrosis: rehabilitation, oxygen if needed, no antifibrotic outside trials; ILD clinic if progressive.`]},{heading:`Follow-up`,points:[`PFT and HRCT at 6-12 months; screen for PE and pulmonary hypertension.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table comparing organising pneumonia and fibrotic-like change on HRCT and treatment`],markSplit:[{part:`Features and imaging`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment`,marks:2},{part:`Follow-up`,marks:1}],keywords:[`post-COVID organising pneumonia`,`post-COVID fibrosis`,`prednisolone`,`HRCT`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - COVID-19 and post-acute sequelae`,`World Health Organization. A clinical case definition of post COVID-19 condition by a Delphi consensus, 2021`,`NICE guideline NG188: COVID-19 rapid guideline - managing the long-term effects of COVID-19, updated 2024`,`National Academies of Sciences, Engineering, and Medicine. A Long COVID Definition: A Chronic, Systemic Disease State with Profound Consequences, 2024`,`MoHFW, Government of India. National Comprehensive Guidelines for Management of Post-COVID Sequelae, 2021`,`British Thoracic Society. Guidance on respiratory follow-up of patients with a clinico-radiological diagnosis of COVID-19 pneumonia, 2020`,`Myall KJ, et al. Persistent post-COVID-19 interstitial lung disease: an observational study of corticosteroid treatment. Ann Am Thorac Soc 2021`,`ICMR/MoHFW. Evidence-based advisory on screening, diagnosis and management of COVID-associated mucormycosis, 2021`]}),u.push({id:`respiratory-upper-airway`,title:`Upper airway obstruction in adults: stridor, vocal cord dysfunction, tracheal stenosis and foreign body`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`**Upper (central) airway obstruction** is narrowing of the pharynx, larynx, trachea or main bronchi; it presents with **stridor** (inspiratory if extrathoracic, expiratory if intrathoracic, **biphasic if fixed**), is shown by a **flattened flow-volume loop**, and its common adult causes are **post-intubation tracheal stenosis, inducible laryngeal obstruction (vocal cord dysfunction), foreign body, angioedema, infection and tumour** - many of which are misdiagnosed as asthma.`,keywords:[`central airway obstruction`,`stridor`,`monophonic wheeze`,`flow-volume loop`,`Empey index`,`tracheal stenosis`,`subglottic stenosis`,`Myer-Cotton`,`vocal cord dysfunction`,`inducible laryngeal obstruction`,`EILO`,`paradoxical vocal fold motion`,`foreign body aspiration`,`Heimlich manoeuvre`,`rigid bronchoscopy`,`angioedema`,`icatibant`,`heliox`,`cricothyroidotomy`,`supraglottitis`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Upper airway obstruction (UAO)** is narrowing anywhere from the nose and pharynx through the larynx to the trachea and main bronchi; **central airway obstruction** refers to the trachea and main bronchi. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Stridor** is a harsh, high-pitched, monophonic sound from **turbulent flow through a narrowed large airway**; in an adult it always needs an explanation and is a potential emergency. [Harrison 22e]`,`Breathlessness on exertion appears when the tracheal lumen falls to about **8 mm**, and **stridor at rest at about 5 mm** - so by the time stridor is heard at rest, the airway is critically narrow. [Murray and Nadel 7e]`,`**Post-intubation laryngotracheal stenosis** occurs at about **5 per million population per year** in the UK, and is rising after prolonged ventilation and tracheostomy in COVID-19 survivors. [Nouraei Laryngoscope 2007]`,`**Inducible laryngeal obstruction (ILO)** is found in a substantial minority of patients referred with difficult asthma and **coexists with asthma** in many - it is a common cause of apparent treatment-resistant asthma. [ERS/ELS/ACCP ILO 2017]`,`**Foreign-body aspiration** in adults is commonest in the **elderly, people with dementia, stroke, alcohol or sedative use, and poor dentition** - in India, **betel (areca) nut, dental fragments and scarf pins** held in the mouth are classic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Choking** causes thousands of deaths a year in the elderly worldwide; first-aid back blows and abdominal thrusts save most if started at once. [ERC 2021]`,`India still reports **diphtheria outbreaks** each year, so membranous obstruction remains a real cause of stridor in unimmunised adolescents and adults. [NCDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Acute**: foreign body, **anaphylaxis and angioedema** (allergic, ACE inhibitor, hereditary), **supraglottitis**, deep neck abscess and **Ludwig angina**, diphtheria, inhalational burns, post-extubation laryngeal oedema, and haemorrhage after neck surgery. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Subacute or chronic**: **post-intubation or post-tracheostomy stenosis**, laryngeal cancer, tracheal tumours (squamous cell, **adenoid cystic carcinoma**), retrosternal goitre, bilateral vocal cord palsy after thyroidectomy, and mediastinal masses. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Inflammatory**: **granulomatosis with polyangiitis (subglottic stenosis)**, relapsing polychondritis, sarcoidosis, **tuberculosis of the larynx or trachea** (common in India), rhinoscleroma, amyloidosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Idiopathic subglottic stenosis** affects almost only **women aged 30-50 years**. [Murray and Nadel 7e]`,`**Dynamic causes**: **inducible laryngeal obstruction**, tracheobronchomalacia and excessive dynamic airway collapse. [ERS/ELS/ACCP ILO 2017]`,`**Risk factors for post-intubation stenosis**: **cuff pressure over 30 cmH2O**, prolonged intubation (over 7-10 days), large tube, traumatic or repeated intubation, high tracheostomy (through the cricoid), infection, diabetes, obesity and reflux. [Murray and Nadel 7e]`,`**ILO triggers**: exercise, strong odours and irritants, reflux, postnasal drip, stress and anxiety; it is commoner in **young women and elite athletes**. [ERS/ELS/ACCP ILO 2017]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**Airway resistance rises with the fourth power of a falling radius** (Poiseuille) - halving the tracheal radius raises resistance 16-fold - and turbulent flow makes the work of breathing climb steeply. [Murray and Nadel 7e]`,`**Variable extrathoracic obstruction** (larynx, upper trachea): during inspiration the intraluminal pressure falls below atmospheric and the airway **collapses inward**, so obstruction and noise are **inspiratory**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Variable intrathoracic obstruction** (lower trachea, main bronchi): during forced expiration pleural pressure exceeds airway pressure and compresses the airway, so obstruction is **expiratory**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fixed obstruction** (rigid stenosis, circumferential tumour) does not change with pressure, limiting **both inspiration and expiration - biphasic stridor**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cuff injury**: tracheal mucosal capillary perfusion pressure is about **25-35 mmHg**; a cuff above about 30 cmH2O causes ischaemia, ulceration, chondritis and healing by **circumferential fibrosis** over 3-6 weeks. [Murray and Nadel 7e]`,`**Tracheostomy stenosis** is at the stoma - granulation and collapse of fractured cartilage give an **A-frame deformity**. [Murray and Nadel 7e]`,`**ILO** is inappropriate, transient **adduction of the vocal folds or supraglottic structures during inspiration**, from laryngeal hyperresponsiveness after irritation, reflux or infection, and learned patterns. [ERS/ELS/ACCP ILO 2017]`,`**Bradykinin-mediated angioedema** (ACE inhibitor, C1-inhibitor deficiency) increases vascular permeability without mast cells, so there is **no urticaria and no response to adrenaline, antihistamine or steroid**. [WAO/EAACI HAE 2021]`,`In adults, **aspirated objects go to the right** because the **right main bronchus is wider, shorter and more vertical**; a ball-valve object causes air trapping, a complete one causes collapse. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and grading`,points:[`**By site and behaviour**: variable extrathoracic (inspiratory stridor), variable intrathoracic (expiratory), and **fixed (biphasic)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Myer-Cotton grading of subglottic stenosis** (lumen obstruction): **Grade I 0-50%, Grade II 51-70%, Grade III 71-99%, Grade IV no detectable lumen**. [Myer Ann Otol 1994]`,`**Stenosis morphology**: **simple** (web-like, short, under 1 cm, no cartilage loss - suited to bronchoscopic treatment) versus **complex** (over 1 cm, cartilage damage or malacia - needs surgery). [Murray and Nadel 7e]`,`**ILO subtypes**: glottic (vocal fold adduction) and **supraglottic** (arytenoid prolapse, commoner in exercise-induced ILO); **exercise-induced (EILO)** versus irritant-induced. [ERS/ELS/ACCP ILO 2017]`,`**Angioedema types**: histaminergic (allergic, with urticaria), **bradykinin-mediated** (ACE inhibitor, hereditary C1-INH deficiency type 1 low level, type 2 dysfunctional), and idiopathic. [WAO/EAACI HAE 2021]`,`**Choking severity**: **mild (effective cough, can speak)** versus **severe (ineffective cough, cannot speak or breathe, cyanosis)**. [ERC 2021]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Stridor or noisy breathing**, breathlessness on exertion, and an inability to clear secretions; symptoms worsen when lying down or with exercise. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Post-intubation stenosis** presents **weeks to months after extubation** (classically 3-6 weeks) with exertional breathlessness and noisy breathing - often labelled **late-onset asthma** that does not respond to inhalers. [Murray and Nadel 7e]`,`**ILO**: sudden **throat tightness, difficulty breathing in**, inspiratory noise, voice change and choking feeling; starts and stops abruptly, usually at peak exercise or after an odour, and is not helped by salbutamol. [ERS/ELS/ACCP ILO 2017]`,`**Foreign body**: a witnessed **choking episode** followed by cough, then sometimes a silent interval of days or weeks before recurrent pneumonia or wheeze in the same lobe. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Angioedema**: swelling of lips, tongue and uvula; ask about **ACE inhibitors (even after years of use)**, NSAIDs, a family history and urticaria. [WAO/EAACI HAE 2021]`,`**Supraglottitis**: severe sore throat out of proportion to the throat findings, **painful swallowing, drooling, muffled (hot-potato) voice** and fever. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chronic progressive** stridor with **hoarseness, dysphagia, weight loss and smoking** suggests laryngeal or tracheal cancer; hoarseness after thyroid surgery suggests recurrent laryngeal nerve injury. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Stridor versus wheeze**: stridor is loudest over the neck, monophonic, mostly inspiratory; wheeze is polyphonic, expiratory and loudest over the chest. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Listen over the trachea**: noise louder over the neck than the chest points to the upper airway. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Severity signs**: tripod position, **suprasternal and intercostal recession**, use of accessory muscles, inability to speak, drooling, cyanosis and agitation - falling noise with falling consciousness means impending arrest. [ERC 2021]`,`**Unilateral monophonic wheeze or reduced breath sounds** with hyperresonance suggests a ball-valve foreign body or endobronchial tumour. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for a **tracheostomy scar**, thyroid enlargement or retrosternal goitre (**Pemberton sign** - facial plethora on raising arms), neck mass and lymph nodes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Saddle-nose, ear cartilage inflammation** (relapsing polychondritis) and nasal crusting (GPA) point to inflammatory subglottic stenosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Grey adherent pseudomembrane** on tonsils with **bull neck** suggests diphtheria. [Harrison 22e]`,`**SpO2 is normal** in most ILO attacks and in early stenosis - normal oximetry does not mean the airway is safe. [ERS/ELS/ACCP ILO 2017]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Asthma versus central airway obstruction**: asthma has polyphonic expiratory wheeze, variable PEF and bronchodilator response; central obstruction has stridor, **flattened flow-volume loop** and **Empey index over 10**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ILO versus asthma**: ILO is inspiratory, felt in the throat, abrupt, with normal SpO2 and no response to bronchodilators; the two often coexist. [ERS/ELS/ACCP ILO 2017]`,`**Allergic versus bradykinin angioedema**: urticaria, itch and response to adrenaline in allergic; no urticaria, slower onset over hours and **ACE inhibitor or family history** in bradykinin. [WAO/EAACI HAE 2021]`,`**Supraglottitis versus pharyngitis**: pain and odynophagia out of proportion to a normal-looking pharynx means supraglottitis until proved otherwise. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tracheobronchomalacia** gives expiratory collapse over 50% on dynamic CT and a barking cough. [Murray and Nadel 7e]`,`**Dysfunctional breathing** gives sighing, tingling and breathlessness without stridor. [BTS 2016]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**In an emergency the airway comes before tests** - never send a patient with stridor alone to radiology. [DAS 2015]`,`**Flow-volume loop**: **flattened inspiratory limb** in variable extrathoracic obstruction, **flattened expiratory limb** in variable intrathoracic, and **both limbs flattened (box shape)** in fixed obstruction. [ERS/ATS Interpretation 2022]`,`**Empey index (FEV1 in mL divided by PEF in L/min) over 10** suggests upper airway obstruction; **FEV1/PEF ratio** rises because PEF falls more than FEV1. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ratio of mid-expiratory to mid-inspiratory flow (FEF50/FIF50)**: about 1 in fixed obstruction, **over 1 (often over 2) in variable extrathoracic**, low in variable intrathoracic. [ERS/ATS Interpretation 2022]`,`**Flexible nasendoscopy or laryngoscopy** shows the larynx and vocal cords; in ILO, **continuous laryngoscopy during exercise (CLE test)** or provocation is the gold standard - often showing anterior cord adduction with a **posterior diamond-shaped chink**. [ERS/ELS/ACCP ILO 2017]`,`**CT neck and thorax with multiplanar and 3D reconstruction (virtual bronchoscopy)** measures stenosis length and diameter; **dynamic expiratory CT** for malacia. [Murray and Nadel 7e]`,`**Chest X-ray in foreign body**: most organic objects are radiolucent - look for **unilateral hyperinflation, mediastinal shift away on expiration films, collapse or consolidation**; a normal film does not exclude it. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lateral neck X-ray** in supraglottitis: swollen epiglottis (**thumb sign**); **bronchoscopy** is diagnostic and therapeutic for foreign body and stenosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Angioedema without urticaria**: check **C4 (low in hereditary angioedema)**, C1-inhibitor level and function; tryptase within 1-2 hours if anaphylaxis is suspected. [WAO/EAACI HAE 2021]`,`Serology - **ANCA (GPA)** - and biopsy for TB, tumour or amyloid in unexplained stenosis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`Any **new stridor in an adult** needs same-day ENT or respiratory assessment - it is not managed in the OPD. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Asthma that does not respond**, especially after a recent ICU stay or intubation, should prompt a **flow-volume loop** before escalating steroids. [GINA 2025]`,`**ILO treatment**: explanation and reassurance, **speech and language therapy** (laryngeal control and rescue breathing - relaxed throat, sniff in, purse-lip out), treat **rhinitis and reflux**, and psychological support; stop unneeded asthma drugs. [ERS/ELS/ACCP ILO 2017]`,`**Reflux** contributing to ILO or stenosis: lifestyle measures and a trial of **omeprazole 20-40 mg daily for 8 weeks**. [ERS/ELS/ACCP ILO 2017]`,`**ACE-inhibitor angioedema**: **stop the ACE inhibitor permanently**; an ARB carries a small cross-risk - choose another class if possible; record the allergy prominently. [WAO/EAACI HAE 2021]`,`**Hereditary angioedema**: specialist plan with on-demand icatibant or C1-INH, and prophylaxis before dental or surgical procedures. [WAO/EAACI HAE 2021]`,`**Choking first aid (conscious adult)**: if cough is effective, encourage coughing; if ineffective, give **up to 5 back blows, then up to 5 abdominal thrusts (Heimlich)**, alternating; **chest thrusts in pregnancy or obesity**. [ERC 2021]`,`If the choking victim becomes **unconscious, start CPR** with chest compressions - no blind finger sweeps; remove only a visible object. [ERC 2021]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Keep the patient sitting up**, do not lie them flat, give high-flow oxygen, call **anaesthesia and ENT early**, and prepare for a difficult airway with front-of-neck access ready. [DAS 2015]`,`**Nebulised adrenaline** (1 mg/mL, **5 mL nebulised**) temporarily reduces mucosal oedema in laryngeal swelling and post-extubation stridor. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dexamethasone 8-10 mg IV** reduces inflammatory oedema over hours; **heliox (helium 70-80% with oxygen)** lowers turbulent flow resistance as a bridge to a definitive airway. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anaphylaxis**: **adrenaline 0.5 mg IM (0.5 mL of 1 mg/mL)** in the anterolateral thigh, repeat after 5 minutes; IV fluids; antihistamines and steroids are not first-line. [Resus Council UK 2021]`,`**Bradykinin angioedema**: **icatibant 30 mg SC** or **C1-INH concentrate 20 units/kg IV**; where these are unavailable (common in India), **fresh frozen plasma 2 units** is used. [WAO/EAACI HAE 2021]`,`**Can't intubate, can't oxygenate (CICO)**: **scalpel-bougie-tube cricothyroidotomy** through the cricothyroid membrane. [DAS 2015]`,`**Post-extubation stridor prevention**: in high-risk patients with a **failed cuff-leak test**, give **methylprednisolone 20 mg IV every 4 hours, starting 12 hours before extubation**. [SCCM/ATS Extubation 2017]`,`**Foreign body in the airway**: **flexible bronchoscopy** removes most adult foreign bodies (forceps, baskets, cryoprobe); **rigid bronchoscopy** under general anaesthesia for large, sharp or impacted objects, failed flexible attempt, and in children. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Supraglottitis**: admit to a monitored area, IV **ceftriaxone 2 g daily** (plus metronidazole if abscess), steroids, and airway team on standby; examine with flexible nasendoscopy only where an airway can be secured. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tracheal stenosis**: **bronchoscopic dilatation** (balloon or rigid), **radial laser or electrocautery incisions**, topical **mitomycin C**, and **silicone stents** for simple stenosis; **tracheal resection with end-to-end anastomosis** is definitive for complex stenosis. [Murray and Nadel 7e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: airway oedema makes intubation harder; use **chest thrusts, not abdominal thrusts**, for choking; avoid ACE inhibitors anyway. [ERC 2021]`,`**Elderly**: silent aspiration of food or dentures presents as recurrent pneumonia; check dentures after any choking. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Children**: foreign body peaks at 1-3 years; infants get **5 back blows and 5 chest thrusts**, never abdominal thrusts; croup and epiglottitis are paediatric differentials. [ERC 2021]`,`**Athletes**: exercise-induced ILO is misread as exercise-induced asthma - laryngoscopy during exercise settles it. [ERS/ELS/ACCP ILO 2017]`,`**After COVID ventilation or tracheostomy**: any breathlessness or noisy breathing in the following months is tracheal stenosis until proved otherwise. [BTS COVID Follow-up 2020]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: Heimlich and positioning, nebulised adrenaline, steroids, oxygen and early transfer; **FFP** for bradykinin angioedema; **anti-diphtheritic serum** with penicillin or erythromycin for diphtheria. [NCDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Asphyxia and hypoxic brain injury** or death - the main danger of acute obstruction. [ERC 2021]`,`**Negative-pressure pulmonary oedema** after forceful inspiration against an obstructed airway (laryngospasm, choking), usually resolving in 24-48 hours. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Retained foreign body**: post-obstructive pneumonia, lung abscess, **bronchiectasis** and haemoptysis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tracheal stenosis recurs** after dilatation in many patients, needing repeated procedures; surgery has high cure rates in suitable patients. [Murray and Nadel 7e]`,`**ILO** has a good outlook with therapy; harm comes from **misdiagnosis** - repeated steroids, unnecessary intubation and tracheostomy. [ERS/ELS/ACCP ILO 2017]`,`**Hereditary angioedema** laryngeal attacks were historically fatal in up to a third before modern therapy. [WAO/EAACI HAE 2021]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and prevention`,points:[`**Refer urgently** any adult with stridor, suspected foreign body, angioedema of the tongue or larynx, or suspected supraglottitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Refer to **ENT or interventional pulmonology** for suspected stenosis, and to **speech and language therapy** for confirmed ILO. [ERS/ELS/ACCP ILO 2017]`,`**Follow-up of stenosis**: symptoms, flow-volume loop and bronchoscopy at intervals after treatment; early review for any recurrence of noisy breathing. [Murray and Nadel 7e]`,`**Prevent post-intubation stenosis**: keep **cuff pressure 20-30 cmH2O** checked with a manometer, smallest suitable tube, early extubation and correctly placed tracheostomy (2nd-3rd rings, not the cricoid). [Murray and Nadel 7e]`,`**Prevent choking**: soft diet and supervised eating after stroke, dentures checked, no pins or nuts in the mouth, swallow assessment. [ERC 2021]`,`**Diphtheria prevention**: complete **Td** in adolescents and adults and maintain **DPT/pentavalent** coverage under the Universal Immunization Programme. [MoHFW UIP 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ERS/ELS/ACCP 2017 statement** (still current): the term **inducible laryngeal obstruction** replaces vocal cord dysfunction; laryngoscopy during symptoms is the diagnostic standard. [ERS/ELS/ACCP ILO 2017]`,`**ERC 2021 and AHA 2020/2025 first-aid guidance**: back blows and abdominal thrusts for severe choking in adults; chest thrusts in pregnancy and obesity; CPR if unresponsive. [ERC 2021]`,`**WAO/EAACI hereditary angioedema guideline 2021 (2025 revision)**: on-demand treatment for every attack with C1-INH, icatibant or ecallantide; long-term prophylaxis with lanadelumab, berotralstat or C1-INH. [WAO/EAACI HAE 2021]`,`**Resuscitation Council UK anaphylaxis 2021**: IM adrenaline is the only first-line drug; antihistamines and steroids are no longer part of the initial emergency treatment. [Resus Council UK 2021]`,`**GINA 2025** lists ILO among conditions to exclude in difficult-to-treat asthma before escalation to biologics. [GINA 2025]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Viva: stridor localisation** - inspiratory extrathoracic, expiratory intrathoracic, biphasic fixed. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Viva: draw the three flow-volume loops** - inspiratory limb flat, expiratory limb flat, both flat. [ERS/ATS Interpretation 2022]`,`**Classic MCQ**: Empey index over 10 suggests upper airway obstruction. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Classic MCQ**: aspirated foreign body in an adult goes to the right main bronchus or bronchus intermedius. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake**: treating ACE-inhibitor angioedema with repeated adrenaline and steroids and then restarting the ACE inhibitor. [WAO/EAACI HAE 2021]`,`**Common mistake**: labelling post-ICU breathlessness as asthma without a flow-volume loop. [Murray and Nadel 7e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs used in upper airway obstruction`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Adrenaline IM (anaphylaxis)`,`0.5 mg IM (0.5 mL of 1 mg/mL) anterolateral thigh`,`Repeat every 5 minutes if needed`,`Tachycardia, arrhythmia; never give 1 mg/mL IV`],[`Nebulised adrenaline`,`5 mL of 1 mg/mL nebulised`,`Repeat as needed; effect lasts 1-2 hours`,`Tachycardia; rebound swelling - observe`],[`Dexamethasone`,`8-10 mg IV, then 4 mg 6-8 hourly`,`24-72 hours`,`Hyperglycaemia; slow onset over hours`],[`Methylprednisolone (pre-extubation)`,`20 mg IV every 4 hours`,`Start 12 hours before planned extubation`,`Hyperglycaemia; only if failed cuff-leak test`],[`Heliox (helium 70-80%, oxygen 20-30%)`,`Via tight-fitting non-rebreathing mask`,`Bridge to definitive airway`,`Limits FiO2; not for hypoxaemic patients`],[`Icatibant (bradykinin B2 antagonist)`,`30 mg SC`,`Repeat after 6 hours if needed, max 3 doses in 24 hours`,`Injection-site pain; for HAE and ACE-inhibitor angioedema`],[`C1-inhibitor concentrate`,`20 units/kg IV`,`Single dose per attack`,`Rare thrombosis, hypersensitivity`],[`Fresh frozen plasma`,`2 units IV`,`When icatibant or C1-INH unavailable`,`May transiently worsen swelling; transfusion risks`],[`Ceftriaxone (supraglottitis)`,`2 g IV once daily`,`7-10 days total with oral switch`,`Allergy; biliary sludge`],[`Omeprazole (reflux in ILO)`,`20-40 mg oral once daily before breakfast`,`8-week trial`,`Headache; long-term hypomagnesaemia`]]},{heading:`Flow-volume loop patterns in airway obstruction`,columns:[`Pattern`,`Site`,`Loop shape`,`Examples`],rows:[[`Variable extrathoracic`,`Larynx, upper trachea above the thoracic inlet`,`Flattened inspiratory limb; FEF50/FIF50 over 1`,`Vocal cord palsy, ILO, laryngeal tumour, OSA-prone pharynx`],[`Variable intrathoracic`,`Lower trachea, main bronchi`,`Flattened expiratory limb; FEF50/FIF50 low`,`Tracheomalacia, intrathoracic tumour`],[`Fixed`,`Anywhere, rigid narrowing`,`Both limbs flattened - box shape; ratio about 1`,`Post-intubation stenosis, goitre encasing trachea`],[`Diffuse small-airway obstruction`,`Small airways`,`Concave (scooped) expiratory limb, normal inspiration`,`Asthma, COPD`]]},{heading:`Stridor and wheeze - differential diagnosis`,columns:[`Condition`,`Onset`,`Key discriminating feature`,`Confirming test`],rows:[[`Asthma`,`Variable, nocturnal`,`Polyphonic expiratory wheeze, bronchodilator response`,`Spirometry with reversibility, PEF variability`],[`Inducible laryngeal obstruction`,`Abrupt, at exercise or odour`,`Throat tightness, inspiratory noise, normal SpO2`,`Laryngoscopy during symptoms or exercise`],[`Post-intubation tracheal stenosis`,`Weeks to months after extubation`,`Biphasic stridor, no bronchodilator response`,`Flow-volume loop, CT, bronchoscopy`],[`Foreign body`,`After choking, may be delayed`,`Unilateral monophonic wheeze, recurrent lobar pneumonia`,`Expiratory X-ray, CT, bronchoscopy`],[`Allergic angioedema / anaphylaxis`,`Minutes`,`Urticaria, hypotension, responds to adrenaline`,`Clinical; tryptase`],[`Bradykinin angioedema`,`Hours`,`No urticaria, ACE inhibitor or family history`,`C4, C1-INH level and function`],[`Supraglottitis`,`Hours`,`Severe odynophagia, drooling, muffled voice`,`Nasendoscopy, lateral neck X-ray thumb sign`],[`Laryngeal or tracheal tumour`,`Months`,`Hoarseness, weight loss, smoker`,`Laryngoscopy, CT, biopsy`]]},{heading:`Grading of laryngotracheal stenosis and treatment fit`,columns:[`Myer-Cotton grade`,`Lumen obstruction`,`Usual symptoms`,`Usual approach`],rows:[[`Grade I`,`0-50%`,`Often none or exertional`,`Observe or endoscopic dilatation if symptomatic`],[`Grade II`,`51-70%`,`Exertional breathlessness, noisy breathing`,`Endoscopic dilatation, radial incisions, mitomycin C`],[`Grade III`,`71-99%`,`Stridor at rest`,`Endoscopic treatment as bridge; resection if complex`],[`Grade IV`,`No detectable lumen`,`Tracheostomy-dependent`,`Laryngotracheal resection and reconstruction`]]},{heading:`Choking first aid by age`,columns:[`Group`,`Effective cough`,`Ineffective cough, conscious`,`Unconscious`],rows:[[`Adult`,`Encourage cough`,`5 back blows then 5 abdominal thrusts, repeat`,`CPR; look in mouth, no blind sweep`],[`Pregnant or obese adult`,`Encourage cough`,`5 back blows then 5 chest thrusts`,`CPR with left uterine displacement in pregnancy`],[`Child over 1 year`,`Encourage cough`,`5 back blows then 5 abdominal thrusts`,`5 rescue breaths, then CPR`],[`Infant`,`Encourage cough`,`5 back blows then 5 chest thrusts (no abdominal thrusts)`,`5 rescue breaths, then CPR`]]}],redFlags:[`Stridor at rest, drooling, inability to swallow saliva or speak - critical airway narrowing.`,`Tongue, floor-of-mouth or laryngeal swelling - angioedema threatening the airway.`,`Choking with ineffective cough, cyanosis or loss of consciousness.`,`Severe sore throat with muffled voice and normal-looking pharynx - supraglottitis.`,`Breathlessness or noisy breathing in the months after intubation or tracheostomy.`,`Falling noise, falling consciousness and silent chest in a patient with stridor - impending arrest.`,`Neck swelling and stridor after thyroid or neck surgery - haematoma; open the wound.`],pearls:[`Inspiratory stridor = extrathoracic; expiratory = intrathoracic; biphasic = fixed.`,`Flow-volume loop: flat top (inspiration) extrathoracic, flat bottom (expiration) intrathoracic, box both = fixed.`,`Empey index (FEV1 mL / PEF L/min) over 10 suggests upper airway obstruction.`,`Stridor at rest means a lumen of about 5 mm - the airway is critical.`,`ILO: throat, inspiration, abrupt, normal SpO2, no salbutamol response - laryngoscopy during symptoms.`,`No urticaria and no response to adrenaline = bradykinin angioedema - think ACE inhibitor; icatibant or FFP.`,`Choking adult: 5 back blows, 5 abdominal thrusts; infant: 5 back blows, 5 chest thrusts.`,`Cuff pressure 20-30 cmH2O prevents post-intubation stenosis.`,`Adult foreign bodies go right - wider, shorter, more vertical right main bronchus.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-upper-airway-q1`,stem:`A 45-year-old man ventilated for 3 weeks with COVID-19 presents 2 months after extubation with exertional breathlessness and noisy breathing in both inspiration and expiration. Inhalers have not helped. What flow-volume loop is expected?`,options:[`Flattening of both inspiratory and expiratory limbs`,`Scooped (concave) expiratory limb with normal inspiration`,`Flattening of the inspiratory limb only`,`Flattening of the expiratory limb only`],answer:0,explanation:`Post-intubation tracheal stenosis is a fixed obstruction, limiting flow in both phases and giving a box-shaped loop with biphasic stridor. A scooped expiratory limb is diffuse small-airway obstruction (asthma, COPD). Isolated inspiratory flattening indicates variable extrathoracic obstruction and isolated expiratory flattening variable intrathoracic obstruction.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-upper-airway-q2`,stem:`A 24-year-old athlete has recurrent episodes of throat tightness and noisy breathing in at peak exercise, settling within minutes of stopping. SpO2 is normal during attacks and salbutamol does not help. What is the diagnostic investigation of choice?`,options:[`Methacholine challenge test`,`Continuous laryngoscopy during exercise`,`High-resolution CT of the chest`,`Fractional exhaled nitric oxide`],answer:1,explanation:`Exercise-induced inducible laryngeal obstruction is confirmed by seeing inappropriate laryngeal closure on continuous laryngoscopy during exercise. Methacholine challenge and FeNO test for asthma and can mislead because the two coexist; HRCT does not show a dynamic laryngeal problem.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-upper-airway-q3`,stem:`A 58-year-old woman on ramipril for 4 years develops progressive swelling of the tongue and lips over 3 hours without urticaria or itch. IM adrenaline and IV chlorpheniramine give no improvement. Which treatment targets the mechanism?`,options:[`Repeat IM adrenaline every 5 minutes`,`IV hydrocortisone 200 mg`,`Switch ramipril to losartan and observe`,`Icatibant 30 mg subcutaneously`],answer:3,explanation:`ACE-inhibitor angioedema is bradykinin-mediated, so it does not respond to adrenaline, antihistamines or steroids; the bradykinin B2-receptor antagonist icatibant (or C1-INH concentrate, or fresh frozen plasma where unavailable) targets the mechanism. The ACE inhibitor must be stopped permanently, and an ARB carries a cross-risk, so simply switching and observing an evolving airway is unsafe.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`respiratory-upper-airway-q4`,stem:`A 70-year-old man chokes on a piece of meat at dinner. He cannot speak or cough and is clutching his throat but is conscious. What is the immediate first-aid sequence?`,options:[`Blind finger sweep of the mouth, then rescue breaths`,`Lie him flat and start chest compressions at once`,`Up to 5 back blows, then up to 5 abdominal thrusts, repeated`,`Encourage him to keep coughing and wait for an ambulance`],answer:2,explanation:`Severe choking in a conscious adult (ineffective cough, cannot speak) is treated with up to 5 back blows followed by up to 5 abdominal thrusts, alternating until relieved. Encouraging cough is for mild obstruction with an effective cough. Chest compressions start only if he becomes unresponsive, and blind finger sweeps can push the object further in.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-upper-airway-q5`,stem:`A patient has FEV1 1.8 L and PEF 120 L/min. What does the Empey index suggest?`,options:[`Normal airway`,`Upper airway obstruction`,`Restrictive lung disease`,`Diffuse small-airway obstruction`],answer:1,explanation:`The Empey index is FEV1 in mL divided by PEF in L/min: 1800/120 = 15, which is over 10 and suggests upper airway obstruction, because a central lesion limits peak flow far more than FEV1. Small-airway disease lowers FEV1 and PEF more evenly, and restriction lowers volumes rather than disproportionately cutting peak flow.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-upper-airway-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Discuss the causes, clinical localisation, investigation and management of stridor in an adult, with a note on post-intubation tracheal stenosis.`,openingLines:[`Stridor is a harsh, high-pitched monophonic sound produced by turbulent airflow through a narrowed larynx, trachea or main bronchus; in an adult it is always pathological and potentially life-threatening.`,`Its timing localises the lesion - inspiratory extrathoracic, expiratory intrathoracic, biphasic fixed - and the flow-volume loop confirms it.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes`,points:[`Acute: foreign body, anaphylaxis and angioedema, supraglottitis, deep neck infection, diphtheria, burns, post-extubation oedema.`,`Chronic: post-intubation or tracheostomy stenosis, laryngeal and tracheal tumours, goitre, vocal cord palsy, GPA, relapsing polychondritis, TB; dynamic - ILO, malacia.`]},{heading:`Localisation and pathophysiology`,points:[`Variable extrathoracic: inspiratory collapse; variable intrathoracic: expiratory compression; fixed: both.`,`Resistance rises with the fourth power of falling radius; rest stridor at about 5 mm lumen.`]},{heading:`Clinical assessment`,points:[`Severity: speech, drooling, recession, cyanosis, consciousness; history of intubation, ACE inhibitor, choking, smoking, surgery.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Airway first; then flow-volume loop, Empey index over 10, nasendoscopy, CT with 3D reconstruction, bronchoscopy; C4 and C1-INH for angioedema.`]},{heading:`Emergency management`,points:[`Sit up, oxygen, call anaesthesia and ENT; nebulised adrenaline, dexamethasone, heliox; IM adrenaline for anaphylaxis; icatibant or FFP for bradykinin angioedema; CICO - scalpel cricothyroidotomy.`]},{heading:`Post-intubation tracheal stenosis`,points:[`Cuff pressure over 30 cmH2O causes ischaemia and circumferential fibrosis; presents 3-6 weeks or later after extubation, often misdiagnosed as asthma.`,`Myer-Cotton grades I-IV; simple - dilatation, radial incisions, mitomycin C, stent; complex - tracheal resection and anastomosis.`,`Prevention: cuff pressure 20-30 cmH2O, early extubation, correct tracheostomy level.`]}],mustDraw:[`Three flow-volume loops: variable extrathoracic, variable intrathoracic and fixed obstruction`,`Flow chart of emergency stridor management`],markSplit:[{part:`Causes`,marks:2},{part:`Localisation and flow-volume loops`,marks:2},{part:`Investigation`,marks:2},{part:`Management`,marks:2},{part:`Post-intubation stenosis`,marks:2}],keywords:[`stridor`,`flow-volume loop`,`tracheal stenosis`,`Myer-Cotton`,`heliox`,`cricothyroidotomy`]},{id:`respiratory-upper-airway-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Differentiate between inducible laryngeal obstruction (vocal cord dysfunction) and asthma.`,openingLines:[`Inducible laryngeal obstruction is inappropriate, transient laryngeal closure during inspiration triggered by exercise or irritants (ERS/ELS/ACCP 2017).`,`It mimics and often coexists with asthma, and is a leading cause of apparently treatment-resistant asthma.`],answer:[{heading:`Clinical differences`,points:[`ILO: throat tightness, inspiratory noise, abrupt onset and offset, voice change; asthma: chest tightness, expiratory wheeze, gradual, nocturnal.`,`ILO: normal SpO2, no response to bronchodilator; asthma: may desaturate, responds to SABA and ICS.`]},{heading:`Investigation differences`,points:[`ILO: truncated inspiratory flow-volume loop during symptoms; laryngoscopy during symptoms shows adduction with posterior chink.`,`Asthma: bronchodilator reversibility over 12% and 200 mL, PEF variability, raised FeNO or eosinophils.`]},{heading:`Treatment differences`,points:[`ILO: speech and language therapy breathing techniques, treat reflux and rhinitis, psychology; avoid escalating steroids.`,`Asthma: ICS-formoterol per GINA.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column comparison table of ILO versus asthma`],markSplit:[{part:`Clinical`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment`,marks:1}],keywords:[`inducible laryngeal obstruction`,`vocal cord dysfunction`,`asthma`,`laryngoscopy`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Disorders of the upper airway and central airway obstruction`,`Murray and Nadel's Textbook of Respiratory Medicine, 7th edition, 2022 - Upper airway disorders and tracheal stenosis`,`Halvorsen T, et al. Inducible laryngeal obstruction: an official joint ERS and ELS statement. Eur Respir J 2017`,`European Resuscitation Council Guidelines 2021: First Aid and Basic Life Support (foreign body airway obstruction)`,`Maurer M, et al. The international WAO/EAACI guideline for the management of hereditary angioedema, 2021 revision and update`,`Frerk C, et al. Difficult Airway Society 2015 guidelines for management of unanticipated difficult intubation in adults. Br J Anaesth 2015`,`Resuscitation Council UK. Emergency treatment of anaphylaxis: guidelines for healthcare providers, 2021`,`Stanojevic S, et al. ERS/ATS technical standard on interpretive strategies for routine lung function tests, 2022`]}),u.push({id:`respiratory-end-stage-lung-palliation`,title:`End-stage lung disease: palliative care, breathlessness management and transplant referral`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`**End-stage lung disease** (advanced COPD, fibrotic ILD, bronchiectasis, CF, pulmonary hypertension, post-TB lung disease) brings **chronic breathlessness syndrome** - breathlessness persisting despite optimal treatment of the cause and causing disability - which is managed by **needs-based palliative care alongside disease treatment**: **handheld fan, breathing techniques and rehabilitation, oxygen only for hypoxaemia, an individualised trial of low-dose opioid**, **advance care planning**, and timely **lung transplant referral by ISHLT 2021 criteria** in eligible patients.`,keywords:[`chronic breathlessness syndrome`,`total dyspnoea`,`palliative care`,`advance care planning`,`advance directive`,`living will`,`low-dose morphine`,`handheld fan`,`Breathing Thinking Functioning`,`SPICT`,`surprise question`,`BODE index`,`ISHLT 2021`,`lung transplant`,`CLAD`,`NPPC`,`NDPS 2014`,`end-of-life care`,`syringe driver`,`death rattle`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**End-stage (advanced) lung disease** is chronic lung disease with severe, irreversible impairment, frequent exacerbations or hypoxaemia, where function and survival are limited despite optimal treatment. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chronic breathlessness syndrome** is breathlessness that **persists despite optimal treatment of the underlying disease and results in disability** - it names the symptom as a treatment target in its own right. [Johnson Eur Respir J 2017]`,`**Palliative care** (WHO) improves quality of life of patients and families facing life-threatening illness through early identification, assessment and treatment of pain and other physical, psychosocial and spiritual problems - it is **based on need, not prognosis**, and runs alongside active treatment. [WHO 2020]`,`**Chronic respiratory diseases caused about 11% of all deaths in India in 2016**, COPD being the major part, and India carries a disproportionate share of global COPD deaths. [ICMR India State-Level Disease Burden 2018]`,`Only a small minority (estimated **1-4%**) of Indians who need palliative care receive it, and **access to oral morphine** has historically been among the lowest in the world. [Lancet Commission on Palliative Care 2018]`,`**Breathlessness is the commonest distressing symptom in advanced COPD and ILD**, reported by over 90%, yet patients with non-malignant lung disease are referred to palliative care far less and later than those with lung cancer. [Harrison 22e]`,`Indian end-stage lung disease includes a large burden of **post-tuberculosis lung disease** and **biomass-related COPD in women**, both under-recognised. [ICMR 2023]`]},{heading:`Diseases and risk factors leading to end-stage lung disease`,points:[`**COPD**: tobacco smoking, **biomass smoke**, outdoor air pollution, occupational dust, and previous TB. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Fibrotic ILD**: idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (median survival **3-5 years** untreated), progressive pulmonary fibrosis in CTD-ILD, hypersensitivity pneumonitis and pneumoconioses. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT IPF 2022]`,`**Bronchiectasis and post-TB lung disease** with chronic infection, haemoptysis and hypoxaemia. [ERS Bronchiectasis 2017]`,`**Cystic fibrosis** - less common in India but under-diagnosed; CFTR modulators are changing its course. [ECFS 2023]`,`**Pulmonary arterial hypertension** and chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension - see the Pulmonary hypertension topic in cardiovascular. [ESC/ERS PH 2022]`,`**Neuromuscular and chest-wall disease** with ventilatory failure (see that topic), and **obesity hypoventilation**. [BTS NIV 2016]`,`**Markers of a poor outlook**: frequent hospital admissions, **hypercapnic respiratory failure needing NIV**, long-term oxygen, cachexia (BMI under 21), cor pulmonale, and dependence in daily activities. [GSF PIG 2023]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology of breathlessness`,points:[`Breathlessness arises from a **mismatch between the drive to breathe (from the brainstem) and the actual ventilation achieved** - the brain compares the motor command with feedback from lung, chest-wall and muscle receptors (neuromechanical dissociation). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Increased drive**: hypoxaemia, hypercapnia, acidosis, J-receptor stimulation in interstitial disease and pulmonary vascular receptors raise the central respiratory drive. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Increased load**: hyperinflation in COPD places the diaphragm at a mechanical disadvantage, and stiff lungs in ILD raise elastic work - the muscles work harder for less volume. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dynamic hyperinflation** on exertion in COPD (end-expiratory lung volume rises because there is no time to exhale) is the main cause of exertional breathlessness. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Perception is processed in the limbic system and insula**, so anxiety, fear, past experience and attention amplify it - the basis of **total dyspnoea** (physical, psychological, social, spiritual). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Breathlessness-anxiety-deconditioning spiral**: fear of breathlessness leads to inactivity, muscle deconditioning, more breathlessness at lower loads, and social isolation. [Booth Cambridge BTF 2019]`,`**How treatments work**: a **fan on the face cools trigeminal (V2) receptors** and reduces perceived breathlessness; opioids reduce central drive and the perception of air hunger; rehabilitation lowers ventilatory demand for a given workload. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Oxygen helps only when breathlessness is linked to hypoxaemia** - in non-hypoxaemic patients, oxygen was no better than room air delivered by nasal cannula in a large RCT. [Abernethy Lancet 2010]`]},{heading:`Recognising the palliative phase - tools and staging`,points:[`**Surprise question**: would you be surprised if this patient died in the next 12 months? A 'no' triggers a needs assessment and advance care planning. [GSF PIG 2023]`,`**SPICT (Supportive and Palliative Care Indicators Tool)**: general indicators (unplanned admissions, declining function, weight loss) plus disease-specific respiratory indicators (breathless at rest or minimal exertion, LTOT, NIV for respiratory failure). [SPICT 2022]`,`**GSF prognostic indicators for COPD**: FEV1 under 30% predicted, **3 or more admissions a year**, mMRC grade 4, LTOT, cor pulmonale, recurrent NIV, cachexia. [GSF PIG 2023]`,`**BODE index** (BMI, Obstruction FEV1, Dyspnoea mMRC, Exercise 6MWD; 0-10): a score of **7-10 carries about 80% 4-year mortality** (roughly 20% survival). [Celli NEJM 2004]`,`**ILD prognosis**: **GAP index** (Gender, Age, Physiology - FVC and DLCO) stages I-III; an annual FVC decline of 10% or more or need for oxygen marks poor survival. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT IPF 2022]`,`**Breathlessness scales**: **mMRC 0-4** for daily impact, **modified Borg 0-10** or a numerical rating scale for intensity, and Dyspnoea-12 for multidimensional assessment. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Performance status**: Karnofsky or Australia-modified Karnofsky (AKPS) and ECOG guide prognosis and referral. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Breathlessness**: at rest or on minimal exertion (talking, dressing), episodic crises, triggers, what helps, and its effect on daily life and carers. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Other symptoms**: cough, sputum, **fatigue**, anorexia and weight loss, pain (often musculoskeletal or from coughing), poor sleep, constipation from opioids, and dry mouth from oxygen. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Psychological**: **anxiety and panic in up to half**, depression in about 40% of advanced COPD - ask directly and screen with PHQ-9 and GAD-7. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Social and financial**: carer burden, cost of oxygen and inhalers, loss of income, housing with stairs or biomass cooking. [WHO 2020]`,`**Spiritual and existential concerns**: fear of suffocating to death, meaning, faith and wishes about place of death. [WHO 2020]`,`**Patient understanding and preferences**: what they know about the illness, what they fear most, what matters most, and who should decide if they cannot. [NICE NG142 2019]`,`**Admission history**: number of admissions, ICU, intubation and NIV in the last year - the best guide to prognosis in COPD. [GOLD 2025]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Respiratory rate, SpO2 at rest and on exertion**, use of accessory muscles, pursed-lip breathing and tripod position. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Signs of hypercapnia**: flapping tremor, bounding pulse, warm peripheries, morning headache, drowsiness. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cor pulmonale**: raised JVP, loud P2, parasternal heave, hepatomegaly and pedal oedema. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cachexia and sarcopenia**: BMI, mid-arm circumference, temporal wasting; **BMI under 21** predicts death in COPD. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Fine end-inspiratory 'Velcro' crackles and clubbing** in fibrotic ILD. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT IPF 2022]`,`**Pressure areas, oral thrush and mouth dryness** in bed-bound patients on oxygen and steroids. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of worsening breathlessness in advanced disease`,points:[`**Exacerbation of the underlying disease** (infection, acute exacerbation of IPF) - treat if consistent with goals of care. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Pulmonary embolism** - commoner in immobile, hospitalised patients. [ESC PE 2019]`,`**Heart failure, arrhythmia (atrial fibrillation) and ischaemia** - common comorbidities in COPD. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Pneumothorax** in bullous emphysema or fibrotic lung - sudden worsening with unilateral reduced breath sounds. [BTS Pleural Guideline 2023]`,`**Anaemia, pleural effusion, lung cancer** (smokers, ILD carries high cancer risk). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anxiety and panic** - a diagnosis made only after reversible causes are excluded. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Drug effects**: opioid or benzodiazepine sedation causing hypercapnia, or beta-blocker bronchospasm. [BNF 2025]`]},{heading:`Investigations - proportionate to goals`,points:[`**Pulse oximetry at rest and on exertion** in every visit; **ABG** if SpO2 is 92% or less at rest or hypercapnia is suspected. [BTS Home Oxygen 2015]`,`**Spirometry and DLCO** for staging and transplant referral; **6-minute walk test** for distance and desaturation (used in BODE and ISHLT criteria). [ISHLT 2021]`,`**Echocardiography** for pulmonary hypertension and heart failure; **NT-proBNP**. [ESC/ERS PH 2022]`,`**Blood tests**: CBC (anaemia, polycythaemia), renal function (opioid choice), glucose (steroids), albumin. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chest X-ray or CT** when a treatable cause (pneumothorax, effusion, cancer, infection) would change management. [Harrison 22e]`,`**In the last days of life, stop routine tests and observations** that do not change comfort care. [NICE NG31 2015]`,`**Transplant work-up** (at the transplant centre): right heart catheterisation, CT, cardiac evaluation, bone density, infection screen, psychosocial assessment. [ISHLT 2021]`]},{heading:`Breathlessness management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Optimise the underlying disease first**: inhaled therapy and technique, pulmonary rehabilitation, LTOT if criteria met, NIV for chronic hypercapnia, antifibrotics, PH therapy. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Handheld fan** directed at the face for 5 minutes during breathlessness - cheap, safe and effective. [Cochrane 2024]`,`**Breathing, Thinking, Functioning (Cambridge BTF) approach**: breathing techniques (pursed lip, recovery breathing), managing thoughts and anxiety, and pacing and positioning (forward lean, arms on knees). [Booth Cambridge BTF 2019]`,`**Pulmonary rehabilitation** improves breathlessness, exercise capacity and quality of life in COPD and ILD - offer to all with mMRC 2 or more. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Opioids**: low-dose oral morphine may be considered for severe refractory breathlessness - start **immediate-release morphine 1-2.5 mg 4-hourly as needed** or **modified-release 5 mg twice daily (10 mg/day)**, titrate weekly to a usual **maximum of 30 mg/day**, with a **laxative**; stop if no benefit. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Evidence caution**: large recent RCTs (BEAMS 2022, MORDYC) of regular low-dose morphine in COPD showed **no clear improvement in chronic breathlessness over placebo**, so opioids are an individualised trial with a stop rule, while ASCO 2021 recommends them for dyspnoea in advanced cancer. [JAMA BEAMS 2022]`,`**Benzodiazepines are not first-line** for breathlessness; use only for panic or in the terminal phase (**lorazepam 0.5-1 mg sublingual**). [Cochrane 2016]`,`**Oxygen only for hypoxaemia**: LTOT for **PaO2 7.3 kPa (55 mmHg) or less**, or **7.3-8 kPa (55-60 mmHg)** with cor pulmonale, polycythaemia or pulmonary hypertension, for **at least 15 hours a day**; no oxygen for breathlessness with normal SpO2. [BTS Home Oxygen 2015]`,`**Other symptoms**: cough (opioid linctus, treat reflux), depression (**sertraline 50 mg**, CBT), anorexia (small frequent meals), constipation (senna plus osmotic laxative). [GOLD 2025]`]},{heading:`Advance care planning and end-of-life care`,points:[`**Advance care planning (ACP)** is a voluntary, ongoing conversation about values, goals and preferences for future care - **start it early**, at diagnosis of advanced disease or after a hypercapnic admission, not in the ICU. [NICE NG142 2019]`,`Topics: understanding of illness, **ceiling of treatment** (ward-based care, NIV as ceiling, ICU and intubation), **CPR decision**, preferred place of care and death, and a surrogate decision-maker. [NICE NG142 2019]`,`**Legal position in India**: the Supreme Court (**Common Cause v Union of India, 2018**) recognised the right to die with dignity, permitting **withdrawal of life support and advance medical directives (living wills)**; the procedure was **simplified in 2023** (notarised directive, hospital medical boards). [Supreme Court of India 2023]`,`**Last days of life**: recognise dying (bed-bound, minimal intake, drowsy), stop non-essential drugs, give **anticipatory medicines SC**, and support the family. [NICE NG31 2015]`,`**Terminal breathlessness**: **morphine 2.5 mg SC (opioid-naive) as needed** or by syringe driver (oral dose divided by 2), with **midazolam 2.5 mg SC** for distress. [NICE NG31 2015]`,`**Noisy respiratory secretions (death rattle)**: reposition first; **glycopyrronium 200 microgram SC** or **hyoscine butylbromide 20 mg SC** 4-6 hourly. [NICE NG31 2015]`,`**NIV as palliation**: can be used as the ceiling of treatment to relieve breathlessness, but **stop it when it no longer helps comfort**, with sedation planned beforehand. [BTS NIV 2016]`,`**Admit or refer to inpatient palliative care or hospice** for uncontrolled symptoms, carer breakdown, or when home death is not possible or not wanted. [WHO 2020]`]},{heading:`Lung transplant referral - ISHLT 2021`,points:[`**General principle**: refer early - when there is a **high (over 50%) risk of death from lung disease within 2 years** without transplant, and a high likelihood (over 80%) of 5-year survival after it if medically feasible. [ISHLT 2021]`,`**COPD referral**: progressive disease despite maximal treatment including rehabilitation and oxygen, not a candidate for valves or LVRS, **BODE 5-6**, **PaCO2 over 50 mmHg and/or PaO2 under 60 mmHg**, or **FEV1 under 25% predicted**. [ISHLT 2021]`,`**COPD listing**: **BODE 7 or more**, **FEV1 15-20%**, **3 or more severe exacerbations in the last year**, one severe exacerbation with acute hypercapnic respiratory failure, or moderate-severe pulmonary hypertension. [ISHLT 2021]`,`**ILD referral**: **at diagnosis of IPF or UIP** regardless of lung function; **FVC under 80% or DLCO under 40%**; relative FVC decline 10% or more, DLCO decline 15% or more, or FVC decline 5% or more with worsening symptoms or imaging over 2 years; or any oxygen need. [ISHLT 2021]`,`**ILD listing**: **FVC decline 10% or more in 6 months**, DLCO decline 10% or more in 6 months, **6MWT SpO2 under 88% or distance under 250 m** or a fall over 50 m in 6 months, pulmonary hypertension, or admission for decline, pneumothorax or acute exacerbation. [ISHLT 2021]`,`**Cystic fibrosis**: refer with **FEV1 under 30% predicted** in adults (or under 40% with markers of poor prognosis such as hypercapnia, massive haemoptysis or rapid decline). [ISHLT 2021]`,`**Pulmonary arterial hypertension**: refer when **ESC/ERS intermediate-high or high risk** despite optimised therapy including parenteral prostanoids, or WHO functional class III-IV. [ISHLT 2021]`,`**Absolute contraindications** include recent malignancy (usually within 5 years, shorter for low-risk tumours), **active smoking or substance misuse**, uncontrolled sepsis or **active TB**, severe untreatable other-organ dysfunction, **BMI 35 or more**, poor adherence or no social support, and severe limited rehabilitation potential. [ISHLT 2021]`,`**Age over 70 years**, BMI 30-34.9, malnutrition and prior thoracic surgery are **risk factors**, weighed together rather than absolute exclusions. [ISHLT 2021]`,`**Outcomes**: about **85% survive 1 year** and the **median survival is about 6-7 years**; **chronic lung allograft dysfunction (CLAD/bronchiolitis obliterans)** affects about half by 5 years and is the main limit. [ISHLT Registry 2023]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Elderly and frail**: start opioids at the lowest dose (morphine 1 mg), watch for delirium and falls; comprehensive geriatric assessment. [GOLD 2025]`,`**CKD (eGFR under 30)**: morphine metabolites accumulate - use lower doses at longer intervals or switch to **oxycodone or fentanyl** with palliative advice. [BNF 2025]`,`**Hypercapnic patients**: opioids and benzodiazepines are not contraindicated at low dose, but **titrate slowly and monitor drowsiness**; low-dose oral morphine did not raise admissions or deaths in cohort data. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Diabetes**: steroids for exacerbations worsen glucose; relax glycaemic targets at the end of life to avoid hypoglycaemia. [NICE NG31 2015]`,`**Post-TB lung disease**: confirm TB is not active before labelling disease end-stage; many are young and need rehabilitation and income support. [ICMR 2023]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: fan, breathing techniques, oral morphine (NLEM), oxygen concentrator under district programmes, and community palliative networks (**Kerala neighbourhood network model**). [NPPC 2012]`,`**Children and young adults** (CF, PCD): involve paediatric palliative and transplant teams early; transition planning. [ECFS 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**After a hospital admission for hypercapnic COPD exacerbation needing NIV, about one in three die within a year** - a recognised trigger for ACP. [BTS NIV 2016]`,`**IPF**: median survival 3-5 years untreated; antifibrotics slow FVC decline by about half but do not reverse fibrosis; **acute exacerbation** carries 50% or more in-hospital mortality. [ATS/ERS/JRS/ALAT IPF 2022]`,`**Opioid adverse effects**: constipation (almost universal - always co-prescribe laxatives), nausea, drowsiness, and rarely respiratory depression at low doses. [BNF 2025]`,`**Psychological complications**: depression and anxiety worsen survival, readmission and quality of life. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Carer burden and financial toxicity**: catastrophic health expenditure from repeated admissions and oxygen. [Lancet Commission on Palliative Care 2018]`,`**Post-transplant complications**: primary graft dysfunction, rejection, infection (CMV, fungal), CLAD, renal impairment from calcineurin inhibitors, and malignancy. [ISHLT Registry 2023]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and national programmes`,points:[`**Refer to specialist palliative care** for complex or refractory symptoms, psychological or existential distress, family conflict, or uncertainty about goals. [WHO 2020]`,`**Follow up every 1-3 months** with symptoms (mMRC, NRS), SpO2, weight, mood, carer strain and an updated plan; more often after any admission. [GOLD 2025]`,`**National Programme for Palliative Care (NPPC, 2012)** under NHM, now delivered with NP-NCD at district hospitals and **Ayushman Arogya Mandir** home-based care. [MoHFW NPPC 2012]`,`**NDPS (Amendment) Act 2014** created **essential narcotic drugs** (morphine, fentanyl, methadone) with simpler state-uniform licensing through **Recognised Medical Institutions (RMIs)**; oral morphine is in the **NLEM**. [NDPS Amendment 2014]`,`**Hospices and NGOs**: Pallium India (Thiruvananthapuram), CanSupport (Delhi), Karunashraya (Bengaluru), and Kerala's community-based networks are models. [Pallium India 2023]`,`**Organ transplantation** is regulated by the **Transplantation of Human Organs and Tissues Act (THOTA, 1994, amended 2011; rules 2014)**; deceased-donor allocation through **NOTTO, ROTTO and SOTTO**. [MoHFW NOTTO 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ISHLT 2021 consensus** on lung transplant candidate selection: earlier ILD referral (at diagnosis), BODE-based COPD criteria, and more risk factors treated as relative rather than absolute. [ISHLT 2021]`,`**GOLD 2025**: palliative approaches (fan, rehabilitation, opioids considered for severe breathlessness) and ACP are part of routine advanced COPD care. [GOLD 2025]`,`**BEAMS trial (JAMA 2022)**: regular low-dose morphine did not improve worst breathlessness in COPD - prescribing shifted to an individualised trial with a stop rule. [JAMA BEAMS 2022]`,`**NOTTO 2023**: removal of the **upper age limit of 65 years** and the **domicile requirement** for registration on deceased-donor waiting lists in India. [MoHFW NOTTO 2023]`,`**Supreme Court 2023**: simplified procedure for advance medical directives and withdrawal of life support; MoHFW released **draft guidelines on withdrawal of life support in terminally ill patients (2024)**. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**ESC/ERS 2022 pulmonary hypertension guideline**: three-strata and four-strata risk assessment used to time transplant referral. [ESC/ERS PH 2022]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Viva: define chronic breathlessness syndrome** - persists despite optimal treatment of the cause, results in disability. [Johnson Eur Respir J 2017]`,`**Viva: non-drug measures** - fan to face, pursed-lip breathing, positioning, pacing, pulmonary rehabilitation, CBT. [Booth Cambridge BTF 2019]`,`**Common mistake**: giving oxygen to a breathless patient with normal SpO2 - it is no better than air. [Abernethy Lancet 2010]`,`**Common mistake**: prescribing morphine without a laxative, or benzodiazepines as first-line for breathlessness. [BNF 2025]`,`**Classic MCQ**: ISHLT 2021 - refer IPF at diagnosis regardless of lung function. [ISHLT 2021]`,`**Classic MCQ**: Common Cause v Union of India (2018) - passive euthanasia and living wills permitted in India. [Supreme Court of India 2023]`,`**Long question**: palliative care in end-stage COPD - recognising the phase, symptom control, ACP, end-of-life drugs and Indian programmes. [GOLD 2025]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for breathlessness and end-of-life care`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Morphine immediate-release (oral)`,`1-2.5 mg 4-hourly as needed (1 mg in frail elderly)`,`Review at 1 week; stop if no benefit`,`Constipation, nausea, drowsiness; reduce in renal failure`],[`Morphine modified-release (oral)`,`5 mg twice daily (10 mg/day)`,`Titrate weekly to usual max 30 mg/day`,`As above; always with a laxative`],[`Morphine SC (terminal phase)`,`2.5 mg SC as needed (opioid-naive); syringe driver = oral 24 h dose divided by 2`,`Hourly as needed; review daily`,`Accumulates if eGFR under 30`],[`Oxycodone (renal impairment)`,`1-2 mg oral 4-6 hourly as needed`,`Titrate slowly`,`Constipation, sedation`],[`Lorazepam`,`0.5-1 mg sublingual`,`As needed for panic, not first-line`,`Sedation, falls, respiratory depression with opioids`],[`Midazolam`,`2.5 mg SC as needed; 10 mg/24 h by syringe driver`,`Terminal agitation or distress`,`Sedation; titrate to comfort`],[`Glycopyrronium`,`200 microgram SC 4-hourly as needed; 600-1200 microgram/24 h by driver`,`Last days for secretions`,`Dry mouth, urinary retention`],[`Hyoscine butylbromide`,`20 mg SC 4-hourly as needed (max 120 mg/24 h)`,`Last days for secretions`,`Dry mouth, tachycardia`],[`Senna plus lactulose`,`Senna 15 mg at night; lactulose 15 mL twice daily`,`Whenever an opioid is given`,`Cramps, bloating`],[`Oxygen (LTOT)`,`1-4 L/min via concentrator, target SpO2 88-92% in COPD`,`At least 15 hours a day`,`Fire risk with smoking; dry nose`],[`Sertraline`,`50 mg oral once daily`,`Titrate; 6 months after remission`,`Nausea, hyponatraemia, bleeding with antiplatelets`]]},{heading:`ISHLT 2021 lung transplant referral and listing criteria`,columns:[`Disease`,`Refer when`,`List when`],rows:[[`COPD`,`Progressive despite max therapy; BODE 5-6; PaCO2 over 50 or PaO2 under 60 mmHg; FEV1 under 25%`,`BODE 7 or more; FEV1 15-20%; 3 or more severe exacerbations a year; hypercapnic failure; moderate-severe PH`],[`IPF / fibrotic ILD`,`At diagnosis of IPF/UIP; FVC under 80% or DLCO under 40%; decline FVC 10% (or 5% with symptoms) or DLCO 15% in 2 years; any oxygen need`,`FVC fall 10% or DLCO fall 10% in 6 months; 6MWT SpO2 under 88% or under 250 m; PH; admission for decline`],[`Cystic fibrosis`,`FEV1 under 30% (or under 40% with poor markers)`,`Rapid decline, hypercapnia, PH, frequent severe exacerbations`],[`Pulmonary arterial hypertension`,`Intermediate-high or high risk despite therapy; WHO FC III-IV`,`High risk despite maximal therapy including parenteral prostanoid`]]},{heading:`Breathlessness interventions and the evidence`,columns:[`Intervention`,`Effect`,`Who benefits`,`Note`],rows:[[`Handheld fan to face`,`Reduces breathlessness intensity`,`All, including at rest`,`Cheap, no side effects`],[`Pulmonary rehabilitation`,`Less breathlessness, more exercise capacity`,`COPD and ILD with mMRC 2 or more`,`Best evidence of all measures`],[`Breathing techniques, positioning, pacing`,`Reduces crises and anxiety`,`All`,`Cambridge BTF model`],[`Low-dose oral morphine`,`Inconsistent - recent COPD RCTs negative`,`Severe refractory breathlessness, last weeks of life, cancer`,`Trial with stop rule; laxative`],[`Oxygen`,`Helps only if hypoxaemic`,`PaO2 55 mmHg or less (or 55-60 with complications)`,`No better than air if normoxaemic`],[`Benzodiazepines`,`No consistent benefit for breathlessness`,`Panic, terminal phase`,`Not first-line`],[`CBT and psychological support`,`Reduces anxiety and panic`,`Anxiety, depression`,`Part of total dyspnoea care`]]},{heading:`Recognising the palliative phase in chronic lung disease`,columns:[`Tool or marker`,`Threshold`,`Use`],rows:[[`Surprise question`,`Not surprised if death within 12 months`,`Trigger needs assessment and ACP`],[`BODE index`,`7-10`,`About 80% 4-year mortality; transplant listing in COPD`],[`FEV1`,`Under 30% predicted`,`GSF indicator in COPD`],[`Admissions`,`3 or more a year, or NIV for hypercapnia`,`High 1-year mortality`],[`mMRC`,`Grade 4 (too breathless to leave the house)`,`Severe disability`],[`BMI`,`Under 21`,`Cachexia, poor prognosis`],[`IPF FVC decline`,`10% or more in a year`,`Progression, poor survival`]]},{heading:`Lung transplant contraindications - ISHLT 2021`,columns:[`Category`,`Absolute contraindication`,`Risk factor (relative)`],rows:[[`Malignancy`,`Recent cancer (usually within 5 years)`,`Low-risk skin cancer, cured localised tumours`],[`Substance use`,`Active smoking, vaping, alcohol or drug misuse`,`Past use with documented abstinence`],[`Body habitus`,`BMI 35 or more`,`BMI 30-34.9, malnutrition`],[`Infection`,`Uncontrolled sepsis, active TB`,`Colonisation with resistant organisms, HIV with undetectable viral load`],[`Other organs`,`Severe untreatable heart, liver or kidney dysfunction`,`Coronary disease amenable to treatment, osteoporosis`],[`Psychosocial`,`Non-adherence, no social support`,`Limited support, cognitive impairment`],[`Age and function`,`Severe limited rehabilitation potential`,`Age over 70 years, frailty`]]}],redFlags:[`Sudden worsening of breathlessness with unilateral signs - pneumothorax or PE needing treatment if consistent with goals.`,`New drowsiness, confusion or flapping tremor on opioids or benzodiazepines - hypercapnia or toxicity.`,`Breathlessness crisis uncontrolled by the home plan, or carer unable to cope.`,`Suicidal thoughts or severe depression.`,`Massive haemoptysis in bronchiectasis or post-TB lung disease.`,`Rapid FVC decline, new oxygen need or PH in ILD - urgent transplant referral if eligible.`,`Terminal agitation, pain or secretions not controlled by anticipatory medicines.`],pearls:[`Chronic breathlessness syndrome: persists despite optimal treatment of the cause and causes disability.`,`Palliative care is based on need, not prognosis, and runs alongside active treatment.`,`Fan to the face works through the trigeminal nerve - cheap and effective.`,`Oxygen treats hypoxaemia, not breathlessness.`,`Morphine for breathlessness: low dose (10 mg/day MR), slow titration, max about 30 mg/day, always with a laxative.`,`Benzodiazepines are not first-line for breathlessness.`,`ISHLT 2021: refer IPF at diagnosis; COPD at BODE 5-6; list COPD at BODE 7 or more.`,`Common Cause v Union of India (2018, simplified 2023): living wills are legal in India.`,`NDPS Amendment 2014: essential narcotic drugs made morphine easier to stock through RMIs.`,`Death rattle: reposition first, then glycopyrronium or hyoscine butylbromide SC.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-end-stage-lung-palliation-q1`,stem:`A 68-year-old man with very severe COPD is breathless at rest. SpO2 on air is 95% and PaO2 is 78 mmHg. He asks for home oxygen to ease his breathlessness. What is the best response?`,options:[`Prescribe long-term oxygen for at least 15 hours a day`,`Prescribe ambulatory oxygen for exertion`,`Explain that oxygen does not relieve breathlessness without hypoxaemia, and offer a handheld fan and breathing techniques`,`Prescribe oxygen at night only`],answer:2,explanation:`In patients who are not hypoxaemic, oxygen relieves breathlessness no better than room air, and long-term oxygen requires a PaO2 of 55 mmHg or less (or 55-60 with complications). A fan to the face, breathing techniques and rehabilitation are the evidence-based measures. Long-term, ambulatory or nocturnal oxygen adds cost, fire risk and dependence without benefit here.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-end-stage-lung-palliation-q2`,stem:`When considering low-dose opioids for refractory breathlessness in advanced COPD, which starting regimen is most appropriate?`,options:[`Modified-release morphine 5 mg twice daily with a regular laxative`,`Modified-release morphine 30 mg twice daily`,`Diazepam 5 mg three times daily`,`Fentanyl 25 microgram/hour patch`],answer:0,explanation:`The usual start is low-dose morphine - 10 mg/day as modified-release 5 mg twice daily, or 1-2.5 mg immediate-release as needed - titrated slowly to about 30 mg/day, always with a laxative and a stop rule if there is no benefit. Starting at 60 mg/day or with a fentanyl patch in an opioid-naive patient risks respiratory depression, and benzodiazepines are not first-line for breathlessness.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-end-stage-lung-palliation-q3`,stem:`According to the ISHLT 2021 consensus, when should a 62-year-old man newly diagnosed with idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, FVC 82% predicted, be referred to a lung transplant centre?`,options:[`Only when FVC falls below 50%`,`Only after an acute exacerbation`,`When he needs long-term oxygen`,`Now, at diagnosis, regardless of lung function`],answer:3,explanation:`ISHLT 2021 recommends referral at diagnosis of IPF or radiological/histological UIP, regardless of lung function, because decline can be rapid and work-up takes time. Waiting for FVC under 50%, an acute exacerbation or oxygen dependence risks the patient becoming too ill to list.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-end-stage-lung-palliation-q4`,stem:`A man with end-stage COPD is dying at home. He is unconscious and his family is distressed by noisy rattling breathing. After repositioning, which drug is appropriate?`,options:[`Furosemide 40 mg IV`,`Glycopyrronium 200 microgram subcutaneously`,`Salbutamol nebulisation`,`Oral dexamethasone 8 mg`],answer:1,explanation:`Noisy respiratory secretions in the last days of life are managed by repositioning and an antisecretory drug such as glycopyrronium 200 microgram SC or hyoscine butylbromide 20 mg SC. Furosemide is for pulmonary oedema, salbutamol for bronchospasm, and an oral drug is unsuitable in an unconscious patient.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-end-stage-lung-palliation-q5`,stem:`Which Indian legal development permits a person to make an advance medical directive (living will) refusing life-prolonging treatment?`,options:[`The NDPS (Amendment) Act 2014`,`The Transplantation of Human Organs and Tissues Act`,`The Supreme Court judgement in Common Cause v Union of India (2018), simplified in 2023`,`The Mental Healthcare Act 2017 alone`],answer:2,explanation:`The Supreme Court in Common Cause v Union of India (2018) recognised the right to die with dignity, permitting withdrawal of life support and advance medical directives, with the procedure simplified in 2023. The NDPS Amendment 2014 eased access to essential narcotic drugs such as morphine, THOTA regulates organ donation, and the Mental Healthcare Act covers advance directives for mental health treatment only.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-end-stage-lung-palliation-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Discuss palliative care in a patient with end-stage COPD, with emphasis on management of breathlessness, advance care planning and end-of-life care in the Indian setting.`,openingLines:[`Palliative care improves the quality of life of patients and families facing life-threatening illness by early identification and treatment of physical, psychosocial and spiritual problems; it is based on need, not prognosis, and runs alongside COPD treatment.`,`Chronic breathlessness syndrome - breathlessness persisting despite optimal treatment and causing disability - is its central target in end-stage COPD.`],answer:[{heading:`Recognising the palliative phase`,points:[`Surprise question, SPICT, GSF indicators: FEV1 under 30%, 3 or more admissions a year, LTOT, NIV, BMI under 21, BODE 7-10.`]},{heading:`Mechanism of breathlessness`,points:[`Mismatch between drive and achieved ventilation; dynamic hyperinflation; anxiety and the deconditioning spiral; total dyspnoea.`]},{heading:`Non-drug management`,points:[`Optimise inhalers, pulmonary rehabilitation, fan to face, pursed-lip breathing, positioning, pacing, CBT (Cambridge BTF).`,`Oxygen only if hypoxaemic: PaO2 55 mmHg or less, at least 15 h/day.`]},{heading:`Drug management`,points:[`Low-dose morphine MR 5 mg twice daily or IR 1-2.5 mg as needed, max about 30 mg/day, laxative, stop rule (BEAMS caution).`,`Benzodiazepines only for panic or terminal phase; treat depression, cough, constipation.`]},{heading:`Advance care planning`,points:[`Values, ceiling of treatment (NIV as ceiling, ICU), CPR decision, place of death, surrogate; living will under Common Cause 2018 (simplified 2023).`]},{heading:`End-of-life care`,points:[`Stop non-essential drugs; anticipatory SC morphine 2.5 mg, midazolam 2.5 mg, glycopyrronium 200 microgram; family support.`]},{heading:`Indian setting`,points:[`NPPC 2012 with NP-NCD and Ayushman Arogya Mandir; NDPS 2014 essential narcotic drugs and RMIs; oral morphine in NLEM; Kerala community model; hospices.`,`Consider transplant referral (ISHLT 2021: BODE 5-6) in eligible patients.`]}],mustDraw:[`Breathlessness-anxiety-deconditioning cycle`,`Ladder of breathlessness management from disease optimisation to opioid trial`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and recognising the phase`,marks:2},{part:`Breathlessness management`,marks:3},{part:`Advance care planning`,marks:2},{part:`End-of-life care`,marks:2},{part:`Indian programmes and law`,marks:1}],keywords:[`palliative care`,`chronic breathlessness syndrome`,`morphine`,`advance care planning`,`NPPC`,`living will`]},{id:`respiratory-end-stage-lung-palliation-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on lung transplant referral criteria (ISHLT 2021).`,openingLines:[`Referral is advised when a patient has over 50% risk of death from lung disease within 2 years without transplant and a high likelihood of 5-year survival after it.`,`ISHLT 2021 emphasises early referral, especially at diagnosis of IPF.`],answer:[{heading:`Disease-specific referral`,points:[`COPD: BODE 5-6, FEV1 under 25%, PaCO2 over 50 or PaO2 under 60 mmHg; list at BODE 7 or more, 3 severe exacerbations a year.`,`ILD: at diagnosis of IPF/UIP, FVC under 80% or DLCO under 40%, any decline or oxygen need; list when FVC falls 10% in 6 months or 6MWT under 250 m.`,`CF: FEV1 under 30%; PAH: intermediate-high or high risk despite therapy.`]},{heading:`Contraindications`,points:[`Absolute: recent cancer, active smoking or substance use, BMI 35 or more, uncontrolled infection or active TB, severe other-organ failure, no social support.`,`Relative: age over 70, BMI 30-34.9, frailty.`]},{heading:`Outcomes and India`,points:[`1-year survival about 85%, median about 6-7 years; CLAD main limit.`,`THOTA, NOTTO allocation; 2023 removal of age 65 cap and domicile rule.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table of referral versus listing criteria by disease`],markSplit:[{part:`Referral criteria`,marks:3},{part:`Contraindications`,marks:1},{part:`Outcomes and Indian context`,marks:1}],keywords:[`ISHLT 2021`,`lung transplant`,`BODE`,`IPF`,`CLAD`,`NOTTO`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Dyspnoea and palliative and end-of-life care`,`Leard LE, et al. Consensus document for the selection of lung transplant candidates: an update from the International Society for Heart and Lung Transplantation. J Heart Lung Transplant 2021`,`Global Initiative for Chronic Obstructive Lung Disease (GOLD) Report, 2025`,`Johnson MJ, et al. Towards an expert consensus to delineate a clinical syndrome of chronic breathlessness. Eur Respir J 2017`,`Ekstrom M, et al. Effect of regular, low-dose, extended-release morphine on chronic breathlessness in COPD: the BEAMS randomized clinical trial. JAMA 2022`,`NICE guideline NG31: Care of dying adults in the last days of life, 2015 (updated 2017); NICE NG142 End of life care for adults: service delivery, 2019`,`MoHFW, Government of India. National Programme for Palliative Care, 2012; NDPS (Amendment) Act 2014`,`Supreme Court of India. Common Cause v Union of India, 2018, and modification order 2023 (advance medical directives)`]});var d=[];d.push({id:`respiratory-lung-pregnancy`,title:`Respiratory disease in pregnancy: asthma, TB, pneumonia and influenza`,oneLiner:`Pregnancy raises **minute ventilation by 30-50%** (progesterone) and lowers **FRC by about 20%**, giving a **compensated respiratory alkalosis (PaCO2 28-32 mmHg)**; asthma, TB, pneumonia, influenza and COVID-19 are treated with the **same proven drugs as outside pregnancy** - because **maternal hypoxaemia and uncontrolled disease harm the fetus far more than ICS, first-line ATT, beta-lactams or oseltamivir** - with lower thresholds for admission, oxygen to **SpO2 95% or more**, and needed imaging never withheld.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`asthma in pregnancy`,`physiological dyspnoea of pregnancy`,`rule of thirds`,`TB in pregnancy`,`congenital tuberculosis`,`Cantwell criteria`,`varicella pneumonia`,`influenza in pregnancy`,`oseltamivir`,`COVID-19 in pregnancy`,`radiation dose fetus`,`CTPA pregnancy`,`pregnancy-adapted YEARS`,`Mendelson syndrome`,`carboprost asthma`,`budesonide pregnancy`,`streptomycin ototoxicity`,`MEOWS`,`amniotic fluid embolism`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Respiratory disease in pregnancy** covers pre-existing lung disease that pregnancy alters (asthma, TB, bronchiectasis, cystic fibrosis, pulmonary hypertension) and acute illness that is commoner or more severe in pregnancy (pneumonia, influenza, COVID-19, PE, aspiration, pulmonary oedema). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Asthma** is the commonest chronic respiratory disease in pregnancy, affecting about **3-8% of pregnancies**; about **20% have an exacerbation needing medical care and about 6% are admitted**. [GINA 2025]`,`Globally about **2.2 lakh pregnant women (216,500 in a 2011 estimate)** have active TB each year, with South-East Asia carrying the largest share; India has about **26-27% of the world's TB**. [WHO Global TB Report 2024]`,`An Indian cohort found maternal TB raised **perinatal death about 6-fold** and **low birth weight about 2-fold**, with worse outcomes when diagnosis was late or treatment irregular. [Jana et al, Int J Gynaecol Obstet 1994]`,`**Pneumonia** complicates roughly **0.5-1.5 per 1000 pregnancies** and is the commonest fatal non-obstetric infection in pregnancy. [Harrison 22e]`,`In the **2009 H1N1 pandemic** pregnant women were about **1% of the US population but about 5% of influenza deaths**, with the highest risk in the **third trimester**. [Siston et al, JAMA 2010]`,`In **COVID-19**, pregnant women had about **2-fold higher ICU admission and 2.6-fold higher invasive ventilation** than non-pregnant women of the same age with COVID-19, and more preterm birth. [PregCOV-19, BMJ 2020]`,`India's **maternal mortality ratio is 93 per 100,000 live births (SRS 2019-21)**; indirect causes such as infection, TB, anaemia and heart disease form a growing share as haemorrhage and eclampsia fall. [SRS 2019-21]`]},{heading:`Respiratory physiology of pregnancy - the numbers examiners ask`,points:[`**Progesterone** stimulates the respiratory centre and sensitises it to CO2, so **tidal volume rises 30-50%** while **respiratory rate is unchanged** - minute ventilation rises 30-50% by term. [Harrison 22e]`,`The result is a **compensated respiratory alkalosis**: **PaCO2 28-32 mmHg**, bicarbonate **18-22 mmol/L**, pH **7.40-7.45** and PaO2 about **100-105 mmHg** at sea level. [Williams Obstetrics 26e]`,`The low maternal PaCO2 creates the **maternal-fetal CO2 gradient** the fetus needs to offload CO2 - so a **'normal' PaCO2 of 40 mmHg in a breathless pregnant woman means hypoventilation and impending respiratory failure**. [Harrison 22e]`,`The **diaphragm rises by up to 4 cm**, the **subcostal angle widens from about 68 to 103 degrees** and chest circumference rises 5-7 cm, so diaphragm excursion and vital capacity are preserved. [Williams Obstetrics 26e]`,`**FRC falls by about 20%** (expiratory reserve and residual volume both fall), TLC is unchanged or slightly lower, and **oxygen consumption rises by 20-30%** - so a pregnant woman **desaturates fast during apnoea or intubation**. [Williams Obstetrics 26e]`,`**FEV1, FVC, FEV1/FVC and PEF do not change** in normal pregnancy, so **an abnormal spirometry or PEF is disease, not pregnancy**, and PEF monitoring is as valid as outside pregnancy. [GINA 2025]`,`**Physiological dyspnoea of pregnancy** affects **60-70% of women by 30 weeks**; it starts early, is gradual, does not limit daily activity, and comes with a normal examination and SpO2. [Williams Obstetrics 26e]`,`**Nasal mucosal oedema** (oestrogen, rhinitis of pregnancy), a higher **Mallampati grade** and a full stomach make the obstetric airway difficult - failed intubation is several times commoner than in non-pregnant adults. [Miller's Anesthesia 10e]`,`After 20 weeks the gravid uterus compresses the IVC when supine, cutting venous return - examine and resuscitate in **left lateral tilt (15-30 degrees) or with manual left uterine displacement**. [RCOG 2019]`,`Fetal oxygen delivery depends on maternal PaO2 and uterine blood flow; **maternal PaO2 under about 70 mmHg (SpO2 under about 95%)** reduces fetal oxygenation, which is why oxygen targets are higher in pregnancy. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Asthma in pregnancy - course and risks`,points:[`**Rule of thirds**: about one-third of women improve, one-third stay the same and one-third worsen; worsening is commonest at **24-36 weeks** and the course tends to repeat in later pregnancies. [Schatz et al 1988; GINA 2025]`,`Exacerbations are uncommon in the **last 4 weeks and in labour**, and asthma usually returns to its pre-pregnancy pattern within about 3 months after delivery. [GINA 2025]`,`Commonest causes of exacerbation are **stopping ICS for fear of harm to the baby** and **viral respiratory infection** - so the first counselling point is to continue controller treatment. [GINA 2025]`,`**Uncontrolled asthma** (not the drugs) raises the risk of **preeclampsia, preterm birth, low birth weight, small-for-gestational-age babies and perinatal death**. [GINA 2025]`,`GINA states that the **advantages of actively treating asthma in pregnancy markedly outweigh any potential risks** of usual controller and reliever medicines. [GINA 2025]`,`**Stepping down is not advised during pregnancy**; if a woman is well controlled, keep her on the same step and review step-down after delivery. [GINA 2025]`,`**GERD, rhinitis of pregnancy and sinusitis** are commoner in pregnancy and worsen asthma control - treat them alongside the asthma. [GINA 2025]`]},{heading:`Asthma drugs in pregnancy and breastfeeding`,points:[`**Inhaled corticosteroids** are safe and reduce exacerbations; **budesonide** has the most human safety data, but a woman well controlled on another ICS should not be switched. [GINA 2025]`,`**Short- and long-acting beta2-agonists (salbutamol, formoterol, salmeterol)** are safe; **ICS-formoterol as reliever (AIR) or MART** can be continued - each component has reassuring pregnancy data. [GINA 2025]`,`**Montelukast** has not shown an increase in major malformations and can be continued in a woman who had a good response before pregnancy. [GINA 2025]`,`**Oral prednisolone** must not be withheld in a severe exacerbation; older data linked first-trimester OCS with a small rise in **oral clefts**, and OCS are linked with preeclampsia, gestational diabetes and preterm birth - the attack itself is riskier. [GINA 2025]`,`**Theophylline** is acceptable but its clearance falls in the third trimester - check levels and watch for toxicity. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Biologics**: registry data for **omalizumab (EXPECT)** showed no rise in major malformations; data for anti-IL-5, dupilumab and tezepelumab are limited - decide case by case with a specialist. [GINA 2025]`,`If **high-dose SABA** was given in the **48 hours before delivery** (especially preterm), monitor the **newborn's blood glucose** for hypoglycaemia. [GINA 2025]`,`All usual asthma drugs, including prednisolone, are **compatible with breastfeeding**; theophylline can cause irritability in the infant. [GINA 2025]`,`The **US FDA removed the A/B/C/D/X pregnancy letter categories in 2015** (Pregnancy and Lactation Labeling Rule) - quote human data, not letters, in the exam. [US FDA PLLR 2015]`]},{heading:`Acute asthma, labour and delivery`,points:[`Treat an acute attack **exactly as outside pregnancy**: salbutamol by spacer or nebuliser, ipratropium if severe, **prednisolone 40-50 mg within an hour**, and IV magnesium sulphate 2 g over 20 minutes in severe attacks. [GINA 2025; BTS/NICE/SIGN 2024]`,`Give oxygen to keep **SpO2 at 95% or above** and start continuous **fetal heart monitoring** after viability in a severe attack. [GINA 2025; RCOG]`,`Admit at a **lower threshold** than outside pregnancy and involve the obstetrician early; a severe attack should be managed where **maternal and fetal monitoring** are both available. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`Continue all usual inhalers in labour; women who took **prednisolone over 7.5 mg/day for more than 2 weeks** before delivery should receive **hydrocortisone 100 mg IV 6-8 hourly in labour**. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`**Regional (epidural or spinal) anaesthesia** is preferred to general anaesthesia, as it avoids airway instrumentation that can trigger bronchospasm. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`For **postpartum haemorrhage avoid carboprost (15-methyl PGF2-alpha)**, a bronchoconstrictor; oxytocin, misoprostol and dinoprostone (PGE2) are safe. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`,`For **preeclampsia in an asthmatic** avoid **labetalol** (a non-selective beta-blocker) and use **nifedipine** or methyldopa; check for **aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease** before low-dose aspirin prophylaxis. [NICE NG133 2023]`]},{heading:`Tuberculosis in pregnancy - diagnosis`,points:[`TB symptoms (fatigue, breathlessness, weight loss masked by pregnancy weight gain) overlap with normal pregnancy, so **screen every pregnant woman at ANC with the four-symptom screen** (cough, fever, weight loss, night sweats). [NTEP Training Modules 2023]`,`Send sputum for **NAAT (CBNAAT or Truenat) upfront**, which also gives **rifampicin resistance**; smear alone misses many cases. [NTEP 2023]`,`A **chest X-ray with abdominal shielding** should never be withheld when TB is suspected - fetal dose is negligible. [ACOG CO 723 2017, reaffirmed]`,`**TST and IGRA remain valid in pregnancy**; pregnancy does not cause false-negative results to any important degree. [WHO TB Consolidated Guidelines Module 3 2024]`,`**Extrapulmonary and disseminated TB** are relatively common in pregnancy and postpartum; consider **TB meningitis or miliary TB** in a pregnant or postpartum woman with unexplained fever. [Harrison 22e]`,`Test every pregnant woman with TB for **HIV and diabetes**, and test HIV-positive pregnant women for TB, as both co-morbidities sharply worsen outcomes. [NTEP 2023]`,`**Congenital TB (Cantwell criteria)**: proven TB lesion in the infant plus one of - lesions in the **first week of life**, a **primary hepatic complex or caseating hepatic granuloma**, **placental or endometrial TB**, or exclusion of postnatal transmission. [Cantwell et al, NEJM 1994]`]},{heading:`Tuberculosis in pregnancy - treatment`,points:[`Drug-sensitive TB is treated with the standard **2HRZE/4HRE** daily weight-band FDC regimen - **isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide and ethambutol are all safe in pregnancy** and treatment must start without delay. [NTEP 2023; WHO 2022]`,`**Streptomycin and other aminoglycosides are contraindicated** - they cross the placenta and cause **fetal eighth-nerve damage (congenital deafness)**. [WHO 2022; Harrison 22e]`,`Give **pyridoxine 10-25 mg daily** with isoniazid to pregnant and breastfeeding women to prevent peripheral neuropathy. [WHO 2022]`,`The **4-month rifapentine-moxifloxacin regimen (2HPZM/2HPM)** is **not recommended in pregnancy or breastfeeding** for lack of data. [WHO Module 4 2022]`,`**Drug-resistant TB in pregnancy** needs a specialist DR-TB centre; WHO 2024-25 updates allow the **6-month BDLLfxC regimen** in pregnancy, while **BPaLM is not used** for lack of pretomanid data - see the Drug-resistant TB topic. [WHO Module 4 2025]`,`**Rifampicin induces CYP3A4** and makes hormonal contraception unreliable; for postpartum contraception use a **copper IUD or DMPA injection**. [WHO MEC 2015]`,`**Breastfeeding continues** on first-line ATT; a sputum-positive mother wears a mask while feeding until non-infectious, and the baby is **not separated**. [NTEP 2023; WHO 2022]`,`The **newborn** of a mother with pulmonary TB is evaluated for congenital TB and, once active disease is excluded, given **isoniazid TPT for 6 months**. [NTEP TPT Guidelines 2021]`,`Antenatal **isoniazid TPT in HIV-positive women** was not safer than starting it postpartum in the **TB APPRISE trial**, with more adverse pregnancy outcomes - TPT timing is individualised. [Gupta et al, NEJM 2019]`,`Enrol every patient for **Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana (Rs 1000/month since November 2024)** and link to a **Ni-kshay Mitra** for nutrition support. [MoHFW 2024]`]},{heading:`Pneumonia in pregnancy`,points:[`Organisms are the same as outside pregnancy - **S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae, Mycoplasma, influenza and other viruses** - plus **varicella pneumonia**, which is more severe in pregnancy. [Harrison 22e]`,`Physiological tachycardia, raised WBC and breathlessness distort scores; **CURB-65 underestimates severity** in pregnancy - use an obstetric early warning chart (**MEOWS**) and lower thresholds to admit. [RCOG 2019]`,`**Safe antibiotics**: amoxicillin 1 g TDS, co-amoxiclav 625 mg TDS or 1.2 g IV TDS, **ceftriaxone 1-2 g IV OD** and **azithromycin 500 mg OD** for 3-5 days for atypical cover. [ICMR Antimicrobial Guidelines 2022]`,`**Avoid**: **doxycycline/tetracyclines** after the first trimester (teeth and bone), **fluoroquinolones** unless no alternative, **co-trimoxazole** in the first trimester (folate antagonist) and near term (kernicterus), and **clarithromycin** (animal teratogenicity). [BNF 2025]`,`**Varicella pneumonia** develops in up to **10% of pregnant women with chickenpox**; give **IV aciclovir 10 mg/kg 8-hourly** and admit, as maternal mortality can be high. [RCOG GTG 13]`,`Oral **aciclovir 800 mg five times a day for 7 days** within 24 hours of the rash (from 20 weeks) reduces complications of chickenpox; a susceptible exposed pregnant woman needs **VZIG** or antiviral post-exposure prophylaxis. [RCOG GTG 13]`,`**Aspiration pneumonitis (Mendelson syndrome, 1946)** follows acid aspiration during obstetric anaesthesia; prevention is **fasting, antacid prophylaxis (H2 blocker or PPI plus sodium citrate) and regional anaesthesia**. [Miller's Anesthesia 10e]`]},{heading:`Influenza and COVID-19 in pregnancy`,points:[`Pregnant women and those **up to 2 weeks postpartum** are a high-risk group: start **oseltamivir 75 mg BD for 5 days** on clinical suspicion, **without waiting for a test**, ideally within 48 hours but **still worthwhile later**. [CDC 2025; MoHFW]`,`Under the **MoHFW seasonal influenza categorisation**, pregnant women with influenza-like illness are **Category B(ii)** - give oseltamivir; breathlessness, low SpO2 or altered sensorium is **Category C** - admit and test. [MoHFW Seasonal Influenza Guidelines]`,`**Post-exposure chemoprophylaxis**: oseltamivir **75 mg once daily for 10 days** after close household exposure. [CDC 2025]`,`**Baloxavir is not recommended in pregnancy or breastfeeding** for lack of data; oseltamivir is the drug of choice and inhaled zanamivir is avoided in asthma. [CDC 2025]`,`**Inactivated influenza vaccine** is safe in **any trimester** and protects the infant too - the Bangladesh **Mother's Gift trial** showed about **63% fewer confirmed influenza cases in infants**; live attenuated vaccine is contraindicated. [Zaman et al, NEJM 2008]`,`**COVID-19 vaccination** in pregnancy was approved by **MoHFW in July 2021** and is safe; it reduces severe maternal disease and gives infants passive protection. [MoHFW 2021; WHO SAGE 2023]`,`In COVID-19 needing oxygen, give **prednisolone 40 mg OD or IV hydrocortisone 80 mg BD for 10 days** (less placental transfer than dexamethasone); if steroids for **fetal lung maturity** are also due, give **dexamethasone 6 mg IM 12-hourly for 4 doses** first. [RCOG COVID-19 in Pregnancy 2022]`,`**Remdesivir** can be used in pregnancy; **nirmatrelvir-ritonavir** can be considered for high-risk mild disease; **molnupiravir is not recommended** (mutagenic potential). [NIH COVID-19 Guidelines 2024]`,`Give **LMWH thromboprophylaxis** to pregnant women admitted with COVID-19 or pneumonia unless delivery or bleeding is imminent, because pregnancy and infection both raise VTE risk. [RCOG GTG 37a 2015]`]},{heading:`Breathlessness in pregnancy - differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Physiological dyspnoea**: gradual, early onset, at rest or mild exertion, no orthopnoea, normal SpO2, RR and examination - a diagnosis of exclusion. [Williams Obstetrics 26e]`,`**Pulmonary embolism**: sudden onset, pleuritic pain, haemoptysis, leg swelling, tachycardia, low SpO2 - VTE is a leading direct cause of maternal death in high-income countries; see the Pulmonary embolism topic. [ESC PE 2019]`,`**Peripartum cardiomyopathy**: heart failure in the **last month of pregnancy or within 5 months postpartum** with **LVEF under 45%** - orthopnoea, PND, basal crepitations; check BNP and echo. [ESC Pregnancy 2025]`,`**Rheumatic mitral stenosis** is the classic Indian cause of **pulmonary oedema in the second and third trimester**, when heart rate and blood volume peak - listen for the mid-diastolic murmur. [CSI; ESC Pregnancy 2025]`,`**Preeclampsia** and **tocolytic (beta-agonist or magnesium) pulmonary oedema** follow capillary leak and fluid overload; nifedipine has replaced beta-agonist tocolysis. [RCOG 2019]`,`**Amniotic fluid embolism**: sudden hypotension or arrest plus hypoxia and **DIC**, during labour or **within 30 minutes of placental delivery**, without fever. [SMFM 2016]`,`**Anaemia** (very common in India - NFHS-5: **52% of pregnant women**) causes exertional breathlessness and tachycardia and lowers reserve in any respiratory illness. [NFHS-5 2019-21]`]},{heading:`Imaging and investigations - safety in pregnancy`,points:[`**Fetal dose from a chest X-ray is 0.0005-0.01 mGy**; doses **under 50 mGy are not associated with fetal anomalies, growth restriction or pregnancy loss**. [ACOG CO 723 2017, reaffirmed]`,`**CT chest or CTPA gives the fetus about 0.01-0.66 mGy** - far below the threshold - but a higher **maternal breast dose**; never withhold a needed scan because of pregnancy. [ACOG CO 723]`,`A lead apron over the abdomen adds little protection (most fetal dose is internal scatter) but is reasonable for reassurance during chest imaging. [ACOG CO 723]`,`**Iodinated contrast** can be given; the newborn's routine thyroid screening covers the small theoretical risk. **Gadolinium is avoided** unless essential. [ACOG CO 723]`,`**D-dimer rises through normal pregnancy**, so a fixed cut-off loses specificity; use the **pregnancy-adapted YEARS algorithm** (Artemis study) - it avoided CTPA in about **39%** of women safely. [van der Pol et al, NEJM 2019]`,`If leg symptoms are present in suspected PE, do **compression ultrasound first**: a DVT allows treatment without chest imaging. [ESC PE 2019]`,`Interpret **ABG against pregnancy norms**: PaCO2 28-32 mmHg is normal, so a PaCO2 over 35-40 mmHg in an asthma attack is a danger sign. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pulmonary arterial hypertension** carries high maternal mortality (**mWHO class IV - pregnancy contraindicated**); offer early termination and effective contraception; see the Pulmonary hypertension topic. [ESC Pregnancy 2025]`,`**Cystic fibrosis and severe restrictive disease**: pre-pregnancy FEV1 under about 50% predicted and hypoxaemia predict maternal and fetal complications - pre-conception counselling is essential. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bronchiectasis and post-TB lung disease** (common in India) need airway clearance, early antibiotics for exacerbations and pre-pregnancy optimisation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Obstructive sleep apnoea** is linked with gestational hypertension and diabetes; CPAP is safe in pregnancy. [AASM]`,`**HIV-positive pregnant women** with cough need TB testing (NAAT and urine **LF-LAM** if advanced disease), ART continuation and co-trimoxazole where indicated. [NACO 2021]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: SpO2, RR, MEOWS chart, a CXR and a NAAT sample are enough to decide on referral; start oseltamivir, antibiotics and steroids before transfer when indicated. [ICMR STW]`]},{heading:`Complications, referral and follow-up`,points:[`Maternal complications: **respiratory failure, ARDS, preterm labour, preeclampsia, VTE and death**; fetal: **growth restriction, prematurity, hypoxic injury and stillbirth**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Refer or admit** any pregnant woman with SpO2 under 95%, RR over 24/min, pulse over 110-120/min, inability to speak in sentences, altered sensorium, haemoptysis or reduced fetal movements. [RCOG; MoHFW]`,`Asthma: review **every 4-6 weeks** during pregnancy with symptom control and PEF, check technique and adherence, and give a written action plan. [GINA 2025]`,`TB: monthly clinical review, **weight-band dose adjustment** as weight rises, LFT if symptoms (pregnancy and postpartum increase hepatotoxicity risk), and sputum follow-up as per NTEP. [NTEP 2023]`,`Plan **delivery at a facility with ICU and neonatal care** for severe asthma, DR-TB, PAH or severe pneumonia. [RCOG]`,`Postpartum: continue asthma treatment, reassess step-down after 3 months, plan contraception compatible with rifampicin, and keep infant follow-up for TPT. [GINA 2025; NTEP]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme links`,points:[`**Pre-conception counselling**: achieve asthma control, **do not stop controller inhalers on finding pregnancy**, stop smoking and avoid biomass smoke exposure. [GINA 2025]`,`**Vaccines in pregnancy**: inactivated influenza (any trimester), **Td/Tdap** per schedule, COVID-19, and **RSV prefusion F vaccine at 32-36 weeks** where available for infant protection; see the Adult respiratory vaccines topic. [ACIP 2023-25]`,`TB screening at every ANC contact is built into **PMSMA (9th of every month)**, **Ayushman Arogya Mandir** and NTEP active case finding. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana**, Ni-kshay Mitra food baskets and free diagnosis and drugs under NTEP reduce default among pregnant women. [MoHFW 2024]`,`Teach **red-flag symptoms** (breathlessness at rest, chest pain, haemoptysis, fever with breathlessness, reduced fetal movements) and when to come to hospital. [RCOG]`,`Treat **anaemia** with IFA (NFHS-5 shows half of pregnant women are anaemic) as it worsens tolerance of any lung disease. [Anemia Mukt Bharat 2018]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**GINA 2024-2025** keeps ICS-formoterol (AIR or MART) as acceptable in pregnancy, emphasises not stepping down during pregnancy, and adds neonatal glucose monitoring after high-dose SABA before delivery. [GINA 2025]`,`**BTS/NICE/SIGN 2024** joint asthma guideline replaced BTS/SIGN 2019; the pregnancy advice (treat as non-pregnant, oxygen, steroids not withheld) is unchanged. [BTS/NICE/SIGN 2024]`,`**WHO DR-TB updates (2024-2025)** allow the 6-month **BDLLfxC** regimen in pregnant and breastfeeding women, while BPaLM remains not recommended in pregnancy. [WHO Module 4 2025]`,`**Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana** support was doubled to **Rs 1000 per month** from November 2024. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**RSV prefusion F vaccine (Abrysvo)** was approved for pregnant women at **32-36 weeks** to protect infants (FDA and ACIP 2023). [ACIP 2023]`,`**ESC 2025 cardiovascular disease in pregnancy guideline** updates peripartum cardiomyopathy and PAH advice relevant to the breathless pregnant woman. [ESC 2025]`,`**CDC 2024-25 influenza guidance** reaffirms oseltamivir for all pregnant women with suspected influenza and that baloxavir is not recommended in pregnancy. [CDC 2025]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic viva: **'Normal ABG in pregnancy?'** - pH 7.40-7.45, PaCO2 28-32 mmHg, HCO3 18-22 mmol/L; PaCO2 40 mmHg in an asthma attack is a danger sign. [Williams Obstetrics 26e]`,`Classic MCQ: **'Which anti-TB drug is contraindicated in pregnancy?'** - streptomycin (ototoxic to the fetus); pyrazinamide is safe. [WHO 2022]`,`Common mistake: **stopping or halving inhaled steroids** because of pregnancy - this, not the drug, causes the exacerbation that harms the fetus. [GINA 2025]`,`Common mistake: **delaying oseltamivir for a test** or because more than 48 hours have passed - treat on suspicion at any time in pregnancy. [CDC 2025]`,`Common mistake: **refusing a chest X-ray or CTPA** in pregnancy - fetal dose is tiny and the cost of a missed TB or PE is a maternal death. [ACOG CO 723]`,`Remember the **three drugs to avoid in an asthmatic mother**: carboprost (PPH), labetalol and other non-selective beta-blockers (hypertension), and NSAIDs/aspirin if aspirin-sensitive. [BTS/SIGN 2019]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Respiratory physiology in pregnancy`,columns:[`Parameter`,`Change at term`,`Clinical meaning`],rows:[[`Tidal volume`,`Up 30-50%`,`Drives the rise in minute ventilation`],[`Respiratory rate`,`Unchanged`,`RR over 20-24/min is abnormal, not pregnancy`],[`Minute ventilation`,`Up 30-50%`,`Progesterone effect; physiological dyspnoea`],[`PaCO2 / HCO3`,`28-32 mmHg / 18-22 mmol/L`,`Compensated respiratory alkalosis; PaCO2 40 is a warning`],[`PaO2`,`About 100-105 mmHg`,`Keep maternal SpO2 at 95% or above for the fetus`],[`FRC (ERV + RV)`,`Down about 20%`,`Rapid desaturation during apnoea or intubation`],[`TLC and vital capacity`,`Unchanged or slightly lower`,`Chest widens as diaphragm rises 4 cm`],[`FEV1, FVC, FEV1/FVC, PEF`,`Unchanged`,`Abnormal spirometry or PEF means disease`],[`Oxygen consumption`,`Up 20-30%`,`Less reserve in pneumonia or asthma`],[`DLCO`,`Slightly lower after first trimester`,`Small effect; not diagnostic`]]},{heading:`Respiratory drugs in pregnancy and breastfeeding`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Budesonide-formoterol (ICS-formoterol)`,`200/6 mcg 1 inhalation as needed (AIR) or 1-2 BD plus as needed (MART)`,`Continue throughout pregnancy; do not step down`,`Thrush, dysphonia; safe in pregnancy and lactation`],[`Salbutamol`,`100 mcg 2-4 puffs by spacer as needed; 4-10 puffs q20 min in attack`,`As needed`,`Tremor, tachycardia; neonatal hypoglycaemia after high doses near delivery`],[`Prednisolone`,`40-50 mg orally once daily`,`5-7 days for an exacerbation`,`Hyperglycaemia, possible small oral cleft risk in first trimester; never withhold in severe attack`],[`Hydrocortisone (labour cover)`,`100 mg IV 6-8 hourly`,`During labour if prednisolone over 7.5 mg/day for over 2 weeks`,`Hyperglycaemia; prevents adrenal crisis`],[`Isoniazid + rifampicin + pyrazinamide + ethambutol (FDC)`,`H 5, R 10, Z 25, E 15 mg/kg orally daily by weight band`,`2 months HRZE then 4 months HRE`,`Hepatitis (check LFT if symptoms), rifampicin cuts hormonal contraception; streptomycin contraindicated`],[`Pyridoxine`,`10-25 mg orally daily`,`Throughout isoniazid therapy`,`Prevents isoniazid neuropathy`],[`Oseltamivir`,`75 mg orally BD (30 mg BD if CrCl 30-60)`,`5 days; prophylaxis 75 mg OD for 10 days`,`Nausea, vomiting; safe in all trimesters`],[`Azithromycin`,`500 mg orally or IV once daily`,`3-5 days`,`QT prolongation, GI upset; preferred macrolide (avoid clarithromycin)`],[`Ceftriaxone`,`1-2 g IV once daily`,`5-7 days, switch to oral when stable`,`Allergy; safe in pregnancy`],[`Aciclovir (varicella pneumonia)`,`10 mg/kg IV 8-hourly`,`7-10 days, switch to oral 800 mg 5 times daily when improving`,`Nephrotoxicity - hydrate; dose by renal function`],[`Enoxaparin (LMWH)`,`Prophylaxis 40 mg SC OD (weight-adjusted); treatment 1 mg/kg SC BD`,`Prophylaxis during admission and as risk dictates; treatment for pregnancy plus at least 6 weeks postpartum (minimum 3 months total)`,`Bleeding, HIT (rare); warfarin and DOACs contraindicated in pregnancy`]]},{heading:`Breathlessness in pregnancy - differential diagnosis`,columns:[`Condition`,`Timing`,`Discriminating features`,`Key test`],rows:[[`Physiological dyspnoea`,`First-second trimester, gradual`,`No limitation, normal RR, SpO2 and examination`,`None - diagnosis of exclusion`],[`Asthma exacerbation`,`Commonest 24-36 weeks`,`Wheeze, known asthma, stopped ICS, low PEF`,`PEF, SpO2; ABG if severe`],[`Pneumonia / influenza / COVID-19`,`Any time, seasonal`,`Fever, cough, crackles, consolidation`,`CXR, NAAT/PCR, CBC, CRP`],[`Pulmonary embolism`,`Any trimester, highest postpartum`,`Sudden onset, pleuritic pain, leg swelling, tachycardia`,`Leg CUS, pregnancy-adapted YEARS, CTPA`],[`Peripartum cardiomyopathy`,`Last month to 5 months postpartum`,`Orthopnoea, PND, S3, raised JVP`,`BNP, echo (LVEF under 45%)`],[`Mitral stenosis decompensation`,`Second-third trimester`,`Mid-diastolic murmur, AF, pulmonary oedema`,`Echo`],[`Amniotic fluid embolism`,`Labour or within 30 min of delivery`,`Collapse, hypoxia, DIC, no fever`,`Clinical; coagulation profile`],[`Tocolytic / preeclampsia pulmonary oedema`,`Preterm labour or late pregnancy`,`Beta-agonist or magnesium use, hypertension, proteinuria`,`CXR, BP, urine protein`]]},{heading:`Radiation dose to the fetus from chest imaging`,columns:[`Investigation`,`Approximate fetal dose`,`Comment`],rows:[[`Chest X-ray (2 views)`,`0.0005-0.01 mGy`,`Negligible; never withhold`],[`CT chest / CTPA`,`0.01-0.66 mGy`,`Well below threshold; higher maternal breast dose`],[`Threshold for anomalies or pregnancy loss`,`50 mGy or more`,`No diagnostic chest test approaches this`],[`Compression ultrasound of legs`,`None`,`First test in suspected PE with leg symptoms`],[`Iodinated contrast`,`No radiation; crosses placenta`,`Allowed; newborn thyroid screening covers the risk`],[`Gadolinium MRI`,`No radiation`,`Avoid unless essential`]]},{heading:`Antimicrobials in pregnancy - safe or avoid`,columns:[`Use freely`,`Use with caution`,`Avoid`,`Reason to avoid`],rows:[[`Penicillins (amoxicillin, co-amoxiclav)`,`Fluoroquinolones (only if no alternative)`,`Tetracyclines/doxycycline after first trimester`,`Tooth discolouration, bone growth`],[`Cephalosporins (ceftriaxone, cefuroxime)`,`Co-trimoxazole in second trimester`,`Co-trimoxazole in first trimester and near term`,`Folate antagonism; kernicterus`],[`Azithromycin`,`Metronidazole in first trimester`,`Aminoglycosides including streptomycin`,`Fetal ototoxicity`],[`First-line ATT (HRZE)`,`Linezolid, bedaquiline (DR-TB, specialist)`,`Clarithromycin`,`Animal teratogenicity`],[`Oseltamivir, aciclovir`,`Remdesivir`,`Molnupiravir, baloxavir`,`Mutagenic potential; no human data`]]}],redFlags:[`SpO2 under 95% on air, or RR over 24/min, in a pregnant woman`,`Inability to complete sentences, silent chest, drowsiness, or PaCO2 of 35-40 mmHg or more during an asthma attack`,`Fever with breathlessness in pregnancy or up to 2 weeks postpartum (influenza, COVID-19, varicella pneumonia)`,`Sudden breathlessness, pleuritic pain, haemoptysis or unilateral leg swelling (suspected PE)`,`Orthopnoea, PND or new murmur in late pregnancy or postpartum (cardiomyopathy, mitral stenosis)`,`Collapse with hypoxia and bleeding during labour (amniotic fluid embolism)`,`Reduced fetal movements during a respiratory illness`,`Rifampicin-resistant TB on NAAT in a pregnant woman`],pearls:[`Pregnancy raises tidal volume, not respiratory rate - **tachypnoea is never physiological**.`,`**PaCO2 40 mmHg in a pregnant asthmatic = respiratory failure** until proved otherwise.`,`**Rule of thirds** in asthma: one-third better, one-third same, one-third worse; worst at 24-36 weeks.`,`**Stopping ICS**, not taking ICS, is the main cause of asthma attacks in pregnancy.`,`**Streptomycin is the only first-line-era ATT contraindicated** in pregnancy; HRZE and pyridoxine are given.`,`**Treat influenza on suspicion** with oseltamivir at any gestation and up to 2 weeks postpartum - do not wait for the swab.`,`A chest X-ray gives the fetus about **1/5000th to 1/100,000th of the 50 mGy threshold** - never withhold it.`,`**Avoid carboprost and labetalol** in asthmatic mothers; oxytocin and nifedipine are safe.`,`In COVID-19 needing oxygen use **prednisolone or hydrocortisone**, not dexamethasone, unless fetal lung maturity steroids are also due.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-lung-pregnancy-q1`,stem:`A 28-year-old woman at 32 weeks of gestation presents with an acute asthma attack. She is speaking in phrases, RR 28/min, SpO2 93% on air. ABG shows pH 7.38, PaCO2 40 mmHg, PaO2 68 mmHg. What is the best interpretation of the PaCO2?`,options:[`Normal, as it is within the non-pregnant reference range`,`Reassuring, showing she is not hyperventilating excessively`,`Abnormally high for pregnancy, suggesting tiring and impending respiratory failure`,`Expected compensation for a metabolic alkalosis of pregnancy`],answer:2,explanation:`Normal pregnancy causes a compensated respiratory alkalosis with PaCO2 28-32 mmHg. In an acute attack PaCO2 should be even lower from hyperventilation, so a 'normal' 40 mmHg means she is tiring and hypoventilating - a life-threatening sign needing ICU input. Calling it normal uses non-pregnant reference values; pregnancy causes a respiratory, not metabolic, alkalosis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-lung-pregnancy-q2`,stem:`A 24-year-old primigravida at 14 weeks has sputum NAAT positive for M. tuberculosis, rifampicin resistance not detected. Which drug should NOT be part of her treatment?`,options:[`Pyrazinamide`,`Ethambutol`,`Rifampicin`,`Streptomycin`],answer:3,explanation:`Streptomycin, an aminoglycoside, crosses the placenta and causes fetal eighth-nerve damage and congenital deafness, so it is contraindicated. Isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide and ethambutol are all considered safe in pregnancy by WHO and NTEP, and the standard 2HRZE/4HRE regimen with pyridoxine is started without delay.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-lung-pregnancy-q3`,stem:`A woman at 26 weeks of gestation has had fever, myalgia and cough for 3 days during the influenza season. She is alert, SpO2 97%, RR 18/min. What is the most appropriate management?`,options:[`Start oseltamivir 75 mg twice daily for 5 days now, without waiting for a swab result`,`Symptomatic treatment only, as more than 48 hours have passed since onset`,`Send a throat swab and start oseltamivir only if positive`,`Prescribe baloxavir as a single dose for convenience`],answer:0,explanation:`Pregnant women are a high-risk group (MoHFW Category B(ii)), so oseltamivir should start on clinical suspicion without waiting for a test; benefit persists even after 48 hours in high-risk patients. Withholding treatment because 48 hours have passed is a classic error, waiting for a swab delays effective therapy, and baloxavir is not recommended in pregnancy for lack of data.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-lung-pregnancy-q4`,stem:`A 30-year-old asthmatic woman develops postpartum haemorrhage due to uterine atony that does not respond to oxytocin. Which uterotonic should be avoided?`,options:[`Misoprostol`,`Carboprost (15-methyl prostaglandin F2-alpha)`,`Oxytocin infusion`,`Tranexamic acid as an adjunct`],answer:1,explanation:`Carboprost is a prostaglandin F2-alpha analogue and a potent bronchoconstrictor, so it can precipitate severe bronchospasm in asthma. Misoprostol, a PGE1 analogue, and oxytocin do not cause bronchospasm and are safe; tranexamic acid is an antifibrinolytic with no airway effect and is recommended in PPH.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-lung-pregnancy-q5`,stem:`A 31-year-old woman at 30 weeks has sudden pleuritic chest pain and breathlessness, with no leg symptoms. Using the pregnancy-adapted YEARS algorithm, which statement is correct?`,options:[`D-dimer is useless in pregnancy and CTPA must be done in every case`,`If she has no YEARS items, a D-dimer under 1000 ng/mL safely excludes PE without imaging`,`A V/Q scan is mandatory because CTPA exposes the fetus to over 50 mGy`,`Warfarin should be started empirically while awaiting imaging`],answer:1,explanation:`The Artemis study validated pregnancy-adapted YEARS: with no YEARS item (clinical DVT signs, haemoptysis, PE the most likely diagnosis) a D-dimer under 1000 ng/mL excludes PE, avoiding CTPA in about 39%. D-dimer is therefore still useful, CTPA delivers well under 1 mGy to the fetus (not over 50 mGy), and warfarin is teratogenic - LMWH is used while awaiting imaging.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-lung-pregnancy-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Describe the changes in respiratory physiology during pregnancy. Discuss the management of bronchial asthma in pregnancy, including acute exacerbation and labour.`,openingLines:[`Pregnancy raises minute ventilation by 30-50% through a progesterone-driven rise in tidal volume, producing a compensated respiratory alkalosis (PaCO2 28-32 mmHg) and a 20% fall in FRC, while FEV1 and PEF are unchanged.`,`Asthma affects 3-8% of pregnancies; uncontrolled asthma - not asthma drugs - causes preeclampsia, preterm birth and low birth weight, so active treatment is recommended (GINA 2025).`],answer:[{heading:`Respiratory physiology`,points:[`Tidal volume and minute ventilation up 30-50%; respiratory rate unchanged.`,`PaCO2 28-32 mmHg, HCO3 18-22 mmol/L, PaO2 100-105 mmHg - compensated respiratory alkalosis.`,`Diaphragm up 4 cm, subcostal angle widens; FRC, ERV and RV down about 20%; TLC and VC preserved.`,`Oxygen consumption up 20-30%; rapid desaturation in apnoea; spirometry and PEF unchanged.`,`Physiological dyspnoea in 60-70%; upper airway oedema makes intubation difficult.`]},{heading:`Effect of pregnancy on asthma and asthma on pregnancy`,points:[`Rule of thirds; worst at 24-36 weeks; uncommon in labour.`,`Triggers: stopping ICS, viral infection, GERD, rhinitis.`,`Uncontrolled asthma: preeclampsia, preterm birth, LBW, SGA, perinatal death.`]},{heading:`Chronic management`,points:[`Confirm diagnosis (spirometry valid); assess control every 4-6 weeks with PEF.`,`Continue ICS (budesonide most data) or ICS-formoterol AIR/MART; do not step down in pregnancy.`,`Montelukast if responded before; theophylline with level monitoring; biologics case by case.`,`Inhaler technique, adherence, trigger and smoke avoidance, influenza and COVID-19 vaccination, written action plan.`]},{heading:`Acute exacerbation`,points:[`Assess severity; SpO2 target 95% or above; fetal monitoring after viability.`,`Salbutamol by spacer or nebuliser, ipratropium if severe, prednisolone 40-50 mg within 1 hour.`,`IV magnesium sulphate 2 g over 20 minutes if severe; ICU if PaCO2 35-40 mmHg or more, drowsy or silent chest.`,`Low threshold to admit; joint care with obstetrician.`]},{heading:`Labour, delivery and postpartum`,points:[`Continue inhalers; hydrocortisone 100 mg IV 6-8 hourly if prednisolone over 7.5 mg/day for over 2 weeks.`,`Prefer regional anaesthesia; avoid carboprost for PPH; avoid labetalol for hypertension.`,`Neonatal glucose check after high-dose SABA; breastfeeding safe with all asthma drugs.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table of respiratory physiological changes in pregnancy (parameter, change, meaning)`,`Flow chart of acute asthma management in pregnancy`],markSplit:[{part:`Respiratory physiology`,marks:3},{part:`Interaction of asthma and pregnancy`,marks:1},{part:`Chronic management and drug safety`,marks:3},{part:`Acute attack, labour and postpartum`,marks:3}],keywords:[`tidal volume`,`PaCO2 28-32`,`FRC`,`rule of thirds`,`budesonide`,`carboprost`,`hydrocortisone cover`]},{id:`respiratory-lung-pregnancy-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on tuberculosis in pregnancy.`,openingLines:[`About 2.2 lakh pregnant women develop active TB each year worldwide; maternal TB raises perinatal death about 6-fold and low birth weight about 2-fold (Jana, India).`,`First-line ATT (2HRZE/4HRE) is safe in pregnancy and must not be delayed; only streptomycin is contraindicated.`],answer:[{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Four-symptom screen at every ANC visit; NAAT on sputum upfront.`,`CXR with abdominal shielding is safe; IGRA/TST valid; test for HIV and diabetes.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`2HRZE/4HRE daily weight-band FDC plus pyridoxine 10-25 mg daily.`,`Avoid streptomycin (fetal ototoxicity); 4-month HPMZ regimen not recommended.`,`DR-TB at specialist centre; BDLLfxC allowed, BPaLM not used in pregnancy (WHO 2025).`]},{heading:`Neonate, breastfeeding and contraception`,points:[`Exclude congenital TB (Cantwell criteria), then 6 months isoniazid TPT for the newborn.`,`Breastfeed; mother masks until non-infectious; no separation.`,`Rifampicin reduces hormonal contraception - use copper IUD or DMPA.`]},{heading:`Programme support`,points:[`Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana Rs 1000/month, Ni-kshay Mitra, free drugs and NAAT under NTEP.`]}],mustDraw:[`Box: safe vs avoid ATT drugs in pregnancy`],markSplit:[{part:`Burden and effects`,marks:1},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:1},{part:`Treatment and drug safety`,marks:2},{part:`Neonate, breastfeeding, contraception`,marks:1}],keywords:[`streptomycin`,`pyridoxine`,`Cantwell criteria`,`isoniazid TPT newborn`,`Ni-kshay Poshan`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`Global Initiative for Asthma (GINA) Global Strategy for Asthma Management and Prevention, 2025 update`,`BTS/NICE/SIGN Asthma: diagnosis, monitoring and chronic asthma management, 2024; BTS/SIGN British Guideline on the Management of Asthma, 2019`,`National TB Elimination Programme (NTEP) Training Modules and Guidelines for Programmatic Management of TB Preventive Treatment in India, MoHFW, 2021-2023`,`WHO Consolidated Guidelines on Tuberculosis, Module 4: Treatment, 2022 and 2025 updates`,`ACOG Committee Opinion No. 723: Guidelines for Diagnostic Imaging During Pregnancy and Lactation, 2017 (reaffirmed)`,`CDC Influenza Antiviral Medications: Summary for Clinicians, 2024-25; MoHFW Guidelines on Categorisation of Seasonal Influenza Cases`,`RCOG Coronavirus (COVID-19) Infection in Pregnancy, 2022; ESC Guidelines on Acute Pulmonary Embolism, 2019; ESC Guidelines on Cardiovascular Disease and Pregnancy, 2025`]}),d.push({id:`respiratory-lung-systemic-disease`,title:`The lung in systemic disease: hepatopulmonary syndrome, sickle cell, CKD, HIV and rheumatological`,oneLiner:`The lung is a target organ in liver, blood, kidney, HIV and rheumatic disease: **hepatopulmonary syndrome** is liver disease or portal hypertension with **intrapulmonary vascular dilatation** and an **A-a gradient of 15 mmHg or more** (platypnoea-orthodeoxia, delayed bubbles on contrast echo, cured by transplant); **portopulmonary hypertension** is PAH (mPAP over 20, PVR over 2 WU, PAWP 15 or less) with portal hypertension; **acute chest syndrome** is a new infiltrate plus fever or respiratory symptoms in sickle cell disease; and the uraemic, HIV and rheumatoid lung each follow a predictable pattern.`,frequency:`less-common`,keywords:[`hepatopulmonary syndrome`,`HPS`,`platypnoea-orthodeoxia`,`agitated saline contrast echocardiography`,`Tc-99m MAA scan`,`portopulmonary hypertension`,`hepatic hydrothorax`,`acute chest syndrome`,`sickle cell disease India`,`hydroxyurea`,`exchange transfusion`,`uraemic lung`,`metastatic pulmonary calcification`,`HIV lung CD4`,`Pneumocystis`,`rheumatoid nodule`,`Caplan syndrome`,`rheumatoid pleural effusion`,`shrinking lung syndrome`,`macitentan`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`The lung receives the **entire cardiac output**, filters venous blood and shares connective tissue and immune targets with other organs - so liver, haematological, renal, infectious and autoimmune diseases all show in the lung. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hepatopulmonary syndrome (HPS)** occurs in about **10-30% of cirrhotic patients evaluated for liver transplant**; **portopulmonary hypertension (POPH)** in about **5-6%**. [ERS/ILTS Task Force 2016; ILTS 2016]`,`India has a large and rising cirrhosis burden (alcohol, MASLD, hepatitis B and C) and a distinctive non-cirrhotic cause - **extrahepatic portal vein obstruction (EHPVO)** - in which HPS also occurs, especially in children. [INASL]`,`India has one of the world's largest **sickle cell disease** burdens; carrier rates reach **up to 35-40% in some tribal communities** of central and western India (Chhattisgarh, Madhya Pradesh, Maharashtra, Odisha, Gujarat, Jharkhand). [ICMR; NSCAEM 2023]`,`**Acute chest syndrome (ACS)** is the **leading cause of death** and the **second commonest cause of admission** in sickle cell disease, after the painful crisis. [Harrison 22e; NHLBI 2014]`,`India has about **25 lakh people living with HIV (adult prevalence about 0.2%)**, and **TB is the commonest opportunistic infection and leading cause of death** among them. [NACO HIV Estimations 2023]`,`Chronic kidney disease affects about **10-17% of Indian adults (SEEK-India)**; dialysis patients have several-fold higher TB risk and frequent fluid-related lung disease. [SEEK-India 2013; NTEP]`,`**Rheumatoid arthritis** affects about **0.75% of Indian adults**; lung disease (ILD, nodules, pleura, airways) occurs in up to 30-40% and is the second commonest cause of RA death. [Malaviya et al 1993; Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Hepatopulmonary syndrome - pathophysiology`,points:[`**Defining triad**: liver disease or portal hypertension + **intrapulmonary vascular dilatation (IPVD)** + abnormal arterial oxygenation (A-a gradient 15 mmHg or more). [ERS/ILTS 2016]`,`Portal hypertension and endotoxaemia raise **endothelin-1**, which acts on **ETB receptors** to increase endothelial **NO synthase**; bacterial translocation recruits macrophages that make **iNOS and haem oxygenase-1 (carbon monoxide)** - both dilate pulmonary capillaries. [Harrison 22e]`,`**VEGF-driven angiogenesis** adds new vessels, and capillaries dilate from the normal **8-15 micrometres to as much as 500 micrometres**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Oxygen cannot diffuse to red cells in the centre of a wide capillary (**diffusion-perfusion defect**), giving hypoxaemia that **improves with 100% oxygen** - unlike true anatomical shunts and direct AV communications, which do not. [ERS/ILTS 2016]`,`Dilatations are **basal**: upright posture sends more blood through basal vessels, so **PaO2 falls on standing (orthodeoxia)** and breathlessness worsens upright (**platypnoea**). [Harrison 22e]`,`The hyperdynamic circulation of cirrhosis (high output, low systemic resistance) shortens red-cell transit time and worsens the diffusion limit. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Severity of HPS does not correlate with Child-Pugh or MELD score** - HPS can occur in compensated cirrhosis and in non-cirrhotic portal hypertension (EHPVO, Abernethy malformation). [ERS/ILTS 2016]`]},{heading:`Hepatopulmonary syndrome - clinical features, diagnosis and grading`,points:[`Clues: **progressive exertional dyspnoea, cyanosis, clubbing and spider naevi** (a marker of systemic vasodilatation) in a patient with liver disease; spider naevi are more numerous in HPS. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Orthodeoxia** = fall in PaO2 of **5% or more, or 4 mmHg or more**, from supine to upright; seen in about 25% of HPS and highly suggestive. [ERS/ILTS 2016]`,`**Screen with pulse oximetry**: SpO2 **under 96%** in a cirrhotic patient should prompt ABG - it detects most patients with PaO2 under 70 mmHg. [AASLD/ILTS 2016]`,`**ABG** sitting at rest on room air: **A-a gradient 15 mmHg or more (20 mmHg or more if aged over 64)** defines abnormal oxygenation. [ERS/ILTS 2016]`,`**Transthoracic contrast echocardiography with agitated saline** is the diagnostic test for IPVD: microbubbles appear in the left heart **3 or more cardiac cycles** after the right heart; bubbles within 3 beats mean an **intracardiac shunt (PFO, ASD)**. [ILTS 2016]`,`**Tc-99m macroaggregated albumin (MAA) perfusion scan**: **brain uptake over 6%** shows shunting and quantifies it; it helps decide how much hypoxaemia is due to HPS when COPD or ILD co-exists. [ERS/ILTS 2016]`,`**Grading by PaO2 (with A-a 15 or more)**: mild **80 mmHg or more**, moderate **60-79**, severe **50-59**, very severe **under 50 mmHg**. [ERS/ILTS 2016]`,`Spirometry and CXR are usually normal or show only basal reticular shadowing; **DLCO is reduced** - always exclude co-existing COPD (alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency), effusion and ascites. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Hepatopulmonary syndrome - management and prognosis`,points:[`**Long-term oxygen therapy** for resting PaO2 under 55-60 mmHg or exertional desaturation; it relieves symptoms but does not reverse the disease. [AASLD/ILTS 2016]`,`**No drug is proven**: garlic, methylene blue, pentoxifylline, norfloxacin and somatostatin analogues have failed or are experimental; **TIPS is not a treatment** for HPS. [ILTS 2016]`,`**Liver transplantation is the only cure**: oxygenation normalises in **over 80%** of patients within 6-12 months. [ILTS 2016]`,`HPS with **PaO2 under 60 mmHg** earns **MELD exception points** in the US allocation system because untransplanted mortality is high. [UNOS/OPTN]`,`Without transplant, HPS roughly **doubles mortality** compared with cirrhosis alone; median survival is about 2 years in severe disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PaO2 of 44-50 mmHg or less** before transplant predicts **severe post-transplant hypoxaemia** and higher peri-operative mortality - refer early. [ILTS 2016]`]},{heading:`Portopulmonary hypertension and other liver-lung disease`,points:[`**POPH** = pulmonary arterial hypertension (**mPAP over 20 mmHg, PVR over 2 Wood units, PAWP 15 mmHg or less** on right heart catheterisation) with portal hypertension and no other cause - WHO **Group 1 PAH**. [ESC/ERS PH 2022]`,`Mechanism is the **opposite of HPS**: vasoconstriction, **endothelin and thromboxane excess, prostacyclin deficiency**, shear stress from high flow, and vasoactive substances bypassing the liver through portosystemic shunts, causing plexogenic arteriopathy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Screen every liver transplant candidate by transthoracic echo**; RVSP over about **45-50 mmHg** or RV dysfunction leads to **right heart catheterisation**. [AASLD 2022; ILTS 2016]`,`Treat with **PAH drugs**: endothelin receptor antagonists (**macitentan 10 mg OD**, proven in the **PORTICO trial 2019**; ambrisentan), PDE5 inhibitors (sildenafil, tadalafil) and prostacyclins. [ESC/ERS PH 2022]`,`**Stop non-selective beta-blockers** (propranolol, carvedilol) in POPH - they lower cardiac output and exercise capacity; **avoid TIPS**, which raises preload. [ESC/ERS PH 2022]`,`**Transplant**: mPAP **under 35 mmHg** - proceed; **35-45 mmHg** - proceed only if PVR is under 5 WU (some centres 3) after PAH treatment; **45-50 mmHg or more** despite treatment - contraindicated (high peri-operative death). [ILTS 2016]`,`**HPS vs POPH**: HPS is a dilated, low-resistance circulation (cyanosis, orthodeoxia, positive bubble study); POPH is a constricted, high-resistance circulation (loud P2, RV heave, syncope, raised PVR). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hepatic hydrothorax**: a **transudative effusion, right-sided in about 85%**, over 500 mL, in cirrhosis without heart or lung disease - ascites passes through diaphragmatic defects; treat with sodium restriction, diuretics and TIPS, and **avoid an indwelling chest drain**; see the Pleural effusion topic. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency** causes both cirrhosis and **panacinar lower-lobe emphysema** in a young patient - check the AAT level in early COPD with liver disease. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Sickle cell disease - why the lung is a target`,points:[`A single **glutamate to valine substitution at position 6 of the beta chain** (HbS) makes deoxygenated haemoglobin polymerise, so red cells sickle, become rigid, adhere to endothelium and cause **vaso-occlusion and haemolysis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`The lung is a **vicious circle**: hypoxia causes more sickling, sickling occludes pulmonary vessels, occlusion worsens V/Q mismatch and hypoxia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Haemolysis releases free haemoglobin**, which scavenges **nitric oxide**, causing endothelial dysfunction - the basis of sickle-associated pulmonary hypertension and leg ulcers. [Harrison 22e]`,`The Indian **Arab-Indian haplotype** carries **higher HbF**, so disease is often milder than African haplotypes, but severe crises and ACS still occur and deaths are under-recorded in tribal areas. [ICMR]`,`**ACS triggers** (NACSS study): **infection** (Chlamydophila, Mycoplasma, viruses, pneumococcus), **fat embolism** from infarcted bone marrow, **in situ thrombosis**, and **hypoventilation from rib infarcts or excess opioids** - a cause is found in under half. [Vichinsky et al, NEJM 2000]`,`ACS often develops **24-72 hours after admission for a painful crisis**, especially with chest, back or abdominal pain and opioid use - hence incentive spirometry in every crisis. [BSH 2015]`]},{heading:`Acute chest syndrome - diagnosis and management`,points:[`**Definition**: a **new segmental (not atelectatic) infiltrate on chest X-ray** plus at least one of **fever over 38.5 degrees C, chest pain, tachypnoea, wheeze or cough, or hypoxaemia**. [NHLBI 2014; BSH 2015]`,`Signs may precede X-ray change, so **repeat the CXR** in a deteriorating patient; check SpO2 frequently and ABG if SpO2 falls. [BSH 2015]`,`**Oxygen** to keep **SpO2 95% or above** (or at baseline); **incentive spirometry every 2 hours while awake**. [BSH 2015]`,`**Antibiotics**: an IV **third-generation cephalosporin (ceftriaxone 2 g OD or cefotaxime)** plus a **macrolide (azithromycin 500 mg OD)**; add oseltamivir in the influenza season. [NHLBI 2014; BSH 2015]`,`**Analgesia** with careful opioid titration (enough to allow deep breathing, not so much as to cause hypoventilation); **fluids at maintenance only** - overload causes pulmonary oedema. [BSH 2015]`,`**Simple (top-up) transfusion** if Hb has fallen **more than 1 g/dL below baseline** or there is hypoxaemia; do not raise Hb above about **10-11 g/dL** (hyperviscosity). [NHLBI 2014; BSH 2015]`,`**Exchange transfusion** (target **HbS under 30%**) for severe or progressing ACS: SpO2 under 90-95% despite oxygen, rising work of breathing, multilobar infiltrates or falling Hb despite top-up. [NHLBI 2014]`,`**Bronchodilators** if wheeze or known asthma; **thromboprophylaxis with LMWH** in adults; ICU early for any deterioration. [BSH 2015]`]},{heading:`Chronic sickle lung, prevention and the national mission`,points:[`**Hydroxyurea** (raises HbF) roughly **halves ACS and painful crises** (MSH trial 1995); start **15-20 mg/kg/day** and titrate by 5 mg/kg every 8 weeks to a maximum of **35 mg/kg/day** with blood count monitoring. [NHLBI 2014; MSH, NEJM 1995]`,`Offer hydroxyurea to adults with **3 or more painful crises a year or any ACS**, and to all children from **9 months**; it is available free under the national mission. [NHLBI 2014; NSCAEM 2023]`,`**Chronic transfusion** is used for recurrent ACS despite hydroxyurea; **haematopoietic stem cell transplant** and **gene therapy** (exa-cel, approved by FDA December 2023) are curative options. [ASH 2023]`,`**Sickle pulmonary hypertension**: TRV on echo of **2.5 m/s or more** marks higher mortality; confirm by right heart catheterisation before PAH therapy. [ATS 2014]`,`**Chronic sickle lung disease** (restrictive defect, fibrosis after repeated ACS), **VTE** and **co-existing asthma** (which raises ACS risk) all need follow-up. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Prevention of infection**: penicillin prophylaxis in children, pneumococcal, Hib, meningococcal and **annual influenza vaccines**, and malaria prevention in endemic tribal districts. [NHLBI 2014]`,`**National Sickle Cell Anaemia Elimination Mission** was launched on **1 July 2023 at Shahdol (MP)** to eliminate SCD as a public-health problem by **2047**; it aims to screen **7 crore people aged 0-40 years** in 17 high-burden states and issue genetic status cards. [MoHFW/MoTA 2023]`]},{heading:`The kidney and the lung - uraemic lung and CKD`,points:[`**Uraemic lung** = **perihilar 'bat-wing' pulmonary oedema** in advanced kidney failure from **volume overload plus raised capillary permeability**; it may occur at normal wedge pressure and **clears with dialysis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Uraemic pleuritis** is a **fibrinous, exudative, often haemorrhagic** effusion in patients on dialysis; it usually resolves with intensified dialysis but can leave a fibrothorax. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Metastatic pulmonary calcification** (calcium phosphate in alveolar septa, upper lobes) follows a high **calcium-phosphate product and secondary hyperparathyroidism**; it shows on **Tc-99m MDP bone scan** and HRCT. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pulmonary-renal syndromes** - anti-GBM disease (Goodpasture), ANCA vasculitis (GPA, MPA) and SLE - present with haemoptysis plus rapidly progressive GN; see the Diffuse alveolar haemorrhage topic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nephrotic syndrome**: transudative effusions and **pulmonary embolism** from renal vein thrombosis (highest in **membranous nephropathy** with albumin under 2.5 g/dL). [KDIGO 2021]`,`**Peritoneal dialysis hydrothorax**: dialysate crosses the diaphragm into the **right pleural space**; pleural fluid has **very high glucose relative to serum** and low protein. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TB risk is much higher in CKD and dialysis** (often extrapulmonary, with low-grade fever and weight loss); screen at dialysis initiation and before transplant, and dose-adjust ethambutol and pyrazinamide (thrice weekly after dialysis). [NTEP 2023; WHO 2022]`,`**Pulmonary hypertension (WHO Group 5)** occurs in CKD and dialysis, partly from **high-flow AV fistulae**, volume and anaemia. [ESC/ERS PH 2022]`]},{heading:`HIV and the lung - a CD4-based approach`,points:[`**Any CD4 count**: bacterial pneumonia (pneumococcus, H. influenzae - several-fold commoner than in HIV-negative), TB, influenza, COPD and lung cancer. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CD4 over 350**: TB looks **typical (upper lobe, cavitation, smear-positive)**; **CD4 under 200**: TB looks **atypical** - lower or mid-zone, no cavity, lymphadenopathy, miliary, effusion, smear-negative or even a **normal CXR**. [Harrison 22e; WHO 2024]`,`**CD4 under 200: Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PCP)** - subacute dry cough, exertional desaturation, bilateral perihilar ground-glass, **raised LDH and beta-D-glucan**, pneumothorax; treat with high-dose co-trimoxazole for **21 days**; see the immunocompromised infections topic. [NIH OI Guidelines 2024]`,`**CD4 under 100**: cryptococcus, histoplasmosis and **Talaromyces marneffei** (endemic in **Manipur and north-east India** - umbilicated skin papules); **CD4 under 50**: **MAC and CMV**. [NIH OI Guidelines 2024]`,`**Non-infectious HIV lung**: Kaposi sarcoma, lymphoma, **HIV-associated PAH**, emphysema at a young age, and **lymphocytic interstitial pneumonitis** (mainly children). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Urine LF-LAM** (AlereLAM) is recommended for TB testing in PLHIV who are **seriously ill or have CD4 200 or less (100 or less in outpatients)**, alongside sputum NAAT. [WHO Module 3 2024; NACO]`,`**Start ART within 2 weeks of starting ATT** (except TB meningitis, delay 4-8 weeks); give **co-trimoxazole prophylaxis** to PLHIV with CD4 under 350 or WHO stage 3-4, and TPT (6H or 3HP) after excluding active TB. [NACO 2021; WHO 2021]`,`**IRIS**: worsening fever, lymph nodes or infiltrates **2-12 weeks after ART starts**; continue ART and give prednisolone for moderate-severe TB-IRIS. [WHO 2021]`]},{heading:`Rheumatoid arthritis - nodules, Caplan and the pleura`,points:[`**RA lung** includes ILD (**UIP pattern commonest in RA**, unlike NSIP in other CTDs), pleural disease, nodules, bronchiectasis, obliterative and follicular bronchiolitis, and drug toxicity; see the CTD-ILD topic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Risk factors**: **male sex, smoking, high-titre RF and anti-CCP**, and long-standing seropositive nodular disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rheumatoid (necrobiotic) nodules**: single or multiple, **subpleural, 0.5-5 cm**, in seropositive men who smoke with subcutaneous nodules; they may **cavitate** and cause **pneumothorax or bronchopleural fistula** - exclude TB and cancer. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Caplan syndrome** (Caplan, Welsh coal miners, 1953): RA plus coal, silica or asbestos dust exposure causing **multiple well-defined 0.5-5 cm peripheral nodules** that appear rapidly, often before arthritis; seen in Indian silica-exposed miners and stone workers. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rheumatoid pleural effusion**: exudate with **very low glucose (under 30 mg/dL)**, **low pH (under 7.20)**, **high LDH (over 700 IU/L)**, high RF, low complement and sometimes **cholesterol crystals (pseudochylothorax)**. [Light's Pleural Diseases 6e]`,`**Cricoarytenoid arthritis** causes hoarseness and **stridor**, and **atlanto-axial subluxation** threatens intubation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drug lung in RA**: methotrexate pneumonitis (rare, subacute, eosinophilia, early in treatment), and **TB reactivation with anti-TNF and JAK inhibitors** - screen with IGRA or TST and CXR, and give TPT before starting. [ACR 2022; NTEP TPT 2021]`,`Recent cohorts show **methotrexate does not cause RA-ILD** and may delay it, so pre-existing mild ILD is not an absolute bar to methotrexate. [Juge et al, Eur Respir J 2021]`]},{heading:`SLE, systemic sclerosis, myositis and other systemic diseases`,points:[`**SLE**: **pleuritis is the commonest lung lesion (30-50%)**, plus acute lupus pneumonitis, **diffuse alveolar haemorrhage**, **shrinking lung syndrome** (diaphragm weakness, restrictive PFT, clear lung fields), PAH and **PE from antiphospholipid antibodies**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Systemic sclerosis**: ILD (NSIP, **anti-Scl-70/topoisomerase I**, diffuse cutaneous) and **PAH** (limited cutaneous, **anticentromere**) are the **two leading causes of death**; screen with annual PFT with DLCO and echo. [EULAR 2023]`,`An **FVC/DLCO ratio over 1.6**, or isolated low DLCO, in scleroderma suggests **PAH** rather than ILD. [ESC/ERS PH 2022]`,`**Inflammatory myositis**: **anti-synthetase syndrome (anti-Jo-1)** with ILD, mechanic's hands and arthritis; **anti-MDA5 dermatomyositis** with rapidly progressive ILD and high mortality. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sjogren syndrome**: lymphocytic interstitial pneumonia, follicular bronchiolitis, **thin-walled cysts** and **MALT lymphoma**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ankylosing spondylitis**: chest wall restriction and **apical fibrobullous disease** that mimics TB and can harbour aspergilloma. [Harrison 22e]`,`Other systemic links: **IBD** (bronchiectasis, large-airway inflammation), **diabetes** (TB 2-3 fold), **hypothyroidism** (effusion, OSA), **amyloidosis** and **sarcoidosis** (see its own topic). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Approach in the OPD - examination clues and investigations`,points:[`Ask in every breathless patient about **liver disease, alcohol, sickle cell status, kidney disease, HIV risk and joint, skin or Raynaud's symptoms** - the systemic disease often explains the lung. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bedside clues**: spider naevi, clubbing and cyanosis (HPS); jaundice with loud P2 (POPH); icterus, pallor and bossing in a tribal patient (sickle cell); oral thrush and lymph nodes (HIV); joint deformity and subcutaneous nodules (RA); sclerodactyly and telangiectasia (SSc). [Harrison 22e]`,`**First line**: SpO2 lying and standing, CBC, LFT, creatinine, urine protein, HIV test, **sickling test (solubility) and HPLC**, chest X-ray and sputum NAAT. [ICMR STW]`,`**SpO2 that falls on sitting or standing** in a cirrhotic patient points to HPS; confirm with ABG and **agitated saline contrast echo**. [ERS/ILTS 2016]`,`**Tertiary tests**: HRCT, PFT with DLCO, Tc-99m MAA scan, right heart catheterisation, bronchoscopy with BAL (PCP, TB, DAH), autoantibody panel (RF, anti-CCP, ANA, anti-Scl-70, myositis panel, ANCA, anti-GBM). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pleural fluid clues**: transudate (hepatic hydrothorax, nephrotic, PD), very low glucose and pH (rheumatoid), haemorrhagic exudate (uraemic), high ADA (TB - common in HIV and CKD). [Light's Pleural Diseases 6e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and prevention`,points:[`**Refer to a liver transplant centre** any cirrhotic patient with HPS (especially PaO2 under 60 mmHg) or POPH, with ABG every 6 months while listed. [ILTS 2016]`,`**Sickle cell**: register under NSCAEM, start hydroxyurea, keep vaccines up to date, teach **fever and chest pain as emergencies**, and review CBC every 4-8 weeks on hydroxyurea; annual echo from adolescence where available. [NSCAEM 2023; NHLBI 2014]`,`**HIV**: ART, co-trimoxazole prophylaxis and TPT under NACO and NTEP; four-symptom TB screen at every visit. [NACO 2021]`,`**CKD and dialysis**: control volume and phosphate, TB screening at dialysis start, influenza and pneumococcal vaccination. [KDIGO; NTEP]`,`**RA and CTD**: baseline CXR and PFT with DLCO, smoking cessation, IGRA before biologics, and HRCT for any new cough or breathlessness. [ACR 2022]`,`Programme links: **NSCAEM** (sickle cell), **NACO/NACP Phase V** (HIV), **NTEP** (TB in all these groups), **Pradhan Mantri National Dialysis Programme** (CKD) and **NP-NCD** (screening at Ayushman Arogya Mandir). [MoHFW 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**ESC/ERS 2022 PH guideline** lowered the PH threshold to **mPAP over 20 mmHg** and PAH to **PVR over 2 WU**, which now defines POPH. [ESC/ERS PH 2022]`,`**National Sickle Cell Anaemia Elimination Mission (July 2023)** made screening, genetic cards and free hydroxyurea a national programme. [MoHFW/MoTA 2023]`,`**Voxelotor (Oxbryta) was withdrawn worldwide in September 2024** because of excess deaths and vaso-occlusive crises; **crizanlizumab** failed to reduce crises in the STAND trial. [FDA 2024]`,`**Gene therapies exa-cel (CRISPR) and lovo-cel** were approved for severe sickle cell disease by the US FDA in **December 2023**. [FDA 2023]`,`**WHO 2024 TB diagnostics update** keeps urine LF-LAM for PLHIV and endorses targeted next-generation NAATs; NACO continues TPT and co-trimoxazole prophylaxis. [WHO Module 3 2024]`,`**EULAR 2023 systemic sclerosis recommendations** add nintedanib and tocilizumab for SSc-ILD and reinforce annual PAH screening. [EULAR 2023]`,`**ACR/CHEST 2023 guideline on ILD in rheumatic disease** recommends HRCT and PFT screening for at-risk RA, SSc, myositis and Sjogren patients. [ACR/CHEST 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic viva: **'What is platypnoea-orthodeoxia?'** - breathlessness and desaturation on sitting up that improve lying down; causes are **HPS, PFO/ASD with right-to-left shunt, and basal pulmonary AVMs**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Classic MCQ: **contrast echo in HPS** shows bubbles in the left heart **after 3 or more beats**; within 3 beats means intracardiac shunt. [ILTS 2016]`,`Common mistake: giving **beta-blockers or TIPS** to a patient with POPH - both worsen right heart failure. [ESC/ERS PH 2022]`,`Common mistake: **overhydrating** a sickle patient in crisis - fluid overload precipitates ACS and pulmonary oedema. [BSH 2015]`,`Remember the **rheumatoid effusion signature**: glucose under 30, pH under 7.2, LDH high, RF high, complement low. [Light's Pleural Diseases 6e]`,`**Caplan** = RA + dust + rapidly appearing peripheral nodules; **HPS** = dilated vessels; **POPH** = constricted vessels - opposite physiology, opposite treatment. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Hepatopulmonary syndrome vs portopulmonary hypertension`,columns:[`Feature`,`Hepatopulmonary syndrome`,`Portopulmonary hypertension`],rows:[[`Basic lesion`,`Intrapulmonary vascular dilatation`,`Pulmonary arterial vasoconstriction and remodelling`],[`Mediators`,`Excess NO, CO, VEGF`,`Excess endothelin and thromboxane, low prostacyclin`],[`Prevalence in LT candidates`,`10-30%`,`5-6%`],[`Symptoms and signs`,`Cyanosis, clubbing, spider naevi, platypnoea-orthodeoxia`,`Exertional dyspnoea, syncope, loud P2, RV heave, oedema`],[`Key test`,`ABG (A-a 15 or more) plus delayed bubbles on contrast echo`,`Echo RVSP, then RHC (mPAP over 20, PVR over 2 WU, PAWP 15 or less)`],[`Treatment`,`Oxygen; liver transplant`,`PAH drugs (macitentan, sildenafil); stop beta-blockers`],[`Effect of transplant`,`Cures over 80%`,`Contraindicated if mPAP 45-50 or more despite therapy`]]},{heading:`Grading of hepatopulmonary syndrome (A-a gradient 15 mmHg or more)`,columns:[`Grade`,`PaO2 on room air`,`Implication`],rows:[[`Mild`,`80 mmHg or more`,`Observe; ABG yearly`],[`Moderate`,`60-79 mmHg`,`ABG 6-monthly; transplant assessment`],[`Severe`,`50-59 mmHg`,`Oxygen; MELD exception; expedite transplant`],[`Very severe`,`Under 50 mmHg`,`Oxygen; high post-transplant hypoxaemia risk; check MAA shunt fraction`]]},{heading:`Drugs used for the lung in systemic disease`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Oxygen (HPS)`,`To keep SpO2 88-92% or above at rest and exertion; PaO2 under 55-60 qualifies for LTOT`,`Long term, until transplant`,`Fire risk; does not reverse HPS`],[`Macitentan (POPH)`,`10 mg orally once daily`,`Long term; often with tadalafil`,`Anaemia, oedema, hepatotoxicity (check LFT); teratogenic`],[`Sildenafil (POPH)`,`20 mg orally TDS`,`Long term`,`Headache, hypotension; never with nitrates`],[`Ceftriaxone + azithromycin (ACS)`,`Ceftriaxone 2 g IV OD + azithromycin 500 mg OD`,`7-10 days`,`Allergy, QT prolongation`],[`Hydroxyurea (sickle cell)`,`15-20 mg/kg/day orally, titrate by 5 mg/kg every 8 weeks`,`Lifelong; maximum 35 mg/kg/day`,`Neutropenia, thrombocytopenia (CBC every 4-8 weeks); avoid in pregnancy`],[`Co-trimoxazole (PCP treatment)`,`TMP 15-20 mg/kg/day IV or oral in 3-4 doses`,`21 days, then prophylaxis`,`Rash, cytopenia, hyperkalaemia, raised creatinine`],[`Prednisolone (PCP adjunct)`,`40 mg BD days 1-5, 40 mg OD days 6-10, 20 mg OD days 11-21`,`If PaO2 under 70 mmHg or A-a 35 or more`,`Hyperglycaemia; start within 72 h`],[`Isoniazid TPT (before anti-TNF, in PLHIV)`,`5 mg/kg (300 mg) orally daily plus pyridoxine`,`6 months`,`Hepatitis, neuropathy`]]},{heading:`Lung involvement by systemic disease - quick differential`,columns:[`Disease`,`Typical lung lesion`,`Discriminating clue`,`Key test`],rows:[[`Cirrhosis (HPS)`,`Basal vascular dilatation`,`Orthodeoxia, spider naevi, clubbing`,`ABG, contrast echo`],[`Cirrhosis (hepatic hydrothorax)`,`Right transudative effusion`,`Ascites, no heart disease`,`Pleural fluid, exclude infection`],[`Sickle cell (ACS)`,`New segmental infiltrate`,`Fever, chest pain, crisis, tribal origin`,`CXR, HPLC, Hb trend`],[`CKD (uraemic lung)`,`Bat-wing perihilar oedema`,`Clears with dialysis`,`CXR, urea, creatinine`],[`HIV, CD4 under 200`,`PCP, atypical TB`,`Ground-glass and high LDH (PCP); lymph nodes (TB)`,`CD4, NAAT, LF-LAM, induced sputum`],[`Rheumatoid arthritis`,`Nodules, UIP-ILD, low-glucose effusion`,`Seropositive smoking man`,`HRCT, pleural fluid glucose`],[`SLE`,`Pleuritis, shrinking lung, DAH`,`Raised diaphragms with clear lungs`,`PFT, ANA, dsDNA`],[`Systemic sclerosis`,`NSIP-ILD, PAH`,`Scl-70 (ILD), anticentromere (PAH)`,`HRCT, DLCO, echo`]]},{heading:`HIV - lung infection by CD4 count`,columns:[`CD4 count (cells/microlitre)`,`Infections`,`Radiology clue`],rows:[[`Any`,`Bacterial pneumonia, TB, influenza`,`Lobar consolidation; typical TB if CD4 high`],[`Under 200`,`PCP, atypical or disseminated TB`,`Perihilar ground-glass; lower-zone TB, nodes, miliary, normal CXR`],[`Under 100`,`Cryptococcus, histoplasma, Talaromyces, toxoplasma`,`Nodules, miliary shadows, effusion`],[`Under 50`,`MAC, CMV`,`Diffuse infiltrates; often with disseminated disease`]]}],redFlags:[`SpO2 under 90% or PaO2 under 60 mmHg in a cirrhotic patient (severe HPS - expedite transplant referral)`,`Syncope, RV failure or echo RVSP over 45-50 mmHg in portal hypertension (possible POPH)`,`Sickle cell patient with fever, chest pain, tachypnoea or falling SpO2 (acute chest syndrome)`,`Falling Hb, multilobar infiltrate or SpO2 under 90-95% despite oxygen in ACS (exchange transfusion)`,`HIV with CD4 under 200 and exertional desaturation or bilateral ground-glass (PCP)`,`Haemoptysis with rising creatinine or haematuria (pulmonary-renal syndrome)`,`Stridor in rheumatoid arthritis (cricoarytenoid arthritis)`,`Rapidly progressive breathlessness in dermatomyositis (anti-MDA5 ILD)`],pearls:[`**HPS = dilated vessels; POPH = constricted vessels** - opposite physiology, opposite answer to transplant.`,`**Orthodeoxia**: PaO2 falls 4 mmHg or more (or 5%) on standing because the dilatations are basal.`,`**Bubble study**: left-heart bubbles **after 3 beats = intrapulmonary shunt**; within 3 beats = intracardiac shunt.`,`HPS severity does **not** track Child-Pugh or MELD; it can occur in non-cirrhotic portal hypertension (EHPVO).`,`**Beta-blockers and TIPS worsen POPH** - stop them.`,`ACS = **new infiltrate + fever or chest symptoms** in SCD; ceftriaxone + azithromycin, oxygen, incentive spirometry, transfuse early.`,`**Hydroxyurea halves ACS** - free under the National Sickle Cell Anaemia Elimination Mission (2023).`,`**Uraemic lung** = bat-wing oedema that clears with dialysis.`,`**Rheumatoid effusion**: glucose under 30, pH under 7.2, LDH high, RF high - mimics empyema.`,`**Caplan syndrome**: RA + mineral dust (coal, silica) + multiple peripheral nodules.`],mcqs:[{id:`respiratory-lung-systemic-disease-q1`,stem:`A 48-year-old man with alcohol-related cirrhosis complains of breathlessness that worsens when he sits up. He has spider naevi and clubbing. SpO2 is 94% lying and 88% sitting. Chest X-ray and spirometry are normal. Which test best confirms the likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Right heart catheterisation`,`Transthoracic contrast echocardiography with agitated saline`,`CT pulmonary angiography`,`Ventilation-perfusion scan`],answer:1,explanation:`Platypnoea-orthodeoxia with spider naevi and clubbing in cirrhosis suggests hepatopulmonary syndrome; agitated saline contrast echo showing bubbles in the left heart after 3 or more beats confirms intrapulmonary vascular dilatation. Right heart catheterisation is the test for portopulmonary hypertension, which does not cause orthodeoxia, and CTPA or V/Q look for embolism rather than dilated capillaries.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-lung-systemic-disease-q2`,stem:`A 40-year-old woman with cirrhosis and portal hypertension, on propranolol for varices, has syncope on exertion. Echo shows RVSP 65 mmHg. Right heart catheterisation shows mPAP 42 mmHg, PAWP 10 mmHg, PVR 6 Wood units. Which step is most appropriate?`,options:[`Increase propranolol to reduce portal pressure`,`Insert a TIPS to decompress the portal system`,`List for immediate liver transplantation without further therapy`,`Stop propranolol and start PAH-specific therapy such as macitentan`],answer:3,explanation:`This is portopulmonary hypertension (precapillary PH with portal hypertension). Non-selective beta-blockers lower cardiac output and worsen POPH, so they are stopped, and PAH drugs such as macitentan (PORTICO trial) are started. TIPS increases preload and worsens right heart failure, and with mPAP 42 and PVR 6 WU transplant is unsafe until therapy brings PVR under 5 WU.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`respiratory-lung-systemic-disease-q3`,stem:`A 17-year-old boy from a tribal district of Chhattisgarh with sickle cell disease is admitted with a painful crisis. On day 2 he develops fever 39 degrees C, chest pain and SpO2 91%, and the CXR shows a new right lower lobe infiltrate. Which management is most appropriate?`,options:[`Oxygen, IV ceftriaxone plus azithromycin, incentive spirometry, analgesia and transfusion assessment`,`Oral amoxicillin and discharge with follow-up in 2 days`,`Aggressive IV fluids at twice maintenance to reduce sickling`,`Withhold opioids completely to avoid hypoventilation`],answer:0,explanation:`This is acute chest syndrome: new infiltrate plus fever, chest pain and hypoxaemia. Treatment is oxygen to SpO2 95%, a cephalosporin plus a macrolide, incentive spirometry, careful analgesia and transfusion (exchange if severe). Oral amoxicillin and discharge ignores the leading cause of sickle death, fluid overload precipitates pulmonary oedema, and withholding opioids causes splinting and atelectasis.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`respiratory-lung-systemic-disease-q4`,stem:`A 55-year-old male smoker with seropositive rheumatoid arthritis has a left pleural effusion. Which pleural fluid profile is most typical of rheumatoid effusion?`,options:[`Transudate with glucose equal to serum`,`Exudate with high ADA and lymphocytes, glucose 80 mg/dL`,`Exudate with glucose under 30 mg/dL, pH under 7.20 and high LDH`,`Milky fluid with triglycerides over 110 mg/dL`],answer:2,explanation:`Rheumatoid effusion is a classic exudate with very low glucose (under 30 mg/dL), low pH, high LDH, high RF and low complement, and may contain cholesterol crystals. A transudate fits heart failure or hepatic hydrothorax, a high-ADA lymphocytic exudate with normal glucose fits tuberculosis, and triglycerides over 110 mg/dL define a true chylothorax rather than pseudochylothorax.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`respiratory-lung-systemic-disease-q5`,stem:`A 35-year-old man with HIV, CD4 count 90 cells/microlitre, has 3 weeks of dry cough, fever and exertional breathlessness. SpO2 is 96% at rest and 86% after walking. CXR shows bilateral perihilar ground-glass opacities; LDH is raised. What is the most likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Mycobacterium avium complex`,`Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia`,`Kaposi sarcoma`,`Pneumococcal lobar pneumonia`],answer:1,explanation:`Subacute dry cough, exertional desaturation, bilateral perihilar ground-glass and raised LDH with CD4 under 200 are classic for PCP. MAC usually occurs with CD4 under 50 and causes disseminated disease, Kaposi sarcoma shows nodular or peribronchovascular lesions often with skin or palate lesions, and pneumococcal pneumonia is acute with lobar consolidation.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`respiratory-lung-systemic-disease-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Define hepatopulmonary syndrome. Describe its pathogenesis, clinical features, diagnosis and management. How will you differentiate it from portopulmonary hypertension?`,openingLines:[`Hepatopulmonary syndrome is the triad of liver disease or portal hypertension, intrapulmonary vascular dilatation and abnormal arterial oxygenation (A-a gradient 15 mmHg or more, 20 if aged over 64) (ERS/ILTS 2016).`,`It affects 10-30% of liver transplant candidates, does not track MELD score, and is cured by liver transplantation in over 80%.`],answer:[{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Endothelin-1 via ETB receptors raises eNOS; bacterial translocation brings macrophage iNOS and haem oxygenase (CO).`,`VEGF angiogenesis; capillaries dilate from 8-15 to up to 500 micrometres.`,`Diffusion-perfusion defect (corrects with 100% oxygen) plus true shunts; basal predominance explains orthodeoxia.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Progressive exertional dyspnoea, platypnoea, cyanosis, clubbing, spider naevi.`,`Orthodeoxia: PaO2 falls 5% or 4 mmHg on standing.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Pulse oximetry screen (SpO2 under 96%), ABG sitting on air for A-a gradient.`,`Agitated saline contrast echo: left-heart bubbles after 3 or more beats.`,`Tc-99m MAA scan brain uptake over 6% when lung disease co-exists; exclude COPD, effusion, ascites.`,`Grade: mild 80 or more, moderate 60-79, severe 50-59, very severe under 50 mmHg.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Long-term oxygen for hypoxaemia; no proven drug; TIPS not a treatment.`,`Liver transplant; MELD exception if PaO2 under 60; PaO2 under 44-50 predicts post-transplant hypoxaemia.`,`Follow ABG 6-monthly while waiting.`]},{heading:`HPS vs POPH`,points:[`HPS: dilated low-resistance vessels, orthodeoxia, positive bubble study, cured by transplant.`,`POPH: mPAP over 20, PVR over 2 WU, PAWP 15 or less; PAH drugs; stop beta-blockers; transplant contraindicated if mPAP 45-50 or more.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart of HPS pathogenesis (portal hypertension, NO/CO/VEGF, dilatation, hypoxaemia)`,`Comparison table HPS vs POPH`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and pathogenesis`,marks:3},{part:`Clinical features and diagnosis`,marks:3},{part:`Management`,marks:2},{part:`Differentiation from POPH`,marks:2}],keywords:[`intrapulmonary vascular dilatation`,`A-a gradient 15`,`orthodeoxia`,`contrast echo`,`MAA scan`,`liver transplant`]},{id:`respiratory-lung-systemic-disease-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on acute chest syndrome in sickle cell disease.`,openingLines:[`Acute chest syndrome is a new segmental pulmonary infiltrate with fever, chest pain, tachypnoea, wheeze, cough or hypoxaemia in a patient with sickle cell disease (NHLBI 2014).`,`It is the leading cause of death in sickle cell disease, which is concentrated in the tribal belt of central and western India.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes`,points:[`Infection (Chlamydophila, Mycoplasma, viruses), fat embolism from marrow infarction, in situ thrombosis, hypoventilation from rib infarcts or opioids.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Oxygen to SpO2 95%, incentive spirometry, careful analgesia, maintenance fluids only.`,`Ceftriaxone plus azithromycin; bronchodilator if wheeze; LMWH prophylaxis.`,`Simple transfusion if Hb more than 1 g/dL below baseline; exchange to HbS under 30% if severe.`]},{heading:`Prevention`,points:[`Hydroxyurea 15-35 mg/kg/day halves ACS; vaccines, penicillin prophylaxis in children.`,`National Sickle Cell Anaemia Elimination Mission 2023: screening, genetic cards, free hydroxyurea.`]}],mustDraw:[`Vicious-cycle diagram: hypoxia, sickling, vaso-occlusion, V/Q mismatch`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and causes`,marks:2},{part:`Management`,marks:2},{part:`Prevention and programme`,marks:1}],keywords:[`new infiltrate`,`exchange transfusion`,`HbS under 30%`,`hydroxyurea`,`NSCAEM`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`ERS Task Force / International Liver Transplant Society (ILTS) Practice Guidelines: Diagnosis and Management of Hepatopulmonary Syndrome and Portopulmonary Hypertension, 2016`,`2022 ESC/ERS Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Treatment of Pulmonary Hypertension`,`NHLBI Evidence-Based Management of Sickle Cell Disease: Expert Panel Report, 2014; British Society for Haematology Guideline on Management of Acute Chest Syndrome in Sickle Cell Disease, 2015`,`National Sickle Cell Anaemia Elimination Mission, Ministry of Health and Family Welfare and Ministry of Tribal Affairs, Government of India, 2023`,`NACO National Guidelines for HIV Care and Treatment, 2021; NIH Guidelines for Prevention and Treatment of Opportunistic Infections in Adults with HIV, 2024`,`Light RW. Pleural Diseases, 6th edition, 2013`,`ACR/CHEST Guideline for Screening and Monitoring of Interstitial Lung Disease in Systemic Autoimmune Rheumatic Diseases, 2023`]});var f=[...e,...t,...n,...r,...i,...a,...o,...s,...c,...l,...u,...d];export{f as default};